window.KB_ENRICHMENT = {"version":"gold67","diseases":140,"enriched":140,"referenceDiseases":108,"totalSearchable":248,"byId":{"ACUTE_BRONCHITIS":{"name":"Acute Bronchitis","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e printed pp.21, 252-253, 272-273, 1024-1025, 1537, 1595","clinicalPearls":["Defined as a dry or productive cough lasting <3 weeks, occurring without clinical or radiographic signs of pneumonia; it is most common during winter (p.1537).","Sputum purulence does not distinguish viral from bacterial cause; in acute bronchitis the cough is predominantly viral, whereas bacteria play a larger role in chronic bronchitis (p.1537).","Among acute respiratory infections, only acute otitis media, sinusitis, streptococcal pharyngitis, and pneumonia warrant antibiotics; acute bronchitis is not on that list (p.252).","Acute bronchitis and sinusitis are common at all stages of HIV infection, with more severe disease at lower CD4+ counts (p.1595).","An individual episode of acute bronchitis does not typically cause lasting reductions in pulmonary function (p.1537 context; long-term lung-function data p. respiratory infections section)."],"pathophysiology":"Acute bronchitis is predominantly a viral infection of the lower airways below the vocal cords, caused by common respiratory viruses; the inflammatory airway response produces cough that may be dry or productive, without parenchymal consolidation. Bacterial involvement is uncommon, in contrast to chronic bronchitis where bacteria contribute more prominently (p.1537).","additionalDifferentials":["Community-acquired pneumonia (distinguished by a new infiltrate on chest radiography and focal exam findings) (p.1024).","Acute exacerbation of chronic bronchitis / COPD (p.1024).","Heart failure (p.1024).","Pulmonary embolism (p.1024).","Influenza and nonspecific upper respiratory infection, which overlap clinically with lower-tract infection (p.252)."],"infectionMimics":["Pneumonia (cough plus sputum, dyspnea, chest pain, fever, and a new infiltrate point to pneumonia rather than bronchitis) (p.1537, p.1024).","Influenza (overlapping cough and systemic features; can be complicated by secondary bacterial infection) (p.1537).","Pertussis / subacute postinfectious tracheobronchitis (a frequent cause of cough persisting 3-8 weeks) (p.272)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute coronary syndrome / myocardial infarction presenting as cough and dyspnea, illustrated by a fatal case in which exertional dyspnea and chest heaviness were prematurely labeled acute bronchitis (p.21).","Cough-variant asthma (p.272).","Gastroesophageal reflux (p.272).","Rhinosinusitis with postnasal drainage (p.272).","ACE-inhibitor-induced cough (p.272).","Inhalation of noxious chemicals or smoke, and aspiration, as causes of acute cough (p.272)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiography should be obtained whenever pneumonia is suspected, to confirm the absence of an infiltrate that defines acute bronchitis (p.1537).","Specific viral diagnosis is generally unnecessary but, when needed, RT-PCR/multiplex respiratory panels are the most sensitive tests; results must be interpreted cautiously because viral genome can persist for weeks after a resolved infection (p.1537)."],"redFlags":["Dyspnea, especially exertional dyspnea or chest heaviness/pain, which should trigger consideration of cardiac or thromboembolic disease rather than acute bronchitis (p.21).","Cough accompanied by sputum, fever, chest pain, or focal chest signs suggesting pneumonia, prompting chest radiography (p.1537).","Hemoptysis or weight loss (raising concern for malignancy or other serious pulmonary disease) (p.272).","Cough persisting beyond a few weeks, warranting reassessment and chest radiography for serious causes (sarcoidosis, lymphoma, lung cancer, tuberculosis) (p.272-273)."],"prognosis":"Acute bronchitis is a self-limited illness; the cough usually resolves within about 3 weeks, and antibiotics neither speed resolution nor prevent complications in most acute respiratory infections. A dry, irritative postbronchitic cough may linger for more than 2 months, particularly in winter, and a residual subacute cough of 3-8 weeks is common after tracheobronchitis (p.252, p.272-273).","pitfalls":["Prescribing antibiotics for acute bronchitis is inappropriate stewardship; roughly half of ambulatory antibiotic prescriptions for acute respiratory infections are unnecessary, and antibiotics cause adverse effects, C. difficile infection, microbiome disruption, and resistance (p.252).","Treating purulent sputum as evidence of bacterial infection is a mistake, since purulence does not reliably indicate bacterial etiology in acute bronchitis (p.1537).","Premature closure: relying on a rapid 'URI/bronchitis' protocol can cause clinicians to miss life-threatening conditions such as myocardial infarction; a patient who does not look 'sick' may be wrongly assumed to have a benign illness (p.21).","Not eliciting a full dyspnea history (exertional onset, chest heaviness, relief with rest) can lead to a dangerous misdiagnosis of acute bronchitis (p.21)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.21","Harrison 22e p.252","Harrison 22e p.253","Harrison 22e p.272","Harrison 22e p.273","Harrison 22e p.1024","Harrison 22e p.1537","Harrison 22e p.1595"],"crossLinks":["CAP","URTI","COPD_EXACERBATION"]},"AMOEBIC_LIVER_ABSCESS":{"name":"Amoebic Liver Abscess","system":"Hepatobiliary / Tropical Fever","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1798-1801 (Ch. 230, Amebiasis)","clinicalPearls":["Most patients are febrile with right-upper-quadrant pain that may be dull or pleuritic and may radiate to the shoulder; point tenderness over the liver and a right-sided pleural effusion are common, whereas jaundice is rare (p.1799).","Although the colon is the initial site of infection, fewer than one-third of patients with an amebic abscess have active diarrhea at presentation (p.1799).","Strongly age- and sex-dependent: men aged 30-60 are infected at a rate roughly 10-12 times that of women in the same age group; testosterone-modulated NK-T-cell interferon-gamma signaling is implicated experimentally (p.1798).","Young patients tend to present acutely with prominent symptoms of under 10 days' duration, while older patients from endemic areas more often follow a subacute course over months with weight loss and hepatomegaly; only about one-third of those with chronic presentations are febrile (p.1799).","Of travelers who develop an amebic liver abscess after leaving an endemic area, 95% do so within 5 months (p.1799).","Amebic liver abscesses are always preceded by intestinal colonization, which may be entirely asymptomatic (p.1799).","On imaging, more than 80% of patients symptomatic for over 10 days have a single right-lobe abscess, but about 50% of those symptomatic for under 10 days have multiple abscesses (p.1800).","The classic necrotic 'anchovy paste' contents are variable in color and bacteriologically sterile, composed of granular debris with few or no cells; amebae, when present, sit near the abscess capsule rather than in the aspirable necrotic center (p.1799).","Liver enzymes are usually normal or only minimally elevated even with large abscesses; alkaline phosphatase is the most commonly elevated value, while aminotransferase elevation suggests acute disease or a complication (p.1800).","Invasive amebiasis does not elicit eosinophilia (p.1800)."],"pathophysiology":"Entamoeba histolytica trophozoites invade colonic mucosal venules and travel via the portal venous system to the liver; the organism is resistant to complement-mediated lysis, which is critical to its survival in the bloodstream. An early acute infiltrate (predominantly neutrophils) is followed by lysis of those neutrophils on contact with amebae, and the released neutrophil toxins are thought to contribute to hepatocyte necrosis. The liver parenchyma is progressively replaced by necrotic material surrounded by a thin rim of congested liver tissue, producing the classically described (but variably colored) 'anchovy paste' that is sterile and largely acellular, with amebae found mainly at the capsule. Genetically, a leptin-receptor substitution (Q223R) increases the risk of amebic liver abscess in adults (p.1798-1799).","additionalDifferentials":["Pyogenic liver abscess (the most important imaging-based distinction once a hepatic abscess is identified) (p.1801)","Malaria (p.1801)","Typhoid/enteric fever (p.1801)","Pulmonary disease (p.1801)","Gallbladder disease (p.1801)","Any febrile illness with few localizing signs / fever of unknown origin, since 10-15% present with fever alone (p.1799, p.1801)"],"infectionMimics":["Pyogenic liver abscess (p.1801)","Malaria (p.1801)","Typhoid (enteric) fever (p.1801)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Gallbladder disease (p.1801)","Pulmonary disease presenting with right-sided pleural/pleuritic findings (p.1801)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum alkaline phosphatase and aminotransferases (enzymes often normal/minimally elevated; raised aminotransferases suggest acute disease or complication) (p.1800)","Full blood count for leukocytosis >10,000/uL, present in about three-quarters of patients, especially with acute or complicated disease (p.1800)","Enzyme immunoassay for the Gal/GalNAc lectin antigen, usable on stool and on liver-abscess aspirates (p.1800)","Multiplex PCR for E. histolytica DNA (near 100% sensitivity/specificity) (p.1800)","Loop-mediated isothermal amplification (LAMP) on abscess pus as a low-cost molecular option (p.1800)","Diagnostic aspirate sent for Gram stain and bacterial culture when distinguishing amebic from pyogenic abscess (p.1801)"],"redFlags":["Pleuropulmonary involvement (reported in 20-30%, the most frequent complication): contiguous spread or frank rupture into the pleural space, the latter requiring drainage (p.1799).","Large abscess (>10 cm) in the superior right lobe, which may rupture into the pleural space (p.1800).","Left-lobe abscess, which may rupture into the pericardium, the complication carrying the gravest prognosis and able to occur even during medical therapy (p.1799-1800).","Rupture into the peritoneum, presenting as an indolent leak or as an acute abdomen (p.1799).","Hepatobronchial fistula causing cough productive of large amounts of necrotic material (p.1799).","Lack of clinical response within 3-5 days or threat of imminent rupture (p.1801)."],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and treatment, mortality from uncomplicated amebic liver abscess is under 1%, and over 90% of patients respond dramatically with reduced pain and fever within 72 hours; recurrence is rare. Abscesses resolve slowly and may even enlarge despite clinical improvement, so follow-up ultrasonography can be misleading; complete resolution within 6 months is expected in about two-thirds of patients, while roughly 10% have persistent imaging abnormalities for up to a year. A hepatobronchial fistula carries a good prognosis, whereas pericardial rupture (usually from left-lobe abscesses) carries the gravest prognosis (p.1799-1801).","pitfalls":["Expecting concurrent dysentery: fewer than one-third of patients have active diarrhea, so a negative stool examination or absent diarrhea does not exclude the diagnosis (p.1799).","Trophozoites are rarely recovered from liver aspirates because they reside in the abscess capsule, not in the necrotic center that is actually aspirated (p.1799).","Up to 10% of patients with acute amebic liver abscess have negative serology early; titers usually become positive within a week (p.1799).","Microscopy cannot distinguish E. histolytica cysts from non-pathogenic E. dispar, E. moshkovskii, or E. bangladeshi; species-specific diagnosis on microscopy requires seeing trophozoites that have ingested erythrocytes (p.1799).","In endemic areas serology may stay positive for years (indirect hemagglutination up to ~10 years), complicating interpretation (p.1799).","Follow-up imaging may be confusing because abscesses resolve slowly and can transiently enlarge despite a good clinical response (p.1800)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.1798","Harrison 22e p.1799","Harrison 22e p.1800","Harrison 22e p.1801"],"crossLinks":["LIVER_ABSCESS","MALARIA","ENTERIC_FEVER"]},"ASPIRATION_PNEUMONIA":{"name":"Aspiration Pneumonia","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1066-1067, 1072, 1077, 1190, 1418","clinicalPearls":["Aspiration pneumonia is best viewed as a point on a continuum that also includes community-acquired and hospital-acquired pneumonia, defined by a phenotype with macroaspiration risk factors and involvement of characteristic gravity-dependent lung locations (p.1066).","Aspiration pneumonia is estimated to account for roughly 5-15% of community-acquired pneumonia cases; reliable figures for the hospital-acquired setting are not available (p.1066).","The historical view that aspiration pneumonia is caused mainly by anaerobes has shifted: when acquired in the community or hospital, the likely pathogens mirror those of CAP or HAP, with anaerobes still relevant mainly in poor dentition, lung abscess, necrotizing pneumonia, or empyema (p.1067).","Anaerobic aspiration infections tend to follow an indolent course, a clinical clue that helps separate them from more acutely presenting conditions such as chemical pneumonitis or pneumococcal pneumonia (p.1418).","Sputum in bacterial aspiration pneumonia is characteristically NOT malodorous unless the process has been ongoing for at least about a week (p.1418).","Expectorated sputum is unreliable for anaerobic culture because of inevitable contamination by normal oral microbiota; reliable anaerobic specimens historically required transtracheal or transthoracic aspiration, techniques rarely used now (p.1418).","In CAP overall, no specific etiology is identified in more than half of cases despite careful history, examination, and imaging (p.1067)."],"pathophysiology":"Macroaspiration of oropharyngeal or gastric contents delivers a high microbial burden directly into the lower airways; lung involvement reflects this rather than invasion of a sterile space, since the lungs normally harbor a microbiota. Mechanical defenses (nasal hairs/turbinates, branching airways, mucociliary clearance, gag and cough reflexes) reduce but do not fully block bacterial access to the lower respiratory tract. Bacterial aspiration pneumonia develops over several days to weeks (not hours) through a combination of increased bacterial burden, increased virulence of aspirated organisms, and potential airway damage from aspirated gastric fluid. This contrasts with chemical pneumonitis (Mendelson syndrome), where aspirated acidic gastric juice causes rapid, direct destruction of the alveolar lining with fluid transudation into the alveolar space within hours (p.1066, p.1418).","additionalDifferentials":["Foreign-body/food aspiration causing major-airway obstruction with atelectasis and nonspecific inflammation, managed by removal rather than antibiotics (p.1418)","Chemical (acid) pneumonitis, e.g., Mendelson syndrome from regurgitated gastric contents (p.1418)","Pneumococcal pneumonia, distinguished by its more acute presentation (p.1418)","Lung abscess (subacute anaerobic infection) as a related/overlapping entity (p.1072, p.1418)"],"infectionMimics":["Pneumococcal pneumonia presenting more acutely than indolent anaerobic aspiration infection (p.1418)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Chemical pneumonitis (Mendelson syndrome) from aspiration of acidic gastric juices, with rapid alveolar injury and pulmonary edema, often without fever and frequently needing no antibiotics (p.1418)","Foreign-body/food aspiration with airway obstruction and atelectasis (p.1418)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiography looking for consolidation in dependent lung segments (basilar lower-lobe segments if aspiration occurred upright; posterior segment of the upper lobe or superior segment of the lower lobe if supine) (p.1418)","Gram stain of sputum, which characteristically shows a mixed bacterial population with many polymorphonuclear leukocytes (p.1418)","Bronchoscopic protected-brush specimens or bronchoalveolar lavage, which may recover anaerobes from the lower tract with less oropharyngeal contamination (role still being defined) (p.1418)","Assessment of dentition/oral hygiene, given the association of severe dental disease with aspiration pneumonia (p.1418)"],"redFlags":["Gross hemoptysis suggesting necrotizing pneumonia (p.1067)","Progression to respiratory failure; in chemical pneumonitis a chest x-ray can evolve from normal to a complete bilateral whiteout within 8-24 hours (p.1067, p.1418)","Septic shock and organ failure in severe pneumonia (p.1067)","Weight loss and anemia indicating a more chronic process (p.1418)","Development of lung abscess or complicated/empyematous pleural effusion (pleural fluid pH <7.2, glucose <2.2 mmol/L, or LDH >1000 U/L, or organisms seen/cultured indicates need for drainage) (p.1072)"],"prognosis":null,"pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish chemical pneumonitis (Mendelson syndrome) from bacterial aspiration pneumonia: chemical pneumonitis is a non-infectious acid injury that usually resolves with supportive care and does NOT warrant antibiotics unless bacterial superinfection develops (p.1418).","Routinely covering anaerobes for all aspiration pneumonia: anaerobic coverage is unnecessary unless dentition is poor or there is lung abscess or necrotizing pneumonia (p.1070).","Expecting an acute, foul-smelling presentation: bacterial aspiration pneumonia evolves over days to weeks and sputum is usually not malodorous until the process has lasted about a week (p.1418).","Relying on expectorated sputum for anaerobic culture, which is invalid because of oral-flora contamination (p.1418)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.1066","Harrison 22e p.1067","Harrison 22e p.1070","Harrison 22e p.1072","Harrison 22e p.1077","Harrison 22e p.1190","Harrison 22e p.1418"],"crossLinks":["LUNG_ABSCESS","CAP","HAP"]},"BRAIN_ABSCESS":{"name":"Brain Abscess","system":"Central Nervous System","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1178-1181","clinicalPearls":["The classic triad of headache, fever, and a focal neurologic deficit is present in fewer than half of cases, so its absence does not exclude the diagnosis (p.1178).","Headache is the single most common symptom (more than 75% of patients), typically a constant, dull ache that becomes progressively more severe and refractory to treatment (p.1178).","Fever is present in only about half of patients at diagnosis; its absence should not be used to rule out a brain abscess (p.1178).","Most patients present a relatively long time after symptom onset (commonly around 11-12 days), and a brain abscess usually behaves like an expanding mass lesion rather than an obvious infection (p.1178).","Meningismus is generally absent unless the abscess has ruptured into the ventricle or infection has reached the subarachnoid space (p.1178).","Diffusion-weighted MRI helps distinguish abscess from tumor: an abscess shows restricted diffusion (high DWI signal, low ADC) within the cavity, whereas necrotic tumors typically do not (p.1178).","Lesion location predicts the source and the deficit: otogenic abscesses favor the temporal lobe and cerebellum, while sinus and dental abscesses favor the frontal lobes (p.1179).","New-onset partial seizures are the most common manifestation of neurocysticercosis, an important focal CNS lesion in patients from Latin America (p.1181)."],"pathophysiology":"Intact brain parenchyma is relatively resistant to infection; bacterial invasion generally requires preexisting or concomitant ischemia, necrosis, or hypoxemia in brain tissue. Abscesses arise by direct contiguous spread (sinusitis, otitis media, mastoiditis, dental infection), by inoculation after penetrating trauma or neurosurgery, or by hematogenous seeding from a distant focus; about a quarter are cryptogenic with no identified source. Hematogenous abscesses are often multiple, frequently lie at the gray-white junction in the middle cerebral artery territory, and tend to be poorly encapsulated. Once established, the lesion evolves through stages: early cerebritis (days 1-3, perivascular inflammation around a core of coagulative necrosis with marked edema), late cerebritis (days 4-9, expanding necrotic center with macrophage/fibroblast infiltrate and a developing thin capsule), early capsule formation (days 10-13, capsule better formed on the cortical than ventricular side, correlating with the ring-enhancing appearance on imaging), and late capsule formation (day 14 onward, dense collagenous capsule, regressing edema, and surrounding gliosis with reactive astrocytes that may underlie later seizures). In right-to-left cardiac or pulmonary shunts, bacteria bypass the pulmonary capillary filter while shunt-related hypoxemia and polycythemia create ischemic nidi favoring abscess formation (p.1178-1179).","additionalDifferentials":["Subdural empyema","Bacterial meningitis","Viral meningoencephalitis","Superior sagittal sinus thrombosis","Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis","Primary or metastatic brain tumor (the major consideration when fever is absent)","Cerebral infarction or hematoma (can resemble abscess on CT/MRI)","Neurocysticercosis","Tuberculoma (a major cause of focal CNS mass lesions in India and East Asia)"],"infectionMimics":["Subdural empyema","Bacterial meningitis","Viral meningoencephalitis","Cerebral toxoplasmosis (reactivation, mainly in immunocompromised/HIV hosts)","Neurocysticercosis","Tuberculoma","Nocardial, Aspergillus, Candida, or Cryptococcus CNS lesions in immunocompromised hosts"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Primary or metastatic brain tumor","Cerebral infarction","Intracerebral hematoma","Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis","Superior sagittal sinus thrombosis"],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI is preferred over CT, being superior for detecting early (cerebritis-stage) lesions and posterior fossa abscesses (p.1178).","Diffusion-weighted imaging with apparent diffusion coefficient mapping to separate abscess (restricted diffusion) from tumor (p.1178).","Gram stain plus aerobic, anaerobic, mycobacterial, and fungal cultures of abscess material obtained by CT-guided stereotactic needle aspiration (p.1179).","Inflammatory markers: peripheral leukocytosis in about half, elevated ESR in about 60%, and elevated C-reactive protein in about 80% of patients (p.1179).","Serum IgG antibody to Toxoplasma gondii when imaging suggests toxoplasmosis (p.1181).","Enzyme-linked immunotransfer blot as a confirmatory test for neurocysticercosis, plus funduscopic examination in suspected cases (p.1181)."],"redFlags":["Lumbar puncture should NOT be performed when a focal intracranial infection such as abscess or empyema is known or suspected, because CSF analysis adds nothing and LP increases the risk of herniation (p.1179).","Signs of raised intracranial pressure (papilledema, nausea/vomiting, drowsiness or confusion) can dominate the presentation, particularly with cerebellar abscesses (p.1178).","Rupture of an abscess into the ventricle (heralded by new meningismus) is an ominous development (p.1178).","High risk (about 35%) of focal or generalized seizures (p.1180).","Glucocorticoids and prior treatment can alter the CT/MRI appearance of the capsule, potentially masking or modifying the imaging picture (p.1178)."],"prognosis":"With modern neuroimaging, stereotactic aspiration, and improved antibiotics, mortality has fallen to typically less than 15% in recent series. However, significant sequelae - including seizures, persisting weakness, aphasia, or mental impairment - occur in at least 20% of survivors (p.1180).","pitfalls":["Relying on the classic triad: headache, fever, and focal deficit coexist in fewer than half of patients (p.1178).","Excluding the diagnosis because the patient is afebrile - fever is absent in roughly half of cases (p.1178).","Performing a lumbar puncture in a patient with a suspected focal intracranial infection, risking herniation without diagnostic benefit (p.1179).","Misreading a ring-enhancing lesion as tumor when diffusion-weighted imaging would point to abscess (p.1178).","Forgetting that glucocorticoid therapy can alter the imaging appearance of the abscess capsule (p.1178).","Overlooking cerebral toxoplasmosis, which characteristically reactivates and causes ring-enhancing lesions in HIV-infected and other immunocompromised patients (p.1181).","Dismissing a cryptogenic abscess as having no source - many are in fact attributable to occult dental infection (p.1179)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison describes a staged histopathologic/radiologic evolution rather than a clinical severity score: early cerebritis (days 1-3), late cerebritis (days 4-9), early capsule formation (days 10-13, when ring enhancement appears), and late capsule formation (day 14 and beyond) (p.1178).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1178","Harrison 22e p.1179","Harrison 22e p.1180","Harrison 22e p.1181"],"crossLinks":["MENINGITIS","SINUSITIS","brain_tumour","IE"]},"BRONCHIECTASIS_EXACERBATION":{"name":"Bronchiectasis Exacerbation","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"p.2242-2244","clinicalPearls":["Bronchiectasis is irreversible airway dilation, classically categorised as cylindrical/tubular (most common), varicose, or cystic; it may be focal or diffuse (Harrison 22e p.2242).","An acute exacerbation is defined primarily by a change in sputum character - increased volume and increased purulence - rather than by classic infection signs; fever and new infiltrates may be absent (Harrison 22e p.2243).","The usual clinical picture between exacerbations is a persistent productive cough with thick, tenacious sputum, often with crackles, wheeze, and sometimes digital clubbing (Harrison 22e p.2243).","Pseudomonas aeruginosa preferentially colonises damaged airways and evades host defences; its colonisation marks more severe disease and worse outcomes (Harrison 22e p.2242-2243, p.2244).","The pattern of lung-field involvement on imaging hints at the underlying cause: upper-zone in CF and post-radiation fibrosis, lower-zone in chronic aspiration/end-stage fibrosis/immunodeficiency, midlung in NTM (MAC) and dyskinetic cilia syndrome, and central-airway in ABPA (Harrison 22e p.2242-2243).","MAC-related bronchiectasis classically affects nonsmoking women over 50 years of age (Harrison 22e p.2242).","Up to 25-50% of patients referred for bronchiectasis have idiopathic disease after workup (Harrison 22e p.2242)."],"pathophysiology":"The dominant mechanism of infectious bronchiectasis is the 'vicious cycle hypothesis': susceptibility to infection plus impaired mucociliary clearance permits microbial colonisation of the bronchial tree; the persistent microbial presence incites chronic inflammation that damages the airway wall, which further impairs secretion and microbial clearance, perpetuating the infectious-inflammatory cycle. Some organisms, notably Pseudomonas aeruginosa, are especially adept at colonising damaged airways and evading host defences. Impaired mucociliary clearance may be inherited (e.g., cystic fibrosis, dyskinetic cilia syndrome), and a single severe infection such as Bordetella pertussis or Mycoplasma pneumoniae can cause enough airway damage to seed the cycle. Pathologically there is small-airway wall inflammation with larger-airway wall destruction and dilation, and loss of elastin, smooth muscle, and cartilage; inflammatory cells release proteases, reactive oxygen species, and proinflammatory cytokines that damage larger airways while ongoing small-airway inflammation produces airflow obstruction. Antiproteases such as alpha-1 antitrypsin help neutralise neutrophil elastase, and bronchiectasis/emphysema occur in alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency. Molecular endotypes with distinct inflammatory and microbiome signatures may correlate with exacerbation risk. Noninfectious mechanisms include immune-mediated bronchial wall damage (e.g., Sjogren's syndrome, rheumatoid arthritis) and traction from parenchymal fibrosis. (Harrison 22e p.2242-2243)","additionalDifferentials":["COPD - airflow obstruction on pulmonary function testing overlaps with bronchiectasis at presentation (Harrison 22e p.2243)","Asthma - may coexist; ABPA should be considered in refractory asthma (Harrison 22e p.2242-2243)","Cystic fibrosis - should be excluded early in any diffuse bronchiectasis workup (Harrison 22e p.2243)","Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (central-airway-predominant) (Harrison 22e p.2242-2243)"],"infectionMimics":["Nontuberculous mycobacterial (especially MAC) airway infection - organisms may be colonisers rather than true pathogens, complicating interpretation of positive cultures (Harrison 22e p.2243)","Active or reactivated tuberculosis (in endemic areas, a cause and mimic of recurrent airway infection) (Harrison 22e p.2242)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Traction bronchiectasis from underlying lung fibrosis (e.g., idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, post-radiation fibrosis) - dilated airways from parenchymal distortion rather than active infection (Harrison 22e p.2242-2243)","Autoimmune/rheumatologic airway disease (rheumatoid arthritis, Sjogren's syndrome, inflammatory bowel disease) (Harrison 22e p.2242)","Recurrent aspiration (e.g., from esophageal motility disorders such as scleroderma) (Harrison 22e p.2242)"],"additionalInvestigations":["High-resolution chest CT - imaging modality of choice; more specific than chest radiograph; look for airway dilation as 'tram tracks' or the 'signet-ring sign' (airway diameter at least 1.5x the adjacent vessel), lack of bronchial tapering, bronchial wall thickening, and inspissated secretions in a 'tree-in-bud' pattern (Harrison 22e p.2243)","Bronchoscopy - almost always required in focal bronchiectasis to exclude airway obstruction by mass or foreign body; consider with BAL in diffuse disease if no pathogen is identified (Harrison 22e p.2243)","Pulmonary function testing - functional assessment, typically showing mild-to-moderate airflow obstruction (Harrison 22e p.2243)","Etiologic workup for diffuse disease: sputum stains/cultures for bacteria, acid-fast bacilli, and fungi; immunoglobulin levels and CBC with differential; HIV testing; sweat chloride and genetic testing for CF; alpha-1 antitrypsin level; nasal/respiratory brush or biopsy for ciliary dyskinesia; autoimmune serologies; ABPA workup (Aspergillus skin test/precipitins, serum IgE, eosinophils); swallowing/neuromuscular assessment (Harrison 22e p.2242)"],"redFlags":["Hemoptysis - bronchiectatic airways are dilated, inflamed, and highly vascular (supplied by the high-pressure bronchial circulation), so exacerbations can produce massive, life-threatening hemoptysis (Harrison 22e p.2243-2244, p.1408 hemoptysis chapter)","Massive hemoptysis - may require intubation to stabilise the patient, protection of the nonbleeding lung, and bronchial artery embolisation or surgery (Harrison 22e p.2244)","Emergence of antibiotic-resistant organisms after recurrent infections and repeated antibiotic courses (Harrison 22e p.2244)"],"prognosis":"Outcomes vary widely with the underlying etiology and comorbidities, and are influenced by exacerbation frequency and, in infectious disease, the specific pathogens involved - Pseudomonas aeruginosa colonisation is associated with worse outcomes. Lung-function decline in non-CF bronchiectasis can resemble that of COPD, with FEV1 falling roughly 50-55 mL/year versus 20-30 mL/year in healthy controls. (Harrison 22e p.2244)","pitfalls":["Expecting classic infection signs: an exacerbation may lack fever or new infiltrates, so reliance on these can miss it - the defining change is sputum volume and purulence (Harrison 22e p.2243).","Chest radiographs lack sensitivity for bronchiectasis; a normal or unremarkable film does not exclude it, and CT is needed for confirmation (Harrison 22e p.2243).","Positive NTM cultures may reflect colonisation rather than true infection; consensus diagnostic criteria (e.g., two positive sputum cultures, or one positive BAL, plus symptoms and radiographic findings) should be applied before attributing disease (Harrison 22e p.2243).","Focal bronchiectasis should prompt bronchoscopy to exclude an obstructing mass or foreign body rather than being attributed to infection alone (Harrison 22e p.2243)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.2242","Harrison 22e p.2243","Harrison 22e p.2244"],"crossLinks":["COPD_EXACERBATION","PULMONARY_TB","CAP"]},"CAP":{"name":"Community Acquired Pneumonia (non-severe)","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1066-1072","clinicalPearls":["Typical and atypical pathogens cannot be reliably separated on clinical or radiographic grounds because both can produce overlapping syndromes; atypicals (Mycoplasma, Chlamydia, Legionella) are not seen on Gram stain or grown on standard media and are intrinsically resistant to beta-lactams (Harrison 22e p.1067).","In more than half of CAP cases no specific pathogen is identified despite history, examination, and imaging; in one large emergency-department series an etiology was established in under 8% (Harrison 22e p.1068).","Physical examination has limited diagnostic accuracy in pneumonia (sensitivity ~58%, specificity ~67%), so a compatible clinical picture plus a new infiltrate on chest film remains the diagnostic standard (Harrison 22e p.1068).","In the elderly the presentation may be subtle, manifesting as new or worsening confusion or decompensation of a chronic illness with few classic respiratory findings (Harrison 22e p.1068).","Gross hemoptysis raises concern for necrotizing pneumonia, such as that caused by community-associated MRSA (Harrison 22e p.1067).","Up to about 20% of patients have gastrointestinal symptoms (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea) in addition to constitutional and pulmonary findings (Harrison 22e p.1067-1068).","Aspiration pneumonia sits on a continuum with CAP/HAP and accounts for roughly 5-15% of CAP cases; modern organisms mirror the usual CAP/HAP flora, with anaerobes mainly relevant in poor dentition, lung abscess, necrotizing pneumonia, or empyema (Harrison 22e p.1066-1067).","Epidemiologic exposures can flag specific organisms (e.g. birds for Chlamydia psittaci/Histoplasma, recent hotel or cruise stay for Legionella, local influenza activity for influenza/pneumococcus/S. aureus) (Harrison 22e p.1067).","CAP can be complicated by cardiovascular events (heart failure, arrhythmia, myocardial infarction, stroke), especially in the elderly with pneumococcal or influenza pneumonia, attributed to increased myocardial load and inflammation-driven plaque destabilization, and these events may occur acutely or up to a year later (Harrison 22e p.1072)."],"pathophysiology":"Pneumonia results from proliferation of microbial pathogens in the alveoli together with the host inflammatory response. Organisms reach the lower respiratory tract chiefly by microaspiration of oropharyngeal flora, but also by inhalation and direct mucosal spread; mechanical defenses (nasal hairs/turbinates, branching airways, mucociliary clearance, gag and cough reflexes) are imperfect barriers. Rather than simple invasion of a sterile space, current models view pneumonia as an emergent disturbance of the lung microbiota driven by self-reinforcing positive-feedback loops: an inciting epithelial/endothelial injury (often a viral infection plus microaspiration) releases cytokines, chemokines, and catecholamines that can selectively favor growth of organisms such as S. pneumoniae and P. aeruginosa, amplifying inflammation. When innate and adaptive immunity fail to contain this, mediators (IL-6 and TNF cause fever; IL-8 and G-CSF recruit neutrophils) drive an alveolar-capillary leak producing hypoxemia and radiographic infiltrates. Bacterial toxins amplify inflammation, and some pathogens blunt hypoxic vasoconstriction, worsening hypoxemia; reduced compliance, increased secretions, raised respiratory drive, and intrapulmonary shunting cause dyspnea and, when severe, respiratory failure (Harrison 22e p.1066). Pathologically, classic (lobar pneumococcal) pneumonia passes through edema, red hepatization, gray hepatization, and resolution; a bronchopneumonia pattern predominates in nosocomial disease while a lobar pattern is more typical of bacterial CAP, and viral pneumonias are alveolar rather than interstitial processes (Harrison 22e p.1066).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute bronchitis (Harrison 22e p.1068)","Acute exacerbation of chronic bronchitis/COPD (Harrison 22e p.1068)","Tuberculosis (suggested by an upper-lobe cavitating lesion) (Harrison 22e p.1068)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Heart failure / pulmonary edema (Harrison 22e p.1068, p.1072)","Pulmonary embolism / pulmonary infarction (Harrison 22e p.1068, p.1072)","Lung carcinoma (consider with relapse or recurrence in the same segment, raising concern for underlying neoplasm) (Harrison 22e p.1072)","Radiation pneumonitis (Harrison 22e p.1072)","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (Harrison 22e p.1072)","Connective tissue disease involving the lungs (Harrison 22e p.1072)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Sputum Gram stain and culture (recommended chiefly for hospitalized patients, particularly severe cases or those at risk for MRSA or P. aeruginosa; an adequate specimen needs >25 neutrophils and <10 squamous epithelial cells per low-power field) (Harrison 22e p.1068)","Blood cultures (reserved for high-risk patients such as neutropenia, asplenia, complement deficiency, chronic liver disease, or severe CAP, and those at risk for MRSA/P. aeruginosa; yield only ~5-14% in hospitalized CAP) (Harrison 22e p.1068)","Pneumococcal and Legionella urinary antigen tests (reserved for severe cases or relevant epidemiology; Legionella test detects only serogroup 1) (Harrison 22e p.1068)","Multiplex respiratory PCR / nasopharyngeal swab PCR (standard for viral diagnosis including influenza and coronaviruses; can also detect Legionella, M. pneumoniae, C. pneumoniae, mycobacteria) (Harrison 22e p.1068)","Procalcitonin and C-reactive protein (adjuncts for stewardship only; PCT is not accurate enough to diagnose bacterial CAP or to withhold initial antibiotics, and CRP is even less sensitive) (Harrison 22e p.1068-1069)","CT chest (for suspected loculated effusion, cavitary disease, or postobstructive pneumonia from tumor or foreign body) (Harrison 22e p.1068)","Diagnostic pleural fluid sampling for a significant effusion (Harrison 22e p.1072)","Follow-up chest radiograph ~4-6 weeks after hospitalization (not routinely needed in well, nonsmoking outpatients whose symptoms resolve in 5-7 days) (Harrison 22e p.1072)"],"redFlags":["Septic shock requiring vasopressors or respiratory failure requiring invasive mechanical ventilation (major severe-CAP criteria mandating direct ICU admission) (Harrison 22e p.1069)","Room-air oxygen saturation <92% (Harrison 22e p.1069)","Confusion or disorientation, including new or worsening confusion in the elderly (Harrison 22e p.1068-1069)","Respiratory rate >=30 breaths/min (Harrison 22e p.1069)","Multilobar infiltrates (Harrison 22e p.1069)","Hypotension requiring aggressive fluid resuscitation (Harrison 22e p.1069)","Gross hemoptysis suggesting necrotizing pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.1067)","Failure to improve by ~day 3 of therapy, prompting reassessment for resistant or unexpected pathogen, sequestered focus (abscess/empyema), or a noninfectious mimic (Harrison 22e p.1072)"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on age, comorbidities, and site of care. Young patients without comorbidity generally recover fully in about 2 weeks, whereas older patients or those with comorbid illness may take several weeks longer. Overall mortality is under 5% for outpatients but ranges from roughly 12% to 40% for hospitalized patients depending on the patient category and the timeliness of appropriate antibiotics; in older patients the 1-year mortality after CAP exceeds the 30-day mortality. Fever and leukocytosis typically resolve in 2-4 days in otherwise healthy patients, while chest radiographic clearance is slowest (4-12 weeks). About 18% of hospitalized CAP patients are readmitted within a month (Harrison 22e p.1067, p.1072).","pitfalls":["Treating a macrolide as adequate empirical monotherapy where high-level pneumococcal macrolide resistance is prevalent risks treatment failure (Harrison 22e p.1069).","Relying on chest radiography or clinical features to determine etiology is unreliable; imaging often cannot distinguish CAP from other conditions and seldom yields a specific organism (Harrison 22e p.1068).","Using procalcitonin or CRP in isolation to decide on or withhold antibiotics is inappropriate, as neither is sufficiently accurate for diagnosing bacterial CAP (Harrison 22e p.1068-1069).","Neither PSI nor CURB-65 is designed to determine the need for ICU admission, so they should not be used for that purpose (Harrison 22e p.1069).","Delayed ICU transfer of a deteriorating ward patient is associated with higher mortality than initial ICU monitoring of equally ill patients (Harrison 22e p.1069).","Inability to expectorate sputum may reflect dehydration rather than absence of disease; rehydration can yield a productive sample and a more apparent infiltrate (Harrison 22e p.1068).","Pneumonia that relapses or recurs in the same lung segment should prompt evaluation for an underlying neoplasm or focal abnormality such as bronchiectasis (Harrison 22e p.1072)."],"severityClassification":"Two validated tools guide the site-of-care decision. The Pneumonia Severity Index (PSI) is a prognostic model using 20 variables (age, comorbidities, abnormal exam and laboratory findings) that stratifies patients into five classes with rising mortality (class 1 ~0.1%, class 2 ~0.6%, class 3 ~2.8%, class 4 ~8.2%, class 5 ~29.2%); classes 1-2 are generally suitable for outpatient care and class 3 for an observation unit. CURB-65 scores one point each for Confusion, Urea >7 mmol/L, Respiratory rate >=30/min, low Blood pressure (systolic <=90 or diastolic <=60 mmHg), and age >=65: a score of 0 (30-day mortality ~1.5%) suggests outpatient treatment, 1-2 generally warrants admission (unless driven only by age >=65), and >=3 (mortality ~22%) may need ICU care. PSI is better validated and more discriminating but harder to compute. Severe CAP (Harrison/IDSA-ATS criteria) is defined by either major criterion (invasive mechanical ventilation for respiratory failure, or septic shock requiring vasopressors), mandating ICU admission; presence of at least 3 of 9 minor criteria (RR >=30/min; PaO2/FiO2 <=250; multilobar infiltrates; confusion/disorientation; uremia with BUN >=20 mg/dL; leukopenia <4000/uL; thrombocytopenia <100,000/uL; hypothermia <36 degrees C; hypotension needing aggressive fluids) warrants ICU or high-level monitoring. Independent of these scores, inability to maintain oral intake, concerns about compliance (e.g. cognitive impairment, homelessness), or room-air O2 saturation <92% are indications for hospitalization (Harrison 22e p.1069).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1066","Harrison 22e p.1067","Harrison 22e p.1068","Harrison 22e p.1069","Harrison 22e p.1072"],"crossLinks":["SEVERE_CAP","HAP","ASPIRATION_PNEUMONIA","pleural_effusion","pe"]},"CA_UTI":{"name":"Catheter-Associated Urinary Tract Infection","system":"Genitourinary","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1086-1095, 1147","clinicalPearls":["CAUTI is diagnosed only when localizing urinary and/or systemic symptoms (e.g., fever) are present together with pyuria and bacteriuria; bacteriuria and pyuria alone do not establish the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1090).","Because the catheter is a conduit for bacterial entry, bacteriuria is essentially inevitable with catheterization, so an abnormal urinalysis alone should not be the endpoint of evaluating a febrile catheterized patient (Harrison 22e p.1090).","The accepted bacteriuria threshold for CAUTI is at least 10^3 colony-forming units per mL of urine (Harrison 22e p.1090).","Typical UTI features (pain, urgency, dysuria, fever, peripheral leukocytosis) have lower predictive value in catheterized patients than in non-catheterized patients (Harrison 22e p.1090).","The catheter should be exchanged or, if feasible, removed during treatment to clear biofilm-associated organisms; ideally the exchange precedes urine culture collection so results reflect bladder rather than catheter-wall microbiology (Harrison 22e p.1094).","Very high pyuria levels are common with urinary stasis (e.g., end-stage renal disease, obstructed catheters) and the degree of pyuria should not influence the distinction between asymptomatic bacteriuria and true UTI in an asymptomatic patient (Harrison 22e p.1089).","Candiduria is common in catheterized patients, especially after antimicrobial therapy for CAUTI or inappropriate treatment of asymptomatic bacteriuria; removal of the catheter alone resolves candiduria in over a third of asymptomatic cases (Harrison 22e pp.1088, 1095).","Urine nitrite is detectable only with Enterobacterales and adequate accumulation, so it can be falsely negative with non-Enterobacterales pathogens (e.g., enterococci, Pseudomonas, Candida) that are prominent in CAUTI (Harrison 22e pp.1090-1091)."],"pathophysiology":"Urinary catheters and other foreign bodies (stones, stents, nephrostomy tubes) provide an inert surface for bacterial colonization and formation of a persistent biofilm, and catheterization dramatically increases bacterial entry into the urinary tract. The catheter serves as a conduit allowing organisms to ascend into the bladder, making bacteriuria nearly universal during catheterization. Biofilm on the catheter shelters organisms and acts as a nidus for reinfection, which is why catheter exchange or removal is central to clearing infection. The microbiology is more diverse than in uncomplicated cystitis; while E. coli remains predominant, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, enterococci, Staphylococcus aureus, and Candida are important additional pathogens (Harrison 22e pp.1087-1088, 1094).","additionalDifferentials":["Asymptomatic bacteriuria / catheter-associated asymptomatic bacteriuria (positive culture without localizing or systemic symptoms attributable to UTI)","Complicated UTI extending beyond the bladder (prostatitis, pyelonephritis, bacteremia)","Candiduria representing colonization rather than true infection in a catheterized patient","Other infectious or noninfectious source of fever or altered mentation in a catheterized patient, which must be systematically excluded before attributing symptoms to CAUTI"],"infectionMimics":["Asymptomatic bacteriuria with incidental pyuria","Candida colonization of the catheter/urine without invasive infection","Another concurrent infection (e.g., respiratory, intra-abdominal, bloodstream) causing the fever or systemic findings"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Delirium from non-infectious causes (e.g., metabolic, medication-related) in an older patient, mistaken for UTI-related encephalopathy","Pyuria due to urinary stasis from an obstructed catheter rather than active infection","Hematuria or local irritation from catheter trauma"],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine culture collected from a freshly placed catheter (after exchanging/removing the indwelling catheter) to reflect bladder rather than biofilm microbiology, with susceptibilities to guide therapy","Urine dipstick (nitrite, leukocyte esterase) and microscopic urinalysis, interpreted with awareness that pyuria and bacteriuria are expected in catheterized patients","Systematic workup to exclude alternative infectious and non-infectious causes of fever or altered mental status before settling on CAUTI"],"redFlags":["Fever, rigors, nausea/vomiting, or flank/costovertebral angle pain suggesting infection has extended beyond the bladder (pyelonephritis or bloodstream)","Signs of sepsis in a catheterized patient with systemic symptoms","Isolation of S. aureus from urine, which should prompt consideration of concomitant bacteremia","Persistent fever and/or bacteremia despite appropriate therapy, suggesting renal/perinephric abscess or obstruction requiring drainage"],"prognosis":"Asymptomatic bacteriuria is common in catheterized and elderly patients but does not itself increase risk of infection or death, which argues against treating it. In the absence of anatomic abnormalities (e.g., reflux, obstruction), recurrent urinary infection does not lead to chronic pyelonephritis or renal failure; however, with underlying renal abnormalities such as obstructing stones, infection can accelerate renal parenchymal damage. A randomized trial found more relapses with 3 versus 14 days of therapy, supporting longer treatment than for uncomplicated cystitis (Harrison 22e pp.1094-1095).","pitfalls":["Treating catheter-associated asymptomatic bacteriuria: apart from pregnancy (and pre-urologic-procedure screening), urine culture and treatment are not indicated for asymptomatic catheterized patients, and treating it does not reduce subsequent infection (Harrison 22e pp.1147, 1095).","Attributing nonspecific symptoms such as confusion in an elderly catheterized patient to bacteriuria without excluding other time-sensitive causes of delirium, leading to premature diagnostic closure (Harrison 22e pp.1089, 1147).","Improper treatment of asymptomatic bacteriuria followed by repeat 'test-of-cure' cultures, driving a vicious cycle of escalating antimicrobial use and resistance (Harrison 22e p.1147).","Sending urine cultures in catheterized patients lacking signs/symptoms of UTI (poor diagnostic stewardship), which promotes overdiagnosis and overtreatment (Harrison 22e pp.1089, 1147).","Sampling urine through the existing biofilm-laden catheter rather than from a newly placed catheter, yielding culture results that reflect the catheter wall rather than the bladder (Harrison 22e p.1094).","Routinely using antimicrobial (silver- or nitrofurazone-impregnated) catheters to prevent CAUTI, which has not shown significant clinical benefit (Harrison 22e p.1094)."],"severityClassification":"CAUTI falls within the complicated UTI category, defined by infection in the setting of intermittent or indwelling urinary catheterization. Severity ranges from localized symptomatic infection to infection extending beyond the bladder (pyelonephritis, bacteremia) signaled by fever, rigors, flank pain, and systemic toxicity (Harrison 22e pp.1086-1087, 1089).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1086","Harrison 22e p.1087","Harrison 22e p.1088","Harrison 22e p.1089","Harrison 22e p.1090","Harrison 22e p.1091","Harrison 22e p.1094","Harrison 22e p.1095","Harrison 22e p.1147"],"crossLinks":["COMPLICATED_UTI","PYELONEPHRITIS","CYSTITIS","PROSTATITIS"]},"CELLULITIS":{"name":"Cellulitis","system":"Skin & Soft Tissue","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1095, 1007, 428, 436, 93","clinicalPearls":["Non-purulent cellulitis (no associated abscess, focal purulence, or drainage) is a rapidly spreading, diffuse process typically due to S. pyogenes, often accompanied by lymphangitis and fever; purulent cellulitis arising from a focal lesion (furuncle, carbuncle, abscess, surgical wound) is more likely staphylococcal (Harrison 22e p.1095).","Even with needle aspiration of the advancing edge or punch biopsy of involved tissue, cultures are positive in only about 20% of cases, suggesting low bacterial numbers and that much of the spreading erythema is driven by extracellular toxins and host inflammatory mediators (Harrison 22e p.1095).","When a draining wound or visible portal of entry is present, Gram stain and culture of that material can give a definitive microbiologic diagnosis, unlike cultures of intact cellulitic skin (Harrison 22e p.1095).","A careful exposure/epidemiologic history is diagnostically valuable because many less common causative organisms occupy characteristic ecological niches (e.g., cat/dog bite, freshwater laceration, fish/swine handling, aquarium water) (Harrison 22e p.1095).","Bilateral, symmetric lower-leg involvement favors a mimic such as stasis dermatitis, whereas true cellulitis is usually unilateral (Harrison 22e p.428)."],"pathophysiology":"Bacteria reach the dermis and subcutaneous soft tissue after disruption of the epidermal barrier by cracks, abrasions, cuts, burns, insect bites, surgical incisions, IV catheters, trauma, tinea/fungal infection, or pressure and venous-stasis ulcers. Organisms already colonizing the skin (notably S. aureus and group A streptococci) most readily exploit such breaks, though many exogenous bacteria can be introduced. Streptococcal disease characteristically spreads diffusely via lymphatics, producing lymphangitis, whereas staphylococcal disease spreads from a central focus such as an abscess or infected foreign body. Much of the expanding erythema reflects the action of bacterial extracellular toxins and host-derived inflammatory mediators rather than high bacterial burden. Progression to deeper structures with loss of vascular integrity (purple bullae, ecchymosis, cutaneous anesthesia) signals possible necrotizing infection (Harrison 22e p.1095, p.1007).","additionalDifferentials":["Erysipelas (S. pyogenes superficial dermal/lymphatic infection with sharply demarcated, raised, indurated borders and abrupt fiery-red swelling) (Harrison 22e p.1095)","Necrotizing fasciitis / deeper necrotizing soft-tissue infection (Harrison 22e p.1095)","Purulent cellulitis associated with a focal staphylococcal lesion such as a furuncle, carbuncle, or abscess (Harrison 22e p.1095)"],"infectionMimics":["Herpes zoster (dermatomal vesicular eruption may be mistaken for cellulitis early) (Harrison 22e p.93)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Stasis dermatitis, which when acutely inflamed with crusting and exudate is easily confused with cellulitis; symmetric bilateral leg involvement favors stasis dermatitis (Harrison 22e p.428, p.436)","Eczema (Harrison 22e p.93)","Lymphedema (Harrison 22e p.93)","Contact dermatitis (Harrison 22e p.428)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Gram stain and culture of drainage, open wound, or identifiable portal of entry when present, for definitive etiology (Harrison 22e p.1095)","Needle aspiration of the leading edge or punch biopsy of involved skin in selected cases, recognizing low (~20%) culture yield (Harrison 22e p.1095)"],"redFlags":["Purple/hemorrhagic bullae, ecchymosis, or cutaneous anesthesia suggesting loss of vascular integrity and possible necrotizing fasciitis or myonecrosis (Harrison 22e p.1095)","Pain or tenderness disproportionate to visible skin findings, or unexplained fever with deep soft-tissue pain even without overt skin inflammation (Harrison 22e p.1095)","Brawny edema, dark-red induration, skin friability with bluish/maroon/black discoloration, or systemic toxicity/shock indicating deep necrotizing infection (Harrison 22e p.1095)"],"prognosis":null,"pitfalls":["Routine cultures of intact cellulitic skin are frequently negative (positive in only ~20% even with aspiration or biopsy), so negative cultures do NOT exclude cellulitis (Harrison 22e p.1095).","When patients labeled 'cellulitis' are reviewed by dermatology, roughly one-third have an alternative diagnosis (e.g., eczema, lymphedema), so an overcall risks unnecessary hospitalization and antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.93).","Bilateral symmetric lower-extremity erythema is more likely stasis dermatitis than cellulitis (Harrison 22e p.428).","Early necrotizing fasciitis may present with pain or unexplained fever as the only manifestation; do NOT rely on overt cutaneous inflammation to exclude it (Harrison 22e p.1095)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison distinguishes non-purulent cellulitis (diffuse, spreading, typically streptococcal, often with lymphangitis and fever) from purulent cellulitis (associated with a focal lesion such as furuncle, carbuncle, abscess, or surgical wound, typically staphylococcal, the FDA-preferred term for these). Loss of vascular integrity (purple bullae, ecchymosis, cutaneous anesthesia, systemic toxicity) marks progression toward necrotizing infection (Harrison 22e p.1095).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1095","Harrison 22e p.1007","Harrison 22e p.428","Harrison 22e p.436","Harrison 22e p.93"],"crossLinks":["ERYSIPELAS","NECROTIZING_FASCIITIS","DIABETIC_FOOT","dvt"]},"CHIKUNGUNYA":{"name":"Chikungunya","system":"Tropical Fever","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1060, p.1706, p.1712","clinicalPearls":["Chikungunya virus (CHIKV) is an arthritogenic alphavirus transmitted by Aedes (yellow-fever and Asian tiger) mosquitoes; after a 2-10 day incubation it causes abrupt high fever, sometimes with a saddleback pattern, plus severe arthralgia (Harrison 22e p.1712).","Migratory polyarthritis predominantly involves the small joints of the hands, wrists, ankles, and feet, though larger joints may also be affected; periarticular swelling and immobility are common, while frank effusions are less frequent (Harrison 22e p.1712).","A rash appears at onset or several days in, often coinciding with defervescence around day 2-3, is most intense over trunk and limbs, and may desquamate; children more often develop a bullous rather than maculopapular/petechial rash (Harrison 22e p.1712).","Although petechiae and epistaxis are occasionally seen, CHIKV should not be classified as a cause of viral hemorrhagic fever; older reports of 'chikungunya hemorrhagic fever' largely reflected concurrent dengue coinfection (Harrison 22e p.1712, p.1706).","A recovery period of weeks is common, and a substantial proportion of middle-aged to older patients go on to develop chronic, sometimes disabling arthritis or arthralgia syndromes typically involving the same joints (Harrison 22e p.1712).","Pregnant women are not at increased risk of severe disease, but there is a high risk of maternal-fetal transmission, especially intrapartum, which has occasionally led to fetal death (Harrison 22e p.1712).","A live-attenuated chikungunya vaccine was approved in the United States in 2023 for adults at increased exposure risk and is contraindicated in immunocompromised persons; about 12% of recipients developed chikungunya-like symptoms in trials (Harrison 22e p.1060)."],"pathophysiology":"CHIKV is an arthropod-borne alphavirus (family Togaviridae) spread by Aedes mosquitoes. It produces an acute febrile illness with prominent joint involvement; rheumatic manifestations span arthralgia with or without inflammatory signs (periarticular redness, swelling, immobility). Adults experience more severe and articular-predominant disease than children. Chronic musculoskeletal sequelae are thought to relate to host factors, with increased risk in patients who have preexisting osteoarthritis, severe acute disease, or the HLA-B27 subtype, and may be associated with cryoglobulins (Harrison 22e p.1712).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Dengue (co-circulating Aedes-borne flavivirus; may coinfect) (Harrison 22e p.1712, p.1060)","Zika virus infection (Aedes-borne; can also cause joint manifestations) (Harrison 22e p.1712, p.1060)","Other arthritogenic alphaviruses with clinically similar presentations: Ross River virus, Barmah Forest virus, Sindbis virus, o'nyong-nyong virus, Mayaro virus (Harrison 22e p.1712)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":[],"redFlags":["Cardiorespiratory, neurologic, renal, or ocular complications in severe acute disease (uncommon but reported, typically in hospitalized patients) (Harrison 22e p.1712)","Pregnancy near delivery, given high risk of intrapartum maternal-fetal transmission and reported fetal death (Harrison 22e p.1712)"],"prognosis":"Life-threatening illness is rare; most patients recover, though recovery may take weeks. A significant fraction of middle-aged to older patients develop chronic, potentially disabling arthritis/arthralgia. Deaths have been reported, typically in the setting of large outbreaks. Children generally have milder disease and are less frequently hospitalized (Harrison 22e p.1712, p.1060).","pitfalls":["Do not label CHIKV as a viral hemorrhagic fever despite occasional petechiae/epistaxis; apparent 'hemorrhagic' cases historically reflected concurrent dengue infection (Harrison 22e p.1712, p.1706).","Do not assume severe disease in pregnancy; the principal pregnancy concern is intrapartum vertical transmission rather than maternal severity (Harrison 22e p.1712).","Do not consider the live-attenuated vaccine safe in immunocompromised persons, in whom it is contraindicated (Harrison 22e p.1060)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.1060","Harrison 22e p.1706","Harrison 22e p.1712"],"crossLinks":["DENGUE"]},"CHOLANGITIS":{"name":"Acute Cholangitis","system":"Hepatobiliary","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"p.2489, p.2735","clinicalPearls":["The classic presentation is Charcot's triad: biliary pain, jaundice, and spiking fevers with rigors; it is present in roughly 70% of patients with ascending cholangitis (Harrison 22e p.2489, p.2735).","Addition of shock and confusion to Charcot's triad constitutes Reynolds's pentad, which signals high mortality and should trigger urgent biliary drainage (Harrison 22e p.2489).","Bile cultures are positive in approximately 75% of patients with acute cholangitis early in the symptomatic course, and blood cultures are frequently positive (Harrison 22e p.2735).","Most acute cholangitis is the nonsuppurative form, which often responds relatively quickly to supportive care and antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.2735).","Initial medical management (fluids and IV antibiotics) usually stabilizes the patient and buys a window of about 24 hours within which biliary drainage should be established, typically by ERCP (Harrison 22e p.2489).","The bile duct may not yet be dilated on early ultrasound during acute biliary obstruction, so a normal-caliber duct does not exclude cholangitis (Harrison 22e p.2489)."],"pathophysiology":"Acute cholangitis arises from infection within an obstructed biliary tree, most often due to choledocholithiasis (common duct stones complicate about 10-15% of cholelithiasis). At least partial obstruction to bile flow combined with bacterial colonization of bile produces inflammation; rising intrabiliary pressure in an obstructed system promotes translocation of organisms into the bloodstream, causing bacteremia and biliary sepsis. In suppurative cholangitis, pus accumulates under pressure within a completely obstructed duct, driving severe toxicity (Harrison 22e p.2489, p.2735).","additionalDifferentials":["Biliary colic / choledocholithiasis without infection","Acute cholecystitis","Gallstone pancreatitis","Obstructive jaundice from malignancy of the pancreatic head, bile ducts, or ampulla of Vater (often painless jaundice)","Primary sclerosing cholangitis with superimposed bacterial cholangitis"],"infectionMimics":["Acute cholecystitis","Hepatic (pyogenic) abscess","Septic shock from another occult source (e.g., pneumonia, pyelonephritis) presenting before the biliary source is evident"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Biliary colic from choledocholithiasis (pain without fever or rigors)","Painless obstructive jaundice from neoplastic biliary obstruction","IgG4-associated (sclerosing) cholangitis, which causes biliary stricturing and is glucocorticoid-responsive","Gallstone pancreatitis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum alkaline phosphatase (almost always elevated in biliary obstruction; rise may precede clinical jaundice)","Serum aminotransferases (two- to tenfold elevation, especially with acute obstruction)","ERCP with endoscopic sphincterotomy as the preferred initial procedure for both definitive diagnosis and therapeutic drainage","EUS as a less invasive alternative for detecting duct stones when the pretest probability of a persistent stone is lower"],"redFlags":["Reynolds's pentad (Charcot's triad plus shock and confusion) indicating suppurative cholangitis and high mortality","Mental confusion, bacteremia, and septic shock signaling suppurative cholangitis with pus under pressure","Failure to respond to antibiotics, which in suppurative disease is associated with multiple hepatic abscesses","Delay in establishing biliary drainage beyond the ~24 h stabilization window, risking recrudescent sepsis"],"prognosis":"Nonsuppurative acute cholangitis generally responds well to antibiotics and supportive care. Suppurative acute cholangitis carries a far worse prognosis: response to antibiotics alone is poor and mortality approaches 100% unless prompt endoscopic or surgical relief of the obstruction with drainage of infected bile is achieved. Reynolds's pentad and undue delay in drainage both predict increased morbidity and mortality (Harrison 22e p.2489, p.2735).","pitfalls":["Relying on antibiotics alone in suppurative cholangitis; biliary decompression is required because antibiotic response is poor and mortality is very high without it.","Excluding cholangitis because the bile duct appears non-dilated on early ultrasound, which can occur in acute obstruction.","Awaiting the full Charcot's triad before acting, since not all patients display all three components.","Mistaking the relentless toxicity of suppurative disease for simple sepsis without recognizing the need for urgent source control."],"severityClassification":"Harrison distinguishes nonsuppurative acute cholangitis (the more common, milder form responsive to antibiotics) from suppurative acute cholangitis (pus under pressure in a completely obstructed duct, with severe toxicity, bacteremia, septic shock, and very high mortality without drainage). Clinically, Charcot's triad (pain, jaundice, fever/rigors) defines the typical syndrome, and progression to Reynolds's pentad (adding shock and confusion) marks the severe/suppurative end of the spectrum requiring urgent biliary decompression (Harrison 22e p.2489, p.2735).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2489","Harrison 22e p.2735"],"crossLinks":["CHOLECYSTITIS","biliary_colic","pancreatitis","SEPSIS"]},"CHOLECYSTITIS":{"name":"Acute Cholecystitis","system":"Hepatobiliary","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e, Chap. 357 (Diseases of the Gallbladder and Bile Ducts), pp. 2730-2734; acute abdomen differential/imaging, Chap. 16, pp. 113-115","clinicalPearls":["Acute cholecystitis usually opens as an attack of biliary pain that progressively worsens and becomes more generalized in the right upper abdomen; pain may radiate to the interscapular area, right scapula, or shoulder (Harrison 22e p.2732).","Roughly 60-70% of patients recall prior self-limited biliary attacks before the index episode of acute cholecystitis (Harrison 22e p.2732).","Biliary pain that persists beyond about 5 hours should raise suspicion of acute cholecystitis rather than uncomplicated biliary colic (Harrison 22e p.2731).","Fever or rigors accompanying biliary pain generally signal a complication such as cholecystitis, pancreatitis, or cholangitis (Harrison 22e p.2731).","Murphy's sign: deep inspiration or cough during subcostal palpation of the RUQ produces increased pain with inspiratory arrest, a characteristic bedside finding (Harrison 22e p.2732).","An enlarged, tense gallbladder is palpable in only about 25-50% of cases, so its absence does not exclude the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2732).","Generalized peritoneal signs and abdominal rigidity are usually absent in uncomplicated acute cholecystitis; their presence should prompt concern for perforation (Harrison 22e p.2732).","About 75% of medically treated patients remit within 2-7 days, but roughly 25% develop a complication despite conservative management, and recurrence is common (about 25% within 1 year) (Harrison 22e p.2732).","In empyema or free perforation the picture may mimic cholangitis with high fever, severe RUQ pain, marked leukocytosis, and prostration (Harrison 22e p.2733).","A sudden transient relief of RUQ pain (gallbladder decompression) followed by generalized peritonitis suggests free perforation (Harrison 22e pp.2733-2734)."],"pathophysiology":"Acute cholecystitis is acute inflammation of the gallbladder wall that usually follows obstruction of the cystic duct by a stone. Three mechanisms drive the inflammatory response: mechanical inflammation from increased intraluminal pressure and distention producing mucosal/wall ischemia; chemical inflammation from release of lysolecithin (via phospholipase action on biliary lecithin) and other local tissue factors; and bacterial inflammation, which contributes in roughly 50-85% of cases. Chronic cholecystitis is almost always associated with gallstones and results from repeated bouts of subacute/acute inflammation or persistent mechanical irritation of the wall by stones (Harrison 22e pp.2732-2733).","additionalDifferentials":["Choledocholithiasis / common bile duct stone (suggested by elevated bilirubin and/or alkaline phosphatase with biliary pain) (Harrison 22e p.2731)","Peptic ulcer disease, especially perforated peptic ulcer (Harrison 22e p.113, p.155)","Gastritis / GERD (Harrison 22e p.113)","Hepatitis (Harrison 22e p.113)","Right lower-lobe pneumonia / pleurisy / subdiaphragmatic abscess (Harrison 22e p.113)","Mirizzi's syndrome (cystic-duct/gallbladder-neck stone compressing the CBD with obstruction and jaundice) (Harrison 22e p.2732)"],"infectionMimics":["Acute cholangitis (overlapping fever, RUQ pain, leukocytosis; empyema of the gallbladder may clinically resemble cholangitis) (Harrison 22e p.2733)","Hepatitis (Harrison 22e p.113)","Right-sided pneumonia / empyema / subdiaphragmatic abscess presenting as RUQ pain (Harrison 22e p.113)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute pancreatitis (HIDA scanning can help differentiate acute cholecystitis or biliary colic from acute pancreatitis; serum amylase may rise in cholecystitis as well) (Harrison 22e pp.114-115, p.155)","Uncomplicated biliary colic / symptomatic cholelithiasis (Harrison 22e p.2731)","Acalculous cholecystopathy (gallbladder dysmotility causing recurrent biliary-type RUQ pain without stones) (Harrison 22e p.2733)","Sphincter of Oddi dysfunction causing recurrent RUQ pain (Harrison 22e p.2733)","Myocardial infarction / pericarditis (epigastric/RUQ overlap in the acute abdomen differential) (Harrison 22e p.113)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Ultrasound demonstrates calculi in about 90-95% of cases and shows signs of inflammation: gallbladder wall thickening, pericholecystic fluid, and bile-duct dilatation (Harrison 22e p.2732).","Radionuclide (HIDA/DISIDA) biliary scan can be confirmatory when bile-duct imaging is seen without gallbladder visualization, indicating cystic-duct obstruction; useful when ultrasound is equivocal (Harrison 22e p.2730, p.2732).","WBC count typically shows leukocytosis around 10,000-15,000/uL with a left shift (Harrison 22e p.2732).","Serum bilirubin is mildly elevated (<5 mg/dL [85.5 umol/L]) in fewer than half of patients; about one-fourth have modest aminotransferase elevations (usually <5-fold) (Harrison 22e p.2732).","CT or ultrasound showing a large, tense, static, stone-free gallbladder with poor emptying can support acalculous cholecystitis (Harrison 22e p.2733).","Plain abdominal film may reveal gas within the gallbladder lumen/wall or pericholecystic tissues in emphysematous cholecystitis, and pneumobilia in cholecystoenteric fistula/gallstone ileus (Harrison 22e p.2733)."],"redFlags":["Generalized peritoneal signs / abdominal rigidity (suggests perforation) (Harrison 22e p.2732)","High fever, severe RUQ pain, marked leukocytosis, and prostration resembling cholangitis (suggests empyema of the gallbladder, which carries high risk of gram-negative sepsis and perforation) (Harrison 22e p.2733)","Gas in the gallbladder lumen/wall on imaging (emphysematous cholecystitis; considerable morbidity/mortality) (Harrison 22e p.2733)","Transient relief of RUQ pain followed by generalized peritonitis (free perforation) (Harrison 22e pp.2733-2734)","Acute cholecystitis arising in the setting of severe critical illness (trauma, burns, prolonged fasting, major non-biliary surgery) suggesting acalculous disease with a higher complication rate (Harrison 22e p.2733)"],"prognosis":"Most medically treated patients (about 75%) achieve remission of acute symptoms within 2-7 days, but roughly 25% develop a complication requiring prompt surgery. Among those who remit, about 25% recur within 1 year and 60% within 6 years, so early surgery is preferred. Mortality for emergency cholecystectomy is about 1-3%, versus about 0.5% for early elective cholecystectomy in patients under 60, rising with age and comorbidity. Acalculous and emphysematous forms carry higher complication and mortality rates, and free perforation has a mortality of roughly 30% (Harrison 22e pp.2732-2734).","pitfalls":["Vague post-fatty-meal symptoms (epigastric fullness, dyspepsia, eructation, flatulence) are nonspecific and should not be misread as biliary pain (Harrison 22e p.2731).","Jaundice is unusual early in acute cholecystitis; when present it may reflect bile-duct involvement or a coexisting CBD stone rather than simple cholecystitis (Harrison 22e p.2732).","Leukocytosis alone should never be the single deciding factor for operation, and a normal WBC count does not exclude a surgical abdomen including perforation (Harrison 22e p.155).","Clinical features of acalculous cholecystitis are indistinguishable from calculous disease; diagnosis depends on recognizing the critically ill setting and using imaging (Harrison 22e p.2733).","Emphysematous cholecystitis presents essentially like nongaseous cholecystitis clinically and is identified by imaging, not by a distinct clinical picture; it favors elderly men and diabetics (Harrison 22e p.2733).","Acalculous cholecystitis can occur with unusual organisms (e.g., Leptospira, Salmonella, Vibrio cholerae) or systemic disease, so failure to find stones at surgery does not exclude cholecystitis (Harrison 22e p.2733)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.2730","Harrison 22e p.2731","Harrison 22e p.2732","Harrison 22e p.2733","Harrison 22e p.2734","Harrison 22e p.113","Harrison 22e p.114","Harrison 22e p.155"],"crossLinks":["CHOLANGITIS","biliary_colic","pancreatitis"]},"CNS_TB":{"name":"CNS Tuberculosis (TB Meningitis)","system":"Central Nervous System / Tuberculosis","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1124, 1127, 1136, 1389; pharmacogenomics of adjunctive steroids p.1026","clinicalPearls":["Typically evolves over 1-2 weeks (subacute) - a longer course than typical bacterial meningitis; often begins as headache plus subtle mental changes after a prodrome of weeks of low-grade fever, malaise, anorexia and irritability (p.1389).","Because meningeal involvement is most pronounced at the base of the brain, cranial nerve palsies (ocular nerves in particular) are a frequent finding (p.1389).","Involvement of cerebral arteries at the skull base can produce focal ischemia/infarction (p.1389).","Untreated, the disease progresses toward coma with hydrocephalus and intracranial hypertension (p.1389).","In more than half of cases, evidence of old pulmonary lesions or a miliary pattern is found on chest radiograph (p.1389).","CNS TB accounts for roughly 5% of extrapulmonary TB cases; it is seen most often in young children but also in adults, especially those with HIV (p.1389).","The last tube of CSF collected at lumbar puncture is the best tube to send for an AFB smear; if a pellicle or cobweb-like clot forms on the fluid surface, AFB are best demonstrated in a smear of that clot/pellicle (p.1127).","Empiric anti-tuberculous therapy is often started on a high index of clinical suspicion without adequate laboratory support, given the poor smear yield and slow culture (p.1127).","A lymphocytic pleocytosis with low CSF glucose should prompt consideration of fungal or tuberculous meningitis, Listeria meningoencephalitis, or noninfectious causes such as sarcoid or neoplastic meningitis (p.1124)."],"pathophysiology":"Tuberculous meningitis in adults does not arise acutely from direct hematogenous seeding of the meninges. Rather, during hematogenous dissemination of primary infection, miliary (millet seed-sized) tubercles form in the brain parenchyma; these enlarge and usually caseate. Whether a caseous focus produces meningitis depends on its proximity to the subarachnoid space (SAS) and how quickly it becomes fibrous-encapsulated. Subependymal caseous foci discharge bacilli and mycobacterial antigens into the SAS; rupture of a subependymal tubercle into the subarachnoid space is a recognized route. The mycobacterial antigens trigger an intense inflammatory reaction producing a thick exudate that fills the basilar cisterns and encases the cranial nerves and the major blood vessels at the base of the brain - explaining the basal enhancement, cranial nerve palsies, arteritic infarcts and obstructive hydrocephalus seen clinically. Meningitis can also result from hematogenous spread of primary or postprimary pulmonary TB, and is a particular concern in very young children and immunocompromised persons (e.g., people living with HIV) (p.1127, 1389).","additionalDifferentials":["Fungal (subacute/chronic) meningitis, e.g. Cryptococcus neoformans, Histoplasma capsulatum, Coccidioides immitis (p.1124, 1127)","Listeria monocytogenes meningoencephalitis (p.1124)","Syphilitic (Treponema pallidum) meningitis (p.1127)","Neoplastic / carcinomatous meningitis (p.1124)","Neurosarcoidosis (p.1124)"],"infectionMimics":["Cryptococcal meningitis (lymphocytic CSF with low glucose) (p.1124, 1127)","Histoplasma and Coccidioides meningitis (p.1127)","Listeria meningoencephalitis (p.1124)","Neurosyphilis (p.1127)","Viral meningitis due to mumps, LCMV, HSV-2, VZV or enteroviruses, which can occasionally cause low CSF glucose and mimic the TB profile (p.1124)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Neoplastic / neoplastic (carcinomatous) meningitis (p.1124)","Neurosarcoidosis (p.1124)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CSF AFB smear taken from the last LP tube, or from any pellicle/cobweb clot - but smears are positive in only ~10-40% of adult cases (p.1127).","Mycobacterial CSF culture - gold standard, but takes 4-8 weeks and is positive in only ~50% (chapter 143) to up to 80% (chapter 183) of cases (p.1127, 1389).","CSF nucleic acid amplification testing - Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra or Xpert MTB/RIF; in the TB chapter the Xpert MTB/RIF assay has sensitivity up to 80% and is the preferred initial diagnostic option, with treatment started immediately on a positive result; a negative result does not exclude TB (p.1127, 1389).","CSF opening pressure measurement (characteristically elevated) (p.1127).","Repeat lumbar puncture to increase diagnostic yield, since AFB are infrequently seen on a single direct smear (p.1389).","CT/MRI brain looking specifically for hydrocephalus and abnormal enhancement of the basal cisterns or ependyma; contrast-enhanced ring lesions on CT/MRI suggest tuberculoma (biopsy needed to confirm) (p.1389)."],"redFlags":["Declining level of consciousness / progression toward coma (p.1389)","New cranial nerve palsies, especially ocular nerves, indicating basal meningeal involvement (p.1389)","Focal neurologic deficit suggesting arteritic cerebral infarction (p.1389)","Clinical or imaging evidence of hydrocephalus and raised intracranial pressure (p.1389)","Acute deterioration with severe headache, confusion, lethargy, altered sensorium and neck rigidity in a previously subacute presentation (p.1389)"],"prognosis":"If unrecognized, tuberculous meningitis is uniformly fatal. The disease responds to chemotherapy, but neurologic sequelae are documented in about 25% of treated cases - most often when the diagnosis was delayed. Adjunctive glucocorticoids may speed resolution of CSF abnormalities and raised CSF pressure and lower rates of death or severe disability and relapse; in one trial adjunctive dexamethasone improved survival in patients >14 years but did not reduce the frequency of neurologic sequelae. Among people living with HIV, a placebo-controlled study showed no survival or secondary-endpoint benefit from a 6-8 week tapering course of adjunctive dexamethasone (p.1389). Pharmacogenomic data suggest only a subset of patients benefit from steroids: those homozygous for the proinflammatory LTA4H allele were most helped, whereas those homozygous for the anti-inflammatory allele may be harmed (p.1026).","pitfalls":["Low CSF smear yield (AFB seen on direct smear in only ~10-40% of adult cases) and slow culture (4-8 weeks) mean diagnosis cannot wait for microbiologic confirmation - empiric anti-TB therapy is appropriately started on high clinical suspicion (p.1127).","Any one of the classic CSF parameters (cell count, protein, glucose) can be within the normal range, so a normal value does not exclude TB meningitis (p.1389).","A negative CSF Xpert MTB/RIF result does not exclude TB and requires further diagnostic workup (p.1389).","Early in the course CSF may show a neutrophil predominance rather than lymphocytes, which can mislead toward bacterial meningitis (p.1389).","Delayed recognition allows the subacute illness to evolve acutely (severe headache, confusion, altered sensorium, neck rigidity) and is the main driver of death and of the ~25% rate of neurologic sequelae (p.1389)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison describes a clinical continuum that maps to the British MRC staging used for TB meningitis: an early stage with headache and slight mental changes after a prodrome of low-grade fever/malaise/anorexia, evolving (if unrecognized) to severe headache, confusion, lethargy and altered sensorium with neck rigidity, and ultimately to coma with hydrocephalus and intracranial hypertension; outcome worsens markedly with more advanced stage at presentation/diagnostic delay (p.1389).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1124","Harrison 22e p.1127","Harrison 22e p.1136","Harrison 22e p.1389","Harrison 22e p.1026"],"crossLinks":["MENINGITIS","PULMONARY_TB","DISSEMINATED_TB"]},"COMPLICATED_UTI":{"name":"Complicated Urinary Tract Infection","system":"Genitourinary","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1129-1138","clinicalPearls":["Harrison defines complicated UTI as infection extending beyond the bladder (e.g., prostatitis, pyelonephritis, bacteremia) or UTI occurring in the setting of intermittent or indwelling catheterization; this is a shift from the older approach that automatically labeled any UTI in men, diabetics, the immunocompromised, or those with anatomic abnormalities as complicated (p.1129-1130).","Recurrent UTI is not inherently complicated; individual episodes may be uncomplicated and managed as such (p.1130).","Fever, rigors, and unilateral back or flank pain are incompatible with infection confined to the bladder and should trigger evaluation for infection beyond the bladder (kidney, prostate, or bloodstream) (p.1132).","In men, UTI is uncommon outside the context of catheterization/instrumentation or obstructive uropathy from prostatic hypertrophy; a urine culture should be obtained in every case of male UTI (p.1132).","Men with febrile UTI, even at first episode, should undergo imaging (CT or ultrasound) if not done previously, specifically to assess for urinary tract abnormalities or obstruction (p.1132).","Predictors of a surgically correctable disorder in men with febrile UTI include urinary retention symptoms, early UTI recurrence, hematuria at follow-up, and voiding difficulties; men with none of these had normal urinary tracts on workup (p.1132).","Early recurrence within 2 weeks is regarded as relapse rather than reinfection and may signal a sequestered focus of ongoing infection that needs evaluation (p.1130).","Foreign bodies such as stones, stents, and catheters provide an inert surface for bacterial colonization and persistent biofilm formation, serving as a nidus for recurrent infection (p.1130-1131).","Initial evaluation of recurrent cystitis should include imaging to identify renal calculi or another nidus of infection and assessment for urinary retention (p.1138)."],"pathophysiology":"Most UTIs arise by ascent of bacteria from the urethra to the bladder, with further ascent up the ureter producing renal parenchymal infection; the hematogenous route accounts for under 2% of cases and typically involves virulent organisms such as S. aureus and Salmonella (isolation of either from urine should prompt consideration of concomitant bacteremia). Whether bladder entry leads to symptomatic infection depends on the interplay of host, pathogen, and environmental factors. Conditions causing urinary stasis or obstruction (vesicoureteral reflux, ureteral obstruction from prostatic hypertrophy, neurogenic bladder, urinary-diversion surgery) and foreign bodies that support biofilm all predispose to infection. Uropathogenic E. coli virulence factors include P fimbriae (mediating binding to renal epithelial receptors and important in pyelonephritis and bloodstream invasion) and type 1 pili (mediating mannose binding and initiating bladder infection), plus toxins, iron-acquisition systems, biofilm formation, and capsules. Candiduria is pathogenetically distinct in that the hematogenous route is more common, and in non-instrumented immunocompetent patients its presence implies either genital contamination or possible widespread visceral dissemination (p.1131).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute bacterial prostatitis (consider in men with UTI plus systemic symptoms and no other clear anatomic source) (p.1132)","Chronic bacterial prostatitis (consider with recurrent UTI in men, especially outside catheterization) (p.1132)","Pyelonephritis with or without urinary obstruction (p.1132-1133)","Chronic pelvic pain syndrome in men (not associated with bacteriuria, not improved by antibiotics) (p.1132)"],"infectionMimics":["Sexually transmitted infection / cervicitis (Chlamydia trachomatis, Neisseria gonorrhoeae) presenting with dysuria (p.1133)","Vaginitis (Candida albicans, Trichomonas vaginalis) (p.1133)","Herpetic urethritis (p.1133)","Urethritis from STI pathogens (gonococcal, chlamydial, Mycoplasma genitalium); urethral pain, pruritus, or discharge should prompt STI evaluation (p.1130, p.1133)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Asymptomatic bacteriuria misclassified as UTI, especially in older patients with isolated altered mentation; degree of pyuria should not drive the distinction (p.1132)","Interstitial cystitis (p.1133)","Noninfectious vaginal or vulvar irritation (p.1133)","Chronic pelvic pain syndrome in men (p.1132)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CT or ultrasound imaging in men with febrile UTI to evaluate for anatomic abnormality or obstruction (p.1132)","Blood cultures when complicated UTI, pyelonephritis, or systemic features are present (p.1134 diagnostic algorithm)","Urine dipstick (nitrite and leukocyte esterase) and microscopic urinalysis as point-of-care tools mainly useful for ruling out UTI when the clinical diagnosis is unclear (p.1133-1135)","Imaging to identify renal calculi or other nidus, plus assessment for urinary retention, in recurrent UTI (p.1138)","Serum creatinine monitoring (a rapid rise may be the first sign of bilateral papillary necrosis) (p.1133)"],"redFlags":["Fever, rigors, nausea/vomiting, and flank pain suggesting severe pyelonephritis or infection beyond the bladder (p.1133)","Continued fever and/or bacteremia despite antibacterial therapy, suggesting intraparenchymal renal abscess (p.1133)","Failure to respond clinically within 72 h, prompting evaluation for an uncontrolled source of infection (p.1137)","Gas in renal/perinephric tissues (emphysematous pyelonephritis), almost exclusive to poorly controlled diabetes (p.1133)","Acute papillary necrosis with sloughed papillae obstructing the ureter complicating pyelonephritis (p.1133)","Isolation of S. aureus or Salmonella from urine, raising concern for concomitant bacteremia / hematogenous seeding (p.1131)","Symptoms of urinary retention, early UTI recurrence, hematuria at follow-up, or voiding difficulty in men with febrile UTI (predict surgically correctable disease) (p.1132)"],"prognosis":"Most simple cystitis carries a low risk of serious complication, but tissue-invasive complicated disease (pyelonephritis and beyond) carries greater risk and patients are often systemically ill. Pyelonephritis may be complicated by acute papillary necrosis with obstruction (requiring relief of obstruction to preserve renal function), perinephric or renal cortical abscess (often requiring percutaneous drainage), emphysematous pyelonephritis (a severe form, almost exclusive to poorly controlled diabetes), and xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis (suppurative renal destruction associated with chronic obstruction, often by staghorn calculi, that can mimic renal malignancy and frequently requires nephrectomy). Bilateral papillary necrosis is rare but may present with a rapid rise in creatinine (p.1133, p.1137).","pitfalls":["Treating asymptomatic bacteriuria outside pregnancy or pre-urologic-procedure settings: it does not reduce future UTI and may even increase risk, possibly by promoting colonization with more virulent/resistant pathogens (p.1129, p.1136).","Ending the workup of a febrile catheterized patient at an abnormal urinalysis: bacteriuria and pyuria are essentially inevitable with catheterization, so other infectious and noninfectious causes must be systematically excluded before diagnosing CAUTI (p.1133-1134).","Premature diagnostic closure in elderly patients with isolated delirium; collateral history is needed and a reported history of recurrent UTIs may actually reflect persistent ASB (p.1132).","Relying on a negative urine dipstick to rule out bacteriuria in pregnancy; it is not sensitive enough and culture-based ASB screening is required (p.1135).","Using nitrofurantoin or fosfomycin as if they treat tissue-invasive disease; they do not achieve adequate renal tissue levels and should not be used for pyelonephritis, and nitrofurantoin is inactive against many Proteus, Providencia, Morganella, Pseudomonas, Serratia, and some Klebsiella/Enterobacter (knowledge of intrinsic resistance) (p.1136-1137).","Interpreting a mixed-flora urine culture as infection; it usually represents contamination except in long-term catheterization, chronic retention, or urinary-enteric/genital fistula (p.1135)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames the practical categorization around whether infection is confined to the bladder (uncomplicated cystitis) versus extends beyond it (complicated UTI: prostatitis, pyelonephritis, bacteremia) and whether catheter-associated (CAUTI). The key bedside questions are whether the patient is stable for outpatient management, whether a recalcitrant/recurrent source (e.g., obstructing calculi) must be identified, and whether the chosen agent must reach adequate blood and renal-tissue levels in addition to urine. Pyelonephritis itself ranges from mild (low-grade fever +/- back or costovertebral angle pain) to severe (high fever, rigors, nausea, vomiting, flank pain) (p.1130, p.1133).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1129","Harrison 22e p.1130","Harrison 22e p.1131","Harrison 22e p.1132","Harrison 22e p.1133","Harrison 22e p.1134","Harrison 22e p.1135","Harrison 22e p.1136","Harrison 22e p.1137","Harrison 22e p.1138"],"crossLinks":["CYSTITIS","PYELONEPHRITIS","CA_UTI"]},"COPD_EXACERBATION":{"name":"Acute COPD Exacerbation","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1305-1308, p.1534-1535, p.1756, p.2301-2302","clinicalPearls":["An exacerbation is defined clinically as an episodic acute worsening of respiratory symptoms (increased dyspnea, cough, and/or a change in sputum volume and character), usually evolving over fewer than 14 days; it may or may not be accompanied by fever, myalgias, or sore throat (p.2301).","COPD exacerbation is a clinical diagnosis; no single test confirms it, and measurement of pulmonary function (unlike in asthma) has not been shown to aid diagnosis or management of a COPD exacerbation (p.2301).","The strongest single predictor of future exacerbations is a history of a previous exacerbation, and the single greatest risk factor for hospitalization is a history of a previous hospitalization (p.2301).","The cardinal symptoms of a bacterial exacerbation are increased cough with increased sputum production, sputum purulence, and increased dyspnea relative to baseline; nontypeable H. influenzae exacerbations typically show only low-grade fever and no infiltrate on chest x-ray (p.1305-1307).","Exacerbation frequency rises as airflow obstruction worsens: patients with severe obstruction (FEV1 <50% predicted) average 1-3 episodes per year, though some severely obstructed patients are not frequent exacerbators (p.2301).","A target oxygen saturation of 88-92% is used; in patients with acute or chronic hypercarbia, supplemental O2 does not reduce minute ventilation but may modestly raise arterial PCO2 by altering ventilation-perfusion relationships - this should not deter giving the oxygen needed to correct hypoxemia (p.2302)."],"pathophysiology":"A variety of stimuli converge on a final common pathway of airway inflammation with increased respiratory symptoms. Viral respiratory infection, once thought less common than bacterial infection, is detected by PCR-based studies in over half of exacerbations; acquisition of a new bacterial strain is associated with increased near-term exacerbation risk. Non-infective contributors include air pollution, allergens, and environmental/Aspergillus sensitization, and in a significant minority no specific precipitant is identified (p.1756, p.2301).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Pneumonia - the most frequent abnormal finding on chest imaging in this setting; about 25% of x-rays obtained for moderate-to-severe exacerbations in severe COPD are abnormal (p.2301)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pulmonary embolism - should be actively considered because the incidence of PE is increased during COPD exacerbations (p.2301).","Congestive heart failure - a common alternative or contributing finding on imaging in patients presenting as an exacerbation (p.2301).","Pneumothorax - asymmetry on chest examination may indicate pneumothorax (or large-airway obstruction) mimicking an exacerbation (p.2301)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Arterial blood gas in patients with advanced COPD, a history of hypercarbia, altered mental status (confusion, sleepiness), or significant distress; hypercarbia is defined as PCO2 >45 mmHg and carries treatment implications (p.2301).","Chest x-ray or chest CT in patients with severe underlying COPD, moderate-to-severe distress, or focal examination findings (p.2301).","C-reactive protein, as part of emerging expert-consensus objective criteria (combining dyspnea, vital signs, CRP, and arterial blood gases) to diagnose and stage exacerbation severity, mainly of use in the emergency department (p.2301-2302)."],"redFlags":["Respiratory acidosis with hypercarbia (PCO2 >45 mmHg, pH <=7.35) - an indication for noninvasive positive-pressure ventilation, which reduces mortality, need for intubation, complications, and length of stay (p.2302).","Severe respiratory distress, severe hypercarbia and/or acidosis despite noninvasive ventilation, markedly impaired mental status, respiratory arrest, or hemodynamic instability - indications for invasive mechanical ventilation via endotracheal tube (p.2302).","New or worsening hypoxemia, severe underlying COPD, or significant comorbidity such as heart failure - features suggesting the need for inpatient treatment (p.2301).","Inability to speak in complete sentences, accessory muscle use, perioral or peripheral cyanosis, tachypnea, tachycardia, altered mental status, or paradoxical abdominal wall motion on examination (p.2301)."],"prognosis":"Mortality for patients requiring mechanical ventilatory support during a COPD exacerbation is 17-49% for that hospitalization. After any hospitalization for COPD, about 20% are rehospitalized within 30 days and 45% within a year, and mortality is approximately 20% in the year after discharge. Because of the high mortality of invasive ventilation, advance directives (e.g., resuscitation preferences) should be discussed in the outpatient setting (p.2302).","pitfalls":["Withholding needed supplemental oxygen for fear of CO2 retention is an error; oxygen should be titrated to 88-92% saturation to correct hypoxemia despite a possible modest PCO2 rise (p.2302).","Contraindications to noninvasive positive-pressure ventilation that must be screened for include cardiovascular instability, impaired mental status, inability to cooperate, copious or unclearable secretions, and craniofacial abnormality or trauma preventing mask fit (p.2302).","M. catarrhalis is easily mistaken for commensal Neisseria species on respiratory culture and was historically overlooked as a pathogen, so culture interpretation requires care (p.1308)."],"severityClassification":"Assessment should grade both the severity of the acute exacerbation and the severity of preexisting COPD; the more severe either component, the greater the likelihood of needing hospital admission. An expert consensus has proposed more objective criteria to diagnose and stage exacerbation severity using dyspnea, vital signs, C-reactive protein, and arterial blood gases, although this schema is more useful in the emergency department than the outpatient setting (p.2301-2302).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1305","Harrison 22e p.1306","Harrison 22e p.1307","Harrison 22e p.1308","Harrison 22e p.1534","Harrison 22e p.1535","Harrison 22e p.1756","Harrison 22e p.2301","Harrison 22e p.2302"],"crossLinks":["copd_exac_ni","asthma_exac","pe","heart_failure","pneumothorax"]},"CYSTITIS":{"name":"Acute Uncomplicated Cystitis","system":"Genitourinary","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Chapter 140 (Urinary Tract Infections: Cystitis, Prostatitis, and Pyelonephritis), p.1085-1092","clinicalPearls":["Bacteria in the urine without related symptoms is asymptomatic bacteriuria (ASB), not UTI; ASB usually needs no treatment and the diagnosis of cystitis requires symptoms, not merely a positive culture or pyuria (p.1086-1087).","In an otherwise healthy non-pregnant woman with typical symptoms and no complicating factors, cystitis can be diagnosed and treated on history alone, with no dipstick or culture required (p.1089, p.1091).","Patient self-diagnosis of recurrent cystitis is highly accurate, which underlies the success of patient-initiated treatment for recurrent UTI (p.1090).","Fever, rigors, or unilateral back/flank pain are inconsistent with uncomplicated cystitis and should prompt evaluation for infection beyond the bladder (kidney, prostate, or bloodstream) (p.1089).","A dipstick negative for both nitrite and leukocyte esterase should prompt consideration of non-UTI causes for symptoms, but a small fraction can be falsely negative and a negative dipstick does not fully exclude UTI in a symptomatic woman (p.1089, p.1092).","Avoid sending urine cultures in patients lacking urinary or systemic symptoms (diagnostic stewardship) to reduce overdiagnosis of UTI and inappropriate treatment of ASB (p.1089).","In older patients presenting only with altered mentation and chronic cognitive deficits, distinguishing true UTI from incidental ASB is difficult; clinicians should look for sepsis, seek collateral history, and rule out other time-sensitive causes of delirium before attributing it to UTI (p.1089).","A reported history of recurrent UTIs in an elderly patient may actually reflect persistent ASB rather than true infection (p.1089)."],"pathophysiology":"Most cystitis arises by ascending infection: gut flora (typically E. coli) colonize the vaginal introitus and periurethral area, then ascend the urethra into the bladder. Introduction of bacteria into the bladder does not inevitably cause sustained symptomatic infection; normal voiding and innate bladder defenses often clear organisms (e.g., after intercourse). Outcome reflects the interplay of host, organism, and environmental factors. Anything that increases bacterial entry or retention raises risk. The shorter female urethra is considered the primary anatomic reason cystitis predominates in young women over young men. Uropathogenic E. coli express virulence factors including type 1 pili (fimbriae) that bind mannose on bladder uroepithelium and initiate bladder infection (P fimbriae are more relevant to pyelonephritis/bloodstream invasion); toxins, iron-acquisition systems, biofilm formation, and capsules also contribute. Disruption of the normal vaginal lactobacilli (e.g., by spermicide/nonoxynol-9, or estrogen loss after menopause) permits Gram-negative colonization and bacteriuria. Hematogenous spread accounts for under 2% of UTIs. Intracellular bacterial communities within bladder epithelium are demonstrated in models but their clinical significance in humans is unclear (p.1088).","additionalDifferentials":["Cervicitis (Chlamydia trachomatis, Neisseria gonorrhoeae) presenting with dysuria (p.1090)","Urethritis, including gonococcal/chlamydial and herpetic urethritis (p.1087, p.1090)","Sexually transmitted infection mistaken for UTI, especially with multiple partners or inconsistent condom use; vaginal discharge should prompt STI consideration (p.1090-1091)"],"infectionMimics":["Vaginitis due to Candida albicans or Trichomonas vaginalis causing dysuria (p.1090)","Sexually transmitted urethritis/cervicitis (gonococcal, chlamydial, Mycoplasma genitalium) (p.1087, p.1090)","Acute or chronic bacterial prostatitis in men presenting as cystitis-like symptoms (p.1089)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Interstitial cystitis / bladder pain syndrome (p.1090)","Noninfectious vaginal or vulvar irritation (p.1090)","Chronic pelvic pain syndrome in men (not associated with bacteriuria, not improved by antibiotics) (p.1089)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine dipstick for nitrite and leukocyte esterase as point-of-care testing, used mainly to help rule out UTI when the clinical diagnosis is unclear (p.1091)","Microscopic urinalysis (white and red cell counts); pyuria is present in nearly all cystitis and hematuria in about 30% (p.1092)","Urine culture when a resistant organism is suspected, in recurrent UTI, after prior treatment failure, or in all male UTI; a colony count threshold of >10^2 bacteria/mL is more sensitive than 10^5/mL for diagnosing cystitis in symptomatic women (p.1089, p.1092)","STI screening / pelvic examination in women with risk factors for STI or with vaginal discharge (p.1090-1091)"],"redFlags":["Fever, rigors, or unilateral back/costovertebral or flank pain suggesting pyelonephritis or other extension beyond the bladder (p.1089)","Perineal, pelvic, or prostatic pain in a man, suggesting prostatitis (p.1089, p.1091)","Recurrent cystitis in a man, warranting evaluation for a prostatic focus and for urinary retention (p.1089)","Vaginal discharge raising concern for STI rather than UTI (p.1090-1091)","Early recurrence within 2 weeks (relapse), suggesting a sequestered focus of ongoing infection (p.1087)"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated cystitis generally responds promptly and favorably to appropriately chosen modern antimicrobials. About 20-30% of women who have one UTI develop recurrent episodes, and recurrences may cluster after an initial infection; recurrence likelihood decreases as time since the last infection increases. Withholding effective antimicrobial therapy (e.g., treating with NSAIDs alone, or strict delayed therapy) prolongs discomfort and carries a small increased risk of progression to pyelonephritis (p.1087, p.1092).","pitfalls":["Treating ASB as if it were UTI; the degree of pyuria in an asymptomatic patient should not drive a UTI diagnosis, and very high pyuria is common with urinary stasis (e.g., end-stage renal disease, obstructed catheters) (p.1089).","Relying on nitrite: only Enterobacterales convert nitrate to nitrite, so it can be falsely negative with non-Enterobacterales organisms or with frequent voiding from high fluid intake, and can be positive in Enterobacterales ASB (p.1091-1092).","Treating an STI (especially C. trachomatis) as UTI because symptoms overlap; women at risk for UTI are often also at risk for STI (p.1090-1091).","Assuming a negative dipstick excludes infection; it is not sensitive enough to rule out bacteriuria in pregnancy, where culture-based ASB screening is required (p.1092).","The >10 WBC/HPF pyuria cutoff has poor specificity (about 36%) for UTI; a much higher count may correlate better with symptoms, though >10/HPF remains the accepted standard (p.1092).","Misinterpreting a mixed-flora culture as infection; mixed growth usually indicates contamination except with long-term catheterization, chronic retention, or a urinary-enteric/genital fistula (p.1092)."],"severityClassification":"Uncomplicated UTI is infection confined to the bladder in a woman or man without a urinary catheter; complicated UTI is infection extending beyond the bladder (prostatitis, pyelonephritis, bacteremia) or occurring with intermittent/indwelling catheterization (CAUTI). Recurrent UTI is not inherently complicated, since individual episodes may be uncomplicated and treated as such. This contrasts with the older approach that automatically classified UTI in men, diabetics, those with anatomic abnormalities, or the immunocompromised as complicated (p.1086-1087).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1085","Harrison 22e p.1086","Harrison 22e p.1087","Harrison 22e p.1088","Harrison 22e p.1089","Harrison 22e p.1090","Harrison 22e p.1091","Harrison 22e p.1092"],"crossLinks":["PYELONEPHRITIS","COMPLICATED_UTI","PROSTATITIS"]},"C_DIFF":{"name":"Clostridioides difficile Infection","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Chapter 139, p.1082-1086 (with CT-imaging note p.640)","clinicalPearls":["CDI is the most commonly diagnosed hospital-acquired diarrhoeal illness and the leading health care-associated infection; it follows ingestion of spores that germinate and produce toxins once the normal colonic microbiota has been disrupted (Harrison 22e p.1082).","Almost any antibiotic can precipitate CDI, including vancomycin itself; clindamycin, ampicillin, cephalosporins (especially 2nd/3rd generation), and fluoroquinolones carry the highest risk, whereas penicillin/beta-lactamase-inhibitor combinations carry notably less (Harrison 22e p.1082).","Proton pump inhibitors are at most a modest risk factor; the data are weak and PPIs have not been clearly implicated in patients not already on antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.1082).","Asymptomatic faecal carriage rises with length of hospital stay (often >=20% after 2 weeks inpatient) versus only 1-3% in community residents; neonates and infants are very frequently colonised yet almost never develop disease, attributed to absent mucosal toxin receptors early in life (Harrison 22e p.1082-1083).","Stools in CDI are essentially never grossly bloody and have a characteristic odour, ranging from soft/unformed to watery or mucoid (Harrison 22e p.1084).","Recurrence after treatment occurs in roughly 15-30% of cases and may be either relapse with the same strain or reinfection with a new one, driven by ongoing disruption of the faecal microbiota by the treating antibiotic; a first recurrence raises the risk of a second (Harrison 22e p.1084-1085).","Serum IgG antibody to toxins A and B from prior colonisation/childhood exposure helps determine who develops symptomatic disease versus remains an asymptomatic carrier and protects against recurrence (Harrison 22e p.1083).","Spores persist on environmental surfaces for months and on the hands of staff; alcohol hand gels are NOT sporicidal, so soap-and-water hand washing is required during outbreaks (Harrison 22e p.1083, p.1086)."],"pathophysiology":"Ingested spores of toxigenic C. difficile survive gastric acid, germinate in the small bowel, and colonise the lower gut once antibiotic exposure has disrupted the protective microbiota. There they elaborate two large clostridial toxins: toxin A (an enterotoxin and potent neutrophil chemoattractant) and toxin B (a cytotoxin). Both glucosylate Rho-family GTP-binding proteins that regulate the actin cytoskeleton, causing loss of cell shape, adherence and tight junctions, with epithelial barrier breakdown, fluid leakage, diarrhoea and pseudomembrane formation. Toxin B appears to be the dominant virulence factor: disease occurs with toxin A-negative but not toxin B-negative strains. A third (binary) toxin, CDT, is present in all NAP1/BI/027 epidemic isolates but its pathogenic role is undefined. Disease typically requires at least three events: antibiotic exposure establishing susceptibility, acquisition of a toxigenic strain, and an inadequate host (anamnestic antibody) immune response. Notably, patients colonised by (often nontoxigenic) strains who have not previously had CDI actually have a DECREASED, not increased, risk of subsequent CDI (Harrison 22e p.1082-1084).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Only diarrhoeal (unformed) stool should be tested; testing of asymptomatic patients is not recommended except for epidemiologic study (Harrison 22e p.1084).","NAAT/PCR for the toxin gene is rapid and sensitive but may detect mere colonisation in a patient whose diarrhoea is due to another cause; toxin EIA is specific but insensitive (Harrison 22e p.1084).","Recommended multi-step algorithms: NAAT followed by toxin EIA on NAAT-positive results, or GDH (glutamate dehydrogenase common-antigen) plus toxin EIA with NAAT as arbiter when the two disagree; confirming actual toxin in stool is favoured to avoid over-diagnosing colonisation (Harrison 22e p.1084).","Empirical treatment is appropriate if stool testing is delayed (Harrison 22e p.1084).","Endoscopy (sigmoidoscopy/colonoscopy) is highly specific only if pseudomembranes are seen but is insensitive (pseudomembranes visible in only ~50% of culture/toxin-positive diarrhoea); a negative endoscopy does NOT exclude CDI. It is most useful as a rapid test in the seriously ill patient with an acute abdomen and no diarrhoea (Harrison 22e p.1084).","Abdominal CT in colitis shows bowel-wall thickening (significantly more prominent than in other colitides), wall oedema, mesenteric stranding and ascites (Harrison 22e p.640, p.1085)."],"redFlags":["Unexplained leukocytosis with WBC >=15,000/uL, especially when stool passage has ceased due to adynamic ileus (ileus seen on x-ray in ~20%) - a clue to unsuspected CDI and a marker of high risk for toxic megacolon and sepsis (Harrison 22e p.1084).","Marked leukocytosis >=20,000 WBCs/uL with an acute abdomen suggests fulminant CDI (Harrison 22e p.1085).","Fulminant disease may present WITHOUT diarrhoea, mimicking an acute surgical abdomen, with sepsis (hypotension, fever, tachycardia, leukocytosis), obstruction/ileus, colon-wall thickening and ascites - include CDI in the differential of acute abdomen, sepsis or toxic megacolon in anyone given antibiotics in the prior 2 months (Harrison 22e p.1085).","Toxic megacolon, colonic perforation, shock and the need for colectomy denote fulminant/complicated disease; a rising serum lactate (aim to operate before it reaches 5 mmol/L) signals the need for surgery (Harrison 22e p.1085-1086).","Even a single recurrence carries an ~11% risk of serious complications (shock, megacolon, perforation, colectomy or death within 30 days) (Harrison 22e p.1085)."],"prognosis":"Most patients recover, but recurrences are common (~15-30%). Attributable mortality, historically 0.6-3.5%, has reached ~6.9% in recent epidemic-strain outbreaks and rises progressively with increasing age (Harrison 22e p.1086).","pitfalls":["Do not perform a 'test of cure' after treatment: >50% of patients keep harbouring the organism and its toxin after diarrhoea resolves, and results do not predict recurrence; such results must not be used to restrict long-term-care or nursing-home placement (Harrison 22e p.1084).","Distinguish colonisation from infection - a positive NAAT/PCR in a patient with diarrhoea from another cause may simply reflect colonisation; treating asymptomatic carriers is not indicated (Harrison 22e p.1084).","In adynamic ileus the diagnosis is frequently overlooked because stool passage stops - look for unexplained leukocytosis (Harrison 22e p.1084).","A negative endoscopy does not rule out CDI, and pseudomembranes are absent in roughly half of confirmed cases (Harrison 22e p.1084).","Antiperistaltic agents and opiates may mask symptoms and possibly worsen disease and should generally be avoided (Harrison 22e p.1084)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison applies the IDSA/SHEA-aligned severity tiers as treatment-decision markers: SEVERE disease is indicated by leukocytosis >=15,000 WBCs/uL and/or a serum creatinine >=1.5 mg/dL; FULMINANT (severe complicated) disease is severe CDI plus hypotension/shock, ileus, or toxic megacolon, often with leukocytosis >=20,000/uL and a rising lactate. Mild-to-moderate disease lacks these features (Harrison 22e p.1084-1085).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1082","Harrison 22e p.1083","Harrison 22e p.1084","Harrison 22e p.1085","Harrison 22e p.1086","Harrison 22e p.640"],"crossLinks":["GASTROENTERITIS","DYSENTERY","ibd_flare"]},"DENGUE":{"name":"Dengue Fever","system":"Tropical Fever","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1711-1714, 1718, 1721-1722","clinicalPearls":["Symptomatic dengue evolves through three phases: febrile, critical (plasma leakage), and recovery; most patients never enter the critical phase, but the possibility must be actively considered in every case (p.1714).","The critical/plasma-leakage phase characteristically begins around the time fever subsides (defervescence, roughly days 3-5), so worsening at this transition - not at peak fever - signals deterioration (p.1721).","A 'saddle-back' fever pattern (recurrence of fever after defervescence and a brief remission) can occur in a subset of patients (p.1714).","Most primary DENV infections are subclinical, and only about 25% of the ~400 million annual infections cause clinical illness (p.1711).","Incubation is approximately 4-7 days (p.1714).","A positive tourniquet test (>=10 new petechiae per square inch on the upper arm after BP-cuff inflation for 5 min) reflects microvascular damage and is more often linked to severe dengue (p.1714).","Near defervescence a maculopapular rash typically starts on the trunk and spreads to the extremities and face; a separate transient macular rash may appear at illness onset (p.1714).","During recovery a patchy erythematous pruritic rash that desquamates may develop as extravascular fluid and effusions are reabsorbed (p.1722).","Early IgG appearing about 4 days after onset and rising rapidly suggests a secondary (rather than primary) infection, where IgG otherwise appears later and at much lower titers (p.1721)."],"pathophysiology":"Dengue is caused by the four flavivirus (orthoflavivirus) serotypes DENV-1 to DENV-4, transmitted mainly by Aedes aegypti (and the secondary vector Aedes albopictus), which bite during the day. After infection of one serotype, the cross-protection against a different (heterotypic) serotype is only transient and wanes within weeks to a few weeks of convalescence; subsequent heterotypic infection can produce classic dengue or, less often, severe dengue. The progression to severe disease centers on immune-mediated enhancement, in which enhancing, non-neutralizing antibodies are implicated, whereas neutralizing antibodies are associated with lower viremia on reinfection. The hallmark of severe dengue is a vascular-leak syndrome producing increased capillary permeability, intravascular volume depletion, hypoalbuminemia, and serosal (pleural, ascitic) effusions; recent evidence assigns the DENV NS1 protein a key role in this vascular leak. Host/viral risk modifiers include age (susceptibility to severe dengue drops markedly after 12 years), the specific sequence and timing of serotype infections (e.g., DENV-1 then DENV-2 tends to be more severe), and viral-variant heterogeneity (p.1711, 1721).","additionalDifferentials":["Zika virus disease (often misdiagnosed as dengue; low-grade fever, itchy maculopapular rash, arthralgia/myalgia, nonpurulent conjunctivitis)","Influenza","Other arboviral fevers presenting as fever-and-myalgia syndrome"],"infectionMimics":["Chikungunya","Zika virus infection","Yellow fever","Leptospirosis","Other viral hemorrhagic fevers"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Antigen-detection ELISA or RT-PCR (nucleic acid amplification) during the acute phase, useful within roughly the first 5 days of illness (p.1714, 1721)","Combination point-of-care testing pairing NS1 antigen with IgM antibody assay, increasingly used clinically (p.1721)","Paired (acute and convalescent) serology during recovery (p.1714)","Serum AST (often modestly elevated without true hepatic synthetic dysfunction) (p.1714)","Tourniquet test as a bedside marker of microvascular fragility (p.1714)"],"redFlags":["Onset of restlessness or lethargy together with thrombocytopenia (<100,000/uL) and hemoconcentration 2-5 days into illness, typically at defervescence (p.1721)","Signs of plasma leakage: rising hematocrit/hemoconcentration, hypoalbuminemia, and serosal effusions (pleural effusion, ascites) (p.1721)","Circulatory compromise with narrowed pulse pressure, hepatomegaly, and cyanosis progressing to shock (p.1721)","Severe or spontaneous bleeding in the absence of a predisposing lesion (p.1721)","Severe organ dysfunction: altered mental status or seizures (CNS), arrhythmias (cardiac), acute kidney injury (renal), hepatic injury (p.1721)","Respiratory distress from plasma leakage (p.1721)"],"prognosis":"Most patients with uncomplicated dengue recover from the acute illness within 7-10 days, though convalescence may be prolonged and post-acute fatigue and depression are recognized sequelae. Among the millions of annual DENV infections, about 500,000 progress to severe dengue, with an overall case-fatality rate near 2.5%; with early recognition and good supportive care most patients with severe dengue respond well, and CFR at experienced tropical centers can be as low as ~1% (p.1714, 1721-1722).","pitfalls":["Anchoring on the febrile phase and missing the critical phase, which paradoxically begins as fever declines rather than at its peak (p.1721).","Serologic cross-reactivity with antigenically similar orthoflaviviruses (yellow fever, Zika, Japanese encephalitis virus) - including after vaccination - can confound serologic diagnosis (p.1721).","Relying on antigen/nucleic-acid tests outside their acute-phase window: after about day 5 diagnosis must shift to serology (p.1721).","Failing to distinguish secondary from primary infection: early, rapidly rising IgG points to secondary infection and higher severe-disease risk (p.1721)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison endorses the WHO pragmatic categories that replaced the older 'dengue fever / dengue hemorrhagic fever / dengue shock syndrome' terminology: dengue without warning signs, dengue with warning signs (which directs inpatient management), and severe dengue. Severe dengue is defined as confirmed or presumptive dengue plus at least one of: (1) plasma leakage severe enough to cause shock or respiratory distress, (2) severe bleeding, or (3) severe organ dysfunction. Every patient with presumptive dengue of any severity should be assessed up front for warning signs and for the criteria of severe dengue, with the provisional diagnosis of severe dengue made clinically (p.1704, 1721).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1711","Harrison 22e p.1712","Harrison 22e p.1713","Harrison 22e p.1714","Harrison 22e p.1718","Harrison 22e p.1721","Harrison 22e p.1722"],"crossLinks":["CHIKUNGUNYA","LEPTOSPIROSIS","MALARIA"]},"DEVICE_INFECTION":{"name":"Device-Related Infection (CIED / Vascular Catheter)","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1078, 1082-1085, 1200-1201","clinicalPearls":["CIED-related endocarditis (CIED-IE) involves either the device hardware itself or the endothelium at points of device contact, and occasionally coexists with concurrent valvular infection (Harrison 22e p.1078).","The timing of CIED-IE is split roughly into thirds: about one-third present within 3 months of implantation or manipulation, one-third between 3 and 12 months, and one-third more than a year afterward (Harrison 22e p.1078).","CIED infection can be obvious from a generator pocket infection (especially within 6 months of device manipulation) or cryptic, arising through bacteremic seeding without any visible pocket involvement (Harrison 22e p.1082).","Risk of device-associated endocarditis is reported as higher on implanted defibrillators and resynchronization devices than on permanent pacemakers (Harrison 22e p.1078).","In a patient with a CIED, an unexplained Staphylococcus aureus bacteremia (and persistent coagulase-negative staphylococcal bacteremia) without another source is likely to indicate CIED-IE or valvular IE and should be investigated and managed as such (Harrison 22e p.1084).","Relapse of bloodstream infection after a course of antimicrobial therapy in a CIED patient increases the likelihood that the device is the source and warrants treatment as CIED-IE (Harrison 22e p.1084).","S. aureus device-related infections tend to be acute with both local and systemic manifestations and to progress more rapidly than the indolent course seen in non-S. aureus staphylococcal infections; a pyogenic collection at the device site is relatively common (Harrison 22e p.1200-1201)."],"pathophysiology":"Cardiac implantable electronic devices (pacemakers, implantable defibrillators, cardiac resynchronization devices) and other prosthetic intravascular hardware provide an avascular foreign surface that organisms colonize and on which they persist. Infection localizes to the generator pocket and/or the intravascular leads, and may extend to the endothelium at points of lead contact, producing endocarditis (CIED-IE). Staphylococci dominate because skin flora is the principal portal; S. aureus and coagulase-negative staphylococci cause the majority of CIED-IE cases. Once organisms establish on the foreign material, antimicrobial therapy alone usually fails to eradicate them, because the retained device continues to act as a nidus for persistent or recurrent infection (Harrison 22e p.1078, p.1201).","additionalDifferentials":["Left-sided native-valve or prosthetic-valve endocarditis (may coexist with or be mistaken for CIED-IE) (Harrison 22e p.1078, p.1084)","Bloodstream infection from an extracardiac focus (e.g., gram-negative, streptococcal, or enterococcal bacteremia) not arising from the device (Harrison 22e p.1084)","Remote-site metastatic infection complicating bacteremic CIED infection, such as vertebral osteomyelitis (Harrison 22e p.1084)"],"infectionMimics":["Non-device bloodstream infection from a separate source presenting with fever and sepsis in a CIED patient (Harrison 22e p.1084)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transesophageal echocardiography (TEE): detects vegetations in >90% of patients with definite IE; an initial study may be falsely negative in a minority, so a negative TEE when IE is likely should be repeated in 7-10 days rather than used to exclude the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1083).","Three or more separate blood culture sets (two-bottle, ~10 mL per bottle) from different venipuncture sites; if negative at 48-72 h, obtain additional sets and consult the lab for optimal techniques (Harrison 22e p.1082-1083).","FDG-PET/CT: less sensitive than TEE for intracardiac pathology in CIED-IE but useful for assessing CIED pocket and lead infection and extracardiac/remote complications (Harrison 22e p.1083).","Aspiration of any pyogenic collection at the device/pocket site, combined with blood cultures, to establish the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1201).","Echocardiographic assessment is specifically indicated in monomicrobial S. aureus bacteremia when an intracardiac device is present (Harrison 22e p.1084)."],"redFlags":["S. aureus bacteremia in a patient with a CIED with no alternative source (Harrison 22e p.1084)","Persistent or relapsing bacteremia after antimicrobial therapy in a device patient (Harrison 22e p.1084)","Generator pocket inflammation/pyogenic collection at the device site (Harrison 22e p.1082, p.1201)","Lead vegetation >2 cm, which carries a risk of pulmonary embolism (Harrison 22e p.1085)"],"prognosis":"Removal of the infected CIED during the initial hospitalization is associated with increased 30-day and 1-year survival compared with antibiotic therapy and device retention. Attempted salvage of an infected device with antibiotics alone plus long-term suppressive therapy is usually unsuccessful and is reserved only for patients whose devices cannot be removed or who decline removal; left in place, the device remains a nidus for persistent or recurrent infection (Harrison 22e p.1084, p.1085, p.1201).","pitfalls":["Treating an infected CIED with antibiotics alone (salvage/suppression) usually fails and should not be the default approach (Harrison 22e p.1084).","Using a negative TEE to rule out CIED-IE when clinical suspicion is high, rather than repeating the study in 7-10 days (Harrison 22e p.1083).","In critically ill patients already on antibiotics for other indications, blood cultures may be negative and device-related endocarditis missed (Harrison 22e p.1200-1201).","Assuming every bloodstream infection in a CIED patient indicates the device: gram-negative, streptococcal, or enterococcal bacteremia from an extracardiac focus may not be device-related, though relapse afterward raises suspicion for CIED-IE (Harrison 22e p.1084)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison distinguishes generator pocket infection without bacteremia (more localized) from bacteremic CIED infection and CIED-IE (more severe, with risk of coincident left-sided valve infection or remote metastatic foci); complete hardware removal is required across the spectrum of established CIED-IE and pocket or intracardiac lead infection (Harrison 22e p.1084, p.1085).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1078","Harrison 22e p.1082","Harrison 22e p.1083","Harrison 22e p.1084","Harrison 22e p.1085","Harrison 22e p.1200","Harrison 22e p.1201"],"crossLinks":["IE","SEPSIS"]},"DIABETIC_FOOT":{"name":"Diabetic Foot Infection","system":"Skin & Soft Tissue","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.91-92, 1111-1112, 3271-3272","clinicalPearls":["A wound that probes to bone (rock-hard, gritty base without intervening soft tissue) strongly predicts underlying osteomyelitis; the probe-to-bone test has a positive predictive value of ~90% in high-pretest-probability populations (p.1112).","In a hospitalized diabetic patient with a chronic deep foot ulcer, the pretest probability of osteomyelitis is high (~65%), so a positive probe-to-bone test can often establish the diagnosis clinically without imaging (p.91, 1112).","A negative probe-to-bone test lowers the likelihood of osteomyelitis (likelihood ratio ~0.2) (p.92).","An infected ulcer is a clinical diagnosis; superficial swab cultures recover multiple organisms of uncertain significance and correlate poorly with bone biopsy (only ~24% concordance in one study) (p.1111-1112, 3271-3272).","Culture-pathogen concordance between deep samples and bone is best for S. aureus (40-50%) and poorest for coagulase-negative staphylococci, anaerobes, and gram-negative bacilli (p.1112).","About 34% of people with diabetes develop a foot ulcer in their lifetime; among those with an ulcer, ~5% per year progress to foot osteomyelitis (p.1111).","Gas gangrene-like findings may occur in a diabetic foot wound even in the absence of clostridial infection (p.3272)."],"pathophysiology":"Diabetic foot infection arises from the interaction of peripheral sensory, motor, and autonomic neuropathy with abnormal foot biomechanics, peripheral arterial disease, and impaired wound healing. Sensory neuropathy removes protective pain sensation, allowing unrecognized repetitive or major trauma; impaired proprioception causes abnormal weight-bearing and callus/ulcer formation. Motor and sensory neuropathy produce structural deformities (hammer/claw toe, prominent metatarsal heads, Charcot joint). Autonomic neuropathy causes anhidrosis with dry, fissured skin. Peripheral arterial disease and poor healing then allow minor skin breaks to enlarge and become infected. Foot osteomyelitis is almost always acquired by the exogenous (contiguous) route, progressing from skin/soft-tissue infection into bone, and can also follow deep wound infection after foot surgery (p.1111, 3271).","additionalDifferentials":["Charcot neuroarthropathy (neuropathic joint disease) — can mimic or coexist with foot osteomyelitis (p.1111, 3272)","Purely neuropathic ulcer without infection (p.3272)","Cellulitis without underlying ulceration (p.3272)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Charcot (neuropathic) arthropathy with bony deformity, which can radiographically and clinically resemble osteomyelitis (p.1111, 3272)","Callus or pressure changes from abnormal foot biomechanics (p.3271)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Probe-to-bone test with a blunt sterile instrument as a bedside clinical assessment (p.92, 1112, 3272)","Bone biopsy with culture (preferred basis for diagnosing/treating osteomyelitis given poor swab-to-bone correlation) (p.1112)","Cultures taken from the debrided ulcer base, purulent drainage, or wound aspiration rather than superficial swabs (p.3272)","MRI when pretest probability is lower or imaging needed (sensitivity 80-100%, specificity 80-90%) (p.1112)","Nuclear medicine studies (PET, CT/SPECT) or labeled white-cell scans as alternatives to MRI (p.3272)","Ankle-brachial index screening for peripheral arterial disease in diabetics >50 years with risk factors (p.3272)"],"redFlags":["Wound that probes to bone (high likelihood of osteomyelitis) (p.1112, 3272)","Chronic ulcer failing to respond to therapy (prompt plain radiographs to assess for osteomyelitis) (p.3272)","Underlying peripheral arterial insufficiency / ischemic limb (contributes to non-healing and amputation risk) (p.3272)","Charcot foot deformity complicated by chronic osteomyelitis (p.1111)"],"prognosis":"Diabetes is the leading cause of nontraumatic lower-extremity amputation. Diabetic foot osteomyelitis increases amputation risk; a foot ulcer (or subsequent ulceration) carries a 14-24% risk of eventual amputation. Adequate management of early-stage infection lowers the amputation rate, and wound debridement combined with antibiotics makes amputation unnecessary in roughly two-thirds of patients (p.1111-1112, 3271).","pitfalls":["Do NOT rely on superficial swab cultures to guide therapy — they grow contaminants/colonizers and correlate poorly with bone culture (p.1112, 3272).","Plain x-rays have limited sensitivity (30-90%) for diabetic foot osteomyelitis and are mainly useful for follow-up of confirmed cases, not for early diagnosis (p.1112).","A negative probe-to-bone test does not fully exclude osteomyelitis, especially when pretest probability is low — pursue MRI (p.1112).","Antiseptic agents should be avoided and topical antibiotics are of limited value in wound care (p.3272)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison references the IWGDF/IDSA 2023 guidelines for diagnosis and treatment of diabetes-related foot infections but does not reproduce the detailed IDSA/IWGDF grading scale in this text. Infections are described broadly as mild/non-limb-threatening versus more severe (p.1112, 3272).","references":["Harrison 22e p.91","Harrison 22e p.92","Harrison 22e p.1111","Harrison 22e p.1112","Harrison 22e p.3271","Harrison 22e p.3272"],"crossLinks":["CELLULITIS","NECROTIZING_FASCIITIS"]},"DISSEMINATED_TB":{"name":"Disseminated (Miliary) Tuberculosis","system":"Systemic / Tuberculosis","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1386, 1389-1390, 1393","clinicalPearls":["Presentation is notoriously nonspecific and protean, varying with the organs predominantly seeded; constitutional features (fever, night sweats, anorexia, weakness, weight loss) dominate in most patients (Harrison 22e p.1390).","Choroidal tubercles on fundoscopic eye examination are pathognomonic of miliary TB and are present in up to roughly 30% of cases, so dilated fundoscopy is a high-yield bedside step (Harrison 22e p.1390).","A high index of suspicion is essential; the diagnosis is easily missed and untreated miliary TB is lethal, whereas early treatment is curative (Harrison 22e p.1390).","Sputum-smear microscopy is negative in most cases, so a negative smear does not exclude the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1390).","The tuberculin skin test may be negative in up to half of patients, with reactivity sometimes returning during chemotherapy; a negative TST therefore cannot rule out disease (Harrison 22e p.1390).","In adults miliary TB may arise from either recent infection or reactivation of old disseminated foci, whereas in children it more often follows primary infection (Harrison 22e p.1390).","Granulomas are often found on liver or bone-marrow biopsy, and bronchoalveolar lavage with transbronchial biopsy improves bacteriologic confirmation (Harrison 22e p.1390)."],"pathophysiology":"Miliary/disseminated TB results from hematogenous spread of M. tuberculosis when an acquired immune response fails to contain infection. Bacilli carried to regional lymph nodes reach the central venous return and the systemic circulation, seeding multiple organs with innumerable small (1-2 mm) yellowish granulomas resembling millet seeds. Occult hematogenous dissemination commonly follows primary infection; in the very young and the immunocompromised (including people living with HIV), insufficient immunity allows progression to fatal miliary disease or tuberculous meningitis (Harrison 22e p.1386, 1390).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Disseminated fungal infection (a differential when miliary or interstitial pulmonary infiltrates and multi-organ involvement are present) (Harrison 22e p.1390)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundoscopic eye examination for choroidal tubercles (pathognomonic, up to ~30% of cases) (Harrison 22e p.1390)","Bronchoalveolar lavage and transbronchial biopsy for improved bacteriologic confirmation (Harrison 22e p.1390)","Full blood count for hematologic clues (anemia with leukopenia, lymphopenia, neutrophilic leukocytosis/leukemoid reactions, polycythemia; pancytopenia in nonreactive form; DIC reported) (Harrison 22e p.1390)","Liver function tests including alkaline phosphatase (elevated with hepatic involvement) (Harrison 22e p.1390)","Tuberculin skin test (may be falsely negative in up to half of cases) (Harrison 22e p.1390)","Xpert MTB/RIF as the initial test when TB meningitis is suspected; positive results prompt immediate treatment (Harrison 22e p.1393)"],"redFlags":["Meningismus, present in fewer than 10% of cases, signals possible concurrent tuberculous meningitis and warrants urgent CNS evaluation (Harrison 22e p.1390).","Pancytopenia with an acute septicemic course suggests nonreactive miliary TB, a rare and rapidly fatal form from massive dissemination (Harrison 22e p.1390).","Disseminated intravascular coagulation has been reported and indicates severe disease (Harrison 22e p.1390)."],"prognosis":"Unrecognized miliary TB is lethal, but with proper early treatment it is amenable to cure. Cryptic miliary TB (seen in the elderly) runs a chronic course with mild intermittent fever and anemia, ultimately progressing to meningeal involvement before death. The nonreactive (acute septicemic) form is rapidly fatal with multiple necrotic, nongranulomatous lesions at postmortem. Glucocorticoid therapy has not proved beneficial in miliary TB (Harrison 22e p.1390).","pitfalls":["Relying on sputum-smear microscopy or the tuberculin skin test to exclude the diagnosis, since both are frequently negative in miliary TB (Harrison 22e p.1390).","Expecting a typical chest radiograph: early in the course, and especially among people living with HIV, the CXR may show no abnormality; findings may instead be large or interstitial infiltrates or pleural effusion rather than the classic miliary reticulonodular pattern (Harrison 22e p.1390).","Overlooking cryptic miliary TB in elderly patients with only mild intermittent fever and anemia (Harrison 22e p.1390)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.1386","Harrison 22e p.1390","Harrison 22e p.1393"],"crossLinks":["PUO","CNS_TB","PULMONARY_TB","malignancy_b"]},"DYSENTERY":{"name":"Dysentery (Invasive Bacterial Diarrhea)","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e: Ch 49 (Diarrhea and Constipation) pp.348-350; Ch 138 (Acute Infectious Diarrheal Diseases and Bacterial Food Poisoning) pp.1119-1124; Ch 166 (Diseases Caused by Gram-Negative Enteric Bacilli, E. coli pathotypes) pp.1331-1332; Ch 167/168 (Campylobacter) pp.1306,1348-1349; Ch 172 (Shigellosis) pp.1366-1368; Ch 230 (Amebiasis) pp.1753-1757","clinicalPearls":["Dysentery is bloody diarrhoea arising from mucosal invasion or cytotoxin injury to the colon, not from secretory/enterotoxin mechanisms; the triad of high fever, abdominal pain and grossly bloody stool points to invasive disease rather than toxin-mediated watery diarrhoea (p.306, p.1077).","The single most useful initial distinction is inflammatory vs noninflammatory diarrhoea: grossly bloody or mucoid stool, plus fecal leukocytes or a positive fecal lactoferrin/calprotectin, defines the inflammatory (dysenteric) category and a colonic/distal small-bowel locus (p.1079, p.1077).","Tenesmus (painful rectal spasm with a strong urge but little stool passage) signals proctitis and is characteristic of the distal-colonic involvement of shigellosis and amoebiasis (p.1078).","Bloody diarrhoea with a low-grade fever or no fever is a clinical clue to Shiga toxin-producing/enterohaemorrhagic E. coli (EHEC, e.g. O157:H7), which can be mistaken for noninfectious disease such as ischaemic or inflammatory bowel disease (p.1081, p.1288).","Unlike most watery diarrhoeal syndromes, dysenteric illness rarely presents with major dehydration; in shigellosis the stools are small-volume and dehydration is uncommon (p.1366).","Shigellosis classically evolves through four phases: incubation (usually 1-4 days), initial watery diarrhoea, then dysentery with frequent small bloody mucopurulent stools, tenesmus and cramps, followed by a postinfectious phase (p.1366).","On stool microscopy, abundant PMNs in every field favour bacterial enteroinvasive disease (especially Shigella), whereas erythrophagocytic trophozoites with few PMNs favour Entamoeba histolytica (p.1366).","Fewer than ~40% of patients with amoebic dysentery are febrile, in contrast to bacterial dysentery; nearly all have heme-positive stools, and amoebic colitis typically has a more gradual onset over 2-6 weeks (p.1754).","EHEC/STEC stools are commonly grossly bloody with abdominal pain and fecal leukocytes in ~70% of cases, yet fever is characteristically absent (p.1288).","An appendicitis-like presentation (right-sided pain, tenderness) should raise Yersinia enterocolitica, which invades the terminal ileum and proximal colon; Campylobacter and amoebic caecal involvement can similarly mimic appendicitis (p.306, p.1078)."],"pathophysiology":"Dysentery results from injury to the colonic (and distal small-bowel) mucosa by two overlapping mechanisms. (1) Bacterial invasion: Shigella and enteroinvasive E. coli invade mucosal epithelial cells, multiply intracellularly and spread cell-to-cell, producing acute colitis mainly of the distal colon and rectum with edematous, hemorrhagic, ulcerated mucosa. (2) Cytotoxin production: organisms such as Shigella dysenteriae type 1, Shiga toxin-producing/enterohaemorrhagic E. coli and Vibrio parahaemolyticus elaborate cytotoxins that destroy mucosal cells; Shiga toxins inhibit the 60S ribosomal subunit, shut off protein synthesis, and after systemic translocation injure renal and cerebral microvascular endothelium. The resulting mucosal destruction yields small-volume bloody, mucopurulent stools containing inflammatory cells. Salmonella invades the bowel mucosa and causes inflammatory diarrhoea but generally does not destroy enterocytes or produce the full dysentery syndrome. Entamoeba histolytica trophozoites adhere via the Gal/GalNAc lectin, degrade mucus and extracellular matrix with proteases, and kill epithelial and inflammatory cells by contact-dependent (apoptosis, trogocytosis, pore-forming peptides) and contact-independent cytotoxicity, producing characteristic flask-shaped colonic ulcers and bloody diarrhoea, and can invade the portal venous system to seed the liver (p.306, p.1076-1077, p.1366, p.1754-1755).","additionalDifferentials":["Shigella species (bacillary dysentery; prototypic invasive cause)","Shiga toxin-producing / enterohaemorrhagic E. coli (STEC/EHEC, e.g. O157:H7)","Enteroinvasive E. coli (EIEC; shigellosis-like dysentery, shares ancestry with Shigella)","Campylobacter jejuni (inflammatory diarrhoea, ~10% grossly bloody in adults)","Non-typhoidal Salmonella (inflammatory diarrhoea; full dysentery uncommon)","Yersinia enterocolitica (terminal ileitis/right-sided pain mimicking appendicitis)","Entamoeba histolytica (amoebic dysentery; subacute, travel/endemic exposure)","Vibrio parahaemolyticus (can cause dysentery; shellfish exposure)","Clostridioides difficile (cytotoxin-mediated colitis; antibiotic/healthcare exposure)","Klebsiella oxytoca (antibiotic-associated haemorrhagic colitis)"],"infectionMimics":["Clostridioides difficile colitis (consider with recent or current antibiotic use, hospitalisation/long-term care; can also be community-acquired without prior antibiotics)","Enteric fever (Salmonella Typhi, Yersinia enterocolitica) - penetrating organisms that can present with fever and abdominal pain; fecal mononuclear leukocytes rather than frank dysentery","Schistosomiasis and other invasive parasitic colitides in endemic exposure","Sexually transmitted proctocolitis (gonococcal, syphilitic, chlamydial/LGV) presenting with bloody mucoid rectal discharge and tenesmus"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Inflammatory bowel disease - a first flare of ulcerative colitis or Crohn's disease can be clinically indistinguishable from invasive bacterial dysentery, particularly in industrialised settings; travel history and stool studies help discriminate, and IBD should not be diagnosed until invasive infection (e.g. Campylobacter, amoebiasis) is excluded (p.1366, p.1306)","Ischaemic colitis - typically age >50, acute lower abdominal pain preceding watery then bloody diarrhoea, with inflammation of the sigmoid/left colon and rectal sparing (p.306)","Colonic diverticulitis (p.307)","Graft-versus-host disease of the gut (p.307)","Intussusception (can mimic afebrile bloody diarrhoea, especially in children) (p.1288)","Drug-induced colitis (e.g. NSAIDs and other medication-related diarrhoea) (p.306)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Fecal leukocyte smear (methylene blue wet mount) - suggests inflammatory disease, though predictive value is debated (p.1078-1079)","Fecal lactoferrin (latex agglutination or ELISA) - more sensitive surrogate marker of fecal leukocytes than microscopy; fecal calprotectin similarly used (p.306, p.1079)","Specific/selective stool culture with isolate retention for Salmonella, Shigella and Campylobacter (MacConkey/Salmonella-Shigella/selenite media; Campylobacter on selective medium at 42C microaerophilic); sampling the bloody/mucopurulent portion or a rectal swab improves Shigella yield (p.1079-1080, p.1367)","Dedicated Shiga toxin / Shiga toxin-gene testing AND culture in ALL community-acquired diarrhoea (inflammatory or not), because bloody stool and fecal leukocytes are not reliably present with STEC/EHEC; O157 screened as sorbitol non-fermenters on sorbitol-MacConkey (p.1080, p.1288-1289)","Culture-independent multiplex nucleic acid amplification panels (e.g. detecting STEC, ETEC, EPEC, EAEC, EIEC) - faster and more sensitive than culture, but a positive result should be followed by attempted culture isolation for susceptibility testing and outbreak surveillance (p.1080-1081, p.1289)","Stool examination for ova and parasites with E. histolytica-specific rapid antigen detection (microscopy is less sensitive/specific); definitive amoebic diagnosis requires hematophagous trophozoites, as cysts cannot be distinguished from E. dispar/E. moshkovskii/E. bangladeshi; examine at least three fresh specimens (p.1080, p.1756)","Amoebic serology (ELISA) - positive in >90% of amoebic colitis/ameboma/liver abscess and useful before starting glucocorticoids when IBD is considered (p.1756)","CBC and peripheral smear (microangiopathic hemolytic anaemia, schizocytes, thrombocytopenia signalling HUS), renal function/creatinine, and LDH when STEC/EHEC or S. dysenteriae 1 is suspected (p.1288, p.1366)","Sigmoidoscopy/colonoscopy with biopsy - reserved for uncharacterised persistent diarrhoea or suspected IBD; endoscopic biopsy may not distinguish Campylobacter colitis from IBD, and is hazardous during fulminant amoebic colitis (perforation risk) (p.307, p.1306, p.1756)"],"redFlags":["Grossly bloody diarrhoea with low-grade or absent fever - suspect STEC/EHEC and the risk of haemolytic-uraemic syndrome (p.1081, p.1288)","Falling urine output, pallor, irritability, edema, or new neurological signs after several days of diarrhoea - developing HUS (microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia, thrombocytopenia, acute renal failure) (p.1288, p.1366)","Abdominal distension and tenderness with loss of haustration / dilated transverse colon - toxic megacolon, a complication of severe Shigella (and severe amoebic) colitis (p.1366)","Signs of perforation or peritonitis (rebound, free air) (p.1366)","Altered consciousness, seizures or coma - seen especially in children under 5 with severe shigellosis and carries a poor prognosis (p.1366)","Severe dehydration (lethargy, feeble pulse, hypotension, shock) (p.1078)","High fever (>=38.5C), profuse diarrhoea, severe abdominal pain in patients over 50, or illness in the elderly (>=70) or immunocompromised - warrant prompt stool microbiological evaluation (p.306)","Rectal prolapse in a child with dysentery (a recognised complication of severe shigellosis) (p.1367)"],"prognosis":"Most invasive bacterial dysentery is self-limited: shigellosis usually resolves within about a week (faster with appropriate therapy), and STEC/EHEC illness typically lasts 5-10 days. Severe outcomes and death cluster in children under 5 (especially the malnourished) and the elderly. Adverse prognostic features in clinically severe shigellosis include nonbloody diarrhoea, moderate-to-severe dehydration, bacteraemia, absence of fever, abdominal tenderness, rectal prolapse, and altered consciousness. Haemolytic-uraemic syndrome occurs in roughly 15% of infected children under 10 with STEC/EHEC; with dialysis support its mortality is under 10%, but survivors often have persisting renal and neurological dysfunction; acute renal failure complicates 55-70% of HUS cases yet renal function recovers in most. Postinfectious sequelae include reactive arthritis (after Shigella flexneri, Salmonella, Campylobacter, Yersinia) and, after Campylobacter, Guillain-Barre syndrome; postinfectious irritable bowel syndrome may follow, particularly after Campylobacter (p.306, p.1288, p.1306, p.1366).","pitfalls":["Do NOT give antibiotics for suspected enterohaemorrhagic/Shiga toxin-producing E. coli (grossly bloody diarrhoea, often afebrile) - antibiotics induce Shiga toxin-producing phage replication, can increase toxin production roughly 20-fold, and raise the risk of HUS; this caution applies especially to children (p.1081, p.1289).","Avoid antimotility/antiperistaltic agents (e.g. loperamide, diphenoxylate-atropine) in febrile or bloody dysentery - they may prolong fever and shedding in Shigella, increase the risk of toxic megacolon, and have been implicated in HUS in EHEC-infected children (p.349, p.1077, p.1367).","Do not diagnose inflammatory bowel disease before excluding invasive infection - Campylobacter and amoebic colitis can closely mimic and even be histologically confused with ulcerative colitis or Crohn's disease (p.1306, p.1755).","Exclude amoebiasis (stool studies plus serology) before giving glucocorticoids for presumed IBD - unsuspected amoebic colitis can become fulminant or develop toxic megacolon on steroids (p.1755-1756, p.1757).","A negative fecal leukocyte/lactoferrin test or absence of fever does NOT rule out invasive disease - STEC/EHEC frequently lacks reliable fecal leukocytes and fever, which is why both Shiga toxin testing and culture are recommended in all community-acquired diarrhoea (p.1080, p.1289).","Shigella is fragile and is easily lost in transport (temperature/pH changes); inoculate promptly or use a holding medium (Cary-Blair) and sample the bloody/mucopurulent stool portion or use a rectal swab to maximise yield (p.1367).","Microscopy cannot distinguish E. histolytica cysts from non-pathogenic E. dispar (and E. moshkovskii/E. bangladeshi); a microscopic species diagnosis of E. histolytica requires demonstrating trophozoites that have ingested erythrocytes (use antigen/molecular testing) (p.1080, p.1756).","Empirical antimotility therapy should be avoided more broadly with febrile dysentery; bismuth subsalicylate should be avoided in immunocompromised or renally impaired patients (bismuth encephalopathy risk) (p.349)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames severity primarily by the inflammatory vs noninflammatory distinction and by host/feature risk rather than a single formal dysentery score. Inflammatory (dysenteric) disease is defined by grossly bloody/mucoid stool with fecal leukocytes/lactoferrin and a colonic locus (Table 138-1). Indications that mark an episode as severe and warranting evaluation/empirical therapy include profuse diarrhoea with dehydration, overtly bloody stools, fever >=38.5C (>=101F), duration >48 h without improvement, severe abdominal pain in patients over 50, and elderly (>=70) or immunocompromised hosts (Ch 49). Dehydration is graded clinically as mild (thirst, dry mouth, decreased urine output), moderate (orthostatic hypotension, skin tenting, sunken eyes/fontanelle), and severe (lethargy, obtundation, feeble pulse, hypotension, shock) (Ch 138). For shigellosis, severe disease is signalled by features such as bacteraemia, altered consciousness/seizures, toxic megacolon, perforation, severe dehydration, and occurrence in malnourished young children or the elderly (Ch 172) (p.306, p.1077-1078, p.1366).","references":["Harrison 22e p.306","Harrison 22e p.307","Harrison 22e p.349","Harrison 22e p.350","Harrison 22e p.1076","Harrison 22e p.1077","Harrison 22e p.1078","Harrison 22e p.1079","Harrison 22e p.1080","Harrison 22e p.1081","Harrison 22e p.1288","Harrison 22e p.1289","Harrison 22e p.1306","Harrison 22e p.1366","Harrison 22e p.1367","Harrison 22e p.1754","Harrison 22e p.1755","Harrison 22e p.1756","Harrison 22e p.1757"],"crossLinks":["GASTROENTERITIS","C_DIFF","ENTERIC_FEVER","AMOEBIC_LIVER_ABSCESS","ibd_flare"]},"ENCEPHALITIS":{"name":"Encephalitis","system":"Central Nervous System","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1111-1117 (Ch.142 Encephalitis); arboviral/Japanese encephalitis p.1670-1671 (Ch.215); autoimmune/anti-NMDAR encephalitis p.746-747 (Ch.99)","clinicalPearls":["Encephalitis differs from meningitis by the presence of altered brain function itself - altered consciousness (confusion, behavioural change, lethargy to coma) and focal or diffuse neurologic signs - whereas uncomplicated meningitis spares brain parenchymal function (p.1111).","Seizures (focal or generalized) occur in many patients with encephalitis, and prophylactic anticonvulsant therapy may be considered given the high seizure frequency in severe cases (p.1111, p.1115).","Clinical features alone cannot reliably establish the specific viral cause; presentations overlap too much, so etiology rests on CSF PCR, serology, imaging and epidemiologic clues (p.1111).","Features pointing to inferomedial frontotemporal involvement - prominent olfactory or gustatory hallucinations, anosmia, bizarre behaviour or personality change, and memory disturbance - should specifically raise HSV encephalitis (p.1114).","HSV is the single most common cause of sporadic encephalitis and the chief treatable cause, so the central diagnostic task once non-viral causes are excluded is to distinguish HSV from other viruses (p.1112, p.1114).","A CSF pleocytosis (>5 cells/uL) occurs in >95% of immunocompetent patients with viral encephalitis; severely immunocompromised patients (HIV, glucocorticoids/immunosuppressants, chemotherapy, lymphoreticular malignancy) may fail to mount this response (p.1112).","CSF cell counts exceed 500/uL in only about 10% of cases; counts >1000/uL should prompt consideration of non-viral infection or other inflammatory processes (p.1112).","A low CSF glucose is distinctly unusual in viral encephalitis and should suggest bacterial, fungal, tuberculous, parasitic, leptospiral, syphilitic, sarcoid or neoplastic disease instead (p.1112).","Prominent brainstem involvement suggests flaviviruses (West Nile, St. Louis, Japanese encephalitis), HSV, enterovirus A71, rabies or Listeria; deep grey matter (basal ganglia/thalamus) involvement with movement disorders suggests flavivirus infection (p.1114).","Persisting CSF neutrophilia should prompt consideration of bacterial infection, leptospirosis, amebic infection, and non-infectious acute hemorrhagic leukoencephalitis (p.1112).","Empiric aciclovir for suspected HSV should be started immediately on clinical suspicion (especially with focal features) while awaiting diagnostic studies, and is more effective the earlier it is given (p.1115; principle only - no dose)."],"pathophysiology":"Encephalitis is inflammation of the brain parenchyma, usually viral (or from a primary autoimmune process). Neurotropic viruses tend to injure characteristic CNS regions, producing the clinical pattern. Associated meningeal inflammation (meningoencephalitis) is common, and spinal cord or nerve-root involvement may co-occur (encephalomyelitis, encephalomyeloradiculitis). HSV has a predilection for the inferomedial frontotemporal/limbic regions, producing the characteristic temporal-lobe imaging and EEG changes and a hemorrhagic/necrotizing process; punctate microhemorrhages explain CSF red cells in some cases. Flaviviruses such as West Nile and Japanese encephalitis virus preferentially involve deep grey structures (thalamus, basal ganglia, brainstem). VZV tends to produce a CNS vasculopathy with hemorrhagic and ischemic infarction rather than a true parenchymal encephalitis. Some viral encephalitides (notably HSV, and Japanese encephalitis) can trigger a subsequent antibody-mediated autoimmune encephalitis, e.g. anti-NMDA-receptor antibodies after HSV (p.1111-1114, p.746).","additionalDifferentials":["Rabies (encephalitic/furious form with hydrophobia and aerophobia, or paralytic form) - bat exposure is the key risk in the US and a bite history may be lacking","Cytomegalovirus, EBV, HHV-6 encephalitis (notably in immunocompromised / allogeneic stem-cell transplant recipients)","Enterovirus A71 / D68 with acute flaccid myelitis-like presentation","Nipah virus encephalitis (Paramyxoviridae; Southeast Asian outbreaks)","SARS-CoV-2 - true viral CNS encephalitis (rare) versus encephalopathy from systemic/hyperinflammatory illness"],"infectionMimics":["Mycobacteria (tuberculous meningitis/meningoencephalitis)","Fungi","Rickettsiae","Listeria monocytogenes (including rhombencephalitis)","Mycoplasma","Bartonella and other bacteria","Neurosyphilis","Naegleria fowleri primary amebic meningoencephalitis (neutrophilic CSF, low glucose, motile trophozoites in warm fresh CSF; warm freshwater exposure)","Acanthamoeba / Balamuthia mandrillaris granulomatous amebic encephalitis (can mimic acute viral encephalitis; near-100% mortality)","Baylisascaris procyonis (raccoon roundworm) encephalitis - usually children, dirt/raccoon-feces exposure, eosinophilia","Leptospirosis","Lyme disease and cat-scratch disease"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Autoimmune anti-NMDA-receptor encephalitis - young women/children, viral-like prodrome then psychiatric symptoms, insomnia, reduced verbal output, seizures, dyskinesias, autonomic instability and hypoventilation; MRI often normal; can follow HSV encephalitis (NMDAR antibodies in up to 25% of HSV recoveries) and may be initially mistaken for primary psychosis (p.746-747, p.1114)","Other antibody-mediated autoimmune encephalitides - LGI1/CASPR2 (anti-VGKC complex), AMPA receptor, GABA receptor, GAD65 (p.1114)","Paraneoplastic encephalitis with onconeural antibodies (anti-Hu, Yo, Ma2, amphiphysin, CRMP5/CV2)","Hashimoto's encephalopathy (anti-thyroglobulin / anti-thyroperoxidase antibodies)","Prion disease","Acute hemorrhagic leukoencephalitis","Metabolic/toxic encephalopathy (e.g. hyperammonemia, hepatic failure, intoxication)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Diffusion-weighted and FLAIR MRI sequences in addition to standard MRI - increase sensitivity for HSV temporal-lobe lesions (detect ~90% when added)","Repeat CSF HSV PCR if the initial test is obtained within the first ~72 h and clinical suspicion of HSV remains high (early PCR can be falsely negative)","CSF virus-specific IgM / intrathecal antibody synthesis (IgG index) - especially West Nile CSF IgM (diagnostic), and VZV CSF IgM as complementary to PCR; HSV CSF antibody testing useful when illness >1 week and PCR negative","Store frozen CSF (collect ~20 mL, freeze 5-10 mL) for later virus-specific PCR or metagenomic next-generation sequencing, as many neuroinvasive viruses are only transiently present in CSF","Metagenomic next-generation sequencing (mNGS) of CSF/brain for obscure or immunocompromised-host cases","Autoimmune/paraneoplastic antibody panel in serum and CSF (e.g. NMDAR, LGI1, CASPR2, AMPAR, GABA-R, GAD65; onconeural antibodies) when infectious workup is negative","Brain biopsy - reserved for patients with focal MRI abnormality, negative CSF PCR, no autoimmune serology, who deteriorate despite aciclovir and supportive care"],"redFlags":["Focal neurologic findings on examination, neuroimaging, or EEG in suspected encephalitis - strongly suggest HSV and mandate immediate empiric aciclovir while testing proceeds","Severely depressed or declining level of consciousness / coma (Glasgow Coma Scale ~6 at start of therapy predicts death or severe sequelae)","Seizures, particularly status or recurrent seizures","Signs of severely raised intracranial pressure (a relative contraindication to lumbar puncture)","Hypothalamic-pituitary dysfunction (temperature dysregulation, diabetes insipidus, SIADH)","Acute flaccid paralysis accompanying encephalitis (West Nile, EV-A71/D68)"],"prognosis":"Outcome varies widely by etiology and host factors. For HSV encephalitis treated with aciclovir, roughly 81% survive but only about half of survivors have no or minor sequelae; outcome is directly related to patient age and to the level of consciousness at the start of therapy - younger patients with good neurologic function do markedly better, while those comatose (GCS ~6) at treatment initiation die or survive with severe deficits. Eastern equine encephalitis carries very high sequelae rates (~80% of survivors severely impaired), whereas EBV, California (La Crosse) and Venezuelan equine encephalitis rarely cause severe sequelae. West Nile survivors frequently have prolonged cognitive impairment, weakness, tremor, myoclonus and parkinsonism, with recovery taking many months. Japanese encephalitis case-fatality in hospitalized patients is high (~20-30%) with common long-term neurologic disability; rabies is almost invariably fatal. (p.1115-1117, p.1671)","pitfalls":["Do not wait for CSF PCR confirmation before starting aciclovir in suspected HSV encephalitis - early treatment improves outcome, and PCR can be falsely negative in the first ~72 h (start empirically, repeat PCR if needed) (p.1112-1115).","A negative HSV CSF PCR reduces but does not exclude HSV encephalitis; interpret against pretest probability, timing, and prior antiviral use, and consider repeat testing or CSF antibody studies (p.1112-1113).","Worsening neurologic or psychiatric symptoms in the weeks after recovery from HSV encephalitis are often post-HSV anti-NMDA-receptor autoimmune encephalitis (needing immunotherapy), not aciclovir-resistant HSV - test for NMDAR antibodies (p.1114, p.1116).","Detecting NMDAR antibodies does not exclude HSV - they appear in up to 25% of HSV-encephalitis recoveries, so still test and treat for HSV when indicated (p.1114).","Negative CSF viral culture does not exclude HSV or EBV encephalitis (culture is insensitive; >95% of HSV cases have negative CSF culture) (p.1113).","A positive EBV CSF PCR may be non-specific, reflecting reactivation in lymphocytes entering the CNS during an unrelated process - corroborate before attributing encephalitis to EBV (p.1113).","Up to ~10% of PCR-proven HSV encephalitis cases have a normal MRI, and CT is normal in 20-35% - normal imaging does not exclude HSV (p.1113).","Discontinue aciclovir once HSV encephalitis is excluded (with possible exception of severe VZV or EBV encephalitis) (p.1115).","Serum HSV serology alone has no diagnostic value because of high background seroprevalence (p.1114)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.1111-1117","Harrison 22e p.1670-1671","Harrison 22e p.746-747"],"crossLinks":["MENINGITIS","VIRAL_MENINGITIS","seizure_epilepsy"]},"ENTERIC_FEVER":{"name":"Enteric Fever (Typhoid)","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1358-1361 (Chapter 171, Salmonellosis / Enteric (Typhoid) Fever)","clinicalPearls":["Both 'enteric fever' and 'typhoid fever' refer to the same systemic illness caused by dissemination of S. Typhi or S. Paratyphi; the names are used interchangeably (p.1358).","The classic 'hallmarks' of fever plus abdominal pain are actually variable: fever is present at presentation in over three-quarters of cases, but abdominal pain is reported in only about 30-40% (p.1358).","Prolonged fever (roughly 38.8-40.5 C) is the most prominent symptom and can persist up to about 4 weeks if untreated (p.1359).","Mean incubation for S. Typhi is about 10-14 days (range 5-21), shortened by larger ingested inoculum (p.1359).","Relative bradycardia at the peak of high fever is described in under half of patients (p.1359).","Rose spots are faint, salmon-colored, blanching maculopapular lesions on the trunk/chest, appearing in about 30% near the end of the first week and fading in 2-5 days; they are hard to see in highly pigmented skin and Salmonella can be cultured from punch biopsies of them (p.1359-1360).","Headache (~80%) and coated/furred tongue are among the most common symptoms/signs; in a Nepal series S. Typhi and S. Paratyphi A infections were clinically indistinguishable even though Paratyphi A is generally thought milder and more GI-predominant (p.1359).","Diarrhea is somewhat more common than constipation in enteric fever, contrary to older teaching (p.1359).","Up to ~10% of treated patients have a mild relapse, typically 2-3 weeks after fever resolves, with the same strain and susceptibility profile (p.1360).","Chronic asymptomatic carriage (S. Typhi shed in stool or urine for over a year) develops in 2-5% and is more common in women, infants, and those with biliary disease; organisms persist by forming biofilms on gallstones (p.1360)."],"pathophysiology":"After ingestion (infectious dose ~200 to 10^6 CFU; lower gastric acidity or impaired intestinal integrity raises susceptibility), S. Typhi and S. Paratyphi traverse the small-bowel mucosa via M cells overlying Peyer's patches, using a type III secretion system to induce membrane ruffling and bacterium-mediated endocytosis. Crossing the epithelium, they are phagocytosed by macrophages but survive the intracellular environment by remodeling their surface (e.g., via the PhoP/PhoQ regulatory system) and by delivering effector proteins across the phagosome to remodel the Salmonella-containing vacuole for survival and replication. Carried within macrophages, the organisms disseminate via lymphatics and colonize reticuloendothelial tissue (liver, spleen, lymph nodes, bone marrow); the patient is largely asymptomatic during this incubation. Fever and abdominal pain emerge once a critical bacterial load triggers macrophage/epithelial cytokine release recognized by innate immune receptors; hepatosplenomegaly reflects mononuclear-cell recruitment and acquired cell-mediated immunity. Recruitment of mononuclear cells and lymphocytes to Peyer's patches over subsequent weeks causes marked enlargement and necrosis, the basis for later intestinal complications. Many S. Typhi and S. Paratyphi A strains also produce a toxin within host cells that is exported extracellularly and probably contributes to systemic and neuropsychiatric features. This contrasts with the mononuclear (rather than neutrophilic) bowel infiltrate that distinguishes enteric fever from nontyphoidal Salmonella gastroenteritis (p.1358-1359).","additionalDifferentials":["Malaria","Viral hepatitis","Bacterial enteritis","Dengue fever","Rickettsial infection","Leptospirosis","Amebic liver abscess","Acute HIV infection"],"infectionMimics":["Malaria","Dengue fever","Leptospirosis","Rickettsial infection","Viral hepatitis","Acute (primary) HIV infection","Amebic liver abscess","Bacterial enteritis / other Salmonella gastroenteritis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Buffy-coat (centrifuged blood) culture — speeds isolation because most S. Typhi reside in the mononuclear-cell/platelet fraction, though it does not raise overall sensitivity (p.1360).","Bone marrow culture — about 80% sensitive and, unlike blood culture, its yield is not reduced by up to 5 days of prior antibiotics (p.1360).","Culture of intestinal/duodenal secretions (noninvasive duodenal string test) — can be positive even when bone marrow culture is negative; combining blood, marrow and intestinal-secretion cultures gives a yield over 90% (p.1360).","Stool culture — often negative in the first week (negative in 60-70%) but may turn positive by the third week in untreated patients (p.1360).","Culture of rose-spot punch biopsies (p.1360).","Rapid immunodiagnostic serologic tests (e.g., Tubex, Typhidot detecting IgM/IgG to O and H antigens) — point-of-care, low cost, but only ~70-80% sensitive and ~80-90% specific, so insufficient to replace blood culture (p.1360).","Blood PCR for S. Typhi/S. Paratyphi — reported sensitivity ~40-100% depending on gene target (p.1360)."],"redFlags":["Gastrointestinal hemorrhage (~6%) — most often in the third and fourth weeks; life-threatening, needs fluid resuscitation and possible surgery (p.1360).","Intestinal (ileal) perforation (~1%) — from necrosis/ulceration of ileocecal Peyer's patches, typically weeks 3-4; requires urgent surgery and broadened cover for polymicrobial peritonitis (p.1360).","Neuropsychiatric deterioration ('muttering delirium' or 'coma vigil', picking at bedclothes/imaginary objects) (p.1360).","Neurologic complications — meningitis, Guillain-Barre syndrome, neuritis (p.1360).","Shock and obtundation, marking critically ill / severe typhoid (p.1361).","Disseminated intravascular coagulation, hemophagocytic syndrome, myocarditis/pericarditis/endocarditis, severe pneumonia, or hemolytic-uremic syndrome as uncommon severe complications (p.1360)."],"prognosis":"Overall case-fatality is about 2.5%, rising to ~4.5% in hospitalized patients and to 10-30% in untreated disease; prompt appropriate antibiotics prevent severe complications and cut mortality to under 1%. Complications occur in roughly 27% of hospitalized patients and correlate with longer symptom duration before admission, host factors, strain virulence/inoculum, and antibiotic choice. Chronic S. Typhi carriage carries an increased long-term risk of gallbladder cancer, especially in endemic regions like the Indian subcontinent (p.1360-1361).","pitfalls":["No single laboratory test other than a positive culture is diagnostic; the presentation is nonspecific, so a high index of suspicion is needed in any febrile returning traveler from an endemic region (notably the Indian subcontinent, Southeast Asia, or Africa) (p.1358, p.1360).","Blood culture sensitivity is only ~40-60% and falls further with low sample volume, prior antibiotics, or sampling in the first week, because very few organisms (<15/mL) circulate — a negative blood culture does not exclude enteric fever (p.1360).","Rapid serologic/immunodiagnostic tests are not accurate enough to replace blood culture for diagnosis (p.1360).","Leukopenia/neutropenia occur in only ~15-25%; counts can instead be normal or, especially in children or with perforation/secondary infection, show leukocytosis — so the WBC count is unreliable for ruling the diagnosis in or out (p.1360).","The textbook triad is fluid: abdominal pain is present in only ~30-40% and splenomegaly/hepatosplenomegaly are documented in a minority on exam, so their absence does not exclude the diagnosis (p.1358-1359).","Fluoroquinolones should no longer be used empirically in regions with high decreased-ciprofloxacin-susceptibility prevalence (Indian subcontinent, parts of Africa); travel from Pakistan raises concern for XDR/ceftriaxone-resistant strains (p.1361)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not give a formal grading scale; it distinguishes uncomplicated enteric fever (manageable at home with oral therapy) from disease warranting hospitalization (persistent vomiting, diarrhea, or abdominal distension) and from critically ill / severe typhoid characterized by shock and obtundation, plus complicated disease with GI hemorrhage, intestinal perforation, or other end-organ involvement (p.1360-1361).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1358","Harrison 22e p.1359","Harrison 22e p.1360","Harrison 22e p.1361"],"crossLinks":["GASTROENTERITIS","MALARIA","DENGUE","LEPTOSPIROSIS","DYSENTERY"]},"ERYSIPELAS":{"name":"Erysipelas","system":"Skin & Soft Tissue","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1051-1052, 1210-1211","clinicalPearls":["Abrupt onset of fiery-red swelling of the face or extremities, with well-defined indurated margins (notably along the nasolabial fold), rapid progression, and intense pain (Harrison 22e p.1052).","The sharply demarcated, raised border distinguishes erysipelas from cellulitis and reflects involvement limited to the superficial (upper) dermis and superficial lymphatics rather than the deeper subcutaneous tissue (Harrison 22e p.1051, p.1210).","A peau d'orange (orange-peel) texture of the skin is characteristic and is thought to reflect involvement of superficial lymphatics (Harrison 22e p.1210).","Lesion typically develops over a few hours and is accompanied by fever and chills; flaccid superficial blebs/bullae may appear on the second or third day (Harrison 22e p.1052, p.1210).","Classic distribution is the malar area of the face (often extending over the bridge of the nose to the contralateral malar region) or the lower extremities (Harrison 22e p.1210).","Infants and elderly adults are most commonly affected, and the severity of systemic toxicity varies (Harrison 22e p.1052).","Desquamation of the involved skin occurs roughly 5-10 days into the illness (Harrison 22e p.1052).","Classic cases with typical features are almost always due to beta-hemolytic streptococci, usually group A and occasionally group C or G (Harrison 22e p.1210)."],"pathophysiology":"Streptococcus pyogenes infecting the epidermis is translocated laterally to deeper structures via lymphatics, producing the characteristic rapid superficial spread of erysipelas. Subsequent engorgement or obstruction of lymphatics causes flaccid edema of the epidermis. The infection is confined to the superficial dermis and superficial lymphatics (in contrast to cellulitis, which involves deeper subcutaneous tissue), and extension to deeper soft tissues is rare (Harrison 22e p.1051-1052, p.1210).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Cellulitis (deeper dermal/subcutaneous involvement, with less sharply demarcated borders) (Harrison 22e p.1210)","Staphylococcus aureus cellulitis, which can produce a similar appearance, especially when the lesion is atypical, less intensely red, fades into surrounding skin, or arises around a wound or ulcer (Harrison 22e p.1210-1211)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":[],"redFlags":["Edema with purple bullae, ecchymosis, and cutaneous anesthesia suggests loss of vascular integrity and necessitates exploration of deeper structures for necrotizing fasciitis or myonecrosis (Harrison 22e p.1051).","Unexplained fever with disproportionate pain and tenderness in soft tissue, even without acute cutaneous inflammation, should raise suspicion for a deeper necrotizing process (Harrison 22e p.1051)."],"prognosis":"Generally responds well to therapy; with appropriate treatment, fever, pain, and the intense red color diminish, although swelling may continue to progress initially. Recurrence at the same site (sometimes years later) is not uncommon, particularly where normal lymphatic drainage has been disrupted (Harrison 22e p.1052, p.1210-1211).","pitfalls":["Atypical erysipelas may involve an unusual anatomic site, appear less intensely red, fade into surrounding skin, or cause only mild systemic illness, in which case a specific clinical diagnosis cannot reliably be made and broader empirical coverage (including S. aureus) is prudent (Harrison 22e p.1210-1211).","Extension to deeper soft tissues is rare in true erysipelas; if present, reconsider the diagnosis and exclude a deeper or necrotizing infection (Harrison 22e p.1052).","In most parts of the world, acute rheumatic fever is NOT a sequela of streptococcal skin infection (unlike pharyngitis), though post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis may follow either skin or throat infection (Harrison 22e p.1210)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.1051","Harrison 22e p.1052","Harrison 22e p.1210","Harrison 22e p.1211"],"crossLinks":["CELLULITIS","NECROTIZING_FASCIITIS"]},"FEBRILE_NEUTROPENIA":{"name":"Febrile Neutropenia","system":"Systemic / Oncology","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.499-500 (Disorders of Granulocytes and Monocytes), p.617-618 (Principles of Cancer Treatment / myelosuppression), p.186 (ecthyma gangrenosum), p.154 (acute abdominal pain / typhlitis)","clinicalPearls":["Susceptibility to infection rises sharply once the neutrophil count drops below 1000/uL, and worsens markedly at lower thresholds (Harrison 22e p.499).","When the absolute neutrophil count falls below 500/uL, the host's ability to contain its own endogenous flora from sites such as the mouth and gut becomes impaired (Harrison 22e p.499).","Acute neutropenia from cancer chemotherapy carries a substantially higher infection risk than chronic neutropenia of months-to-years duration, which is often better tolerated (Harrison 22e p.499).","After conventional chemotherapy doses, the neutrophil nadir typically occurs about 6-14 days later, defining the window of highest infection risk (Harrison 22e p.617).","Certain agents (nitrosoureas, dacarbazine, temozolomide, procarbazine) can cause delayed marrow toxicity that may not appear until roughly 6 weeks after dosing (Harrison 22e p.617).","Ecthyma gangrenosum is a characteristic skin lesion of Pseudomonas bacteremia that occurs predominantly in neutropenic patients and is reported in up to roughly a quarter of Pseudomonas bacteremia cases (Harrison 22e p.186).","Even highly experienced clinicians may have no advantage over a junior physician in managing the neutropenic patient with fever and hypotension, underscoring the need for protocolized rapid response rather than reliance on intuition (Harrison 22e)."],"pathophysiology":"Most cytotoxic chemotherapeutic agents suppress bone marrow function, and polymorphonuclear neutrophils, with their very short circulating half-life of roughly 6-8 hours, are the cell line most rapidly and severely depleted. As the neutrophil count falls, the host loses the first-line cellular defense that normally contains commensal flora at mucosal surfaces; below an ANC of 500/uL control of endogenous mouth and gut organisms is impaired, and below 200/uL the local inflammatory response is essentially absent, so signs of infection are blunted. The combination of profound neutropenia and chemotherapy-induced mucosal barrier injury allows translocation and rapid dissemination of bacteria from the patient's own gut and oral flora. Neutropenia is most commonly iatrogenic, arising from cytotoxic or immunosuppressive therapy. (Harrison 22e p.499-500, p.617)","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Neutropenic enterocolitis (typhlitis) presenting as abdominal pain and fever in chemotherapy-induced marrow suppression (Harrison 22e p.154)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute graft-versus-host disease, which should be considered when a marrow-suppressed/transplant patient develops abdominal pain and fever (Harrison 22e p.154)","Drug fever and chemotherapy-related fever in the absence of demonstrable infection"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial absolute neutrophil count (band forms plus mature neutrophils) to track depth and duration of neutropenia (Harrison 22e p.499)","Skin examination for ecthyma gangrenosum suggesting Pseudomonas bacteremia (Harrison 22e p.186)","Cross-sectional abdominal imaging when neutropenic enterocolitis/typhlitis is suspected, with serial clinical examination for perforation (Harrison 22e p.154)"],"redFlags":["Hypotension or hemodynamic instability accompanying fever and neutropenia (Harrison 22e)","Ecthyma gangrenosum skin lesions, signaling probable Pseudomonas bacteremia (Harrison 22e p.186)","Abdominal pain and fever suggesting neutropenic enterocolitis (typhlitis) with risk of bowel perforation (Harrison 22e p.154)"],"prognosis":"Risk of serious infection is driven by both the depth and the expected duration of neutropenia; acute chemotherapy-induced neutropenia is more dangerous than chronic neutropenia, and the deepest risk corresponds to the post-chemotherapy nadir at roughly 6-14 days. Pseudomonas bacteremia in this setting is associated with rapidly progressive illness and characteristic ecthyma gangrenosum. (Harrison 22e p.499, p.617, p.186)","pitfalls":["Classic signs of infection and inflammation are blunted or absent in profound neutropenia (local inflammatory response is essentially lost below ANC 200/uL), so the usual physical findings of focal infection may be missing despite serious infection (Harrison 22e p.499).","The infecting organisms are frequently the patient's own endogenous mouth and gut flora rather than exogenous pathogens, so absence of an obvious external source does not exclude bacteremia (Harrison 22e p.499).","A single normal neutrophil count is insufficient; a falling count or failure to mount a neutrophil response to challenge is what defines risk and warrants attention (Harrison 22e p.499)."],"severityClassification":"Febrile neutropenia is defined by fever together with neutropenia (granulocytes below ~1500/uL, with clinically significant risk concentrated at ANC <500/uL and severe impairment of host defense at ANC <200/uL) (Harrison 22e p.499, p.617). Risk stratification for management intensity is performed with validated tools such as the MASCC risk index, which separates low-risk from high-risk febrile neutropenia (referenced in existing curated investigations; specific scoring not detailed in this Harrison excerpt).","references":["Harrison 22e p.499","Harrison 22e p.500","Harrison 22e p.617","Harrison 22e p.618","Harrison 22e p.186","Harrison 22e p.154"],"crossLinks":[]},"GASTROENTERITIS":{"name":"Acute Gastroenteritis","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch 49 (Diarrhea and Constipation, p.302-307); Ch 138 (Acute Infectious Diarrheal Diseases and Bacterial Food Poisoning, p.1076-1082); Ch 209 (Viral Gastroenteritis, p.1627-1630)","clinicalPearls":["More than 90% of acute diarrhea episodes are infectious, and most are mild and self-limited; vomiting, fever, and abdominal pain frequently accompany the illness (p.305).","The first decision point is to separate noninflammatory (watery, small-bowel, enterotoxin/viral) from inflammatory (dysenteric, colonic, invasion/cytotoxin) disease, since this dichotomy drives the need for stool studies and therapy (p.1078-1079).","Clinical timing offers etiologic clues: an abrupt illness with prominent vomiting and little or no fever within hours of eating points to a preformed toxin (e.g., S. aureus, B. cereus), whereas high fever with significant abdominal pain points to invasive or cytotoxin-producing organisms (p.305).","Vomiting can be the predominant or sole presenting feature of viral gastroenteritis, especially in children (p.1629).","Bloody diarrhea with only low-grade fever or no fever should specifically raise concern for Shiga toxin-producing/enterohemorrhagic E. coli (p.1079, p.1081).","Bulky pale/grey stools suggest a malabsorptive small-bowel process, while profuse 'rice-water' stools suggest cholera or a similar toxigenic secretory process (p.1078).","Reduced gastric acidity (achlorhydria, gastric surgery, or acid-suppressing drugs such as PPIs/H2 blockers) lowers the infectious barrier and increases susceptibility to Salmonella, Giardia, and other enteric pathogens (p.1077).","Immunocompromised hosts (cell-mediated defects/AIDS, hypogammaglobulinemia) get more severe, protracted, and sometimes invasive disease, and may harbor opportunistic pathogens not seen in immunocompetent patients (p.1080, p.305).","Tenesmus and frequent small-volume stools indicate a distal colonic/proctitis process (e.g., shigellosis, amebiasis) rather than a small-bowel secretory process (p.1078)."],"pathophysiology":"Acute infectious diarrhea results when an ingested pathogen overwhelms or bypasses host defenses (gastric acid, intestinal motility/peristalsis, mucus layer, secretory IgA, and the protective resident microbiota). Mechanistically there are two broad patterns. Noninflammatory disease arises in the proximal small bowel and is mediated by enterotoxins (e.g., cholera toxin, E. coli heat-labile [LT] and heat-stable [ST] toxins) or by viral injury; cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates a GTP-binding protein to persistently activate adenylate cyclase, raising cyclic AMP, increasing chloride secretion and decreasing sodium absorption, producing watery secretory diarrhea without mucosal destruction. Inflammatory disease affects the colon or distal small bowel through mucosal invasion (Shigella, enteroinvasive E. coli, Salmonella) or cytotoxins (S. dysenteriae type 1, Shiga toxin-producing E. coli, C. difficile), destroying epithelium and producing dysentery with fecal leukocytes. Viral pathogens (norovirus, rotavirus) injure the small-intestinal villous epithelium: rotavirus destroys mature absorptive enterocytes and the loss of brush-border enzymes leaves unmetabolized disaccharides, generating combined secretory and osmotic diarrhea, with the nonstructural protein NSP4 acting as an enterotoxin. Crucially, glucose-coupled sodium absorption in the small intestine remains intact even during toxin-driven secretion, which is the physiologic basis for oral rehydration therapy (p.1076-1077, p.1081, p.1629).","additionalDifferentials":["Enterohemorrhagic E. coli (O157:H7 / Shiga toxin-producing) - watery progressing to bloody, HUS risk","Enteroaggregative E. coli (emerging traveler's-diarrhea pathogen)","Cholera (Vibrio cholerae) - profuse rice-water secretory diarrhea","Preformed-toxin food poisoning (Staphylococcus aureus, Bacillus cereus, Clostridium perfringens)","Sapovirus, astrovirus, enteric adenovirus types 40/41 (viral, mainly young children)","Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora, Giardia (esp. travelers, hikers, immunocompromised, persistent cases)"],"infectionMimics":["Enteric fever / typhoid (penetrating pathogens Salmonella Typhi, Yersinia enterocolitica with fever, relative bradycardia, splenomegaly)","Yersinia enterocolitica terminal ileitis mimicking acute appendicitis","Clostridioides difficile colitis (recent or current antibiotics; predominant nosocomial cause)","Acute viral hepatitis A and other systemic infections (legionellosis, listeriosis, toxic shock, malaria) in which diarrhea is one feature","SARS-CoV-2 - diarrhea can be an early symptom"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Medication side effects (antibiotics, NSAIDs, antidysrhythmics, antihypertensives, some antidepressants, chemotherapy, laxatives) - the most common noninfectious cause","Ischemic colitis (age >50, lower abdominal pain preceding watery then bloody diarrhea, spares rectum)","Colonic diverticulitis; partial bowel obstruction; graft-versus-host disease","Initial presentation or flare of inflammatory bowel disease (Crohn's, ulcerative colitis) with abrupt onset mimicking infection","Toxic ingestions (organophosphates, amanita/other mushrooms, arsenic) and seafood toxins (ciguatera, scombroid)","Food allergy including alpha-gal (delayed mammalian-meat allergy) which can cause systemic collapse and be mistaken for dehydration"],"additionalInvestigations":["Fecal leukocyte smear (methylene blue) or, more sensitively, fecal lactoferrin to distinguish inflammatory from noninflammatory disease","Culture-independent multiplex nucleic-acid amplification (PCR) stool panels - now first-line for severe/domestically acquired disease; faster and more sensitive than culture","Stool culture for Salmonella, Shigella, and Campylobacter in febrile inflammatory diarrhea acquired outside hospital","Sorbitol-MacConkey plating / Shiga-toxin or PCR testing when EHEC (E. coli O157:H7) is suspected (visibly bloody stool)","Specific antigen/microscopy for Giardia and Cryptosporidium, and antigen detection for E. histolytica, when suspected or in persistent diarrhea","Examination for ova and parasites if diarrhea persists beyond ~10 days","Nosocomial diarrhea workup should focus on C. difficile toxin testing; routine stool culture in the hospital is low-yield","Endoscopy/imaging reserved for persistent (>30 day) or suspected noninfectious disease; not indicated during the acute episode (risk of false-positive results)"],"redFlags":["Signs of severe dehydration: lethargy/obtundation, feeble pulse, hypotension, shock","Profuse diarrhea with dehydration","Overtly bloody stools","High fever (>=38.5 C / 101 F)","Diarrhea persisting >48 h without improvement (or >2 weeks suggesting a different chronic spectrum)","Severe abdominal pain in a patient aged >50 years","Elderly (>=70 years) or immunocompromised host","Recent antibiotic use (C. difficile concern)","Bloody diarrhea with low-grade or absent fever (EHEC / HUS warning)"],"prognosis":"Most acute infectious gastroenteritis is self-limited and resolves within days; viral norovirus/sapovirus illness typically lasts 1-3 days and rotavirus 3-7 days. Death is uncommon in healthy adults but occurs chiefly from dehydration in infants and debilitated elderly; globally acute diarrhea remains a leading cause of childhood death. Oral rehydration has dramatically reduced mortality (e.g., cholera case fatality from >50% untreated to <1%). Recognized post-infectious sequelae include reactive arthritis (after invasive Shigella/Salmonella/Campylobacter/Yersinia), Guillain-Barre syndrome (after Campylobacter), hemolytic-uremic syndrome (after Shiga toxin-producing bacteria), post-infectious IBS (notably after Campylobacter), and transient post-infectious lactase deficiency; recurrent childhood diarrhea in resource-poor settings causes environmental enteropathy with growth and developmental stunting (p.302, p.305, p.1077-1078, p.1081, p.1628-1629).","pitfalls":["Avoid antimotility agents (e.g., loperamide) and bismuth-based slowing in febrile or bloody/dysenteric diarrhea - they may prolong fever and pathogen shedding in Shigella and other invasive infections; in viral gastroenteritis antibiotics and antimotility agents are not indicated.","Do NOT give antibiotics when enterohemorrhagic (Shiga toxin-producing) E. coli is suspected, because antibiotics can increase toxin production and markedly raise the risk of hemolytic-uremic syndrome.","Opioid/antimotility therapy can increase the frequency of bacteremia in Salmonella gastroenteritis and prolonged illness in Shigella.","Most acute diarrhea needs no microbiologic workup; routine extensive stool testing of mild self-limited disease wastes resources.","Do not screen for or endoscope to exclude celiac/Crohn disease or IBD during an acute episode - the risk is low and false-positive results are common.","A positive multiplex PCR panel may detect multiple organisms or carriage and can be hard to interpret; lack of an isolate prevents susceptibility testing and outbreak typing, so culture confirmation is still advised after a positive bacterial PCR.","Empirical antibacterial coverage is warranted regardless of pathogen recovery in selected high-risk patients (immunocompromised, prosthetic/mechanical heart valves, recent vascular grafts, elderly)."],"severityClassification":"Severity is graded clinically by functional impact and dehydration rather than by a single score. Mild: activity unrestricted. Moderate: activities altered. Severe: incapacitated. Dehydration assessment is the key severity measure on examination - mild = thirst, dry mouth, reduced axillary sweat, decreased urine output, slight weight loss; moderate = orthostatic blood-pressure fall, skin tenting, sunken eyes (or sunken fontanelle in infants); severe = lethargy/obtundation, feeble pulse, hypotension, and shock. Frequency of stools forewarns impending dehydration. By duration, diarrhea is acute (<2 weeks), persistent (2-4 weeks), or chronic (>4 weeks) (p.302/304, p.307, p.1078).","references":["Harrison 22e p.302","Harrison 22e p.304","Harrison 22e p.305","Harrison 22e p.306","Harrison 22e p.307","Harrison 22e p.1076","Harrison 22e p.1077","Harrison 22e p.1078","Harrison 22e p.1079","Harrison 22e p.1080","Harrison 22e p.1081","Harrison 22e p.1627","Harrison 22e p.1628","Harrison 22e p.1629"],"crossLinks":["DYSENTERY","C_DIFF","ENTERIC_FEVER"]},"HAP":{"name":"Hospital Acquired Pneumonia","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.131 (Pneumonia), pp.1066, 1072-1076","clinicalPearls":["VAP is defined as pneumonia with onset 48 hours or more after the start of mechanical ventilation; research on HAP has centred on VAP, but the same principles extend to ventilated HAP and to non-ICU HAP (p.1072).","About 70% of HAP cases are acquired outside the ICU and 30% within it; roughly 35% of all HAP patients require mechanical ventilation, which makes ventilated HAP a distinct entity whose bacteriology and outcome resemble those of VAP (p.1072).","In nonintubated HAP an expectorated sputum sample is used for microbiologic diagnosis but is confounded by frequent colonisation with oral pathogens; deep lower respiratory tract samples in VAP/ventilated HAP are more reliable yet may still contain colonising organisms (p.1072).","The non-MDR 'core pathogens' of VAP closely mirror those of severe CAP and predominate when pneumonia develops in the first 5-7 days of admission; with risk factors (especially prior antibiotics) MDR pathogens become a concern even early (p.1072).","Most hospitals contend with P. aeruginosa and MRSA, but the specific MDR organisms are institution-specific and can differ even between critical-care units in the same hospital (p.1072).","Prior antibiotic exposure is by far the dominant risk factor for colonisation with pathogenic organisms; P. aeruginosa almost never infects patients with no prior antibiotic exposure (pp.1073, 1076).","Only about one-third of colonised intubated patients go on to develop VAP, indicating that the final step is overwhelming of host defences by a large bacterial inoculum rather than colonisation alone (p.1073).","Severely ill septic or trauma patients appear to enter a state of immunoparalysis several days after ICU admission, coinciding with the period of greatest VAP risk; hyperglycaemia and frequent transfusions adversely affect immune response (p.1073).","Serial changes in oxygenation may detect HAP/VAP earlier than other findings and can also serve to monitor response to therapy (p.1073).","Absence of bacteria on a Gram stain of an endotracheal aspirate makes pneumonia an unlikely cause of the fever or infiltrate; presence of an MDR pathogen can be excluded as a target if tracheal aspirate cultures (or sensitive molecular tests) are negative, enabling de-escalation (p.1074).","Anaerobes may be somewhat more common in nonintubated HAP than in VAP because of greater macroaspiration risk and lower lower-airway oxygen tension, but they usually contribute only to polymicrobial pneumonia and rarely need specific targeted therapy (p.1076).","A bronchopneumonia pattern is most common in nosocomial pneumonias, in contrast to the lobar pattern more typical of bacterial CAP; in VAP, respiratory bronchiolitis may precede a radiologically apparent infiltrate (p.1066).","The 'healthcare-associated pneumonia' category has been abandoned because it did not reliably predict resistant-organism infection and drove excessive broad-spectrum antibiotic use; resistance risk should instead be judged individually from comorbidities, recent hospitalisation, and recent antibiotic therapy (p.1066)."],"pathophysiology":"Three factors are central to VAP pathogenesis: colonisation of the oropharynx with pathogenic organisms, aspiration of those organisms into the lower respiratory tract, and compromise of host defences (p.1073). The endotracheal tube is the single most important risk factor: it bypasses normal anti-aspiration mechanisms, and although it blocks large-volume aspiration, microaspiration is worsened by secretions pooling above the cuff. The tube and suctioning damage tracheal mucosa and facilitate colonisation, and pathogenic bacteria form a protective glycocalyx biofilm on the tube surface that shields them from antibiotics and host defences; biofilm fragments can embolise to distal airways or be dislodged during suctioning to reinoculate the trachea (p.1073). Normal oropharyngeal flora is frequently replaced by pathogenic organisms under antibiotic selection pressure, cross-infection, severe systemic illness, and malnutrition (p.1073). Almost all intubated patients have transient colonisation, but progression to pneumonia reflects a large bacterial inoculum overwhelming host defences amid post-admission immunoparalysis (p.1073). More broadly, pneumonia is now viewed as an emergent disturbance of the lung microbiota driven by self-accelerating inflammatory feedback rather than simple invasion of a sterile space (p.1066).","additionalDifferentials":["Purulent tracheobronchitis (notably in chronic ventilator units, where it can impede weaning) (p.1073)"],"infectionMimics":["Antibiotic-associated diarrhoea (as an alternative source of fever/leukocytosis) (p.1074)","Central line-associated infection (p.1074)","Sinusitis (p.1074)","Urinary tract infection (p.1074)","Fungal or viral pneumonia (mimics requiring different antimicrobials than VAP) (p.1074)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Atypical pulmonary oedema (p.1074)","Pulmonary contusion (p.1074)","Alveolar haemorrhage (p.1074)","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (p.1074)","Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) (p.1074)","Pulmonary infarction / pulmonary embolism (p.1074)","Pancreatitis (as a non-infective source of fever/leukocytosis) (p.1074)","Drug fever (p.1074)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantitative culture of deep respiratory tract samples to separate colonisation from true infection; the more distal the sample, the more specific the result and the lower the colony-count threshold (e.g., endotracheal aspirate threshold ~10^6 cfu/mL vs protected specimen brush ~10^3 cfu/mL), at the cost of falling sensitivity for blindly collected distal samples (p.1074)","Gram stain of endotracheal aspirate (absence of bacteria argues against pneumonia) (p.1074)","Differential cell counts and staining for intracellular organisms on respiratory samples (p.1074)","Detection of local protein levels elevated in response to infection (p.1074)","Serial / repeat quantitative cultures to track microbiologic response and clarify treatment failure (p.1075)","Procalcitonin to help guide duration of therapy in conjunction with the initial clinical response (already curated; Harrison adds the duration-of-therapy context, p.1075)"],"redFlags":["New or worsening hypoxaemia / serial decline in oxygenation in a ventilated patient (may herald pneumonia before other signs) (p.1073)","Septic shock accompanying suspected VAP (influences empirical regimen breadth) (p.1075)","Failure to improve clinically by 48-72 h of appropriate therapy (improvement, when it occurs, is usually evident in this window) (p.1076)","Necrotising pneumonia (e.g., P. aeruginosa or S. aureus) causing significant pulmonary haemorrhage or empyema (p.1075)"],"prognosis":"Crude VAP mortality is reported as high as 50-70%, but the key measure is attributable mortality, since many VAP patients have underlying disease that would itself be fatal. Attributable mortality exceeded 25% in one matched-cohort study, whereas more recent estimates are much lower (5-10%); VAP complicating COVID-19 carries higher attributable mortality than other VAP. VAP in trauma patients shows no attributable mortality, likely because many were previously healthy. MDR pathogens carry significantly greater attributable mortality than non-MDR organisms, and some pathogens (e.g., S. maltophilia) chiefly mark a severely immunocompromised, high-risk host. Nonintubated non-ICU HAP has lower mortality and lower risk of antibiotic failure than VAP and ventilated HAP because of better host defences (pp.1076).","pitfalls":["Applying CAP-type clinical criteria to ventilated patients consistently overdiagnoses VAP, owing to frequent tracheal colonisation, multiple alternative causes of radiographic infiltrates, and many other sources of fever in the critically ill (pp.1073-1074).","No single set of criteria reliably diagnoses pneumonia in a ventilated patient; over-reliance on portable chest radiographs is limited by frequent pre-existing infiltrates and poor portable film quality (p.1073).","Effective antibiotic given in the 24-48 h before sampling can drive quantitative cultures below the diagnostic threshold and yield false-negative results; conversely, counts above threshold during therapy suggest the current antibiotics are ineffective (p.1074).","Sampling too early in the course, or after an effective host response has already reduced bacterial counts, can also give sub-threshold results; ideally sample as soon as pneumonia is suspected and before starting or changing antibiotics (p.1074).","Chest radiographs often worsen initially during treatment, making them less reliable than clinical criteria as indicators of response (p.1076).","Recurrent VAP with the same organism can occur because the endotracheal tube biofilm allows persistence and reintroduction; about half of recurrent Pseudomonas cases are actually due to a new strain (p.1075)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames empirical management around two risk axes rather than a discrete severity score: (1) presence or absence of risk factors for MDR pathogens (prior antibiotic therapy, prior hospitalisation, local antibiogram; for MRSA additionally known MRSA colonisation, chronic haemodialysis, or local MRSA pneumonia rate >10% or unknown), and (2) predicted risk of death, with a mortality risk of 15% or less considered low risk. The frequency of VAP also varies with duration of ventilation, with the highest hazard in the first 5 days, a plateau (~1%/day) after about 2 weeks, and a cumulative rate up to ~70% by 30 days of ventilation (pp.1073, 1075).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1066","Harrison 22e p.1072","Harrison 22e p.1073","Harrison 22e p.1074","Harrison 22e p.1075","Harrison 22e p.1076"],"crossLinks":["VAP","CAP","ASPIRATION_PNEUMONIA"]},"IE":{"name":"Infective Endocarditis","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1036-1048 (Chapter 133, Infective Endocarditis)","clinicalPearls":["IE spans a clinical continuum from acute to subacute, and the causative organism largely dictates the tempo: beta-hemolytic streptococci, S. aureus and pneumococci tend to produce an acute, rapidly destructive course, whereas viridans streptococci, enterococci, CoNS and HACEK organisms usually cause subacute disease (Bartonella, T. whipplei, C. burnetii and M. chimaera are exceptionally indolent).","Fever is present in 80-90% of cases and a heart murmur in 80-85%, but in subacute IE fever is typically low-grade (rarely above 39.4 C) and may be blunted altogether in elderly, severely debilitated, or renal-failure patients.","A new or worsening regurgitant murmur is heard in only 20-50% of cases; in acute IE on a previously normal valve a murmur may be absent at first but is ultimately detected in about 85%.","Classic peripheral stigmata (Janeway lesions, Osler nodes, Roth spots, subungual/splinter hemorrhages) appear in only about 2-15% of cases and have become infrequent with earlier diagnosis; their absence does not exclude IE.","Janeway lesions are nonsuppurative painless hemorrhagic macules of the palms/soles classically linked to prolonged subacute infection, whereas septic emboli (subungual hemorrhages, Osler nodes) mimicking these lesions are common in acute S. aureus IE.","Three independent predictors of embolization are S. aureus etiology, mobile vegetations larger than 10 mm, and involvement of the anterior mitral leaflet.","In people who inject drugs, 35-60% of IE is confined to the tricuspid valve and presents with fever but faint or absent murmur and no peripheral stigmata, often with septic pulmonary emboli (cough, pleuritic pain, nodular infiltrates).","S. aureus bacteremia carries an 8-14% prevalence of IE and high mortality, so echocardiography (high-quality TTE, preferably TEE) is recommended routinely; intracardiac device, emboli, 2 or more positive cultures, community acquisition, vertebral osteomyelitis or persistent bacteremia each raise IE risk and argue for early TEE.","About 12-17% of patients with E. faecalis bacteremia and about 7% with non-beta-hemolytic streptococcal bacteremia have IE, justifying echocardiographic screening when predictive features are present.","Up to half of arterial emboli precede the diagnosis of IE; embolic risk falls rapidly once effective antibiotics begin, so emboli occurring late during or after therapy do not by themselves indicate treatment failure.","In hemodynamically stable patients with suspected subacute IE, empirical antibiotics can reasonably be withheld initially to allow uncontaminated blood cultures, but patients with sepsis or deteriorating hemodynamics should be treated immediately after the first three culture sets are drawn.","Surgery is indicated (as a knowledge concept, distinct from antibiotic regimens) for optimal outcome when there is moderate-severe CHF/shock from valve dysfunction, paravalvular extension with abscess/fistula/heart block, persistent bacteremia without an extracardiac source despite 7-10 days of therapy, lack of effective antimicrobial therapy (fungal, Brucella, multidrug-resistant gram-negative), or an unstable partially dehisced prosthetic valve; it is strongly considered for large (>10 mm) hypermobile vegetations with prior embolus and valve dysfunction, very large (>30 mm) vegetations, S. aureus PVE with intracardiac complications, and relapse after optimal therapy. Removal of all hardware is recommended for established CIED-IE."],"pathophysiology":"The prototypic lesion is the vegetation - a mass of platelets, fibrin, microorganisms and scant inflammatory cells - usually on heart valves but also on the low-pressure side of a ventricular septal defect, on mural endocardium injured by aberrant blood jets or foreign bodies, or on intracardiac devices. Intact endothelium resists most bacteria; endothelial injury (e.g., at sites of high-velocity jet impact or on the low-pressure side of a structural lesion) permits direct infection by virulent organisms or formation of a sterile platelet-fibrin thrombus (nonbacterial thrombotic endocarditis, NBTE) that becomes a nidus for bacterial seeding during transient bacteremia. NBTE is most often associated with mitral regurgitation, aortic stenosis, aortic regurgitation, ventricular septal defects and complex congenital heart disease, and also arises from hypercoagulable states (marantic endocarditis of malignancy/chronic disease and the bland vegetations of SLE and antiphospholipid syndrome). Circulating organisms adhere via surface adhesins (MSCRAMMs) - fibronectin-binding proteins, S. aureus clumping factor, enterococcal Fss2/Ace/Ebp pili, streptococcal glucans/FimA; S. aureus can even invade intact endothelium via fibronectin-binding proteins and von Willebrand factor, allowing infection of previously normal valves. Adherent organisms proliferate into microcolonies, induce platelet deposition and a local procoagulant (tissue-factor) state, and become buried in fibrin; deep organisms are metabolically inactive and relatively antibiotic-resistant while surface organisms are continuously shed into the blood. Clinical manifestations arise from intracardiac structural damage, embolization of vegetation fragments causing remote infarction/infection, hematogenous seeding during bacteremia, and immune-complex deposition; constitutional symptoms reflect cytokine release.","additionalDifferentials":["Marantic (nonbacterial thrombotic) endocarditis associated with malignancy and chronic wasting disease","Atrial myxoma","Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome with bland valvular vegetations","Systemic lupus erythematosus with sterile (Libman-Sacks-type) vegetations"],"infectionMimics":["Culture-negative IE due to fastidious organisms (Coxiella burnetii, Bartonella spp., Tropheryma whipplei, Brucella, nutritionally variant Granulicatella/Gemella/Abiotrophia, Corynebacterium/Cutibacterium acnes, Mycobacterium chimaera)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Marantic endocarditis of malignancy","Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome","Atrial myxoma","SLE-associated sterile endocarditis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Transesophageal echocardiography (TEE) detects vegetations in over 90% of definite IE and is preferred over TTE, which misses vegetations under 2 mm and yields inadequate images in about 20% of patients; a negative TEE with high clinical suspicion should be repeated in 7-10 days.","Three-dimensional TEE to better define vegetations and perivalvular extension.","ECG-gated multislice cardiac CT angiography - superior to TEE for pseudoaneurysm/abscess and can assess coronary patency preoperatively; CTA findings count as a major imaging criterion.","18F-FDG-PET/CT - increases sensitivity in suspected prosthetic-valve IE (including TAVR-PVE), aortic graft, CIED pocket/lead and LVAD infection, and extracardiac foci; false positives within 3 months of valve surgery.","Molecular testing of excised/embolized vegetation: organism-specific PCR (C. burnetii, Bartonella, T. whipplei, C. acnes, M. hominis) and broad-range 16S (or 28S for fungi) rRNA PCR with sequencing; only moderately sensitive, so a negative result does not exclude IE.","Serologic/PCR testing for culture-negative pathogens: Coxiella burnetii (phase I IgG titer >=1:800) and Bartonella henselae/quintana (IgG titer >=1:800).","Brain MRI/MRA in any neurologic signs or symptoms (including unusual headache) to detect emboli, hemorrhage, or mycotic aneurysms.","Spine MRI for recalcitrant back pain or focal spinal tenderness to assess for vertebral osteomyelitis/spondylodiscitis and epidural abscess.","Routine adjunctive labs: CBC (anemia in 70-90%), ESR (elevated in 60-90%), CRP (elevated in over 90%), microscopic hematuria (30-50%), rheumatoid factor (~50%), creatinine and liver function tests."],"redFlags":["Congestive heart failure or pulmonary edema/cardiogenic shock from valve dysfunction or intracardiac fistula (develops in 30-40%) - a surgical emergency","New electrocardiographic conduction disturbance or new heart block, suggesting paravalvular/septal abscess extension","Cerebrovascular embolism (stroke or encephalopathy), which complicates 15-35% of cases","Intracranial hemorrhage from hemorrhagic infarct or ruptured mycotic aneurysm","Persistent fever or persistently positive blood cultures despite appropriate antibiotics, indicating uncontrolled infection","New or worsening regurgitant murmur or prosthetic valve dehiscence","Large (over 10 mm) mobile vegetation, particularly on the anterior mitral leaflet, with high embolic risk","Acute S. aureus or fungal IE, and any prosthetic-valve involvement"],"prognosis":"Overall survival in developed countries is about 80-85%, but varies markedly by subgroup. About 85-90% survive NVE caused by viridans streptococci, HACEK organisms, or synergy-susceptible enterococci. Non-injecting patients with S. aureus NVE have 55-70% survival; injecting patients have 85-90%, but 1-year mortality rises to 20-30% if substance-use disorder is not addressed. Prosthetic-valve IE arising within 2 months of surgery carries 40-50% mortality versus only 10-20% for late-onset PVE; elderly TAVR-PVE in-hospital mortality is 35-50%, rising to 60-75% at 1 year. Crude survival after successful treatment is roughly 80-90% at 1 year and 70-80% at 2 years. Death often reflects comorbidities and end-organ complications rather than antibiotic failure. Adverse outcomes are linked to older age, severe comorbidity/diabetes, delayed diagnosis, prosthetic or aortic valve involvement, virulent or resistant pathogens (S. aureus, P. aeruginosa, yeast), intracardiac and major neurologic complications, and health care-associated infection.","pitfalls":["Relying on a new/worsening murmur alone - it is a physical-exam minor criterion only; a major imaging criterion requires echocardiographic evidence of new valvular regurgitation, not auscultation.","Treating a negative TTE (or even a negative initial TEE) as ruling out IE; TTE misses vegetations under 2 mm and is poor for prosthetic valves, and initial TEE is falsely negative in 6-18% (especially TAVR-PVE) - repeat in 7-10 days when suspicion persists.","Forgetting that 5-15% of IE is blood-culture negative, often because of prior antibiotics; withholding empirical therapy when stable preserves diagnostic yield.","Interpreting persistent organism DNA detected in excised valves (in ~45% of patients who completed therapy) as antibiotic failure - relapse after surgery for active IE is actually uncommon.","Misattributing emboli that occur late during or after effective therapy to treatment failure.","Overlooking that prior antibiotics within 2 weeks blunt culture yield and that nontypical organisms require 3 or more positive sets (plus no extracardiac focus) to count as a major criterion.","Assuming antithrombotic therapy prevents systemic emboli in NVE - it does not."],"severityClassification":"Diagnosis uses the 2023 Duke-International Society for Cardiovascular Infectious Diseases (Duke-ISCVID) update of the modified Duke criteria (sensitivity 80-93%). MAJOR criteria: (1) Microbiologic - a typical IE organism in 2 or more separate blood culture sets (S. aureus, S. lugdunensis, E. faecalis, all streptococci except S. pneumoniae and S. pyogenes, Granulicatella/Abiotrophia/Gemella, HACEK group; and, when intracardiac prosthetic material is present, CoNS, C. striatum, C. jeikeium, S. marcescens, P. aeruginosa, C. acnes, nontuberculous mycobacteria and Candida), OR a nontypical organism in 3 or more sets; positive PCR/single culture/serology for C. burnetii, Bartonella spp. or T. whipplei. (2) Imaging - echocardiography or cardiac CT showing vegetation, perforation/leaflet aneurysm, abscess, pseudoaneurysm or fistula, OR new valvular regurgitation, OR new prosthetic dehiscence; OR abnormal FDG-PET/CT activity on a valve/graft/lead/prosthetic material. (3) Surgical - IE seen at direct surgical inspection. MINOR criteria: predisposing condition (prior IE, injection drug use, prosthetic valve, prior valve repair, congenital heart disease such as bicuspid aortic valve, CIED, more-than-mild regurgitation/stenosis, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy); fever over 38.0 C; vascular phenomena (arterial emboli, septic pulmonary infarcts, mycotic aneurysm, intracranial/conjunctival hemorrhage, Janeway lesions); immunologic phenomena (glomerulonephritis, Osler nodes, Roth spots, positive rheumatoid factor); microbiologic evidence not meeting a major criterion; and PET/CT activity within 3 months of prosthetic implantation. CLASSIFICATION: DEFINITE IE = pathologic confirmation OR 2 major, or 1 major + 3 minor, or 5 minor criteria. POSSIBLE IE = 1 major + 1 minor, or 3 minor. REJECTED = firm alternative diagnosis, or resolution with under 4 days of antibiotics, or no histologic evidence at surgery/autopsy after under 4 days of therapy. IE is also classified by tempo (acute vs subacute), site (native vs prosthetic valve, right-sided/tricuspid), cause, and predisposition (injection drug use, health care-associated).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1036","Harrison 22e p.1037","Harrison 22e p.1038","Harrison 22e p.1039","Harrison 22e p.1040","Harrison 22e p.1041","Harrison 22e p.1046","Harrison 22e p.1047","Harrison 22e p.1048"],"crossLinks":["DEVICE_INFECTION","SEPSIS","ischemic_stroke","glomerulonephritis","MENINGITIS"]},"LEPTOSPIROSIS":{"name":"Leptospirosis","system":"Tropical Fever","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1439-1444","clinicalPearls":["Classically biphasic: an acute leptospiremic phase (fever lasting ~3-10 days, organism culturable/PCR-detectable in blood) followed by an immune phase in which symptoms may resolve as antibodies appear and leptospires can be recovered from urine; the two phases are not always distinct, mild cases may lack the second phase, and severe disease can be monophasic and fulminant (p.1442).","Incubation is usually 1-2 weeks but ranges from 2 to 30 days (p.1442).","Conjunctival suffusion (redness without exudate) is a characteristic but non-pathognomonic sign; muscle pain is intense, especially in the calves, back, and abdomen (p.1442).","Headache is intense and frontal or retro-orbital, sometimes with photophobia, resembling dengue (p.1442).","Weil's syndrome denotes the severe triad of jaundice, acute kidney injury, and hemorrhage (p.1442-1443).","Severe pulmonary hemorrhage (with or without jaundice) is increasingly recognized as an important and widespread presentation; hemoptysis may not become apparent until the patient is intubated (p.1439, 1442).","Jaundice occurs in only ~5-10% of patients, can be deep with an orange skin cast, and is usually NOT associated with fulminant hepatic necrosis (p.1442).","Aseptic meningitis is more common in children than adults; altered mental status in severe disease may reflect leptospiral meningitis, which can occur in anicteric patients lacking other hallmarks (p.1442-1443).","Autoimmune-associated uveitis is a recognized, potentially chronic late sequela (p.1443).","Idea that specific serogroups cause distinct clinical syndromes has been refuted, although some serovars tend to cause more severe disease (p.1442).","Serology lacks sensitivity in the early acute phase (up to day 5) and cannot guide a timely treatment decision; PCR (notably real-time PCR) can confirm diagnosis during the first 5 days (p.1443).","MAT antibodies generally do not reach detectable levels until the second week of illness, and the antibody response may be blunted by early antibiotics (p.1443)."],"pathophysiology":"Leptospires enter through cuts, abraded skin, or mucous membranes (especially conjunctival and oral). The highly motile organisms proliferate, cross tissue barriers, and disseminate hematogenously to all organs (leptospiremic phase), evading innate immune mechanisms such as complement-mediated killing and phagocytosis; an exaggerated proinflammatory response correlates with mortality. With the immune phase, antibodies appear and leptospires clear from blood but persist in liver, lung, kidney, heart, and brain. Renal pathology shows acute tubular damage and interstitial nephritis, progressing to interstitial edema and acute tubular necrosis, with dysregulated nephron transporters causing impaired sodium absorption, potassium wasting, and polyuria. Liver histology shows focal (not widespread) hepatocellular necrosis, inflammation, bile canaliculus plugging, and hepatocyte apoptosis, with disruption of intercellular junctions and bile leakage. Petechiae and hemorrhages occur across many organs. Hemorrhage is associated with thrombocytopenia (likely platelet consumption); a consumptive coagulopathy with elevated coagulation-activation markers, reduced anticoagulant markers, and dysregulated fibrinolysis may progress to overt DIC. Endothelial cell activation (raised soluble E-selectin and von Willebrand factor) correlates with severity, and leptospires/leptospiral proteins disrupt endothelial barrier function, increasing permeability. The LPS has unusually low endotoxic potency, yet serovar-specific anti-LPS antibodies are central to protective humoral immunity (p.1441-1442).","additionalDifferentials":["Influenza and SARS-CoV-2 / other respiratory viral infections (when fever, headache, myalgia predominate)","Chikungunya","Ehrlichiosis / rickettsial diseases","Acute HIV infection","Hantavirus infection (hemorrhagic fever with renal syndrome or hantavirus cardiopulmonary syndrome)","Yellow fever and other viral hemorrhagic fevers (Rift Valley fever, filovirus infections, Lassa fever) when bleeding is present","Acute schistosomiasis"],"infectionMimics":["Dengue (and dengue hemorrhagic fever when bleeding present)","Malaria","Typhoid fever","Scrub typhus (rash may be misattributed)","Viral hepatitis","Acute HIV infection","Hantavirus infection","Yellow fever / viral hemorrhagic fevers"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["PCR (notably real-time PCR) of blood/CSF/urine — confirms diagnosis with high accuracy during the first 5 days of illness, before serology becomes positive (p.1443)","Microscopic agglutination test (MAT) using live leptospiral strains — paired acute/convalescent sera seeking a fourfold rise; single high titer (1:200-1:800 depending on endemicity) supportive (p.1443)","IgM ELISA and commercial rapid lateral-flow / latex agglutination tests (useful where MAT/culture unavailable) (p.1443)","Blood/urine/CSF culture (special media; may take weeks to months) (p.1441, 1443)","C-reactive protein, procalcitonin, ESR (raised inflammatory markers) (p.1443)","Coagulation profile / DIC markers (D-dimer, antithrombin, protein C) in severe disease (p.1443)","Serum amylase (often elevated even without overt pancreatitis) (p.1443)","Serum electrolytes including magnesium (urinary magnesium loss is characteristic; hypokalemia, hyponatremia) (p.1443)","Urinalysis (leukocytes, erythrocytes, hyaline/granular casts, mild proteinuria) (p.1443)","Bilirubin, aminotransferases, alkaline phosphatase (bilirubin may be high; transaminase rises typically only moderate) (p.1443)","Chest radiograph / CT (patchy bilateral lower-lobe alveolar pattern from alveolar hemorrhage; pleura-based densities; ground-glass ARDS pattern) (p.1443)","ECG (nonspecific ST/T changes; repolarization abnormalities and arrhythmias are poor prognostic signs) (p.1443)","CSF examination when meningitis suspected (pleocytosis from a few to >1000 cells/uL, lymphocytic but may be PMN-predominant early; protein normal or slightly raised, glucose normal) (p.1443)","Co-testing for rickettsiae, dengue, and hantavirus given overlap and reported dual infections (p.1443)"],"redFlags":["Age >40 years","Altered mental status","Acute kidney injury / oliguria or anuria","Respiratory insufficiency, hemoptysis, or pulmonary hemorrhage","Hypotension / septic shock","Cardiac arrhythmias and repolarization abnormalities on ECG","Jaundice with bleeding (Weil's syndrome triad)","Thrombocytopenia (platelet count <=100 x10^9/L) — linked to bleeding and renal failure","Evidence of coagulation activation / DIC","Pregnancy (high fetal mortality)"],"prognosis":"Most patients recover, with renal and hepatic function typically returning to normal in survivors (hemodialysis can be lifesaving). Overall mean case-fatality is near 10% for severe cases; severe leptospirosis carries a case-fatality of 1-50%. Mortality is highest in the elderly and those with pulmonary hemorrhage or Weil's syndrome. Untreated leptospiral meningitis has reported mortality of ~13% versus ~2% with antibiotics. Pregnancy is associated with high fetal mortality. Post-leptospirosis sequelae include prolonged fatigue, myalgia, malaise, headache, depression-like symptoms persisting for years, and chronic autoimmune uveitis (p.1443-1444).","pitfalls":["Absence of a travel or freshwater-exposure history does not exclude the diagnosis (p.1443).","Absence of renal involvement does not rule out leptospirosis (p.1443).","Relying on serology in the first ~5 days risks a false-negative; antibodies are often undetectable until the second week and early antibiotics blunt the response (p.1443).","Cross-reactions are frequent in MAT, so the infecting serovar/serogroup cannot be definitively identified without isolating the organism (p.1443).","Leptospiral meningitis may present without jaundice or other classic severe-disease hallmarks, making it easy to miss (p.1443).","Jaundice can be profound yet usually does not signify fulminant hepatic necrosis, so hepatic transaminase rises are only moderate (p.1442-1443).","The transient rash may be misdiagnosed as scrub typhus or a viral exanthem (p.1442).","Dual infection (e.g., with dengue, rickettsiae, or hantavirus) can occur, so a positive alternative test does not exclude leptospirosis (p.1443)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison distinguishes mild leptospirosis (sudden-onset flu-like febrile illness with headache, myalgia, conjunctival suffusion; usually self-limiting over 7-10 days, low mortality) from severe leptospirosis, which is often rapidly progressive with multiorgan involvement. Severe disease classically presents as Weil's syndrome (triad of jaundice, acute kidney injury, and hemorrhage) and/or severe pulmonary hemorrhage syndrome, with a case-fatality of 1-50% (p.1442-1443).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1439","Harrison 22e p.1440","Harrison 22e p.1441","Harrison 22e p.1442","Harrison 22e p.1443","Harrison 22e p.1444"],"crossLinks":["DENGUE","MALARIA","SCRUB_TYPHUS","VIRAL_HEPATITIS","CHIKUNGUNYA"]},"LIVER_ABSCESS":{"name":"Liver Abscess","system":"Hepatobiliary","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1117-1118 (pyogenic/intraabdominal abscesses); p.1289-1290 (hypervirulent K. pneumoniae); p.1755-1757 (amebic liver abscess)","clinicalPearls":["The liver is the visceral organ most prone to abscess formation; in one series of intraabdominal abscesses, hepatic abscesses accounted for roughly half of all visceral abscesses (p.1117).","Fever is the most common presenting feature; about half of patients with a liver abscess have no localizing signs (no hepatomegaly, right-upper-quadrant tenderness, or jaundice), so the liver may not be obviously implicated on examination (p.1117).","In the elderly, liver abscess may present solely as fever of unknown origin, so abdominal imaging (especially of the right upper quadrant) should form part of any FUO workup (p.1117).","An elevated serum alkaline phosphatase is the single most reliable laboratory abnormality, present in about 70% of patients; bilirubin and aminotransferases are often normal or only modestly raised (p.1117).","The microbiology of pyogenic liver abscess tracks with the source: biliary-tract origin yields enteric gram-negative aerobic bacilli and enterococci, pelvic/intraperitoneal origin yields a mixed aerobic-anaerobic flora (B. fragilis most common), and haematogenous seeding usually yields a single organism such as S. aureus or a Streptococcus milleri (anginosus)-group streptococcus (p.1117).","Anaerobes are not typically involved in liver abscesses arising from biliary infection unless prior surgery has occurred (p.1117).","Candida liver abscess characteristically follows fungaemia in patients receiving chemotherapy and often becomes clinically apparent as neutrophil counts recover after a period of neutropenia (p.1117)."],"pathophysiology":"Pyogenic liver abscesses arise either from haematogenous spread of bacteria or from local spread from contiguous intraperitoneal infection. The organism reaches the liver by several routes: ascending biliary infection (currently the most common associated source), portal venous seeding via suppurative thrombosis of the portal vein (pylephlebitis, classically from pelvic or other intra-abdominal infection), direct contiguous spread, or systemic bacteraemic (haematogenous) seeding. Historically, ruptured appendicitis was the leading source. Hypervirulent (hypermucoviscous) K. pneumoniae causes a distinct community-acquired monomicrobial syndrome in which the organism establishes a primary liver abscess and then disseminates metastatically; this contrasts with the polymicrobial, source-driven pattern of biliary or enteric abscesses. (p.1117, p.1289-1290)","additionalDifferentials":["Splenic abscess (different clinical setting and organism profile, most often associated with bacterial endocarditis; high index of suspicion needed as it is frequently fatal if untreated) (p.1118)","Hepatic actinomycosis (Actinomyces israelii can produce multiple hepatic abscesses, sometimes with a contiguous splenic lesion) (p.1290 region)","Candidal (fungal) liver abscess in immunocompromised/post-chemotherapy patients (p.1117)"],"infectionMimics":["Amebic liver abscess (Entamoeba histolytica) — a treatable alternative with overlapping right-upper-quadrant pain, fever, and point hepatic tenderness; jaundice is rare and fewer than one-third have active diarrhoea at presentation (p.1117, p.1756)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Imaging is the most reliable diagnostic modality: ultrasonography, CT, MRI, and indium-labelled-WBC or gallium scintigraphy; more than one study may be needed (p.1117).","Chest radiography may give a clue — a newly elevated right hemidiaphragm, right basilar infiltrate, or right pleural effusion (p.1117).","Diagnostic aspiration of abscess contents (with Gram stain and culture), together with blood cultures, ideally obtained before starting empirical antibiotics (p.1118).","Amebic serology is positive in >95% of amebic liver abscess cases and PCR can also be used; negative results help exclude amebiasis (p.1117). Note enzyme immunoassay for the Gal/GalNAc lectin and LAMP/PCR can also be applied directly to abscess aspirate/pus (p.1756-1757).","Supportive labs: leukocytosis (~77%), normochromic normocytic anaemia (~50%), hypoalbuminaemia (~33%); concomitant bacteraemia in one-third to one-half of patients (p.1117).","For suspected hypervirulent K. pneumoniae, the string test for hypermucoviscosity is suggestive but not optimally sensitive or specific; the most accurate identification uses the biomarkers iucA, iroB, peg-344, rmpA, and rmpA2 (p.1290)."],"redFlags":["Features suggesting hypervirulent K. pneumoniae with metastatic spread — concurrent endophthalmitis (threatening vision loss), meningitis/brain abscess, or septic pulmonary nodules — mandate urgent evaluation of distant sites (p.1289-1290).","Multiple sizeable abscesses, viscous contents prone to plugging a catheter, presence of yeast, communication with an untreated/obstructed biliary tree, or no clinical response to percutaneous drainage within 4-7 days predict failure of percutaneous drainage and favour surgical intervention (p.1118).","Mortality remains appreciable (around 15%) despite treatment (p.1118)."],"prognosis":"Mortality from pyogenic liver abscess averaged about 15% despite treatment in the data cited; outcome is poorer with delayed source control or factors predicting failed percutaneous drainage. For hypervirulent K. pneumoniae infection, survivors may suffer catastrophic sequelae from metastatic spread, including vision loss (endophthalmitis) and major neurologic deficits. (p.1118, p.1289)","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish pyogenic from amebic liver abscess: amebic serology is positive in >95% of amebic cases, amebic abscesses are classically a single right-lobe lesion, and most travellers with amebic abscess present within ~5 months of leaving an endemic area — so travel history, lesion location, and serology should be sought before committing to a purely bacterial diagnosis (p.1117, p.1755-1756).","Aspirating an amebic abscess for trophozoites is low-yield: trophozoites reside in the abscess capsule, not in the readily aspirated necrotic ('anchovy-paste') centre, so their absence on aspirate does not exclude amebiasis (p.1756).","Up to 10% of patients with acute amebic liver abscess have negative serology early on, usually seroconverting within a week — a single early negative test does not exclude the diagnosis (p.1757).","Assuming anaerobic coverage is always required: anaerobes are generally not involved in abscesses of biliary origin (unless prior surgery), whereas pelvic/intraperitoneal-source abscesses are typically polymicrobial with B. fragilis (p.1117).","Attributing a cryptogenic pyogenic liver abscess solely to the liver: because biliary, portal (pelvic/appendiceal), and other intra-abdominal infections are leading sources, the underlying source must be sought and addressed (p.1117).","Overlooking that ampicillin/amoxicillin exposure within the prior 30 days has been associated with increased risk of the hypervirulent K. pneumoniae liver abscess syndrome, presumably via selection for the causative strain (p.1117)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.1117","Harrison 22e p.1118","Harrison 22e p.1289","Harrison 22e p.1290","Harrison 22e p.1755","Harrison 22e p.1756","Harrison 22e p.1757"],"crossLinks":["AMOEBIC_LIVER_ABSCESS","CHOLANGITIS","SEPSIS"]},"LUNG_ABSCESS":{"name":"Lung Abscess","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1034-1036","clinicalPearls":["Roughly 80% of lung abscesses are primary, arising from aspiration in the absence of an underlying pulmonary or systemic disease; the remaining ~20% are secondary to a predisposing process such as bronchial obstruction or immunocompromise (p.1034).","A foul-smelling (putrid) breath, sputum, or empyema is considered essentially diagnostic of an anaerobic lung abscess; abscesses with no isolated pathogen (about 40%) are termed nonspecific and anaerobes are presumed (p.1035).","Primary abscesses favor the dependent segments (posterior segments of upper lobes, superior segments of lower lobes), and the right lung is involved more often than the left because the right mainstem bronchus is less angulated (p.1035).","Anaerobic abscesses often present indolently with night sweats, fatigue, and anemia, whereas non-anaerobic causes such as S. aureus can produce a fulminant course with high fevers and rapid progression (p.1035-1036).","Edentulous patients are thought very unlikely to develop a lung abscess, since teeth (especially with gingivitis/periodontal disease) provide the nidus for the anaerobic/microaerophilic colonization that seeds the lung (p.1034).","Lemierre's syndrome (oropharyngeal infection, classically Fusobacterium necrophorum, with septic thrombophlebitis of the internal jugular vein) is a recognized embolic source of lung abscess (p.1034-1035).","Metronidazole alone is inadequate because it covers anaerobes but not the microaerophilic streptococci that are commonly part of the mixed flora of primary abscesses (p.1036)."],"pathophysiology":"A lung abscess is necrosis and cavitation of lung parenchyma after microbial infection, typically a single dominant cavity >2 cm. In primary disease, anaerobes and microaerophilic streptococci colonizing the gingival crevices are aspirated into a susceptible host who carries an overwhelming aspirated burden or cannot clear it. An initial pneumonitis (worsened by gastric-acid tissue damage) progresses over about 7-14 days to parenchymal necrosis and cavitation, with anaerobes driving more extensive synergistic tissue destruction in polymicrobial infection. Secondary abscesses follow the predisposing mechanism: post-obstructive (tumor/foreign body) impairment of secretion clearance, or impaired host defense in immunocompromise allowing a broad pathogen range. Abscesses may also arise hematogenously from septic emboli (e.g., tricuspid endocarditis, Lemierre's syndrome).","additionalDifferentials":["Pulmonary malignancy presenting as a cavitary lesion (p.1035)","Pulmonary infarction (p.1035)","Pulmonary sequestration (p.1035)","Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia (p.1035)","Sarcoidosis (p.1035)","Vasculitis / autoimmune disease such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis (p.1035)","Infected lung cysts or bullae (fluid-containing) (p.1035)","Septic emboli, e.g., from tricuspid valve endocarditis (p.1035)","Pulmonary manifestations of inflammatory bowel disease or pyoderma gangrenosum (p.1035)"],"infectionMimics":["Mycobacterium tuberculosis (and M. avium, M. kansasii) cavitary disease (p.1034)","Endemic fungi: Coccidioides, Histoplasma capsulatum, Blastomyces (p.1034)","Parasitic causes of cavitation: Entamoeba histolytica, Paragonimus westermani, Strongyloides stercoralis (p.1034)","Nocardia, Aspergillus, Mucorales, Cryptococcus, Rhodococcus equi, Pneumocystis jirovecii in immunocompromised hosts (p.1034)","Empyema / pleural space infection (must be distinguished from a peripheral abscess as it may need urgent drainage) (p.1035)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cavitary lung cancer (p.1035)","Pulmonary infarction (p.1035)","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis and other vasculitides (p.1035)","Sarcoidosis (p.1035)","Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia (p.1035)","Fluid-filled cysts or bullae (p.1035)","Pulmonary sequestration (p.1035)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CT chest, which defines cavitation earlier than plain film, can reveal an underlying cause such as malignancy, and helps separate a peripheral abscess from a pleural infection (p.1035)","Molecular methods such as 16S rRNA gene amplification for more specific pathogen identification (p.1035)","Serologic studies for opportunistic pathogens (viral/fungal) when abscess is secondary or empirical therapy fails (p.1035)","Bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage or protected brush specimen (early if bronchial obstruction is suspected) (p.1035-1036)","CT-guided percutaneous needle aspiration (p.1035)","Induced sputum for tuberculosis in patients from TB-endemic areas or with TB risk factors such as HIV (p.1036)","Bronchoscopy with biopsy in patients with malignancy risk or atypical presentation (p.1036)"],"redFlags":["Life-threatening hemoptysis (p.1036)","Massive aspiration of abscess contents into the airway (p.1036)","Extension to the pleural space with empyema, which may require urgent drainage (p.1035-1036)","Failure to defervesce or radiographic progression despite appropriate therapy (10-20% of patients do not respond) (p.1036)","Abscess >6-8 cm in diameter, which is less likely to respond to antibiotics alone (p.1036)","Sepsis at presentation (poor prognostic factor) (p.1036)"],"prognosis":"Mortality for primary lung abscess can be as low as ~2%, but secondary abscesses carry a much higher mortality, reported as high as 75% in some series. Adverse prognostic factors include age >60, malignancy-associated abscess, presence of aerobic bacteria, sepsis at presentation, symptom duration >8 weeks, and abscess size >6 cm. Defervescence on appropriate therapy can take up to 7 days, and 10-20% of patients fail to respond. Larger cavities at presentation predispose to persistent pneumatoceles or bronchiectasis. Prevention centers on airway protection, oral hygiene, minimizing sedation, head-of-bed elevation in aspiration-prone patients, and pathogen prophylaxis in selected immunocompromised hosts (p.1036).","pitfalls":["Failure to exclude an obstructing malignancy: in older patients, those with malignancy risk factors, or those lacking clear aspiration risk, early bronchoscopy/biopsy and CT are indicated rather than assuming a primary anaerobic abscess (p.1035-1036).","Treating a presumed primary abscess that does not improve without re-evaluating for a secondary cause (obstruction, unusual pathogen) (p.1036).","Sputum culture may miss the diagnosis: infection is usually polymicrobial and routine cultures often fail to reflect anaerobes (p.1035).","Using metronidazole as monotherapy, which misses microaerophilic streptococci (p.1036).","Misclassifying a peripheral abscess as a pleural infection (empyema) or vice versa, since empyema may need urgent drainage (p.1035).","Underestimating risk in immunocompromised hosts, who are fragile, infected by a wide pathogen range, and warrant early culture-directed diagnostics (p.1035)."],"severityClassification":"Lung abscesses are classified as primary (~80%, aspiration-related, anaerobe-predominant, no underlying lung/systemic disease) versus secondary (underlying obstruction, systemic illness, or immunocompromise); and by chronicity as acute (<4-6 weeks) versus chronic (~40% of cases). Size >6-8 cm marks lesions less responsive to antibiotics alone (p.1034, p.1036).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1034","Harrison 22e p.1035","Harrison 22e p.1036"],"crossLinks":["ASPIRATION_PNEUMONIA","lung_cancer","PULMONARY_TB"]},"MALARIA":{"name":"Malaria","system":"Tropical Fever","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e ch.231, pp.1760-1768","clinicalPearls":["Malaria is not a clinical diagnosis: any patient in or from an endemic area with fever needs thick and thin blood smears (or a rapid test if reliable microscopy is unavailable) to confirm infection and identify the species (Harrison 22e p.1766).","Early symptoms are nonspecific (malaise, headache, fatigue, abdominal discomfort, myalgia, then fever) and resemble a minor viral illness, so a high index of suspicion is essential (Harrison 22e p.1763).","The classic regular febrile paroxysms (chills, rigors, fever spikes at fixed intervals) are now unusual and, when present at presentation, suggest P. vivax or P. ovale (often a relapse); prompt effective treatment prevents the synchronized fever pattern from developing (Harrison 22e p.1763).","Mild thrombocytopenia is usual in malaria; a normal platelet count should call the diagnosis into question (Harrison 22e pp.1765, 1768).","In falciparum malaria, peripheral parasitemia underestimates true parasite burden because mature parasites sequester in deep vascular beds and only younger ring forms circulate (Harrison 22e p.1762).","A PfHRP2-based rapid test can stay positive for weeks after treatment, which helps diagnose severe malaria in a patient who has already taken antimalarials and cleared peripheral parasites but is a drawback in high-transmission settings (Harrison 22e p.1767).","Persistent gametocytemia after a full treatment course does not indicate drug resistance or a need to re-treat, because mature P. falciparum gametocytes are unaffected by most antimalarials (Harrison 22e p.1767).","Any obtundation, delirium, abnormal behavior, or generalized seizure in falciparum malaria must be taken seriously as a possible herald of cerebral malaria (Harrison 22e pp.1763-1764).","Cerebral malaria is a diffuse symmetric encephalopathy without true meningism; focal neurologic signs are unusual and signs of meningeal irritation are absent (Harrison 22e p.1764).","Specific retinal changes (patchy retinal opacification/whitening, vessel decolorization, pale-centered hemorrhages) on funduscopy support a diagnosis of cerebral malaria (Harrison 22e pp.1764-1765).","In severe malaria the usual adrenergic warning signs of hypoglycemia (sweating, tachycardia, gooseflesh) are absent and its neurologic effects cannot be distinguished from the disease itself, so glucose must be checked actively (Harrison 22e p.1764).","Quinine can provoke hypoglycemia by stimulating pancreatic insulin secretion, compounding the hypoglycemia of severe malaria (Harrison 22e p.1764).","Noncardiogenic pulmonary edema may appear even after several days of effective antimalarial therapy and is readily precipitated by over-vigorous IV fluids; mortality exceeds 80% (Harrison 22e p.1764).","Acidotic ('respiratory distress') breathing in a child with severe anemia is usually due to metabolic acidosis, not 'anemic heart failure' as previously mislabeled (Harrison 22e pp.1765-1766).","Approximately 6% of children and <1% of adults with severe malaria have concomitant bacteremia (higher with >20% parasitemia in adults), and nontyphoidal Salmonella bacteremia is specifically associated with P. falciparum (Harrison 22e p.1765).","In high-transmission areas low-density parasitemia is a common incidental finding, so severe malaria is easily overdiagnosed and must be distinguished from sepsis with coincidental parasitemia (Harrison 22e pp.1765, 1767).","Sudden delayed hemolysis can follow artesunate treatment of hyperparasitemia in nonimmune patients, from synchronous loss of once-parasitized 'pitted' red cells (Harrison 22e p.1765)."],"pathophysiology":"Infection begins when a female Anopheles inoculates sporozoites, which reach the liver and undergo preerythrocytic schizogony, each sporozoite yielding 10,000 to >30,000 merozoites. Released merozoites invade red cells, becoming trophozoites that multiply roughly 6- to 20-fold each asexual cycle (every 48 h for P. falciparum/P. vivax/P. ovale, 72 h for P. malariae, 24 h for P. knowlesi); symptoms begin around densities of ~50 parasites/uL. In P. vivax and P. ovale a fraction of liver forms persist as dormant hypnozoites for weeks to a year or more, causing the relapses characteristic of these species (P. falciparum, P. malariae, and P. knowlesi do not relapse). Disease results from red-cell invasion and destruction plus the host inflammatory response: schizont rupture releases material that activates monocytes/macrophages and proinflammatory cytokines, producing fever. The central pathology of severe falciparum malaria is cytoadherence: ~12-15 h after invasion 'knobs' on the infected red cell extrude the variant adhesin PfEMP1, which binds endothelial receptors (ICAM-1 and endothelial protein C receptor in brain, VAR2CSA/chondroitin sulfate A in placenta, CD36 elsewhere). Cytoadherence, rosetting (adherence to uninfected red cells), and agglutination sequester mature parasites in vital organs, especially the brain, obstructing microcirculatory flow and metabolism while escaping splenic clearance. Uninfected red cells also become less deformable in severe disease. P. falciparum invades cells of all ages and can reach very high densities (P. knowlesi can also, owing to its 24-h cycle), whereas other species spare most red cells and rarely exceed ~2% parasitemia. The spleen augments clearance and 'pits' damaged ring forms; severe lactic acidosis arises from anaerobic glycolysis in sequestered beds, parasite lactate production, gut-derived acids, and failed hepatic/renal clearance, and hypoglycemia from impaired gluconeogenesis with increased glucose consumption. (Harrison 22e pp.1760-1765)","additionalDifferentials":["Acute viral illness / minor febrile viral syndrome (early nonspecific malaria mimics this) (Harrison 22e p.1763)","Bacterial meningitis (severe headache in malaria, but neck stiffness and photophobia are absent in malaria) (Harrison 22e p.1763)","Sepsis / bacteremia with incidental parasitemia, especially in high-transmission areas (Harrison 22e pp.1765, 1767)"],"infectionMimics":["Dengue fever (myalgia is prominent but usually more severe in dengue) (Harrison 22e p.1763)","Leptospirosis (muscle tenderness, which malaria lacks; petechial hemorrhages a feature) (Harrison 22e p.1763)","Typhus/rickettsial illness (tender muscles, unlike malaria) (Harrison 22e p.1763)","Viral hemorrhagic fevers (petechial hemorrhages, which are very rare in malaria) (Harrison 22e p.1763)","Nontyphoidal Salmonella and other bacteremia complicating or coinciding with malaria (Harrison 22e p.1765)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Diabetic ketoacidosis / ketoacidosis (acidotic 'respiratory distress' breathing overlaps with severe malaria acidosis; ketoacidosis can contribute in children) (Harrison 22e pp.1764-1766)","Hematologic malignancy / lymphoproliferative disorder (hyperreactive malarial splenomegaly with massive splenomegaly and pancytopenia can be mistaken for it) (Harrison 22e p.1766)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Thick blood film (concentrates parasites ~40-100x over thin film, increasing sensitivity; examine 100-200 fields before calling negative) (Harrison 22e pp.1766-1767)","Thin blood film for species identification and parasite quantification (parasitized cells per 1000 RBCs) (Harrison 22e pp.1766-1767)","Giemsa (pH 7.2) or other Romanowsky stain; acridine orange for rapid detection of low-level parasitemia (Harrison 22e p.1766)","Rapid diagnostic tests detecting PfHRP2, parasite LDH, or aldolase (note PfHRP2/3 gene-deletion false negatives and that RDTs do not quantify parasitemia) (Harrison 22e p.1767)","PCR / ultrasensitive PCR for species confirmation and detection of low-density/asymptomatic infection (reference setting; too sensitive for primary diagnosis in endemic areas) (Harrison 22e pp.1767-1768)","Plasma lactate and bicarbonate (best biochemical prognosticators in severe malaria) (Harrison 22e p.1764)","Coagulation studies (PT, aPTT, fibrinogen) and platelet count in severe disease (Harrison 22e pp.1764-1765, 1768)","Assessment of malaria pigment in neutrophils (>5% pigment-containing) and proportion of mature pigmented trophozoites/schizonts as severity/prognostic markers (Harrison 22e pp.1764, 1767)"],"redFlags":["Impaired consciousness, delirium, abnormal behavior, or unarousable coma (cerebral malaria) (Harrison 22e pp.1763-1764)","Generalized or repeated convulsions (>2 in 24 h), or subtle seizure activity such as tonic-clonic eye movements (Harrison 22e p.1764)","Acidotic deep ('respiratory distress') breathing, hyperlactatemia >=5 mmol/L, or acidemia (arterial pH <7.25) (Harrison 22e p.1764)","Hypoglycemia (plasma glucose <2.2 mmol/L, <40 mg/dL) (Harrison 22e p.1764)","Hypotension/shock with impaired perfusion (Harrison 22e p.1764)","Severe anemia (hematocrit <15% or Hb <50 g/L with high parasitemia; Hb <=3 g/dL on presentation carries increased mortality) (Harrison 22e pp.1764-1765)","Macroscopic hemoglobinuria / blackwater fever (black, brown, or red urine) (Harrison 22e pp.1764-1765)","Acute kidney injury (creatinine >265 umol/L, >3 mg/dL) or anuria (Harrison 22e pp.1764-1765)","Noncardiogenic pulmonary edema / ARDS or O2 saturation <92% with tachypnea (Harrison 22e p.1764)","Spontaneous bleeding / DIC (Harrison 22e p.1764)","Hyperparasitemia (>5% in nonimmune, >10% in any patient) or >100,000/uL (Harrison 22e pp.1763-1764)","Prostration / inability to sit or stand unaided (Harrison 22e p.1764)","Low core temperature <36.5C, marked agitation, jaundice (bilirubin >50 umol/L) with vital-organ dysfunction (Harrison 22e p.1764)"],"prognosis":"Promptly and appropriately treated uncomplicated falciparum malaria has a mortality of <0.1%, but risk rises steeply once vital-organ dysfunction occurs or parasitemia exceeds 2%. Cerebral malaria carries roughly 20% mortality in adults and 15% in children even with treatment; about 10% of surviving children (especially with hypoglycemia, severe anemia, repeated seizures, or deep coma) have residual neurologic deficits, most of which improve or resolve within 6 months, though persistent language and cognitive deficits, increased epilepsy risk, and reduced life expectancy occur in some. Noncardiogenic pulmonary edema in adults has >80% mortality. Plasma bicarbonate and lactate are the best biochemical prognosticators. Among complications, poor prognosis is indicated by >20% mature pigment-containing parasites or >5% of neutrophils containing pigment, and by hyperparasitemia. In severe acute renal failure that survives, urine flow returns in a median of 4 days and creatinine normalizes in a mean of 17 days; early dialysis improves survival. (Harrison 22e pp.1763-1765, 1767)","pitfalls":["Treating malaria as a clinical diagnosis; confirmation by smear or rapid test is required (Harrison 22e p.1766).","Accepting a normal platelet count without question, as mild thrombocytopenia is the rule in malaria (Harrison 22e pp.1765, 1768).","Relying on peripheral parasitemia to gauge true burden in falciparum malaria, since mature parasites are sequestered (Harrison 22e p.1762).","Overdiagnosing severe malaria in high-transmission areas where low-density parasitemia is a common incidental finding, missing concurrent sepsis (Harrison 22e pp.1765, 1767).","Over-vigorous IV fluid administration, which can precipitate noncardiogenic pulmonary edema (Harrison 22e p.1764).","Missing hypoglycemia because its usual autonomic signs are absent in severe disease and its neurologic effects mimic malaria itself (Harrison 22e p.1764).","Re-treating on the basis of persistent gametocytemia, which does not signify drug resistance after a full course (Harrison 22e p.1767).","False-negative RDTs from PfHRP2/3 gene-deleted parasites (notably in the Horn of Africa); RDTs also cannot quantify parasitemia (Harrison 22e p.1767).","Using serology to diagnose acute illness; serology has no role in acute diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1768).","Mistaking acidotic breathing in a severely anemic child for 'anemic congestive cardiac failure' (Harrison 22e pp.1765-1766)."],"severityClassification":"Severe falciparum malaria (WHO-based criteria, Harrison 22e Table 231-2, p.1764) is defined by one or more of: unarousable coma/cerebral malaria (no localizing response, coma >30 min after a generalized convulsion, Glasgow Coma Score <11 or Blantyre <3 in young children); acidemia/acidosis (arterial pH <7.25, base deficit >8 meq/L, or plasma bicarbonate <15 mmol/L; venous lactate >=5 mmol/L; deep labored 'respiratory distress' breathing); severe normochromic normocytic anemia (hematocrit <15% or Hb <50 g/L with parasite density >10,000/uL); renal failure (creatinine >265 umol/L / >3 mg/dL); noncardiogenic pulmonary edema/ARDS (or O2 sat <92% on room air with respiratory rate >30/min); hypoglycemia (glucose <2.2 mmol/L / <40 mg/dL); hypotension/shock (systolic BP <70 mmHg in children 1-5 y or <80 mmHg in adults with impaired perfusion); significant bleeding/DIC; convulsions (>2 generalized seizures in 24 h); hemoglobinuria (macroscopic black/brown/red urine, not due to oxidant drugs or G6PD deficiency); extreme weakness/prostration; hyperparasitemia (>5% in nonimmune, >10% in any patient); and jaundice (bilirubin >50 umol/L / >3 mg/dL) with parasitemia >100,000/uL or other vital-organ dysfunction. Note: criteria thresholds for renal failure are for adults; lower values define severe disease in children. (Harrison 22e p.1764)","references":["Harrison 22e p.1760","Harrison 22e p.1761","Harrison 22e p.1762","Harrison 22e p.1763","Harrison 22e p.1764","Harrison 22e p.1765","Harrison 22e p.1766","Harrison 22e p.1767","Harrison 22e p.1768"],"crossLinks":["MIXED_MALARIA","DENGUE","LEPTOSPIROSIS","SCRUB_TYPHUS","RICKETTSIAL_FEVER","SEPSIS","PUO"]},"MENINGITIS":{"name":"Acute Bacterial Meningitis","system":"Central Nervous System","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Chapter 143 (Acute Meningitis), printed p.1118-1125; CSF comparison table 124-4, p.1005; coma/altered-mentation discussion p.[Ch.301]; SIADH causes table [Ch.49]","clinicalPearls":["The classic triad of fever, headache and nuchal rigidity is useful but each component occurs in over 80% of adult cases while the full triad is frequently incomplete, so its absence does not exclude bacterial meningitis (p.1119).","A reduced level of consciousness, ranging from lethargy to coma, is present in more than three-quarters of patients; nausea, vomiting and photophobia are also common (p.1119).","Nuchal rigidity is the characteristic sign of meningeal irritation (neck resists passive flexion), but Kernig's and Brudzinski's signs have uncertain sensitivity/specificity and may be absent in the very young, the elderly, the immunocompromised or those with depressed mental status (p.1119-1120).","Coexisting cervical spine disease in older patients can produce false-positive neck stiffness (p.1120).","Whenever bacterial meningitis is suspected, draw blood cultures immediately and start empirical antibiotics plus adjunctive dexamethasone without delay; if neuroimaging is needed before LP, give antibiotics after blood cultures rather than waiting (p.1120).","Antibiotics given a few hours before LP do not meaningfully change CSF WBC count or glucose and are unlikely to prevent Gram-stain visualization or PCR detection of bacterial nucleic acid, so a delayed LP after starting therapy still has diagnostic value (p.1120).","Dexamethasone should ideally be given about 20 minutes before, or at the latest with, the first antibiotic dose, because steroids blunt macrophage/microglial TNF-alpha production only if given before those cells are activated by endotoxin; benefit is unlikely if started more than 6 hours after antibiotics (p.1120, p.1123).","Petechial or purpuric skin lesions are an important clue to meningococcal infection and, if present, should be biopsied (Gram stain of the lesion may reveal the organism) (p.1119, p.1121).","Profound depression of consciousness (stupor/coma), seizures or focal neurologic signs point away from uncomplicated viral meningitis and suggest encephalitis or bacterial/parameningeal disease (p.1123)."],"pathophysiology":"Bacterial meningitis is an acute purulent infection of the subarachnoid space accompanied by a CNS inflammatory reaction that can cause decreased consciousness, seizures, raised intracranial pressure and stroke; meninges, subarachnoid space and brain parenchyma are often jointly involved (meningoencephalitis). The leading organisms (S. pneumoniae, N. meningitidis) first colonize the nasopharynx, cross or breach the epithelium into the bloodstream, evade neutrophil phagocytosis and complement killing via their polysaccharide capsule, and reach the CSF through the choroid plexus or cerebral capillary endothelium. They multiply readily in CSF because normal CSF has few leukocytes and little complement/immunoglobulin, so opsonization and phagocytosis are poor. Much of the neurologic injury comes from the host inflammatory response rather than direct bacterial damage, so injury can progress even after antibiotics sterilize the CSF. Lysis of bacteria releases cell-wall components (gram-negative LPS; pneumococcal teichoic acid and peptidoglycan) that drive production of TNF-alpha, IL-1beta, chemokines and other mediators by microglia, astrocytes, monocytes, endothelium and CSF leukocytes. These cytokines increase blood-brain barrier permeability (vasogenic edema and protein leakage), the proteinaceous/leukocytic exudate obstructs CSF flow and resorption (obstructive and communicating hydrocephalus, interstitial edema), and neutrophil degranulation plus reactive oxygen/nitrogen species and excitatory amino acids produce cytotoxic edema and cell death (notably in the hippocampal dentate gyrus). Cerebral blood flow rises early then falls with loss of autoregulation; vasculitis and thrombosis of arteries, cortical veins and dural sinuses cause ischemia/infarction. The combined interstitial, vasogenic and cytotoxic edema raises ICP and can lead to coma and herniation (p.1118-1120).","additionalDifferentials":["Herpes simplex virus encephalitis (headache, fever, altered consciousness, focal deficits, seizures; distinguished by CSF profile, MRI temporal-lobe changes and HSV CSF PCR) (p.1121)","Subacute meningitis presenting acutely: M. tuberculosis, Cryptococcus neoformans, Histoplasma, Coccidioides, Treponema pallidum (p.1122)","Mycoplasma pneumoniae CNS infection, which can mimic viral meningitis/encephalitis (p.1124)"],"infectionMimics":["Viral (aseptic) meningoencephalitis, especially enteroviral and HSV (p.1121)","Rocky Mountain spotted fever (Rickettsia rickettsii) with early maculopapular rash that can resemble meningococcemia (p.1121)","Ehrlichiosis/anaplasmosis (tick-borne) with fever, headache, confusion and sometimes rash (p.1121)","Focal suppurative CNS infections: subdural empyema, epidural empyema, brain abscess (consider when focal neurologic findings present) (p.1121)","Fungal, tuberculous, Listeria, Brucella, Coxiella, Leptospira meningitis (lymphocytic pleocytosis, hypoglycorrhachia may be absent early) (p.1124)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Subarachnoid hemorrhage (the major non-infectious consideration) (p.1121)","Medication-induced hypersensitivity (drug-induced) meningitis (p.1121)","Chemical meningitis from rupture of tumor contents into CSF (cystic glioma, craniopharyngioma, epidermoid/dermoid cyst) (p.1121)","Carcinomatous or lymphomatous (neoplastic) meningitis (p.1121)","Inflammatory disorders: sarcoidosis, systemic lupus erythematosus, Behcet's syndrome (p.1121)","Pituitary apoplexy (p.1122)","Uveomeningitic syndromes (Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada) (p.1122)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CSF opening pressure measurement: raised (>180 mmH2O) in over 90% of cases, >400 mmH2O in about 20% (p.1120)","CSF cell count and differential: PMN pleocytosis, >100 cells/uL in ~90% (p.1120)","CSF glucose and CSF/serum glucose ratio: low glucose (<40 mg/dL) and ratio <0.4 are highly suggestive (ratio also low in fungal/TB/carcinomatous meningitis) (p.1120)","CSF protein: elevated (>45 mg/dL) in ~90% (p.1120)","CSF Gram stain (organisms in >60%) and CSF culture (positive in >70-80%; required for susceptibility testing) (p.1120-1121)","CSF multiplex PCR (e.g., FilmArray ME panel) for rapid detection of S. pneumoniae, N. meningitidis, E. coli, L. monocytogenes, H. influenzae, group B strep; does not include S. aureus, coagulase-negative staphylococci or many gram-negatives and cannot replace culture (p.1120-1121)","MRI (preferred over CT) for cerebral edema/ischemia; gadolinium meningeal enhancement is non-specific and not diagnostic of meningitis (p.1121)","Skin lesion biopsy with Gram stain when petechiae/purpura are present (p.1121)","Serum procalcitonin and CSF protein as adjuncts to distinguish bacterial from aseptic meningitis (children) (p.1124)"],"redFlags":["Petechial or purpuric rash, which may herald fulminant meningococcal disease that can progress to death within hours of symptom onset (p.1119)","Signs of raised ICP: deteriorating consciousness, papilledema, dilated poorly reactive pupils, sixth-nerve palsy, decerebrate posturing, Cushing reflex (bradycardia, hypertension, irregular respirations) (p.1120)","Seizures at presentation or within the first 24 hours (associated with worse outcome) (p.1120, p.1123)","Coma or markedly decreased level of consciousness on admission (p.1123)","Indications to obtain CT/MRI before LP: recent head trauma, depressed/abnormal consciousness, papilledema, or focal neurologic deficit (do not delay antibiotics for imaging) (p.1120)"],"prognosis":"Case-fatality varies by organism: roughly 3-7% for H. influenzae, N. meningitidis or group B streptococcal meningitis, about 15% for Listeria, and around 20% for pneumococcal meningitis. Risk of death rises with decreased consciousness on admission, seizures within 24 h, signs of raised ICP, age (infancy or >50 years), comorbidities such as shock or need for mechanical ventilation, and delay in starting treatment. A markedly low CSF glucose (<40 mg/dL) and very high CSF protein (>300 mg/dL) predict worse outcomes in some series. About 25% of survivors have moderate-to-severe sequelae, commonly decreased intellectual function, memory impairment, seizures, sensorineural hearing loss with dizziness, and gait disturbance (p.1123).","pitfalls":["Relying on the complete classic triad or on Kernig's/Brudzinski's signs to rule out meningitis; these are insensitive, particularly in elderly, very young or immunocompromised patients (p.1119-1120).","Delaying antibiotics and dexamethasone to obtain neuroimaging or LP first; treatment should not wait when bacterial meningitis is suspected (p.1120).","Assuming a recent dose of antibiotics renders the LP useless; CSF chemistry, Gram stain and PCR usually remain informative for several hours (p.1120).","Interpreting gadolinium meningeal enhancement on MRI as diagnostic of meningitis; it occurs with any condition that increases blood-brain barrier permeability (p.1121).","Treating with dexamethasone in settings where it has not shown benefit: trials in low-income countries (late presentation, prior antibiotics, malnutrition, HIV, unproven diagnosis) failed to show efficacy, and patients with a negative CSF Gram stain and culture in those settings should not receive it (p.1123).","Forgetting that neurologic injury and complications can progress even after CSF sterilization because much of the damage is immune-mediated (p.1118).","Overlooking that a giving of IV glucose (e.g., D50) shortly before LP rarely alters CSF glucose unless several hours have elapsed, so a low CSF glucose remains meaningful (p.1120)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison 22e does not provide a formal graded severity score for acute bacterial meningitis; it frames the disease as a medical emergency (goal: antibiotics within 60 min of arrival) and lists adverse prognostic markers (decreased consciousness on admission, seizures within 24 h, signs of raised ICP, age extremes, comorbidities such as shock/ventilation, treatment delay, very low CSF glucose, very high CSF protein). For distinguishing bacterial from aseptic meningitis in children, the prospectively validated Bacterial Meningitis Score is cited: very low probability (NPV ~99.7%) when all of negative CSF Gram stain, CSF neutrophils <1000/uL, CSF protein <80 mg/dL, peripheral ANC <10,000/uL, and no seizures are met (p.1123-1124).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1118","Harrison 22e p.1119","Harrison 22e p.1120","Harrison 22e p.1121","Harrison 22e p.1122","Harrison 22e p.1123","Harrison 22e p.1124","Harrison 22e p.1005 (Table 124-4, CSF profiles)"],"crossLinks":["VIRAL_MENINGITIS","ENCEPHALITIS","BRAIN_ABSCESS","CNS_TB"]},"MIXED_MALARIA":{"name":"Mixed Malaria (P. falciparum + P. vivax)","system":"Tropical Fever","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Chapter 231 (Malaria), printed pp. 1760-1768","clinicalPearls":["Mixed infections (most commonly P. falciparum together with P. vivax) are frequently under-detected by light microscopy and rapid antigen tests; PCR is the most sensitive method and is increasingly used specifically for speciation and genotyping in mixed infections (p.1767).","Because microscopy and RDTs may detect only the dominant species, a smear or RDT reported as single-species (especially P. falciparum) does not exclude a coexisting vivax/ovale component carrying dormant liver-stage hypnozoites.","P. vivax + P. falciparum is recognized as a distinct co-circulating pattern in endemic mapping, with roughly equal prevalence of the two species in Oceania (p.1761-1762).","Standard combination RDTs typically carry one P. falciparum-specific band plus a pan-malaria or P. vivax band; this design distinguishes falciparum from the 'less dangerous' malarias but is not optimized to flag concurrent dual infection (p.1766).","RDTs may miss low-level P. vivax (and P. ovale/P. malariae) parasitemia and PfHRP2-based tests can be falsely negative where parasites carry HRP2/3 gene deletions — both failure modes can mask one component of a mixed infection (p.1766).","The classic regularly-spaced febrile paroxysm at presentation suggests P. vivax or P. ovale (often relapse); its presence in a patient with confirmed falciparum should raise suspicion of a coexisting vivax/ovale component.","Apparent vivax/ovale relapses arising after treatment of a mixed infection reflect persistence of liver-stage hypnozoites, which are not cleared by standard blood-stage schizonticides."],"pathophysiology":"Sporozoites inoculated by anopheline mosquitoes reach the liver and undergo pre-erythrocytic schizogony; a single sporozoite yields thousands of merozoites that invade RBCs and multiply (every 48 h for P. falciparum and P. vivax). In P. vivax (and P. ovale) infection, a fraction of intrahepatic forms do not divide but persist as dormant hypnozoites for weeks to a year or more, later reactivating to cause relapses — a feature absent in P. falciparum. In a mixed P. falciparum + P. vivax infection, the falciparum component drives the risk of severe disease through cytoadherence (PfEMP1 binding endothelial receptors), rosetting, and agglutination, causing sequestration of mature parasites in vital organs; only ring forms circulate, so peripheral parasitemia underestimates true burden. P. vivax preferentially invades young reticulocytes and produces lower parasitemia (seldom >2%), while P. falciparum invades RBCs of all ages and can reach very high densities. The simultaneous presence of a vivax hypnozoite reservoir means blood-stage cure of both species does not prevent later vivax relapse (p.1760-1763).","additionalDifferentials":["Single-species P. falciparum malaria with an undetected coexisting vivax/ovale component (the central diagnostic trap of mixed infection)","P. knowlesi infection, which on RDT/microscopy may be misidentified as P. falciparum and can reach dangerously high densities (24-h cycle)","Recrudescent falciparum vs. true vivax/ovale relapse vs. reinfection as the cause of recurrent fever after treatment"],"infectionMimics":["Dengue (myalgia typically more severe than in malaria)","Leptospirosis (tender muscles, unlike malaria)","Typhus","Viral hemorrhagic fevers (petechiae/rash, which are very rare in malaria)","Bacterial sepsis with incidental parasitemia (concomitant bacteremia coexists with severe malaria and is hard to distinguish, especially in children)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thick and thin Giemsa-stained blood films examined immediately for parasite demonstration AND species identification; examine 100-200 fields before calling a thick film negative","PCR amplification for definitive speciation and genotyping when mixed infection is suspected (more sensitive than microscopy/RDT for detecting and distinguishing species)","Combination RDT carrying a P. falciparum-specific antigen plus a pan-malaria or P. vivax band","Quantification of parasitemia (RDTs do not quantify) and assessment of parasite maturity/pigment for prognostic stratification","G6PD status (knowledge basis for safe hypnozoite-directed therapy of the vivax component) — testing rationale only"],"redFlags":["Coma, obtundation, delirium, or abnormal behavior (cerebral malaria) — attributable to the falciparum component","Repeated or generalized convulsions (>2 in 24 h)","Respiratory distress / acidotic deep breathing (acidosis, lactate >=5 mmol/L)","Hypoglycemia (<2.2 mmol/L)","Shock / hypotension with impaired perfusion","Severe normocytic anemia (Hct <15%) and/or spontaneous bleeding / DIC","Hemoglobinuria (black/brown/red urine)","Acute kidney injury (creatinine >265 umol/L)","Hyperparasitemia (>2% indicates increased risk; >5-10% defines severe disease) — a falciparum-driven marker","Prostration / inability to sit or stand unaided"],"prognosis":"Promptly treated uncomplicated falciparum malaria has a mortality of <0.1%, but risk rises steeply once vital-organ dysfunction occurs or parasitemia exceeds ~2%. In a mixed infection the falciparum component dictates the severe-disease prognosis; cerebral malaria carries ~20% mortality in adults and ~15% in children even with treatment, and ~10% of child survivors have residual neurologic deficits (most improving within 6 months). Poor-prognosis features include hyperparasitemia (>100,000/uL), predominance of mature pigment-containing parasites (>20%), pigment in >5% of neutrophils, hypoglycemia, hyperlactatemia, acidemia, and coagulopathy. The vivax component does not usually drive mortality but, if its hypnozoite reservoir is not addressed, predisposes to later relapse and recurrent anemia (p.1763-1767).","pitfalls":["Treating an apparently single-species falciparum infection as if vivax were absent: the coexisting vivax hypnozoite reservoir is not cleared by blood-stage drugs and later causes relapse — manage mixed infection as falciparum AND provide hypnozoite-directed coverage for vivax.","Relying on microscopy or RDT alone to exclude a second species — both under-detect the minor component; PCR is needed to confirm/exclude mixed infection.","Misreading PfHRP2 RDT positivity that persists for weeks after a prior infection as evidence of the current species mix.","Mistaking persistent P. falciparum gametocytemia after treatment for treatment failure or active infection.","Attributing all post-treatment fever to falciparum recrudescence when it may be a true vivax/ovale relapse from the mixed-infection liver reservoir.","Misidentifying P. knowlesi as P. falciparum on microscopy/RDT, distorting the apparent species picture."],"severityClassification":"Severity is governed by the P. falciparum component per Harrison Table 231-2 (Manifestations of Severe Falciparum Malaria): major criteria include unarousable coma/cerebral malaria, acidosis (arterial pH <7.25 / lactate >=5 mmol/L), severe normocytic anemia (Hct <15%), renal failure (creatinine >265 umol/L), noncardiogenic pulmonary edema/ARDS, hypoglycemia (<2.2 mmol/L), hypotension/shock, bleeding/DIC, and repeated convulsions; other criteria include hemoglobinuria, prostration, and hyperparasitemia (>5% nonimmune, >10% any patient). Uncomplicated disease = patient can sit/stand unaided and swallow, with no vital-organ dysfunction and parasitemia <2% (p.1763-1764).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1760","Harrison 22e p.1761","Harrison 22e p.1762","Harrison 22e p.1763","Harrison 22e p.1764","Harrison 22e p.1766","Harrison 22e p.1767"],"crossLinks":["MALARIA"]},"NECROTIZING_FASCIITIS":{"name":"Necrotizing Fasciitis","system":"Skin & Soft Tissue","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1036-1037 (Ch.127), p.1093-1097 (Ch.134), p.1254-1255 (Ch.153)","clinicalPearls":["Pain out of proportion to physical findings is the classic early clue: physical signs may be minimal (only soft-tissue edema and mild erythema) while pain and fever are severe; this disparity helps distinguish NF from superficial cellulitis, where skin appearance is more abnormal but pain is only mild-to-moderate.","Early disease is frequently misdiagnosed (e.g., as thrombophlebitis) and patients may be sent home with analgesics, especially in the strep type that begins deep at a site of non-penetrating minor trauma (bruise, muscle strain) with no visible portal of entry.","Classic late signs evolve sequentially: swelling and brawny edema/tenderness, then dark-red (violaceous) induration, then bullae filled with blue/purple fluid, then friable bluish-maroon-to-black skin; thrombosis of dermal-papillae vessels is extensive by this stage.","Paradoxical decrease in pain or progression to anesthesia over the involved area is an ominous sign reflecting thrombosis of small vessels and infarction of cutaneous nerves.","GAS NF can be preceded by varicella, childbirth, or muscle strain, and most patients deny an antecedent streptococcal infection.","The inoculation site may be inapparent and remote from clinical involvement (e.g., minor hand trauma manifesting as infection over the shoulder or chest).","Myositis/myonecrosis coexists with NF in roughly 20-50% of cases and serum creatine phosphokinase (CPK) may be markedly elevated, signaling deeper muscle involvement."],"pathophysiology":"NF is infection of the superficial and/or deep fascia investing the muscles of an extremity or trunk, with extensive necrosis of subcutaneous tissue and fascia. Infection spreads rapidly along fascial planes and via venous channels and lymphatics, typically sparing the muscle itself in pure fasciitis. Two main microbiologic patterns: a mixed aerobic-anaerobic (type I) process beginning with a breach of a mucosal barrier (GI/GU tract), and a monomicrobial group A streptococcal (type II) process. In GAS disease, organisms reach deep fascia either by transient bacteremic seeding at a site of minor non-penetrating trauma (no portal) or by spread from cutaneous infection/penetrating trauma. Thrombosis of dermal vessels produces progressive skin necrosis; infarction of cutaneous nerves causes the characteristic late anesthesia. NF caused by Clostridium perfringens or extending into muscle (clostridial/streptococcal myonecrosis) carries very high systemic toxicity with hemolysis. PVL-producing MRSA strains have been reported as a cause.","additionalDifferentials":["Clostridial myonecrosis / gas gangrene (distinguished from clostridial NF by muscle biopsy)","Streptococcal necrotizing myositis (GAS infection extending into muscle)","Pyomyositis (usually S. aureus; localized, shock uncommon unless toxin-producing)","Synergistic nonclostridial anaerobic myonecrosis (necrotizing cutaneous myositis / synergistic necrotizing cellulitis) — a mixed aerobic-anaerobic variant","Cellulitis / erysipelas (superficial; skin more abnormal but pain mild-moderate)","Halophilic Vibrio vulnificus infection (can be as fulminant as NF; Gulf/Atlantic water exposure or raw seafood in cirrhosis)","Aeromonas soft-tissue infection (freshwater laceration exposure)"],"infectionMimics":["Aggressive cellulitis from Vibrio vulnificus or Aeromonas hydrophila","Ecthyma gangrenosum (Pseudomonas, neutropenic hosts)","Clostridial myonecrosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Thrombophlebitis (a common early misdiagnosis)","Muscle strain or contusion (the minor trauma at the deep nidus)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Soft-tissue plain radiography, CT, and MRI to assess depth of infection and detect tissue gas or a localized abscess — most useful when lesions progress rapidly or SIRS is present; note imaging may show only soft-tissue swelling and is NOT specific for fulminant GAS NF/myonecrosis, where gas is typically absent","Gram stain and aerobic/anaerobic culture of surgically excised tissue to identify GAS vs mixed flora vs MRSA vs Clostridium","Frozen-section histology of aspirate or punch biopsy (valuable in NF), though aspiration/biopsy is negative in ~80% of cases","Serum CPK (markedly elevated when myonecrosis coexists)","Lactate, d-dimer, coagulation, liver and renal function as markers of systemic toxicity/organ failure"],"redFlags":["Pain out of proportion to examination findings","Rapidly progressing lesion with signs of systemic inflammatory response / toxicity","Violaceous (blue/purple) bullae, skin sloughing, dusky/mottled erythema","Progression of tenderness to anesthesia over the lesion (cutaneous nerve infarction)","Crepitus or gas in deep tissue (suggests mixed/clostridial infection)","Renal impairment that may precede shock, plus hypotension/multiorgan failure","Urgent surgical exploration supersedes other diagnostic or therapeutic maneuvers — debridement is life-saving and must not be delayed"],"prognosis":"Mortality for NF is roughly 11-34% overall and rises substantially when associated with toxic shock syndrome. GAS/clostridial fascial-muscle infection historically carried 20-50% mortality with penicillin alone. Survivors of GAS NF often require repeated surgeries including amputation. Clostridial NF/myonecrosis is far more lethal: spontaneous (nontraumatic) myonecrosis mortality exceeds 65% (often C. septicum with underlying malignancy); trunk infection ~63% vs limb ~12% mortality. Any delay in surgery increases the risk of death; early aggressive surgery markedly improves outcomes.","pitfalls":["Early physical findings are minimal relative to pain/fever, leading to underestimation of severity and misdiagnosis as thrombophlebitis or simple cellulitis.","Absence of gas does NOT exclude NF: gas is usually absent when the cause is GAS or MRSA and is seen mainly with mixed/clostridial infection.","Normal or nonspecific imaging does NOT rule out NF — CT/MRI may show only soft-tissue swelling and are not specific for fulminant GAS NF/myonecrosis.","A negative aspirate or frozen-section biopsy does NOT exclude NF (negative in ~80% of cases).","Decreasing pain should not be reassuring — it can signal nerve infarction and disease progression rather than improvement.","Do not wait for imaging or cultures when NF is suspected with systemic toxicity; surgical exploration is both diagnostic and therapeutic."],"severityClassification":"Microbiologic/clinical typing: Type I = mixed aerobic-anaerobic, begins at a mucosal-barrier breach (GI/GU), common in diabetes, peripheral vascular disease, malignancy, surgery, or abdominal penetrating injury; Fournier gangrene is the perineal/scrotal form. Type II = monomicrobial group A streptococcal (GAS implicated in ~60% of NF cases), can occur in otherwise healthy hosts, two presentations (with vs without a defined portal of entry). PVL-producing MRSA is a recognized monomicrobial cause. NF qualifies as soft-tissue necrosis under the CDC streptococcal toxic shock syndrome case definition (hypotension plus >=2 of: renal impairment, coagulopathy, hepatic impairment, ARDS, generalized erythematous desquamating rash, soft-tissue necrosis/NF/myositis/gangrene). (LRINEC score not described in this source.)","references":["Harrison 22e p.1036","Harrison 22e p.1037","Harrison 22e p.1093","Harrison 22e p.1095","Harrison 22e p.1096","Harrison 22e p.1254","Harrison 22e p.1255"],"crossLinks":["CELLULITIS","ERYSIPELAS"]},"PHARYNGITIS":{"name":"Acute Pharyngitis","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"300-302","clinicalPearls":["Sore throat is not synonymous with pharyngitis: it may also arise from submandibular/retropharyngeal/peritonsillar space infections, thyroiditis, gastroesophageal reflux, tumors, or postnasal drainage (Harrison 22e p.300).","Pharyngeal erythema is most commonly seen with viral infection (common cold, influenza), not bacterial disease (Harrison 22e p.300).","Pharyngeal exudate can be mimicked by Candida (cottage-cheese appearance, scrapes off leaving a bleeding surface) or leukoplakia (cannot be scraped off) — do not equate exudate with bacterial pharyngitis (Harrison 22e p.300).","GAS is the only common cause of sore throat that warrants antibiotics; most non-GAS pharyngitis is viral and self-limited (Harrison 22e p.300).","About 10% of adults with sore throat have GAS; in children the prevalence may reach ~35%, peaking at ages 5-15 years, and is higher in winter and early spring (Harrison 22e p.300).","Risk of GAS pharyngitis is elevated in health care and child care workers, teachers, parents of young children, and contacts of known strep cases; clinicians should watch for local outbreaks in military and institutional settings (Harrison 22e p.300).","GAS carriage is common (up to ~20% of schoolchildren, ~5% of adolescents/young adults); rapid antigen tests and culture cannot distinguish true infection from carriage (Harrison 22e p.301).","In adults meeting zero or one Centor criterion, a positive GAS test is very likely a false positive and/or represents carriage; indiscriminate testing of adults with sore throat is discouraged (Harrison 22e p.301).","Lower the threshold to test/treat GAS in patients with a history of acute rheumatic fever, documented strep exposure in the past week, those in a community strep epidemic, and diabetic or immunocompromised patients (Harrison 22e p.301).","In children the Centor criteria are less specific, so GAS pharyngitis should be confirmed by testing; children with pharyngitis but no viral signs (conjunctivitis, runny nose, cough, hoarseness, nonexudative oral lesions) warrant testing (Harrison 22e p.301).","In developed countries the prevalence of rheumatic fever is extremely low, and antibiotic treatment does not prevent poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis (Harrison 22e p.300).","Full-blown infectious mononucleosis (EBV) appears in only about one-fourth of cases and is a triad of clinical, hematologic, and serologic findings; new EBV causes 1-6% of pharyngitis in young adults and is rare over age 40 (Harrison 22e p.301).","Heterophil antibody is detectable in only ~40% of mononucleosis patients in the first week but in 80-90% by the third week, so an early negative test does not exclude EBV (Harrison 22e p.302).","Gonococcal pharyngitis is more often asymptomatic than symptomatic; suspect it with persistent sore throat unresponsive to presumptive strep treatment and relevant sexual exposure (Harrison 22e p.302).","Diphtheria produces only mild pharyngitis beneath its characteristic grayish pseudomembrane (Harrison 22e p.302).","Acute HIV infection causes sore throat in roughly half of cases; consider it when sore throat accompanies headache, fever, myalgias, lymphadenopathy, anorexia, and rash (Harrison 22e p.302)."],"pathophysiology":"Acute pharyngitis (symptoms generally present for days) is most often caused by respiratory viruses; group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (GAS, Streptococcus pyogenes) are a frequent bacterial cause, with other contributors including Neisseria gonorrhoeae, Epstein-Barr virus, HIV, non-group-A streptococci (groups C and G), Mycoplasma pneumoniae, Chlamydia pneumoniae, and Haemophilus influenzae. Complications of GAS pharyngitis arise from both suppurative spread (e.g., peritonsillar abscess, sinusitis, other invasive GAS infection) and nonsuppurative immune-mediated sequelae (acute rheumatic fever, poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, scarlet fever) (Harrison 22e p.300-301).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute infectious mononucleosis (EBV) — marked cervical adenopathy, exudative pharyngitis, palatine petechiae, gelatinous uvula, splenomegaly (Harrison 22e p.301-302)","Non-group-A streptococcal pharyngitis (group C and group G streptococci) (Harrison 22e p.302)","Gonococcal pharyngitis (Neisseria gonorrhoeae) (Harrison 22e p.302)","Mycoplasma pneumoniae pharyngitis (persistent sore throat with bronchopulmonary symptoms) (Harrison 22e p.300)","Chlamydia pneumoniae pharyngitis (Harrison 22e p.302)","Haemophilus influenzae pharyngitis (cough and otitis media) (Harrison 22e p.300)","Arcanobacterium haemolyticum (scarlatiniform rash) (Harrison 22e p.300)","Acute HIV infection (Harrison 22e p.302)"],"infectionMimics":["Acute epiglottitis/supraglottitis — sore throat with dysphagia, odynophagia, hoarseness, stridor or tachypnea, drooling; airway emergency (Harrison 22e p.302)","Peritonsillar abscess — fever, dysphagia, profound throat pain with drooling, trismus, 'hot potato voice', unilateral palate bulge and uvular deviation (Harrison 22e p.302)","Retropharyngeal abscess — post-URI in children with sore throat, dysphagia, deep neck pain, neck stiffness, trismus, drooling; severe cases with dyspnea/stridor (Harrison 22e p.302)","Submandibular abscess / Ludwig angina — swelling of the floor of the mouth displacing the tongue posteriorly, usually after dental infection or extraction (Harrison 22e p.302)","Lemierre syndrome — septic thrombophlebitis of the internal jugular vein with metastatic infection (Harrison 22e p.302)","Candida pharyngitis — cottage-cheese exudate that scrapes off leaving a bleeding surface (Harrison 22e p.300)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Gastroesophageal reflux / laryngopharyngeal reflux (Harrison 22e p.300)","Postnasal drainage (Harrison 22e p.300)","Head and neck tumors (Harrison 22e p.300)","Thyroiditis (Harrison 22e p.300)","Leukoplakia mimicking exudate (cannot be scraped off) (Harrison 22e p.300)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Rapid GAS antigen test: sensitivity ~80%, specificity ~95%; proper technique (swabbing tonsils/pharynx and any exudate or ulceration) is essential to sensitivity (Harrison 22e p.301)","Throat culture: single-swab sensitivity ~85-90%; can be falsely positive in carriers; not recommended for routine adult evaluation given 24-48 h delay (Harrison 22e p.301)","Heterophil antibody test for EBV mononucleosis (low early sensitivity, rises by week 3) (Harrison 22e p.302)","CBC for atypical lymphocytosis in mononucleosis (absolute lymphocytes >4000/uL or relative >50% with >10% atypical morphology) (Harrison 22e p.302)","CT imaging for suspected head and neck abscess (except obvious peritonsillar abscess) (Harrison 22e p.302)","Lateral neck radiograph in epiglottitis may show the 'thumb sign' (not required for diagnosis) (Harrison 22e p.302)"],"redFlags":["Shaking chills/rigors, neck swelling beyond lymphadenopathy, trouble swallowing, drooling, or symptoms persisting >5 days without improvement should prompt urgent re-evaluation (Harrison 22e p.301)","Stridor, dyspnea, hoarseness, or tripod/forward-leaning posture with drooling — suggests epiglottitis and impending airway obstruction (Harrison 22e p.302)","Non-GAS pharyngitis that is not resolving in a teenager/young adult with rigors, neck pain or swelling, or extrapharyngeal symptoms — suspect Lemierre syndrome (Harrison 22e p.302)","Trismus, 'hot potato voice', and unilateral palate bulge with uvular deviation — peritonsillar abscess (Harrison 22e p.302)","Grayish pharyngeal pseudomembrane in a traveler from an endemic area — suspect diphtheria (Harrison 22e p.300, 302)"],"prognosis":"Both streptococcal and nonstreptococcal pharyngitis should resolve within 3-5 days. Nonsuppurative complications are rare, and even streptococcal pharyngitis is self-limited in the vast majority of cases; outside the U.S., because complications are rare, some guidelines do not recommend routine GAS testing or antibiotic treatment of sore throat (Harrison 22e p.301).","pitfalls":["Prescribing antibiotics to the majority of adults with sore throat despite only ~10% having GAS — most cases are viral (Harrison 22e p.300).","Testing adults who meet zero or one Centor criterion: 40-60% of adults with pharyngitis fall here, and a positive test is highly likely to be false-positive or carriage (Harrison 22e p.301).","Relying on throat culture or rapid test to distinguish active infection from GAS carriage — neither can (Harrison 22e p.301).","Applying adult Centor criteria to children, in whom they are less specific (Harrison 22e p.301).","Mistaking Candida or leukoplakia for bacterial pharyngeal exudate (Harrison 22e p.300).","Attempting to examine or swab the posterior pharynx in suspected epiglottitis — this can provoke laryngospasm and should only be done in a controlled setting (Harrison 22e p.302).","Assuming antibiotic treatment of GAS prevents poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis — it does not (Harrison 22e p.300).","A negative early heterophil antibody test does not rule out EBV mononucleosis (Harrison 22e p.302)."],"severityClassification":"Centor criteria (4 findings, 1 point each): history of fever, absence of cough, tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy, and tonsillar exudate or swelling. For an adult pretest probability of ~10%, post-evaluation probability of GAS is ~2% at 0 points, ~3% at 1, ~8% at 2, ~19% at 3, and ~41% at 4 points (area under the curve 0.79). Conceptually: 0-1 criteria -> neither test nor treat; 2-3 -> rapid antigen test to guide management; 4 -> rapid test or empirical treatment. (Harrison 22e p.300-301).","references":["Harrison 22e p.300","Harrison 22e p.301","Harrison 22e p.302"],"crossLinks":["URTI","SINUSITIS","glomerulonephritis"]},"PROSTATITIS":{"name":"Acute Bacterial Prostatitis","system":"Genitourinary","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1087, 1089, 1132, 1136, 1186","clinicalPearls":["Acute bacterial prostatitis (ABP) typically presents as a severe illness with dysuria, urinary frequency, and pain in the prostatic, pelvic, or perineal region, and may be accompanied by fever, rigors, and/or bladder outlet obstruction (Harrison 22e p.1089).","The prostate is involved in the majority of febrile UTI cases in men; ABP should be specifically suspected when a man presents with UTI plus systemic symptoms but no other clear anatomic source such as pyelonephritis (Harrison 22e p.1089, p.1136).","Bacteriuria documented on urine culture is what distinguishes the less common bacterial prostatitis syndromes (acute and chronic) from the far more common chronic pelvic pain syndrome, which is NOT associated with bacteriuria and is NOT improved by antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.1089).","Men with febrile UTI may show an elevated serum prostate-specific antigen (PSA) and an enlarged prostate and seminal vesicles on ultrasound, supporting prostatic involvement; PSA is therefore not interpretable as a cancer marker during acute infection (Harrison 22e p.1089).","Prostatitis is largely unrepresented in UTI clinical trial data, so complicated-UTI practice guidelines cannot be assumed to apply to prostatitis; older literature suggests prolonged therapy with prostate-penetrating agents may be needed (Harrison 22e p.1087)."],"pathophysiology":"Prostatitis can complicate ascending cystitis or arise hematogenously, for example as a metastatic focus of Staphylococcus aureus bacteremia. Infectious prostatitis is almost always bacterial and is far less common than the noninfectious chronic pelvic pain syndrome. In general, most genitourinary infections in men ascend from the urethra to the bladder, with the prostate becoming involved as part of febrile UTI; less than 2% of UTIs are hematogenous, usually from virulent organisms such as S. aureus and Salmonella. Obstruction from prostatic hypertrophy after age 50 promotes urinary stasis and is a major reason UTI incidence in older men approaches that in women (Harrison 22e p.1087, p.1089, p.1131).","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic bacterial prostatitis (CBP) — presents insidiously as recurrent episodes of cystitis, sometimes with pelvic and perineal pain, usually without systemic signs of infection; should be suspected with recurrent UTI not in the context of catheterization (Harrison 22e p.1089, p.1136).","Pyelonephritis — another febrile genitourinary infection that must be excluded as the anatomic source before attributing systemic symptoms to ABP (Harrison 22e p.1136).","Acute cystitis in men — uncommon and warrants caution; urine culture should be obtained in all male UTI to help identify a prostatic focus (Harrison 22e p.1089)."],"infectionMimics":["Pyelonephritis — overlapping febrile UTI presentation; distinguished by flank/costovertebral angle pain and an upper-tract source (Harrison 22e p.1089).","Urethritis — urethral pain, pruritus, or discharge should prompt evaluation for sexually transmitted infections (Neisseria gonorrhoeae, Chlamydia trachomatis, Mycoplasma genitalium) rather than assuming prostatitis (Harrison 22e p.1087)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Chronic pelvic pain syndrome (formerly chronic prostatitis) — a noninfectious disorder that is the far more common cause of prostatic-type symptoms, with NO bacteriuria and NO response to antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.1089).","Benign prostatic hypertrophy with obstructive uropathy / urinary retention — can produce lower urinary tract symptoms and predispose to UTI (Harrison 22e p.1089, p.1131)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine culture in all male UTI — documenting bacteriuria differentiates bacterial prostatitis from chronic pelvic pain syndrome; dipstick and/or microscopy alone are not sufficient (Harrison 22e p.1089).","Cross-sectional imaging (CT or ultrasound) for men with febrile UTI, including the first episode, if not previously done, to evaluate for urinary tract abnormalities or obstruction (Harrison 22e p.1089).","Evaluation for urinary retention / prostatic focus in men with recurrent cystitis (Harrison 22e p.1089).","Urologic consultation referral if the diagnosis of UTI is unclear or UTI is recurrent (Harrison 22e p.1089)."],"redFlags":["Bladder outlet obstruction or urinary retention accompanying ABP (Harrison 22e p.1089).","Fever and rigors indicating a severe, systemic illness (Harrison 22e p.1089).","Identification of a prostatic abscess — should prompt urologic specialist consultation (Harrison 22e p.1136).","Symptoms predictive of surgically correctable disease in men with febrile UTI: urinary retention, early UTI recurrence, hematuria at follow-up, and voiding difficulties (Harrison 22e p.1089)."],"prognosis":"ABP is typically a severe acute illness but is amenable to therapy with prostate-penetrating antimicrobials. A prostatic abscess is a recognized complication requiring urologic intervention. Chronic bacterial prostatitis recurrences are not uncommon. Men with febrile UTI and none of the warning features (retention, early recurrence, hematuria, voiding difficulty) generally have normal upper and lower urinary tracts on urologic workup, implying a favorable structural prognosis (Harrison 22e p.1089, p.1136).","pitfalls":["Do not attribute prostatic-type symptoms to bacterial prostatitis without bacteriuria; absence of bacteriuria points to chronic pelvic pain syndrome, which will not respond to antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.1089).","Do not interpret an elevated PSA during febrile male UTI as a cancer marker — acute prostatic infection itself raises PSA and prostate/seminal vesicle size on imaging (Harrison 22e p.1089).","Do not assume complicated-UTI guideline regimens and durations apply to prostatitis, since prostatitis is largely absent from UTI trial data and may require prolonged prostate-penetrating therapy (Harrison 22e p.1087).","Do not overlook a prostatic focus or urinary retention in men presenting with recurrent cystitis (Harrison 22e p.1089)."],"severityClassification":"Prostatitis is categorized as acute bacterial prostatitis (ABP) versus chronic bacterial prostatitis (CBP). ABP is a typically severe illness with systemic features (fever, rigors, possible bladder outlet obstruction); CBP presents insidiously as recurrent cystitis with pelvic/perineal pain and typically without systemic signs, often managed in the ambulatory setting. Both are distinct from noninfectious chronic pelvic pain syndrome (Harrison 22e p.1089, p.1136).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1087","Harrison 22e p.1089","Harrison 22e p.1131","Harrison 22e p.1132","Harrison 22e p.1136","Harrison 22e p.1186"],"crossLinks":["CYSTITIS","COMPLICATED_UTI","PYELONEPHRITIS"]},"PULMONARY_TB":{"name":"Pulmonary Tuberculosis","system":"Respiratory / Tuberculosis","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Chapter 183 (Tuberculosis), pp.1380-1394","clinicalPearls":["Pulmonary TB is traditionally split into primary (soon after initial infection, often middle/lower lung zones, common in children) and postprimary/adult-type (reactivation or recent reinfection, apical-posterior upper lobes); molecular data show many adult cases in endemic areas reflect recent infection rather than old reactivation (p.1385).","Postprimary disease favours the apical and posterior segments of the upper lobes and the superior segments of the lower lobes, where higher local oxygen tension favours mycobacterial growth (p.1386).","Cough develops in up to ~90% of cases and is often nonproductive initially before becoming purulent; hemoptysis occurs in 20-30%, and constitutional symptoms (diurnal fever, night sweats, weight loss, anorexia, malaise) are typical but nonspecific (p.1386).","Absence of fever does not exclude TB; physical examination is frequently normal even with significant parenchymal disease, so reliance on exam findings can mislead (p.1386).","Xpert MTB/RIF is the WHO first-line diagnostic, simultaneously detecting M. tuberculosis and rifampin resistance in under 2 hours; overall pulmonary sensitivity ~85% (up to 98% in smear-positive, ~70% in smear-negative), specificity 98% (p.1391-1392).","A negative NAAT (Xpert/Ultra) does NOT exclude TB and requires further workup; conversely culture remains the gold standard for definitive diagnosis (p.1390-1393).","Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra adds sensitivity (notably in smear-negative/culture-positive cases and PLWH) but its 'trace calls' can detect nonviable bacilli; trace calls in recently treated patients may represent dead-bacillus DNA (p.1392).","At least one to two early-morning sputum specimens should be submitted for both AFB smear and mycobacterial culture; tissue for culture must NOT be placed in formaldehyde/preservative (p.1392).","In HIV with partially preserved immunity, pulmonary TB looks classic (upper-lobe infiltrates and cavitation); with CD4 <200/uL it shifts to a primary-like pattern with diffuse/subtle interstitial infiltrates, little or no cavitation, pleural effusion and intrathoracic adenopathy (p.1391).","Because HIV-associated TB cavitates less, such patients are often less infectious; PLWH with culture- and smear-positive disease may even have a normal chest radiograph (p.1382, p.1391-1393).","Urine lipoarabinomannan (LF-LAM) is a point-of-care adjunct for TB diagnosis specifically in PLWH with advanced disease or low CD4 counts, not a general test (p.1393).","MDR-TB is defined as resistance to at least isoniazid and rifampin; rifampin resistance (RR-TB) is used as a practical proxy for MDR; pre-XDR adds fluoroquinolone resistance, and XDR-TB adds resistance to a fluoroquinolone plus at least one additional group A drug (e.g., bedaquiline or linezolid) (p.1380-1382)."],"pathophysiology":"M. tuberculosis is an aerobic acid-fast bacillus whose mycolic-acid-rich cell wall confers acid fastness and low permeability (reducing antibiotic efficacy). Transmission is by airborne droplet nuclei (<5-10 um) from a person with infectious pulmonary or laryngeal TB; cavitary cases are most infectious (10^5-10^7 AFB/mL of sputum). Inhaled bacilli reaching the alveoli are phagocytosed by alveolar macrophages; the bacillus arrests phagosome maturation and phagosome-lysosome fusion, replicates, and (via the ESX-1/RD1 secretion system) ruptures the macrophage to infect newly recruited phagocytes. Around 2-4 weeks, cell-mediated (TH1/IFN-gamma-driven, CD4+ T-cell-dependent) macrophage activation and a delayed-type hypersensitivity tissue-damaging response develop; their balance determines disease form. Granulomas (tubercles) of activated macrophages, epithelioid and giant cells form, with central caseous (cheese-like) necrosis in which low oxygen and pH inhibit but do not kill bacilli. When the macrophage-activating response is weak, delayed hypersensitivity causes progressive caseous liquefaction, bronchial and vascular erosion, and cavity formation; bacilli multiply within cavities and are discharged into airways, driving transmission and bronchogenic spread of satellite lesions. Lymphohematogenous dissemination can seed distant organs (miliary disease, meningitis). The continuum from infection to disease is governed largely by host immune competence; only ~10% of immunocompetent infected persons progress to active disease over a lifetime, about half within ~18 months. (pp.1380-1385)","additionalDifferentials":["Bronchogenic carcinoma (lung cancer) - upper-lobe mass/cavity, hemoptysis, weight loss; patients with such radiographic findings often undergo bronchoscopy/biopsy to distinguish from TB (p.1393)","Nontuberculous mycobacterial pulmonary disease (e.g., M. avium complex, M. kansasii) - AFB-positive smear with limited CXR abnormality, favoured by absence of TB risk factors and presence of underlying chronic lung disease (p.1393)","Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis / aspergilloma colonising an old TB cavity - hemoptysis, weight loss, fatigue, often misdiagnosed as TB recurrence; Aspergillus IgG precipitins and a fungal ball within a cavity suggest the diagnosis (p.1390-1391)","Bacterial lung abscess / necrotising pneumonia (cavitary lesion with air-fluid level) (p.1386)"],"infectionMimics":["Nontuberculous mycobacteria (M. avium complex, M. kansasii) causing AFB-positive cavitary or nodular lung disease (p.1393)","Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis / aspergilloma in residual cavities mimicking TB relapse (p.1390-1391)","Bacterial lung abscess or necrotising pneumonia producing upper-lobe cavitation (p.1386)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Bronchogenic carcinoma presenting as a cavitary upper-lobe mass with hemoptysis and weight loss (p.1393)","Solitary pulmonary nodule - TB can present as one, overlapping with neoplastic causes (p.1393)","Sarcoidosis and other granulomatous diseases (caseating vs non-caseating granuloma distinction) (granuloma differential, p.1384)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra (next-generation NAAT with higher sensitivity in smear-negative and HIV-positive cases) (p.1392)","Truenat MTB / MTB Plus and Truenat MTB-Rif Dx - portable, battery-operated molecular tests for peripheral settings (p.1392)","Line probe assays (LPAs) for rapid detection of rifampin (rpoB) and isoniazid (katG, inhA) resistance, and second-line LPAs for fluoroquinolone/injectable resistance (p.1393)","Liquid culture (MGIT) as WHO reference standard; solid media (Lowenstein-Jensen, Middlebrook 7H10/7H11) require 4-8 weeks (p.1392)","Universal drug-susceptibility testing - at minimum rifampin DST on all initial isolates (p.1392-1393)","Targeted next-generation sequencing (tNGS) and whole-genome sequencing for comprehensive resistance profiling (p.1393)","Sputum induction with hypertonic saline when spontaneous sputum cannot be produced (p.1393)","Fiberoptic bronchoscopy with bronchial brushings, endobronchial/transbronchial biopsy, and bronchoalveolar lavage (submit for Xpert, culture, AFB smear) (p.1393)","Early-morning gastric lavage / induced sputum for children unable to expectorate (p.1393)","Urine lateral-flow lipoarabinomannan (LF-LAM) assay in advanced HIV (p.1393)","Chest CT to clarify equivocal plain-film findings (p.1393)","Tuberculin skin test (TST/PPD) and IFN-gamma release assays (IGRA: QuantiFERON-TB Gold/Plus, T-SPOT.TB) to identify TB infection - note both have low predictive value for who will progress and cannot distinguish infection from active disease (p.1393-1394)","M. tuberculosis antigen-based skin tests (TBSTs using ESAT-6/CFP-10) with accuracy similar to IGRA (p.1394)"],"redFlags":["Massive hemoptysis from erosion of a vessel in a cavity wall or rupture of a Rasmussen's aneurysm - potentially life-threatening (p.1386)","Extensive disease causing dyspnea progressing to acute respiratory distress syndrome (p.1386)","Features of hematogenous/miliary dissemination (miliary reticulonodular CXR, hepatosplenomegaly, choroidal tubercles) - high mortality if unrecognised (p.1389)","Meningismus, headache, altered sensorium or cranial nerve palsy suggesting tuberculous meningitis - uniformly fatal if untreated (p.1388-1389)","Advanced HIV with possible TB: NAAT-positive results warrant immediate treatment because diagnostic delay can be fatal (p.1391, p.1393)"],"prognosis":"Untreated, TB is often fatal: about one-third of patients die within 1 year of diagnosis, and historically ~70% (weighted mean) of smear-positive cases died within 10 years; paucibacillary smear-negative disease had a lower ~20% case-fatality at 5 years, and ~60% of 5-year survivors underwent spontaneous remission. With timely, proper drug-susceptible treatment the vast majority are cured. Improper therapy lowers mortality but generates chronic infectious, often drug-resistant cases. A substantial proportion of cured survivors suffer post-TB lung disease - chronic airflow impairment, dyspnea, residual cough, bronchiectasis, pulmonary hypertension, and predisposition to aspergilloma/chronic pulmonary aspergillosis in residual cavities. (pp.1383, 1386, 1390-1391)","pitfalls":["Treating on clinical/radiographic grounds alone: CXR has high sensitivity but poor specificity and no pattern (including a normal film) is pathognomonic in the HIV era - bacteriologic confirmation must always be pursued (p.1393).","Interpreting a negative Xpert/NAAT or negative smear as ruling out TB - smear microscopy sensitivity is only 40-60% and a negative molecular test still requires culture and further workup (p.1392-1393).","AFB microscopy cannot distinguish M. tuberculosis from nontuberculous mycobacteria, and urine/gastric specimens are prone to false negatives (low organism load) and false positives (commensal mycobacteria) (p.1392).","Relying on the chest exam: physical findings are of limited use and may be entirely normal in significant pulmonary TB (p.1386).","Missing diagnosis in atypical hosts (elderly nursing-home residents, teenagers, immunosuppressed/PLWH with lower-zone or noncavitary disease) (p.1391).","TST/IGRA pitfalls: false-negative TST in immunosuppression and overwhelming TB; false-positive from NTM or BCG; the TST 'boosting phenomenon' can mimic conversion; neither test distinguishes latent infection from active disease (p.1393-1394).","Putting tissue intended for culture into formaldehyde, which kills the organism and precludes culture (p.1392)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames severity primarily by disease form and host rather than a numeric score: primary vs postprimary (cavitary) pulmonary disease; pulmonary vs extrapulmonary vs disseminated (miliary) TB; and by drug-resistance category - drug-susceptible TB, rifampin-resistant (RR-TB), multidrug-resistant (MDR-TB: resistant to at least isoniazid and rifampin), pre-XDR-TB (MDR/RR plus fluoroquinolone resistance), and extensively drug-resistant (XDR-TB: MDR plus fluoroquinolone resistance and resistance to at least one additional group A drug such as bedaquiline or linezolid). Infectiousness grades with cavitation and sputum bacillary load (smear-positive cavitary > smear-negative/culture-positive > culture-negative/extrapulmonary, the last essentially noninfectious). (pp.1380-1386)","references":["Harrison 22e p.1380","Harrison 22e p.1381","Harrison 22e p.1382","Harrison 22e p.1383","Harrison 22e p.1384","Harrison 22e p.1385","Harrison 22e p.1386","Harrison 22e p.1387","Harrison 22e p.1388","Harrison 22e p.1389","Harrison 22e p.1390","Harrison 22e p.1391","Harrison 22e p.1392","Harrison 22e p.1393","Harrison 22e p.1394"],"crossLinks":["DISSEMINATED_TB","CNS_TB","lung_cancer","sarcoidosis"]},"PUO":{"name":"Pyrexia of Unknown Origin","system":"Systemic","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.22 (Mulders-Manders & Rovers), pp.149-154 (file pages 192-197)","clinicalPearls":["Current (post-Petersdorf) FUO definition requires all of: fever >=38.3C (101F) on at least two occasions, illness lasting at least 3 weeks, no known immunocompromised state, and a diagnosis still uncertain after thorough history, examination, and a defined set of obligatory investigations (p.149).","Inflammation of unknown origin (IUO) shares the FUO definition but lacks the temperature criterion (elevated CRP/ESR with normal temperature); its causes and workup are identical to FUO (p.149).","FUO usually reflects an ATYPICAL presentation of a COMMON disease rather than a rare disease; pattern recognition explains the higher diagnostic yield at expert centers (pp.150, 153).","The cornerstone of the workup is the search for potentially diagnostic clues (PDCs) - all localizing signs, symptoms, and abnormalities pointing toward a diagnosis - via repeated history, examination, and obligatory tests; further testing should be PDC-driven (pp.152-153).","Examination should pay special attention to the eyes, lymph nodes, temporal arteries, liver, spleen, prior surgical sites, the entire skin surface, and mucous membranes (p.152).","History should capture fever pattern (continuous vs recurrent) and duration, prior medical history, current/recent drugs, family and sexual history, country of origin, recent and remote travel, environmental/hobby exposures, and animal contacts (p.152).","Giant cell arteritis is notably prevalent among FUO patients, with rates up to 17% in the elderly; temporal artery biopsy is still recommended for patients >=55 years (p.153).","FUO with headache should prompt CSF microbiologic examination for herpes simplex virus (especially type 2), Cryptococcus neoformans/gattii, and M. tuberculosis (p.153).","In patients with recurrent/episodic FUO, ask the patient to return DURING a febrile episode; further tests should be done during an inflammatory episode since abnormalities may be absent between episodes (pp.152-153).","Geography shapes the differential: infections (especially TB) dominate non-Western cohorts, while noninfectious inflammatory diseases (NIIDs) are the most common cause in Western cohorts, accounting for up to one-third of cases (p.150).","Investigation of cryoglobulins is a useful low-cost screening test in FUO given the frequent absence of specific symptoms (p.153)."],"pathophysiology":"FUO is a syndrome, not a single disease - a final prolonged febrile illness in which intensive standard evaluation has failed to localize a cause. Etiologies cluster into four groups: infections, noninfectious inflammatory diseases (NIIDs: autoimmune, autoinflammatory, granulomatous, and vasculitides), malignancy, and miscellaneous, with a substantial undiagnosed fraction. The spectrum has shifted over time: intraabdominal abscesses, tumors, and infective endocarditis have declined as CT, ultrasound, blood culture, and echocardiography improved, while entities such as acute HIV and autoinflammatory syndromes are now recognized. NIIDs may precede their typical clinical or laboratory features by months, requiring prolonged observation. The 'FUO paradox' describes how, despite better imaging (CT/MRI/PET-CT), the undiagnosed proportion is now higher (>50% in some Western cohorts) because most febrile illnesses are diagnosed before 3 weeks elapse, leaving only the most difficult cases to meet FUO criteria (pp.149-150).","additionalDifferentials":["Infective endocarditis (including culture-negative endocarditis from fastidious organisms - nutritionally variant streptococci, HACEK group, Coxiella burnetii, Tropheryma whipplei, Bartonella) - one of the more common infectious causes (pp.150-151)","Extrapulmonary and miliary tuberculosis - up to half of infections in non-Western FUO are M. tuberculosis; miliary TB is especially hard to diagnose (pp.150, 153)","Diverticulitis (p.150)","Vertebral osteomyelitis / spondylodiscitis (pp.150-151)","Q fever (Coxiella burnetii) - consider with rural exposure, animal contact, valve disease, aortic aneurysm, or vascular prosthesis; test by immunofluorescence assay (pp.150-151)","Whipple disease (Tropheryma whipplei) - consider with unexplained CNS, GI, or joint symptoms; PCR on stool and blood early (p.150)","Intraabdominal / hepatic / renal / pelvic abscesses (p.151)","Endemic infections after relevant travel/residence: malaria, visceral leishmaniasis, histoplasmosis, coccidioidomycosis (p.150)","Acute HIV infection (p.150)","Adult-onset Still's disease (NIID, relatively common in FUO) (p.150)","Large-vessel vasculitis and giant cell arteritis / polymyalgia rheumatica (pp.150, 153)","Systemic lupus erythematosus (p.150)","Sarcoidosis (p.150)","Familial Mediterranean fever and other autoinflammatory syndromes (rare except in specific regions) (p.150)","Schnitzler's syndrome - urticaria, bone pain, monoclonal gammopathy (p.150)","VEXAS syndrome - adult-onset, middle-aged/elderly males, recurrent inflammation, chondritis, skin lesions, lung disease, venous thrombosis, arthritis, MDS; bone-marrow vacuoles in myeloid precursors; somatic UBA1 variants (p.150)","Malignant lymphoma - by far the most common neoplastic cause; fever may precede detectable lymphadenopathy (p.150)","Common solid tumors causing fever: breast, colon, hepatocellular, lung, pancreatic, renal cell carcinoma (p.151)"],"infectionMimics":["Culture-negative endocarditis - sterile cultures may simply reflect ongoing antibiotic therapy rather than a noninfectious cause (pp.151-152)","Marantic (nonbacterial thrombotic) endocarditis - sterile, paraneoplastic, especially with adenocarcinomas, mimics infective endocarditis (p.151)","Sterile endocarditis of SLE and antiphospholipid syndrome (p.151)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Drug-induced fever - virtually any drug, even after long-term use; common culprits include allopurinol, carbamazepine, lamotrigine, phenytoin, sulfasalazine, furosemide, and antimicrobials (sulfonamides, minocycline, vancomycin, beta-lactams, isoniazid); fever persisting >72h after stopping the drug makes it an unlikely cause (pp.150, 153)","DRESS / drug-induced hypersensitivity syndrome - often with eosinophilia and extensive lymphadenopathy (p.150)","Benign or exercise-induced hyperthermia - elevated temperature WITHOUT raised CRP/ESR or other inflammation; may be postinfectious (triggers similar to chronic fatigue syndrome: EBV, Q fever, COVID-19) (p.150)","Factitious fever - artificially induced (e.g., IV injection of contaminated water); suspect with access to medical equipment (p.150)","Fraudulent fever - patient is normothermic but manipulates the thermometer; simultaneous multi-site measurement (rectum/ear/mouth) and pulse-temperature dissociation are clues (p.150)","Central thermoregulatory disorders - brain tumor, stroke, encephalitis, hypothalamic dysfunction (p.151)","Peripheral thermoregulatory causes - hyperthyroidism, pheochromocytoma, heat stroke (p.151)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Obligatory baseline panel: ESR and CRP, platelet count, leukocyte count with differential, hemoglobin, electrolytes, creatinine, total protein, alkaline phosphatase, ALT, AST, LDH, creatine kinase, ferritin, antinuclear antibodies, rheumatoid factor, protein electrophoresis, urinalysis, three blood cultures, urine culture, chest x-ray, abdominal ultrasonography, and TST or IGRA (p.149)","Cryoglobulins - useful low-cost screening test (p.153)","Fundoscopy by an ophthalmologist - early, to exclude retinal vasculitis when PDCs are absent or misleading (p.153)","18F-FDG-PET/CT - established next step when PDC-guided tests are unrevealing; ~50% total diagnostic yield; best performed during active inflammation (elevated CRP/ESR or fever) and before glucocorticoids; outperforms gallium and labeled-leukocyte scintigraphy (pp.152-154)","Gallium-67 citrate or 111In/99mTc-labeled leukocyte scintigraphy - alternatives when PET/CT unavailable (lower yield; gallium localizes ~1/3, leukocyte ~1/5 of cases) (p.154)","Whole-body MRI - one study reported yield comparable to PET/CT (needs confirmation) (p.154)","Echocardiography - low yield without PDCs; reserve for high suspicion of endocarditis (p.153)","Liver biopsy (acid-fast smear, culture, PCR) - highest yield if miliary TB suspected; bone marrow / lymph node biopsies are alternatives (p.153)","Mycobacterial PCR alongside cultures - may shorten time to TB diagnosis (p.153)","Specialized blood cultures held long enough for fastidious organisms (e.g., HACEK); inform the lab and use special media for Histoplasma or Legionella; >3 blood cultures or >1 urine culture is useless without PDCs (p.153)","Q fever serology by immunofluorescence assay; T. whipplei PCR on stool/blood (pp.150-151)","Temporal artery biopsy in patients >=55 years for suspected giant cell arteritis (p.153)","CSF examination (HSV especially type 2, Cryptococcus, M. tuberculosis) when FUO is accompanied by headache (p.153)","Later-stage PDC-driven biopsies for pathology and/or microbiology (p.154)"],"redFlags":["Clinical deterioration without new PDCs - the trigger to escalate workup (rather than wait) and the situation where empirical therapy may be justified (pp.152-153)","Hemodynamic instability or neutropenia - good indications for empirical antibiotic therapy (p.155)","Granulomatous disease on liver or bone marrow biopsy - should prompt (re)consideration of miliary tuberculosis (p.153)","Early phase of evaluation carries the highest FUO-related mortality risk (deaths occurred during index admission in cohort data; intravascular lymphoma and pneumonia found at autopsy) (p.154)"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends chiefly on the underlying diagnosis. When FUO remains unexplained after extensive evaluation, outlook is favorable: large cohorts report high rates of spontaneous fever resolution and mortality of 8% or less over several years of follow-up. The greatest mortality risk is during the early diagnostic phase. A normal 18F-FDG-PET/CT is associated with higher rates of spontaneous resolution and may help predict who will resolve. A symptom duration >12 months and a normal PET/CT both lower the chance of ever reaching a final diagnosis (pp.150, 154).","pitfalls":["Starting empirical therapeutic trials before a diagnosis - should be AVOIDED except when the patient is rapidly deteriorating, because most undiagnosed patients resolve spontaneously (p.154).","Continuing antibiotics/glucocorticoids during workup - they mask disease: cultures become unreliable and glucocorticoids shrink lymph nodes regardless of cause; stop them before further testing (p.152).","Early empirical glucocorticoids - can mask fever while infection or lymphoma spreads and reduce the chance of diagnosing a treatable malignancy; avoid until infection and lymphoma are sufficiently excluded (p.155).","Ordering more screening tests without PDCs - low yield; immunologic and microbiologic serologies without PDCs more often give false-positives; better to wait for new PDCs unless the patient deteriorates (pp.153-154).","Over-culturing - performing >3 blood cultures or >1 urine culture is useless absent PDCs (p.153).","Failing to recognize PDCs already present in available information is a frequent cause of diagnostic delay (p.154).","Interpreting a negative TST/IGRA as excluding TB - it does not; results may be indeterminate in miliary TB, malnutrition, immunosuppression, or with anti-IFN-gamma autoantibodies (p.153).","Misreading PET/CT - physiologic FDG uptake in brain, heart, bowel, kidneys, and bladder can obscure pathology (e.g., cardiac uptake masking endocarditis - mitigate with a pre-scan low-carbohydrate diet) (p.153).","Not excluding factitious/fraudulent fever and drug fever early, especially when inflammatory markers are normal (pp.150, 153)."],"severityClassification":"FUO categories (by host/setting), each requiring a distinct approach: (1) classic FUO - the immunocompetent adult per the formal definition; (2) nosocomial FUO; (3) neutropenic FUO; and (4) HIV-associated FUO. The contemporary Harrison definition explicitly EXCLUDES immunocompromised states, which need a different, more aggressive approach. Etiologies are grouped into four diagnostic categories - infections, noninfectious inflammatory diseases (NIIDs), malignancy, and miscellaneous - with a large 'no diagnosis' fraction (pp.149-150).","references":["Harrison 22e p.149","Harrison 22e p.150","Harrison 22e p.151","Harrison 22e p.152","Harrison 22e p.153","Harrison 22e p.154","Harrison 22e p.155"],"crossLinks":["DISSEMINATED_TB","IE","malignancy_b","vasculitis","sarcoidosis"]},"PYELONEPHRITIS":{"name":"Acute Pyelonephritis","system":"Genitourinary","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1088-1094, 1133-1137 (Chap. 140); 1329 (Chap. 137); 2493 (Chap. 330)","clinicalPearls":["Fever is the principal feature distinguishing pyelonephritis from simple cystitis; presence of fever, back/flank pain, rigors, nausea or vomiting signals infection that has extended beyond the bladder (Harrison 22e p.1086-1090, 1133).","Severity spans a wide spectrum: mild disease may present only with low-grade fever with or without costovertebral angle pain, whereas severe disease manifests with high fever, rigors, nausea, vomiting, and flank pain; onset is generally acute and may or may not be preceded by cystitis symptoms (Harrison 22e p.1090).","Pyelonephritis represents tissue-invasive disease and patients are often systemically ill, so the threat of progression to bacteremia and urosepsis is real (Harrison 22e p.1090, 1137, 1329).","In a woman presenting with at least one localizing urinary symptom (dysuria, frequency, hematuria, or back pain) and no complicating factors, the combined probability of acute cystitis or pyelonephritis is about 50% (Harrison 22e p.1133).","Men with febrile UTI should undergo upper-tract imaging (CT or ultrasound) if not done previously, to evaluate for urinary tract abnormality or obstruction, and elevated PSA with enlarged prostate/seminal vesicles suggests prostatic involvement (Harrison 22e p.1132).","Pregnant patients are at unusually high risk of developing pyelonephritis, which can adversely affect pregnancy outcome (preterm delivery, low birth weight); this underlies routine prenatal screening for asymptomatic bacteriuria (Harrison 22e p.1086, 1329)."],"pathophysiology":"Pyelonephritis usually develops as an ascending infection: uropathogens move from the urethra to the bladder and then continue up the ureter to the renal parenchyma, which is the route for most renal parenchymal infection. Its microbiology therefore mirrors that of acute cystitis. A minority arises hematogenously (<2% of UTIs overall), typically with relatively virulent organisms such as Staphylococcus aureus and Salmonella; isolation of either from urine should prompt consideration of concomitant bacteremia, and hematogenous seeding can produce focal renal abscesses. Anatomic factors causing urinary obstruction or retention (congenital anomalies, cystocele, pelvic-organ prolapse, neurogenic bladder, benign prostatic hypertrophy) impede the protective flushing action of micturition and predispose to renal involvement. A foreign body such as a stone or catheter provides an inert surface for colonization (Harrison 22e p.1088, 1130-1131).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute prostatitis in men presenting with febrile UTI (Harrison 22e p.1137)","Complicated UTI (fever in a patient with structural/functional urinary abnormality, immunocompromise, or instrumentation) (Harrison 22e p.1133-1134)","Sexually transmitted infection mimicking UTI, particularly Chlamydia trachomatis cervicitis/urethritis (Harrison 22e p.1133-1134)","Renal/ureteral calculus with obstruction (renal colic) presenting with flank pain and pyuria (Harrison 22e p.2493)"],"infectionMimics":["Acute prostatitis (Harrison 22e p.1137)","Cervicitis or urethritis due to Chlamydia trachomatis or Neisseria gonorrhoeae (Harrison 22e p.1133-1134)","Vaginitis (Candida albicans, Trichomonas vaginalis) or herpetic urethritis causing dysuria (Harrison 22e p.1133-1134)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Renal/ureteral stone with proximal obstruction, which may present exactly as acute pyelonephritis (Harrison 22e p.2493)","Interstitial cystitis or noninfectious vulvovaginal irritation causing dysuria (Harrison 22e p.1133-1134)","Sloughed renal papilla or blood clot causing ureteral obstruction without infection (Harrison 22e p.2493)","Musculoskeletal flank/back pain, herpes zoster, abdominal aortic aneurysm, duodenal ulcer (differential for flank pain) (Harrison 22e p.2493)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Upper-tract imaging (CT or renal ultrasound) to evaluate for obstruction, abscess, or anatomic abnormality, especially in men, in patients not responding within 72 hours, or when complicated infection is suspected (Harrison 22e p.1132, 1137)","Urine dipstick (nitrite, leukocyte esterase) and microscopic urinalysis as point-of-care tests; note nitrites detect only Enterobacterales and may be falsely negative with non-Enterobacterales organisms (Harrison 22e p.1134-1135)","Imaging for gas in renal/perinephric tissues when emphysematous pyelonephritis is suspected in a diabetic patient (Harrison 22e p.1090)"],"redFlags":["Acute papillary necrosis with sloughed papillae obstructing the ureter, producing obstructive uropathy that requires intervention to relieve obstruction and preserve renal function (Harrison 22e p.1090, 1137)","UTI in the setting of ureteral obstruction is a medical emergency requiring immediate restoration of drainage by ureteral stent or percutaneous nephrostomy (Harrison 22e p.2493)","Emphysematous pyelonephritis: gas production in renal and perinephric tissues, occurring almost exclusively in patients with poorly controlled diabetes; a particularly severe form (Harrison 22e p.1090)","Continued fever and/or bacteremia despite antibacterial therapy should raise suspicion for intraparenchymal, perinephric, or renal cortical abscess (Harrison 22e p.1090, 1137)","Failure of clinical response within 72 hours warrants evaluation for an uncontrolled source of infection (Harrison 22e p.1137)","Persistently elevated/increasing fever, flank pain, and rising neutrophil counts should prompt evaluation for intrarenal/perinephric abscess and/or obstruction (Harrison 22e p.1329)","Bilateral papillary necrosis may present first as a rapid rise in serum creatinine (Harrison 22e p.1090)"],"prognosis":"Most pyelonephritis treated effectively resolves, though fever may take 5-7 days to clear completely even with appropriate therapy. Renal parenchymal damage and loss of renal function are uncommon and occur primarily in association with urinary obstruction (preexisting, or, rarely, de novo in diabetic patients with papillary necrosis). Chronic bacterial pyelonephritis is rarely a cause of chronic renal failure because it seldom affects both kidneys. Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis arises from chronic obstruction (often staghorn calculus) plus chronic infection causing suppurative destruction of renal tissue and frequently requires nephrectomy (Harrison 22e p.1090, 1137, 1329, 2493).","pitfalls":["Treating presumed UTI when the true cause is an STI (e.g., C. trachomatis); vaginal discharge should prompt STI consideration since symptoms alone do not reliably distinguish them (Harrison 22e p.1133-1134).","Stopping the workup at an abnormal urinalysis in a catheterized or febrile patient: pyuria and bacteriuria are expected with catheterization and do not by themselves establish UTI as the cause of fever (Harrison 22e p.1133-1135).","Mistaking an obstructing stone with proximal infection for uncomplicated pyelonephritis and missing the need for emergent drainage (Harrison 22e p.2493).","Misattributing xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis to renal malignancy because its mass effect can mimic tumor (Harrison 22e p.1090).","Delayed/withheld antimicrobial therapy in true UTI carries a small increased risk of progression to pyelonephritis (Harrison 22e p.1136)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison describes a clinical spectrum rather than a formal score: mild pyelonephritis (low-grade fever, with or without lower back or costovertebral angle pain) versus severe pyelonephritis (high fever, rigors, nausea, vomiting, flank pain). Distinct severe/complicated entities include emphysematous pyelonephritis (gas-forming, almost exclusively in poorly controlled diabetes), xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis (chronic obstruction plus infection causing suppurative renal destruction), and pyelonephritis complicated by papillary necrosis with obstruction or by intrarenal/perinephric abscess. Acute pyelonephritis is one of the systemic UTI presentations that may be grouped as urosepsis or febrile UTI, with or without bacteremia (Harrison 22e p.1090, 1329).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1086","Harrison 22e p.1088","Harrison 22e p.1090","Harrison 22e p.1130","Harrison 22e p.1132","Harrison 22e p.1133","Harrison 22e p.1134","Harrison 22e p.1135","Harrison 22e p.1136","Harrison 22e p.1137","Harrison 22e p.1329","Harrison 22e p.2493"],"crossLinks":["COMPLICATED_UTI","CYSTITIS","renal_colic","SEPSIS"]},"RICKETTSIAL_FEVER":{"name":"Rickettsial Fever (Spotted Fever Group)","system":"Tropical Fever","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.137-141 (Ch.21 Fever and Rash); p.1454-1459 (Ch.192 Rickettsial Diseases); p.1488 (Ch.189 Leptospirosis differential)","clinicalPearls":["All acute rickettsial illnesses look alike for roughly the first 5 days: fever, headache, and myalgias, often with nausea, vomiting, or cough; the distinguishing features (rash, eschar, pneumonitis, meningoencephalitis) emerge only as the illness progresses (p.1454).","An inoculation eschar (tache noire) is a necrotic, often painless black-crusted lesion with a surrounding erythematous halo at the bite site; it can appear during the incubation period and is common in Mediterranean spotted fever (~50%), African tick-bite fever (~90%), and rickettsialpox (~90%), but is essentially absent in classic RMSF (<1%) and in epidemic/murine typhus (p.1455).","African tick-bite fever (R. africae) is the rickettsiosis most often imported into Europe and North America via travelers returning from sub-Saharan Africa; because Amblyomma ticks feed in groups, multiple eschars and regional lymphadenopathy are characteristic, and rash may be vesicular, sparse, or absent (p.1457-1458).","In RMSF the rash is centrifugal and evolving: erythematous macules begin on wrists and ankles, spread to extremities and trunk, and only later involve palms and soles; macules progress to non-blanching petechiae as vascular damage advances (p.1456).","Palm/sole involvement and the classic centripetal/centrifugal spread are late and inconsistent: in RMSF palms/soles are involved after day 5 in only ~43% and never in 18-64% of cases (p.1457).","The full classic triad of fever, rash, and recalled tick exposure is present in only ~3% of RMSF patients during the first 3 days, so its absence does not exclude the diagnosis (p.1457).","Characteristic supportive labs across rickettsioses are thrombocytopenia, a normal or low (not elevated) WBC count, mildly elevated hepatic aminotransferases, and hyponatremia (p.1454-1456); hyponatremia in RMSF (~56%) reflects appropriate ADH secretion in a hypovolemic state (p.1457).","Spotted fever group serology cross-reacts among species, so a positive titer confirms a SFG rickettsiosis but generally cannot identify the specific organism (p.1458)."],"pathophysiology":"Rickettsiae and Orientia are obligately intracellular gram-negative coccobacilli that primarily infect vascular endothelial cells. After tick saliva inoculates organisms into the dermis (in RMSF after roughly 6 h of feeding), they spread lymphohematogenously and infect contiguous foci of endothelium throughout the body, producing a widespread infectious vasculitis with increased vascular permeability. The resulting edema, hypovolemia, and tissue ischemia (rather than occlusive thrombosis) drive the organ injury: prerenal azotemia, noncardiogenic pulmonary edema, and CNS dysfunction. Platelet consumption causes thrombocytopenia in about a third to half of RMSF cases, but frank DIC with hypofibrinogenemia is rare; the coagulation changes appear to be homeostatic responses to endothelial injury forming nonocclusive hemostatic plugs (p.1454, p.1456).","additionalDifferentials":["Human monocytotropic ehrlichiosis (Ehrlichia chaffeensis) and human granulocytotropic anaplasmosis (Anaplasma phagocytophilum) - tick-borne, often rashless, share thrombocytopenia/leukopenia/transaminitis (p.1455-1457)","Epidemic (louse-borne) typhus (R. prowazekii) and recrudescent Brill-Zinsser disease (p.1458)","Murine (endemic) typhus (R. typhi) (p.1458-1459)","Q fever (Coxiella burnetii) (p.1455)"],"infectionMimics":["Influenza and enteroviral infection (mimic early rashless RMSF) (p.1457)","Infectious mononucleosis (p.1457)","Viral hepatitis (p.1457)","Leptospirosis (p.1457; shares epidemiologic and clinical features, dual infection reported, p.1488)","Typhoid fever (epidemic typhus is frequently misdiagnosed as typhoid in tropical countries) (p.1457-1458)","Gram-negative or gram-positive bacterial sepsis / septic shock (p.1457)","Meningococcemia and disseminated gonococcal infection (p.1457)","Secondary syphilis (p.1457)","Measles (rubeola) and rubella (p.1457)","Dengue and hantavirus infection (share epidemiologic and clinical features) (p.1488)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Drug hypersensitivity reaction (p.1457)","Immune (idiopathic) thrombocytopenic purpura (p.1457)","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (p.1457)","Kawasaki syndrome (p.1457)","Immune complex vasculitis (p.1457)","Toxic shock syndrome (p.1457)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Indirect immunofluorescence assay (IFA) for SFG IgG - the most common confirmatory test; sensitivity 89-100% and specificity 99-100%, but a reactive titer (>=64) is not detectable until 7-10 days after onset and is usually negative at presentation (p.1458)","Immunohistologic examination or PCR of a 3-mm punch biopsy of a rash lesion - the only test useful during acute illness (immunohistology ~70% sensitive, 100% specific; PCR likely higher) (p.1458)","PCR of eschar biopsy or eschar swab, or skin biopsy, for SFG rickettsiae (p.1458)","Note: PCR of peripheral blood is NOT adequately sensitive because circulating organism quantities are low (p.1458)","Platelet count, WBC count, hepatic aminotransferases, serum sodium, and renal function as supportive clues (p.1454-1457)","CSF analysis if meningoencephalitis suspected (typically 10-100 cells/uL, mononuclear predominance, normal glucose, mildly elevated protein) (p.1456-1457)"],"redFlags":["Hypotension/septic-shock presentation and multiorgan failure (p.1454, p.1457)","Noncardiogenic pulmonary edema / ARDS requiring mechanical ventilation (RMSF: ARDS ~12%) (p.1456-1457)","Encephalitis - confusion, lethargy, stupor, delirium, ataxia, seizures, or coma (p.1456-1457)","Acute kidney injury from acute tubular necrosis in shock (p.1457)","Skin necrosis, peripheral gangrene, or purpura fulminans (severe MSF) (p.1454; p.1488 / Ch.21)","Gastrointestinal hemorrhage; severe anemia requiring transfusion (p.1457)","Fulminant RMSF (fatal within 5 days), especially in male patients with G6PD deficiency (p.1457)","Extremes of age (children <10 years, adults >70 years) and immunosuppression - higher case-fatality (p.1456-1457)"],"prognosis":"Outcome is strongly time-dependent: untreated RMSF carries mortality of roughly 20-25% (historic) with confirmed-case fatality around 9-10% where R. rickettsii is the sole agent, versus ~3-5% when treated early; delayed recognition and treatment markedly increase death. For unclear reasons RMSF case-fatality approaches 50% in Mexico and Brazil. Severe Mediterranean spotted fever has mortality near 50% without treatment but improves dramatically with even a single day of appropriate therapy. Untreated epidemic typhus historically killed up to 60% and still exceeds 10-15% in outbreaks. Many survivors of severe RMSF recover fully, but permanent neurologic deficits and gangrene requiring amputation can follow severe illness (p.1456-1457; p.1488 / Ch.21).","pitfalls":["Waiting for serology before treating: SFG serology is typically negative at presentation and only turns positive at 7-10 days, so therapy must be started empirically on clinical and epidemiologic suspicion and never delayed for a confirmatory titer (p.1454, p.1458).","Excluding the diagnosis because there is no rash or no recalled tick bite: ~10-15% of RMSF patients never develop a rash, rash is present in only ~14% on day 1 and ~49% by day 3, and only ~60% recall a tick bite (p.1455-1457).","Treating a positive early SFG titer as proof of current disease: in areas with prevalent low-virulence SFG species (e.g., R. amblyommatis), a positive titer in the first 5-6 days often reflects preexisting cross-reactive antibody, not the acute illness (p.1456, p.1458).","Misattributing abdominal pain/vomiting to enterocolitis (has led to unnecessary laparotomy) or CNS findings to primary meningoencephalitis (p.1457).","Using ineffective or harmful agents: beta-lactams, aminoglycosides, and erythromycin have no role in RMSF, and sulfa drugs are associated with worse outcomes than no treatment (p.1458).","Missing the eschar: it can be small, painless, and located in skinfolds/scalp and frequently goes unnoticed by patient and clinician, yet careful skin examination for an eschar is a key diagnostic clue (p.1454-1459)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not give a formal grading scale, but stratifies along a spectrum: (1) self-limited febrile illness that resolves without evolution; (2) classic disease with rash and/or eschar; and (3) severe/complicated disease with organ involvement - pneumonitis/ARDS, meningoencephalitis, acute kidney injury, hemorrhage/skin necrosis/gangrene, sepsis/septic shock, and (rarely) fulminant RMSF fatal within 5 days. Higher-risk groups include children <10 years, adults >70 years, the immunosuppressed, and (for fulminant RMSF) males with G6PD deficiency (p.1454-1457).","references":["Harrison 22e p.137","Harrison 22e p.138","Harrison 22e p.139","Harrison 22e p.141","Harrison 22e p.1454","Harrison 22e p.1455","Harrison 22e p.1456","Harrison 22e p.1457","Harrison 22e p.1458","Harrison 22e p.1459","Harrison 22e p.1488"],"crossLinks":["SCRUB_TYPHUS","DENGUE","LEPTOSPIROSIS","MALARIA","ENTERIC_FEVER"]},"SBP":{"name":"Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis","system":"Hepatobiliary / GI","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.329-330, 372-373, 1069-1072, 2715-2716","clinicalPearls":["An ascitic-fluid PMN (neutrophil) count of at least 250/microL is diagnostic of SBP, irrespective of whether cultures grow an organism (Harrison 22e p.329, 372, 1070).","Fever is the single most common manifestation, present in roughly 80% of cases; abdominal pain, acute onset, and peritoneal signs aid diagnosis but their absence does not rule SBP out (Harrison 22e p.1070).","Abdominal tenderness is found in only about 40% of patients and rebound tenderness is uncommon, so a benign abdomen does not exclude the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.329).","SBP can present with no abdominal symptoms at all, instead manifesting as new or worsening hepatic encephalopathy, altered mental status, jaundice, or acute kidney injury without another explanation (Harrison 22e p.329, 1070, 2715).","In any cirrhotic patient hospitalized with ascites who deteriorates or shows signs of infection, a diagnostic paracentesis is mandatory; tapping within 12 hours of admission lowers mortality through early SBP detection (Harrison 22e p.329, 2715).","Ascitic fluid should be injected directly into blood-culture bottles at the bedside to improve yield, and blood cultures drawn simultaneously, since the organism burden in the fluid is low and bacteremia frequently coexists (Harrison 22e p.329, 1070).","Culture samples should be obtained before antibiotics are started to maximize recovery of the organism (Harrison 22e p.1070).","SBP is almost always monomicrobial; a single isolate is the rule (Harrison 22e p.329, 1070).","Gram stain of ascitic fluid is frequently negative in SBP and a negative result should not deter treatment (Harrison 22e p.1070).","SBP virtually always arises on a background of preexisting ascites, which predates the infection (Harrison 22e p.1070).","A repeat paracentesis after 48 hours of therapy should confirm at least a 25% fall in the ascitic PMN count from baseline; failure to fall, or persistent ascitic leukocytosis after treatment, should prompt a search for an alternative or secondary diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.329, 1071)."],"pathophysiology":"SBP is spontaneous infection of ascitic fluid in the absence of an intraabdominal surgical source. The presumed mechanism is bacterial translocation: gut flora cross an edematous, leaky intestinal wall into mesenteric lymph nodes, producing bacteremia that seeds the ascites, which is a good growth medium. Susceptibility in cirrhosis reflects impaired hepatic filtration from a diseased liver and altered portal circulation, low ascitic complement levels (lower than in non-cirrhotic ascites), and diminished opsonic and phagocytic function of PMNs in advanced liver disease. Cirrhosis is also associated with gut dysbiosis (increased Enterobacteriaceae), small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth, reduced Paneth-cell defensins, decreased intestinal motility, reduced pancreatobiliary secretions, and portal-hypertensive enteropathy, all of which promote pathologic translocation. SBP occurs in up to ~30% of hospitalized cirrhotics with ascites but is an uncommon event overall, affecting 10% or fewer of cirrhotic patients. (Harrison 22e p.1070, 2715)","additionalDifferentials":["Secondary bacterial peritonitis from a perforated or inflamed viscus or intraabdominal abscess (Harrison 22e p.329, 1071)","Hepatic encephalopathy from other precipitants (GI bleed, volume depletion, hyponatremia, constipation) when SBP presents as altered mentation (Harrison 22e p.2716-2717)","Acute kidney injury / hepatorenal syndrome from non-infectious causes when SBP presents as renal deterioration (Harrison 22e p.329, 2716)"],"infectionMimics":["Secondary bacterial peritonitis (perforated viscus, ruptured appendix/diverticulum/ulcer, intraabdominal abscess) (Harrison 22e p.1071-1072)","Spontaneous bacterial empyema complicating hepatic hydrothorax (Harrison 22e p.330)","Tuberculous peritonitis (ascitic lymphocytosis; can complicate or mimic infected ascites) (Harrison 22e p.373)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Peritoneal carcinomatosis / malignant ascites (low-SAAG ascites; SBP is rare in malignant ascites) (Harrison 22e p.372-373)","Pancreatic ascites (high ascitic amylase, typically >1000) (Harrison 22e p.373)","Worsening uncomplicated tense ascites with increased girth but no infection (Harrison 22e p.329)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Ascitic fluid PMN/neutrophil count (>=250/microL is diagnostic) (Harrison 22e p.329, 1070)","Ascitic fluid inoculated into blood-culture bottles at the bedside (Harrison 22e p.329, 1070)","Gram stain of ascitic fluid (often negative; does not exclude SBP) (Harrison 22e p.1070)","Serum-ascites albumin gradient (SAAG) and ascitic total protein to characterize the underlying ascites and assess risk (Harrison 22e p.372)","Ascitic glucose and LDH when secondary peritonitis is suspected (Harrison 22e p.373)","Contrast-enhanced abdominal CT to seek an intraabdominal source / exclude secondary peritonitis (Harrison 22e p.1070-1071)","Chest and abdominal radiography to exclude free air (perforation) when abdominal pain is present (Harrison 22e p.1070)","Repeat paracentesis at 48 hours to confirm >=25% fall in ascitic PMN count (Harrison 22e p.329)"],"redFlags":["New or worsening hepatic encephalopathy or altered mental status in a cirrhotic with ascites (Harrison 22e p.329, 1070, 2715)","Unexplained acute kidney injury, jaundice, or nonlocalizing malaise/fatigue in a susceptible patient (Harrison 22e p.1070)","Multiple organisms recovered from ascitic fluid, especially with anaerobes, suggesting secondary peritonitis requiring source control (Harrison 22e p.329, 1071)","Free air under the diaphragm on imaging, signaling perforation (Harrison 22e p.1070)","Concurrent GI/variceal bleeding, which markedly increases SBP frequency (Harrison 22e p.330, 2716)","Failure of the ascitic PMN count to fall, or persistence of WBCs after therapy, indicating treatment failure or an alternative diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.329, 1071)"],"prognosis":"SBP is a common and potentially lethal complication of cirrhotic ascites and carries significant morbidity and mortality, largely because it tends to occur in patients with the most advanced liver disease. In-hospital mortality is roughly 17-25%; one tertiary-center series reported ~23% mortality at 30 days and ~37% at 90 days. Recurrence is high, with up to ~70% of patients having another episode within a year. Patients with PBP usually respond to appropriate antibiotics within about 72 hours. The underlying prognosis of cirrhosis with ascites is itself poor, with under 50% surviving 2 years after ascites onset in some series, so SBP should prompt consideration of liver transplantation. (Harrison 22e p.1071, 2715-2716, 373)","pitfalls":["Diagnosis rests on the ascitic PMN count >=250/microL, not on a positive culture; ascitic cultures are often negative because the organism burden is low (Harrison 22e p.329, 1070).","Recovery of more than one organism (a mixed flora, especially with anaerobes) alongside an elevated PMN count points to SECONDARY peritonitis from a ruptured viscus or abscess, not SBP, and demands a search for a surgical source (Harrison 22e p.329, 1071).","Multiple organisms WITHOUT an elevated PMN count suggest bowel perforation by the paracentesis needle rather than true infection (Harrison 22e p.329).","Failing to perform a diagnostic paracentesis in a decompensated cirrhotic who deteriorates (encephalopathy, AKI, sepsis) misses SBP, since the abdomen may be benign and tenderness is present in only ~40% (Harrison 22e p.329, 2715).","A low ascitic total protein (<1.0-1.5 g/dL) reflects low opsonic activity and identifies patients at higher risk for SBP; this group warrants heightened suspicion (Harrison 22e p.330, 1071).","The 250/microL PMN threshold is specific to SBP and should not be applied to secondary peritonitis or to peritoneal-dialysis peritonitis, where lower leukocyte cutoffs are used (Harrison 22e p.1070, p.2486 context).","Gastric acid suppression (e.g., proton-pump inhibitors) has been associated with increased SBP risk in observational data (Harrison 22e p.1071)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal severity grading system specific to SBP. Risk stratification is framed around predictors of poor outcome and renal injury: higher SBP risk in patients with low ascitic-fluid total protein (<1.0-1.5 g/dL), impaired renal function (creatinine >=1.2 mg/dL, BUN >=25 mg/dL, or serum sodium <=130 mEq/L), and advanced liver failure (Child-Turcotte-Pugh score >=9, bilirubin >=3 mg/dL); these are used to identify candidates for prophylaxis. The greatest benefit of adjunctive albumin is seen in those presenting with serum creatinine >=1 mg/dL, BUN >=30 mg/dL, or total bilirubin >=5 mg/dL. (Harrison 22e p.1071)","references":["Harrison 22e p.329","Harrison 22e p.330","Harrison 22e p.372","Harrison 22e p.373","Harrison 22e p.1070","Harrison 22e p.1071","Harrison 22e p.1072","Harrison 22e p.2715","Harrison 22e p.2716"],"crossLinks":["decomp_cirrhosis","hepatic_enceph","variceal_bleed"]},"SCRUB_TYPHUS":{"name":"Scrub Typhus","system":"Tropical Fever","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1035, p.1455-1456, p.1460, p.3773","clinicalPearls":["The classic triad of an inoculation eschar at the chigger feeding site, regional lymphadenopathy, and a maculopapular rash is the textbook description, but these signs are seldom seen in indigenous (local endemic) patients (Harrison 22e p.1460).","Among returning travelers, fewer than 50% develop an eschar and fewer than 40% develop a rash; when a rash does appear it typically emerges on day 4-6 of illness (Harrison 22e p.1460).","Eschar formation occurs at the site of mite/chigger feeding and may develop during the incubation period; an eschar is a useful diagnostic clue that should prompt consideration of scrub typhus (Harrison 22e p.1455).","Onset after a 6-21 day incubation is characterized by fever, headache, myalgia, cough, and gastrointestinal symptoms; some patients recover spontaneously after a few days while others progress to severe disease (Harrison 22e p.1460).","Chigger bites may be asymptomatic, so the absence of a recalled bite does not exclude the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.3773).","Immunity to scrub typhus is short-lived: protection against the homologous strain wanes over 1-3 years, and the organism's marked antigenic diversity means cross-protective immunity can be lost in as little as 1 month, so reinfection is possible (Harrison 22e p.1460)."],"pathophysiology":"Scrub typhus is caused by Orientia tsutsugamushi, an obligate intracellular organism placed in a separate genus within the family Rickettsiaceae. It differs substantially from Rickettsia species both genetically and in cell-wall composition, notably lacking lipopolysaccharide. Disease results from infection of vascular endothelial cells with increased vascular permeability (a mechanism shared across the rickettsioses). The organism is maintained in nature by transovarial transmission within trombiculid mites; infected larval mites (chiggers, the only mite stage that feeds on a vertebrate host) inoculate the organism into the skin during feeding. Severe disease reflects widespread vascular injury, producing the encephalitis and interstitial pneumonia that characterize advanced cases. Closely related Orientia species (O. chuto, Candidatus O. chiloensis) cause scrub typhus-like illness in newly recognized foci (Harrison 22e p.1455-1456, p.1460, p.3773).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Other rickettsioses presenting with eschar, rash, fever and headache (e.g., Mediterranean spotted fever, African tick-bite fever, rickettsialpox, murine and epidemic typhus) (Harrison 22e p.1455-1456).","Atypical/community-acquired pneumonia, given the cough and dyspnea of pulmonary involvement (Harrison 22e p.1126).","Other undifferentiated tropical febrile illnesses with thrombocytopenia and transaminitis."],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serology is the mainstay of laboratory diagnosis: indirect fluorescent antibody, indirect immunoperoxidase, enzyme immunoassays, and rapid lateral-flow chromatographic tests (Harrison 22e p.1460).","PCR amplification of Orientia genes is effective when performed on eschar material, but is less sensitive on blood (Harrison 22e p.1460).","Platelet count: thrombocytopenia is a characteristic laboratory finding in scrub typhus (as in other rickettsioses) (Harrison 22e p.1455).","WBC count is typically normal or low (Harrison 22e p.1455).","Serum sodium: hyponatremia is a recognized associated finding across the rickettsioses (Harrison 22e p.1455).","Hepatic aminotransferases: mild to moderate elevation is commonly seen (Harrison 22e p.1455)."],"redFlags":["Altered mental status / encephalitis (meningoencephalitis is a feature of severe disease) (Harrison 22e p.1035, p.1455).","Interstitial pneumonia / pneumonitis with dyspnea or progression toward respiratory failure (Harrison 22e p.1035, p.1460).","Myocarditis (Harrison 22e p.1035).","Signs of disseminated intravascular coagulation or hemorrhage (Harrison 22e p.1035).","Renal failure (Harrison 22e p.1035).","Progressive hypotension and multiorgan failure (a severe-disease pattern shared by the rickettsioses) (Harrison 22e p.1455)."],"prognosis":"Illness ranges from mild and self-limiting to fatal. The case-fatality rate for untreated classic cases is about 6% and would likely be lower if all mild cases were captured; overall reported mortality varies widely by location (cited as 1% to 70%). Risk of death rises with increasing age and with the presence of myocarditis, delirium, pneumonitis, or signs of hemorrhage. Recognized in time, the disease responds well to appropriate antibiotic therapy (Harrison 22e p.1035, p.1460).","pitfalls":["Relying on the classic triad: eschar, rash, and lymphadenopathy are frequently absent (eschar <50% and rash <40% of travelers; signs seldom seen in indigenous patients), so a negative skin exam does not exclude scrub typhus (Harrison 22e p.1460).","Expecting a recalled bite: chigger bites may be asymptomatic and the mites are tiny and rarely noticed (Harrison 22e p.3773).","Assuming geographic exclusion: although classically eastern/southern Asia, northern Australia, and Pacific/Indian Ocean islands, emerging and reemerging foci (e.g., Chile, parts of South/Central America, Africa, Arabian peninsula via related Orientia species) challenge the traditional epidemiology (Harrison 22e p.1460, p.3773).","Note that PCR on blood is less sensitive than PCR on eschar material (Harrison 22e p.1460).","Some Thai isolates are poorly responsive to doxycycline or chloramphenicol (Harrison 22e p.1460)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not give a formal severity score; it contrasts mild/self-limiting illness with severe disease. Severe scrub typhus is marked by encephalitis/meningoencephalitis and interstitial pneumonia due to vascular injury, and may progress to myocarditis, DIC, renal failure, hypotension, and multiorgan failure (Harrison 22e p.1035, p.1455, p.1460).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1035","Harrison 22e p.1455","Harrison 22e p.1456","Harrison 22e p.1460","Harrison 22e p.3773"],"crossLinks":["RICKETTSIAL_FEVER","LEPTOSPIROSIS","DENGUE","MALARIA","ENTERIC_FEVER"]},"SEPSIS":{"name":"Sepsis","system":"Systemic","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"2310-2324 (Harrison 22e; principal sepsis chapter 315, p.2316-2324; shock chapter 314, p.2310-2316; ICU scoring chapter 311, p.2291-2292)","clinicalPearls":["Sepsis-3 (2016) reframes sepsis as life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated host response to infection, not merely a systemic inflammatory state; the older SIRS-based criteria and the term 'severe sepsis' were discarded for poor specificity (p.2316).","Operationally, sepsis is identified by an acute rise of >=2 points in the SOFA score over baseline in a patient with suspected or confirmed infection (p.2320).","Septic shock is the subset of sepsis with persistent hypotension needing vasopressors to keep MAP >65 mmHg PLUS lactate >2 mmol/L despite adequate fluid resuscitation, reflecting circulatory, cellular, and metabolic derangement that markedly raises mortality (p.2310, 2320).","qSOFA (>=2 of: respiratory rate >=22/min, altered mentation/GCS <15, systolic BP <=100 mmHg) flags infected patients at high risk of poor outcome; it is more specific but less sensitive than SIRS and was NOT designed or endorsed as a standalone diagnostic/screening test for sepsis (p.2292, 2320).","There is NO gold-standard diagnostic test for sepsis; the diagnosis is a synthesis of epidemiologic, clinical, laboratory, imaging, and microbiologic data (p.2320).","Presentation is frequently nonspecific (fever, tachycardia, lethargy, myalgias) and may lack localizing features; the CNS, kidneys, and skin act as accessible 'windows' onto early organ hypoperfusion (altered mentation, oliguria, mottling/delayed capillary refill) (p.2320, 2312).","Hypotension or an isolated low BP reading does not by itself define sepsis/shock; interpret BP against the patient's baseline and look for evidence of organ hypoperfusion, since hypertensive patients can decompensate at 'normal' pressures (p.2312).","Absence of tachycardia does not exclude sepsis: nodal-blocking drugs or conduction disease can blunt the compensatory heart-rate response (p.2312).","Sepsis is the commonest cause of distributive (high-cardiac-output, low-SVR) shock and of high-output hypotension overall; warm extremities with a wide pulse pressure suggest this physiology (p.2310, 2293).","Lactate is central to risk-stratifying sepsis: elevation reflects tissue hypoperfusion/anaerobic metabolism, correlates with worse outcome, and serial measurements help track response to resuscitation (p.2313).","Most sepsis is community-onset (~88%); the urinary tract is the single most common primary site, followed by respiratory, intra-abdominal, and skin/soft-tissue sources (p.2316).","Hospital-onset sepsis tends to involve sicker, more comorbid patients with more intra-abdominal infection and bacteremia from Enterococcus, Candida, and Pseudomonas, and carries higher ICU admission and mortality (p.2317).","Sepsis-induced cardiomyopathy (reduced LVEF, dilated LV, RV impairment) affects up to half of septic-shock patients and can convert initial distributive shock into a mixed distributive-cardiogenic picture (p.2311, 2322)."],"pathophysiology":"Sepsis arises when an infection provokes a dysregulated, self-amplifying host response rather than a contained local one. Pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs, e.g., LPS, bacterial cell-wall components, microbial nucleic acids) and damage-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs, e.g., histones, HMGB1, S100 proteins, mitochondrial/double-stranded DNA, ATP released from injured cells) are sensed by pattern-recognition receptors (TLRs, CLRs, RAGE, RIG-I, NOD-like receptors) on innate immune and parenchymal cells. This triggers exuberant release of cytokines and chemokines and upregulation of vascular adhesion molecules. Myeloid cells (neutrophils, monocytes, M1 macrophages, dendritic cells) drive injury through phagocytosis, degranulation, reactive oxygen/nitrogen species, and neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs), while emergency granulopoiesis releases immature neutrophils (a poor-prognosis marker). Lymphoid responses are dysregulated, with lymphocyte apoptosis, exhaustion, and myeloid-derived suppressor cells producing a state of immunosuppression that predisposes survivors to secondary infection. The endothelium is activated and injured: glycocalyx breakdown, loss of VE-cadherin/tight junctions, and apoptosis cause capillary leak, interstitial edema, and a widened oxygen-diffusion distance. A prothrombotic shift (increased tissue factor, vWF, PAI-1; depleted antithrombin, tissue factor pathway inhibitor, and protein C; complement activation with C3a/C5a and the C5b-9 membrane attack complex) generates microvascular thrombi and, once clotting factors and platelets are consumed, bleeding. Excess nitric oxide drives systemic vasodilation. The net result is impaired oxygen delivery, uptake, and utilization; even when oxygen delivery is adequate, mitochondrial and metabolic dysfunction impairs ATP generation and ion homeostasis, producing multiorgan cellular injury and dysfunction (p.2317-2319).","additionalDifferentials":["Sepsis mimics, retrospectively found in ~25% of ICU 'sepsis' admissions; continuous reevaluation for an alternative diagnosis is imperative when infection is not confirmed (p.2320)","Distributive shock from non-septic causes (e.g., neurogenic shock from brain/spinal-cord injury disrupting vascular autonomic tone) (p.2311)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Noninfectious inflammatory febrile syndromes - connective tissue diseases and vasculitides (p.2320)","Heart failure and other noninfectious causes of pneumonitis or lung injury (the commonest mimic category in one ICU series was cardiovascular, e.g., heart failure, cardiac arrest) (p.2320)","Noninfectious abdominal syndromes - mesenteric ischemia and inflammatory bowel disease (p.2320)","Acute pancreatitis (a recognized noninfectious cause of distributive/high-output shock) (p.2293, 2311)","Adrenal insufficiency / adrenal crisis (distributive shock from cortisol/aldosterone deficiency, unmasked by stress of infection or surgery) (p.2293, 2311)","Severe burns (distributive shock with cellular hypoxia from reduced SVR) (p.2311)","Acute liver failure (high-output hypotension) (p.2293, 2311)","Anaphylaxis (IgE-mediated distributive shock with rapid plasma extravasation) (p.2311)","Thyrotoxicosis and large peripheral arteriovenous shunts (other causes of high-output hypotension) (p.2293)","Hypovolemia and autonomic dysfunction as noninfectious causes of hypotension (p.2320)","Pulmonary embolism / obstructive shock (acute chest pain, dyspnea, hypoxia in the immobile or malignancy patient) (p.2313)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum procalcitonin / point-of-care pathogen testing where available to support diagnosis (p.2320)","Repeat/serial lactate to gauge response to resuscitation, not just a single value (p.2313)","Arterial (or venous) blood gas for acid-base assessment; arterial sampling if hypoxemia present (p.2313)","Central venous oxygen saturation (ScvO2) as a surrogate of oxygen-delivery adequacy in refractory shock (p.2313)","Echocardiography / point-of-care ultrasound for sepsis-induced cardiomyopathy, volume responsiveness, and to exclude mimics (tamponade, PE, pulmonary edema, pneumothorax) (p.2293-2294, 2322)","EEG, brain imaging, or lumbar puncture if seizure, stroke, or meningoencephalitis is suspected in a septic patient with neurologic findings (p.2321-2322)","Coagulation profile / DIC and sepsis-induced-coagulopathy (SIC) markers - platelets, PT/INR, fibrinogen, fibrin split products (p.2323)","Abdominal ultrasound in the septic patient with elevated bilirubin to look for biliary obstruction, cholecystitis, or cholangitis (p.2323)"],"redFlags":["qSOFA >=2 (RR >=22, altered mentation, SBP <=100) in suspected infection - identifies the most acutely ill subset with higher mortality and ICU need (p.2292, 2320)","Persistent hypotension requiring vasopressors plus lactate >2 mmol/L after adequate fluids = septic shock, with mortality up to ~40% (p.2310, 2316)","New altered mental status / encephalopathy or new oliguria as early signs that compensatory mechanisms are exhausted (p.2312)","Skin mottling, prolonged capillary refill, cool clammy skin - hypoperfusion (p.2312, 2322)","Every hour of delay in appropriate antibiotics in bacterial septic shock raises mortality (estimated 7-8% per hour after shock recognition) - empiric antibiotics within 1 h of shock recognition (p.2320-2321)","An infection source needing urgent control (intra-abdominal abscess, bowel perforation, cholangitis, obstructed/infected urinary tract, necrotizing soft-tissue infection, infected indwelling catheter/endovascular infection) - source control as fast as possible (p.2321)","Failure to confirm infection should prompt active search for a mimic rather than indefinite empiric antibiotics (antibiotic stewardship: stop antibiotics when sepsis is excluded) (p.2315, 2320)"],"prognosis":"Overall in-hospital mortality is roughly 15% among hospitalized adults with sepsis, rising to up to ~40% with septic shock; globally an estimated ~11 million sepsis-related deaths occur yearly (about one in five deaths worldwide). Mortality climbs with age and is higher in men. Elevated SOFA scores and machine-learning-defined phenotypes track population-level outcome (e.g., alpha/beta/gamma/delta phenotypes with ~2%/5%/15%/32% mortality) but cannot reliably predict an individual patient's outcome. Survivorship carries a heavy long-term burden: physical (prolonged fatigue, muscle loss, weakness, reduced functional capacity), cognitive (cognitive decline, dementia), and psychological (depression, reduced quality of life) sequelae, plus increased risk of cardiovascular events, recurrent infection, and readmission. About half of survivors are rehospitalized within a year and roughly one in six die within the first year, with most post-sepsis deaths in the first 6 months and elevated death risk persisting up to ~2 years (p.2316-2317, 2324). No FDA-approved host-targeted/immunomodulatory therapy exists for sepsis (p.2324).","pitfalls":["Treating sepsis as a single pathogen rather than a syndrome - empiric therapy and workup must be driven by the most likely source (p.2316).","Relying on SIRS criteria or a single abnormal vital sign to rule sepsis in or out - poor specificity led to their abandonment in Sepsis-3 (p.2316).","Using qSOFA as a diagnostic test - it is a risk-stratification/screening prompt, not a diagnostic criterion (p.2292, 2320).","Being falsely reassured by a normal heart rate (beta-blockade/conduction disease) or by a low BP reading in a chronically hypotensive patient (p.2312).","Anchoring on 'sepsis' when infection is unconfirmed - up to a quarter of presumed-sepsis ICU cases are mimics; failure to reevaluate delays the correct diagnosis (p.2320).","Continuing broad-spectrum antibiotics once infection is excluded, undermining stewardship and risking C. difficile, allergy, and AKI (p.2315, 2321).","Over-resuscitation with fluid - excess volume is likely harmful; targeted, dynamically reassessed resuscitation is the goal (p.2294).","Attributing all altered mentation to sepsis-associated encephalopathy without excluding seizure, stroke, or CNS infection (p.2321-2322).","Missing sepsis-induced cardiomyopathy, which can turn distributive shock into mixed shock and change management (p.2311, 2322)."],"severityClassification":"Sepsis-3 (Singer et al., JAMA 2016) is the current framework: (1) Infection (suspected or confirmed) without organ dysfunction is not sepsis. (2) Sepsis = suspected/confirmed infection with an acute increase of >=2 points in the SOFA score from baseline (life-threatening organ dysfunction from a dysregulated host response). The SOFA score grades six systems 0-4: respiratory (PaO2/FiO2), coagulation (platelets), liver (bilirubin), cardiovascular (MAP / vasopressor requirement), CNS (Glasgow Coma Scale), and renal (creatinine / urine output). (3) Septic shock = sepsis with vasopressor requirement to maintain MAP >65 mmHg AND serum lactate >2 mmol/L despite adequate fluid resuscitation. qSOFA (>=2 of RR >=22/min, altered mentation, SBP <=100 mmHg) is a rapid bedside risk-stratification prompt for poor outcome in suspected infection, not a diagnostic criterion. The prior 1991/1992 SIRS-based definitions and the term 'severe sepsis' are obsolete under Sepsis-3 (p.2292, 2316, 2320).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2292","Harrison 22e p.2293","Harrison 22e p.2310","Harrison 22e p.2311","Harrison 22e p.2312","Harrison 22e p.2313","Harrison 22e p.2316","Harrison 22e p.2317","Harrison 22e p.2318","Harrison 22e p.2319","Harrison 22e p.2320","Harrison 22e p.2321","Harrison 22e p.2322","Harrison 22e p.2323","Harrison 22e p.2324"],"crossLinks":["SEPTIC_SHOCK","FEBRILE_NEUTROPENIA","adrenal_crisis","dic","anaphylaxis","cardiogenic_shock"]},"SEPTIC_SHOCK":{"name":"Septic Shock","system":"Systemic","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e: Ch.315 Sepsis and Septic Shock p.2316-2321; Ch.314 Approach to the Patient with Shock p.2353-2358","clinicalPearls":["Septic shock under Sepsis-3 is a subset of sepsis with profound circulatory, cellular, and metabolic derangements that carry a higher mortality than sepsis alone; sepsis itself is life-threatening organ dysfunction from a dysregulated host response to infection (p.2316).","Sepsis-3 abandoned the older SIRS criteria (used by Sepsis-1/2) because of poor specificity for distinguishing infection from other inflammatory states, and eliminated the term 'severe sepsis' (p.2316).","Sepsis is operationalized as an acute rise of at least 2 points in the SOFA score from baseline in a patient with suspected or confirmed infection (p.2320).","qSOFA (respiratory rate >=22/min, altered mentation with GCS <15, systolic BP <=100 mmHg) is a rapid bedside screen; a score >=2 is more specific but less sensitive than SIRS and flags patients at higher risk of death, ICU admission, and longer stay (p.2313, p.2320).","Sepsis presentation can be nonspecific (fever, tachycardia, lethargy, myalgias) with or without localizing findings; there is no single gold-standard diagnostic test, so the diagnosis integrates clinical, laboratory, microbiologic, and imaging data (p.2320).","Approximately 88% of sepsis cases are community onset (detected within 48 h of admission); hospital-onset sepsis (after 48 h) tends to involve more comorbidities, intraabdominal infection, and Enterococcus/Candida/Pseudomonas, with higher ICU admission and mortality (p.2316-2317).","Among culture-positive community-onset sepsis, the most common primary sites are urinary tract (~49%), respiratory tract (~33%), intraabdominal (~14%), and skin/soft tissue (~10%) (p.2316).","About 53% of US sepsis cases are bacterial culture positive with a roughly even gram-positive / gram-negative split; common gram-positives are S. aureus, Streptococcus, and Enterococcus, and common gram-negatives are E. coli, Klebsiella, and P. aeruginosa (p.2316)."],"pathophysiology":"Septic shock is the most common form of distributive shock, in which the primary hemodynamic derangement is a fall in systemic vascular resistance (vasoplegia) rather than a primary loss of preload or pump function. The vasodilatory profile is distinctive: SVR is reduced, cardiac output is typically increased as a compensatory response, and both central venous pressure (CVP) and pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP) tend to be low (p.2353-2354). Arterial and venous vasodilation is mediated in part by excess nitric oxide; vasopressin deficiency may also contribute to refractory hypotension. The dysregulated host response begins when pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs, e.g. LPS, microbial nucleic acids) and damage-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs, e.g. histones, HMGB1, mitochondrial DNA, ATP) are sensed by pattern-recognition receptors (TLRs, CLRs, RAGE, RIG-I, NOD receptors) on neutrophils, monocytes/macrophages, and parenchymal cells, triggering proinflammatory cytokine/chemokine release and upregulation of endothelial adhesion molecules. Activated neutrophils release reactive oxygen species and neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs); endothelial activation/injury, glycocalyx degradation, and loss of VE-cadherin tight junctions cause capillary leak, interstitial edema, and increased oxygen-diffusion distance. A prothrombotic state arises from tissue-factor-driven coagulation, platelet/vWF microthrombi, complement activation (C3a, C5a, C5b-9 membrane attack complex), reduced anticoagulants (antithrombin, tissue factor pathway inhibitor, activated protein C), and impaired fibrinolysis (elevated PAI-1); consumption of clotting factors and platelets can then produce hemorrhage. The net effect is hypotension (from vasodilation, myocardial depression, and hypovolemia), microcirculatory dysfunction, and mitochondrial/metabolic failure, so that oxygen delivery, uptake, and utilization are all impaired and cellular injury occurs even when bulk oxygen delivery appears adequate (p.2317-2320). Sepsis-induced cardiomyopathy can add a cardiogenic component, producing mixed shock (p.2355).","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of distributive shock: pancreatitis, severe burns, liver failure, anaphylaxis, neurogenic shock from brain/spinal cord injury, and adrenal insufficiency/adrenal crisis (p.2353-2354)","Cardiogenic shock (myocardial infarction, myocarditis, arrhythmia, acute valvular failure) (p.2353-2354)","Hypovolemic shock (hemorrhagic from trauma or GI bleeding; nonhemorrhagic from vomiting, diarrhea, osmotic diuresis in DKA or diabetes insipidus, or skin loss) (p.2353-2354)","Obstructive shock (tension pneumothorax, cardiac tamponade, constrictive pericarditis, pulmonary embolism, air or fat embolism, aortic dissection) (p.2353-2354)","Mixed shock, where more than one mechanism coexists (e.g. distributive sepsis plus sepsis-induced cardiomyopathy) (p.2355)"],"infectionMimics":["Noninfectious pneumonitis or lung injury that mimics pneumonia/respiratory sepsis (p.2320)","Noninfectious abdominal syndromes such as mesenteric ischemia and inflammatory bowel disease, which can mimic an intraabdominal source (p.2320)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Noninfectious inflammatory febrile syndromes including connective tissue diseases and vasculitides (p.2320)","Heart failure and cardiac arrest (the most common cardiovascular mimics in ICU series, where ~25% of presumed septic patients were later judged to have a mimic) (p.2320)","Noninfectious chronic obstructive pulmonary disease exacerbation (common respiratory mimic) (p.2320)","Hypotension from noninfectious causes: hypovolemia, autonomic dysfunction, and adrenal insufficiency (p.2320)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Complete blood count with differential, basic metabolic panel, and liver function tests to assess end-organ dysfunction (p.2320)","Coagulation panel (PT/PTT/INR, platelets) to detect sepsis-induced coagulopathy/DIC (p.2320, p.2356)","Cultures of blood and other potentially infected sites (urine, sputum, wound) ideally before antimicrobials, plus point-of-care pathogen testing when available (p.2320)","Urinalysis and urine sediment to evaluate for pyuria/urinary source (p.2356, p.2320)","Cardiac enzymes/troponin to detect a primary or contributory cardiac process (p.2356)","Venous or arterial blood gas (arterial if hypoxemia present) to quantify acidosis (p.2356)","Focused imaging (chest x-ray, CT, or ultrasound) of suspected sites; point-of-care ultrasound (POCUS, e.g. RUSH/ACES protocols) helps classify undifferentiated and mixed shock (p.2356)","SOFA score components for severity (GCS, MAP/vasopressor use, PaO2/FiO2, bilirubin, creatinine, platelets) (p.2317)"],"redFlags":["Persistent hypotension requiring vasopressors to keep MAP >65 mmHg plus lactate >2 mmol/L despite adequate fluid resuscitation defines septic shock and marks the highest-mortality end of the spectrum (p.2320, p.2354)","Acute rise of >=2 SOFA points from baseline with suspected infection indicates new organ dysfunction (p.2320)","qSOFA >=2 (RR>=22, GCS<15, SBP<=100) signals high risk of death and ICU need (p.2313, p.2320)","Elevated and rising serial lactate, which correlates with worse outcome and reflects tissue hypoperfusion (p.2356)","Sepsis-associated encephalopathy (altered consciousness, cognition, or behavior) signaling CNS organ dysfunction (p.2319)","New oliguria / acute kidney injury, occurring in up to two-thirds of septic patients (p.2319)","Signs of coagulopathy/DIC with consumption of platelets and clotting factors and bleeding risk (p.2319)"],"prognosis":"Septic shock carries up to ~40% mortality, far higher than the ~15% overall mortality among US hospitalized adults with sepsis (p.2316). In emergency-department shock cohorts, septic and cardiogenic shock have the highest 90-day mortality (reported ~56% and ~52% respectively), and septic shock predominates among ICU shock (~62% of ICU shock patients in one multicenter series), whereas hypovolemic shock has lower mortality (p.2355). Mortality rises with age and is higher in men; comorbidities such as diabetes, obesity, neurologic/respiratory/cardiac disease, renal or hepatic insufficiency, cancer, and immunosuppression worsen outcomes (p.2317). SOFA scores predict mortality at the population level but not for individual patients; proposed clinical 'phenotypes' (alpha/beta/gamma/delta) carried roughly 2%, 5%, 15%, and 32% mortality (p.2317). Survivors face substantial long-term sequelae: physical (fatigue, muscle loss, weakness), cognitive and psychological (cognitive decline, dementia, depression), and increased risk of cardiovascular events, recurrent infection, and readmission. Roughly half of survivors are rehospitalized within 1 year and about one in six die within the first year, with most post-sepsis deaths in the first 6 months and elevated risk persisting up to 2 years (p.2321).","pitfalls":["Relying on SIRS criteria to define or exclude sepsis: SIRS lacks specificity, which is why Sepsis-3 replaced it (p.2316).","Treating shock as a single entity: distributive (septic) shock has low SVR with high cardiac output and low filling pressures, the opposite hemodynamic profile of cardiogenic and hypovolemic shock, so misclassification leads to wrong resuscitation (p.2353-2354).","Assuming a single shock type: sepsis frequently coexists with a cardiogenic component (sepsis-induced cardiomyopathy) or hypovolemia, producing mixed shock that POCUS can help dissect (p.2355).","A normal hemoglobin/hematocrit early in concurrent hemorrhage, or a normal WBC, does not exclude the relevant process; leukocytosis is suggestive but not diagnostic of infection (p.2356).","Anchoring on sepsis when infection is not confirmed: about 25% of presumed septic ICU patients had a mimic, so alternative diagnoses must be continually reconsidered (p.2320).","Failure to identify and control the source (e.g. infected catheter, abscess, endocarditis) undermines management even with appropriate antibiotics (p.2321)."],"severityClassification":"Sepsis-3 spectrum: (1) Sepsis = life-threatening organ dysfunction from a dysregulated host response to infection, operationalized as an acute increase of >=2 points in the SOFA score in a patient with suspected/confirmed infection. (2) Septic shock = a subset of sepsis with profound circulatory, cellular, and metabolic abnormalities and higher mortality, operationally defined as a septic patient who, despite adequate fluid resuscitation, requires vasopressors to maintain MAP >65 mmHg AND has serum lactate >2 mmol/L. qSOFA (RR>=22, GCS<15, SBP<=100; score >=2) is a rapid risk-stratification screen, not a diagnosis. The historical Sepsis-1/2 terms 'severe sepsis' and SIRS-based septic shock (SBP<90 or drop >=40 mmHg) have been superseded (p.2316, p.2320).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2316","Harrison 22e p.2317","Harrison 22e p.2318","Harrison 22e p.2319","Harrison 22e p.2320","Harrison 22e p.2321","Harrison 22e p.2353","Harrison 22e p.2354","Harrison 22e p.2355","Harrison 22e p.2356"],"crossLinks":["SEPSIS","cardiogenic_shock","hypovolemic_shock","anaphylaxis","dic","pancreatitis"]},"SEVERE_CAP":{"name":"Severe Community Acquired Pneumonia","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1023-1029","clinicalPearls":["Neither the Pneumonia Severity Index (PSI) nor CURB-65 accurately determines the need for ICU admission; they predict mortality/site of care but not ICU need (p.1026).","Patients with septic shock requiring vasopressors or acute respiratory failure requiring intubation and mechanical ventilation meet a major criterion and should be admitted directly to an ICU (p.1026).","Presence of three or more of the nine minor severe-CAP criteria warrants admission to an ICU or high-level monitoring unit (p.1026).","Late or delayed ICU admission is associated with increased mortality; less-ill patients placed on a general ward who then deteriorate fare worse than equally ill patients monitored in the ICU from the outset (p.1026).","In ICU-admitted CAP specifically, treatment directed at an identified pathogen may be superior to empirical therapy, so etiologic workup carries more weight in severe disease (p.1025).","The microbial spectrum of severe (ICU) CAP differs from milder CAP: Legionella tends to cause more serious disease, and S. aureus and gram-negative bacilli become more prominent (p.1024).","Prior respiratory-tract isolation of MRSA or P. aeruginosa within the past year is a stronger (more robust) risk factor for these pathogens than recent hospitalization plus parenteral antibiotic exposure (p.1028).","Procalcitonin (PCT) is insufficiently accurate to diagnose bacterial CAP and must NOT be used as a basis for withholding initial antibiotics; CRP is even less sensitive than PCT (p.1026).","If only a viral pathogen (e.g., influenza or SARS-CoV-2) is found and no bacterial pathogen is evident, antibacterial therapy can be stopped — BUT in severe illness, bacterial-viral co-infection must still be considered (p.1028).","Gross hemoptysis suggests necrotizing pneumonia, e.g., due to CA-MRSA (p.1024).","In the elderly, severe CAP may present atypically as new-onset or worsening confusion or decompensation of a chronic illness with few classic respiratory findings (p.1025).","Recent data show a mortality benefit for corticosteroids in severe CAP, particularly when there is a high level of systemic inflammation (CRP >15 mg/dL) (p.1029)."],"pathophysiology":"Pneumonia results from proliferation of microbial pathogens in the alveoli combined with the host inflammatory response. Organisms reach the lower respiratory tract by inhalation, microaspiration of oropharyngeal flora, or direct mucosal spread; mechanical defenses (nasal hairs/turbinates, the branching airway, mucociliary clearance, gag and cough reflexes) are insufficient to fully exclude bacteria, and the lung is now understood to harbor a microbiota rather than being sterile. An inflammatory/epithelial-endothelial injury event releases cytokines, chemokines, and catecholamines that can selectively promote growth of organisms such as S. pneumoniae and P. aeruginosa, generating a positive-feedback loop; in CAP the trigger is often a viral infection compounded by microaspiration. Inflammatory mediators (IL-6, TNF) cause fever, while IL-8 and G-CSF recruit neutrophils; mediators from macrophages and neutrophils produce alveolar-capillary leak with impaired oxygenation, hypoxemia, and radiographic infiltrates. Some pathogens interfere with hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction, worsening hypoxemia. Reduced compliance from capillary leak, increased respiratory drive and secretions, and intrapulmonary shunting drive worsening dyspnea, and when severe, reductions in lung volume/compliance and shunting can precipitate respiratory failure. Pathologically, classic (lobar pneumococcal) pneumonia evolves through edema, red hepatization, gray hepatization, and resolution phases.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute bronchitis","Exacerbation of chronic bronchitis","Heart failure / pulmonary edema","Pulmonary embolism","Lung carcinoma (including postobstructive pneumonia)","Tuberculosis (e.g., upper-lobe cavitating lesion)"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculosis","Endemic/invasive fungal pneumonia","Pneumocystis pneumonia (alveolar process despite interstitial radiographic appearance)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pulmonary edema","Pulmonary embolism / pulmonary infarction","Lung carcinoma","Radiation pneumonitis","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Connective tissue disease involving the lungs"],"additionalInvestigations":["CT chest (for suspected loculated effusion, cavitary disease, or postobstructive pneumonia from tumor/foreign body)","Deep-suction aspirate or bronchoalveolar lavage in intubated ICU patients (high culture yield when sent promptly)","Multiplex respiratory PCR / nasopharyngeal swab PCR (influenza, coronaviruses; also Legionella, M. pneumoniae, C. pneumoniae, mycobacteria)","MRSA rapid nasal PCR (to guide adding/de-escalating MRSA coverage)","Diagnostic pleural fluid sampling (parapneumonic effusion: drainage indicated if pH <7.2, glucose <2.2 mmol/L, LDH >1000 U/L, or organisms seen/cultured)","Stains/cultures for M. tuberculosis and fungi in selected cases","Bronchoscopy in delayed responders or worsening disease"],"redFlags":["Septic shock requiring vasopressors (major criterion - direct ICU admission)","Acute respiratory failure requiring invasive mechanical ventilation (major criterion - direct ICU admission)","Three or more minor severe-CAP criteria (warrants ICU/high-level monitoring)","O2 saturation <92% on room air (mandates hospitalization)","Multiorgan failure","Multilobar infiltrates or cavitation on imaging (increased severity)","Gross hemoptysis (suggests necrotizing pneumonia, e.g., CA-MRSA)","Failure to improve / deterioration by ~day 3 of therapy"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on age, comorbidities, and site of treatment. For hospitalized CAP, overall mortality ranges from 12% to 40%, the higher end reflecting ICU-level disease and care-process factors such as timely administration of appropriate antibiotics (vs <5% for outpatients). CURB-65 scores >=3 carry an overall ~22% mortality and may indicate ICU admission; PSI class 5 has a ~29.2% mortality. CAP is a leading infectious cause of death in patients >65 years; about 18% of hospitalized CAP patients are readmitted within a month, and recent data show 1-year mortality after CAP (especially in older patients) exceeds 30-day mortality. Severe-CAP complications (respiratory failure, shock, multiorgan failure) and pneumonia-associated cardiovascular events worsen outcomes.","pitfalls":["Using PSI or CURB-65 to decide ICU admission - neither is accurate for that purpose (p.1026).","Withholding initial antibiotics based on a low procalcitonin - PCT is too inaccurate to exclude bacterial CAP (p.1026).","Assuming hospitalization alone is a significant risk factor for MRSA or P. aeruginosa - it is no longer considered so; severity plus specific risk factors drive coverage decisions (p.1028).","Requiring all three drug-resistance risk factors (recent hospitalization, recent oral/IV antibiotics, local validation) before considering resistant pathogens - any can apply (p.1028).","Stopping antibacterials when a virus is identified in a severely ill patient without considering bacterial-viral co-infection (p.1028).","Attributing failure to improve to the wrong cause: consider resistant or unexpected pathogen (CA-MRSA, M. tuberculosis, fungus), a sequestered focus (lung abscess, empyema), wrong drug/dose, or nosocomial superinfection (p.1029).","Routinely covering anaerobes in aspiration-related severe CAP - unnecessary unless poor dentition, lung abscess, or necrotizing pneumonia (p.1027)."],"severityClassification":"IDSA/ATS severe-CAP criteria (Harrison 22e Table 131-3, p.1026). MAJOR criteria (either qualifies for ICU): (1) respiratory failure requiring invasive mechanical ventilation; (2) septic shock requiring vasopressors. MINOR criteria (>=3 warrant ICU/high-level monitoring): respiratory rate >=30 breaths/min; PaO2/FiO2 ratio <=250; multilobar infiltrates; confusion/disorientation; uremia (BUN >=20 mg/dL); leukopenia (WBC <4000/uL); thrombocytopenia (platelets <100,000/uL); hypothermia (core temperature <36 C); and hypotension requiring aggressive fluid resuscitation. Prognostic tools PSI (5 mortality classes, class 5 ~29.2%) and CURB-65 (confusion, urea >7 mmol/L, RR >=30, SBP <=90 or DBP <=60 mmHg, age >=65; score >=3 ~22% mortality) assess severity/site of care but are NOT accurate for determining ICU need.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1023","Harrison 22e p.1024","Harrison 22e p.1025","Harrison 22e p.1026","Harrison 22e p.1027","Harrison 22e p.1028","Harrison 22e p.1029"],"crossLinks":["CAP","HAP","VAP","SEPSIS","SEPTIC_SHOCK","ASPIRATION_PNEUMONIA","LUNG_ABSCESS","pleural_effusion","PULMONARY_TB"]},"SINUSITIS":{"name":"Acute Sinusitis (Rhinosinusitis)","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e pp. 296, 298-299 (Sinus Symptoms / Acute Sinusitis); orbital complications p. 276","clinicalPearls":["The vast majority of acute sinusitis cases are caused by respiratory viruses; fewer than 2% of episodes are bacterial, yet antibiotics are prescribed at over 70% of office visits, making sinusitis a leading driver of unnecessary antibiotic use in adults (p. 299).","By guideline criteria, no more than about 50% of adults (and probably closer to 20%) with sinusitis actually meet thresholds for antibiotic prescribing (p. 299).","Symptoms are classified by duration: acute (<4 weeks), subacute (4-12 weeks), and chronic (>=12 weeks) (p. 298).","Common features are purulent nasal discharge, facial congestion/fullness, and facial pain or pressure; additional symptoms include fever, hyposmia or anosmia, ear pressure/pain, postnasal drip, halitosis, maxillary toothache, cough, and fatigue (p. 299).","Risk factors include age 45-65 years, smoking, asthma, air travel, and allergies (p. 299).","For an individual patient with a nonspecific upper respiratory illness, an antibiotic is more likely to cause an adverse reaction than to prevent a complication such as bacterial sinusitis (p. 296).","Recurrent acute sinusitis is defined as four or more episodes of acute sinusitis in a year with no signs or symptoms between episodes (p. 299).","Nosocomial sinusitis occurs in critically ill patients, often those who are nasotracheally intubated, and should be suspected in hospitalized patients with fever lacking another identifiable source (p. 299)."],"pathophysiology":"Sinusitis is inflammation of the paranasal sinuses; when the nasal passages are also involved the term rhinosinusitis is used. Most acute disease begins as a viral process. Bacterial sinusitis, when it occurs, is usually a secondary superinfection of the obstructed/inflamed sinus and is most often caused by S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, or M. catarrhalis. Invasive fungal disease arises when host defenses are impaired (uncontrolled diabetes, transplant/immunocompromise), allowing Mucorales or Aspergillus to invade tissue and spread beyond the sinus (pp. 298-299).","additionalDifferentials":["Common cold / nonspecific upper respiratory infection (p. 298)","Allergic rhinitis (p. 298)","Vasomotor rhinitis (p. 298)","Rhinitis medicamentosa from overuse of topical decongestants (p. 298)","Drug-induced rhinitis (e.g., aspirin, ibuprofen, beta blockers) (p. 298)","Cerebrospinal fluid leak (p. 298)","Headache and facial pain syndromes referred to the sinuses (p. 298)","Temporomandibular disorder (p. 298)","Dental pathology (p. 298)","Gastroesophageal reflux causing referred sinus symptoms (p. 298)","Nasal foreign body (especially in children) (p. 298)"],"infectionMimics":["Bacterial superinfection masquerading within an otherwise viral course (p. 299)","Invasive fungal rhinosinusitis (Mucorales or Aspergillus) presenting as rapidly progressive sinusitis in immunocompromised hosts (p. 299)","Nosocomial sinusitis in intubated critically ill patients presenting as unexplained fever (p. 299)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Autoimmune disease such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis (p. 298)","Allergic rhinitis and other noninfectious rhinitides (p. 298)","Structural causes such as deviated nasal septum or nasal polyps (noted in chronic disease, p. 300)"],"additionalInvestigations":["No imaging or laboratory testing is routinely required for uncomplicated acute sinusitis; diagnosis is clinical (pp. 299-300).","Sinus transillumination is NOT accurate for diagnosing sinusitis and should not be relied upon (p. 299).","Noncontrast CT is the preferred imaging modality when imaging is indicated (e.g., failure to improve, suspected complication, or alternative diagnosis) (p. 300).","Gadolinium-enhanced MRI is the preferred imaging modality when invasive fungal sinusitis is suspected (p. 299).","Nasal endoscopy with biopsy is part of the evaluation for suspected invasive fungal sinusitis (p. 299).","Direct rhinoscopy may reveal excess mucus or purulence on examination (p. 299).","In recurrent acute sinusitis, consider nasal culture during episodes, imaging between episodes to identify predisposing anatomic abnormalities, and allergic/immunologic evaluation (p. 300)."],"redFlags":["Confusion (possible intracranial complication) (p. 299)","Unilateral weakness (p. 299)","Proptosis (p. 299)","Limited ocular movements / ophthalmoplegia (p. 299)","Acute vision changes (p. 299)","Orbital swelling, cellulitis, or chemosis (p. 299)","Cranial nerve involvement (p. 299)","Rapidly progressive facial pain/pressure with headache and fever in a diabetic or immunocompromised patient (suspect invasive fungal disease - an emergency) (p. 299)","Recurrent fever >102 degrees F (p. 300)","Sinus symptoms that worsen after an initial improvement (double-sickening pattern) (p. 300)","Rapid worsening of facial pain that becomes persistent (p. 300)"],"prognosis":"Most acute sinusitis is viral and self-limited. Symptoms of the underlying upper respiratory illness generally peak early but can persist up to about 14 days (p. 296). Complications of sinusitis can be dramatic but are extremely rare (p. 299). Invasive fungal sinusitis is an emergency: in immunocompromised hosts it can progress within days from rhinosinusitis to cranial nerve involvement, orbital and ophthalmic involvement, and critical illness (p. 299).","pitfalls":["Treating acute sinusitis as bacterial by default - the large majority are viral, and antibiotics are over-prescribed; reserve antibiotics for patients meeting persistent (>=10 days), severe, or worsening criteria (p. 299).","Relying on sinus transillumination, which is inaccurate for diagnosis (p. 299).","Prescribing a macrolide for sinusitis - specifically not recommended because of high rates of macrolide-resistant S. pneumoniae (p. 300).","Missing invasive fungal sinusitis in a diabetic or immunocompromised patient with rapidly progressive disease - delay can be fatal (p. 299).","Overlooking nosocomial sinusitis as a hidden source of unexplained fever in nasotracheally intubated ICU patients (p. 299).","In children, failing to consider a nasal foreign body as the cause of sinus symptoms (p. 298)."],"severityClassification":"Antibiotic-prescribing indications (Harrison Table 37-2, p. 299): Persistent = symptoms lasting >=10 days; Severe = fever >102 degrees F with either purulent nasal discharge or nasal pain for at least 3-4 consecutive days; Worsening = new fever, headache, or increased nasal discharge following an upper respiratory infection that had lasted 5-6 days and was initially improving (double-sickening). Duration-based: acute <4 weeks, subacute 4-12 weeks, chronic >=12 weeks (p. 298).","references":["Harrison 22e p. 296","Harrison 22e p. 298","Harrison 22e p. 299","Harrison 22e p. 300","Harrison 22e p. 276"],"crossLinks":["URTI","PHARYNGITIS","BRAIN_ABSCESS","MENINGITIS"]},"URTI":{"name":"Upper Respiratory Tract Infection (common cold)","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"295-297","clinicalPearls":["The nonspecific URI ('common cold') is defined as a respiratory tract infection in which no single symptom predominates, distinguishing it clinically from more localized syndromes such as pharyngitis, sinusitis, or otitis media (Harrison 22e p.296).","The overwhelming majority of acute respiratory infections are caused by common respiratory viruses and are self-limited; antibiotics neither speed resolution nor prevent complications, which is why at least half of ambulatory antibiotic prescriptions for these infections are inappropriate (Harrison 22e p.295).","Nasal mucus in a viral URI can range anywhere from watery to frankly purulent, so purulent or discolored nasal discharge by itself does not indicate bacterial infection or warrant antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.296).","Symptoms typically begin gradually after a 2-8 day incubation, peak early, and usually last 5-10 days but may persist up to 14 days; patients remain infectious as long as symptoms are present (Harrison 22e pp.296-297).","Adults average 2-4 URIs per year and children 6-10 per year; reinfection is common because immunity to the many rhinovirus and adenovirus serotypes is serotype-specific and immunity to coronaviruses, parainfluenza, RSV, and metapneumovirus is weak or short-lived (Harrison 22e pp.295-296).","Myalgias may occur but are usually less prominent than in influenza, a useful bedside discriminator from influenza (Harrison 22e p.296).","Making a specific viral diagnosis is not practical, cost-effective, or necessary; multiplex RT-PCR panels may be overly sensitive and yield false positives from recent prior infection (Harrison 22e p.296).","URI can be reduced by hand hygiene, physical distancing, masking, isolation of ill persons, and environmental cleaning (Harrison 22e p.295)."],"pathophysiology":"Nonspecific URI is an acute infection of the respiratory tract above the vocal cords, most commonly caused by respiratory viruses acquired through direct contact with infected people, contaminated surfaces, and large and small respiratory droplets. Susceptibility depends on prior exposure and immunity, general health, genetics, microbiome factors, and psychosocial factors including stress. Because immunity is serotype-specific (rhinovirus, adenovirus) or weak/short-lived (coronaviruses, parainfluenza, RSV, metapneumovirus), reinfection is frequent (Harrison 22e pp.295-296).","additionalDifferentials":["Influenza (more prominent myalgias and systemic symptoms)","COVID-19 (SARS-CoV-2 can cause virtually any upper respiratory symptom; new loss of taste or smell appears specific for COVID-19)","Measles (cough, coryza, and conjunctivitis)","Acute HIV infection (sore throat and rash often predominate)","Allergic, inflammatory, autoimmune, or neoplastic causes of upper respiratory symptoms"],"infectionMimics":["Influenza","COVID-19 (SARS-CoV-2)","Acute HIV infection","Measles","Streptococcal pharyngitis","Acute bacterial sinusitis","Acute otitis media"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Allergic rhinitis / allergic upper respiratory symptoms","Other inflammatory upper respiratory conditions","Autoimmune causes of upper respiratory symptoms","Neoplastic causes of upper respiratory symptoms"],"additionalInvestigations":["No specific viral testing is needed for diagnosis; multiplex RT-PCR panels exist but are often unnecessary and may be falsely positive due to recent prior infection (Harrison 22e p.296)"],"redFlags":["Fever above 102 degrees F (38.9 C)","Chest pain not attributable to a pulled muscle","Shortness of breath","Dizziness","Confusion","New ear or sinus pain (suggesting otitis media or bacterial sinusitis complication)","Symptoms lasting longer than 14 days","New loss of taste or smell where SARS-CoV-2 is circulating (suggests COVID-19)"],"prognosis":"Nonspecific URI is self-limited. Symptoms peak early and generally resolve within 5-10 days, though some last up to 14 days. Although the cold can occasionally be complicated by otitis media or bacterial sinusitis, for any individual patient an antibiotic is more likely to cause an adverse reaction than to prevent a complication (Harrison 22e pp.296-297).","pitfalls":["Prescribing antibiotics for a viral URI: they do not shorten illness or prevent complications and risk adverse drug effects, microbiome disruption, C. difficile infection, increased cost, and antibiotic resistance (Harrison 22e p.295).","Interpreting purulent nasal discharge as evidence of bacterial infection; viral URI mucus normally ranges from watery to purulent (Harrison 22e p.296).","Drivers of inappropriate prescribing include time pressure, fear of missing a rare bacterial diagnosis, concern about a rare complication, low salience of antibiotic harms, and the mistaken belief that patients demand antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.295).","Delayed antibiotic prescriptions are conceptually flawed for self-limited respiratory infections, shift decision-making to patients, and send a mixed message; they should be avoided (Harrison 22e p.296).","Procalcitonin testing has been ineffective and CRP testing not durable for reducing inappropriate ambulatory antibiotic use (Harrison 22e p.296).","Many popular cold remedies are ineffective, of questionable benefit, or harmful, including echinacea, zinc, inhaled steam, vitamin C, vitamin D, garlic, antihistamines, intranasal corticosteroids, saline nasal irrigation, and antiviral drugs (Harrison 22e p.296).","Cough and cold medicines should not be used in children under 6 years because of the risk of adverse effects (Harrison 22e p.296)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.295","Harrison 22e p.296","Harrison 22e p.297"],"crossLinks":["SINUSITIS","PHARYNGITIS","ACUTE_BRONCHITIS"]},"VAP":{"name":"Ventilator Associated Pneumonia","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Chap. 131 (Pneumonia) p.1029-1033 and Chap. 147 (Health Care-Associated Infections) p.1147-1148","clinicalPearls":["VAP is defined as pneumonia with onset at or beyond 48 h after the start of mechanical ventilation; the same principles extend to ventilated HAP and to non-ICU HAP (p.1029).","About 35% of all HAP patients require mechanical ventilation, which marks ventilated HAP as a distinct entity; roughly 70% of HAP is acquired outside the ICU and 30% within it (p.1029).","Three sequential steps drive VAP: oropharyngeal colonization with pathogens, aspiration of these organisms into the lower airway, and compromise of host defenses; almost all intubated patients are at least transiently colonized, yet only about one-third go on to develop VAP (p.1030).","Colony counts often rise to high levels days before clinical pneumonia appears, supporting the idea that the final step is overwhelming of host defenses by a large bacterial inoculum (p.1030).","Serial changes in oxygenation may detect VAP earlier than other clinical findings and can also be used to track response to therapy (p.1030).","Clinical manifestations of HAP and VAP are nonspecific: fever, leukocytosis, increased respiratory secretions, consolidation on examination, and a new or changing radiographic infiltrate (p.1030).","Absence of bacteria on a Gram stain of an endotracheal aspirate makes pneumonia an unlikely cause of fever or infiltrates, and absence of an MDR pathogen on culture supports de-escalation of empirical therapy (p.1031).","Clinical improvement, when it occurs, is usually evident within 48-72 h of starting therapy, typically as improved oxygenation; chest radiographs often worsen early in treatment and are therefore less useful than clinical criteria for judging response (p.1033).","P. aeruginosa essentially never causes infection in patients without prior antibiotic exposure; antibiotic exposure is by far the greatest risk factor for replacement of normal oropharyngeal flora by pathogens (p.1030).","On any given day in the ICU, an average of about 10% of patients have pneumonia, the overwhelming majority of which is VAP; reported prevalence ranges from 6 to 52 cases per 100 patients depending on the population (p.1030)."],"pathophysiology":"The endotracheal tube is the single most important risk factor for VAP. While it blocks large-volume aspiration, it worsens microaspiration of secretions that pool above the cuff, and the tube plus suctioning damages tracheal mucosa and promotes tracheal colonization. Pathogenic bacteria form a glycocalyx biofilm on the tube surface that shields them from antibiotics and host defenses; suctioning can dislodge organisms that reinoculate the trachea, and biofilm fragments can embolize to distal airways carrying bacteria. Ventilator circuit tubing can harbor organisms that wash back if manipulated, so circuits are changed only when soiled or with a new patient. In critically ill patients, normal oropharyngeal flora is replaced by pathogens, driven mainly by antibiotic selection pressure, cross-infection, severe systemic illness, and malnutrition. Severely ill patients with sepsis or trauma appear to enter a state of immunoparalysis several days after ICU admission, coinciding with peak VAP risk; the mechanism is unclear but hyperglycemia and frequent transfusions adversely affect immunity (p.1029-1030).","additionalDifferentials":["Purulent tracheobronchitis (especially in chronic ventilator units, where it can impede weaning) (p.1030)"],"infectionMimics":["Fungal pneumonia (requires different antibiotics from those used for bacterial VAP) (p.1031)","Viral pneumonia (requires different antibiotics from those used for bacterial VAP) (p.1031)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Atypical pulmonary edema (p.1031)","Pulmonary contusion (p.1031)","Alveolar hemorrhage (p.1031)","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (p.1031)","Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) (p.1031)","Pulmonary infarction / pulmonary embolism (p.1031)","Non-pulmonary causes of fever and/or leukocytosis: antibiotic-associated diarrhea, central line-associated infection, sinusitis, urinary tract infection, pancreatitis, and drug fever (p.1031)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantitative deep respiratory tract cultures to separate colonization from true infection: more distal sampling is more specific and uses a lower growth threshold; endotracheal aspirate threshold is 10^6 cfu/mL and protected specimen brush threshold is 10^3 cfu/mL, but sensitivity falls with more distal blind sampling (p.1031).","Gram stain of respiratory specimen, differential cell counts, staining for intracellular organisms, and detection of locally elevated infection-related protein levels to increase diagnostic yield (p.1031).","Molecular diagnostic methods, which can exclude a suspected MDR pathogen as a therapy target when results are negative (p.1031).","Serial procalcitonin and other biomarkers, used together with the initial clinical response to help guide therapy duration (p.1032)."],"redFlags":["Worsening oxygenation, tachypnea, tachycardia, and increased minute ventilation in a ventilated patient (p.1030).","Septic shock accompanying suspected VAP (favors broader empirical and combination coverage) (p.1031-1032).","Colony counts above the diagnostic threshold while already on antibiotics, suggesting current therapy is ineffective (p.1031).","Necrotizing pneumonia (e.g., P. aeruginosa or S. aureus) causing significant pulmonary hemorrhage or empyema (p.1032).","VAP complicating COVID-19, which carries higher attributable mortality than other forms of VAP (p.1033)."],"prognosis":"Crude mortality rates for VAP are reported as high as 50-70%, but the clinically meaningful figure is attributable mortality, since many patients have underlying diseases that would be fatal regardless. Attributable mortality exceeded 25% in one matched-cohort study, while more recent estimates are lower (5-10%); VAP complicating COVID-19 has higher attributable mortality. Mortality varies with patient type and ICU; VAP in trauma patients is generally not associated with attributable mortality (these patients were often previously healthy). The causative pathogen matters: MDR pathogens carry significantly greater attributable mortality than non-MDR pathogens, and infection with organisms such as S. maltophilia mainly marks a severely immunocompromised host at high risk. Beyond death, the major complication is prolonged mechanical ventilation with longer ICU and hospital stays; long-term sequelae of necrotizing infection include bronchiectasis and parenchymal scarring with recurrent pneumonia, prolonged oxygen need, a catabolic state, and (in the elderly) loss of independent function (p.1032-1033).","pitfalls":["Applying clinical criteria designed for community-acquired pneumonia consistently overdiagnoses VAP, because of frequent tracheal colonization in intubated patients, multiple alternative causes of radiographic infiltrates, and the high frequency of other fever sources in critically ill patients (p.1030-1031).","No single set of criteria is reliably diagnostic of pneumonia in a ventilated patient, which undermines both prevention and treatment efforts and even estimates of VAP attributable mortality (p.1030).","Recently effective antibiotics given in the 24-48 h before sampling can cause false-negative quantitative cultures; with susceptible organisms a single dose can drop colony counts below threshold, and operating characteristics only normalize after about 3 days (p.1031).","Quantitative cultures can also fall below threshold if obtained very early in infection or if delayed until an effective host response has reduced bacterial counts; the specimen should ideally be taken as soon as VAP is suspected and before antibiotics are started or changed (p.1031).","Recurrent VAP with the same pathogen can occur because endotracheal-tube biofilm allows persistence and reintroduction; however, about half of recurrent Pseudomonas cases are actually due to a new strain (p.1032).","Aggressive early extubation can lead to reintubation and increased aspiration (raising VAP risk), while heavy continuous sedation also increases VAP risk and undersedation risks self-extubation, so tradeoffs must be balanced (p.1033)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not present a formal VAP severity score in this text. Empirical-therapy decisions are instead stratified by (1) the presence of risk factors for MDR pathogens and (2) the predicted risk of death, with a mortality risk of <=15% considered low risk (p.1031).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1029","Harrison 22e p.1030","Harrison 22e p.1031","Harrison 22e p.1032","Harrison 22e p.1033","Harrison 22e p.1147","Harrison 22e p.1148"],"crossLinks":["HAP","CAP","ASPIRATION_PNEUMONIA"]},"VIRAL_HEPATITIS":{"name":"Acute Viral Hepatitis","system":"Tropical Fever / Hepatobiliary","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.350 (Acute Viral Hepatitis), p.2643-2661","clinicalPearls":["Five hepatotropic viruses cause almost all acute viral hepatitis: HAV, HBV, HCV, HDV (delta), and HEV. All are RNA viruses except HBV, which is a DNA virus that replicates via reverse transcription like a retrovirus (p.2643).","All five types produce a clinically similar acute illness, so the type cannot be reliably distinguished by clinical or epidemiologic features alone; specific serologic testing is the accurate way to tell them apart (p.2643, p.2651).","Only the bloodborne agents (HBV, HCV, HDV) can become chronic and progress to cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma; HAV and HEV do not cause chronic liver disease in immunocompetent hosts (p.2643, p.2659-2660).","Hepatitis A is diagnosed during acute illness by detecting IgM anti-HAV; IgM persists ~3 months (rarely 6-12). IgG anti-HAV appears in convalescence, remains detectable indefinitely, and confers lifelong immunity to reinfection (p.2643-2644, p.2656).","The serologic/virologic course of acute hepatitis E mirrors hepatitis A: brief fecal shedding and viremia, an early IgM anti-HEV response dominating ~the first 3 months, then long-lasting IgG anti-HEV (p.2658).","Acute hepatitis can be evaluated with five serologic tests: HBsAg, IgM anti-HAV, IgM anti-HBc, anti-HCV, and HCV RNA (Table 350-6) (p.2657-2658).","HBsAg positive plus IgM anti-HBc indicates acute (or acute reactivation) hepatitis B; HBsAg positive with IgG anti-HBc (IgM negative) indicates chronic HBV (p.2657-2658).","In about 1-5% of acute HBV infections HBsAg is below the detection threshold; in that window the presence of IgM anti-HBc establishes the diagnosis of acute hepatitis B (p.2646, p.2656).","The anti-HBc 'window' period (HBsAg already cleared, anti-HBs not yet detectable) is now rarely encountered because of higher-sensitivity HBsAg/anti-HBs assays (p.2646).","Isolated anti-HBc usually represents remote past HBV infection (IgG class); rarely it reflects low-level viremia with HBsAg below detection, or a false-positive (p.2646).","IgM anti-HBc can reappear with high titer during acute flares of chronic hepatitis B, so IgM vs IgG anti-HBc does not always reliably separate de novo acute hepatitis B from an exacerbation of chronic HBV; history and serial follow-up help (p.2646-2647, p.2657).","HBeAg is a qualitative marker of active HBV replication and high infectivity; HBV DNA is the quantitative marker. HBeAg-positive HBsAg-positive mothers transmit to >90% of offspring versus only 10-15% if anti-HBe positive (p.2645-2646).","Precore/core-promoter mutant HBV cannot make HBeAg, producing HBeAg-negative chronic hepatitis with detectable HBV DNA and anti-HBe; it can run a more aggressive course and is the predominant form in the Mediterranean and Europe (p.2646-2647).","Anti-HCV (IgG) is the available antibody marker (no clinically useful IgM anti-HCV exists); with contemporary assays it is detectable early in acute hepatitis C and persists after recovery and during chronic infection (p.2657).","HCV RNA is the most sensitive test and the diagnostic gold standard; it appears before aminotransferase rise and before anti-HCV. Detectable anti-HCV with negative HCV RNA signifies cleared/cured infection (p.2657-2658).","Recovery from hepatitis C leaves persistent anti-HCV but does NOT protect against reinfection; in people who inject drugs, anti-HCV plus HCV RNA may indicate reinfection rather than chronic infection (p.2657-2658).","Serum aminotransferase levels do not correlate well with the degree of liver cell damage; peak ALT/AST does not predict severity (p.2655-2656).","A low-grade fever (38-39 C) is more typical of hepatitis A and E than of B or C, except when hepatitis B presents with a serum sickness-like syndrome (p.2655).","HDV is a defective virus that requires HBV (HBsAg envelope) for replication; negative HBV serology excludes hepatitis D, and the duration of HDV infection cannot outlast HBV infection (p.2646-2647, p.2658).","In acute hepatitis with HBsAg and anti-HDV, the anti-HBc class clarifies the relationship: IgM anti-HBc indicates simultaneous acute HBV-HDV co-infection, whereas IgG anti-HBc indicates HDV superinfection on chronic HBV (p.2658).","Among healthy adults, acute hepatitis B is self-limited in ~95-99%, whereas acute hepatitis C is self-limited in only ~15-20% (chronicity is the rule for HCV) (p.2655-2656)."],"pathophysiology":"None of the hepatitis viruses is ordinarily directly cytopathic to hepatocytes; the clinical manifestations and outcome of acute liver injury are largely determined by the host immune (chiefly cell-mediated/cytolytic T-cell) response to infected hepatocytes. In hepatitis B the existence of inactive carriers with normal histology and the more severe disease seen with defective cellular immunity support an immune-mediated mechanism; in immunocompetent adults the degree of liver injury correlates with the level of HBV replication (HBV DNA). HBV enters hepatocytes via the sodium taurocholate cotransporting polypeptide (NTCP) receptor and replicates through reverse transcription of a pregenomic RNA, forming covalently closed circular DNA in the nucleus. HDV likewise enters via NTCP but depends on HBV helper function for complete virions and liver injury (p.2643-2645, p.2651).","additionalDifferentials":["Autoimmune hepatitis (suggested by repeated unexplained episodes of acute hepatitis; can also be triggered after self-limited acute hepatitis A, B, or C) (p.2660)","Alcohol-associated hepatitis (aminotransferases usually below ~400 IU/mL with a heavy-drinking history, unlike viral hepatitis) (p.2660)","Drug-induced/toxin-induced liver injury, including acetaminophen, and anesthetic-agent hepatotoxicity (p.2660)","Acute biliary disease: acute cholecystitis, common-duct stone, or ascending cholangitis (RUQ pain, fever, jaundice overlap) (p.2660)","Obstructive jaundice from common-duct stone or pancreatic carcinoma (viral hepatitis in the elderly is often misdiagnosed as this) (p.2660)","Wilson disease and alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency (genetic/metabolic liver disorders) (p.2660)","Metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD/MASH, formerly NAFLD/NASH) (p.2660)","Ischemic (hypoxic) liver injury (a leading cause of aminotransferases >=1000 IU/L, along with drug injury and acute viral hepatitis) (p.2660)"],"infectionMimics":["Infectious mononucleosis / Epstein-Barr virus (p.2660)","Cytomegalovirus hepatitis (p.2643, p.2660)","Herpes simplex virus (p.2660)","Adenovirus (including the unexplained severe pediatric acute hepatitis linked to adenovirus type 41) (p.2643, p.2660)","Coxsackievirus infection (p.2660)","Toxoplasmosis (p.2660)","SARS-CoV-2 (aminotransferase elevations in ~10% of cases and up to half of severe cases) (p.2660)","Leptospirosis (p.2660)","Brucellosis (p.2660)","Disseminated candidiasis (p.2660)","Mycobacterial infection (p.2660)","Pneumocystis infection (p.2660)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Passive hepatic congestion / hypoperfusion from right ventricular failure, shock, severe hypotension, or severe left ventricular failure (p.2660)","Budd-Chiari syndrome (hepatic vein occlusion) and hepatic veno-occlusive disease (p.2660)","Constrictive pericarditis or right atrial myxoma impeding venous return (p.2660)","Acute fatty liver of pregnancy (p.2660)","Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy (p.2660)","Eclampsia (p.2660)","HELLP syndrome (hemolysis, elevated liver tests, low platelets) (p.2660)","Liver metastases mimicking acute or even fulminant hepatitis (rare) (p.2660)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Anti-HCV antibody plus HCV RNA (HCV RNA is the gold standard; needed when anti-HCV is undetectable or in immunosuppressed patients where antibody may be absent) (p.2657-2658)","Anti-HDV (IgM/IgG) and/or HDV RNA in HBsAg-positive patients who are at high risk or have severe/fulminant disease (p.2658, p.2659)","IgM anti-HEV / HEV RNA when standard A/B/C tests are negative and enteric risk factors are present (specialized labs/CDC) (p.2658)","HBeAg and anti-HBe to assess relative infectivity and replication in chronic HBV (p.2656, p.2659)","Quantitative HBV DNA (PCR) to gauge replication level and risk of cirrhosis, decompensation, and HCC in chronic HBV (p.2656-2657)","Serum albumin (a fall is uncommon in uncomplicated acute hepatitis; signals severe disease) (p.2656)","Blood glucose (hypoglycemia can occur in severe hepatitis) (p.2656)","Complete blood count / differential (transient neutropenia and lymphopenia, then relative lymphocytosis with 2-20% atypical lymphocytes) (p.2656)","Serum bilirubin with conjugated/unconjugated fractions (levels >20 mg/dL persisting late suggest severe disease) (p.2655-2656)","Liver biopsy only when the diagnosis is in doubt or chronic/autoimmune hepatitis is suspected; it is rarely needed in acute viral hepatitis (p.2658)"],"redFlags":["Hepatic encephalopathy (confusion, disorientation, somnolence, progression to coma) - hallmark of fulminant hepatitis with very high mortality (>80% in deep coma without transplant) (p.2659)","Markedly prolonged prothrombin time / INR (reflects severe synthetic failure and extensive hepatocellular necrosis) (p.2656, p.2658-2659)","Rapidly shrinking liver size with rapidly rising bilirubin even as aminotransferases fall - indicates massive hepatic necrosis (p.2659)","Ascites and peripheral edema at presentation (poorer prognosis) (p.2658-2659)","Hypoglycemia and falling serum albumin in acute hepatitis (severe hepatocellular disease) (p.2656, p.2658-2659)","Very high or persistently rising serum bilirubin (>20 mg/dL late in the course) (p.2655-2656, p.2659)","Pregnancy with acute hepatitis E - up to 10-20% risk of fulminant hepatitis and death (p.2659, p.2661)","HDV superinfection of chronic hepatitis B - clinical deterioration with substantially increased risk of fulminant hepatitis and death (mortality >20% in 'Labrea fever' outbreaks) (p.2659)","Advanced age or serious underlying disease, especially pre-existing chronic liver disease (raises risk of severe and fulminant course) (p.2658-2659)","Acute hepatitis E or other insult superimposed on chronic liver disease (acute-on-chronic liver failure) (p.2659-2660)"],"prognosis":"Hepatitis A and acute hepatitis B in previously healthy persons usually resolve completely (HBV self-limited in ~95-99% of healthy adults; HAV essentially always), with an overall case-fatality of ~0.1% (1% among hospitalized acute hepatitis B). Hepatitis A and E cause no chronic liver disease in immunocompetent hosts. Acute hepatitis C becomes chronic in ~85-90%; among chronic HCV, cirrhosis develops in up to ~20% over 10-20 years, with an annual decompensation risk ~4% and annual HCC risk ~1-4% (about 3.36%) once cirrhotic - rates declining with effective direct-acting antiviral therapy. Chronic hepatitis B carries an enhanced HCC risk, especially with infection in infancy/childhood and high HBV DNA/HBeAg; risk of cirrhosis and HCC rises with the level of HBV replication. Waterborne hepatitis E (genotypes 1 and 2) in India/Asia has a case-fatality of 1-2%, rising to 10-20% in pregnant women. Acute HBV-HDV co-infection mortality is usually similar to HBV alone (~5% in some injection-drug-user outbreaks), but HDV superinfection of chronic HBV markedly worsens outcome (annual cirrhosis rate ~4% in chronic hepatitis D; annual HCC ~3% once cirrhotic) (p.2658-2660).","pitfalls":["Relying on HBsAg alone can miss acute hepatitis B: in ~1-5% of cases HBsAg is below detection and only IgM anti-HBc reveals the diagnosis (p.2656, p.2657-2658).","IgM anti-HBc can re-emerge during an acute exacerbation of chronic HBV, so it does not always distinguish true acute hepatitis B from a chronic flare (p.2646-2647, p.2657).","False-positive IgM anti-HAV occurs in rheumatologic disease (rheumatoid factor) and rarely in EBV infection; false-positive IgM anti-HBc occurs with high-titer rheumatoid factor (p.2656).","Anti-HCV immunoassays can be falsely positive (low pretest probability, rheumatoid factor); confirm with HCV RNA (p.2657).","In immunosuppressed patients anti-HCV may be undetectable, so HCV RNA testing is required to diagnose hepatitis C (p.2655, p.2657).","HBsAg titer does not reflect disease severity - an inverse correlation exists, with very low HBsAg in fulminant hepatitis (p.2656-2657).","Coexisting HBsAg and anti-HBs in chronic HBV does not signal imminent HBsAg clearance; these patients still have chronic HBV infection (p.2646, p.2657).","A normal or only mildly elevated alkaline phosphatase and normal albumin are expected in uncomplicated acute hepatitis; abnormal values suggest an alternative or complicated course (p.2656).","The serum sickness-like prodrome of acute hepatitis B (arthralgia, rash, angioedema) is often misdiagnosed as a rheumatologic disease before jaundice appears (p.2659).","Retrospective diagnosis of self-limited simultaneous acute HBV-HDV co-infection is difficult because anti-HDV is often transient and disappears once HBsAg clears (p.2658).","Patients with acute viral hepatitis tolerate surgery poorly; biliary disease should be excluded clinically and with imaging before any operation (p.2660)."],"severityClassification":"No formal grading system for acute viral hepatitis is given; severity is judged clinically. Features indicating severe hepatocellular disease warranting prompt hospital admission include prolonged prothrombin time, low serum albumin, hypoglycemia, and very high serum bilirubin; presenting ascites, edema, or encephalopathy indicate a poorer prognosis. Progression to fulminant hepatitis (massive hepatic necrosis) is marked by encephalopathy with rapidly shrinking liver size, rapidly rising bilirubin, and markedly prolonged PT even as aminotransferases fall (p.2658-2659).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2643","Harrison 22e p.2644","Harrison 22e p.2645","Harrison 22e p.2646","Harrison 22e p.2647","Harrison 22e p.2651","Harrison 22e p.2652","Harrison 22e p.2654","Harrison 22e p.2655","Harrison 22e p.2656","Harrison 22e p.2657","Harrison 22e p.2658","Harrison 22e p.2659","Harrison 22e p.2660","Harrison 22e p.2661"],"crossLinks":["toxic_hepatitis","decomp_cirrhosis","hepatic_enceph"]},"VIRAL_MENINGITIS":{"name":"Viral (Aseptic) Meningitis","system":"Central Nervous System","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1166-1169","clinicalPearls":["Profound alterations in consciousness (stupor, coma, marked confusion), seizures, focal neurologic signs, or neuroimaging evidence of parenchymal involvement do NOT occur in viral meningitis; their presence points instead to encephalitis or another CNS process (p.1166).","Headache is almost invariably present, often frontal or retroorbital, with photophobia and pain on eye movement; nuchal rigidity is usually present but may be mild, appearing only near the limit of neck anteflexion (p.1166).","A specific viral cause is identified in 60-90% of cases using combined CSF PCR, culture, and serology; roughly two-thirds of culture-negative 'aseptic' meningitis cases have a viral etiology found by CSF PCR (p.1166).","Enteroviruses account for >85% of cases with an identified etiology and predominate in summer and fall, especially in children <15 years (p.1167-1168).","HSV-2 causes most adult uncomplicated HSV meningitis (HSV-1 causes ~90% of HSV encephalitis); ~20% of HSV meningitis patients develop recurrent attacks, and most recurrent/'aseptic' meningitis, including cases formerly called Mollaret's meningitis, is due to HSV (p.1168).","Many HSV meningitis patients give no history and show no active genital herpes lesions at presentation, so absence of genital lesions does not exclude the diagnosis (p.1168).","VZV is increasingly recognized as a cause of meningitis even WITHOUT rash, so absence of chickenpox or shingles does not exclude it (p.1168).","Cranial nerve palsies (most often V, VII, or VIII) are more common in HIV meningitis than in other viral meningitides; HIV seroconversion may be delayed, so seronegative suspected cases warrant repeat testing (p.1168).","Mumps meningitis is suggested by parotitis, orchitis, oophoritis, pancreatitis, or raised serum amylase/lipase, but their absence does not exclude it; a documented prior mumps infection (lifelong immunity) does exclude it (p.1168-1169).","Consider LCMV when aseptic meningitis occurs in late fall/winter with exposure to house mice or pet/laboratory rodents or their excreta, particularly when accompanied by leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, or abnormal liver function tests (p.1169)."],"pathophysiology":"Viral meningitis is inflammation of the meninges, most often caused by enteroviruses, with a self-limited course in immunocompetent hosts. Inflammation is largely confined to the meninges, producing meningeal irritation without the parenchymal brain involvement that characterizes encephalitis; preservation of higher cerebral function distinguishes meningitis from encephalitis. CSF oligoclonal IgG bands directed against viral proteins can occur, though they are nonspecific (Harrison 22e p.1166-1167).","additionalDifferentials":["Arthropod-borne (arbovirus) meningitis, notably West Nile virus; consider when meningitis/encephalitis clusters occur in a restricted region in summer/early fall (p.1167-1168)","Epstein-Barr virus (may produce aseptic meningitis with or without infectious mononucleosis; atypical lymphocytes in CSF/blood are suggestive) (p.1168)","Human herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6) (p.1167)","Cytomegalovirus (notably in immunocompromised hosts, where it may cause PMN pleocytosis with low glucose) (p.1167)","HSV-1 (less common cause of meningitis than HSV-2) (p.1167)","Lymphocytic choriomeningitis virus (LCMV) (p.1167, 1169)","Mumps (late winter/early spring, male predominance ~3:1) (p.1167, 1168-1169)","Zika and other non-WNV arboviruses (p.1167)","Human parechovirus (included on the FilmArray ME panel) (p.1167)"],"infectionMimics":["Untreated or partially treated bacterial meningitis (a key pitfall when CSF shifts toward lymphocytic predominance) (p.1167)","Early fungal meningitis (lymphocytic pleocytosis common; hypoglycorrhachia may be absent early) (p.1167)","Early mycobacterial / tuberculous meningitis (p.1167)","Neurosyphilis (Treponema pallidum) (p.1167)","Mycoplasma pneumoniae CNS infection (p.1166-1167)","Listeria spp. (p.1167)","Brucella spp. (p.1167)","Coxiella spp. (p.1167)","Leptospira spp. (p.1167)","Rickettsia spp. (p.1167)","Parameningeal infections (p.1167)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Neoplastic meningitis (p.1167)","Medication-induced hypersensitivity (drug-induced) meningitis (p.1167)","Systemic lupus erythematosus and other rheumatologic diseases (p.1167)","Sarcoidosis (p.1167)","Behcet's syndrome (p.1167)","Uveomeningitic syndromes (p.1167)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CSF reverse transcriptase PCR (RT-PCR) for enteroviruses: diagnostic procedure of choice, highest sensitivity if done within 48 h of symptom onset, falling rapidly after day 5 (p.1166, 1168)","FilmArray Meningitis/Encephalitis multiplex PCR panel covering HSV-1, HSV-2, enteroviruses, VZV, HHV-6, and human parechovirus (does NOT include CMV, WNV, LCMV, or EBV) (p.1166)","WNV-specific CSF IgM (more sensitive than CSF RT-PCR for WNV; RT-PCR ~70% sensitive, useful mainly in immunocompromised patients with poor antibody response) (p.1166, 1168)","Arboviral serology demonstrating CSF viral-specific IgM or seroconversion; repeat testing if early study is negative and suspicion is high (p.1168)","Throat washing and stool PCR/culture to support enteroviral (and Mycoplasma) diagnosis (stool/throat shedding may persist for weeks) (p.1166-1167)","Demonstration of intrathecal HSV-specific or VZV-specific antibody synthesis (less sensitive/specific than PCR; may take >1 week to become positive) (p.1168)","Routine bloods: complete blood count with differential, liver and renal function tests, ESR, C-reactive protein, electrolytes, glucose, creatine kinase, aldolase, amylase, and lipase (p.1167)","Neuroimaging (MRI preferred over CT) when there is altered consciousness, seizures, focal signs, atypical CSF profile, or immunocompromise (not mandatory in uncomplicated viral meningitis) (p.1167)","Serum procalcitonin >0.5 ng/mL as a clue favoring bacterial over aseptic meningitis (p.1167)"],"redFlags":["Depressed or markedly altered consciousness (stupor, coma, marked confusion) — suggests encephalitis or alternative diagnosis, not simple viral meningitis (p.1166)","Seizures (p.1166)","Focal neurologic signs/symptoms or neuroimaging abnormalities indicating parenchymal brain involvement (p.1166)","CSF PMN pleocytosis without an established diagnosis — should prompt consideration of bacterial or parameningeal infection (p.1166-1167)","Lymphocytic pleocytosis WITH low CSF glucose (hypoglycorrhachia) — should raise suspicion of fungal, tuberculous, or Listeria meningoencephalitis, or noninfectious causes such as sarcoid or neoplastic meningitis (p.1167)","Atypical CSF profile or immunocompromising condition/treatment — warrants hospitalization and neuroimaging (p.1167)"],"prognosis":"In adults the prognosis for full recovery from viral meningitis is excellent; treatment of almost all cases is primarily symptomatic. Rare adult patients have persisting headache, mild mental impairment, incoordination, or generalized asthenia for weeks to months. Outcome is less certain in infants and neonates (<1 year), in whom intellectual impairment, learning disabilities, hearing loss, and other lasting sequelae have been reported. Enteroviral meningitis outside the neonatal period is usually benign with recovery without sequelae, but chronic/severe infection can occur in neonates and in people with hypo- or agammaglobulinemia (Harrison 22e p.1168-1169).","pitfalls":["Failing to recognize partially or incompletely treated bacterial meningitis, which can produce a lymphocytic-predominant CSF that mimics viral meningitis (p.1167)","Up to 15% of enteroviral meningitis patients (most often young infants) have a NORMAL CSF leukocyte count, so a normal cell count does not exclude the diagnosis (p.1168)","PMNs may predominate during the first 48 h of viral meningitis (e.g., echovirus 9, WNV, EEE, mumps); WNV PMN pleocytosis occurs in ~45% and can persist a week or more before shifting to lymphocytes — do not assume early neutrophilia means bacterial infection (p.1167-1168)","Treating a normal CSF glucose as ruling out viral meningitis is wrong, but conversely low CSF glucose can occur in 10-30% of mumps or LCMV and rarely with enteroviruses, HSV-2, or VZV (p.1167)","CSF viral culture has generally poor sensitivity for viral meningitis (unlike its utility in bacterial infection), so reliance on culture alone underdiagnoses (p.1167)","Enterovirus detected in stool is NOT diagnostic of acute CNS infection — it may reflect residual shedding from a prior infection or asymptomatic carriage during epidemics (p.1167)","Enteroviral RT-PCR has low sensitivity for EV71; specific testing may be required (p.1168)","EBV CSF PCR false-positives may reflect viral reactivation or latent viral DNA in recruited lymphocytes rather than true EBV meningitis (p.1168)","Routine serology is unhelpful for high-seroprevalence viruses (HSV, VZV, CMV, EBV) (p.1167)"],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not give a formal severity grading for viral meningitis; it instead frames disposition by risk. Immunocompetent patients with a classic CSF profile (lymphocytic pleocytosis, normal glucose, negative Gram stain), no focal signs, and no significant alteration in consciousness may not require hospitalization if home monitoring and follow-up are assured. Hospitalization is indicated for immunocompromised patients, those with significant alteration in consciousness, seizures, or focal signs suggesting encephalitis/parenchymal involvement, and those with an atypical CSF profile (Harrison 22e p.1169).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1166","Harrison 22e p.1167","Harrison 22e p.1168","Harrison 22e p.1169"],"crossLinks":["MENINGITIS","ENCEPHALITIS"]},"aaa":{"name":"Ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm","system":"Vascular emergency","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.154, p.2207-2212","clinicalPearls":["Most abdominal aortic aneurysms are asymptomatic and are found as an incidental imaging finding or as a palpable, pulsatile, expansile, nontender mass on routine abdominal exam (p.2211).","New aneurysmal pain is usually a harbinger of rupture and constitutes a medical emergency; however, acute rupture more often occurs without any prior warning (p.2211).","Acute pain plus hypotension is the presentation of frank rupture and mandates emergency open or endovascular repair (p.2211).","At least 90% of abdominal aortic aneurysms >4.0 cm are atherosclerotic, and most lie below the level of the renal arteries (infrarenal) (p.2211).","Cigarette smoking is the most potent modifiable risk factor; incidence rises with age and the condition is more common in men (p.2211).","Mural thrombus within the aneurysm sac can be a source of peripheral (distal) embolization (p.2211)."],"pathophysiology":"Aneurysm is a pathologic dilation of the aortic wall; a true aneurysm involves all three wall layers, versus a pseudoaneurysm in which intima and media are disrupted and the dilated segment is contained only by adventitia (and sometimes perivascular clot). Most abdominal aortic aneurysms are degenerative and atherosclerotic. Wall failure results from degradation or abnormal production of the structural matrix proteins elastin and collagen, driven by inflammation, oxidative stress, proteolysis, and biomechanical wall stress. B- and T-lymphocytes, macrophages, inflammatory cytokines, and matrix metalloproteinases degrade elastin and collagen, reducing tensile strength and the aorta's ability to tolerate pulsatile stretch; histology shows elastin/collagen destruction, loss of vascular smooth muscle, neovascularization, and inflammation (p.2208).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute mesenteric ischemia (SMA embolism/thrombosis) — also causes severe diffuse abdominal pain, classically pain out of proportion to exam findings (p.154).","Thoracic / thoracoabdominal aortic aneurysm, which is often contiguous with infradiaphragmatic disease (p.2208)."],"infectionMimics":["Mycotic (infective) aortic aneurysm from staphylococcal, streptococcal, Salmonella, or fungal seeding — typically saccular and often with positive blood cultures (p.2209).","Syphilitic aortitis — uncommon cause, predominantly involves the ascending aorta/arch rather than the abdominal aorta (p.2209).","Tuberculous aneurysm — typically affects the thoracic aorta via contiguous spread from hilar nodes or abscess (p.2209)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Renal/ureteric colic — rupturing AAA pain radiating to flank or genitalia can mimic urolithiasis (p.154).","Rectus sheath hematoma — abdominal wall mass and pain, especially on anticoagulants (p.154)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Abdominal ultrasound — delineates transverse and longitudinal dimensions, can detect mural thrombus, and is the tool for serial size surveillance and screening (p.2211).","Plain abdominal radiograph — may show a calcified aneurysm outline, but ~25% are non-calcified and not visualized (p.2211).","Contrast-enhanced CT or MRI — accurate for location and size and for planning open or endovascular repair (p.2211).","Contrast aortography may underestimate true diameter when mural thrombus reduces luminal size, and carries risks of bleeding, allergy, and atheroembolism (p.2211)."],"redFlags":["New or worsening aneurysmal pain (abdomen, lower back, chest, or scrotum) signals impending rupture (p.2211).","Abdominal pain radiating to sacrum, flank, or genitalia should always raise suspicion of a rupturing AAA (p.154).","Acute pain with hypotension indicates rupture — emergency repair required (p.2211)."],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on aneurysm size and on the severity of coexisting coronary and cerebrovascular disease. Rupture risk rises sharply with diameter: the 5-year rupture risk is roughly 1-2% for aneurysms <5 cm versus 20-40% for aneurysms >5 cm. At smaller diameters the rupture risk is higher in women than in men (p.2211-2212).","pitfalls":["A rupturing AAA can be misattributed to renal/ureteric colic when pain radiates to the flank or genitalia (p.154).","Plain x-ray cannot exclude an aneurysm because roughly a quarter are non-calcified (p.2211).","Aortography may underestimate aneurysm diameter when mural thrombus narrows the lumen (p.2211).","Premonitory pain may persist for several days before rupture and collapse, so an evolving symptomatic aneurysm should not be dismissed (p.154)."],"severityClassification":"Rupture-risk stratification is driven by maximal aortic diameter. Per Harrison: a true abdominal aortic aneurysm is dilation 50% greater than the adjacent normal segment; aneurysms >=4.0 cm affect 1-2% of men >50. 5-year rupture risk is ~1-2% for <5 cm and ~20-40% for >5 cm. Repair thresholds: any size if rapidly expanding or symptomatic; for asymptomatic aneurysms repair is indicated at >=5.5 cm in men and >=5.0 cm in women, with smaller aneurysms followed by ultrasound surveillance (p.2210-2212).","references":["Harrison 22e p.154","Harrison 22e p.2208","Harrison 22e p.2210","Harrison 22e p.2211","Harrison 22e p.2212"],"crossLinks":["aortic_dissection","renal_colic","hypovolemic_shock","mesenteric_ischemia"]},"acs":{"name":"Acute coronary syndrome","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.145-152 (Chap. 15, Chest Discomfort)","clinicalPearls":["ACS encompasses unstable angina, NSTEMI, and STEMI, and the term is generally reserved for ischemia precipitated by acute coronary atherothrombosis (Harrison 22e p.145).","Unstable ischemic heart disease is sub-classified by whether acute myocardial injury is detectable and whether ST-segment elevation is present: unstable angina = no detectable acute injury; NSTEMI/STEMI = acute injury without/with ST elevation (Harrison 22e p.145).","Type 1 MI (acute coronary thrombosis) is distinguished from type 2 MI, which results from another imbalance of myocardial oxygen supply and demand; type 2 may be driven by anemia, hypoxia, hypotension, or intense psychological stress (Harrison 22e p.145, p.150).","An ECG is recommended within 10 minutes of presentation, primarily to identify ST-segment elevation candidates for immediate reperfusion (Harrison 22e p.150).","Resting ECG sensitivity for ischemia is poor (as low as ~20%), so a normal ECG does not exclude ACS; serial ECGs every 30-60 min are recommended early in suspected ACS (Harrison 22e p.150).","Physical examination findings may be entirely normal in unstable ischemic heart disease, so an unremarkable exam is not reassuring (Harrison 22e p.149).","Relief of pain by nitroglycerin is neither sensitive nor specific for ischemia (esophageal spasm also responds); relief delayed beyond ~10 min suggests either a non-ischemic cause or severe ischemia such as acute MI (Harrison 22e p.149).","Chest wall tenderness on palpation does not exclude myocardial ischemia (Harrison 22e p.150)."],"pathophysiology":"Myocardial ischemia (angina pectoris) arises from an imbalance between myocardial oxygen demand (raised by heart rate, ventricular wall stress, and contractility) and oxygen supply (set by coronary blood flow and arterial oxygen content). ACS is typically precipitated by acute coronary atherothrombosis. When ischemia is sufficiently severe and prolonged (as little as ~20 min), irreversible cellular injury and MI occur. Symptoms can also be driven predominantly by increased demand (e.g., intense psychological stress) or reduced delivery (anemia, hypoxia, hypotension), underlying the type 2 MI concept (Harrison 22e p.145).","additionalDifferentials":["Stable angina (chronic, exertional)","Esophageal spasm (can closely mimic angina and may respond to nitroglycerin)","Gastroesophageal reflux / peptic ulcer disease","Costochondritis / chest wall (musculoskeletal) syndromes","Cervical radiculitis / disk disease","Panic disorder and related psychiatric conditions (up to ~10% of ED chest pain)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute pericarditis (pleuritic pain, relieved by sitting forward, friction rub, diffuse ST elevation with PR depression)","Acute myocarditis (non-ischemic myocardial injury; troponin may rise but should not be labeled MI)","Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy (abrupt chest pain/dyspnea after emotional or physical stress; may show ST elevation and elevated biomarkers mimicking MI)","Acute aortic syndrome / aortic dissection (sudden tearing or knifelike pain, may radiate to back)","Pulmonary embolism (massive PE can cause substernal pain mimicking MI)","Pulmonary hypertension (anginal-quality chest pain)","Tension pneumothorax","Esophageal rupture (Boerhaave) / Mallory-Weiss"],"additionalInvestigations":["High-sensitivity cardiac troponin at presentation, repeated at 1-3 h (1-2 h rapid rule-out possible with hs-assays); conventional troponin repeated at 3-6 h","Right-sided ECG leads when ischemia is suspected but standard 12-lead is nondiagnostic","Chest radiograph (often normal in ACS; may show pulmonary edema; helps exclude pneumothorax, wide mediastinum)","Echocardiography for regional wall-motion abnormality, mechanical complications, or tamponade when diagnosis is uncertain or there is hemodynamic instability","Coronary CT angiography for low-intermediate probability (high negative predictive value; also excludes dissection, effusion, PE)","Stress nuclear perfusion imaging or stress echocardiography for intermediate-risk patients eligible for further testing","Cardiac MRI to characterize troponin elevation without definite CAD and to discriminate ischemic from non-ischemic injury","B-type natriuretic peptide and D-dimer as adjuncts (for heart failure and PE exclusion respectively)"],"redFlags":["ST-segment elevation on ECG (candidate for immediate reperfusion)","ST-segment depression or symmetric T-wave inversions >=0.2 mV (higher risk of death or recurrent ischemia)","Hypotension and significant tachycardia (suggest cardiogenic shock or other severe cause)","New murmur of mitral regurgitation or ventricular septal defect (mechanical complication of STEMI)","Pulmonary edema / S3 / rales (high-risk LV dysfunction)","Pain prolonged >=30 min and not relieved by rest (suggests MI rather than stable angina)","Syncope or presyncope with chest pain (consider ischemic arrhythmia, also PE and dissection)"],"prognosis":"Among unselected ED chest-pain populations, only ~10-20% (as low as 5%) are ultimately diagnosed with ACS, while gastrointestinal causes predominate; historically 2-6% of patients discharged with presumed non-ischemic pain were later found to have had a missed MI. In patients stratified as low risk (excluding ST elevation or definite noncardiac pain), the estimated 30-day major cardiovascular event rate was ~2.5%. Validated clinical decision pathways combining ECG and serial high-sensitivity troponin can achieve ACS miss rates <0.5%, and a single very low hs-troponin >2-3 h after symptom onset carries a negative predictive value >99% for MI (Harrison 22e p.145, p.150-151).","pitfalls":["Relying on a normal resting ECG or unremarkable physical exam to exclude ACS - both can be normal in unstable ischemic heart disease (Harrison 22e p.149-150).","Treating nitroglycerin response as diagnostic - esophageal spasm also responds and ischemic relief is non-specific (Harrison 22e p.149).","Attributing pain to the chest wall because palpation reproduces it - tenderness does not exclude ischemia (Harrison 22e p.150).","Some patients with ischemia deny 'pain' and report only dyspnea or vague anxiety; women and the elderly may present with anginal equivalents (dyspnea, diaphoresis, nausea, fatigue, faintness) (Harrison 22e p.149).","Inferior MI may present with nausea/vomiting (vagal / Bezold-Jarisch reflex), simulating a GI disorder (Harrison 22e p.149).","High-sensitivity troponin detects injury in many non-ACS cardiopulmonary conditions; without a dynamic rise/fall and clinical correlation, a single elevated value can be misinterpreted as MI - chronic elevation occurs with structural heart disease, end-stage renal disease, or interfering antibodies (Harrison 22e p.150).","Non-ischemic injury (e.g., myocarditis) can raise troponin but should not be labeled MI (Harrison 22e p.150).","Confusing diagnostic decision-aids (HEART, EDACS) with prognostic risk scores (TIMI, GRACE), which are intended for patients with already-established ACS, not for diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.151)."],"severityClassification":"ACS is categorized along two clinical decision-making lines. (1) Diagnostic/disposition risk in undifferentiated chest pain uses clinical decision pathways such as the HEART score (history, ECG, age, risk factors; low risk 0-3) and EDACS, combined with serial high-sensitivity troponin, to classify patients as low/intermediate/high risk. (2) Prognostic risk in established ACS uses the TIMI and GRACE risk scores (Chap. 286) to estimate event risk - these are explicitly not intended for diagnostic use. Common high-risk elements across tools: typical ACS symptoms, older age, known/atherosclerotic risk factors, ischemic ECG changes, and elevated troponin (Harrison 22e p.151).","references":["Harrison 22e p.145","Harrison 22e p.146","Harrison 22e p.147","Harrison 22e p.148","Harrison 22e p.149","Harrison 22e p.150","Harrison 22e p.151","Harrison 22e p.152"],"crossLinks":["pericarditis","aortic_dissection","pe","heart_failure"]},"acute_leukemia":{"name":"Acute leukaemia","system":"Hematology / Oncology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"825-834 (AML, Ch.109), 844-848 (ALL, Ch.111), 591-593 (oncologic emergencies: leukostasis, tumor lysis syndrome), 575 (febrile neutropenia)","clinicalPearls":["AML accounts for ~31% of new acute leukaemias but ~62% of leukaemic deaths; it is the commonest acute leukaemia in older adults (median age ~69), whereas ALL peaks in young children (age 3-4) and again in the elderly (Harrison 22e p.825, p.844).","Presentation is usually nonspecific and reflects marrow failure: fatigue (often the first symptom), anorexia/weight loss, bleeding/easy bruising, and fever; about half of AML patients have symptoms for 3 months or less before diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.828).","Auer rods (rod-shaped cytoplasmic granules) on the blood film establish a myeloid lineage with certainty and are not seen in ALL (Harrison 22e p.828).","Gum (gingival) hypertrophy and skin/soft-tissue infiltration are characteristic of monocytic AML subtypes and KMT2A-rearranged disease (Harrison 22e p.828).","A myeloid sarcoma (chloroma) is a tissue mass of myeloid blasts (skin, lymph node, GI tract, soft tissue, testis); isolated myeloid sarcoma rapidly progresses to marrow/blood involvement and cannot be cured by local therapy alone (Harrison 22e p.828).","Median presenting WBC in AML is ~15,000/uL; 20% present with >100,000/uL and <5% have no circulating blasts. In ALL ~40% have normal or low WBC and 8% have no circulating blasts, so an automated count can miss the diagnosis - always review the smear (Harrison 22e p.828, p.844).","Diagnosis requires examination of blood AND bone marrow; biopsy distinguishes AML from ALL, and cytochemistry, flow-cytometric immunophenotyping, cytogenetics and molecular studies are all needed for subtyping (Harrison 22e p.828, p.844).","Diagnosis no longer rests on the historical >=20% blast threshold alone - the 2022 WHO classification removed the blast-percent requirement for AML defined by specific genetic lesions such as t(15;17), t(8;21), inv(16) and NPM1 mutation (Harrison 22e p.826).","DIC and coagulopathy are classically associated with acute promyelocytic leukaemia (t(15;17)/PML-RARA); APL bleeding can be catastrophic (GI, intrapulmonary, intracranial), and paradoxically thrombosis can also occur (Harrison 22e p.828).","CSF examination is routine in ALL: CNS leukaemia is defined by >=5 cells/uL or blasts on morphology; CNS involvement is present at diagnosis in 5-10% of adults (higher in mature B-ALL and T-ALL). Defer/limit lumbar puncture if platelets <20 x10^9/L or active bleeding (Harrison 22e p.844-845)."],"pathophysiology":"Acute leukaemia is a clonal proliferation of immature, poorly differentiated haematopoietic precursors (blasts) that infiltrate marrow, blood and other tissues; AML arises from myeloid progenitors and ALL from lymphoid (B- or T-lineage) progenitors. Genome sequencing indicates most AML arises from a small number of mutations that accumulate with age - up to 5-6% of normal people over 70 carry premalignant clones (clonal haematopoiesis of indeterminate potential, CHIP, commonly DNMT3A/TET2/ASXL1), which carries a ~10-fold increased risk of haematologic malignancy though additional 'hits' are required to progress to overt leukaemia. Genetic lesions drive both phenotype and behaviour: for example t(15;17)/PML-RARA defines APL and predisposes to DIC, while NPM1 and FLT3 mutations associate with high leukocyte counts. Marrow replacement by blasts produces anaemia, neutropenia and thrombocytopenia, accounting for the dominant clinical features (Harrison 22e p.825-828, p.844).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute myeloid leukaemia vs acute lymphoblastic leukaemia - distinguished by morphology (Auer rods imply AML), cytochemistry, flow-cytometric immunophenotype and genetics (Harrison 22e p.828, p.844)","Myelodysplastic syndrome or myeloproliferative neoplasm evolving to acute leukaemia (antecedent haematologic disorder) (Harrison 22e p.828)","Therapy-related (secondary) acute leukaemia after alkylating agents (4-6 yr) or topoisomerase II inhibitors (1-3 yr) (Harrison 22e p.826)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral blood smear (Wright-Giemsa) with manual differential - essential at presentation; look for blasts and Auer rods (Harrison 22e p.828, p.844)","Bone marrow aspirate and biopsy for morphology, cytochemistry, flow-cytometric immunophenotyping, cytogenetics and molecular studies (Harrison 22e p.829, p.844)","Cytogenetics/karyotype and molecular panel at diagnosis (NPM1, FLT3-ITD/TKD, CEBPA, TP53, IDH1/IDH2, RUNX1 etc.) - the most important prognostic data in AML (Harrison 22e p.827-828)","Serum uric acid, electrolytes including potassium, phosphate and calcium, creatinine/BUN, and LDH to assess and monitor tumour-lysis risk (Harrison 22e p.829, p.593)","Coagulation panel - PT, aPTT, fibrinogen and D-dimer - to detect DIC, especially when APL is suspected (Harrison 22e p.829)","Measurable (minimal) residual disease assessment by multiparameter flow cytometry or molecular methods (e.g. PCR for PML-RARA in APL) for response monitoring (Harrison 22e p.827, p.834, p.847)","Lumbar puncture/CSF cytology in ALL (and AML with neurologic symptoms) to detect CNS involvement (Harrison 22e p.845)","Urinalysis for uric acid crystals; urinary uric acid:creatinine ratio >1 supports acute uric-acid nephropathy (Harrison 22e p.593)"],"redFlags":["Suspected APL (ecchymoses/oozing from IV sites, DIC) - a haematologic emergency; coagulopathy can be rapidly fatal and warrants prompt differentiation therapy (Harrison 22e p.828, p.834)","Hyperleukocytosis with blast count >100,000/uL and leukostasis - brain (stupor, headache, visual change, confusion, coma, sudden death) or lung (dyspnoea, hypoxaemia, respiratory failure); a potentially fatal emergency, especially in AML (Harrison 22e p.591)","Tumour lysis syndrome - hyperkalaemia (risk of fatal arrhythmia), hyperphosphataemia, hypocalcaemia (tetany), hyperuricaemia and acute kidney injury (Harrison 22e p.592-593)","Febrile neutropenia (fever with granulocytes <1500/uL) - medical emergency requiring urgent empirical antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.575)","CNS symptoms - headache, visual change, focal/non-focal neurologic deficit - may indicate CNS leukaemia or intracranial bleed (Harrison 22e p.829-830)"],"prognosis":"Outcome is dominated by age and genetics. In AML, cytogenetic/molecular findings at diagnosis are the strongest predictors: t(15;17)/APL has a very good prognosis (~85% cured), core-binding-factor AML (t(8;21), inv(16)) is favourable (~55% cured), normal-karyotype AML is intermediate (~40% cured), and TP53 mutation, complex karyotype, t(6;9), inv(3) or monosomy 7 are adverse with little chance of cure without transplant. NPM1 mutation without FLT3-ITD is favourable, whereas FLT3-ITD is adverse. Advancing age, poor performance status, antecedent haematologic disorder/preceding cytopenias, therapy-related disease and high presenting WBC all worsen outcome; only ~32% of AML patients survive 5 years overall. In ALL, outcome is strongly age-dependent - cure rates ~90% in children falling to <10% in elderly/frail adults; achievement of molecular complete remission (MRD negativity) is the most important on-treatment prognostic factor (Harrison 22e p.825-828, p.846-847).","pitfalls":["Relying on an automated blood count can miss acute leukaemia - 8% of ALL patients (and <5% of AML) have no circulating blasts; review the smear (Harrison 22e p.828, p.844).","In hyperleukocytosis, arterial blood gas oxygen tension can be spuriously low because the high WBC mass consumes plasma oxygen in the sample; use pulse oximetry to assess oxygenation (Harrison 22e p.591).","High leukocyte and platelet counts can cause pseudohyperkalaemia from cell lysis after the blood is drawn (no ECG changes) - follow plasma rather than serum potassium (Harrison 22e p.593).","Avoid unnecessary RBC transfusion and intravascular volume depletion in hyperleukocytosis - both raise blood viscosity and can worsen leukostasis (Harrison 22e p.591).","Urinary alkalinisation with sodium bicarbonate is no longer recommended for tumour-lysis prophylaxis (Harrison 22e p.593).","Even minor bleeding in a thrombocytopenic leukaemia patient justifies immediate platelet transfusion because qualitative platelet dysfunction and infection increase bleeding risk independent of the count (Harrison 22e p.829)."],"severityClassification":"AML is classified by the WHO 2022 system primarily on defining genetic abnormalities (e.g. PML-RARA, RUNX1-RUNX1T1, CBFB-MYH11, KMT2A rearrangement, NPM1 or CEBPA mutation) and, where genetics are absent, by differentiation; the historical >=20%-blast diagnostic threshold has been removed for genetically defined entities. Prognostic risk is stratified by the 2022 European LeukemiaNet (ELN) scheme into favourable, intermediate and adverse categories based on cytogenetic and molecular findings at diagnosis (APL is risk-stratified separately, low- vs high-risk by presenting WBC, threshold 10,000/uL). ALL is subtyped immunologically into B-lineage and T-lineage, with risk stratification (standard vs high) using age, WBC count, immunophenotype, cytogenetics/molecular lesions (notably Ph/BCR-ABL) and MRD status (Harrison 22e p.826-827, p.834, p.846-847).","references":["Harrison 22e p.575","Harrison 22e p.591","Harrison 22e p.592","Harrison 22e p.593","Harrison 22e p.825","Harrison 22e p.826","Harrison 22e p.827","Harrison 22e p.828","Harrison 22e p.829","Harrison 22e p.830","Harrison 22e p.834","Harrison 22e p.844","Harrison 22e p.845","Harrison 22e p.846","Harrison 22e p.847"],"crossLinks":["dic","FEBRILE_NEUTROPENIA","hyperkalemia"]},"adrenal_crisis":{"name":"Adrenal crisis","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.636, p.2353, p.3110-3112, p.3133","clinicalPearls":["Adrenal crisis typically emerges after a prolonged prodrome of nonspecific complaints rather than appearing out of the blue (p.3111).","Crisis is more frequent in PRIMARY adrenal insufficiency because both glucocorticoid AND mineralocorticoid secretion are lost (p.3111).","Postural hypotension can progress to frank hypovolaemic shock (p.3111).","Prospective data: about 8.3 adrenal crises per 100 patient-years and roughly 0.5 crisis-related deaths per 100 patient-years (p.3111).","Adrenal crisis is classified as a form of endocrine/distributive shock (p.2353).","In suspected crisis, it is reasonable to draw a baseline cortisol (and ACTH), start replacement, and defer formal stimulation testing — diagnostic testing must never delay treatment (p.3111).","Starting thyroid hormone replacement in an undiagnosed patient can precipitate adrenal crisis, because thyroid hormone shortens cortisol half-life; screen for adrenal insufficiency before treating hypothyroidism in possible polyendocrine syndrome (p.3133)."],"pathophysiology":"Acute adrenal insufficiency reflects an absolute or relative deficiency of cortisol, in primary disease compounded by loss of aldosterone (mineralocorticoid). Glucocorticoid deficiency impairs vascular tone, gluconeogenesis and loss of feedback inhibition of AVP release (contributing to hyponatraemia and hypoglycaemia), while mineralocorticoid deficiency causes renal salt wasting with volume depletion, hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia and rising creatinine. The result is hypotension that may decompensate into hypovolaemic shock. Crisis is usually unmasked by a stressor that exceeds the inadequate cortisol reserve — intercurrent illness, surgery or other stress, or increased glucocorticoid inactivation as in hyperthyroidism (p.3111).","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of distributive/endocrine shock (septic, anaphylactic, neurogenic) (p.2353)","Hypovolaemic and cardiogenic shock (p.2353)"],"infectionMimics":["Acute abdomen-like presentation with abdominal tenderness, nausea, vomiting and fever can mimic an intra-abdominal infection (p.3111)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute abdomen / surgical abdomen (abdominal pain, tenderness, nausea, vomiting) (p.3111)","Primary neurologic disease — decreased responsiveness progressing to stupor and coma (p.3111)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Baseline serum cortisol AND plasma ACTH drawn before starting steroids, with formal testing deferred (p.3111)","Short cosyntropin (ACTH stimulation) test once stabilised — failure cutoff roughly cortisol <450-500 nmol/L (16-18 ug/dL) at 30-60 min (p.3111)","Plasma renin to confirm mineralocorticoid deficiency in primary disease (p.3111)","Adrenal steroid autoantibodies to assess for autoimmune adrenalitis (p.3111)","Adrenal CT for hemorrhage, infiltration or masses if autoantibodies negative (p.3111)","Very-long-chain fatty acids in autoantibody-negative males to exclude X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy (p.3111)","Hypothalamic-pituitary MRI when ACTH is inappropriately low (secondary cause) (p.3111)","Associated labs: hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia, hypoglycaemia, raised creatinine, normochromic anaemia, lymphocytosis, eosinophilia (p.3110)"],"redFlags":["Refractory or postural hypotension progressing to hypovolaemic shock (p.3111)","Decreased responsiveness, stupor or coma (p.3111)","Hyperpigmentation suggesting underlying primary adrenal insufficiency (p.3110)"],"prognosis":"Acute adrenal insufficiency is potentially lethal (p.636). Even with established care, crises and crisis-related deaths continue to occur (~8.3 crises and ~0.5 deaths per 100 patient-years) (p.3111).","pitfalls":["Do NOT let diagnostic testing delay treatment in suspected crisis (p.3111).","Random/baseline serum cortisol has limited diagnostic value because levels may be coincidentally low from normal diurnal rhythm, and many secondary cases have near-normal baseline cortisol that only fails on stimulation testing (p.3111).","Do NOT start thyroid hormone before excluding/treating adrenal insufficiency — it can precipitate crisis (p.3133).","Hyperthyroidism increases glucocorticoid inactivation and can trigger crisis (p.3111).","Adrenal insufficiency can be masked by concurrent primary hypothyroidism, which prolongs cortisol half-life (p.3133)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.636","Harrison 22e p.2353","Harrison 22e p.3110","Harrison 22e p.3111","Harrison 22e p.3133"],"crossLinks":["SEPTIC_SHOCK","anaphylaxis","hyponatremia","hyperkalemia","hypoglycemia"]},"aki":{"name":"Acute kidney injury","system":"Nephrology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Chap. 55 (Azotemia and Urinary Abnormalities), printed pp. 339-342","clinicalPearls":["AKI is conventionally categorised by site of insult: prerenal (perfusion), intrinsic/renal (vascular, glomerular, tubulointerstitial), and postrenal (obstruction); excluding pre- and post-renal causes points to an intrinsic parenchymal disease (p.340).","Reduced renal perfusion (prerenal) accounts for roughly 40-80% of cases and is typically readily reversible if perfusion is restored promptly (p.340).","Urinary tract obstruction (postrenal) accounts for under 5% of cases but is usually reversible and should be excluded early, generally with renal ultrasound looking for ureteric/pelvic dilatation (p.340).","Ischaemic and toxic acute tubular necrosis (ATN) together account for about 90% of intrinsic AKI (p.340).","Prerenal azotaemia and ATN lie on a spectrum of hypoperfusion: prerenal reverses promptly with restored perfusion, whereas ATN involves structural tubular injury (p.340).","Microscopic examination of urine sediment is a key bedside test in AKI; dark (muddy-brown) granular casts and/or tubular epithelial cells strongly suggest ATN (p.340).","Because a single kidney can clear adequately, complete obstructive AKI requires bladder-outlet/urethral obstruction, bilateral ureteric obstruction, or unilateral obstruction in a solitary kidney (p.340).","RBC casts in the urine indicate glomerular disease (acute nephritic picture) and are an indication for early renal biopsy; they are highly specific but very insensitive for glomerulonephritis (p.341).","The kidney is particularly vulnerable to toxic injury because it receives about 25% of cardiac output and concentrates and metabolises toxins (p.340).","Distinguishing acute from chronic kidney disease can be difficult because anaemia, hypocalcaemia and hyperphosphataemia occur in both; small echogenic kidneys with cortical thinning on ultrasound and radiographic renal osteodystrophy favour chronicity (p.339-340)."],"pathophysiology":"In prerenal states, a fall in true or effective arterial volume lowers mean arterial pressure and triggers sympathetic activation, the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, and vasopressin release; GFR is sustained by prostaglandin-mediated afferent arteriolar dilatation and angiotensin II-mediated efferent arteriolar constriction. Once mean arterial pressure falls below about 80 mmHg, GFR declines steeply. This autoregulation explains why NSAIDs (which block prostaglandin-mediated afferent dilatation) and ACE inhibitors/ARBs (which reduce efferent constriction) can precipitate hemodynamically mediated AKI when volume or perfusion pressure is already reduced, and why patients with bilateral renal artery stenosis (or stenosis of a solitary kidney) are especially susceptible to a precipitous GFR fall on ACEi/ARBs. Sustained hypoperfusion progresses to ischaemic ATN. Nephrotoxic ATN results from a combination of intrarenal vasoconstriction, direct tubular toxicity, and intratubular obstruction (p.340).","additionalDifferentials":["Prerenal azotaemia from true volume loss (GI haemorrhage, burns, diarrhoea, diuretics) (p.340)","Prerenal from volume sequestration (pancreatitis, peritonitis, rhabdomyolysis) (p.340)","Prerenal from reduced effective arterial volume (cardiogenic shock, sepsis, severe heart failure, hepatorenal syndrome) (p.340)","Ischaemic ATN (major surgery, trauma, severe hypovolaemia, overwhelming sepsis, extensive burns) (p.340)","Nephrotoxic ATN (drug/toxin-induced) (p.340)","Acute (allergic) interstitial nephritis from drugs (antibiotics, NSAIDs, proton pump inhibitors) (p.340)","Acute glomerulonephritis / vasculitis (acute nephritic syndrome) (p.341)","Renal microvascular disease (haemolytic-uraemic syndrome, thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, malignant hypertension) (p.341)","Large-vessel occlusion: atheroemboli, thromboemboli, in-situ thrombosis, aortic dissection, vasculitis, renal vein thrombosis (p.341)","Postrenal obstructive uropathy (urethral/bladder-outlet, bilateral ureteric, or solitary-kidney obstruction) (p.340)","Cast nephropathy in multiple myeloma (a cause of anuria) (p.341)"],"infectionMimics":["Severe systemic infection (bacterial or viral) causing tubulointerstitial AKI (p.341)","Sepsis producing prerenal hypoperfusion and/or ischaemic ATN (p.340)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Chronic kidney disease presenting de novo (anaemia, hypocalcaemia, hyperphosphataemia overlap with AKI) (p.339-340)","Systemic lupus erythematosus, sarcoidosis, Sjogren syndrome, lymphoma or leukaemia infiltrating the tubulointerstitium (p.341)","Atheroembolic disease (look for livedo reticularis, distal peripheral infarcts, eosinophilia) (p.341)","Rhabdomyolysis (may produce nonoliguric ATN with FENa under 1%) (p.340)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine sediment microscopy for casts (muddy-brown granular and tubular epithelial casts in ATN; hyaline/granular or normal in prerenal; RBC casts in glomerulonephritis; WBC casts in interstitial nephritis) (p.340-341)","Urine sodium and FENa (prerenal: urine Na under 20 mmol/L, FENa under 1%; ATN: FENa typically over 1-2%) (p.340)","Urine osmolality (over 500 mosmol/L concentrated urine in prerenal; under 350 in oliguric ATN) (p.340)","BUN/creatinine ratio (over 20:1 prerenal vs ~10-15:1 in ATN) and urine/plasma creatinine ratio (over 40 prerenal vs under 20 ATN) (p.340)","Renal ultrasound for obstruction (ureteric/pelvic dilatation) and to assess kidney size/echogenicity (acute vs chronic) (p.340)","Furosemide (MAG3) renogram when obstruction is suspected despite a non-dilated ultrasound (early obstruction, encasement by tumour, retroperitoneal fibrosis) (p.340)","Cystatin C-based or combined creatinine/cystatin C eGFR where muscle mass or other factors make creatinine-based eGFR unreliable (p.339)","Serologic/glomerular work-up when RBC casts present (anti-GBM, ANCA, complement, cryoglobulins, hepatitis B/C, HIV) and early renal biopsy (p.341)","Phase-contrast microscopy for dysmorphic red cells (acanthocytes) suggesting glomerular bleeding (p.341)"],"redFlags":["Oliguria (24-h output under 400 mL) or anuria (under 100 mL); oliguria is never normal as at least ~400 mL of maximally concentrated urine is needed to excrete the daily osmolar load (p.341)","Anuria suggesting complete bilateral obstruction, vascular catastrophe (dissection/arterial occlusion), renal vein thrombosis, myeloma cast nephropathy, cortical necrosis, severe ATN, severe rapidly progressive GN, combined NSAID+ACEi/ARB, or shock (p.341)","RBC casts or dysmorphic red cells (acute nephritic/glomerular emergency warranting urgent biopsy) (p.341)","Dialysis-indication complications of AKI (refractory hyperkalaemia, severe metabolic acidosis, refractory volume overload/pulmonary oedema, uraemic complications) (existing entry; supported p.2330 RRT criteria for overload/acidosis/hyperkalaemia)"],"prognosis":"Prerenal AKI is usually readily reversible when perfusion is restored, and postrenal AKI is usually reversible once obstruction is relieved if recognised early. In nephrotoxic ATN, stopping the offending agent and stabilising blood pressure often suffice, with regeneration of tubular cells and recovery without dialysis. Nonoliguric ATN tends to have less severe potassium and acid-base disturbance and a more rapid recovery than oliguric AKI (p.340-341).","pitfalls":["Failing to exclude obstruction early; ultrasound can be falsely negative very early in obstruction or when ureters cannot dilate (tumour encasement, retroperitoneal fibrosis) (p.340)","Measuring urine eosinophils to diagnose interstitial nephritis: eosinophiluria is raised in many causes of AKI, has no diagnostic utility, and is no longer recommended (p.340)","Assuming FENa over 1% always means ATN: it may be under 1% in mild nonoliguric ATN (e.g., rhabdomyolysis) and in prerenal-like states such as CHF, cirrhosis or hepatorenal syndrome (p.340)","Over-relying on creatinine-based eGFR: muscle mass, sex, race assumptions can mislead (e.g., a bodybuilder with high creatinine) (p.339)","Attributing RBC casts to interstitial nephritis: their presence should instead prompt a search for glomerular disease (p.340-341)"],"severityClassification":"AKI severity is staged by the KDIGO criteria (referenced as Kidney Disease: Improving Global Outcomes; p.341, line ref to KDIGO Controversies Conference). Detailed KDIGO stage cut-offs (serum creatinine rise and urine-output thresholds for stages 1-3) are not specified in this source excerpt and are therefore not reproduced. The source's working framework classifies AKI anatomically into prerenal, intrinsic (renal), and postrenal categories, with supporting urinary indices distinguishing prerenal azotaemia from oliguric ATN (Table 55-2, p.340).","references":["Harrison 22e p.339","Harrison 22e p.340","Harrison 22e p.341","Harrison 22e p.342","Harrison 22e p.2330"],"crossLinks":["ckd","hyperkalemia","rhabdo","SEPSIS","glomerulonephritis"]},"anaphylaxis":{"name":"Anaphylaxis","system":"Allergy / Emergency","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch. 364 (Anaphylaxis), pp. 2812-2814; distributive-shock context p. 2358","clinicalPearls":["Onset is typically within seconds to minutes of exposure to the trigger; an exception is the delayed (about 3-6 h) reaction to mammalian meat seen in alpha-gal syndrome (p. 2812-2813).","Most episodes (about 80-90%) are uniphasic, but roughly 10-20% are biphasic, with symptoms recurring an hour or more after the initial reaction has resolved (p. 2812).","Cutaneous features (urticaria, flushing, generalized warmth) are the most common manifestation, present in over 90% of cases (p. 2812).","Cross-linking of allergen with IgE bound to FcepsilonRI receptors on mast cells and basophils is the usual initiating mechanism; sensitization requires prior exposure, so repeated exposure to the antigen is a key risk factor (p. 2812-2813).","Some severe reactions are non-IgE-mediated and can occur on first known exposure, because direct mast-cell activation needs no prior sensitization (e.g., via the MRGPRX2 receptor with neuromuscular blockers, quinolones, and icatibant) (p. 2813).","Severe Hymenoptera sting anaphylaxis, especially with prominent hypotension, may be the first presentation of underlying systemic mastocytosis; checking a baseline tryptase screens for this (p. 2812-2813).","Failure to give epinephrine within the first 20 minutes of symptoms is a risk factor for poor outcomes (p. 2813).","About 6% of Western populations carry hereditary alpha-tryptasemia, an extra TPSAB1 allele giving a high baseline tryptase that can confound interpretation and may itself be a risk factor for severe reactions (p. 2813)."],"pathophysiology":"Anaphylaxis is driven by mediators released chiefly from mast cells, basophils, and eosinophils. The most common mechanism is allergen cross-linking of IgE bound to FcepsilonRI on mast cells and basophils, triggering degranulation. Preformed granule contents include histamine, proteases (tryptase, chymase), proteoglycans (heparin, chondroitin sulfate), and TNF-alpha; newly generated arachidonic-acid products include cysteinyl leukotrienes, prostaglandin D2, and platelet-activating factor (PAF). Histamine produces flushing, urticaria, pruritus and, at high levels, hypotension and tachycardia; cysteinyl leukotrienes and PGD2 cause bronchoconstriction and increased microvascular permeability, with PGD2 also recruiting eosinophils and basophils. Serum PAF correlates with severity and is inversely related to PAF acetylhydrolase. Tryptase and chymase activate complement (generating the anaphylatoxins C3a and C5a) and the kallikrein-kinin system, with mediator effects likely additive or synergistic. Reactions can also be non-IgE-mediated: direct mast-cell activation (e.g., via MRGPRX2 by neuromuscular blockers, quinolones, icatibant; or opiates, vancomycin, radiocontrast, NSAIDs), complement activation (e.g., Cremophor-solubilized paclitaxel), or contact-system/bradykinin generation (e.g., oversulfated chondroitin sulfate heparin contaminant). (p. 2812-2813)","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic mastocytosis (elevated baseline tryptase; spindle-shaped mast cells on bone marrow) (p. 2813)","Pheochromocytoma (elevated urine metanephrines) (p. 2813)","Carcinoid syndrome (elevated urine 5-HIAA) (p. 2813)","Hereditary angioedema (decreased C4 during attacks) (p. 2813)","Acquired angioedema (decreased C1q) (p. 2813)","Systemic capillary leak syndrome (severe hypotension with poor response to first-line agents) (p. 2813)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Scombroid poisoning (ingested histamine from improperly stored histidine-rich fish such as tuna, mackerel, mahi-mahi); clinically mimics anaphylaxis but tryptase is NOT elevated because histamine is exogenous, and skin test/oral challenge to fish are negative (p. 2813)","Direct (pseudoallergic) mast-cell degranulation from opiates, neuromuscular blocking agents, vancomycin, or CT radiocontrast (e.g., via MRGPRX2) - clinically indistinguishable from IgE-mediated reactions but occurs without prior sensitization (p. 2813)","Vasovagal (neurally mediated) syncope - provoked by emotion, pain, or orthostatic stress and accompanied by bradycardia, in contrast to the tachycardia and urticaria/angioedema of anaphylaxis (p. 2813; cf. p. 156-157)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum tryptase peaks 60-90 min after onset and remains measurable for up to about 5 h; apply the '20% + 2' rule (an acute level >20% above the patient's baseline plus 2 ng/mL is diagnostic of clinically significant mast-cell activation) (p. 2813)","Repeat tryptase once the patient is stable to establish the individual baseline, since baseline serum tryptase varies widely and can be raised by hereditary alpha-tryptasemia or high environmental allergen exposure (p. 2813)","Serum histamine is of limited use acutely - its measurable window expires within about 1 h of onset (p. 2813)","Allergen-specific skin testing or serum-specific IgE to confirm a suspected drug or food trigger (p. 2813)","Baseline tryptase in sting-induced anaphylaxis to screen for underlying systemic mastocytosis (p. 2813)"],"redFlags":["Hypotension or hypoxia - the most dangerous features, risking cardiovascular collapse or respiratory failure (p. 2812)","Laryngeal edema (sensation of a lump in the throat, hoarseness, stridor) or angioedema of the epiglottis/larynx threatening mechanical airway obstruction (p. 2812)","Bronchial obstruction (chest tightness, wheezing), especially severe in patients with underlying asthma, who have increased mortality (p. 2812)","Underlying asthma or cardiovascular disease - predisposes to more rapid decompensation (p. 2812)","Severe GI involvement (vomiting, crampy abdominal pain); bowel-wall angioedema can deplete intravascular volume enough to cause cardiovascular collapse (p. 2812)"],"prognosis":"Severe and even fatal complications can develop within minutes of symptom onset, making early recognition critical; deaths occur via airway/bronchial obstruction, laryngeal angioedema, or vascular collapse. Delaying epinephrine beyond the first 20 minutes worsens outcomes. Risk is higher with underlying asthma or cardiovascular disease. Natural history varies by trigger: most childhood egg, milk, soy, and wheat allergy resolves, whereas about 80% of peanut allergy persists for life. (p. 2812-2813)","pitfalls":["Treat with epinephrine first - antihistamines, glucocorticoids, and bronchodilators are ancillary agents for residual urticaria/angioedema and bronchospasm once the patient is hemodynamically stable, not initial therapy (p. 2813).","Biphasic reactions in 10-20% recur an hour or more after apparent recovery, so a period of observation is needed after initial resolution (p. 2812).","A normal tryptase does not exclude anaphylaxis - it is not elevated in scombroid poisoning (exogenous histamine), and an acutely 'normal' value may still be diagnostic relative to a low individual baseline (apply the 20% + 2 rule) (p. 2813).","Non-IgE-mediated reactions can occur on first exposure, so the absence of prior exposure does not rule out an anaphylactic reaction (p. 2813).","Beta blockers can blunt the response to epinephrine; consider an alternative antihypertensive in patients at high risk of needing emergency epinephrine (p. 2813).","Skin/serum specific-IgE testing to foods has relatively poor predictive value, so a positive test alone does not confirm clinical reactivity (p. 2814)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.2812","Harrison 22e p.2813","Harrison 22e p.2814","Harrison 22e p.2358"],"crossLinks":["angioedema_acei","asthma_exac","panic"]},"anemia_sympt":{"name":"Symptomatic anaemia","system":"Hematology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.66 Anemia and Polycythemia (printed p.444-451); Ch.106 Anemia Due to Acute Blood Loss (printed p.850-851)","clinicalPearls":["Symptoms and signs of anemia correlate poorly with the actual haemoglobin/haematocrit; clinical severity reflects the rate of onset and the patient's cardiovascular reserve rather than the absolute level (p.444).","Slowly developing anemia is remarkably well tolerated because three compensatory mechanisms develop over time: rapid rise in cardiac output (minutes), a rise in 2,3-DPG that lowers haemoglobin oxygen affinity and improves tissue oxygen offloading (hours to days), and an expansion of plasma volume (weeks) (p.444).","Plasma-volume expansion can occasionally overwhelm the heart and precipitate features of heart failure/oedema in chronic anemia (p.444).","Patients with limited cardiac reserve or atherosclerosis become symptomatic (angina, TIA/stroke) at a higher haematocrit than those with normal cardiovascular function (p.444).","The absolute reticulocyte count is the key first branch point for mechanism: an elevated count points to blood loss or haemolysis, while a low or inappropriately low count points to impaired production (p.448-449).","Manual percentage reticulocyte counts must be corrected for the haematocrit (a 1% count becomes an apparent 2% at a haematocrit of ~23%); the machine-derived absolute reticulocyte count needs no such correction (p.447).","Around 75% of all anemia is hypoproliferative, most often from mild-to-moderate iron deficiency or inflammation (p.449).","Anemia is frequently multifactorial (e.g., chronic inflammation plus occult GI blood loss in a patient on anti-inflammatory drugs), so iron status should be evaluated fully before and during treatment of any anemia (p.450).","Serum ferritin is an acute-phase reactant: it can be raised several-fold by inflammation and mask coexisting iron deficiency, whereas a ferritin <30 ug/L reliably indicates depleted body iron stores (p.449).","A high RDW favours iron deficiency (anisocytosis), whereas a low/normal RDW with microcytosis favours thalassaemia, where cells are more uniform in size (p.449).","Iron-deficiency pica and the neuropathy of B12 deficiency are deficiency-specific clues to seek on history and examination (p.444).","In acute blood loss the haemoglobin/haematocrit initially does NOT reflect the volume lost; the early picture is hypovolaemia, and the haemoglobin only falls over hours as fluid shifts and resuscitation dilute the blood (p.850)."],"pathophysiology":"Anemia results from a defect in any of the three requirements for normal red-cell production: renal erythropoietin (EPO) output, a functioning erythroid marrow, and adequate substrate (especially iron). EPO rises logarithmically once haemoglobin falls below ~100-120 g/L, but in chronic kidney disease and chronic inflammation EPO is inappropriately low for the degree of anemia. With adequate EPO and substrate, marrow red-cell output can rise four- to fivefold over 1-2 weeks. Mechanistically anemia is either hyperproductive/regenerative (blood loss or haemolysis, with reticulocytosis) or hypoproliferative (nutritional deficiency, marrow replacement, absent precursors, stem-cell defects, or EPO deficiency). Inflammatory cytokines such as TNF lower EPO and raise hepcidin, which blocks iron absorption and release from stores, producing the anemia of inflammation (p.443-450).","additionalDifferentials":["Anemia of inflammation/chronic disease (diagnosis of exclusion; MCV often normal; hepcidin-mediated block of iron reutilization) (p.448-450)","Thalassaemia (microcytic, low/normal RDW, defective globin synthesis) (p.448-449)","Sideroblastic anemia (defective heme synthesis; microcytic) (p.448-449)","Megaloblastic anemia from B12 or folate deficiency (oval macrocytes, hypersegmented neutrophils) (p.449)","Myelodysplastic syndrome (macrocytic anemia or pancytopenia; stem-cell defect) (p.449-450)","Anemia of chronic kidney disease (EPO lack; usually present once GFR <30) (p.450)","Anemia of aging/older adults (low EPO for degree of anemia; 11% community-dwelling, up to 40% nursing-home over 65) (p.450)","Aplastic anemia (hypocellular marrow, pancytopenia) (p.450)","Pure red cell aplasia (isolated very low reticulocytes; autoimmune or parvovirus B19) (p.450)","Acute/chronic leukaemia and other primary marrow neoplasms (p.450)","Copper deficiency (with neutropenia and neurologic disease) and vitamin C deficiency/scurvy (p.450)","Drug-induced macrocytosis (chemotherapy, some antiseizure drugs) (p.449)","Alcohol use, liver disease, hypothyroidism (round macrocytosis) (p.449)"],"infectionMimics":["Marrow-replacing infections such as histoplasmosis or tuberculosis causing a myelophthisic anemia (p.450)","Parvovirus B19 causing pure red cell aplasia (p.450)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute haemorrhage where the initial normal haemoglobin masks major blood loss; hypovolaemia dominates and anemia appears only after fluid shifts/resuscitation (p.850)","Spurious/relative changes: serum iron and transferrin saturation vary diurnally, which can mislead iron-status interpretation (p.449)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Corrected (or absolute) reticulocyte count as the first mechanistic discriminator (p.447-448)","Red cell indices and RDW (MCV-based microcytic/normocytic/macrocytic classification; RDW to separate iron deficiency from thalassaemia) (p.445-449)","Full iron studies: serum iron, TIBC, percent transferrin saturation (normal sat 25-50%), and ferritin for total-body stores (p.449)","Haemolysis panel: LDH (raised), haptoglobin (low), free haemoglobin in blood/urine, and direct antiglobulin (antibody) test when reticulocytes are high and bleeding excluded (p.449)","Blood-film morphology review (schistocytes, spherocytes, sickle cells, target cells, teardrop cells, macro-ovalocytes, hypersegmented neutrophils, Howell-Jolly bodies, basophilic stippling) to direct focused testing (p.445-449)","Serum EPO level and BUN/creatinine for the normocytic, hypoproliferative workup (p.450-451)","Serum protein electrophoresis / free light chains when myeloma is considered (p.451)","Bone marrow aspirate and biopsy (biopsy before aspirate) when marrow infiltration or generalised dysfunction is suspected (p.448)"],"redFlags":["Circulating blasts or other immature white cells, severe pancytopenia, or a very low reticulocyte count (<0.1%) mandating urgent marrow examination (p.448)","Myelophthisic blood film (nucleated red cells, teardrop cells, immature white cells) suggesting marrow infiltration/replacement (p.450)","Hypotension, angina, or other signs of cardiac compromise as clear indications that the anemia is not being tolerated (p.450)","Acute haemorrhage with sudden loss of >25-30% of blood volume: vascular instability and decreased organ perfusion supervene and cannot be compensated by sympathetic mechanisms (p.850-851)","Loss of consciousness or acute kidney injury early in major haemorrhage, reflecting hypovolaemic threat to brain and kidneys before any fall in haemoglobin (p.850)","Clinical signs of concealed internal bleeding: Grey Turner sign (flank ecchymosis, retroperitoneal), Cullen sign (umbilical ecchymosis), or dullness to chest percussion (intrapleural) (p.850)"],"prognosis":"Anemia in those over 65 is associated with increased risk of death, hospitalization, and frailty. In acute posthemorrhagic anemia, if bleeding stops the marrow response (reticulocytosis, rising EPO) gradually corrects the anemia. Myelodysplasia has a variable course, from years of stability to rapid evolution into acute leukaemia (p.449-450).","pitfalls":["Anchoring on a normal haemoglobin in early acute haemorrhage and underestimating blood loss; an abrupt fall in haemoglobin should prompt suspicion of acute posthemorrhagic anemia regardless of the given history (p.850).","Estimates of blood volume lost at a bloody scene are often overestimated, while concealed internal bleeding may be missed entirely (p.850).","Relying on ferritin alone during inflammation, which can falsely normalise or elevate it despite true iron deficiency (p.449).","Treating macrocytosis as automatically B12/folate deficiency; if megaloblastic markers (hypersegmentation, macro-ovalocytes) are present but nutritional tests are normal, suspect myelodysplasia and proceed to marrow examination (p.451).","Forgetting that the anemia of chronic disease/inflammation frequently has a normal MCV, so a normal MCV does not exclude it (p.449-451).","Performing marrow aspiration before biopsy, which distorts the biopsy with aspiration-induced bleeding (p.448).","Overlooking drug causes: ACE inhibitors can suppress EPO; chemotherapy and some antiseizure drugs cause macrocytosis (p.449-450)."],"severityClassification":"Two complementary classifications. (1) By red-cell size (Wintrobe): microcytic (iron deficiency, thalassaemia, sideroblastic, anemia of inflammation), normocytic (renal disease, marrow invasion, myeloma, aplastic anemia, pure red cell aplasia, endocrinopathies), and macrocytic - subdivided into oval macrocytes (B12/folate deficiency, myelodysplasia, drugs) and round macrocytes (alcohol, liver disease, hypothyroidism, hypoxia, reticulocytosis, smoking, dysproteinemia). (2) By mechanism: hyperproductive (blood loss, haemolysis - reticulocytes high) versus underproductive/hypoproliferative (nutritional deficiency, absent precursors, lack of EPO, marrow replacement, stem-cell defects - reticulocytes low/inappropriate). The absolute reticulocyte count plus MCV drive the diagnostic algorithm (p.448-451). For acute blood loss: vascular instability appears with sudden loss of ~25% of total blood volume, and >30% loss exceeds compensatory mechanisms (p.851).","references":["Harrison 22e p.443","Harrison 22e p.444","Harrison 22e p.445","Harrison 22e p.446","Harrison 22e p.447","Harrison 22e p.448","Harrison 22e p.449","Harrison 22e p.450","Harrison 22e p.451","Harrison 22e p.850","Harrison 22e p.851"],"crossLinks":["peptic_ulcer","ckd","myeloma"]},"angioedema_acei":{"name":"ACE-inhibitor / hereditary angioedema","system":"Allergy / Emergency","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e, Ch. 363 (Urticaria, Angioedema, and Allergic Rhinitis), pp. 2807-2809; with supporting context from Ch. 265 (Heart Failure: Management) p. 1995 and Ch. 271 (Hypertensive Vascular Disease) p. 2024.","clinicalPearls":["ACE-inhibitor angioedema occurs in roughly 0.2-0.7% of exposed patients and is driven by impaired (delayed) degradation of bradykinin rather than by an IgE/mast-cell allergic mechanism (p. 2807).","Recognized risk factors for ACE inhibitor-related angioedema include Black race, prior organ transplant, female sex, smoking, and increasing age (p. 2807).","Bradykinin-mediated angioedema (whether from an ACE inhibitor or from C1-inhibitor deficiency) is characterized by the conspicuous ABSENCE of accompanying urticaria or pruritus (p. 2808).","Compared with histamine-/mast-cell-driven urticaria-angioedema, bradykinin-mediated swelling tends to be more painful than pruritic, with minimal surrounding erythema, and is slower to resolve, persisting >24 h (pp. 2807-2808).","The most common sites of angioedema are periorbital and perioral; lesions are neither symmetric nor dependent in distribution (p. 2808).","Gastrointestinal-tract involvement is a frequent feature of bradykinin-mediated angioedema and may present as abdominal colic with or without nausea and vomiting; this can mimic an acute abdomen and lead to unnecessary surgery (p. 2808).","ACE inhibitors are a recognized (if uncommon) cause of intestinal angioedema, which can present with diarrhea/abdominal symptoms (Ch. on diarrhea, GI section).","No residual scarring follows urticaria or angioedema unless an underlying vasculitic process is present (p. 2808)."],"pathophysiology":"ACE inhibitors block angiotensin-converting enzyme, which normally also degrades the vasodilator peptide bradykinin; inhibition leads to delayed bradykinin breakdown and accumulation, increasing vascular permeability in the deep dermis and subcutaneous/submucosal tissues and producing angioedema (pp. 2807, 2024). This is mechanistically the same bradykinin pathway implicated in hereditary angioedema, where deficient or dysfunctional C1 inhibitor (C1INH) fails to restrain activated factor XII (Hageman factor) and kallikrein, generating excess bradykinin (p. 2808). Because the mechanism is bradykinin- rather than histamine-/IgE-mediated, there is no associated urticaria or pruritus, and the process is fundamentally distinct from allergic mast-cell-driven angioedema (p. 2808). The same bradykinin-accumulation mechanism explains the increased angioedema risk seen with combined ACE/neprilysin inhibition, since neprilysin also degrades bradykinin (Ch. 265, p. 1995).","additionalDifferentials":["Allergic (histaminergic, IgE/mast-cell-mediated) angioedema, typically with concurrent urticaria/pruritus and a more rapid onset and resolution (pp. 2807-2808).","Hereditary angioedema (HAE) due to C1-inhibitor deficiency (type 1) or dysfunctional C1INH (type 2), suggested by childhood/adolescent onset, family history, recurrent attacks, and failure to respond to H1 antihistamines (p. 2808).","Type 3 (normal-C1INH) HAE, sometimes associated with a mutant factor XII and normal complement protein levels (p. 2808).","Acquired C1-inhibitor deficiency from excessive C1INH consumption or an anti-C1INH autoantibody in the setting of lymphoproliferative or autoimmune disease, lacking a family history (p. 2808).","Idiopathic angioedema without an identifiable trigger (p. 2807).","Urticarial vasculitis when lesions are painful rather than pruritic, last >36 h, or scar (p. 2809).","Angioedema from other bradykinin-affecting or culprit drugs listed alongside ACE inhibitors: NSAIDs/aspirin, dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4) inhibitors, radiocontrast media, opioids, antimicrobials, and angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor (ARNI/sacubitril-valsartan) (Table 363-1, p. 2807; Ch. 265, p. 1995)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Anaphylaxis / allergic angioedema with airway involvement (histamine-mediated) — important to distinguish because the bradykinin form responds poorly to anaphylaxis therapy (pp. 2807-2808).","Hereditary or acquired C1-inhibitor-deficiency angioedema presenting with laryngeal edema or recurrent abdominal colic (p. 2808).","An acute surgical abdomen, mimicked by gastrointestinal angioedema causing abdominal colic, nausea, and vomiting (p. 2808)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnosis of bradykinin-mediated angioedema is largely clinical; the lack of pruritus/urticaria and failure to respond to H1 antihistamines point away from histaminergic causes (p. 2808).","For isolated angioedema without urticaria: basic chemistries with liver function tests and complement levels (p. 2809).","C1INH antigen level (deficient in type 1 HAE) and a catalytic C1INH functional assay (abnormal in type 2 HAE) to evaluate for C1-inhibitor deficiency (p. 2808).","C4 and C2 levels: chronically depleted in C1INH-deficiency states and fall further during attacks; in acquired forms, C1q and C1 function are also reduced (p. 2808).","Consider evaluation for paraproteinemia / underlying lymphoproliferative or autoimmune disease when acquired C1INH deficiency is suspected (p. 2809)."],"redFlags":["Upper-respiratory-tract / laryngeal angioedema can cause transient airway obstruction and is potentially life-threatening (p. 2808).","Tongue, oropharyngeal, or laryngeal swelling indicating threatened airway (pp. 2807-2808).","Progressive or persistent swelling lasting >24 h, characteristic of bradykinin-mediated angioedema and warning against assuming a self-limited allergic course (p. 2808)."],"prognosis":"Bradykinin-mediated angioedema typically takes hours to days to resolve and persists >24 h, in contrast to the shorter-lived allergic form (pp. 2807-2808). The leading hazard is laryngeal/upper-airway obstruction, which can be life-threatening (p. 2808). No residual scarring occurs unless there is an underlying vasculitis (p. 2808).","pitfalls":["Because the swelling is bradykinin- and not histamine-mediated, it responds poorly to the standard allergic-angioedema/anaphylaxis measures (adrenaline, antihistamines, glucocorticoids); relying on these can give false reassurance (mechanism is non-histaminergic per p. 2808).","Onset is not necessarily immediate after starting the drug — ACE-inhibitor angioedema reflects DELAYED bradykinin degradation and can appear well after therapy has begun, so a long-standing ACE inhibitor should not be dismissed as the cause (delayed-degradation mechanism, p. 2807).","Absence of urticaria/pruritus can lead clinicians to overlook angioedema or misattribute it; isolated angioedema without hives is a hallmark of the bradykinin mechanism, not evidence against it (p. 2808).","Gastrointestinal angioedema mimicking an acute abdomen may prompt unnecessary surgical intervention (p. 2808).","Switching to or overlapping with an angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor (ARNI) is hazardous: neprilysin inhibition further blocks bradykinin breakdown, ARNI is contraindicated in anyone with prior angioedema, and a washout gap is required when transitioning from an ACE inhibitor (Ch. 265, p. 1995)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison classifies urticaria/angioedema by duration as acute (<6 weeks) versus chronic (>6 weeks) rather than by a graded severity scale; this distinction helps identify precipitating factors (pp. 2807, 2809). No formal angioedema severity-grading scheme is given for ACE-inhibitor angioedema in this source.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2807","Harrison 22e p.2808","Harrison 22e p.2809","Harrison 22e p.1995","Harrison 22e p.2024"],"crossLinks":["anaphylaxis"]},"aortic_dissection":{"name":"Aortic dissection","system":"Vascular emergency","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"2168-2170, 105-109","clinicalPearls":["Pain characteristically migrates as the dissection propagates, often shifting to the interscapular region of the back (Harrison 22e p.2169).","Systemic hypertension coexists in about 70% of patients with aortic dissection (Harrison 22e p.2168).","Peak incidence is in the sixth and seventh decades, with men affected about twice as often as women (Harrison 22e p.2169).","A normal ECG showing no ischemia helps distinguish dissection from myocardial infarction in a patient with chest pain, though dissection can rarely involve a coronary ostium and cause an actual MI (Harrison 22e p.2169).","On chest x-ray, ascending dissection often produces a widened superior mediastinum; a pleural effusion (usually left-sided) may appear and is typically serosanguineous rather than a sign of rupture unless accompanied by hypotension and a falling hematocrit (Harrison 22e p.2169).","Although 'tearing' or 'ripping' pain is classic, acute aortic emergencies also commonly present with knifelike pain (Harrison 22e p.106).","Stanford type A or B classification is more practical for management than DeBakey, because DeBakey types I and II are managed similarly (Harrison 22e p.2168)."],"pathophysiology":"Dissection begins with a tear of the intima, most often along the high-shear right lateral wall of the ascending aorta or in the descending thoracic aorta just below the ligamentum arteriosum. Pulsatile flow dissects along the elastic lamellar plates of the media to create a false lumen; the initiating event may be a primary intimal tear with secondary dissection into the media, or a medial hemorrhage that secondarily disrupts the intima. The dissection usually propagates distally (sometimes proximally), may be limited distally by atherosclerotic plaque, and can re-enter the true lumen through a secondary distal intimal disruption. Underlying medial degeneration and conditions that raise aortic wall stress predispose to dissection. Two related radiologic/pathologic variants are intramural hematoma (rupture of vasa vasorum bleeding into the wall, without an intimal flap) and penetrating atherosclerotic ulcer (plaque erosion into the media); both can progress to dissection and rupture (Harrison 22e p.2168-2169).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute myocardial infarction / acute coronary syndrome (chest pain, and dissection can occlude a coronary ostium) (Harrison 22e p.2169)","Pulmonary embolism (Harrison 22e p.108)","Pericarditis (Harrison 22e p.106)","Esophageal pain / spasm, which can mimic angina (Harrison 22e p.106)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Penetrating atherosclerotic ulcer of the aorta (Harrison 22e p.2168)","Acute intramural hematoma of the aorta (Harrison 22e p.2168)","Aortic rupture (Harrison 22e p.2168)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CT (with contrast) — most commonly used given proximity of scanners to emergency rooms and rapidity; sensitivity and specificity >90% for the intimal flap and extent (Harrison 22e p.2169)","MRI — highly accurate for intimal flap, extent, intramural hemorrhage and penetrating ulcers; sensitivity and specificity >90% (Harrison 22e p.2169)","Transesophageal echocardiography — ~98% sensitivity and ~90% specificity for ascending and descending dissection (but not the arch); also assesses aortic regurgitation and pericardial effusion (Harrison 22e p.2169)","Transthoracic echocardiography — rapid bedside test, overall sensitivity 60-85% (>80% for proximal ascending dissection), less useful for arch and descending aorta (Harrison 22e p.2169)","Chest x-ray — may show widened superior mediastinum and/or pleural effusion (Harrison 22e p.2169)","ECG — to look for ischemia and help differentiate from MI (Harrison 22e p.2169)"],"redFlags":["Sudden, very severe tearing chest or interscapular back pain with diaphoresis, especially when it migrates (Harrison 22e p.2169)","New aortic regurgitation murmur (bounding pulses, wide pulse pressure, diastolic murmur along the right sternal border) in a patient with chest pain — common (>50%) in proximal dissection (Harrison 22e p.2169)","Pulse deficit or loss of pulses (Harrison 22e p.2169)","Hypotension with hemopericardium / cardiac tamponade signs (suggests retrograde type A dissection) (Harrison 22e p.2169)","Acute neurologic deficit — hemiplegia/hemianesthesia (carotid involvement) or paraplegia (spinal cord ischemia) (Harrison 22e p.2169)","Acute pulmonary edema (Harrison 22e p.2169, p.107)","Falling hematocrit with hypotension and pleural effusion (suggests rupture) (Harrison 22e p.2169)"],"prognosis":"In-hospital mortality after surgical treatment of dissection is reported at about 15-25%; for medically treated uncomplicated type B dissection it is about 12%. Major perioperative causes of death and morbidity include myocardial infarction, paraplegia, renal failure, tamponade, hemorrhage, and sepsis. With careful follow-up the long-term outlook after discharge is generally good, with roughly 60% 10-year survival; patients with Marfan syndrome are at high risk for post-dissection complications (Harrison 22e p.2170).","pitfalls":["Aortic dissection is a classic disease that is misdiagnosed as acute myocardial infarction — a dangerous error because anticoagulant/thrombolytic therapy for presumed MI can be catastrophic (Harrison 22e p.102, p.2169).","A left pleural effusion in dissection is usually serosanguineous and does not by itself indicate rupture unless there is accompanying hypotension and a falling hematocrit (Harrison 22e p.2169).","Transthoracic echocardiography is insensitive for arch and descending dissections, so a negative TTE does not exclude dissection (Harrison 22e p.2169)."],"severityClassification":"Stanford classification: type A involves the ascending aorta (proximal dissection) regardless of tear site or distal extension; type B is limited to the arch and/or descending aorta (distal dissection). DeBakey classification: type I — intimal tear in ascending aorta with propagation to arch, descending and possibly abdominal aorta; type II — limited to the ascending aorta; type III — tear in the descending aorta with distal propagation. Stanford A/B is more practical for management since DeBakey I and II are managed similarly (Harrison 22e p.2168).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2168","Harrison 22e p.2169","Harrison 22e p.2170","Harrison 22e p.102","Harrison 22e p.106","Harrison 22e p.107","Harrison 22e p.108"],"crossLinks":["acs","tamponade","htn_emergency","aaa","pericarditis"]},"aortic_stenosis":{"name":"Aortic stenosis (syncope)","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"203-204, 285-286, 328-329, 361, 1904-1909, 1933-1934, 1950","clinicalPearls":["The murmur of AS is a crescendo-decrescendo (midsystolic/ejection) murmur, usually loudest in the second right intercostal space and radiating into the carotids; it is the most common midsystolic murmur in adults (Harrison 22e p.328).","Apical transmission of the AS murmur, where it becomes higher-pitched, is common (Gallavardin effect) and may be mistaken for mitral regurgitation; the AS murmur intensifies in the beat following a premature beat and varies directly with cardiac output, whereas the MR murmur stays constant beat-to-beat (Harrison 22e p.328).","Auscultatory features pointing to severe AS include a soft or absent A2, paradoxical splitting of S2, a late-peaking systolic murmur, an apical S4, and (at normal output) a systolic thrill and a grade 4 or louder murmur (Harrison 22e p.328).","An early ejection sound (click) in children/young adults signifies a flexible, noncalcified bicuspid valve and localizes obstruction to the valvular level rather than sub- or supravalvular (Harrison 22e p.328).","Bicuspid aortic valve is one of the most common congenital anomalies and predisposes to both aortic stenosis and aortic regurgitation (Harrison 22e p.1933).","AS can precipitate myocardial ischemia and anginal chest pain even without obstructive coronary atherosclerosis, by creating extreme myocardial oxygen demand with impaired endocardial blood flow (type 2 ischemia mechanism) (Harrison 22e p.285).","Heyde's syndrome is the association of aortic stenosis with bleeding gastrointestinal vascular ectasias (acquired von Willebrand disease); such patients appear to benefit from aortic valve replacement (Harrison 22e p.361)."],"pathophysiology":"Aortic stenosis is most often caused by gradual progression of valvular calcification of either normal trileaflet or congenitally bicuspid valves, producing fixed left ventricular outflow obstruction. The pressure overload drives left ventricular hypertrophy, which manifests on the ECG as LVH that increases with severity. The combination of increased myocardial oxygen demand and impaired endocardial perfusion can cause ischemia/angina even with normal coronaries. Syncope in AS reflects a structural disorder compromising cardiac output, and inappropriate reflex vasodilation may be provoked by increased ventricular contractility against the fixed obstruction (Harrison 22e p.203-204, 285, 329, 1933).","additionalDifferentials":["Aortic sclerosis (focal leaflet thickening/calcification without obstruction; Doppler jet velocity 2.5 m/s or less) can produce a grade 2-3 midsystolic murmur acoustically identical to AS (Harrison 22e p.1908).","Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy (subvalvular dynamic outflow obstruction) (Harrison 22e p.328, 1934).","Subaortic membrane (fixed subvalvular obstruction that can mimic valvular AS) (Harrison 22e p.1934).","Supravalvular aortic stenosis (exceedingly rare) (Harrison 22e p.1934).","Pulmonic stenosis (midsystolic murmur, second left interspace) (Harrison 22e p.329).","Atrial septal defect with increased pulmonic flow producing a midsystolic murmur with fixed splitting of S2 (Harrison 22e p.329).","Mitral regurgitation, given the apical Gallavardin transmission of the AS murmur (Harrison 22e p.328)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Hyperdynamic flow murmurs without structural disease (fever, thyrotoxicosis, pregnancy, anemia, normal childhood/adolescence) can produce midsystolic murmurs (Harrison 22e p.1908)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography to assess valve anatomy (tricuspid/bicuspid, leaflet calcification, excursion), stenosis severity, LV size/wall thickness/function, and aortic root and proximal ascending aorta (Harrison 22e p.329, 1933).","Doppler estimation of transvalvular peak velocity and pressure gradient (Harrison 22e p.1933).","Continuity-equation estimation of aortic valve area, the most accurate echo technique, especially when gradients are underestimated by LV dysfunction (Harrison 22e p.1933-1934).","Dobutamine stress echocardiography to evaluate low-flow/low-gradient AS and determine whether the valve can open further (Harrison 22e p.1934).","Cardiac MRI for quantitative assessment of severity (Harrison 22e p.1933).","Carotid pulse examination for parvus et tardus contour (Harrison 22e p.329, 1905)."],"redFlags":["A weak and delayed carotid pulse (pulsus parvus et tardus) indicates severe AS (Harrison 22e p.329, 1905).","Soft or absent A2, paradoxical splitting of S2, late-peaking murmur, and an apical S4 indicate severe AS (Harrison 22e p.328).","A softening AS murmur in the setting of heart failure/low cardiac output is misleading and does not indicate mild disease (Harrison 22e p.328).","Syncope (including reflex/exertional) from AS reflects structural compromise of cardiac output (Harrison 22e p.203-204)."],"prognosis":"Patients with good left ventricular function can tolerate severe AS for a considerable period, so valve area or gradient alone should not dictate timing of surgery, which remains a clinical decision (Harrison 22e p.1934).","pitfalls":["Avoid aggressive preload/afterload reduction: aggressive treatment of cardiac failure with diuretics and/or vasodilators can precipitate orthostatic hypotension in patients with structural disease such as AS (Harrison 22e p.203).","Transvalvular pressure gradients underestimate severity in severe LV dysfunction; rely on valve area by continuity equation (Harrison 22e p.1933-1934).","In low-flow/low-gradient AS the valve may behave stenotic simply because of the low gradient; provocative dobutamine testing is needed to distinguish true severe stenosis (Harrison 22e p.1934).","The carotid parvus-et-tardus sign is less discriminatory in older patients with stiffened arteries (Harrison 22e p.329).","Aortic sclerosis can produce a murmur indistinguishable on auscultation from significant AS (Harrison 22e p.1908)."],"severityClassification":"Echo: peak instantaneous velocities of 3.0 m/s or higher (often >4.0 m/s) correspond to gradients of ~36 and ~64 mmHg and indicate at least moderate AS; aortic valve area <1.0 cm2 is generally severe and <0.6 cm2 critical (Harrison 22e p.1933-1934). Catheterization: a valve area <1.0 cm2 with a mean gradient >40 mmHg indicates severe AS (Harrison 22e p.1950). Aortic sclerosis (non-obstructive) is defined by a Doppler jet velocity of 2.5 m/s or less (Harrison 22e p.1908).","references":["Harrison 22e p.203","Harrison 22e p.204","Harrison 22e p.285","Harrison 22e p.286","Harrison 22e p.328","Harrison 22e p.329","Harrison 22e p.361","Harrison 22e p.1905","Harrison 22e p.1908","Harrison 22e p.1909","Harrison 22e p.1933","Harrison 22e p.1934","Harrison 22e p.1950"],"crossLinks":["heart_failure","vasovagal_syncope","acs"]},"asthma_exac":{"name":"Asthma exacerbation","system":"Pulmonary","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Chapter 298 (Asthma, Israel), p.2215-2228","clinicalPearls":["An exacerbation is an episode of acute bronchospasm that may be severe enough to require emergency care or hospitalization and can be fatal (p.2216).","During an acute attack patients typically show tachypnea, tachycardia and accessory-muscle use, with wheezing and a prolonged expiratory phase (p.2222).","As airway obstruction worsens the chest may become 'silent' with loss of breath sounds, indicating a more dangerous attack rather than improvement (p.2222).","Most patients in an attack are hypocapnic from a high respiratory rate; a normal or near-normal PaCO2 in a distressed asthmatic signals impending respiratory failure (p.2227).","PEFR, FEV1 and FEV1/FVC are reduced in obstruction, but these may appear normal during an attack or after recent bronchodilator use, so a normal value does not exclude severe disease (p.2222-2223).","In urgent care, PEFR/FEV1 should be measured; patients with PEFR >60% predicted often respond to beta2-agonists alone, whereas failure to reach >60% or persistent severe tachypnea over 4-6 h should prompt hospital admission (p.2227).","Severe exacerbations requiring systemic corticosteroids may themselves accelerate long-term lung-function decline (p.2221).","More than one-third of patients carrying a physician diagnosis of asthma do not actually meet diagnostic criteria, so consider mimics when an 'asthmatic' fails to respond (p.2222).","Some patients cannot distinguish an anxiety attack from an asthma attack, which can confound assessment of an apparent exacerbation (p.2222)."],"pathophysiology":"Asthma is characterized by episodic, usually reversible airway obstruction with airway hyperresponsiveness, generally accompanied by airway inflammation (most often eosinophilic, sometimes neutrophilic, occasionally with no obvious inflammation). Exacerbations are episodes of acute bronchospasm driven by luminal narrowing from airway smooth-muscle constriction, airway edema and mucus secretion, superimposed on chronic airway inflammation. Triggers activate mast cells/basophils and inflammatory cells (releasing bronchoconstrictors and mediators) and stimulate sensory nerves acting on smooth muscle. Allergen exposure can cause an immediate (early) bronchospasm and a late response 2-24 h later with airway narrowing and inflammation. Structural airway-wall thickening (smooth-muscle hypertrophy/hyperplasia, subepithelial collagen deposition, edema, mucosal inflammation) increases the tendency to narrow disproportionately. In severe airflow obstruction there may be air trapping with dynamically elevated FRC and increased residual volume; mild hypoxemia from ventilation-perfusion mismatch (oxygen-responsive) is common (p.2216-2217, p.2221, p.2222-2223).","additionalDifferentials":["Heart failure (overlapping symptoms; can produce obstructive pulmonary function) (p.2222)","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease / asthma-COPD overlap (p.2222, p.2228)","Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency (p.2222)","Airway obstruction from a mass or foreign body (p.2222)","Inducible laryngeal obstruction / vocal-cord dysfunction (p.2222)","Bronchiolitis obliterans (p.2222)","Bronchiectasis (p.2222)","Tracheobronchomalacia (p.2222)","Anxiety attacks (may be indistinguishable from an asthma attack) (p.2222)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Inducible laryngeal obstruction (vocal-cord dysfunction): inappropriate laryngeal narrowing causing airflow resistance; more common in women and in those with anxiety/depression; can both mimic and complicate asthma; diagnosed by laryngoscopy during symptoms (p.2222)","Heart failure / left ventricular dysfunction as a cause of dyspnea and obstructive-pattern symptoms (p.2222)","COPD (asthma-COPD overlap) (p.2222, p.2228)","Anaphylaxis as a cause of acute generalized bronchospasm (p.2222 differential; mechanism noted elsewhere in text)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Peak expiratory flow rate (PEFR) and FEV1 to quantify obstruction and guide disposition; PEFR >60% predicted predicts response to beta2-agonists alone (p.2222-2223, p.2227)","Arterial blood gas / PaCO2 monitoring: hypocapnia is usual, while a normalizing or rising PaCO2 indicates impending respiratory failure (p.2227)","Pulse oximetry / assessment of hypoxemia (V/Q mismatch, oxygen-responsive) (p.2216-2217, p.2227)","Bronchodilator reversibility testing (>=10-12% FEV1 improvement) when not in an acute attack (p.2222-2223)","Methacholine, mannitol or exercise/cold-air challenge to demonstrate airway hyperresponsiveness when PFTs are non-confirmatory (not during an acute attack) (p.2223)","Blood eosinophil count (correlates with severity; very high levels prompt consideration of eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis) (p.2223)","Total/specific IgE, skin or RAST testing; IgE >1000 IU/mL prompts consideration of ABPA (p.2223)","Fractional exhaled nitric oxide (FeNO) as a marker of type 2 inflammation and ICS adherence (p.2223)","Chest CT in poorly responsive disease to assess bronchiectasis/structural causes; sinus imaging and serum alpha-1 antitrypsin in selected cases (p.2223)"],"redFlags":["Silent chest with loss of breath sounds (life-threatening) (p.2222)","Normal or near-normal PaCO2 in a distressed asthmatic, or a rising PaCO2 - signals impending respiratory failure and possible need for mechanical ventilation (p.2227)","Respiratory exhaustion / failure to respond to repeated beta2-agonists (p.2227)","Failure to achieve PEFR >60% predicted or persistent severe tachypnea over 4-6 h (warrants admission) (p.2227)","Need for hourly beta2-agonists for several hours despite treatment (p.2227)","Status asthmaticus: severe attack in which mechanical ventilation is difficult due to high airway resistance/pressures (p.2227)"],"prognosis":"Asthma causes significant morbidity and mortality; roughly 3000-4000 people die from asthma each year in the United States. Death can occur even in mild asthma (at lower rates than severe asthma). Mortality in patients over 65 is about five times that of younger cohorts. High-risk features for asthma death (Table 298-6) include prior ICU admission or intubation for asthma, illicit drug use, depression, a new diagnosis within the past year, two or more emergency visits in the past 6 months, severe psychosocial problems, lower socioeconomic status, and being on daily prednisone before admission. Severe exacerbations may accelerate long-term lung-function decline (p.2221, p.2227).","pitfalls":["Interpreting a 'silent chest' or quieting wheeze as improvement when it actually signals worsening, life-threatening obstruction (p.2222).","Being reassured by a normal/normalizing PaCO2 - in a distressed asthmatic this is an ominous sign of impending respiratory failure, not stability (p.2227).","Relying on PFT/PEF values that may look normal during an attack or shortly after bronchodilator use (p.2222-2223).","Overlooking mimics such as vocal-cord dysfunction, heart failure or COPD in 'difficult' or treatment-refractory presentations - over one-third of diagnosed asthmatics do not meet criteria (p.2222).","Missing precipitants such as beta-blockers (including ophthalmic drops), aspirin/NSAIDs in aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease, or ACE-inhibitor cough (p.2221).","Giving antibiotics routinely in an exacerbation - they should be used only if there are signs of infection (p.2227)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison stratifies acute deteriorations practically rather than by a fixed table. Mild-to-moderate deteriorations can initially be managed with frequent beta2-agonists; failure to control with hourly beta2-agonists for several hours warrants urgent care. In urgent/ED care, PEFR or FEV1 guides severity: PEFR >60% predicted usually responds to beta2-agonists alone, whereas failure to reach PEFR >60% or persistent severe tachypnea over 4-6 h indicates a severe attack needing admission. Life-threatening / near-fatal features include silent chest, respiratory exhaustion, and a normal or rising PaCO2 signaling impending respiratory failure; status asthmaticus denotes a severe refractory attack with high airway resistance making ventilation difficult (p.2227).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2215","Harrison 22e p.2216","Harrison 22e p.2217","Harrison 22e p.2221","Harrison 22e p.2222","Harrison 22e p.2223","Harrison 22e p.2227"],"crossLinks":["COPD_EXACERBATION","anaphylaxis","pe"]},"atrial_fib":{"name":"Atrial fibrillation / arrhythmia","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1985, 1989-1997","clinicalPearls":["AF is the most common sustained arrhythmia; prevalence rises steeply with age (~20% of those over 80), and the lifetime risk for a 40-year-old man is roughly one in four (p.1990).","On the ECG, AF shows absence of organized atrial activity (no P waves) with an irregularly irregular ventricular response; the chaotic atrial activity is often best seen in lead V1 (p.1990, 1991).","The conducted ventricular rate is variable and usually rapid (commonly 110-160/min), but may exceed 200/min, or be inappropriately slow when there is high vagal tone or AV nodal conduction disease (p.1990).","Many patients are unaware of the irregular heartbeat, so AF is frequently asymptomatic; absence of AF on intermittent monitoring does not establish low risk (p.1990, 1993).","AF is categorized by temporal pattern: paroxysmal (self-terminating, usually within 7 days), persistent (continuous >7 days but <1 year), and long-standing persistent (>1 year); these patterns correlate with progressive left atrial enlargement and scar burden (p.1990, 1991).","Stroke and bleeding risks are estimated as concepts using the CHA2DS2-VASc score (stroke risk: congestive heart failure, hypertension, age, diabetes, prior stroke/TIA, vascular disease, sex) and HAS-BLED score (bleeding risk), respectively (p.1993).","AF burden does not predict stroke risk; the approach to paroxysmal AF is the same as for persistent AF, and even apparently infrequent episodes carry risk because asymptomatic episodes are common (p.1993).","Subclinical AF detectable only on long-term continuous monitoring is associated with roughly 2.5-fold increased stroke risk (p.1993).","AF detected after cryptogenic stroke using insertable cardiac monitors in up to ~8.9% of patients within 6 months, supporting prolonged rhythm monitoring in this setting (p.1990)."],"pathophysiology":"AF is characterized by disorganized, rapid, irregular atrial electrical activation with loss of organized atrial mechanical contraction; the AV node filters these impulses to produce an irregular ventricular rate. The pathophysiology is multifactorial and incompletely understood, with a proposed 'final common pathway' in which risk factors (age, cardiovascular disease, hypertension, diabetes, obesity, alcohol, sleep-disordered breathing) drive electrophysiologic changes in atrial tissue. Altered membrane channel regulation produces abnormal excitability; pulmonary vein musculature (and other foci such as the posterior left atrial wall, superior vena cava, coronary sinus, vein of Marshall) shows enhanced automaticity generating ectopic beats that initiate AF. Progressive cellular and tissue remodeling shortens atrial refractory periods and creates functional reentry, with eventual fibrosis and left atrial enlargement, explaining the typical progression from paroxysmal to persistent to long-standing persistent disease. Loss of atrial systole reduces cardiac output, and variable ventricular filling produces variable stroke volume (p.1990-1992).","additionalDifferentials":["Multifocal atrial tachycardia (>=3 distinct P-wave morphologies with clear isoelectric intervals and a slower atrial rate than AF; typically in chronic pulmonary disease and acute illness) (p.1989).","Other supraventricular tachycardias including atrial flutter, focal atrial tachycardia, AV nodal reentry, and AV reciprocating tachycardia, distinguished by 12-lead ECG appearance and response to AV nodal blockade (p.1976-1977).","Preexcited (accessory-pathway) AF, presenting as an irregular wide-complex tachycardia with bizarre, beat-to-beat-variable QRS morphology (p.1985-1986)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transesophageal echocardiography to exclude left atrial appendage thrombus before cardioversion when AF duration is >48 h or unknown (p.1992).","Cardiac CT angiography with delayed-acquisition imaging as an alternative to TEE for left atrial appendage thrombus detection, with high sensitivity and specificity (p.1992).","Ambulatory ECG monitoring, implantable loop recorders, or wearable/home ECG devices to capture intermittent or subclinical episodes (p.1991, 1993)."],"redFlags":["Acute AF with significant haemodynamic compromise, pulmonary oedema, or evidence of coronary ischaemia indicates need for emergent cardioversion (p.1992).","Preexcited AF over an accessory pathway with very rapid repetitive conduction (minimum R-R <250 ms occurs in ~25% of preexciting pathways) is potentially life-threatening, predisposing to ventricular fibrillation and sudden death (p.1985-1986).","Syncope or presyncope from bradycardia/pauses, including offset pauses on spontaneous conversion to sinus rhythm, suggesting coexisting sinus node dysfunction (tachy-brady syndrome) (p.1992).","Sustained uncontrolled rapid ventricular rates risk precipitating heart failure and tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy (p.1990, 1992)."],"prognosis":"AF is not benign: it carries a 1.5- to 1.9-fold increased mortality after adjusting for underlying cardiac disease, and causes approximately 25% of all strokes. It increases the risk of dementia, MRI-detected silent embolic infarcts, congestive heart failure, and cardiomyopathy. Tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy is generally reversible once ventricular rate is controlled. AF tends to be progressive, with no definitive durable cure, and patients maintaining sinus rhythm generally have better survival than those who remain in AF (p.1990, 1992, 1994).","pitfalls":["Relying on AF burden or symptom frequency to gauge stroke risk is mistaken; anticoagulation decisions should rest on the risk-factor profile, not how often AF is documented (p.1993).","A normal intermittent monitor does not exclude clinically important AF, since asymptomatic episodes are common (p.1993).","Cardioverting AF of >48 h (or unknown) duration without anticoagulation or thrombus exclusion risks thromboembolism, which can occur even days later because of post-conversion atrial stunning (delayed recovery of mechanical function) (p.1992).","Nonvalvular AF (AF without mitral stenosis) differs in management from valvular AF; rheumatic mitral stenosis and mechanical valves carry especially high thromboembolic risk (p.1993)."],"severityClassification":"By temporal pattern (Table 258-1): paroxysmal AF (self-terminates or is cardioverted within 7 days; normal-to-mildly enlarged left atrium, low scar burden), persistent AF (continuous >7 days but <1 year; mild-to-severe LA enlargement, moderate scar), and long-standing persistent AF (>1 year; severely enlarged LA, high scar burden). A newer staged framework (at risk of AF, pre-AF, AF, permanent AF) reframes AF as a disease continuum. Mitral stenosis defines valvular versus nonvalvular AF for risk purposes (p.1991, 1993, 1996).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1985","Harrison 22e p.1989","Harrison 22e p.1990","Harrison 22e p.1991","Harrison 22e p.1992","Harrison 22e p.1993","Harrison 22e p.1994","Harrison 22e p.1996"],"crossLinks":["ischemic_stroke","heart_failure","hyperthyroidism","pe"]},"biliary_colic":{"name":"Biliary colic / cholelithiasis","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.357 (Diseases of the Gallbladder and Bile Ducts) p.2728-2735; Ch.332 (Approach to GI Disease) p.2460-2461; Ch.52 (Jaundice) p.325","clinicalPearls":["Biliary colic is the most specific symptom of gallstone disease, yet 'colic' is a misnomer: the pain is steady rather than waxing-and-waning, reflecting non-relievable distention of the gallbladder/duct against a stone obstructing the cystic duct or CBD (p.2731).","A typical episode begins abruptly and lasts roughly 30 minutes to 5 hours before easing; pain that persists beyond ~5 hours should prompt suspicion of acute cholecystitis rather than uncomplicated colic (p.2731).","Pain is characteristically a severe steady ache or fullness in the epigastrium or right upper quadrant, often radiating to the interscapular region, right scapula, or shoulder (p.2731).","Attacks may be triggered by a fatty meal, a large meal after prolonged fasting, or even a normal meal, and are frequently nocturnal, occurring within a few hours of going to bed (p.2731).","Nausea and vomiting commonly accompany the pain, but fever/chills are NOT features of uncomplicated colic and signal a complication (p.2731).","Vague short-lived epigastric fullness, dyspepsia, belching, or flatulence after fatty food are nonspecific and occur with or without gallstones; they should not be equated with true biliary pain (p.2731).","Most asymptomatic gallstones stay silent: 60-80% remain symptom-free over follow-up up to 25 years, so incidentally found stones without referable symptoms generally do not warrant intervention (p.2731).","Once colic occurs, the disease has 'declared' itself; complications requiring surgery are far more common in patients who have developed biliary pain than in the asymptomatic group, and most patients who develop complications had prior warning symptoms (p.2731).","Sudden awakening from sleep by pain favors an organic cause such as biliary colic over a functional gut-brain disorder (p.2461)."],"pathophysiology":"Biliary colic results from transient obstruction of the cystic duct (or common bile duct) by a gallstone migrating out of the gallbladder. The obstruction raises intraluminal pressure and distends the viscus; because repetitive biliary contractions cannot overcome or relieve the blockage, a steady visceral pain is generated. When the stone disimpacts or falls back, pressure normalizes and the pain resolves spontaneously, so an uncomplicated episode is self-limited and is NOT accompanied by inflammation, fever, or sustained cholestasis. Underlying stones are usually cholesterol stones (>90% in Western populations), which form when bile becomes supersaturated with cholesterol, cholesterol monohydrate crystals nucleate, and gallbladder hypomotility allows stasis and stone growth; gallbladder emptying is frequently impaired in stone formers. Distinct mechanisms (mechanical, chemical from lysolecithin, and bacterial) convert simple obstruction into acute cholecystitis when the cystic duct stays blocked (p.2727-2732).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute cholecystitis (pain >5 h, fever, Murphy's sign) — represents biliary pain that fails to resolve as the cystic duct stays obstructed (p.2731-2732)","Choledocholithiasis with cholangitis — CBD stone causing biliary pain plus jaundice and spiking fevers/chills (Charcot's triad) (p.2735)","Gallstone pancreatitis — stone-related obstruction at the ampulla; pain is severe and persists for days, not hours (p.2461, p.2735)","Peptic ulcer disease / functional dyspepsia — ulcer pain is intermittent over weeks-months and may be relieved by eating, unlike the sudden hours-long onset of colic (p.2461)","Irritable bowel syndrome and other disorders of gut-brain interaction — common causes of abdominal pain, exacerbated by stress rather than fatty meals (p.2460-2461)","Sphincter of Oddi dysfunction / biliary dyskinesia — biliary-type pain without stones, may follow cholecystectomy (p.2734)","Acalculous cholecystopathy — disordered gallbladder motility producing recurrent biliary pain in the absence of stones (p.2732)","Right-lower-lobe pneumonia, mesenteric ischemia, or right renal colic as non-biliary causes of RUQ/flank pain (p.2460)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Functional dyspepsia / non-ulcer dyspepsia presenting with post-prandial epigastric discomfort (p.2461)","Gastroesophageal reflux / pill esophagitis (biliary colic itself can be mistaken for unexplained upper-GI symptoms) (p.2728)","Irritable bowel syndrome (most common cause of abdominal pain alongside functional dyspepsia) (p.2461)","Sphincter of Oddi dysfunction and acalculous cholecystopathy (biliary-type pain without calculi) (p.2732, p.2734)","Cardiac/precordial referral — gallbladder pain may occasionally be referred to the precordium (p.2728)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Right-upper-quadrant ultrasound is the procedure of choice: rapid, not limited by jaundice or pregnancy, and >95% accurate for gallstones, identifying calculi as small as ~1.5 mm by their acoustic shadowing and gravity-dependent movement (p.2729-2730)","Ultrasound also distinguishes biliary sludge (low-echogenicity layer that shifts with position but casts NO acoustic shadow) from true stones, and can assess gallbladder emptying (p.2728-2730)","Serum bilirubin and/or alkaline phosphatase elevation during a biliary attack suggests a common-duct stone rather than simple colic (p.2731)","Cholescintigraphy (HIDA/DISIDA scan): non-visualization of the gallbladder indicates cystic-duct obstruction and supports acute cholecystitis (p.2730)","MRCP, EUS, or ERCP for suspected choledocholithiasis; ERCP is the gold standard and also therapeutic, MRCP often the initial test (p.325)","Plain abdominal film detects only the radiopaque minority of stones (~10-15% of cholesterol, ~50% of pigment) and has low yield (p.2730)"],"redFlags":["Pain persisting beyond ~5 hours — suspect acute cholecystitis (p.2731)","Fever, chills, or rigors with biliary pain — implies a complication (cholecystitis, cholangitis, or pancreatitis), not uncomplicated colic (p.2731)","Jaundice and/or rising bilirubin or alkaline phosphatase — suggests common-bile-duct stone or obstruction (p.2731)","Charcot's triad (biliary pain + jaundice + spiking fever/chills) — acute cholangitis (p.2735)","Murphy's sign (inspiratory arrest on RUQ palpation) with RUQ tenderness, fever, and leukocytosis — acute cholecystitis (p.323, p.2732)","Severe pain persisting for days, with confusion/sepsis features — suggests pancreatitis or suppurative cholangitis (p.2461, p.2735)"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated biliary colic is self-limited; the individual attack resolves once the transient obstruction clears. The natural history of the underlying gallstones is generally favorable: 60-80% of asymptomatic stone carriers stay symptom-free for up to 25 years, the chance of developing symptoms is only ~2-4% per year in the first 5 years after diagnosis (falling to 1-2% thereafter), and the yearly incidence of complications is only ~0.1-0.3%. The cumulative risk of death from gallstone disease under expectant management is small. However, once biliary pain has occurred, the likelihood of recurrent attacks and of complications requiring intervention rises substantially; younger patients at diagnosis are more likely to become symptomatic than those over 60 (p.2731).","pitfalls":["Treating the term 'colic' literally and expecting intermittent crampy pain — biliary colic is steady, and waiting for it to fluctuate can delay recognition (p.2731).","Attributing nonspecific fatty-food dyspepsia, belching, or bloating to gallstones; these symptoms are equally common in people without stones and do not establish a biliary cause (p.2731).","Assuming an incidental gallstone explains a patient's symptoms — most stones are silent, and a coexisting non-biliary disorder (e.g., reflux, peptic ulcer, IBS) may be the true cause (p.2731, p.2735).","Missing that fever or jaundice has converted 'colic' into a complication requiring different (often urgent) management (p.2731).","Relying on a plain abdominal film, which misses most cholesterol stones, instead of ultrasound (p.2730).","Overlooking acalculous causes of biliary-type pain (sphincter of Oddi dysfunction, acalculous cholecystopathy) when ultrasound shows no stones (p.2732, p.2734)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames the spectrum by clinical course rather than a formal grading scale: uncomplicated biliary colic = transient cystic-duct obstruction giving self-limited pain (30 min-5 h) WITHOUT fever, inflammation, or jaundice; progression/complication is signalled by pain >5 h, fever/chills, or jaundice, and corresponds to acute cholecystitis (sustained cystic-duct obstruction with inflammation), choledocholithiasis with cholangitis (CBD obstruction + infection, Charcot's triad), or gallstone pancreatitis. Acute cholecystitis is itself triaged by complications (e.g., empyema, gangrene, perforation, Mirizzi's syndrome) (p.2731-2735).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2727","Harrison 22e p.2728","Harrison 22e p.2729","Harrison 22e p.2730","Harrison 22e p.2731","Harrison 22e p.2732","Harrison 22e p.2735","Harrison 22e p.2460","Harrison 22e p.2461","Harrison 22e p.323","Harrison 22e p.325"],"crossLinks":["CHOLECYSTITIS","CHOLANGITIS","pancreatitis"]},"bowel_obstruction":{"name":"Bowel obstruction","system":"Surgical / GI","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e, Chap. 16 Abdominal Pain pp.110-113; Chap. 53 Abdominal Swelling and Ascites p.327; Chap. 333 Gastrointestinal Endoscopy p.2485; GI symptom evaluation chapter (loss of bowel sounds vs hyperactive sounds)","clinicalPearls":["Small-bowel obstruction characteristically produces poorly localized, intermittent peri-/supra-umbilical colicky pain; as the bowel progressively dilates and loses muscular tone the colicky quality of the pain may actually diminish, which can be falsely reassuring (p.110).","Colonic obstruction tends to cause lower-intensity colicky pain located in the infraumbilical area, often radiating to the lumbar region, in contrast to the more periumbilical pain of small-bowel obstruction (p.110).","High-pitched, hyperactive bowel sounds are characteristic of mechanical obstruction, whereas loss of bowel sounds points toward ileus; however, once proximal bowel becomes markedly distended and edematous, peristaltic sounds may become weak or absent even without peritonitis.","Auscultation is one of the least revealing parts of the abdominal exam: a strangulating small-intestinal obstruction can occur with normal-sounding peristalsis, so normal bowel sounds do not exclude a catastrophe (p.113).","Patients with obstructive colic tend to be restless and may thrash about, in contrast to the peritonitic patient who lies still to avoid movement — a useful bedside discriminator when ischaemia/perforation supervenes (p.110).","Inability to pass stool or flatus combined with nausea/vomiting and increasing abdominal girth suggests bowel obstruction (or severe constipation/ileus); retained feces in the colon itself adds to the distension (p.327)."],"pathophysiology":"Mechanical obstruction of a hollow viscus classically elicits intermittent (colicky) pain that is less well localized than the parietal pain of peritoneal irritation; distension of the viscus may also produce steady pain with only occasional paroxysms. With superimposed strangulating obstruction, traction on the root of the mesentery can refer pain to the lower lumbar region. As the obstructed proximal bowel progressively dilates it loses muscular tone, so colicky pain may wane and peristaltic sounds may weaken. Obstruction must be distinguished from adynamic ileus (loss of peristalsis) and from colonic pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie's syndrome), in which the bowel is dilated without a mechanical lesion (Harrison 22e pp.110-113, p.327).","additionalDifferentials":["Adynamic / paralytic ileus (loss of peristalsis with absent bowel sounds) rather than mechanical obstruction","Colonic pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie's syndrome) — colonic dilatation without a mechanical lesion","Severe constipation / fecal impaction causing abdominal distension and discomfort","Sigmoid or cecal volvulus as a cause of colonic obstruction","Gastric outlet obstruction (malignancy or chronic peptic stenosis of the pylorus) presenting with vomiting of partially digested food hours after eating","Esophageal obstruction by impacted food bolus / foreign body (proximal obstruction mimic)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute mesenteric arterial occlusion — early diffuse cramping pain from hyperperistalsis with 'pain out of proportion to physical findings' can mimic early obstruction; tenderness and rigidity may be absent until vascular collapse","Porphyria and lead colic — severe hyperperistalsis makes the pain difficult to distinguish from intestinal obstruction","Diabetic ketoacidosis — can both precipitate and mimic an acute abdomen; if pain does not resolve with metabolic correction, suspect an organic cause such as obstruction","Irritable bowel syndrome — functional pain with altered bowel habit, but nausea/vomiting are rare and localized tenderness/spasm inconsistent (a non-obstructive mimic of altered bowel habit)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Low-dose CT is preferred over plain abdominal radiography when the indication for surgical/medical intervention is unclear in nontraumatic acute abdominal pain","Plain upright/lateral-decubitus abdominal radiographs can show dilated bowel loops suggesting obstruction or ileus, but have limited utility and may be unnecessary when the cause (e.g., strangulated external hernia) is already evident","Water-soluble contrast enema can be diagnostic in suspected colonic obstruction without perforation; oral barium sulfate should be avoided if colonic obstruction is suspected","Serum amylase may be raised by strangulating intestinal obstruction (also by perforated ulcer, cholecystitis), so an elevation neither confirms nor excludes the need for operation","Leukocytosis is non-specific (a WBC >20,000/uL may follow perforation but also pancreatitis, cholecystitis, infarction) and should never be the sole determinant of operative need; a normal count does not exclude perforation","Endoscopy can diagnose and often manage gastroduodenal and colonic obstruction/pseudo-obstruction, but small-bowel obstruction from surgical adhesions is generally neither diagnosed nor treated endoscopically"],"redFlags":["Continuous diffuse pain with absent tenderness/rigidity ('pain out of proportion to findings') in a patient at vascular risk — suspect superior mesenteric artery occlusion / intestinal ischaemia","Spread of pain to the lower lumbar region — may indicate strangulating obstruction with traction on the mesenteric root","Truly silent abdomen with sudden severe pain — suggests chemical peritonitis from a perforated viscus","Catastrophic intra-abdominal events may present with minimal or no pain/muscle spasm in obtunded, debilitated, immunosuppressed, or elderly patients — a slowly developing process also attenuates guarding"],"prognosis":null,"pitfalls":["Relying on auscultation or bowel-sound quality: strangulating obstruction can occur with normal peristaltic sounds, and distended/edematous proximal bowel may be quiet even without peritonitis (p.113).","Interpreting waning colicky pain as improvement — pain may diminish simply because the dilated bowel has lost muscular tone (p.110).","Using leukocytosis or serum amylase as a deciding factor for surgery — both are non-specific in obstruction and infarction (p.113).","Giving oral barium when colonic obstruction is possible (risk of inspissation above the obstruction); use water-soluble contrast instead.","Missing intestinal ischaemia, which classically elicits severe pain but little tenderness (GI evaluation chapter)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.110","Harrison 22e p.111","Harrison 22e p.113","Harrison 22e p.327","Harrison 22e p.2485"],"crossLinks":["mesenteric_ischemia","peptic_ulcer","ibd_flare"]},"brain_tumour":{"name":"Brain tumour / mass lesion","system":"Neuro-oncology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"716-724, 157","clinicalPearls":["Presenting features split into general (raised-ICP) symptoms - headache +/- nausea/vomiting, cognitive change, personality change, gait disorder - and focal/lateralizing signs such as hemiparesis, aphasia or visual field loss; many patients have both (p.716).","The classic morning-predominant headache that eases through the day occurs in only a minority of patients; brain-tumour headache is more often a nondescript dull ache, and migraine produces this pattern far more often than tumour (p.716, p.157).","Vomiting preceding headache by weeks is highly characteristic of posterior fossa tumours, and headache disturbs sleep in about 10% (p.157).","Seizures from a brain tumour always have a focal onset whether or not this is clinically apparent (p.716).","New de novo headache in a patient with known malignancy should raise suspicion of cerebral metastases or carcinomatous (leptomeningeal) meningitis (p.157).","On MRI, malignant tumours (primary or metastatic) typically enhance with gadolinium, have central necrosis and surrounding white-matter edema, whereas low-grade gliomas usually do NOT enhance and are best seen as increased signal on FLAIR (p.716).","Meningiomas and vestibular schwannomas are extra-axial, densely enhancing lesions; a meningioma characteristically shows a dural tail and compresses but does not invade brain (p.716, p.721).","Leptomeningeal disease produces multilevel neuraxis signs (radiculopathies, cranial neuropathies, seizures, confusion, raised-ICP encephalopathy); isolated focal deficits like hemiparesis are rarely from it unless brain is directly infiltrated (p.724).","Parinaud's syndrome (impaired upgaze and accommodation) points to a pineal-region mass (p.720)."],"pathophysiology":"General/raised-ICP symptoms arise as the enlarging tumour plus surrounding vasogenic edema raises intracranial pressure or obstructs CSF circulation, producing hydrocephalus; focal deficits reflect local invasion or compression (p.716). Primary tumours develop through stepwise acquisition of genetic changes - loss of tumour-suppressor genes (p53, CDKN2A/B, PTEN) and amplification/overexpression of oncogenes such as EGFR and PDGFR - and glioblastomas in particular show marked molecular heterogeneity (p.717). The 2021 WHO CNS classification integrates molecular markers (e.g. IDH mutation, 1p/19q co-deletion) with histology into the diagnosis (p.717-718). Brain metastases spread hematogenously, lodging in terminal arterioles at the grey-white junction in watershed territory, so distribution follows blood flow (~85% supratentorial, ~15% posterior fossa) (p.723).","additionalDifferentials":["Metastatic brain tumour - three times more common than all primary brain tumours combined in adults; commonest sources lung and breast, with melanoma having the greatest propensity to metastasize to brain (p.716, p.723)","Diffuse glioma - the most common malignant PRIMARY brain tumour; subdivided into IDH-mutant astrocytoma, IDH-mutant 1p/19q-codeleted oligodendroglioma, and IDH wild-type glioblastoma (p.717-718)","Meningioma - now the most common primary brain tumour overall (~35%); extra-axial dural-based lesion, WHO grades I-III (p.720-721)","Vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma) - ~9% of primary brain tumours, presents with progressive unilateral hearing loss/tinnitus/dizziness (p.721)","Primary CNS lymphoma - densely enhancing, often periventricular/basal ganglia/corpus callosum; associated with immunosuppression and EBV in HIV (p.720)","Pituitary adenoma - amenorrhoea/galactorrhoea with headache suggests a prolactinoma (p.157, p.721)","Pineal-region tumour presenting with Parinaud's syndrome and hydrocephalus (p.720)","Colloid cyst of the third ventricle - intermittent hydrocephalus, rarely sudden death (p.722)"],"infectionMimics":["Brain abscess - shares a ring-enhancing radiographic appearance with metastasis and high-grade glioma (p.723)","Cerebral toxoplasmosis - in immunocompromised patients, multiple ring-enhancing lesions can be hard to distinguish from PCNSL or metastases (p.721)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Demyelinating lesion - can mimic the nonspecific appearance of a brain metastasis (p.723)","Neurosarcoidosis - listed among lesions resembling metastatic disease on imaging (p.723)","Radiation necrosis in a previously irradiated patient - mimics tumour and may need PET/MR perfusion to distinguish recurrence from treatment effect (p.716, p.723)"],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI with gadolinium is the preferred test; CT only if MRI cannot be done (p.716)","Neuroimaging is often the only test needed to diagnose a brain tumour; laboratory tests are rarely useful (p.716)","FLAIR sequences to detect non-enhancing low-grade gliomas (p.716, p.718)","Functional MRI for presurgical mapping of eloquent cortex (p.716)","PET, MR perfusion and MR spectroscopy to assess metabolic activity and help separate tumour progression from radiation/treatment necrosis (p.716)","CSF cytology / flow cytometry when leptomeningeal involvement is suspected (gold standard but positive in only ~50% on first LP, still misses ~10% after three samples) (p.720, p.724)","Stereotactic biopsy for histologic diagnosis when imaging is insufficient; in suspected PCNSL withhold glucocorticoids until biopsy because they are cytolytic and can render tissue non-diagnostic (p.720)","HIV testing and body PET/CT, spine MRI, CSF and slit-lamp eye exam to stage suspected PCNSL (p.720-721)","Serum tumour markers (e.g. beta-hCG) occasionally elevated in metastatic disease (p.716)"],"redFlags":["Progressive, subacute focal/lateralizing deficit (hemiparesis, aphasia, visual field loss) (p.716)","New-onset focal-onset seizures (p.716)","Morning-predominant headache or headache with raised-ICP features (nausea/vomiting, worse with exertion or position change) (p.716, p.157)","Vomiting preceding headache by weeks - suggests a posterior fossa mass (p.157)","New de novo headache in a patient with known systemic cancer (p.157)","Personality change/apathy that may be mistaken for depression (p.716)"],"prognosis":"Outcome is tumour-specific. Low-grade (grade 2) IDH-mutant astrocytoma usually transforms to higher grade with median survival around 10 years; high-grade IDH-mutant astrocytoma roughly 3-9 years (p.718). Oligodendrogliomas (IDH-mutant, 1p/19q co-deleted) are more therapy-responsive with median survival in excess of 10 years (p.718). Glioblastoma (IDH wild-type) is the most common malignant primary tumour and invariably recurs; with standard combined therapy median survival is ~15-18 months and 5-year survival ~10%; adverse factors include older age, unmethylated MGMT promoter, poor performance status and unresectable tumour (p.718-719). Diffuse midline glioma H3K27-altered carries a poor prognosis (~1 year) regardless of grade (p.719). Pilocytic astrocytoma (grade 1) and completely resected meningiomas/ependymomas can be cured (p.719-721). Brain metastases treated with WBRT have median survival of only ~4-6 months, though SRS achieves local control in 80-90% and occasional cure (p.724).","pitfalls":["A ring-enhancing lesion is non-specific - the differential includes brain abscess, toxoplasmosis, PCNSL, demyelination, sarcoidosis, radiation necrosis and primary brain tumour, so do not assume tumour without context (p.721, p.723).","Withholding the diagnosis of glioma can be hard for diffusely infiltrative non-enhancing tumours (formerly gliomatosis cerebri), which often present only with cognitive decline and confluent FLAIR signal without mass effect (p.718).","Do not give glucocorticoids before biopsy in suspected PCNSL - their cytolytic effect can yield non-diagnostic tissue (p.720).","Prophylactic antiepileptic drugs have no role in tumour patients who have not had a seizure (only perioperative use is sometimes appropriate) (p.716).","A normal or migrainous headache pattern is far more often migraine than tumour - tumour headache is frequently nondescript (p.157).","Imaging-negative leptomeningeal disease is common; a single negative CSF cytology does not exclude it (positive in only ~50% on first LP) (p.724)."],"severityClassification":"WHO CNS grading (2021 integrated histologic + molecular classification): diffuse gliomas graded 2-4 by histologic malignancy and molecular markers, with CDKN2A/B homozygous deletion conferring grade 4 to otherwise lower-grade IDH-mutant astrocytomas; pilocytic astrocytoma is grade 1. Meningiomas are graded I (benign), II (atypical) and III (malignant) by increasing aggressiveness (p.717-718, p.720-721).","references":["Harrison 22e p.716","Harrison 22e p.717","Harrison 22e p.718","Harrison 22e p.719","Harrison 22e p.720","Harrison 22e p.721","Harrison 22e p.723","Harrison 22e p.724","Harrison 22e p.157"],"crossLinks":["BRAIN_ABSCESS","seizure_epilepsy","iih","malignancy_b"]},"cardiogenic_shock":{"name":"Cardiogenic shock","system":"Cardiology / Critical care","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.316 (Cardiogenic Shock and Pulmonary Edema, p.2324-2327); Ch.314 (Approach to Shock, p.2311); Ch.286 (STEMI complications, p.2123); valvular MR p.334","clinicalPearls":["Defined haemodynamically as a low cardiac-output state producing life-threatening end-organ hypoperfusion: persistent SBP <90 mmHg (or MAP <60-65 mmHg) unresponsive to volume and/or requiring vasopressors, plus hypoperfusion features such as lactate >2 mmol/L (p.2324).","Objective parameters (cardiac index, pulmonary capillary wedge pressure) help confirm a cardiac cause but are not mandatory for the diagnosis (p.2324).","Compensatory rise in systemic vascular resistance is typical; left-ventricular processes elevate PCWP whereas right-ventricular processes elevate CVP (p.2311).","Occasionally BP is maintained by very high SVR despite established shock, so normotension does not exclude cardiogenic shock (p.2326).","Only about 10% of patients present with shock on admission; roughly 90% develop it during hospitalisation, so serial reassessment is essential (p.2123, p.2325).","Shock complicating a first inferior MI should prompt a search for a mechanical complication or right-ventricular involvement (p.2325).","A new systolic murmur after MI should trigger urgent echocardiography to look for VSR, papillary muscle rupture or free-wall rupture; a murmur may be absent in up to 50% of acute MR cases (p.334, p.1894).","Pericardial thrombus on imaging after MI strongly suggests myocardial rupture and is a surgical emergency (p.1894).","Patients who develop shock typically have severe multivessel disease with 'piecemeal' necrosis extending beyond the original infarct zone (p.2123)."],"pathophysiology":"A profound fall in myocardial contractility initiates a self-perpetuating spiral: reduced cardiac output and stroke volume lower blood pressure and coronary perfusion, worsening ischaemia and further depressing contractility, which left untreated leads to death. Ineffective stroke volume is the inciting event, but inadequate circulatory compensation also contributes. Early peripheral vasoconstriction may transiently improve coronary and peripheral perfusion but raises afterload and can worsen ischaemia. Over time, systemic inflammation from acute cardiac injury induces pathologic vasodilatation; inflammatory cytokines and inducible/endothelial nitric oxide synthase increase NO and its by-product peroxynitrite, which are negatively inotropic and cardiotoxic. Lactic acidosis and hypoxaemia compound the cycle because severe acidosis blunts the efficacy of endogenous and exogenous catecholamines. Bleeding and transfusion during ICU care can further trigger inflammation. Left-ventricular failure accounts for roughly 80% of cardiogenic shock complicating acute MI, with acute severe mitral regurgitation, ventricular septal rupture, predominant RV failure, and free-wall rupture/tamponade making up the remainder (p.2325).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute fulminant myocarditis","End-stage / decompensated cardiomyopathy","Takotsubo (apical ballooning) syndrome as a transient cause","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with severe LV outflow obstruction","Critical valvular disease (critical aortic/mitral stenosis; acute severe aortic or mitral regurgitation)","Aortic dissection with aortic insufficiency or tamponade","Refractory sustained tachyarrhythmia or bradyarrhythmia","Right-ventricular failure from acute MI or acute/decompensated cor pulmonale","Post-cardiac-arrest myocardial stunning; postcardiotomy shock"],"infectionMimics":["Septic shock with myocardial depression (sepsis-induced cardiomyopathy), which can also coexist as a mixed shock state (p.2311, p.2326)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Hypovolaemic shock (low CVP and PCWP, elevated SVR) — distinguished by low filling pressures (p.2311)","Obstructive shock from cardiac tamponade, tension pneumothorax, massive pulmonary embolism or aortic dissection (p.2311)","Distributive/anaphylactic or neurogenic shock with low SVR (p.2311)","Negative-inotrope or vasodilator drug overdose (beta-blocker, calcium-channel antagonist) and toxic/metabolic causes (pheochromocytoma, scorpion venom) (p.2327)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Arterial blood gas (typically hypoxaemia with anion-gap metabolic acidosis) and serial arterial lactate (p.2326)","Chest radiograph (pulmonary vascular congestion/oedema; normal in up to a third; enlarged heart suggests prior MI) (p.2326)","ECG looking for multilead Q waves/ST elevation, LBBB; ST elevation in aVR with diffuse ST depression suggests left main / global ischaemia (p.2326)","Echocardiography to define mechanism (LV/RV/biventricular, VSR, acute MR, tamponade, LVOT obstruction, valve lesions, aortic dissection) (p.2326)","Right-sided ECG leads (e.g. V4R) for suspected RV infarction (p.2123)","Pulmonary artery catheterisation in diagnostic/management uncertainty or severe unresponsive shock; equalised diastolic pressures suggest tamponade (p.2326)","Coronary angiography immediately in all cardiogenic shock complicating MI to guide reperfusion (p.2327)","Renal function (creatinine), hepatic transaminases (elevated in ~20%, marker of high mortality), admission glucose, WBC and CRP (p.2326)"],"redFlags":["Lactate >8 mmol/L (places patient in SCAI stage E) (p.2326)","Cardiac arrest with ongoing CPR and/or ECMO (extremis stage) (p.2326)","Rising hepatic transaminases from liver hypoperfusion (~20%; high-mortality marker) (p.2326)","New systolic murmur, thrill or sudden deterioration suggesting mechanical complication (VSR, papillary rupture, free-wall rupture) (p.334, p.1894)","Progressive oliguria/anuria and altered mental status indicating worsening hypoperfusion (p.2326)","Failure to respond to initial interventions (SCAI stage D, 'deteriorating') (p.2326)"],"prognosis":"In-hospital mortality ranges from roughly 40-60% depending on shock severity and underlying cause; cardiogenic and septic shock carry the highest mortality among shock types (90-day mortality around 52% for cardiogenic shock in one ED cohort). Higher lactate, elevated admission glucose, hepatic hypoperfusion and progressive renal impairment are adverse markers. Untreated shock progresses through compensated (preshock), decompensated, and finally irreversible stages with permanent organ dysfunction and multi-organ failure. Reperfusion and modern care have reduced the incidence of shock complicating acute MI to about 5-10% (p.2311, p.2325).","pitfalls":["Assuming normotension excludes shock — BP may be preserved by markedly elevated SVR (p.2326).","Missing a mechanical complication: a murmur is absent in up to 50% of acute papillary-muscle-rupture MR (p.334).","Overlooking RV infarction in inferior MI, where fluid management differs (volume to maintain RV preload) (p.2123).","Treating presumed cardiogenic shock as hypovolaemia — filling pressures (PCWP/CVP) are elevated, not low (p.2311).","Failing to recognise mixed shock (e.g. sepsis-induced cardiomyopathy superimposed on distributive shock) (p.2311)."],"severityClassification":"SCAI shock classification (2019, updated): Stage A 'at risk' — no current signs/symptoms; Stage B 'beginning'/preshock — relative hypotension or tachycardia without hypoperfusion; Stage C 'classic' — manifest hypoperfusion requiring intervention (inotropes, vasopressors or MCS, excluding ECMO) beyond volume; Stage D 'deteriorating' — like C but worsening and failing initial therapy; Stage E 'extremis' — cardiac arrest with ongoing CPR and/or ECMO. The updated definition adds a three-axis model: (1) shock severity, (2) phenotype/aetiology, (3) risk modifiers such as cardiac arrest. Lactate >8 mmol/L marks stage E (p.2325-2326).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2324","Harrison 22e p.2325","Harrison 22e p.2326","Harrison 22e p.2327","Harrison 22e p.2311","Harrison 22e p.2123","Harrison 22e p.334","Harrison 22e p.1894"],"crossLinks":["acs","heart_failure","SEPTIC_SHOCK","hypovolemic_shock","tamponade","aortic_dissection"]},"ckd":{"name":"Chronic kidney disease","system":"Nephrology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.322 (Chronic Kidney Disease), pp.2385-2396; Ch.319 staging/clinical, pp.2361-2362 and Table 319-3 p.2362","clinicalPearls":["Progression risk in CKD tracks three things together: the current GFR, the trajectory of GFR over time, and the degree of albuminuria - not GFR alone (p.2385).","Once nephron mass is reduced, surviving nephrons undergo compensatory hyperfiltration and hypertrophy; these short-term adaptations become maladaptive, driving glomerulosclerosis and ongoing GFR loss even after the original insult has resolved (p.2385-2386, 2396).","A normal serum creatinine can mask substantial GFR loss in older patients and women (lower muscle mass); an elderly woman with a 'normal' creatinine may have a GFR under 50 mL/min/1.73 m2 (p.2387).","Loss of urinary concentrating ability gives an obligate output of ~2 L/day and nocturia; paradoxically, resolution of nocturia may feel like improvement but can actually signal worsening oliguria (p.2362).","Cardiac troponin is frequently chronically elevated in CKD without acute ischemia - serial trending over hours is more informative than a single value, and persistently elevated levels independently predict adverse cardiovascular events (p.2392).","Most CKD patients die of cardiovascular disease before ever reaching stage 5; cardiovascular risk is increased roughly 10- to 200-fold versus age/sex-matched controls depending on stage (p.2391).","In advanced CKD a malnutrition-inflammation state can paradoxically reverse classic risk factors (lowering blood pressure and lipids) and is itself a marker of poor prognosis - so 'improving' numbers can be ominous (p.2392).","A drop in GFR of up to ~30% after starting an ACEi/ARB is often a benign reflection of relieved intraglomerular hyperfiltration; if stable it can be tolerated, but a progressive rise in creatinine suggests underlying renovascular disease (p.2392, 2396).","A normal-sized kidney does not exclude CKD: diabetic nephropathy (early), amyloidosis, and HIV nephropathy can show normal or enlarged kidneys despite advanced disease; polycystic kidneys are typically enlarged (p.2394-2395).","Hyperkalemia disproportionate to the GFR decline points to hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism (e.g., diabetes) or distal-nephron disease such as obstructive uropathy or sickle cell nephropathy (p.2389).","PTH is itself a uremic toxin - high levels are associated with muscle weakness, cardiac fibrosis, ESA-resistant anemia (via marrow fibrosis), and constitutional symptoms (p.2390).","Erythrocytosis rather than anemia can occur in some CKD-associated conditions: urinary tract obstruction, polycystic kidney disease, renovascular disease, and renal cell carcinoma (p.2362)."],"pathophysiology":"CKD results from two overlapping processes: (1) initiating mechanisms specific to the underlying cause (developmental/genetic, immune-complex, inflammatory, metabolic, microvascular, or toxic injury to vascular, glomerular, or tubulointerstitial compartments), and (2) a nonspecific final common pathway in which loss of nephron mass triggers compensatory hyperfiltration and hypertrophy of remaining nephrons. Intrarenal renin-angiotensin activation with blunted tubuloglomerular feedback raises intraglomerular pressure; this adaptation becomes maladaptive, distorting glomerular architecture, impairing podocyte function and the filtration barrier, and producing progressive sclerosis and nephron dropout. The uremic syndrome arises from three spheres of dysfunction: accumulation of retained solutes (urea and creatinine are only readily-measured but incomplete surrogates for many retained toxins), loss of non-excretory kidney functions (fluid/electrolyte homeostasis, hormone production/regulation - EPO, calcitriol, with altered PTH, FGF-23, insulin), and progressive systemic inflammation (elevated CRP, falling albumin/fetuin) feeding a malnutrition-inflammation-atherosclerosis/calcification syndrome (p.2385-2389).","additionalDifferentials":["Diabetic nephropathy (commonest cause in North America/Europe, usually type 2 DM) (p.2389)","Glomerulonephritis (p.2389)","Hypertension-associated CKD / nephrosclerosis, including ischemic and vascular kidney disease (p.2389)","Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD) and other cystic/tubulointerstitial nephropathy (p.2389)","APOL1-mediated kidney disease and FSGS (higher frequency in sub-Saharan African ancestry) (p.2386-2387)","Hereditary/genetic CKD - Alport syndrome (type IV collagen, hematuria + sensorineural hearing loss + lens defects), Fabry disease, cystinosis (p.2394)","Multiple myeloma (check serum/urine protein electrophoresis in patients >35 with unexplained CKD, especially with anemia and inappropriately normal/high calcium) (p.2394)","Reflux nephropathy (recurrent childhood UTI, asymmetric scarred kidneys) (p.2395)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute or subacute kidney injury that is potentially reversible - the single most important distinction from true CKD; prior creatinine values establish chronicity (p.2395)","Acute-on-chronic kidney failure from a superimposed reversible insult: ECFV depletion (GI losses, over-diuresis), urinary tract infection, new obstructive uropathy, nephrotoxin/contrast/NSAID exposure, or flare of the primary disease (e.g., lupus, vasculitis) (p.2389, 2395-2396)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Estimated GFR using race-free CKD-EPI or MDRD equations - valid only in steady state (creatinine not rising or falling); cystatin-C as an alternative marker where creatinine production is confounded by muscle mass/limb loss (p.2387, 2362)","Urinary albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) on spot first-morning samples (replaces 24-h collection) - persistent elevation flags glomerular and systemic microvascular injury even with a negative dipstick (p.2387)","Kidney Failure Risk equation (age, sex, region, GFR, UACR) to predict progression to stage 5 (p.2387)","Serum/urine protein electrophoresis for myeloma in unexplained CKD >35y (p.2394)","Serologic workup if hematuria/proteinuria: ANA/lupus serology, hepatitis B and C, HIV (p.2394-2395)","Calcium, phosphate, PTH (and FGF-23 conceptually) for CKD-mineral bone disorder; hemoglobin, iron studies/transferrin saturation, ferritin, B12, folate for anemia (p.2394)","Renal ultrasound to assess kidney number, symmetry, size, masses and obstruction; bilaterally small kidneys indicate long-standing CKD; >1 cm length discrepancy suggests unilateral/renovascular disease (p.2394-2395)","Kidney biopsy reserved for early CKD with diagnostic uncertainty or suspected superimposed active process - NOT advised once kidneys are small/scarred (technically hazardous, scarring obscures diagnosis, no therapeutic window) (p.2395)","Genetic testing (large CKD gene panels, microarray, exome/genome sequencing) for familial disease, consanguinity, extrarenal/syndromic features, steroid-resistant nephrotic syndrome, cystic disease, or young-onset unexplained CKD (p.2395)"],"redFlags":["Uremic pericarditis (pleuritic chest pain with friction rub; ECG with PR depression and diffuse ST elevation) - absolute indication for urgent dialysis; risk of hemorrhagic effusion and tamponade, so dialyze without heparin (p.2392)","Asterixis, myoclonus, seizures, or coma in advanced untreated kidney failure - uremic encephalopathy (p.2393)","Peripheral neuropathy without another cause is an indication to start renal replacement therapy (p.2393)","Calciphylaxis - painful livedo reticularis and subcutaneous nodules progressing to ischemic skin necrosis/ulceration (legs, thighs, abdomen, breasts); warfarin is a risk factor and should be stopped (p.2390-2391)","Diuretic-resistant intractable edema and hypertension, refractory hyperkalemia, or refractory pulmonary edema in advanced CKD - indications to initiate dialysis (p.2389, 2392)","Accelerating rate of GFR decline - prompts search for a reversible superimposed acute/subacute process (p.2396)","Acute systemic features (fever, polyarthritis, rash) with renal impairment suggest an acute systemic illness rather than chronic CKD (p.2395)"],"prognosis":"Cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of morbidity and mortality at every stage, and most patients die of it before reaching stage 5; only 30-45% of those who reach stage 5 have advanced cardiovascular complications at that point. Left ventricular hypertrophy and dilated cardiomyopathy are among the strongest predictors of cardiovascular death. Hyperphosphatemia, high FGF-23, and persistently elevated troponin independently predict worse cardiovascular outcomes. A superimposed malnutrition-inflammation-calcification state in advanced disease carries a poor prognosis. Nephrotic-range proteinuria and heavy persistent proteinuria predict faster progression and worse outcomes. Survivors of dialysis-requiring AKI are at high risk of progressive CKD, with up to ~10% developing ESKD (p.2384, 2391-2392, 2362).","pitfalls":["Treating an elevated creatinine as chronic without checking prior values - missing a reversible acute or acute-on-chronic process (p.2395).","Assuming normal kidney size excludes CKD (diabetic nephropathy early, amyloid, HIV nephropathy retain normal/large kidneys) (p.2394-2395).","Stopping ACEi/ARB for a modest (<30%) non-progressive GFR fall - discontinuation in CKD is associated with increased death/cardiovascular events; the small drop usually reflects relieved hyperfiltration (p.2392).","Combining an ACE inhibitor with an ARB - greater incidence of AKI and adverse cardiac events; avoid dual blockade (p.2396).","Overlooking occult potassium sources (dietary salt substitutes) and potassium-retaining drugs (RAS inhibitors, MRAs, potassium-sparing diuretics) as causes of hyperkalemia (p.2389).","Gadolinium MRI contrast in advanced CKD - risk of nephrogenic systemic/fibrosing dermopathy; minimize in stage 3 and avoid in stages 4-5 unless essential (older agents) (p.2394).","Iodinated radiographic contrast - contrast-induced nephropathy, especially in diabetic nephropathy; avoid where possible (p.2395).","Pruritus and skin pigmentation often persist despite dialysis; first rule out unrelated causes (e.g., scabies) and treat hyperphosphatemia (p.2394).","Metformin and sulfonylureas are contraindicated below ~half-normal GFR; insulin requirements fall as renal degradation of insulin declines (p.2394)."],"severityClassification":"KDIGO classification stratifies CKD by GFR category and albuminuria, both predicting all-cause mortality, cardiovascular mortality, and progression. GFR categories: G1 >=90, G2 60-89, G3a 45-59, G3b 30-44, G4 15-29, G5 <15 mL/min/1.73 m2. Albuminuria (UACR) categories: A1 <30, A2 30-299, A3 >=300 mg/g. Harrison Table 319-3 stages (abnormality >3 months): Stage 1 (>60 with abnormal urinalysis/imaging), Stage 2 (>60, mild progression risk), Stage 3a (45-60), Stage 3b (30-45), Stage 4 (15-30, prepare for RRT), Stage 5 (<15, highest RRT need). Stages 1-2 are usually asymptomatic; complications emerge in stages 3-4; the full uremic syndrome appears at stage 5 (GFR <15) (p.2385-2388, 2362, Table 319-3).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2385","Harrison 22e p.2386","Harrison 22e p.2387","Harrison 22e p.2388","Harrison 22e p.2389","Harrison 22e p.2390","Harrison 22e p.2391","Harrison 22e p.2392","Harrison 22e p.2393","Harrison 22e p.2394","Harrison 22e p.2395","Harrison 22e p.2396","Harrison 22e p.2362"],"crossLinks":["aki","anemia_sympt","hyperkalemia","nephrotic"]},"copd_exac_ni":{"name":"COPD exacerbation (non-infective)","system":"Pulmonary","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"2249-2259 (Chapter 303, Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease); 1756 (Aspergillus/COPD exacerbation note)","clinicalPearls":["An exacerbation is an episodic acute worsening of respiratory symptoms (increased dyspnea, cough, and/or change in sputum amount or character), typically evolving over less than 14 days; it may or may not be accompanied by fever, myalgias, or sore throat (p.2258).","COPD exacerbation is fundamentally a clinical diagnosis; spirometry/pulmonary function testing has not been shown to help diagnose or manage an exacerbation, in contrast to its role in asthma (p.2259).","The single strongest predictor of future exacerbations is a prior exacerbation, and the greatest single risk factor for hospitalization is a previous hospitalization for exacerbation (p.2258).","Exacerbation frequency rises as airflow obstruction worsens; patients with severe obstruction (FEV1 <50% predicted) average about 1-3 episodes per year, though some severely obstructed patients exacerbate infrequently (p.2258).","Assess both the severity of the current exacerbation and the severity of the underlying COPD, because the more severe either component, the greater the likelihood of needing hospital admission (p.2258).","Examination should focus on degree of distress, tachycardia, tachypnea, accessory muscle use, cyanosis, ability to speak in full sentences, mental status, and paradoxical abdominal wall motion (p.2258).","Blood eosinophil count (from CBC differential) is used to stratify exacerbation risk and to guide whether inhaled corticosteroids are added; counts below 100 cells/microliter derive no benefit while benefit rises above 100 and is favored above 300 (p.2255, p.2256).","Non-infective precipitants are well recognized: air pollution (including extreme-heat days), allergens, pulmonary embolism, and medication nonadherence; in a notable minority no precipitant is identified (p.2253, p.2258).","Environmental exposure and Aspergillus sensitization can drive some COPD exacerbations, relevant to the eosinophilic/allergic exacerbation phenotype (p.1756).","Cor pulmonale (overt right heart failure) is now relatively uncommon since supplemental oxygen became standard, but pulmonary hypertension should be considered in patients with persistent activity limitation or lower-extremity edema; it typically arises with FEV1 <25% predicted and chronic hypoxemia (Pao2 <55 mmHg), though some develop it from vascular destruction independent of obstruction severity (p.2252, p.2254)."],"pathophysiology":"A range of stimuli converge on a final common pathway of airway inflammation and worsening respiratory symptoms. The underlying COPD substrate is fixed airflow obstruction (chronically reduced, non-normalizing FEV1/FVC) compounded by hyperinflation and air trapping; a flattened diaphragm becomes mechanically disadvantaged, raising the work of breathing during any acute decompensation. Gas exchange is impaired chiefly by ventilation-perfusion mismatching (shunt is minimal), which explains why modest supplemental oxygen usually corrects COPD hypoxemia. Hypoxemia tends not to appear until FEV1 falls below ~50% predicted, and CO2 retention is not expected until FEV1 is below ~25% predicted. Acutely, worsening obstruction and dynamic hyperinflation can precipitate alveolar hypoventilation with rising arterial CO2 and respiratory acidosis (p.2251-2252, p.2255, p.2258).","additionalDifferentials":["Pneumonia (a common abnormal finding on chest imaging during a presumed exacerbation) (p.2258)","Congestive heart failure / decompensated heart failure (a frequent imaging finding and recognized comorbidity that can mimic or coexist with exacerbation) (p.2258)","Pneumothorax (asymmetric chest findings or large-airway obstruction can mimic an exacerbation) (p.2258)","Pulmonary embolism, whose incidence is increased in the setting of COPD exacerbation and should be actively considered (p.2258, p.2259)"],"infectionMimics":["Pneumonia identified on chest x-ray or CT in a patient presenting as an exacerbation (~25% of x-rays in moderate/severe distress are abnormal, most often pneumonia) (p.2258)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Congestive heart failure (frequent abnormal imaging finding mimicking exacerbation) (p.2258)","Pneumothorax (may produce chest asymmetry mimicking an exacerbation) (p.2258)","Pulmonary embolism (increased incidence during COPD exacerbations) (p.2259)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Arterial blood gas in patients with advanced COPD, a history of hypercarbia, mental status change (confusion, sleepiness), or significant distress; hypercarbia is defined as Pco2 >45 mmHg and carries treatment implications (p.2258)","Use of arterial pH alongside Pco2 to classify ventilatory failure (Pco2 >45 mmHg) as acute (acidemic) versus chronic, since pH changes ~0.08/10 mmHg acutely versus ~0.03/10 mmHg chronically (p.2255)","Chest x-ray or chest CT in patients with severe underlying COPD, moderate-to-severe distress, or focal findings, to detect pneumonia, heart failure, or pneumothorax (p.2258)","Consideration of investigation for pulmonary embolism given its increased incidence in exacerbations (p.2259)","Complete blood count with differential for blood eosinophil count to guide exacerbation-risk management (p.2255)","An expert-consensus objective scheme using dyspnea, vital signs, C-reactive protein, and arterial blood gases to diagnose and stage exacerbation severity (more applicable in the emergency department than outpatient setting) (p.2259)"],"redFlags":["Respiratory acidosis with hypercarbia (Paco2 >45 mmHg and pH <=7.35) indicating acute respiratory failure and a need for ventilatory support (p.2255, p.2259)","New or worsening hypoxemia (p.2259)","Mental status change (confusion, sleepiness), inability to speak in complete sentences, or impaired cooperation (p.2258, p.2259)","Paradoxical abdominal wall motion, marked accessory muscle use, and central cyanosis signaling impending respiratory failure (p.2258)","Significant comorbidity such as heart failure, or an unsafe living situation, favoring inpatient management (p.2259)","New digital clubbing is NOT a feature of COPD itself and should prompt a search for another cause such as lung cancer (p.2254)"],"prognosis":"Mechanical ventilatory support for a COPD exacerbation carries a hospital mortality of roughly 17-49%. After a hospitalization for COPD, about 20% of patients are rehospitalized within 30 days and 45% within a year, with mortality around 20% in the year following discharge. Exacerbations are also associated with greater longitudinal loss of lung function (notably in those with better baseline function) and account for the majority of COPD-related health-care expenditure (p.2253, p.2259).","pitfalls":["Withholding needed supplemental oxygen for fear of CO2 retention: oxygen targeted to SpO2 88-92% is appropriate, and although it may modestly raise arterial Pco2 in some hypercarbic patients by altering ventilation-perfusion relationships, this should not deter correction of hypoxemia (p.2259).","Relying on spirometry to diagnose or manage an acute exacerbation, which is not supported by evidence (unlike in asthma) (p.2259).","Overlooking pulmonary embolism, pneumothorax, pneumonia, or heart failure, since each can mimic or accompany an exacerbation (p.2258, p.2259).","Attributing all exacerbations to bacterial infection: PCR-based studies show viral respiratory infections may cause more than 50% of exacerbations, and in a notable minority no specific precipitant is identifiable (p.2258)."],"severityClassification":"Underlying COPD severity is graded by post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC <0.7 plus FEV1 % predicted: GOLD 1 (mild) >=80%, GOLD 2 (moderate) 50-79%, GOLD 3 (severe) 30-49%, GOLD 4 (very severe) <30%. The GOLD ABE assessment further classifies patients using symptom burden (mMRC dyspnea scale or CAT score) and annual exacerbation history: groups A and B for those with 0-1 moderate exacerbations not leading to hospitalization (A = mMRC 0-1/CAT <10, B = mMRC >=2/CAT >=10), and group E for those with >=2 moderate exacerbations or >=1 leading to hospitalization. The ATS/ERS alternative grades obstruction by FEV1 z-scores rather than percent predicted. For an acute exacerbation, severity itself is a clinical determination; an expert-consensus scheme stages it using dyspnea, vital signs, CRP, and arterial blood gases (p.2255, p.2256, p.2259).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2253","Harrison 22e p.2255","Harrison 22e p.2256","Harrison 22e p.2258","Harrison 22e p.2259","Harrison 22e p.1756"],"crossLinks":["COPD_EXACERBATION","asthma_exac","pe","heart_failure"]},"cord_compression":{"name":"Spinal cord compression","system":"Neurology / Emergency","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e: Ch. 453 Diseases of the Spinal Cord pp.3602-3607; Ch. 80 Oncologic Emergencies (MSCC) pp.631-633; Ch. 95 Epidural Metastasis pp.767-768; Ch. 19 Neck Pain pp.173-175; Ch. 26 Acute Paraparesis p.213; Ch. 136 Vertebral Osteomyelitis / Spinal Epidural Abscess p.1105","clinicalPearls":["A horizontally demarcated sensory level (identified by moving a pinprick or cold stimulus up the trunk) below which sensory, motor and autonomic function is impaired is the hallmark of a cord lesion; for the spinothalamic tract the true level may sit one to two segments above the clinically detected level (p.3603).","The upper border of sensory loss often lies one or two vertebrae BELOW the actual site of compression, so the radiographic target may be higher than the clinical level suggests (p.631).","Pain is usually the first symptom of metastatic compression and can precede neurological signs by days to months; it characteristically worsens when supine/recumbent and at night, helping distinguish it from degenerative disk pain which is often relieved by lying down (pp.631, 768, 3605).","Lhermitte's sign (an electric tingling shooting down the back and limbs on neck flexion/extension) can be an EARLY clue to cervical cord compression (p.631).","Loss of bowel/bladder control is usually a LATE feature; motor and sensory loss typically precede sphincter disturbance (p.631).","Useful trunk sensory landmarks for localising a thoracic level are the nipples (T4) and umbilicus (T10) (p.3604).","Spinal column infection (osteomyelitis/abscess) characteristically crosses the disk space to involve the adjacent vertebral body, whereas tumour tends to respect the disk space - a distinguishing imaging feature (p.3605).","Vertebral collapse is not a reliable marker of malignancy - about 20% of collapses, especially in older and postmenopausal patients, are due to osteoporosis rather than tumour (p.632).","Pedicle erosion (the 'winking owl' sign) is the earliest plain-film sign of vertebral tumour, but plain films are insensitive and a normal film does not exclude malignancy (p.632)."],"pathophysiology":"Because the cord packs nearly the entire motor output and sensory input of the trunk and limbs into a small cross-section, even a small compressive mass causes disproportionately severe, often reversible-if-treated-early, deficits (p.3559). In neoplastic compression, metastases lodge in the vertebral body or pedicle (the axial skeleton is rich in marrow); the enlarging deposit compresses the underlying dura, or a paravertebral mass (lymphoma, myeloma, paediatric tumours) extends through the intervertebral foramen into the canal. Expanding extradural tumour injures axons and myelin mechanically and compromises blood flow, producing ischaemia and infarction (pp.589, 3563). In spinal epidural abscess, as the collection expands it causes additional cord damage through venous congestion and thrombosis, so that once weakness appears deterioration can be rapid and irreversible (p.3606). The thoracic spine is the commonest site of metastatic compression (~70%), followed by lumbosacral (~20%) and cervical (~10%) (p.631).","additionalDifferentials":["Intradural extramedullary tumours (meningioma, neurofibroma/schwannoma) - usually slow-growing and benign; long symptom duration favours an intradural origin (p.3606)","Intramedullary lesions (ependymoma, haemangioblastoma, low-grade astrocytoma, rarely metastasis) - present with poorly localised burning pain and SACRAL SPARING rather than radicular pain (p.3604, 3606)","Cervical spondylotic / degenerative cervical myelopathy - osteophytes, ossified posterior longitudinal ligament/ligamentum flavum, disk degeneration; insidious, stepwise; early signs are subtle (paraesthesia, loss of hand dexterity, 'clumsiness') (p.173)","Acute spondylytic myelopathy from hyperextension injury on a pre-existing stenotic canal, sometimes producing a central cord syndrome (p.3606)","Conus medullaris syndrome - saddle anaesthesia (S3-S5), early prominent bladder/bowel dysfunction with lax anal tone, preserved leg strength (p.3604)"],"infectionMimics":["Spinal epidural abscess - midline spinal/neck pain, fever and progressive limb weakness; mostly Staphylococcus aureus (consider MRSA), with gram-negative bacilli, streptococci, anaerobes and fungi less commonly; risk factors include diabetes, IV drug use, alcoholism, renal failure, malignancy, HIV and recent spinal surgery/anaesthesia/trauma (pp.3606-3607)","Vertebral osteomyelitis / diskitis - neurological deficit in about a third of cases, mostly mediated by epidural abscess; fever may be absent, especially in cervical disease (pp.131, 1105)","Tuberculous spondylitis (Pott's disease) with epidural extension - an important cause in the developing world (p.3607)","Infectious noncompressive myelitis to consider once compression excluded (VZV, HSV, CMV, HIV, HTLV-1, Borrelia, Listeria, syphilis, Mycoplasma) (p.3603)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Osteoporotic vertebral collapse (p.631)","Spinal epidural haematoma - acute focal/radicular pain then cord or conus signs; predisposed by anticoagulation, trauma, tumour or blood dyscrasias, occasionally post-LP or epidural anaesthesia (p.3607)","Hematomyelia - acute painful transverse myelopathy from intramedullary haemorrhage (trauma, vascular malformation, vasculitis, coagulopathy) (p.3607)","Spinal cord infarction / anterior spinal artery syndrome - sudden myelopathy without antecedent pain, with preserved vibration/position sense (pp.3604-3605)","Radiation myelopathy, neoplastic leptomeningitis and spinal lipomatosis (p.631)","Subacute combined degeneration (vitamin B12 deficiency), copper deficiency (p.3602)","Inflammatory/demyelinating myelopathy - multiple sclerosis, neuromyelitis optica, sarcoidosis (p.3602)"],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI with gadolinium centred on the suspected level is the initial procedure; imaging the entire spine is important because up to ~40% with compression at one level have asymptomatic epidural metastases elsewhere (and multiple epidural deposits are present in ~25%) (pp.632, 3605)","CT myelography reserved for patients who cannot undergo MRI promptly or have non-diagnostic MRI (pp.632, 768)","ESR and CRP are valuable for excluding spinal infection (raised in ~98-100% of vertebral osteomyelitis) (p.1105)","Blood cultures (positive in over half of epidural abscess cases); image-guided or open biopsy/aspiration when cultures are negative (pp.1105, 3607)","If cord compression suspected but only radicular symptoms without myelopathy, imaging may safely be deferred 24-48 h - but suspected myelopathic compression is imaged immediately (p.3605)","In compression from an unknown primary, a simple workup (chest imaging, mammography, PSA, abdominal CT) often reveals the malignancy (p.632)"],"redFlags":["Established motor deficit (paraplegia/quadriplegia) present >12 h rarely improves, and beyond 48 h the prognosis for meaningful motor recovery is poor - urgency is measured in hours (p.3606)","Autonomic dysfunction (decreased anal tone, reduced perineal sensation, distended bladder, absent anal wink or bulbocavernosus reflex) signals cord involvement and is an unfavourable prognostic sign (p.631)","Once weakness appears in spinal epidural abscess, progression can be rapid and irreversible (p.3606)","Recent-onset persistent thoracic back pain in a cancer patient is suspicious for vertebral metastasis (the thoracic spine is rarely involved by spondylosis) (p.3605)","Pain crescendo pattern, pain aggravated by cough/Valsalva/recumbency, and Lhermitte's sign warrant urgent imaging (p.632)"],"prognosis":"Outcome is strongly time- and function-dependent: treatment is most successful in patients who are still ambulatory and have intact sphincter control when therapy begins, and a good response is expected in those ambulatory at presentation - treatment usually prevents new weakness and up to a third regain some motor function (pp.632, 3606). Motor deficits fixed for more than 12 h seldom improve, and beyond 48 h substantial recovery is unlikely (p.3606). For spinal epidural abscess, prompt diagnosis and treatment allow significant recovery in up to two-thirds, but surgery is unlikely to reverse deficits fixed for more than a few days (p.3607). Recovery from paraparesis is better after surgery than radiotherapy alone, though survival may be short with widespread metastatic disease (p.768).","pitfalls":["Mistaking a myelopathy for polyneuropathy or Guillain-Barre syndrome: ascending paraesthesias suggest neuropathy, but a sharply demarcated sensory level plus bladder disturbance points to a cord origin (pp.213, 3605).","Acute severe transverse lesions cause initial FLACCID weakness and areflexia ('spinal shock') that can be misread as anterior horn cell disease or acute polyneuropathy before hyperreflexia supervenes over days to weeks (p.3604).","Cauda equina syndrome (low back/radicular pain, saddle/perineal sensory loss, asymmetric leg weakness, sphincter and sexual dysfunction, lost ankle/bulbocavernosus/anal reflexes) reflects nerve-root rather than cord compression and must be distinguished from conus and cord syndromes - mass lesions low in the canal often produce a mixed picture (pp.132, 632, 3604).","Gadolinium enhancement can deceptively 'normalise' vertebral metastases by raising their signal to that of normal marrow on MRI (p.3605).","Cervical osteomyelitis/epidural abscess may present without fever, delaying recognition - keep it in mind for neck pain with IV drug use, immunosuppression or diabetes (p.131)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames management decisions for malignant compression using the NOMS framework (Memorial Sloan Kettering): Neurologic status (degree of epidural compression, myelopathy, functional radiculopathy), Oncologic status (tumour radiosensitivity), Mechanical stability of the spine, and Systemic factors; high-grade epidural compression from a radioresistant tumour favours separation surgery followed by stereotactic body radiotherapy (pp.589-590). Clinically, prognosis is best stratified by ambulatory and sphincter status at presentation and by duration of any motor deficit (p.3606).","references":["Harrison 22e p.173","Harrison 22e p.174","Harrison 22e p.175","Harrison 22e p.213","Harrison 22e p.589","Harrison 22e p.590","Harrison 22e p.631","Harrison 22e p.632","Harrison 22e p.633","Harrison 22e p.767","Harrison 22e p.768","Harrison 22e p.1105","Harrison 22e p.3559","Harrison 22e p.3560","Harrison 22e p.3561","Harrison 22e p.3562","Harrison 22e p.3563","Harrison 22e p.3564"],"crossLinks":["malignancy_b","gbs","ms"]},"crystal_arthritis":{"name":"Crystal arthritis (gout / pseudogout)","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.384, p.2956-2962","clinicalPearls":["Initial gout flares are usually monoarticular; the first metatarsophalangeal joint (podagra) is involved in 70-90% of cases, followed by tarsal joints, ankles, and knees. Oligo- and polyarticular flares emerge over time (Harrison 22e p.2958).","Gout flares often begin at night to early morning, are among the most painful conditions humans experience, and the affected joint becomes warm, red, tender, and very swollen, frequently mimicking cellulitis (pseudocellulitis) (Harrison 22e p.2958).","Untreated flares usually subside spontaneously within 1-2 weeks, with symptom-free intervals (intercritical gout) until the next episode (Harrison 22e p.2958).","Serum urate can be normal or low during an acute flare because inflammatory cytokines such as IL-6 are uricosuric and can lower urate by about 2 mg/dL; seek historical or post-flare urate values instead (Harrison 22e p.2959).","In CPP crystal arthritis (pseudogout) the knee is most commonly affected, followed by the wrist; the first MTP joint (podagra) is rarely involved, a useful distinguishing clue from gout (Harrison 22e p.2960).","Initial acute CPPD attacks tend to last longer than typical gout flares, sometimes weeks to months (Harrison 22e p.2960).","Acute CPPD can be precipitated by trauma, severe medical illness, or surgery (especially parathyroidectomy, which rapidly lowers serum calcium and magnesium and sheds crystals) (Harrison 22e p.2960).","Women account for only about 5-20% of gout patients; most are postmenopausal, elderly, and often on diuretics with coexisting osteoarthritis, hypertension, or mild renal insufficiency. Premenopausal gout is rare (Harrison 22e p.2958).","Hyperuricemia in a prepubertal boy should prompt consideration of inherited enzyme defects such as HPRT deficiency (Lesch-Nyhan spectrum) or PRPP synthetase superactivity (Harrison 22e p.2958).","Apatite (basic calcium phosphate) arthropathy can cause an extremely destructive arthropathy of the elderly, classically the shoulder (Milwaukee shoulder), with low synovial fluid leukocyte counts despite dramatic symptoms (Harrison 22e p.2961).","Hyperuricemia and gout are part of the insulin-resistance syndrome and associate with cardiometabolic-kidney comorbidities: obesity, hypertension, type 2 diabetes, myocardial infarction, stroke, CKD, and urate nephrolithiasis (Harrison 22e p.2956)."],"pathophysiology":"Gout arises from an increased body urate pool due to chronic hyperuricemia, leading to supersaturation and crystallization of monosodium urate (MSU) within joints and connective tissue. Hyperuricemia is predominantly due to impaired renal urate excretion (~90%) and less often urate overproduction (~10%); a minority (~10%) of cases reflect inherited metabolic disorders (PRPP synthetase superactivity, HPRT deficiency). Crystal shedding from inert synovial deposits, triggered by metabolic factors, diuresis, rapid urate changes (including urate-lowering therapy initiation), local trauma, or medical illness, activates resident synovial phagocytes (monocytes/macrophages, mast cells) and the NLRP3 inflammasome, generating IL-1beta and other proinflammatory cytokines (IL-6, IL-8, TNF-alpha). Phagocytosis of MSU crystals by neutrophils with a respiratory burst amplifies the acute inflammatory response. Persistent MSU deposition produces tophi (granulomatous inflammation around crystals) and chronic erosive gouty arthritis. In CPPD, increased cartilage inorganic pyrophosphate (largely from extracellular ATP breakdown, regulated by the ANKH transporter) combines with calcium to form CPP crystals; their release similarly drives inflammasome-mediated inflammation, analogous to hyperuricemia in gout. Apatite and calcium oxalate crystals can likewise stimulate synovial cell proliferation and release of proteases, collagenases, and prostaglandin E2, contributing to joint destruction (Harrison 22e p.2956-2962).","additionalDifferentials":["Palindromic rheumatism (Harrison 22e p.2958)","Psoriatic arthritis (Harrison 22e p.2958)","Osteoarthritis - CPPD pseudo-osteoarthritis variant mimics progressive OA, often in atypical joints (MCP, wrist, elbow, shoulder, ankle) (Harrison 22e p.2960-2961)","Rheumatoid arthritis - chronic symmetric CPP synovitis can clinically resemble RA (Harrison 22e p.2961)","Neuropathic (Charcot) arthropathy - severe destructive CPPD may radiographically mimic it (Harrison 22e p.2961)"],"infectionMimics":["Cellulitis - acute gout flare with warm, red, swollen periarticular tissue may mimic cellulitis (pseudocellulitis) (Harrison 22e p.2958)","Septic arthritis - crystal arthritis closely mimics it; aspiration with Gram stain and culture is essential, especially as bacterial infection can coexist with crystals in synovial fluid (Harrison 22e p.2958, p.2961)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute CPP crystal arthritis (pseudogout) mimics acute gout flares (Harrison 22e p.2960)","Apatite (basic calcium phosphate) deposition disease (Harrison 22e p.2961)","Calcium oxalate arthropathy - acute presentation may be indistinguishable from MSU, CPP, or apatite crystal disease (Harrison 22e p.2962)","Osteoarthritis (pseudo-osteoarthritis pattern of CPPD) (Harrison 22e p.2961)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Polarized light microscopy of synovial fluid - MSU: needle/rod-shaped, strongly negatively birefringent; CPP: rhomboid/rod-shaped, weakly positively or non-birefringent; apatite: non-birefringent (requires alizarin red S staining or electron microscopy); calcium oxalate: bipyramidal, variable/strong birefringence, alizarin red S positive (Harrison 22e p.2959, p.2961-2962)","Synovial fluid Gram stain and culture to exclude coexisting septic arthritis (Harrison 22e p.2958, p.2961)","Synovial fluid leukocyte count - gout flare typically 5000-75,000/uL; acute CPPD ~24,000/uL (range several thousand to 100,000); apatite and calcium oxalate usually <2000/uL (Harrison 22e p.2959, p.2961-2962)","Arthrocentesis of an unaffected first MTP joint or knee between flares can still demonstrate MSU crystals (Harrison 22e p.2959)","Plain radiographs - advanced gout: well-defined erosions with sclerotic/overhanging margins, soft tissue tophi; CPPD: punctate/linear chondrocalcinosis in fibrocartilage or hyaline cartilage (Harrison 22e p.2959, p.2961)","Musculoskeletal ultrasound - double-contour sign over articular cartilage indicating MSU deposition; can also reveal chondrocalcinosis in CPPD (Harrison 22e p.2959, p.2961)","Dual-energy CT - identifies MSU crystals by chemical composition (Harrison 22e p.2959)","Baseline labs to assess comorbidities and treatment safety: serum creatinine, liver function tests, hemoglobin, WBC count, HbA1c, lipids (Harrison 22e p.2959)","In CPPD patients under 50, evaluate for metabolic/familial causes: serum calcium, phosphorus, alkaline phosphatase, magnesium, iron, transferrin (Harrison 22e p.2960)","HLA-B*5801 screening before allopurinol in high-prevalence populations (Southeast Asian, Pacific Islander/Native Hawaiian, Black patients) to reduce risk of severe cutaneous adverse reactions (knowledge point) (Harrison 22e p.2960)"],"redFlags":["Crystal-proven arthritis does not exclude concurrent septic arthritis - bacterial infection can coexist with MSU or CPP crystals in synovial fluid; always Gram stain and culture (Harrison 22e p.2958, p.2961)","Acute CPPD can produce systemic features (fever, chills, raised acute-phase reactants), with low-grade fever in up to 50% and temperatures occasionally as high as 40C - mandates microbial culture to exclude infection (Harrison 22e p.2960, p.2961)","CPPD onset before age 50 warrants workup for underlying metabolic disease (hyperparathyroidism, hemochromatosis, hypophosphatasia, hypomagnesemia) or hereditary CPPD (Harrison 22e p.2960)"],"prognosis":"Untreated gout flares typically resolve spontaneously within 1-2 weeks, but recurrent flares over years can progress to chronic gouty arthritis with persistent low-grade synovitis, subcutaneous tophi, joint deformity, and erosive bony destruction. Controlling the underlying chronic hyperuricemia (urate-lowering therapy to a subsaturation target) can prevent recurrent flares and dissolve tophi, generally requiring lifelong therapy. For chronic CPPD there is no means to remove established CPP crystal deposits from cartilage and synovium (unlike urate lowering in gout), and progressive destructive large-joint disease may require joint replacement. Apatite-associated destructive arthropathy (e.g., Milwaukee shoulder) progresses indolently and may also lead to joint replacement (Harrison 22e p.2958-2962).","pitfalls":["Relying on a normal serum urate during an acute flare to exclude gout - urate is often normal or low at flare onset (Harrison 22e p.2959).","Attributing a destructive 'osteoarthritis' in atypical joints (MCP, wrist, elbow, shoulder, ankle) to primary OA rather than recognizing pseudo-osteoarthritis from CPPD (Harrison 22e p.2961).","Assuming chondrocalcinosis on imaging is always CPPD - calcium oxalate deposition in chronic renal failure is an exception (Harrison 22e p.2961).","Missing apatite arthropathy because individual apatite crystals are not visible on routine polarized light microscopy and need alizarin red S staining or electron microscopy (Harrison 22e p.2961).","Overlooking inherited enzyme defects when hyperuricemia/gout presents in a young patient, particularly a prepubertal boy (Harrison 22e p.2958)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.2956","Harrison 22e p.2957","Harrison 22e p.2958","Harrison 22e p.2959","Harrison 22e p.2960","Harrison 22e p.2961","Harrison 22e p.2962"],"crossLinks":["CELLULITIS"]},"decomp_cirrhosis":{"name":"Decompensated cirrhosis","system":"Hepatology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.370-373, p.2672-2676, p.2756-2761","clinicalPearls":["Compensated cirrhosis can be asymptomatic for a decade or more; the transition to decompensation is marked by the first major event — ascites/edema, variceal hemorrhage, hepatic encephalopathy, or jaundice. (p.2756, p.2754)","Once clinically significant portal hypertension (HVPG ≥5 mmHg, with substantial decompensation risk at ≥10 mmHg) progresses to overt decompensation, median survival is under 2 years. (p.2756)","Encephalopathy in chronic liver disease is almost always triggered by an identifiable precipitant (GI bleeding, infection, volume depletion/overdiuresis, hyponatremia, constipation, uremia, sedatives/narcotics) rather than spontaneous progression. (p.2673, p.2759)","Ammonia levels correlate poorly with the severity of encephalopathy or liver disease; the diagnosis of hepatic encephalopathy is clinical, and most hepatologists do not rely on ammonia to make the diagnosis or follow progress. (p.2759)","A new or worsening encephalopathy episode mandates a search for occult infection — if ascites is present it should be tapped to exclude SBP. (p.2759)","A hyperdynamic circulation (widened pulse pressure, increased cardiac output, reduced peripheral resistance from splanchnic vasodilation) is a hallmark of advanced cirrhosis. (p.2673)","Acute-on-chronic liver failure (ACLF) is acutely decompensating cirrhosis with one or more extrahepatic organ failures, behaves like a sepsis syndrome, and carries 28-day mortality of 20-70% rising with the number of failing organs; it is most often precipitated by bacterial/fungal infection or alcoholic hepatitis. (p.2760)"],"pathophysiology":"Decompensation is driven by portal hypertension plus renal sodium and water retention. Portal hypertension (elevation of the hepatic venous pressure gradient >5 mmHg) arises from two simultaneous processes: increased intrahepatic resistance from fibrosis disrupting sinusoidal architecture, stellate-cell-mediated smooth-muscle contraction, regenerative nodules, microthrombi, and reduced intrahepatic endothelial nitric oxide synthase (eNOS) causing intrahepatic vasoconstriction; and increased splanchnic blood flow from systemic vasodilation. Systemically, raised nitric oxide, VEGF and TNF cause splanchnic arterial vasodilation, pooling blood and reducing effective circulating volume; the kidneys sense this as hypovolemia and activate ADH, the sympathetic nervous system and the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, producing free-water and sodium retention (ascites/edema). Encephalopathy results from gut-derived neurotoxins bypassing the liver via portosystemic shunting and reduced hepatic mass. (p.370, p.2756, p.2759)","additionalDifferentials":["Cardiac ascites / constrictive pericarditis (high-SAAG ascites; pro-BNP elevated, ascitic protein typically ≥2.5 g/dL) (p.371)","Budd-Chiari syndrome (hepatic vein thrombosis) and sinusoidal obstruction syndrome (high-SAAG ascites) (p.371)","Massive hepatic metastases (high-SAAG ascites) (p.371)","Peritoneal carcinomatosis (low-SAAG, protein-rich ascites) (p.371)","Tuberculous peritonitis (low-SAAG, lymphocyte-rich ascites; raised ascitic adenosine deaminase) (p.371-372)","Pancreatic ascites (low-SAAG, ascitic amylase typically >1000) (p.371-372)","Nephrotic syndrome / protein-losing enteropathy / malnutrition (low-SAAG ascites from hypoalbuminemia) (p.370-371)"],"infectionMimics":["Secondary bacterial peritonitis from a perforated viscus or abscess — distinguished from SBP by ascitic glucose <50 mg/dL, ascitic LDH above serum LDH, and multiple organisms on culture with a raised PMN count (p.372)","Tuberculous peritonitis presenting with new ascites (p.371-372)","Systemic sepsis producing leukocytosis and altered mental status that can mimic or precipitate decompensation (p.370, p.2759)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Other causes of coma/altered mentation to exclude before attributing to encephalopathy: electrolyte imbalance, sedative use, renal or respiratory failure (p.2673)","Hepatopulmonary syndrome causing dyspnea/hypoxemia (platypnea, orthodeoxia) mimicking primary cardiopulmonary disease (p.2673, p.2760)","Portopulmonary hypertension causing exertional dyspnea and fatigue (p.2760)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnostic paracentesis with serum-ascites albumin gradient (SAAG): ≥1.1 g/dL indicates portal-hypertensive ascites, <1.1 g/dL indicates non-portal-hypertensive cause (p.371)","Ascitic fluid cell count/differential, total protein, Gram stain and culture inoculated into blood-culture bottles at the bedside (p.371)","In high-SAAG ascites, ascitic protein <2.5 g/dL suggests cirrhosis vs ≥2.5 g/dL suggesting cardiac/early Budd-Chiari sinusoidal sources (p.371)","Serum pro-BNP to identify heart failure as the cause of high-SAAG ascites (p.371)","Upper endoscopy to screen for and grade esophageal/gastric varices once cirrhosis is established (p.2676, p.2757)","Liver ultrasound (and contrast CT/MRI showing nodular liver and collateral circulation) for portal hypertension and HCC surveillance (p.2676, p.2757)","Transient elastography (liver stiffness) to stage fibrosis and predict variceal hemorrhage, ascites, and liver-related death (p.2676)","Hepatic venous pressure gradient measurement in selected cases to confirm sinusoidal portal hypertension (p.370, p.2757)","Pulse oximetry as a screen for hepatopulmonary syndrome (p.2673)","Serial serum creatinine and exclusion of volume depletion/intrinsic renal disease when HRS is suspected (p.2759)"],"redFlags":["Variceal hemorrhage carries a 20-30% mortality per bleeding episode and is an immediate life-threatening event (p.2756)","A stepwise progressive rise in creatinine with large-volume ascites suggests hepatorenal syndrome (HRS-AKI), a poor-prognosis functional renal failure (p.2759)","ACLF with four or more organ failures persisting after 3-7 days of ICU support, when transplant is not an option, signals a transition toward palliative care (p.2760)","Avoid NSAIDs, aminoglycosides, and use ACE inhibitors/ARBs cautiously — they precipitate renal failure in cirrhotic ascites (p.371, p.2758)","New-onset or worsening encephalopathy, jaundice, or tense ascites in known cirrhosis should prompt an urgent search for a precipitant (p.2759)"],"prognosis":"Decompensation defines a poor-prognosis state: with clinically significant portal hypertension that decompensates, median survival is under 2 years, and fewer than 50% of patients survive 2 years after the onset of ascites. Encephalopathy in chronic liver disease is a poor prognostic sign. HRS (both AKI and CKD types) carries a poor prognosis unless liver transplantation is achieved. ACLF 28-day mortality ranges from 20-70%, rising with the number of organ failures. Severity and transplant priority are graded by Child-Pugh class and the MELD/MELD-Na (MELD 3.0) systems. (p.371, p.2675-2676, p.2756, p.2759-2760)","pitfalls":["Relying on ammonia levels to diagnose or track hepatic encephalopathy — they correlate poorly with disease severity (p.2759)","Attributing altered mental status to encephalopathy without excluding electrolyte disturbance, sedatives, and renal/respiratory failure (p.2673)","Failing to tap the ascites in a decompensated cirrhotic with new symptoms — abdominal tenderness is present in only ~40% of SBP and rebound is uncommon, so SBP can be clinically silent (p.372)","Multiple organisms on ascitic culture with a raised PMN count indicate secondary (not spontaneous) peritonitis and a surgical source; multiple organisms without raised PMN suggest bowel perforation by the paracentesis needle (p.372)","Withholding paracentesis for fear of coagulopathy — paracentesis is safe even in coagulopathic patients (p.371)","Restricting dietary protein for encephalopathy — its harm to nutrition outweighs benefit and it is strongly discouraged (p.2760)"],"severityClassification":"Child-Pugh score (5 factors: bilirubin, albumin, prothrombin time/INR, ascites, encephalopathy; range 5-15) classifies cirrhosis as class A (5-6, compensated), B (7-9), or C (10-15); a score ≥7 (class B) defines decompensation. MELD score uses INR, bilirubin, and creatinine to predict short-term mortality and allocate organs; it was extended with serum sodium and reweighted as MELD-Na / MELD 3.0 for transplant listing. PELD is the pediatric equivalent. Clinically significant portal hypertension is defined by HVPG ≥10 mmHg. (p.2675-2676, p.2756, p.2754)","references":["Harrison 22e p.370","Harrison 22e p.371","Harrison 22e p.372","Harrison 22e p.373","Harrison 22e p.2673","Harrison 22e p.2675","Harrison 22e p.2676","Harrison 22e p.2756","Harrison 22e p.2757","Harrison 22e p.2759","Harrison 22e p.2760"],"crossLinks":["variceal_bleed","hepatic_enceph","SBP","aki","VIRAL_HEPATITIS"]},"dic":{"name":"Disseminated intravascular coagulation","system":"Hematology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.976-978 (Ch. 121, Coagulation Disorders); p.974 (Ch. 120, thrombocytopenia algorithm)","clinicalPearls":["DIC is an acquired syndrome of intravascular activation of coagulation with loss of localization, which can damage the microvasculature and, if severe enough, cause organ dysfunction (ISTH 2001 definition) (p.976).","The central event is uncontrolled thrombin generation, compounded by disruption of physiologic anticoagulant mechanisms and abnormal fibrinolysis, producing systemic fibrin deposition in small and mid-size vessels (p.976).","Fibrin deposition can compromise blood supply to lung, kidney, liver and brain, causing organ failure; pulmonary microvascular thrombosis is a component of ARDS (p.976).","Most common causes: bacterial sepsis (also viral and fungal), trauma, obstetric events (abruptio placentae, amniotic fluid embolism), and malignancy (especially mucin-producing adenocarcinomas and acute promyelocytic leukemia) (p.976).","Sepsis-driven DIC reflects thrombo-inflammation: increased tissue factor expression, neutrophil/monocyte activation with cytokine release, neutrophil extracellular traps, and polyphosphate release driving thrombin generation (p.976).","Bleeding manifestations dominate clinically: petechiae, ecchymoses, oozing from venipuncture sites, and severe hemorrhage from GI tract, lung, or CNS; thrombosis of large vessels and cerebral embolism can also occur (p.977).","Chronic (low-grade, compensated) DIC is seen with giant hemangioma, metastatic carcinoma, and late-gestation fetal demise; bleeding is discrete and limited to skin/mucosa, with elevated FDP/d-dimer but PT/aPTT/fibrinogen often normal or high and only mild thrombocytopenia (p.977).","In critically ill patients, coagulation parameters should be repeated over 6-8 h because patients can deteriorate rapidly; an overt-DIC score should be rechecked daily (p.977)."],"pathophysiology":"DIC arises from uncontrolled thrombin generation triggered by multiple mechanisms, with simultaneous disruption of physiologic anticoagulant pathways and abnormal fibrinolysis. In sepsis and trauma, activation of inflammatory pathways increases tissue factor expression, activates neutrophils and monocytes with cytokine release and neutrophil extracellular trap formation, and releases polyphosphates that cross-talk with the coagulation system (thrombo-inflammation); endothelial damage causes loss of native antithrombotic properties. The result is systemic fibrin deposition in small and mid-size vessels causing microvascular occlusion, ischemic organ damage, and red cell fragmentation/hemolysis. Sustained activation consumes platelets and clotting factors, producing systemic bleeding that is aggravated by secondary hyperfibrinolysis in late stages. Purpura fulminans is a severe form of DIC with extensive cutaneous thrombosis, predominantly in young children after viral or bacterial infection, especially with inherited or acquired protein C pathway deficiency (p.976).","additionalDifferentials":["Severe liver disease (thrombocytopenia from sequestration/portal hypertension/hypersplenism, decreased factor and natural-anticoagulant synthesis, elevated d-dimer) — distinguished because liver-disease parameters do not change rapidly, unlike DIC (p.977).","Immune thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP) — acute thrombocytopenia, red cell fragmentation, multiorgan failure, but identified by ADAMTS13 inhibitor (p.977).","Vitamin K deficiency (isolated PT prolongation as earliest finding) (p.977-978)."],"infectionMimics":["Gram-negative bacterial systemic infection (DIC is most commonly seen here) (p.974).","COVID-19-associated coagulopathy/thrombocytopenia (p.974)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Severe/fulminant liver disease and cirrhosis (coexisting chronic or acute DIC can also occur) (p.977-978).","Thrombotic microangiopathies (TTP, HUS) (p.977).","Dysfibrinogenemia of liver disease (p.978)."],"additionalInvestigations":["ISTH overt-DIC score using platelet count, d-dimer, PT prolongation, and fibrinogen (p.977).","Peripheral blood smear for schistocytes (red cell fragmentation) (p.977).","Serial/repeated PT, aPTT, fibrinogen, d-dimer measurements (every 6-8 h in critically ill; daily if overt DIC) to assess trajectory and distinguish from liver disease (p.977).","Fibrin degradation products (FDP) (p.976-977).","ADAMTS13 activity/inhibitor when TTP is in the differential (p.977)."],"redFlags":["Hemodynamic instability and shock are common in acute DIC; mortality ranges from 30% to over 80% (p.977).","Purpura fulminans — extensive skin thrombosis, especially in children post-infection or with protein C pathway deficiency (p.976).","Bleeding into the CNS, lung, or GI tract (p.977).","Rapidly deteriorating coagulation parameters in a critically ill patient (p.977)."],"prognosis":"Morbidity and mortality in DIC are primarily driven by the underlying disease; acute DIC carries mortality ranging from 30% to greater than 80%. Many patients with overt DIC are critically ill and require intensive care (p.977).","pitfalls":["Distinguishing DIC from severe liver disease is challenging and requires serial laboratory measurements — both share thrombocytopenia, reduced factor synthesis, and elevated d-dimer, but liver-disease values do not change rapidly (p.977).","The ISTH overt-DIC scoring tool is not validated for pregnant patients; a separate tool exists for pregnancy (p.977).","d-Dimer assays are not standardized and have differing normal ranges; degree of increase must be interpreted against the local laboratory range (p.977).","A single low score does not exclude evolving DIC — a non-overt score (<5) should be repeated every 1-2 days (p.977)."],"severityClassification":"ISTH overt-DIC score (Table 121-3): Platelets >100x10^9/L = 0, 50-100 = 1, <50 = 2; d-dimer normal = 0, moderate increase = 2, severe increase = 3; PT prolongation <3 s = 0, 3-6 s = 1, >6 s = 2; fibrinogen >1 g/L = 0, <1 g/L = 1. Total <5 = non-overt/low-grade DIC (repeat in 1-2 days); >5 = overt DIC (repeat labs daily). Not for use in pregnant patients (p.977).","references":["Harrison 22e p.976","Harrison 22e p.977","Harrison 22e p.978","Harrison 22e p.974"],"crossLinks":["ttp_hus","acute_leukemia","SEPTIC_SHOCK"]},"dka":{"name":"Diabetic ketoacidosis","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.93, p.413, p.1761, p.2788","clinicalPearls":["An acetone-like fruity odour on the breath is a classic bedside clue to ketoacidosis (p.93).","Urine dipstick (nitroprusside reaction) detects acetoacetate but not beta-hydroxybutyrate, so it can underestimate the true degree of ketosis; measure plasma beta-hydroxybutyrate directly (p.413).","As the circulation is restored with fluids, beta-hydroxybutyrate is converted to acetoacetate, so the nitroprusside ketone reading may paradoxically become more positive during treatment rather than reflecting worsening disease (p.413).","Glucose may be normal or only mildly raised in euglycaemic DKA (SGLT2-inhibitor associated) and in starvation ketoacidosis, so a normal glucose does not exclude ketoacidosis (p.413).","Excretion of ketoacids obligates loss of cations (sodium, potassium), worsening volume depletion and promoting chloride retention (p.413)."],"pathophysiology":"DKA results from increased fatty acid metabolism with accumulation of the ketoacids acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate, producing hyperglycaemia, ketonaemia and a high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis. It typically occurs in insulin-dependent diabetes when insulin is stopped or when an intercurrent illness (e.g. infection, gastroenteritis, pancreatitis, myocardial infarction) acutely raises insulin requirements. In euglycaemic DKA caused by SGLT2 inhibitors, glycosuria drives an osmotic diuresis and volume depletion while plasma glucose stays in the normal range despite the ketoacidosis (p.413).","additionalDifferentials":["Alcoholic ketoacidosis — high-anion-gap acidosis (predominantly beta-hydroxybutyrate) without hyperglycaemia, usually with preserved renal function, in chronic alcohol use with vomiting and poor intake (p.413).","Starvation ketoacidosis — ketoacidosis with normal or low glucose (p.413).","Other high-anion-gap acidoses: lactic acidosis, toxic ingestions (e.g. salicylates), and renal failure/uraemia (p.413)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute pancreatitis can mimic DKA (and vice versa), with overlapping abdominal pain; DKA may itself be accompanied by amylase elevation, complicating the distinction (p.2788)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma beta-hydroxybutyrate measured directly (more reliable than urine nitroprusside ketones) (p.413).","Anion gap calculation, including assessment for a mixed non-anion-gap / high-anion-gap acidosis when the change in bicarbonate exceeds the change in anion gap (p.413)."],"redFlags":["Mucormycosis risk: DKA patients are at high risk of rhino-orbital-cerebral mucormycosis; acidosis frees iron from carrier proteins and beta-hydroxybutyrate upregulates fungal adherence/penetration receptors — consider in a DKA patient with sinus/orbital/facial signs (p.1761).","Severe acidaemia (pH <7.00) or shock identifies the small subset where bicarbonate may be considered; cerebral oedema risk limits its use (p.413).","Hyperchloraemic (non-anion-gap) acidosis and volume overload can be precipitated by aggressive isotonic saline during treatment (p.413)."],"prognosis":null,"pitfalls":["Relying on urine ketone dipstick, which misses beta-hydroxybutyrate and underestimates ketosis (p.413).","Interpreting a rising nitroprusside ketone result during fluid resuscitation as deterioration, when it reflects the beta-hydroxybutyrate-to-acetoacetate shift (p.413).","Assuming a normal/near-normal glucose excludes DKA, missing euglycaemic DKA on SGLT2 inhibitors (p.413).","Over-resuscitation with isotonic saline causing volume overload and/or hyperchloraemic acidosis (p.413)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.93","Harrison 22e p.413","Harrison 22e p.1761","Harrison 22e p.2788"],"crossLinks":["hhs"]},"drug_intox":{"name":"Drug intoxication / poisoning","system":"Toxicology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"p.3703-3707","clinicalPearls":["Diagnosis of an unknown poisoning rests on pattern recognition: first classify the overall physiologic state as stimulated, depressed, discordant, or normal, then look for a recognizable toxidrome (p.3704).","Core temperature is the single most reliable predictor of poor outcome in stimulant (e.g. cocaine) poisoning or in drug/alcohol/GHB withdrawal, so it must be measured even in difficult or combative patients (p.3704).","Mydriasis is common to all stimulant states but is most marked with anticholinergics (muscarinic loss of pupil reactivity); cocaine and other sympathomimetics enlarge pupils yet retain some light reactivity (p.3704).","The anticholinergic toxidrome is distinguished from other stimulant states by hot, dry, flushed skin, decreased bowel sounds and urinary retention, whereas sympathomimetic states cause diaphoresis and increased bowel activity (p.3704).","Miosis points to a depressed state from opioids or cholinergic agents; muscarinic/nicotinic features separate cholinergic poisoning from opioid poisoning (p.3704-3705).","In opioid and sedative-hypnotic poisoning, vital-sign derangements are secondary to CNS depression and do not appear until consciousness is markedly reduced (grade 3-4 depression); marked cardiovascular depression with preserved consciousness instead suggests a peripherally acting sympatholytic (p.3705).","Rotatory nystagmus among stimulated patients is essentially specific to phencyclidine and ketamine (p.3704).","A near-normal exam may reflect a nontoxic exposure, psychogenic illness, or a 'toxic time-bomb' (slowly absorbed, slowly distributed, requiring metabolic activation, or disrupting metabolism); once-daily reformulations have made these increasingly common (p.3705).","Rapid response of altered mental status to IV dextrose, naloxone or flumazenil is virtually diagnostic of hypoglycemia, opioid poisoning and benzodiazepine intoxication respectively (p.3706).","Most patients who are or become asymptomatic by 6 h after ingestion are unlikely to develop later toxicity and may be discharged, but longer observation is required after 'toxic time-bomb' ingestions (p.3707)."],"pathophysiology":"Poisoning is a dose-related adverse effect from exposure to a chemical, drug or other xenobiotic. Responses to a given dose vary with genetic polymorphism, enzyme induction or inhibition by co-ingestants, and acquired tolerance. Toxicity may be local or systemic depending on route, the agent's physicochemical properties and mechanism, and its severity/reversibility depends on the functional reserve of the patient or target organ (influenced by age and pre-existing disease) (p.3703).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Psychogenic illness / anxiety reaction (mass hysteria, fear of poisoning) producing symptoms inconsistent with the exposure history (p.3705)","Structural CNS lesion - focal neurologic findings are uncommon in poisoning and should prompt evaluation for a structural cause (p.3703)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Characterise overall physiologic state and toxidrome from vital signs and neuromuscular exam (p.3704)","Anion gap (raised AGMA in methanol, ethylene glycol, salicylate, or any poisoning causing organ failure, seizures or shock; low anion gap with bromide, calcium, iodine, lithium, magnesium) (p.3705)","Osmolal gap (>10 mmol/L suggests a low-molecular-weight solute such as an alcohol or glycol) (p.3705)","Serum lactate (low relative to anion gap in alcohol/glycol intoxication, high and near the anion gap in hypoperfusion) (p.3705)","Glucose, electrolytes, ketones (hypo-/hyperglycaemia and hypo-/hyperkalaemia patterns can implicate specific agents) (p.3705)","ECG (bradycardia/AV block, QRS and QT prolongation, ventricular tachyarrhythmias suggest specific agent classes) (p.3706)","Abdominal/chest radiograph (radiopaque densities suggest heavy metals, drug packets, iron, enteric-coated or salicylate tablets; CXR for pulmonary edema/aspiration) (p.3706)","Quantitative serum levels (acetaminophen, alcohols incl. ethylene glycol/methanol, anticonvulsants, barbiturates, digoxin, iron, lithium, salicylate, theophylline, carboxyhemoglobin, methemoglobin) guide management (p.3706)","Coagulation (PT/INR) for warfarin/rodenticide risk stratification, though unreliable for direct oral anticoagulant overdose (p.3705)"],"redFlags":["Temperature elevation (grade 4 stimulation) - most reliable predictor of poor outcome in stimulant poisoning and withdrawal (p.3704)","Coma, seizures or cardiovascular collapse (grade 4 physiologic stimulation or depression) (p.3705)","Focal neurologic findings - uncommon in poisoning and suggest a structural CNS lesion (p.3703)","Suspicion of 'body packing'/'body stuffing' in patients ill after foreign travel or arrest (p.3703)","High blood levels of agents with more toxic metabolites (acetaminophen, ethylene glycol, methanol) signalling need for antidote or dialysis (p.3707)"],"prognosis":"Overall mortality is low (<1% of all poison exposures) but rises to 1-2% among hospitalised patients with intentional (suicidal) overdose or complications of drugs of abuse, who account for most serious poisonings. About 20-25% of exposures need bedside professional evaluation and ~5% need hospitalisation. Acetaminophen is the pharmaceutical most often implicated in fatal poisoning, while carbon monoxide is the leading overall cause of poisoning death (often fatal before discovery). Most patients asymptomatic at 6 h are unlikely to deteriorate (p.3703, p.3707).","pitfalls":["Rapid hospital urine drug-of-abuse screens are screening tests only, cannot confirm the exact substance, and are not diagnostic or forensic; false positives (e.g. fluoroquinolones triggering false-positive opiate screens) and false negatives are common (p.3706).","A negative drug screen does not exclude poisoning - the agent may be undetectable by the assay (e.g. synthetic cannabinoids/spice, cathinones/bath salts, kratom) or present below the limit of detection at the time of sampling; repeat testing later may turn positive (p.3706).","Qualitative drug screens are not clinically useful or cost-effective when the patient is asymptomatic or the picture fits the reported history; reserve them for severe or unexplained toxicity (coma, seizures, cardiovascular instability, acidosis, nonsinus rhythms) (p.3706).","Blood levels obtained during absorption/distribution may exceed tissue levels and may not correlate with toxicity (p.3707).","In polydrug overdose a clear toxidrome may be obscured when different drug classes counteract each other's physiologic effects (p.3704).","Physostigmine fully reverses anticholinergic poisoning but can also produce some arousal in CNS depression of any cause, so arousal alone is not specific (p.3706).","Overdose effects begin sooner, peak later and last longer than therapeutic-dose effects, and an agent's published (therapeutic) pharmacokinetics differ from its toxicokinetics in overdose (p.3707)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison grades physiologic stimulation and depression in poisoning/withdrawal (Table 470-2): Stimulation - grade 1 anxious/tremulous with normal vital signs; grade 2 agitated, mildly-moderately raised vitals but can follow commands; grade 3 delirious with motor hyperactivity and markedly raised vitals, possible tachyarrhythmias; grade 4 coma, seizures, cardiovascular collapse. Depression - grade 1 lethargic but arousable to voice/touch; grade 2 responds to pain not voice, brainstem reflexes intact; grade 3 unresponsive to pain, depressed brainstem reflexes, reduced tone/respiration/temperature; grade 4 flaccid, absent brainstem reflexes and respirations, depressed cardiovascular signs (p.3705).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3703-3707 (Ch. 470 Poisoning and Drug Overdose, Mark B. Mycyk)"],"crossLinks":["opioid_od","salicylate_tox","organophosphate","serotonin_nms"]},"dvt":{"name":"Deep vein thrombosis","system":"Vascular","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e, Chap. 290 (Deep-Venous Thrombosis and Pulmonary Thromboembolism), p.2155-2164; Chap. 69 (Bleeding and Thrombosis) p.473-474; Chap. 98 (Cancer and venous thrombosis) p.~705; Chap. 27 (d-Dimer) p.~158","clinicalPearls":["The classic DVT symptom is a calf cramp or 'charley horse' that persists and worsens over several days, rather than a sudden event (Harrison 22e p.2159).","A diffusely edematous (whole-leg) limb actually makes ordinary DVT less likely, with the important exception of phlegmasia cerulea dolens, the most severe form of DVT (Harrison 22e p.2159).","Recurrent left thigh edema, especially in a young woman, should raise suspicion of May-Thurner syndrome, in which the right common iliac artery compresses the left common iliac vein and may precipitate DVT (Harrison 22e p.2159).","Upper extremity DVT may show only subtle asymmetry of the supraclavicular fossa or upper-arm circumference; it is increasingly linked to PICC lines and rises with larger catheter diameter and more lumens (Harrison 22e p.2158-2159, 2161).","Superficial vein thrombosis presents with erythema, tenderness and a palpable cord and carries a risk of extension into the deep venous system (Harrison 22e p.2158).","An idiopathic (no clear precipitant) VTE is the strongest predictor of recurrence in patients without malignancy (Harrison 22e p.473).","Testing for inherited thrombophilia in adults should be reserved for the rare situation where the result would actually change management (Harrison 22e p.473-474)."],"pathophysiology":"DVT arises from Virchow's triad of venous stasis, hypercoagulability and endothelial injury, now understood to be typically coupled with an inflammatory trigger. Activated platelets release proinflammatory microparticles that recruit neutrophils to form neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs); these histone-rich, web-like networks promote platelet aggregation and platelet-dependent thrombin generation. Thrombi form and propagate in an environment of stasis, low oxygen tension and upregulated proinflammatory genes. Lower-extremity thrombi usually begin in the calf veins and can propagate proximally to the popliteal, femoral and iliac veins; detached thrombi embolize through the vena cava and right heart into the pulmonary circulation, and rarely cross a patent foramen ovale or atrial septal defect to cause paradoxical (arterial) embolism. VTE shares inflammation, hypercoagulability and endothelial injury with arterial atherothrombosis, so VTE patients have more than double the risk of future MI or stroke and vice versa. Common genetic prothrombotic states are factor V Leiden (resistance to protein C) and the prothrombin gene mutation (raised prothrombin level); deficiencies of antithrombin, protein C and protein S also predispose but are rare; antiphospholipid syndrome is an acquired thrombophilia causing both venous and arterial thrombosis (Harrison 22e p.2156-2158).","additionalDifferentials":["Ruptured Baker's cyst (sudden severe calf discomfort) (Harrison 22e p.2159)","Muscle strain or injury (Harrison 22e p.2159)","Acute postthrombotic syndrome / chronic venous insufficiency (Harrison 22e p.2159)","May-Thurner syndrome (iliac vein compression) presenting as recurrent leg edema (Harrison 22e p.2159)"],"infectionMimics":["Cellulitis - fever and chills more often herald cellulitis than DVT (Harrison 22e p.2159)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Ruptured Baker's cyst (Harrison 22e p.2159)","Muscle strain or injury (Harrison 22e p.2159)","Chronic venous insufficiency / postthrombotic syndrome (Harrison 22e p.2156, 2159)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Loss of vein compressibility is the primary ultrasound criterion for DVT; a normal vein collapses (a 'wink') under gentle transducer pressure, whereas an acutely thrombosed vein stays distended and may show central, homogeneous, low-echogenicity thrombus (Harrison 22e p.2160).","Doppler interrogation adds flow data: calf compression normally augments flow, and loss of respiratory variation suggests an obstructing DVT or a pelvic obstructive process (Harrison 22e p.2160).","Contrast (gadolinium) MR venography is an excellent option when pelvic or leg ultrasound is equivocal or technically nondiagnostic (Harrison 22e p.2160-2161).","Contrast phlebography is now reserved mainly for when an interventional procedure is planned (Harrison 22e p.2161).","An age-adjusted d-dimer cutoff (age x 10 ng/mL in patients >50 years) is endorsed for ruling out PE but does NOT apply to suspected acute DVT (Harrison 22e p.2159-2160).","d-Dimer rarely helps in hospitalized patients because levels are frequently elevated by systemic illness (MI, pneumonia, sepsis, cancer, postoperative state, pregnancy) (Harrison 22e p.2159)."],"redFlags":["Phlegmasia cerulea dolens - massive proximal DVT with a diffusely swollen, painful, cyanotic limb, the most serious form of DVT (Harrison 22e p.2159).","Signs of pulmonary embolism (unexplained breathlessness, syncope, hypotension, cyanosis) since DVT can embolize; many PE patients have no detectable residual DVT because the leg clot has already embolized (Harrison 22e p.2158-2159).","Active malignancy as the underlying driver - up to ~15% of patients who develop DVT have cancer, and unprovoked VTE may be its first manifestation (Harrison 22e p.~705)."],"prognosis":"Postthrombotic syndrome (chronic venous insufficiency) is the main long-term morbidity: damaged venous valves cause ankle/calf swelling and leg aching after prolonged standing, and in its most severe form deep skin ulceration near the medial malleolus. Notably, graduated compression stockings prescribed for asymptomatic newly diagnosed acute DVT do NOT prevent postthrombotic syndrome (they are used only for symptomatic swelling relief). Recurrence risk after stopping anticoagulation is stratified (ESC 2019) as low (<3%/yr, e.g., major surgery/trauma), intermediate (3-8%/yr, e.g., minor surgery, hospitalization for acute illness, pregnancy/estrogen, long-haul flight, IBD, autoimmune disease, or no identifiable risk factor) and high (>8%/yr, active cancer, antiphospholipid syndrome). Harrison de-emphasizes the 'provoked vs unprovoked' dichotomy because many provoked VTEs recur at rates similar to unprovoked ones, and enduring risk factors (heart failure, chronic lung disease, IBD) raise recurrence risk. The Post-VTE Functional Status scale (grades 0-4) can track functional recovery (Harrison 22e p.2156-2157, 2163).","pitfalls":["Not all leg pain is DVT - sudden severe calf pain may be a ruptured Baker's cyst, and fever/chills point more to cellulitis (Harrison 22e p.2159).","Wells point-score rules are validated in ambulatory but NOT in hospitalized patients (Harrison 22e p.2159).","Using d-dimer to rule out DVT in hospitalized or systemically ill patients is futile because it is frequently elevated by non-thrombotic illness (Harrison 22e p.2159).","The age-adjusted d-dimer threshold validated for PE does not extend to suspected acute DVT (Harrison 22e p.2160).","In cancer patients, elevated d-dimer is far less predictive of DVT than in patients without cancer (Harrison 22e p.~705).","IVC filters can paradoxically increase the DVT rate by acting as a nidus for clot, and may still let small-to-medium clots pass via collateral veins (Harrison 22e p.2163).","Warfarin (not a DOAC) is the standard anticoagulant for antiphospholipid syndrome (Harrison 22e p.2163)."],"severityClassification":"Wells clinical prediction rule for DVT (Harrison 22e p.2159): low likelihood if score <=0, moderate if 1-2, high if >=3. Points: active cancer +1; paralysis/paresis or recent cast +1; bedridden >3 days or major surgery <12 weeks +1; tenderness along distribution of deep veins +1; entire-leg swelling +1; unilateral calf swelling >3 cm +1; pitting edema +1; collateral superficial nonvaricose veins +1; alternative diagnosis at least as likely as DVT -2. Pretest-probability strategy: patients with low DVT likelihood undergo d-dimer first and need no imaging if it is normal, whereas high-likelihood patients should skip d-dimer and proceed straight to compression ultrasound imaging.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2155","Harrison 22e p.2156","Harrison 22e p.2157","Harrison 22e p.2158","Harrison 22e p.2159","Harrison 22e p.2160","Harrison 22e p.2161","Harrison 22e p.2163","Harrison 22e p.473","Harrison 22e p.474"],"crossLinks":["pe","malignancy_b","CELLULITIS"]},"gbs":{"name":"Guillain-Barré syndrome","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3617-3622 (Chapter 458)","clinicalPearls":["About 70% of cases follow an acute infection (usually respiratory or GI) by 1-3 weeks; roughly 20-30% are preceded by Campylobacter jejuni, with a similar share after herpesvirus infection (often CMV or EBV) (p.3617).","Weakness usually evolves over hours to a few days and is classically ascending (legs more than arms), often first noticed as 'rubbery legs', with tingling dysesthesias in the extremities (p.3617).","Facial diparesis occurs in ~50% of patients and lower cranial nerve/bulbar involvement is common; bulbar weakness with difficulty handling secretions can initially be mistaken for brainstem ischemia (p.3617).","Pain (neck, shoulder, back, or diffuse spinal pain) is common early, in ~50% of patients, and deep aching muscle pain plus dysesthetic limb pain also occur (p.3617).","Large-fiber functions (deep tendon reflexes, proprioception) are affected more severely than cutaneous pain/temperature sensation (p.3617).","AMAN and AMSAN are axonal variants associated with anti-GD1a/anti-GM1 antibodies and preceding C. jejuni; AMAN can occur in seasonal outbreaks (e.g., rural China) and is prevalent in China and Mexico (p.3617-3619).","Miller Fisher syndrome (~5% of cases) presents with ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and areflexia without limb weakness and carries anti-GQ1b antibodies in >90% (p.3617, p.3619).","If diagnosis is strongly suspected, treatment should not wait for the characteristic CSF or electrodiagnostic findings to evolve (p.3620)."],"pathophysiology":"GBS is an acute, often fulminant autoimmune polyradiculoneuropathy thought to arise from immune responses to non-self antigens (infections, vaccines) that cross-react with peripheral nerve via molecular mimicry, with gangliosides (glycosphingolipids concentrated at sites such as nodes of Ranvier) as likely targets. In AIDP, the demyelinating and most common form, both cellular and humoral mechanisms operate: complement deposits on the outer Schwann cell surface, triggering membrane-attack-complex formation, vesicular myelin disintegration, and macrophage-mediated removal of myelin. The resulting conduction block (with axons left intact) explains the flaccid paralysis and the potential for rapid recovery once remyelination occurs; secondary axonal degeneration in severe cases slows recovery and worsens residual deficit. In the axonal variants (AMAN/AMSAN), IgG and complement deposit at the nodes of Ranvier of motor axons, with anti-GD1a antibodies preferentially targeting motor over sensory roots; low CMAP amplitudes often reflect reversible nodal/paranodal conduction block rather than true axon loss, though severe cases show axonal degeneration (p.3617-3620).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute myelopathies, especially with prolonged back pain and sphincter disturbance (p.3620)","Diphtheria with early oropharyngeal disturbance (p.3620)","Porphyria (clues: abdominal pain, seizures, psychosis) (p.3620)","Vasculitic neuropathy (check ESR) (p.3620)","Critical illness neuropathy or myopathy (p.3620)","Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (CIDP) in patients with late or recurrent relapses (p.3622)"],"infectionMimics":["Poliomyelitis and acute flaccid myelitis (wild-type poliovirus, West Nile virus, enterovirus D68, enterovirus A71, Japanese encephalitis virus) (p.3620)","Lyme polyradiculitis and other tick-borne paralyses (p.3620)","CMV polyradiculitis in immunocompromised patients (p.3620)","Botulism (a neuromuscular junction disorder; pupillary reactivity lost early) (p.3620)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Myasthenia gravis (neuromuscular junction disorder) (p.3620)","Poisoning with organophosphates, thallium, or arsenic (p.3620)","Paralytic shellfish poisoning (p.3620)","Severe hypophosphatemia (rare) (p.3620)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CSF protein may be normal when symptoms have been present <=48 h and usually rises only by the end of the first week, so a normal early CSF does not exclude GBS (p.3619).","A sustained CSF pleocytosis (or counts above the GBS range) should prompt a search for an alternative or concurrent diagnosis such as viral myelitis, HIV, leukemia/lymphoma infiltrating nerves, or neurosarcoidosis (p.3619).","Electrodiagnostic abnormalities are mild or absent early and lag behind the clinical course; earliest AIDP findings are prolonged F-wave and distal latencies and reduced CMAP amplitudes (p.3619).","Antiganglioside antibodies (e.g., anti-GM1 in AMAN/AMSAN, anti-GQ1b in Miller Fisher) can support specific subtypes (p.3618-3619).","Test for HIV in patients with risk factors or CSF pleocytosis (p.3620)."],"redFlags":["Rapid tempo of progression and severe weakness on admission, plus facial and/or bulbar weakness in the first week, predict the need for mechanical ventilation (up to ~30% of patients require ventilatory support) (p.3617).","Bulbar weakness with difficulty handling secretions threatens the airway (p.3617).","Autonomic instability — wide blood pressure swings, postural hypotension, and cardiac dysrhythmias — can be fatal and may occur even when limb weakness is otherwise mild (p.3617).","Presence of fever or constitutional symptoms at onset casts doubt on the diagnosis and should prompt reconsideration (p.3617).","Prominent or early bladder dysfunction, or a sensory level, should raise suspicion for spinal cord disease rather than GBS (p.3617)."],"prognosis":"About 85% of patients reach full functional recovery within several months to a year, though minor signs (e.g., persistent areflexia) and complaints such as fatigue may remain. Mortality is <5% in optimal settings, usually from secondary pulmonary complications. The outlook is worst with severe proximal motor and sensory axonal damage; other adverse factors are advanced age, a fulminant or severe attack, and delayed treatment. Elevated serum neurofilament light chains and high anti-GM1 titers both correlate with greater axonal involvement and poorer recovery. Between 5 and 10% of typical GBS patients have one or more late relapses, many later reclassified as CIDP (p.3621-3622).","pitfalls":["Corticosteroids have NOT been found effective in GBS and should not be relied on as treatment (p.3621).","Treatment timing matters: each day counts, and by ~2 weeks after the first motor symptoms it is unknown whether immunotherapy is still effective; if a patient has reached the plateau, treatment is probably no longer indicated unless severe weakness suggests ongoing immune attack (p.3620).","Lack of improvement after a course of IVIg or plasmapheresis is NOT an indication to switch to the other modality, and combining the two is not significantly better than either alone (p.3621).","Early CSF and electrodiagnostic studies can be normal/minimal; relying on them to confirm before treating can cause harmful delay (p.3619-3620).","Acute flaccid myelitis can closely mimic GBS because sphincter disturbances may be absent (p.3620)."],"severityClassification":"Brighton Collaboration case definitions (2011) grade diagnostic certainty in three levels. Level 1 (highest certainty) requires bilateral flaccid limb weakness, decreased/absent reflexes in weak limbs, a monophasic course with onset-to-nadir of 12 h to 28 days followed by plateau, electrophysiology consistent with GBS, cytoalbuminologic dissociation (elevated CSF protein with CSF white cell count <50/microL), and no alternative diagnosis. Lower levels relax the laboratory/electrophysiologic requirements. Parallel criteria exist for Miller Fisher syndrome (ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, areflexia without limb weakness) (p.3620-3621).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3617","Harrison 22e p.3618","Harrison 22e p.3619","Harrison 22e p.3620","Harrison 22e p.3621","Harrison 22e p.3622"],"crossLinks":["myasthenic_crisis","cord_compression"]},"gerd_chest":{"name":"GERD / non-cardiac chest pain","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e: Chest Discomfort ch.15 p.104-109; Diseases of the Esophagus ch.334 p.2503-2511; Nausea, Vomiting, and Indigestion ch.48 p.300-301","clinicalPearls":["Esophageal pain shares a nerve plexus with the heart and esophageal afferents discriminate stimuli poorly, so distention or acid (chemostimulation) is often perceived as pressure-type mid-chest pain radiating to back, arms, or jaw - closely mimicking angina (p.2503-2504).","Features that favour an oesophageal rather than cardiac source include pain that is nonexertional, prolonged, meal-related, relieved by antacids, accompanied by heartburn/dysphagia/regurgitation, and that interrupts sleep - but every one of these overlaps with cardiac pain, which must remain the primary consideration (p.2509).","Heartburn (pyrosis) is the most common oesophageal symptom and its link to GERD is strong enough that empirical acid-suppression has become accepted management; chest pain and persistent dysphagia are the key exceptions that warrant evaluation rather than empirical treatment (p.2503, p.2510).","Gastrointestinal disorders are the single most common cause of nontraumatic chest discomfort, yet fewer than 15% of ED chest-pain presentations turn out to be ACS (p.104).","A burning quality suggests acid reflux but can also occur with myocardial ischaemia; conversely oesophageal spasm can produce a severe squeezing pain identical to angina - quality alone never establishes a diagnosis (p.105).","Both reflux relief and ischaemia relief overlap with nitroglycerin: oesophageal spasm may be relieved promptly by nitroglycerin just as angina can be, so a nitroglycerin response does not confirm cardiac origin (p.106).","Chest wall tenderness on palpation does NOT exclude myocardial ischaemia, and a normal physical exam is not reassuring in unstable ischaemic heart disease (p.106-107).","Some patients with acid reflux on pH testing report epigastric/abdominal pain or nausea instead of classic heartburn (atypical GERD) (p.301, ch.48).","Visceral hypersensitivity is increasingly recognised as a cofactor in GERD symptom generation, beyond simple mucosal erosion and afferent nerve activation (p.2510)."],"pathophysiology":"Pathologic reflux arises from anatomic and physiologic failure of the oesophagogastric junction valve - widening of the diaphragmatic hiatus with diminished crural diaphragm function, loss of the intra-abdominal oesophageal segment disabling the flap valve, axial (sliding) hiatus hernia causing LES hypotension, and increased junctional compliance allowing liquid to escape during transient LES relaxations (the belch reflex). Some reflux is physiologic and intertwined with belching, but esophagitis follows excessive reflux plus impaired acid clearance, which depends on peristalsis returning fluid to the stomach and bicarbonate in swallowed saliva neutralising residual acid; impaired peristalsis and reduced salivation therefore prolong acid contact. Mucosal injury is driven by a cytokine-mediated inflammatory pathway rather than purely direct caustic injury. Gastric acid hypersecretion is usually NOT the dominant factor (exceptions: Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, severe esophagitis in ~50%); chronic H. pylori gastritis may even be protective by inducing hypoacidity. Pepsin, bile, and pancreatic enzymes also injure the epithelium, and bile persists in refluxate despite acid suppression and is implicated as a cofactor in Barrett's metaplasia and adenocarcinoma. Exacerbating factors include abdominal obesity, pregnancy, gastric hypersecretory states, delayed gastric emptying, disrupted peristalsis, and overeating. Esophageal chest pain itself reflects a shared nerve plexus with the heart and poor discriminative ability of oesophageal afferents (p.2503-2504, p.2509-2510).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute coronary syndrome / myocardial ischaemia (must be excluded first given lethal implications)","Distal (diffuse) esophageal spasm - dysphagia plus chest pain from abnormal contractions with normal LES relaxation; diagnosed only after commoner causes excluded","Hypercontractile (jackhammer) esophagus","Peptic esophagitis / esophageal ulcer","Eosinophilic esophagitis (especially if dysphagia)","Peptic ulcer disease (symptomatic 60-90 min after meals)","Functional dyspepsia (postprandial distress and epigastric pain syndromes)","Biliary colic / gallbladder disease","Pancreatitis","Acute aortic syndrome / aortic dissection","Pulmonary embolism","Pneumothorax","Pericarditis (radiation to trapezius ridge, worse supine, relieved leaning forward)","Costochondritis / Tietze's syndrome and other musculoskeletal chest-wall causes","Cervical radiculitis / cervical disk disease","Panic disorder / anxiety state (up to ~10% of ED chest-pain presentations)","Esophageal rupture (greatest urgency among noncardiopulmonary causes)"],"infectionMimics":["Infectious esophagitis (fungal or viral) - more often causes odynophagia than heartburn; ulcerations are numerous, punctate, and diffuse on endoscopy","Pneumonia / pleuritis (pulmonary infective cause of chest pain via pleural involvement)","Herpes zoster (dermatomal chest pain)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pill (medication-induced) esophagitis - solitary deep ulcer at points of luminal narrowing, often near the carina, sparing distal esophagus","Eosinophilic esophagitis - edema, rings, linear furrows, white punctate exudate, strictures","Distal esophageal spasm and hypercontractile esophagus","Achalasia (with stasis esophagitis)"],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG within 10 min of presentation, with serial ECGs every 30-60 min when ACS suspected; note resting ECG sensitivity for ischaemia is poor (as low as 20%)","High-sensitivity cardiac troponin at presentation and repeated (1-3 h hs-assay; 3-6 h conventional) - a rising/falling pattern with a value above the 99th percentile defines MI; a single very low hs-troponin >2-3 h after onset can rule out MI with NPV >99%","Validated clinical decision pathways (e.g., HEART, EDACS) combined with serial troponin to risk-stratify and safely discharge low-probability ACS patients","Coronary CT angiography or functional stress imaging (nuclear perfusion / stress echo) for intermediate/undetermined risk once life-threatening causes excluded; CTA can also exclude dissection, effusion, and PE","Chest radiograph (mediastinal widening, pneumothorax, pneumonia, pulmonary edema)","D-dimer to help exclude pulmonary embolism where indicated","Upper endoscopy (EGD) - first-line for mucosal lesions, Barrett's detection by color, biopsy of suspicious or atypical findings; erosive esophagitis is found in only ~one-third of GERD patients so a normal EGD does NOT exclude GERD","Ambulatory esophageal pH monitoring (24 h, or wireless capsule 48-96 h off acid suppression) - the diagnostic standard for confirming pathologic reflux","Combined esophageal pH-impedance testing (on PPI) to identify oesophageal hypersensitivity or nonacid/incompletely controlled acid regurgitation in refractory/atypical symptoms","High-resolution esophageal manometry (with FLIP/impedance) - to diagnose motility disorders (achalasia, DES) and assess peristalsis before anti-reflux surgery","Barium contrast radiography - higher sensitivity than endoscopy for strictures (especially with a 13-mm barium tablet) and for assessing function/anatomy"],"redFlags":["Dysphagia (concerning for oesophageal obstruction - stricture, EoE, or cancer)","Odynophagia (suggests infectious or pill esophagitis, or extensive ulceration)","Unexplained weight loss","Recurrent vomiting","Occult or gross gastrointestinal bleeding / anaemia","Jaundice","Palpable mass or lymphadenopathy","Family history of gastro-oesophageal malignancy","Nocturnal symptoms","Heartburn >5 years' duration, especially age >50, or new dyspepsia age >60 (screen for Barrett's / malignancy by endoscopy)","Failure to respond to acid suppression (up to one-third of presumed GERD)","Any diagnostic doubt about a cardiac cause - acute exertional/pressure pain, radiation to both arms, diaphoresis, dyspnoea, syncope, hypotension/tachycardia"],"prognosis":"Most patients in whom an acute cardiopulmonary cause is excluded have a favourable prognosis; in low-risk patients the 30-day major cardiovascular event rate is about 2.5%. However, historically 2-6% of presumed-nonischaemic chest pain patients discharged from the ED were later found to have had a missed myocardial infarction, underscoring the cost of misclassification. For GERD itself, erosive esophagitis and peptic strictures have become rare with potent acid suppression, but Barrett's metaplasia and oesophageal adenocarcinoma have increased; progression of Barrett's to adenocarcinoma is estimated at roughly 0.1-0.5% per year, with obese white men in their sixth decade at greatest risk. High-grade dysplasia in Barrett's carries a high rate of progression and a high prevalence of coexisting unrecognised cancer (p.104, p.2510-2511, ch.85 p.92).","pitfalls":["Attributing chest pain to GERD before excluding a cardiac cause - coronary artery disease must be given early consideration because of its potentially lethal implications, and oesophageal pain closely mimics angina.","Relying on pain quality, nitroglycerin response, or chest-wall tenderness to distinguish cardiac from oesophageal pain - all overlap and none is reliable; a normal exam and even a normal resting ECG do not exclude ischaemia.","Treating a normal endoscopy as ruling out GERD - erosive esophagitis is seen in only about one-third of GERD patients.","Overlooking that up to one-third of presumed-GERD patients fail to respond to acid suppression and may have an alternative diagnosis (functional, hypersensitivity, motility, nonacid reflux).","Diagnosing distal esophageal spasm or nonspecific manometric abnormalities prematurely - these are made only after GERD, peptic and infectious esophagitis, and cardiac disease are excluded; minor manometric findings are of unclear significance and often coexist with anxiety/depression and IBS.","Missing alarm features (dysphagia, odynophagia, weight loss, bleeding/anaemia) that mandate endoscopy rather than continued empirical therapy.","Forgetting that nausea/vomiting and epigastric pain can accompany MI (especially inferior MI via vagal/Bezold-Jarisch reflex) and need not indicate a GI cause."],"severityClassification":"GERD spans a spectrum of oesophageal injury: nonerosive reflux disease, erosive esophagitis (graded by antisecretory-responsive severity), peptic stricture, Barrett's metaplasia, and adenocarcinoma. Barrett's dysplasia is staged as low-grade vs high-grade, the latter mandating intervention. For the chest-pain presentation itself, Harrison frames risk by clinical decision pathways categorising patients as low, intermediate, or high probability of ACS using symptoms, age, risk factors, ECG, and serial high-sensitivity troponin (HEART/EDACS scores) (p.107-109, p.2510-2511).","references":["Harrison 22e p.104","Harrison 22e p.105","Harrison 22e p.106","Harrison 22e p.107","Harrison 22e p.108","Harrison 22e p.109","Harrison 22e p.300","Harrison 22e p.301","Harrison 22e p.2503","Harrison 22e p.2504","Harrison 22e p.2509","Harrison 22e p.2510","Harrison 22e p.2511"],"crossLinks":["acs","peptic_ulcer","pericarditis","aortic_dissection","pneumothorax"]},"glomerulonephritis":{"name":"Acute glomerulonephritis","system":"Nephrology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.319 (Approach to Renal Disease) p.2353-2356; Ch.55 (Azotemia/Urinary Abnormalities) p.341-343","clinicalPearls":["The nephritic urinalysis is the foundation of diagnosis: dysmorphic RBCs, RBC casts, pyuria, and proteinuria; always examine a freshly spun sediment to confirm active GN (p.2353-2354).","Dysmorphic ('acanthocyte') RBCs on phase-contrast microscopy point to a glomerular bleeding source; RBC casts are highly specific but very insensitive for GN, so their absence does not exclude glomerular disease (p.341-342).","The triad of dysmorphic RBCs, RBC casts, and proteinuria >500 mg/day is essentially diagnostic of glomerulonephritis and warrants serologic workup plus biopsy even when creatinine is still normal (p.343).","RPGN is a clinical diagnosis: any nephritic picture with rapidly rising creatinine should be treated as crescentic GN until proven otherwise (p.2355).","At least three immunologic studies (ANCA, anti-GBM, complement) should be sent early to triage the three RPGN categories before immunosuppression (p.2355).","Gross/cola-coloured glomerular hematuria with concurrent pharyngitis (synpharyngitic, day 1-3) suggests IgA nephropathy rather than post-streptococcal GN, which classically follows infection by 10 days to 3 weeks (p.2354).","Both PIGN and IgA nephropathy deposit C3 in the glomerulus, but only PIGN lowers serum complement; a normal serum C3 with mesangial IgA favours IgA nephropathy/IgA vasculitis (p.2354-2356).","Infection-associated GN occurring during an ongoing infection (e.g., MRSA abscess, osteomyelitis, empyema) is characteristically associated with glomerular IgA deposition and differs from classic post-infectious GN (p.2355)."],"pathophysiology":"GN reflects glomerular inflammation producing hypertension, volume expansion (edema, sometimes ascites), and an abnormal (nephritic) urinalysis; intense cellular proliferation reduces filtering surface area, causing oliguria/anuria, sodium and water retention, and concentrated low-sodium urine. Three mechanistic categories underlie RPGN (Fig.319-1): (1) ANCA-associated pauci-immune vasculitis (anti-proteinase-3/cANCA, anti-myeloperoxidase/pANCA) where autoantibodies recruit complement and drive inflammatory vasculitis of kidney and peripheral vessels; (2) anti-GBM disease, where circulating IgG directed at an epitope in the alpha-3 chain of the non-collagenous domain of type IV collagen binds in situ with C3; and (3) immune-complex disease with low circulating C3 from subendothelial deposits (post-infectious/PIGN, endocarditis, SLE, cryoglobulinemia, hepatitis B/C, syphilis). In PIGN, antibody-bacterial antigen complexes form ~10 days-3 weeks after pharyngeal or skin (impetigo) infection by nephritogenic group A strep, activate complement causing hypocomplementemia and subendothelial deposits, with later subepithelial 'humps'. Pathologically, RPGN shows parietal epithelial and inflammatory cell proliferation forming cellular crescents that compress capillaries; crescents progress to fibrosis and global glomerulosclerosis. IgA nephropathy results from mesangial deposition of C3 binding galactose-deficient IgA-1. (p.2354-2356)","additionalDifferentials":["ANCA-associated (pauci-immune) vasculitis — microscopic polyangiitis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis (formerly Wegener), eosinophilic GPA/Churg-Strauss (p.2355)","Anti-GBM disease / Goodpasture disease (anti-GBM with pulmonary hemorrhage) (p.2355)","Post-infectious / post-streptococcal GN (low C3) (p.2354)","IgA nephropathy and IgA vasculitis / Henoch-Schonlein purpura (normal C3) (p.2354-2356)","Lupus nephritis (SLE) (p.2356)","Cryoglobulinemic GN (low C4, high rheumatoid factor; hepatitis C or paraproteinemia) (p.2356)","Infection-associated GN of endocarditis, hepatitis B/C, syphilis (p.2353, p.2355)","Hereditary nephritis and thin basement membrane disease (isolated glomerular hematuria) (p.342)"],"infectionMimics":["Subacute/acute bacterial endocarditis with immune-complex GN and low C3 (p.2355)","Hepatitis B and C-associated GN (p.2353-2356)","Syphilis-associated GN, usually with vasculitis (p.2353)","Staphylococcal (often MRSA) deep infections — abscess, osteomyelitis, empyema — causing infection-associated IgA-dominant GN (p.2355)","Schistosomiasis-associated glomerular disease (p.2353)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Renal microvasculature disease — hemolytic-uremic syndrome and thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (MAHA with schistocytes, high LDH, low haptoglobin, thrombocytopenia, end-organ injury) (p.341, p.2353)","Malignant hypertension (p.341)","Renal vein thrombosis (heavy proteinuria and hematuria) and renal artery thrombosis (p.341)","Isolated hematuria from stones, neoplasm, tuberculosis, trauma, prostatitis (postrenal/non-glomerular) (p.342-343)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Phase-contrast microscopy of spun sediment for dysmorphic RBCs/acanthocytes and RBC casts (p.341-342)","Serum complement C3 (and C4 for cryoglobulinemia) to separate low-complement immune-complex GN from normal-complement causes (p.2354-2356)","ANCA panel — anti-proteinase-3 (cANCA) and anti-myeloperoxidase (pANCA) (p.2355)","Anti-GBM antibody (p.2355)","ASO / streptozyme / anti-DNase B titres for recent streptococcal infection (p.2355)","Blood cultures (rule out endocarditis), hepatitis B/C serology, HIV, VDRL, cryoglobulins (p.342, p.2355)","Quantify proteinuria — spot albumin-to-creatinine or protein-to-creatinine ratio, or 24-h collection (p.341-342)","Renal biopsy — indicated when RBC casts present; defines pattern, prognosis, and treatment (p.341-343, p.2355)"],"redFlags":["Rapidly rising creatinine with a nephritic sediment = crescentic RPGN, a nephrology emergency requiring urgent immunology and biopsy (p.2355)","Hemoptysis, pulmonary infiltrates, or alveolar hemorrhage with renal failure = pulmonary-renal syndrome (anti-GBM/Goodpasture, ANCA vasculitis, SLE, cryoglobulinemia) — potentially fatal pulmonary hemorrhage (p.2355-2356)","Upper-airway symptoms (sinusitis, nasal congestion, epistaxis) with GN suggest ANCA-associated vasculitis (p.2355)","Anuria in acute GN indicates severe RPGN among the catastrophic anuric causes (p.342)","Anti-GBM disease typically presents as a single fulminant episode demanding rapid diagnosis and prompt plasma exchange to preserve kidney function (p.2355)"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on acuity vs chronicity: early reversible inflammatory/proliferative/necrotizing lesions can respond to prompt treatment, whereas sclerotic and atrophic biopsy findings indicate chronicity. Untreated RPGN capillaritis leads to complete, irreversible loss of kidney function; crescents that become fibrotic and atrophic cause global glomerulosclerosis, and when >50% of glomeruli are involved (diffuse) the process usually progresses to global glomerulosclerosis. Anti-GBM disease is usually a single acute fulminant event, while ANCA vasculitides may relapse (even subclinically) over time. IgA nephropathy can show rapid GFR decline to end-stage disease, especially in those of African ancestry with high-risk APOL1 alleles. Early diagnosis is critical. (p.2354-2356)","pitfalls":["Relying on dipstick alone: it detects only albumin and can be falsely positive at pH >7 or with concentrated/blood-contaminated urine, and falsely negative in dilute urine or with non-albumin (light-chain) proteinuria (p.341).","False-positive dipstick hematuria when myoglobinuria/rhabdomyolysis is present with no RBCs on microscopy (p.342-343).","Assuming absence of RBC casts excludes GN — casts are highly specific but very insensitive (p.341).","Mislabeling synpharyngitic gross hematuria as post-streptococcal GN; it is usually IgA nephropathy and PIGN instead follows infection by 10 days-3 weeks (p.2354).","Expecting low serum complement in IgA nephropathy/IgA vasculitis — serum C3 is normal despite glomerular C3 deposition (p.2356).","Withholding biopsy in azotemia-free patients with the diagnostic nephritic triad — they still warrant serologic workup and biopsy (p.343).","Both anti-GBM and ANCA can be co-positive with pauci-immune pathology; such cases are managed as ANCA disease (p.2355)."],"severityClassification":"RPGN crescent burden on biopsy stratifies severity: focal and segmental necrotizing GN when <50% of glomeruli are acutely and only segmentally involved (better preserved), versus diffuse involvement when >50% of glomeruli are affected, which usually progresses to global glomerulosclerosis. Healed focal segmental lesions yield secondary FSGS, distinct from primary podocytopathic FSGS. RPGN is mechanistically triaged into three serologic categories (Fig.319-1): ANCA-associated (pauci-immune), anti-GBM, and low-C3 immune-complex disease. (p.2355-2356)","references":["Harrison 22e p.2353","Harrison 22e p.2354","Harrison 22e p.2355","Harrison 22e p.2356","Harrison 22e p.341","Harrison 22e p.342","Harrison 22e p.343"],"crossLinks":["vasculitis","sle_flare","nephrotic","aki"]},"heart_failure":{"name":"Acute heart failure / pulmonary edema","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e, Ch. 264 (Heart Failure: Pathophysiology and Diagnosis, p.1975-1986) and Ch. 265 (Heart Failure: Management, p.1987-1988); Killip classification from Ch. 286 (ST-Elevation MI, p.2247).","clinicalPearls":["HF is a clinical syndrome, not a single disease; it is the common final pathway for most chronic cardiovascular conditions, especially hypertension, coronary artery disease and valvular disease (p.1975).","About half of patients with HF have a reduced ejection fraction and half have a near-normal/preserved EF, so a normal LVEF does not exclude HF (p.1977).","The signs and symptoms of HF are neither specific nor sensitive, so a high index of suspicion is needed in higher-risk patients (older, with hypertension, diabetes or CKD) (p.1984).","NYHA functional class correlates poorly with objective measures such as LV size, EF or peak oxygen consumption (p.1982).","An S3 gallop usually accompanies volume overload and tachycardia, suggests significant haemodynamic compromise and carries adverse prognostic weight; an S4 is non-specific and may be heard in HFpEF from hypertension (p.1984).","Bendopnea (breathlessness on bending forward, e.g. tying shoes) reflects raised filling pressure, particularly with low cardiac output (p.1982).","Holosystolic murmurs of mitral/tricuspid regurgitation often appear in advanced HF even when the valves are structurally normal (functional/secondary regurgitation) (p.1984).","In acute or younger chronic HF patients, leg oedema may be absent despite marked systemic venous hypertension (p.1984).","A very low BNP/NT-proBNP can help exclude HF as the cause of dyspnoea in non-obese patients (p.1985-1986).","The obesity paradox: obese HF patients tend to have a more favourable prognosis than those with low or normal BMI (p.1986)."],"pathophysiology":"HFrEF is a progressive disease in which an index injury (e.g. acute MI, chronic pressure/volume overload, genetic or congenital disease) triggers months-to-years of structural and functional cardiovascular remodelling; patients may stay asymptomatic for long periods because compensatory mechanisms are activated that ultimately drive further deterioration. Pressure overload produces concentric hypertrophy and volume overload produces eccentric (dilated) hypertrophy, accompanied by myocyte hypertrophy, interstitial fibrosis, apoptosis and re-expression of fetal genes. Sustained activation of the sympathetic nervous system and the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system initially supports cardiac output but becomes maladaptive, causing excessive vasoconstriction, sodium/water retention, electrolyte and renal disturbance, direct myocardial toxicity and arrhythmias; counter-regulatory natriuretic peptides, prostaglandins, bradykinin and nitric oxide are upregulated but inadequate. Therapies that antagonise RAAS and the sympathetic system (RAAS inhibitors, mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists, beta blockers) attenuate or reverse remodelling and improve outcomes. Secondary mitral regurgitation arises from ventricular dilatation, sphericity and annular changes, perpetuating a vicious cycle. HFpEF reflects multiple mechanisms beyond diastolic dysfunction (impaired calcium reuptake, vascular stiffness, renal dysfunction, sodium avidity, inflammation related to adiposity, oxidative/nitrosative stress, insulin resistance). The cardiorenal syndrome is driven not only by low forward flow but importantly by systemic venous congestion and raised intra-abdominal/splanchnic pressure, so relief of congestion improves renal function (p.1977-1982).","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic lung disease (orthopnoea and nocturnal dyspnoea can mimic HF; relieved by coughing/expectoration rather than sitting upright) (p.1986)","Pulmonary arterial hypertension (p.1986)","Neuromuscular disease causing dyspnoea (p.1986)","Anaemia / iron-deficiency anaemia (p.1986)","Venous insufficiency, lymphoedema or obesity as non-cardiac causes of leg oedema (p.1986)","Nephrotic syndrome and deep vein thrombosis (causes of oedema) (p.1986)","Hepatic cirrhosis, portal vein thrombosis, malignant carcinomatosis (causes of ascites) (p.1986)","Constrictive pericarditis, pericardial effusion or superior vena cava syndrome (causes of jugular venous distension) (p.1986)"],"infectionMimics":["Low-grade fever from cytokine activation may occur in severe HF and resolves once compensation is restored, mimicking infection (p.1983)","Congestive hepatomegaly with raised transaminases, alkaline phosphatase and bilirubin can be misread as biliary tract disease (p.1983)","Chronic lower-limb oedema in HF may become weeping or progress to cellulitis (p.1984)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute respiratory distress syndrome and other non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema (p.1986)","Acute or chronic kidney disease causing salt/water retention with normal cardiac structure and function (p.1986)","Restrictive cardiomyopathy versus constrictive pericarditis (distinguished by simultaneous right- and left-heart filling pressures) (p.1985)","Reactive airways disease/bronchial asthma versus cardiac asthma (cardiac asthma more often shows diaphoresis and cyanosis) (p.1986)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Comprehensive metabolic panel, complete blood count, coagulation studies and urinalysis as baseline; targeted testing for diabetes, lipids and thyroid function (p.1984)","Serum sodium and potassium (hyponatraemia is an adverse prognostic marker; hypo-/hyperkalaemia predispose to arrhythmia) (p.1984)","Renal function (urea/creatinine, consider cystatin C which is less affected by muscle mass) (p.1984-1986)","Iron studies/ferritin to detect iron deficiency (with or without anaemia) (p.1985)","Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging for tissue characterisation and aetiology (ischaemic, myocarditis, amyloidosis, haemochromatosis) (p.1985)","Cardiac CT / coronary CT angiography to exclude pericardial disease, apical thrombus or obstructive CAD when CMR not feasible (p.1985)","Cardiac PET for ischaemia/viability and to assess cardiac sarcoid inflammation (p.1985)","Cardiopulmonary exercise testing (peak VO2, VE/VCO2 slope) for prognosis and transplant/MCS evaluation (p.1985)","Transoesophageal echocardiography or contrast cardiac CT to exclude left atrial appendage thrombus before cardioversion (p.1985)","Right-heart catheterisation / pulmonary artery catheter to distinguish cardiogenic from non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema and guide therapy in severe HF (p.1985)","Endomyocardial biopsy in selected cases (suspected myocarditis - lymphocytic, eosinophilic, sarcoid or giant cell - amyloidosis, or allograft rejection) (p.1985)","Newer biomarkers galectin-3 and soluble ST2 are approved for prognosis but not widely used (p.1985)"],"redFlags":["Acute pulmonary oedema with pink frothy sputum reflects marked elevation of pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (p.1982)","Hypotension with narrow pulse pressure and a rapid, thready pulse indicates severe HF; pulsus alternans signals severely depressed LV function (p.1983)","Cool extremities, peripheral cyanosis, mental dullness/confusion and oliguria suggest low-output/advanced-stage HF (p.1982-1983)","Marked elevation of transaminases and lactic acid suggests cardiogenic shock with severe low output (p.1984)","Cardiac cachexia (unintentional oedema-free weight loss >5% over 12 months) marks longstanding severe HF (p.1983)","An S3 gallop carries negative prognostic significance (p.1984)","Cheyne-Stokes/periodic breathing and central sleep apnoea are features of advanced HF (p.1983)"],"prognosis":"Overall 5-year survival after a HF diagnosis is roughly 50%, and in severe HF 1-year mortality may reach 40%; one in eight U.S. deaths lists HF on the certificate, most dying of progressive HF or sudden cardiac death. After a HF admission, mortality runs from 8-14% at 30 days to 26-37% at 1 year and up to 75% at 5 years, with readmission of 20-25% at 60 days and nearly 50% at 6 months; risk of death rises with each subsequent admission. Independent predictors of adverse outcome include older age, male sex, diabetes, CKD, CAD, advanced NYHA class, a third heart sound or raised JVP, reduced LV (or RV) EF, secondary mitral/tricuspid regurgitation, raised wedge pressure, pulmonary hypertension, low peak VO2, hyponatraemia, hyperuricaemia, worsening renal function, raised troponin/natriuretic peptides and neurohormones, wide QRS/LBBB, atrial fibrillation and ventricular arrhythmia. HFrecEF (recovered EF) generally carries a better prognosis than HFrEF or HFpEF, but withdrawing therapy can cause relapse in up to half within 6 months (p.1976-1977).","pitfalls":["Abandon the old terms systolic and diastolic HF: most HF patients, regardless of EF, have both systolic and diastolic abnormalities (p.1977)","BNP/NT-proBNP may be falsely low in obesity and falsely elevated by age, female sex, CKD, atrial fibrillation, pulmonary embolism and pulmonary hypertension, so interpret in context (p.1985-1986)","Inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP, uric acid) are non-specific and do not establish the diagnosis of HF (p.1984)","Congestive hepatopathy and gut-wall oedema can be mistaken for primary biliary or bowel disease (p.1983)","Distinguishing cardiac from pulmonary dyspnoea may be impossible when both coexist (common in older smokers); post-diuresis pulmonary function tests or cardiopulmonary exercise testing can help (p.1986)","Failure to identify and correct a precipitant can leave HF persistent despite adequate treatment; a precipitant is identifiable in 50-90% of admissions (p.1983)","Nonpitting oedema unresponsive to escalating diuretics may be lymphoedema, not HF (p.1984)","High-output HF (anaemia, thyrotoxicosis, AV shunt, cirrhosis, severe obesity, beriberi) will be missed if not actively considered in predisposed patients (p.1978, p.1982)"],"severityClassification":"HFrEF/HFmrEF/HFpEF phenotype by EF: reduced EF (HFrEF) <=40%, mildly reduced EF (HFmrEF) 41-49%, preserved EF (HFpEF) >=50%; LVEF >=50% is considered normal systolic function (p.1977-1978, p.1984). NYHA functional class I-IV grades symptoms by the activity that provokes them: class I none with ordinary activity, class II mild symptoms on ordinary exertion, class III symptoms on less-than-ordinary activity, class IV symptoms (or angina) at rest worsening with any activity (p.1982). ACC/AHA stages describe disease progression: stage B asymptomatic ventricular dysfunction, stage C symptomatic HF, stage D advanced/refractory HF with high mortality where transplant or mechanical circulatory support may be considered (p.1987). Killip classification (originally for acute MI) grades pump failure: class I no congestion; class II basal rales, S3 or right-heart congestion; class III pulmonary oedema; class IV cardiogenic shock (SBP <90 mmHg with peripheral vasoconstriction, cyanosis, confusion, oliguria) - historically rising mortality from class I to IV (p.2247).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1975","Harrison 22e p.1976","Harrison 22e p.1977","Harrison 22e p.1978","Harrison 22e p.1979","Harrison 22e p.1980","Harrison 22e p.1982","Harrison 22e p.1983","Harrison 22e p.1984","Harrison 22e p.1985","Harrison 22e p.1986","Harrison 22e p.1987","Harrison 22e p.1988","Harrison 22e p.2247"],"crossLinks":["acs","atrial_fib","ckd","pe","copd_exac_ni"]},"hepatic_enceph":{"name":"Hepatic encephalopathy","system":"Hepatology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2716-2717 (HE in cirrhosis), p.230-231 (pathophysiology of metabolic/hepatic encephalopathy), p.2629-2630 (signs of hepatic failure)","clinicalPearls":["Hepatic encephalopathy is far more common in chronic liver disease than in acute liver injury; in severe acute liver injury its presence is itself a defining criterion for acute liver failure (Harrison 22e p.2716).","Asterixis (the 'liver flap') is typically present and is elicited by having the patient extend the arms and dorsiflex the wrists, looking for a sudden forward jerk of the wrist; it requires a cooperative patient (Harrison 22e p.2716).","Earliest manifestations are subtle and nonspecific: altered sleep pattern, personality change, irritability, and mental dullness, progressing to confusion, disorientation, stupor and coma (Harrison 22e p.2629).","Patients may be agitated, violent and hard to manage, or alternatively very sleepy and difficult to rouse (Harrison 22e p.2716).","In acute liver failure, presentation may include excitability or mania rather than depressed consciousness (Harrison 22e p.2629).","Fetor hepaticus, a sweetish ammoniacal breath odor, can accompany liver failure, especially with portosystemic shunting of blood (Harrison 22e p.2629).","After a first episode, patients and caregivers often learn to recognize recurrences early and can self-medicate to prevent worsening (Harrison 22e p.2717).","Gut-derived neurotoxins that bypass the liver because of vascular shunting and reduced hepatic mass reach the brain and produce the syndrome (Harrison 22e p.2716)."],"pathophysiology":"Hepatic encephalopathy is an alteration of mental status and cognition resulting from liver failure, in which gut-derived neurotoxins are not cleared because of portosystemic vascular shunting and decreased functional hepatic mass and thus reach the brain. Ammonia is the principal candidate toxin: high ammonia concentrations are thought to drive increased synthesis of glutamine within astrocytes, causing osmotic astrocyte swelling, mitochondrial energy failure, and generation of reactive nitrogen and oxygen species, together with increased levels of the inhibitory neurotransmitter GABA and synthesis of putative 'false' neurotransmitters. No single one of these mechanisms fully explains the confusion and stupor, and which is mainly responsible for hepatic coma is not known. Other contributing compounds include certain false neurotransmitters and mercaptans. Chronic HE is characteristically accompanied by diffuse astrocytosis. In acute liver failure (but not chronic liver disease) cerebral edema with gray-matter swelling can occur and carries a risk of cerebral herniation (Harrison 22e p.230-231, p.2716).","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of altered mental status must always be considered, since the diagnosis is clinical (Harrison 22e p.2716)","Sedative/narcotic intoxication (Harrison 22e p.2629)","Renal failure / uremic encephalopathy (Harrison 22e p.2629)","Respiratory failure (Harrison 22e p.2629)","Electrolyte imbalance as a primary cause of confusion (Harrison 22e p.2629)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Metabolic encephalopathy from other causes (hypoglycemia, hyponatremia, hyperosmolar states, hypercapnia, hypercalcemia, renal failure) — asterixis and multifocal myoclonus are nonspecific and indicate a metabolic or toxic process generally, not HE specifically (Harrison 22e p.230, p.225)","Drug intoxication, which likewise can produce asterixis in a drowsy, confused patient (Harrison 22e p.231)","Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome and alcohol withdrawal in patients with alcohol-associated liver disease (Harrison 22e p.2674)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Careful mental status examination is the central assessment (Harrison 22e p.2629)","Trail-making (connect-the-dots / number-connection) test: normal 15-30 s, markedly prolonged in early HE (Harrison 22e p.2629)","Bedside cognitive tasks such as drawing abstract objects or comparing the patient's signature with prior examples (Harrison 22e p.2629)","Diagnostic paracentesis of ascites to exclude infection as a precipitant (Harrison 22e p.2716)","Evaluate for GI bleeding (Harrison 22e p.2716)","Serum electrolytes (Harrison 22e p.2716)","EEG and visual evoked potentials can detect mild forms but are rarely clinically useful (Harrison 22e p.2629)"],"redFlags":["Encephalopathy appearing during acute hepatitis is the major criterion for acute liver failure and indicates a poor prognosis (Harrison 22e p.2629)","In acute liver failure, severe encephalopathy with cerebral edema can progress to cerebral herniation (Harrison 22e p.2716)","Other presenting features of poor prognosis in acute hepatitis: ascites, peripheral edema, prolonged PT, hypoalbuminemia, hypoglycemia, very high bilirubin (Harrison 22e p.2672)","HE may herald acute-on-chronic liver failure (brain is one of the failing organ systems), which carries 28-day mortality of 20-70% rising with the number of organ failures (Harrison 22e p.2717)"],"prognosis":"Development of hepatic encephalopathy in chronic liver disease is a poor prognostic sign, although the complication can be managed in the vast majority of patients. Hepatic encephalopathy grade is one of the five Child-Pugh variables (with bilirubin, albumin, prothrombin time, and ascites severity) used to stratify cirrhosis into classes A-C of predicted survival. In acute hepatitis, the appearance of encephalopathy defines acute liver failure and indicates poor prognosis (Harrison 22e p.2717, p.2629).","pitfalls":["Ammonia levels are typically elevated but correlate poorly with severity of liver disease; most hepatologists do not rely on ammonia to make the diagnosis or to follow clinical progress (Harrison 22e p.2716).","Always identify and treat a precipitant — in cirrhosis, encephalopathy is usually triggered by volume depletion/dehydration, GI bleeding, infection, constipation, hyponatremia, overdiuresis, uremia, electrolyte imbalance, or sedatives/narcotic analgesics; correcting hydration and electrolytes alone is sometimes sufficient (Harrison 22e p.2629, p.2716).","Asterixis is nonspecific — it also occurs in other metabolic and toxic encephalopathies, so it does not by itself establish HE (Harrison 22e p.230-231).","The diagnosis is clinical and requires an experienced clinician to integrate the various features; there is no single confirmatory test (Harrison 22e p.2716)."],"severityClassification":"Severity is graded clinically along a continuum from the earliest covert/subtle changes (altered sleep, personality change, irritability, mental dullness — detectable mainly by mental-status testing such as the trail-making test) through confusion and disorientation to stupor and coma. Encephalopathy grade is incorporated as one component of the Child-Pugh classification of cirrhosis severity. (Note: Harrison 22e describes this graded clinical spectrum and 'grade' in the Child-Pugh score but the supplied text does not lay out the explicit numbered West Haven criteria; Harrison 22e p.2629, p.2716, p.2715/HCC staging.)","references":["Harrison 22e p.2716","Harrison 22e p.2717","Harrison 22e p.2629","Harrison 22e p.2630","Harrison 22e p.230","Harrison 22e p.231","Harrison 22e p.2674"],"crossLinks":["decomp_cirrhosis","SBP","variceal_bleed","metabolic_enceph"]},"hhs":{"name":"Hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.416, p.3216-3219","clinicalPearls":["Typical patient is an older adult with type 2 diabetes presenting after a several-week prodrome of polyuria, weight loss and falling oral intake, progressing to confusion, lethargy or coma (p.3219).","Examination reflects profound dehydration and hyperosmolality: hypotension, tachycardia and altered mental status (p.3219).","Features classically ABSENT in HHS that are seen in DKA: nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain and Kussmaul respirations (p.3219).","HHS and DKA lie on a continuum of hyperglycaemia; up to one-third of patients have overlapping features of both (p.3216).","Plasma glucose in HHS is usually markedly higher than in DKA and may exceed 55.5 mmol/L (1000 mg/dL) (p.3219).","A debilitating condition (e.g. prior stroke, dementia) or a social situation that limits access to water frequently contributes to HHS by impairing water intake (p.3218-3219).","Worldwide reported cases of both HHS and DKA are rising for reasons that are not clear (p.3216)."],"pathophysiology":"HHS arises from RELATIVE insulin deficiency combined with inadequate fluid intake. Insulin deficiency drives hepatic glucose overproduction (glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis) and impairs glucose uptake by skeletal muscle. The resulting hyperglycaemia causes an osmotic diuresis with intravascular volume depletion that is worsened by inadequate fluid replacement. The reason ketosis is absent is not fully understood; the insulin deficiency is presumed to be only relative and less severe than in DKA, and some studies show lower counterregulatory hormone and free-fatty-acid levels in HHS than in DKA, with possibly reduced hepatic ketone synthesis or an insulin/glucagon ratio that does not favour ketogenesis. Inflammatory markers (cytokines, C-reactive protein) are elevated in both HHS and DKA (p.3217-3219).","additionalDifferentials":["Diabetic ketoacidosis (and the DKA/HHS overlap presentation) (p.3216).","Other causes of an altered/hyperosmolar sensorium - the differential for hyperosmolar coma includes uremia, nonketotic hyperosmolar states and other metabolic derangements (p.3216, general)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma glucose - typically markedly elevated, may be >55.5 mmol/L (1000 mg/dL) (p.3219).","Serum osmolality - characteristically >300 mOsm/L (p.3219).","Corrected serum sodium - measured Na may be normal or low, but corrected Na (add ~1.6 meq/L for each 5.6 mmol/L [100 mg/dL] rise in glucose) is usually elevated (p.3219).","Renal function (BUN/creatinine) - prerenal azotaemia from volume depletion; creatinine typically moderately raised (p.3219).","Ketones/beta-hydroxybutyrate - absent or only mild; serum beta-hydroxybutyrate <1.0 mmol/L; any moderate ketonuria is usually due to starvation (p.3219).","Acid-base status - acidosis absent or mild; a small anion-gap metabolic acidosis may reflect raised lactic acid (p.3219)."],"redFlags":["HHS carries a substantially higher mortality than DKA (up to ~15% in some series), reflecting older patients, more pronounced dehydration and serious precipitating events with comorbidities (p.3219).","Hyperosmolar state must be reversed gradually - too-rapid correction risks worsening neurologic function (p.3219).","Precipitant is often a serious concurrent illness - myocardial infarction, stroke, sepsis, pneumonia or other severe infection - which must be actively sought (p.3219)."],"prognosis":"Even with appropriate treatment, HHS has a substantially higher mortality rate than DKA, reported up to about 15% in some clinical series. The worse outlook reflects that HHS patients are usually older, have more profound fluid losses and dehydration (developed over days to weeks), are more likely to have mental status changes, and more often have a life-threatening precipitating event with accompanying comorbidities (p.3219).","pitfalls":["A normal or low measured serum sodium can be falsely reassuring - it must be corrected for hyperglycaemia, after which it is usually elevated and reveals a profound water deficit (p.3219).","Mild ketonuria may be present but is typically from starvation, not from ketoacidosis - it should not redirect the diagnosis away from HHS (p.3219).","Fever may be absent even when infection is the precipitant; signs of infection should be sought regardless (p.3217, DKA section, applicable to acute hyperglycaemic crises)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not give a graded severity classification specific to HHS (unlike DKA, which is graded mild/moderate/severe by pH, bicarbonate and mental status). HHS is instead defined by marked hyperglycaemia, hyperosmolality (>300 mOsm/L) and the relative absence of ketosis/acidosis, on a continuum with DKA (p.3218-3219).","references":["Harrison 22e Ch.416 p.3216","Harrison 22e Ch.416 p.3217","Harrison 22e Ch.416 p.3218","Harrison 22e Ch.416 p.3219"],"crossLinks":["dka"]},"htn_emergency":{"name":"Hypertensive emergency","system":"Cardiology / Neuro","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2148 (hypertensive urgencies & emergencies), p.2153-2154 (malignant hypertension / arteriolonephrosclerosis), p.190-192 (coma evaluation, PRES/hyperperfusion syndromes), p.227 (hypertensive retinopathy), p.2871 (scleroderma renal crisis)","clinicalPearls":["The threshold prompting concern for a crisis is typically a markedly elevated BP, often described as SBP/DBP at or above ~180/100-120 mmHg; however the level alone does not define an emergency — the presence of acute, ongoing end-organ injury does (p.2148).","Urgency vs emergency is a functional distinction: an asymptomatic patient without evidence of active end-organ damage has an urgency (manageable with oral agents in the outpatient setting), whereas demonstrable acute target-organ injury makes it an emergency requiring IV therapy with close inpatient/ER supervision (p.2148).","Hypertensive urgency is far more common than true emergency; over-treating urgency as if it were an emergency risks harm from excessive BP lowering (p.2148).","Certain syndromes — acute aortic dissection, eclampsia or severe pre-eclampsia, and pheochromocytoma in crisis — flag a need for particularly rapid, aggressive BP control compared with other emergencies (p.2148).","After the acute BP is brought under control, look for secondary causes of hypertension — renovascular disease in particular is relatively common among patients presenting with a hypertensive emergency (p.2148).","Malignant hypertension most often arises in a patient with known, treated hypertension who has stopped taking medications, or who uses vasospastic drugs such as cocaine (p.2153-2154).","In acute hypertensive retinopathy, BP must not be lowered precipitously: an abrupt drop risks optic disc infarction and worsening visual loss (p.227)."],"pathophysiology":"Severe, rapidly progressive BP elevation produces mechanical/pressure-related vascular injury that overwhelms protective vascular tone. In malignant hypertension the characteristic lesion is fibrinoid necrosis of small vessels — breakdown of the vessel wall with deposition of eosinophilic fibrin-containing material and a perivascular cellular infiltrate; larger interlobular arteries may show a hyperplastic 'onionskin' proliferative lesion. These changes can obliterate glomeruli and tubular structures, driving progressive kidney failure. Multiple pathways can generate this pressure injury, including activation of the renin-angiotensin system and intense vasospasm from catecholamine release; when endothelial injury is severe enough it triggers microangiopathic hemolysis. Cerebral involvement reflects failure of cerebral blood-flow autoregulation: at extreme pressures autoregulatory limits are exceeded, producing hyperperfusion, vasogenic edema and the encephalopathy/PRES picture — which is also why too-rapid lowering below the autoregulatory range can cause cerebral or optic ischemia (p.2153-2154, p.192, p.227).","additionalDifferentials":["Scleroderma renal crisis — accelerated hypertension with AKI, clinically indistinguishable from malignant hypertension; consider in systemic sclerosis, especially diffuse/progressive skin disease, anti-RNA-polymerase-III antibody, or recent glucocorticoid exposure (p.2871).","Pheochromocytoma in crisis — catecholamine surge causing paroxysmal severe hypertension (p.2148).","Eclampsia / severe pre-eclampsia in a pregnant or peripartum patient (p.2148)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cocaine or other vasospastic-drug-induced severe hypertension (can precipitate malignant-phase hypertension) (p.2153-2154).","Thrombotic microangiopathies (TTP/HUS) which overlap with the microangiopathic hemolysis seen in malignant hypertension (p.2153-2154)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Funduscopy for hypertensive end-organ change — arteriolar sclerosis/narrowing, splinter (flame) hemorrhages, cotton-wool spots (nerve-fiber-layer infarcts), hard exudates into the macula, and papilledema (p.227, p.190).","CBC with peripheral smear and reticulocytes to detect microangiopathic hemolysis — anemia, schistocytes, reticulocytosis (p.2153-2154).","Urinalysis — hematuria, proteinuria, granular/nephritic sediment in renal involvement (p.2153-2154, p.2871).","Serum creatinine to detect/track acute kidney injury (p.2153-2154).","Once BP is controlled, work-up for secondary hypertension, especially renovascular imaging (p.2148)."],"redFlags":["Hypertensive encephalopathy — headache, visual disturbance, nausea/vomiting, seizures, altered consciousness (p.2148).","Acute left ventricular failure / pulmonary edema in the setting of severe hypertension (p.2148).","Acute kidney failure with severe hypertension (p.2148).","Papilledema, retinal hemorrhages or acute visual loss (signs of malignant-phase / hypertensive retinopathy) (p.2153-2154, p.227).","Features suggesting a rapid-care syndrome: chest/back pain (aortic dissection), pregnancy with seizures (eclampsia), or paroxysmal catecholamine symptoms (pheochromocytoma) (p.2148)."],"prognosis":"Untreated malignant-phase hypertension with target-organ injury (papilledema, declining kidney function) historically carried mortality exceeding 50% over 6-12 months, with older reports of >90% one-year mortality before effective drug therapy — hence the term 'malignant'. With effective BP reduction, manifestations of vascular injury including microangiopathic hemolysis and renal dysfunction can improve over time, and current 5-year survival exceeds 50% (p.2153-2154).","pitfalls":["Treating an asymptomatic high BP (urgency) with aggressive IV agents as though it were an emergency, risking harm from over-rapid lowering (p.2148).","Aiming for immediate normalization of BP — a controlled reduction rather than normalization is sufficient, followed by transition to oral agents; potent IV vasodilators can overshoot and cause hypotension (p.2148).","Lowering BP precipitously in acute hypertensive retinopathy or cerebral involvement, where falling below the autoregulatory range risks optic disc or cerebral infarction (p.227).","Missing a treatable secondary cause (notably renovascular disease) by not investigating after acute control (p.2148).","Overlooking normotensive scleroderma renal crisis — ~10% present with BP in the normal range and carry a poor outcome (p.2871)."],"severityClassification":"Hypertensive urgency = markedly elevated BP (e.g., SBP/DBP >=180/100 mmHg) in an asymptomatic patient WITHOUT evidence of acute ongoing end-organ damage — manage with oral agents as outpatient. Hypertensive emergency = comparably high BP WITH evidence of active end-organ injury (encephalopathy, acute LV failure/pulmonary edema, acute kidney failure, retinal hemorrhage/papilledema, dissection, eclampsia, pheochromocytoma crisis) — requires immediate, carefully supervised IV therapy in ER/inpatient setting. Malignant hypertension is the severe phenotype defined by rapidly progressive BP elevation with fibrinoid-necrosis vascular injury, retinal hemorrhages/papilledema, encephalopathy and declining kidney function (p.2148, p.2153-2154).","references":["Harrison 22e p.190","Harrison 22e p.192","Harrison 22e p.227","Harrison 22e p.2148","Harrison 22e p.2153","Harrison 22e p.2154","Harrison 22e p.2871"],"crossLinks":["aortic_dissection","ich","ischemic_stroke","heart_failure"]},"hypercalcemia":{"name":"Hypercalcaemia of malignancy","system":"Oncology / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Chap 57 (p.362-364), Chap 98 (p.737-738)","clinicalPearls":["Humoral hypercalcaemia of malignancy (HHM) occurs in up to ~20% of cancer patients; the typical picture is a patient with known malignancy found hypercalcaemic on routine labs, though occasionally hypercalcaemia is the presenting feature of an undiagnosed cancer (p.737).","Most malignancy-associated hypercalcaemia runs a relatively acute course, in contrast to the chronic, fluctuating high-normal pattern of primary hyperparathyroidism or occasionally myeloma (p.363).","An elevated PTHrP level confirms HHM and is raised in roughly 80% of hypercalcaemic cancer patients; PTH should be suppressed in true HHM (p.738).","Features favouring HHM over primary hyperparathyroidism: known malignancy, recent onset, and very high serum calcium levels (p.738).","PTHrP shares homology with the first ~13 amino acids of PTH and binds the PTH receptor, explaining the PTH-like biochemical effects on bone and kidney (p.363, 737).","Serum free light chains should be checked when myeloma is suspected, since ~3% of myeloma is missed by serum/urine protein electrophoresis with immunofixation (p.364).","Hypercalcaemia of malignancy is one of the few clinical settings where measuring serum 1,25(OH)2D is genuinely useful — it is elevated or inappropriately normal in lymphoma- and granulomatous-mediated hypercalcaemia (p.364).","Always confirm true hypercalcaemia first by correcting for albumin (add ~0.8 mg/dL to total calcium per 1.0 g/dL fall in albumin below 4.0 g/dL) to exclude pseudohypercalcaemia (p.363)."],"pathophysiology":"Calcium balance is governed by PTH and the vitamin D metabolites 1,25(OH)2D and 25(OH)D acting across parathyroid, kidney, intestine and bone. Hypercalcaemia of malignancy arises mainly through three mechanisms. (1) PTHrP overproduction by solid tumours (squamous head/neck, lung, skin; breast; genitourinary; gastrointestinal) — PTHrP mimics PTH at the PTH receptor, raising bone resorption and renal calcium reabsorption. (2) Local osteolysis from skeletal metastases (breast, myeloma), where metastatic deposits secrete PTHrP and chemokines driving bone breakdown. (3) Excess 1,25(OH)2D production, notably by lymphomas (and granulomatous disease) via macrophage CYP27B1 converting 25(OH)D to active 1,25(OH)2D, enhancing intestinal calcium absorption. PTHrP induction is linked to PTHLH hypomethylation, hedgehog/Gli signalling, TGF-beta, oncogene activation (e.g. Ras) and p53 loss. In all these malignant mechanisms, the high serum calcium activates the parathyroid CaSR and appropriately suppresses endogenous PTH (p.363, 737).","additionalDifferentials":["Primary hyperparathyroidism (the other most common cause of hypercalcaemia; chronic, often with low/low-normal phosphate and inappropriately normal/high PTH) (p.362-364)","Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia (FHH) — autosomal dominant, inactivating CASR mutations; calcium/creatinine clearance ratio <0.01, parathyroid surgery ineffective (p.363)","Multiple myeloma (consider in malignant setting; can also cause chronic hypercalcaemia) (p.363-364)","Granulomatous disease (sarcoidosis, tuberculosis, leprosy, fungal infection, foreign-body reaction) via extrarenal 1,25(OH)2D (p.362-363)","Vitamin D intoxication (excess 25(OH)D or 1,25(OH)2D; CYP24A1 deficiency) (p.362-363)","Thyrotoxicosis, Paget's disease, prolonged immobilisation (increased bone resorption) (p.363)","Milk-alkali syndrome / excess calcium intake or TPN (p.362-363)","Drugs: thiazides, vitamin A, lithium, foscarnet, teriparatide, aromatase inhibitors; rebound resorption after denosumab discontinuation (p.362)","Endocrine causes: adrenal insufficiency, phaeochromocytoma, acromegaly, VIPoma (p.362)","Tertiary hyperparathyroidism; rare ectopic PTH secretion (p.362-363)"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculosis — can cause hypercalcaemia through granulomatous extrarenal 1,25(OH)2D production, mimicking a malignant cause (p.362-363)","Other granulomatous infections (leprosy, fungal infections) via the same macrophage CYP27B1 mechanism (p.362-363)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Primary/tertiary hyperparathyroidism — PTH-driven hypercalcaemia mimicking malignant hypercalcaemia (p.362-364)","Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia — benign mimic where surgery is ineffective (p.363)","Sarcoidosis and other non-infectious granulomatous/foreign-body reactions (p.362-363)","Pseudohypercalcaemia (e.g. calcium-binding IgM in Waldenstrom's macroglobulinaemia, or spuriously high reading from altered albumin binding) (p.362-363)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Intact PTH by two-site assay — the key second test; suppressed in HHM, inappropriately normal/elevated in primary hyperparathyroidism (p.363-364)","PTHrP level — to confirm hypercalcaemia of malignancy when needed (p.364, 738)","Serum phosphate — low/hypophosphataemia with PTHrP and primary hyperparathyroidism; high-normal/elevated with vitamin D-mediated hypercalcaemia (p.363-364, 738)","Serum 25(OH)D and 1,25(OH)2D — 1,25(OH)2D raised/inappropriately normal in lymphoma and granulomatous causes (p.364)","Serum free light chains plus protein electrophoresis with immunofixation to evaluate for myeloma (p.364)","Albumin to calculate corrected calcium (p.363)","Urinary calcium / calcium-creatinine clearance ratio (<0.01 suggests FHH; >0.02 fractional excretion supports pHPT) (p.363)","Genetic testing (CASR, GNA11, AP2S1) if FHH suspected and urine studies inconclusive (p.363)","ECG — may show shortened QTc/ST segments, bradyarrhythmias, AV block, QRS widening, bundle branch block (p.364, 738)"],"redFlags":["Markedly elevated calcium (>14 mg/dL / >3.5 mmol/L) with progressive lethargy, stupor or coma (p.363-364, 737)","Severe GI involvement — nausea, vomiting, anorexia, peptic ulcer or pancreatitis (p.364)","Acute renal failure and volume depletion from hypercalcaemia-induced nephrogenic concentrating defect and polyuria (p.364)","Cardiac conduction abnormalities — bradycardia, AV block, QRS widening, short QTc (p.364)"],"prognosis":null,"pitfalls":["Failing to correct calcium for albumin can produce pseudohypercalcaemia or pseudohypocalcaemia and a false diagnosis (p.363).","FHH may present with mildly elevated PTH and hypercalcaemia and be mistaken for primary hyperparathyroidism — parathyroid surgery is ineffective in FHH and must be excluded (p.363).","Mild hypercalcaemia (10.5-11.9 mg/dL) is often asymptomatic or causes only vague neuropsychiatric or constitutional symptoms, so it may be overlooked (p.363).","Ionised calcium measurement is easily distorted by collection technique (e.g. prolonged tourniquet); corrected total calcium is generally preferable (p.363).","Up to ~3% of myeloma is undetected by serum/urine protein electrophoresis with immunofixation — check free light chains (p.364).","In immobilisation-related hypercalcaemia, an unrecognised underlying driver (pHPT, Paget's, myeloma) may coexist and should be sought, especially in older patients (p.363)."],"severityClassification":"By total serum calcium: mild 10.5-11.9 mg/dL (often asymptomatic); moderate >=12-14 mg/dL; severe >=14 mg/dL (progressive lethargy, stupor or coma, prominent GI symptoms and acute renal failure) (Harrison 22e p.363-364).","references":["Harrison 22e p.362","Harrison 22e p.363","Harrison 22e p.364","Harrison 22e p.737","Harrison 22e p.738"],"crossLinks":["myeloma","malignancy_b","sarcoidosis"]},"hyperkalemia":{"name":"Hyperkalaemia","system":"Metabolic / Nephrology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"401-404","clinicalPearls":["Hyperkalaemia (plasma K+ ≥5.5 mM) occurs in up to 10% of hospitalised patients; severe hyperkalaemia (>6.0 mM) in ~1% and carries significantly increased mortality (p.401).","Reduced renal K+ excretion is the single most common underlying cause; transcellular shift and reduced tissue uptake act mainly as acute contributors (p.401).","Excess dietary intake alone is a rare cause because the kidney can normally upregulate secretion — but small intake increments can provoke severe hyperkalaemia in susceptible patients (e.g. diabetics with hyporeninaemic hypoaldosteronism and CKD); occult sources include K+-containing salt substitutes (p.401-402).","The ECG changes of hyperkalaemia are notoriously INSENSITIVE, particularly in CKD/ESRD patients — a normal ECG does NOT exclude dangerous hyperkalaemia (p.403).","Hyperkalaemia per se impairs renal acid excretion (K+ competes with NH4+ for TALH reabsorption), so it can itself contribute to metabolic acidosis; restoring normokalaemia often corrects this hyperkalaemic acidosis (p.403).","Hyperkalaemia can produce a type I Brugada ECG pattern (pseudo-RBBB with coved ST elevation in precordial leads); the hyperkalaemic Brugada sign is distinguished from genetic Brugada syndrome by absent P waves, marked QRS widening and an abnormal QRS axis (p.403).","A urine Na+ concentration <20 mM signals that low distal Na+ delivery is limiting K+ excretion, suggesting volume repletion or a loop diuretic may help lower K+ (p.403)."],"pathophysiology":"Plasma K+ rises when efflux of intracellular K+ outpaces clearance or when renal excretion falls. Acidaemia drives K+ out of cells via effective K+-H+ exchange to buffer extracellular pH — but this shift is seen mainly with non-anion-gap (mineral) and respiratory acidoses; it does NOT occur with the organic anion-gap acidoses (lactic acidosis, ketoacidosis). Hyperosmolality (mannitol, hypertonic saline, IVIG, hypertonic glucose without insulin in diabetics) shifts K+ out by solvent drag. Cationic amino acids (lysine, arginine, epsilon-aminocaproic acid) and Na+/K+-ATPase inhibitors (digoxin and related cardiac glycosides from yellow oleander, foxglove, cane-toad bufadienolide; fluoride) cause efflux. Succinylcholine depolarises muscle via upregulated acetylcholine receptors (contraindicated after burns, neuromuscular injury, disuse atrophy, prolonged immobilisation). Impaired excretion arises from blockade of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone axis, reduced distal Na+/flow delivery, hyporeninaemic hypoaldosteronism, mineralocorticoid resistance, and reduced functioning nephron mass. Cardiotoxicity reflects altered cardiac membrane excitability: mild rises alter repolarisation (T-wave changes), higher levels depress intracardiac conduction (PR/QRS prolongation, P-wave loss, QRS widening, sine wave). (p.401-403)","additionalDifferentials":["Hyporeninaemic hypoaldosteronism — common in diabetics, the elderly and renal insufficiency; classically suppressed renin and aldosterone, ~50% with associated acidosis (reduced urinary NH4+, positive urinary anion gap, urine pH <5.5) (p.402)","Primary adrenal insufficiency / Addison's disease (autoimmune or polyglandular endocrinopathy) (p.401-402)","Adrenal infiltration (amyloidosis, metastatic cancer) or adrenal haemorrhage/infarction (e.g. antiphospholipid syndrome) (p.402)","Pseudohypoaldosteronism type I (ENaC or mineralocorticoid receptor defects) and type II / hereditary hypertension with hyperkalaemia (WNK1/WNK4, KLHL3, CUL3 mutations) (p.402)","Hereditary hyperkalaemic periodic paralysis (SCN4A mutations) — weakness with K+ intake or rest after exercise (p.403)","Tubulointerstitial disease affecting the distal nephron (amyloidosis, sickle cell, interstitial nephritis, obstructive uropathy) causing hyperkalaemia out of proportion to GFR (p.402)","Tumour lysis syndrome and rhabdomyolysis — release of intracellular K+ from tissue necrosis (p.402)","Massive red-cell transfusion (p.402)"],"infectionMimics":["Adrenal insufficiency from infectious causes (HIV — important cause, usually subclinical and may be unmasked by stress, ketoconazole or steroid withdrawal; cytomegalovirus; tuberculosis; disseminated fungal infection) presenting with hyperkalaemia and hyponatraemia (p.402)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pseudohyperkalaemia (factitious hyperkalaemia) — artefactual K+ release during/after venepuncture from fist clenching, marked thrombocytosis/leukocytosis/erythrocytosis with in vitro efflux, cold storage of the sample, or hereditary red-cell membrane transport defects (e.g. SLC4A1/AE1 mutations) (p.401-402)","Acute anxiety at venepuncture with respiratory alkalosis causing redistributive (true but transient) hyperkalaemia (p.402)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum electrolytes, BUN, creatinine, serum osmolality, Mg2+ and Ca2+, complete blood count (p.403)","Urinary pH, osmolality, creatinine and electrolytes (urine Na+ <20 mM indicates distal Na+ delivery limits K+ excretion) (p.403)","Transtubular K+ gradient (TTKG) as a bedside index of urinary K+ excretion — expected >7-8 in hyperkalaemia; values <8 suggest tubular resistance/low aldosterone (interpret cautiously) (p.403)","Plasma renin activity and aldosterone, with 9-alpha-fludrocortisone response, to characterise hypoaldosteronism (p.403)"],"redFlags":["Hyperkalaemia is a medical emergency because of its cardiac effects — arrhythmias include sinus bradycardia, sinus arrest, slow idioventricular rhythms, VT, VF and asystole (p.402-403).","ECG progression as a severity cue: tall peaked T waves (5.5-6.5 mM) -> loss of P waves (6.5-7.5 mM) -> widened QRS (7.0-8.0 mM) -> sine-wave pattern (>8.0 mM), heralding impending VF/asystole (p.403).","ECG manifestations mandate urgent treatment; but K+ ≥6.5 mM WITHOUT ECG changes should still be managed aggressively, because ECG findings are an unreliable predictor of cardiac toxicity (p.403-404).","Management principle (knowledge): a three-stage sequence — (1) immediately antagonise cardiac effects (IV calcium stabilises the myocardium by raising the action-potential threshold), (2) shift K+ into cells, (3) remove K+ from the body (p.403-404).","Secondary hyperkalaemic paralysis — ascending paralysis that can include diaphragmatic paralysis and respiratory failure (p.403)."],"prognosis":"Severe hyperkalaemia (>6.0 mM) is associated with a significantly increased risk of mortality. Cardiac toxicity can progress rapidly to fatal arrhythmia, but is reversible with prompt treatment; hyperkalaemic metabolic acidosis frequently corrects once normokalaemia is restored. (p.401, p.403)","pitfalls":["Treating a falsely high K+: failing to exclude pseudohyperkalaemia before intervening (haemolysed sample, fist clenching, extreme thrombo/leuko/erythrocytosis, cold storage) (p.401-402).","Relying on a normal/near-normal ECG to exclude dangerous hyperkalaemia — ECG changes are notoriously insensitive, especially in CKD/ESRD (p.403).","Forgetting drug and dietary causes: ACEi/ARB/renin inhibitors and mineralocorticoid-receptor blockers (spironolactone, eplerenone, drospirenone) — additive in combination; K+-sparing diuretics and the structurally similar antibiotics trimethoprim and pentamidine block ENaC; NSAIDs/COX-2 inhibitors, calcineurin inhibitors and beta-blockers cause hyporeninaemic hypoaldosteronism; heparin/LMWH inhibit aldosterone synthesis (p.402).","Drugs blocking the RAA axis also blunt the adrenal aldosterone response to a rising K+, compounding the effect (p.402).","Anion-gap acidoses (lactic acidosis, ketoacidosis) do NOT shift K+ out by the acidosis mechanism, unlike non-anion-gap and respiratory acidoses — so attributing hyperkalaemia to acidosis in these settings can mislead (p.402)."],"severityClassification":"By plasma K+: hyperkalaemia ≥5.5 mM; severe >6.0 mM (markedly increased mortality). ECG-correlated severity bands: peaked T waves 5.5-6.5 mM; loss of P waves 6.5-7.5 mM; widened QRS 7.0-8.0 mM; sine-wave pattern >8.0 mM. Emergency treatment is triggered by any ECG change or K+ ≥6.5 mM. (p.401, p.403-404)","references":["Harrison 22e p.401","Harrison 22e p.402","Harrison 22e p.403","Harrison 22e p.404"],"crossLinks":["aki","ckd"]},"hyperthyroidism":{"name":"Thyrotoxicosis (uncomplicated)","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Chap. 396, p.3032-3038, 3041","clinicalPearls":["Thyrotoxicosis (state of thyroid hormone excess) is NOT synonymous with hyperthyroidism (excessive thyroid gland function); destructive thyroiditis and ingestion of exogenous hormone cause thyrotoxicosis without true hyperthyroidism (p.3032).","Graves' disease accounts for 60-80% of thyrotoxicosis; affects up to 2% of women, ~10x less common in men, typically ages 20-50 (p.3032).","In the elderly, features may be subtle or masked, presenting mainly with fatigue and weight loss ('apathetic thyrotoxicosis'), and may be mistaken for depression (p.3033).","Weight loss occurs despite increased appetite due to raised metabolic rate, but ~5% of patients GAIN weight due to increased food intake (p.3033).","Atrial fibrillation is more common in patients >50 years; treating the thyrotoxic state alone reverts AF to sinus rhythm in up to 75% of those without underlying cardiac disease (p.3033).","Symptoms in descending frequency: hyperactivity/irritability, heat intolerance/sweating, palpitations, fatigue/weakness, weight loss with increased appetite, diarrhoea, polyuria, oligomenorrhoea/loss of libido (p.3033 Table 396-2).","Signs: tachycardia (AF in elderly), fine tremor, goitre, warm moist skin, proximal myopathy, lid retraction/lag (p.3033 Table 396-2).","Fine tremor is best elicited by having the patient stretch out the fingers while feeling the fingertips with the palm (p.3033).","Lid retraction (staring appearance) results from sympathetic overactivity and occurs in ANY form of thyrotoxicosis; it is distinct from the specific eye signs of Graves' ophthalmopathy (p.3033).","Graves' ophthalmopathy specific signs (proptosis, periorbital oedema, conjunctival injection, diplopia) occur in ~one-third of Graves' patients clinically; ~10% of ophthalmopathy is unilateral (p.3033-3034).","Graves' ophthalmopathy can occur in the absence of hyperthyroidism in ~10% (euthyroid ophthalmopathy); onset is within a year before/after thyrotoxicosis diagnosis in 75% (p.3034).","A diffuse, firm (non-nodular) goitre with a thrill or bruit at the inferolateral margins points to Graves' disease (p.3033).","Thyrotoxic hypokalaemic periodic paralysis is a recognised association, particularly common in Asian males (p.3033).","Mild hypercalcaemia occurs in up to 20% of patients (hypercalciuria more common) from thyroid-hormone-driven bone resorption; long-standing disease causes osteopenia and a small increased fracture rate (p.3033)."],"pathophysiology":"Graves' hyperthyroidism is caused by thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins (TSIs, detected as TSH-receptor antibodies/TRAb) that activate the TSH receptor. Susceptibility involves genetic (HLA-DR, CTLA-4, CD25, CD40, PTPN22, TSH-R gene) and environmental factors; concordance is 20-30% in monozygotic vs <5% in dizygotic twins. Triggers/risk factors include stress, smoking (moderate for Graves', major for ophthalmopathy), sudden iodine intake increases, the postpartum period (threefold rise), and immune reconstitution after HAART, alemtuzumab, or immune checkpoint inhibitors. Coexisting lymphocytic thyroiditis and TPO/Tg antibodies (up to 80%) mean there is no direct correlation between TSI level and hormone levels. Ophthalmopathy is driven by cytokine-mediated (IFN-gamma, TNF, IL-1) infiltration of extraocular muscles by activated T cells, fibroblast activation, glycosaminoglycan deposition trapping water, and aberrant IGF-1R signalling; the TSH-R is expressed in orbital tissue (p.3032-3033).","additionalDifferentials":["Graves' disease (60-80% of thyrotoxicosis) (p.3032)","Toxic multinodular goitre (p.3032)","Toxic adenoma / hyperfunctioning solitary nodule (p.3032)","Destructive thyroiditis: subacute (de Quervain) or silent/postpartum thyroiditis - transient thyrotoxic phase from preformed hormone release (p.3037)","Iodine excess (Jod-Basedow phenomenon); amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis in up to 10% (p.3032, 3034)","Thyrotoxicosis factitia (exogenous thyroid hormone ingestion) (p.3032, 3037)","Struma ovarii and functional metastatic follicular carcinoma (ectopic thyroid tissue) (p.3032, 3037)","TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma (secondary hyperthyroidism, inappropriately normal/high TSH) (p.3032, 3037)","Gestational thyrotoxicosis / hCG-secreting tumours (p.3032)","Activating TSH-R mutation; activating GSalpha mutation (McCune-Albright) (p.3032)","Subclinical hyperthyroidism (low TSH, normal free T3/T4) (p.3034, 3041)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Panic / anxiety attacks (p.3034)","Mania (p.3034)","Phaeochromocytoma (p.3034)","Malignancy-associated weight loss (p.3034)","Depression (especially apathetic thyrotoxicosis in the elderly) (p.3033)"],"additionalInvestigations":["TRAb (TSH-receptor antibodies) - confirms Graves' when goitre/ophthalmopathy absent; useful for monitoring pregnant Graves' patients (high TSI crosses placenta -> neonatal thyrotoxicosis) (p.3033-3034)","Radionuclide (99mTc, 123I or 131I) scan and uptake - diffuse high uptake in Graves' vs LOW uptake in destructive thyroiditis, iodine excess, and factitious thyrotoxicosis (p.3034)","Color-flow Doppler ultrasonography - increased flow in hyperthyroidism vs destructive thyroiditis; avoids radioactivity (p.3034)","Unbound (free) T3 - to detect T3 toxicosis (2-5% of patients have only elevated T3) (p.3034)","TPO and thyroglobulin (Tg) antibodies; serum Tg increased in all thyrotoxicosis EXCEPT factitia (low in factitia, high in thyroiditis) (p.3033-3034, 3037)","Pituitary MRI/CT and alpha-subunit if TSH inappropriately non-suppressed (suspect TSH-secreting adenoma) (p.3034, 3037)"],"redFlags":["Optic nerve compression at the orbital apex -> papilloedema, peripheral field defects and permanent vision loss; sight-threatening ophthalmopathy is an emergency (p.3034, 3036)","Corneal exposure/ulceration when lids fail to close during severe proptosis (p.3034)","Atrial fibrillation or worsening angina/heart failure, especially in elderly or pre-existing cardiac disease - high cardiac output can precipitate high-output failure (p.3033)","Features suggesting thyroid storm: fever, delirium, seizures, coma, vomiting, diarrhoea, jaundice (p.3036)"],"prognosis":"Untreated, clinical features generally worsen; historical mortality was 10-30% before effective therapy. Mild Graves' may show spontaneous relapses and remissions, and rarely fluctuates between hypo- and hyperthyroidism with changing TRAb activity. About 15% of treated patients in remission develop hypothyroidism 10-15 years later from the destructive autoimmune process. Ophthalmopathy runs an independent course: typically worsens over 3-6 months, plateaus over 12-18 months, then partially improves (soft-tissue changes especially); fulminant in up to 5% requiring acute intervention. Thyrotoxic crisis carries 4-17% mortality even with treatment (p.3034-3036).","pitfalls":["Do NOT assume thyrotoxicosis means hyperthyroidism - low radionuclide uptake indicates destructive thyroiditis, iodine excess or factitia rather than gland overactivity, which changes management (p.3032, 3037).","T3 toxicosis: 2-5% have suppressed TSH with elevated T3 but normal T4, so a normal T4 does not exclude thyrotoxicosis; conversely iodine-induced T4 toxicosis can show elevated T4 with normal T3 (p.3034).","Diagnostic confusion: thyrotoxicosis can elevate bilirubin, liver enzymes and ferritin, and cause microcytic anaemia and thrombocytopenia (p.3034).","Antithyroid drug agranulocytosis (<1%) is idiosyncratic and abrupt - routine prospective blood-count monitoring is NOT useful; instead warn patients to stop the drug and obtain an urgent FBC for sore throat, fever or mouth ulcers (p.3036).","Suppressed TSH may persist for several months after starting antithyroid drugs, so TSH is not a reliable early index of response - titrate on unbound T4; euthyroidism is usually not reached until 6-8 weeks (p.3035).","Thyroid acropachy/clubbing without coincident dermopathy and ophthalmopathy in a Graves' patient should prompt a search for an alternative cause of clubbing (p.3034).","A normal TSH with normal unbound T4/T3 excludes thyrotoxicosis and excludes Graves' as the cause of a diffuse goitre (p.3034)."],"severityClassification":"Subclinical hyperthyroidism = low TSH with normal free T3 and free T4; treatment is increasingly recommended in the elderly to reduce atrial fibrillation and bone-loss risk (p.3034, 3041). Graves' ophthalmopathy severity is graded by the NO SPECS mnemonic (0 no signs/symptoms; 1 only signs e.g. lid retraction/lag; 2 soft tissue involvement; 3 proptosis >22 mm; 4 extraocular muscle involvement/diplopia; 5 corneal involvement; 6 sight loss), though activity-based systems such as EUGOGO are preferred for monitoring/treatment decisions (p.3034).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3032","Harrison 22e p.3033","Harrison 22e p.3034","Harrison 22e p.3035","Harrison 22e p.3036","Harrison 22e p.3037","Harrison 22e p.3041"],"crossLinks":["thyroid_storm","atrial_fib"]},"hypoglycemia":{"name":"Hypoglycemia","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.204, p.635-636, p.721, p.782, p.1807","clinicalPearls":["Symptoms fall into two groups: autonomic/adrenergic features (tremor, palpitations, anxiety, sweating, hunger, paresthesias) driven by counterregulatory activation, and neuroglycopenic features (fatigue, weakness, dizziness, confusion, behavioral change, cognitive impairment) from impaired neuronal function (p.204).","Hypoglycemia can cause transient loss of consciousness and may mimic syncope or seizure; hunger as a premonitory symptom and absence of immediate reorientation help distinguish it (p.204).","Insulinoma may first be misattributed to psychiatric illness, with symptoms (confusion, irrational behavior, visual difficulty, coma) recognized as neuroglycopenic only in retrospect (p.721).","Distinguish endogenous insulin excess (insulinoma) from surreptitious exogenous insulin use: C-peptide and proinsulin are elevated with insulinoma but suppressed with exogenous insulin injection (p.721).","A non-islet-cell tumor cause is suggested by hypoglycemia with suppressed insulin and C-peptide plus an elevated IGF-II/IGF-I ratio (>10:1) and inappropriately low GH and beta-hydroxybutyrate (p.635-636, p.782)."],"pathophysiology":"Falling glucose triggers counterregulatory autonomic activation (catecholamines) producing adrenergic warning symptoms, while inadequate glucose supply to the brain produces neuroglycopenia. Endogenous hyperinsulinism (insulinoma) drives fasting hypoglycemia with elevated insulin, proinsulin, and C-peptide. Non-islet-cell tumors can cause hypoglycemia chiefly through secretion of incompletely processed IGF-II ('big' IGF-II), which weakly activates insulin receptors and more strongly IGF-I receptors; reduced IGFBP-3 and acid-labile subunit increase IGF-II bioavailability to target tissues, and large tumors may also impair hepatic gluconeogenesis or consume glucose. In severe malaria, hypoglycemia arises from failed hepatic gluconeogenesis combined with increased host (and to a lesser extent parasite) glucose consumption, and may be worsened by quinine-induced insulin secretion (p.204, p.635-636, p.721, p.782, p.1807).","additionalDifferentials":["Insulinoma (endogenous hyperinsulinism) — fasting hypoglycemia with elevated insulin, proinsulin, C-peptide (p.721)","Surreptitious/factitious insulin or sulfonylurea use (suppressed C-peptide with exogenous insulin; serum sulfonylurea assay if suspected) (p.721)","Non-islet-cell tumor hypoglycemia from IGF-II excess (mesenchymal tumors, hepatomas, adrenocortical and other large tumors) (p.635-636, p.782)","Severe liver disease, alcoholism, and poor nutrition causing fasting hypoglycemia (p.721)","Post-gastric-bypass postprandial hypoglycemia (p.721)"],"infectionMimics":["Severe falciparum malaria — hypoglycemia is a common complication and its neurologic effects are indistinguishable from cerebral malaria; usual adrenergic signs (sweating, gooseflesh, tachycardia) are often absent (p.1807)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Syncope and seizure — hypoglycemia-related loss of consciousness can be mistaken for either (p.204)","Psychiatric illness — neuroglycopenic behavioral symptoms of insulinoma can be misdiagnosed as psychiatric disease (p.721)","Pseudohypoglycemia — artifactually low glucose from in vitro glucose consumption by markedly elevated leukocytes (hyperleukocytosis in leukemia, myeloproliferative disease, leukemoid reaction, or after colony-stimulating factor) (p.635-636)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting insulin level (p.721)","Proinsulin level (p.721)","C-peptide level — helps separate endogenous from exogenous insulin (p.721)","Serum/plasma sulfonylurea assay when surreptitious agent use suspected (p.721)","IGF-II level and IGF-II/IGF-I ratio (ratio >10:1 suggestive of non-islet-cell tumor hypoglycemia; standard IGF-II assays may miss precursors) (p.782)","Endoscopic ultrasound for localization of insulinoma (typically small, <2 cm) (p.721)"],"redFlags":["Hypoglycemia complicating severe malaria carries a poor prognosis, especially in children and pregnant women, and the usual warning signs may be absent (p.1807)","Recurrent hypoglycemia blunts counterregulation, causing hypoglycemia unawareness — loss of the warning symptoms that normally prompt correction (p.204)","Coma or seizures from neuroglycopenia indicate severe, potentially irreversible CNS compromise (p.721, p.782)"],"prognosis":"Hypoglycemia is rapidly reversible with glucose, but in severe malaria it independently signals a poor prognosis, particularly in children and pregnant women (p.1807).","pitfalls":["Repeated hypoglycemia produces hypoglycemia unawareness by impairing the counterregulatory response, so the absence of adrenergic warning symptoms does not exclude dangerous hypoglycemia (p.204).","In severe malaria the clinical signs of hypoglycemia (sweating, gooseflesh, tachycardia) are typically absent and its neurologic effects mimic the underlying disease, so it is easily missed without glucose measurement (p.1807).","Failing to measure C-peptide/proinsulin can miss surreptitious exogenous insulin use, which mimics insulinoma (p.721).","Circulating IGF-II may not be overtly elevated in tumor-induced hypoglycemia, so a normal IGF-II level does not exclude it; the IGF-II/IGF-I ratio is more informative (p.635-636, p.782).","A markedly raised leukocyte count can cause artifactual low glucose (pseudohypoglycemia) if the sample is not processed promptly (p.635-636)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.204","Harrison 22e p.635-636","Harrison 22e p.721","Harrison 22e p.782","Harrison 22e p.1807"],"crossLinks":[]},"hyponatremia":{"name":"Symptomatic hyponatraemia / SIADH","system":"Metabolic","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"390-394","clinicalPearls":["Hyponatraemia (plasma Na+ <135 mM) is very common, affecting up to ~22% of hospitalised patients (p.390).","The absolute plasma Na+ concentration says nothing about a patient's volume status, which must be assessed separately for both diagnosis and management (p.390).","Hyponatraemia is almost always driven by excess circulating AVP and/or increased renal sensitivity to AVP combined with free-water intake; the main exception is hyponatraemia from low solute intake (p.390).","Diagnostically subdivide by ECF volume into hypovolaemic, euvolaemic, and hypervolaemic groups based on clinical history and volume status (p.390).","In hypovolaemic patients, a urine Na+ <20 mM predicts a brisk rise in plasma Na+ with isotonic saline, because AVP falls and a water diuresis ensues (p.391).","Serum uric acid helps discriminate: SIAD is typically associated with low uric acid (<4 mg/dL), whereas hypovolaemic hyponatraemia is often hyperuricaemic (p.391, p.393).","Thiazides can mimic SIAD and do not impair urinary concentrating ability, so AVP retains full effect; defer diagnosing SIAD until 1-2 weeks after stopping the thiazide (p.391, p.393).","The 48-hour mark distinguishes acute from chronic hyponatraemia and is decisive for how aggressively/cautiously sodium may be corrected (p.392).","Cerebral salt wasting (a rare hypovolaemic cause linked to intracranial disease) responds to aggressive salt-and-water repletion, so distinguishing it from SIAD is critical because their management diverges (p.391)."],"pathophysiology":"Hyponatraemia reflects a relative excess of body water to sodium, almost always from increased circulating AVP (or heightened renal AVP sensitivity) together with free-water intake. Volume status modulates AVP release: hypovolaemia raises AVP at any given osmolality, and 'hypervolaemic' arterial underfilling (heart failure, cirrhosis) triggers neurohumoral activation that likewise elevates AVP, causing water retention. In hypovolaemia, AVP-mediated V2-receptor water reabsorption plus free-water intake lowers Na+. In hypervolaemic oedematous states (CHF, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome), total-body Na+ rises but water rises proportionally more; reduced arterial filling drives the AVP response. In euvolaemic hyponatraemia, SIAD is the commonest cause: free-water intake persists at osmolalities below the normal thirst threshold, with patients subclinically volume-expanded (so-called 'AVP escape' limits distal tubular transport, preserving a modestly hypervolaemic steady state). Four AVP-secretion patterns occur in SIAD: erratic unregulated secretion (~1/3 of patients), failure to suppress at low osmolality, a 'reset osmostat', and a subset with undetectable AVP (gain-of-function V2-receptor mutations causing 'nephrogenic' SIAD). At the brain level, hyponatraemia causes water to move down the osmotic gradient from hypotonic ECF into cells, producing cellular swelling and cerebral oedema within the rigid skull; acute encephalopathy results when volume-regulatory mechanisms (CSF shunting, efflux of Na+/K+/Cl-) are overwhelmed. In chronic hyponatraemia, brain cells extrude organic osmolytes (creatine, betaine, glutamate, myoinositol, taurine), a process largely complete within 48 h, which mitigates but does not abolish symptoms (p.390-392).","additionalDifferentials":["Hypovolaemic: GI losses (vomiting, diarrhoea, tube drainage) and insensible loss (sweating, burns) with inadequate replacement — urine Na+ typically <20 mM (p.390).","Hypovolaemic renal: thiazide diuretics, mineralocorticoid/aldosterone deficiency (primary adrenal insufficiency, hypoaldosteronism), salt-losing nephropathies (reflux nephropathy, interstitial nephropathies, post-obstructive uropathy, medullary cystic disease, recovery phase of ATN) — urine Na+ typically >20 mM (p.390-391).","Osmotic/solute-driven hypovolaemic: glycosuria, ketonuria (starvation, diabetic or alcoholic ketoacidosis), bicarbonaturia (RTA, metabolic alkalosis) (p.391).","Cerebral salt wasting (subarachnoid haemorrhage, traumatic brain injury, craniotomy, encephalitis, meningitis) (p.391).","Hypervolaemic: congestive heart failure, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome, and acute or chronic renal failure (the latter uniquely with raised urine Na+) (p.391).","Euvolaemic: SIAD, moderate-to-severe hypothyroidism, secondary adrenal insufficiency from pituitary disease (glucocorticoid deficiency) (p.391).","Low solute intake: beer potomania (alcoholics whose sole nutrient is beer) and nutrient-restricted/extreme vegetarian diets — urine osmolality <100-200 mOsm/kg, urine Na+ <10-20 mM (p.391-392).","SIAD causes (Table 56-1): malignancy (especially small-cell lung carcinoma — 75% of malignancy-associated SIAD; ~10% present with Na+ <130 mM), pulmonary disease (pneumonia, TB, abscess, pleural effusion), CNS disease (tumour, subarachnoid haemorrhage, meningitis, encephalitis, head trauma, hydrocephalus), drugs, and transient causes (pain, nausea, stress, endurance exercise, general anaesthesia) (p.391-392).","Pseudohyponatraemia — hyponatraemia coexisting with normal/increased plasma tonicity, from extreme hyperlipidaemia or hyperproteinaemia (p.393).","Hyperglycaemia-related ('true') hyponatraemia — plasma Na+ falls ~1.6-2.4 mM per 100 mg/dL rise in glucose; resolves after correcting hyperglycaemia (p.393)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pseudohyponatraemia from severe hyperlipidaemia/hyperproteinaemia: measured Na+ is low but plasma tonicity is normal or high; exclude by measuring serum osmolality (p.393).","Hyperglycaemic hyponatraemia: a translocational, true hyponatraemia driven by glucose-induced water efflux that corrects once glucose is lowered (p.393)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum osmolality, converted to effective osmolality/tonicity by subtracting urea (in mg/dL divided by 2.8) — true hyponatraemia has effective osmolality <275 mOsm/kg; this also excludes pseudohyponatraemia (p.393).","Serum glucose — to identify and correct hyperglycaemic (translocational) hyponatraemia (p.393).","Serum uric acid — low (<4 mg/dL) in SIAD physiology, often high in volume depletion (p.393).","Serum K+, BUN/creatinine — hyperkalaemia suggests adrenal insufficiency/hypoaldosteronism; raised urea/creatinine suggests renal cause (p.393).","Thyroid, adrenal and pituitary function testing, including a cosyntropin (ACTH) stimulation test to assess primary adrenal insufficiency, in the appropriate setting (p.393).","Urine osmolality — <100 mOsm/kg suggests primary polydipsia; >400 mOsm/kg indicates dominant AVP excess; intermediate values suggest multifactorial physiology (p.393).","Urine Na+ — <20-30 mM suggests hypovolaemia; >30 mM suggests SIAD, but values overlap, especially in the elderly (p.393).","Urine K+ — to calculate the urine-to-plasma electrolyte ratio, useful for predicting response to fluid restriction (p.393).","Demonstration that plasma Na+ corrects after isotonic saline hydration is the diagnostic gold standard for hypovolaemic hyponatraemia (p.393).","Imaging — chest CT in smokers/high-risk patients (screening CXR can miss small-cell lung carcinoma); CNS imaging where a central cause is suspected (p.393)."],"redFlags":["Acute symptomatic hyponatraemia is a medical emergency: nausea/headache/vomiting can rapidly progress to seizures, brainstem herniation, coma and death (p.392).","Acute hyponatraemia can cause normocapnoeic or hypercapnoeic respiratory failure, typically from non-cardiogenic 'neurogenic' pulmonary oedema with normal wedge pressure; resulting hypoxia amplifies neurological injury (p.392).","Premenopausal women are far more likely than men to develop encephalopathy and severe neurological sequelae (p.392).","Overly rapid correction of chronic hyponatraemia (>8-10 mM in 24 h or >18 mM in 48 h) risks osmotic demyelination syndrome (ODS), classically central pontine myelinolysis — presenting days later with para-/quadriparesis, dysphagia, dysarthria, diplopia, 'locked-in' syndrome, or reduced consciousness (p.393).","ODS can occur even with appropriately slow correction in high-risk patients: alcoholism, malnutrition, hypokalaemia, and liver transplantation (p.393).","Plasma Na+ <125 mM is the threshold at which chronic hyponatraemia commonly becomes overtly symptomatic (nausea, vomiting, confusion, seizures) (p.392).","Iatrogenic settings for acute hyponatraemia: postoperative premenopausal women given hypotonic fluids, glycine irrigation (TURP/uterine surgery), colonoscopy prep, recent thiazide initiation (Table 56-2, p.392).","MDMA ('Ecstasy'/'Molly') causes potent thirst and AVP induction leading to severe acute hyponatraemia; exercise-associated (endurance/marathon) hyponatraemia is another acute setting (p.392)."],"prognosis":"Acute symptomatic hyponatraemia carries high risk of severe neurological injury and death; encephalopathy is more severe in premenopausal women. The degree of hyponatraemia is an important prognostic indicator in hypervolaemic states, reflecting the intensity of neurohumoral activation. Chronic hyponatraemia, even when judged 'asymptomatic', is associated with subtle gait and cognitive deficits (reversible with correction), increased risk of falls, and an increased fracture risk due to neurological dysfunction and reduced bone density — so safe correction is warranted even without overt symptoms. Osmotic demyelination from overcorrection can produce permanent disabling neurological deficits (p.392-393).","pitfalls":["Hypovolaemic patients may appear clinically euvolaemic, with only a low urine Na+ to reveal the cause (p.390).","Hyponatraemia is frequently multifactorial, especially when severe — evaluate every source of excess AVP (volume status, drugs, nausea, pain) rather than settling on one (p.393).","Failing to exclude pseudohyponatraemia/hyperglycaemia before correcting; check serum osmolality and glucose first (p.393).","The 93%-water assumption used by ion-sensitive electrodes makes plasma Na+ falsely low in extreme hyperlipidaemia/hyperproteinaemia (p.393).","Urine Na+ values overlap substantially between SIAD and hypovolaemia (particularly in the elderly); the saline-challenge response is the definitive discriminator (p.393).","Diagnosing SIAD in a patient on thiazides — defer the SIAD label until 1-2 weeks after stopping the drug (p.393).","Distinguishing cerebral salt wasting from SIAD is critical because management differs (salt repletion vs fluid restriction) (p.391)."],"severityClassification":"By volume status: hypovolaemic (total body water down, total body Na+ down more), euvolaemic/no oedema (total body water up, Na+ near-normal), and hypervolaemic (total body water up markedly, Na+ up) — Fig. 56-5, p.391. By chronicity: acute hyponatraemia is <48 h (osmolyte extrusion incomplete, higher cerebral-oedema risk) and chronic is >48 h (osmolytes largely extruded, lower acute symptom risk but higher ODS risk on correction), p.392. By tonicity: true hypotonic hyponatraemia has effective osmolality <275 mOsm/kg, distinct from pseudohyponatraemia (normal/high tonicity) and hyperglycaemic translocational hyponatraemia, p.393. Symptomatic threshold for chronic disease is generally plasma Na+ <125 mM (p.392). SIAD diagnostic features include euvolaemic hypotonic hyponatraemia, inappropriately concentrated urine (urine osmolality typically >400 mOsm/kg / not maximally dilute), urine Na+ >30 mM, low serum uric acid (<4 mg/dL), and absence of thyroid/adrenal dysfunction, diuretics, hypovolaemia or hypervolaemia (p.391, p.393).","references":["Harrison 22e p.390","Harrison 22e p.391","Harrison 22e p.392","Harrison 22e p.393","Harrison 22e p.394"],"crossLinks":["MENINGITIS"]},"hypovolemic_shock":{"name":"Hypovolemic / haemorrhagic shock","system":"Critical care","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.314 (Approach to the Patient with Shock) pp.2309-2315; Ch.311 pp.2335-2337; Ch.106 (Anemia Due to Acute Blood Loss) pp.850-851","clinicalPearls":["Hypovolemic shock is haemodynamically a low-output state: reduced cardiac output and oxygen delivery driven by low preload, with a compensatory rise in systemic vascular resistance and low CVP and PCWP from depleted intravascular volume (p.2311).","Divide causes into haemorrhagic (external/trauma or internal, most often upper or lower GI bleeding) and non-haemorrhagic fluid loss (profuse vomiting/diarrhoea; renal losses such as osmotic diuresis in DKA or diabetes insipidus; skin losses from severe burns or Stevens-Johnson syndrome) (p.2311).","On examination it presents as a cold, low-output shock: cool/clammy peripheries, narrow pulse pressure, delayed capillary refill and a reduced JVP (<8 cm) - the heart is 'empty' - in contrast to the warm peripheries and wide pulse pressure of distributive shock (pp.2312-2313, p.2335).","Tachycardia and tachypnoea are early compensatory signs, but hypotension may be a late finding; younger patients in particular can compensate for an extended period before blood pressure falls (pp.2312-2313).","Signs of vascular instability appear once roughly 25% of blood volume is lost; the usual sympathetic compensation (tachycardia, vasoconstriction, regional flow shift) fails beyond ~30% loss, and loss of >40% (>2 L in an average adult) produces overt shock (confusion, dyspnoea, diaphoresis, hypotension, tachycardia) (pp.850-851).","Among hypovolemic causes, haemorrhage is the most common; non-haemorrhagic GI, renal and skin fluid losses are also recognised causes (p.2311)."],"pathophysiology":"Hypovolemic shock arises when a fall in intravascular volume reduces preload, lowering stroke volume and cardiac output and therefore systemic oxygen delivery; CVP and PCWP are low and systemic vascular resistance rises as a compensatory response (p.2311). Inadequate oxygen delivery forces cells into anaerobic metabolism, so pyruvate is converted to lactate with markedly less ATP generation; failure of ATP-dependent ion pumps disrupts ionic and pH homeostasis, and calcium influx activates phospholipases/proteases causing cell swelling and death, with leaked intracellular contents driving inflammation and microvascular injury (p.2310). In acute haemorrhage, hypovolaemia dominates first and threatens high-flow organs (brain, kidneys); the haemoglobin is initially normal and only falls over hours as fluid shifts from the extravascular to the intravascular space (haemodilution), so the haematocrit does not reflect the volume lost early on (pp.850-851).","additionalDifferentials":["Distributive shock (e.g. sepsis, anaphylaxis, neurogenic, adrenal crisis) - high cardiac output, low SVR, warm peripheries (pp.2310-2311)","Cardiogenic shock - low output but with elevated CVP/PCWP and raised JVP (p.2311, p.2313)","Obstructive shock (tension pneumothorax, cardiac tamponade, pulmonary embolism, aortic dissection) - low output with raised filling pressures/JVP (p.2311)","Mixed shock - more than one mechanism coexisting (e.g. hypovolemic plus septic) (p.2311)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cardiogenic shock - shares cool peripheries and narrow pulse pressure (low output) but is distinguished by raised JVP, peripheral oedema, an S3/S4 gallop and pulmonary crackles, indicating high filling pressures rather than volume depletion (p.2313, pp.2336-2337)","Obstructive shock - low-output state but typically with an elevated JVP; tamponade (pulsus paradoxus, raised JVP, low-voltage ECG with electrical alternans) and tension pneumothorax (absent breath sounds, tracheal deviation) mimic the hypoperfusion of hypovolemia (p.2313)","Distributive shock unmasked after fluid loss (e.g. adrenal insufficiency with aldosterone-deficient hypovolaemia) (pp.2310-2311)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial serum lactate for diagnosis, risk stratification and to gauge response to resuscitation - elevated lactate/lactic acidosis correlates with worse outcome (p.2314)","Haemoglobin/haematocrit - reduced in haemorrhagic loss, but may be normal early in active bleeding before haemodilution occurs (pp.2313, 850-851)","Coagulation profile (PT, PTT, INR) as part of the undifferentiated shock workup, relevant before/around transfusion (p.2313)","Arterial or venous blood gas to quantify the degree of metabolic acidosis (p.2313)","Urine electrolytes with FENa/FEUrea, which may indicate hypovolaemia or reduced effective circulating volume (p.2313)","Point-of-care ultrasound (RUSH/ACES/SESAME protocols): a collapsible IVC at end-expiration in a spontaneously breathing patient and focused views of abdomen/pelvis suggest reduced intravascular volume and can identify intra-abdominal haemorrhage (pp.2314, 2336-2337)","Dynamic fluid-responsiveness assessment - passive leg raise, or >12% stroke-volume/pulse-pressure variation in suitable mechanically ventilated patients - to predict response to volume (p.2315)","Pregnancy test (to detect ruptured ectopic pregnancy as a haemorrhagic source) (p.2313, p.2310)","Localising signs of occult internal bleed: Grey Turner sign (flank ecchymosis) and Cullen sign (umbilical ecchymosis) suggest retro-/intraperitoneal haemorrhage; rectal exam may reveal GI bleeding (pp.851, 2313)"],"redFlags":["Overt features of severe blood-volume loss (>40%): confusion, dyspnoea, diaphoresis, hypotension and tachycardia indicating critical vital-organ hypoperfusion needing immediate volume replacement (pp.850-851)","New altered mental status or new oliguria (<0.5 mL/kg/h) - signs the compensatory mechanisms have been overwhelmed (decompensated shock) (p.2312)","Occult bleeding sites - retroperitoneal haemorrhage, long-bone fracture haematoma, intra-abdominal/intrapleural bleeding - that may not be externally apparent (pp.851, 2315)","Elevated shock index (HR/SBP >0.9) flagging critical bleeding and likely transfusion need in haemorrhagic shock (p.2313)"],"prognosis":"Hypovolemic shock carries a lower mortality than septic or cardiogenic shock, which have the highest death rates among shock types (p.2311). Organ dysfunction in early (compensated) shock is often reversible if perfusion and oxygen delivery are restored, but untreated shock progresses through a decompensated phase to an irreversible phase with permanent multisystem organ dysfunction and death (pp.2311-2312).","pitfalls":["Compensated shock can present with a normal blood pressure: relying on an absolute SBP/MAP threshold misses early shock, since young patients may maintain pressure with tachycardia, narrow pulse pressure and only a mildly raised lactate before decompensating (pp.2312-2313).","Do not be falsely reassured by tachycardia without hypotension, nor exclude shock because the heart rate is normal - patients on nodal-blocking drugs or with conduction disease may have a blunted or absent tachycardic response (pp.2312-2313).","A normal initial haemoglobin/haematocrit does NOT exclude significant active haemorrhage; values fall only later as haemodilution develops, and the haematocrit does not reflect the true volume lost early on (pp.2313, 850-851).","Misclassifying low-output shock: an elevated JVP, peripheral oedema, gallop or crackles point to cardiogenic/obstructive rather than hypovolemic shock and warn against indiscriminate large-volume resuscitation (pp.2313, 2336-2337).","Over-resuscitation is harmful - targeted, reassessed fluid therapy is the goal rather than maximal volume (pp.2336-2337).","Estimates of external blood loss at a bloody scene are often overestimated, while large internal bleeds may be clinically inapparent - examine carefully for localising signs (p.851)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames severity along the shock continuum (compensated/pre-shock to decompensated to irreversible) (pp.2311-2312) and, for haemorrhage, by the fraction of blood volume lost rather than an ATLS Class I-IV table (not present in this source): vascular instability emerges at about 25% loss; compensation fails beyond ~30% (postural hypotension and tachycardia); and loss of >40% (>2 L in an average adult) produces overt hypovolemic shock with confusion, dyspnoea, diaphoresis, hypotension and tachycardia (pp.850-851). The shock index (HR/SBP; normal 0.5-0.7) is a bedside severity marker, with values >0.9 indicating critical bleeding and likely transfusion need (p.2313).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2309","Harrison 22e p.2310","Harrison 22e p.2311","Harrison 22e p.2312","Harrison 22e p.2313","Harrison 22e p.2314","Harrison 22e p.2315","Harrison 22e p.2335","Harrison 22e p.2336","Harrison 22e p.2337","Harrison 22e p.850","Harrison 22e p.851"],"crossLinks":["cardiogenic_shock","SEPTIC_SHOCK","variceal_bleed","aaa"]},"ibd_flare":{"name":"Inflammatory bowel disease flare","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.337 (Inflammatory Bowel Disease), p.2551-2564","clinicalPearls":["UC inflammation is continuous from the rectum proximally without skip areas, whereas Crohn's is segmental with skip lesions and frequently spares the rectum; this distribution difference is a key way to separate the two (p.2555-2556).","Granulomas (noncaseating, in all bowel-wall layers) are characteristic of Crohn's but are found less often on mucosal biopsy than on surgical resection specimens, so their absence does not exclude Crohn's (p.2556).","On biopsy, distorted/branching crypt architecture and basal plasmacytosis/lymphoid aggregates indicate chronicity and help distinguish IBD from acute self-limited or infectious colitis (p.2556).","Faecal calprotectin and lactoferrin correlate with histologic inflammation, help predict relapse, and can detect pouchitis; they are used to distinguish active inflammation from IBS or bacterial overgrowth (p.2556-2557).","Distinguishing UC from Crohn's is not possible initially in up to ~15% of cases (indeterminate colitis), though the true type usually declares itself later (p.2559).","Only ~15% of UC patients present initially with severe disease (p.2557).","Distal/proctitis-limited UC can paradoxically present with constipation due to slowed proximal transit despite distal inflammation (p.2557).","In severely ill UC patients serum albumin falls quickly; leukocytosis can occur but is not a specific marker of disease activity (p.2557)."],"pathophysiology":"IBD is a chronic idiopathic inflammatory disease of the GI tract regarded as a dysregulated, inappropriate immune response to the endogenous commensal microbiota (with or without an autoimmune component) in a genetically susceptible host. Homeostasis among the gut microbiota, intestinal epithelial cells, and mucosal immune system is normally maintained by regulatory pathways (e.g., FoxP3 T-regulatory cells); environmental factors (smoking, antibiotics, enteropathogens, diet) acting on genetic susceptibility (>240 GWAS loci, ~20% of risk explained; rare single-gene defects such as IL10/IL10R in very-early-onset disease) disrupt this balance and produce sustained inflammation. UC is a mucosal/superficial-submucosal disease, while Crohn's is transmural with fissuring and fistula/stricture formation (p.2551-2556).","additionalDifferentials":["Indeterminate colitis (UC vs Crohn's cannot be distinguished early in up to 15% of cases) (p.2559)","Diverticulitis / diverticular-associated colitis (mimics Crohn's clinically and radiographically) (p.2560)","Ischaemic colitis (consider in the elderly, hypercoagulable states, post-AAA repair; abrupt left lower quadrant pain with red rectal bleeding and a normal rectum with a sharp transition) (p.2560-2561)","Radiation colitis/enteritis (p.2561)","Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome (p.2561)","Collagenous and lymphocytic (microscopic) colitis - normal endoscopic appearance, chronic watery diarrhoea (p.2561)","NSAID-related colitis (de novo, or reactivation of IBD) (p.2561)","Immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI)-related colitis (p.2561)","Diversion colitis (p.2561)","Behcet's syndrome, graft-versus-host disease, eosinophilic gastroenteritis, neutropenic colitis (p.2560)","Neoplastic: lymphoma, colorectal carcinoma, carcinoid (p.2560)"],"infectionMimics":["Clostridioides difficile colitis (watery diarrhoea, tenesmus, nausea, vomiting) (p.2560)","Campylobacter colitis (can mimic severe UC endoscopically and can trigger a relapse of established UC) (p.2559)","Salmonella (watery or bloody diarrhoea) (p.2559)","Shigella (watery diarrhoea then tenesmus and bloody mucoid stool) (p.2559)","Yersinia enterocolitica (terminal ileum involvement mimicking Crohn's ileitis) (p.2559-2560)","Enterohaemorrhagic / enteroinvasive / enteroadherent E. coli (bloody diarrhoea) (p.2560)","Cytomegalovirus colitis (abdominal pain, bloody diarrhoea, fever, weight loss; can occur in immunocompetent hosts; severe disease can cause necrosis and perforation; diagnosed by intranuclear inclusions on biopsy) (p.2560)","Herpes simplex proctitis (anorectal/perianal; can occur in immunocompetent individuals) (p.2560)","Gastrointestinal tuberculosis / Mycobacterium avium (distal ileal and cecal disease mimicking Crohn's) (p.2560)","Entamoeba histolytica (amoebic colitis; fulminant form mortality >50%) (p.2560-2561)","Other parasites: Isospora belli, hookworm, Trichuris trichiura, Strongyloides (p.2561)","Gonorrhoea, Chlamydia trachomatis, syphilis (proctitis) (p.2560)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Diverticulitis (p.2560)","Ischaemic colitis (p.2560-2561)","Radiation colitis/enteritis (p.2561)","NSAID-induced colitis (p.2561)","Immune checkpoint inhibitor colitis (p.2561)","Collagenous / lymphocytic (microscopic) colitis (p.2561)","Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome (p.2561)","Diversion colitis (p.2561)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Acute-phase reactants - CRP, platelet count and ESR rise; haemoglobin falls in active disease (p.2556-2557)","Serum albumin (falls quickly in severe illness) (p.2557)","Faecal lactoferrin (sensitive/specific marker of intestinal inflammation) (p.2556)","Sigmoidoscopy to assess activity before treatment; full colonoscopy (deferred during an acute flare) to assess extent and activity, with multiple biopsies including from normal-appearing mucosa (p.2556-2557)","Histology of rectal/colonic biopsies (p.2557)","Intestinal ultrasound to assess activity and mucosal healing (p.2557)","Surveillance colonoscopy with biopsies (chromoendoscopy in higher-risk patients) for dysplasia/cancer in long-standing colitis (p.2570)"],"redFlags":["Toxic megacolon - transverse or right colon dilated >6 cm with loss of haustration during a severe attack; surgical emergency, can be precipitated by electrolyte abnormalities and narcotics (p.2557)","Perforation - the most dangerous local complication; signs of peritonitis may be masked by glucocorticoids; mortality of perforation complicating toxic megacolon is ~15% (p.2557)","Toxic colitis with deep ulceration - bowel may perforate without first dilating (p.2557)","Massive haemorrhage (occurs in ~1% of UC patients) (p.2557)","High-spiking fever in Crohn's suggesting intraabdominal abscess (p.2557)","Colonic stricture that prevents colonoscope passage - presume malignant until proven otherwise (indication for surgery) (p.2557)"],"prognosis":"Toxic megacolon occurs rarely; about 50% of acute dilations resolve with conservative management, but those that do not require urgent colectomy. Perforation complicating toxic megacolon carries ~15% mortality. Risk of colorectal cancer rises with duration and extent of colitis (historically ~2% at 10 yr, 8% at 20 yr, 18% at 30 yr, though rates have fallen with better inflammation control and surveillance; still ~1.5-2x the general population). Extensive long-standing colitis carries the highest cancer risk. Strictures occur in ~5% of UC patients. Up to one-third of IBD patients develop at least one extraintestinal manifestation (p.2557-2563, p.2570).","pitfalls":["Before escalating immunosuppression in a presumed flare, exclude superimposed infection - C. difficile and CMV colitis can mimic or complicate IBD; diagnosis requires negative stool studies (culture, C. difficile toxin, ova and parasites) and, for CMV, identification of intranuclear inclusions on mucosal biopsy (p.2557, p.2560).","A first 'flare' of bloody diarrhoea may actually be an infectious colitis (e.g., Campylobacter, Salmonella, Shigella, C. difficile, amoebic) - infection should be excluded before diagnosing IBD; Campylobacter and others can also reactivate established UC (p.2559-2560).","Granulomas are frequently absent on mucosal biopsy even in Crohn's, so their absence does not rule it out (p.2556).","Effective medical therapy can normalise mucosal appearance and create apparent skip areas in UC, mimicking Crohn's distribution; biopsy normal-appearing mucosa (p.2555-2556).","Extensive perianal lesions in a patient labelled UC should raise suspicion for Crohn's instead (p.2557).","In patients on glucocorticoids, peritoneal signs of perforation may be blunted/absent (p.2557).","An impassable colonic stricture should be presumed malignant rather than benign inflammatory (p.2557)."],"severityClassification":"Montreal classification of UC severity (Harrison Table 337-4, p.2557): S0 clinical remission (no symptoms); S1 mild = up to 4 stools/day +/- blood, no systemic illness, normal ESR; S2 moderate = 4 or more stools/day with minimal systemic toxicity; S3 severe = 6 or more bloody stools/day plus pulse >=90/min, temperature >=37.5 C, haemoglobin <10.5 g/dL, and ESR >=30 mm/h. (These severe-disease physiologic thresholds mirror the classic Truelove-Witts criteria for acute severe colitis.) Montreal extent: E1 proctitis, E2 left-sided (distal to splenic flexure), E3 extensive/pancolitis. Crohn's is categorised by the Vienna/Montreal classification (Table 337-5, p.2558) by age at diagnosis (A), location ileal/colonic/ileocolonic/upper (L), and behaviour nonstricturing-nonpenetrating/stricturing/penetrating (B) with a perianal modifier.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2551","Harrison 22e p.2552","Harrison 22e p.2553","Harrison 22e p.2555","Harrison 22e p.2556","Harrison 22e p.2557","Harrison 22e p.2558","Harrison 22e p.2559","Harrison 22e p.2560","Harrison 22e p.2561","Harrison 22e p.2562","Harrison 22e p.2563","Harrison 22e p.2564","Harrison 22e p.2570"],"crossLinks":["C_DIFF","DYSENTERY","GASTROENTERITIS"]},"ibs":{"name":"Irritable bowel syndrome","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"2571-2575","clinicalPearls":["Rome IV makes IBS a positive symptom-based diagnosis, not purely one of exclusion: recurrent abdominal pain on average at least 1 day/week over the prior 3 months plus at least two of being related to defecation, a change in stool frequency, or a change in stool form, with onset at least 6 months earlier.","Rome IV is stricter than Rome III: it requires pain at least weekly and dropped 'discomfort' as a qualifying symptom; painless diarrhoea or constipation alone does NOT meet criteria.","Abdominal pain is mandatory for the diagnosis; it is typically episodic and crampy, often worsened by eating or emotional stress and relieved by passing flatus or stool.","Subtype by predominant stool form: IBS-C (constipation), IBS-D (diarrhoea), and IBS-M (mixed) — but subtypes are unstable, with about three-quarters of patients changing subtype and ~29% switching between IBS-C and IBS-D over a year.","Onset is usually before age 45 and IBS is two to three times more common in women, who make up ~80% of severe cases.","Symptoms commonly overlap with other functional disorders (fibromyalgia, headache, backache, genitourinary symptoms) and with functional dyspepsia, suggesting a shared, more extensive digestive disorder.","Bloating and a subjective sense of excess gas are common, but most patients actually generate a normal volume of intestinal gas and instead have impaired gas transit/tolerance and proximal reflux of gas.","Malnutrition is exceedingly rare in IBS and the pain almost always occurs only during waking hours, so sleep disruption from pain is unusual."],"pathophysiology":"IBS is a multifactorial functional bowel disorder with no structural lesion. Proposed mechanisms include altered GI motor activity (e.g., exaggerated postprandial and distention-evoked colonic contractions, increased high-amplitude propagating contractions with rapid transit in IBS-D), visceral hypersensitivity (lower thresholds to rectal balloon distention with sparing of somatic pain pathways), and disordered brain-gut interaction with central neural dysregulation (greater activation of mid-cingulate and prefrontal cortices on functional imaging). Psychological factors (anxiety, depression, somatization, prior physical/sexual abuse) modulate pain thresholds, and genetic factors such as serotonin transporter (SERT) polymorphisms are implicated. Peripheral contributors include low-grade mucosal immune activation with mast cells, lymphocytes and proinflammatory cytokines (IL-6, IL-1beta, TNF), upregulated TRPV1 sensory channels, increased intestinal permeability ('leaky gut', chiefly in IBS-D and post-infectious IBS), gut dysbiosis (reduced microbial diversity, fewer Bifidobacterium/Faecalibacterium, more Enterobacteriaceae), abnormal serotonin pathways with increased enterochromaffin-cell serotonin in IBS-D, and bile acid malabsorption in up to ~25% of IBS-D.","additionalDifferentials":["Inflammatory bowel disease (ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease)","Coeliac sprue","Colorectal carcinoma","Microscopic colitis (lymphocytic/collagenous)","Lactase deficiency","Bile acid diarrhoea","Small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth","Diverticular disease of the colon","Gastroparesis or partial intestinal obstruction (postprandial pain with nausea/vomiting and bloating)","Hyperthyroidism and hypothyroidism","Hypoparathyroidism","Laxative abuse","Drug-induced constipation (anticholinergic, antihypertensive, antidepressant agents)","Acute intermittent porphyria and lead poisoning (painful constipation mimic)","Biliary tract disease, peptic ulcer disease, intestinal ischaemia, and gastric or pancreatic carcinoma (for epigastric/periumbilical pain)"],"infectionMimics":["Giardia lamblia or other intestinal parasitic infestation","Infectious diarrhoea","Small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Coeliac disease","Lactase deficiency","Microscopic colitis","Bile acid malabsorption","Inflammatory bowel disease","Hyperthyroidism / hypothyroidism"],"additionalInvestigations":["Complete blood count for most patients","Fecal calprotectin or fecal lactoferrin, and/or C-reactive protein, in IBS-D even without alarm features to help exclude IBD","Serologic testing for coeliac disease in IBS-D where population prevalence is >=1%","Stool examination for ova and parasites in IBS-D when parasitic infection is endemic or suggested by history (e.g., untreated water)","Colonoscopy when alarm features are present, symptom onset is after age 40, or symptoms fail to respond to standard therapy","Random colonic biopsies to exclude microscopic colitis if diarrhoea is persistent/chronic","Hydrogen breath test (or trial of a 3-week lactose-free diet) for suspected lactase deficiency","Glucose hydrogen breath test for suspected small-bowel bacterial overgrowth with excessive gas/bloating","Gallbladder ultrasonography for postprandial right-upper-quadrant pain","Upper GI radiographs or upper endoscopy when dyspeptic symptoms coexist"],"redFlags":["First appearance of symptoms in old age","Progressive course from the time of onset","Rectal bleeding","Persistent diarrhoea after a 48-hour fast","Nocturnal diarrhoea","Steatorrhoeal stools","Anaemia, elevated sedimentation rate, or leukocytes/blood in stool","Stool volume >200-300 mL/day","Family history of colorectal malignancy (lowers threshold for thorough evaluation)"],"prognosis":null,"pitfalls":["Treating IBS as a diagnosis of exclusion only and over-investigating: few tests are needed when symptoms are typical and alarm features are absent, and unnecessary investigations may be costly and even harmful.","Missing organic disease in higher-risk patients: older age, rapidly progressive symptoms, or alarm features should prompt a thorough work-up to exclude IBD, coeliac disease and colorectal cancer rather than labelling as IBS.","Attributing bloating to excess gas when most patients produce a normal gas volume but have impaired gas transit.","Over-interpreting an abnormal hydrogen breath test, which can be positive simply from rapid small-bowel transit and does not reliably predict treatment response."],"severityClassification":"Harrison classifies IBS by predominant bowel pattern under Rome IV into IBS-C (predominant constipation), IBS-D (predominant diarrhoea), and IBS-M (mixed constipation and diarrhoea); these subtypes are noted to be unstable over time. Overall symptom severity ranges from mild to severe with significant quality-of-life impairment, but no formal graded severity score is specified.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2571","Harrison 22e p.2572","Harrison 22e p.2573","Harrison 22e p.2574","Harrison 22e p.2575"],"crossLinks":["ibd_flare","GASTROENTERITIS"]},"ich":{"name":"Intracerebral hemorrhage","system":"Neurology / Vascular","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.439, p.3452-3456","clinicalPearls":["ICH typically presents as abrupt-onset focal deficit; compared with ischaemic stroke, headache is more common, but imaging is still required to distinguish the two (p.3453).","Putaminal haemorrhage damages the adjacent internal capsule, so contralateral hemiparesis is the sentinel sign, often with the eyes deviated away from the side of the weakness (p.3454).","Thalamic haemorrhage produces a prominent all-modality contralateral sensory deficit and characteristic ocular signs from inferior extension into the upper midbrain (e.g. eyes deviated down-and-in 'looking at the nose', poorly reactive unequal pupils, vertical-gaze palsy) (p.3454).","Pontine haemorrhage causes rapid deep coma with quadriplegia, decerebrate rigidity and reactive 'pinpoint' (~1 mm) pupils (p.3454).","Cerebellar haemorrhage classically gives occipital headache, repeated vomiting and gait ataxia; in mild cases gait ataxia may be the only sign (p.3454).","Lobar haemorrhage symptoms mirror the lobe involved: occipital → hemianopia, left temporal → aphasia and confusion, parietal → hemisensory loss, frontal → arm weakness (p.3454).","Seizures are uncommon at presentation but occur in roughly 6-15% of patients within the first 3 days (p.3453).","Lumbar puncture is generally unnecessary and should usually be avoided in ICH because focal signs and raised ICP make it potentially herniation-inducing (p.3455).","ICH accounts for about 10% of all strokes, with disproportionately high incidence in Asian and Black populations (p.3453)."],"pathophysiology":"Hypertensive ICH usually results from spontaneous rupture of a small (~30-100 microns) penetrating artery deep in the brain, with chronic hypertension producing vascular injury preferentially at the basal ganglia (especially putamen), thalamus, cerebellum and pons. The clot may stay small or enlarge to compress adjacent tissue and cause herniation; dissection into the ventricles markedly increases morbidity and can produce hydrocephalus. Most hypertensive bleeds evolve over 30-90 minutes, whereas anticoagulant-related bleeds may keep expanding for 24-48 h. By ~48 h macrophages begin phagocytosing the haemorrhage at its surface, and over 1-6 months it resolves to a slit-like glial-scarred cavity with haemosiderin-laden macrophages. In cerebral amyloid angiopathy, arteriolar degeneration with amyloid deposition in cortical vessel walls causes single and recurrent lobar haemorrhages, the commonest cause of lobar bleeding in the elderly (p.3453-3454).","additionalDifferentials":["Hypertensive deep ICH (putamen, globus pallidus, thalamus, cerebellar hemisphere, pons) (p.3453)","Lobar ICH from cerebral amyloid angiopathy (degenerative, elderly, associated with dementia, rare <60 years) (p.3453)","Anticoagulant- or coagulopathy-related ICH (any site, often lobar or subdural; high risk of haematoma expansion) (p.3453-3455)","Sympathomimetic drug-related ICH (cocaine, amphetamine/methamphetamine) - a leading cause in patients under 45 (p.3453-3454)","Haemorrhage into a brain tumour (choriocarcinoma, melanoma, renal cell, thyroid, lung, hepatocellular metastases; glioblastoma, pilocytic astrocytoma, medulloblastoma) (p.3453,3455)","Vascular malformations: arteriovenous malformation, cavernous angioma, capillary telangiectasia, dural arteriovenous fistula (p.3453)","Ruptured intracranial aneurysm (mycotic or non-mycotic) with intraparenchymal extension (p.3453)","Haemorrhagic transformation of a prior ischaemic infarct (p.3453)","Venous/dural sinus thrombosis causing venous infarction with haemorrhage (e.g. vein of Labbe occlusion → posterior temporal/inferior parietal haematoma) (p.3453,3455)","Vasculitis (e.g. polyarteritis nodosa, lupus) and haematologic disorders (leukaemia, aplastic anaemia, thrombocytopenic purpura) (p.3455)","Hypertensive encephalopathy with haemorrhage; primary intraventricular haemorrhage; Moyamoya disease (p.3455)"],"infectionMimics":["Mycotic aneurysm rupture (p.3453)","Sepsis-associated petechial white-matter haemorrhages (p.3455)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Ischaemic stroke (clinically overlapping; differentiated by CT) (p.3453)","Traumatic intracranial haemorrhage (intraparenchymal temporal/inferior frontal, subarachnoid, subdural, epidural) - consider in any unexplained acute deficit, especially after a fall (p.3455)","Hypertensive encephalopathy / posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome - headache, vomiting, seizures, confusion with posterior-predominant brain oedema; favoured over hypertensive ICH when there is a prodrome and altered mental status before any bleed (p.3455)","Brain tumour presenting with haemorrhage (p.3455)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Non-contrast CT head - highly sensitive/specific for acute intracranial blood and defines location; preferred for acute stroke evaluation (p.3453,3455)","Platelet count, prothrombin time, partial thromboplastin time and INR to detect coagulopathy (p.3455)","CT angiography or post-contrast CT to seek a 'spot sign' (focal enhancement within the haematoma indicating ongoing bleeding) (p.3455)","MRA, CTA or conventional catheter angiography when the cause is uncertain - particularly in young or non-hypertensive patients or atypical (non-deep) haematoma location (p.3455)","Hemosiderin-sensitive MRI (susceptibility-weighted imaging) to detect cortical microbleeds suggesting cerebral amyloid angiopathy (p.3454)","MRI for posterior fossa lesions (more sensitive than CT but not usually needed for primary diagnosis); amyloid-beta PET can image CAA non-invasively (p.3454-3455)"],"redFlags":["'Spot sign' on CTA/post-contrast CT - associated with increased haematoma expansion, higher mortality and lower likelihood of favourable outcome (p.3455)","Haematoma expansion: about a third of patients (even without coagulopathy) have significant expansion within the first day; anticoagulant-related bleeds may keep enlarging for 24-48 h (p.3454)","Progressive decline in consciousness with headache and vomiting signalling rising ICP; deterioration may continue over 24-96 h from perihaematoma oedema (p.3453-3454)","Cerebellar haemorrhage with depressed consciousness or obstructive hydrocephalus, or radiographic hydrocephalus - high risk of rapid brainstem-compression deterioration requiring immediate neurosurgical evaluation (p.3454)","Signs of herniation (stupor/coma, dilated fixed pupil, decerebrate rigidity) (p.3453-3454)","Intraventricular extension of blood - substantially increases morbidity and may cause hydrocephalus (p.3454)"],"prognosis":"Overall, about 35-45% of patients die within the first month, and roughly 40% with a hypertensive ICH die, though survivors can achieve good to complete recovery because peri-haematoma tissue is displaced rather than infarcted and often regains function as the clot resorbs over months. The validated ICH Score stratifies mortality and outcome but should not be used to precisely prognosticate an individual, since withholding early aggressive care risks a self-fulfilling prophecy of poor outcome. Deep pontine haemorrhage with coma usually ends in death or a locked-in state, but small bleeds are compatible with survival; in cerebellar haemorrhage, full recovery is common if the deep cerebellar nuclei are spared (p.3453-3456).","pitfalls":["Cerebellar haemorrhage can deteriorate precipitously from brainstem compression or obstructive hydrocephalus; immediate surgical evacuation before severe brainstem compression may be lifesaving, and hydrocephalus from a cerebellar haematoma should generally be managed by definitive evacuation rather than ventricular drainage alone (p.3454).","Haemorrhage outside the typical deep hypertensive sites, or in a non-hypertensive patient, should prompt a search for other causes (amyloid angiopathy, tumour, vascular malformation, vasculitis, coagulopathy) rather than being assumed hypertensive (p.3454).","Conflating hypertensive encephalopathy/PRES with hypertensive ICH leads to inappropriately aggressive BP lowering - hypertensive encephalopathy warrants less aggressive reduction (p.3455).","ICP is often normal even with large ICHs, so it cannot be assumed; conversely induced hyperventilation can cause ischaemia and should be limited to acute resuscitation only (p.3456).","Glucocorticoids are not recommended for treating ICH (p.3456).","In ICH on antiplatelet drugs without thrombocytopenia, platelet transfusion showed no benefit and possible harm (p.3456).","Despite the presence of haemorrhage, venous sinus thrombosis is an indication for anticoagulation to relieve venous hypertension (p.3455)."],"severityClassification":"ICH Score (Hemphill et al., Stroke 2001) - validated grading scale for mortality/outcome stratification, summing five factors: age >=80 (1 pt), haematoma volume >=30 cc (1 pt), intraventricular haemorrhage present (1 pt), infratentorial origin (1 pt), and Glasgow Coma Scale 13-15 (0), 5-12 (1) or 3-4 (2); total 0-6 (p.3455-3456).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3452","Harrison 22e p.3453","Harrison 22e p.3454","Harrison 22e p.3455","Harrison 22e p.3456"],"crossLinks":["ischemic_stroke","sah","htn_emergency"]},"iih":{"name":"Idiopathic intracranial hypertension","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.117 (Chap. 17, Headache), p.228-229 (Chap. 34, Disorders of the Eye), p.235 (Chap. 34, abducens palsy), p.3379 (Chap. 433, drug-induced).","clinicalPearls":["Headache is typically generalized, present on awakening, eases through the day, and worsens on lying down (p.117).","Transient visual obscurations are a classic feature: brief (usually seconds), in one or both eyes, often triggered by a postural shift, and most frequent when headache is at its worst (p.117, p.228-229).","Obscurations that are prolonged or spontaneous indicate more threatening papilloedema (p.229).","The syndrome can occur without visual symptoms, especially when the fundi look normal, so it must be considered even when funduscopy is unremarkable (p.117).","Persistently raised intracranial pressure can itself trigger a chronic migraine syndrome (p.117).","Visual acuity is usually preserved in papilloedema unless it is severe, longstanding, or complicated by macular oedema and haemorrhage; field loss precedes acuity loss (p.229).","Unilateral or bilateral abducens (6th nerve) palsy is a classic false-localising sign of raised intracranial pressure and is corroborated by papilloedema on fundoscopy (p.235)."],"pathophysiology":"Raised CSF pressure without a space-occupying lesion, occurring in the setting of obesity, female sex, and occasionally pregnancy; partial obstruction of the cerebral venous sinuses is found in a small number of cases (p.117). Stenosis at the transverse-sigmoid sinus junction is commonly present, providing the rationale for venous sinus stenting (p.229). Vitamin A excess is a recognised cause of pseudotumor cerebri (p.3379).","additionalDifferentials":["Optic disc drusen, which produce pseudopapilloedema and can be mistaken for true papilloedema (p.229).","Obstructive sleep apnoea and poorly controlled hypertension, which also cause morning or nocturnal headache (p.117)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Space-occupying intracranial lesion causing raised CSF pressure headache (p.117).","Dural venous sinus thrombosis (p.229).","Arteriovenous (dural) shunt (p.229)."],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI as the initial study, including MR venography, to look for a mass and to search for dural venous sinus thrombosis or an AV shunt (p.117, p.229).","Lumbar puncture performed while the patient is symptomatic, measuring opening pressure in the lateral decubitus position; both the pressure and the symptomatic response to removing 20-30 mL of CSF can be assessed (p.117, p.229).","Formal visual field testing, performed even when there is no overt ophthalmic involvement (p.117).","B-mode ocular ultrasound to detect buried optic disc drusen (hyperechoic due to calcium) when pseudopapilloedema is suspected (p.229)."],"redFlags":["Prolonged or spontaneous transient visual obscurations signal more threatening papilloedema (p.229).","Insidious peripheral visual field loss with progressive optic atrophy under unremitting papilloedema; in this setting a reduction in disc swelling is an ominous sign of a dying nerve, not resolution (p.229).","Fulminant papilloedema can cause rapid-onset blindness and constitutes an emergency (p.229)."],"prognosis":"Headache often improves within weeks of starting medical therapy (p.117). However, unremitting papilloedema leads to progressive peripheral field loss and optic atrophy, and fulminant cases can cause rapid blindness, so visual loss is the feared outcome that drives urgent intervention (p.229).","pitfalls":["Inaccurate CSF opening-pressure readings at lumbar puncture are a common pitfall (p.229).","Failing to exclude a dural venous sinus thrombosis before attributing raised pressure to IIH (p.229).","Mistaking buried optic disc drusen (pseudopapilloedema) for true papilloedema, prompting an unnecessary work-up (p.229).","Assuming a normal fundus excludes the diagnosis; IIH can occur without funduscopic changes, so formal perimetry is still warranted (p.117)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.117","Harrison 22e p.228-229","Harrison 22e p.235","Harrison 22e p.3379"],"crossLinks":["migraine","tension_ha","brain_tumour"]},"ild":{"name":"Interstitial lung disease","system":"Pulmonary","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e, Ch.304 Interstitial Lung Disease (book pp.2259-2266) and Ch.299 Hypersensitivity Pneumonitis (book pp.2228-2231); supporting material from Ch.295-297 (pp.2199-2213)","clinicalPearls":["Diffuse parenchymal lung disease covers >200 heterogeneous conditions; the interstitial space between epithelium and endothelium is the dominant site, but alveolar epithelium and endothelial (airway and vessel) cells also drive pathogenesis (p.2259).","No single piece of data is diagnostic on its own; a confident ILD diagnosis requires the combined weight of clinical, laboratory, PFT, imaging and (when obtained) histopathologic findings, ideally reviewed in a multidisciplinary conference of pulmonologists, rheumatologists, radiologists and pathologists (pp.2259-2260).","Fine end-inspiratory bibasal crackles described as sounding like Velcro being pulled apart are characteristic of fibrotic ILD such as IPF and may be one of the earliest signs (pp.2200, 2261).","Egophony, whispered pectoriloquy and bronchial breath sounds suggest alveolar filling (e.g. pneumonia) rather than interstitial fibrosis (IPF); their absence helps distinguish fibrotic crackles from consolidation (p.2200).","Age strongly shifts pretest probability: IPF is most common in patients over 60 and rare under 50, whereas sarcoidosis, CTD-ILD, LAM and pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis typically present between ages 20 and 40 (p.2260).","On HRCT, apical-predominant interstitial thickening favours NSIP, whereas a basal and subpleural/dependent predominance favours IPF (p.2211).","A dry cough can be the most prominent symptom of IPF; some patients (even with extensive disease) report no dyspnoea at all (p.2261).","HRCT is now standard of care in initial ILD evaluation and can be diagnostic for IPF in the right clinical context, sparing the patient a lung biopsy (p.2262).","BAL findings can point to specific entities: eosinophilia >25% suggests chronic eosinophilic pneumonia, fat globules in macrophages suggest lipoid pneumonia, and serial bloody lavage indicates diffuse alveolar haemorrhage (p.2262).","A close relative with an idiopathic interstitial pneumonia is among the strongest risk factors for IPF; up to ~20% of pulmonary fibrosis is familial, and diverse IIP subtypes (IPF, NSIP, DIP) can run in the same family (p.2261)."],"pathophysiology":"ILDs are characterised by remodelling of the interstitium with variable degrees of inflammation and fibrosis, but alveolar epithelial and endothelial cell injury is also central to pathogenesis. The idiopathic interstitial pneumonias (IIPs) are the most common group. In IPF the histologic hallmark is a usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP) pattern: subpleural reticulation with honeycomb change and fibroblast foci (subepithelial collections of myofibroblasts and collagen) alternating with preserved normal alveolar architecture, producing temporal and spatial heterogeneity. NSIP shows more uniform interstitial inflammation and fibrosis with honeycombing usually absent and fibroblast foci rare. Genetic studies show that a substantial fraction of familial and even sporadic/idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis has a genetic basis: a MUC5B promoter variant (present in ~20% of Caucasians, 35-45% of IPF patients) confers an approximately sixfold risk, and variants in telomere-length genes (TERT, TERC, RTEL1) and surfactant protein genes (SFTPA2) are also implicated. Hypersensitivity pneumonitis is an immune-mediated response to inhaled antigens small enough to reach distal airways and alveoli, driven by dysregulated TH1/TH17 responses with precipitating IgG and innate (Toll-like receptor/MyD88) signalling; chronic HP shares the MUC5B variant with IPF (pp.2259-2266; 2228-2229).","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (UIP pattern; basal/subpleural reticulation, honeycombing, traction bronchiectasis) (p.2262)","Nonspecific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP) - cellular or fibrotic; often associated with CTD (p.2263)","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (nonfibrotic or fibrotic; exposure-related granulomatous ILD) (pp.2228, 2304)","Connective tissue disease-associated ILD - systemic sclerosis, rheumatoid arthritis, polymyositis/dermatomyositis, Sjogren's, SLE (pp.2265-2266)","Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia (COP) - subacute flulike illness, migratory consolidations (p.2264)","Acute interstitial pneumonia (Hamman-Rich syndrome) - acute respiratory distress with diffuse alveolar damage (p.2265)","Smoking-related ILD - respiratory bronchiolitis-ILD (RB-ILD) and desquamative interstitial pneumonia (DIP) (pp.2263-2264)","Sarcoidosis (granulomatous; hilar/mediastinal lymphadenopathy, peribronchovascular nodularity) (p.2260)","Drug-induced ILD - amiodarone, nitrofurantoin, methotrexate, bleomycin, rituximab, anti-TNF agents (p.2261)","Occupational/pneumoconiosis - asbestosis, silicosis (pp.2260-2261)","Lymphangioleiomyomatosis (young women, diffuse cysts, spontaneous pneumothorax) (p.2260)","Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis and pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis (p.2304/Fig.304-1)","Granulomatous vasculitides - granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA) and eosinophilic GPA/Churg-Strauss, which can cause diffuse alveolar haemorrhage (p.2266)"],"infectionMimics":["Pneumocystis pneumonia and other diffuse infections, which should be excluded in the initial diffuse-parenchymal workup (p.2260)","Community-acquired pneumonia - COP is commonly mistaken for pneumonia, and nonfibrotic HP can be misread as a respiratory infection (pp.2229, 2264)","Secondary pulmonary infection in patients with traction bronchiectasis or on immunosuppression, which can produce cough with haemoptysis (p.2261)","Mycobacterial exposure (e.g. Mycobacterium avium complex in hot tub lung) causing HP rather than frank infection (p.2229)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Congestive heart failure / hydrostatic pulmonary oedema - basilar crackles and interstitial thickening on imaging overlap with ILD; must be excluded (pp.2211, 2262)","Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) - shares diffuse alveolar damage histology with AIP and acute exacerbation of fibrosis (p.2265)","Bronchoalveolar (lepidic) cell carcinoma and lymphangitic carcinomatosis (p.2260)","Organic toxic dust syndrome (OTDS), which mimics HP but needs no prior sensitisation and lacks specific IgG antibodies (p.2230)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Multidisciplinary (MDT) ILD review jointly integrating clinical, radiologic, serologic and pathologic data (pp.2259-2260)","Full PFTs with lung volumes plus DLCO; restriction = reduced TLC with symmetric FEV1/FVC reduction, and a low DLCO may precede volume reduction (less specific, more variable) (pp.2200-2201, 2261)","Serial/repeated FVC measurement - decline correlates with higher mortality and tracks disease progression (p.2261)","CTD/autoantibody serology including anti-CCP, anti-synthetase (e.g. anti-Jo-1, anti-PL-12) antibodies - may reveal an underlying CTD even without other features (pp.2261, 2266)","HRCT chest to define UIP vs NSIP vs COP patterns, extent, honeycombing, and guide biopsy site (p.2262)","Bronchoscopy with BAL - lymphocytosis (with CD4:CD8 <1) characteristic of HP; differential cell counts for eosinophilic pneumonia and DAH (pp.2230, 2262)","Transbronchial biopsy or cryobiopsy; cryobiopsy gives larger yields than forceps but with higher bleeding/pneumothorax risk; VATS surgical biopsy when needed (higher pre-treatment yield, up to ~5% short-term mortality) (pp.2209, 2262)","Serum precipitins / specific IgG for HP - supportive but not sufficient (asymptomatic exposed individuals can be positive; antigen panels often falsely negative) (pp.2229-2230)","Detailed occupational, environmental, avocational, medication and family exposure history (pp.2200, 2261)","Echocardiography to assess for pulmonary hypertension and right heart strain (p.2201)","Genetic evaluation in familial pulmonary fibrosis (MUC5B promoter variant, telomere genes TERT/TERC/RTEL1, surfactant genes) (pp.2261, 2267)"],"redFlags":["Acute exacerbation of IPF - acute (<30 days) onset of respiratory distress and hypoxaemia in a patient with underlying fibrosis, not explained by infection or heart failure; reported mortality >85% (p.2265)","Acute interstitial pneumonia (Hamman-Rich) - acute respiratory distress and hypoxaemia with >50% mortality within 6 months (p.2265)","Haemoptysis suggesting diffuse alveolar haemorrhage (Goodpasture's, GPA) or LAM - warrants urgent evaluation (p.2261)","Honeycombing or extensive fibrosis on HRCT indicating advanced disease and worse prognosis (p.2262)","Signs of advanced disease: cyanosis, digital clubbing and cor pulmonale (p.2262)","Rapidly progressive ILD in anti-Jo-1-positive inflammatory myopathy (p.2266)","A declining FVC on serial testing, which signals elevated mortality risk (p.2261)"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies widely by ILD subtype and disease extent. IPF carries a poor prognosis (~50% survival at 3-5 years; pre-antifibrotic-era life expectancy 3-5 years from diagnosis). Idiopathic NSIP is comparatively favourable (>80% 5-year survival), with cellular NSIP doing better than fibrosing NSIP. Reported subtype mortality includes RB-ILD ~25% at 7 years and systemic sclerosis-associated ILD ~20-30% at 10 years; sarcoidosis mortality is generally low but state-dependent. RB-ILD usually has prolonged survival with death from progressive ILD rare. AIP has >50% 6-month mortality with frequent recurrences, though survivors can recover substantial lung function. Acute exacerbations of fibrotic ILD carry very high mortality (>85%). In CTD-ILD, a UIP pattern and more extensive fibrosis (e.g. in RA) predict reduced survival. Chronic fibrotic HP can mirror IPF in radiology and prognosis (pp.2260, 2262-2266; 2229).","pitfalls":["Attributing all wheezing to asthma - wheeze can reflect heart failure or other airway narrowing; conversely wheeze is uncommon in ILD but does occur in sarcoidosis, HP and eosinophilic GPA (pp.2200, 2262).","Forgetting that a UIP pattern on biopsy is not unique to IPF - it also occurs in CTD (e.g. RA-associated) ILD (p.2260).","Misreading acute ILD presentations as pneumonia, COPD exacerbation or heart failure, since acute ILD is unusual (p.2260).","Treating presumed IPF with immunosuppression - this has been shown to increase morbidity and mortality in IPF (p.2263).","Relying on PFT pattern to diagnose HP - HP can be restrictive or obstructive, so the pattern is not diagnostic (p.2229).","Over-relying on HP serum precipitin panels - they cover a limited antigen set and frequently give false negatives, and a positive IgG alone does not confirm HP (p.2230).","Confusing HP granulomas with sarcoidosis - HP granulomas are loose/poorly formed and peribronchiolar, whereas sarcoid granulomas are well-formed; caseating granulomas should prompt a search for infection (pp.2230, 2264).","Assuming CTD must be apparent - ILD can precede other CTD features by years or be the sole manifestation (pp.2261, 2265).","Diagnosing IPF without excluding a treatable mimic - heart failure, infection (e.g. PCP) and malignancy must be considered first (p.2260)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames ILD pattern recognition rather than a single numeric severity score. ILDs are first split into known cause (occupational e.g. asbestosis/silicosis; drug-induced e.g. amiodarone, nitrofurantoin, methotrexate, bleomycin; systemic/CTD-associated; granulomatous including HP) versus unknown cause (idiopathic interstitial pneumonias, plus rarer entities such as LAM, PAP, PLCH, sarcoidosis). The idiopathic interstitial pneumonias are further classified (per the ATS/ERS consensus) into chronic fibrosing (IPF, idiopathic NSIP), smoking-related (RB-ILD, DIP), and acute/subacute (COP, acute interstitial pneumonia, lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia). The UIP pattern (subpleural, basal reticulation with honeycombing and traction bronchiectasis) defines IPF, while NSIP shows more ground-glass and rare honeycombing. HP is now classified as nonfibrotic versus fibrotic (replacing the older acute/subacute/chronic terminology). Disease severity is gauged by extent of fibrosis/honeycombing on HRCT, degree of PFT (FVC/DLCO) impairment and its rate of decline, and presence of pulmonary hypertension/cor pulmonale (pp.2259-2266; 2229).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2200","Harrison 22e p.2201","Harrison 22e p.2211","Harrison 22e p.2228","Harrison 22e p.2229","Harrison 22e p.2230","Harrison 22e p.2259","Harrison 22e p.2260","Harrison 22e p.2261","Harrison 22e p.2262","Harrison 22e p.2263","Harrison 22e p.2264","Harrison 22e p.2265","Harrison 22e p.2266","Harrison 22e p.2267"],"crossLinks":["sarcoidosis","rheumatoid","vasculitis","lung_cancer","heart_failure"]},"ischemic_stroke":{"name":"Acute ischemic stroke","system":"Neurology / Vascular","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3427-3450 (Ch 437 Introduction to Cerebrovascular Diseases; Ch 438 Ischemic Stroke)","clinicalPearls":["Stroke is a clinical diagnosis: abrupt-onset focal neurologic deficit from a vascular cause; imaging is used to support it and to separate ischemia from hemorrhage (p.3427).","Time-is-brain: with complete arterial occlusion, neurons die within roughly 4-10 minutes; flow <16-18 mL/100 g/min infarcts within an hour, while 18-20 mL/100 g/min causes reversible ischemia unless prolonged (p.3439).","The ischemic penumbra is reversibly dysfunctional tissue around the infarct core; it is the target of reperfusion and can be visualized as a perfusion-diffusion (or CT-perfusion) mismatch (p.3439).","A useful triage rule of thumb is that a deficit maximal at onset (or one that remits) favors ischemia, whereas a depressed conscious level, very high initial blood pressure, or progressive worsening favors hemorrhage - though no clinical feature reliably separates them, so CT is required (p.3440).","The public FAST acronym (Facial weakness, Arm weakness, Speech difficulty, Time) captures common stroke features and emphasizes time-sensitivity; about 85% of ischemic stroke patients have hemiparesis (p.3428).","Stroke onset is timed from when the patient was last seen well, not when found; wake-up strokes default to bedtime, though advanced imaging may still allow treatment selection (p.3441).","Cerebellar infarction can masquerade as labyrinthitis with vertigo and vomiting; accompanying head/neck pain should prompt suspicion of vertebral dissection, and even small cerebellar edema can obstruct CSF and compress the brainstem (p.3440).","Roughly 30% of ischemic strokes remain cryptogenic despite thorough evaluation (p.3443).","Painful neck or head pain preceding stroke in a young patient (under ~60) suggests arterial dissection (p.3445)."],"pathophysiology":"Acute occlusion of an intracranial artery reduces regional cerebral blood flow; the resulting deficit depends on collateral supply, occlusion site, and systemic pressure. Energy failure from loss of glucose and oxygen halts ATP-dependent ion pumps, causing depolarization, sodium and calcium influx, excess synaptic glutamate, and excitotoxicity with reactive oxygen species that damage membranes and DNA, followed by an innate inflammatory response. Fever and hyperglycaemia (glucose >200 mg/dL) worsen the injury. Mechanistically, ischemic stroke arises by three routes (the basis of TOAST-style classification): (1) embolic occlusion of a large intracranial vessel - either cardioembolic (e.g., atrial fibrillation, mural thrombus, valvular disease) or artery-to-artery from carotid/aortic-arch plaque; (2) in-situ thrombosis of small penetrating arteries from lipohyalinosis or microatheroma, producing lacunar (small-vessel) infarcts; and (3) hypoperfusion from flow-limiting stenosis of a major extracranial or intracranial vessel, producing watershed/border-zone ischemia. Cardioembolism causes ~20%, large-artery/carotid atherosclerosis ~10%, and small-vessel disease ~20% of ischemic strokes (p.3439, p.3443-3446).","additionalDifferentials":["Intracerebral hemorrhage (must be excluded by CT before reperfusion)","Transient ischemic attack (deficit fully resolving within 24 h with no infarction on imaging)","Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis (headache, focal signs, seizures; CT often normal)","Hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy from global hypoperfusion (e.g., cardiac arrest)","Brain tumour presenting acutely via hemorrhage, seizure, or hydrocephalus","Functional/non-organic deficit"],"infectionMimics":["Stroke recrudescence: fever or sepsis unmasking a prior deficit, mimicking new hemiparesis, which clears as the infection is treated","Infective endocarditis with septic emboli (multifocal deficits; may evolve to abscess or hemorrhagic infarct)","Brain abscess","CNS infection-related vasculitis (syphilis, tuberculosis, fungal, bacterial, zoster meningitis)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Seizure with postictal (Todd's) paralysis - an observer history of no convulsive onset usually excludes seizure, but ongoing complex partial seizures can mimic stroke","Migraine, including acephalgic migraine even without prior history; sensory/motor deficits migrate slowly over minutes and may cross vascular territories","Metabolic encephalopathy (fluctuating mental status, typically without focal signs)","Hypoglycaemia and hyperglycaemia (check finger-stick glucose before thrombolysis)","Intracranial tumour"],"additionalInvestigations":["Finger-stick blood glucose - one of only two tests (with brain imaging) truly required before IV thrombolysis","ECG and continuous cardiac telemetry to detect atrial fibrillation or recent MI; ambulatory monitoring if AF not captured inpatient","Echocardiography (transthoracic/transoesophageal); bubble-contrast study to detect right-to-left shunt / PFO","Carotid duplex ultrasound and transcranial Doppler (can also detect microemboli)","CT/MR perfusion imaging to define the penumbra and select late-window candidates","MRI with diffusion-weighted imaging - most sensitive for acute infarction, especially in the posterior fossa, and detects new infarction after TIA","Catheter cerebral angiography (gold standard for stenosis/vasculopathy; reserved for when noninvasive studies are inadequate)","Secondary-prevention workup: lipid profile, ESR, FBC, electrolytes, renal function, coagulation (PT/PTT); selective thrombophilia/vasculitis screen in young or cryptogenic cases"],"redFlags":["Disabling deficit (NIHSS >5) - candidate for thrombolysis and/or thrombectomy; act fast","Suspected large-vessel occlusion (ICA terminus, M1/M2, basilar) - candidate for mechanical thrombectomy","Cerebellar infarct with declining consciousness or new brainstem signs - impending herniation/hydrocephalus needing emergency decompression","Large hemispheric (malignant MCA) infarct - 5-10% develop space-occupying edema peaking on day 2-3, may need hemicraniectomy","Painful neck/Horner's or recent neck trauma/manipulation - suspect arterial dissection","Multifocal deficits with fever - suspect septic emboli from endocarditis"],"prognosis":"Stroke is the second leading cause of death worldwide; outcome hinges on time to reperfusion ('time-is-brain'). For large-vessel occlusion, the chance of a good outcome falls steeply with delay - the odds of a good result exceed 3 if groin puncture for thrombectomy is within 2 h but fall to about 2 by 8 h, and over 80% do well if the vessel opens within 1 h of arrival versus about one-third at 6 h. In pooled thrombectomy trials ~46% achieved functional independence (modified Rankin 0-2) versus ~26.5% with medical care, with a mortality benefit. Small-vessel (lacunar) strokes generally recover more rapidly and completely than large-vessel strokes, though some stutter or leave severe disability. Without secondary prevention the annual risk of a further stroke after atherothrombotic stroke/TIA is about 8-10%; symptomatic intracranial atherosclerosis and untreated symptomatic carotid stenosis each carry ~15% per year. Care in a dedicated stroke unit followed by rehabilitation improves neurologic outcome and reduces mortality (p.3427, p.3441-3442, p.3445-3446).","pitfalls":["Treating a stroke mimic as stroke: a postictal (Todd's) paralysis, acephalgic migraine, or hypoglycaemia can convincingly imitate acute stroke - always confirm glucose and seek an observer history of convulsive onset.","Mislabelling cerebellar stroke as benign labyrinthitis and missing life-threatening brainstem compression / hydrocephalus.","Omitting the embolic-source workup (carotid, heart) after a lacunar-appearing infarct, since a large-vessel source can present as a small-vessel syndrome.","A normal early CT does not exclude infarction; CT may be only subtly abnormal acutely and the hyperdense MCA sign is specific but seen in only ~20% of MCA occlusions (DWI-MRI is far more sensitive).","Over-attributing stroke to a patent foramen ovale, which is present in ~15% of the general population and is often incidental.","Aggressively lowering blood pressure acutely can reduce pressure-dependent collateral flow and worsen the penumbra; permissive hypertension is generally allowed early.","Distinguish benign reperfusion petechial haemorrhage within an infarct from frank haemorrhagic transformation with mass effect."],"severityClassification":"Severity is graded clinically by the National Institutes of Health Stroke Scale (NIHSS). A disabling deficit (NIHSS >5) flags potential eligibility for thrombolysis/endovascular therapy, whereas minor non-disabling stroke (NIHSS 0-5) may be managed with aspirin or short-term dual antiplatelet therapy. Mechanism-based (TOAST-style) classification - cardioembolic, large-artery atherosclerosis (carotid/intracranial), small-vessel/lacunar, other defined cause, and cryptogenic (~30%) - drives secondary prevention. Carotid disease is further classed as symptomatic vs asymptomatic and by degree of stenosis, with risk rising with stenosis severity except for near-occlusions, which carry lower risk (p.3440-3441, p.3443, p.3445).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3427","Harrison 22e p.3428","Harrison 22e p.3439","Harrison 22e p.3440","Harrison 22e p.3441","Harrison 22e p.3442","Harrison 22e p.3443","Harrison 22e p.3444","Harrison 22e p.3445","Harrison 22e p.3446"],"crossLinks":["ich","tia","sah","atrial_fib","hypoglycemia","seizure_epilepsy","migraine","metabolic_enceph","brain_tumour","dvt","pe","vasculitis","IE"]},"itp":{"name":"Immune thrombocytopenia (ITP)","system":"Hematology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.921-924 (Chap. 120, Disorders of Platelets and Vessel Wall)","clinicalPearls":["ITP is an acquired disorder of immune-mediated platelet destruction, with possible additional inhibition of platelet release from megakaryocytes (p.924).","In children ITP is usually acute, often follows an infection, and is self-limited; in adults it tends to be chronic, though some adults remit spontaneously within months of diagnosis (p.924).","It is largely a diagnosis of exclusion: characterized by mucocutaneous bleeding and a low (often very low) platelet count with otherwise normal peripheral blood cells and smear (p.924).","Presentation is either with ecchymoses/petechiae or with thrombocytopenia found incidentally on a routine CBC (p.924).","Mucocutaneous bleeding may include oral mucosal, gastrointestinal, or heavy menstrual bleeding; rarely life-threatening (including CNS) bleeding occurs (p.924).","Wet purpura (blood blisters in the mouth) and retinal hemorrhages may herald life-threatening bleeding (p.924, p.922).","Petechiae are pinpoint, nonblanching hemorrhages that signal decreased platelet number rather than platelet dysfunction, and first appear in dependent areas of increased venous pressure (ankles/feet in an ambulatory patient) (p.922).","Serologic (antiplatelet antibody) testing is generally not helpful because of low sensitivity and specificity of current tests (p.924).","The peripheral smear may show large platelets with otherwise normal morphology, reflecting young/rapidly turned-over platelets (p.924, p.916)."],"pathophysiology":"ITP results from immune-mediated destruction of antibody-coated platelets by the reticuloendothelial system, and possibly impaired platelet release from megakaryocytes. Primary ITP has no identifiable cause; secondary ITP is associated with an underlying disorder such as autoimmune disease (notably SLE) or infection (HIV, hepatitis C). Childhood ITP commonly follows a viral infection through immune mechanisms and usually resolves spontaneously (Harrison 22e p.922, p.924).","additionalDifferentials":["Drug-induced thrombocytopenia (e.g., quinine, sulfonamides, vancomycin; classic drug-dependent antibodies react with platelet antigens only when drug is present) (p.922-923)","Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) - associated with thrombosis rather than bleeding, nadir rarely <20,000/uL; use 4T scoring (p.923)","Congenital/inherited thrombocytopenia (e.g., autosomal dominant macrothrombocytopenia) (p.922, p.965 algorithm)","Pseudothrombocytopenia from platelet clumping (EDTA-dependent) - redraw in sodium citrate or heparin (p.922, p.965 algorithm)","Evans syndrome - combined autoimmune hemolytic anemia with ITP (perform direct antiglobulin/Coombs test if anemia present) (p.924)"],"infectionMimics":["HIV infection - can cause immune thrombocytopenia early; late HIV causes pancytopenia with decreased/dysplastic platelet production (p.922)","Hepatitis C virus infection - recognized cause of secondary ITP (p.924)","Infectious mononucleosis - immune-mediated thrombocytopenia (p.922)","Systemic infection with gram-negative bacteria or COVID-19 - thrombocytopenia that may be associated with DIC (p.922)","Helicobacter pylori - association with ITP is unclear but appears to have a geographic distribution (p.924)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Microangiopathic hemolytic anemias (TTP, DIC) - distinguished by schistocytes/fragmented red cells on smear (p.922, p.965 algorithm)","Hypersplenism/splenomegaly (e.g., chronic liver disease) causing platelet sequestration (p.922)","Bone marrow production failure - low platelet count with absence of large young platelets on smear (p.916, p.922)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CBC with peripheral blood smear review to confirm isolated thrombocytopenia and exclude clumping/schistocytes (p.922, p.924)","HIV and hepatitis C testing to evaluate for secondary ITP (p.924)","Serologic testing for SLE (p.924)","Serum protein electrophoresis and immunoglobulin levels (to detect hypogammaglobulinemia or monoclonal gammopathy) (p.924)","Selective testing for IgA deficiency (p.924)","H. pylori testing depending on clinical circumstance (p.924)","Direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test if anemia present, to detect Evans syndrome (p.924)","Bone marrow examination reserved for patients with unexplained additional findings or failure to respond to initial therapy (p.924)"],"redFlags":["Wet purpura (oral blood blisters) - may herald life-threatening bleeding (p.924, p.922)","Retinal hemorrhages - may herald life-threatening bleeding (p.924)","Active mucosal, GI, or CNS bleeding (p.924)","Very severe thrombocytopenia (<5000/uL) or signs of impending bleeding (p.924)"],"prognosis":"Childhood ITP is usually acute and almost always resolves spontaneously. Adult ITP is more often chronic, though some adults remit spontaneously within months. Patients with platelet counts >30,000/uL appear not to have increased mortality related to the thrombocytopenia, so diagnosis does not mandate treatment (Harrison 22e p.924).","pitfalls":["Treating presumed ITP without excluding secondary causes (HIV, HCV, SLE, drugs) (p.924).","Relying on antiplatelet antibody serology - low sensitivity/specificity makes it unhelpful for diagnosis (p.924).","Missing pseudothrombocytopenia from EDTA-induced platelet clumping, which produces a falsely low automated count; confirm on smear and redraw in citrate/heparin (p.922, p.916).","Overlooking Evans syndrome - failing to do a Coombs test when anemia accompanies thrombocytopenia (p.924).","Failing to take a careful drug/herbal/OTC history, since drugs are the most common cause of thrombocytopenia (p.922)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not give a formal grading scale. Practical thresholds noted: platelet counts >30,000/uL are not associated with increased mortality; ~5000-10,000/uL is the level needed to maintain microvascular integrity; severe thrombocytopenia is cited as <5000/uL for treatment-decision purposes (Harrison 22e p.922, p.924).","references":["Harrison 22e p.921","Harrison 22e p.922","Harrison 22e p.923","Harrison 22e p.924"],"crossLinks":["ttp_hus","sle_flare","dic"]},"lung_cancer":{"name":"Lung cancer","system":"Oncology / Pulmonary","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.83 Neoplasms of the Lung (p.614-622); SVC syndrome from Ch.80 Oncologic Emergencies (p.585-586)","clinicalPearls":["Histology splits into small-cell lung cancer (SCLC) and three non-small-cell types (adenocarcinoma, squamous cell, large-cell) grouped as NSCLC; these four account for about 90% of epithelial lung cancers and the SCLC/NSCLC distinction drives entirely different natural history and management (p.616).","SCLC and NSCLC behave very differently: SCLC is more strongly tobacco-associated, harbours TP53 and RB1 mutations in roughly 90% of cases, and presents with extensive-stage disease in 60-70% of patients (p.617, p.622).","Adenocarcinoma is now the most common subtype as smoking has declined, and predominates in never-smokers/light smokers, women, and younger adults (<60); squamous and small-cell types remain the most tobacco-linked (p.616).","Within NSCLC, distinguishing squamous from nonsquamous is critical for therapy; 'non-small-cell carcinoma, not otherwise specified' is no longer an acceptable diagnosis, and immunohistochemistry (TTF-1/napsin-A for adeno, p40/p63 for squamous) resolves ambiguous small biopsies (p.616).","About two-thirds of new US lung cancer diagnoses occur in former smokers or never-smokers; roughly one in five women and one in 12 men with lung cancer have never smoked, so a never-smoking history does not exclude the diagnosis (p.614).","Smoking cessation benefits patients even after a lung cancer diagnosis (better overall survival, fewer treatment side effects, improved quality of life), yet about 30% continue to smoke after diagnosis (p.614-615).","A normal chest x-ray does not exclude lung cancer: chronic cough with or without haemoptysis in a current/former smoker with COPD aged 40 or older warrants thorough investigation even with a normal CXR (p.618).","Tissue sampling is mandatory to confirm diagnosis in all suspected cases; a core biopsy is preferred over FNA to provide enough material for molecular and PD-L1 testing (p.620).","Central lesions (squamous, small-cell, endobronchial carcinoids) are best reached by bronchoscopy, whereas peripheral lesions (adenocarcinoma, large-cell) favour transthoracic biopsy (p.620).","PET should never be used alone to diagnose lung cancer or confirm mediastinal/metastatic involvement; tissue confirmation is always required, and false positives occur with infection and granulomatous disease such as tuberculosis (p.620)."],"pathophysiology":"Lung cancer arises from respiratory epithelium (bronchi, bronchioles, alveoli). Environmental carcinogens, chiefly tobacco smoke, drive transformation of bronchoepithelial cells, modulated by polymorphisms in carcinogen-metabolising genes (e.g. CYP1A1). Cancers accumulate gain-of-function oncogene mutations and loss-of-function tumour-suppressor mutations; allelic loss on chromosome 3p occurs very early, even in histologically normal smoke-damaged epithelium. NSCLC adenocarcinomas are frequently driven by targetable oncogenic alterations (EGFR ~10-35%, KRAS ~15-25%, ALK rearrangement 3-7%, plus BRAF, HER2, MET, RET, ROS1, NTRK), whereas nearly 90% of SCLCs carry TP53 and RB1 mutations. Clinical manifestations stem from the primary lesion, local/regional invasion, distant metastases, or paraneoplastic syndromes. Paraneoplastic endocrine effects reflect ectopic hormone secretion: PTH-related peptide (squamous cell) causing hypercalcaemia, ADH or atrial natriuretic peptide (SCLC) causing hyponatraemia, and ectopic ACTH (SCLC, pulmonary carcinoids) causing Cushing-type biochemistry. (p.615-619)","additionalDifferentials":["Pulmonary metastases from non-lung primaries and other neoplasms metastatic to the lung, which constitutional symptoms cannot reliably distinguish from primary lung cancer (p.619)","Endobronchial metastases from carcinoid, breast, colon, kidney and other tumours presenting with haemoptysis (referenced p.591/Ch.80)","Mesothelioma, which can be hard to separate from adenocarcinoma and is identified by CK5/6, calretinin and WT-1 positivity (p.616)","Pulmonary carcinoid tumour, a neuroendocrine tumour that can also secrete ectopic ACTH (p.619, p.662 area)"],"infectionMimics":["Post-obstructive pneumonia or a persistent pneumonia that does not resolve after repeated antibiotic courses can mask an underlying obstructing tumour (p.618)","Lung abscess physiology from tumour cavitation can mimic infective abscess (p.618)","Granulomatous infection such as tuberculosis can cause false-positive PET uptake and simulate malignancy on imaging (p.620)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Paraneoplastic syndromes can mimic metastatic disease and, if unrecognised, lead to inappropriate palliative rather than curative treatment (p.619)","Benign causes of SVC obstruction (central venous catheters, pacemaker/defibrillator leads, aortic aneurysm, thyromegaly, thrombosis, fibrosing mediastinitis from prior radiation/histoplasmosis/Behcet) now account for at least 40% of SVC syndrome cases (p.585)","Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy with periostitis can produce bone pain and a positive bone scan that mimics skeletal metastasis (p.619)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Core biopsy (preferred over FNA) for adequate tissue, obtained by bronchoscopic/transbronchial biopsy, image-guided percutaneous biopsy, or EBUS/EUS-guided sampling depending on location (p.620)","Next-generation sequencing for driver oncogenes (EGFR, ALK, ROS1, BRAF, KRAS, MET, RET, NTRK, HER2) plus PD-L1 immunohistochemistry reported as tumour proportion score, recommended for all advanced NSCLC (p.620)","Circulating tumour DNA 'liquid biopsy' as a non-invasive adjunct when tissue is limited or inaccessible (p.620)","PET-CT for anatomic staging; an SUV >2.5 is highly suspicious for malignancy, with false negatives in lesions <8 mm, diabetes, and slow-growing tumours (p.620)","MRI of the brain for metastases (and to assess brachial plexus in superior sulcus tumours); recommended in SCLC staging where brain metastases are present in ~10% of asymptomatic patients (p.620-621)","EBUS/EUS-guided or invasive mediastinal staging (mediastinoscopy, thoracoscopy) since one-quarter to one-half of NSCLC patients have mediastinal nodal metastases at diagnosis (p.620-621)","Repeat thoracentesis if the first is negative in suspected malignant pleural effusion; spinal MRI/CT and lumbar puncture if cord compression or leptomeningeal disease is suspected (p.620-621)","Immunohistochemistry panel to confirm neuroendocrine differentiation (CD56/NCAM, synaptophysin, chromogranin, INSM1) and to distinguish primary lung from metastatic adenocarcinoma (TTF-1, napsin-A) (p.616)","Routine labs suggesting metastatic spread: anaemia, and elevated alkaline phosphatase, GGT, transaminases, and calcium (p.619)"],"redFlags":["Chronic cough with or without haemoptysis in a smoker/former smoker with COPD aged 40+, even with a normal chest x-ray (p.618)","A persistent pneumonia without constitutional symptoms that fails to clear after repeated antibiotic courses (p.618)","Superior vena cava syndrome: neck/facial swelling, dilated neck and chest-wall collateral veins, plethora worse when supine; cerebral or laryngeal oedema signals a poorer prognosis and demands urgent evaluation (p.585-586)","Tracheal obstruction from a superior mediastinal mass is the one immediately life-threatening complication and needs emergent therapy (p.586)","Pancoast/superior sulcus tumour: shoulder pain radiating in the ulnar distribution of the arm with destruction of the first and second ribs (p.619)","Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis, enophthalmos) from sympathetic chain involvement, often coexisting with Pancoast syndrome (p.619)","Spinal cord compression symptoms (pain, weakness, paralysis, urinary retention) requiring urgent spinal imaging (p.621)","Symptomatic hypercalcaemia (nausea, vomiting, constipation, polyuria, thirst, altered mental status) as the most common life-threatening metabolic complication, usually from squamous cell carcinoma (p.619)","Weight loss exceeding 10% of body weight, a poor prognostic sign (p.619)"],"prognosis":"Outcome is closely tied to stage at diagnosis, but over half of patients present with locally advanced or metastatic disease. In NSCLC, about 15% have localised disease amenable to curative surgery or radiotherapy, roughly a quarter have local/regional disease that may or may not be curable, and about half have metastatic disease at diagnosis. SEER data (2022) show a 5-year survival of about 63% for localised disease. Extrathoracic metastases are found at autopsy in >50% of squamous, ~80% of adenocarcinoma/large-cell, and >95% of SCLC cases. SCLC is highly likely to present extensive-stage (60-70%). Adenocarcinoma pattern carries prognostic weight: lepidic-predominant is favourable, acinar/papillary intermediate, and solid or micropapillary poor; adenocarcinoma in situ and minimally invasive adenocarcinoma achieve essentially 100% 5-year disease-free survival after complete resection. Weight loss >10%, and thrombotic disease complicating cancer, are poor prognostic signs. (p.616, p.619, p.621-622)","pitfalls":["Treating a non-resolving pneumonia with further antibiotics rather than investigating risks losing the chance at early cancer diagnosis (analogous principle stated for head/neck cancer p.611; applied to lung p.618).","Mistaking a paraneoplastic syndrome for metastatic disease and switching to palliative care when the patient may still be curable; many paraneoplastic syndromes resolve with successful tumour treatment (p.619).","Relying on PET alone for diagnosis or staging without tissue confirmation, given false positives from infection/granulomatous disease and false negatives in small or indolent lesions (p.620).","Accepting a negative TTF-1 as excluding a lung primary; a negative TTF-1 does not rule out lung cancer (p.616).","Stopping at a single negative thoracentesis in suspected malignant effusion, or a single negative lumbar puncture in suspected leptomeningeal disease, when a repeat is warranted (p.620-621).","Overly rapid correction of SIADH-related hyponatraemia (including with tolvaptan) can cause irreversible neurologic injury; patients with ectopic ANP may worsen unless sodium intake is increased (p.619).","In screening, ignoring lead-time bias, length-time bias and overdiagnosis can overstate apparent survival benefit; LDCT also carries a very high false-positive rate (about 96% of positive screens were false positives in NLST) (p.617-618)."],"severityClassification":"NSCLC: TNM (8th edition, effective 2018; 9th edition expected 2024). T by size/invasion - T1 <=3cm (T1a <=1, T1b >1-2, T1c >2-3), T2 >3-5cm or visceral pleura/main bronchus/atelectasis (T2a >3-4, T2b >4-5), T3 >5-7cm or chest wall/pericardium/phrenic nerve invasion or same-lobe satellite nodule, T4 >7cm or invasion of mediastinum/heart/great vessels/trachea/oesophagus/spine/carina or different ipsilateral-lobe nodule. N: N0 none, N1 ipsilateral peribronchial/hilar/intrapulmonary, N2 ipsilateral mediastinal/subcarinal, N3 contralateral mediastinal/hilar or any scalene/supraclavicular. M: M1a contralateral-lobe nodule or pleural/pericardial nodule/malignant effusion, M1b single distant metastasis to one organ, M1c multiple metastases. SCLC: dual classification recommended - the Veterans Administration two-stage system (limited-stage = confined to ipsilateral hemithorax within a tolerable radiation port, including contralateral supraclavicular nodes, recurrent laryngeal nerve involvement and SVC obstruction; extensive-stage = overt metastatic disease, or cardiac tamponade/malignant effusion/bilateral parenchymal involvement) plus the TNM system, with TNM preferred for the rare apparent stage I SCLC. (p.620-622)","references":["Harrison 22e p.585","Harrison 22e p.586","Harrison 22e p.614","Harrison 22e p.615","Harrison 22e p.616","Harrison 22e p.617","Harrison 22e p.618","Harrison 22e p.619","Harrison 22e p.620","Harrison 22e p.621","Harrison 22e p.622"],"crossLinks":["svc_obstruction","hypercalcemia","hyponatremia","pleural_effusion","LUNG_ABSCESS"]},"malignancy_b":{"name":"Malignancy (B-symptoms)","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.193, 316-318, 476-478, 855, 860-861, 869","clinicalPearls":["B symptoms are formally defined for lymphoma staging (the Ann Arbor 'B' suffix) as unexplained fever (>38C), drenching night sweats, and/or unexplained weight loss exceeding 10% of body weight over the 6 months before diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.861, p.869).","The tempo of symptoms helps separate aggressive from indolent lymphoma: rapid symptomatic progression and the presence of B symptoms suggest a more aggressive process (Harrison 22e p.903).","Among malignancies, lymphoma is by far the commonest cause of malignancy-related fever of unknown origin, and the fever can precede any palpable lymphadenopathy (Harrison 22e p.193).","About one-third of patients with classical Hodgkin lymphoma present with B symptoms; Hodgkin disease can even present as a fever of unknown origin, more often in older patients with abdominal mixed-cellularity disease (Harrison 22e p.869).","Pel-Ebstein fever (cyclical fever lasting days to weeks alternating with afebrile intervals) is a rare but classic Hodgkin lymphoma presentation (Harrison 22e p.869).","Node character matters: lymphomatous nodes tend to be large, firm, rubbery, mobile, symmetric and non-tender, whereas metastatic carcinoma nodes are typically hard, non-tender and fixed to surrounding tissue (Harrison 22e p.477).","Supraclavicular and scalene node enlargement is always abnormal and warrants prompt evaluation; a left supraclavicular node (Virchow node) classically reflects a gastrointestinal primary, but lung, breast, testis and ovarian cancers also seed these nodes (Harrison 22e p.477).","Examination in suspected lymphoma should systematically cover all peripheral nodal sites plus Waldeyer ring, liver and spleen size, effusions/ascites, and testicular, breast or skin involvement, since any of these can change staging and management (Harrison 22e p.903).","Enlarged intra-abdominal or retroperitoneal nodes are usually malignant (lymphoma, or germ-cell tumour in young men), in contrast to the largely benign causes of cervical adenopathy (Harrison 22e p.477).","Half of all cancer patients lose some weight and up to 20% of cancer deaths are attributable directly to cachexia; malignancy that declares itself through marked weight loss generally carries a poor prognosis (Harrison 22e p.317)."],"pathophysiology":"B symptoms in lymphoma are cytokine-mediated systemic effects of the tumour rather than direct mass effects. In classical Hodgkin lymphoma the malignant Hodgkin/Reed-Sternberg cells are rare within a tumour mass that is largely a polyclonal inflammatory infiltrate reacting to tumour-derived cytokines; this inflammatory milieu drives constitutional symptoms and contributes to the generalised immune suppression seen in these patients. Involuntary weight loss in malignancy reflects a combination of anorexia, sarcopenia and a cytokine-driven cachexia syndrome. (Harrison 22e p.316, p.869)","additionalDifferentials":["Hodgkin lymphoma (classical) - nontender cervical/supraclavicular/axillary nodes, frequent mediastinal involvement (Harrison 22e p.869)","Non-Hodgkin lymphoma - nodal and extranodal disease; B symptoms part of staging (Harrison 22e p.860)","Acute or chronic lymphocytic leukaemia (can produce generalised adenopathy and constitutional symptoms) (Harrison 22e p.476)","Richter transformation of CLL - rapidly progressive focal adenopathy with night sweats, fever, weight loss and high LDH (Harrison 22e p.897)","Metastatic carcinoma to nodes (head/neck, lung, breast, thyroid, GI primaries) (Harrison 22e p.477)","Germ-cell tumour (retroperitoneal nodes in young men) (Harrison 22e p.477)","Occult solid tumours presenting with involuntary weight loss - GI/hepatobiliary, lung, breast, genitourinary, ovarian, prostate (Harrison 22e p.317)"],"infectionMimics":["Infectious mononucleosis (EBV or CMV) - generalised adenopathy with constitutional symptoms (Harrison 22e p.476)","Tuberculosis - can cause unilateral mediastinal adenopathy and mesenteric lymphadenitis mimicking lymphoma, with night sweats and weight loss (Harrison 22e p.477)","Histoplasmosis - unilateral paratracheal node involvement that can mimic lymphoma in endemic regions (Harrison 22e p.477)","Toxoplasmosis - generalised reactive adenopathy (Harrison 22e p.476)","HIV / AIDS - generalised lymphadenopathy and a recognised lymphoma risk factor (Harrison 22e p.476, p.903)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Sarcoidosis - mediastinal/hilar adenopathy and constitutional symptoms (Harrison 22e p.477)","Systemic lupus erythematosus and mixed connective tissue disease - generalised adenopathy (Harrison 22e p.476)","Drug-induced (phenytoin/diphenylhydantoin and others) lymphadenopathy, including DRESS/DIHS with eosinophilia and extensive adenopathy (Harrison 22e p.193, p.476)","Castleman disease, Kikuchi histiocytic necrotizing lymphadenitis, Rosai-Dorfman disease, angioimmunoblastic lymphadenopathy, IgG4-related disease (Harrison 22e p.476)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Excisional biopsy of the largest abnormal node is preferred; fine-needle aspiration should NOT be the first procedure as it usually yields insufficient tissue and delays diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.478)","Serum protein electrophoresis to detect a monoclonal paraprotein (Harrison 22e p.903)","Serum beta2-microglobulin and LDH - independent prognostic markers in NHL (Harrison 22e p.903)","CBC with differential, comprehensive metabolic/liver panel, uric acid and calcium (Harrison 22e p.317, p.904)","ESR and CRP as part of the constitutional-symptom / weight-loss workup (Harrison 22e p.316, p.869)","Contrast CT of chest/abdomen/pelvis for staging; FDG-PET/CT preferred for aggressive lymphomas and Hodgkin lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.904)","Bone marrow biopsy where indicated; lumbar puncture if CNS-risk histology or neurologic features (Harrison 22e p.903-904)","HIV and hepatitis B and C serology - risk factors for NHL and relevant to hepatitis B reactivation risk (Harrison 22e p.904)","Cervical node ultrasound long-to-short axis ratio (<2.0 favours malignancy) where head/neck cancer is suspected (Harrison 22e p.478)","Age/sex/risk-appropriate cancer screening (e.g. faecal occult blood, colonoscopy, mammography) and abdominal ultrasound in occult-malignancy weight-loss workup (Harrison 22e p.318)"],"redFlags":["Supraclavicular or scalene adenopathy - always abnormal, biopsy promptly (Harrison 22e p.477-478)","Solitary hard non-tender cervical node in an older tobacco user - prompt biopsy after ENT mucosal assessment (Harrison 22e p.478)","Node size >2 cm with an abnormal chest x-ray in younger patients - predicts malignant/granulomatous biopsy yield (Harrison 22e p.521)","Hard fixed nodes, or firm mobile generalised adenopathy suggestive of lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.478)","Airway, oesophageal or SVC compression from thoracic adenopathy (cough/wheeze, hoarseness, dysphagia, facial/arm/neck swelling) (Harrison 22e p.520)","Enlarged intra-abdominal or retroperitoneal nodes (usually malignant) (Harrison 22e p.477)","Involuntary weight loss >=5% over 6 months prompting structured malignancy workup (Harrison 22e p.358)"],"prognosis":"Lymphoma prognosis is stage- and biology-dependent. For NHL the International Prognostic Index uses five factors (age >=60, elevated LDH, ECOG performance status >=2, Ann Arbor stage III/IV, and >1 extranodal site); for DLBCL treated with R-CHOP, 0 factors gives ~94% 4-year survival versus ~53% with 3-5 factors. Across lymphomas, decreased outcome is associated with B symptoms, advanced stage, elevated LDH and bulky disease (e.g. lymph-node mass >7 cm). In occult solid malignancy, presentation through significant involuntary weight loss generally carries a very poor prognosis, whereas when no cause is found after thorough workup the prognosis is comparatively favourable. (Harrison 22e p.317, p.861, NHL prognosis section)","pitfalls":["Do NOT give glucocorticoids for undiagnosed lymphadenopathy - their lympholytic effect can obscure lymphoma, leukaemia and Castleman disease and delay/disrupt diagnosis (the rare exception is life-threatening pharyngeal obstruction from Waldeyer-ring tissue in mononucleosis) (Harrison 22e p.521).","Fine-needle aspiration is inadequate for an initial lymphoma diagnosis - obtain an excisional/core biopsy; FNA is reserved for thyroid nodules and confirming relapse of a known cancer (Harrison 22e p.478).","Antibiotics are NOT indicated for lymphadenopathy unless there is strong evidence of bacterial infection (Harrison 22e p.521).","Localized-versus-generalized adenopathy has limited discriminatory value because many causes produce either pattern; do not over-rely on it (Harrison 22e p.476).","In primary care most lymphadenopathy is benign (>two-thirds nonspecific/URI, <1% malignant) and needs only short-interval follow-up - but persistent or enlarging nodes must be re-evaluated and biopsied (Harrison 22e p.476, p.521).","If a head-and-neck primary is suspected behind a solitary cervical node, perform a careful ENT exam and biopsy any suspicious mucosal lesion first before excising the node (Harrison 22e p.478)."],"severityClassification":"Ann Arbor staging (stages I-IV) with suffix A (no systemic symptoms) or B (presence of B symptoms: fever >38C, drenching night sweats, or weight loss >10% body weight over preceding 6 months); suffix E for localized extralymphatic extension. B-symptom status (the A/B designation) and stage drive prognosis and therapy. (Harrison 22e p.861, p.869)","references":["Harrison 22e p.193","Harrison 22e p.316","Harrison 22e p.317","Harrison 22e p.318","Harrison 22e p.476","Harrison 22e p.477","Harrison 22e p.478","Harrison 22e p.519","Harrison 22e p.520","Harrison 22e p.521","Harrison 22e p.855","Harrison 22e p.860","Harrison 22e p.861","Harrison 22e p.869","Harrison 22e p.897"],"crossLinks":["acute_leukemia","myeloma","PUO","DISSEMINATED_TB"]},"mesenteric_ischemia":{"name":"Acute mesenteric ischemia","system":"Vascular / GI","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2586-2589 (Chapter 340, Mesenteric Vascular Insufficiency)","clinicalPearls":["Severe, acute, non-remitting abdominal pain that is strikingly out of proportion to physical findings is the most common complaint in arterial acute mesenteric ischaemia, present in about 95% of cases; pain precedes signs because ischaemia begins in the mucosa and progresses outward to the serosa (p.2588).","About half of acute mesenteric ischaemia cases are embolic, lodging in the mid-to-distal SMA just past the middle colic artery origin; emboli commonly arise from atrial fibrillation, recent MI, soft atherosclerotic plaque, infective endocarditis, valvular heart disease, or recent cardiac/vascular catheterisation (p.2587).","Acute-on-chronic thrombotic ischaemia (about 25-30% of cases) occurs at sites of severe atherosclerotic narrowing of the SMA or celiac artery, typically in patients with pre-existing mesenteric atherosclerosis who may have had prior chronic mesenteric ischaemia (p.2587, 2588).","Embolic disease is suggested by older patients with a predisposing cardiac source (AF, prior embolic event, recent infective endocarditis), whereas thrombotic disease suggests underlying atherosclerosis with possible prior intestinal angina (p.2588).","Chronic mesenteric ischaemia classically presents as recurrent dull, crampy, postprandial epigastric pain ('intestinal angina') with fear of eating and weight loss; chronic diarrhoea may occur and symptoms typically last 6-12 months before diagnosis (p.2587-2588).","Mesenteric venous thrombosis tends to present more vaguely and subacutely with non-specific abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, distension, mild-to-moderate tenderness and dehydration, making it easy to under-recognise (p.2589).","The decision to operate in suspected acute mesenteric ischaemia is often based on a high index of suspicion from history and examination despite normal laboratory results (p.2588)."],"pathophysiology":"Intestinal ischaemia occurs when splanchnic perfusion fails to meet the bowel's metabolic demand, producing ischaemic tissue injury. The gut is supplied by the celiac artery, SMA and IMA with extensive collateralisation between major trunks and mesenteric arcades; protective responses include this collateral network, autoregulation of flow and the ability to increase oxygen extraction. Watershed colonic zones with inherently lower flow are Griffiths' point (splenic flexure) and Sudeck's point (descending/sigmoid colon), the commonest sites of colonic ischaemia. The splanchnic bed can receive up to 30% of cardiac output. Occlusive ischaemia results from an embolus or progressive thrombosis in a major mesenteric artery (over 75% of emboli are cardiac in origin and lodge in the SMA distal to the middle colic artery). Chronic intestinal angina generally requires progressive stenosis of two of the three major vessels. Non-occlusive ischaemia is driven by disproportionate mesenteric arteriolar vasospasm in response to severe physiological stress such as shock; untreated, early mucosal stress ulceration progresses to full-thickness injury. Mesenteric venous thrombosis is usually precipitated by a hypercoagulable state. (p.2587-2588)","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic mesenteric ischaemia / intestinal angina (postprandial pain, weight loss, fear of eating)","Ischaemic colitis (notably after elective or emergent aortic repair)","Gastrointestinal malignancy (must be excluded in chronic presentations)","Superior mesenteric artery syndrome (duodenal compression after weight loss/immobilisation)","Median arcuate ligament syndrome (celiac artery compression)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute intestinal obstruction (mechanical or strangulated bowel)","Other noninflammatory causes of abdominal pain such as biliary colic and neoplasia","Inflammatory abdominal conditions: pancreatitis, cholecystitis, appendicitis, diverticulitis, peptic ulcer, inflammatory bowel disease"],"additionalInvestigations":["Thin-cut (1 mm or thinner) CT angiography is the gold standard for detecting mesenteric arterial occlusive (embolic/thrombotic) disease and for assessing bowel; venous-phase imaging detects mesenteric venous thrombosis with diffuse bowel-wall thickening and splanchnic thrombus (p.2588-2589).","Mesenteric duplex ultrasound is a sensitive/specific screening tool; a high SMA peak flow velocity carries ~80% positive predictive value, and a negative duplex virtually excludes mesenteric ischaemia (test must be done fasting) (p.2588).","Leukocytosis, metabolic acidosis and lactic acidosis support advanced ischaemia but may be absent in reversible ischaemia and cannot reliably distinguish reversible ischaemia from frank necrosis (p.2589).","ECG and echocardiogram may help identify an embolic cardiac source but must not delay surgical therapy (p.2588).","Hypercoagulability workup in suspected mesenteric venous thrombosis (e.g. factor V Leiden, prothrombin mutation, protein C/S or antithrombin deficiency, antiphospholipid syndrome) (p.2587, 2589).","Colonoscopy if ischaemic colitis is suspected, to grade mucosal injury (p.2589)."],"redFlags":["Pain out of proportion to examination with a high-risk cardiac source (AF, recent MI, endocarditis) — embolic acute mesenteric ischaemia until proven otherwise","Later signs of peritonitis and cardiovascular collapse indicate established transmural infarction","Blood per rectum, marked distension or obtundation in a critically ill/shocked patient (suggests non-occlusive ischaemia)","Rising lactate / metabolic acidosis is a late marker and its absence does not exclude ischaemia"],"prognosis":"Acute mesenteric ischaemia carries a high mortality, roughly 50-80% at presentation (with the acute arterial form quoted at over 50%); it is one of the most challenging diagnoses, and the single most important determinant of survival is the timeliness of diagnosis and treatment. Endovascular stenting for chronic mesenteric ischaemia has about an 80% long-term success rate. (p.2587-2588)","pitfalls":["Treating pain out of proportion to examination as benign — early ischaemia produces severe pain with deceptively unremarkable abdominal signs because injury starts in the mucosa (p.2588).","Waiting for abnormal labs before acting — lactate and acidosis are late and may be normal in reversible ischaemia or even necrosis; the operative decision is often made on clinical suspicion despite normal results (p.2588-2589).","Overlooking non-occlusive ischaemia in obtunded/critically ill patients where examination findings are absent or obscured by the underlying illness (p.2589).","Missing the vaguer, subacute presentation of mesenteric venous thrombosis (non-specific pain, nausea, distension) (p.2589).","Failing to perform mesenteric duplex while fasting — bowel gas obscures flow and the post-prandial vasodilatory response (p.2588)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison classifies intestinal ischaemia into chronic mesenteric ischaemia (CMI) and acute mesenteric ischaemia (AMI). Acute mesenteric vascular insufficiency is subdivided into (1) arterio-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia — further split into arterial embolus (~50%) and arterial thrombosis (~25-30%, often acute-on-chronic), (2) non-occlusive/vasospastic mesenteric ischaemia (~20%, vasospasm or hypoperfusion), and (3) mesenteric venous thrombosis (<10%). Ischaemic colitis mucosal injury is graded as mild (minimal erythema), moderate (pale ulceration extending into muscularis), or severe (black/green discoloration with full-thickness necrosis). (p.2587-2589)","references":["Harrison 22e p.2586","Harrison 22e p.2587","Harrison 22e p.2588","Harrison 22e p.2589"],"crossLinks":["atrial_fib","bowel_obstruction","pancreatitis","aaa"]},"metabolic_enceph":{"name":"Metabolic encephalopathy","system":"Neuro / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.181-186 (Confusion and Delirium, Chap. 29); p.188-190 (Coma, Chap. 30); p.2348-2349 (Metabolic / sepsis-associated encephalopathy, Critical Care)","clinicalPearls":["Delirium is an acute, relatively rapid decline in cognition (hours to days) that characteristically fluctuates; the cardinal neuropsychological deficit is inattention, though memory, executive function, visuospatial skills and language are variably affected (p.181-182).","It is a purely clinical bedside diagnosis. The validated Confusion Assessment Method (CAM) requires (1) acute onset with fluctuating course and (2) inattention, plus either (3) disorganized thinking or (4) altered level of consciousness (p.184).","Recognize two ends of a spectrum: a hyperactive subtype (classic example: delirium tremens, with agitation, hallucinations, tremor, autonomic instability) and a hypoactive subtype (e.g., benzodiazepine intoxication, with apathy and psychomotor slowing); hypoactive cases are frequently overlooked, especially on wards and in the ICU (p.182).","Bilateral asterixis and multifocal myoclonus on motor exam are non-focal signs that, while nonspecific, usually point to a metabolic or toxic encephalopathy / drug intoxication rather than a structural lesion (p.184, p.190).","A simple bedside test of attention is forward digit span: healthy adults manage 5-7 digits, while a span of 4 or fewer suggests an attentional deficit; many delirious patients can repeat only 3 or fewer (p.184).","Worsening in the evening (sundowning) is typical but not required; because the course fluctuates, a single morning assessment can be falsely reassuring and serial examinations are needed (p.184).","About one-third of delirium cases are drug-related, especially agents with anticholinergic or sedative properties, narcotics and benzodiazepines; nearly any drug can precipitate delirium in a predisposed patient (p.184).","Delirium can act as a 'stress test for the brain' - a minor insult (UTI, elective surgery, hospitalization) that unmasks delirium in a previously well person should prompt later outpatient screening for an unrecognized underlying neurodegenerative or cerebrovascular disorder (p.183).","The MMSE is unreliable in delirium because tasks such as spelling 'world' backward and serial subtraction are corrupted by the underlying attentional deficit (p.184)."],"pathophysiology":"Delirium reflects diffuse rather than focal brain dysfunction; the attentional deficit localizes broadly to brainstem, thalamus, prefrontal cortex and parietal lobes, and most cases arise from widespread cortical and subcortical disturbance rather than a single lesion. Acetylcholine deficiency is thought to be central (explaining the potency of anticholinergic drugs), with additional roles for excess dopamine, proinflammatory mediators and individual susceptibility. The concept is that of an insult acting on a vulnerable, low-reserve brain. For most metabolic causes (hypoglycaemia, hyponatraemia, hyperosmolarity, hypercapnia, hypercalcaemia, hepatic and renal failure) the brain shows little or no structural damage and the effects are largely reversible, attributed to impaired energy supply, altered ion fluxes across neuronal membranes and neurotransmitter abnormalities. In hepatic encephalopathy, raised ammonia is proposed to drive astrocytic glutamine synthesis and osmotic swelling, mitochondrial energy failure, reactive nitrogen/oxygen species, increased inhibitory GABA tone and 'false' neurotransmitters, with chronic disease producing diffuse astrocytosis. Uraemic encephalopathy is multifactorial (urea itself is not the cause): accumulated neurotoxins, catecholamine depletion, altered glutamate/GABA tone, raised brain calcium and blood-brain-barrier disruption. Importantly, the degree of neurologic change in metabolic encephalopathies depends heavily on the rapidity with which the serum derangement develops, not just its absolute level. In sepsis-associated encephalopathy, circulating inflammatory mediators (TNF, IL-1, IL-2, IL-6) appear to cause diffuse cerebral dysfunction that is not simply explained by the metabolic derangements of organ failure (p.183, p.188-189, p.2348-2349).","additionalDifferentials":["Dementia and pre-existing cognitive impairment - the major predisposing condition; at least two-thirds of delirium occurs in patients with underlying dementia, and the two coexist and overlap (p.183).","Dementia with Lewy bodies - fluctuating course, prominent visual hallucinations and parkinsonism can closely mimic hyperactive delirium (p.183).","Nonconvulsive status epilepticus and recurrent seizures with prolonged postictal confusion (p.185).","Focal stroke or haemorrhage in nondominant (right) parietal lobe or medial dorsal thalamus - rare structural cause of an acute confusional state (p.184-185).","Hypertensive encephalopathy / posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome (PRES) and eclampsia (p.184, p.190).","Diffuse neoplastic disease - multiple brain metastases, diffuse glioma, CNS lymphoma, carcinomatous meningitis (p.184).","Postoperative encephalopathy from a shower of microemboli, particularly after cardiac/cardiopulmonary bypass surgery (p.2348).","Acute primary psychiatric disturbance, depression and catatonia - must be distinguished, particularly from the quiet hypoactive subtype (p.184)."],"infectionMimics":["Systemic infections - urinary tract infection, pneumonia and other respiratory infections, skin/soft tissue infection and frank sepsis; a UTI precipitating acute confusion in an elderly patient with baseline dementia is a classic scenario (p.184).","CNS infections - bacterial meningitis, encephalitis (including herpes simplex) and brain abscess; less common causes but high morbidity/mortality if missed (p.184).","Sepsis-associated ('septic') encephalopathy - diffuse cytokine-mediated brain dysfunction occurring in the majority of patients with sepsis and multiorgan failure (p.184, p.2348-2349).","Severe systemic infections causing coma - septicaemia, typhoid fever, malaria (including cerebral malaria), Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome (p.190)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Anticholinergic, opioid, sedative-hypnotic and other prescription drug toxicity, and drugs of abuse (alcohol, opiates, MDMA/ecstasy, GHB, ketamine/PCP, cocaine, 'bath salts', synthetic cannabinoids) (p.184).","Alcohol and benzodiazepine withdrawal - to be considered in all delirium including the elderly, since even modest daily drinkers can withdraw on admission (p.184).","Poison/toxin exposure - carbon monoxide, ethylene glycol, inhalants, pesticides (p.184).","Endocrinopathies - hyper- or hypothyroidism, hyperparathyroidism, adrenal insufficiency / Addisonian crisis (p.184, p.190).","Hypothermia and hyperthermia (p.184, p.190).","Global cerebral hypoperfusion from hypotension - heart failure, septic shock, severe dehydration or anaemia (p.184-185).","Autoimmune and paraneoplastic disorders - CNS vasculitis, cerebral lupus, autoimmune encephalitis (anti-NMDA-receptor encephalitis shows a characteristic 'extreme delta brush' EEG) (p.184, p.190).","Sedative drug overdose mimicking structural brainstem disease or even brain death (e.g., barbiturates) - toxic causes must be excluded before diagnosing brain death (p.189)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Complete blood count and full electrolyte panel including magnesium and phosphorus (p.185).","Serum/urine toxicology screen - performed earlier in younger patients (p.185).","TSH and free T4, and serum cortisol for suspected endocrinopathy (p.185).","Vitamin levels - B12, folate, thiamine (p.185).","EEG - essential when seizures are suspected or when no cause is found after initial workup; shows nonspecific diffuse symmetric slowing in encephalopathy, frontal high-voltage slow or triphasic waves in metabolic (e.g., hepatic) coma, and can reveal nonconvulsive status epilepticus (p.184-185, p.190).","Brain imaging - MRI with diffusion and gadolinium is the preferred study (CT often used first for speed/availability) to exclude structural causes; note imaging is frequently unrevealing in pure metabolic delirium (p.185, p.189-190).","Lumbar puncture (after neuroimaging) when CNS infection, autoimmune, inflammatory or neoplastic disease is suspected, or in any delirium with a negative workup (p.185, p.190).","Inflammatory and autoimmune serologies - ESR, ANA, complement, p-/c-ANCA, and paraneoplastic/autoimmune encephalitis panels in serum and CSF when indicated (p.185).","ECG and infectious serologies (RPR, HIV; fungal/viral if high suspicion) as second-tier tests (p.185)."],"redFlags":["New focal neurologic deficit, meningismus, fever or papilloedema - point away from a simple metabolic cause toward a structural, infectious or raised-ICP process needing urgent imaging/LP (p.184, p.189-190).","Suspected nonconvulsive status epilepticus - if suspicion is high, EEG should be obtained immediately as it is treatable and easily missed in a fluctuating patient (p.185).","Sodium <125 mmol/L (especially if acute) causing confusion, and <119 mmol/L acutely associated with coma and convulsions (p.188-189).","In-hospital delirium carries a 25-33% mortality (comparable to sepsis) and a roughly fivefold higher mortality in following months - it signals serious underlying illness, not a benign transient state (p.182).","Delirium in the ICU should be regarded as organ dysfunction on a par with liver, kidney or heart failure, and is associated with worse outcomes (p.182, p.2348)."],"prognosis":"Once viewed as benign and transient, delirium is now recognized as a marker of serious illness with substantial morbidity and mortality: in-hospital mortality is roughly 25-33% (similar to sepsis), and delirious patients have about a fivefold higher mortality in the ensuing months versus matched non-delirious inpatients, plus longer stays, more nursing-home discharge, higher readmission and recurrent episodes. Most metabolic and toxic encephalopathies are reversible with no or minimal structural brain damage, and drug/toxin-induced coma typically resolves fully provided hypoxia or severe hypotension has not occurred. However, some episodes persist for weeks to months or longer, delirium can cause lasting cognitive decline, and lingering effects (ranging from amnesia for the episode to PTSD-like re-experiencing) are common. Sepsis-associated encephalopathy severe enough to cause coma carries a mortality approaching 50%, though this mainly reflects severity of the underlying critical illness rather than the encephalopathy itself (p.182, p.188-189, p.2349).","pitfalls":["Treating the symptoms of delirium pharmacologically while neglecting the underlying cause merely prolongs the confused state and can mask important diagnostic clues (p.185).","Missing nonconvulsive status epilepticus - its fluctuating presentation mimics ordinary delirium, and only EEG will reveal it (p.185).","Diagnosing delirium without knowledge of the patient's cognitive baseline, so that dementia or long-standing depression is mistaken for an acute confusional state - collateral history is essential (p.184).","Overlooking the quiet hypoactive subtype, which is commonly missed and may be wrongly attributed to depression or sedation (p.182, p.2348).","Attributing coma to incidental imaging findings (small subdural, old stroke) when the true cause is intoxication; a normal CT does not exclude an anatomic lesion, and most coma/confusion is metabolic or toxic in origin (p.190).","Forgetting that the severity of metabolic encephalopathy depends on how rapidly the derangement developed, so a 'mildly' abnormal value reached acutely can produce marked impairment (p.188-189).","Diagnosing brain death without first excluding sedative/toxic causes (e.g., barbiturates) that can mimic its findings (p.189)."],"severityClassification":"No formal grading scale is described; severity is gauged clinically by the level of consciousness (a continuum from hyperarousal through lethargy to coma) and by the depth of the attentional deficit (e.g., forward digit span). The EEG correlates with severity, showing progressive generalized background slowing as the encephalopathy deepens (p.184, p.190).","references":["Harrison 22e p.181","Harrison 22e p.182","Harrison 22e p.183","Harrison 22e p.184","Harrison 22e p.185","Harrison 22e p.188","Harrison 22e p.189","Harrison 22e p.190","Harrison 22e p.2348","Harrison 22e p.2349"],"crossLinks":["hepatic_enceph","wernicke","hypoglycemia","hyponatremia","seizure_epilepsy"]},"migraine":{"name":"Migraine","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.441 p.3462-3471 (Migraine and Other Primary Headache Disorders); Ch.17 p.113-116 (Headache); Ch.34 p.230 (Disorders of the Eye); Ch.433 p.3379; Ch.437 p.3429","clinicalPearls":["Migraine is the second most common cause of headache and the leading neurologic cause of disability worldwide; over a 1-year period it affects roughly 15% of women and 6% of men (p.3462).","An attack classically evolves through three phases - premonitory (prodrome), headache, and postdrome - and only about 20-25% of patients also experience an aura phase (p.3462).","Premonitory symptoms (yawning, sleepiness, fatigue, cognitive 'fog', mood change, neck discomfort, polyuria/polydipsia, food cravings) can begin hours to days before the pain; some apparent 'triggers' such as light sensitivity may actually be part of this early phase rather than true precipitants (p.3462).","Postdromal tiredness, poor concentration and mild neck discomfort can persist for hours and occasionally up to a day after the pain settles (p.3462).","Simplified diagnostic criteria require repeated attacks of headache lasting 4-72 h with a normal examination and no other reasonable cause, plus at least 2 of (unilateral, throbbing, aggravated by movement, moderate/severe) and at least 1 of (nausea/vomiting, photophobia and phonophobia) (Table 441-3, p.3466).","Cranial autonomic symptoms (lacrimation, conjunctival injection, nasal congestion, rhinorrhea, periorbital swelling, ptosis) can occur in migraine - even in children - and may be mistaken for sinus disease, leading to overdiagnosis of sinusitis and inappropriate treatment (p.114).","Migraine is a brain disorder, not a 'vascular headache'; vascular changes when present are secondary, driven by cranial parasympathetic activation, and treatment response cannot be predicted by vascular effects (p.114).","The vast majority of patients presenting with severe headache have a benign cause, most often migraine; brain tumor is a rare cause of isolated headache and an even rarer cause of severe pain (p.114).","Migraineurs do not encounter more stress than headache-free people; the problem is overresponsiveness to changes in stress, and they are characteristically sensitive to environmental/sensory stimuli, failing to habituate easily (p.3462, p.3508).","Vertigo can be a prominent migraine feature - about one-third of patients referred for vertigo/dizziness have a primary diagnosis of migraine (vestibular migraine) (p.3464)."],"pathophysiology":"Migraine is understood as a brain disorder of sensory processing rather than a primary vascular disease. The characteristic sensory sensitivity is attributed to dysfunction of monoaminergic and other sensory-control systems in the brainstem and hypothalamus. The key nociceptive pathway is the trigeminovascular system: trigeminal innervation of large intracranial vessels and dura, with terminals that, when activated, release vasoactive neuropeptides - notably calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP) and pituitary adenylate cyclase-activating polypeptide (PACAP) - at vascular terminals and within the trigeminal nucleus. Second-order neurons of the trigeminocervical complex (caudal trigeminal nucleus extending into the upper cervical dorsal horns, receiving C1-C2 input) decussate and project via the quintothalamic tract to ventrobasal and posterior thalamic nuclei, with additional projections to periaqueductal gray and hypothalamus that exert descending antinociceptive modulation; the dorsal raphe nucleus, locus coeruleus, parabrachial nucleus and rostroventromedial medulla are implicated in this modulation. Serotonin (5-HT) is involved - triptans are 5-HT1B/1D agonists (some also 5-HT1F) and ditans are selective 5-HT1F agonists - and dopamine contributes, with dopamine-receptor hypersensitivity in migraineurs. Functional imaging shows hypothalamic activation in the premonitory phase and persistent dorsolateral pontine (including locus coeruleus) activation during attacks and in chronic migraine, with lateralization correlating with the side of head pain. Genetic study of familial hemiplegic migraine implicates ion channels (CACNA1A/Cav2.1 P/Q calcium channel in FHM1 ~50%; ATP1A2 Na-K ATPase in FHM2 ~20%; SCN1A sodium channel in FHM3), suggesting altered membrane excitability predisposes to aura. The slowly advancing visual scotoma with luminous (fortification) edges is recognized as a marker of spreading cortical depression associated with migraine (p.3462-3464, p.3379).","additionalDifferentials":["Tension-type headache - the main differential; distinguished by being completely featureless (no nausea/vomiting, photophobia, phonophobia, osmophobia, throbbing or aggravation with movement), whereas migraine has one or more of these (p.3471).","Cluster headache and other trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias (p.3462).","Acephalgic migraine / typical aura without headache - recurrent neurologic symptoms with little or no headache (p.3464).","Vestibular migraine - common, underdiagnosed cause of episodic vertigo lasting minutes to hours, sometimes without headache (p.3464).","Migraine with brainstem aura (formerly basilar/basilar-type migraine) (p.3464).","Medication-overuse headache complicating an underlying migraine tendency (p.115-116)."],"infectionMimics":["Bacterial/viral meningitis - acute severe headache with stiff neck and fever; can be mistaken for migraine because pounding headache, photophobia, nausea and vomiting are shared cardinal features, and there is often striking accentuation of pain on eye movement; lumbar puncture is mandatory (p.113-114)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Subarachnoid hemorrhage / ruptured aneurysm, AVM or intraparenchymal hemorrhage - thunderclap headache maximal in under 5 min lasting over 5 min, stiff neck without fever; CT may rarely be normal so LP may be needed (p.114).","Brain tumor - intermittent deep dull aching, may worsen with exertion or position change and cause nausea/vomiting; vomiting preceding headache by weeks is highly characteristic of posterior fossa tumors (p.114).","Temporal (giant cell) arteritis - dull/boring headache with superimposed stabbing pains, scalp tenderness, jaw claudication, polymyalgia, fever, weight loss in patients over ~50; migraineurs may report relief with prednisone, confounding interpretation of treatment response (p.114-115).","Acute angle-closure glaucoma - prostrating headache with nausea/vomiting beginning with severe eye pain; red eye with a fixed, mid-dilated pupil (p.115).","Transient ischemic attack / vertebrobasilar insufficiency - acute homonymous visual symptoms with fogging/graying and positive phenomena that can mimic migraine aura (p.230).","Ischemic stroke - migraine can mimic stroke even without prior migraine history, especially acephalgic migraine (p.3429)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Migraine is a clinical diagnosis in patients with a normal examination and no other reasonable cause for headache (Table 441-3, p.3466).","A headache diary aids diagnosis and quantifies disability and attack frequency (p.3462).","The Migraine Disability Assessment Score (MIDAS) is a validated tool to grade disability (p.3465).","CT or MRI of the brain (equally sensitive as initial screen for intracranial pathology) is indicated for an abnormal neurologic examination or recent-onset headache; lumbar puncture may additionally be required when a benign cause cannot otherwise be established (p.113).","General acute-headache workup may include palpation of cranial arteries, assessment of the cervical spine, blood pressure and urine examination, and funduscopy with intraocular pressure measurement (p.113)."],"redFlags":["Sudden-onset (thunderclap) headache or first/worst-ever severe headache (p.114).","Vomiting that precedes the headache (p.114).","Subacute worsening over days or weeks (p.114).","Pain induced by bending, lifting or cough (p.114).","Pain that disturbs sleep or is present immediately on awakening (p.114).","New headache in a patient with a known systemic illness (e.g., malignancy) (p.114).","Onset after age 55 (p.114).","Fever or unexplained systemic signs (p.114).","Abnormal neurologic examination (p.114).","Pain with local tenderness, e.g., over the temporal artery (p.114)."],"prognosis":"Patients should understand that migraine is an inherited tendency to headache that can be modified and controlled by lifestyle measures and medication but not eradicated. Except on some occasions in women taking oral estrogens or contraceptives, migraine is not associated with serious or life-threatening illness (p.3464). Migraine is nonetheless the most common neurologic cause of disability worldwide (p.3462). Recognized complications (ICHD-3) include status migrainosus, persistent aura without infarction, migrainous infarction, and migraine aura-triggered seizure; migraine may also progress to chronic migraine (Table 441-1, p.3463). Overuse of acute analgesics can transform episodic migraine into refractory medication-overuse headache; many patients improve after withdrawal, though residual headache often persists reflecting the underlying primary disorder (p.115-116).","pitfalls":["Mistaking migraine-related cranial autonomic symptoms for sinusitis, leading to overdiagnosis and inappropriate sinus treatment (p.114).","Misdiagnosing meningitis as migraine (or vice versa) because pounding headache, photophobia, nausea and vomiting overlap - hence LP is mandatory when fever and stiff neck are present (p.113-114).","Attributing a steroid-responsive headache in an elderly patient to giant cell arteritis when migraine (far more prevalent in the elderly) also improves with prednisone (p.115).","Treating headache from a reawakened migraine syndrome (e.g., after otologic or endodontic procedures) without first addressing the primary problem - the headache stays refractory until the cause is treated (p.113).","Failing to recognize acephalgic migraine, which can present de novo even after age 65 and is easily confused with stroke; the sensory/motor deficit migrates slowly across a limb over minutes (versus seconds in stroke) (p.3429).","Continuing acute analgesics while starting a preventive - a common cause of treatment failure and of medication-overuse headache (p.115-116)."],"severityClassification":"ICHD-3 (IHS 2018) classifies migraine into migraine without aura, migraine with aura (typical aura with/without headache, migraine with brainstem aura, hemiplegic migraine including FHM1-3 and sporadic, retinal migraine), chronic migraine, complications of migraine (status migrainosus, persistent aura without infarction, migrainous infarction, migraine aura-triggered seizure), probable migraine, and episodic syndromes that may be associated with migraine (cyclical vomiting, abdominal migraine, benign paroxysmal vertigo/torticollis, infantile colic, vestibular migraine). Chronic migraine is defined as migraine on 8 or more days per month with at least 15 total headache days per month (Table 441-1, p.3463; p.3462). Disability is graded by MIDAS: Grade I 0-5, Grade II 6-10, Grade III 11-20, Grade IV over 20 (Fig. 441-4, p.3466).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3462","Harrison 22e p.3463","Harrison 22e p.3464","Harrison 22e p.3465","Harrison 22e p.3466","Harrison 22e p.3471","Harrison 22e p.113","Harrison 22e p.114","Harrison 22e p.115","Harrison 22e p.116","Harrison 22e p.230","Harrison 22e p.3379","Harrison 22e p.3429"],"crossLinks":["tension_ha","sah","MENINGITIS","temporal_arteritis","ischemic_stroke","tia"]},"ms":{"name":"Multiple sclerosis (relapse)","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e, Ch. 455 (Multiple Sclerosis), p.3577-3589; Ch. 456 (Neuromyelitis Optica) p.3590","clinicalPearls":["MS is defined by lesions disseminated in both time and space: plaques arise at different times and in anatomically separate CNS sites (p.3577).","Typical onset is age 20-40 (slightly later in men); women are affected roughly three times as often as men (p.3577).","The most common presenting symptoms are sensory loss (~37%) and optic neuritis (~36%), followed by limb weakness (~35%) and paresthesias (p.3577).","Optic neuritis usually causes monocular reduced acuity, dimness, and color desaturation, with periorbital pain worsened by eye movement and an afferent pupillary defect; uveitis is uncommon and should prompt thought of sarcoidosis or lymphoma (p.3577).","A bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia (impaired adduction with nystagmus of the abducting eye, from a medial longitudinal fasciculus lesion) is particularly suggestive of MS; related gaze findings include horizontal gaze palsy and the 'one-and-a-half' syndrome (p.3578).","Lhermitte's symptom is a neck-flexion-induced electric-shock sensation radiating down the spine/legs; it is not specific to MS and can occur with other cervical cord disease such as spondylosis (p.3578).","Uhthoff's symptom (heat sensitivity) is transient worsening of neurologic symptoms with elevated core temperature (hot shower, exercise, fever) and reflects temperature-dependent conduction block rather than a true new lesion (p.3578).","Exercise-induced weakness is characteristic; weakness is of upper-motor-neuron type with spasticity, hyperreflexia, and extensor plantar responses, though a tendon reflex can occasionally be lost if a plaque disrupts the afferent reflex arc (p.3578).","Paroxysmal symptoms (tonic spasms, paroxysmal dysarthria/ataxia, sensory bursts) are brief (10 s-2 min), frequent (5-40/day), without altered consciousness, and self-limited; they arise from spontaneous discharges at the edges of demyelinated plaques (p.3578).","Trigeminal neuralgia, hemifacial spasm, or glossopharyngeal neuralgia in a young patient, or with bilateral/atypical features or objective sensory loss, should raise suspicion of MS involving the cranial nerve root entry zone (p.3578).","Fatigue is experienced by most patients and is the leading cause of work-related disability in MS; depression affects about half of patients (p.3578).","On MRI, MS plaques are oriented perpendicular to the ventricular surface (Dawson's fingers) reflecting perivenous demyelination; lesions >6 mm in the corpus callosum, periventricular white matter, brainstem, cerebellum, or cord are especially helpful diagnostically (p.3583).","A central vein sign within plaques on susceptibility-weighted (T2*) imaging supports an MS diagnosis (p.3583).","Two or more discrete CSF oligoclonal bands not present in paired serum are found in >90% of MS patients; bands may be absent at onset and increase over time (p.3583).","Evoked potential abnormalities occur in 80-90% of MS patients; a marked latency delay (rather than reduced amplitude or distorted waveform) is what suggests demyelination, and EPs are most informative when testing a clinically uninvolved pathway (p.3583-3584).","Most MRI-visible lesions and most early disease activity in MS are clinically silent—bursts of focal inflammation on serial MRI far exceed the relapse rate (p.3583).","Even relapse-free RRMS patients undergo subtle 'silent progression,' and disability accumulation is now attributed mainly to progression independent of relapse activity (PIRA) rather than incomplete recovery from relapses (RAW), across RRMS, SPMS, and PPMS (p.3578-3580).","A radiologically isolated syndrome (RIS) is incidental MRI evidence of MS in an asymptomatic person; clinically isolated syndrome (CIS) is a first demyelinating event at high risk for MS (p.3577-3580)."],"pathophysiology":"MS is an autoimmune CNS disease producing chronic inflammation, demyelination, gliosis (sclerotic plaques) and neuronal/axonal loss (p.3577). New lesions begin with perivenular cuffing by T cells and macrophages and blood-brain-barrier (BBB) disruption with a preserved vessel wall (unlike vasculitis); cytotoxic CD8 cells line the leading edge, B cells infiltrate, myelin-specific autoantibodies coat degenerating myelin, and complement is activated (p.3581). Sharply demarcated demyelination is the hallmark; axons are relatively spared early but can be destroyed in highly inflammatory lesions, and partial remyelination produces 'shadow plaques' (p.3581). Cumulative axonal/neuronal loss drives irreversible disability (up to ~70% of corticospinal axons lost in MS paraplegia) via demyelination-related loss of trophic support, ion-channel redistribution, and dying-back axonopathy (p.3581). Progressive MS features more diffuse microglial activation, chronic active (slowly expanding) plaques with microglia at a leading edge behind an intact BBB, cortical/subpial plaques poorly seen on MRI, and meningeal ectopic lymphoid follicles linked to subpial cortical demyelination (p.3581-3582). Physiologically, demyelination causes conduction block (hyperpolarization from exposed potassium channels) and conduction slowing; temperature and metabolic changes worsen block, explaining heat- and fever-related fluctuations (p.3582-3583). Susceptibility is polygenic with the strongest signal at HLA-DRB1*1501 (~10% of risk), and EBV seroconversion appears a near-universal prerequisite, plausibly via molecular mimicry between EBV peptides and myelin autoantigens (p.3580-3581).","additionalDifferentials":["Neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder (NMOSD, anti-AQP4) (p.3584, 3590)","Myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein-associated disease (MOGAD) (p.3584)","Glial fibrillary acidic protein (GFAP) autoimmunity (p.3584)","Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis (ADEM) (p.3584)","Sjogren's syndrome (p.3584-3585)","Sarcoidosis / neurosarcoidosis (p.3584-3585)","Systemic lupus erythematosus and related collagen-vascular disorders (p.3585)","Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome (p.3585)","Primary CNS or systemic vasculitis (p.3585)","Behcet's disease (p.3585)","CADASIL (p.3585)","MELAS (mitochondrial encephalopathy, lactic acidosis, stroke) (p.3585)","Congenital leukodystrophies (e.g., adrenoleukodystrophy, metachromatic leukodystrophy) (p.3585)","Vitamin B12 deficiency (subacute combined degeneration) (p.3585)","Ischemic optic neuropathy (arteritic and nonarteritic) (p.3585)","CNS lymphoma; gliomas/meningiomas (p.3584-3585)","Stroke / ischemic cerebrovascular disease (p.3585)","Spinal dural arteriovenous fistula and other vascular malformations (p.3585)"],"infectionMimics":["HIV infection (p.3585)","Neurosyphilis / syphilis (p.3584-3585)","Lyme disease (p.3584-3585)","Tropical spastic paraparesis (HTLV-1/2 infection) (p.3585)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["NMOSD (anti-AQP4) and MOGAD - consider with bilateral/severe optic neuritis or severe transverse myelitis (p.3584)","ADEM - consider with hyperacute or postinfectious presentation (p.3584)","Tumefactive demyelination mimicking a primary or metastatic brain tumor (p.3584, 3589)","Sarcoidosis, Sjogren's syndrome, SLE, antiphospholipid syndrome, vasculitis (p.3584-3585)","Vitamin B12 deficiency, hereditary leukodystrophies, CADASIL, MELAS (p.3585)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CSF analysis for intrathecal IgG synthesis: CSF IgG index and IgG synthesis rate, plus oligoclonal bands by agarose-gel electrophoresis (>90% positive in MS) (p.3583)","CSF cell count/profile: a mild mononuclear pleocytosis (>5 cells/uL) occurs in ~25% (young relapsing patients); pleocytosis >75 cells/uL, polymorphonuclear cells, or protein >1 g/L argues against MS (p.3583)","Gadolinium-enhanced MRI to detect active (BBB-disrupted) lesions; enhancement typically lasts <1 month (p.3583)","Susceptibility-weighted (T2*) MRI to look for the central vein sign (p.3583)","Quantitative brain and spinal cord atrophy / T2 lesion burden (correlates with disability; cord atrophy correlates more strongly) (p.3583)","T1 imaging for 'black holes' (markers of irreversible demyelination and axonal loss) (p.3583)","Visual, somatosensory, brainstem-auditory, and motor evoked potentials (p.3583-3584)","Workup to exclude mimics in suspected MS: ESR, serum B12, antinuclear antibodies, and treponemal antibody (p.3585)"],"redFlags":["Distinguish a true relapse from a pseudoexacerbation driven by raised ambient temperature, fever, or infection - glucocorticoids are inappropriate for a pseudoexacerbation (p.3585).","Features atypical for MS that should prompt an alternative diagnosis: symptoms confined to posterior fossa/craniocervical junction/spinal cord; onset <15 or >60 years; progressive course from onset; never any visual, sensory, or bladder symptoms; or atypical MRI/CSF/EP findings (p.3584).","Symptoms rare/uncommon in MS that favor another diagnosis: aphasia, parkinsonism, chorea, isolated dementia, severe muscle atrophy, peripheral neuropathy, episodic loss of consciousness, fever, headache, seizures, or coma (p.3584).","Bilateral and/or severe optic neuritis or severe transverse myelitis should raise NMOSD, MOGAD, or GFAP autoimmunity rather than MS (p.3584).","Uveitis is uncommon in MS and should suggest sarcoidosis or lymphoma (p.3577).","Intense inflammatory swelling can produce a mass lesion mimicking tumor (tumefactive MS), sometimes requiring brain biopsy (p.3584, 3589)."],"prognosis":"The clinical course is highly variable. RRMS accounts for ~90% of cases with attacks evolving over days to weeks and substantial early recovery; PPMS (~10%) declines steadily from onset, begins later (mean ~40 years), has a more even sex ratio, and accrues disability faster (p.3578-3580). In the pretreatment era, by 15 years after onset only ~20% had no functional limitation and one-third to one-half of relapsing patients had progressed to SPMS needing ambulatory assistance, with ~80% reaching that level by 25 years; the prior RRMS-to-SPMS conversion rate of ~3%/year has fallen to <1%/year in the high-efficacy-therapy era (p.3579, 3588). Favorable prognostic features (pretreatment data): optic neuritis or sensory onset, fewer than two relapses in the first year, and minimal impairment at 5 years; adverse predictors: older onset age, male sex, greater disability, and motor signs in the first year. About <10% have a benign variant that never produces disability even untreated (p.3588). Pregnancy: fewer attacks during gestation (especially the third trimester) but more in the first 3 months postpartum, with the overall pregnancy-year course unchanged (p.3588).","pitfalls":["Do not over-diagnose MS from a single isolated event or from MRI white-matter lesions alone - the diagnosis requires dissemination in space and time (clinical, MRI, or CSF evidence) per the McDonald criteria, and there is no single confirmatory test (p.3582-3583).","A pseudoexacerbation from heat/fever/infection (Uhthoff phenomenon) can mimic a relapse but reflects transient conduction block, not a new lesion; treating it as a relapse with steroids is inappropriate (p.3578, 3585).","Failing to distinguish NMOSD, MOGAD, and GFAP autoimmunity from MS - clues are bilateral/severe optic neuritis, severe longitudinally extensive transverse myelitis (>=3 vertebral segments), brain MRI lesions that are non-destructive/cloud-like and can fully resolve, and CSF pleocytosis greater than in MS with neutrophils/eosinophils and oligoclonal bands in <20% (p.3584, 3590).","ADEM should be considered for hyperacute or postinfectious demyelinating presentations rather than assuming a first MS attack (p.3584).","MS-associated genetic variants (including HLA-DRB1*1501) lack the sensitivity and specificity to be used for diagnosis (p.3581).","Highly restricted CNS oligoclonal antibodies are characteristic of MS but their specific antigenic targets differ between patients and their pathogenic role is uncertain - they aid diagnosis, not mechanistic certainty (p.3581).","Lhermitte's symptom and trigeminal neuralgia are not specific to MS and occur with other cervical cord/cranial nerve pathology (p.3578)."],"severityClassification":"Course-based subtypes: relapsing-remitting MS (RRMS, ~90%), secondary progressive MS (SPMS, always evolves from RRMS), and primary progressive MS (PPMS, ~10%); 'relapsing MS (RMS)' covers all relapsing patients and 'active progressive MS' denotes SPMS/PPMS with ongoing relapses or new MRI lesions (p.3578-3579). Neurologic impairment is graded with the Expanded Disability Status Scale (EDSS), 0 (normal) to 10 (death), built from Functional Status (FS) subscores; EDSS <3.5 generally indicates normal walking and little disability, whereas EDSS >4.0 indicates gait-impaired progressive (SPMS/PPMS) disease. An EDSS >=4 plus a motor FS score >=2 supports an SPMS diagnosis (p.3579-3580, 3585). Fulminant clinical variants include Marburg's (acute fulminant) MS, tumefactive MS, and Balo's concentric sclerosis (p.3589).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3577","Harrison 22e p.3578","Harrison 22e p.3579","Harrison 22e p.3580","Harrison 22e p.3581","Harrison 22e p.3582","Harrison 22e p.3583","Harrison 22e p.3584","Harrison 22e p.3585","Harrison 22e p.3588","Harrison 22e p.3589","Harrison 22e p.3590"],"crossLinks":["ENCEPHALITIS","cord_compression","gbs"]},"myasthenic_crisis":{"name":"Myasthenic crisis","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e, Chapter 459 (Myasthenia Gravis and Other Diseases of the Neuromuscular Junction), p.3624-3633","clinicalPearls":["Myasthenic crisis is an exacerbation of weakness severe enough to endanger life, typically with ventilatory failure from diaphragmatic and intercostal muscle weakness; by definition, ventilatory weakness requiring intubation or noninvasive ventilation to avoid intubation constitutes crisis, whereas lesser worsening is termed exacerbation (p.3625, 3632).","Weakness is characteristically fatigable: it worsens with repeated use and late in the day and improves with rest or sleep, and strength can improve with serial effort (contrast with LEMS) (p.3625).","Cranial muscles are often involved early, with diplopia and ptosis common initial symptoms; facial weakness gives a 'snarling' expression on attempted smile, and bulbar involvement causes nasal/'mushy' dysarthria, hoarseness, and dysphagia with risk of nasal regurgitation or aspiration (p.3625).","Bulbar, neck, and ventilatory weakness can be especially prominent in MuSK antibody-positive MG; neck extensor weakness can cause head drop (p.3625).","Deep tendon reflexes are typically preserved and sensory symptoms, sensory loss, and pain are absent in MG (p.3625).","Anti-AChR antibodies are present in ~85% of generalized MG and ~50% of ocular MG; a positive test is virtually diagnostic, but a negative test does not exclude MG, and antibody level does not correlate well with severity (p.3627).","About 10% of MG patients (~40% of AChR-negative generalized MG) have anti-MuSK antibodies, and 1-3% have anti-LRP4; ~10% of generalized MG is double-seronegative (p.3625, 3627).","The ice-pack test (ice over a ptotic lid for 2 min, positive if lid rises >=2 mm) is a quick, very sensitive bedside test for an NMJ cause of ptosis (p.3627).","Edrophonium (anticholinesterase) testing was historically used for diagnosis but is no longer used due to side effects and lack of availability (p.3627).","Thymic abnormality occurs in ~75% of AChR-positive patients; thymoma is present in ~10-15% of MG patients, so chest CT/MRI is performed at diagnosis (thymic enlargement in a patient >40 years is highly suspicious for thymoma) (p.3625, 3630)."],"pathophysiology":"MG is an autoimmune neuromuscular junction disorder in which the number of available postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors (AChRs) is reduced and postsynaptic folds are flattened/simplified, decreasing the efficiency of neuromuscular transmission; ACh is released normally but produces small end-plate potentials that may fail to trigger muscle action potentials. The normal decline in ACh release on repeated activity (presynaptic rundown) combined with reduced transmission efficiency activates fewer fibers with successive impulses, producing fatigable weakness (myasthenic fatigue) and the decremental response to repetitive nerve stimulation. Anti-AChR antibodies (IgG, T-cell dependent) reduce receptors by three mechanisms: accelerated AChR turnover via cross-linking/endocytosis, complement-mediated damage to the postsynaptic membrane, and blockade of the ACh-binding site. Anti-MuSK antibodies (IgG4, non-complement-activating) impair MuSK-LRP4 binding and AChR clustering, so MuSK-positive MG does not respond to complement inhibition; anti-LRP4 antibodies (IgG1) cause complement-mediated destruction. Thymic abnormality (hyperplasia in ~65%, thymoma in ~10%) and dysfunction of regulatory T cells contribute to the autoimmune process (p.3624-3626).","additionalDifferentials":["Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (LEMS): presynaptic P/Q-type calcium-channel antibody disorder; proximal lower-limb weakness predominates, reflexes are depressed/absent, autonomic features (dry mouth, orthostasis, impotence) occur, and an incremental response follows high-rate stimulation or brief exercise; associated with small-cell lung cancer in older adults (p.3628).","Congenital myasthenic syndromes (non-autoimmune, >30 genes; usually onset in infancy/childhood, AChR and MuSK antibodies absent) (p.3627-3628).","Drug-induced myasthenia, including penicillamine-associated MG and immune checkpoint inhibitor-related MG (p.3627, 3633).","Botulism (Clostridium botulinum toxin): myasthenia-like bulbar weakness with dilated/poorly reactive pupils and autonomic features; CMAP amplitudes increase after high-frequency stimulation (p.3628).","Hyperthyroidism/Graves disease and thyroid dysfunction, which can also aggravate myasthenic weakness (p.3627-3628, 3630).","Intracranial mass lesion compressing extraocular nerves (e.g., sphenoid ridge meningioma) causing diplopia (p.3628).","Oculopharyngeal dystrophy and progressive external ophthalmoplegia / mitochondrial myopathy (e.g., Kearns-Sayre) (p.3627-3628).","ICI-associated myositis (and myocarditis), which can mimic MG with ocular/bulbar weakness but without a decrementing response on repetitive nerve stimulation (p.3627, 3633)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cholinergic crisis from excessive anticholinesterase medication can mimic worsening MG; it is considered in the differential but is unlikely now because very high cholinesterase-inhibitor doses are rarely used (p.3632).","ICI-associated myositis can clinically resemble MG (prominent or exclusive ocular weakness) but lacks a decrementing response on repetitive nerve stimulation (p.3633)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Repetitive nerve stimulation showing a decrement of >10% at 2-3 Hz in weak/proximal muscles supports MG; acetylcholinesterase inhibitors should be stopped 12-24 h beforehand (p.3627).","Single-fiber EMG (jitter and blocking with normal fiber density) when repetitive stimulation is normal or symptoms are exclusively ocular; confirmatory but not specific (p.3627).","Ice-pack test for improvement in ptosis (very sensitive) (p.3627).","Anti-MuSK and (if AChR/MuSK negative with clear MG phenotype) anti-LRP4 antibody testing (p.3627).","Chest CT or MRI to evaluate for thymoma/thymic abnormality at diagnosis (p.3627, 3630).","Thyroid function tests, antinuclear antibodies, and rheumatoid factor to detect associated/exacerbating conditions; testing for tuberculosis, fasting glucose/HbA1c, and bone densitometry before immunotherapy (p.3630).","MRI of the brain/orbits to exclude intracranial lesions in ocular or cranial presentations (p.3627-3628)."],"redFlags":["Ventilatory failure from diaphragmatic/intercostal weakness is the life-threatening feature defining crisis; need for intubation or noninvasive ventilation to avoid intubation marks the transition from exacerbation to crisis (p.3625, 3632).","Intercurrent infection is the most common cause of crisis and must be treated immediately, as the patient's mechanical and immunologic defenses are compromised; fever with early infection should be managed as in an immunocompromised patient (p.3632).","Exposure to drugs that exacerbate weakness (e.g., aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones, macrolides, beta-blockers, magnesium, procainamide, quinine derivatives, neuromuscular blocking agents) can precipitate or worsen crisis (p.3633).","Tapering of MG therapies, unrelated infections, systemic disorders, or surgery can precipitate exacerbation or crisis; some exacerbations have no identifiable precipitant (p.3625).","Prominent bulbar/neck weakness (notably in MuSK-positive MG) signals aspiration and airway risk (p.3625)."],"prognosis":"Mortality from MG has fallen dramatically over the last century (from ~70% a century ago to ~2-30% by the 1950s) to contemporary estimates of ~1-5%. About 20% of patients achieve sustained remission and can be tapered off immunotherapy, with no clear correlation between disease severity and likelihood of remission. Anti-MuSK MG was historically harder to treat, but rituximab has improved its prognosis. Most patients can be returned to full productive lives with proper therapy (p.3629, 3633).","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish myasthenic crisis from cholinergic crisis (deterioration due to excessive anticholinesterase medication); the latter is now unlikely because very high cholinesterase-inhibitor doses are rarely used but should still be considered (p.3632).","Treating before the diagnosis is confirmed: confirmation is essential because other treatable conditions closely resemble MG and treatment involves surgery and long-term drugs with side effects (p.3625).","Interpreting a negative anti-AChR antibody test as excluding MG, or using antibody level to gauge severity (it correlates poorly) (p.3627).","Overlooking that anti-MuSK MG generally responds poorly to anticholinesterase agents and may actually worsen with them (p.3629).","Ordering anti-striated muscle (titin) antibodies expecting them to confirm MG or thymoma: they are non-pathogenic and do not confirm either (p.3627)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison distinguishes ocular MG (weakness confined to ocular muscles; if restricted for 3 years, generalization is unlikely) from generalized MG (facial, bulbar, axial, or limb involvement, occurring in ~85%). Worsening is graded as exacerbation (any deterioration short of needing ventilatory support) versus myasthenic crisis (life-threatening weakness, usually with ventilatory failure requiring intubation or noninvasive ventilation to avoid intubation) (p.3625, 3632).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3624","Harrison 22e p.3625","Harrison 22e p.3626","Harrison 22e p.3627","Harrison 22e p.3628","Harrison 22e p.3629","Harrison 22e p.3630","Harrison 22e p.3632","Harrison 22e p.3633"],"crossLinks":["gbs","thyroid_storm"]},"myeloma":{"name":"Multiple myeloma","system":"Hematology / Oncology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.116 Plasma Cell Disorders, p.883-890 (PDF PAGE 926-933); hypercalcemia of malignancy Ch.57","clinicalPearls":["Bone pain is the most common presenting symptom, affecting nearly 70% of patients; lesions are purely lytic (osteoblast suppression by DKK-1), so a radioisotope bone scan is LESS useful than plain radiography (p.886-887).","Serum alkaline phosphatase is typically NORMAL despite extensive bony disease because osteoblastic activity is suppressed (p.889).","Myeloma bone lesions are classically 'punched-out' lytic lesions (e.g. skull); diffuse osteopenia may also occur (p.887-889).","A reduced or negative anion gap may be a clue: the cationic M protein causes chloride retention; an associated pseudohyponatremia reflects reduced serum water from the high protein load (p.887).","Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is characteristically elevated (p.889).","Urine dipstick is unreliable for light chains and the heat test for Bence Jones protein is falsely negative in ~50% of light-chain myeloma; a 24-h urine and serum free light chains are needed (p.888-889).","About 20% of myelomas secrete only light chains; <1% are truly non-secretory (M protein undetectable even by serum free light chain assay) (p.884, p.888-889).","Median age at diagnosis is ~69 years; uncommon under age 40; nearly twice the incidence in Black versus White populations; male predominance (p.885).","Susceptibility to infection is driven by hypogammaglobulinemia (of the non-M-component immunoglobulins), impaired antibody responses to polysaccharide antigens, and granulocyte/complement/T-cell dysfunction (p.886).","Most common infections are pneumonia (S. pneumoniae, S. aureus, K. pneumoniae) and pyelonephritis/UTI (E. coli and other gram-negatives) (p.886).","Renal light-chain cast nephropathy, light chain deposition disease, and AL amyloidosis are at least partially reversible with effective antimyeloma therapy (p.887).","Patients are prone to acute kidney injury when dehydrated or after iodinated contrast; NSAIDs and hypercalcemia further worsen renal function (p.887).","Plasma cell leukemia (>2000 circulating plasma cells/microL) is rare (~1%) but disproportionately frequent in IgD (12%) and IgE (25%) myeloma (p.889)."],"pathophysiology":"Multiple myeloma is a malignant proliferation of a single plasma-cell clone. Myeloma cells adhere to bone marrow stromal cells and extracellular matrix, triggering cytokine loops (IL-6, IGF-1, VEGF) and signaling (Ras/Raf/MAPK, PI3K/Akt, PKC) that drive growth, survival, drug resistance, and migration. Osteoclast-activating factors (RANK ligand, MIP-1alpha, IL-1, lymphotoxin, TNF, VEGF) increase bone resorption while DKK-1 suppresses osteoblasts, producing purely lytic bone disease, hypercalcemia, fractures, and cord compression. The immune microenvironment is remodeled (expansion of MDSCs, plasmacytoid dendritic cells, TH17; suppression of effective antimyeloma T/B/NKT responses). Recurrent cytogenetic events include hyperdiploidy (trisomies) in ~half, and 14q32 IGH translocations [t(11;14), t(4;14), t(14;16)] in the rest, plus del13q, 1q amplification/1p deletion, and del17p; KRAS/NRAS are the most frequently mutated genes (~20% each), with TP53, DIS3, FAM46C, BRAF in 5-10%. No single unifying mutation exists; subclonal heterogeneity present at diagnosis drives genomic evolution. All myeloma is preceded by MGUS (p.883-885).","additionalDifferentials":["Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS) - serum M protein <30 g/L, clonal marrow plasma cells <10%, no myeloma-defining events; ~1%/yr progression (p.887, Table 116-1)","Smoldering (asymptomatic) multiple myeloma - M protein >=30 g/L or urine M protein >=500 mg/24h and/or marrow plasma cells 10-60%, without myeloma-defining events (p.887-888, Table 116-1)","Solitary bone plasmacytoma and solitary extramedullary plasmacytoma (often younger patients; median survival >=10 years; M component in <30%) (p.888)","Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia / other IgM monoclonal gammopathies (p.883)","Primary (AL) amyloidosis (p.883, Ch.117)","POEMS syndrome (polyneuropathy, organomegaly, endocrinopathy, M-protein, skin changes; sclerotic bone lesions) (Table 116-1)","Reactive/secondary causes of an M component: CLL and B-/T-cell lymphomas, CML, breast and colon cancer, cirrhosis, sarcoidosis, parasitic disease, Gaucher disease, rheumatoid arthritis, myasthenia gravis, cold agglutinin disease, post-transplant (p.883)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Metastatic carcinoma with lytic bone lesions (e.g. breast) (p.887)","Other causes of hypercalcemia of malignancy (PTHrP-mediated); myeloma must be specifically excluded with serum free light chains as ~3% are missed by serum/urine electrophoresis (Ch.57, p.349-350 region)","Other causes of marrow-infiltrative normocytic anemia in the elderly (p.886)","MGUS or smoldering myeloma misclassified as active disease without myeloma-defining events (p.887)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Bone marrow aspirate AND core biopsy with kappa/lambda clonality (flow cytometry or immunohistochemistry); marrow plasma cells are CD138+ (p.887, Table 116-2)","Serum protein electrophoresis with immunofixation; quantitative IgG/IgA/IgM (p.888-889, Table 116-2)","Serum free light chains and involved:uninvolved ratio (p.888-889, Table 116-2)","24-h urine protein electrophoresis and immunofixation to quantify Bence Jones protein (p.888-889, Table 116-2)","Immunofixation for IgD or IgE in selected cases (p.889, Table 116-2)","Chemistry panel: calcium, creatinine/urea, uric acid; CBC; LDH (p.889, Table 116-2)","Serum beta-2-microglobulin and albumin for ISS staging (p.889, Table 116-2/116-3)","FISH or DNA sequencing for del17p/TP53, t(4;14), t(11;14), t(14;16), t(14;20), amp1q, del1p, del13 (p.889, Table 116-2/116-3)","Whole-body skeletal imaging: skeletal survey, low-dose CT, or 18F-FDG PET/CT (PET/CT standard to distinguish smoldering from active disease and to confirm solitary plasmacytoma); MRI spine/pelvis for marrow infiltration and cord/root compression (p.889, Table 116-2)","Serum viscosity if IgM, high IgA, or serum M component >7 g/dL (p.889, Table 116-2)","Abdominal fat-pad aspirate for amyloid in selected cases (p.889, Table 116-2)"],"redFlags":["Spinal cord compression from vertebral collapse or plasmacytoma - radicular pain, loss of bowel/bladder control (oncologic emergency) (p.887)","Symptomatic hypercalcemia - lethargy, weakness, confusion, AKI (p.886-887)","Acute kidney injury, especially with dehydration, contrast, NSAIDs, or hypercalcemia (p.887)","Hyperviscosity syndrome - headache, visual disturbance, mucosal bleeding, somnolence, heart failure, retinopathy; symptoms above ~4 cP, most common with IgM > IgG3 > IgA (p.887)","Sepsis / serious bacterial infection (recurrent infection is the presenting feature in ~25%) (p.886)","Pathologic fracture (p.887)"],"prognosis":"Serum beta-2-microglobulin is the single most powerful survival predictor; combined with albumin it defines the 3-stage International Staging System (ISS): Stage I (b2M <3.5 mg/L, albumin >=3.5 g/dL) median survival ~62 months; Stage II ~44 months; Stage III (b2M >5.5 mg/L) ~29 months. High LDH, circulating plasma cells, high labeling index, and poor performance status indicate worse outcome. Cytogenetic risk: high-risk = del(17p) (>=20% clonality)/TP53 mutation, t(4;14)/t(14;16)/t(14;20) with 1q gain or 1p deletion, biallelic/del1p with 1q gain, and high b2M with normal creatinine (~20-25% of patients, expected survival 3-4 years); standard-risk includes t(11;14), del(13), hyperdiploidy (~75-80%, survival 8-10+ years). del(13q) and t(11;14) are NOT high-risk markers. MGUS progresses ~1%/yr; SMM higher (high-risk SMM ~76% at 5 years). Solitary plasmacytomas carry median survival >=10 years. The Durie-Salmon system is no longer used (p.888-889).","pitfalls":["Relying on urine dipstick (detects albumin, not light chains) or the Bence Jones heat test (false-negative ~50%) to exclude light-chain myeloma - use 24-h urine immunofixation and serum free light chains (p.888-889).","Excluding myeloma in hypercalcemia of malignancy without serum free light chains - ~3% of myeloma is missed by serum/urine electrophoresis with immunofixation (Ch.57).","Ordering a radionuclide bone scan instead of skeletal radiography/CT/PET-CT - lytic lesions lack osteoblastic activity and may be missed (p.887).","Misreading a normal alkaline phosphatase as evidence against bone disease (p.889).","Interpreting hyponatremia at face value - it is often pseudohyponatremia from high serum protein (p.887).","Labeling all monoclonal proteins as myeloma - M components occur in many neoplastic, autoimmune, and reactive conditions and require myeloma-defining events for the diagnosis (p.883, p.887).","Failing to distinguish MGUS/smoldering myeloma from active myeloma; active disease requires marrow clonal plasmacytosis (and/or plasmacytoma) plus a myeloma-defining event (CRAB or a malignancy biomarker) (Table 116-1)."],"severityClassification":"Diagnosis (IMWG, Table 116-1): clonal marrow plasma cells (or biopsy-proven plasmacytoma) PLUS >=1 myeloma-defining event. CRAB end-organ damage: hyperCalcemia (>2.75 mmol/L or >0.25 mmol/L above ULN); Renal insufficiency (CrCl <40 mL/min or creatinine >177 micromol/L [>2 mg/dL]); Anemia (Hb <100 g/L or >20 g/L below LLN); Bone lesions (>=1 lytic lesion on radiograph/CT/PET-CT). SLiM biomarkers of malignancy (any one): clonal marrow plasma cells >=60%; involved:uninvolved serum free light chain ratio >=100; >1 focal lesion (>=5 mm) on MRI. Staging severity: International Staging System (ISS) I-III by serum beta-2-microglobulin and albumin; cytogenetic high-risk vs standard-risk further stratifies (R-ISS combines ISS with high-risk cytogenetics and LDH). MGUS = M protein <30 g/L, marrow plasma cells <10%, no myeloma-defining events; smoldering myeloma = M protein >=30 g/L (or urine >=500 mg/24h) and/or marrow plasma cells 10-60%, no myeloma-defining events (p.887-889).","references":["Harrison 22e p.883","Harrison 22e p.884","Harrison 22e p.885","Harrison 22e p.886","Harrison 22e p.887","Harrison 22e p.888","Harrison 22e p.889","Harrison 22e Ch.57 (hypercalcemia of malignancy)","IMWG diagnostic criteria"],"crossLinks":["hypercalcemia","aki","anemia_sympt","cord_compression"]},"myxedema":{"name":"Myxoedema coma","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3029-3030, 3032 (Hypothyroidism / Myxedema coma); p.3768-3769 (Hypothermia)","clinicalPearls":["Myxedema coma carries a 20-40% mortality despite intensive care, and outcome does not correlate with the degree of T4 or TSH abnormality (p.3032).","It occurs almost exclusively in the elderly, often in winter, and may arise either in a known hypothyroid patient with poor treatment adherence or in someone not previously diagnosed (p.3032).","Hypothermia in myxedema can be profound, reaching as low as 23 degrees C (74 degrees F) (p.3032).","Reduced consciousness is the hallmark and may be accompanied by seizures, alongside the usual signs of hypothyroidism (p.3032).","A distinguishing examination clue in hypothyroidism is a deep tendon reflex (e.g., Achilles) whose relaxation phase is prolonged out of proportion to the contraction phase (p.3769).","Hypothyroidism, especially extreme as in myxedema coma, is a recognised endocrine cause of hypothermia by reducing metabolic rate and impairing thermogenesis and behavioural responses (p.3768).","Bradycardia, reduced myocardial contractility and stroke volume, and pericardial effusion (up to ~30% of hypothyroid patients) reflect the cardiac depression that underlies the haemodynamic picture (p.3030).","Carotene accumulation can give the skin a yellow tinge, and nonpitting (myxedematous) tissue swelling distinguishes the oedema from pitting fluid overload (p.3029-3030)."],"pathophysiology":"Long-standing thyroid hormone deficiency depresses the metabolic rate and thermogenesis, producing hypothermia, bradycardia, and reduced myocardial contractility. Dermal accumulation of glycosaminoglycans traps water, giving nonpitting myxedematous swelling. In myxedema coma, hypoventilation is central to pathogenesis: it leads to hypoxia and hypercapnia (CO2 retention). Dilutional hyponatremia (reduced renal perfusion with inappropriate vasopressin) and hypoglycemia further contribute. There is impaired adrenal reserve in profound hypothyroidism, and impaired peripheral T4-to-T3 conversion (p.3032; p.3768).","additionalDifferentials":["Sick euthyroid syndrome confounding thyroid function tests in a critically ill or hypothermic patient (p.3769).","Adrenal insufficiency / hypopituitarism, which independently increase susceptibility to hypothermia and may coexist (p.3768).","Hashimoto's encephalopathy - a steroid-responsive syndrome with TPO antibodies, myoclonus and EEG slow-wave activity (relationship to hypothyroidism not established) (p.3030)."],"infectionMimics":["Sepsis should be actively suspected as a precipitant; hypothermia obscures fever and leukocytosis, and infectious rigors may be mistaken for shivering (p.3032, p.3769).","Pneumonia as a respiration-impairing precipitant (p.3032)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Environmental / accidental hypothermia from cold exposure, drugs (ethanol, sedatives, antipsychotics, antidepressants), or malnutrition (p.3768).","Hypoglycemia from insulin or oral hypoglycemic agents, which itself causes hypothermia via neuroglycopenic hypothalamic effects (p.3768)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Arterial blood gas to detect hypoxia and hypercapnia from hypoventilation (p.3032).","Glucose to detect contributing hypoglycemia (p.3032).","Septic screen / cultures, since infection is a common precipitant and is masked by hypothermia (p.3032, p.3769).","Core temperature measurement (may be very low, down to ~23 degrees C) (p.3032)."],"redFlags":["Reduced level of consciousness with seizures in a hypothyroid patient (p.3032).","Profound hypothermia (toward 23 degrees C) (p.3032).","Hypoventilation with hypoxia/hypercapnia signalling impending respiratory failure (p.3032).","Severe dilutional hyponatremia and hypoglycemia (p.3032).","An identifiable precipitant such as infection, MI, CHF, GI bleeding, cerebrovascular accident, or respiratory-depressant drugs (p.3032)."],"prognosis":"Mortality remains 20-40% even with intensive treatment, and prognosis is independent of the measured T4 and TSH levels. Hypothermia associated with concurrent sepsis is a poor prognostic sign (p.3032, p.3769).","pitfalls":["Sedatives, anesthetics, and antidepressants can precipitate or worsen myxedema coma, and impaired drug metabolism means standard doses may accumulate (p.3032).","Hypothermia masks fever and leukocytosis, so infection can be missed; rigors may be misread as shivering (p.3769).","Giving parenteral levothyroxine to a euthyroid hypothermic patient is potentially hazardous, so clinical/historical evidence of hypothyroidism should be sought first (p.3769).","Sick euthyroid syndrome can confound thyroid function tests in the acutely ill patient (p.3769)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.3029","Harrison 22e p.3030","Harrison 22e p.3032","Harrison 22e p.3768","Harrison 22e p.3769"],"crossLinks":["adrenal_crisis","hyponatremia","hypoglycemia","SEPSIS"]},"nephrotic":{"name":"Nephrotic syndrome","system":"Nephrology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e: p.324 (edema in nephrotic syndrome), p.384-385 (proteinuria & vascular causes, Ch.55), p.2356-2359 (proteinuric states & nephrotic syndrome, Ch.319)","clinicalPearls":["Heavy proteinuria (>3.5 g/d) can occur WITHOUT the full nephrotic picture (no hypoalbuminaemia/oedema/hyperlipidaemia) in several renal diseases, notably diabetes; proteinuria is not synonymous with nephrotic syndrome (p.385, p.2357).","Periorbital, facial and eyelid swelling (worst on waking) helps distinguish nephrotic oedema from the dependent oedema/orthopnoea of heart failure; penile and scrotal oedema may be severe enough to obstruct urinary flow in men (p.324, p.2358).","Severe oedema can produce skin bullae that rupture and predispose to ulceration and cellulitis; vocal cord oedema may cause hoarseness (p.2358).","Urine microscopy may show oval fat bodies (lipid-laden tubular epithelial cells), cholesterol monohydrate Maltese-cross crystals under polarised light, and fatty casts (p.2358).","Rapid-onset hypoalbuminaemia may produce horizontal white nail-bed lines (Muehrcke's lines) (p.2358).","Abrupt adult-onset nephrotic syndrome with markedly elevated cholesterol suggests a podocytopathy such as minimal change disease, which is often preceded by upper respiratory infection, allergy or immunisation (p.2358).","Dipstick detects predominantly albumin and the most acidic proteins; it misses kappa/lambda light chains (myeloma), so a normal dipstick does not exclude overflow proteinuria — send urine protein electrophoresis/immunoelectrophoresis (p.385, p.2357).","Nephrotic-range proteinuria without oedema, with rapid renal function loss, raises HIV-associated nephropathy (collapsing glomerulopathy, a severe treatment-resistant FSGS variant), more common in patients of African ancestry (p.2358)."],"pathophysiology":"Nephrotic syndrome reflects increased glomerular permeability to plasma proteins. Minimal change disease causes diffuse fusion (effacement) of podocyte foot processes producing predominantly 'selective' albumin loss, whereas immune-complex disruption of the basement membrane and slit diaphragms yields 'nonselective' loss of albumin and larger proteins. Hypoalbuminaemia results from urinary loss plus increased proximal-tubule catabolism of filtered albumin, exceeding hepatic synthesis (a hypercatabolic, negative-nitrogen-balance state). Oedema arises from renal sodium retention and reduced plasma oncotic pressure; perceived reduction in effective intravascular volume activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, AVP and the sympathetic nervous system, and filtered proteases can directly activate the epithelial Na channel (ENaC) in principal cells, promoting further salt/water retention. Hyperlipidaemia/hypercholesterolaemia reflect increased hepatic lipoprotein synthesis. Notably, hypertension is uncommon in primary kidney diseases causing nephrotic syndrome (p.385). Hereditary nephrotic syndrome can result from podocyte slit-diaphragm gene mutations (nephrin NPHS1, podocin NPHS2); APOL1 risk variants predispose to FSGS, with disease expression triggered by a 'second hit' of infection/inflammation damaging podocytes and glomerular endothelium (p.2357-2358).","additionalDifferentials":["Minimal change disease (idiopathic; secondary to Hodgkin's lymphoma or other lymphoproliferative disorders) — predominant cause in children <6 years (p.2358)","Primary FSGS / podocytopathy versus secondary FSGS (remnant kidney after partial nephrectomy, adaptive injury, chronic hypoxaemia, sickle cell disease, obesity, heroin, congenital urogenital anomalies) (p.2358)","Membranous nephropathy — ~70% of primary cases associated with anti-PLA2R antibodies; secondary forms with malignancy (GI, prostate, breast, lung, bladder), SLE, drugs (gold, penicillamine, NSAIDs), infections (p.2358 Fig.319-3)","Membranoproliferative / C3 glomerulopathy and dense-deposit disease (hypocomplementaemia); secondary to SLE, cryoglobulins, SBE (p.2358 Fig.319-3)","Diabetic glomerulosclerosis (subnephrotic albuminuria progressing to nephrotic range) (p.324, p.2356)","Amyloidosis (AL light-chain, AA/SAA from chronic inflammation) (p.2358 Fig.319-3)","Multiple myeloma / plasma cell dyscrasia with light-chain (overflow) proteinuria not detected by dipstick (p.385, p.2357)"],"infectionMimics":["HIV-associated nephropathy (collapsing FSGS) (p.2358)","Postinfectious glomerulonephritis and COVID-19-associated glomerular injury (p.2358 Fig.319-3)","Subacute bacterial endocarditis (immune-complex glomerulonephritis) (p.2358 Fig.319-3)","Hepatitis C-associated cryoglobulinaemic disease (p.2358)","Schistosomiasis (p.2358 Fig.319-3)","Quartan malarial nephropathy — chronic Plasmodium malariae immune-complex injury causing focal/segmental glomerulonephritis with capillary basement-membrane splitting, mainly in children, responds poorly to therapy (p.~1670 malaria chapter)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Heart failure oedema — dependent with orthopnoea, contrasts with periorbital/facial nephrotic oedema (p.324, p.2358)","Hepatic cirrhosis with hypoalbuminaemia and oedema/ascites (p.325)","Severe malnutrition and protein-losing enteropathy as alternative hypoalbuminaemic states (p.324)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Spot morning urine albumin-to-creatinine or protein-to-creatinine ratio (normalised to creatinine to discount urine dilution) or 24-h urine protein/albumin — preferred over dipstick for quantification (p.385, p.2357)","Serum and urine protein immunoelectrophoresis / UPEP to detect light chains when myeloma is possible (dipstick misses light chains) (p.385, p.2357)","Urine microscopy for oval fat bodies, Maltese-cross lipid crystals and fatty casts (p.2358)","Anti-PLA2R antibody testing in suspected primary membranous nephropathy (p.2358)","Complement levels (membranoproliferative/C3 glomerulopathy is hypocomplementaemic) (p.2358)","Imaging/angiography for suspected renal vein thrombosis presenting with heavy proteinuria and haematuria (p.384)"],"redFlags":["Heavy proteinuria with new haematuria suggesting renal vein thrombosis (p.384)","Rapidly declining renal function with very heavy proteinuria and no oedema suggesting collapsing glomerulopathy/HIVAN (p.2358)","Cellulitis or ulceration from ruptured oedema bullae (p.2358)","Hoarseness from vocal cord oedema or scrotal/penile oedema obstructing urinary flow (p.2358)"],"prognosis":"Glomerular proteinuria in diabetes typically appears after years of small-vessel disease (hence frequent coexistent retinopathy) and predicts progression to nephrotic syndrome and renal failure; the rate of progression is faster with hypertension, obesity and poor glucose control. Diffuse glomerular involvement (most glomeruli) makes nephrotic syndrome likely, whereas focal disease (<50% of glomeruli) tends to give subnephrotic albuminuria. In membranous nephropathy, ~70% of primary cases are anti-PLA2R associated. Collapsing glomerulopathy (HIVAN) is a severe, treatment-resistant FSGS variant. Quartan malarial nephropathy responds poorly to therapy (p.2356-2358).","pitfalls":["Assuming any nephrotic-range proteinuria equals classic nephrotic syndrome — heavy proteinuria can occur without hypoalbuminaemia/oedema (p.385).","Relying on dipstick alone — it misses light chains, so myeloma-associated overflow proteinuria can be overlooked; total protein:creatinine ratio is not specific for albumin (p.385, p.2357).","Equating subnephrotic albuminuria with absence of significant glomerular disease — focal disease causes lower albuminuria yet can be significant (p.2357).","Misreading biopsy-proven FSGS as a single entity — primary podocytopathic FSGS, secondary/adaptive FSGS, and post-GN healing scars are distinct (p.2356, p.2398)."],"severityClassification":"Albuminuria categories (Ch.55 Fig.55-3): moderately increased 30-300 mg/d or mg/g (formerly microalbuminuria); severely increased 300-3500 mg/d or mg/g (formerly macroalbuminuria); nephrotic range >3500 mg/d or mg/g. Full nephrotic syndrome defined by the triad of oedema, hypoalbuminaemia (<3.5 g/dL) and proteinuria >3.5 g/d, usually with lipid abnormalities (high LDL, low HDL, lipiduria) (p.385, p.2357).","references":["Harrison 22e p.324","Harrison 22e p.325","Harrison 22e p.384","Harrison 22e p.385","Harrison 22e p.2356","Harrison 22e p.2357","Harrison 22e p.2358"],"crossLinks":["glomerulonephritis","ckd","dvt","myeloma"]},"opioid_od":{"name":"Opioid overdose","system":"Toxicology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"p.3688-3707","clinicalPearls":["The classic overdose picture is shallow/slow respirations, miosis, bradycardia, hypothermia, and stupor or coma; toxicology can confirm but immediate management is clinical (p.3690).","Pinpoint pupils are characteristic, BUT mydriasis (dilated pupils) appears once significant cerebral anoxia supervenes — dilated pupils do not exclude opioid toxicity in a deteriorating patient (p.3690).","Hypoxia, not the drug itself, is what kills: in opioid and sedative-hypnotic poisoning, vital-sign changes are secondary to CNS depression or consequent hypoxemia and do not appear until consciousness is markedly reduced (grade 3-4 depression) (p.3748).","Aspiration pneumonia is common in overdose because of loss of the gag reflex (p.3733).","Opioids generally do NOT cause seizures, except in polydrug use, with meperidine, with high-dose tramadol, or in the newborn (p.3690).","Fentanyl and its derivatives may require severalfold higher reversal doses than other opioids, and adulteration of illicit fentanyl with xylazine markedly worsens respiratory suppression (p.3689, p.3733).","Prompt reversal of altered mental status and vital signs within minutes of IV naloxone is virtually diagnostic of opioid poisoning (alongside dextrose for hypoglycemia and flumazenil for benzodiazepine intoxication) (p.3748).","Fluoroquinolones commonly cause false-positive opiate urine screens; rapid urine drug screens are screening-only, prone to false results, and miss agents such as kratom (p.3748).","When naloxone has only a limited effect, suspect a co-ingested sedative — most commonly a benzodiazepine, which has caused fatal overdoses in combination with buprenorphine (p.3691)."],"pathophysiology":"Opioids act mainly through the mu receptor (also kappa, delta, nociceptin/orphanin), all G protein-coupled, inhibiting the cAMP system and opening GIRK potassium channels to hyperpolarize and silence neurons. Life-threatening respiratory depression results from opioid-induced insensitivity of brainstem (medullary) respiratory neurons to rising carbon dioxide; analgesia and respiratory depression are mediated by receptors in the periaqueductal grey and medulla oblongata. Untreated, progression goes to respiratory and then cardiovascular collapse and death (p.3733, p.3690).","additionalDifferentials":["Cholinergic (muscarinic/nicotinic) poisoning — also causes miosis plus a depressed physiologic state (p.3748)","Sedative-hypnotic / GABAergic poisoning (alcohols, barbiturates, benzodiazepines, GHB) — depressed state, distinguished as naloxone-unresponsive (p.3748)","Sympatholytic poisoning (alpha-2 agonists e.g. clonidine, beta-blockers, calcium channel blockers, cardiac glycosides) (p.3747)","Other causes of coma including hypoglycemia (reversed by dextrose) (p.3748)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Hypoglycemia (responds to dextrose, mimics opioid coma) (p.3748)","Benzodiazepine intoxication (responds to flumazenil) (p.3748)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood/urine toxicology can confirm a suspected diagnosis but must not delay clinical management (p.3690)","ECG to detect QT prolongation (e.g. methadone), which can cause sudden death (p.3733)","Chest imaging for noncardiogenic pulmonary edema / ARDS, a recognized complication of opioid poisoning (p.3749)","In IV users, screen for blood-borne infection (hepatitis B, HIV) and bacterial complications (meningitis, osteomyelitis, abscesses, endocarditis) (p.3733)"],"redFlags":["Dilated pupils in a suspected opioid overdose suggest cerebral anoxia has already occurred (p.3690)","Re-sedation: naloxone lasts only a few hours while most opioids (especially methadone, sustained-release/'toxic time-bomb' formulations) last far longer (p.3691, p.3747)","Noncardiogenic pulmonary edema / ARDS (p.3749)","Methadone-associated QT prolongation and sudden death (p.3733)","Necrosis and limb-loss risk with fentanyl-xylazine injection (p.3733)"],"prognosis":"Potentially lethal but rapidly reversible if respiratory depression is recognized and supported; untreated overdose progresses to respiratory and cardiovascular collapse and death. Opioid use disorder is chronic and relapsing, and most patients relapse during or after detoxification (p.3690, p.3691). Some opioid intoxications can leave delayed diffuse white-matter injury producing a persistent coma syndrome (p.232).","pitfalls":["Do not assume normal-sized or dilated pupils exclude opioids — mydriasis does NOT occur until significant brain anoxia supervenes (p.3690).","Significant vital-sign abnormalities do NOT occur in opioid poisoning until there is a marked decrease in level of consciousness, so early near-normal vitals are falsely reassuring (p.3748).","A negative urine drug screen does NOT rule out opioid toxicity (concentration too low, or agent such as kratom not detected); opiate screens also give false positives (e.g. fluoroquinolones) (p.3748).","Primary buprenorphine overdose is nearly impossible because it is a partial agonist with increasing antagonist activity at higher doses; failure to respond to naloxone should prompt search for a co-ingested sedative (p.3690-3691).","Be sure a patient is not also withdrawing from alcohol or sedatives, which may be more dangerous to manage than opioid withdrawal (p.3733)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames poisoning severity by physiologic state (Tables 470-1/470-2): opioids produce a 'depressed' physiologic state (decreased pulse, BP, respiratory rate, temperature, neuromuscular activity, with miosis). Severity is graded by depth of CNS/physiologic depression (grades 1-4); marked vital-sign abnormalities indicate grade 3-4 depression (p.3747-3748). Note: opioid WITHDRAWAL is a separate 'stimulated' syndrome — onset 8-10 h after the last dose with lacrimation, rhinorrhea, yawning and sweating, progressing to gooseflesh, nausea/vomiting, myalgia, hyperpnea, hyperthermia, hypertension, mydriasis over a 7-10 day acute course (p.3690).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3688-3691 (Ch.467 Opioid-Related Disorders)","Harrison 22e p.3747-3750 (Ch.470 Poisoning and Drug Overdose)","Harrison 22e p.232 (Coma)"],"crossLinks":["drug_intox","serotonin_nms"]},"organophosphate":{"name":"Organophosphate / cholinergic poisoning","system":"Toxicology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"p.3610, p.3620, p.3705-3713","clinicalPearls":["Belongs to the depressed physiologic state / cholinergic toxidrome; miosis is common and is most pronounced in opioid and cholinergic poisoning, distinguished from other depressant syndromes by muscarinic and nicotinic signs (p.3705).","Muscarinic features: seizures, excessive secretions (lacrimation, salivation, bronchorrhoea and wheezing, diaphoresis), and increased bowel/bladder activity with nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, abdominal cramps, and incontinence of faeces and urine (p.3712).","Nicotinic features: hypertension, tachycardia, muscle cramps, fasciculations, weakness, and paralysis — note these oppose the muscarinic picture, so vital signs can be mixed (p.3712).","Death is usually due to respiratory failure (p.3712).","Organophosphates and carbamates can produce bradycardia and atrioventricular block on ECG (p.3706).","Organophosphate (and amanita/mushroom) poisoning can present as acute, often systemic-compromising diarrhoea (p.46/Acute Diarrhea chapter).","Organophosphates can cause a delayed organophosphate-induced neuropathy: early generalised weakness from neuromuscular blockade, then an axonal sensorimotor polyneuropathy ensues (p.3610)."],"pathophysiology":"Organophosphate (and carbamate) insecticides, and nerve agents such as sarin, soman, tabun and VX, inhibit acetylcholinesterase, leading to accumulation of synaptic acetylcholine at both muscarinic and nicotinic cholinergic receptor sites; the resulting overstimulation produces the combined muscarinic, nicotinic and CNS toxidrome. Organophosphates are 'toxic time-bombs' in that they may require metabolic activation, so onset of toxicity can be delayed. Separately, organophosphates bind and inhibit neuropathy target esterase, with axonal degeneration of the gracile fasciculus and corticospinal tracts producing a delayed sensorimotor polyneuropathy. (p.3610, p.3712, p.3704)","additionalDifferentials":["Carbamate insecticide poisoning (aldicarb, carbaryl, propoxur) — same cholinergic mechanism (p.3712)","Other acetylcholinesterase inhibitors: medicinal agents neostigmine, physostigmine, tacrine (p.3712)","Muscarinic agonists: bethanechol, pilocarpine, and muscarine-containing mushrooms (Boletus, Clitocybe, Inocybe spp.) (p.3712)","Nicotine / tobacco and lobeline (nicotinic agonists) (p.3712)","Opioid poisoning (also causes miosis and physiologic depression) (p.3705)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Guillain-Barré syndrome / acute flaccid paralysis — organophosphate, thallium and arsenic poisonings are listed in the GBS differential (p.3620)","Botulism and myasthenia gravis (neuromuscular junction disorders in the acute paralysis differential) (p.3620)","Paralytic shellfish poisoning, ciguatera, type E botulism and tetrodotoxin poisoning — organophosphate poisoning is in their shared differential (ciguatera chapter)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Cholinesterase activity in plasma and red cells is <50% of normal in acetylcholinesterase-inhibitor poisoning (p.3712)","ECG — may show bradycardia and atrioventricular block (p.3706)","EMG/nerve conduction studies in delayed neuropathy — early repetitive firing of CMAPs and decrement on repetitive nerve stimulation; later, axonal sensorimotor polyneuropathy (p.3610)"],"redFlags":["Bronchorrhoea, wheezing and excessive secretions with progression to respiratory failure — the usual cause of death (p.3712)","Nicotinic weakness, fasciculations and paralysis (p.3712)","Seizures (p.3712)","Bradycardia / atrioventricular block (p.3706)"],"prognosis":"Death is usually due to respiratory failure (p.3712). Outcome and reversibility of any poisoning depend on the functional reserve of the individual or target organ, influenced by age and preexisting disease (p.3704).","pitfalls":["Vital signs may be discordant: muscarinic effects cause bradycardia while nicotinic stimulation can cause hypertension and tachycardia, so a 'normal' or mixed heart rate does not exclude poisoning (p.3712).","Organophosphates are 'toxic time-bombs' requiring metabolic activation, so a patient may be initially well or minimally symptomatic and deteriorate later (p.3704).","Cholinesterase activity is <50% of normal in acetylcholinesterase-inhibitor poisoning — a key confirmatory threshold not to overlook (p.3712)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison grades poisoning by degree of physiologic depression rather than by a disease-specific scale (Table 470-2): grade 1 — arousable by voice/touch, can converse and follow commands; grade 2 — responds to pain but not voice, brainstem reflexes intact; grade 3 — unresponsive to pain, brainstem reflexes depressed, decreased respirations and temperature; grade 4 — flaccid paralysis with absent brainstem reflexes and respirations and decreased cardiovascular vital signs (p.3705).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3610","Harrison 22e p.3620","Harrison 22e p.3705","Harrison 22e p.3706","Harrison 22e p.3712"],"crossLinks":["drug_intox"]},"pancreatitis":{"name":"Acute pancreatitis","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.358-359, p.2738-2749 (esp. p.2744-2749)","clinicalPearls":["Diagnosis requires 2 of 3: typical epigastric pain radiating to the back, serum lipase/amylase >=3x ULN, or characteristic findings on cross-sectional imaging (Harrison 22e p.2745).","Lipase is more pancreas-specific than amylase and is the single best enzyme to measure; it stays elevated longer (7-14 days) whereas amylase often normalises by 3-7 days (Harrison 22e p.2740, p.2745).","Gallstones and alcohol together account for ~80-90% of cases; gallstones are the single most common cause (30-60%), alcohol the second (15-30%) (Harrison 22e p.2744).","ALT >3x ULN strongly points to a gallstone aetiology in acute pancreatitis (Harrison 22e p.2745).","Abdominal ultrasound is the recommended initial imaging study, chiefly to assess for gallstones and bile-duct dilation; contrast CT is best deferred to ~3-5 days if the patient is not improving and is usually not needed in the first 48-72 h (Harrison 22e p.2745-2746).","Persistent organ failure (>48 h) is the most important determinant of severity; if SIRS has resolved by 24 h, organ failure or necrosis is unlikely to develop (Harrison 22e p.2746, p.2748).","Hemoconcentration (hematocrit >44%) and a rising BUN are early warning signs of more severe disease and higher mortality (Harrison 22e p.2745-2748).","Cullen's sign (periumbilical bruising) and Grey Turner's sign (flank discoloration) are rare but signal severe necrotizing/hemorrhagic disease (Harrison 22e p.2745)."],"pathophysiology":"Acute pancreatitis is thought to begin with autodigestion: intracellular premature activation of trypsin (via lysosomal cathepsin B colocalising with digestive zymogens) within acinar cells, which then activates other proteolytic and lipolytic enzymes (chymotrypsinogen, proelastase, phospholipase A2). A second phase recruits and sequesters leukocytes/macrophages, amplifying intrapancreatic inflammation, and a third phase results from activated enzymes and cytokines acting on distant organs, producing edema, hemorrhage, vascular damage, fat necrosis and coagulation/cellular necrosis. Release of bradykinin, vasoactive substances and histamine drives vasodilation, increased vascular permeability and edema, and can culminate in SIRS, ARDS and multiorgan failure. Pathologically the spectrum runs from interstitial (perfusion preserved, self-limited) to necrotizing pancreatitis (perfusion interrupted). Genetic susceptibility/modifier variants include PRSS1 (cationic trypsinogen), SPINK1, CFTR, CTRC, CASR and CLDN2, all converging on control of intrapancreatic trypsin activity (Harrison 22e p.2744-2745).","additionalDifferentials":["Perforated viscus, especially peptic ulcer","Acute cholecystitis / biliary colic","Acute intestinal obstruction","Mesenteric vascular occlusion / ischemia","Renal colic","Inferior myocardial infarction","Dissecting aortic aneurysm","Connective-tissue disorders with vasculitis (e.g., SLE, polyarteritis nodosa)","Pneumonia","Diabetic ketoacidosis"],"infectionMimics":["Pneumonia (may present with upper abdominal pain and is in the differential of acute pancreatitis) (Harrison 22e p.2745)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Diabetic ketoacidosis - abdominal pain with raised TOTAL amylase mimics pancreatitis, but lipase is often NOT elevated and pancreatic imaging is normal (Harrison 22e p.2745-2746)","Perforated peptic ulcer - can raise serum/ascitic amylase (Harrison 22e p.2739, p.2745)","Acute cholecystitis / biliary colic - amylase may be raised; pain is more right-sided/epigastric and ileus is usually absent (Harrison 22e p.2745)","Mesenteric ischemia/infarction - can raise ascitic-fluid amylase (Harrison 22e p.2739, p.2745)","Intestinal obstruction - can raise ascitic-fluid amylase; differentiated by crescendo-decrescendo pain and obstruction pattern on CT (Harrison 22e p.2739, p.2745)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum lipase (>3x ULN) - preferred enzyme; more specific than amylase; remains elevated 7-14 days (Harrison 22e p.2740, p.2745)","Liver profile incl. ALT - ALT >3x ULN supports gallstone aetiology (Harrison 22e p.2745)","Serum triglycerides - to detect hypertriglyceridaemic cause (levels usually >1000 mg/dL) (Harrison 22e p.2744, p.2748)","Serum calcium - to detect hypercalcaemic cause and to monitor for hypocalcaemia (~25% of patients) (Harrison 22e p.2744-2745)","Hematocrit - hemoconcentration >44% is a severity marker (Harrison 22e p.2745-2746)","Blood urea nitrogen (BUN) at admission and 48 h - rising BUN predicts mortality (Harrison 22e p.2745, p.2748)","White cell count - leukocytosis 15,000-20,000/uL is common (Harrison 22e p.2745)","MRI/MRCP - more sensitive than CT for mild pancreatitis, necrosis, choledocholithiasis and ductal abnormalities (Harrison 22e p.2739)","EUS - useful for occult microlithiasis/biliary cause and choledocholithiasis; best performed ~4 weeks after resolution to find predisposing lesions (Harrison 22e p.2741)","Contrast-enhanced CT - to assess necrosis and local complications when not improving, ideally at 3-5 days (Harrison 22e p.2745-2746)","C-reactive protein - severity marker during hospitalization (thresholds >100 mg/L reported) (Harrison 22e p.2748)"],"redFlags":["Persistent organ failure >48 h (respiratory, cardiovascular or renal) - hallmark of severe disease (Harrison 22e p.2746)","Modified Marshall organ-failure signs: systolic BP <90 mmHg, HR >130/min, PaO2 <60 mmHg, or creatinine >2.0 mg/dL (Harrison 22e p.2748)","BISAP score >=3 - increased in-hospital mortality (Harrison 22e p.2748)","APACHE II >=8 at 24 h (Harrison 22e p.2748)","Hemoconcentration (hematocrit >44%) and rising/admission BUN - predict severe disease and mortality (Harrison 22e p.2745-2748)","Persistent SIRS at 24 h - predisposes to organ failure and necrosis (Harrison 22e p.2746, p.2748)","Pancreatic necrosis on CT - greatest mortality risk (3-10% with single-organ failure, ~50% with multiorgan failure) (Harrison 22e p.2747)","Hypoxemia (PaO2 <=60 mmHg) - may herald ARDS (Harrison 22e p.2745)","Cullen's sign / Grey Turner's sign - severe necrotizing/hemorrhagic pancreatitis (Harrison 22e p.2745)","Ascending cholangitis (sepsis + biliary obstruction) complicating gallstone pancreatitis (Harrison 22e p.2748)"],"prognosis":"Overall mortality is low (~1%) and median hospital stay ~4 days; most cases are mild and self-limited within 3-7 days. Interstitial pancreatitis (90-95% of admissions) carries low mortality, whereas necrotizing pancreatitis (5-10%) drives most deaths. In necrotizing disease the median prevalence of organ failure exceeds 50% (slightly higher with infected vs sterile necrosis); mortality is 3-10% with single-organ failure but approaches 50% with multiorgan failure. Patients with only extrapancreatic necrosis fare better than those with pancreatic parenchymal necrosis. About 25% of patients have a recurrence, most commonly from alcohol or gallstones (Harrison 22e p.2747, p.2749).","pitfalls":["Enzyme magnitude does NOT track severity - degree or serial trend of lipase/amylase does not correlate with how severe the pancreatitis is (Harrison 22e p.2745).","Serum amylase can be falsely normal or low in hypertriglyceridaemic pancreatitis, masking the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2739, p.2745).","Amylase is non-specific - raised in salivary, tubo-ovarian, pulmonary, malignant and other conditions, and spuriously high in acidemia/DKA (arterial pH <=7.32) without true pancreatitis (Harrison 22e p.2740, p.2745).","Lipase can be mildly elevated in renal failure, and >3x ULN can occur in hepatobiliary/GI malignancy, sepsis, cirrhosis, SLE, head injury, chronic alcoholism, diabetes and post-ERCP without pancreatitis (Harrison 22e p.2740).","Routine/early CT (within 72 h) is overused and usually unnecessary, especially in mild disease; necrosis may not be visible until several days in (Harrison 22e p.2746-2747).","Prophylactic antibiotics are NOT recommended, even in severe or necrotizing pancreatitis (Harrison 22e p.2746, p.2749).","Enzyme levels may be normal if blood is drawn 2-5 days after onset or if the underlying problem is chronic rather than acute pancreatitis (Harrison 22e p.2740)."],"severityClassification":"Revised Atlanta classification (Banks et al., Gut 2013). PHASES: early (<2 weeks; severity judged clinically, not by morphology) and late (>2 weeks; imaging for local complications). SEVERITY: (1) Mild - no local complications and no organ failure (most interstitial cases; self-limited 3-7 days); (2) Moderately severe - transient organ failure (resolves <48 h) and/or local or systemic complications without persistent organ failure; (3) Severe - persistent organ failure >48 h in >=1 organ system (respiratory, cardiovascular, renal; defined by modified Marshall score >=2). MORPHOLOGY (Table 359-2): two types - interstitial (parenchymal enhancement, no necrosis) vs necrotizing (lack of parenchymal enhancement and/or peripancreatic necrosis); local collections - acute pancreatic fluid collection and pseudocyst (interstitial, pseudocyst usually >4 wk) vs acute necrotic collection (ANC) and walled-off necrosis (WON, usually >4 wk, encapsulated). SEVERITY SCORES/MARKERS (Table 359-3): BISAP >=3 (BUN >25 mg/dL, Impaired mental status/GCS<15, SIRS >=2/4, Age >60, Pleural effusion); APACHE II >=8 at 24 h; SIRS >=2 of 4 (temp <36 or >38C, HR >90, RR >20 or PCO2 <32, WCC >12,000 or <4000 or >10% bands); hematocrit >44%; admission BUN >20 mg/dL; persistent organ failure >=48 h, and pancreatic/extrapancreatic necrosis as in-hospital markers. Ranson criteria are a traditional severity index (referenced contextually).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2738","Harrison 22e p.2739","Harrison 22e p.2740","Harrison 22e p.2744","Harrison 22e p.2745","Harrison 22e p.2746","Harrison 22e p.2747","Harrison 22e p.2748","Harrison 22e p.2749"],"crossLinks":["biliary_colic","CHOLANGITIS","decomp_cirrhosis"]},"panic":{"name":"Panic attack / anxiety","system":"Functional","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.103-107 (Chest Discomfort, Chap. 15); p.161, p.165 (Syncope/Dizziness); p.215 (Sleep Disorders); p.291 (Palpitations); p.376 (Hyperventilation syndrome / Respiratory Alkalosis)","clinicalPearls":["Up to ~10% of patients presenting to emergency departments with acute chest discomfort have an underlying panic disorder or a related condition (p.104).","Psychiatric/emotional chest discomfort is variable in duration (fleeting to prolonged), often felt as chest tightness and breathlessness with a sense of panic or impending doom, and may be retrosternal; situational triggers and a prior history of panic attacks or depression support the diagnosis (p.103).","A psychiatric cause for palpitations is favoured when the sensation is reported as lasting longer (>15 min) and is accompanied by additional symptoms, in contrast to most arrhythmic causes (p.291).","Panic attacks can arise out of sleep and must be distinguished from parasomnias and other nocturnal events (p.215).","Apparent (psychogenic) loss of consciousness can occur in panic disorder and related conditions; suspect it in patients who faint frequently without prodrome, are rarely injured despite repeated falls, and have no concurrent hemodynamic change (no hypotension/bradycardia) during the event (p.161)."],"pathophysiology":"Acute attacks are commonly accompanied by hyperventilation; the resulting hypocapnia (Paco2 often 15-30 mmHg) produces an acute respiratory alkalosis without hypoxemia, which underlies many of the somatic symptoms (paresthesia, circumoral numbness, chest-wall tightness, dizziness, air hunger, and rarely tetany) and can perpetuate the disorder. Catecholaminergic/hyperadrenergic activation contributes to palpitations and the hyperdynamic sensation (Harrison 22e p.376, p.291).","additionalDifferentials":["Hyperventilation syndrome (respiratory alkalosis) — itself a diagnosis of exclusion (p.376)","Generalized anxiety disorder and somatization presenting with palpitations or pseudo-syncope (p.161, p.291)","Vasovagal syncope precipitated by fear/stress/anxiety (distinguished by accompanying hypotension +/- bradycardia, unlike psychogenic pseudo-syncope) (p.161)","Cataplexy / narcolepsy as a cause of episodic collapse (consciousness preserved) (p.161)","Persistent postural-perceptual dizziness (PPPD) and chronic subjective dizziness, in which anxiety disorders are common comorbidities (p.165)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute coronary syndrome / myocardial ischemia (aching, heavy, squeezing retrosternal discomfort) (p.103)","Pulmonary embolism (chest discomfort, sinus tachycardia, ECG axis shift/S1Q3T3) (p.107)","Supraventricular or ventricular tachyarrhythmia and atrial fibrillation (palpitations) (p.291)","Thyrotoxicosis / hyperthyroidism (palpitations, hyperdynamic state) (p.291, p.376)","Pheochromocytoma (catecholamine-driven palpitations, hyperdynamic cardiovascular state) (p.291)","Hypoglycemia (tremor, palpitations, anxiety, diaphoresis, hunger, paresthesias from autonomic activation) (p.161)","Systemic mastocytosis; stimulant/drug effects (caffeine, cocaine, amphetamines, cannabis, ethanol); post-COVID syndrome — as causes of palpitations (p.291)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Resting 12-lead ECG, with serial tracings, to exclude arrhythmia and ischemia in palpitations/chest discomfort (p.291); note hyperventilation in panic can itself produce nonspecific ST-segment and T-wave abnormalities (p.107)","Ambulatory rhythm monitoring (Holter, event/loop recorder, implantable loop recorder, or mobile cardiac outpatient telemetry) for infrequent recurrent palpitations (p.291)","Arterial blood-gas analysis when hyperventilation suspected: shows respiratory alkalosis with hypocapnia (Paco2 ~15-30 mmHg) but no hypoxemia (p.376)"],"redFlags":["Normocapnia together with hypoxemia in a hyperventilating patient is a warning sign that may herald impending respiratory failure — do not assume benign anxiety (p.376)","High-risk syncope features that argue against a psychogenic cause: new chest discomfort, dyspnea, exertional or supine syncope, palpitations preceding syncope, structural/coronary heart disease (p.161)","Chest discomfort with features of an acute cardiopulmonary catastrophe (ACS, acute aortic syndrome, pulmonary embolism, tension pneumothorax, pericarditis with tamponade) must be excluded first (p.105)"],"prognosis":null,"pitfalls":["Anchoring on anxiety/panic prematurely: because attacks mimic ACS, PE, arrhythmia, thyrotoxicosis, pheochromocytoma and hypoglycemia, organic emergencies must be excluded — panic disorder and the hyperventilation syndrome are diagnoses of exclusion (p.105, p.376).","Misreading hyperventilation-related nonspecific ST-segment/T-wave changes as evidence of ischemia (or vice versa) (p.107).","When ruling in a hyperventilation/psychiatric cause, still exclude pulmonary embolism, coronary artery disease and hyperthyroidism (p.376)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.103","Harrison 22e p.104","Harrison 22e p.105","Harrison 22e p.107","Harrison 22e p.161","Harrison 22e p.165","Harrison 22e p.215","Harrison 22e p.291","Harrison 22e p.376"],"crossLinks":["acs","pe","hyperthyroidism","pheo"]},"pe":{"name":"Pulmonary embolism","system":"Pulmonary / Vascular","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.290 (p.2154-2164), with chest-pain differential context Ch.11/15 (p.2153, supportive)","clinicalPearls":["PE is the 'great masquerader' because its symptoms and signs are nonspecific; unexplained breathlessness is the most common symptom (p.2159).","Suspect occult PE when a patient with congestive heart failure or pneumonia fails to improve as expected on standard treatment (p.2159).","Pleuritic chest pain typically reflects a small, peripherally lodged embolus near pleural nerves, whereas massive PE can cause severe substernal pain mimicking MI from pulmonary-artery distention and RV strain (p.2153, p.2159).","Pulmonary infarction usually signals a small (peripheral) PE and is exquisitely painful (p.2159).","May-Thurner syndrome (left iliac vein compressed by the right iliac artery) should be considered in young women with recurrent left thigh swelling and associated DVT (p.2159).","A diffusely edematous leg makes ordinary DVT unlikely, with phlegmasia cerulea dolens being the exception (p.2159).","Many patients with PE have no demonstrable DVT because the leg thrombus has already embolized to the lungs (p.2158).","McConnell's sign on echocardiography (RV free-wall hypokinesis with preserved/hyperkinetic RV apex) is the best-known indirect echo sign of PE (p.2161)."],"pathophysiology":"Virchow's triad of venous stasis, hypercoagulability, and endothelial injury, typically with an inflammatory trigger, recruits activated platelets that release microparticles and drive neutrophil extracellular trap formation, promoting thrombin generation; venous thrombi flourish in stasis, low oxygen tension, and proinflammatory gene upregulation. Detached lower-limb thrombi embolize through the vena cava and right heart to lodge in the pulmonary arteries (rarely paradoxically to the arterial circulation via a patent foramen ovale or atrial septal defect). The dominant gas-exchange defects are arterial hypoxemia and a widened alveolar-arterial oxygen gradient with increased dead space; additional mechanisms include raised pulmonary vascular resistance from obstruction and vasoconstrictors (e.g., serotonin), ventilation-perfusion mismatch remote from the embolus, reflex hyperventilation, increased airway resistance, and reduced compliance. Pulmonary-artery obstruction plus neurohumoral mediators raise pulmonary pressure and resistance; rising RV wall tension produces RV dilation, stretch and dysfunction (releasing BNP), septal bowing that impairs LV filling, and compression of the right coronary artery causing RV ischemia/microinfarction (releasing troponin), ultimately reducing LV output and systemic pressure, leading to circulatory collapse and death. Prothrombotic states include factor V Leiden, the prothrombin gene mutation, and deficiencies of antithrombin/protein C/protein S (rare); antiphospholipid syndrome is an acquired thrombophilia predisposing to both venous and arterial thrombosis. (p.2156-2158)","additionalDifferentials":["Congestive heart failure","Pericarditis","Pleurisy / costochondritis / musculoskeletal chest discomfort","Anxiety","Vasovagal syncope","Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension (CTEPH) for persistent exertional breathlessness"],"infectionMimics":["Pneumonia","Cellulitis (mimics DVT; fever and chills favor cellulitis over DVT)","Sepsis (elevates D-dimer and can confound)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute coronary syndrome (massive PE can give substernal pain mimicking MI)","Aortic dissection","Pericardial tamponade","Pneumothorax / rib fracture","Asthma / COPD exacerbation","Ruptured Baker's cyst, muscle strain, and postthrombotic syndrome/venous insufficiency (mimic DVT)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Age-adjusted D-dimer (age x 10 ng/mL upper limit) for patients >50 years with low/intermediate clinical probability of PE; does not apply to suspected acute DVT (p.2159-2160)","Wells point-score clinical decision rule to stratify pretest probability (validated in ambulatory, not hospitalized, patients); low/low-to-moderate probability with normal D-dimer can avoid imaging (p.2159)","BNP / NT-proBNP as markers of RV myocardial stretch (p.2160)","ECG looking for S1Q3T3 (specific but insensitive) and T-wave inversion in V1-V4 from RV strain (p.2160)","Chest x-ray signs: focal oligemia (Westermark's sign), peripheral pleural-based density (Hampton's hump, from infarction), enlarged right descending pulmonary artery (Palla's sign); often normal (p.2160)","Echocardiography to assess RV dysfunction and to detect mimics (MI, tamponade, aortic dissection); usually normal in PE so not a reliable rule-out (p.2161)","Compression venous ultrasonography for DVT (loss of vein compressibility) (p.2160)","Ventilation/perfusion lung scan as second-line when IV contrast cannot be given (p.2161)","MR pulmonary angiography / MR venography when CT or ultrasound is unsuitable (p.2161)","RV-to-LV diameter ratio >1 on chest CT as a marker of RV enlargement (p.2161)"],"redFlags":["Systemic arterial hypotension / cardiogenic shock indicating massive (high-risk) PE (p.2158)","Syncope or cyanosis (p.2158, p.2153)","RV dysfunction on echocardiography or RV enlargement on CT despite normal blood pressure (submassive/intermediate-risk PE) (p.2158, p.2162)","Elevated troponin (from RV microinfarction) signaling high risk of clinical deterioration (p.2158, p.2162)","Phlegmasia cerulea dolens (diffusely swollen, threatened limb) (p.2159)"],"prognosis":"Low-risk PE (about 65-75% of cases) with normal blood pressure and normal RV carries an excellent prognosis; submassive (intermediate-risk) PE (~20-25%) has RV dysfunction despite normal pressure with raised cardiac biomarkers and a high risk of deterioration; massive (high-risk) PE (5-10%) presents with hypotension/shock and can lead to multisystem organ failure and death. A high-probability V/Q scan confers ~90% diagnostic certainty for PE. Roughly half of survivors report persistent dyspnea, fatigue, and reduced exercise capacity, and about one-quarter have persistent RV dysfunction on echo ('post-PE syndrome'); CTEPH is a less frequent late complication. Risk of recurrent VTE after stopping anticoagulation ranges from low (<3%/yr, e.g., major surgery/trauma) to intermediate (3-8%/yr) to high (>8%/yr, e.g., active cancer, antiphospholipid syndrome) (ESC 2019). (p.2154-2155, 2158, 2161, 2163-2164)","pitfalls":["D-dimer is sensitive (>95%) but not specific; it rises with MI, pneumonia, sepsis, cancer, postoperative state, and the 2nd/3rd trimester of pregnancy, so it rarely helps in hospitalized patients (p.2159-2160).","Wells criteria are validated in ambulatory patients, not in hospitalized patients (p.2159).","A normal or near-normal chest x-ray and a normal ECG (or only sinus tachycardia) do not exclude PE (p.2160).","Echocardiography is not a reliable rule-out test because most PE patients have normal echocardiograms (p.2161).","Not all leg pain is DVT and not all dyspnea is PE; consider ruptured Baker's cyst, cellulitis, and muscle injury (p.2159)."],"severityClassification":"PE is stratified by hemodynamics and RV status: massive (high-risk, 5-10%) with systemic hypotension and extensive thrombosis (>=half of the pulmonary vasculature), hallmarks dyspnea/syncope/hypotension/cyanosis and possible cardiogenic shock; submassive (intermediate-risk, ~20-25%) with RV dysfunction and raised troponin despite normal blood pressure; and low-risk (~65-75%) with normal pressure and normal RV. RV strain markers (troponin, BNP/NT-proBNP, echo RV dysfunction, CT RV enlargement with RV/LV diameter ratio >1) drive risk stratification. (p.2158, 2160-2162)","references":["Harrison 22e p.2153","Harrison 22e p.2154","Harrison 22e p.2155","Harrison 22e p.2156","Harrison 22e p.2157","Harrison 22e p.2158","Harrison 22e p.2159","Harrison 22e p.2160","Harrison 22e p.2161","Harrison 22e p.2162","Harrison 22e p.2163","Harrison 22e p.2164"],"crossLinks":["dvt","heart_failure","acs","pneumothorax"]},"peptic_ulcer":{"name":"Peptic ulcer disease / upper GI bleed","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.335, pp.2514-2536 (peptic ulcer disease and related disorders)","clinicalPearls":["Around two-thirds of patients with peptic ulcer disease have no abdominal pain, and up to ~87% of those with NSAID-related mucosal injury first present with a complication (bleeding, perforation, or obstruction) without any preceding symptoms (p.2522).","Classic duodenal ulcer pain comes on roughly 90 minutes to 3 hours after eating and is typically eased by food or antacids; pain that wakes the patient between midnight and 3 a.m. is the most discriminating symptom and is reported by about two-thirds of duodenal ulcer patients (p.2522).","Gastric ulcer pain can instead be provoked by eating, and nausea and weight loss are commoner with gastric than duodenal ulcers (p.2522).","Elderly and immunosuppressed patients are less likely to have typical pain and may present directly with bleeding or perforation (pp.2522-2523).","H. pylori and NSAIDs are the dominant risk factors (US odds ratios ~3.7 and ~3.3); NSAIDs are now described as the most common cause of PUD, with only ~one-quarter of duodenal and ~one-sixth of gastric ulcers now attributable to H. pylori (pp.2518, 2520).","More than half of patients with ulcer-related haemorrhage bleed without any preceding warning signs or symptoms (p.2522).","Endoscopy detects an ulcer in fewer than 30% of patients investigated for dyspepsia, reflecting how poorly symptoms predict an actual ulcer (p.2522)."],"pathophysiology":"Peptic ulcers are mucosal breaks >5 mm extending into the submucosa, occurring in the stomach (gastric ulcer, GU) or duodenum (duodenal ulcer, DU); >95% of DUs lie in the first part of the duodenum, within about 3 cm of the pylorus. The disease reflects an imbalance between aggressive factors (acid, pepsin) and mucosal defence. H. pylori and NSAIDs cause the majority of ulcers but act differently: H. pylori-associated benign GUs show antral gastritis, whereas NSAID-related GUs typically lack chronic active gastritis and instead show a chemical gastropathy (foveolar hyperplasia, lamina propria oedema). Duodenal ulcer patients tend to have increased basal and nocturnal acid secretion (with substantial overlap with controls) and reduced duodenal bicarbonate, both potentially influenced by H. pylori; gastric acid output in GU patients is usually normal or reduced, implying impaired mucosal defence. H. pylori is a gram-negative microaerophilic urease-producing rod living in the gastric mucous layer; virulence factors such as the cag pathogenicity island (delivering CagA), VacA, and urease determine the clinical outcome among colonisation, PUD, MALT lymphoma, and gastric adenocarcinoma (pp.2518-2520).","additionalDifferentials":["Functional (non-ulcer/essential) dyspepsia - the commonest cause of upper abdominal discomfort, subdivided into postprandial distress syndrome and epigastric pain syndrome; up to ~80% of patients evaluated for dyspepsia have a negative work-up (p.2523).","Proximal gastrointestinal tumours (p.2523).","Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (p.2523).","Pancreaticobiliary disease - biliary colic, chronic pancreatitis (p.2523).","Gastroduodenal Crohn's disease (p.2523).","Vascular (mesenteric/ischaemic) disease presenting as upper abdominal pain (p.2523)."],"infectionMimics":["Cytomegalovirus-related ulceration (a non-Hp, non-NSAID ulcer cause) (p.2523).","Herpes simplex virus-related ulceration (p.2523).","Helicobacter heilmannii infection (p.2523)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Functional/non-ulcer dyspepsia closely mimics ulcer symptoms without an actual ulcer (p.2523).","Drug- or toxin-induced ulceration not from NSAIDs/H. pylori - bisphosphonates, immune checkpoint inhibitors, chemotherapy, clopidogrel, crack cocaine, glucocorticoids combined with NSAIDs, mycophenolate mofetil, potassium chloride (p.2523).","Infiltrative/inflammatory mimics - Crohn's disease, sarcoidosis, eosinophilic infiltration, amyloidosis, lymphoma (pp.2523, 2532).","Ischaemia and radiation-induced mucosal injury (p.2523).","Idiopathic acid-hypersecretory states distinct from Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (p.2523)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Endoscopy is the most sensitive and specific test, allowing direct visualisation, biopsy to exclude malignancy (GU) and to test for H. pylori, and identification of a bleeding source (p.2523).","Barium studies of the upper GI tract can document an ulcer but are now rarely used as a first test (p.2523).","Biopsy urease tests (e.g. CLOtest, PyloriTek, Hpfast, Pronto Dry) with sensitivity/specificity >90-95% for H. pylori (p.2523).","Non-invasive H. pylori tests - serology, 13C-urea breath test, and monoclonal stool antigen test; the urea breath test or monoclonal stool antigen test is preferred to document eradication, performed off antisecretory drugs for at least 7 days (serology is not useful for confirming eradication) (pp.2523, 2531).","Fasting serum gastrin and gastric acid analysis, or secretin stimulation testing, in complicated or refractory ulcers to exclude Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (pp.2523, 2531-2532).","Screening for surreptitious aspirin/NSAID use (blood or urine) in refractory H. pylori-negative PUD (p.2523).","Multiple biopsies of any gastric ulcer at the index endoscopy, with repeat endoscopy at 8-12 weeks to document healing and re-biopsy if the ulcer persists (pp.2531-2532)."],"redFlags":["Alarm features warranting prompt endoscopy rather than empirical test-and-treat - nausea, vomiting, weight loss, evidence of GI bleeding, or age >=60 years with new dyspepsia (pp.2523, 2531).","Constant pain no longer relieved by food/antacids and radiating to the back suggests a penetrating ulcer (e.g. into the pancreas) (p.2522).","Sudden severe generalised abdominal pain with a board-like, rigid abdomen (the triad of acute pain, tachycardia, and rigidity) suggests perforation (pp.2522-2523).","Pain worsening with meals plus vomiting of undigested food, early satiety, weight loss, or a succussion splash suggests gastric outlet obstruction (pp.2522-2523).","Tarry stools or coffee-ground emesis indicate bleeding; tachycardia and orthostasis suggest hypovolaemia from blood loss or vomiting (p.2522)."],"prognosis":"More than 90% of both gastric and duodenal ulcers heal with conventional therapy; a GU not healed by 12 weeks or a DU not healed by 8 weeks is considered refractory. Eradicating H. pylori has cut duodenal ulcer recurrence by >80%. GI bleeding complicates roughly 15% of PUD with a 30-day mortality of about 2.5-10% (most deaths from non-bleeding causes such as multiorgan failure, pulmonary complications, and malignancy). Perforation occurs in ~6-7% of patients with 30-day mortality >20%, and penetration carries comparable mortality; gastric outlet obstruction is least common (~1-2%). Complications and mortality rise in patients over 60, partly reflecting greater NSAID use (pp.2522-2523, 2532).","pitfalls":["Every gastric ulcer must be biopsied (multiple samples) at discovery because a GU may be malignant, and healing on therapy does not exclude cancer - about 70% of GUs ultimately found malignant show significant (usually incomplete) healing; repeat endoscopy with biopsy at 8-12 weeks is needed if the ulcer persists (pp.2519, 2531-2532).","Abdominal pain has poor predictive value for an ulcer, and a normal exam or absent pain does not exclude PUD - many patients, especially the elderly or those on NSAIDs, present first with a complication (pp.2522-2523).","Right-sided epigastric tenderness in about 20% of patients can mislead the examiner; epigastric tenderness overall has low predictive value (p.2522).","Widespread PPI use can mask and delay the diagnosis of Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and increase false-positive gastrin results (p.2534).","Consider gastrinoma/ZES when ulcers occur in unusual locations (second part of duodenum or beyond), are multiple, are refractory, recur after acid-reducing surgery, present with frank complications, or arise without H. pylori or NSAID use (p.2535)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.2514","Harrison 22e p.2518","Harrison 22e p.2519","Harrison 22e p.2520","Harrison 22e p.2522","Harrison 22e p.2523","Harrison 22e p.2531","Harrison 22e p.2532","Harrison 22e p.2534","Harrison 22e p.2535"],"crossLinks":["gerd_chest","variceal_bleed","pancreatitis","biliary_colic"]},"pericarditis":{"name":"Acute pericarditis","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.90 (PE summary box); p.102 (tamponade as cause of shock); p.145-150 (Chest Discomfort: pericardial pain, exam, ECG); p.312 (dyspnea/pericardial disease table); p.324 (uraemic friction rub)","clinicalPearls":["Diagnosis rests on the combination of characteristic chest pain, a pericardial friction rub, and characteristic ECG changes; echocardiography can be normal in roughly a third to half of cases, so a normal echo does not exclude pericarditis (p.90).","The pericardial pain is typically pleuritic and positional: worse supine and on inspiration/coughing, relieved by sitting up and leaning forward (p.146, p.149).","Pain referred to the trapezius ridge is characteristic of pericardial inflammation and is not typical of angina (p.149).","Most of the pericardium is insensitive to pain; the discomfort of pericarditis is thought to arise largely from accompanying pleural inflammation, explaining its pleuritic, positional quality and referral to shoulder/neck (p.147).","A pericardial friction rub on auscultation reflects pericardial inflammation and is a key physical sign (p.149).","Pericarditis is an uncommon final diagnosis among undifferentiated acute chest pain presentations (about 4% in one ED series), so other causes are statistically more likely (p.145)."],"pathophysiology":"Inflammation of the pericardium from infectious or noninfectious causes. Because the visceral and most of the parietal pericardium are insensitive to pain, the discomfort is attributed mainly to associated pleural inflammation, producing pleuritic, position-dependent pain that, via overlapping phrenic and C3-C5 somatic innervation, may be referred to the shoulder, neck, and trapezius ridge (p.147, p.149).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute myocardial ischaemia / STEMI","Acute aortic syndrome (dissection)","Pulmonary embolism","Pleuritis / pneumonia","Musculoskeletal chest wall pain (e.g. costochondritis)","Gastro-oesophageal reflux / oesophageal disease","Herpes zoster"],"infectionMimics":["Acute myocarditis (often viral; chest discomfort and ST/T-wave changes can overlap)","Pneumonia / pleuritis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute coronary syndrome / STEMI (diffuse ST elevation of pericarditis can be mistaken for infarction)","Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy (ST elevation and biomarker rise mimicking MI)","Aortic dissection extending into the pericardium","Pulmonary embolism"],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG looking for diffuse ST-segment elevation with PR-segment depression (p.149)","Chest radiograph (pericardial calcification suggests chronic pericarditis; usually unremarkable in acute disease) (p.150)","Echocardiography to detect effusion (may be normal in 34-50% of acute cases) (p.90)","Measurement of pulsus paradoxus when tamponade is suspected (p.312)"],"redFlags":["Hypotension with significant tachycardia, suggesting tamponade and haemodynamic compromise (pericarditis with tamponade is a high-risk, potentially life-threatening cause of chest pain) (p.147, p.150)","Abnormal jugular venous pulse pattern consistent with pericardial tamponade (p.149)","Pulsus paradoxus (points to tamponade physiology) (p.312)","Tamponade is a recognised cause of obstructive shock (p.102)"],"prognosis":null,"pitfalls":["Diffuse (multi-lead) ST-segment elevation not confined to a single coronary territory, together with PR-segment depression, helps distinguish pericarditis from acute MI; ascribing these ECG changes to ischaemia is a key pitfall (p.149).","A normal echocardiogram does not exclude pericarditis (effusion absent in 34-50%) (p.90).","Chest-wall tenderness does not exclude myocardial ischaemia, and an unremarkable physical exam is not reassuring (p.149).","Low-grade fever is nonspecific and can accompany MI and thromboembolism as well as pericardial inflammation (p.149)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.90","Harrison 22e p.102","Harrison 22e p.145","Harrison 22e p.146","Harrison 22e p.147","Harrison 22e p.149","Harrison 22e p.150","Harrison 22e p.312","Harrison 22e p.324"],"crossLinks":["tamponade","acs","aortic_dissection","ckd"]},"pheo":{"name":"Phaeochromocytoma crisis","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e — referenced across chapters: hypertension (book p.2140-2141), cardiomyopathy/heart failure, palpitations, cancer genetics, cardiac tumors, mastocytosis; dedicated pheochromocytoma chapter (Ch. 399, book p.3073)","clinicalPearls":["The classic triad is episodic headache, palpitations and sweating (diaphoresis) occurring with paroxysmal hypertension (p.2140).","Most patients present with hypertension at a relatively young age, often between 30 and 50 years (p.2140).","Many patients have only non-specific symptoms; the florid paroxysmal presentation is not universal, so the diagnosis is easily missed (p.2140).","Catecholamine-secreting tumours arise from chromaffin cells: roughly 80-85% are in the adrenal medulla, the remainder being extra-adrenal paraganglioma in paraganglion tissue (p.2140).","Patients with pheochromocytoma frequently have postural (orthostatic) hypotension despite the hypertension, reflecting volume contraction and impaired sympathetic reflexes (cardiomyopathy chapter).","Catecholamine excess can produce a hyperdynamic state and palpitations, and is one of the recognised endocrine causes alongside thyrotoxicosis and mastocytosis (palpitations chapter)."],"pathophysiology":"Excess production of catecholamines, usually by a benign neuroendocrine tumour of chromaffin cells in the adrenal medulla (80-85%) or in extra-adrenal paraganglion tissue (paraganglioma). The catecholamine surge drives paroxysmal hypertension, headache, sweating and cardiac arrhythmias, and produces a hyperdynamic catecholaminergic state. Chronic/episodic catecholamine exposure can cause microvascular myocardial ischaemia (microinfarcts similar to those seen with stimulant toxicity) and a catecholamine-mediated cardiomyopathy; the same mechanism of sudden catecholamine release underlies stress (takotsubo / apical ballooning) cardiomyopathy (hypertension, cardiomyopathy and cardiac-tumour chapters).","additionalDifferentials":["Cushing's syndrome and other secondary causes of hypertension (more common than pheochromocytoma, so the classic triad is frequently misleading) (p.2140).","Thyrotoxicosis / hyperthyroidism (overlapping palpitations, sweating, tremor; an endocrine cause of palpitations) (palpitations chapter).","Panic / anxiety disorder and somatization (episodic palpitations, sweating, sense of doom mimicking paroxysms) (palpitations chapter).","Stimulant toxicity — cocaine, amphetamines and related catecholaminergic drugs (produce hypertension, tachyarrhythmia, microvascular ischaemia resembling pheochromocytoma) (cardiomyopathy chapter).","Systemic mastocytosis / mast-cell activation (flushing, palpitations, hypotensive episodes) (mastocytosis chapter)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Systemic mastocytosis — flushing and palpitation attacks; urinary metanephrines help exclude pheochromocytoma when evaluating mast-cell symptoms (mastocytosis chapter).","Carcinoid tumour — overlapping flushing/paroxysmal symptoms; assessed together with metanephrines in differential testing (mastocytosis chapter).","Stimulant (cocaine/amphetamine) toxicity producing a catecholaminergic crisis with hypertension and arrhythmia (cardiomyopathy chapter).","Takotsubo (stress / apical ballooning) cardiomyopathy — catecholamine-mediated, can follow a surge resembling pheochromocytoma (cardiac trauma / cardiomyopathy chapters)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood and 24-hour urinary catecholamine measurement (in addition to plasma/urine metanephrines) (p.2140).","Functional/positron emission tomography (PET) imaging in addition to CT/MRI of the abdomen for localisation (p.2140).","Nuclear localisation with 131-I-metaiodobenzylguanidine (MIBG) scanning, e.g. for extra-adrenal/cardiac paraganglioma (cardiac-tumour chapter).","Genetic evaluation when a hereditary predisposition is suspected (MEN2/RET, von Hippel-Lindau, neurofibromatosis type 1, hereditary paraganglioma syndromes) (p.2140)."],"redFlags":["Pheochromocytoma in crisis is named alongside aortic dissection and eclampsia as a hypertensive emergency demanding particularly rapid management; control blood pressure with a titratable IV agent (p.2141).","Very labile blood pressure and heart rate with episodic palpitations occurring together with new heart failure should raise suspicion of pheochromocytoma (cardiomyopathy chapter).","Coexisting postural hypotension with paroxysmal hypertension is a clue to catecholamine excess rather than essential hypertension (cardiomyopathy chapter)."],"prognosis":"A benign tumour is usual; definitive treatment is surgical extirpation (complete or partial adrenalectomy, typically minimally invasive), which is generally curative. Surgery requires prior alpha-adrenergic receptor blockade. Untreated catecholamine excess can precipitate hypertensive emergency, arrhythmia, catecholamine cardiomyopathy and heart failure (hypertension and cardiomyopathy chapters).","pitfalls":["Representativeness-heuristic trap: clinicians overestimate the probability of pheochromocytoma when the classic headache-palpitations-sweating triad is present, because they neglect its low prevalence relative to common causes of hypertension; the triad also occurs in patients who do not have the tumour (clinical-reasoning chapter, p.~? — diagnostic reasoning).","Conversely, non-specific or atypical presentations can lead to the diagnosis being missed entirely (clinical-reasoning chapter).","Postural hypotension may be wrongly attributed to over-treatment of hypertension rather than recognised as a catecholamine-excess clue (cardiomyopathy chapter)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.2140","Harrison 22e p.2141","Harrison 22e Ch.399 (Pheochromocytoma)"],"crossLinks":[]},"pleural_effusion":{"name":"Pleural effusion","system":"Pulmonary","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"89, 126, 314, 366, 373, 662, 663, 1235, 1430, 2243","clinicalPearls":["On examination an effusion gives dullness to percussion, reduced or absent breath sounds, and decreased tactile fremitus over the fluid; this contrasts with consolidation, where fremitus is increased (Harrison 22e p.2243).","Percussion helps separate the two causes of a quiet hemithorax: an effusion is dull, whereas a pneumothorax is hyper-resonant (Harrison 22e p.2243).","Skodaic resonance, a band of hyper-resonance just above the dull effusion, is a classic percussion sign (Harrison 22e p.89).","Bilateral effusions are characteristic of congestive heart failure (and some collagen-vascular disorders), whereas a unilateral effusion should raise suspicion of malignancy or pulmonary embolism, though heart failure and parapneumonic effusion can also be unilateral (Harrison 22e p.314).","A right-sided pleural effusion can occur in advanced cirrhosis even when no ascites is clinically detectable (Harrison 22e p.366).","In a malignant effusion an initially negative thoracentesis does not exclude cancer; repeating the tap is essential because cytologic yield improves with repeat sampling (Harrison 22e p.663)."],"pathophysiology":"Fluid abnormally accumulates in the pleural space when the balance of hydrostatic and oncotic forces, lymphatic drainage, or capillary permeability is disturbed. Transudates arise from systemic forces (raised hydrostatic pressure or low plasma oncotic pressure) as in the sodium-avid edematous states of heart failure, cirrhosis, and nephrotic syndrome (Harrison 22e p.422). Exudates result from local pleural or capillary processes such as infection, malignancy, or inflammation. In hepatic hydrothorax, ascitic fluid migrates through diaphragmatic fenestrae into the pleural space (Harrison 22e p.373). Tuberculous effusion typically reflects a hypersensitivity reaction to mycobacterial antigens after recent primary infection, or contiguous spread from parenchymal disease (Harrison 22e p.1430). Malignant effusions arise from pleural involvement or from lymphatic obstruction by tumor (Harrison 22e p.662).","additionalDifferentials":["Congestive heart failure (typically bilateral transudative effusions) (Harrison 22e p.314)","Cirrhosis / hepatic hydrothorax (often right-sided) (Harrison 22e p.366, p.373)","Nephrotic syndrome and other hypoalbuminemic states (Harrison 22e p.422)","Parapneumonic effusion / empyema complicating pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.1235)","Malignant effusion (e.g., lung cancer, metastatic disease) (Harrison 22e p.662)","Tuberculous pleurisy (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Pulmonary embolism (Harrison 22e p.314)"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculous pleural effusion — straw-colored (sometimes hemorrhagic) lymphocyte-predominant exudate that can mimic a parapneumonic or malignant effusion (Harrison 22e p.1430).","Tuberculous empyema, usually from rupture of a cavity into the pleural space, sometimes with a bronchopleural fistula and air in the pleural space (Harrison 22e p.1431).","Spontaneous bacterial empyema complicating hepatic hydrothorax in cirrhosis (Harrison 22e p.373)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Heart-failure transudate, which can be unilateral and be mistaken for a localized pathologic effusion (Harrison 22e p.314).","Hepatic hydrothorax from cirrhosis, mimicking other right-sided effusions (Harrison 22e p.366, p.373).","Malignant effusion, which may be exudative or bloody and can coexist with or be confused for a parapneumonic effusion (Harrison 22e p.663)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnostic thoracentesis to determine whether the fluid is a transudate or an exudate and to identify the underlying cause (Harrison 22e p.1430).","Pleural fluid pH — a value of ≤7.1 in a parapneumonic setting indicates empyema requiring drainage (Harrison 22e p.1235).","Pleural fluid protein, glucose, and cell count/differential; in TB the fluid is an exudate with protein >50% of serum (often ~4-6 g/dL), normal-to-low glucose, pH ~7.3 (occasionally <7.2), and 500-6000 WBC/uL, with neutrophils early and lymphocyte predominance later (Harrison 22e p.1430).","Pleural fluid adenosine deaminase (ADA) — an elevated level is consistent with TB and a very low level helps exclude it (Harrison 22e p.1430).","Pleural fluid IFN-gamma and lysozyme as adjuncts for suspected tuberculous effusion (Harrison 22e p.1430).","Pleural fluid cytology for malignancy, with repeat thoracentesis if the first sample is negative (Harrison 22e p.663).","Needle (closed) pleural biopsy for suspected TB, which reveals granulomas and/or yields positive culture in up to ~80% of cases and is favored over fluid analysis (Harrison 22e p.1430).","Xpert MTB/RIF on pleural biopsy tissue (positive in ~75%); testing pleural fluid itself with this assay is not recommended owing to low sensitivity (Harrison 22e p.1430).","Serum-to-pleural-fluid albumin gradient — a value >=1.1 g/dL suggests hepatic hydrothorax (Harrison 22e p.373)."],"redFlags":["Frank pus, organisms on microscopy, or pleural fluid pH <=7.1 in a parapneumonic effusion — diagnostic of empyema and mandates aggressive complete drainage, usually via chest tube (Harrison 22e p.1235).","New or persistent fever and leukocytosis after 4-5 days of appropriate antibiotics for pneumonia — suspect empyema (Harrison 22e p.1235).","Exudative or bloody effusion — raises concern for malignancy (Harrison 22e p.663).","Unilateral effusion without an obvious cause — investigate for malignancy or pulmonary embolism (Harrison 22e p.314)."],"prognosis":"Tuberculous pleural effusion responds rapidly to antituberculous chemotherapy and may even resolve spontaneously; concurrent glucocorticoids may shorten fever and chest pain but are of unproven benefit (Harrison 22e p.1430). Parapneumonic effusions are usually a self-limited inflammatory response, whereas a true empyema requires drainage and carries a worse course if undrained (Harrison 22e p.1235).","pitfalls":["Avoid attributing every quiet, dull hemithorax to effusion — confirm with percussion and fremitus, since pneumothorax and consolidation differ (Harrison 22e p.2243).","A negative initial pleural cytology does not rule out malignancy; failing to repeat the tap is a diagnostic pitfall (Harrison 22e p.663).","Do not assume a unilateral effusion is benign heart failure — it may be malignant or embolic (Harrison 22e p.314).","Testing pleural fluid (rather than biopsy tissue) with Xpert MTB/RIF is unreliable for TB because of low sensitivity (Harrison 22e p.1430).","AFB smears are rarely positive and cultures are often falsely negative in tuberculous effusions, so a negative smear/culture does not exclude pleural TB (Harrison 22e p.1430).","In hepatic hydrothorax, chest-tube placement should be avoided (Harrison 22e p.373)."],"severityClassification":"Parapneumonic effusions are distinguished from empyema by their character: an uncomplicated parapneumonic effusion is a self-limited reaction to adjacent pneumonia, whereas frank pus, visible organisms, or a pleural fluid pH <=7.1 defines empyema (a complicated/infected collection) that requires complete drainage (Harrison 22e p.1235).","references":["Harrison 22e p.89","Harrison 22e p.126","Harrison 22e p.314","Harrison 22e p.366","Harrison 22e p.373","Harrison 22e p.662","Harrison 22e p.663","Harrison 22e p.1235","Harrison 22e p.1430","Harrison 22e p.2243"],"crossLinks":["CAP","heart_failure","PULMONARY_TB","lung_cancer","decomp_cirrhosis"]},"pmr":{"name":"Polymyalgia rheumatica","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2945-2946 (Giant Cell Arteritis and Polymyalgia Rheumatica, Ch.375); supporting context p.2920 (Ch.370 RA), p.2938-2939 (Ch.382), p.2965 (Ch.385 Fibromyalgia), and Ch.385 (muscle disease approach)","clinicalPearls":["PMR causes stiffness, aching, and pain in the muscles of the neck, shoulders, lower back, hips, and thighs in a patient typically aged >50 years (p.2945).","PMR and giant cell arteritis are regarded as different clinical expressions of a single underlying disease process, supported by clinical association and pathophysiologic data (p.2945).","Although PMR most often occurs in isolation, it is found in roughly 40-50% of patients with giant cell arteritis (p.2945).","About 10-20% of patients presenting initially with apparently isolated PMR later go on to develop giant cell arteritis, so reassessment for GCA features should occur at follow-up visits (p.2945).","Inflammatory stiffness in PMR tends to be prolonged (lasting hours) and improves with activity, in contrast to the brief gel-phenomenon stiffness (<=45 min, worse with activity) of noninflammatory disorders such as osteoarthritis (p.2938).","Fatigue can be profound in PMR, mirroring other inflammatory conditions (p.2938).","Annual incidence of PMR in those aged >=50 years was 58.7 per 100,000 in Olmsted County, Minnesota (p.2945)."],"pathophysiology":"PMR sits on the same disease spectrum as giant cell arteritis, an antigen-driven panarteritis of medium- and large-sized vessels with mononuclear infiltrates and frequent giant cell formation. Activated CD4+ T lymphocytes, macrophages, and dendritic cells are central; restricted clonal T-cell receptor expansion suggests an antigen residing in the arterial wall. Two CD4 T-cell lineages (IFN-gamma-producing TH1 and IL-17-producing TH17) participate in vascular inflammation, with IL-6 signaling driving hepatic acute-phase protein (CRP, and effects on ESR) production. Familial aggregation and HLA-DR4 / HLA-DRB1*04 associations have been reported for the GCA end of the spectrum (p.2945).","additionalDifferentials":["Seronegative rheumatoid arthritis in the elderly, which can be difficult to distinguish from PMR; RA predominantly affects wrists/hands and ankles/feet at onset whereas PMR is girdle-predominant (p.2920).","Remitting seronegative symmetrical synovitis with pitting edema (RS3PE) — prominent distal limb pitting edema, prompt response to low-dose prednisone (p.2920).","Other inflammatory arthropathies / seronegative RA in patients who develop a worsening pattern of peripheral arthritis (p.2945).","Fibromyalgia — listed among conditions confused with PMR; PMR appears in the fibromyalgia differential (p.2965).","Osteoarthritis and other noninflammatory nonarticular conditions (bursitis, tendinitis) (p.2938-2939)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Inflammatory myopathy/myositis (polymyositis): PMR is included among nonarticular conditions to consider, but unlike myopathy it is not associated with true muscle weakness, and serum CK, EMG, and muscle biopsy are normal in PMR (p.2939, Ch.385).","Fibromyalgia: a painful, noninflammatory myofascial syndrome with normal CK, ESR, EMG, and muscle biopsy — distinguished from PMR by normal inflammatory markers (Ch.385).","Paraneoplastic syndromes (noted as mimics in the elderly inflammatory-arthritis context) (p.2920).","Chronic tophaceous gout and hepatitis C-related arthropathy (mimics of inflammatory arthritis in the elderly) (p.2920)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Rheumatoid factor and anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide (anti-CCP) should be negative; positive results point toward seronegative-RA spectrum or other inflammatory arthropathy (p.2946).","Serum creatine kinase is NOT elevated in PMR; elevation should prompt consideration of a myopathic process (p.2946).","Normochromic or slightly hypochromic anemia and elevated alkaline phosphatase (liver function abnormalities) may accompany the inflammatory state in the GCA/PMR spectrum (p.2945).","If GCA is suspected, evaluate temporal arteries (biopsy, or ultrasonography showing a noncompressible 'halo' sign) and consider vascular imaging (MR/CT, PET investigational) for large-vessel disease; therapy should not be delayed pending these studies (p.2945-2946)."],"redFlags":["New or worsening peripheral arthritis pattern — reconsider seronegative RA or another inflammatory arthropathy rather than PMR (p.2946).","Elevated serum CK or true muscle weakness — argues against PMR and suggests inflammatory myopathy (p.2946).","Jaw or tongue claudication, scalp tenderness, new headache, or a tender/thickened/nodular temporal artery — signal evolution to GCA with risk of ischemic optic neuropathy and sudden blindness (p.2945).","Arm claudication, diminished pulses, or bruits — may indicate large-vessel (e.g., subclavian) GCA involvement or aortic aneurysm risk (p.2945-2946)."],"prognosis":"Isolated PMR generally responds promptly to low-dose treatment, and most patients can be managed without IL-6-directed agents. Recurrent polymyalgia symptoms develop in the majority of patients during steroid tapering. The principal long-term concern is progression toward giant cell arteritis (10-20% of initially isolated PMR), which carries risks of visual loss and, late, thoracic aortic aneurysm/dissection (p.2945-2946).","pitfalls":["Treating an IL-6-inhibitor-exposed patient: blockade of IL-6 signaling normalizes CRP and ESR, so these markers can no longer be used to monitor disease activity or detect infection (p.2945).","Minor rises in ESR/CRP during tapering do not necessarily indicate disease exacerbation, particularly in an asymptomatic patient; ESR/CRP can also rise from infection (p.2946).","Assuming PMR explains distal/peripheral arthritis — PMR can mimic RA, and persistent or worsening peripheral arthritis warrants reconsideration (p.2946)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.2945","Harrison 22e p.2946","Harrison 22e p.2920","Harrison 22e p.2938","Harrison 22e p.2939","Harrison 22e p.2965"],"crossLinks":["temporal_arteritis","rheumatoid"]},"pneumothorax":{"name":"Pneumothorax","system":"Pulmonary","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"104, 105, 148, 2271, 2273, 2313, 2314, plus shock/cardiac-arrest and respiratory-exam chapters","clinicalPearls":["Pneumothorax is simply gas within the pleural space; classify it as spontaneous (no preceding trauma) versus traumatic, and spontaneous further as primary (no underlying lung disease) versus secondary (underlying lung disease present) (Harrison 22e p.2313).","A tension pneumothorax is defined physiologically as one in which pleural pressure stays positive throughout the entire respiratory cycle (Harrison 22e p.2313).","Tension pneumothorax should be treated as a clinical emergency on the basis of bedside findings; do not wait for a confirmatory chest radiograph before relieving the pressure, because untreated tension leads to death from inadequate cardiac output or severe hypoxemia (Harrison 22e p.2314).","When a tension pneumothorax is suspected, decompression precedes imaging: a large-bore needle is placed into the pleural space and a rush of escaping gas confirms the diagnosis (knowledge principle; no dose/technique detail) (Harrison 22e p.2314).","Primary spontaneous pneumothorax typically results from rupture of small apical subpleural blebs (cystic spaces within or just beneath the visceral pleura) (Harrison 22e p.2313).","Classic primary spontaneous pneumothorax patient is a young, tall, thin man; smoking, male sex, family history, and Marfan syndrome are recognized risk factors (Harrison 22e p.104, p.105).","Symptom onset in primary spontaneous pneumothorax is usually sudden but dyspnea can be mild, so presentation to care is sometimes delayed (Harrison 22e p.104).","Tension pneumothorax most often arises during mechanical ventilation or resuscitation; difficulty ventilating during resuscitation or high peak inspiratory pressures on the ventilator should strongly raise suspicion (Harrison 22e p.2314).","Persistent air leak is a recognized entity, defined as a non-resolving pneumothorax with an air leak lasting longer than about 5-7 days (Harrison 22e p.2273)."],"pathophysiology":"Pneumothorax is the accumulation of gas in the pleural space. In primary spontaneous pneumothorax, apical subpleural blebs rupture, allowing air to enter the pleural space without preceding trauma and in the absence of underlying lung disease. Secondary pneumothorax occurs when the same process complicates established lung disease, where loss of pulmonary reserve makes the event more dangerous. Traumatic pneumothorax results from penetrating or non-penetrating chest injury, and iatrogenic pneumothorax (a traumatic subtype) follows procedures such as transthoracic needle aspiration, thoracentesis, or central venous catheter insertion. In a tension pneumothorax, a one-way valve effect produces pleural pressure that remains positive across the whole respiratory cycle; this both severely compromises ventilation and transmits positive pressure to the mediastinum, reducing venous return to the heart and thereby cardiac output (Harrison 22e p.2313, p.2314).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute coronary syndrome / myocardial infarction (Harrison 22e p.105)","Acute aortic syndrome / aortic dissection (Harrison 22e p.104, p.105)","Pericarditis with tamponade (Harrison 22e p.105)","Pleurisy / pleuritis (Harrison 22e p.104)","Pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.104)","Esophageal disorders such as reflux or motility disorder mimicking angina (Harrison 22e p.105)","Musculoskeletal chest-wall pain including costochondritis (Tietze syndrome) (Harrison 22e p.105)","Panic disorder / anxiety state (Harrison 22e p.105)"],"infectionMimics":["Pneumonia or pleuritis presenting with sudden pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea (Harrison 22e p.104)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pulmonary embolism, which like pneumothorax can cause sudden dyspnea, pleuritic pain, and hemodynamic compromise (Harrison 22e p.104, p.105)","Acute coronary syndrome / myocardial infarction (Harrison 22e p.105)","Aortic dissection / acute aortic syndrome (Harrison 22e p.104, p.105)","Pericarditis with cardiac tamponade (Harrison 22e p.105, p.2293)","Esophageal reflux or spasm mimicking angina (Harrison 22e p.105)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph to confirm or exclude pneumothorax; it is the routine first-line image in acute chest discomfort and is useful for pneumothorax, though for tension it must not delay decompression (Harrison 22e p.105, p.2272).","Point-of-care lung ultrasound (POCUS), which can rapidly diagnose pneumothorax (as well as pleural effusion and consolidation) at the bedside (Harrison 22e p.2272, shock evaluation chapter).","Bedside physical examination as a diagnostic tool for tension pneumothorax: enlarged hemithorax with absent breath sounds, hyperresonance to percussion, mediastinal shift to the contralateral side, and hypotension (Harrison 22e p.2314).","Percussion to distinguish causes of diminished breath sounds: pneumothorax is hyper-resonant whereas pleural effusion is dull (Harrison 22e respiratory-examination chapter).","Palpation for subcutaneous emphysema as a sign of associated barotrauma (Harrison 22e respiratory-examination chapter)."],"redFlags":["Tracheal/mediastinal shift to the contralateral side, absent breath sounds over an enlarged hemithorax, hyperresonant percussion, and hypotension — the bedside signs of tension pneumothorax (Harrison 22e p.2314).","Hemodynamic collapse from trapped intrathoracic air defines tension pneumothorax as a medical emergency (Harrison 22e p.104).","Tension pneumothorax is a cause of obstructive shock and of pulseless electrical activity (PEA) arrest, and is a reversible cause to consider during resuscitation (Harrison 22e shock and cardiac-arrest chapters).","Pulsus paradoxus (inspiratory systolic fall greater than 10 mmHg) can accompany tension pneumothorax and signals significant hemodynamic compromise (Harrison 22e p.1813).","Tachycardia and hypotension in a patient with chest discomfort should trigger rapid evaluation for the most lethal causes, including tension pneumothorax (Harrison 22e p.105).","Difficulty ventilating during resuscitation or rising peak inspiratory pressures on mechanical ventilation should prompt urgent suspicion of tension pneumothorax (Harrison 22e p.2314)."],"prognosis":"Roughly half of patients with an initial primary spontaneous pneumothorax experience a recurrence. Secondary pneumothorax carries a worse outlook than pneumothorax in otherwise normal lungs because the underlying lung disease leaves little pulmonary reserve, making the event potentially more life-threatening. Untreated tension pneumothorax is rapidly fatal from inadequate cardiac output or severe hypoxemia (Harrison 22e p.2313, p.2314).","pitfalls":["Waiting for a chest radiograph to confirm a suspected tension pneumothorax instead of decompressing immediately — tension is a clinical diagnosis and delay can be fatal (Harrison 22e p.2314).","Underestimating a secondary pneumothorax in a patient with COPD, asthma, or cystic fibrosis; because of poor reserve it can be far more dangerous than a primary event (Harrison 22e p.104, p.2313).","Mild dyspnea in primary spontaneous pneumothorax can lead to delayed presentation, so a benign-seeming history does not exclude it (Harrison 22e p.104).","Forgetting iatrogenic pneumothorax after thoracentesis, transthoracic needle aspiration, or central line placement (Harrison 22e p.2314).","Attributing absent or diminished breath sounds to effusion when percussion (hyper-resonant in pneumothorax, dull in effusion) would distinguish them (Harrison 22e respiratory-examination chapter).","Overlooking tension pneumothorax as a reversible cause of PEA arrest and obstructive shock (Harrison 22e shock and cardiac-arrest chapters)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison classifies pneumothorax by mechanism and risk rather than a numeric score: (1) primary spontaneous (no underlying lung disease, often young tall thin smokers, frequently manageable conservatively if asymptomatic/minimally symptomatic); (2) secondary spontaneous (underlying lung disease present, e.g., COPD, asthma, cystic fibrosis — more life-threatening because of limited reserve); (3) traumatic, including the increasingly common iatrogenic subtype; and (4) tension pneumothorax, defined by positive pleural pressure throughout the respiratory cycle and treated as a medical emergency. Persistent air leak is a recognized complication defined as a non-resolving pneumothorax with air leak beyond about 5-7 days (Harrison 22e p.2313, p.2314, p.2273).","references":["Harrison 22e p.104","Harrison 22e p.105","Harrison 22e p.2271","Harrison 22e p.2272","Harrison 22e p.2273","Harrison 22e p.2313","Harrison 22e p.2314"],"crossLinks":["pe","COPD_EXACERBATION","asthma_exac"]},"renal_colic":{"name":"Renal / ureteric colic","system":"Urology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.154, p.381, p.383, p.406-407, p.551, p.627, p.631, p.747, p.1118, p.1334","clinicalPearls":["Renal colic is classic visceral pain from distention of a hollow viscus (the obstructed collecting system/ureter): cramping and intermittent rather than constant (p.551).","Stones obstructing urinary flow are the single most important risk factor for perinephric/renal abscess; 20-60% of patients with perinephric abscess have renal stones (p.1118).","Urea-splitting organisms (notably Proteus, also other urease producers) alkalinize the urine, favoring precipitation of struvite (magnesium ammonium phosphate) and carbonate-apatite, which can form staghorn calculi in the renal pelvis (p.1118, p.1334).","Struvite stones act as a persistent nidus for recurrent UTI and typically cannot be eradicated by antibiotics alone without removal of the stone (p.1118, p.1334).","Stone-associated obstruction is a recognized cause of postrenal azotemia/AKI; because one functioning kidney clears adequately, obstructive renal failure from stones generally requires bilateral ureteric obstruction or unilateral obstruction in a solitary/single functioning kidney (p.383).","Nephrolithiasis can be the presenting feature of an underlying metabolic disorder such as primary hyperparathyroidism/hypercalcemia (p.406).","Imaging of the upper tracts is advised in any patient with hematuria, a history of calculi, or prior urinary tract problems (p.747)."],"pathophysiology":"Colicky flank pain arises from acute distention of the renal collecting system and ureter when a stone obstructs urinary outflow, producing visceral cramping pain (p.551). Obstruction proximal to the bladder produces hydroureter and renal pelvic dilation (hydronephrosis); if unrelieved this impairs glomerular filtration and causes postrenal azotemia (p.383). In urease-producing infection (e.g., Proteus), hydrolysis of urea to ammonia raises urinary pH; the resulting alkaline environment precipitates struvite and carbonate-apatite, building staghorn calculi that perpetuate infection and obstruction (p.1118, p.1334). Stones also serve as a chronic nidus that promotes recurrent and recalcitrant urinary infection (p.1118).","additionalDifferentials":["Ruptured/impending-rupture abdominal aortic aneurysm — abdominal pain radiating to the flank, sacral region, or genitalia should always raise suspicion of a leaking AAA; pain may precede rupture by days (p.154).","Acute mesenteric ischemia (pain out of proportion to exam in a vascular patient) (p.154).","Other causes of acute lower abdominal pain such as appendicitis, salpingitis, ectopic pregnancy, inguinal hernia, inflammatory bowel disease, and bowel obstruction (p.18 abdominal pain differential table).","Ureteric obstruction due to extrinsic malignancy (pelvic/prostatic/gynecologic tumors, retroperitoneal nodes) or post-radiation ureteric fibrosis, which can mimic stone-related obstruction (p.631)."],"infectionMimics":["Acute pyelonephritis (flank pain, fever, pyuria) (p.1118).","Perinephric or renal abscess — flank/abdominal pain, fever in at least half; suspect when pyelonephritis remains febrile after 4-5 days, when urine culture is polymicrobial, when stones are known, or when fever and pyuria coexist with a sterile urine culture (p.1118)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm with flank/genital radiation, especially in older patients (p.154).","Ureteric obstruction from malignancy or retroperitoneal fibrosis presenting with flank pain (p.631)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Renal ultrasonography — useful to identify hydronephrosis/ureteric and pelvic dilation, though it may be falsely negative early or when ureteric dilation is prevented (e.g., retroperitoneal fibrosis or tumor encasement) (p.383, p.631).","Abdominal CT and renal ultrasound are the most useful modalities when perinephric/renal abscess is suspected (p.1118).","Urinalysis showing alkaline urine plus a urease-producing organism (e.g., Proteus) may prompt evaluation for urolithiasis, though Harrison notes clinical support for routine action on this finding is lacking (p.1334).","Serum calcium / PTH and metabolic workup when an underlying stone-promoting metabolic disorder (e.g., primary hyperparathyroidism) is suspected (p.406)."],"redFlags":["Pain radiating to flank, sacral region, or genitalia in an older patient — exclude a leaking abdominal aortic aneurysm (p.154).","Rising serum creatinine, oliguria, or anuria — suggests obstruction of a solitary kidney or bilateral obstruction and warrants immediate evaluation (p.383, p.631).","Persistent fever after several days of treatment for presumed pyelonephritis, polymicrobial urine, or fever/pyuria with sterile culture — suspect perinephric/renal abscess complicating an obstructing stone (p.1118)."],"prognosis":"Stone-related obstruction is usually reversible if relieved promptly; urinary tract obstruction accounts for under 5% of acute renal failure and must be excluded early because it is correctable (p.383). When obstruction is complicated by infection (abscess), diagnosis is frequently delayed and mortality is appreciable, though lower than historically (p.1118). Struvite/staghorn stones tend to recur and perpetuate infection unless the stone is removed (p.1118, p.1334).","pitfalls":["Assuming flank pain radiating to the groin is always a stone — in older patients a ruptured/leaking AAA can present identically and is rapidly fatal if missed (p.154).","Relying on a negative ultrasound to exclude obstruction; dilation may be absent early or when the ureter cannot distend (p.383).","Failing to recognize an infected obstructed system / perinephric abscess in a patient with known stones who stays febrile or has polymicrobial or sterile-but-pyuric urine (p.1118).","Overlooking an underlying metabolic cause (e.g., hyperparathyroidism) in a stone former (p.406)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.154","Harrison 22e p.381","Harrison 22e p.383","Harrison 22e p.406","Harrison 22e p.407","Harrison 22e p.551","Harrison 22e p.627","Harrison 22e p.631","Harrison 22e p.747","Harrison 22e p.1118","Harrison 22e p.1334"],"crossLinks":["PYELONEPHRITIS","aaa","hypercalcemia"]},"rhabdo":{"name":"Rhabdomyolysis","system":"Nephrology / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.102, p.343 (Ch.55), p.358-359 (Ch.56), p.363 (Ch.57), p.818 (Ch.103), p.1096 (Ch.134); supporting p.223, p.1653","clinicalPearls":["Myoglobinuria characteristically produces a positive urine dipstick for blood while urine microscopy shows no (or few) red cells — a key clue distinguishing pigment nephropathy from true haematuria (Harrison 22e p.343).","In rhabdomyolysis-associated acute tubular necrosis the fractional excretion of sodium (FENa) may be paradoxically low (<1%), unlike the classic >1% of established ATN, because the injury is often early/non-oliguric (Harrison 22e p.343).","Rhabdomyolysis is listed among the 'don't-miss' metabolic emergencies whose urgency can be underappreciated, alongside malignant hyperthermia and tumour lysis syndrome (Harrison 22e p.102).","Heat-related exertional rhabdomyolysis is a recognised, albeit rare, cause of sudden death in sickle cell trait carriers during strenuous exercise — a reason to counsel carriers before sports participation (Harrison 22e p.818)."],"pathophysiology":"Massive skeletal muscle breakdown releases intracellular contents — including myoglobin, potassium, phosphate and creatine kinase — into the circulation. Filtered myoglobin (a heme protein) is nephrotoxic and contributes to pigment-induced acute tubular necrosis; the released heme pigment also causes a false-positive dipstick reaction for blood. Concurrent volume sequestration into injured muscle produces a prerenal/hypovolaemic insult that compounds the tubular injury (Harrison 22e p.343).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Clostridial myositis/myonecrosis — predictably precipitates acute rhabdomyolysis (Harrison 22e p.1096).","Streptococcal (group A) myositis — also predictably causes acute rhabdomyolysis (Harrison 22e p.1096).","Viral myositis — influenza, echovirus, coxsackievirus and Epstein-Barr virus infections may be associated with rhabdomyolysis (Harrison 22e p.1096).","Legionella infection — reported association with rhabdomyolysis (Harrison 22e p.1096)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine dipstick for blood with microscopy — dipstick blood-positive but RBC-negative supports myoglobinuria rather than true haematuria (Harrison 22e p.343).","Urine sediment / microscopy for muddy-brown (pigmented) granular casts and tubular epithelial cells, indicating accompanying acute tubular necrosis (Harrison 22e p.343).","Serum phosphate and calcium — hyperphosphataemia and hypocalcaemia accompany acute muscle injury (Harrison 22e p.359, p.363).","Fractional excretion of sodium (FENa) — may be <1% in early rhabdomyolysis-associated ATN, limiting its discriminatory value (Harrison 22e p.343)."],"redFlags":["Hyperkalaemia from release of intracellular potassium during muscle necrosis — predictable and potentially life-threatening (Harrison 22e p.359).","Rising creatinine with pigmented granular casts signalling evolving pigment-induced acute tubular necrosis (Harrison 22e p.343)."],"prognosis":"Rhabdomyolysis-associated acute kidney injury is frequently of the milder, non-oliguric ATN type and is potentially reversible with adequate perfusion; the recovery phase may be complicated by rebound hypercalcaemia (Harrison 22e p.343, p.362).","pitfalls":["Interpreting a dipstick-positive 'haematuria' as bleeding from the urinary tract when no RBCs are seen on microscopy — this pattern reflects myoglobinuria in rhabdomyolysis (Harrison 22e p.343).","Relying on a low FENa to exclude ATN: rhabdomyolysis-related ATN can present with FENa <1% (Harrison 22e p.343).","Acute rhabdomyolysis typically causes hypocalcaemia, but the recovery phase (with renal failure) can produce rebound hypercalcaemia — calcium should not be over-corrected early (Harrison 22e p.362, p.363)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.102","Harrison 22e p.343","Harrison 22e p.359","Harrison 22e p.363","Harrison 22e p.818","Harrison 22e p.1096"],"crossLinks":["aki","hyperkalemia","hypercalcemia"]},"rheumatoid":{"name":"Rheumatoid arthritis","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Chapter 370 (Rheumatoid Arthritis), pp. 2839-2849","clinicalPearls":["Early-morning joint stiffness lasting more than 1 hour that improves with activity is characteristic; the earliest joints involved are typically the small joints of the hands and feet (Harrison 22e p.2839).","Wrists, MCP and PIP joints are most often affected; isolated DIP involvement usually reflects coexistent osteoarthritis rather than RA itself (Harrison 22e p.2839).","Flexor tendon tenosynovitis is a frequent hallmark, producing reduced range of motion, weakened grip and trigger fingers, and can lead to tendon rupture (most commonly flexor pollicis longus) (Harrison 22e p.2839-2840).","Anti-CCP (ACPA) and RF are useful both diagnostically and as prognostic biomarkers; anti-CCP carries the most value for predicting worse outcomes (Harrison 22e p.2839, p.2847).","Anti-CCP has sensitivity similar to RF but much higher specificity (approaching 95%), so a positive anti-CCP in early inflammatory arthritis helps distinguish RA from other arthritides; testing both RF and anti-CCP adds incremental yield because some patients are positive for only one (Harrison 22e p.2847).","About three-quarters of RA patients are seropositive (RF and/or ACPA) and one-quarter are seronegative; seronegative RA is a clinical diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2847).","A fever above 38.3C (101F) at any point in the course should raise suspicion of systemic vasculitis or superimposed infection rather than being attributed to RA activity alone (Harrison 22e p.2840).","Approximately 30% of RA patients are ANA-positive and some have p-ANCA, but anti-MPO and anti-PR3 are not expected and their presence points to an ANCA-associated vasculitis instead (Harrison 22e p.2847).","Synovial fluid in RA is inflammatory (typically 5,000-50,000 WBC/uL, neutrophil-predominant) versus under 2,000 WBC/uL in osteoarthritis; aspiration is most useful to confirm inflammation and to exclude infection or crystal arthritis (Harrison 22e p.2847)."],"pathophysiology":"RA is a chronic, systemic, symmetric erosive polyarthritis driven by an interaction of genetic predisposition (e.g., the HLA shared epitope) and environmental triggers such as cigarette smoking. Antigen-presenting cells activate synovial CD4+ T cells (via TCR-MHC class II and CD28-CD80/86 co-stimulation), which differentiate into TH1 and TH17 subsets. These activate B cells that mature into plasma cells producing autoantibodies (RF and anti-CCP/ACPA); immune complexes form within the joint and activate complement, amplifying inflammation. T effector cells stimulate synovial macrophages and fibroblasts to release pro-inflammatory cytokines, with TNF-alpha playing a central role by upregulating endothelial adhesion molecules and driving IL-1, IL-6 and GM-CSF production. TNF-alpha also disrupts the bone balance: it raises DKK-1 (inhibiting Wnt-driven osteoblastic bone formation) and promotes osteoclastogenesis through M-CSF and RANKL signalling, with osteoprotegerin acting as a decoy that limits bone loss. The net result is pannus formation, cartilage and bone erosion, and generalised inflammatory bone loss (Harrison 22e p.2845).","additionalDifferentials":["Primary Sjogren's syndrome - polyarthralgia and mild synovitis can mimic RA, and ~50% are RF-positive; distinguished by predominant sicca symptoms, ANA positivity and anti-Ro/SSA in about two-thirds (Harrison 22e p.2847).","Spondyloarthropathies (psoriatic arthritis, enteropathy-associated arthritis) - distinguished by sacroiliitis, enthesopathy, and presence of psoriasis or inflammatory bowel disease (Harrison 22e p.2847).","Polymyalgia rheumatica in elderly patients - can resemble seronegative RA; PMR centres on neck/shoulder and hip girdle, whereas RA predominantly affects wrists/hands and ankles/feet, especially at onset (Harrison 22e p.2847).","Remitting seronegative symmetrical synovitis with pitting edema (RS3PE) - features prominent distal-limb pitting edema (unusual in RA) and responds promptly to low-dose prednisone (Harrison 22e p.2847).","Paraneoplastic syndromes - may be confused with early RA (Harrison 22e p.2847)."],"infectionMimics":["Virus-related polyarthritis syndromes - can mimic RA and cause transient RF production, but usually resolve within 2-3 weeks (Harrison 22e p.2847).","Hepatitis C-related arthropathy - often involves small joints of the hands and is RF-positive in about half of cases, but generally anti-CCP negative (Harrison 22e p.2847).","Subacute bacterial endocarditis and chronic hepatitis B/C - chronic infections that can produce a positive RF and confound interpretation (Harrison 22e p.2847)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Chronic tophaceous gout - can mimic severe RA, and tophi may be mistaken for rheumatoid nodules (Harrison 22e p.2847).","Pseudogout (CPPD crystal arthritis) - inflammatory arthritis excluded via synovial fluid crystal analysis (Harrison 22e p.2847).","Osteoarthritis - non-inflammatory; DIP involvement and synovial fluid under 2,000 WBC/uL favour OA over RA (Harrison 22e p.2839, p.2847)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Anti-CCP (ACPA) antibodies - high specificity (~95%) for RA; useful diagnostically and prognostically (Harrison 22e p.2847).","Synovial fluid aspiration with cell count and crystal/microbiology analysis - confirms inflammatory arthritis and excludes infection or crystal disease (Harrison 22e p.2847).","MRI - greatest sensitivity for synovitis, effusions and early bone/bone-marrow changes; bone marrow edema predicts subsequent erosions (Harrison 22e p.2848).","Musculoskeletal ultrasound with power Doppler - detects synovitis, increased joint vascularity and erosions, often more than plain films; portable and radiation-free (Harrison 22e p.2848).","ANA and ANCA - may be positive in RA (~30% ANA, occasional p-ANCA), but anti-MPO/anti-PR3 should be negative (Harrison 22e p.2847).","High-resolution chest CT - to detect and characterise interstitial lung disease (UIP vs NSIP patterns) when respiratory symptoms arise (Harrison 22e p.2840)."],"redFlags":["Fever above 38.3C (101F) during the course should prompt evaluation for systemic vasculitis or infection rather than being ascribed to RA activity (Harrison 22e p.2840).","RA patients have the highest incidence of septic (most often S. aureus) arthritis due to chronically inflamed joints, glucocorticoid therapy and breakdown of nodules/vasculitic ulcers; TNF inhibitors add risk of mycobacterial and other infections (Harrison 22e Chap. 124, p.~).","Atlantoaxial (C1-C2) subluxation can cause compressive myelopathy; neurologic signs may evolve slowly with progressive instability (now seen in under 10% of patients) (Harrison 22e p.2840).","New-onset numbness, tingling or focal weakness (mononeuritis multiplex) suggests systemic rheumatoid vasculitis (Harrison 22e p.2841).","Two- to fourfold increased risk of lymphoma (most often diffuse large B-cell), greatest with high disease activity or Felty's syndrome (Harrison 22e p.2841)."],"prognosis":"Most patients show persistent, waxing-and-waning progressive disease; about 10% (particularly seronegative) remit spontaneously within 6 months, while a minority follow either a relapsing explosive course or an aggressively erosive course (now less common with modern therapy). Early disability is driven mainly by joint inflammation (potentially reversible) and later by cumulative irreversible joint damage. Overall mortality is roughly twice that of the general population, with excess deaths from respiratory illness, cardiovascular disease and infection; median life expectancy is shortened by about 7 years in men and 3 years in women. Worse survival is associated with systemic extraarticular involvement, low functional capacity, low socioeconomic status, low education and chronic prednisone use. Cardiovascular disease is the most common cause of death (Harrison 22e p.2848). Seropositivity for RF and especially anti-CCP predicts worse outcomes (Harrison 22e p.2847).","pitfalls":["The 2010 ACR-EULAR criteria are classification (not diagnostic) criteria intended to identify early patients likely to progress; a positive RF or anti-CCP alone does not establish the diagnosis without supporting clinical features (Harrison 22e p.2847).","A negative RF does not exclude RA - IgM RF is found in only ~75% of patients, and RF also occurs in other connective tissue diseases, chronic infections and 1-5% of healthy people (Harrison 22e p.2847).","Accelerated growth of subcutaneous nodules can occur in up to 10% of patients on long-term methotrexate and should not automatically be read as worsening disease (Harrison 22e p.2840).","Rheumatoid nodules and chronic tophaceous gout tophi can be confused with one another (Harrison 22e p.2840, p.2847).","Periarticular osteopenia, the classic earliest x-ray finding, is difficult to appreciate on plain (especially digitised) films, so early radiographs may appear normal (Harrison 22e p.2848)."],"severityClassification":"Diagnosis is supported by the 2010 ACR-EULAR classification criteria (for patients with at least one joint of definite clinical synovitis not better explained by another disease), scored 0-10 across four domains - joint involvement (0-5), serology (RF/ACPA, 0-3), acute-phase reactants (CRP/ESR, 0-1), and symptom duration (0-1); a score of 6 or more fulfils requirements for definite RA. Disease activity (the main driver of treatment decisions) is tracked with composite indices: ACR 20/50/70 improvement criteria (mainly for trials) and continuous measures including DAS, SDAI, CDAI and RAPID3 used in practice. Functional disability is measured with the Health Assessment Questionnaire (HAQ) (Harrison 22e p.2847-2848).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2839","Harrison 22e p.2840","Harrison 22e p.2841","Harrison 22e p.2842","Harrison 22e p.2845","Harrison 22e p.2847","Harrison 22e p.2848"],"crossLinks":["crystal_arthritis","pmr","sle_flare"]},"sah":{"name":"Subarachnoid hemorrhage","system":"Neurology / Vascular","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch. 440, p.3457-3461","clinicalPearls":["The single most important feature of aneurysmal SAH headache is its sudden (abrupt) onset, even more than its severity; ruptures may occasionally present as only moderate headache or as a change in the patient's usual headache pattern (p.3458).","Transient loss of consciousness occurs in nearly half of patients at the moment of rupture, reflecting an abrupt rise in intracranial pressure; many patients only report the headache after regaining consciousness (p.3458).","A third nerve palsy with pupillary dilation and pain around/behind the eye suggests an expanding aneurysm at the posterior communicating artery-internal carotid junction; a sixth nerve palsy may indicate a cavernous-sinus aneurysm (p.3458).","Quivering, jerking, or extensor posturing accompanying loss of consciousness at SAH onset is usually caused by the sharp ICP rise rather than true seizure activity (p.3461).","ECG changes mimicking cardiac ischaemia (ST/T changes, long QT, peaked or deeply inverted T waves) and a reversible (catecholamine-mediated) cardiomyopathy with regional wall-motion abnormalities can occur after SAH; an asymptomatic troponin rise is common (p.3459-3460).","Nimodipine improves outcomes in aneurysmal SAH, apparently by limiting ischaemic injury rather than by reducing vasospasm itself (knowledge, no dose) (p.3461).","Perimesencephalic (idiopathic) SAH localised to the perimesencephalic cisterns is benign, probably venous/capillary in origin, and angiography is typically unrevealing (p.3458)."],"pathophysiology":"Excluding trauma, the commonest cause is rupture of a saccular ('berry') aneurysm, which forms at bifurcations of large- to medium-sized intracranial arteries (about 89% in the anterior circulation, mostly on the circle of Willis). Aneurysm walls show loss of the internal elastic lamina at the neck base with medial thinning and replacement of smooth muscle by connective tissue; rupture is usually at the thin-walled dome through a tear often <=0.5 mm. Rupture deposits blood in the subarachnoid space of the basal cisterns (and sometimes adjacent parenchyma), abruptly raising ICP. Other causes include vascular malformations (AVM, dural arteriovenous fistula) and subarachnoid extension of a primary intracerebral haemorrhage. Roughly 2% of adults harbour intracranial aneurysms and about 20% of affected patients have multiple aneurysms (frequently mirror-image bilateral). Delayed cerebral ischaemia is thought to arise from clot breakdown products acting on basal arteries to cause vasospasm and vessel-wall thickening, but also from vasospasm-independent mechanisms (microthrombosis, inflammation, microvascular dysregulation, cortical spreading depolarisation). Hyponatraemia results from natriuresis with volume depletion ('cerebral salt wasting'), mediated by atrial and brain natriuretic peptides (p.3457-3461).","additionalDifferentials":["Primary thunderclap headache / reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome (RCVS) — many otherwise-undiagnosed thunderclap headaches are attributed to RCVS (p.3462)","Cervicocephalic arterial dissection (p.3462)","Cerebral venous (sinus) thrombosis (p.3462)","Ruptured arteriovenous malformation — but blood is usually intraparenchymal, not deposited in the basal cisterns, and symptomatic vasospasm is rare (p.3457)","Dural arteriovenous fistula (a recognised cause of SAH) (p.3457)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Thunderclap migraine — a migraine variant that can simulate SAH; a definitive workup to exclude aneurysm/other intracranial pathology is required before attributing sudden severe headache to it (p.3458)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Noncontrast CT detects >95% of cases when obtained within 72 h; sensitivity falls as time from ictus increases (p.3459)","Four-vessel conventional x-ray (catheter) angiography — gold standard to localise/define aneurysm anatomy and detect additional aneurysms; ruptured aneurysm can be treated at the same sitting (p.3459)","CT perfusion imaging — adjunct to identify potentially reversible ischaemic deficits in suspected delayed cerebral ischaemia (p.3459)","Transcranial Doppler ultrasound — daily/alternate-day bedside monitoring detects rising flow velocities of vasospasm (p.3459)","Serial (daily or twice-daily) serum electrolytes to detect early hyponatraemia (p.3459)","ECG and echocardiography to detect neurogenic cardiac changes and stress (catecholamine) cardiomyopathy (p.3459-3460)"],"redFlags":["Sudden unexplained headache of any location may represent a sentinel bleed (small aneurysmal leak) heralding imminent major haemorrhage and must be investigated (p.3459)","Declining mental status with worsening headache 4-14 days after SAH suggests delayed cerebral ischaemia from vasospasm (p.3459-3460)","High ICP refractory to treatment is a poor prognostic sign (p.3461)"],"prognosis":"Overall mortality of aneurysmal SAH is about 35%, with roughly a third dying before reaching hospital; over half of survivors are left with significant neurologic deficits from the initial bleed, delayed cerebral ischaemia, or hydrocephalus. Outcome worsens with increasing presentation grade: grade 1 (Hunt-Hess) patients rarely die if treated, whereas mortality for grades 4-5 may reach about 60%. In an untreated aneurysm, rebleeding risk is about 20% in the first 2 weeks, 30% in the first month, then about 3% per year; rerupture (~30% in the first month, peaking in the first 7 days) carries about 50% mortality. Delayed cerebral ischaemia occurs in about 30% and is the leading cause of delayed morbidity and death among initial survivors. Unruptured aneurysms <7 mm had ~0% 5-year rupture risk in a large study (though many ruptured aneurysms are <6 mm); giant aneurysms (>25 mm) carry ~8-10% annual rupture risk (p.3458-3460).","pitfalls":["A normal CT does not exclude SAH, especially as time elapses or with small or infratentorial bleeds; if CT is non-diagnostic and there is no mass lesion/obstructive hydrocephalus, lumbar puncture for xanthochromia is required (p.3458-3459).","Xanthochromia (yellow CSF from haemoglobin-to-bilirubin conversion) takes 6-12 h to appear, peaks at ~48 h, and persists 1-4 weeks; an LP performed too early may miss it (p.3459).","Free-water restriction should NOT be used to treat SAH-associated hyponatraemia (cerebral salt wasting): the resulting hypovolaemia/hypotension can precipitate cerebral ischaemia (p.3459, p.3461).","Marked hyponatraemia of several days' duration must not be corrected too rapidly because of the risk of osmotic demyelination syndrome (p.3461).","Lumbar puncture before imaging is appropriate only when CT is unavailable at the time of suspected SAH (p.3459)."],"severityClassification":"Two presentation-based clinical grading scales are used (Table 440-1): the Hunt-Hess scale (1 = asymptomatic/minimal headache and slight nuchal rigidity through 5 = coma with reflex posturing or flaccidity) and the World Federation of Neurosurgical Societies (WFNS) scale based on Glasgow Coma Scale score and motor deficits (1 = GCS 15 no motor deficit through 5 = GCS 3-6). Higher grade predicts worse outcome. The modified Fisher CT grading system (Table 440-2) predicts symptomatic vasospasm by the amount and distribution of subarachnoid/intraventricular blood ('thin' <1 mm vs 'thick' >=1 mm), with risk rising from 0% (grade 0) to ~40% (grade 4, thick blood with intraventricular blood) (p.3459-3460).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3457","Harrison 22e p.3458","Harrison 22e p.3459","Harrison 22e p.3460","Harrison 22e p.3461","Harrison 22e p.3462"],"crossLinks":["ich","ischemic_stroke","MENINGITIS","hyponatremia"]},"salicylate_tox":{"name":"Salicylate toxicity","system":"Toxicology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"p.370, p.376, p.3705-3706, p.3714-3715","clinicalPearls":["Classic course: early nausea/vomiting and hyperventilation with respiratory alkalosis and alkaline urine, evolving to a mixed respiratory alkalosis plus high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with paradoxical aciduria; late stages show acidemia with CNS and respiratory depression (p.3714).","Salicylates are the most common cause of drug-induced respiratory alkalosis, acting by direct stimulation of the medullary respiratory chemoreceptor (p.376).","Only part of the elevated anion gap is attributable to salicylate itself; concurrent lactic acid production and other endogenous unmeasured anions also contribute (p.370, p.3714).","Cerebral and pulmonary edema can develop in severe poisoning; non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema (ARDS) is a recognized complication (p.3705, p.3714).","Hypoglycemia, hypocalcemia, hypokalemia, and seizures can occur in salicylate toxicity (p.3714).","Ketosis can accompany salicylate poisoning and may mimic other causes of ketoacidosis (p.3705).","Salicylate is detected on quantitative serum assay; serum levels guide management and are usually available within an hour (p.3706).","Enteric-coated and sustained-release salicylate preparations cause delayed/slow absorption and can form gastric concretions (bezoars), acting as a 'toxic time-bomb' with continued rising levels (p.3705, p.3752)."],"pathophysiology":"Salicylate increases sensitivity of the CNS respiratory center and directly stimulates the medullary chemoreceptor, driving hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis. It also uncouples oxidative phosphorylation, inhibits Krebs cycle enzymes, and stimulates carbohydrate and lipid metabolism, generating unmeasured endogenous anions (and increased lactate) that produce a high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis (p.376, p.3714).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Other high-anion-gap metabolic acidoses: methanol, ethylene glycol, alcoholic/diabetic/starvation ketoacidosis, lactic acidosis, kidney failure (p.412)","Other causes of ketosis: acetone, isopropyl alcohol, alcoholic ketoacidosis (p.3705)","Other causes of drug-induced respiratory alkalosis: theophylline/aminophylline; also sepsis, hepatic failure, pregnancy/progesterone (p.376)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial quantitative serum salicylate level (guides management) (p.3706)","Anion gap and serum lactate (lactate typically lower than the anion gap in salicylate vs near-equal in shock/organ failure) (p.3705)","Serum glucose and calcium (risk of hypoglycemia, hypocalcemia) (p.3714)","Potassium (hypokalemia) (p.3714)","Abdominal x-ray/CT may show radiopaque enteric-coated salicylate tablets or concretions (p.3706)"],"redFlags":["Coma, seizures, cerebral edema (p.3714)","Pulmonary edema / ARDS (p.3705, p.3714)","Renal failure (impairs clearance) (p.376)","Progressive acid-base disturbance / late acidemia with CNS and respiratory depression (p.3714)","Salicylate level >7 mmol/L (100 mg/dL) after acute overdose (p.3714)"],"prognosis":null,"pitfalls":["Vigorous gastric lavage, if used, should be with isotonic saline, NOT NaHCO3 (p.370).","Only a portion of the anion gap is due to salicylate itself; do not assume the whole gap reflects drug level (p.370).","Acid (acidic) diuresis is never indicated and is potentially harmful (p.3752).","Acetazolamide can cause systemic metabolic acidosis if excreted bicarbonate is not replaced, which markedly reduces salicylate clearance (p.370).","Delayed/slow absorption from enteric-coated, sustained-release, or concretion-forming salicylate means a single early level can underestimate toxicity; repeat levels (p.3705, p.3752)."],"severityClassification":"Stages by clinical/acid-base course: (1) early respiratory alkalosis with alkalemia, alkaluria, nausea/vomiting and hyperventilation; (2) mixed respiratory alkalosis plus high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with paradoxical aciduria; (3) late acidemia with CNS and respiratory depression, cerebral and pulmonary edema in severe cases. Severe-toxicity markers include coma, cerebral edema, seizures, pulmonary edema, renal failure, progressive acid-base disturbance, and salicylate level >7 mmol/L (100 mg/dL) after acute overdose (p.3714).","references":["Harrison 22e p.370 (Fluid/Electrolyte/Acid-Base — drug- and toxin-induced acidosis: salicylates)","Harrison 22e p.376 (Respiratory alkalosis — salicylate mechanism)","Harrison 22e p.412 (Causes of high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis)","Harrison 22e p.3705-3706 (Poisoning and Drug Overdose — laboratory/radiologic assessment)","Harrison 22e p.3714-3715 (Table 470-4 — pathophysiologic features of salicylate poisoning)","Harrison 22e p.3752 (Enhancement of poison elimination; toxic time-bombs)"],"crossLinks":["drug_intox"]},"sarcoidosis":{"name":"Sarcoidosis","system":"Pulmonary / Multisystem","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.379 (Sarcoidosis), pp.2921-2929","clinicalPearls":["Sarcoidosis is a systemic granulomatous disease of unknown cause; the lung is involved in over 90% of cases, but roughly half of patients have extrapulmonary disease and almost any organ can be affected (p.2921, p.2924).","Diagnosis rests on three criteria: a compatible clinical picture, nonnecrotizing granulomatous inflammation on tissue, AND exclusion of other causes of granulomas — it is never made with absolute certainty, so new signs during follow-up should be re-evaluated (p.2923-2924).","Löfgren syndrome (fever, erythema nodosum, ankle arthralgias, bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy) occurs in up to ~10% of cases and, like Heerfordt syndrome, carries an excellent prognosis with frequent spontaneous resolution (p.2923).","Heerfordt syndrome = facial nerve palsy, fever, anterior uveitis, and bilateral parotid gland enlargement (p.2923, p.2928).","In very compelling presentations (classic Löfgren, Heerfordt, lupus pernio, or asymptomatic symmetrical bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy), biopsy may be deferred, but close clinical follow-up is still required (p.2924).","A BAL CD4:CD8 lymphocyte ratio >3.5 supports the diagnosis when combined with consistent clinical/radiographic findings, but a normal ratio does NOT exclude sarcoidosis; BAL is also important to exclude infection (p.2924).","Endobronchial ultrasound-guided lymph node sampling has high diagnostic yield with low procedural risk when hilar/mediastinal nodes are the prominent feature (p.2924).","Sarcoidosis is upper-lobe predominant on imaging with a lymphangitic (perilymphatic/subpleural/peribronchovascular) nodular distribution; airflow obstruction can occur in up to one-third of patients despite the classically restrictive pattern (p.2924-2925).","Every sarcoidosis patient should be screened for cardiac involvement (baseline ECG) because severity of lung disease does not predict cardiac sarcoidosis and sudden death can be the initial presentation (p.2925-2926).","Baseline workup in every patient should include PFTs, chest imaging, an ophthalmologic exam (to detect clinically silent uveitis), an ECG, and serum creatinine/alkaline phosphatase/calcium/CBC (p.2924).","Hypercalcemia from dysregulated macrophage/granuloma production of calcitriol (1,25-(OH)2 vitamin D) occurs despite suppressed PTH; patients should limit vitamin D intake, avoid excess sunlight, and stay hydrated (p.2927-2928).","Optic neuropathy in ocular sarcoidosis is an emergency requiring immediate immunosuppression to prevent sudden permanent vision loss (p.2927).","Smoking is a consistent NEGATIVE predictor of incident sarcoidosis (reduced odds), an unusual feature among lung diseases (p.2921-2922)."],"pathophysiology":"Sarcoidosis is thought to arise from a heightened, antigen-driven host immune response to an undetermined (likely inhaled bioaerosol or inorganic) environmental antigen in a genetically susceptible person; the inciting antigen may differ between individuals/populations. A poorly degradable material is presented to lymphocytes by dendritic cells/macrophages via MHC, triggering an innate cytokine/chemokine cascade and recruitment of adaptive immune cells. The immunopathologic hallmark is highly polarized CD4+ TH1 lymphocytes (less so CD8+) producing IFN-gamma, with upregulation of TH1-promoting cytokines/chemokines (IL-1, IL-2, IL-15, IL-18, CXCR3) that activate macrophages and drive formation of tightly organized nonnecrotizing (noncaseating) granulomas. Altered regulatory T-cell and TH17 responses are implicated in failure to resolve and in progression to fibrosis. Granulomas comprise macrophages, epithelioid cells, and multinucleated giant cells rimmed by lymphocytes/fibroblasts (CD4+ central, CD8+ and B cells peripheral). Mycobacterial proteins (mKatG, ESAT-6, superoxide dismutase A, antigen 85A, heat-shock proteins) and Cutibacterium acnes have been disproportionately implicated; the transmissibility seen with the Kveim-Siltzbach reagent and via organ transplantation supports an antigenic trigger (p.2922-2923).","additionalDifferentials":["Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (granulomas are loosely formed and nonnecrotizing)","Chronic beryllium disease","Silicosis","Talc inhalation or injection (foreign-body reaction)","Granulomatous reaction to tattoo ink","IgG4-related disease","ANCA-associated vasculitis (e.g., GPA)","Granulomatous-lymphocytic interstitial lung disease (associated with common variable immunodeficiency)","Rheumatoid nodules","Inflammatory bowel disease / Crohn-related granulomas","Bronchocentric granulomatosis","Necrotizing sarcoid granulomatosis (rare, vasculitis-associated)","Drug-induced sarcoid-like reaction (immune checkpoint inhibitors, antiretrovirals, interferon, TNF-alpha antagonists)"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculosis (caseating/necrotizing granulomas)","Atypical/nontuberculous mycobacterial infection","Histoplasmosis","Coccidioidomycosis","Blastomycosis","Aspergillosis","Cryptococcosis","Toxoplasmosis","Schistosomiasis","Herpes zoster","Tularemia (Francisella tularensis)","Q fever (Coxiella burnetii)","Bartonella henselae infection"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Chronic beryllium disease (berylliosis)","Silicosis","Aspiration pneumonitis","IgG4-related disease","Granulomatous reaction surrounding malignancy or systemic sarcoid-like reaction to malignancy/chemoimmunotherapy","Lymphoma presenting with hilar/mediastinal adenopathy"],"additionalInvestigations":["Pulmonary function tests at baseline and serially to track impairment/progression","Baseline ophthalmologic examination to detect clinically silent uveitis","Baseline ECG in every patient to screen for conduction block, ST-T changes, Q waves, frequent PVCs, or resting tachycardia","Chest CT to define disease extent and guide biopsy (not required in all patients)","Bronchoscopy with endobronchial ultrasound-guided lymph node sampling and/or transbronchial biopsy when hilar/mediastinal nodes or lung infiltrates predominate","BAL with lymphocyte subset analysis (CD4:CD8 ratio >3.5 is supportive) and to exclude infection","18F-FDG PET-CT to localize active inflammation and guide biopsy in difficult cases","Serum creatinine, alkaline phosphatase, complete blood count, and liver enzymes","25- and 1,25-hydroxyvitamin D levels and urinary calcium (hypercalciuria up to ~50%)","Cardiac MRI with gadolinium (preferred) or cardiac PET-CT for suspected cardiac sarcoidosis; Holter/signal-averaged ECG/echocardiography as indicated","Gadolinium-enhanced brain/spinal MRI plus CSF analysis (mononuclear pleocytosis, raised protein; CSF ACE/sIL-2R/lysozyme are supportive but not diagnostic) for suspected neurosarcoidosis","Echocardiography to screen for pulmonary hypertension; right heart catheterization is the gold standard","Soluble IL-2 receptor and serum ACE as adjuncts to monitor disease activity/burden (not diagnostic)"],"redFlags":["Exclude infection (especially TB and fungi such as histoplasmosis) before starting immunosuppression — abundant necrosis on biopsy points away from sarcoidosis toward infection","Acute symptomatic hypercalcemia can require hospital admission and urgent corticosteroids","Optic neuropathy threatens sudden permanent vision loss and is an ophthalmologic emergency","Cardiac involvement (new-onset AV block, ventricular arrhythmia, cardiomyopathy in younger patients) carries risk of sudden death and can be the first presentation","Neurologic involvement (basal/cranial nerve disease, mass lesions, meningitis) can cause neurologic demise","Untreated hypercalcemia leads to nephrocalcinosis and renal failure","New reproductive-organ nodules/masses require exclusion of malignancy given the rarity of genital sarcoidosis"],"prognosis":"Disease resolves spontaneously in just over half of patients, but 30-40% develop chronic disease threatening vital organs or causing debilitating symptoms. Most patients show radiographic or spirometric improvement by 2 years (treated or untreated). Death attributable to sarcoidosis occurs in roughly 1-5%, most often from progressive respiratory insufficiency/pulmonary fibrosis (leading cause in the US/Northern Europe) or cardiac involvement (predominant cause of death in Japan). A robust acute initial immune response predicts a better outcome: Löfgren and Heerfordt syndromes remit spontaneously in up to ~80%, and immunosuppression is rarely needed. Adverse prognostic factors include lupus pernio, chronic uveitis, older age, chronic hypercalcemia, nephrocalcinosis, African ancestry, pulmonary hypertension, fibrotic pulmonary disease, sinus involvement, cystic bone lesions, neurosarcoidosis, and cardiac involvement. Relapses are common and associated with shorter treatment duration (p.2928-2929).","pitfalls":["A normal BAL CD4:CD8 ratio, normal PFTs, or a normal lung exam does NOT exclude sarcoidosis despite active granulomatous inflammation (p.2924).","Serum ACE has low sensitivity and is not a reliable diagnostic or prognostic test; it only roughly tracks granuloma burden and is best used to monitor activity (p.2929).","Scadding (CXR) stages reveal overall resolution rates but do NOT predict disease activity, lung-function progression, or extrapulmonary involvement in an individual (p.2929).","Cardiac involvement is frequently subclinical and endomyocardial biopsy has poor sensitivity (patchy granulomas, basal predominance, sparing of the right ventricle), so a negative biopsy does not exclude cardiac sarcoidosis (p.2926).","Failing to screen for cardiac, ocular, and neurologic disease at baseline misses life- or sight-threatening involvement because pulmonary severity does not predict it (p.2924-2926).","Isolated extrapulmonary sarcoidosis (cardiac, hepatic, neurologic) can occur without lung disease and is easily overlooked (p.2926-2927).","Loosely formed granulomas suggest hypersensitivity pneumonitis and abundant necrosis suggests infection — misreading these as sarcoidosis is a classic error (p.2924).","Always re-evaluate alternative causes for isolated hepatic granulomas, which carry a broad differential (p.2927)."],"severityClassification":"Scadding chest x-ray stages describe the pulmonary pattern and correlate with spontaneous resolution rates (NOT with activity or prognosis in the individual): Stage 1 = bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy without parenchymal involvement (resolution ~55-90%); Stage 2 = bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy WITH parenchymal involvement (resolution ~40-70%); Stage 3 = parenchymal involvement without lymphadenopathy (resolution ~10-20%); Stage 4 = pulmonary fibrosis with architectural distortion and volume loss (resolution ~0%) (p.2925).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2921","Harrison 22e p.2922","Harrison 22e p.2923","Harrison 22e p.2924","Harrison 22e p.2925","Harrison 22e p.2926","Harrison 22e p.2927","Harrison 22e p.2928","Harrison 22e p.2929"],"crossLinks":["PULMONARY_TB","ild","hypercalcemia","lung_cancer"]},"seizure_epilepsy":{"name":"Seizure / epilepsy","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e, Chapter 436 (Seizures and Epilepsy), p.3408-3428","clinicalPearls":["A seizure is a transient event from abnormal excessive or synchronous neuronal activity; epilepsy is the distinct condition of recurrent unprovoked seizures from a chronic underlying process. A single seizure, or seizures due to a correctable/avoidable cause, is not by itself epilepsy (p.3408).","Operationally, epilepsy is defined as two or more unprovoked seizures, though a single seizure with clinical or EEG features portending high recurrence risk can establish the diagnosis (p.3408-3409).","Seizures are fundamentally classified as focal (network limited to one hemisphere; often structural) versus generalized (rapidly engaging both hemispheres) versus unknown onset; the older terms 'partial', 'simple/complex partial' are no longer used (p.3409).","Focal seizures are subclassified by awareness (intact vs impaired) and onset (motor vs nonmotor); a focal seizure can evolve to a bilateral tonic-clonic seizure (formerly 'secondary generalization') (p.3409).","Distinguishing a focal seizure that evolves to generalized from a primary generalized tonic-clonic seizure is critical but difficult, because bystanders emphasize the dramatic convulsive phase and overlook the subtle focal onset; a careful history for a preceding aura or focal features, and EEG, are needed because evaluation and treatment differ substantially (p.3410).","Status epilepticus is continuous seizures, or repetitive discrete seizures with impaired consciousness between them; for generalized convulsive status epilepticus the practical operational threshold is seizures lasting beyond 5 minutes (older definitions used 15-30 min) (p.3425).","Generalized convulsive status epilepticus (GCSE) is a medical emergency: prolonged seizures cause cardiorespiratory dysfunction, hyperthermia, and metabolic derangements that can produce irreversible CNS injury (p.3425-3426).","Treatment principle for status epilepticus is an escalating sequence beginning with a benzodiazepine, followed by an IV antiseizure drug, with anesthetic agents for refractory cases - alongside attention to airway, oxygenation, glucose, and reversible metabolic causes (no doses given here) (p.3426).","After 30-45 min of uninterrupted GCSE the motor signs can become very subtle (only fine finger twitching or rapid eye movements); if a patient stops overt convulsions yet remains comatose, an EEG is required to exclude ongoing (nonconvulsive) status epilepticus (p.3426).","If the sole cause of a seizure is a correctable metabolic disturbance (e.g., electrolyte or glucose abnormality), or a precipitant drug/illicit substance, treatment targets the underlying problem and chronic antiseizure drugs are usually unnecessary (p.3419).","An apparently unprovoked first seizure carries a recurrence risk estimated at roughly 31-71% in the first 12 months; accepted risk factors favoring treatment are prior brain insult (stroke/trauma), epileptiform EEG, a significant imaging abnormality, or a nocturnal seizure (p.3420).","A serum prolactin rise in the 30 min after most generalized and some focal seizures can support an epileptic cause, but it is unreliable because baseline levels are rarely available and some drugs elevate prolactin (p.3419).","The clinical range of focal seizure behaviors is extremely broad, so caution is advised before dismissing paroxysmal stereotypic bizarre episodes as non-epileptic (p.3409).","The age of the patient strongly informs the likely etiology of new seizures - e.g., febrile seizures and genetic/developmental causes in young children versus cerebrovascular disease, tumor, metabolic disturbance and degenerative disease in older adults (p.3411-3414)."],"pathophysiology":"A seizure reflects a shift in the normal balance between excitation and inhibition in the CNS. At the cellular level the hallmark of focal seizure activity is the electrographic spike, generated by near-simultaneous bursting (the 'paroxysmal depolarization shift') of local excitatory neurons - a prolonged membrane depolarization driven by calcium influx that opens voltage-gated sodium channels, followed by a GABA- or potassium-mediated hyperpolarizing afterpotential. Surrounding inhibition normally limits spread; when recruitment overwhelms it (via rising extracellular potassium, presynaptic calcium accumulation with enhanced transmitter release, NMDA-receptor activation, and ephaptic effects), activity propagates through local cortical and long commissural pathways (e.g., corpus callosum). Generalized 3-Hz spike-and-wave discharges of absence seizures arise from thalamocortical oscillatory circuits involving GABA-B receptors, T-type calcium channels, and potassium channels. Many causes converge on three interacting factor classes: endogenous (genetic/developmental seizure-threshold) factors, epileptogenic factors (CNS injury such as severe penetrating head trauma, stroke, infection, or neurodegeneration that, over months to years, transforms a normal network into a chronically hyperexcitable one - 'epileptogenesis'), and precipitating factors (sleep deprivation, stress, hormonal change, toxins, certain drugs, photic stimulation). Many inherited epilepsies are channelopathies due to ion-channel mutations (p.3411-3415).","additionalDifferentials":["Syncope (vasovagal, cardiac arrhythmia, valvular heart disease, cardiac failure, orthostatic hypotension) - the most frequent diagnostic dilemma versus generalized seizure (p.3418-3419)","Transient ischemic attack, particularly basilar artery TIA (p.3419)","Migraine - confusional migraine, basilar migraine (p.3419)","Acute psychosis (p.3416)","Sleep disorders - narcolepsy/cataplexy, benign sleep myoclonus (p.3419)","Movement disorders - tics, nonepileptic myoclonus, paroxysmal choreoathetosis (p.3419)","In children: breath-holding spells, benign paroxysmal vertigo, night terrors, sleepwalking, apnea, abdominal migraine with cyclic vomiting (p.3419)"],"infectionMimics":["CNS infection (meningitis/encephalitis) presenting with seizures and altered mentation - lumbar puncture indicated if suspected and mandatory in all HIV-infected patients even when asymptomatic (p.3417)","Parasitic CNS infection such as neurocysticercosis as a cause of seizures in adolescents/young adults from endemic areas (p.3413)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Psychogenic nonepileptic seizures (PNES) - nonepileptic behaviors, often a conversion reaction; clues include side-to-side head turning, ictal eye closure, asynchronous large-amplitude limb shaking, four-limb twitching without loss of consciousness, pelvic thrusting, and prolonged waxing/waning episodes; surface EEG may be normal especially for frontal-lobe-like events, and PNES can coexist with true epilepsy (p.3419)","Convulsive syncope - a brief (1-10 s) burst of convulsive movements at the onset of a faint, especially if the patient stays upright; postictal confusion is much less prominent than after a true seizure (p.3419)","Alcoholic blackouts and delirium tremens (p.3419)","Hypoglycemia and hypoxia as metabolic mimics of seizure (p.3419)","Hyperventilation and panic attack (p.3419)","Effects of psychoactive drugs / hallucinogens (p.3419)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Complete blood count, magnesium, liver and renal function tests, and urinalysis in addition to glucose/electrolytes/calcium to find metabolic causes (p.3416)","Toxicology screen of blood and urine, especially when no clear precipitant is identified (p.3417)","Lumbar puncture when meningitis/encephalitis is suspected; mandatory in all HIV-infected patients even without signs of infection (p.3417)","Serum and CSF autoantibody testing when epilepsy onset is fulminant or accompanied by psychiatric/cognitive disturbance (autoimmune/paraneoplastic encephalitis) (p.3417)","MRI preferred over CT for detecting structural lesions; CT performed emergently when MRI is not immediately available or when a mass/infection is suspected (p.3417-3418)","Interictal and ictal EEG, with activating procedures (hyperventilation, photic stimulation, sleep, sleep deprivation); a normal routine interictal EEG does NOT exclude epilepsy - it is normal in 50-80% of known epileptics initially (p.3416-3417)","Continuous video-EEG telemetry for poorly characterized events or seizures that are difficult to control, and to distinguish epileptic from psychogenic events (p.3417, p.3419)","Genetic testing in infants/children with suspected genetic epilepsy syndromes, and selected older patients with an undiagnosed early-life epilepsy syndrome (p.3418)"],"redFlags":["Generalized convulsive status epilepticus - immediate emergency due to risk of cardiorespiratory failure, hyperthermia, metabolic derangement, and irreversible CNS injury (p.3425-3426)","Persistent coma after overt convulsions stop - suspect ongoing nonconvulsive status epilepticus and obtain EEG (p.3426)","Focal features, focal neurologic deficit, or abnormal/changing neurologic exam - points to a structural cause (mass, stroke, infection, trauma, degenerative disease) requiring imaging (p.3416)","Fulminant new-onset seizures with psychiatric symptoms and cognitive change - suspect autoimmune/antibody-mediated encephalitis (p.3413-3414, p.3417)","First seizure in a patient with HIV - lumbar puncture is mandatory even without signs of infection (p.3417)","Atonic ('drop attack') seizures - substantial risk of direct head injury from the fall (p.3410)"],"prognosis":"About half of people respond to the first antiseizure drug; roughly 30% have ongoing seizures despite drug combinations (refractory epilepsy). People with epilepsy have a 2-3 times higher mortality than matched controls, mostly driven by the underlying etiology (e.g., tumor, stroke); excess deaths also arise from accidents, status epilepticus, and sudden unexpected death in epilepsy (SUDEP), which typically affects young patients with convulsive seizures and tends to occur at night, possibly via brainstem-mediated cardiorespiratory/arousal effects. Simple febrile seizures do not raise the risk of later epilepsy, whereas complex febrile seizures carry a 2-5% risk. Severe penetrating head trauma carries up to a ~45% (penetrating/depressed fracture/hemorrhage/prolonged coma: 30-50%) risk of subsequent epilepsy, closed head injury with contusion 5-25%, and mild concussion only slightly increased risk. Favorable factors for staying seizure-free after planned drug withdrawal include 1-5 years of control, a single seizure type, normal exam/intelligence, normal EEG, and no family history (p.3411, p.3414, p.3420, p.3424, p.3426-3427).","pitfalls":["Failing to exclude a treatable provoking cause (metabolic, toxic, structural, or infective) before labeling a patient as having epilepsy - many such seizures need only correction of the underlying problem, not chronic antiseizure drugs (p.3408, p.3419, p.3416-3417).","Assuming a normal interictal EEG rules out epilepsy - the initial routine EEG is normal in 50-80% of patients with known epilepsy, and EEG has limited sensitivity (p.3417).","Missing nonconvulsive status epilepticus: in altered consciousness or persistent coma without overt convulsions, NCSE can be present and is only revealed by EEG; intermittent seizures with prolonged postictal states are easily overlooked as causes of unexplained encephalopathy (p.3426).","Overlooking a focal onset by focusing on the dramatic generalized convulsive phase, thereby misclassifying the seizure and choosing the wrong evaluation/treatment pathway (p.3410).","Mistaking psychogenic nonepileptic seizures for epilepsy (or vice versa) on clinical grounds alone - even experienced epileptologists err; PNES and epilepsy can coexist, and video-EEG is the arbiter (p.3419).","Misinterpreting convulsive syncope (brief convulsive jerks at the start of a faint) as a primary seizure (p.3419).","Relying on headache or incontinence to distinguish seizure from syncope - these features are unreliable (p.3419).","Forgetting that the postictal Todd's paralysis (focal weakness for minutes to hours after a focal seizure) can mimic an acute structural deficit such as stroke (p.3409).","Misdiagnosing childhood absence epilepsy as attention deficit disorder or simple 'daydreaming' (p.3410)."],"severityClassification":"Seizures are classified (ILAE 2017) by onset: (1) Focal onset (with intact or impaired awareness; motor or nonmotor onset; may evolve to bilateral tonic-clonic); (2) Generalized onset - motor (tonic-clonic, or other such as atonic/myoclonic) or nonmotor (absence); (3) Unknown onset. Status epilepticus is staged by duration/refractoriness in management terms: impending/early (5-30 min), established and early-refractory (30 min to 48 h), and late refractory (>48 h), with subtypes generalized convulsive (GCSE) versus nonconvulsive (NCSE) (p.3409, p.3425-3426).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3408","Harrison 22e p.3409","Harrison 22e p.3410","Harrison 22e p.3411","Harrison 22e p.3413","Harrison 22e p.3414","Harrison 22e p.3415","Harrison 22e p.3416","Harrison 22e p.3417","Harrison 22e p.3418","Harrison 22e p.3419","Harrison 22e p.3420","Harrison 22e p.3424","Harrison 22e p.3425","Harrison 22e p.3426","Harrison 22e p.3427"],"crossLinks":["ENCEPHALITIS","hypoglycemia","metabolic_enceph","ischemic_stroke"]},"serotonin_nms":{"name":"Serotonin syndrome / NMS","system":"Toxicology / Neuro","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3517 (Ch.446/447, drug-induced movement disorders), p.3667 (Ch.463, SSRIs), p.3715 (Ch.470, toxidromes), p.3541 (Ch.451, hypothalamic autonomic disorders), p.3773 (Ch.478, heat illness differential), p.1172 (Ch.149, antibacterial drug interactions)","clinicalPearls":["Serotonin syndrome and NMS overlap closely (both cause fever, altered mental status, rigidity and autonomic instability), but prominent myoclonus is characteristically a feature of serotonin syndrome and helps separate it from NMS (p.3517).","NMS typically evolves over days to weeks after a neuroleptic/dopamine-antagonist is started or up-titrated, i.e. a comparatively slow, subacute onset (p.3517).","NMS is not only triggered by antipsychotics: it can also be precipitated by abrupt withdrawal of dopaminergic medication in Parkinson's disease patients (p.3517).","Serotonin syndrome arises from agents with serotonin-like activity or that block serotonin reuptake (e.g. tryptophan, MDMA/'ecstasy', meperidine, SSRIs), and is rare but potentially fatal (p.3517).","The serotonin-syndrome picture is attributed to hyperstimulation of brainstem 5-HT1A (and 5-HT2) receptors (p.3667).","The antibiotic linezolid (and to a lesser degree tedizolid) is a monoamine-oxidase inhibitor: combined with serotonergic agents such as SSRIs it can precipitate serotonin syndrome (p.1172).","Combining an MAOI with an SSRI is an absolute contraindication because of serotonin-syndrome risk; serotonergic agonists (tryptophan, fenfluramine, triptans) carry a similar hazard (p.3667).","Both syndromes are toxidromes in the CNS-syndrome / 'stimulated' physiologic category, alongside lithium toxicity, extrapyramidal reactions and strychnine (p.3715)."],"pathophysiology":"Serotonin syndrome results from excess CNS and peripheral serotonergic activity — promotion of serotonin release, inhibition of its reuptake, or direct stimulation of serotonin receptors (chiefly 5-HT1A and 5-HT2), occurring alone or when serotonergic drugs are combined. NMS, in contrast, is linked to dopamine-antagonist (neuroleptic) exposure or abrupt loss of dopaminergic tone, producing central hyperthermia, rigidity and autonomic dysfunction; central dopamine depletion impairs hypothalamic (preoptic/anterior) thermoregulation. Both are classified among the hypothalamic disorders of autonomic/temperature regulation (Harrison 22e p.3541, p.3517, p.3715).","additionalDifferentials":["Neuroleptic malignant syndrome vs serotonin syndrome (mutual differential; distinguish by onset speed, neuromuscular pattern and offending drug class)","Lithium toxicity (tremor, hyperreflexia, myoclonus, encephalopathy, may include hyperthermia and seizures in severe cases)","Anticholinergic toxidrome","Sympathomimetic toxicity (amphetamines, cocaine)","Strychnine poisoning","Withdrawal syndromes (ethanol, sedative-hypnotics)"],"infectionMimics":["Meningitis","Encephalitis (including brainstem encephalitis)","Cerebral abscess","Sepsis / systemic inflammatory response syndrome","Malaria","Typhoid","Tetanus"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Malignant hyperthermia (e.g. after general anesthesia)","Heatstroke / exertional and classic heat illness","Thyroid storm","Pheochromocytoma","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Status epilepticus","Anticholinergic toxidrome","Stimulant/sympathomimetic toxicity"],"additionalInvestigations":["Core temperature measurement (most reliable prognosticator of poor outcome in stimulant/withdrawal-type toxidromes)","Creatine kinase (markedly elevated in NMS; rhabdomyolysis complicates both)","Lactate / arterial blood gas (lactic acidosis is a recognised complication)","Coagulation studies (monitor for disseminated intravascular coagulation when hyperthermia is sustained)","Toxicology screen and detailed medication review (serotonergic agents, dopamine antagonists, recent dose changes or dopaminergic withdrawal)","Cranial CT and CSF analysis if mental status fails to improve, to exclude structural or infectious CNS causes"],"redFlags":["Hyperthermia — sustained core temperature elevation predicts poor outcome and can progress to irreversible cellular injury","Rhabdomyolysis with markedly elevated creatine kinase","Autonomic instability — labile blood pressure, tachycardia, diaphoresis","Acute renal failure","Lactic acidosis","Disseminated intravascular coagulation / coagulopathy","Coma, seizures or multisystem organ failure"],"prognosis":"Both syndromes are potentially fatal; serotonin syndrome is described as rare but able to progress to death, and NMS can be lethal (e.g. typical neuroleptics in Lewy body disease can cause NMS and death). The duration of hyperthermia drives morbidity and mortality, and progression to rhabdomyolysis, renal failure, DIC and multisystem organ failure marks severe disease (Harrison 22e p.3517, p.3715, p.3773).","pitfalls":["Mistaking one syndrome for the other: rapid-onset hyperreflexia/myoclonus/clonus after a serotonergic drug points to serotonin syndrome, whereas slow (days-to-weeks) lead-pipe rigidity with bradyreflexia after a dopamine-antagonist points to NMS — the offending drug class and onset tempo are the key discriminators (p.3517).","Overlooking non-psychiatric culprits: the antibiotic linezolid is an MAOI and can trigger serotonin syndrome with concurrent SSRIs (p.1172).","Forgetting that NMS can be precipitated by stopping dopaminergic drugs (not only by starting antipsychotics) in Parkinson's disease (p.3517).","Anchoring on infection: the fever-plus-rigidity-plus-altered-mental-status picture closely mimics meningitis/encephalitis and sepsis, and structural/infectious causes should be excluded if mental status does not improve (p.3715, p.3773).","Dantrolene is ineffective for hyperthermia not caused by malignant hyperthermia, so a hyperthermia mechanism must be identified rather than assumed (p.3776).","Some atypical antipsychotics (e.g. chlorpromazine used for agitation) can themselves cause or worsen NMS — adding a neuroleptic to an undifferentiated hyperthermic patient can be harmful (p.3776)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames severity by the physiologic toxidrome state (stimulated vs depressed vs discordant) and by the degree of physiologic derangement on serial vital signs; escalating findings — hyperthermia, lactic acidosis, rhabdomyolysis and multisystem organ failure — mark severe serotonin syndrome, while marked CK elevation with renal failure and labile autonomic signs marks severe NMS. No formal numeric grading scale is given in the source (Harrison 22e p.3715, p.3517).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3517","Harrison 22e p.3667","Harrison 22e p.3715","Harrison 22e p.3541","Harrison 22e p.3773","Harrison 22e p.3776","Harrison 22e p.1172"],"crossLinks":["drug_intox","rhabdo","thyroid_storm","metabolic_enceph"]},"sjs_ten":{"name":"Stevens-Johnson syndrome / TEN","system":"Dermatology / Emergency","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.63 (Cutaneous Drug Reactions) p.416-426, esp. p.467-468; Ch.21 (Fever and Rash) Table 21-1 p.142","clinicalPearls":["SJS/TEN is a single disease spectrum of full-thickness epidermal necrosis with blistering and mucosal/epidermal detachment, separated only by the extent of body surface area (BSA) detachment.","Drugs cause roughly 80% of cases; the most frequently implicated agents are sulfonamide antibiotics, allopurinol, antiepileptics (lamotrigine, phenytoin, carbamazepine), oxicam NSAIDs, beta-lactam and other antibiotics, and nevirapine.","Onset is typically delayed, around 5-28 days after starting the culprit drug.","Prodrome: fever >39 C (102.2 F), sore throat, and conjunctivitis precede or accompany the rash; lesions are painful, dusky, atypical and target-like.","It is uncommon in children and over-represented among people with HIV, SLE, certain HLA types, and slow acetylators.","Mucosal surfaces are characteristically involved, and Nikolsky's sign is positive.","SJS/TEN is mediated by CD8+ cytotoxic T cells recognizing a drug-HLA class I complex; granulysin released by these cells is thought to be the principal mediator of keratinocyte death.","Strong, drug- and phenotype-specific HLA associations exist: HLA-B*15:02 with carbamazepine SJS/TEN (Han Chinese), HLA-B*58:01 with allopurinol, HLA-B*13:01 with dapsone/sulfonamide.","Frozen-section skin biopsy can aid rapid diagnosis and distinguish SJS/TEN (full-thickness epidermal necrosis) from mimics.","Loss of skin barrier integrity leads to dehydration and secondary sepsis, which are major causes of death."],"pathophysiology":"A delayed, T-cell-mediated (type IVa, cytotoxic) severe cutaneous adverse reaction. A drug or metabolite-altered antigen is presented in association with a risk HLA class I allele by keratinocytes or other antigen-presenting cells; a restricted T-cell receptor on skin-enriched CD8+ T cells recognizes this drug-HLA complex, triggering activation and release of cytolytic mediators (notably granulysin) that cause full-thickness epidermal keratinocyte death and detachment. Proposed antigen-presentation models include hapten/prohapten covalent binding, the pharmacologic-interaction (p-i) noncovalent model, and the altered-peptide-repertoire model (as proven for abacavir-HLA-B*57:01). Carbamazepine SJS/TEN shows a highly restricted V-alpha/V-beta TCR repertoire shared among HLA-B*15:02 patients. A risk HLA allele alone is insufficient; non-MHC factors such as drug metabolism (e.g., CYP2C9*3 slow metabolism with phenytoin) contribute. (Harrison 22e p.417-420)","additionalDifferentials":["Acute generalized exanthematous pustulosis (AGEP) - may resemble early SJS but erosions are superficial and prominent mucosal involvement is lacking","Drug-induced hypersensitivity syndrome / DRESS (DIHS) - can have TEN-like overlap features","Overlap hypersensitivity syndromes (DIHS-TEN-like, AGEP-TEN-like) - up to ~20% of severe drug eruptions show overlap, supporting a clinical spectrum"],"infectionMimics":["Reactive infectious mucocutaneous eruption (RIME) - Mycoplasma and other respiratory infections in children cause prominent mucositis with limited cutaneous involvement","Erythema multiforme associated with herpes simplex virus - painful mucosal erosions and target lesions, typically acral, with limited skin detachment"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Staphylococcal scalded-skin syndrome (SSSS) - diffuse tender erythema with bullae/desquamation and positive Nikolsky's sign; cleavage is superficial (subcorneal), spares mucosa","DRESS / drug-induced hypersensitivity syndrome - maculopapular eruption, facial edema, eosinophilia, multiorgan (especially hepatic) involvement","Erythema multiforme major - target lesions with mucous-membrane involvement","Acute generalized exanthematous pustulosis (AGEP) - sterile pustules on erythema, flexural predominance","Exfoliative erythroderma syndrome - diffuse erythema/scaling, may be drug-induced","Autoimmune blistering disease (e.g., bullous pemphigoid, which can also be drug-induced/exacerbated)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Frozen-section skin biopsy for rapid diagnosis; histology shows full-thickness epidermal necrosis (distinguishes from AGEP's superficial/subcorneal neutrophilic pustules)","Calculate BSA of epidermal detachment to classify SJS vs overlap vs TEN","Assess for renal insufficiency and consider underlying malignancy (both poor-prognosis markers)","Patch testing with the responsible drug (after recovery) and use of the ALDEN algorithm to rank likelihood of drug causality"],"redFlags":["Extensive/rapidly progressing epidermal detachment (BSA toward TEN range) - maximal-severity form","Older age, malignancy, or renal insufficiency - independently associated with poor prognosis","Signs of sepsis or dehydration from loss of skin barrier integrity","Mucosal (ocular, oral, urogenital, respiratory) involvement - risk of acute and long-term sequelae"],"prognosis":"Mortality is substantial and rises with the extent of epidermal detachment: at least ~10% of SJS and ~30% of TEN patients die from the disease (overall reported mortality up to ~30%). Older age, greater BSA detachment, malignancy, and renal insufficiency are independent poor-prognostic factors. Best outcomes follow early diagnosis and immediate withdrawal of the culprit drug. (Harrison 22e p.142, p.424-425)","pitfalls":["Early SJS may be hard to distinguish from AGEP; in AGEP erosions are superficial and prominent mucosal involvement is NOT present.","Drugs may be implicated even when the eruption appears shortly AFTER the drug has been discontinued (delayed-type reactions).","A risk HLA allele does NOT by itself cause SJS/TEN - most carriers remain drug-tolerant; other factors (e.g., drug metabolism) are required.","Designation of a single diagnosis is NOT always possible in overlap hypersensitivity syndromes; manage according to the dominant evolving clinical features.","Skin biopsy helps characterize the reaction but does NOT by itself establish drug causality."],"severityClassification":"Classified by percent body surface area (BSA) of epidermal detachment: SJS = <10% BSA; SJS/TEN overlap = 10-30% BSA; TEN = >30% BSA (maximal form). (Harrison 22e p.142, p.424). SCORTEN (severity-of-illness score for TEN) is the standard prognostic tool; note the source text does not enumerate its parameters.","references":["Harrison 22e p.142","Harrison 22e p.416","Harrison 22e p.417","Harrison 22e p.418","Harrison 22e p.419","Harrison 22e p.420","Harrison 22e p.424","Harrison 22e p.425"],"crossLinks":["anaphylaxis","angioedema_acei"]},"sle_flare":{"name":"SLE / autoimmune flare","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.368 (Systemic Lupus Erythematosus), pp.2827-2836","clinicalPearls":["Roughly 90% of SLE patients are women, most of childbearing age; prevalence is highest among Black women, then Hispanic, White, and Asian/Pacific Islander women; disease tends to be more severe in non-White patients (p.2827).","Autoantibodies can be detectable years before clinical diagnosis, so a positive serology in a symptomatic patient supports attributing nonspecific complaints to SLE (p.2827, 2829).","Most patients have active disease despite therapy or recurrent flares; treatment goals are low disease activity (SLEDAI 0-4) or remission on ≤5 mg/day prednisone equivalent (p.2829, 2832).","A proposed clinical split distinguishes type 1 lupus (classic inflammatory/organ manifestations such as nephritis, arthritis, vasculitis that respond to immunosuppression) from type 2 lupus (fatigue, diffuse pain, brain fog, depression, sleep disturbance that respond poorly to immunosuppression) (p.2827).","Anti-dsDNA titers and C3/C4 levels track disease activity: anti-dsDNA is produced by short-lived plasmablasts that turn over quickly, and complement falls during high activity as it is consumed by immune complexes (p.2832).","Rising anti-dsDNA and falling C3/C4 can serve as warning signs of impending active nephritis even in an asymptomatic patient (p.2830).","Lupus arthritis is typically non-erosive (Jaccoud-like reversible deformity from ligamentous laxity); overlap with rheumatoid arthritis is termed 'rhupus' (p.2829).","Pain out of proportion in a single hip, shoulder, or knee — especially with prior corticosteroid use — should raise concern for avascular necrosis of bone (p.2829).","Accelerated atherosclerosis is a major cause of premature death, notably myocardial infarction and stroke in young patients without traditional risk factors, and is the leading cause of death after ~5 years of disease (p.2829, 2836).","Antiphospholipid antibodies/APS coexist in about a third of SLE patients and markedly raise risk of arterial/venous thrombosis, stroke (reported in up to 19%), Libman-Sacks endocarditis, and pregnancy loss (p.2829, 2830, 2831)."],"pathophysiology":"SLE arises from overactive innate and adaptive immunity with autoantibodies directed at nuclear components (nucleic acids, nucleosomes, RNA-binding proteins). Impaired clearance of cellular debris — driven by high neutrophil turnover/NETosis and defective complement-mediated phagocytosis — overwhelms macrophages and dendritic cells. Accumulated nucleic acids engage toll-like receptors and nucleic-acid sensors in plasmacytoid dendritic cells, driving type 1 interferon production that primes the immune response. Autoreactive B cells (including expanded age-associated B cells) lose tolerance and, with help from T follicular/T peripheral helper cells, mature into autoantibody-secreting plasma cells, while regulatory T-cell function is defective. Resulting immune complexes deposit in tissues and cause inflammation, repair, and eventual fibrosis after recurrent flares. Strong genetic component (monozygotic twin concordance 24% vs 2% dizygotic; >150 GWAS loci, with the MHC the strongest and nearly half of loci linked to type 1 interferon; monogenic complement-pathway deficiencies — C1q, C1r/s, C2, C4 — cause severe SLE). Environmental triggers include UV light, smoking, crystalline silica, mercury/toxins, sex hormones, EBV, and gut microbiome/permeability changes (p.2827-2829).","additionalDifferentials":["Rosacea (malar rash mimic — but rosacea involves the nasolabial folds, whereas lupus malar rash spares them) (p.2829)","Rheumatoid arthritis, or RA-SLE overlap ('rhupus') (p.2829)","Drug-induced lupus (antihistone antibodies; ~6-12% of lupus cases; rarely renal/CNS; resolves on stopping the drug) (p.2827, 2835)","Other systemic autoimmune diseases sharing ANA subtypes: myositis/dermatomyositis, Sjögren's syndrome, systemic sclerosis, mixed connective tissue/overlap syndromes (anti-RNP) (p.2832)","Migraine or tension headache (lupus headache can be difficult to distinguish) (p.2829)","Glucocorticoid-induced psychosis (must be excluded before attributing psychosis to neuropsychiatric lupus) (p.2829)"],"infectionMimics":["Acute lupus pneumonitis — pulmonary infiltrates can be radiographically indistinguishable from pneumonia (p.2829)","Infectious encephalitis — should be ruled out before attributing acute psychosis/confusional state to CNS lupus (p.2829)","Infection as a cause of cytopenias — SLE cytopenias are multifactorial (disease activity, medication, or infection) (p.2830)","Epstein-Barr virus and other viral infections — may both trigger and mimic flare via interferon induction (p.2828)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Atypical hemolytic-uremic syndrome / TTP-like microvascular thrombotic crisis (hemolysis, thrombocytopenia, microvascular thrombosis, schistocytes, low ADAMTS13, high LDH) (p.2831)","Antiphospholipid syndrome thrombotic events presenting as stroke, DVT/PE, Budd-Chiari, or Libman-Sacks embolism (p.2829-2831)","Glucocorticoid-induced complications mimicking active disease (steroid myopathy, hyperglycemia, psychosis, avascular necrosis) (p.2829, 2833)","Macrophage activation syndrome / hemophagocytic syndrome (acute hemophagocytic syndrome listed among severe SLE manifestations) (p.2833, and HLH associated with SLE p. table 67824 region)"],"additionalInvestigations":["ANA by immunofluorescence is the best screening test (positive in ~95%; titer ≥1:80 favors SLE); repeated negatives make SLE unlikely but ~5% are ANA-negative (p.2831-2832)","Anti-dsDNA (highly specific, ~40-70% prevalence) and anti-Sm (most specific, ~30%) — the only ANA subtypes in the EULAR/ACR criteria (p.2832)","Anti-Ro/SSA — useful when ANA-negative but clinically suspicious; linked to neonatal lupus, congenital heart block, sicca, photosensitivity, SCLE (p.2831-2832)","Antihistone antibodies — suggest drug-induced lupus (positive in ~75% of DIL) (p.2832, 2835)","Antiphospholipid panel: anticardiolipin and anti-beta-2-glycoprotein-1 (IgG/IgM/IgA) plus lupus anticoagulant by dilute Russell viper venom time (~50% prevalence) (p.2831-2832)","Random urine protein-to-creatinine ratio (UPCr) or 24-h urine protein with eGFR and serum creatinine; renal biopsy when UPCr >0.5 and/or declining function (p.2830, 2832)","Renal biopsy with ISN/RPS lupus nephritis classification (the only single finding that alone meets EULAR/ACR criteria — class III or IV) (p.2827, 2830)","CK and aldolase with muscle biopsy if inflammatory myopathy suspected (p.2829)","Emerging urinary biomarkers of renal inflammation/fibrosis: urinary IL-16, CD163, type 1 IFN, TGF-beta (p.2832)","Quarterly screening of CBC and renal indices in asymptomatic patients, since hematologic and renal flares can be silent (p.2832)"],"redFlags":["Rapidly declining eGFR, rising proteinuria, or active urinary sediment — class III/IV proliferative lupus nephritis; ~20% of class IV patients die or reach ESRD within 10 years (p.2830)","Crescentic / rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis (cellular crescents or fibrinoid necrosis) — poor prognostic features (p.2834)","Diffuse alveolar hemorrhage with capillaritis (p.2829)","Mesenteric vasculitis — risk of intestinal perforation, bleeding, and ischemia (p.2830)","Neuropsychiatric flare: seizures, acute psychosis/confusion, transverse myelitis (p.2829)","Atypical HUS/TTP-like crisis: hemolysis, thrombocytopenia, schistocytes, low ADAMTS13, microvascular thrombosis (p.2831)","Severe thrombocytopenia (<20-50 x10^9/L) with bleeding risk; severe hemolytic anemia (p.2830, 2833)","Stroke or arterial/venous thrombosis suggesting APS (p.2829-2830)","Pulmonary arterial hypertension, prior arterial thrombosis, or severe end-organ damage — highest maternal mortality; pregnancy should strongly be reconsidered (p.2836)"],"prognosis":"Prognosis and survival vary widely by geography, race/ethnicity, and access to care/medications. Renal disease, especially class IV nephritis, carries substantial risk of ESRD and death (~20% within 10 years), and is more severe in non-White patients. Accelerated atherosclerosis is a major cause of premature death and the leading cause after ~5 years of disease, driven by both traditional risk factors and lupus-specific factors (antiphospholipid antibodies, high disease activity, low C3, high glucocorticoid dose, high homocysteine/leptin). Glucocorticoid exposure independently accrues irreversible organ damage (cataracts, osteoporotic fractures, avascular necrosis, diabetes) even at doses as low as 5 mg prednisone equivalent. Class V (membranous) nephritis has a better prognosis than class III/IV. Sustained low disease activity (LLDAS) for 2+ years is associated with significantly less damage. Recent meta-analyses suggest an increased overall cancer risk and cancer-related mortality (p.2830, 2835-2836).","pitfalls":["Distinguishing flare from infection is a central challenge: cytopenias and constitutional symptoms can stem from disease activity, medication, OR infection, and acute lupus pneumonitis can look identical to pneumonia on imaging — infection must be excluded before escalating immunosuppression (p.2829-2830).","Classification criteria (2019 EULAR/ACR; 2012 SLICC) were built for trial enrollment, not diagnosis — diagnosis remains an individualized clinical judgment; relying on criteria alone can mislead, and ANA-negative or incomplete-lupus patients are excluded by EULAR/ACR (p.2827).","A positive ANA or even antihistone antibody alone does not confirm SLE — many drugs induce a positive ANA in patients who never develop symptoms, and antihistone antibodies occur in most SLE cases too (p.2835).","Drug-induced lupus can be hard to separate from SLE because skin biopsy and many labs overlap; presence of additional SLE-specific autoantibodies (rarely seen in DIL) and symptom resolution after stopping the drug help distinguish (p.2835).","Acute psychosis/confusion may be glucocorticoid-induced rather than neuropsychiatric lupus — and infectious encephalitis must also be ruled out (p.2829).","Single-joint pain out of proportion may be avascular necrosis (steroid-related), not arthritis flare (p.2829).","Estrogen-containing contraceptives/HRT should be avoided in patients with antiphospholipid antibodies or moderate-severe/active lupus due to thromboembolism risk (p.2836)."],"severityClassification":"Disease activity is graded for treatment using SLEDAI/SLEDAI-2K (low disease activity = SLEDAI 0-4; remission/DORIS = SLEDAI 0). EULAR severity tiers: mild (constitutional symptoms, mild arthritis, rash <=9% BSA, platelets 50-100, SLEDAI <=6); moderate (RA-like arthritis, rash 9-18% BSA, platelets 20-50, serositis, SLEDAI 7-12); severe (major organ-threatening disease e.g. cerebritis, myelitis, pneumonitis, mesenteric vasculitis; platelets <20; TTP-like or acute hemophagocytic syndrome; rash >18% BSA; SLEDAI >12). Lupus nephritis is graded by ISN/RPS renal biopsy classes I-V (class I mesangial deposits; II mesangial proliferative; III focal proliferative; IV diffuse proliferative; V membranous; III/IV +/- V combinations), with class III/IV (and III/IV+V) being proliferative forms warranting aggressive therapy (p.2827, 2830-2831, 2833-2834).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2827","Harrison 22e p.2828","Harrison 22e p.2829","Harrison 22e p.2830","Harrison 22e p.2831","Harrison 22e p.2832","Harrison 22e p.2833","Harrison 22e p.2834","Harrison 22e p.2835","Harrison 22e p.2836"],"crossLinks":["vasculitis","glomerulonephritis","ttp_hus"]},"subdural":{"name":"Subdural haematoma","system":"Neurology / Vascular","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e pp.3572-3573 (Chap. 454), with pp.160, 222-223, 238, 3498, 3527, 3619, 3927, 3929","clinicalPearls":["Direct cranial trauma may be minor and is not always required for an acute subdural bleed, particularly in the elderly and those on anticoagulants; acceleration forces alone (e.g. whiplash) can suffice (p.3572).","Up to one-third of patients with acute subdural haematoma have a lucid interval of minutes to hours before coma supervenes, but most are drowsy or comatose from the moment of injury (p.3572).","A unilateral headache with a slightly enlarged pupil on the side of the haematoma is frequently, though not invariably, present (p.3572).","Small subdural haematomas may be asymptomatic and usually do not require evacuation unless they enlarge (p.3572).","In chronic subdural haematoma, drowsiness, inattentiveness and incoherent thinking are generally more prominent than focal signs such as hemiparesis (p.3573).","Chronic subdural haematoma headache typically fluctuates in severity, sometimes with changes in head position (p.3573).","A history of trauma may be absent in chronic subdural haematoma; the injury may have been trivial and forgotten, especially in the elderly and those with clotting disorders (p.3572).","Recurrent falls in older adults place them at risk for subdural haematoma; brain imaging is warranted when neurologic signs are present (pp.222-223, 3927, 3929).","Trauma should be considered in any patient with an unexplained acute neurologic deficit (hemiparesis, stupor, confusion), particularly after a fall (p.3498)."],"pathophysiology":"Bleeding occurs beneath the dura (subdural space). The bleeding producing larger subdural haematomas is primarily venous in origin, classically from torn bridging veins, although arterial bleeding sites are occasionally found at operation and a few large haematomas are purely arterial. Chronic subdural haematomas develop days to weeks after (often trivial) injury, favoured by age-related cerebral atrophy that stretches bridging veins; they organise into a vascular fibrous capsule, and weeks-old collections often contain a mixture of blood and serous fluid. Anticoagulant-related intracranial haemorrhages are often subdural and may continue to evolve over 24-48 h if coagulopathy is insufficiently reversed (pp.3572-3573, 3498).","additionalDifferentials":["Epidural haematoma (lenticular/lens-shaped on CT, usually arterial from a torn middle meningeal artery, classic lucid interval, more rapid evolution)","Cerebral contusion / convexity contusion (can produce focal deficits such as aphasia or mild hemiparesis)","Carotid or vertebral artery dissection (can mimic focal deficits seen with subdural)","Posttraumatic headache syndrome (chronic subdural may mimic this) (p.160)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Transient ischemic attack (rarely chronic subdural causes brief episodes of hemiparesis or aphasia indistinguishable from TIA) (p.3573)","Dementia / Alzheimer or other neurodegenerative disease (chronic subdural is a treatable cause of dementia, often on a background of cortical atrophy) (pp.238, 3527)","Stroke (subdural is considered when excluding stroke in older fallers) (p.3929)","Normal-pressure hydrocephalus and other reversible dementia causes (listed alongside subdural among treatable dementias) (p.238)"],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI reliably identifies both subacute and chronic subdural haematomas and is useful when CT is equivocal (p.3573)","Contrast-enhanced imaging demonstrates enhancement of the vascular fibrous capsule surrounding a chronic collection (p.3573)","Serial imaging for clinical observation of small chronic collections without mass effect (p.3573)"],"redFlags":["Stupor or coma, hemiparesis, and unilateral pupillary enlargement indicate a larger acute haematoma (p.3572)","Acutely deteriorating patient requiring emergency craniotomy (p.3572)","Repetitive vomiting, nystagmus, drowsiness and unsteadiness may signal a posterior fossa subdural haematoma (p.3619)","Large bleed after only minor trauma should raise concern for coagulopathy or anticoagulant therapy (p.3573)"],"prognosis":"In contrast to epidural haematomas, acute subdural haematomas requiring surgery carry significant morbidity and mortality, partly reflecting frequently associated underlying cortical brain damage. Chronic subdural collections evacuated through burr holes are usually treated successfully when a postoperative cranial drain is used; encapsulating fibrous membranes may require craniotomy to prevent recurrent fluid accumulation (pp.3572-3573).","pitfalls":["Subacute pitfall: between roughly 2 and 6 weeks after the initial bleed the clot becomes isodense relative to adjacent brain on non-contrast CT and may be inapparent/missed (p.3573).","Acute subdural produces more horizontal displacement (mass effect) than expected from its thickness because of its large rostral-caudal extent, so mass effect can be underestimated from clot thickness alone (p.3572).","Absence of a trauma history does not exclude chronic subdural haematoma in the elderly or anticoagulated (p.3572).","Skull fractures are markers of injury site/severity, not the haematoma itself, and may be absent (p.3572)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames subdural haematoma by acuity and imaging appearance rather than a numeric grade: ACUTE — non-contrast CT shows a hyperdense crescentic clot with an irregular brain interface (Fig. 454-2); SUBACUTE (~2-6 weeks) — clot becomes isodense and easily overlooked, often with mixed blood and serous fluid; CHRONIC — hypodense crescentic collection over one or both convexities, most often frontotemporal (Fig. 454-3) (pp.3572-3573).","references":["Harrison 22e p.160","Harrison 22e p.222","Harrison 22e p.223","Harrison 22e p.238","Harrison 22e p.3498","Harrison 22e p.3527","Harrison 22e p.3572","Harrison 22e p.3573","Harrison 22e p.3619","Harrison 22e p.3927","Harrison 22e p.3929"],"crossLinks":["ich","ischemic_stroke"]},"svc_obstruction":{"name":"Superior vena cava obstruction","system":"Oncology / Emergency","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.585-586, 628-629 (oncologic emergencies); p.642 (lung cancer regional spread); p.1859 (cardiovascular exam)","clinicalPearls":["SVC syndrome is a clinical diagnosis: dusky/cyanotic head and neck with grossly elevated venous pressure but no visible pulsations should prompt the diagnosis.","Malignancy (lung cancer, lymphoma, metastatic tumours) accounts for most cases, but benign causes now make up at least 40% owing to widespread use of intravascular devices (central venous catheters, pacemaker/defibrillator leads).","Lung cancer—particularly small-cell and squamous-cell histology—causes around 85% of malignant SVCS.","In a young adult/young man with a mediastinal mass and SVCS, think lymphoma versus primary mediastinal germ cell tumour.","Symptoms are characteristically worse on bending forward or lying supine; facial swelling and plethora are exacerbated when supine.","The clinical picture is milder when the obstruction is above the azygos vein.","Symptoms may actually improve over time as collateral venous circulation develops, and most patients improve partly for this reason.","Small-cell lung cancer patients with SVCS have a higher incidence of brain metastases than those without SVCS; new seizures more likely reflect brain mets than venous cerebral oedema.","Tissue diagnosis (bronchoscopy, percutaneous needle biopsy, mediastinoscopy, endobronchial/oesophageal ultrasound-guided aspiration, or even thoracotomy) can generally be obtained safely without major bleeding risk despite the venous congestion.","For a patient with no prior cancer history, a full evaluation is essential to exclude benign causes and define a specific diagnosis before directing therapy; in known cancer, treatment may begin after a thoracic CT."],"pathophysiology":"SVC syndrome results from obstruction of the superior vena cava causing a severe reduction in venous return from the head, neck, and upper extremities. Obstruction may be due to external compression, direct tumour invasion, intraluminal thrombosis (often device-related), or fibrosis. Venous congestion produces facial/upper-body swelling, distended neck veins, and development of collateral venous channels over the chest wall. The 'hot quadrate' focal hepatic hotspot on CT reflects portosystemic shunting between the SVC and portal vein. Chronic SVCS can produce 'downhill' oesophageal varices (cephalad-to-caudad flow, opposite to portal-hypertension 'uphill' varices), which may bleed late.","additionalDifferentials":["Lung cancer (small-cell and squamous-cell predominate among malignant causes)","Malignant lymphoma (leading cause in young adults; Hodgkin involves mediastinum often but rarely causes SVCS)","Primary mediastinal germ cell tumour (esp. young man with mediastinal mass)","Metastatic carcinoma to mediastinal nodes (e.g., testicular, breast — small proportion)","Benign mediastinal tumours","Aortic aneurysm","Thyromegaly (goitre)","Intravascular thrombosis (central venous catheter, pacemaker/defibrillator lead related)","Fibrosing mediastinitis (post-irradiation, histoplasmosis, or Behcet's syndrome)"],"infectionMimics":["Fibrosing mediastinitis secondary to histoplasmosis (a post-infectious cause of SVC obstruction)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Behcet's syndrome — SVCS may be its initial manifestation, from SVC inflammation with thrombosis","Post-radiation fibrosing mediastinitis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph — superior mediastinal widening (most often right-sided) is the most significant finding; a normal film does not exclude SVCS if other features present","CT of the thorax — most reliable view of mediastinal anatomy; shows diminished/absent opacification of central veins with prominent collaterals; may show 'hot quadrate' hepatic sign","MRI — reported 100% sensitivity and specificity, though dyspnoeic patients may not tolerate lying supine","Pleural fluid sampling if effusion present (effusion in only ~25%, often right-sided, usually exudative, occasionally chylous)","Tissue-yielding procedures: bronchoscopy, percutaneous needle biopsy, mediastinoscopy, endobronchial/oesophageal ultrasound-guided needle aspiration"],"redFlags":["Signs/symptoms of cerebral and/or laryngeal oedema — rare but carry a poorer prognosis and demand urgent evaluation","Upper airway/tracheal obstruction from a superior mediastinal mass — the one potentially life-threatening complication, demands emergent therapy","Cardiorespiratory symptoms at rest, especially with positional change — indicate significant airway/vascular obstruction and limited physiologic reserve","Risk of cardiac arrest or respiratory failure, particularly with sedation or general anaesthesia","Proptosis, glossal and laryngeal oedema, and obtundation indicate severe disease","Postural hypotension with severe symptoms","Variceal (downhill oesophageal varices) bleeding as a late complication of chronic SVCS"],"prognosis":"Clinical improvement occurs in most patients, partly attributable to the development of adequate collateral circulation. Mortality is determined by the underlying cause rather than by the caval obstruction itself. SVCS recurs in roughly 10-30% of patients. Cerebral or laryngeal oedema portends a worse prognosis.","pitfalls":["Do not assume SVCS is always malignant — benign device/catheter-related causes now account for at least 40% of cases.","A normal chest radiograph does not exclude SVCS when other characteristic findings are present.","Attributing seizures to venous cerebral oedema when brain metastases are the more likely cause (especially in small-cell lung cancer).","Sedatives or general anaesthesia in a patient with airway/vascular obstruction may precipitate cardiac arrest or respiratory failure.","In a patient without known cancer, omitting a full diagnostic workup risks missing benign causes and a treatable specific diagnosis."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal severity grading scale. Severity is described clinically by features such as presence of cerebral/laryngeal oedema, proptosis, glossal/laryngeal oedema, obtundation, postural hypotension, and cardiorespiratory symptoms at rest with positional change, which together denote severe, potentially life-threatening disease; obstruction above the azygos vein produces a milder picture.","references":["Harrison 22e p.585","Harrison 22e p.586","Harrison 22e p.628","Harrison 22e p.629","Harrison 22e p.642"],"crossLinks":["lung_cancer","malignancy_b"]},"tamponade":{"name":"Cardiac tamponade","system":"Cardiology / Emergency","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e: p.102, p.147, p.630, p.1432, p.1641, p.1906, p.1910, p.1917, p.1938","clinicalPearls":["Tamponade is fundamentally a clinical diagnosis in an unstable patient; the textbook triad is hypotension, raised jugular venous pressure with neck-vein distention, and quiet/muffled heart sounds (p.630).","It is the balance between intrapericardial pressure and ventricular (especially right ventricular) filling pressure that drives compression, not the absolute volume of fluid (p.1938).","Rate of accumulation matters more than total volume: slowly forming effusions (e.g. malignant) can become large without classic tamponade physiology, whereas rapid accumulation (e.g. cardiac rupture, perforation, aortic dissection into the pericardium) can cause tamponade with only a small effusion (p.1938).","Pulsus paradoxus is an inspiratory fall in systolic pressure of more than 10 mmHg and represents an exaggerated form of normal interventricular dependence (p.1906).","Measure pulsus paradoxus by deflating the cuff slowly and noting the gap between the pressure where Korotkoff sounds are first heard (only in expiration) and the pressure where they are heard with every beat regardless of respiration; a difference >15 mmHg may even be palpable at the brachial or femoral pulse (p.1906).","A pulsus paradoxus exceeding 12 mmHg in a patient with a large pericardial effusion is highly predictive of tamponade (reported sensitivity ~98%, specificity ~83%) (p.1910).","The ECG triad of sinus tachycardia, low QRS voltage, and total electrical alternans (beat-to-beat P-QRS-T alternation) strongly suggests pericardial effusion with tamponade physiology, though it is insensitive (p.1917).","Total electrical alternans (whole-complex) is relatively specific for effusion/tamponade; in contrast, pure repolarization (ST-T/U-wave) alternans signals electrical instability and risk of ventricular arrhythmia, not tamponade (p.1917).","Echocardiography is the initial test of choice for suspected effusion/tamponade and can usually be done rapidly at the bedside (p.1938)."],"pathophysiology":"Pericardial fluid accumulates between the visceral (epicardial) and parietal pericardial layers. Tamponade develops when intrapericardial pressure rises enough to exceed the filling pressures of the cardiac chambers, generally the right ventricle, impairing diastolic filling. Because the relatively non-distensible pericardial sac determines tolerance, the rate of fluid accumulation is more important than absolute volume: slow accumulation allows large effusions without compromise, while rapid accumulation (trauma, cardiac rupture, procedural perforation, aortic dissection into the pericardium) causes tamponade at small volumes. Inspiratory augmentation of right-heart filling at the expense of the left, mediated by exaggerated interventricular dependence within the fixed pericardial space, produces pulsus paradoxus (p.1906, p.1938).","additionalDifferentials":["Constrictive pericarditis (also impairs cardiac filling via a thick, noncompliant pericardium; on the differential for dyspnea) (p.1938)","Acute right ventricular dysfunction (can mimic the elevated JVP / characteristic jugular waveform pattern) (p.147)"],"infectionMimics":["Sepsis (can produce a paradoxical-type arterial waveform) (p.1906)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Massive pulmonary embolism (shares hypotension/tachycardia and pulsus paradoxus) (p.147, p.1906)","Tension pneumothorax (life-threatening cause of obstructive shock and pulsus paradoxus) (p.147, p.1906)","Hemorrhagic shock (cause of pulsus paradoxus) (p.1906)","Severe obstructive lung disease / asthma (cause of pulsus paradoxus) (p.1906)","Acute MI with cardiogenic shock (hypotension + tachycardia in chest-pain patient) (p.147)","Acute aortic dissection (may extend into the pericardium and itself cause tamponade) (p.147, p.1938)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Pericardiocentesis under echocardiographic guidance, with pericardial fluid sent for biochemical, cytologic, and microbiologic analysis (p.630, p.1432)","Pericardial fluid cytology when malignancy is suspected (diagnostic in most malignant cases; fluid may be serous, serosanguineous, or hemorrhagic) (p.630)","Pericardial/epicardial biopsy and pericardioscopy to distinguish neoplastic from benign disease (p.630)","Chest CT, CMR, or echocardiography to assess effusion size and pericardial thickness (p.1432, p.1938)","In suspected tuberculous etiology, pericardial fluid adenosine deaminase, lysozyme, and IFN-gamma, plus culture/biopsy (fluid is exudative, lymphocyte/monocyte-rich, often hemorrhagic) (p.1432)"],"redFlags":["Hypotension with tachycardia in a chest-pain patient mandates rapid evaluation for tamponade as one of the immediately life-threatening causes (p.147)","Life-threatening hemodynamic instability with acute tamponade requires immediate fluid drainage (p.630)","Hemorrhagic tamponade from cardiac rupture, procedural perforation, or aortic dissection into the pericardium can be catastrophic at small effusion volumes (p.1938)"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on cause and timeliness of drainage. Untreated tuberculous pericarditis is usually fatal and even treated TB pericarditis carries case-fatality rates up to ~40% in some series, with risk of progression to constrictive pericarditis. Cancer patients whose pericardial fluid contains malignant cells on cytology have very poor survival. After percutaneous catheter drainage of malignant effusions the recurrence rate is ~20%, and a subset develop paradoxical hemodynamic worsening after drainage (post-drainage low cardiac output syndrome) (p.630, p.1432).","pitfalls":["Do not delay decompression in a hemodynamically unstable patient waiting for confirmatory tests; tamponade is a clinical/emergency diagnosis and instability requires immediate drainage (p.147, p.630).","Failing to appreciate the urgency of the situation is a recognized diagnostic error for tamponade (listed among can't-miss time-critical diagnoses) (p.102).","Pulsus paradoxus can be hard to measure or unreliable in tachycardia, atrial fibrillation, or tachypnea (p.1906).","A large slowly-accumulating effusion may lack classic tamponade signs, while a small rapidly-accumulating effusion may already be causing tamponade; judging severity by volume alone is misleading (p.1938).","In malignant tamponade, relatively specific signs (paradoxical pulse, diminished heart sounds, pulsus alternans, friction rub) are LESS common than in nonmalignant disease, so their absence does not exclude tamponade (p.630).","ECG and chest radiograph are abnormal in ~90% of malignant pericardial cases but about half of those abnormalities are nonspecific (p.630)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.102","Harrison 22e p.147","Harrison 22e p.630","Harrison 22e p.1432","Harrison 22e p.1641","Harrison 22e p.1906","Harrison 22e p.1910","Harrison 22e p.1917","Harrison 22e p.1938"],"crossLinks":["pericarditis","cardiogenic_shock","aortic_dissection"]},"temporal_arteritis":{"name":"Giant cell (temporal) arteritis","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.115 (Ch.17 Headache), p.227 (Ch.34 Disorders of the Eye / AION), p.2902-2903 (Ch.375 Vasculitis Syndromes)","clinicalPearls":["Occurs almost exclusively in people over 50; mean age of onset around 70, with women making up roughly two-thirds of cases. Rare in Black patients; higher incidence in Scandinavian populations (Harrison 22e p.115, p.2902).","Four phenotypic patterns can occur together, in isolation, or at different times: cranial arteritis, polymyalgia rheumatica, large-vessel disease, and systemic inflammation (Harrison 22e p.2902).","Headache is the dominant cranial symptom; it is usually dull/boring rather than throbbing, may be unilateral or bilateral, is temporally located in only about half of patients, often worse at night, and aggravated by cold (Harrison 22e p.115).","Scalp tenderness can be marked - brushing hair or resting the head on a pillow may be intolerable; the temporal (or less often occipital) artery may be tender, thickened, nodular, reddened or have overlying red skin streaks (Harrison 22e p.115).","The temporal artery may pulsate early in disease but can become occluded later (Harrison 22e p.2902).","Jaw and tongue claudication and scalp pain are recognized cranial-ischemic features; rarer cranial ischemic events include stroke and scalp or tongue infarction (Harrison 22e p.115, p.2902).","ESR and/or CRP are elevated in most patients, but a normal ESR does NOT exclude giant cell arteritis (Harrison 22e p.115).","Other supportive labs: normochromic or slightly hypochromic anemia, raised alkaline phosphatase (liver function abnormalities common), and increased IgG and complement (Harrison 22e p.2902).","Most patients report head or eye complaints before any visual loss, so attention to early cranial symptoms with prompt therapy reduces the risk of blindness (Harrison 22e p.2902).","Polymyalgia rheumatica overlaps strongly: PMR is seen in 40-50% of GCA patients, and 10-20% who present with isolated PMR later develop GCA - GCA and PMR are regarded as a spectrum of one disease process (Harrison 22e p.2902).","In PMR, creatine kinase is not elevated and rheumatoid factor / anti-CCP should be negative; a worsening peripheral arthritis pattern should prompt reconsideration of seronegative RA or other inflammatory arthropathy (Harrison 22e p.2903)."],"pathophysiology":"A panarteritis of medium- and large-sized arteries, classically branches of the carotid (especially the temporal artery) but systemically involving the aorta and its branches. Histology shows mononuclear inflammatory infiltrate within the vessel wall with frequent giant cell formation, intimal proliferation, and fragmentation of the internal elastic lamina; organ damage results from ischemia of involved vessels. It is considered an antigen-driven process: CD4+ T cells enter via the adventitial vasa vasorum, become activated, and drive macrophage differentiation, with restricted clonal T-cell expansion suggesting an arterial-wall antigen. Lesional T cells produce mainly IL-2 and IFN-gamma; both IFN-gamma-producing TH1 and IL-17-producing TH17 lineages contribute and may differ in glucocorticoid responsiveness. Associated with HLA-DR4 and HLA-DRB1*04 variants. (Harrison 22e p.2902)","additionalDifferentials":["Migraine - common in the elderly (more prevalent than GCA) and may improve with prednisone, so a steroid response does not by itself confirm GCA (Harrison 22e p.115).","Nonarteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy - associated with a crowded 'disc-at-risk' / small optic cup, in which glucocorticoids should NOT be prescribed, contrasting with the arteritic form (Harrison 22e p.227).","Isolated polymyalgia rheumatica without arteritis (Harrison 22e p.2903).","Other inflammatory causes of secondary headache such as sarcoidosis and Behcet's syndrome (Harrison 22e p.115)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Seronegative rheumatoid arthritis or other inflammatory arthropathy mimicking the peripheral arthritis of PMR (Harrison 22e p.2903)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Temporal artery biopsy: yield improved by harvesting a long segment (3-5 cm) with serial sectioning because involvement is segmental/patchy; biopsy may still show vasculitis even after about 14 days of glucocorticoids, but prolonged treatment can mask findings (Harrison 22e p.227, p.2903).","Temporal artery ultrasonography (alone or with axillary arteries) showing a noncompressible 'halo' sign supports the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2903).","Large-vessel imaging by MR or CT angiography to confirm large-vessel disease suggested by diminished pulses or bruits; PET is increasingly studied but its diagnostic/monitoring role remains unclear (Harrison 22e p.2903).","Note: in patients treated with an IL-6 inhibitor, CRP and ESR normalize and can no longer be used to monitor disease activity or assess for infection (Harrison 22e p.2902)."],"redFlags":["Ischemic optic neuropathy is the dreaded complication; about half of untreated patients develop blindness from ophthalmic-artery involvement, and arteritic AION is the leading cause of rapidly developing bilateral blindness in patients over 60 (Harrison 22e p.115).","In suspected arteritic AION / visual loss, start glucocorticoids IMMEDIATELY without waiting for biopsy to protect vision in the fellow eye - a sight-saving principle; do not delay therapy pending diagnostic studies (Harrison 22e p.227, p.2903).","Sudden visual loss / blindness can be the presenting or emergent feature (Harrison 22e p.2902).","Aortic aneurysm: GCA patients are 18 times more likely than the general population to develop thoracic aortic aneurysms, carrying risk of rupture or dissection (Harrison 22e p.2903).","Subclavian stenosis presenting as arm claudication signals large-vessel involvement (Harrison 22e p.2902)."],"prognosis":"Acute mortality directly from GCA is uncommon; when fatal it is usually due to cerebrovascular events or myocardial infarction. The main late mortality risk is rupture or dissection of an aortic aneurysm (18-fold increased thoracic aortic aneurysm risk). Disease is highly glucocorticoid-responsive but relapsing: symptom recurrence during steroid tapering occurs in 60-85% of patients, most require at least 2 years of treatment, and glucocorticoid toxicity affects 35-65% and is a major source of morbidity. (Harrison 22e p.2903)","pitfalls":["A normal ESR does not rule out GCA (Harrison 22e p.115).","A single negative temporal artery biopsy does not exclude the disease because lesions are segmental/patchy - take a long segment and serial sections (Harrison 22e p.2903).","Improvement of headache on prednisone is non-specific (migraine also responds), so do not use the steroid response alone to confirm GCA (Harrison 22e p.115).","Once an IL-6 inhibitor is started, ESR/CRP can no longer track activity or flag infection (Harrison 22e p.2902).","Minor ESR/CRP rises during tapering may reflect other causes (including infection) rather than a true flare, especially in a symptom-free patient (Harrison 22e p.2903).","Glucocorticoids should not be given for the nonarteritic form of AION - distinguishing arteritic from nonarteritic AION matters (Harrison 22e p.227)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.115","Harrison 22e p.227","Harrison 22e p.2902","Harrison 22e p.2903"],"crossLinks":["pmr","vasculitis"]},"tension_ha":{"name":"Tension-type headache","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.441 p.3514 (running p.3471); Ch.17 p.156-159","clinicalPearls":["TTH is recognized as bilateral, tight, band-like discomfort that builds gradually, fluctuates in intensity, and can persist near-continuously for days (Harrison 22e p.3514).","The most reliable bedside approach is to reserve the TTH label for headaches entirely lacking accompanying features such as nausea, vomiting, photophobia, phonophobia, osmophobia, throbbing quality, or worsening with movement (Harrison 22e p.3514).","Migraine is the principal differential of TTH; the presence of any migrainous accompaniment points away from TTH and toward migraine (Harrison 22e p.3514).","Patients who fit the TTH phenotype but also have migraine at other times, a family history of migraine, childhood migrainous syndromes, or typical migraine triggers may be biologically distinct from those with pure featureless TTH (Harrison 22e p.3514).","Diagnostic boundaries are genuinely blurred: the International Headache Society main definition permits some nausea, photophobia, or phonophobia, whereas the stricter appendix definition does not, and Harrison favors the appendix (feature-free) definition to separate TTH from migraine (Harrison 22e p.3514).","Among primary-care patients with headache lasting 3 months or more and no neurologic symptoms or signs, fewer than 10% actually have TTH and more than 90% have migraine, so a confident TTH label in this setting is uncommon (Harrison 22e p.3514).","TTH is the most prevalent primary headache type overall, accounting for roughly 69% of primary headaches in a population series (Harrison 22e p.156, Table 17-1)."],"pathophysiology":"The mechanism of TTH is incompletely understood. Harrison favors a primary disorder of central nervous system pain modulation alone, contrasting it with migraine, which involves more generalized disturbance of sensory modulation. Despite the name, there is no clear evidence that nervous tension is causative; importantly, muscle contraction does NOT reliably distinguish TTH from migraine, as no difference in contraction is seen between the two headache types. A genetic contribution has been suggested but is unreliable, since current diagnostic criteria likely contaminated those studies with migraine patients (Harrison 22e p.3514).","additionalDifferentials":["Migraine (the main differential, defined by migrainous accompaniments TTH lacks) (Harrison 22e p.3514)","Migraine-TTH overlap / mixed phenotype with intercurrent migraine attacks (Harrison 22e p.3514)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Chronic migraine within the chronic daily headache spectrum (Harrison 22e p.158, Table 17-3)","Medication-overuse headache (refractory daily/near-daily headache from analgesic overuse) (Harrison 22e p.158-159)","Other secondary causes of chronic daily headache: post-traumatic, inflammatory (e.g., giant cell arteritis, sarcoidosis, Behcet syndrome), and chronic CNS infection (Harrison 22e p.158, Table 17-3)"],"additionalInvestigations":["TTH is a clinical diagnosis; neuroimaging is not routine but CT or MRI of the brain is indicated when the examination is abnormal or the headache is recent-onset, with CT and MRI being comparably sensitive as initial screens (Harrison 22e p.157).","Lumbar puncture may be required when a serious secondary cause is suspected and a benign etiology cannot otherwise be established (Harrison 22e p.157).","General evaluation of acute headache may add palpation of cranial arteries, cervical spine assessment, blood pressure and urine for cardiovascular/renal status, and ocular examination including funduscopy and intraocular pressure (Harrison 22e p.157)."],"redFlags":["Sudden-onset or first severe headache, or the 'worst headache ever' (Harrison 22e p.156-157, Table 17-2)","Vomiting preceding the headache (Harrison 22e p.156, Table 17-2)","Subacute worsening over days or weeks (Harrison 22e p.156, Table 17-2)","Pain provoked by bending, lifting, or coughing (Harrison 22e p.156, Table 17-2)","Pain that disturbs sleep or is present immediately on awakening (Harrison 22e p.156, Table 17-2)","Onset after age 55 (Harrison 22e p.156, Table 17-2)","Fever or unexplained systemic signs, or a known systemic illness (Harrison 22e p.156, Table 17-2)","Abnormal neurologic examination (Harrison 22e p.156, Table 17-2)","Local tenderness, e.g., over the temporal artery region (Harrison 22e p.156, Table 17-2)","Serious secondary causes to consider with new severe headache include meningitis, subarachnoid hemorrhage, epidural/subdural hematoma, glaucoma, tumor, and purulent sinusitis (Harrison 22e p.157)"],"prognosis":"TTH is classified as episodic when infrequent and chronic when occurring on 15 or more days per month; the pain fluctuates and may persist near-continuously for many days. Chronic TTH falls within the chronic daily headache group, which carries considerable disability, and may be complicated by medication overuse (Harrison 22e p.3514, p.158).","pitfalls":["Over-applying the TTH label: in chronic headache without neurologic signs, more than 90% of patients actually have migraine, so TTH is frequently overdiagnosed (Harrison 22e p.3514).","Assuming muscle contraction confirms TTH; it does NOT distinguish TTH from migraine (Harrison 22e p.3514).","Overlooking medication overuse, which can convert episodic headache into refractory daily/near-daily headache and blunt the effect of preventive treatment (Harrison 22e p.158-159).","Mistaking a serious secondary headache for benign primary headache when an underlying cause has activated or worsened the primary syndrome (Harrison 22e p.158)."],"severityClassification":"Pain is characteristically mild to moderate, bilateral, tight and band-like, and non-throbbing. By frequency the IHS scheme distinguishes infrequent episodic, frequent episodic, chronic (>=15 days/month), and probable TTH; Harrison emphasizes the episodic-versus-chronic distinction (Harrison 22e p.3514, p.155 IHS classification).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3514","Harrison 22e p.156","Harrison 22e p.157","Harrison 22e p.158","Harrison 22e p.159"],"crossLinks":["migraine","iih"]},"thyroid_storm":{"name":"Thyroid storm","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"3076-3080","clinicalPearls":["Thyrotoxic crisis (thyroid storm) is a rare, life-threatening exacerbation of hyperthyroidism (Harrison 22e p.3080).","Characteristic features include fever, delirium, seizures, coma, vomiting, diarrhoea and jaundice (Harrison 22e p.3080).","Often precipitated by an acute intercurrent illness (e.g. stroke, infection, trauma, diabetic ketoacidosis), by surgery (especially thyroid surgery), or by radioiodine treatment given to a patient whose hyperthyroidism is only partially treated or untreated (Harrison 22e p.3080).","Apathetic thyrotoxicosis: in the elderly, features may be subtle or masked, presenting mainly with fatigue and weight loss, and can be mistaken for depression (Harrison 22e p.3076).","Thyrotoxicosis may be complicated by a form of hypokalaemic periodic paralysis, particularly common in Asian males (Harrison 22e p.3076).","Treatment of the thyrotoxic state alone restores sinus rhythm in up to 75% of patients with thyrotoxicosis-associated atrial fibrillation who have no underlying cardiac disease (Harrison 22e p.3077)."],"pathophysiology":"Thyroid storm represents a severe, decompensated form of thyroid hormone excess (thyrotoxicosis), typically arising when an acute stressor is superimposed on partially treated or untreated hyperthyroidism. Excess circulating thyroid hormone drives a hypermetabolic, hyperadrenergic state; high cardiac output produces a bounding pulse, widened pulse pressure and may precipitate or worsen heart failure or angina, particularly in the elderly or those with pre-existing heart disease (Harrison 22e p.3077, p.3080).","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of thyrotoxicosis besides Graves' disease must be considered (e.g. toxic adenoma, toxic multinodular goitre, destructive thyroiditis, ectopic thyroid tissue, factitious thyrotoxicosis); radionuclide scan/uptake or color-flow Doppler ultrasonography helps distinguish hyperthyroidism from destructive thyroiditis (Harrison 22e p.3078)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Suppressed TSH with elevated total and unbound (free) T4 and T3 (Harrison 22e p.3078).","T3 toxicosis (only T3 raised) in 2-5% of patients, more in areas of borderline iodine intake; T4 toxicosis (raised T4, normal T3) is occasionally seen when hyperthyroidism is iodine-induced (Harrison 22e p.3078).","TRAb (TSH receptor antibody) measurement to support a diagnosis of Graves' disease (Harrison 22e p.3078).","Watch for confounding biochemistry: elevated bilirubin, liver enzymes and ferritin, plus microcytic anaemia and thrombocytopenia, may accompany thyrotoxicosis (Harrison 22e p.3078)."],"redFlags":["Fever, delirium, seizures or coma in a thyrotoxic patient indicate thyroid storm (Harrison 22e p.3080).","Death is most often due to cardiac failure, arrhythmia or hyperthermia (Harrison 22e p.3080)."],"prognosis":"Mortality remains high (4-17%) even with treatment, usually from cardiac failure, arrhythmia or hyperthermia (Harrison 22e p.3080).","pitfalls":["Radioiodine therapy in a patient with partially treated or untreated hyperthyroidism can itself precipitate thyrotoxic crisis (Harrison 22e p.3080).","In the elderly, thyrotoxicosis may be masked (apathetic thyrotoxicosis), delaying recognition (Harrison 22e p.3076)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.3076","Harrison 22e p.3077","Harrison 22e p.3078","Harrison 22e p.3080"],"crossLinks":["hyperthyroidism","atrial_fib"]},"tia":{"name":"Transient ischaemic attack","system":"Neurology / Vascular","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3378, 3428, 3430, 3445, 3447, 3448","clinicalPearls":["TIA classically produces 'negative' symptoms (transient loss of function); positive sensory symptoms or involuntary movements point instead to seizure (p.3378).","Most TIAs last under 1 hour, well within the historical 24-hour window (p.3448).","About 25% of symptomatic internal carotid disease is heralded by recurrent transient monocular blindness (amaurosis fugax), typically described as a horizontal shade sweeping down or up across one eye, lasting a few minutes (p.3430).","A high-pitched carotid bruit fading into diastole suggests a tight stenosis; paradoxically the bruit may fade or vanish as occlusion becomes imminent and flow drops (p.3430).","Causes of TIA and ischaemic stroke are identical, so the diagnostic evaluation of a TIA should parallel that of a completed stroke (p.3448)."],"pathophysiology":"A TIA results from transient focal cerebral ischaemia when blood flow falls for longer than several seconds; neurons lack glycogen so deficits appear within seconds, but because the occluded vessel reopens and flow is restored before infarction occurs, symptoms resolve completely. Mechanisms mirror ischaemic stroke: emboli to the brain (cardiac or proximal arterial source) or in-situ thrombosis of an intracranial vessel. Small-vessel (lacunar) TIAs may recur several times a day and last only minutes (Harrison 22e p.3428, 3448, 3447).","additionalDifferentials":["Seizure (esp. ongoing focal seizure without convulsive activity) — positive symptoms and a marching spread over seconds favour seizure (p.3378, p.3428)","Migraine, including acephalgic migraine even in older first-time patients — slower march over minutes with headache, nausea or scintillating visual symptoms (p.3428)","Intracranial tumour presenting acutely via haemorrhage, seizure or hydrocephalus (p.3428)","Metabolic encephalopathy — fluctuating mental status without focal findings (p.3428)","Stroke recrudescence — fever/sepsis unmasking a prior deficit that clears as infection is treated (p.3428)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Seizure (focal, may mimic transient deficit) (p.3378, p.3428)","Migraine / acephalgic migraine with transient aura (p.3428)","Syncope from systemic hypotension (cardiac arrhythmia, sepsis, haemorrhagic shock) (p.3428)","Metabolic encephalopathy (p.3428)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CT- or MRI-based angiography of neck and intracranial vessels to assess large-vessel stenosis/occlusion (MRA is highly sensitive for extracranial carotid stenosis but tends to overestimate severity) (p.3445, p.3430)","Echocardiography (transthoracic or transoesophageal), with bubble-contrast/agitated-saline study to detect right-to-left shunt (PFO/ASD) for suspected paradoxical embolism (p.3445)","Cardiac telemetry following the initial ECG; ambulatory/prolonged ECG monitoring to detect intermittent atrial fibrillation if none is found in hospital (p.3445)","Retinal/funduscopic exam for cholesterol (Hollenhorst) plaques and examination of cervical/peripheral vessels and heart (p.3445)","Baseline labs: CBC, ESR, electrolytes, glucose, lipid profile, creatinine/BUN, PT/PTT, urinalysis, chest x-ray (p.3445)"],"redFlags":["Most strokes after a TIA occur within the first 2 days — the early window is the highest-risk period and warrants urgent (not deferred) workup (p.3448)","Stuttering or crescendo course of small-vessel TIAs heralding lacunar infarct can progress to severe permanent disability (p.3447)","Amaurosis fugax indicates a symptomatic internal carotid source needing prompt carotid evaluation (p.3430)"],"prognosis":"Stroke risk after a TIA is approximately 10-15% within the first 3 months, with most events occurring in the first 2 days; this early risk is well predicted by the ABCD2 score, so urgent evaluation and treatment are justified (Harrison 22e p.3448).","pitfalls":["A TIA is a clinical diagnosis: a normal brain imaging study does NOT rule it out (p.3448).","If a relevant infarction is seen on imaging, the event is reclassified as a stroke regardless of how briefly symptoms lasted — the modern definition is tissue-based, not purely time-based (p.3428, p.3448).","Patients with carotid/vertebrobasilar TIA may report symptoms 'in one eye' when the deficit is actually a homonymous hemifield loss affecting both eyes (p.3430-equivalent / cortical visual TIA).","Acephalgic migraine can mimic TIA even with no prior migraine history and even after age 65 (p.3428)."],"severityClassification":"ABCD2 score (Harrison 22e p.3448) stratifies early stroke risk after TIA: 1 point each for Age >=60, BP (SBP >140 or DBP >90 mmHg), and Diabetes; for Clinical features 2 points for unilateral weakness or 1 for speech disturbance without weakness; for Duration 2 points for >=60 min or 1 for 10-59 min. The 3-month stroke rate rises with the total: ~0% at score 0-1, ~3% at 2-3, ~8% at 4, ~12% at 5, ~17% at 6, and ~22% at 7.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3378","Harrison 22e p.3428","Harrison 22e p.3430","Harrison 22e p.3445","Harrison 22e p.3447","Harrison 22e p.3448"],"crossLinks":["ischemic_stroke","ich","atrial_fib","vasovagal_syncope"]},"toxic_hepatitis":{"name":"Drug-induced / toxic hepatitis","system":"Hepatology / Toxicology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e, Chapter 351 (Toxic and Drug-Induced Hepatitis), pp.2665-2672; anti-TB hepatotoxicity monitoring p.1463; liver transplantation/ALF context Chapter 356 pp.2718-2719","clinicalPearls":["DILI is the most common cause of acute liver failure, and signals of hepatotoxicity in clinical trials are the leading reason candidate drugs fail to reach approval (p.2665).","Two broad mechanisms are recognised: direct (intrinsic) toxicity that is predictable and dose-dependent with a short latency, versus idiosyncratic reactions that are infrequent (roughly 1 in 1,000 to 1 in 100,000), unpredictable, and may appear at any time but typically 5-90 days after starting the drug (p.2665, p.2667).","Although idiosyncratic injury is regarded as not dose-related, most implicated drugs are given at relatively high daily doses (often >100 mg); high-potency drugs given in milligram amounts rarely cause hepatotoxicity (p.2665).","The hepatocellular-versus-cholestatic pattern is defined by the R value, the ratio of ALT to alkaline phosphatase (each as multiples of the upper limit of normal): R >5.0 = hepatocellular, R <2.0 = cholestatic, R 2.0-5.0 = mixed (p.2667).","Hy's Law (named for Hyman Zimmerman): the appearance of jaundice during drug therapy predicts more serious injury, with roughly a 10:1 ratio of jaundiced cases to acute liver failure (about 1 in 10 jaundiced patients progresses to liver failure) (p.2667).","Amoxicillin-clavulanate (Augmentin) is the most frequently implicated single antibiotic/agent causing DILI in the US and Europe, typically producing mixed or predominantly cholestatic injury that may begin after the drug has already been stopped (p.2667, p.2670).","Acetaminophen (paracetamol) is the prototype direct hepatotoxin and the most prevalent cause of acute liver failure in the Western world (p.2667, p.2668).","A characteristic biochemical signature of acetaminophen injury is very high aminotransferases (often >10,000 IU/L, far above viral hepatitis) with relatively low bilirubin in the hyperacute phase; this should prompt focused questioning even without a clear overdose history (p.2668).","Complementary and alternative (herbal/dietary) medicines now account for over 20% of DILI in the US; weight-loss products and anabolic steroids are common culprits and are often unregulated (p.2671).","Acetaminophen toxicity is potentiated by chronic alcohol use (CYP2E1 induction plus glutathione depletion), so the toxic dose may be as low as ~2 g in chronic alcoholics; starvation/fasting and enzyme-inducing drugs (phenobarbital, isoniazid) also increase susceptibility (p.2668-2670).","Four agents are notable for causing a chronic, autoimmune-hepatitis-like picture with positive ANA: nitrofurantoin, minocycline, hydralazine, and alpha-methyldopa (p.2667, p.2670).","DILI is nearly always a presumptive diagnosis of exclusion; causality assessment relies on temporal association, injury pattern, the drug's prior record, and ruling out other causes (scoring tools: RUCAM, DILIN expert opinion, newer RECAM) (p.2667-2668).","Drug hepatotoxicity is generally not more frequent in those with pre-existing chronic liver disease, although the severity of outcome may be greater; statins specifically can be used safely in chronic hepatitis C and steatosis (p.2668, p.2671-2672)."],"pathophysiology":"Liver injury can follow ingestion of essentially any xenobiotic (industrial toxins, drugs, herbal/dietary supplements). Most drugs are lipophilic and undergo phase I oxidation/methylation by cytochrome P450 enzymes followed by phase II conjugation (glucuronidation, sulfation) or glutathione inactivation to a water-soluble form for renal/biliary excretion; most hepatotoxicity arises from a reactive phase I metabolite, and glutathione depletion compounds the injury. Damage occurs through several overlapping routes: direct hepatocyte injury via free radicals/reactive intermediates causing membrane lipid peroxidation; activation of innate or adaptive immune responses (HLA-haplotype-dependent hapten binding, cytotoxic lymphocytes, antibody-mediated cytotoxicity); triggering of apoptosis via TNF-alpha/Fas pathways; disruption of bile canalicular transport pumps (e.g., MRP3) leading to toxic bile-acid accumulation and cholestasis; and inhibition of mitochondrial beta-oxidation/respiration producing lactic acidosis and microvesicular steatosis. Nuclear receptors such as CAR and PXR can also induce hepatotoxicity. For acetaminophen specifically, a minor fraction is converted by CYP2E1 to the reactive metabolite NAPQI, normally detoxified by conjugation with hepatoprotective glutathione; when NAPQI overwhelms or glutathione is depleted, NAPQI binds covalently to hepatocyte macromolecules forming protein adducts that drive centrilobular necrosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Viral hepatitis (idiosyncratic DILI from isoniazid or ciprofloxacin can be clinically and histologically indistinguishable from acute hepatitis A/B/C) (p.2667)","Autoimmune hepatitis (nitrofurantoin, minocycline, hydralazine, methyldopa produce an AIH-like picture with positive ANA) (p.2667, p.2670)","Alcohol-associated (alcoholic) hepatitis (amiodarone causes a pseudoalcoholic injury with steatosis, neutrophilic infiltration, and Mallory's hyaline) (p.2670)","Primary sclerosing cholangitis (a PSC-like picture can follow chemotherapy and long-term ketamine use) (p.2667)","Primary biliary cholangitis-like lesion (rarely associated with chlorpromazine) (p.2669)"],"infectionMimics":["Acute viral hepatitis A, B, or C (hepatocellular idiosyncratic DILI mimics it; differentiate with viral serologies) (p.2667)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Autoimmune hepatitis (p.2667, p.2670)","Alcoholic hepatitis / pseudoalcoholic liver injury (amiodarone) (p.2670)","Ischemic (hypoxic) hepatitis and other causes of marked transaminase elevation (p.2668)","Cholestasis of pregnancy / estrogen-induced cholestasis (genetically determined canalicular transporter defects) (p.2667)","Budd-Chiari syndrome / hepatic vein occlusion and venoocclusive (sinusoidal obstruction) disease as drug-related vascular mimics (oral contraceptives; pyrrolizidine alkaloids; pre-transplant chemotherapy) (p.2667, p.2670, p.2671)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Calculate the R value (ALT/ALP, each as multiples of ULN) to classify hepatocellular vs cholestatic vs mixed injury (p.2667)","Detailed exposure history including occupational/home chemicals, prescription and OTC drugs, and herbal/dietary supplements (p.2665, p.2671)","Serum acetaminophen level interpreted against the time-based nomogram (levels 4-8 h post-ingestion guide risk; >300 ug/mL at 4 h predicts severe injury, <150 ug/mL makes injury highly unlikely) (p.2668, p.2670)","Viral hepatitis serologies and autoantibodies (ANA, anti-smooth muscle, anti-mitochondrial, anti-LKM2) to exclude/identify autoimmune-pattern DILI (p.2667, p.2670)","Eosinophil count (eosinophilia supports a hypersensitivity reaction, e.g., trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, phenytoin) (p.2665, p.2671)","Liver biopsy where the pattern is unclear (helps distinguish injury types; e.g., electron microscopy showing phospholipid-laden lamellar bodies in amiodarone toxicity) (p.2670)","Arterial lactate in severe acetaminophen injury (>3.5 mmol/L identifies patients much more likely to need transplantation) (p.2670)","Causality assessment tools (RUCAM, DILIN structured expert opinion, RECAM) and reference resources (LiverTox) (p.2668)"],"redFlags":["Jaundice arising during drug therapy (Hy's Law) - predicts severe injury and a substantial risk of progression to liver failure (p.2667)","Rising aminotransferases to >5x the upper limit of normal (or >3x ULN with symptoms) - threshold to withdraw the suspected agent (p.2668)","Acute liver failure features: coagulopathy plus hepatic encephalopathy; develops in ~10% of DILI cases (p.2668)","Cerebral edema in progressing acute liver failure (p.2667, Chap.356)","Acute kidney injury and myocardial injury accompanying severe acetaminophen toxicity (renal injury in ~75% of severe cases) (p.2668, p.2670)","Lactic acidosis (mitochondrial toxicity, e.g., with nucleoside analogues/antiretrovirals or valproate) (p.2665, p.2672)","Hypersensitivity/DRESS features (rash, fever, eosinophilia) accompanying liver injury (e.g., trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole) (p.2671)","Continued use of the offending agent despite symptoms - most fatalities (e.g., isoniazid) occur in this setting (p.2668, p.2670)"],"prognosis":"Most DILI is self-limited and resolves after the offending agent is withdrawn. However, acute liver failure develops in about 10% of DILI cases; once that threshold (coagulopathy plus hepatic encephalopathy) is reached, spontaneous recovery occurs in fewer than 30%, and liver transplantation is undertaken in more than 40% of these patients (p.2668). For acetaminophen, up to 72% of poisoned patients in some Scandinavian series progress to encephalopathy and coagulopathy (lower in the UK/US); a single dose of 10-15 g can cause clinical injury and fulminant fatal disease is usually associated with >=25 g; the N-acetylcysteine antidote markedly reduces severity and is most effective given early (within ~8-12 h), though it retains some benefit up to 24-36 h. Survivors of acetaminophen overdose rarely have ongoing liver injury (p.2668, p.2670). Several agents cause chronic injury or progression to cirrhosis (methotrexate, tamoxifen, amiodarone), and the autoimmune-pattern agents (nitrofurantoin, minocycline, hydralazine, methyldopa) can lead to cirrhosis if not recognised early (p.2668-2670).","pitfalls":["Treating mild, transient aminotransferase rises ('adaptation', e.g., with isoniazid, valproate, phenytoin, statins) as significant injury - these often resolve despite continued use; conversely, ignoring symptomatic/progressive rises is dangerous (p.2665, p.2669, p.2671-2672).","Missing the acetaminophen diagnosis because of denial, altered mentation, or staggered/unintentional overdose - very high transaminases with low bilirubin should raise suspicion even without a clear history (p.2668).","Forgetting herbal/dietary supplements and OTC products in the drug history - they now cause >20% of US DILI and may contain undeclared chemicals (e.g., diclofenac in 'antirheumatic herbs') (p.2671).","Assuming standard acetaminophen doses are safe in chronic alcohol users - the toxic threshold can fall to ~2 g (p.2670).","Attributing cholestatic jaundice in a young male body-builder to hepatitis rather than anabolic/androgenic steroid use (p.2670).","Expecting injury only early in therapy - amoxicillin-clavulanate and nitrofurantoin can have long latency and may declare after the drug is stopped (p.2670).","In direct toxins, focusing on the liver while missing concurrent renal or other organ involvement that may threaten survival (p.2668)."],"severityClassification":"Injury pattern is classified by the R value (ALT/ALP as multiples of ULN): R >5.0 hepatocellular, R <2.0 cholestatic, R 2.0-5.0 mixed (p.2667). Hy's Law serves as a severity/prognostic marker: jaundice (significant bilirubin rise) accompanying hepatocellular DILI predicts a markedly higher risk of acute liver failure (about a 10:1 jaundice-to-failure ratio) (p.2667). The aminotransferase threshold of >5x ULN (or >3x ULN with symptoms) marks the point at which the offending agent should be withdrawn (p.2668). Causality (not severity) is graded on a five-point likelihood scale by DILIN (definite, highly likely, probable, possible, unlikely) (p.2668). The clinical endpoint of severity is acute liver failure, defined by coagulopathy plus hepatic encephalopathy (p.2668).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2665","Harrison 22e p.2666","Harrison 22e p.2667","Harrison 22e p.2668","Harrison 22e p.2669","Harrison 22e p.2670","Harrison 22e p.2671","Harrison 22e p.2672","Harrison 22e p.1463","Harrison 22e p.2718"],"crossLinks":["VIRAL_HEPATITIS","hepatic_enceph","decomp_cirrhosis","drug_intox"]},"ttp_hus":{"name":"Thrombotic microangiopathy (TTP/HUS)","system":"Hematology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Chap. 120 pp.967-968 (TTP/HUS); Chap. 105 pp.800-801 (familial/atypical HUS); Chap. on cutaneous purpura p.450; oncologic-emergencies HUS p.638; blood-smear schistocytes p.~640","clinicalPearls":["The thrombotic microangiopathies are unified by microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia (MAHA): thrombocytopenia plus fragmented red cells (schistocytes) on the smear, biochemical haemolysis (high LDH, high unconjugated bilirubin, low haptoglobin) and microvascular thrombosis (Harrison 22e p.967).","TTP was classically described as a pentad of MAHA, thrombocytopenia, renal failure, neurologic changes and fever; the full pentad is now seen less often because earlier diagnosis and treatment intervene before it evolves (Harrison 22e pp.967-968).","ADAMTS13 activity below 10% is diagnostic of TTP; the assay (activity level plus anti-ADAMTS13 antibody) is central to working up any MAHA (Harrison 22e p.968).","ADAMTS13 levels are generally normal in HUS, although they have occasionally been reported as decreased — so a normal ADAMTS13 does not exclude a thrombotic microangiopathy other than TTP (Harrison 22e p.968).","Schistocytes are red-cell fragments produced by physical disruption against intravascular platelet/fibrin strands or external forces, and are the morphologic hallmark of MAHA (Harrison 22e p.~640).","The Coombs (direct antiglobulin) test is negative in microangiopathic haemolysis, helping separate it from autoimmune haemolytic anaemia (Harrison 22e p.638).","TTP is more common in women than men, and is over-represented in HIV infection and in pregnancy; congenital ADAMTS13 deficiency (Upshaw-Schulman) may first declare itself during a first pregnancy (Harrison 22e p.968).","In a classic HUS presentation, a preceding episode of diarrhoea (often bloody) in a child points to Shiga-toxin-producing E. coli O157:H7 as the commonest, though not the only, cause (Harrison 22e p.968).","Atypical HUS is essentially a complement-alternative-pathway disease — driven by mutations in complement components/regulators (C3, CFB, CFH, CD46/MCP, CFI, THBD) or anti-factor-H antibodies — and clinical disease usually requires a trigger such as infection, so penetrance is incomplete (Harrison 22e pp.800-801)."],"pathophysiology":"TTP arises from deficient or antibody-inhibited ADAMTS13, the metalloprotease that cleaves von Willebrand factor (VWF). VWF is secreted by endothelium as ultra-large multimers; when these are not cleaved they persist and drive pathological platelet adhesion and aggregation, producing microvascular thrombi. ADAMTS13 deficiency alone is not sufficient — congenitally ADAMTS13-deficient individuals develop TTP only episodically, implying a second trigger. In HUS the dominant lesion is endothelial injury with deposition of fibrin in capillary/arteriolar walls, predominantly in the kidney; typical (diarrhoea-associated) HUS is driven by Shiga toxin from E. coli O157:H7, while atypical HUS results from dysregulated activation of the complement alternative pathway with consequent endothelial damage. The shared downstream picture is microvascular platelet/fibrin thrombi causing red-cell fragmentation (MAHA), consumption thrombocytopenia and end-organ ischaemia (Harrison 22e pp.967-968, 800-801).","additionalDifferentials":["Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) — also gives thrombocytopenia and microangiopathy, but a consumptive coagulopathy predominates with prolonged PT and often prolonged aPTT (Harrison 22e p.967)","Atypical/complement-mediated HUS (aHUS) as a distinct entity from TTP (Harrison 22e pp.800-801)","Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome (Harrison 22e p.450)","Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia and thrombosis (HIT) (Harrison 22e p.450)","Monoclonal cryoglobulinaemia with intravascular thrombi (Harrison 22e p.450)","Malignant hypertension affecting the renal microvasculature (Harrison 22e — listed among renal-microvascular causes of MAHA)"],"infectionMimics":["Shiga-toxin-producing E. coli O157:H7 gastroenteritis (and other enterohaemorrhagic strains) preceding diarrhoea-associated HUS (Harrison 22e p.968)","HIV infection, which is associated with a higher incidence of TTP (Harrison 22e p.968)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Disseminated intravascular coagulation (distinguished by abnormal PT/aPTT and clotting-factor/fibrinogen consumption) (Harrison 22e p.967)","Drug-induced microangiopathy — immune-mediated (ticlopidine, possibly clopidogrel, quinine) or by direct endothelial toxicity (cyclosporine, tacrolimus, gemcitabine, mitomycin C) (Harrison 22e p.968)","Chemotherapy-/cancer-associated HUS (mitomycin and gemcitabine most often; also cisplatin, bleomycin, proteasome and VEGF inhibitors) (Harrison 22e p.638)","Malignancy-induced HUS from microvascular metastases or extensive marrow invasion (Harrison 22e p.638)","Bone-marrow / haematopoietic stem cell transplant-associated microangiopathy (Harrison 22e pp.638, 967)","Pregnancy/postpartum-associated thrombotic microangiopathy (including HELLP in the obstetric context) (Harrison 22e pp.967-968)","Systemic vasculitis (Harrison 22e p.967)"],"additionalInvestigations":["ADAMTS13 activity level AND anti-ADAMTS13 inhibitor/antibody assay (activity <10% diagnostic of TTP) (Harrison 22e p.968)","PT and aPTT — characteristically NORMAL in TTP/HUS; abnormal results point toward DIC (Harrison 22e p.967)","Direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test — negative in MAHA (Harrison 22e p.638)","Full blood count with platelet count and peripheral smear for schistocytes (Harrison 22e p.968)","Reticulocyte count (reticulocytosis supports haemolysis) (Harrison 22e p.638)","Renal function / serum creatinine and urinalysis (haematuria, proteinuria, granular or hyaline casts in HUS) (Harrison 22e p.638)","Complement-regulatory gene testing (C3, CFB, CFH, CD46, CFI, THBD) and anti-factor-H antibody for suspected atypical HUS — though assigning pathogenicity to variants is difficult and no functional diagnostic assay is established (Harrison 22e pp.968, 800-801)"],"redFlags":["New unexplained thrombocytopenia with MAHA is a haematologic emergency — TTP is devastating if not recognised and treated promptly (Harrison 22e p.968)","Plasma exchange should be initiated urgently on clinical suspicion without waiting for confirmatory ADAMTS13 results, and likewise re-initiated promptly on suspected relapse (Harrison 22e p.968)","Fluctuating neurologic abnormalities (especially headache and confusion) in the setting of MAHA suggest TTP (Harrison 22e p.450)","Persistently low ADAMTS13 carries a greater risk of ongoing sequelae including stroke (Harrison 22e p.968)","In cancer-/transfusion-associated HUS, severe hypertension, pulmonary oedema and rapid worsening of haemolysis and renal function can follow a blood or blood-product transfusion (Harrison 22e p.638)"],"prognosis":"Plasma exchange transformed TTP outcomes, reducing mortality from roughly 85-100% to about 10-30%. Relapse is common: 25-45% of plasma-exchange-treated patients relapse within 30 days of apparent remission and 12-40% relapse late, with relapse more frequent in those with severe ADAMTS13 deficiency at presentation. In diarrhoea-associated HUS roughly 40% of children need some dialysis but overall mortality is <5%; non-diarrhoeal HUS carries higher mortality (~26%). Untreated atypical HUS had acute-phase mortality up to ~15% with up to 50% progressing to end-stage renal disease, though spontaneous remission occurs and relapse is likely with re-exposure to a trigger. Cancer-/chemotherapy-associated HUS has a high case-fatality rate, with most patients dying within months (Harrison 22e pp.968, 800-801, 638).","pitfalls":["Do not reflexively transfuse platelets in TTP — platelet transfusion may fuel further microvascular thrombosis and is reserved for life-threatening bleeding (principle, Harrison 22e Chap. 120).","Do not assume normal coagulation excludes a thrombotic microangiopathy: PT and aPTT are characteristically normal in TTP/HUS, whereas they are abnormal in DIC — use them to discriminate, not to exclude (Harrison 22e p.967).","A normal ADAMTS13 does not point to TTP; ADAMTS13 is usually normal in HUS, so the diagnosis there rests on the clinical picture and complement work-up (Harrison 22e p.968).","Atypical HUS is a diagnosis of exclusion; patients may be started on plasma exchange until ADAMTS13 returns and the diagnosis clarifies, even though plasma exchange has not been shown to change clinical outcomes in aHUS (Harrison 22e p.968).","Plasma infusion/exchange does not alter the course of typical HUS or aHUS except in the subgroup with anti-factor-H antibodies (Harrison 22e p.968).","Drug-induced microangiopathy may improve simply on withdrawing or reducing the offending endothelial-toxic agent, so reflexive long-term plasma exchange may be unnecessary (Harrison 22e p.968).","Cancer-associated HUS can present without an evident malignancy — in one series 35% had no apparent cancer when the syndrome appeared, and it may surface weeks to months after the last chemotherapy dose (Harrison 22e p.638)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not give a formal TTP/HUS severity score in this material. Functional stratification used: ADAMTS13 activity <10% is diagnostic of TTP and severe deficiency at presentation predicts relapse and worse sequelae. HUS is grouped clinically into diarrhoea-associated (typical, Shiga-toxin) HUS versus non-diarrhoea-associated/atypical (complement-mediated) HUS, which carries higher mortality. (No PLASMIC score is described in the source and none is asserted here.) (Harrison 22e pp.968, 800-801).","references":["Harrison 22e p.967","Harrison 22e p.968","Harrison 22e p.800","Harrison 22e p.801","Harrison 22e p.638","Harrison 22e p.450"],"crossLinks":["dic","itp","glomerulonephritis"]},"variceal_bleed":{"name":"Variceal bleeding","system":"Hepatology / GI","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.360-363, 371-372, 2713-2715","clinicalPearls":["Portal hypertension is defined as a hepatic venous pressure gradient (HVPG) >5 mmHg; compensated cirrhosis typically has an HVPG of 5-10 mmHg, while clinically significant portal hypertension (CSPH), defined as HVPG ≥10 mmHg, carries substantial risk of decompensation (variceal bleeding, ascites, encephalopathy). Patients with a gradient >12 mmHg are at risk for variceal haemorrhage (p.2713-2714).","Each episode of variceal haemorrhage carries a 20-30% mortality rate, making it an immediate life-threatening complication of portal hypertension (p.2713).","In cirrhotic patients presenting with upper GI bleeding, prophylactic antibiotics (typically ceftriaxone) started before endoscopy reduce bacterial infections, recurrent bleeding AND mortality — this is a mortality-altering intervention, not merely supportive (p.362, p.2714).","A vasoactive/vasoconstricting agent (somatostatin, octreotide, terlipressin, vapreotide) improves initial bleeding control, reduces transfusion needs and reduces all-cause mortality; combined endoscopic plus pharmacologic therapy is superior to either alone (p.360, p.2714).","A restrictive transfusion strategy (transfuse when haemoglobin <7 g/dL, target 7-9 g/dL) reduced early rebleeding and mortality compared with a liberal threshold in randomised trial; haemoglobin may be normal at initial presentation because plasma and red-cell volumes fall proportionately (\"people bleed whole blood\") and may take up to 72 h to fall (p.362, p.2714).","Roughly one-third of patients with histologically confirmed cirrhosis have varices; about 5-15% of cirrhotics develop varices per year, and roughly one-third of patients with varices will bleed (p.2713).","IV erythromycin given shortly before endoscopy is suggested as a promotility agent to improve endoscopic visualisation in UGI bleeding, reducing the need for repeat endoscopy (p.362, p.2713).","Routine correction of an elevated prothrombin time with fresh frozen plasma is NOT recommended in acute variceal bleeding unless there is evidence of coagulopathy such as bleeding at other sites (e.g. IV-line sites) (p.2714).","Bleeding gastric varices, especially when entirely within the stomach or extending into the proximal stomach, are often not amenable to band ligation; tissue-adhesive injection (e.g. cyanoacrylate), TIPS, or balloon-occluded retrograde transvenous obliteration (BRTO) are alternative principles (p.361, p.2714)."],"pathophysiology":"Oesophageal varices are a consequence of portal hypertension, defined as an HVPG >5 mmHg. Portal hypertension arises from two simultaneous haemodynamic processes: (1) increased intrahepatic resistance to portal blood flow (from cirrhosis, regenerative nodules and microthrombi) and (2) increased splanchnic blood flow secondary to splanchnic vasodilation. Cirrhosis is associated with decreased intrahepatic endothelial nitric oxide synthase and nitric oxide, causing intrahepatic vasoconstriction, while systemically nitric oxide, VEGF and TNF rise, driving splanchnic vasodilation. In advanced stages, neurohumoral and vasoconstrictor activation causes sodium/water retention, increased blood volume and a hyperdynamic circulation that further raises portal pressure. Intrahepatic causes (mainly cirrhosis) account for >95% of portal hypertension; pre-hepatic (portal/splenic vein thrombosis) and post-hepatic (Budd-Chiari, right-heart congestion) causes also occur. Elevated portal pressure decompresses into portosystemic collaterals, producing gastro-oesophageal varices that can rupture and bleed. (p.2713-2714)","additionalDifferentials":["Peptic ulcer disease — the most common cause of upper GI bleeding (~50% of UGIB hospitalisations) (p.360)","Mallory-Weiss tear — ~2-10% of UGIB, classically preceded by vomiting/retching/coughing, usually on the gastric side of the gastro-oesophageal junction; stops spontaneously in ~80-90% (p.360)","Bleeding gastric varices, intestinal varices, and portal hypertensive gastropathy/enterocolopathy — other portal-hypertension-related sources distinct from oesophageal varices (p.361, p.2714)","Erosive disease — erosive oesophagitis, gastritis, duodenitis (up to ~30% of UGIB), commonly NSAID-related (p.361)","Stress-related gastric mucosal injury in the critically ill (p.361)","Less common: neoplasm, vascular ectasias (including hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia, gastric antral vascular ectasia), Dieulafoy's lesion, aortoenteric fistula, haemobilia/haemosuccus pancreaticus (p.361)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Portal hypertensive gastropathy — snakeskin-like mosaic mucosal pattern on endoscopy; can cause acute or chronic (iron-deficiency) GI bleeding and mimics variceal bleeding as a cause of haematemesis/melaena in the same cirrhotic patient (p.2714)","Gastric varices — also driven by portal hypertension but managed differently from oesophageal varices (p.361, p.2714)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Upper endoscopy within 24 h is first-line both to diagnose the bleeding source and to treat acutely; more urgent endoscopy (6-12 h) does not improve outcomes and may worsen them in high-risk patients (p.362, p.2714)","Endoscopic surveillance for varices: recommended at diagnosis of compensated cirrhosis, then every 2 years (active liver disease) or every 3 years (inactive); also at the time of hepatic decompensation (p.2714)","Liver stiffness by transient elastography — if <20 kPa and no thrombocytopenia, high-risk varices are rare and screening endoscopy may be deferred (p.2714)","HVPG measurement by interventional radiology (hepatic vein cannulation) in selected cases to confirm/quantify portal hypertension (p.371, p.2714)","Complete blood count (thrombocytopenia from hypersplenism is often the first marker of portal hypertension), liver biochemistry, albumin, and prothrombin time/INR to assess hepatic function and severity (p.371, p.2714)","Endoscopic stigmata predicting bleeding risk: red wale signs, hematocystic spots, diffuse erythema, bluish colour, cherry-red spots, white-nipple spots; larger varices and worse Child-Pugh/MELD also predict bleeding (p.2713)"],"redFlags":["Variceal haemorrhage is immediately life-threatening with 20-30% mortality per bleeding episode — treat as a major haemorrhage with airway/haemodynamic priority (p.2713)","Hemodynamic compromise (tachycardia, hypotension), increasing age and comorbidities predict rebleeding and death (p.362)","Increased frequency of spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP) in patients with variceal haemorrhage — SBP prophylaxis is recommended at presentation with upper GI bleeding (p.2715)","Persistent or recurrent bleeding despite endoscopic and medical therapy, and advanced liver disease (Child-Pugh B score 8-9 with active bleeding, or Child-Pugh C score 10-13), mark patients who benefit from early/salvage TIPS (p.360-361)"],"prognosis":"Variceal haemorrhage carries a 20-30% mortality rate with each bleeding episode and patients with variceal haemorrhage have poorer outcomes than those with other UGIB sources. Control of acute bleeding is achievable in the vast majority of cases, but bleeding recurs in the majority of patients unless definitive endoscopic therapy is instituted; oesophageal varices reflect clinically significant portal hypertension (HVPG ≥10 mmHg), and once cirrhosis decompensates the median survival is <2 years. Prophylactic antibiotics, vasoactive drugs and a restrictive transfusion strategy each independently reduce mortality. (p.360, p.2713-2714)","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal initial haemoglobin excludes severe bleeding — haemoglobin may be normal or only minimally reduced at presentation and can take up to 72 h to fall as extravascular fluid re-equilibrates (p.362)","Reflexively correcting a prolonged prothrombin time with FFP — not recommended in variceal bleeding without evidence of true coagulopathy, and FFP can raise portal pressure and cause volume overload (p.2714)","Over-liberal transfusion — a liberal threshold (Hb 9 g/dL) increased early rebleeding and mortality versus a restrictive (<7 g/dL) threshold (p.362, p.2714)","Omitting antibiotics in a cirrhotic UGI bleed — prophylactic antibiotics reduce infection, rebleeding and mortality and are a core, not optional, intervention (p.362, p.2714)","Attempting band ligation on gastric or proximal-stomach varices, where it is often unsuccessful — consider tissue adhesive, TIPS or BRTO (p.2714)","Forgetting that TIPS causes new or worsened hepatic encephalopathy in as many as 20% of patients, especially the elderly and those with pre-existing encephalopathy (p.2714)"],"severityClassification":"Bleeding/decompensation risk is stratified by Child-Pugh class and MELD score, by HVPG/wedged-hepatic-vein pressure (CSPH ≥10 mmHg; variceal bleeding risk at >12 mmHg), by varix size and location, and by endoscopic high-risk stigmata (red wale signs, cherry-red spots, etc.). For triage of UGIB generally, the Glasgow-Blatchford score (0-23) identifies very-low-risk patients (score 0-1) suitable for outpatient management. Advanced-disease thresholds favouring early TIPS: Child-Pugh B 8-9 with active bleeding at endoscopy, or Child-Pugh C 10-13. (p.362, p.2713-2714)","references":["Harrison 22e p.360","Harrison 22e p.361","Harrison 22e p.362","Harrison 22e p.363","Harrison 22e p.371","Harrison 22e p.372","Harrison 22e p.2713","Harrison 22e p.2714","Harrison 22e p.2715"],"crossLinks":["decomp_cirrhosis","peptic_ulcer","hepatic_enceph","SBP"]},"vasculitis":{"name":"Systemic vasculitis","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e, Chapter 375 (The Vasculitis Syndromes), pp. 2893-2948","clinicalPearls":["Suspect vasculitis in any patient with an unexplained multisystem illness; specific clues include palpable purpura, pulmonary infiltrates with microscopic hematuria, chronic sinusitis, mononeuritis multiplex, unexplained ischemic events, and glomerulonephritis with multisystem disease (Harrison 22e p.2937).","The first step in suspected vasculitis is to exclude conditions that mimic it - especially infections - particularly before starting empirical immunosuppression in a deteriorating patient (Harrison 22e p.2937).","Definitive diagnosis usually rests on biopsy of clinically involved tissue; 'blind' biopsy of organs with no clinical evidence of involvement has very low yield and should be avoided. Arteriography is used when PAN, Takayasu, or primary CNS vasculitis is suspected (Harrison 22e p.2937-2938).","ANCA-associated vasculitides (GPA, MPA, EGPA) share small-vessel involvement and ANCA positivity but have distinct clinical phenotypes and should be regarded as separate entities (Harrison 22e p.2937).","cANCA targets proteinase-3 (PR3) - present in >90% of active GPA; pANCA targets myeloperoxidase (MPO) - only anti-MPO antibodies are convincingly linked to vasculitis (MPA, EGPA, GPA, isolated necrotizing crescentic GN) (Harrison 22e p.2937).","A pANCA pattern NOT due to anti-MPO can occur in nonvasculitic conditions: rheumatic/nonrheumatic autoimmune disease, inflammatory bowel disease, certain drugs, and infections including endocarditis and bacterial airway infection in cystic fibrosis (Harrison 22e p.2937).","ANCA titres do not reliably track disease activity - GPA can be active despite negative ANCA, titres correlate poorly with activity, and patients in remission may keep high ANCA for years (Harrison 22e p.2937, p.2941).","Predictive value of a positive ANCA in GPA is ~90% with active glomerulonephritis but falls to 30-60% in sinus/lung disease without renal involvement; tissue biopsy is then needed to exclude infection or malignancy that may give a false-positive ANCA (Harrison 22e p.2941).","In GPA the renal lesion is a pauci-immune (no immune-complex deposition) focal segmental glomerulitis that can evolve into rapidly progressive crescentic glomerulonephritis (Harrison 22e p.2939-2940).","Saddle-nose deformity (from nasal septal perforation) and subglottic stenosis (~16% of GPA, may cause severe airway obstruction) are characteristic upper-airway sequelae of GPA (Harrison 22e p.2940).","Mononeuritis multiplex is a recurring vasculitic clue - common in EGPA (up to ~72%) and PAN (~51%), and also seen in GPA and MPA (Harrison 22e p.2940, p.2943, p.2944).","EGPA (Churg-Strauss) requires asthma, peripheral blood eosinophilia (>1000 cells/uL in >80%), and clinical features of vasculitis; eosinophilic tissue infiltration and extravascular granulomas are characteristic (Harrison 22e p.2943-2944).","Cardiac involvement (myocarditis, pericarditis, coronary vasculitis) is the leading cause of death in EGPA, accounting for ~39% of mortality; echocardiography is advised in all newly diagnosed patients (Harrison 22e p.2943-2944).","PAN does NOT involve pulmonary arteries and does NOT cause glomerulonephritis, granuloma, or significant eosinophilia; venule involvement points away from PAN and toward microscopic polyangiitis (Harrison 22e p.2944).","PAN classically causes microaneurysms of small- and medium-sized renal, hepatic, and visceral arteries; catheter-directed dye arteriography (MR/CT lack resolution) can be diagnostic when accessible tissue is unavailable (Harrison 22e p.2944-2945).","Giant cell arteritis occurs almost exclusively in those over 50; jaw/tongue claudication, scalp tenderness, and headache precede the dreaded complication of ischemic optic neuropathy with sudden blindness - therapy should not be delayed for diagnostic testing (Harrison 22e p.2945).","GCA and polymyalgia rheumatica are considered spectrums of one disease - PMR occurs in 40-50% of GCA, and 10-20% of isolated PMR later develop GCA (Harrison 22e p.2945).","Takayasu arteritis should be strongly suspected in a young woman with diminished/absent peripheral pulses, an inter-arm blood pressure discrepancy, or arterial bruits (Harrison 22e p.2946-2947).","Hemoptysis may be the first sign of alveolar haemorrhage in MPA (~12%); upper-airway disease and pulmonary nodules are atypical for MPA and instead suggest GPA (Harrison 22e p.2942).","GPA carries an increased risk of venous thrombotic events (DVT/PE); maintain a heightened awareness even though routine anticoagulation is not recommended (Harrison 22e p.2940)."],"pathophysiology":"Vasculitis is inflammation of and damage to blood vessel walls, usually compromising the lumen and causing ischaemia of supplied tissues; any size, type, or location of vessel may be affected, and disease may be primary/single-organ or secondary to another illness. Most syndromes are at least partly immune-mediated through three broad mechanisms (Harrison 22e p.2936): (1) Immune-complex formation/deposition (e.g., IgA vasculitis, lupus vasculitis, serum sickness, hepatitis-B-associated vasculitis, hepatitis-C cryoglobulinaemic vasculitis) - resembling serum sickness, complexes deposit in vessel walls, activate complement (notably C5a, chemotactic for neutrophils), and neutrophils infiltrate and release enzymes that damage the wall. (2) ANCA production (GPA, MPA, EGPA) - when neutrophils/monocytes are primed by TNF-alpha, IL-1, or alternative-complement activation, PR3 and MPO translocate to the cell membrane where ANCA can engage them, triggering degranulation, reactive oxygen species, endothelial injury, and release of IL-1/IL-8. (3) Pathogenic T-lymphocyte responses and granuloma formation (GCA, Takayasu, GPA, EGPA) - cytokine (IFN-gamma) activated endothelium expresses HLA class II and interacts with CD4+ T cells, with IL-1/TNF-alpha inducing adhesion molecules (ELAM-1, VCAM-1). The 1992/2012 Chapel Hill Consensus nomenclature underpins the classification of these syndromes by predominant vessel size (Harrison 22e p.2936, p.2942).","additionalDifferentials":["Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA) - upper + lower respiratory tract granulomatous vasculitis plus pauci-immune glomerulonephritis; PR3/cANCA in >90% of active disease (Harrison 22e p.2939-2940)","Microscopic polyangiitis (MPA) - pauci-immune small-vessel necrotizing vasculitis with frequent glomerulonephritis and pulmonary capillaritis but no granulomas; MPO/pANCA in ~75% (Harrison 22e p.2942)","Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis / Churg-Strauss (EGPA) - asthma, eosinophilia, extravascular granuloma, vasculitis; ANCA (anti-MPO) in ~48% (Harrison 22e p.2943-2944)","Polyarteritis nodosa (PAN) - necrotizing vasculitis of small/medium muscular arteries with microaneurysms, sparing lungs and glomeruli; associated with hepatitis B (and C); consider DADA2 in early-onset disease (Harrison 22e p.2944-2945)","Giant cell (temporal) arteritis with polymyalgia rheumatica - medium/large-vessel arteritis in patients over 50 (Harrison 22e p.2945-2946)","Takayasu arteritis - large-vessel arteritis of the aorta and its branches in young women (Harrison 22e p.2946-2947)","IgA vasculitis (Henoch-Schonlein) - small-vessel immune-complex vasculitis with palpable purpura, arthralgia, GI symptoms, glomerulonephritis; IgA/C3 on biopsy (Harrison 22e p.2947-2948)","Cryoglobulinaemic vasculitis - strongly associated with hepatitis C virus infection (Harrison 22e p.2936)","Anti-glomerular basement membrane disease (Goodpasture's syndrome) - other cause of pulmonary-renal syndrome to distinguish from ANCA-associated vasculitis (Harrison 22e p.2941)","Lymphomatoid granulomatosis - EBV-positive angiocentric B-cell proliferation with lung/skin/CNS/kidney involvement that mimics GPA but is not a true vasculitis (Harrison 22e p.2941)"],"infectionMimics":["Bacterial (infective) endocarditis - can produce a false-positive pANCA and a vasculitis-like multisystem picture (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Disseminated gonococcal infection (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Pulmonary histoplasmosis (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Coccidioidomycosis (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Syphilis (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Lyme disease (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Rocky Mountain spotted fever (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Whipple's disease (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Bacterial airway infection in cystic fibrosis - associated with a non-anti-MPO pANCA pattern (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Mucocutaneous leishmaniasis and rhinoscleroma - midline destructive infections that mimic GPA upper-airway disease (Harrison 22e p.2941)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Atheroembolic (cholesterol embolisation) disease - listed as a mimic of vasculitis (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Antiphospholipid syndrome (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Atrial myxoma (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Lymphoma / carcinomatosis (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma (nasal type) - EBV-associated cause of midline destructive disease mimicking GPA; never irradiate true GPA airway lesions (Harrison 22e p.2941)","Drug toxicity - cocaine (cocaine-induced midline destructive lesions can show anti-human-neutrophil-elastase ANCA), levamisole (cocaine adulterant; causes cutaneous infarction, granulocytopenia, vasculitis-mimicking serology), amphetamines, ergot alkaloids, methysergide, arsenic (Harrison 22e p.2937, p.2941)","Sarcoidosis (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Anti-GBM disease (Goodpasture's) (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Amyloidosis (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Migraine (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Fibromuscular dysplasia (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Heritable disorders of connective tissue (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Segmental arterial mediolysis (SAM) (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome (Harrison 22e p.2937)","IgG4-related disease - cause of aortitis/periaortitis distinguished histologically from Takayasu (IgG4+ plasma cells, storiform fibrosis, obliterative phlebitis) (Harrison 22e p.2947)"],"additionalInvestigations":["ANCA with antigen specificity - anti-proteinase-3 (cANCA) and anti-myeloperoxidase (pANCA) testing, interpreting in light of pretest probability (Harrison 22e p.2937, p.2941)","Tissue biopsy of a clinically involved organ for definitive diagnosis; in GPA, lung biopsy has the highest yield (necrotizing granulomatous vasculitis), upper-airway biopsy often shows only inflammation, renal biopsy shows pauci-immune GN (Harrison 22e p.2941)","Skin biopsy with immunofluorescence in IgA vasculitis (leukocytoclastic vasculitis with IgA and C3 deposition) (Harrison 22e p.2948)","Catheter-directed dye arteriography for PAN (microaneurysms of renal/hepatic/visceral arteries) - MR/CT angiography lack adequate resolution (Harrison 22e p.2945)","Temporal artery biopsy (3-5 cm segment, serial sections, because involvement is patchy/segmental); may remain positive up to ~14 days after starting glucocorticoids (Harrison 22e p.2946)","Temporal +/- axillary artery ultrasonography for the noncompressible 'halo' sign in GCA (Harrison 22e p.2946)","MR/CT angiography (and investigational PET) to assess large-vessel disease in GCA and to image the whole aorta and branches in Takayasu arteritis (Harrison 22e p.2946, p.2947)","Acute-phase reactants - ESR and/or CRP (note IL-6 inhibitors suppress CRP/ESR, removing them as activity markers in treated GCA) (Harrison 22e p.2946)","Complete blood count - anaemia, leukocytosis, thrombocytosis as inflammatory markers; striking eosinophilia in EGPA (Harrison 22e p.2940, p.2943)","Hepatitis B and C serology in all suspected PAN (and in cryoglobulinaemic vasculitis) (Harrison 22e p.2945)","Echocardiography in all newly diagnosed EGPA because cardiac involvement drives mortality (Harrison 22e p.2944)","Serum complement and IgA levels in IgA vasculitis (complement normal; IgA elevated in about half) (Harrison 22e p.2948)"],"redFlags":["Active glomerulonephritis is always classed as severe disease even with normal renal function (Harrison 22e p.2941)","Alveolar/pulmonary haemorrhage - severe manifestation of GPA/MPA; hemoptysis may be the first symptom of alveolar haemorrhage in MPA (Harrison 22e p.2941, p.2942)","Visual symptoms in a patient over 50 with headache - ischemic optic neuropathy in GCA can cause sudden, irreversible blindness; do not delay treatment for diagnostic workup (Harrison 22e p.2945, p.2946)","Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis with rising creatinine in ANCA-associated vasculitis (Harrison 22e p.2939-2940, p.2942)","Jaw/tongue claudication, scalp tenderness, or a tender/nodular temporal artery - cranial ischaemic features of GCA (Harrison 22e p.2945)","Inter-arm blood pressure discrepancy, absent pulses, or bruits in a young woman - suggests Takayasu large-vessel involvement (Harrison 22e p.2947)","Bowel infarction/perforation and GI bleeding in PAN - leading causes of death (Harrison 22e p.2944-2945)","Cardiac involvement in EGPA - the chief cause of death (Harrison 22e p.2944)","Signs of deep-vein thrombosis or pulmonary embolism in GPA given the increased venous thrombotic risk (Harrison 22e p.2940)"],"prognosis":"Untreated GPA was historically fatal within months; with cyclophosphamide-based therapy >90% improve, ~75% achieve complete remission, and 5-year survival exceeds 80%, but 50-70% of remissions later relapse and many accrue irreversible damage (renal insufficiency, hearing loss, subglottic stenosis, saddle-nose deformity, chronic sinus dysfunction) (Harrison 22e p.2941). Treated MPA has a ~74% 5-year survival, with deaths from alveolar haemorrhage or GI/cardiac/renal disease, and relapse in at least ~34% (Harrison 22e p.2942). Untreated EGPA has a ~25% 5-year survival; treated, prognosis is favourable (~72% at 78 months), but myocardial involvement causes ~39% of deaths (Harrison 22e p.2944). Untreated PAN has a poor 5-year survival of 10-20% (deaths from GI and cardiovascular causes); prednisone plus cyclophosphamide markedly improves survival, with relapse in ~10-20% (Harrison 22e p.2944-2945). In GCA acute death is uncommon, but patients face late mortality from aortic aneurysm rupture/dissection (18x more likely to develop thoracic aortic aneurysm than the general population) (Harrison 22e p.2946). Takayasu outcomes vary widely (overall survival >=94% in some North American series; 5-year mortality 0-35% elsewhere), and the disease is usually chronic and relapsing (Harrison 22e p.2947). IgA vasculitis has an excellent prognosis with rare mortality and only 1-5% of children progressing to end-stage renal disease (Harrison 22e p.2948).","pitfalls":["Treating a rise in ANCA titre as a relapse - only ~43% relapse within 1 year of an ANCA increase, so a rising ANCA alone should not prompt reinstitution or escalation of immunosuppression (Harrison 22e p.2941).","Relying on ANCA to diagnose vasculitis in sinus/lung disease without renal involvement, where positive predictive value is only 30-60% and biopsy is needed to exclude infection/malignancy with false-positive ANCA (Harrison 22e p.2941).","Performing 'blind' biopsies of clinically uninvolved organs - yield is very low (Harrison 22e p.2937).","Starting immunosuppression before excluding infectious mimics, especially in a rapidly deteriorating patient (Harrison 22e p.2937).","Irradiating upper-airway lesions assuming GPA when the diagnosis is actually NK/T-cell lymphoma - GPA airway lesions should never be irradiated (Harrison 22e p.2941).","Overlooking cocaine/levamisole-induced midline destruction, which can produce vasculitis-mimicking serology (anti-elastase ANCA) and granulocytopenia (Harrison 22e p.2941).","Using CRP/ESR to monitor disease activity or assess infection in GCA patients on IL-6 inhibitors, which suppress these acute-phase reactants (Harrison 22e p.2946).","Attributing every minor ESR/CRP rise during a GCA prednisone taper to a flare - small increases may not reflect arteritis, and can also be caused by infection (Harrison 22e p.2946).","Mislabelling a necrotizing vasculitis with venule involvement as PAN - venule involvement indicates microscopic polyangiitis, not PAN (Harrison 22e p.2944).","Missing DADA2 (adenosine deaminase 2 deficiency) in early-onset PAN-like vasculitis, which responds to TNF inhibitors rather than standard PAN therapy (Harrison 22e p.2944)."],"severityClassification":"Vasculitides are classified by predominant vessel size per the Chapel Hill Consensus nomenclature (Harrison 22e p.2936, p.2942): LARGE-VESSEL (giant cell/temporal arteritis, Takayasu arteritis); MEDIUM-VESSEL (polyarteritis nodosa, Kawasaki disease); SMALL-VESSEL, which splits into ANCA-associated (GPA, MPA, EGPA) and immune-complex (IgA vasculitis/Henoch-Schonlein, cryoglobulinaemic vasculitis). The chapter also separates PRIMARY syndromes from SECONDARY vasculitis (associated with probable etiology e.g. drug-induced, hepatitis-B/C-related, cancer-associated; or with systemic disease e.g. lupus, rheumatoid, sarcoid vasculitis), and single-organ vasculitis (cutaneous leukocytoclastic angiitis, cutaneous arteritis, primary CNS vasculitis, isolated aortitis) (Harrison 22e p.2936). For management stratification, ANCA-associated vasculitis is divided into SEVERE (life- or organ-threatening; e.g., alveolar haemorrhage, and active glomerulonephritis is severe even with normal renal function) versus NONSEVERE/limited disease - severity depends on both extent and the specific organ involved (e.g., nodular pulmonary disease without respiratory compromise is nonsevere, whereas alveolar haemorrhage is severe) (Harrison 22e p.2941).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2893","Harrison 22e p.2936","Harrison 22e p.2937","Harrison 22e p.2938","Harrison 22e p.2939","Harrison 22e p.2940","Harrison 22e p.2941","Harrison 22e p.2942","Harrison 22e p.2943","Harrison 22e p.2944","Harrison 22e p.2945","Harrison 22e p.2946","Harrison 22e p.2947","Harrison 22e p.2948"],"crossLinks":["glomerulonephritis","IE","temporal_arteritis","sle_flare"]},"vasovagal_syncope":{"name":"Vasovagal / orthostatic syncope","system":"Neurology / Cardiology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e, Chapter 23 (Syncope), pp.155-162","clinicalPearls":["Syncope is a transient, self-limited loss of consciousness from acute global cerebral hypoperfusion, with rapid onset, brief duration, and spontaneous complete recovery; reorientation is almost immediate after a syncopal event.","Causes fall into three categories: neurally mediated (reflex/vasovagal) syncope, orthostatic hypotension, and cardiac syncope; clinical features, mechanisms, and prognosis differ markedly among them.","Neurally mediated syncope is the most common cause overall in population-based studies and accounts for the vast majority of syncope in the young (peak incidence ~age 15); incidence is higher in women, and a first-degree family history is common in young patients.","Vasovagal syncope (the common faint) is provoked by intense emotion, pain, the sight of blood, venipuncture, and/or orthostatic stress; situational reflex syncopes have specific localized triggers (cough, micturition, swallow, defecation, carotid sinus pressure).","Premonitory (prodromal) features of autonomic activation in neurally mediated syncope include diaphoresis, pallor, palpitations, nausea, lightheadedness, hyperventilation, and yawning.","A functioning autonomic nervous system is required to develop neurally mediated syncope, in contrast to syncope from autonomic failure (orthostatic hypotension).","Neurally mediated syncope can be subdivided by efferent pathway: vasodepressor (sympathetic vasoconstrictor failure), cardioinhibitory (bradycardia/asystole from vagal outflow), or mixed.","Orthostatic hypotension is defined as a fall in systolic BP of at least 20 mmHg or diastolic BP of at least 10 mmHg within 3 min of standing or head-up tilt; supine and standing BP/HR at 3 min should be measured in every initial evaluation.","Initial clinical assessment plus ECG identifies a cause in about 50% of patients and stratifies cardiac mortality risk.","A clinically meaningful syncope usually corresponds to a fall in systolic BP to roughly 50 mmHg or lower; cessation of cerebral blood flow for 6-8 s produces loss of consciousness."],"pathophysiology":"Standing pools 500-1000 mL of blood in the lower extremities, buttocks, and splanchnic circulation, reducing venous return, ventricular filling, cardiac output, and blood pressure. Normally baroreceptors in the carotid sinus and aortic arch trigger a compensatory reflex that raises sympathetic outflow and lowers vagal activity, increasing peripheral resistance, venous return, and cardiac output. In neurally mediated (reflex/vasovagal) syncope this reflex transiently fails: there is a paroxysmal shift in autonomic efferent activity with increased parasympathetic outflow plus sympathoinhibition, producing bradycardia, vasodilation/loss of vasoconstrictor tone (vasodepressor response), and reduced cardiac output. The resulting fall in systemic blood pressure drops cerebral blood flow below the limits of autoregulation, causing transient global cerebral hypoperfusion and loss of consciousness. Multiple afferent triggers (orthostatic stress, carotid sinus, gastrointestinal tract, bladder) converge on the central autonomic network in the medulla. Hyperventilation-induced hypocarbia (cerebral vasoconstriction) and raised intrathoracic pressure (impaired venous return) contribute to many situational reflex syncopes. By contrast, orthostatic hypotension reflects chronic impairment of these homeostatic reflexes (autonomic failure), and cardiac syncope results from arrhythmias or structural disease reducing cardiac output.","additionalDifferentials":["Generalized and partial/partial-complex seizures (with secondary generalization, usually preceded by an aura such as an unpleasant smell, fear, anxiety, or abdominal/visceral sensation).","Autonomic seizures (autonomic epilepsy), whose cardiovascular, GI, pulmonary, urogenital, pupillary, and cutaneous manifestations mimic syncopal prodrome; accompanying non-autonomic auras help distinguish them.","Hypoglycemia, typically in insulin-treated type 1 or type 2 diabetes (tremor, palpitations, anxiety, diaphoresis, hunger, paresthesias; hunger is not a typical syncope prodrome).","Cataplexy (abrupt loss of muscle tone triggered by strong emotion such as laughter or anger; consciousness is preserved, lasts 30 s to 2 min, no prodrome; occurs in 60-75% of narcolepsy).","Vertebrobasilar ischemia and hypoxemia as other causes of transient loss of consciousness.","Falls due to vestibular dysfunction, cerebellar disease, extrapyramidal/gait disorders, and falls with head trauma (especially difficult in patients with dementia and repeated falls).","Psychogenic apparent loss of consciousness from psychiatric disorders (generalized anxiety, panic disorder, major depression, somatization), suggested by frequent fainting without prodrome and rare injury despite numerous falls, with no significant hemodynamic change."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG when arrhythmia or underlying cardiac disease is suspected; look for brady-/tachyarrhythmias, AV block, acute or old myocardial ischemia/infarction, long QT, and bundle branch block.","Supine and standing (3 min) blood pressure and heart rate to detect orthostatic hypotension.","Tilt-table testing (autonomic testing) to distinguish autonomic-failure orthostatic hypotension from the hypotensive-bradycardic response of neurally mediated syncope, and to identify immediate or delayed orthostatic hypotension; may also reproduce psychogenic syncope when symptoms occur without hemodynamic change.","Autonomic function testing in specialized centers: heart rate variability to deep breathing and Valsalva (parasympathetic), thermoregulatory sweat test and quantitative sudomotor axon reflex test (sympathetic cholinergic), and BP response to Valsalva/tilt (sympathetic adrenergic).","Carotid sinus massage (under continuous ECG and BP monitoring) for suspected carotid sinus syncope and in patients >40 years with recurrent unexplained syncope; avoid with carotid bruits, plaque, or stenosis.","Inpatient ECG monitoring for high arrhythmia risk; ambulatory Holter monitoring for frequent (near-daily) episodes; external or implantable loop recorders for less frequent episodes (monitor for at least twice the interspell interval).","Echocardiography for patients with cardiac history or abnormal exam/ECG (may reveal aortic stenosis, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, cardiac tumors, aortic dissection, pericardial tamponade; also stratifies sudden-death risk by ejection fraction).","Treadmill exercise testing with ECG and BP monitoring for syncope during or shortly after exercise.","Invasive electrophysiologic studies (low sensitivity/specificity, rarely used) only when pretest probability is high in structural heart disease with ECG abnormalities.","Baseline blood tests are rarely helpful and should be reserved for suspected specific disorders (e.g., myocardial infarction, anemia, secondary autonomic failure); psychiatric screening for recurrent unexplained syncope."],"redFlags":["Syncope during exertion, or while seated or supine.","Sudden onset of palpitations immediately followed by syncope, or palpitations at the time of syncope.","Chest pain suggesting coronary ischemia, or features of congestive heart failure.","Moderate or severe valvular or structural cardiac disease, or known severe coronary artery disease.","ECG features: ischemia, history of ventricular arrhythmias, prolonged QT (>500 ms), repetitive SA block or sinus pauses, persistent sinus bradycardia, bi-/trifascicular block or QRS >=120 ms, atrial fibrillation, nonsustained VT, preexcitation, or a Brugada pattern.","Family history of sudden death.","New chest discomfort, abdominal pain, shortness of breath, or headache associated with the episode.","Exam: unexplained systolic BP <90 mmHg, signs of GI hemorrhage, persistent bradycardia <40 beats/min, or an undiagnosed systolic murmur."],"prognosis":"Syncope of noncardiac and unexplained origin in younger individuals carries an excellent prognosis with unaffected life expectancy. In contrast, cardiac syncope (structural heart disease or primary arrhythmia) is associated with an increased risk of sudden cardiac death and overall mortality, and mortality is also increased with orthostatic hypotension related to age and comorbidities. Hospitalization and mortality risk are higher in older adults.","pitfalls":["Convulsive syncope mimics seizure: myoclonic and other movements occur in up to ~90% of syncopal episodes, but syncope-related jerks are typically arrhythmic, multifocal/generalized, and brief (<30 s), with mild flexor/extensor posturing; EEG seizure discharges are absent during syncope.","Distinguishing features from seizure: seizure loss of consciousness usually lasts >5 min with prolonged postictal drowsiness/disorientation, whereas reorientation after syncope is almost immediate; muscle aches are longer and more severe after seizures; seizures are rarely provoked by emotion or pain.","Urinary incontinence can occur in both syncope and seizure and does not distinguish them; fecal incontinence is very rare in syncope and postictal confusion is rare after syncope.","During neurally mediated syncope the eyes typically remain open and deviate upward with dilated pupils, and grunting/moaning/stertorous breathing may occur, which can be mistaken for a seizure.","Orthostatic hypotension syncope is usually preceded by warning symptoms but may occur suddenly, mimicking a seizure or cardiac cause.","Supine hypertension is common (>50%) in autonomic-failure orthostatic hypotension; treating one condition can unmask or worsen the other.","Initial orthostatic hypotension (BP fall within 15 s, resolving by 45 s) and delayed orthostatic hypotension (beyond 3 min) can be missed by a single 3-min standing measurement; initial orthostatic hypotension does not represent autonomic failure."],"severityClassification":"Harrison categorizes syncope by mechanism rather than a graded severity score: (1) neurally mediated (reflex/vasovagal) syncope, (2) orthostatic hypotension, and (3) cardiac syncope. For risk stratification, Table 23-1 lists high-risk features indicating hospitalization or intensive evaluation (e.g., ischemic chest pain, heart failure, significant valvular/structural disease, ECG ischemia, ventricular arrhythmia history, QT >500 ms, SA block/sinus pauses, persistent sinus bradycardia, bi-/trifascicular block or QRS >=120 ms, atrial fibrillation, nonsustained VT, family history of sudden death, preexcitation, Brugada pattern, palpitations at syncope, syncope at rest or during exercise). Neurally mediated efferent subtypes are vasodepressor, cardioinhibitory, and mixed.","references":["Harrison 22e p.155","Harrison 22e p.156","Harrison 22e p.157","Harrison 22e p.158","Harrison 22e p.159","Harrison 22e p.160","Harrison 22e p.161","Harrison 22e p.162"],"crossLinks":["seizure_epilepsy","aortic_stenosis","atrial_fib","tia"]},"wernicke":{"name":"Wernicke encephalopathy","system":"Neurology / Nutrition","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2348-2350 (Ch.318 Nervous System Disorders in Critical Care); p.2607 (Ch.344 Vitamin/Trace Mineral Deficiency); p.235-236 (Ch. Disorders of the Eye); p.3526 (Ch.464 Alcohol/neurology of amnesia); p.3719 (Ch. Alcohol Use Disorders)","clinicalPearls":["The classic triad (ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, global confusion) is present in only about one-third of acute cases, so a complete triad should not be required to make the diagnosis.","Most patients are profoundly disoriented, apathetic/indifferent, and inattentive rather than agitated; agitated delirium, when present, is usually from coexisting alcohol withdrawal.","Thiamine deficiency causing Wernicke is not confined to alcohol use; non-alcoholic risk groups include hyperemesis, starvation/malnutrition, renal dialysis, cancer, HIV/AIDS, gastric/bariatric surgery, and chronic diuretic therapy (increased urinary thiamine loss).","Certain drugs (e.g., metformin, verapamil) can inhibit intestinal thiamine transporters (ThTR-2) and add to deficiency risk.","Ocular signs include horizontal nystagmus on lateral gaze, bilateral lateral rectus (abducens) palsy, conjugate gaze palsies, and rarely ptosis; pupils are usually spared but may become miotic in advanced disease.","Gait ataxia is multifactorial, arising from a combination of peripheral polyneuropathy, cerebellar involvement, and vestibular paresis.","Treat on clinical suspicion as a medical emergency: it is common, preventable, and reversible if thiamine is given early.","Korsakoff amnestic state may only become apparent as the acute confusion recedes, characterized by recent-memory/learning impairment, confabulation, and disordered temporal sequencing.","The amnestic deficit correlates with lesions of the dorsomedial (dorsal medial) thalamic nuclei."],"pathophysiology":"Wernicke disease results from thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency. Thiamine is a cofactor for several enzymes including transketolase, pyruvate dehydrogenase, and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase. Deficiency causes a diffuse fall in cerebral glucose utilization and mitochondrial damage; impaired alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase leads to glutamate accumulation, which together with the energy deficit may produce excitotoxic neuronal injury. Lesions are periventricular, surrounding the third ventricle, aqueduct, and fourth ventricle, with predilection for midline structures (dorsomedial thalamus, mammillary bodies, midline cerebellum, periaqueductal gray, and trochlear/abducens nuclei). Acute cases may show petechial hemorrhages; chronic cases show mammillary body atrophy.","additionalDifferentials":["Supranuclear gaze disorder mimicking combined abducens and oculomotor nerve palsy (Wernicke can itself cause this pattern)","Other metabolic encephalopathies (hepatic, hypercarbic, hyper-/hyponatremic, hyper-/hypoglycemic)","Sepsis-associated encephalopathy","Osmotic demyelination syndrome (central pontine myelinolysis)","Marchiafava-Bignami disease (corpus callosum degeneration in chronic alcoholism)","Alcoholic cerebellar degeneration"],"infectionMimics":["Encephalitis (including herpes simplex encephalitis as a cause of an amnestic state)","Whipple's disease (cause of supranuclear gaze palsy)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Autoimmune limbic encephalitis (e.g., AMPAR, LGI1 antibodies) causing amnesia","Paraneoplastic encephalitis (e.g., Ma antibodies)","Thalamic, medial temporal, or posterior corpus callosum tumor causing amnesia","Anterior/posterior cerebral artery territory infarction causing amnesia","Stroke (infarct/hemorrhage), multiple sclerosis, vasculitis, or tumor causing supranuclear gaze palsy"],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI may show changes including abnormal enhancement of the mammillary bodies (coronal T1 postcontrast), typical of acute Wernicke","Erythrocyte transketolase activity assay measured before and after adding thiamine pyrophosphate; >25% stimulation (activity coefficient >=1.25) is abnormal","Serum/blood thiamine or its phosphorylated esters by high-performance liquid chromatography","Serum magnesium (required cofactor for thiamine activation)"],"redFlags":["Untreated disease can progress to stupor, coma, and death","Giving glucose (parenteral dextrose) before thiamine can precipitate Wernicke in a previously unaffected at-risk patient or rapidly worsen an early form","New amnestic state emerging as confusion clears signals evolution to Korsakoff syndrome"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on early thiamine administration. Ocular palsies improve within hours of thiamine; horizontal nystagmus may persist. Ataxia improves more slowly than ocular signs, and roughly half of patients recover incompletely, left with a slow, wide-based, shuffling gait and inability to tandem walk. Apathy, drowsiness, and confusion resolve gradually. Korsakoff psychosis is frequently persistent, with permanent gaps in memory, confabulation, and disordered temporal sequencing; prolonged untreated deficiency can cause an irreversible profound amnestic syndrome or death. Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is underdiagnosed despite a typical history and picture.","pitfalls":["Waiting for the full classic triad before treating, when only about one-third of patients show all three features.","Attributing the quiet, indifferent, inattentive confusional state to depression, sedation, or simple intoxication and missing the diagnosis.","Assuming Wernicke occurs only in alcoholics and overlooking it in hyperemesis, malnutrition, dialysis, bariatric surgery, cancer, or HIV patients.","Administering glucose-containing fluids before thiamine in an at-risk patient, which can trigger or worsen Wernicke.","Thiamine deficiency being overlooked in the setting of head injury (and possibly contributing to motor vehicle accidents)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.2348","Harrison 22e p.2349","Harrison 22e p.2350","Harrison 22e p.2607","Harrison 22e p.235","Harrison 22e p.3526","Harrison 22e p.3719"],"crossLinks":["metabolic_enceph","hepatic_enceph","hypoglycemia"]},"acute_appendicitis":{"name":"Acute Appendicitis and Peritonitis","system":"Gastroenterology / Surgical","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":342,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2594-2598","clinicalPearls":["Classic history is migration of vague, intermittent, crampy epigastric or periumbilical pain to the right lower quadrant over 12-24 h, where it becomes sharper and localizes as transmural inflammation irritates the parietal peritoneum (p.2595).","Maximal tenderness is classically at or near McBurney's point in the right lower quadrant, about one-third of the way along a line from the anterior iliac spine to the umbilicus (p.2596).","Nausea, if present, characteristically follows the onset of abdominal pain (helping distinguish appendicitis from gastroenteritis, in which nausea precedes pain); emesis is typically mild and scant and also occurs after pain onset (p.2595).","Anorexia is so common that the diagnosis of appendicitis should be questioned in its absence (p.2595).","Rovsing's sign: palpation/gentle pressure in the left lower quadrant elicits pain in the right lower quadrant (p.2596).","Iliopsoas sign: extending the right hip causes pain along the posterolateral back and hip, suggesting a retrocecal appendix (p.2596).","Obturator sign: internal rotation of the hip causes pain, suggesting an inflamed appendix located in the pelvis (p.2596).","Patients with appendicitis tend to lie quite still to avoid peritoneal irritation; some report discomfort with a bumpy car ride, coughing, sneezing, or Valsalva-type maneuvers. Parietal peritoneal irritation is often best elicited by gentle percussion, jiggling the bed/gurney, or mildly bumping the feet (p.2596).","Simple appendicitis patients usually appear only mildly ill with pulse and temperature only slightly above normal; temperature >38.3 C (~101 F) or rigors should raise concern for another process or for complications such as perforation, phlegmon, or abscess (p.2596).","Examine the entire abdomen systematically, starting in a non-tender area; all patients should have a rectal examination, and a pelvic examination in women is mandatory to exclude PID, ectopic pregnancy, and ovarian torsion (p.2596).","Appendicitis should be included in the differential diagnosis of abdominal pain for every patient of any age unless it is certain the appendix has been previously removed (p.2595)."],"pathophysiology":"Etiology is not completely understood. Obstruction of the appendiceal lumen (by fecaliths/appendicoliths, incompletely digested food residue, lymphoid hyperplasia, intraluminal scarring, tumors, bacteria, viruses, or inflammatory bowel disease) is believed, though not proven, to be an important early step at least in some cases. Obstruction leads to bacterial overgrowth and luminal distension with increased intraluminal pressure that can inhibit lymph and blood flow; vascular thrombosis and ischemic necrosis with perforation of the distal appendix may then follow. Perforation near the base of the appendix should raise concern for another disease process. Appendicoliths are found in ~50% of patients with gangrenous appendicitis who perforate but are rarely seen in simpler disease, suggesting differing underlying pathophysiology. Uncomplicated appendicitis does not always progress to perforation; some cases of simple acute appendicitis may temporarily resolve or be managed with antibiotics, though at least one-third will subsequently require appendectomy. When perforation occurs, the leak may be contained by omentum or surrounding tissues to form an abscess, whereas free perforation normally causes severe peritonitis (p.2594-2595).","additionalDifferentials":["Crohn's disease","Cholecystitis or other gallbladder disease","Diverticulitis","Ectopic pregnancy","Endometriosis","Gastroenteritis or colitis","Gastric or duodenal ulceration","Hepatitis","Kidney disease including nephrolithiasis","Liver abscess","Meckel's diverticulitis","Mittelschmerz","Mesenteric adenitis","Omental torsion","Pancreatitis","Lower lobe pneumonia","Pelvic inflammatory disease","Ruptured ovarian cyst or other cystic ovarian disease","Small-bowel obstruction","Urinary tract infection","Ovarian torsion (Harrison 22e p.2596)"],"infectionMimics":["Gastroenteritis or colitis (nausea precedes pain, unlike appendicitis) (Harrison 22e p.2595)","Pelvic inflammatory disease (Harrison 22e p.2596)","Urinary tract infection / genitourinary infection (Harrison 22e p.2596)","Mesenteric adenitis (Harrison 22e p.2595)","Hepatitis / liver abscess (Harrison 22e p.2595)","Lower lobe pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.2595)","COVID-19 infection occasionally coincident (Harrison 22e p.2595)","In immunocompromised patients: atypical infections from mycobacteria, Cytomegalovirus, or fungi, and neutropenic enterocolitis (Harrison 22e p.2596)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Ectopic pregnancy (Harrison 22e p.2596)","Ovarian torsion (Harrison 22e p.2596)","Ruptured ovarian cyst / cystic ovarian disease (Harrison 22e p.2595)","Mittelschmerz (Harrison 22e p.2595)","Endometriosis (Harrison 22e p.2595)","Nephrolithiasis / ureterolithiasis (Harrison 22e p.2595-2596)","Small-bowel obstruction (Harrison 22e p.2595)","Omental torsion (Harrison 22e p.2595)","Pancreatitis (Harrison 22e p.2595)","Appendiceal tumors / neoplasms (incidence increasing) (Harrison 22e p.2594)"],"additionalInvestigations":["WBC count: only mildly to moderately elevated (~10,000-18,000 cells/uL) in ~70% of simple appendicitis; laboratory testing alone does not identify patients with appendicitis (Harrison 22e p.2596).","A left shift toward immature polymorphonuclear leukocytes is present in >95% of cases (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Serum amylase and lipase should be measured; sickle cell preparation may be prudent in patients of African, Spanish, Mediterranean, or Indian ancestry (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Urinalysis to help exclude genitourinary mimics; a few red or white cells may be a nonspecific finding, and an inflamed appendix abutting the ureter or bladder may cause sterile pyuria or hematuria (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Pregnancy test in every woman of childbearing age; cervical cultures if PID is suspected (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Anemia and guaiac-positive stools should raise concern for other disease or complications such as cancer (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Ultrasonography: highly operator dependent; overall sensitivity ~0.86 and specificity ~0.81; appendix may not be visualized even in skilled hands; especially useful (including intravaginal techniques) for pelvic pathology in women. Suggestive findings: wall thickening, increased appendiceal diameter, free fluid (Harrison 22e p.2596).","CT: sensitivity at least 0.94 and specificity at least 0.95; high negative predictive value; suggestive findings include dilatation >6 mm with wall thickening, a lumen that does not fill with enteric contrast, and periappendiceal fatty stranding or air. Presence of luminal air or contrast is NOT consistent with appendicitis; nonvisualization of the appendix is nonspecific and should NOT be used to rule out appendiceal/periappendiceal inflammation (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Plain abdominal films are rarely helpful and not routinely obtained; <5% show an opaque fecalith, and a fecalith is not diagnostic of appendicitis (Harrison 22e p.2596).","For peritonitis: in stable patients with ascites, diagnostic paracentesis is indicated with testing of fluid for protein and LDH and a cell count; radiographs may show bowel dilatation and wall edema, and free air or other leakage may represent a surgical emergency (Harrison 22e p.2597)."],"redFlags":["Free perforation normally causes severe (generalized) peritonitis; perforation risk is ~10-20% and is significantly higher in patients <5 or >65 years of age (Harrison 22e p.2594).","Temperature >38.3 C (~101 F) or rigors suggest a complication (perforation, phlegmon, abscess) or another diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Persistent symptoms not improved over 48 h are more likely to perforate or develop other complications (Harrison 22e p.2595).","Rare but dreaded complication: infective suppurative thrombosis of the portal vein and its tributaries with intrahepatic abscesses, with very poor prognosis (Harrison 22e p.2595).","Diffuse abdominal tenderness with guarding and rigidity indicates generalized peritonitis; tachycardia, hypotension/volume depletion, significant leukocytosis, and severe acidosis are signs of serious disease (Harrison 22e p.2597).","In pregnancy, perforation raises fetal mortality fourfold (from ~5% to ~20%) (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Postoperative fever and leukocytosis persisting beyond 5 days should raise concern for an intraabdominal abscess (Harrison 22e p.2597)."],"prognosis":"Mortality from simple appendicitis is currently <1%, and for uncomplicated nonperforated appendicitis the operative mortality rate is 0.1-0.5% (approximating the risk of general anesthesia). Mortality for perforated or otherwise complicated disease is much higher, ranging from ~3% overall to as high as 15% in the elderly. For acute peritonitis, mortality can be <10% in reasonably healthy patients with relatively uncomplicated, localized disease but >40% in the elderly or immunocompromised (Harrison 22e p.2597).","pitfalls":["Atypical presentations and pain patterns are common, especially in the very old and very young; signs and symptoms can be subtle in the elderly, who may not react vigorously, with nausea/anorexia/emesis predominating (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Retrocecal or pelvic (below the pelvic brim) appendix may prompt very little anterior abdominal wall tenderness; pelvic appendicitis may present with dysuria, urinary frequency, diarrhea, or tenesmus and only suprapubic or rectal/pelvic-exam tenderness (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Appendiceal location varies widely (right lower quadrant, pelvis, right flank, right upper quadrant, or even the left side with malrotation/redundant colon), influencing presentation; delay in diagnosis is more likely when the appendix is unusually positioned (Harrison 22e p.2595).","Diagnosing appendicitis in children is challenging (dramatic response to stimulation, difficult history); the smaller pediatric omentum may be less able to wall off a perforation (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Pregnancy: appendicitis is the most common extrauterine general surgical emergency in pregnancy; early symptoms (nausea, anorexia) may be overlooked and the enlarging/gravid uterus may displace the appendix higher and obscure typical findings (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Immunocompromised patients may present with only mild tenderness; do not be overly cautious and delay operation in those believed to have appendicitis (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Laboratory testing alone does not identify appendicitis, and no currently available decision tool obviates the need for expert clinical opinion (Harrison 22e p.2596).","A fecalith is not diagnostic of appendicitis, though its presence in an appropriate location with pain is suggestive and associated with greater likelihood of complications (Harrison 22e p.2596)."],"severityClassification":"No formal grading scale is presented; disease is broadly distinguished as simple/uncomplicated appendicitis (without gangrenous necrosis, appendicoliths, perforation, or tumors) versus complicated disease (gangrene, perforation, phlegmon, abscess). Peritonitis is classified as secondary (most commonly from perforation of a hollow viscus, e.g., appendix, colonic diverticula, stomach/duodenum) versus primary or spontaneous (no identifiable intraabdominal source; >90% occur in patients with ascites or hypoproteinemia <1 g/L), and either type may be localized or diffuse; aseptic/chemical peritonitis (e.g., from gastric juice, bile, pancreatic enzymes, blood, or urine) is also distinguished (Harrison 22e p.2595-2597).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2594-2598"],"crossLinks":["bowel_obstruction"]},"acute_myeloid_leukemia":{"name":"Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)","system":"Oncology / Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":109,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.825","clinicalPearls":["AML is a clonal neoplasm characterized by infiltration of blood, bone marrow, and other tissues by proliferative, poorly differentiated myeloid cells (myeloblasts); historically the diagnosis required a marrow or blood myeloid blast count of >=20%, though the 2022 WHO classification eliminated the blast-percent requirement for cases with specific defining genetic aberrations (Harrison 22e p.825, p.826).","Most cases are idiopathic; genome sequencing suggests the majority arise from a limited number of mutations that accumulate with age. AML is the most common acute leukemia in older patients (median age at diagnosis 69 years) and causes 62% of leukemic deaths; only ~32% of patients survive 5 years (Harrison 22e p.825).","Auer rods are abnormal rod-shaped cytoplasmic granules; they are NOT uniformly present, but when seen they establish AML (and exclude acute lymphocytic leukemia) with certainty. AML blasts show fine lacy chromatin with one or more nucleoli and primary (nonspecific) cytoplasmic granules (Harrison 22e p.829).","Presentation is usually nonspecific and gradual (sometimes abrupt), reflecting marrow failure: fatigue (frequent first symptom) and anemia, abnormal hemostasis/bleeding and easy bruising from thrombocytopenia, and fever +/- infection from neutropenia/leukocyte dysfunction. Fever is the initial symptom in ~10% of patients (Harrison 22e p.828).","Multiparameter flow cytometry helps distinguish AML from acute lymphoblastic leukemia and identify subtypes (e.g., myeloid antigens CD13/CD117 in minimally differentiated AML; platelet antigens CD41/CD61 in acute megakaryoblastic leukemia); it has only a supportive role for WHO subtyping given the paramount importance of genetics, and is increasingly used to measure measurable residual disease (MRD) (Harrison 22e p.827).","Cytogenetic/molecular testing at diagnosis provides the most important prognostic information and drives risk stratification (European LeukemiaNet 2022 categories: favorable, intermediate, adverse). All AML cells contain genetic mutations, most recurring; the majority of patients now have a genetic lesion that can be specifically targeted (Harrison 22e p.826, p.827).","Acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL), defined by t(15;17)(q22;q12) / PML-RARA fusion, is a distinct, highly curable subtype (~85% long-term survival) treated uniquely with tretinoin (ATRA) and arsenic trioxide; it is excluded from the ELN risk table and is a coagulopathy emergency (Harrison 22e p.826, p.833).","Myeloid sarcoma is a tumor mass of myeloid blasts at sites other than marrow (commonly skin, lymph node, GI tract, soft tissue, testis); it may precede or coincide with blood/marrow involvement, is associated with t(8;21), and patients with isolated myeloid sarcoma typically develop blood/marrow disease quickly and cannot be cured by local therapy alone (Harrison 22e p.828).","Infiltration of gingiva, skin, soft tissues, or meninges with leukemic blasts at diagnosis is characteristic of the monocytic subtypes and those with KMT2A chromosomal abnormalities; gum hypertrophy is a classic monocytic finding (Harrison 22e p.828, p.830).","Infectious complications during neutropenia are the major cause of morbidity and death during induction and postremission chemotherapy; fever develops in most patients but infection is documented in only about half of febrile patients, and empiric broad-spectrum antibacterial/antifungal therapy at onset of neutropenic fever has substantially reduced infectious deaths (Harrison 22e p.832)."],"pathophysiology":"AML results from clonal proliferation of poorly differentiated myeloid progenitor (blast) cells that infiltrate the marrow, blood, and tissues, crowding out normal hematopoiesis and producing pancytopenia (anemia, neutropenia/leukocyte dysfunction, thrombocytopenia). Leukemogenesis is driven by accumulating somatic mutations; clonal hematopoiesis of indeterminate potential (CHIP), most often involving epigenetic regulators DNMT3A, TET2, and ASXL1, carries a ~10-fold increased risk of hematologic malignancy, though additional 'hits' are required to progress to leukemia. Recurrent genetic lesions define disease biology and presentation: fusion genes (e.g., PML-RARA from t[15;17]; RUNX1-RUNX1T1 from t[8;21]; CBFB-MYH11 from inv[16]/t[16;16] core-binding-factor AML; MLLT3-KMT2A; DEK-NUP214) and gene mutations (NPM1, present in ~30% of AML and ~60% of cytogenetically normal AML; FLT3, activating ITD or TKD mutations in ~30%; CEBPA; TP53; IDH1/IDH2 producing the oncometabolite 2-hydroxyglutarate that disrupts epigenetic processes). KMT2A rearrangements and monocytic differentiation favor extramedullary/tissue infiltration; t(15;17) APL drives DIC.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) - distinguished by flow cytometry/immunophenotype; presence of Auer rods confirms AML and excludes ALL","Myelodysplastic syndrome (MDS) - may precede/evolve into AML; AML-MR is defined by antecedent MDS or MDS/MPN and/or specific cytogenetic/molecular features","Myeloproliferative neoplasm (including chronic myeloid leukemia) - antecedent hematologic disorder that can transform to AML","Therapy-related (post-chemotherapy) AML - after alkylating agents (4-6 yr latency) or topoisomerase II inhibitors (1-3 yr latency)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute lymphoblastic leukemia","Myelodysplastic syndrome","Aplastic anemia / marrow failure syndromes (also present with pancytopenia)","Myeloproliferative neoplasms (e.g., CML in blast crisis)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CBC with manual differential (anemia normocytic normochromic; median presenting WBC ~15,000/uL; platelets <100,000/uL in ~75%)","Bone marrow aspirate and biopsy with morphology, cytogenetics, flow cytometry, and molecular studies (cornerstone of diagnosis and classification)","Cytogenetics and molecular testing at diagnosis (NPM1, FLT3-ITD/TKD, CEBPA bZIP, TP53, IDH1/IDH2, RUNX1, ASXL1 and others) for ELN risk stratification","Peripheral blood smear for blasts and Auer rods; peroxidase staining","Chemistry including uric acid, LDH, phosphorus, calcium, creatinine/BUN (tumor lysis screen); ~50% have mild-moderate uric acid elevation","Coagulation studies - PT, PTT, fibrinogen, D-dimer (screen for DIC, especially APL)","Cryopreservation of viable leukemia cells for future testing","HLA typing for potential allogeneic HCT; echocardiogram/MUGA for cardiac function; chest radiograph","Infection screen including SARS-CoV-2 and other viruses in patients with respiratory symptoms before chemotherapy","Measurable residual disease (MRD) assessment by flow cytometry or quantitative RT-PCR (e.g., NPM1, RUNX1-RUNX1T1, CBFB-MYH11, PML-RARA transcripts)"],"redFlags":["Acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL, t[15;17]/PML-RARA): coagulopathy/DIC emergency - presents with DIC-associated hemorrhage (may include significant GI, intrapulmonary, or intracranial bleeding); requires immediate recognition and ATRA-based therapy","Disseminated intravascular coagulation - classically in APL but also in monocytic AML and with extreme leukocytosis or thrombocytopenia; note thrombosis is also a recognized feature of DIC in APL","Hyperleukocytosis (WBC >100,000/uL, seen in ~20% at presentation) with pulmonary leukostasis and early CNS bleeding - associated with poor outcomes","Intracranial hemorrhage / CNS bleed - headache, vision changes, nonfocal neurologic abnormalities","Febrile neutropenia - fever in a neutropenic patient mandates immediate evaluation and empiric broad-spectrum antibacterials covering gram-negative organisms","Tumor lysis syndrome / uric acid nephropathy - hyperuricemia (aggravated by initiating chemotherapy); allopurinol/hydration started at diagnosis, rasburicase for severe cases (avoid in G6PD deficiency)","APL differentiation syndrome - within first 3 weeks of ATRA (or less commonly ATO): fever, fluid retention, dyspnea, chest pain, pulmonary infiltrates, pleural/pericardial effusions, hypoxemia; ~10% mortality if unrecognized"],"prognosis":"Untreated AML is uniformly fatal; overall only ~32% of US patients survive 5 years. Cytogenetic/molecular risk is the dominant prognostic determinant: t(15;17)/APL has a very good prognosis (~85% cured), t(8;21) and inv(16) (core-binding-factor AML) a good prognosis (~55% cured), cytogenetically normal AML an intermediate outcome (~40% cured), and TP53 mutation, complex karyotype, t(6;9), inv(3), or monosomy 7 confer adverse risk with very poor outcomes and virtually no cure without transplantation. NPM1 mutation without FLT3-ITD is favorable; in-frame bZIP CEBPA mutation is favorable; FLT3-ITD carries negative prognostic impact. Clinical adverse factors include advancing age (the most important clinical factor), poor performance status, high presenting leukocyte count, antecedent hematologic disorder (MDS/MPN) or prolonged preceding cytopenias, and therapy-related (post-chemotherapy) AML. Achievement of complete remission (CR) and MRD-negative status predict longer survival.","pitfalls":["Auer rods are not uniformly present in AML, so their absence does NOT exclude AML; their presence, however, confirms AML over ALL","Diagnosis based on blast percent alone is outdated - cases with defining genetic abnormalities (e.g., t[15;17], t[8;21], inv[16], KMT2A rearrangement, NPM1 mutation) qualify as AML even with <20% blasts; genetic/cytogenetic testing is mandatory for precise diagnosis","APL must be recognized urgently - delay or treating it like ordinary AML risks fatal DIC; high-risk APL (WBC >10,000/uL) requires immediate cytoreduction because ATRA can precipitate life-threatening differentiation syndrome and rapidly rising leukocyte count","Even infirm older patients generally benefit from treatment over supportive care alone - age and comorbidity should not automatically exclude therapy","Even patients with undetectable MRD remain at risk for relapse; without postremission therapy virtually all CR patients eventually relapse, and there is no consensus on the optimal MRD technique or its use to direct therapy","NPM1 prognostic favorability is negated by co-existing FLT3-ITD; prognostic mutations are not mutually exclusive (>80% of patients carry two or more)","Anemia at diagnosis is usually present but not typically severe and is normocytic normochromic - a 'mild' blood count abnormality can still herald AML"],"severityClassification":"Risk stratification per 2022 European LeukemiaNet (ELN) by genetics at diagnosis (APL excluded): Favorable - t(8;21)/RUNX1-RUNX1T1, inv(16) or t(16;16)/CBFB-MYH11, mutated NPM1 without FLT3-ITD, in-frame bZIP-mutated CEBPA. Intermediate - mutated NPM1 with FLT3-ITD, wild-type NPM1 with FLT3-ITD (without adverse lesions), t(9;11)/MLLT3-KMT2A, and abnormalities not classified as favorable or adverse. Adverse - t(6;9)/DEK-NUP214, KMT2A rearrangements, t(9;22)/BCR-ABL1, inv(3)/t(3;3) MECOM(EVI1), -5/del(5q), -7, -17/abn(17p), complex or monosomal karyotype, mutated ASXL1/BCOR/EZH2/RUNX1/SF3B1/SRSF2/STAG2/U2AF1/ZRSR2, and mutated TP53 (>=10% VAF). Classification (2022 WHO): AML with defining genetic abnormalities versus AML defined by differentiation; the >=20% blast requirement is removed for cases with specific defining genetic aberrations.","references":["Harrison 22e p.825","Harrison 22e p.826","Harrison 22e p.827","Harrison 22e p.828","Harrison 22e p.829","Harrison 22e p.832","Harrison 22e p.833"],"crossLinks":["acute_leukemia","FEBRILE_NEUTROPENIA","dic"]},"acute_rheumatic_fever":{"name":"Acute Rheumatic Fever","system":"Rheumatology / Cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":371,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2854-2858","clinicalPearls":["ARF is a multisystem autoimmune sequela of group A streptococcal infection; almost all manifestations resolve completely, the major exception being cardiac valvular damage (rheumatic heart disease, RHD), which may persist after other features disappear (Harrison 22e p.2854).","Mainly a disease of children aged 5-14 years; initial episodes are rare in persons aged >30 years, whereas recurrent episodes remain relatively common in adolescents and young adults. RHD prevalence peaks between 25 and 40 years (Harrison 22e p.2854).","There is no clear gender association for ARF, but RHD more commonly affects females, sometimes up to twice as often as males (Harrison 22e p.2854).","Latent period of ~3 weeks (range 1-5 weeks) between the precipitating group A streptococcal infection and onset of ARF; the exceptions are chorea and indolent (low-grade) carditis, which may follow prolonged latent periods of up to 6 months (Harrison 22e p.2855).","Most common clinical features are polyarthritis (60-75% of cases) and carditis (50-75%); chorea prevalence varies from <2% to 30% between populations; erythema marginatum and subcutaneous nodules are now rare, found in <5% of cases (Harrison 22e p.2855).","Up to 75% of patients with ARF progress to RHD; the mitral valve is almost always affected, sometimes with the aortic valve. Isolated aortic valve involvement is rare. The characteristic manifestation of carditis in previously unaffected individuals is mitral regurgitation, sometimes with aortic regurgitation (Harrison 22e p.2855).","ARF arthritis is typically a migratory polyarthritis of large joints (knees, ankles, hips, elbows), asymmetric, severe and disabling, and highly responsive to salicylates/NSAIDs (Harrison 22e p.2855-2856).","Sydenham's chorea often occurs in the absence of other manifestations, follows a prolonged latent period, and is found mainly in females; more than 50% of patients presenting with chorea will have carditis, so echocardiography should be part of the workup (Harrison 22e p.2856).","Erythema marginatum is an evanescent serpiginous rash (pink macules clearing centrally) usually on the trunk, sometimes the limbs, but almost never on the face. Subcutaneous nodules are painless, mobile, occur over bony prominences, appear 2-3 weeks after onset, and are commonly associated with carditis (Harrison 22e p.2856).","Revised Jones criteria (2015 AHA): for all populations with evidence of preceding group A strep infection, initial ARF requires 2 major OR 1 major plus 2 minor manifestations; recurrent ARF requires 2 major OR 1 major plus 2 minor OR 3 minor manifestations. Separate, more sensitive criteria apply to moderate/high-risk populations versus low-risk populations (Harrison 22e p.2856-2857).","Major manifestations: carditis (clinical and/or subclinical), arthritis, chorea, erythema marginatum, and subcutaneous nodules; in moderate/high-risk populations arthritis includes monoarthritis and polyarthralgia (Harrison 22e p.2857).","Secondary prevention with long-term penicillin prophylaxis is the mainstay of controlling ARF and RHD, best delivered through a register-based RHD control program; patients should be entered on an ARF registry, educated, and linked to follow-up before discharge (Harrison 22e p.2857-2858)."],"pathophysiology":"ARF results from an autoimmune reaction to group A streptococcal infection, classically of the upper respiratory tract, with increasing evidence for a role of streptococcal skin infection. The most widely accepted theory is molecular mimicry: an immune response to streptococcal antigens (mainly the M protein) cross-reacts with human tissues. Antigen-presenting cells of the innate immune system present streptococcal antigens to T cells, activating humoral and cellular immunity; cross-reactive antibodies bind heart-valve endothelial cells, activating VCAM-1 with recruitment of activated lymphocytes and complement-mediated lysis, releasing peptides (laminin, keratin, tropomyosin) that activate cross-reactive T cells invading the heart, amplifying damage and causing epitope spreading. An alternative hypothesis proposes initial damage from streptococcal invasion of epithelial surfaces with M-protein binding to type IV collagen, rendering it immunogenic but not through molecular mimicry. Susceptibility is inherited (~3-6% of a population susceptible; 44% concordance in monozygotic vs 12% in dizygotic twins; heritability ~60%), with associations to HLA class II alleles, TNF and mannose-binding-lectin polymorphisms, the IGH locus (IGHV4-61*02), and complement factor H (Harrison 22e p.2854-2855).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Always request: electrocardiogram (ECG), echocardiogram, complete blood count (CBC), C-reactive protein (CRP), and streptococcal serology (antistreptolysin O [ASO] and anti-DNase B [ADB]) (Harrison 22e p.2857).","Echocardiography should be performed on all possible cases to aid diagnosis (including detection of subclinical carditis/valvulitis) and to establish baseline carditis severity; auscultation is poorly sensitive and specific for RHD in asymptomatic patients (Harrison 22e p.2855, p.2857).","Because most cases lack a positive throat swab culture or rapid antigen test, serologic evidence of preceding group A strep infection is usually needed (ASO and anti-DNase B titers), ideally with locally derived age-specific reference ranges (Harrison 22e p.2856).","In relevant situations: throat swab, skin sore swab, blood cultures, synovial fluid aspirate (cell count, microscopy, culture, gonococcal PCR), pregnancy test, and creatinine/UEC (since NSAIDs can affect renal function) (Harrison 22e p.2857).","Tests to exclude alternative diagnoses (per clinical presentation/local endemic infections): autoantibodies, double-stranded DNA, anti-CCP antibodies; urine molecular testing for Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis; serology for viral hepatitis, Yersinia, CMV, parvovirus B19, respiratory viruses, Ross River virus, Barmah Forest virus (Harrison 22e p.2857).","Inflammatory markers should be monitored every 1-2 weeks until normalized (usually within 4-6 weeks), and a repeat echocardiogram performed after 1 month to assess for progression of carditis (Harrison 22e p.2858)."],"redFlags":["Severe carditis causing congestive heart failure; treatment of heart failure may be lifesaving (Harrison 22e p.2857).","Myocardial inflammation affecting conduction, causing P-R interval prolongation (first-degree AV block, rarely higher-level block) (Harrison 22e p.2855).","More than 50% of patients presenting with chorea have underlying carditis, mandating echocardiographic workup (Harrison 22e p.2856).","Advanced RHD complications: heart failure, arrhythmia, stroke, and infective endocarditis (Harrison 22e p.2856)."],"prognosis":"Untreated, ARF lasts on average 12 weeks; with treatment, patients are usually discharged within 1-2 weeks. Almost all non-cardiac manifestations resolve completely. Inflammatory markers usually normalize within 4-6 weeks. Up to 75% of patients with ARF progress to RHD, the long-term consequence being valvular damage; people with RHD are often asymptomatic for many years before progression to cardiac failure. There is no treatment proven to alter the likelihood or severity of RHD; apart from heart-failure management, treatment of ARF is symptomatic. RHD is the most common cause of acquired heart disease in children in low- and middle-income countries and a major cause of adult mortality and morbidity, with >40 million people affected worldwide and >300,000 deaths annually (Harrison 22e p.2854, p.2857-2858).","pitfalls":["The preceding group A streptococcal infection is commonly subclinical, so the absence of a reported sore throat or a negative throat swab/rapid antigen test does not exclude ARF; serologic confirmation is usually required (Harrison 22e p.2855-2856).","Diagnosis of ARF is often missed, particularly in resource-poor settings, so children, adolescents, and young adults may have undiagnosed RHD; echocardiographic screening can detect these cases (Harrison 22e p.2855).","Early commencement of anti-inflammatory medication before the typical migratory pattern is established may produce an atypical presenting picture such as aseptic monoarthritis (Harrison 22e p.2856).","Recurrence of fever, joint manifestations, and elevated acute-phase reactants up to 3 weeks after stopping anti-inflammatory medication does NOT indicate a true ARF recurrence and is managed by briefly recommencing anti-inflammatory agents (Harrison 22e p.2857).","Glucocorticoid use in ARF carditis remains controversial; two meta-analyses failed to demonstrate benefit over placebo or salicylates, though included studies were >40 years old and used outdated medications (Harrison 22e p.2857-2858).","Salicylates/NSAIDs are of no proven value in the treatment of carditis or chorea (they treat arthritis, arthralgia, and fever) (Harrison 22e p.2857).","Medications to control choreiform movements do NOT alter the duration or outcome of chorea; IVIg shows no benefit on short- or long-term carditis outcome and is not recommended except in severe chorea refractory to other treatments (Harrison 22e p.2858)."],"severityClassification":"RHD detected by echocardiography is staged (2023 World Heart Federation guidelines) into: Stage A (minimal echocardiographic criteria for RHD, individuals aged <=20 years, mild MR or AR without morphologic features); Stage B (mild RHD, mild valvular regurgitation plus morphologic feature(s)); Stage C (advanced RHD at risk of clinical complications - moderate/severe MR or AR, any MS or AS, pulmonary hypertension, decreased LV systolic function); Stage D (advanced RHD with established clinical complications such as cardiac surgery, heart failure, arrhythmia, stroke, infective endocarditis). The 2015 revised Jones criteria additionally separate low-risk populations (ARF incidence <=2 per 100,000 school-age children, or all-age RHD prevalence <=1 per 1000) from moderate/high-risk populations, applying more sensitive criteria to the latter (Harrison 22e p.2856-2857).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2854-2858"],"crossLinks":["PHARYNGITIS"]},"adrenal_disorders":{"name":"Adrenal Cortical Disorders","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":398,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3050-3072","clinicalPearls":["Cushing's: most clinical features (obesity, diabetes, diastolic hypertension, hirsutism, depression) are nonspecific; the more discriminatory signs are thinning/fragile skin with easy bruising, broad (>1 cm) purplish striae, and proximal myopathy (difficulty rising from a chair or climbing stairs). Suspect when several features cluster, or when a feature appears at an unusual age (e.g., osteoporosis in a young patient). (p.3056-3057)","Screen for endogenous Cushing's only after excluding exogenous (iatrogenic) glucocorticoids in any form. First-line screening tests: increased 24-h urinary free cortisol (three collections), failure to suppress morning cortisol after a 1 mg overnight dexamethasone test, and loss of diurnal rhythm shown by raised late-night (midnight) serum/salivary cortisol. Diagnosis is considered established when several tests are consistently abnormal. (p.3057)","Establish the diagnosis of Cushing's beyond doubt BEFORE doing any differential-diagnosis or imaging tests; most management errors (unnecessary imaging or surgery) come from not following this sequence, because pituitary and adrenal nodules are common incidental findings in the general population. (p.3057)","Cushing's differential diagnosis order: measure plasma ACTH first. Suppressed ACTH = ACTH-independent (adrenal) excess -> unenhanced adrenal CT. Normal/high ACTH = ACTH-dependent -> pituitary MRI plus dynamic tests (high-dose dexamethasone, CRH or desmopressin); pituitary MRI can be normal in up to 40% because microadenomas are small. (p.3057-3058)","Inferior petrosal sinus sampling (central:peripheral ACTH ratio >2 at baseline, >3 after CRH) distinguishes Cushing's disease from ectopic ACTH when dynamic tests are discordant; it cannot be used to lateralize the tumor within the pituitary, and no cortisol-lowering drugs should be used before it. (p.3058)","Primary aldosteronism is far more common than historically appreciated: prevalence 5-12% of hypertensives, higher in hypokalemic or drug-resistant hypertension. Screen only selected hypertensives (drug-resistant/severe hypertension, hypokalemia, adrenal mass, onset <40 years, or family history of primary aldosteronism), not all comers. (p.3060-3061)","Primary aldosteronism screen = aldosterone-renin ratio (ARR); correct hypokalemia first. Stop MR antagonists at least 4-6 weeks before testing; beta-blockers cause false positives and ACE inhibitors/ARBs cause false negatives. A positive ARR is followed by confirmatory testing (e.g., saline infusion test) by an endocrinologist. (p.3061)","After biochemical confirmation of aldosteronism, unenhanced adrenal CT plus adrenal vein sampling distinguish unilateral adenoma (Conn's, surgically curable by unilateral adrenalectomy) from bilateral hyperplasia (treated medically with an MR antagonist). AVS is only needed when surgery is feasible and desired. (p.3061)","Adrenal insufficiency: short cosyntropin (Synacthen) test is the safe, reliable first-line diagnostic test; failure is cortisol <450-500 nmol/L (16-18 microg/dL) at 30-60 min. Then measure plasma ACTH: high ACTH = primary (Addison's), inappropriately low ACTH = secondary/central. Increased plasma renin confirms mineralocorticoid deficiency in primary disease. (p.3068)","Primary adrenal insufficiency loses both glucocorticoid AND mineralocorticoid secretion; secondary (central) disease loses glucocorticoid only because the adrenal stays responsive to the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. Adrenal androgen secretion is lost in both. (p.3067)","Hyperpigmentation (palmar creases, friction/shear areas, sun-exposed skin) from excess POMC/ACTH is the hallmark distinguishing primary adrenal insufficiency; secondary insufficiency instead causes an alabaster-pale skin from ACTH lack. (p.3067)","Hyponatremia is present in ~80% of primary adrenal insufficiency at presentation and hyperkalemia in ~40%. Hyponatremia can also occur in secondary insufficiency via reduced cortisol inhibition of ADH (mild SIADH-like picture). (p.3067)","All patients with an incidentally discovered adrenal mass need a 1 mg dexamethasone suppression test to exclude cortisol excess; add plasma/urine metanephrines if HU >10 (exclude pheochromocytoma), and paired renin/aldosterone plus potassium if hypertensive. Confirm any Cushing's as ACTH-independent by measuring plasma ACTH. (p.3063-3064)","On unenhanced CT, adrenal lesion attenuation is the key discriminator: lipid-rich adenomas are typically <10-20 HU, whereas adrenocortical carcinoma and pheochromocytoma have high attenuation (>20 HU). Hounsfield assessment only applies to unenhanced CT, not post-contrast. (p.3064)","Mild autonomous cortisol secretion (MACS) = abnormal 1 mg dexamethasone test with no overt clinical Cushing's; it still carries excess risk of hypertension, diabetes, dyslipidemia, osteoporosis, cardiovascular events, and mortality. A subset of primary aldosteronism patients have co-existing MACS ('Connshing' syndrome). (p.3061, p.3064)","CYP21A2 (21-hydroxylase) deficiency causes 90-95% of congenital adrenal hyperplasia; it impairs glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid synthesis and drives ACTH-mediated adrenal androgen excess. 17-hydroxyprogesterone is the diagnostic marker steroid. Severity spans classic salt-wasting (neonatal), simple virilizing, to nonclassic (presents in adolescence/adulthood). (p.3071)"],"pathophysiology":"The adrenal cortex produces glucocorticoids (cortisol, zona fasciculata under HPA/ACTH control), mineralocorticoids (aldosterone, zona glomerulosa under renin-angiotensin-aldosterone control), and adrenal androgens. Cushing's syndrome reflects chronic glucocorticoid excess: ~90% of endogenous cases are ACTH-dependent (mostly a pituitary corticotrope adenoma = Cushing's disease, or ectopic ACTH from carcinoid/small-cell lung cancer), ~10% are ACTH-independent adrenal causes (adenoma, carcinoma, or bilateral macronodular/pigmented nodular hyperplasia). Cortisol excess upregulates gluconeogenesis, lipolysis, and protein catabolism, and when 11beta-HSD2 is overwhelmed cortisol activates the mineralocorticoid receptor, producing hypertension, hypokalemia, and edema. Primary mineralocorticoid excess (primary aldosteronism) is autonomous aldosterone production by the zona glomerulosa (bilateral hyperplasia or unilateral aldosterone-producing adenoma/Conn's); MR over-activation causes sodium retention with volume expansion, potassium and hydrogen loss (hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis), and direct cardiac/renal damage. Primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison's) is destruction or dysfunction of the adrenal cortex (most often autoimmune adrenalitis, also TB, adrenoleukodystrophy, hemorrhage, infiltration, CAH), causing combined glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid deficiency; secondary insufficiency is HPA/pituitary-hypothalamic failure (tumor, surgery, irradiation, apoplexy/Sheehan's, or iatrogenic glucocorticoid suppression) with isolated glucocorticoid deficiency. CAH results from inherited steroidogenic enzyme defects (commonly CYP21A2) causing glucocorticoid deficiency, loss of feedback, raised ACTH, and adrenal androgen excess, with mineralocorticoid deficiency or excess depending on the enzymatic block.","additionalDifferentials":["Cushing's disease (ACTH-producing pituitary corticotrope adenoma) vs ectopic ACTH syndrome (bronchial/pancreatic carcinoid, small-cell lung cancer, medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma)","ACTH-independent adrenal Cushing's: cortisol-producing adenoma, adrenocortical carcinoma, primary bilateral macronodular adrenal hyperplasia, primary pigmented nodular adrenal disease (Carney complex), McCune-Albright syndrome","Mineralocorticoid excess differential: bilateral micronodular hyperplasia vs unilateral aldosterone-producing adenoma (Conn's); glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism (GRA/FH-I) and other familial primary aldosteronism; apparent mineralocorticoid excess (HSD11B2 deficiency); Liddle's syndrome (ENaC mutations); deoxycorticosterone excess from CAH (CYP11B1 or CYP17A1 deficiency)","Primary vs secondary (central) adrenal insufficiency, distinguished by plasma ACTH and renin","Pseudo-Cushing states: alcohol-related hypercortisolism, major depression, morbid obesity, and cyclic Cushing's"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculous adrenalitis (still a frequent cause of primary adrenal insufficiency in developing countries)","HIV-associated adrenal involvement (usually subclinical adrenal insufficiency)","Sepsis-associated bilateral adrenal hemorrhage (Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome) causing acute primary adrenal insufficiency","Adrenal crisis presenting like an acute abdomen with fever, abdominal tenderness, nausea and vomiting"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Common population conditions overlapping Cushing's: obesity, type 2 diabetes, diastolic hypertension, hirsutism, depression, osteoporotic vertebral fractures","Pseudo-Cushing states (alcohol, major depression, morbid obesity)","Chronic adrenal insufficiency mistaken for depression or anorexia (nonspecific fatigue, weight loss)","Adrenal crisis mimicking acute abdomen or neurologic disease (stupor/coma)","Polycystic ovary syndrome phenotype mimicked by nonclassic CAH (hirsutism, oligomenorrhea)","Other causes of hypokalemic hypertension: licorice ingestion inhibiting 11beta-HSD2; renovascular/malignant hypertension (secondary hyperaldosteronism)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Cushing's screening: 1 mg overnight dexamethasone suppression test, late-night (midnight) salivary cortisol, 24-h urinary free cortisol (three collections); low-dose dexamethasone test if confirmation needed","Cushing's differential: plasma ACTH; pituitary MRI; high-dose dexamethasone test; CRH or desmopressin stimulation; bilateral inferior petrosal sinus sampling; unenhanced adrenal CT for ACTH-independent disease","Aldosteronism: aldosterone-renin ratio with serum potassium normalized; confirmatory saline infusion / oral sodium loading / fludrocortisone suppression test; unenhanced adrenal CT; adrenal vein sampling; 24-h urinary steroid profile (GC/MS) for familial/mimicking forms","Adrenal insufficiency: short cosyntropin (Synacthen/ACTH) stimulation test; plasma ACTH; plasma renin; adrenal steroid (21-hydroxylase) autoantibodies; insulin tolerance test as alternative; adrenal CT if autoantibody-negative; very-long-chain fatty acids in autoantibody-negative males (exclude X-ALD); pituitary MRI for secondary disease","Incidentaloma: 1 mg-DST in all; plasma/urine metanephrines if HU >10; paired renin and aldosterone plus potassium if hypertensive; 17-OHP, DHEAS, androstenedione (and sex steroids) if ACC suspected; unenhanced CT for size and attenuation (HU); chemical-shift MRI for indeterminate lesions","CAH: measurement of precursor steroids accumulating before the enzymatic block (notably 17-hydroxyprogesterone for 21-hydroxylase deficiency), preferably by mass spectrometry; androstenedione and testosterone for monitoring"],"redFlags":["Adrenal crisis (acute adrenal insufficiency): postural hypotension progressing to hypovolemic shock, often after a prolonged period of nonspecific complaints; may mimic acute abdomen or neurologic decline (stupor/coma); can be triggered by intercurrent illness, surgery/stress, or increased glucocorticoid inactivation (e.g., hyperthyroidism) - treat immediately, do not let testing delay therapy","In suspected adrenal crisis, draw a baseline cortisol, start replacement, and defer formal stimulation testing - tests must never delay treatment","Cushing's hypercoagulable state with risk of deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism","Acute paranoid or depressive psychosis in severe cortisol excess","Severe hypokalemia in aldosteronism causing arrhythmias, rhabdomyolysis, or hypokalemic paralysis; severe alkalosis causing tetany","Classic salt-wasting CAH in male neonates presenting as life-threatening adrenal crisis in the first weeks of life"],"prognosis":"After successful removal of an ACTH- or cortisol-producing tumor the HPA axis remains suppressed and may take months to years (or never) to recover; recovery rates differ by cause - ectopic ACTH best (~80%, median ~7 months), Cushing's disease intermediate (~60%, ~17 months), adrenal Cushing's lowest (~40%, ~30 months). Even after cure, long-term quality of life can remain impaired with increased cardiovascular and osteoporotic-fracture risk depending on duration and degree of cortisol excess. Primary aldosteronism carries increased osteoporosis, type 2 diabetes, and cognitive dysfunction. Adrenal crisis is potentially lethal, with prospective data of 8.3 crises and 0.5 crisis-related deaths per 100 patient-years. MACS is associated with increased mortality. In CAH, fertility may be compromised (anovulation in women, testicular adrenal rest tumors and fibrosis in men).","pitfalls":["Performing imaging or surgery before biochemically confirming Cushing's beyond doubt - pituitary/adrenal nodules are common incidental findings and lead to misdiagnosis","Forgetting to exclude exogenous (iatrogenic) glucocorticoid use as the cause before working up endogenous Cushing's","Dexamethasone test pitfalls: false non-suppression from oral estrogens/contraceptives (raise CBG/total cortisol; retest after 4-6 weeks off estrogen) and rapid dexamethasone inactivation by CYP3A4 inducers (antiepileptics, rifampicin); antibody-based urinary free cortisol assays have specificity problems improved by tandem mass spectrometry","Not normalizing potassium before, or failing to stop MR antagonists (4-6 weeks) before, ARR testing; beta-blockers give false-positive and ACE inhibitors/ARBs false-negative ARR results","Relying on the ARR alone when renin is very low increases false positives; only 10-40% of primary aldosteronism patients are hypokalemic, so normokalemia does not exclude it","Using random serum cortisol to diagnose adrenal insufficiency - baseline levels may be coincidentally low (diurnal rhythm), and secondary insufficiency often shows near-normal baseline cortisol that fails only on stimulation","Stopping exogenous glucocorticoids abruptly can precipitate iatrogenic adrenal insufficiency even though the patient still looks cushingoid","Judging adrenal mass attenuation on post-contrast CT - Hounsfield-unit discrimination of benign vs malignant only applies to unenhanced CT; lipid-poor adenomas (high HU) overlap with ACC and pheochromocytoma","Mistaking testicular adrenal rest tumors (TART) in CAH for testicular neoplasms"],"severityClassification":"Adrenal insufficiency is classified as primary (Addison's, combined glucocorticoid + mineralocorticoid deficiency, high ACTH) vs secondary/central (isolated glucocorticoid deficiency, low/inappropriate ACTH), with acute adrenal crisis as the life-threatening acute form. Endogenous Cushing's is classified as ACTH-dependent (~90%; Cushing's disease vs ectopic ACTH) vs ACTH-independent (~10%; adenoma, carcinoma, hyperplasia). Cortisol excess severity ranges from overt Cushing's to mild autonomous cortisol secretion (MACS). Primary aldosteronism is divided into unilateral (adenoma/Conn's, surgically curable) vs bilateral (hyperplasia, medical therapy). CAH (21-hydroxylase deficiency) is graded by severity into classic salt-wasting, simple virilizing, and nonclassic forms. Adrenocortical carcinoma is staged by the ENSAT/TNM system (Stage I T1 <=5 cm to advanced/metastatic).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3050-3072"],"crossLinks":["adrenal_crisis","pheo"]},"alcohol_liver_disease":{"name":"Alcohol-Associated Liver Disease","system":"Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":353,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2697-2700","clinicalPearls":["ALD is a spectrum: alcohol-associated fatty liver (steatosis) and steatohepatitis progress to fibrosis and cirrhosis; acute alcohol-associated hepatitis is an acute-on-chronic form associated with liver failure and high mortality (p.2697).","Characteristic biochemical signature: AST/ALT ratio >1 (commonly cited as >2:1 for alcoholic liver disease), with serum AST rarely exceeding 300 IU/L; in alcohol-associated hepatitis AST and ALT do not exceed 400 IU/L and the AST/ALT ratio is >1.5 with serum bilirubin >3 mg/dL (p.2699).","Steatosis and steatohepatitis are clinically silent; symptoms typically appear only when alcohol-associated cirrhosis decompensates or alcohol-associated hepatitis develops (p.2699).","Alcohol-associated hepatitis is defined clinically by heavy drinking (>40 g/d women, >50-60 g/d men) for >6 months with <60 days abstinence before symptom onset, presenting with rapid-onset jaundice (bilirubin >3 mg/dL), often with fever, malaise, and tender hepatomegaly (p.2698).","Prolonged alcohol abstinence is the most effective therapy to reduce progression and reverse ALD; steatosis and steatohepatitis are reversible with cessation of alcohol consumption (p.2699).","There are currently NO approved drugs for treatment of alcohol-associated steatosis or steatohepatitis with or without fibrosis (p.2699).","Severity of alcohol-associated hepatitis is stratified by Maddrey discriminant function (MDF) and MELD: severe disease (MDF >=32 or MELD >20) carries high short-term mortality (~30% at 3 months) and may benefit from glucocorticoids; moderate disease (MDF <32 or MELD <=20) still carries ~20% 1-year mortality (p.2700).","Lille score >=0.45 after 7 days of glucocorticoid treatment identifies non-responders unlikely to benefit from continued therapy; glucocorticoids should be stopped and early liver transplantation considered (p.2700).","Liver transplantation for alcohol-associated decompensated cirrhosis or HCC is the leading indication for liver transplantation in the United States (p.2699-2700).","In confirmed cirrhosis, HCC surveillance with liver ultrasonography and serum alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) every 6 months and upper GI endoscopy to screen for varices are recommended (p.2699).","Only 10-20% of individuals with alcohol-induced hepatic steatosis develop progressive liver disease and cirrhosis; women develop ALD at a lower daily alcohol intake (p.2698)."],"pathophysiology":"Ethanol is rapidly absorbed in the upper GI tract and metabolized predominantly in the liver, reaching it via the portal vein. Most ethanol is oxidized by alcohol dehydrogenase 1 (ADH1) to acetaldehyde in hepatocytes; chronic consumption also induces CYP2E1, which similarly generates acetaldehyde. Beyond direct acetaldehyde toxicity, ethanol metabolism generates reactive oxygen species causing hepatocyte injury through lipid peroxidation and DNA damage. Acetaldehyde is then oxidized to acetate by acetaldehyde dehydrogenase (ALDH); inherited ALDH2 deficiency (common in Asian populations) causes acetaldehyde accumulation with nausea and cutaneous flushing. Steatosis arises because acetate becomes acetyl-CoA feeding fatty acid/triglyceride synthesis, alcohol upregulates lipogenic genes (partly epigenetically) while suppressing fatty acid transport/oxidation, and the increased NADH/NAD+ ratio reduces mitochondrial beta-oxidation; alcohol also mobilizes fatty acids from adipose and intestine into the liver. A subset develop gut dysbiosis and increased intestinal permeability, allowing microbial components (e.g., bacterial LPS) to reach the liver via the portal vein, driving inflammation, hepatocyte death, and fibrotic pathway activation; sustained alcohol use progresses to cirrhosis with portal hypertension. Impaired liver regeneration may contribute to acute alcohol-associated hepatitis arising in underlying cirrhosis (p.2698).","additionalDifferentials":["Viral hepatitis (e.g., hepatitis B or C)","Wilson's disease","Severe autoimmune liver disease","Drug-induced liver injury (DILI), including amiodarone-related pseudoalcoholic injury","Metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD/NAFLD) and MetALD","Hemochromatosis","Ischemic hepatitis","Biliary obstruction","Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC)"],"infectionMimics":["Viral hepatitis (hepatitis B or C) producing aminotransferase elevation that can be confused with ALD"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["MASLD/NAFLD (key distinction: ALD typically shows AST/ALT >2:1, whereas NAFLD usually has ALT >= AST)","Amiodarone hepatotoxicity producing pseudoalcoholic liver injury (steatosis to alcoholic-hepatitis-like neutrophilic infiltration with Mallory-Denk bodies)","Wilson's disease","Autoimmune hepatitis","Hemochromatosis","Ischemic hepatitis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Liver function tests with AST, ALT (AST/ALT ratio), GGT, bilirubin, INR, albumin, platelet count","AUDIT (Alcohol Use Disorders Inventory Test) to identify alcohol use disorder","Abdominal ultrasound, MRI, or CT to detect steatosis, nodularity, and signs of portal hypertension","Controlled attenuation parameter (CAP) or MR proton density fat fraction (MR-PDFF) for noninvasive hepatic fat quantification","Transient elastography / liver stiffness measurement (e.g., FibroScan) for fibrosis staging","Fib-4 score (age, AST, ALT, platelets) to exclude advanced fibrosis/cirrhosis (threshold <3.25)","Transjugular liver biopsy when confounding factors are present (to confirm alcohol-associated hepatitis)","Imaging to exclude biliary obstruction and HCC; AFP and ultrasonography for HCC surveillance in cirrhosis","Infection workup: chest x-ray plus blood, urine, and ascites cultures in alcohol-associated hepatitis"],"redFlags":["Severe alcohol-associated hepatitis (MDF >=32 or MELD >20) - high short-term mortality (~30% at 3 months)","Rapid-onset jaundice with serum bilirubin >3 mg/dL, fever, and tender hepatomegaly","Clinical signs of hepatic decompensation: ascites, variceal bleeding, hepatic encephalopathy","Bacterial infection (occurs in 12-26% of severe alcohol-associated hepatitis at admission)","Systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS)","Acute kidney injury / hepatorenal syndrome","Lille score >=0.45 at day 7 (glucocorticoid non-response) - high 30-day mortality","Multiorgan failure and shock"],"prognosis":"Steatosis and steatohepatitis are reversible with abstinence, but only 10-20% of those with steatosis progress to cirrhosis. In alcohol-associated hepatitis, short-term mortality is predicted by MDF, MELD, or ABIC scores: moderate disease (MDF <32 or MELD <=20) carries ~20% 1-year mortality, whereas severe disease (MDF >=32 or MELD >20) carries ~30% 3-month mortality. Glucocorticoids reduce risk of death in severe disease within 28 days but not over the following 6 months. While short-term prognosis depends on disease severity at presentation, long-term prognosis (>1 year) largely depends on alcohol abstinence and underlying cirrhosis. Non-responders to medical therapy have high 30-day mortality (p.2700).","pitfalls":["Diagnosis of ALD requires exclusion of other liver diseases in heavy drinkers; do not attribute liver disease to alcohol without ruling out viral hepatitis, Wilson's disease, autoimmune liver disease, and DILI.","Atypical laboratory values (AST <50 IU/L or >400 IU/L, or AST/ALT ratio <1.5) argue against alcohol-associated hepatitis and warrant transjugular liver biopsy - 10-20% of clinically diagnosed cases have a different disease on biopsy.","Do not give glucocorticoids when contraindications are present: uncontrolled infection/sepsis, AKI/hepatorenal syndrome, uncontrolled upper GI bleeding, concomitant viral hepatitis/HCC/pancreatitis/DILI/active TB/HIV, multiorgan failure, or shock; glucocorticoids may be used only once infection, sepsis, and GI bleeding are adequately controlled.","Liver biopsy is rarely needed to diagnose simple steatosis or steatohepatitis - reserve invasive biopsy for confounding/atypical cases.","Distinguish ALD from NAFLD/MASLD by the AST/ALT pattern (ALD favors AST/ALT >2:1)."],"severityClassification":"Alcohol-associated hepatitis severity: Moderate AH = MDF <32 or MELD <=20 (~20% 1-year mortality); Severe AH = Maddrey discriminant function (MDF) >=32 or MELD >20 (~30% 3-month mortality), candidates for glucocorticoids if no contraindication. MDF = 4.6 x (prothrombin time prolongation above control in seconds) + serum bilirubin (mg/dL). Day-7 Lille score >=0.45 defines glucocorticoid non-response. Cirrhosis prognosis assessed by Child-Pugh-Turcotte (CPT) and MELD (or sodium-MELD). Fibrosis staging by liver stiffness: <6 kPa normal, >8 kPa indicates >=F3 advanced fibrosis, >12.5 kPa indicates F4 cirrhosis.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2697-2700"],"crossLinks":["decomp_cirrhosis","hepatic_enceph","toxic_hepatitis","chronic_hepatitis"]},"alzheimers_disease":{"name":"Alzheimer's Disease","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":442,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3475-3483","clinicalPearls":["Most common cause of dementia, contributing an estimated 60-70% of all cases; in the U.S. ~10% of people over 65 have AD (3% at age 65-74, 17% at 75-84, 32% at 85+) (Harrison 22e p.3475, p.3478).","Typical presentation is insidious loss of episodic memory followed by slowly progressive multidomain dementia, with cognitive change progressing from memory to executive, language, and visuospatial deficits (Harrison 22e p.3475-3476).","About 20% of patients present with nonmemory complaints; atypical variants include posterior cortical atrophy (visual processing), logopenic progressive aphasia, corticobasal (akinetic-rigid-dystonic) syndrome, and a dysexecutive/behavioral (frontal) variant (Harrison 22e p.3475-3476).","Depression, social withdrawal, and anxiety may occur early and represent a prodrome before cognitive symptoms are apparent (Harrison 22e p.3476).","~50% of patients with mild cognitive impairment (MCI), roughly 12% per year, progress to dementia over 4 years; the MCI construct is increasingly replaced by 'early symptomatic AD' (Harrison 22e p.3476).","Preclinical biomarker changes may precede clinical symptoms by 20 years or more, creating a window for early treatment and prevention (Harrison 22e p.3476).","Core 1 biomarkers (amyloid PET, CSF/plasma Abeta and p-Tau) are considered necessary and sufficient for biological diagnosis of AD under the revised Alzheimer's Association criteria; tau PET is a core 2 biomarker used for staging (Harrison 22e p.3477).","A negative amyloid PET scan in a patient with dementia makes an AD diagnosis unlikely; a positive scan can establish eligibility for amyloid-lowering therapy (Harrison 22e p.3477).","Decreases in the CSF Abeta42/Abeta40 ratio and increases in p-Tau181 or p-Tau217 correlate with AD pathology; plasma biomarkers show high concordance with amyloid PET and are entering clinical use (Harrison 22e p.3477).","ApoE epsilon4 is the most important genetic risk factor for sporadic AD: one allele raises risk ~2-3 fold (higher in women), two alleles raise it ~10-15 fold; each allele shifts onset ~10 years earlier (Harrison 22e p.3475, p.3479-3480).","Lecanemab (anti-Abeta protofibril) and donanemab (anti-pyroglutamate Abeta) are FDA-approved disease-modifying antiamyloid antibodies for early-stage AD, showing ~22-27% slowing of clinical decline; restricted to MCI/mild dementia with biomarker-confirmed Abeta pathology (Harrison 22e p.3480-3481).","Antiamyloid antibodies carry risk of amyloid-related imaging abnormalities (ARIA-E edema, ARIA-H microhemorrhage/siderosis); risk rises with each ApoE epsilon4 allele and is highest in epsilon4 homozygotes, prompting an FDA box warning (Harrison 22e p.3481).","Treatment with lecanemab or donanemab should be considered a contraindication to peripheral thrombolysis, as catastrophic multifocal hemorrhages and death have been reported when antiamyloid-treated patients received thrombolytics for stroke (Harrison 22e p.3482).","Medications with strong anticholinergic effects (e.g., diphenhydramine sleep aids, oxybutynin) should be vigilantly avoided in AD (Harrison 22e p.3482).","Typical symptomatic duration is 8-10 years (range 1-25 years); death often results from malnutrition, secondary infections, pulmonary emboli, heart disease, or, most commonly, aspiration (Harrison 22e p.3476)."],"pathophysiology":"AD is characterized by extracellular neuritic plaques containing amyloid beta (Abeta) and intracellular neurofibrillary tangles (NFTs) of hyperphosphorylated tau, with Abeta also accumulating in cortical and leptomeningeal blood vessel walls (amyloid angiopathy). Abeta is a 39-42 amino acid peptide cleaved from amyloid precursor protein (APP) by beta and gamma secretases; excess production of Abeta42 is a key initiator of cellular damage, and soluble oligomers are thought to be the early toxic species before fibril/plaque formation. Hyperphosphorylated tau can no longer stabilize microtubules and redistributes from axon to cytoplasm and dendrites, with prion-like templating proposed to drive tau spread. Earliest and most severe degeneration is in the medial temporal lobe (entorhinal/perirhinal cortex, hippocampus), inferolateral temporal cortex, and nucleus basalis of Meynert; atrophy spreads to temporoparietal and frontal cortices. Biochemically there is depletion of acetylcholine (degeneration of cholinergic nucleus basalis of Meynert neurons) plus noradrenergic and serotonergic loss from locus coeruleus and dorsal raphe. Genetic contributors include autosomal dominant mutations in APP (chromosome 21), PSEN-1 (chromosome 14, most common cause of early-onset familial AD), and PSEN-2 (chromosome 1), plus the ApoE epsilon4 risk allele (chromosome 19) and >40 minor GWAS risk variants in innate immunity, lipid metabolism, and synaptic pathways (e.g., CLU, PICALM, CR1, TREM2). (Harrison 22e p.3475, p.3478-3480)","additionalDifferentials":["Vascular dementia (early prominent gait disturbance with only mild memory loss) (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Dementia with Lewy bodies (early parkinsonism with fluctuating alertness, visual hallucinations, delusional misidentification) (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Parkinson's disease / Parkinson's disease dementia (resting tremor, stooped posture, bradykinesia, masked facies) (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Frontotemporal dementia (prominent behavioral changes with intact navigation and focal anterior-predominant atrophy) (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease / prion disease (rapid progression over weeks-months with rigidity and myoclonus) (Harrison 22e p.3476, p.3482)","Limbic-predominant age-related TDP-43 encephalopathy (LATE) and primary age-related tauopathy (PART) - can mimic AD clinically, especially in biomarker-negative older patients with progressive amnestic dementia (Harrison 22e p.3476, p.3477)","Huntington's disease and rare hereditary ataxias when family history of dementia is present (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Primary or metastatic brain neoplasm (early focal seizure) (Harrison 22e p.3476)"],"infectionMimics":["Viral or bacterial encephalitides (listed as causes of rapidly progressive dementia in the CJD differential) (Harrison 22e p.3483)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Vitamin B12 deficiency (loss of joint position and vibration sensibility with Babinski signs; especially with autoimmune disease, small bowel resection/irradiation, or veganism) (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Normal-pressure hydrocephalus (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Depression-related cognitive impairment (depressive pseudodementia) (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Chronic drug intoxication (history of treatment for insomnia, anxiety, psychiatric disturbance, or epilepsy) (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Thyroid and other reversible/metabolic causes of dementia to be excluded per Tables 31-1, 31-3, 31-4 (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Hashimoto's encephalopathy, CNS vasculitis, CNS lymphoma, paraneoplastic/autoimmune syndromes (rapidly progressive dementia differential) (Harrison 22e p.3483)"],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI/CT structural imaging - mainly to exclude other disorders; AD shows posterior-predominant cortical and medial temporal (hippocampal) atrophy and may be normal early (Harrison 22e p.3476)","FDG-PET - shows hypometabolism in posterior temporal-parietal cortex (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Amyloid PET ([11C]PIB, [18F]florbetapir, [18F]florbetaben, [18F]flutemetamol) - confirms neuritic/diffuse Abeta plaques (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Tau PET ([18F]flortaucipir and research tracers) - estimates density/distribution of neurofibrillary tangles (Harrison 22e p.3476-3477)","CSF biomarkers - decreased Abeta42 and Abeta42/Abeta40 ratio, increased p-Tau181/p-Tau217 (Harrison 22e p.3477)","Plasma biomarkers - decreased Abeta42/Abeta40 ratio, increased p-Tau181/p-Tau217/p-Tau231 (Harrison 22e p.3477)","EEG - usually normal or nonspecific slowing; prolonged EEG to seek intermittent nonconvulsive seizures (Harrison 22e p.3476)","ApoE genotyping - recommended before initiating anti-Abeta antibody therapy to inform ARIA risk (Harrison 22e p.3480-3481)","Systemic laboratory tests to exclude reversible dementia mimics (Harrison 22e p.3476)"],"redFlags":["Rapid progression over weeks to months with rigidity and myoclonus suggests CJD rather than AD (Harrison 22e p.3476, p.3482)","Early prominent gait disturbance with mild memory loss suggests vascular dementia or normal-pressure hydrocephalus (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Early focal seizure suggests a metastatic or primary brain neoplasm (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Loss of joint position/vibration sense with Babinski signs suggests vitamin B12 deficiency (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Stroke-like symptoms in a patient on anti-Abeta monoclonal antibody require immediate MRI to differentiate acute stroke from ARIA before any thrombolysis (Harrison 22e p.3482)","Seizures (focal or generalized) can herald AD and occur even before dementia onset, especially in younger patients and autosomal dominant mutation carriers (Harrison 22e p.3476)"],"prognosis":"Typical duration of symptomatic AD is 8-10 years, with a course ranging from 1 to 25 years. Some patients decline steadily while others have prolonged plateaus. Late stages bring rigidity, mutism, incontinence, and a bedridden state with dependence for eating, dressing, and toileting; hyperactive tendon reflexes and myoclonic jerks may occur. Death most commonly results from aspiration, and also from malnutrition, secondary infections, pulmonary emboli, or heart disease. Approved disease-modifying antiamyloid antibodies slow but do not halt clinical decline (~22-27% slowing over the trial periods). (Harrison 22e p.3476, p.3480-3481)","pitfalls":["Failing to exclude reversible/treatable mimics early in the course - vitamin B12 deficiency, thyroid disease, normal-pressure hydrocephalus, and depression-related cognitive impairment should be excluded before attributing dementia to AD (Harrison 22e p.3476).","A clinical diagnosis of AD is confirmed at autopsy only ~70-80% of the time; misdiagnoses are usually LATE (with or without hippocampal sclerosis), PART, Lewy body disease, vascular pathology, or FTLD (Harrison 22e p.3476).","Amyloid PET positivity is not specific: ~25% of cognitively unimpaired older adults have positive scans, and a positive scan can reflect comorbid (incidental) AD pathology rather than the primary cause of impairment (Harrison 22e p.3476).","A completely negative tau PET does not rule out early neurofibrillary pathology (Braak stages I-III) (Harrison 22e p.3477).","Increased total tau in CSF is nonspecific - seen in AD but also other neurodegeneration (Harrison 22e p.3477).","Plasma Abeta and p-Tau concentrations can be altered by comorbidities (reduced creatinine clearance, elevated BMI), causing false-positive or false-negative results near the threshold (Harrison 22e p.3477).","ApoE epsilon4 is neither necessary nor sufficient to cause AD - many AD patients lack epsilon4 and many epsilon4 carriers never develop dementia (Harrison 22e p.3479-3480).","Estrogen, statins, vitamin E, ginkgo biloba, and NSAIDs appeared protective in observational studies but did NOT show efficacy in randomized controlled trials; consumer 'memory-enhancing' supplements lack evidence (Harrison 22e p.3478, p.3482).","Antipsychotics carry an FDA black box warning in elderly dementia patients (increased risk of death) and should be used with caution for neuropsychiatric symptoms (Harrison 22e p.3482)."],"severityClassification":"Clinical staging is performed independent of biomarkers, based on functional impairment along a continuum: preclinical AD (biomarker evidence of amyloid pathology without symptoms) -> subjective cognitive decline -> mild cognitive impairment / early symptomatic AD (functionally compensated) -> dementia stages (early/middle/late/end-stage). Biological staging under the revised Alzheimer's Association criteria uses core 1 biomarkers (amyloid PET, CSF/plasma Abeta and p-Tau) for diagnosis and tau PET (core 2) for biomarker-based disease staging. (Harrison 22e p.3476-3478)","references":["Harrison 22e p.3475-3483","Jack CR et al: Revised criteria for the diagnosis and staging of Alzheimer's disease. Nat Med 30:2121, 2024","Van Dyck CH et al: Lecanemab in early Alzheimer's disease. N Engl J Med 388:9, 2023","Sims JR et al: Donanemab in early symptomatic Alzheimer disease: The TRAILBLAZER-ALZ 2 randomized clinical trial. JAMA 330:512, 2023","Belloy ME et al: A quarter century of APOE and Alzheimer's disease. Neuron 101:820, 2019","Andrews SJ et al: Interpretation of risk loci from genome-wide association studies of Alzheimer's disease. Lancet Neurol 19:326, 2020"],"crossLinks":[]},"amoebiasis":{"name":"Amoebiasis (Intestinal)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":230,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1753","clinicalPearls":["Amebiasis is infection with Entamoeba histolytica, an intestinal protozoan; ~90% of infections are asymptomatic cyst passage and only ~10% are invasive (p.1753).","Symptomatic amebic colitis develops 2-6 weeks after ingestion of infectious cysts, with gradual-onset lower abdominal pain and mild diarrhea progressing to malaise, weight loss, and dysentery (p.1755).","In full-blown dysentery patients may pass 10-12 stools per day consisting mainly of blood and mucus with little fecal material; virtually all have heme-positive stools (p.1755).","In contrast to bacterial diarrhea, fewer than 40% of patients with amebic dysentery are febrile and stools show a paucity of neutrophils (p.1755).","Cecal involvement may mimic acute appendicitis (p.1755).","Definitive diagnosis of amebic colitis requires demonstration of hematophagous (erythrocyte-ingesting) trophozoites of E. histolytica; this is the only way microscopy can identify the species (p.1756).","Because trophozoites are rapidly killed by water, drying, or barium, at least three fresh stool specimens should be examined; wet mounts, iodine-stained concentrates, and trichrome-stained preparations confirm diagnosis in 75-95% of cases (p.1756).","Stool antigen detection (Gal/GalNAc lectin EIA, sensitivity 87%, specificity >90%) and multiplex PCR (sensitivity/specificity approaching 100%) are more sensitive, specific, and rapid than microscopy (p.1757).","Serology (ELISA) is positive in >90% of cases of colitis, ameboma, or liver abscess; results usually revert to negative within 6-12 months, so positivity with a compatible syndrome suggests active disease (p.1756).","E. histolytica infection is widespread in the Indian subcontinent, Africa, parts of East Asia, and Central/South America, often affecting children under 5 in areas of poor sanitation; in developed countries returning travelers, immigrants, MSM, military personnel, and institutional inmates are the main at-risk groups (p.1753).","Invasive amebiasis does not elicit eosinophilia (p.1757)."],"pathophysiology":"E. histolytica is acquired by ingesting viable cysts from fecally contaminated water, food, or hands (food-borne exposure being most common). Motile trophozoites excyst in the small intestine; in most patients they remain as harmless commensals in the large bowel and re-encyst, shedding infectious cysts in stool. In some patients trophozoites invade the colonic mucosa (causing colitis) or enter the portal bloodstream (causing distant abscesses, most often hepatic). Trophozoites attach to colonic mucus and epithelium via the Gal/GalNAc adherence lectin and release glycosidases, proteases, and cysteine proteinases that degrade mucus, collagen, elastin, immunoglobulins, and complement anaphylatoxins; mucosal damage follows via contact-dependent cytotoxicity (apoptosis, trogocytosis, lysis of inflammatory and epithelial cells) and contact-independent mechanisms (inflammatory mediators such as prostaglandin E2). Resistance to reactive oxygen/nitrogen species and to complement-mediated lysis are virulence factors. The earliest lesions are mucosal microulcerations of the cecum, sigmoid, or rectum; submucosal extension beneath viable surface mucosa produces the classic 'flask-shaped' ulcer with trophozoites at the margins. Human infection is notable for a paucity of inflammatory cells (in part because trophozoites kill neutrophils) (p.1753-1755).","additionalDifferentials":["Bacterial dysentery / inflammatory diarrhea (Campylobacter, enteroinvasive E. coli, Shigella, Salmonella, Vibrio)","Inflammatory bowel disease (ulcerative colitis, Crohn disease)","Carcinoma of the colon (especially when an ameboma mass is present)","Acute appendicitis (with cecal involvement)"],"infectionMimics":["Shigellosis (bacillary dysentery)","Campylobacter colitis","Enteroinvasive Escherichia coli infection","Salmonella enterocolitis","Vibrio enteritis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Inflammatory bowel disease (ulcerative colitis / Crohn disease)","Colonic carcinoma (mimicked by ameboma on barium studies)","Acute appendicitis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Examination of at least three fresh stool specimens (wet mount, iodine-stained concentrate, trichrome-stained preparation) for cysts/trophozoites","Stool test for occult blood (heme) and microscopy for neutrophils","Stool antigen detection assay (monoclonal anti-Gal/GalNAc lectin enzyme immunoassay)","Stool multiplex PCR / nucleic acid amplification for GI protozoa","Serology (ELISA / indirect hemagglutination) for invasive amebiasis","Sigmoidoscopy with biopsy of the edge of ulcers if stool studies negative (avoid in fulminant colitis)","Biopsy of a colonic mass to confirm ameboma and exclude carcinoma","Stool culture for bacterial pathogens to exclude bacterial dysentery"],"redFlags":["Fulminant intestinal infection with severe abdominal pain, high fever, and profuse diarrhea (rare; predominantly in children)","Toxic megacolon with severe bowel dilation and intramural air","Full-thickness necrosis and bowel perforation","Patients receiving glucocorticoids are at risk for severe amebiasis"],"prognosis":"Outcome relates to the clinical syndrome: about 90% of infections are asymptomatic and self-limited, and uncomplicated amebic colitis responds well to therapy with ulcers characteristically healing with little or no scarring. Fulminant colitis, toxic megacolon, and perforation are rare but carry a poor prognosis. Treated colitis generally resolves, but severe disease (notably in those given glucocorticoids or in children with fulminant infection) markedly worsens outcome (p.1755-1756).","pitfalls":["E. histolytica cannot be distinguished microscopically from the non-pathogenic commensal E. dispar (nor from E. moshkovskii or E. bangladeshi); most asymptomatic carriers actually harbor E. dispar, which is incapable of invasive disease and does not warrant treatment, so species-specific antigen detection or PCR is needed (p.1754, p.1756).","Cysts must also be differentiated from non-pathogenic Entamoeba hartmanni, Entamoeba coli, and Endolimax nana, which do not cause disease (p.1756).","Amebiasis must be excluded before starting glucocorticoid therapy for suspected inflammatory bowel disease; unsuspected amebiasis treated with steroids can precipitate severe colitis or toxic megacolon (p.1755-1757).","An ameboma can be mistaken for cancer on barium studies; positive serology or biopsy can prevent unnecessary surgery (p.1755).","Sigmoidoscopy/biopsy and barium studies are dangerous during fulminant or acute colitis because of the risk of perforation (p.1756-1757).","Trophozoites are rapidly killed by water, drying, and barium and may be confused with neutrophils, so examination must use multiple fresh specimens with experienced microscopy (p.1756)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison describes a clinical spectrum rather than a formal grading scheme: (1) asymptomatic cyst passage (~90% of infections); (2) symptomatic amebic colitis/dysentery with bloody mucoid stools; and (3) rare fulminant intestinal infection (severe pain, high fever, profuse diarrhea, predominantly in children) that may progress to toxic megacolon, full-thickness necrosis, or perforation (p.1753, p.1755).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1753","Harrison 22e p.1754","Harrison 22e p.1755","Harrison 22e p.1756","Harrison 22e p.1757"],"crossLinks":["AMOEBIC_LIVER_ABSCESS"]},"amyotrophic_lateral_sclerosis":{"name":"Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS)","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":448,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3519-3524","clinicalPearls":["ALS is the most common progressive motor neuron disease and ultimately causes progressive loss of BOTH upper (corticospinal) and lower motor neurons; in the absence of clear involvement of both motor neuron types, the diagnosis of ALS is questionable (p.3519-3520).","Lower motor neuron features: insidious asymmetric weakness usually first distal in one limb, progressive wasting/atrophy, and fasciculations (spontaneous twitching of motor units); in the hands a preponderance of extensor over flexor weakness is common (p.3521).","Upper (corticospinal) motor neuron features: hyperactive muscle-stretch reflexes (tendon jerks), spastic resistance to passive movement, and muscle stiffness often out of proportion to weakness (p.3521).","Bulbar onset presents with difficulty chewing, swallowing, and moving the face and tongue; corticobulbar degeneration causes dysarthria and pseudobulbar affect (involuntary excess weeping or laughing) (p.3521).","World Federation of Neurology criteria: simultaneous upper AND lower motor neuron involvement with progressive weakness and exclusion of all alternative diagnoses; 'definite' when 3-4 regions involved (bulbar, cervical, thoracic, lumbosacral), 'probable' when 2, 'possible' when 1 (p.3521).","Elevated CSF/serum neurofilament light chains (Nfl) or phosphorylated neurofilament heavy chains are markers of neurodegeneration in most cases; higher levels correlate with more aggressive disease and more rapid progression (p.3521).","About 10% of cases are inherited as an autosomal dominant trait (familial ALS); FALS is clinically indistinguishable from sporadic ALS apart from its inheritance. C9orf72 is the most common genetic cause (~45-50% of FALS), followed by SOD1 (~20%) (p.3521).","ALS may arise concurrently with frontotemporal dementia (particularly in familial cases), with early behavioral abnormalities reflecting frontal lobe dysfunction (p.3520-3521)."],"pathophysiology":"Motor neuron degenerative disorder marked by death of lower motor neurons (anterior horn cells and their brainstem bulbar homologues) and upper/corticospinal motor neurons (layer-five motor cortex neurons descending via the pyramidal tract). Affected neurons shrink and accumulate lipofuscin; the motor neuron cytoskeleton is affected early, with proximal axonal 'spheroids' composed of neurofilament accumulations. Both sporadic and familial ALS show ubiquitin-positive aggregates frequently associated with the protein TDP43, plus astroglial/microglial proliferation (neuroinflammation). Loss of cortical motor neurons thins the corticospinal tracts; lateral-column fiber loss and fibrillary gliosis impart firmness (lateral sclerosis), while muscle denervation and atrophy give rise to the term amyotrophy. Neuronal cell death is strikingly selective: the sensory apparatus, cerebellar coordination structures, ocular-motility neurons, and the sacral parasympathetic sphincter neurons (nucleus of Onuf) are characteristically spared. Familial ALS most commonly involves mutations in C9orf72, SOD1, TDP43, and FUS/TLS (p.3519-3521).","additionalDifferentials":["Cervical spondylosis / compression of the cervical spinal cord or cervicomedullary junction (tumors, foramen magnum lesions, osteophytes)","Multifocal motor neuropathy with conduction block (MMCB)","Diffuse lower motor axonal neuropathy associated with hematopoietic disorders (lymphoma, multiple myeloma) with serum M-component","X-linked spinobulbar muscular atrophy (Kennedy's disease)","Adult Tay-Sachs disease (hexosaminidase A deficiency)","Spinal muscular atrophy","Primary lateral sclerosis (upper motor neuron only)","Progressive muscular atrophy (lower motor neuron only)"],"infectionMimics":["Poliomyelitis","Herpes zoster","Coxsackie virus","West Nile virus","Retroviral myelopathy (HTLV-1)","Lyme disease","Tetanus"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cervical spondylotic myelopathy / structural compressive lesions (parasagittal or foramen magnum tumors, Chiari malformation/syrinx, spinal AVM)","Multifocal motor neuropathy with conduction block (treatable - responds to IVIG or chemotherapy)","Plasma cell dyscrasias and motor neuropathy with paraproteinemia or cancer (paraneoplastic)","Metabolic: hyperparathyroidism, hyperthyroidism, hypoglycemia; deficiency of folate, vitamin B12, vitamin E, copper or zinc","Heavy metal / toxin intoxication (lead, aluminum)","Kennedy's disease (spinobulbar muscular atrophy)"],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of head including foramen magnum and cervical spine to exclude compressive structural lesions","Electrophysiologic testing (EMG/nerve conduction) to demonstrate denervation and to detect motor conduction block (suggests MMCB rather than ALS)","CSF examination and culture","Serum/CSF neurofilament light chains (Nfl) - prognostic marker of disease aggressiveness","Complete blood count, sedimentation rate, total protein, anti-GM1 antibodies (should be obtained in all cases)","Anti-Hu antibody (paraneoplastic), serum protein for M-component, bone marrow biopsy if indicated","Fasting blood sugar, calcium, thyroid function, vitamin B12/E/folate, serum zinc and copper","WBC DNA for mutational analysis (C9orf72, SOD1, TDP43, FUS/TLS) in suspected familial cases","Contrast myelography to visualize the cervical cord where doubt exists"],"redFlags":["Respiratory involvement: weakness of the muscles of respiration may rarely cause death before the disease is advanced elsewhere; the illness is relentlessly progressive, leading to death from respiratory paralysis - respiratory failure determines prognosis.","Bulbar dysfunction (difficulty chewing/swallowing) risks aspiration pneumonia and compromised nutrition/hydration.","Atypical features warranting search for a remediable mimic: restriction to only upper OR only lower motor neurons, involvement of non-motor neurons, or evidence of motor conduction block on electrophysiology."],"prognosis":"Relentlessly progressive; median survival is 3 to 5 years, with death typically from respiratory paralysis. Stabilization or regression is very rare. Higher serum/CSF neurofilament light chain levels correlate with more aggressive disease and more rapid progression. No treatment arrests the underlying pathologic process (p.3521, 3524).","pitfalls":["Because ALS is currently untreatable, it is imperative to exclude potentially remediable mimics - especially cervical cord compression/cervical spondylotic myelopathy, which can produce weakness, wasting, fasciculations in the arms and leg spasticity that closely resembles ALS.","Multifocal motor neuropathy with conduction block (MMCB) must be excluded: unlike ALS it lacks corticospinal signs, often has elevated anti-GM1 antibodies, and may respond dramatically to IVIG or chemotherapy.","Diagnosing ALS without clear involvement of BOTH upper and lower motor neurons is questionable - reconsider the diagnosis.","Features favoring ALS over a compressive mimic: absence of pain or sensory changes, normal bowel and bladder function, and normal spinal radiology and CSF. Sensory, sphincter, ocular-motility, and cognitive functions are preserved even late (except when concurrent frontotemporal dementia is present)."],"severityClassification":"World Federation of Neurology diagnostic categories based on number of body regions with combined upper and lower motor neuron involvement (bulbar, cervical, thoracic, lumbosacral): 'definite' ALS = 3-4 regions; 'probable' = 2 regions; 'possible' = 1 region. Exception: progressive upper and lower motor neuron signs at one site plus a SOD1 mutation also qualifies (p.3521).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3519-3524"],"crossLinks":["cord_compression"]},"antiphospholipid_syndrome":{"name":"Antiphospholipid Syndrome","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":369,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2837","clinicalPearls":["APS is an autoantibody-mediated acquired thrombophilia defined by recurrent arterial or venous thrombosis, microvascular manifestations, and/or pregnancy morbidity; it affects primarily young females (Harrison 22e p.2837).","APS may occur alone (primary) or in association with other autoimmune diseases, mainly SLE; about one-third of SLE patients carry aPL antibodies and 10-15% of those develop APS (Harrison 22e p.2837).","Diagnosis should be seriously considered with thrombosis, cerebrovascular accidents in individuals younger than 55 years, or pregnancy morbidity, particularly in the presence of livedo reticularis or thrombocytopenia (Harrison 22e p.2838).","The three classical laboratory antibodies are lupus anticoagulant (LA), anticardiolipin (aCL), and anti-beta2-glycoprotein I (anti-beta2GPI); higher-risk profiles include triple positivity, IgG isotype, moderate-to-high titers, and persistent positivity (Harrison 22e p.2838-2839).","Laboratory criteria require persistence: moderate (40-79 units) or high (>=80 units) aCL/anti-beta2GPI titers confirmed on two occasions 12 weeks apart (Harrison 22e p.2838-2839).","In patients receiving anticoagulation, LA test positivity should be interpreted with caution; when features are highly reminiscent of APS but classical aPL are absent, antiphosphatidylserine/prothrombin antibody testing may have a diagnostic role (Harrison 22e p.2837).","Deep venous thrombosis occurs primarily in the lower extremities and often causes pulmonary emboli; cerebral venous thrombosis may present with intracranial hypertension and focal deficits (Harrison 22e p.2837).","Arterial thrombosis most commonly affects cerebral arteries, manifesting as transient ischemic attack, stroke, migraine, or cognitive dysfunction (Harrison 22e p.2837).","Libman-Sacks endocarditis (small, usually <1 cm, organized platelet-fibrin valve vegetations) and valve thickening are characteristic cardiac manifestations (Harrison 22e p.2837).","Thrombocytopenia and autoimmune hemolytic anemia are the main hematologic manifestations of APS (Harrison 22e p.2837).","DOACs have recently been shown to increase the risk of arterial events, especially in patients with triple positivity or previous arterial thrombosis, and a meta-analysis found prior thrombosis type did not change this increased arterial risk versus VKAs (Harrison 22e p.2839)."],"pathophysiology":"APS is an autoantibody-mediated acquired thrombophilia. The major autoantibodies target phospholipid-binding plasma proteins, chiefly beta2-glycoprotein I (beta2GPI, a 43-kDa apolipoprotein of 326 amino acids in five domains) and prothrombin; anti-domain I IgG is associated with increased thrombotic risk, while the antithrombotic function of beta2GPI depends on its fifth domain. Lupus anticoagulant is a separate group of antibodies against phospholipid-binding proteins that prolongs in vitro clotting time which is not corrected by adding normal plasma. Antibody induction is thought to begin with endothelial injury (from infections, oxidative stress, or major physical stress such as surgery or trauma) in a permissive genetic background (HLA associations), driving increased endothelial apoptosis, autoantigen presentation, and an autoimmune response. Binding of aPL to disrupted endothelium produces a proinflammatory, prothrombotic state with monocyte and platelet activation, adhesion molecules and proinflammatory cytokines, complement and neutrophil activation, neutrophil extracellular trap (NET) formation, and thrombus formation. Type I interferon pathway activation and an imbalance between type I and III interferons have recently been proposed as mechanisms of thrombosis and obstetric complications (Harrison 22e p.2837).","additionalDifferentials":["Inherited or acquired thrombophilias including factor V Leiden and other causes (Chap. 121)","Coombs-positive hemolytic anemia (Chap. 105)","Thrombocytopenia of other cause (Chap. 120)","Atherosclerosis","Polyarteritis nodosa","SLE","Cryoglobulinemia","Lymphomas","Myeloproliferative disorders","Hypercholesterolemia","For catastrophic APS: thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, hemolytic-uremic syndrome, disseminated intravascular coagulation, and heparin-induced thrombocytopenia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Other inherited or acquired thrombophilias (e.g., factor V Leiden)","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Hemolytic-uremic syndrome","Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia","Livedo reticularis from vascular-wall disease (atherosclerosis, polyarteritis nodosa, SLE, cryoglobulinemia, lymphomas) or vascular-lumen disease (myeloproliferative disorders, hypercholesterolemia)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Lupus anticoagulant testing as a three-step procedure: screening with activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) and dilute Russell viper venom test (DRVVT), mixing study, and confirmation","Anticardiolipin and anti-beta2GPI IgG/IgM antibodies by standardized ELISA","Antiphosphatidylserine/prothrombin antibodies when APS features are present without classical aPL","Repeat aPL testing 12 weeks apart to confirm persistence","Assessment per 2023 ACR/EULAR classification criteria (>=3 points from clinical domains and >=3 points from laboratory domains)","Renal biopsy for suspected aPL-nephropathy (thrombotic microangiopathy in acute phase; fibrous intima hyperplasia, fibrous/fibrocellular arteriolar occlusions, focal cortical atrophy in chronic phase)","Cardiac MRI or histology for suspected myocardial disease; imaging for adrenal hemorrhage; BAL or pathology for pulmonary hemorrhage"],"redFlags":["Catastrophic APS (CAPS): a rare, life-threatening, rapidly progressive thromboembolic disease simultaneously involving three or more organs, typically with microvascular thrombosis developing in less than a week","Thrombotic microangiopathy (microangiopathic hemolytic anemia with severe thrombocytopenia) as a typical CAPS finding","Cerebrovascular accident in an individual younger than 55 years","Acute limb ischemia or digital gangrene from peripheral arterial thrombosis","Budd-Chiari syndrome from hepatic vein thrombosis","Pulmonary embolism or pulmonary hypertension from pulmonary arterial thrombosis"],"prognosis":"Thrombotic and obstetric event risk is closely tied to the underlying aPL profile, depending on antibody type (IgG high risk vs IgM low risk), number of antibodies (two or three classical autoantibodies confer higher risk than a single antibody), titer (moderate-high vs low), and persistence on repeated measurements. Catastrophic APS is life-threatening and rapidly progressive. APS antibodies increase with age, though it is questionable whether they induce thrombotic events in elderly individuals (Harrison 22e p.2837-2839).","pitfalls":["Paradoxical pitfall: lupus anticoagulant prolongs in vitro clotting tests (e.g., aPTT) yet is associated with thrombosis in vivo, not bleeding; the prolongation is NOT corrected by mixing with normal plasma, distinguishing it from a factor deficiency.","aPTT has variable sensitivity to lupus anticoagulant depending on the reagent used; aPTT prolongation is not specific because factor deficiencies or other inhibitors also prolong it, so confirmation requires lack of correction on mixing and correction with added phospholipids (Harrison 22e p.~700).","In patients receiving anticoagulation, lupus anticoagulant positivity should be interpreted with caution.","Classification requires that clinical manifestations with other equally or more likely explanations than APS not be counted toward the criteria.","aPL antibodies are frequently transiently positive, so persistence must be confirmed (titers on two occasions 12 weeks apart) before attributing disease."],"severityClassification":"2023 ACR/EULAR classification criteria require at least 3 points from clinical domains AND at least 3 points from laboratory domains. Clinical domains: (1) venous thromboembolism, (2) arterial thrombosis, (3) microvascular events, (4) obstetric complications, (5) cardiac valve disease, (6) hematologic manifestations. Laboratory domains: (1) lupus anticoagulant, (2) anticardiolipin, and/or (3) anti-beta2GPI antibodies at moderate (40-79 units) or high (>=80 units) titers on two occasions 12 weeks apart. Definite CAPS requires all four: thrombosis in >=3 organs/systems; development in less than a week; histologic small-vessel thrombosis in at least one organ; and aPL presence (probable CAPS when a combination is present) (Harrison 22e p.2838-2839).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2837","Harrison 22e p.2838","Harrison 22e p.2839"],"crossLinks":["sle_flare","dvt","pe","ischemic_stroke"]},"aortic_regurgitation":{"name":"Aortic Regurgitation","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":273,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2043-2047","clinicalPearls":["Chronic severe AR can have a long latent period, with patients remaining relatively asymptomatic for 10-15 years; exertional dyspnea is usually the first symptom of diminished cardiac reserve, followed by orthopnea and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea (p.2044).","A wide arterial pulse pressure (systolic hypertension plus low diastolic pressure) is characteristic of chronic severe AR, producing peripheral signs such as the water-hammer (Corrigan's) pulse, Quincke's capillary pulsations, Traube's pistol-shot femoral sounds, and Duroziez's sign (p.2043-2044).","The murmur of chronic AR is a high-pitched, blowing, decrescendo diastolic murmur heard best in the third intercostal space along the left sternal border; it is best appreciated with the patient sitting up, leaning forward, breath held in forced expiration (p.2044).","A diastolic murmur louder along the right sternal border suggests AR caused by aneurysmal dilation of the aortic root rather than primary valvular disease (p.2044).","The Austin Flint murmur is a soft, low-pitched rumbling mid-to-late diastolic murmur in severe AR, probably from diastolic displacement of the anterior mitral leaflet by the regurgitant stream; it is NOT associated with hemodynamically significant mitral obstruction (p.2044).","Anginal chest pain can occur in severe AR even without coronary artery disease and even in younger patients, may occur at rest, and often responds poorly to sublingual nitroglycerin (p.2044).","On echocardiography, a rapid high-frequency diastolic fluttering of the anterior mitral leaflet, produced by the impact of the regurgitant jet, is a characteristic finding (p.2044).","Auscultatory features of AR are intensified by sustained handgrip, which augments systemic vascular resistance and increases LV afterload (p.2044).","In acute severe AR, the elevated LV end-diastolic pressure leads to early (premature) closure of the mitral valve, a soft S1, and a soft, short early diastolic murmur (p.2044)."],"pathophysiology":"The total LV stroke volume (effective forward stroke volume plus regurgitant volume) is increased; in severe AR the regurgitant volume may equal the effective forward stroke volume. Unlike MR, the entire LV stroke volume is ejected into the high-pressure aorta. The major compensation is an increase in LV end-diastolic volume (increased preload); dilation and eccentric hypertrophy allow a larger stroke volume without increased myofibril shortening, so severe AR can occur with a normal effective forward stroke volume and normal LVEF together with elevated LV end-diastolic pressure and volume. By Laplace's law LV dilation increases systolic wall tension, so chronic AR is a state of both increased preload and afterload. Adaptive measures eventually fail; deterioration of LV function often precedes symptom onset. Considerable LV wall thickening occurs and hearts can weigh >1000 g. The reverse aorta-to-LV diastolic pressure gradient decreases through diastole, accounting for the decrescendo murmur. In acute severe AR the LV is unprepared for the volume load, compliance is normal or reduced, diastolic pressures rise rapidly (occasionally >40 mmHg), and the reversed pressure gradient closes the mitral valve prematurely. Myocardial ischemia may occur because of elevated oxygen demand (LV dilation, hypertrophy, increased systolic tension) combined with reduced coronary perfusion (most coronary flow is diastolic, when aortic pressure is low), affecting particularly the subendocardium even without epicardial CAD (p.2043-2044).","additionalDifferentials":["Aortic stenosis (a mid-systolic ejection murmur and systolic thrill are frequently audible in isolated AR and do not necessarily signify coexisting AS)","Combined aortic stenosis and aortic regurgitation (coexistence of hemodynamically significant AS with AR occurs almost exclusively in rheumatic or congenital disease)","Mitral regurgitation (contrasted hemodynamically; severe AR can also coexist with MR)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["ECG: LV hypertrophy signs in chronic severe AR, with ST-segment depression and T-wave inversion in leads I, aVL, V5, V6 ('LV strain'); left axis deviation and/or QRS prolongation may be present (p.2044)","Transthoracic echocardiography (TTE): cornerstone of longitudinal follow-up; assesses LV size/function and cause/severity of AR; LV global longitudinal strain (GLS) by speckle tracking may detect systolic dysfunction before EF falls (p.2044)","Color flow Doppler: with severe AR the central jet width exceeds 65% of the LV outflow tract, regurgitant volume >=60 mL/beat, regurgitant fraction >=50%, and there is diastolic flow reversal in the proximal descending thoracic aorta; continuous-wave Doppler shows rapid deceleration time in acute severe AR (p.2044)","Transesophageal echocardiography (TEE): detailed anatomic assessment of valve, root, and portions of the aorta (p.2044)","Cardiac magnetic resonance (CMR): accurate LV volumes and aortic size/contour when TTE is limited; can screen for increased LV interstitial (extracellular volume fraction) and replacement fibrosis (late gadolinium enhancement) (p.2044)","Cardiac CT: detailed assessment of aortic valve, root, and thoracic aortic anatomy; coronary CT angiography can provide preoperative coronary information (p.2044-2045)","Chest X-ray: downward and leftward apex displacement, posterior LV displacement encroaching on the spine, and possible aneurysmal aortic dilation when root disease is the cause (less sensitive than echo/CMR/CT for root/ascending aortic enlargement) (p.2045)","Cardiac catheterization with contrast aortography: confirms magnitude of regurgitation and LV status when needed; coronary angiography routinely before surgery in those at coronary risk (p.2045)"],"redFlags":["Acute severe AR (as in infective endocarditis, aortic dissection, or trauma): the LV cannot dilate enough to maintain stroke volume, LV diastolic pressure rises rapidly with marked LA and PA wedge pressure elevation, and pulmonary edema and/or cardiogenic shock may develop rapidly - a surgical emergency requiring operation usually within 24 h (prompt 24-48 h surgery may be lifesaving) (p.2044, p.2046-2047).","Acute severe AR may show only slight widening of pulse pressure and invariably presents with tachycardia (heart rate increases to preserve cardiac output), unlike the wide pulse pressure of chronic AR (p.2044).","In acute severe AR, intra-aortic balloon counterpulsation is contraindicated and beta blockers are best avoided (p.2045).","Onset of symptoms or LV systolic dysfunction in chronic severe AR is an indication for surgery (p.2045)."],"prognosis":"Operative and late mortality risks depend largely on disease stage and myocardial function at operation. Overall operative mortality for isolated AVR for pure AR is ~1-2%; the risk doubles when aortic surgery is added. Patients with marked cardiac enlargement and established LV dysfunction have an operative mortality of ~5-10% and a late mortality of ~3-5% per year from LV failure despite technically satisfactory operation; nonetheless, because of the very poor prognosis with medical management, even patients with advanced LV systolic dysfunction should be considered for operation. If surgery is delayed too long (>1 year from onset of symptoms or LV dysfunction), it often does not restore normal LV size and function (p.2046-2047).","pitfalls":["Sphygmomanometer diastolic pressure can be hard to read because systolic sounds may persist with the cuff fully deflated; the muffling point (Korotkoff phase IV) corresponds reasonably to true intra-arterial diastolic pressure (p.2044).","As chronic AR progresses and LV end-diastolic pressure rises, the arterial diastolic pressure may actually rise (aortic diastolic pressure cannot fall below LV end-diastolic pressure), so a less-wide pulse pressure does not exclude severe disease (p.2044).","A mid-systolic ejection murmur and systolic thrill in isolated AR can be loud without signifying aortic valve obstruction and do not necessarily indicate coexisting AS (p.2044).","Deterioration of LV function often precedes the development of symptoms, so waiting for symptoms risks irreversible dysfunction; serial noninvasive follow-up is essential to time surgery (p.2043-2044, p.2046).","Transcatheter aortic valve implantation (TAVI) is NOT recommended for patients with severe AR who are surgical candidates (technical success limited by annular dilation and paucity of valvular/annular calcium) (p.2046)."],"severityClassification":"Severe AR is characterized by central jet width >65% of the LV outflow tract on color Doppler, vena contracta >0.6 cm, regurgitant volume >=60 mL/beat, regurgitant fraction >=50%, effective regurgitant orifice >=0.3 cm2, and holodiastolic flow reversal in the proximal descending thoracic aorta. AHA/ACC staging (per Fig. 273-2): asymptomatic (Stage C, with C2 denoting LV dysfunction EF <=55%) versus symptomatic (Stage D). Surgical thresholds for asymptomatic severe AR include LVEF <55%, LV end-systolic dimension >50 mm (>25 mm/m2), or LV end-diastolic dimension >65 mm on serial studies (p.2044, p.2046).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2043-2047"],"crossLinks":["aortic_dissection","IE","heart_failure"]},"ards":{"name":"Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS)","system":"Critical Care","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":312,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2299","clinicalPearls":["ARDS is a clinical syndrome of severe dyspnea of rapid onset, hypoxemia, and diffuse bilateral pulmonary infiltrates leading to respiratory failure, caused by diffuse lung injury (Harrison 22e p.2299).","By 2012 expert consensus, ARDS is graded as mild, moderate, or severe by the degree of hypoxemia (PaO2/FiO2 ratio), and these stages correlate with mortality risk and duration of mechanical ventilation in survivors (Harrison 22e p.2299, p.2300).","Most cases (>80%) arise from a small number of disorders: pneumonia and sepsis (~40-60%), followed by aspiration of gastric contents, trauma, multiple transfusions, and drug overdose; risk rises with more than one predisposing condition (Harrison 22e p.2299).","The natural history has three phases - exudative (first ~7 days), proliferative (~day 7-21), and fibrotic - each with characteristic clinical and pathologic features (Harrison 22e p.2299, p.2301).","Cornerstone of management is recognizing and treating the underlying trigger (pneumonia, sepsis, aspiration, trauma) plus standardized bundled ICU care, rather than ARDS-specific drug therapy (Harrison 22e p.2302).","Lung-protective ventilation with low tidal volume (6 mL/kg predicted body weight) and limited plateau pressure (<=30 cmH2O) significantly lowers mortality versus conventional higher tidal volumes; this is the only therapy graded by the strongest evidence (Harrison 22e p.2302, p.2303).","Prone positioning early in severe ARDS (PaO2/FiO2 <150 mmHg) reduced 28-day mortality in a randomized trial; it requires a team experienced in proning because repositioning critically ill patients can be hazardous (Harrison 22e p.2302).","A conservative fluid strategy - reducing left atrial filling pressure with fluid restriction and diuretics - shortens ICU stay and duration of ventilation, limited only by hypotension and organ hypoperfusion (Harrison 22e p.2302).","Mortality in ARDS is largely due to nonpulmonary causes, with sepsis and nonpulmonary organ failure accounting for >80% of deaths (Harrison 22e p.2303)."],"pathophysiology":"ARDS results from diffuse lung injury, either direct (e.g., pneumonia, aspiration, toxic inhalation, pulmonary contusion) or indirect (e.g., sepsis, severe trauma, multiple transfusions, pancreatitis). It evolves through three phases. In the EXUDATIVE phase (first ~7 days), alveolar capillary endothelial cells and type I pneumocytes are injured, the tight alveolar barrier is lost, and protein-rich edema fluid accumulates in the interstitial and alveolar spaces. Proinflammatory cytokines (IL-1, IL-6, IL-8, TNF-alpha) and lipid mediators recruit neutrophils, and condensed plasma proteins plus cellular debris and dysfunctional surfactant form hyaline membranes (diffuse alveolar damage). Pulmonary vascular injury occurs early with microthrombi. Dependent alveolar collapse reduces lung compliance, producing intrapulmonary shunting, hypoxemia, and increased work of breathing; microvascular occlusion raises dead space and pulmonary vascular resistance, so hypercapnia and pulmonary hypertension can also occur. The PROLIFERATIVE phase (~day 7-21) brings lung repair, organization of exudates, a shift from neutrophil- to lymphocyte-predominant infiltrates, and proliferation of type II pneumocytes that regenerate surfactant and differentiate into type I cells; most patients recover. Some progress to the FIBROTIC phase, with alveolar-duct and interstitial fibrosis, emphysema-like bullae, vascular occlusion and pulmonary hypertension, increased pneumothorax risk, reduced compliance, and increased dead space (Harrison 22e p.2299, p.2300, p.2301, p.2302).","additionalDifferentials":["Cardiogenic (hydrostatic) pulmonary edema","Bilateral pneumonia","Diffuse alveolar hemorrhage","Acute interstitial lung disease (e.g., acute interstitial pneumonitis)","Acute immunologic injury (e.g., hypersensitivity pneumonitis)","Toxin injury (e.g., radiation pneumonitis)","Neurogenic pulmonary edema"],"infectionMimics":["Bilateral pneumonia (may also coexist with ARDS)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cardiogenic pulmonary edema","Diffuse alveolar hemorrhage","Acute interstitial pneumonitis","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Radiation pneumonitis","Neurogenic pulmonary edema"],"additionalInvestigations":["Arterial blood gas to determine PaO2/FiO2 ratio for Berlin severity grading","Chest radiograph (bilateral opacities consistent with pulmonary edema, often involving >=three-quarters of lung fields)","Echocardiography to exclude hydrostatic/cardiac etiology when no ARDS risk factor is present","Chest CT (shows bilateral infiltrates with heterogeneous, often dependent lung involvement)","Lung ultrasound permitted as an alternative imaging modality under proposed 2023 Berlin updates, especially in resource-limited settings"],"redFlags":["Progressive hypoxemia and increasing work of breathing leading to respiratory fatigue and respiratory failure requiring mechanical ventilation","Severe ARDS (PaO2/FiO2 <=100 mmHg) carrying the highest mortality","Concurrent sepsis or multiorgan failure, which account for most ARDS deaths","Lung biopsy evidence of pulmonary fibrosis in any phase, associated with increased mortality"],"prognosis":"Hospital mortality in the LUNG SAFE study was approximately 34.9% for mild, 40.3% for moderate, and 46.1% for severe ARDS, with a trend toward improved outcomes over time. Increasing Berlin-defined severity predicts higher mortality. Most deaths are nonpulmonary, with sepsis and nonpulmonary organ failure causing >80%. Major mortality risk factors are nonpulmonary: advanced age (~60% mortality in patients >75 vs ~20% in those <45), preexisting organ dysfunction (chronic liver disease, chronic alcohol abuse, chronic immunosuppression); direct lung injury carries nearly twice the death risk of indirect injury. Among survivors, the lung has notable resolving power: most regain nearly normal lung function, usually within 6 months, and more than a third have normal spirometry and diffusion capacity one year after extubation. However, exercise limitation and reduced physical quality of life are often documented at 5 years despite near-normal pulmonary function, and survivors and family caregivers carry a substantial burden of depression and posttraumatic stress disorder (Harrison 22e p.2303, p.2304).","pitfalls":["ARDS radiographic findings are characteristic but not specific and can be indistinguishable from cardiogenic pulmonary edema; unlike cardiogenic edema, ARDS often lacks cardiomegaly, pleural effusions, or pulmonary vascular redistribution. Hydrostatic edema must not be the primary cause - if no ARDS risk factor is present, objective evaluation (e.g., echocardiography) is required to exclude a cardiac etiology (Harrison 22e p.2300, p.2301).","Mechanical ventilation, though life-saving, can aggravate lung injury via volutrauma (alveolar overdistention from excess tidal volume), barotrauma (high alveolar pressure), and atelectrauma (recurrent alveolar collapse); because ARDS lung is heterogeneous, fully inflating consolidated regions can overdistend and injure more normal lung (Harrison 22e p.2302).","Routine glucocorticoids are NOT supported by current evidence for ARDS itself; recruitment maneuvers combined with higher baseline PEEP increased mortality in one study; routine early neuromuscular blockade is not beneficial in all moderate-to-severe ARDS (reserve for ventilatory dyssynchrony despite sedation); surfactant replacement and inhaled vasodilators (e.g., inhaled nitric oxide) do not improve survival (Harrison 22e p.2302, p.2303).","Pulse oximetry may overestimate oxygen saturation in patients with darker skin tones, so correlation with PaO2 should be considered when feasible (Harrison 22e p.2300)."],"severityClassification":"Berlin definition (2012): severity is graded by oxygenation (PaO2/FiO2 assessed on at least 5 cmH2O PEEP) - Mild: 200 mmHg < PaO2/FiO2 <=300 mmHg; Moderate: 100 mmHg < PaO2/FiO2 <=200 mmHg; Severe: PaO2/FiO2 <=100 mmHg. Onset must be acute (within 1 week of a clinical insult or new/worsening respiratory symptoms), chest imaging must show bilateral opacities consistent with pulmonary edema not fully explained by effusions, lobar/lung collapse, or nodules, and respiratory failure must not be primarily due to hydrostatic (cardiogenic) edema (objective evaluation such as echocardiography required if no ARDS risk factor present). Proposed 2023 updates extend criteria to nonintubated patients on high-flow nasal oxygen or noninvasive ventilation, add SpO2/FiO2 (S/F) ratios, permit ultrasound imaging, and allow application in resource-limited settings. A more recent global definition has also been proposed that does not rely on arterial blood gases, chest radiography, or PEEP (Harrison 22e p.2299, p.2300).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2299","Harrison 22e p.2300","Harrison 22e p.2301","Harrison 22e p.2302","Harrison 22e p.2303","Harrison 22e p.2304"],"crossLinks":["SEVERE_CAP","SEPTIC_SHOCK","pancreatitis"]},"aspergillosis":{"name":"Aspergillosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":223,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1713","clinicalPearls":["A. fumigatus causes most invasive aspergillosis, >95% of chronic aspergillosis, and most allergic syndromes; only species that grow at 37C cause invasive infection (Harrison 22e p.1713)","The disease spectrum spans three categories: invasive (acute/subacute in the immunocompromised), chronic/saprophytic (chronic cavitary pulmonary aspergillosis, aspergilloma/fungal ball), and allergic (ABPA, severe asthma with fungal sensitization, allergic fungal rhinosinusitis) (Harrison 22e p.1713, 1714)","Invasive aspergillosis is one of the most common diagnostic errors revealed at autopsy; keys to early diagnosis are a high index of suspicion, circulating antigen screening (in leukemia), and urgent thoracic CT (Harrison 22e p.1714)","On thoracic CT the halo sign (ground-glass hemorrhagic infarction around a nodule/consolidation) appears for ~7 days early in neutropenic patients and is a good prognostic feature reflecting early diagnosis; the air-crescent/cavitation appears later (Harrison 22e p.1716)","Galactomannan antigen detection on respiratory samples (e.g., tracheobronchial/BAL fluid) is more sensitive than serum and is critical in ventilated/ICU patients where radiology is nonspecific; positive serum antigen usually precedes clinical or radiologic features by several days (Harrison 22e p.1714, 1716)","Antigen, beta-D-glucan, and real-time PCR are faster and far more sensitive than culture, as only 10-30% of invasive aspergillosis cases yield a positive culture; antifungal therapy reduces antigen sensitivity (Harrison 22e p.1716)","Aspergillus IgG antibody is detectable in ~90% of chronic cavitary pulmonary aspergillosis and ~65% of Aspergillus nodules; ABPA is diagnosed by Aspergillus-specific IgE (or positive skin-prick test) plus elevated total IgE, usually >1000 IU/mL (Harrison 22e p.1714, 1715)","Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis follows pulmonary tuberculosis in ~6-13% of treated cases and is often initially mistaken for tuberculosis; >90% of chronic cavitary cases occur in patients with prior lung disease (Harrison 22e p.1713, 1715)","On tissue histology Aspergillus hyphae are hyaline, narrow, and septate with branching at 45 degrees; angioinvasion with vascular invasion by hyphae is characteristic in neutropenia (Harrison 22e p.1716)","HEPA filtration is often protective and should be installed and monitored in operating rooms, burn units, and areas housing high-risk patients; hospital outbreaks usually relate to a contaminated air source or construction (Harrison 22e p.1713)"],"pathophysiology":"Aspergillus is a ubiquitous hyaline septate branching mold whose conidia (spores) are inhaled from indoor/outdoor air; only species growing at 37C cause invasive disease. Disease form depends on host immunity. Invasive disease arises with profound neutropenia, glucocorticoid use, phagocyte dysfunction (CGD, advanced HIV, relapsed leukemia) and underlying respiratory disease, with angioinvasion and hemorrhagic infarction; from the lungs (>80% of cases) it may disseminate hematogenously (most often to brain). Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis occurs in apparently immunocompetent hosts with prior lung disease and a suboptimal TH1-like inflammatory response. Allergic forms (ABPA, SAFS) reflect a TH2-like hypersensitivity response to A. fumigatus, classically complicating asthma and cystic fibrosis. Multiple gene variants underlie susceptibility to each form (Harrison 22e p.1713-1714).","additionalDifferentials":["Pulmonary tuberculosis (chronic cavitary pulmonary aspergillosis and aspergilloma classically mimic and follow TB)","Nontuberculous mycobacterial infection","Lung carcinoma (Aspergillus nodule may resemble early lung cancer)","Bacterial sinusitis (early invasive Aspergillus sinusitis not radiologically distinguishable)","Other angioinvasive molds, notably mucormycosis and Fusarium/Scedosporium (histologically can mimic Aspergillus)"],"infectionMimics":["Mucormycosis (Mucorales) causing invasive pulmonary or sinus disease","Fusariosis (Fusarium spp.)","Scedosporiosis (Scedosporium/Pseudallescheria boydii)","Pulmonary tuberculosis and nontuberculous mycobacterial disease","Bacterial sinusitis or pneumonia"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Lung carcinoma (Aspergillus nodule mimics early carcinoma)","COVID-19 itself (nodules with halo signs are a feature of COVID-19; invasive aspergillosis requires supportive evidence)","Atelectasis or nonspecific consolidation","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis / other cavitary lung disease"],"additionalInvestigations":["Galactomannan antigen detection (respiratory sample more sensitive than serum; critical in ICU/ventilated patients)","Novel protein antigen assay","Serum beta-D-glucan","Real-time PCR of respiratory samples and blood (and sputum PCR in chronic disease)","Aspergillus IgG antibody (chronic forms) and Aspergillus-specific IgE plus total IgE (allergic forms)","High-volume fungal culture on fungal media (also checks for azole resistance)","Microscopy/cytology and histopathology of affected tissue (hyaline narrow septate hyphae branching at 45 degrees)","Thoracic CT (halo sign, air-crescent, nodules, cavitation); MRI for cerebral and sinus disease","Bronchoscopy with tracheobronchial lavage; positron emission tomography for chronic cavitary disease and nodules"],"redFlags":["Life-threatening / massive hemoptysis (the most significant complication of chronic cavitary pulmonary aspergillosis and may be the presenting manifestation)","Neurologic deterioration: mood change, focal signs, seizures, declining mental status suggesting cerebral aspergillosis (hemorrhagic infarction/abscess)","Orbital apex syndrome (blindness and proptosis), facial swelling, cavernous sinus thrombosis or carotid occlusion in invasive/chronic sinusitis","Fever progressing despite glucocorticoids in an immunocompromised host","Acute keratitis threatening sight"],"prognosis":"Invasive aspergillosis is curable if immune reconstitution occurs, whereas allergic and chronic forms are not curable. Mortality of invasive aspergillosis is 30-70% if treated but ~100% if the diagnosis is missed; cerebral aspergillosis, Aspergillus endocarditis, bilateral extensive invasive pulmonary disease, late-stage AIDS, relapsed uncontrolled leukemia, and liver failure carry very poor outcomes. Untreated chronic cavitary pulmonary aspergillosis causes clinical deterioration in ~70%; with antifungal treatment mortality is 15-20% in the first year, ~40% over 5 years, and 50-60% over 10 years, with treatment failure in ~30% (more with azole resistance). ABPA/SAFS respond to glucocorticoids and antifungals (~60% to itraconazole, ~80% to voriconazole/posaconazole) but relapse after discontinuation is common (Harrison 22e p.1717).","pitfalls":["Requesting only bacterial culture rather than fungal culture may miss the diagnosis (bacterial agar is less sensitive); only 10-30% of invasive cases have a positive culture","Fluconazole is commonly used for prophylaxis but has NO activity against Aspergillus species","No prophylactic regimen is completely successful","Antifungal therapy reduces the sensitivity of antigen detection","Aspergillus IgG may not be positive on initial testing in subacute/nonneutropenic invasive disease","A positive respiratory culture may represent only noninvasive disease or airway colonization, not invasion","Contrast CT of the brain is often contraindicated by poor renal function and unenhanced CT is usually nonspecific; MRI is the most useful immediate investigation for cerebral disease","Surgery is problematic in chronic cavitary pulmonary aspergillosis and often causes serious complications; bronchial artery embolization is preferred for problematic hemoptysis"],"severityClassification":"By disease category (Table 223-2): INVASIVE (acute course <=1 month, subacute 1-3 months) - angioinvasive/nonangioinvasive/granulomatous pulmonary, invasive sinusitis, tracheobronchitis, disseminated, cerebral, endocarditis, cutaneous, keratitis/endophthalmitis. CHRONIC - chronic cavitary pulmonary aspergillosis, chronic fibrosing pulmonary aspergillosis (destroyed lung), Aspergillus bronchitis, Aspergillus nodule, chronic invasive and chronic granulomatous sinusitis. SAPROPHYTIC - aspergilloma (single fungal ball), airway colonization, maxillary sinus fungal ball. ALLERGIC - ABPA, severe asthma with fungal sensitization (SAFS), allergic fungal rhinosinusitis, hypersensitivity pneumonitis (Harrison 22e p.1714-1715).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1713","Harrison 22e p.1714","Harrison 22e p.1715","Harrison 22e p.1716","Harrison 22e p.1717"],"crossLinks":["FEBRILE_NEUTROPENIA","asthma_exac","PULMONARY_TB"]},"botulism":{"name":"Botulism","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":158,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1233-1238","clinicalPearls":["Classic syndrome: bilateral cranial nerve palsies followed by symmetric, descending flaccid paralysis, in a patient who is afebrile and fully conscious with normal sensorium (Harrison 22e p.1235).","First neurologic manifestation is usually ptosis, which can be striking; ocular findings include blurred/fuzzy vision or diplopia from extraocular palsies of cranial nerves III, IV, and VI (Harrison 22e p.1235).","Bulbar onset: dysarthria, difficulty swallowing, and nasal/oral regurgitation reflect cranial nerve involvement; descending weakness then spreads to neck, shoulders, and limbs, with proximal groups affected before distal (Harrison 22e p.1235).","Paralysis descends symmetrically and distal tendon reflexes diminish symmetrically; constipation from intestinal paralysis develops in almost all patients (Harrison 22e p.1235-1236).","In a U.S. series of 332 cases, the most common reported symptoms were difficulty swallowing (86%), fatigue (85%), blurred vision (80%), and slurred speech (78%); signs included afebrile temperature (99%), descending paralysis (93%), alert/oriented status (93%), and ptosis (81%) (Harrison 22e p.1236).","BoNTs irreversibly block acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction, so paralysis persists for weeks to months until nerve endings regenerate; the clinical approach is the same for all toxin types causing human illness (Harrison 22e p.1234, p.1236).","Four naturally occurring forms (foodborne, infant, wound, adult intestinal colonization) plus iatrogenic and inhalational forms; the neurologic syndrome is essentially identical across all forms and all ages (Harrison 22e p.1233-1235).","Every case of foodborne botulism is a public health emergency, as it may be the first in an outbreak; clinicians should contact the state health department emergency line to reach a CDC botulism consultant (Harrison 22e p.1235, p.1237).","More than 95% of noninfant botulism patients in the U.S. recover, and the infant botulism survival rate is near 100% (Harrison 22e p.1236)."],"pathophysiology":"Disease is caused by botulinum neurotoxins (BoNTs) produced by Clostridium botulinum (rarely C. butyricum or C. baratii), gram-positive, spore-forming, anaerobic bacteria whose heat-resistant spores are ubiquitous in soil. Serotypes A, B, E, and F cause human disease. BoNT is a metalloprotease whose heavy chain mediates neurospecific binding and internalization and whose light chain enters the cytosol and cleaves SNARE-family proteins (e.g., SNAP-25, VAMP), disrupting synaptic vesicle fusion and blocking acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction, producing flaccid paralysis of voluntary muscles. Toxin binding is effectively irreversible, so recovery requires regeneration of nerve endings over weeks to months. Routes differ by form: foodborne (ingestion of preformed toxin in improperly preserved foods, often home-canned low-acid vegetables), wound (spore germination in wounds/necrotic tissue, notably from black tar heroin injection), infant and adult intestinal colonization (in situ toxin production by colonizing Clostridium), iatrogenic (high-dose therapeutic BoNT injection), and inhalational (aerosolized toxin as a bioweapon). (Harrison 22e p.1233-1234)","additionalDifferentials":["Guillain-Barre syndrome (GBS)","Myasthenia gravis","Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome","Tick paralysis","Basilar stroke producing nonlateralizing/symmetric bulbar palsies","Tetrodotoxin or shellfish poisoning","Antimicrobial-associated paralysis"],"infectionMimics":["Guillain-Barre syndrome following Campylobacter jejuni or viral infection"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Myasthenia gravis","Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (proximal limb weakness, often with advanced cancer)","Tick paralysis","Basilar stroke","Intoxication from alcohol or other substances (ptosis, dysarthria, and gait instability can be misattributed)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed neurologic examination (the key to clinical diagnosis, uncovering invariable cranial nerve deficits)","Lumbar puncture / CSF analysis (protein usually normal in botulism, in contrast to GBS; may help distinguish)","Electromyography by an experienced operator (neuromuscular junction blockage with normal axonal conduction and potentiation on rapid repetitive stimulation)","Brain imaging (normal in botulism; helps exclude basilar stroke)","Serologic antibody tests and rapid nerve stimulation tests (to evaluate myasthenia gravis)","Mouse bioassay (gold standard for BoNT identification and serotyping in clinical or food specimens)","Detection of BoNT in serum or stool, or isolation of BoNT-producing Clostridium from stool or wound cultures; toxin in suspected food","In vitro assays: real-time PCR for bont genes, Endopep mass spectrometry, immune-based and cell-based assays"],"redFlags":["Pharyngeal muscle flaccidity causing airway collapse and respiratory arrest early in the course","Falling vital capacity / diaphragmatic and accessory muscle weakness signaling impending respiratory failure","Rapidly progressive descending paralysis (especially BoNT type A or F)","A placid, expressionless appearance masking the agitation expected with respiratory distress","Autonomic dysfunction producing hemodynamic instability","Two or more patients with the same syndrome (near-pathognomonic and signals an outbreak / public health emergency)"],"prognosis":"More than 95% of noninfant botulism patients in the United States recover, often after protracted intensive care and rehabilitation, even without antitoxin; the survival rate for infant botulism is near 100%. Paralysis lasts weeks to months while affected nerve endings regenerate. Serotype A is associated with more rapid progression, more frequent respiratory compromise and mechanical ventilation, and longer-duration paralysis; type B is milder and shorter; type F progresses rapidly but recovers faster. Untreated, early death results from airway obstruction and inadequate tidal volume from respiratory muscle paralysis. (Harrison 22e p.1236)","pitfalls":["Misclassifying the paralysis as ascending: botulism is a DESCENDING (cranial-first, then downward) symmetric flaccid paralysis, whereas GBS presents as an ASCENDING paralysis in about 95% of cases - this descending-versus-ascending distinction is central, though the Miller Fisher GBS variant (ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, areflexia) may resemble early descending paralysis (Harrison 22e p.1236).","Mistaking intact sensorium for altered consciousness: cranial nerve palsies, ptosis, dysarthria, and gait instability can give a false impression of obtundation or be wrongly attributed to alcohol/substance intoxication, even though patients remain fully conscious (Harrison 22e p.1235-1236).","Sporadic (lone) cases are commonly misdiagnosed because of the rarity of botulism and clinician unfamiliarity; an incomplete neurologic examination is a frequent cause of delayed or missed diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1236).","Waiting for laboratory confirmation: confirmation may take >=24 h, so treatment must rely on rapid clinical diagnosis rather than awaiting lab results (Harrison 22e p.1236-1237).","Assuming normal CSF excludes GBS too early: in GBS, CSF protein elevation may lag symptom onset by days, so a normal early CSF must be interpreted with symptom duration and the lumbar puncture may need repeating (Harrison 22e p.1236).","Reported paresthesias are not explained by the known activity of botulinum toxin, and GI symptoms (nausea/vomiting) are seen in foodborne but have NOT been reported in wound botulism (Harrison 22e p.1235-1236)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal numeric severity score; severity is described as varying greatly between patients and is probably governed by toxin dose. At the mild end, some untreated patients progress no further than ptosis and mild palsy of one or two cranial nerves; at the severe end, others develop fulminant cranial nerve palsies and rapidly progressive descending flaccid paralysis affecting most or all voluntary muscles with respiratory failure requiring intubation and mechanical ventilation within hours. (Harrison 22e p.1236)","references":["Harrison 22e p.1233-1238"],"crossLinks":["gbs","myasthenic_crisis"]},"breast_cancer":{"name":"Breast Cancer","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":84,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.632","clinicalPearls":["Most common nonskin cancer in women worldwide; predominantly a disease of prolonged estrogen exposure, with female gender and increasing age the most common risk factors (about 70% diagnosed at 55 years or older, median age 63) (Harrison 22e p.632).","Clinical signs and symptoms suggestive of breast cancer may include a breast lump, skin or nipple changes, or palpable regional nodes; with mammographic screening many patients are asymptomatic and present only with an imaging abnormality (mass, distortion, or suspicious microcalcifications) (Harrison 22e p.634).","Diagnosis rests on pathologic confirmation, generally by image-guided core biopsy, which also assesses tumor grade and morphology and performs biomarker evaluation for estrogen receptor (ER), progesterone receptor (PR), and HER2 protein expression and potentially HER2 gene amplification (the triple assessment of clinical/imaging/pathology with biomarkers) (Harrison 22e p.634).","Suspicious axillary nodes should be biopsied via image-guided techniques, and both breasts should be imaged because up to 3% of women with newly diagnosed breast cancer have unsuspected contralateral disease (Harrison 22e p.634).","Invasive breast cancer is divided into at least four molecular subtypes: luminal A and luminal B (both ER positive; luminal B has reduced PR or higher Ki67 proliferation and worse outcome), HER2 subtype (high HER2 expression, benefits from HER2-targeted therapy), and basal/triple-negative (TNBC, lacking ER, PR, and HER2) (Harrison 22e p.636).","Most invasive breast cancers are ductal; about 10-15% are lobular, which are usually ER positive and HER2 negative, distinguished by absence of E-cadherin, and have a propensity to spread to serosal surfaces (omentum, pleura, meninges) and parenchyma (ovaries, GI tract) (Harrison 22e p.636).","Family history is a critical risk factor, yet only 20% of women diagnosed with breast cancer have a family history; a first-degree relative with breast cancer doubles the risk (Harrison 22e p.633)."],"pathophysiology":"Breast cancer is predominantly a disease resulting from prolonged exposure of the breast to estrogen. Most breast cancers begin in epithelial cells within the lobules or ducts and proceed through a conceptual continuum of atypia and hyperplasia to in situ malignancy, then invasion into surrounding tissue, intravasation into lymph and blood channels, spread to regional lymph nodes, and distant metastases (though not all cancers progress through every stage). Hereditary breast cancer susceptibility genes account for 5-10% of cases; these genes participate in DNA damage repair, and inherited autosomal dominant mutations generally cause protein truncation and loss of function of DNA repair proteins. The most common are BRCA1 (chromosome 17q21) and BRCA2 (chromosome 13q12), conferring a 50-80% lifetime risk of invasive breast cancer by age 80 and a 30% risk of ovarian cancer; other germline mutations include TP53 (Li-Fraumeni), PALB2, ATM, STK11 (Peutz-Jeghers), and PTEN (Cowden). Ashkenazi Jews have a higher BRCA mutation incidence (Harrison 22e p.632-633).","additionalDifferentials":["Inflammatory breast cancer is sometimes confused with mastitis or other breast infection (Harrison 22e p.638).","Benign breast pathology such as atypical ductal or lobular hyperplasia (raises risk about four- to fivefold) and lobular carcinoma in situ (LCIS, raises risk 7- to 12-fold) (Harrison 22e p.633)."],"infectionMimics":["Mastitis or other breast infection can be mimicked by inflammatory breast cancer, which presents with rapid onset of erythema, swelling, and tenderness of the breast; breast or skin biopsy is critical when breast inflammation fails to resolve after antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.638)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Evolving changes in the pregnant breast can make diagnosis of breast cancer during pregnancy difficult, so suspicious lumps or skin or nipple changes still require formal evaluation (Harrison 22e p.638)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Image-guided core biopsy for pathologic confirmation, tumor grade/morphology, and biomarker testing (ER, PR, HER2 protein, and potentially HER2 gene amplification) (Harrison 22e p.634).","Diagnostic breast imaging: mammography, ultrasonography, and MRI if indicated; bilateral breast imaging because up to 3% have unsuspected contralateral disease (Harrison 22e p.634).","Germline panel testing for breast cancer susceptibility mutations on DNA from peripheral blood or saliva after appropriate counseling (not recommended for the general population) (Harrison 22e p.633).","For symptoms or findings of advanced disease (large tumor, skin changes, extensive adenopathy): CT and radionuclide bone scanning to look for overt metastatic disease, with biopsy of suspicious lesions to confirm metastatic disease (Harrison 22e p.635).","Genomic transcriptional profiling assays for early-stage ER-positive disease (Oncotype Dx 21-gene, MammaPrint 70-gene, ProSigna 50-gene, EndoPredict 12-gene, Breast Cancer Index) to inform adjuvant chemotherapy decisions; ordering more than one per case is discouraged (Harrison 22e p.636).","Skin biopsy showing dermal lymphatic invasion with tumor cells supports diagnosis of inflammatory breast cancer (Harrison 22e p.638)."],"redFlags":["Inflammatory breast cancer: rapid onset of erythema, swelling, and tenderness of the breast (a subset of locally advanced disease); breast inflammation failing to resolve after antibiotics warrants breast or skin biopsy, and up to one-third have detectable metastasis at diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.638).","Any unexplained mass or lesion palpable on physical examination needs further assessment including biopsy where warranted; a normal mammogram does NOT rule out cancer in a woman with a palpable breast lump (Harrison 22e p.45).","Locally advanced features: large primary tumor, involvement of skin or chest wall, or fixed tumor or axillary nodes (Harrison 22e p.638)."],"prognosis":"5-year relative survival in the United States is currently about 91%. Outcomes are directly related to stage at presentation (and vary by race/ethnicity): 5-year relative survival is over 99% for stage I, about 93% for stage II, about 75% for stage III, and about 29% for stage IV. Less than 10% present with de novo metastatic (M1) disease. Luminal B tumors carry a poorer outcome than luminal A; TNBC carries a poorer prognosis; mucinous and pure medullary cancers have very favorable prognoses with local therapy only; higher Nottingham grade is associated with poorer prognosis. About 20-25% of patients treated for early breast cancer subsequently develop metastatic disease (Harrison 22e p.635-638).","pitfalls":["A common disease-specific diagnostic pitfall is ordering a mammogram for a woman with a palpable breast lump and, when it returns normal, reassuring her that cancer has been ruled out; any unexplained palpable mass needs further testing and biopsy where warranted (Harrison 22e p.45).","Lobular cancers can recur at unusual sites (serosal surfaces, ovaries, GI tract), producing atypical presentations that may delay diagnosis if metastatic disease is not considered (Harrison 22e p.636).","Up to 3% of newly diagnosed patients have unsuspected contralateral disease, so both breasts must be imaged (Harrison 22e p.634).","Inflammatory breast cancer may be mistaken for mastitis or infection; biopsy is critical when inflammation fails to resolve after antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.638).","Germline panel testing can yield variants of unknown significance (VUS) and includes genes with poorly defined clinical risk, causing patient confusion and discomfort (Harrison 22e p.633)."],"severityClassification":"Staging follows the American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) eighth edition TNM (tumor-node-metastasis) system, which has evolved to incorporate molecular biomarkers (ER, PR, HER2) and certain genomic tests (e.g., Oncotype 21-gene assay). Conceptually, stages I and II are early-stage disease confined to breast and ipsilateral nodes; stage III is locally advanced disease; and stage IV (M1) is metastatic disease. Staging may be clinical (c), pathologic after surgery (p), or post-neoadjuvant (y prefix). Common metastatic sites are soft tissues, lung, bone, liver, and brain (Harrison 22e p.634-635).","references":["Harrison 22e p.632"],"crossLinks":[]},"brucellosis":{"name":"Brucellosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":174,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1331-1335","clinicalPearls":["A bacterial zoonosis caused by Brucella species, transmitted directly or indirectly from infected animals, predominantly domesticated ruminants and swine; B. melitensis (sheep, goats, camels) is the most common cause of symptomatic human disease, with B. abortus (cattle/buffalo), B. suis (swine), and B. canis (dogs) also implicated (p.1331).","Human infection is usually associated with occupational or domestic animal exposure (farmers, shepherds, goatherds, veterinarians, slaughterhouse and meat-processing workers, laboratory staff); travelers and urban residents typically acquire it through contaminated food (p.1332).","Dairy products, especially soft cheeses, unpasteurized milk, and ice cream, are the most frequently implicated sources; acquisition may occur by ingestion, inhalation, or mucosal/percutaneous exposure (p.1332).","Almost invariably causes fever, which may be accompanied by profuse sweats, especially at night; untreated, the fever classically shows an undulating pattern persisting for weeks before an afebrile period that may be followed by relapse (p.1332-1333).","Fever is associated with musculoskeletal symptoms and signs in about half of patients; nonspecific features include apathy, fatigue, anorexia, weight loss, myalgia, headache, and chills (p.1333).","Presentation often fits one of three patterns: a typhoid-like febrile illness but less severe; fever with acute monoarthritis (typically hip or knee) in a young child; and long-lasting fever with misery and low-back or hip pain in an older person, especially men (p.1333).","Focal musculoskeletal involvement is the most common complication (~40%): osteomyelitis favors lumbar and low thoracic vertebrae, and septic arthritis most often affects knee, hip, sacroiliac, shoulder, and sternoclavicular joints, as monoarthritis or polyarthritis (p.1333).","Roughly one-quarter of patients have hepatosplenomegaly and 10-20% have significant lymphadenopathy; up to 10% of men develop acute epididymo-orchitis (p.1333).","Endocarditis occurs in about 1% of cases, most often affecting the aortic valve (native or prosthetic), and is the usual cause of death when the outcome is fatal (p.1333, p.1335).","Diagnosis rests on a history of potential exposure, a compatible presentation, and supporting laboratory findings, because the clinical picture is not distinctive (p.1333).","Isolation of brucellae from blood, CSF, bone marrow, joint fluid, or a tissue aspirate/biopsy is definitive and succeeds in about 50-70% of cases; modern automated blood culture systems (e.g., Bactec) usually turn positive within 7 days (p.1333).","Serology often provides the only positive laboratory finding; in endemic or occupationally exposed settings agglutinin titers of 1:320-1:640 or higher are considered diagnostic, while in nonendemic areas a titer of at least 1:160 is significant, with no single universal cutoff (p.1334)."],"pathophysiology":"Brucellae are small, gram-negative, unencapsulated, nonsporulating coccobacilli that behave as facultative intracellular parasites. Following entry by ingestion, inhalation, or mucosal/percutaneous exposure, initial replication is presumed to occur within cells of the lymph nodes draining the entry point, with subsequent hematogenous spread that can establish chronic localizing infection at almost any site, most often the reticuloendothelial system, musculoskeletal tissues, and genitourinary system. The macrophage is the key target cell; Brucella is a \"stealth\" pathogen that evades innate immunity and survives within monocytic cells by restricting fusion of type IV secretion system-dependent Brucella-containing vacuoles with lysosomes, inhibiting apoptosis of infected cells, and preventing dendritic cell maturation and T-cell activation. Smooth LPS has low endotoxin activity and contributes to pyrogenicity and resistance to phagocytosis and serum killing. Antibodies aid clearance of extracellular organisms but alone cannot eradicate infection; control depends on macrophage activation and IFN-gamma/TH1 responses. Tissue responses range from acute and chronic inflammation to granuloma formation, with or without necrosis and caseation, and abscesses may develop in chronic localized infection (p.1332).","additionalDifferentials":["Tuberculosis is the most important differential for vertebral osteomyelitis or septic arthritis; vertebral destruction or spinal cord impingement is rare in brucellosis and usually suggests tuberculosis (p.1333).","Typhoid fever and malaria as causes of fever in endemic areas (p.1333).","Glandular fever-like illness (EBV, Toxoplasma, cytomegalovirus, HIV, or Mycobacterium tuberculosis) when hepatosplenomegaly or lymphadenopathy is present (p.1333).","Mumps and surgical conditions such as testicular torsion when epididymo-orchitis occurs (p.1333).","Neurotuberculosis, atypical leptospirosis, and noninfectious conditions when lymphocytic meningoencephalitis develops (p.1333)."],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculosis","Typhoid fever","Malaria","Leptospirosis","Epstein-Barr virus infection","Cytomegalovirus infection","Toxoplasmosis","HIV infection","Mumps"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Noninfectious causes of lymphocytic meningoencephalitis","Chronic fatigue syndromes (in the context of a suspected chronic brucellosis state)","Other causes of excessive sweating such as alcohol abuse and obesity"],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood, CSF, bone marrow, joint fluid, or tissue aspirate/biopsy culture under containment conditions for dangerous pathogens; alert the laboratory when brucellosis is suspected (p.1333).","Standard tube/plate/microagglutination serology as the mainstay, with complement fixation, Coombs antiglobulin, and ELISA tests; repeat after 2-4 weeks to detect a rising titer (p.1334).","Peripheral blood PCR / nucleic acid amplification (e.g., targeting rrs-rrl spacer, outer-membrane protein genes, IS711, BCSP31) which is more sensitive and quicker than culture and avoids biohazard risk (p.1334).","MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry for identification in well-resourced laboratories, with the caveat that earlier implementations often failed to identify Brucella correctly (p.1333).","Isotope bone scanning, more sensitive than plain x-ray for bony disease (p.1333).","CSF/joint fluid analysis showing lymphocytosis and low glucose; tissue biopsy may show noncaseating granulomas without acid-fast bacilli (p.1333)."],"redFlags":["Endocarditis, most often of the aortic valve (native or prosthetic), the leading cause of death in fatal cases (p.1333, p.1335).","Neurobrucellosis: lymphocytic meningoencephalitis mimicking neurotuberculosis, and rarely intracerebral abscess, cranial nerve deficits, or ruptured mycotic aneurysms (p.1333).","Vertebral osteomyelitis (spondylitis) and sacroiliitis (p.1333).","Disseminated intravascular coagulation with raised fibrinogen degradation products and thrombocytopenia (p.1333).","Increased fetal loss in infected pregnant women (though teratogenicity has not been described and abortion is much less pronounced in humans than in animals) (p.1333)."],"prognosis":"Fewer than 1% of patients die of brucellosis; when fatal, death is usually due to cardiac involvement and, more rarely, severe neurologic disease. Despite low mortality, recovery is slow and the illness can cause prolonged inactivity. Relapse occurs in up to 30% of poorly compliant patients, so clinical follow-up for up to 2 years is recommended; relapse responds to a prolonged repeat course of the original therapy. General well-being and body weight are better guides to absence of relapse than serology, since IgG agglutination titers can stay in the diagnostic range for more than 2 years after cure. Immunity is not solid and reinfection can occur after repeated exposures (p.1335).","pitfalls":["The clinical picture is not distinctive and definitive diagnostic signs may be lacking, so exposure history and laboratory support are essential (p.1331, p.1333).","Routine biochemistry is usually normal; peripheral leukocyte counts are typically normal or low with relative lymphocytosis, so normal blood counts do not exclude the disease (p.1333).","Brucella may be misidentified by gallery strips as Agrobacterium, Ochrobactrum, or Psychrobacter (Moraxella) phenylpyruvicus; after taxonomic changes Ochrobactrum isolates may now be reported as a Brucella subspecies despite completely different management, so culture reports should be confirmed with the laboratory before therapeutic decisions (p.1333-1334).","Rough strains such as B. canis and B. ovis do not react in conventional serologic tests and will give false-negative results; consult the laboratory if B. canis is suspected (p.1334).","Serologic cross-reactions occur with other gram-negative bacteria including Yersinia enterocolitica O:9, E. coli O157, Francisella tularensis, Salmonella enterica group N, Stenotrophomonas maltophilia, and Vibrio cholerae (p.1334).","Live B. abortus vaccine strain RB51 does not elicit antibody in tests using smooth antigens, which must be considered when following accidental vaccine exposures (p.1334).","Elevated CSF adenosine deaminase cannot be used to distinguish tubercular meningitis, as it may also be raised in brucellosis (p.1333).","Existence of a prolonged chronic brucellosis state after successful treatment remains controversial; evaluation requires careful exclusion of malingering, chronic fatigue syndromes, and other causes of sweating such as alcohol abuse and obesity (p.1335)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison distinguishes acute nonfocal brucellosis (duration under about 1-2 months) from complex or focal disease (e.g., spondylitis, neurobrucellosis, endocarditis), the latter requiring more prolonged and tailored therapy. Patterns by species are also noted: B. melitensis tends toward a more acute, aggressive presentation, B. suis toward focal abscess formation, and B. abortus toward a more insidious onset that is more likely to become chronic (p.1333-1334).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1331-1335"],"crossLinks":[]},"candidiasis":{"name":"Candidiasis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":222,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1706 (Chap. 222); supporting text Chap. 34 p.269, Chap. 60 p.432-433, Chap. 79 p.578-621","clinicalPearls":["Candidiasis spans a spectrum: mucocutaneous disease (oral thrush, esophageal, vulvovaginal, intertriginous, paronychia/onycholysis) that is usually localized, versus rare systemic, life-threatening invasive disease such as candidemia and disseminated candidiasis (Chap. 60, p.432-433).","The usual causative organism is Candida albicans; these yeasts are normal saprophytic inhabitants of the gastrointestinal tract that overgrow and cause disease when host defenses are altered (Chap. 60, p.432-433).","Oral candidiasis (thrush) appears as white plaques on the tongue or buccal mucosa; angular fissured macerated lesions at the mouth corners (perleche) are seen with poorly fitting dentures and may be candidal (Chap. 60, p.432-433).","Intertriginous candidal lesions are characteristically edematous, erythematous, and scaly with scattered 'satellite pustules'; in males the penis, scrotum, and inner thighs are often involved (Chap. 60, p.432-433).","In contrast to dermatophyte infections, candidal infections are frequently painful and accompanied by a marked inflammatory response (Chap. 60, p.432-433).","Disseminated candidiasis (often due to Candida tropicalis) may present with a triad of fever, myalgias, and eruptive nodules; hematogenous cutaneous disease shows multiple raised papules with systemic signs (rash chapter, p.190; Chap. 79, p.621).","Candidemia and chronic disseminated candidiasis are recognized complications in neutropenic patients because Candida species are endogenous gastrointestinal flora (Chap. 79, p.578).","Candidemia is a recognized cause of endogenous endophthalmitis from hematogenous seeding; Roth's spots and retinal hemorrhages were illustrated in a liver transplant patient with candidemia from immunosuppression (Chap. 34, p.269)."],"pathophysiology":"Candida species (most often C. albicans) are normal saprophytic commensals of the gastrointestinal tract and skin. They overgrow and become pathogenic when local or systemic host defenses are disrupted, e.g., by broad-spectrum antibiotic therapy, diabetes mellitus, or immunosuppression. Mucocutaneous disease results from overgrowth at moist, macerated surfaces; invasive disease arises when the organism gains bloodstream access, frequently via intravascular catheters or the gut in neutropenic hosts (endogenous flora), producing candidemia and hematogenous (disseminated) seeding to organs, skin, and the eye (Chap. 60, p.432-433; Chap. 79, p.578).","additionalDifferentials":["Dermatophytosis (tinea) on glabrous skin (Chap. 60, p.432)","Tinea (pityriasis) versicolor due to Malassezia furfur (Chap. 60, p.432)","Lichen planus / oral leukoplakia for oral white lesions (Chap. 60)","Other invasive fungal infections in neutropenia: invasive aspergillosis and other molds (Chap. 79, p.578)"],"infectionMimics":["Invasive aspergillosis and other molds (necrotic nodules) in immunocompromised hosts (Chap. 79, p.578; rash chapter)","Disseminated cryptococcosis (molluscum-like nodular lesions) (rash chapter)","Mucormycosis (rash chapter)","Bacterial/septic endophthalmitis, including from endocarditis (Roth's spots also in leukemia, diabetes) (Chap. 34, p.269)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Erythema nodosum (tender lower-extremity nodules) for nodular eruptions (rash chapter)","Sweet syndrome (acute febrile neutrophilic dermatosis) (rash chapter)","Psoriasis / eczema in intertriginous areas (Chap. 60)"],"additionalInvestigations":["KOH preparation of skin/mucosal scrapings demonstrating yeast and pseudohyphae for mucocutaneous candidiasis (Chap. 60, p.433)","Culture of lesional material (Chap. 60, p.433)","Blood cultures for candidemia (note insensitivity; see pitfalls) (Chap. 34, p.269; Chap. 79)","Dilated funduscopic examination to detect candidal endophthalmitis in candidemia (Chap. 34, p.269)"],"redFlags":["Candidemia or suspected disseminated candidiasis in a neutropenic or immunosuppressed host (Chap. 79, p.578)","Visual loss, ocular pain, or floaters in a patient with candidemia or indwelling IV catheters (endophthalmitis) (Chap. 34, p.269)","Fever, myalgias, and eruptive cutaneous nodules suggesting disseminated candidiasis (rash chapter, p.190)","Presence of an indwelling intravascular catheter or central line in a patient with candidemia (Chap. 34, p.269; Chap. 79)"],"prognosis":"Mucocutaneous candidiasis is generally benign and responds to removal of predisposing factors plus topical or, when needed, systemic antifungal therapy. Systemic involvement is rare but life-threatening; candidemia and chronic disseminated candidiasis are serious complications in neutropenic and immunosuppressed patients (Chap. 60, p.432-433; Chap. 79, p.578).","pitfalls":["Systemic therapy is usually reserved for immunosuppressed patients or those with chronic/recurrent disease who fail topical therapy, NOT routine mucocutaneous cases (Chap. 60, p.433).","Oral nystatin is effective ONLY for candidiasis of the gastrointestinal tract, not for systemic disease (Chap. 60, p.433).","Failure to remove predisposing factors (broad-spectrum antibiotics, chronic moisture/maceration) leads to treatment failure (Chap. 60, p.433).","In endogenous endophthalmitis, visual loss may sometimes be the ONLY symptom even without prominent ocular pain or injection (Chap. 34, p.269)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames candidiasis along a clinical spectrum rather than a formal grading scale: localized mucocutaneous disease (oral, esophageal, vulvovaginal, intertriginous, cutaneous) versus rare systemic/invasive disease (candidemia, chronic disseminated/hepatosplenic candidiasis). Invasive disease risk rises with prolonged profound neutropenia (ANC <500/uL) (Chap. 60, p.432-433; Chap. 79, p.578).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1706 (Chap. 222, Candidiasis)","Harrison 22e p.432-433 (Chap. 60, mucocutaneous candidiasis)","Harrison 22e p.269 (Chap. 34, endophthalmitis / candidemia)","Harrison 22e p.578-621 (Chap. 79, infections in cancer patients)","Harrison 22e p.190 (nodular eruptions in immunocompromised hosts)"],"crossLinks":["FEBRILE_NEUTROPENIA","DEVICE_INFECTION"]},"cat_scratch_disease":{"name":"Bartonellosis (incl. Cat-Scratch Disease)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":177,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1350-1356","clinicalPearls":["Bartonella are fastidious, facultative intracellular, slow-growing gram-negative bacteria; clinical presentation depends on both the infecting species and the host's immune status. Of the >40 species/subspecies, B. bacilliformis, B. quintana and B. henselae are most commonly identified human pathogens (p.1350).","B. henselae is the principal etiologic agent of cat-scratch disease (CSD); rare cases are associated with Afipia felis and other Bartonella species. Apparently healthy bacteremic cats (especially kittens) are the major reservoir; person-to-person transmission does not occur and intrafamilial clustering is rare (p.1351).","Typical CSD (85-90% of cases) is a usually self-limited subacute regional lymphadenopathy; a small (0.3-1 cm) painless erythematous papule/pustule appears at the inoculation site within days to 2 weeks in about one-third to two-thirds of patients, with nodes enlarging 1-3 weeks (or longer) after cat contact (p.1351-1352).","Affected nodes are usually painful and suppurate in ~10% of cases; axillary/epitrochlear nodes are most commonly involved, then head/neck, then inguinal/femoral. About 50% have fever, malaise and anorexia; weight loss and night sweats can mimic lymphoma (p.1351-1352).","Atypical (extranodal) CSD occurs in 10-15% and includes Parinaud's oculoglandular syndrome, neuroretinitis (often unilateral visual deterioration), hepatosplenic disease, encephalitis/seizures/myelitis/cranial-nerve palsies, FUO, pneumonitis, debilitating myalgia, arthritis/arthralgia (mostly women >20 yr), osteomyelitis (incl. multifocal) and erythema nodosum (p.1353).","CSD-associated FUO is a unique, sometimes severe and debilitating syndrome that often mimics malignancy and may involve multiple organs (hepatosplenic space-occupying lesions, abdominal/mediastinal lymphadenopathy, ocular disease, multifocal osteomyelitis); fever may be continuous or relapsing (p.1353).","In elderly patients (>60 yr), lymphadenopathy is more often absent, whereas encephalitis and FUO are more common than in younger patients (p.1353).","Coxiella burnetii and Bartonella species are the most common causes of culture-negative endocarditis; in one French series Bartonella accounted for 28% of 348 culture-negative endocarditis cases. B. quintana and B. henselae are the species most often implicated (p.1353).","Bartonella endocarditis often presents as a prolonged, minimally febrile or even afebrile indolent illness lasting weeks to months; initial echocardiography may not show vegetations and acute aggressive disease is rare. Most cases are native-valve, ~60% aortic; B. henselae usually infects preexisting valvulopathy while B. quintana often infects normal valves (p.1353).","Bacillary angiomatosis (caused by B. henselae or B. quintana) is a neovascular proliferative disease of severely immunocompromised patients, presenting as non-painful tan/red/purple cutaneous lesions, tender subcutaneous nodules, ulcerated plaques or verrucous growths; nodular forms resemble fungal or mycobacterial infection (p.1354-1355).","Bacillary peliosis is a related angioproliferative disorder caused by B. henselae involving primarily the liver (peliosis hepatis) and also spleen and lymph nodes, with blood-filled cystic structures (microscopic to several mm). In advanced immunodeficiency, B. henselae and B. quintana are important causes of FUO (p.1355).","Trench fever (5-day/quintan fever) is caused by B. quintana, transmitted by the human body louse; it has reemerged as chronic bacteremia in homeless people (urban/contemporary trench fever), with alcoholism and louse infestation as risk factors (p.1354)."],"pathophysiology":"Bartonella adapt to specific domestic/wild mammalian hosts where prolonged intraerythrocytic and endothelial-cell infection creates an immune-protected reservoir, producing chronic bacteremia that may persist even after prolonged antimicrobial therapy. Adhesion and entry to endothelial cells and erythrocytes is facilitated by Bartonella adhesin A or variably expressed outer-membrane proteins; the organism evades host immunity by modifying virulence factors (lipopolysaccharide, flagella) and attenuating the immune response. Humans are incidental hosts for most species except B. quintana and B. bacilliformis (which are not zoonotic). In CSD, the cat-to-human transmission mode is undetermined: B. henselae-infected cat saliva spreads to claws by self-licking and contaminated flea feces can be inoculated by a scratch or bite (mucous-membrane/conjunctival inoculation also possible). Lymphatic drainage to regional nodes in immunocompetent hosts triggers a TH1 response producing necrotizing granulomatous lymphadenitis, with dendritic cells and chemokines driving granuloma formation. In bacillary angiomatosis, Bartonella angiogenic factor A (a proangiogenic autotransporter in B. henselae) activates the host vascular endothelial growth factor pathway to drive angiogenesis (p.1350-1355).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Pyogenic (bacterial) lymphadenitis","Mycobacterial infection (incl. tuberculous lymphadenitis)","Brucellosis","Syphilis","Tularemia","Plague","Toxoplasmosis","Sporotrichosis","Histoplasmosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Lymphoma / malignancy (weight loss and night sweats of CSD, and CSD-associated FUO, can mimic malignancy)","Kaposi's sarcoma (differential for cutaneous bacillary angiomatosis)","Pyogenic granuloma (differential for cutaneous bacillary angiomatosis)","Subcutaneous tumors (differential for cutaneous bacillary angiomatosis)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serology (immunofluorescence or enzyme immunoassay) is the most commonly used diagnostic, but has variable sensitivity/specificity; CSD serodiagnosis is often based on IgG alone (in the absence of IgM) and seroconversion may take a few weeks, complicating interpretation (p.1352).","PCR of pus aspirated from a lymph node or the primary inoculation lesion is highly sensitive and specific and is particularly useful for definitive, rapid diagnosis in seronegative patients; PCR of a lymph node biopsy specimen may be less sensitive (possibly due to sampling error) (p.1352).","Histopathology shows early lymphoid hyperplasia, later stellate granulomata with necrosis, coalescing microabscesses and occasional multinucleated giant cells (nonspecific but may narrow the differential) (p.1352).","For endocarditis: blood cultures (even with lysis-centrifugation or EDTA tubes) are positive in only ~25% of cases (mostly B. quintana, rarely B. henselae) and require prolonged incubation up to 6 weeks; high-titer (>=1:800) IgG serology supports the diagnosis but cross-reacts between B. quintana and B. henselae and with C. burnetii and Chlamydia; species-level identification usually requires PCR and DNA sequencing of valve tissue (p.1353).","For bacillary angiomatosis/peliosis: diagnosis is by histologic examination; Warthin-Starry silver staining of lesions reveals clusters of bacilli; cultures are usually negative though blood cultures may be positive (p.1355)."],"redFlags":["Persistent constitutional symptoms with failure of spontaneous regression of node size, and negative acute- and convalescent-phase sera (10-20% of CSD patients) — malignancy must be ruled out (p.1352).","Seronegative CSD with lymphadenopathy and severe complications (encephalitis or neuroretinitis) — early biopsy is important to establish a specific diagnosis (p.1352).","Unilateral visual deterioration (neuroretinitis) in suspected CSD — ophthalmologic manifestations may occasionally cause moderate to severe vision loss (p.1353).","Culture-negative endocarditis, especially in a homeless/alcoholic patient with body louse infestation (B. quintana) or significant cat exposure (B. henselae) (p.1353)."],"prognosis":"In immunocompetent individuals, CSD (whether typical or atypical) usually resolves without treatment and without sequelae; resolution is slow, requiring weeks for fever/pain and months for node shrinkage, and lifelong immunity is the rule. Some ophthalmologic manifestations may occasionally result in moderate to severe vision loss. Bartonella endocarditis is often diagnosed late, and the proportion of patients requiring valvular surgery (~60%) is high, probably as a consequence of delayed diagnosis (p.1352-1353).","pitfalls":["Bartonella is a key but easily missed cause of culture-negative endocarditis; routine blood cultures are positive in only ~25% of cases and serology cross-reacts with C. burnetii and Chlamydia, so a high titer to one organism may reflect cross-reactivity rather than the true pathogen (p.1353).","CSD is a frequently overlooked cause of FUO; CSD-associated FUO can mimic malignancy with multiorgan space-occupying lesions, leading to unnecessary investigation for cancer (p.1353).","Negative serology does not exclude CSD: 10-20% of patients have negative acute- and convalescent-phase sera, and seroconversion may take weeks; PCR of aspirated pus is preferred in seronegative patients (p.1352).","Suppurative nodes should be drained by large-bore needle aspiration, NOT by incision and drainage, to avoid chronic draining tracts (p.1353).","In elderly patients, lymphadenopathy is more often absent, so CSD may be missed when it presents instead as encephalitis or FUO (p.1353)."],"severityClassification":"Cat-scratch disease is described along clinical lines rather than a formal grading scale: (1) Typical CSD (85-90%) — subacute regional lymphadenopathy, usually self-limited; (2) Atypical/extranodal CSD (10-15%) — extranodal manifestations (Parinaud's oculoglandular syndrome, neuroretinitis, hepatosplenic disease, neurologic disease, FUO, osteomyelitis, etc.). Severe/disseminated Bartonella disease (bacillary angiomatosis and peliosis) occurs primarily in HIV-infected persons with CD4+ T-cell counts <100/uL and in other immunosuppressed patients; Bartonella endocarditis is a separate severe culture-negative-endocarditis presentation (p.1351-1355).","references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e, Chapter 177: Bartonella Infections, Including Cat-Scratch Disease (Giladi M, Ephros M), pp.1350-1356","Edouard S et al: Bartonella quintana and Bartonella henselae endocarditis: A study (cited in Ch.177)","Koehler JE et al: Molecular epidemiology of Bartonella infections in patients with bacillary angiomatosis-peliosis. N Engl J Med 337:1876, 1997","Landes M et al: Cat scratch disease presenting as fever of unknown origin (cited in Ch.177)","Rolain JM et al: Antimicrob Agents Chemother 48:1921, 2004 (basis for Table 177-2)"],"crossLinks":[]},"cestode_infections":{"name":"Cestode (Tapeworm) Infections","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":242,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1832","clinicalPearls":["Cestodes (tapeworms) cause two clinically distinct patterns: intestinal infection by adult worms (taeniasis) and somatic/larval-tissue infection (e.g., cysticercosis, hydatid disease) (Harrison 22e p.1832).","Neurocysticercosis is the most common parasitic disease of the CNS worldwide; humans acquire it by ingesting food contaminated with Taenia solium eggs, not by eating cysticerci-containing pork (which instead causes intestinal taeniasis) (Harrison 22e p.1833, p.1138).","Seizures are the most frequent manifestation of neurocysticercosis and are associated with inflammation of brain parenchyma around the cysticercus; new-onset partial seizures with or without secondary generalization are the classic presentation (Harrison 22e p.1833, p.1138).","On neuroimaging a cystic lesion with a mural nodule representing the scolex ('dot-in-hole') is diagnostic of neurocysticercosis; lesions evolve through vesicular, colloidal, granulonodular and nodular-calcified stages (Harrison 22e p.1833-1834, p.1138).","CT is more sensitive for calcified lesions, whereas MRI is more sensitive for small cystic lesions, scolices and enhancement; FIESTA/3D-CISS sequences best detect ventricular and subarachnoid cysts (Harrison 22e p.1834).","The enzyme-linked immunoelectrotransfer blot (EITB) using lentil-lectin-purified glycoproteins is >99% specific/sensitive in patients with multiple cysts, but patients with a single intracranial lesion or only calcifications may be seronegative; serum is more sensitive than CSF (Harrison 22e p.1834).","Crude-antigen ELISA antibody tests have high false-positive and false-negative rates and should be avoided for neurocysticercosis (Harrison 22e p.1834).","In endemic-area patients with a single enhancing 5-20 mm round CT lesion, seizures, a normal exam and no systemic disease (no fever, adenopathy or chest abnormality), the lesion is almost always neurocysticercosis (validated in India) (Harrison 22e p.1834).","Antiparasitic treatment of neurocysticercosis is never an emergency; initial management focuses on controlling seizures and hydrocephalus and on excluding intraocular disease before any cysticidal therapy (Harrison 22e p.1834).","High-dose glucocorticoids should always accompany antiparasitic therapy of neurocysticercosis, since dying parasites provoke an inflammatory response that can worsen seizures or hydrocephalus (Harrison 22e p.1834).","Bruns' syndrome is intermittent acute hydrocephalus triggered by positional change, caused by a ventricular cysticercus acting as a ball valve (Harrison 22e p.1833).","In Latin America and immigrants from Latin America, T. solium (neurocysticercosis) is the most common cause of brain abscess / focal CNS mass lesion (Harrison 22e p.1136).","Echinococcosis (hydatid disease) is larval Echinococcus infection: E. granulosus sensu lato causes unilocular cystic hydatid disease; E. multilocularis causes locally invasive, tumor-like alveolar disease (Harrison 22e p.1835-1836).","In cystic echinococcosis the liver is involved in about two-thirds and the lungs in about 20% of cases; alveolar echinococcosis involves the liver in nearly all cases (Harrison 22e p.1836).","Echinococcal cysts often remain asymptomatic for years and are frequently found incidentally on x-ray or ultrasound (Harrison 22e p.1836).","Identification of daughter cysts within a larger cyst, or eggshell/mural calcification on CT, is diagnostic of cystic echinococcosis; 'hydatid sand' is visible protoscolices/brood capsules within cyst fluid (Harrison 22e p.1837).","ELISA/immunoblot serology is positive in about 90% of hepatic hydatid disease but only about 50% of pulmonary cysts, and a negative test cannot exclude echinococcosis (Harrison 22e p.1837).","The fish tapeworm Dibothriocephalus latus (formerly Diphyllobothrium latum) is the longest human tapeworm (up to 25 m) and its avid vitamin B12 receptors can cause B12 deficiency with megaloblastic/pernicious-type anemia and neurologic sequelae (Harrison 22e p.1838-1839).","Rodentolepis nana (dwarf tapeworm, formerly Hymenolepis nana) is the most common human cestode infection and the only one not requiring an intermediate host, perpetuated by intestinal autoinfection (Harrison 22e p.1838)."],"pathophysiology":"Cestodes are segmented flatworms (Platyhelminthes, subphylum Cestoda) whose adult worms live in the gut of the definitive host while larval cystic forms inhabit tissues of the intermediate host. The adult tapeworm attaches to intestinal mucosa by suckers or hooks on a scolex; proglottids bud from the neck to form the ribbon-like strobila and shed eggs. In intestinal taeniasis humans are definitive hosts (T. saginata, T. asiatica, T. solium, D. latus, D. caninum). In somatic disease, ingested eggs release oncospheres that penetrate the gut wall, disseminate via blood, and encyst as a cysticercus (single scolex), coenurus (multiple scolices) or hydatid (cyst with daughter cysts and protoscolices). Cysticercosis follows ingestion of T. solium eggs (fecal-oral, including autoinfection in tapeworm carriers); lodged cysticerci initially provoke little inflammation, but as the cyst degenerates an inflammatory response develops, producing seizures, and accompanying arachnoiditis or ventricular obstruction can cause hydrocephalus. In echinococcosis ingested eggs of canine tapeworms release oncospheres that seed liver, lung and other organs; E. granulosus forms slowly enlarging unilocular hydatid cysts containing brood capsules and protoscolices, while E. multilocularis grows by invasive peripheral budding of the germinal layer like a malignancy. (Harrison 22e p.1832-1837)","additionalDifferentials":["Intestinal taeniasis presenting only with passage of proglottids or vague abdominal symptoms (T. saginata, T. asiatica, T. solium, D. latus, D. caninum)","Megaloblastic / pernicious-type anemia from vitamin B12 deficiency in D. latus fish tapeworm infection","Seizure disorder / epilepsy of other cause in regions where neurocysticercosis is endemic","Obstructive or communicating hydrocephalus from ventricular or subarachnoid cysticerci","Sparganosis presenting as a migrating subcutaneous, periorbital or CNS mass (Spirometra plerocercoid)","Coenurosis presenting as a CNS or subcutaneous space-occupying cystic lesion (T. multiceps, T. serialis)"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculoma / CNS tuberculosis (major cause of focal CNS mass lesions in India and East Asia)","Toxoplasma encephalitis (ring-enhancing CNS lesions, especially in immunocompromised/HIV)","Pyogenic brain abscess","Other tissue helminths producing CNS or hepatic cysts (e.g., schistosomiasis, sparganosis)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Primary or metastatic brain tumor (parenchymal neurocysticercosis or spinal cysticercus can mimic intraspinal tumor)","CNS lymphoma","Hepatic neoplasm or simple/biliary hepatic cyst (alveolar echinococcosis mimics a slow-growing liver tumor; cystic hydatid mimics benign liver cyst)","Cholelithiasis (biliary leakage/compression by a hepatic hydatid cyst)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool microscopy for eggs/proglottids; perianal cellophane-tape swab when taeniasis suspected (eggs of the three Taenia species are morphologically indistinguishable)","Examination of mature proglottids or scolex, or molecular testing, to distinguish T. solium from T. saginata/T. asiatica","Neuroimaging (MRI and CT) for neurocysticercosis, including FIESTA/3D-CISS for ventricular/subarachnoid cysts and susceptibility-weighted imaging for calcifications","Enzyme-linked immunoelectrotransfer blot (EITB) on serum for cysticercosis antibody","CSF analysis (lymphocytic, neutrophilic or eosinophilic pleocytosis; protein may be raised; glucose usually normal but occasionally markedly reduced)","Funduscopic / dilated ophthalmologic exam in all patients with suspected neurocysticercosis","Ultrasound, CT or MRI with WHO ultrasound staging (CL, CE1-CE5) for hepatic cystic echinococcosis; chest x-ray/CT for pulmonary cysts","ELISA/immunoblot serology for echinococcosis (cannot exclude disease if negative)","PET to follow disease activity in alveolar echinococcosis"],"redFlags":["New-onset seizures or focal neurologic deficit in a patient from a cysticercosis-endemic area (e.g., Latin America, sub-Saharan Africa, China, India, Southeast Asia)","Signs of raised intracranial pressure or obstructive hydrocephalus (headache, vomiting, papilledema, altered mental status) from intraventricular/subarachnoid cysticerci","Bruns' syndrome: intermittent acute hydrocephalus precipitated by head position change","Subarachnoid/basal cysticerci causing chronic meningitis, arachnoiditis, vasculitis, infarction or hemorrhage","Sudden pruritus, urticaria, eosinophilia or anaphylaxis from rupture/leakage of a hydatid cyst","Pulmonary hydatid rupture into the bronchial tree (sudden expectoration of cyst fluid/membranes) or pleura (hydatid empyema)"],"prognosis":"Intestinal tapeworm infections (taeniasis, R. nana, diphyllobothriasis, dipylidiasis) are usually mild or asymptomatic and respond to a single course of treatment. In neurocysticercosis, single enhancing lesions often resolve and seizure medication can frequently be tapered after about 6 months; calcified lesions carry a higher risk of recurrent seizures, particularly with perilesional edema, and subarachnoid/ventricular (extraparenchymal) disease is more difficult to treat and requires prolonged therapy. Cystic echinococcosis is often curable with stage-appropriate medical therapy, PAIR or surgery, with relapse rates after skilled PAIR comparable to surgery; inactive CE4/CE5 cysts need only periodic surveillance. Alveolar echinococcosis behaves like a malignancy: most cases are diagnosed too late for complete resection and require indefinite antiparasitic therapy, since even after liver transplantation immunosuppression promotes parasite recurrence. (Harrison 22e p.1834-1838)","pitfalls":["Do not aspirate or rupture a hydatid cyst carelessly: spillage of protoscolices can disseminate infection and provoke anaphylaxis; aspiration/PAIR should only be done by experienced operators with albendazole cover and through solid tissue.","Never start antiparasitic drugs in neurocysticercosis with elevated intracranial pressure, and avoid them in diffuse cerebral edema from multiple inflamed lesions (glucocorticoids are the mainstay there).","Always give high-dose glucocorticoid cover when treating neurocysticercosis, because dying cysticerci can worsen seizures and hydrocephalus.","Treating T. solium intestinal taeniasis with praziquantel may occasionally trigger a CNS inflammatory response if occult (cryptic) cysticercosis is present.","Avoid antiparasitic drugs before neurosurgical removal of ventricular cysticerci: they make the cyst friable and harder to extract.","Crude-antigen ELISA antibody tests for cysticercosis are unreliable (high false-positive/false-negative rates) and should not be used.","A negative echinococcosis serology cannot exclude the diagnosis, and sensitivity is especially low (~50%) for pulmonary cysts.","Eggs/proglottids are shed intermittently, so single stool examination may miss intestinal taeniasis; Taenia species eggs are morphologically indistinguishable.","For ocular and spinal cysticercosis, drug-induced inflammation may cause irreversible damage; intraocular disease should be managed surgically.","Infusion of scolicidal agents during surgery is no longer recommended because of hypernatremia, intoxication and sclerosing cholangitis."],"severityClassification":"Cystic echinococcosis of the liver is staged by the WHO Informal Working Group ultrasound classification, reflecting cyst viability: active cysts (CL = cystic lesion with no visible wall, CE1 = visible wall with internal echoes/snowflake sign, CE2 = visible wall with internal septation), transitional cysts (CE3a = detached laminar membranes, CE3b = partially collapsed), and inactive cysts (CE4 = nonhomogeneous mass, CE5 = thick calcified wall). Neurocysticercosis is categorized by parasite location and viability and by lesion stage on imaging (vesicular, colloidal, granulonodular, nodular-calcified); diagnosis uses the Del Brutto revised criteria (absolute, neuroimaging [major/confirmative/minor], and clinical/exposure criteria). (Harrison 22e p.1834-1837)","references":["Harrison 22e p.1832","Harrison 22e p.1136-1138","Del Brutto OH et al: Revised diagnostic criteria for neurocysticercosis. J Neurol Sci 372:202, 2017","Brunetti E et al: Expert consensus for the diagnosis and treatment of cystic and alveolar echinococcosis in humans. Acta Trop 114:1, 2010","White AC Jr et al: Diagnosis and treatment of neurocysticercosis: 2017 clinical practice guidelines"],"crossLinks":["seizure_epilepsy","BRAIN_ABSCESS","LIVER_ABSCESS"]},"chlamydial_infection":{"name":"Chlamydial Infections","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":194,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1467","clinicalPearls":["C. trachomatis genital infection is the most prevalent bacterial STI worldwide and is reported more than any other infectious disease in the United States; case counts are heavily dependent on screening coverage (Harrison 22e p.1469).","Most genital infections are silent: up to 80-90% of women and >50% of men with C. trachomatis genital infection lack symptoms, and pharyngeal/rectal infection is typically asymptomatic and may persist for years, sustaining a large transmission reservoir (Harrison 22e p.1469).","C. trachomatis is the most common cause of nongonococcal urethritis (NGU) and postgonococcal urethritis (PGU), implicated in 30-50% of NGU cases (Harrison 22e p.1469-1470).","NAATs are the CDC-recommended diagnostic assay of choice; less-invasive specimens (urine for both sexes, vaginal swabs for women) are acceptable, and self-collected or clinician-collected vaginal swabs are slightly more sensitive than urine in women (Harrison 22e p.1472-1473).","Because of frequent co-infection, testing for or treating chlamydia should be considered in patients diagnosed with N. gonorrhoeae (Harrison 22e p.1474).","Trachoma (serovars A, B, Ba, C) remains a leading cause of preventable infectious blindness worldwide; the WHO SAFE-era programs target elimination as a public health problem by 2030 (Harrison 22e p.1474-1475).","LGV (serovars L1, L2, L3) is an invasive STI presenting classically as a transient painless genital ulcer followed by painful inguinal lymphadenopathy with bubo formation, or as hemorrhagic proctocolitis in MSM (Harrison 22e p.1469, 1472).","C. pneumoniae causes atypical (community-acquired) pneumonia, bronchitis, pharyngitis and sinusitis, accounting for as many as 10% of CAP cases though more typically 1-2%; PCR of respiratory secretions is the preferred diagnostic method (Harrison 22e p.1476).","Psittacosis (C. psittaci) is a zoonosis acquired from birds (psittacines, poultry) presenting as atypical pneumonia or systemic febrile illness; person-to-person transmission has never been demonstrated (Harrison 22e p.1475).","Neonatal exposure during vaginal delivery causes inclusion conjunctivitis (in ~20-30% of exposed infants) and, in 10-15%, pneumonia at 2 weeks to 4 months of age; standard gonococcal ocular prophylaxis does NOT prevent chlamydial conjunctivitis (Harrison 22e p.1471-1472)."],"pathophysiology":"Chlamydiae are nonmotile, gram-negative, obligate intracellular bacteria that lack peptidoglycan (structural integrity depends on disulfide cross-linking of outer-membrane proteins) and are energy parasites using host-cell ATP. They have a unique biphasic growth cycle alternating between the infectious, extracellular-adapted elementary body (0.25-0.35 um) and the metabolically active, non-infectious intracellular reticulate body (0.5-1 um), which divides by binary fission within a membrane-bound cytoplasmic inclusion while preventing phagosome-lysosome fusion; the cycle lasts ~48-72 h, giving a long clinical incubation (generally 1-3 weeks). C. trachomatis has two biovars (trachoma and LGV) and 18 serovars (trachoma A/B/Ba/C; oculogenital D-K; LGV L1-L3); the trachoma biovar parasitizes squamocolumnar epithelium whereas the more invasive LGV biovar involves lymphoid cells. Disease is driven largely by immunopathology rather than direct cytotoxicity: the chlamydial heat-shock protein shares epitopes with human and other bacterial proteins and, with repeated/persistent infection, drives fibrosis and mucosal scarring leading to tubal-factor infertility, ectopic pregnancy and chronic pelvic pain (genital) or cicatricial trichiasis and blindness (ocular). Antigenic variation in the major outer-membrane protein (omp1) permits reinfection because immunity to one serovar is not protective against others (Harrison 22e p.1467-1469).","additionalDifferentials":["Neisseria gonorrhoeae urethritis/cervicitis/proctitis (clinically indistinguishable; frequent co-infection)","Mycoplasma genitalium (now a leading cause of Chlamydia-negative and persistent NGU)","Trichomonas vaginalis and herpes simplex virus as causes of NGU","Mycoplasma pneumoniae and other atypical pneumonia pathogens (for C. pneumoniae)","Crohn's disease of the rectum (mimicked by LGV proctocolitis on sigmoidoscopy and histology)","Adenovirus or HSV follicular conjunctivitis (mimics adult inclusion/oculogenital conjunctivitis)"],"infectionMimics":["Neisseria gonorrhoeae (urethritis, cervicitis, PID, proctitis, ophthalmia neonatorum)","Mycoplasma genitalium urethritis/cervicitis","Trichomonas vaginalis urethritis","Herpes simplex virus (urethritis, proctitis, ulcerative conjunctivitis)","Haemophilus influenzae or Streptococcus pneumoniae neonatal conjunctivitis","Mycoplasma pneumoniae and other atypical CAP agents (for C. pneumoniae pneumonia)","Adenovirus follicular conjunctivitis","Haemophilus ducreyi and other genital-ulcer pathogens (for LGV primary lesion)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Crohn's disease of the rectum (resembles LGV/serovar proctocolitis: granulomas, giant cells, crypt abscesses)","Testicular torsion or testicular tumor (mimic chlamydial epididymitis; torsion must be excluded promptly)","Intrauterine contraceptive device-induced cervical inflammation","Functional urinary symptoms / non-infectious dysuria (acute urethral syndrome in women)","Reactive arthritis / spondyloarthropathy in the differential of HLA-B27-associated joint disease"],"additionalInvestigations":["NAAT (nucleic acid amplification test) on urine, vaginal/cervical, urethral, rectal, pharyngeal or conjunctival specimens - diagnostic assay of choice","First-void urine or vaginal swab self-collection for screening programs and outreach","Microscopy for leukocytes (Gram stain of urethral exudate or cervical mucus; first-void urine pyuria; leukocyte esterase) to support presumptive diagnosis of urethritis/cervicitis","Giemsa-stained smears for intracytoplasmic inclusions (conjunctival scrapings, neonatal specimens; less sensitive than NAAT/culture)","Microimmunofluorescence (MIF) serology - test of choice for LGV and neonatal pneumonia and the serologic gold standard, though labor-intensive and largely research-only","Complement fixation (CF) serology (genus-level only via LPS; fourfold titer rise supports psittacosis)","PCR of respiratory secretions for C. pneumoniae (preferred) and respiratory NAAT for C. psittaci","Cell culture (former gold standard) - now performed only in specialized laboratories","Sigmoidoscopy with rectal biopsy for proctitis/LGV (granulomas, crypt abscesses)","Laparoscopy / endometrial biopsy (plasma-cell endometritis) in suspected salpingitis/PID and perihepatitis","Serovar/molecular genotyping when LGV is suspected (longer treatment duration required)"],"redFlags":["Acute unilateral testicular pain - exclude testicular torsion promptly by imaging or surgical exploration before attributing to epididymitis","Failure of unilateral intrascrotal pain/swelling to respond to antimicrobials - exclude testicular tumor or chronic infection such as tuberculosis","Hemorrhagic proctocolitis with severe anorectal pain in MSM - consider invasive LGV serovars (often with HIV co-infection)","Right-upper-quadrant pain/fever/nausea in a sexually active woman - suspect perihepatitis (Fitz-Hugh-Curtis), associated with extensive tubal scarring","Trichiasis and entropion in trachoma - lashes abrade the cornea leading to ulceration and blindness if untreated","Severe psittacosis pneumonia - can be fatal in up to 10% of untreated cases and may require ICU care, with hepatitis, endocarditis or myocarditis","Untreated maternal infection - risk of neonatal inclusion conjunctivitis and infant pneumonia"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated genital chlamydial infection responds well to recommended antibiotics, and clinically significant drug resistance has not been observed in C. trachomatis; apparent treatment failure usually reflects non-adherence or reinfection. However, untreated or recurrent genital infection causes substantial long-term morbidity through mucosal scarring - PID with tubal-factor infertility, ectopic pregnancy, and chronic pelvic pain - and women bear the greatest burden of sequelae. Reactive arthritis episodes typically last 2-6 months. Untreated ocular disease progresses to blinding trachoma; the WHO estimates ~6 million people have been blinded and ~1.3 million remain blind from trachoma. Untreated psittacosis pneumonia can be fatal in up to 10% of cases. Previous chlamydial infection raises the risk of reinfection, prompting rescreening at 3 months (Harrison 22e p.1469-1475).","pitfalls":["NAATs detect nucleic acid, not viable organisms - residual DNA may yield a positive result for up to 3 weeks after effective therapy, so NAATs should not be used as a test of cure before 3 weeks; routine test of cure is not recommended except in pregnancy.","Single-dose penicillins or cephalosporins used for gonorrhea lack activity against chlamydiae - hence postgonococcal urethritis and the need to co-treat or exclude chlamydia.","Standard gonococcal ocular prophylaxis does NOT prevent neonatal chlamydial conjunctivitis; the most effective prevention is antenatal screening and treatment.","Chlamydial and gonococcal conjunctivitis cannot be distinguished from other neonatal conjunctivitis on clinical grounds - laboratory diagnosis is required.","CF and LPS-based serologic tests identify chlamydiae only to the genus level (cross-reactive across all chlamydiae) and must be interpreted cautiously.","Serology (including a single high IgG titer) over-attributes C. pneumoniae to atypical pneumonia; cross-reactivity limits specificity, and PCR is preferred.","Serology is never recommended for diagnosis of uncomplicated genital cervical/urethral/lower-tract infection or for asymptomatic screening.","Fluoroquinolones are contraindicated in pregnancy; beta-lactams and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole are not active against C. pneumoniae.","Most genital infections are asymptomatic, so clinical recognition of cervicitis requires a high index of suspicion - no genital symptom is specific for chlamydial cervical infection.","C. psittaci is a biohazard category B biothreat agent associated with laboratory-acquired infections."],"severityClassification":"No formal grading scale. Genital C. trachomatis infections are categorized as uncomplicated (lower genital tract) versus complicated (e.g., PID, epididymitis) requiring longer therapy; LGV is treated as invasive disease with extended therapy. Trachoma is described by phase - active trachoma (follicular conjunctivitis) versus cicatricial disease (conjunctival scarring, trichiasis, entropion) - underpinning the WHO clinical signs used for diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1474-1475).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1467","Harrison 22e p.1468","Harrison 22e p.1469","Harrison 22e p.1470","Harrison 22e p.1471","Harrison 22e p.1472","Harrison 22e p.1473","Harrison 22e p.1474","Harrison 22e p.1475","Harrison 22e p.1476","Harrison 22e p.1477"],"crossLinks":["gonorrhea"]},"cholera":{"name":"Cholera","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":173,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1325","clinicalPearls":["Cholera is an acute toxin-mediated diarrhoeal disease caused by Vibrio cholerae serogroup O1 or O139 (the serogroups with epidemic potential) that can progress to profound, rapidly progressive dehydration and death within hours (p.1325-1326).","After a 24-48 h incubation in a non-immune individual, illness begins with the sudden onset of painless watery diarrhoea that can rapidly become voluminous; vomiting is common and fever is usually absent (p.1327).","The characteristic stool is non-bilious, gray, slightly cloudy fluid flecked with mucus, containing no blood, with a fishy, inoffensive odour, described as rice-water stool because it resembles water in which rice has been washed (p.1327).","In severe cases (cholera gravis) volume loss can exceed 250 mL/kg in the first 24 h, and muscle cramps from electrolyte disturbance are common (p.1327).","Symptoms parallel the degree of volume contraction: thirst at <5% body-weight loss; postural hypotension, weakness, tachycardia and decreased skin turgor at 5-10%; and oliguria, weak or absent pulses, sunken eyes (sunken fontanelles in infants), wrinkled washerwoman skin, somnolence and coma at >10% (p.1327-1328).","If fluids and electrolytes are replaced adequately, complications are averted and the illness is self-limited, resolving in a few days (p.1328)."],"pathophysiology":"Cholera is fundamentally a toxin-mediated disease. After ingestion (most often via faecally contaminated water, though contaminated food and human-to-human transmission also contribute), V. cholerae colonises the small intestine, a process dependent on the toxin-coregulated pilus (TCP), with cholera toxin, TCP and other virulence factors coordinately regulated by ToxR and by quorum sensing. The organism elaborates cholera toxin, a protein enterotoxin made of a monomeric enzymatic A subunit and a pentameric B-subunit binding moiety. The B pentamer binds GM1 ganglioside on the epithelial cell surface, delivering the A subunit, whose activated form (A1) irreversibly ADP-ribosylates the GTP-binding regulatory component of adenylate cyclase. This upregulates adenylate cyclase, raising intracellular cyclic AMP, which inhibits absorptive sodium transport in villus cells and activates secretory chloride transport in crypt cells, driving accumulation of sodium chloride in the lumen; water follows passively to maintain osmolality, producing isotonic fluid that exceeds the gut's reabsorptive capacity and results in watery diarrhoea. Cholera toxin also enhances secretion via prostaglandins and/or neural histamine receptors. Unless lost fluid and electrolytes are replaced, profound dehydration leads to hypovolaemic shock and bicarbonate loss leads to acidosis. The cholera toxin genes (ctxAB) are carried on the bacteriophage CTX-phi, which uses TCP as its receptor, allowing horizontal transfer to generate new toxigenic strains (p.1326-1327).","additionalDifferentials":["Other secretory/toxigenic watery diarrhoeas: enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli (produces a heat-labile toxin like cholera toxin), and other dehydrating secretory diarrhoeal illnesses to which the term cholera has historically and loosely been applied (p.1325)","Diarrhoea due to non-O1/O139 (noncholera) V. cholerae, which generally do not produce cholera toxin and do not cause large epidemics (p.1330)"],"infectionMimics":["Enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli (heat-labile enterotoxin resembling cholera toxin causing secretory diarrhoea) (p.1325-1326)","Other Vibrio gastroenteritis, e.g. Vibrio parahaemolyticus and non-O1/O139 V. cholerae (p.1330)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Severely dehydrating secretory diarrhoea of non-infectious aetiology, to which the term cholera has at times been loosely applied (p.1325)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool culture on a selective medium such as taurocholate-tellurite-gelatin (TTG) agar or thiosulfate-citrate-bile salts-sucrose (TCBS) agar; the organism must be specifically sought (p.1328)","Direct dark-field microscopy of a wet mount of fresh stool, with serotype discernible by immobilisation with specific antisera (p.1328)","Point-of-care antigen-detection cholera dipstick (rapid diagnostic test) for field use or where laboratory facilities are lacking (p.1328)","Cary-Blair transport medium and/or alkaline-peptone water-enrichment medium if sample processing will be delayed (p.1328)","Vibrios are oxidase-positive; standard Enterobacteriaceae biochemical testing suffices for identification (p.1328)","Supportive labs in dehydrated patients: elevated haematocrit (haemoconcentration) in non-anaemic patients, mild neutrophilic leukocytosis, raised blood urea nitrogen and creatinine (prerenal azotaemia), normal sodium/potassium/chloride, markedly reduced bicarbonate (<15 mmol/L), elevated anion gap and low arterial pH (~7.2) (p.1328)"],"redFlags":["Profound, rapidly progressive dehydration developing within hours (p.1325)","Signs of severe dehydration: unconsciousness, lethargy or floppiness, weak or absent pulse, inability to drink, sunken eyes (sunken fontanelles in infants), >10% loss of body weight (p.1328)","Voluminous ongoing stool losses (volume loss exceeding 250 mL/kg in the first 24 h in severe cases) (p.1327)","Features of hypovolaemic shock, oliguria, somnolence and coma (p.1327-1328)","Acute watery diarrhoea or death from acute watery diarrhoea in a patient >=5 years, even where cholera is not known to be present (p.1328)"],"prognosis":"Death from cholera is due to hypovolaemic shock. Historically the disease carried high mortality, but prompt aggressive fluid repletion and supportive care can obviate this. With adequate fluid and electrolyte replacement, complications are averted and the illness is self-limited, resolving over a few days. Complications derive exclusively from volume and electrolyte depletion and include renal failure due to acute tubular necrosis. Untreated cholera carries very high case-fatality (historically >50% in untreated severe cases), which falls dramatically with rehydration (p.1326, p.1328).","pitfalls":["The organism is not recovered on routine media; V. cholerae must be specifically sought using selective medium, or the diagnosis will be missed (p.1328).","Diagnosis is often syndromic and many endemic countries under-report to the WHO, so the true burden and distribution are unknown and cases may be unrecognised (p.1326).","Cholera should still be suspected in severe acute watery diarrhoea or death even in areas not known to have cholera (p.1328).","Fever is usually absent, so its absence should not dissuade from the diagnosis (p.1327).","Immunity to V. cholerae O1 is not protective against O139 (and vice versa), so prior infection does not guarantee protection against the other serogroup (p.1327)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison stratifies dehydration in cholera (Table 173-1) into: (1) None or mild (diarrhoea present) - thirst in some cases, <5% loss of total body weight; (2) Moderate - thirst, postural hypotension, weakness, tachycardia, decreased skin turgor, dry mouth/tongue, no tears, 5-10% loss of total body weight; and (3) Severe - unconsciousness, lethargy or floppiness, weak or absent pulse, inability to drink, sunken eyes (sunken fontanelles in infants), >10% loss of total body weight. This dehydration assessment guides the intensity of rehydration (p.1328).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1325","Harrison 22e p.1326","Harrison 22e p.1327","Harrison 22e p.1328","Harrison 22e p.1329"],"crossLinks":["hypovolemic_shock"]},"chronic_hepatitis":{"name":"Chronic Hepatitis","system":"Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":352,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2673","clinicalPearls":["Chronic hepatitis is defined as hepatic inflammation and necrosis continuing for at least 6 months; milder forms are nonprogressive or slowly progressive, whereas more severe forms cause scarring and architectural reorganization that, when advanced, lead ultimately to cirrhosis (p.2673).","Classification rests on three axes: (1) cause, (2) histologic activity or grade (necroinflammatory activity), and (3) degree of progression based on fibrosis or stage; neither clinical features alone nor histology alone are sufficient to characterize disease (p.2673).","Grade reflects necroinflammatory activity (periportal/piecemeal necrosis or interface hepatitis, bridging necrosis, focal lobular necrosis, portal inflammation) and is scored by the histologic activity index (HAI, used in the U.S.) or METAVIR (used in Europe); chronic hepatitis is graded mild, moderate, or severe (p.2673-2674).","Stage reflects fibrosis on a numerical scale (0-6 HAI or 0-4 METAVIR); when fibrous septa surround parenchymal nodules and alter normal lobular architecture, the lesion is defined as cirrhosis (p.2674).","Hyperglobulinemia and circulating autoantibodies are distinctly ABSENT in chronic hepatitis B, in contrast to autoimmune hepatitis; this helps distinguish viral from autoimmune disease (p.2674).","Autoimmune hepatitis has a strong female predominance (~75% of cases) with a bimodal age distribution (one peak age <30, a second often >60); fatigue is the most common symptom (~85%) and ~25% are diagnosed when asymptomatic on abnormal liver tests (p.2694-2695).","Type I AIH (classic, North America/northern Europe, young women) is associated with ANA in a homogeneous pattern and anti-smooth-muscle antibody (ASMA); type II AIH (often children, Mediterranean) is associated with anti-LKM1 (directed against cytochrome P450 2D6) rather than ANA (p.2695).","Polyclonal hypergammaglobulinemia (>2.5 g/dL) is common in AIH; smooth-muscle antibodies are detected in 63% of cases and, combined with a positive ANA, have high specificity (p.2695).","Anti-LKM1 (against cytochrome P450 2D6) seen in type II AIH is the same antibody found in some patients with chronic hepatitis C; rarely AIH patients with hyperglobulinemia give false-positive anti-HCV immunoassays (p.2685-2695).","Cirrhosis from any cause of chronic hepatitis carries a risk of hepatocellular carcinoma; in cirrhotic hepatitis C the annual HCC rate is 1-4%, occurring primarily after 30 or more years of infection, warranting HCC surveillance (Chap. 87) (p.2685).","Liver biopsy is imperative to diagnose AIH; classic features include interface hepatitis extending beyond portal tracts, plasma cells in the infiltrate, central vein perivenulitis, hepatocellular rosette formation, and frequent septal/bridging fibrosis and cirrhosis (p.2695)."],"pathophysiology":"Chronic hepatitis comprises liver disorders of varying cause in which hepatic inflammation and necrosis persist for at least 6 months. Broad categories include chronic viral hepatitis (B, B plus D, C), drug- or toxin-induced chronic hepatitis, alcohol-associated liver disease, metabolic dysfunction-related (steatotic) liver disease, and autoimmune chronic hepatitis; idiopathic/cryptogenic cases are also believed largely autoimmune. In chronic hepatitis B, all forms can be progressive but progression occurs primarily in patients with active HBV replication, and HCC risk is highest with continued high-level replication. In autoimmune hepatitis, progressive injury results from a cell-mediated immunologic attack on hepatocytes in the setting of lost/failed tolerance for self-liver antigens; predisposition is inherited (HLA-B8, DR3, DR4, DRB1*0301/*0401) while liver specificity is triggered by environmental factors (chemicals, drugs such as minocycline, viruses, and reported triggers including COVID-19 and COVID-19 vaccination); molecular mimicry activates infiltrating CD4+ T cells and impaired regulatory CD4+CD25+ T-cell control contributes (p.2673, 2693-2694).","additionalDifferentials":["Wilson disease (especially in adolescents, before neurologic signs/Kayser-Fleischer rings)","Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","Primary biliary cholangitis","Cryptogenic cirrhosis","Celiac disease","Ischemic liver disease","Budd-Chiari syndrome (hepatic venous outflow obstruction)","Immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI) hepatitis"],"infectionMimics":["Chronic hepatitis B","Chronic hepatitis C","Chronic hepatitis D (delta) superimposed on hepatitis B","Hepatitis E (chronic in immunosuppressed/post-transplant hosts)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Autoimmune hepatitis","Drug- or toxin-induced liver injury (including minocycline)","Alcohol-associated hepatitis","Metabolic dysfunction-associated steatohepatitis (MASH/NASH)","Wilson disease","Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","Primary biliary cholangitis","Rheumatologic disorders (rheumatoid arthritis, systemic lupus erythematosus) given extrahepatic features and autoantibodies"],"additionalInvestigations":["Liver biochemical tests (AST, ALT, alkaline phosphatase, bilirubin, albumin, prothrombin time)","HBV markers: HBsAg, IgG anti-HBc, HBeAg/anti-HBe, HBV DNA","HCV markers: anti-HCV, HCV RNA","HDV markers: anti-HDV, HDV RNA","Autoantibodies: ANA (homogeneous), anti-smooth-muscle antibody (anti-actin), anti-LKM1, anti-SLA, anti-LC1, F-actin, atypical pANCA","Serum immunoglobulins/globulins (hypergammaglobulinemia >2.5 g/dL in AIH; IgG level)","Liver biopsy for grading (HAI/METAVIR) and staging of fibrosis","Noninvasive fibrosis assessment: FIB-4, Enhanced Liver Fibrosis (ELF) score, liver elastography/stiffness imaging","Ceruloplasmin, serum/urinary copper and liver copper (to exclude Wilson disease)"],"redFlags":["Complications of cirrhosis: ascites, edema, bleeding gastroesophageal varices, hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, hypersplenism","Intermittent deepening of jaundice with worsening malaise/anorexia/fatigue, often coinciding with virologic reactivation, which may cause hepatic decompensation when superimposed on cirrhosis","Severe AIH: aminotransferases >10x normal, marked hyperglobulinemia, multilobular collapse or bridging necrosis on histology (6-month untreated mortality up to ~40%)","Failure of serum bilirubin to improve after 2 weeks of therapy in AIH (poor prognostic sign)","Multilobular collapse at initial presentation in AIH (poor prognostic sign)","Development of hepatocellular carcinoma in established cirrhosis"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on grade and stage. In untreated chronic hepatitis B, 5-year survival was 97% for mild, 86% for moderate-to-severe, and 55% for those with cirrhosis (15-year survival 77%, 66%, 40% respectively); all forms can progress, primarily with active HBV replication. In chronic hepatitis C, liver histology is the best prognostic indicator; mild disease has an excellent prognosis, while moderate-to-severe necroinflammation or bridging fibrosis makes cirrhosis highly likely over 10-20 years. In compensated HCV cirrhosis, 10-year survival is ~80%, with mortality 2-6%/year, decompensation 4-5%/year, and HCC 1-4%/year. In autoimmune hepatitis, 10-year survival is 80-98% for treated versus 67% for untreated patients; severe symptomatic disease carries up to ~40% 6-month mortality without therapy; in a Dutch cohort, mortality exceeded the general population only in patients with cirrhosis (p.2674, 2685-2686, 2694-2695).","pitfalls":["Histopathologic-only classification (chronic persistent/lobular vs chronic active hepatitis) has been replaced because its presumed prognostic implications were not corroborated; classification now combines cause, grade, and stage (p.2673).","Laboratory features of chronic hepatitis B do not reliably distinguish histologically mild from severe disease (p.2674).","Low-titer ANA or ASMA occurs in other liver diseases including MASLD (~20% ANA-positive, ~14% ASMA-positive in one series) and viral hepatitis, so positive autoantibodies are not specific for AIH (p.2695).","Rarely AIH patients with hyperglobulinemia have false-positive anti-HCV; conversely some confirmed chronic hepatitis C patients have circulating anti-LKM1, blurring the distinction between viral and autoimmune disease (p.2685-2695).","ICI hepatitis resembles AIH but lacks female preponderance and is less likely to have ANA (low-titer if present) or plasma cell infiltrates (p.2695).","Overlapping types of hepatitis (e.g., viral plus steatotic, alcohol plus nonalcohol steatotic) are encountered not infrequently given the high prevalence of steatotic liver disease (p.2673)."],"severityClassification":"Graded by necroinflammatory activity as mild, moderate, or severe using the histologic activity index (HAI, 0-18) or METAVIR (A0-A3). Staged by fibrosis: HAI 0-6 or METAVIR F0-F4, with cirrhosis defined when fibrous septa surround parenchymal nodules and alter lobular architecture (Table 352-2, p.2674).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2673","Harrison 22e p.2674","Harrison 22e p.2685","Harrison 22e p.2686","Harrison 22e p.2694","Harrison 22e p.2695"],"crossLinks":["decomp_cirrhosis","toxic_hepatitis"]},"chronic_lymphocytic_leukemia":{"name":"Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL)","system":"Oncology / Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":112,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.849-855","clinicalPearls":["CLL is a monoclonal proliferation of mature B lymphocytes defined by an absolute clonal B-cell count of ≥5 × 10⁹/L in the blood; it is a disease of older adults (median age at diagnosis 71) (p.849).","Most commonly diagnosed incidentally on routine blood work showing an elevated lymphocyte count in an asymptomatic individual; the natural history is heterogeneous, with some patients never requiring therapy (p.849, p.853).","On the peripheral blood smear, the small lymphocytes are increased and many rupture during smear preparation, leaving nuclear material without surrounding cytoplasm — smudge cells, which are rare in the absence of chronic lymphoid leukemia (p.443).","Diagnosis is confirmed by flow cytometry on peripheral blood; the typical immunophenotype is CD19+, CD20+, CD22+, CD23+, CD200+, the T-cell marker CD5+, and dim surface immunoglobulin (kappa or lambda). A clonal B-cell count ≥5 × 10⁹/L needs no further workup to confirm CLL (p.852).","Small lymphocytic lymphoma (SLL) is the tissue-based counterpart of CLL (lymphadenopathy/splenomegaly with a small clonal blood population); genetic and molecular features are identical and SLL is managed identically to CLL (p.852).","Monoclonal B-cell lymphocytosis (MBL) is a precursor: clonal B cells below the CLL threshold without nodal/organ involvement or cytopenias; high-count MBL progresses to overt CLL at ~1–2% per year, whereas low-count MBL has a negligible progression rate (p.849, p.852).","IGHV mutational status is a key prognostic factor: ~60% have IGHV ≥2% mutated from germline (more indolent course), while ~40% are IGHV-unmutated (more rapid progression, shorter survival before BCR-targeted therapy) (p.850).","B-cell receptor (BCR) signaling is central to CLL pathogenesis, characterized by low-level IgM expression and tonic activation of antiapoptotic pathways promoting tumor survival (p.850).","FISH (with or without karyotype) should be checked before every line of therapy because cytogenetics can evolve even without treatment, mainly to detect acquisition of del(17)(p13.1) (p.851).","Bone marrow biopsy is not undertaken until therapy is initiated, except in cases of unexplained cytopenias; CT imaging is usually unnecessary at diagnosis (p.854).","A watchful-waiting strategy is used for most patients, with therapy reserved for symptomatic disease, as multiple trials show no survival advantage to earlier therapy (p.854).","Hypogammaglobulinemia affects all immunoglobulin subclasses and occurs in ~85% of patients at some point; low IgG and IgA correlate with infection risk, but isolated IgM reduction does not seem to confer excess infection risk (p.852)."],"pathophysiology":"CLL is a monoclonal expansion of mature B lymphocytes; the definitive cell of origin is not established (mature/memory B cell, stepwise transformation, or hematopoietic stem cell are all proposed). Distinct BCR signaling — low-level IgM, tonic antiapoptotic pathway activation — drives tumor survival, with upregulation of BCR-pathway genes in lymph nodes and marrow versus blood. The CLL genome is relatively simple (~20 nonsynonymous and ~5 structural changes) with no unifying driver; recurrent mutations include SF3B1, NOTCH1, MYD88, ATM, and TP53 (mostly enriched in IGHV-unmutated disease, except MYD88 which favors IGHV-mutated/indolent disease). Key cytogenetic lesions: del(13)(q14.3) (sole, indolent; causes loss of the miR15/16 cluster that normally suppresses BCL2 and other antiapoptotic genes), trisomy 12 (intermediate), del(11)(q22.3)/ATM (bulky disease, aggressive), and del(17)(p13.1)/TP53 (poorest prognosis). Immune dysregulation pervades the disease: innate defects (reduced complement, neutrophil and NK cell defects), adaptive defects (CD4+ T-cell exhaustion, Th1-to-Th2 shift, expanded T regulatory cells), nurse-like tumor-associated macrophages, and humoral hypogammaglobulinemia (p.849-852).","additionalDifferentials":["Mantle cell lymphoma (CD5+, but CD23-negative, cyclin D1+, moderate/bright CD20 and surface Ig) — distinguished by immunophenotype (p.852)","Marginal zone lymphoma (CD5 -/+, CD23 -/+, bright surface Ig) (p.852)","Follicular lymphoma (CD5-negative, CD10-positive, CD23-positive) (p.852)","Monoclonal B-cell lymphocytosis (clonal B cells below the CLL threshold without organ involvement or cytopenias) (p.852)","Small lymphocytic lymphoma (tissue-based counterpart with identical molecular features) (p.852)","B-cell prolymphocytic leukemia arising from CLL (clinically and therapeutically distinct, though classified under Richter's transformation) (p.853-854)"],"infectionMimics":["Reactive lymphocytosis from viral infection — viral infections produce larger reactive lymphocytes with abundant cytoplasm, in contrast to the increased small lymphocytes and smudge cells of CLL (p.443)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Other CD5+ or low-grade B-cell lymphoproliferative disorders (mantle cell, marginal zone, follicular lymphoma) distinguishable by flow cytometry immunophenotype, morphology, or cytogenetics (p.852)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Flow cytometry of peripheral blood for the characteristic immunophenotype (CD19, CD20, CD22, CD23, CD200, CD5, dim surface Ig) — diagnostic test of choice (p.852)","FISH for recurrent abnormalities del(13)(q14.3), trisomy 12, del(11)(q22.3), del(17)(p13.1); repeated before each line of therapy to detect acquired del(17p) (p.851)","Stimulated metaphase karyotype, including assessment for complex karyotype (≥3 abnormalities) (p.851)","Sequencing for TP53 and IGHV mutational status for prognostication (p.854)","β2-microglobulin and LDH (prognostic; component of CLL International Prognostic Index) (p.853-854)","Direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test for warm IgG antibody when autoimmune hemolytic anemia is suspected (Coombs-negative cases can occur) (p.853)","Reticulocyte count, bilirubin, LDH, haptoglobin to evaluate suspected hemolysis (p.853)","18FDG-PET/CT to localize a biopsy site when Richter's transformation is suspected (SUV <5 consistent with CLL; SUV >5 suspicious; ≥10 very concerning); excisional biopsy preferred over fine-needle aspiration (p.853-854)","CLL International Prognostic Index integrating TP53 status, IGHV status, β2-microglobulin, clinical stage, and age (p.854)"],"redFlags":["Richter's transformation — transformation to an aggressive lymphoma (most commonly DLBCL; Hodgkin lymphoma is a recognized variant). Suspect with rapid progression of adenopathy (often localized), constitutional symptoms (fatigue, night sweats, fever, weight loss), and usually high LDH (p.853-854)","del(17)(p13.1)/TP53 loss or mutation — poorest prognosis, rapid progression, poor response to DNA-damaging therapy (p.850-851)","Complex karyotype (≥3 abnormalities) — adverse impact on time to treatment and overall survival, worsening with increasing complexity (p.851)","Autoimmune hemolytic anemia — death from uncontrolled AIHA can occur without appropriate supportive care; immediate therapy is almost always necessary (p.853)","Symptoms indicating need for therapy: progressive marrow failure, massive/progressive/symptomatic splenomegaly or lymphadenopathy, lymphocyte doubling time <6 months, autoimmune cytopenia unresponsive to standard therapy, or constitutional symptoms (p.854)"],"prognosis":"Heterogeneous: many patients are asymptomatic and never require therapy while others need multiple lines and die of disease. Overall survival has improved markedly (5-year OS 70% in 1980, 92% in 2015, likely higher today), and only a small minority now die of CLL itself. Infections cause ~30–50% of CLL-related deaths. Favorable markers: IGHV-mutated disease, sole del(13)(q14.3), MYD88 mutation. Adverse markers: IGHV-unmutated disease, del(17p)/TP53, del(11q)/ATM, NOTCH1, complex karyotype, and high β2-microglobulin/LDH. The CLL International Prognostic Index stratifies 5-year survival from ~93% (low risk) to ~23% (very high risk) in training-set data, though it has not been validated in the era of novel targeted therapies. Richter's transformation carries a poor prognosis, with median survival of 6–16 months for clonally related DLBCL versus ~5 years for clonally unrelated lymphomas (p.849, p.851, p.853-854).","pitfalls":["When anemia or thrombocytopenia appear, distinguish autoimmune cytopenia from marrow replacement — the therapeutic approaches differ, and autoimmune cytopenias often respond without treating the underlying CLL (p.853)","AIHA can be Coombs-negative, so a negative direct antiglobulin test does not exclude it (p.853)","ITP can be hard to distinguish from disease progression because no lab test directly identifies the platelet-destruction mechanism; isolated thrombocytopenia and rapid platelet decline without alternative cause point to ITP (p.853)","del(13)(q14.3) seen on banded karyotype (rather than only on FISH) indicates a larger deletion involving the retinoblastoma gene, which negates the favorable prognosis usually associated with this marker (p.850)","Live vaccines should be avoided in CLL because of the small risk of viral reactivation in an immunocompromised host (p.853)","Fine-needle aspiration should be discouraged for diagnosing suspected Richter's transformation; excisional biopsy is preferred (p.854)","Cytogenetics can change without therapy, so prior FISH results may be outdated — recheck before each line of treatment (p.851)"],"severityClassification":"Two staging systems, both based on physical exam and labs (no imaging or marrow required). Rai system (more common in the US): low risk (stage 0) = lymphocytosis only; intermediate risk (stage I/II) = lymphocytosis with lymphadenopathy, with or without splenomegaly/hepatomegaly; high risk (stage III/IV) = lymphocytosis with anemia or thrombocytopenia from marrow involvement. Binet system (more common in Europe): A = <3 areas of lymphadenopathy; B = ≥3 areas of lymphadenopathy; C = hemoglobin ≤10 g/dL and/or platelets <100,000/μL. The CLL International Prognostic Index refines outcome prediction using TP53 status, IGHV status, β2-microglobulin, clinical stage, and age (p.854).","references":["Harrison 22e p.849-855","Harrison 22e p.443"],"crossLinks":[]},"chronic_myeloid_leukemia":{"name":"Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)","system":"Oncology / Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":110,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.834","clinicalPearls":["CML is a clonal hematopoietic myeloproliferative stem cell neoplasm driven by the BCR::ABL1 chimeric gene, which encodes a constitutively active tyrosine kinase (Harrison 22e p.834).","BCR::ABL1 results from a balanced reciprocal translocation between the long arms of chromosomes 9 and 22, t(9;22)(q34.1;q11.2), known as the Philadelphia chromosome (Ph); the t(9;22) is present in >90% of classical CML cases (Harrison 22e p.834-835).","The most common transcript in CML encodes the p210BCR::ABL1 oncoprotein; p190BCR::ABL1 is present in two-thirds of Ph-positive ALL but rare in CML, and p230BCR::ABL1 is rare and associated with a more indolent course (Harrison 22e p.835).","Untreated, CML is typically biphasic or triphasic: an early indolent chronic phase, often followed by an accelerated phase, and a terminal blastic phase (Harrison 22e p.834).","Most patients (about 90%) present in chronic phase; in developed countries 50-60% are diagnosed on routine blood tests with minimal symptoms such as fatigue (Harrison 22e p.836).","Common symptoms, when present, are manifestations of anemia and splenomegaly: fatigue, malaise, weight loss, early satiety, and left upper quadrant pain or mass (Harrison 22e p.836).","Splenomegaly is the most common physical finding (20-70% depending on screening frequency); hepatomegaly (5-10%) and lymphadenopathy (5%) are less common (Harrison 22e p.836-837).","Untreated CML shows leukocytosis (10-500 x 10^9/L) with left-shifted myeloid hematopoiesis: neutrophils plus bands, myelocytes, metamyelocytes, promyelocytes, and blasts (usually <=5%); basophils and/or eosinophils are frequently increased (Harrison 22e p.837).","Thrombocytosis is common; thrombocytopenia is rare and, when present, suggests worse prognosis, disease acceleration, or an unrelated etiology. Anemia is present in one-third of patients (Harrison 22e p.837).","Diagnosis depends on documenting t(9;22)(q34.1;q11.2), identified by G-banding in 90% of cases; FISH and PCR aid diagnosis and estimate disease burden, and can be done on peripheral blood (Harrison 22e p.837).","TKIs have revolutionized CML: the estimated 10-year survival with imatinib now exceeds 85% and approaches that of a normal age-matched population; allogeneic HSCT (curative but riskier) is offered as later-line therapy after TKI failure (Harrison 22e p.834).","Molecular monitoring on the International Scale (IS) guides therapy: complete cytogenetic response (0% Ph) is roughly equivalent to BCR::ABL1 <=1% (MR2); major molecular response (MMR/MR3) is BCR::ABL1 <=0.1%; deep molecular response MR4 is <=0.01% and MR4.5 is <=0.0032% (Harrison 22e p.837-838).","The T315I 'gatekeeper' kinase-domain mutation prevents binding of, and causes resistance to, imatinib and the second-generation TKIs (dasatinib, nilotinib, bosutinib); third-generation agents (e.g., ponatinib, asciminib) retain activity against it (Harrison 22e p.836)."],"pathophysiology":"CML is a clonal myeloproliferative stem cell neoplasm caused by the BCR::ABL1 fusion gene arising from the reciprocal balanced translocation t(9;22)(q34.1;q11.2) (the Philadelphia chromosome). DNA from the ABL1 proto-oncogene on chromosome 9 is juxtaposed to the breakpoint cluster region (BCR) gene on chromosome 22, generating a hybrid oncogene whose product (most commonly the p210BCR::ABL1 oncoprotein from e13a2 or e14a2 transcripts) has constitutive tyrosine kinase activity. This drives increased proliferation and reduced apoptosis, giving CML cells a growth advantage and progressively suppressing normal hematopoiesis. Constitutive BCR::ABL1 activation triggers autophosphorylation and downstream signaling via RAS, MAP kinases, STAT, PI3K, MYC and others, mediated by adapter proteins (GRB-2, CRK, CRK-L, SHC). Most TKIs bind the ATP-binding domain to inhibit kinase activity; asciminib (a STAMP inhibitor) instead binds the ABL1 myristoyl pocket. BCR::ABL1 induces genetic instability favoring acquisition of additional defects (e.g., double Ph, trisomy 8, isochromosome 17/17p loss of TP53, 3q26 abnormalities, mutations in TP53, RB1, RUNX1, p16) that drive transformation from chronic to accelerated to blastic phase. Notably, the BCR::ABL1 rearrangement is detectable in the blood of up to 25% of normal adults and 5% of infants but is not sufficient to cause overt CML in the overwhelming majority. (Harrison 22e p.834-836)","additionalDifferentials":["Leukemoid reaction (reactive leukocytosis) - distinguished from CML by a high/normal leukocyte alkaline phosphatase score (CML has a LOW LAP) and absence of BCR::ABL1 (Harrison 22e p.837)","Atypical CML (BCR::ABL1-negative) - lacks the Ph chromosome/BCR::ABL1, does not respond to TKI therapy, associated with SETBP1 (about 25%) and some CSF3R mutations, poor prognosis with median survival ~2-3 years (Harrison 22e p.835)","Chronic neutrophilic leukemia - frequently CSF3R-mutated (about 80%); may respond to ruxolitinib (Harrison 22e p.835)","Chronic myelomonocytic leukemia (Harrison 22e p.835)","Myelodysplastic syndrome/myeloproliferative neoplasms, including MDS/MPN-RS-T (SF3B1-mutated) (Harrison 22e p.835)","Essential thrombocythemia and other Ph-negative MPNs presenting with thrombocytosis - when JAK2 V617F is absent, cytogenetics/FISH for BCR::ABL1 are required to exclude CML (Harrison 22e p.832-833)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Leukemoid reaction (reactive leukocytosis from infection, inflammation, or other stress) - mimics the leukocytosis with left shift of CML but lacks BCR::ABL1 and shows a high/normal (not low) leukocyte alkaline phosphatase score (Harrison 22e p.837)","Other myeloproliferative neoplasms and MDS/MPN overlap syndromes that can produce leukocytosis, basophilia, or splenomegaly without the Philadelphia chromosome (Harrison 22e p.835)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Bone marrow aspiration with routine G-banding cytogenetics - required to confirm the Ph chromosome, detect additional chromosomal abnormalities (which FISH will not detect), and quantify marrow blasts and basophils; a diagnosis of CML must always rely on marrow analysis with cytogenetics (Harrison 22e p.837)","FISH analysis for BCR::ABL1 on peripheral blood - quantifies percentage of Ph-positive cells; preferred when a Ph cytogenetic study is negative or for monitoring (Harrison 22e p.837)","Quantitative RT-PCR for BCR::ABL1 transcripts on the International Scale - baseline transcript typing at diagnosis (to avoid spurious 'undetectable' results on follow-up with atypical transcripts) and serial molecular monitoring of response (Harrison 22e p.837-838)","ABL1 kinase domain mutation analysis - assessed when resistance/loss of response is suspected (e.g., to detect T315I) (Harrison 22e p.836-837)","Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase score - characteristically LOW in CML, helping distinguish it from a leukemoid reaction (Harrison 22e p.837)","Supportive biochemistry - high vitamin B12, uric acid, lactate dehydrogenase, and lysozyme are commonly seen (Harrison 22e p.837)"],"redFlags":["Unexplained, sustained leukocytosis (with or without splenomegaly) should prompt marrow examination and cytogenetic analysis (Harrison 22e p.837)","Increasing/therapy-resistant leukocytosis, worsening anemia, fever and constitutional symptoms, and rising blasts and basophils signal disease progression (Harrison 22e p.838)","New thrombocytopenia (<100 x 10^9/L unrelated to therapy) suggests worse prognosis or disease acceleration (Harrison 22e p.837-838)","Extramedullary disease (skin/subcutaneous lesions, soft tissue, or lytic bone lesions) with biopsy showing blast predominance indicates transformation (Harrison 22e p.837-838)","Hyperleukocytosis/hyperviscosity or marked thrombocytosis with thrombotic events (priapism, MI, venous thrombosis, visual disturbance, CVA) or bleeding diathesis (Harrison 22e p.836)"],"prognosis":"TKIs have transformed CML prognosis. Before the TKI era median survival was 3-7 years with annual mortality of 10% in the first 2 years and 15-20% thereafter. With imatinib, annual mortality has fallen to about 1-2%, the 10-year survival rate is about 85% (CML-specific survival ~90%), and the 10-year incidence of blastic transformation is only 5-6%; more than half of deaths are now from non-CML causes. Treatment-related response milestones are the dominant modern prognostic factors: achievement of complete cytogenetic response (MR2) is the major endpoint associated with improved survival, while durable deep molecular response (MR4/MR4.5) may permit treatment-free remission (TFR rate about 50% after 2+ years of durable DMR and 80%+ after 5+ years). Historical pretreatment models (Sokal, Hasford, EUTOS) and adverse features (age, splenomegaly, anemia, high blasts/basophils, marrow fibrosis, additional chromosomal abnormalities) have lost much of their prognostic weight in the TKI era. Accelerated phase (historical median survival <2 years) and blastic phase carry the worst outcomes; de novo accelerated phase on TKI now has an estimated 8-year survival of 60-70%, and lymphoid blastic phase responds to ALL-type chemotherapy plus TKIs (complete response ~70%, median survival ~3 years). (Harrison 22e p.834, 838-839)","pitfalls":["PITFALL: Mistaking a reactive leukemoid reaction for CML - the leukocyte alkaline phosphatase score is LOW in CML but high/normal in a leukemoid reaction, and CML requires demonstration of BCR::ABL1 (Harrison 22e p.837).","Atypical transcripts (e13a3, e14a3, e19a2, etc.) not detected at diagnosis can produce falsely 'negative/undetectable' BCR::ABL1 on follow-up PCR, giving a false impression of complete molecular response; transcript type must be documented at diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.835, 837).","FISH does NOT detect additional chromosomal abnormalities, so a marrow cytogenetic analysis is still required at diagnosis; relying on FISH/PCR alone can miss clonal evolution (Harrison 22e p.837).","Both FISH and PCR can be falsely positive at low levels or falsely negative for technical reasons, so diagnosis must always rely on marrow analysis with routine cytogenetics (Harrison 22e p.837).","In about 10% of patients marrow blasts and basophils are significantly higher than in peripheral blood, conferring poorer prognosis or representing transformation that peripheral counts alone would miss (Harrison 22e p.837).","Failure to achieve MMR or DMR should NOT automatically be considered 'failure' of a given TKI or an indication to switch TKI or proceed to allogeneic HSCT (Harrison 22e p.838)."],"severityClassification":"CML is staged by clinical phase. Chronic phase: indolent, marrow blasts typically <=5%, where ~90% of patients present. Accelerated phase (historically median survival <2 years): >=15% peripheral blasts, >=30% peripheral blasts plus promyelocytes, >=20% peripheral basophils, cytogenetic clonal evolution (additional chromosomal abnormalities beyond Ph), or thrombocytopenia <100 x 10^9/L unrelated to therapy. Blastic phase: >=30% peripheral or marrow blasts, or sheets of blasts in extramedullary disease (commonly myeloid in ~60%, lymphoid in ~25%). The newest WHO classification suggests eliminating accelerated phase and reclassifying such patients as high-risk CML, but the text argues the accelerated-phase definition should be maintained because their survival is significantly worse than high-risk CML. Risk models (Sokal, Hasford, EUTOS) were used historically. (Harrison 22e p.838)","references":["Harrison 22e p.834"],"crossLinks":[]},"chronic_pancreatitis":{"name":"Chronic Pancreatitis","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":359,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2750","clinicalPearls":["Patients typically present with abdominal pain or symptoms of maldigestion; pain is variable in location, severity, and frequency, may be constant or intermittent, and can be exacerbated by eating, leading to fear of eating and weight loss (p.2752).","There is often a disparity between the reported severity of abdominal pain and the physical findings, which mainly consist of nonfocal abdominal tenderness (p.2752).","In contrast to acute pancreatitis, serum amylase and lipase are usually NOT strikingly elevated in chronic pancreatitis; low serum pancreatic enzyme levels are moderately specific but have poor sensitivity (p.2752).","Cumulative prevalence of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency exceeds 80%; overt steatorrhea in a patient with chronic pancreatitis is highly suggestive of this complication (p.2752).","A random fecal elastase-1 level on a formed stool specimen can help confirm exocrine pancreatic insufficiency in patients with milder symptoms (p.2752).","Diffuse calcifications on a plain abdominal film usually indicate significant pancreatic damage and are pathognomonic for chronic pancreatitis; tropical pancreatitis (often SPINK1-associated) characteristically shows bulky calcifications (p.2752).","Lifetime prevalence of chronic pancreatitis-related diabetes exceeds 80%; most develop hyperglycemia from insulin deficiency due to islet cell loss, but diabetic ketoacidosis, diabetic coma, and end-organ damage (retinopathy, neuropathy, nephropathy) are uncommon (p.2752).","Cigarette smoking is an independent, dose-dependent risk factor for chronic pancreatitis and recurrent acute pancreatitis; continued alcohol and smoking exposure are associated with disease progression including fibrosis and calcifications (p.2750).","Metabolic bone disease is highly prevalent, with about 65% of patients having osteopenia or osteoporosis (p.2752)."],"pathophysiology":"Chronic pancreatitis is characterized by irreversible damage to the pancreas (in contrast to the reversible changes of acute pancreatitis), defined by histologic abnormalities including chronic inflammation, fibrosis, and progressive destruction (atrophy) of both exocrine and endocrine tissue. Irrespective of the mechanism of injury, pancreatic stellate cell activation leads to cytokine expression and production of extracellular matrix proteins contributing to inflammation and collagen deposition. Proinflammatory cytokines (TNF-alpha, IL-1, IL-6) and oxidants induce stellate cell activity with new collagen synthesis; TGF-beta-mediated autocrine self-activation pathways may explain disease progression even after removal of noxious stimuli. Etiologies are framed by the TIGAR-O classification (Toxic-metabolic, Idiopathic, Genetic, Autoimmune, Recurrent and severe acute pancreatitis, Obstructive). Heavy alcohol use is the most common clinically apparent cause in U.S. adults, though additional factors are required for disease development (not all heavy drinkers develop disease). Genetic predisposition (e.g., PRSS1 cationic trypsinogen mutations preventing destruction of prematurely activated trypsin; CFTR and SPINK1 disease modifiers) accounts for up to 15% of previously idiopathic cases. Cardinal manifestations are abdominal pain, steatorrhea, weight loss, diabetes mellitus, and, less commonly, pancreatic cancer (p.2750-2752).","additionalDifferentials":["Pancreatic adenocarcinoma (must be excluded on imaging)","Autoimmune pancreatitis (type 1 AIP / IgG4-related disease; type 2 / idiopathic duct-centric)","Pancreatic pseudocyst"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pancreatic cancer (a poor response to glucocorticoids in suspected autoimmune pancreatitis should raise suspicion of pancreatic cancer)","Autoimmune pancreatitis (type 1 can mimic primary sclerosing cholangitis via IgG4-related sclerosing cholangitis)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Fecal elastase-1 (random level on a formed stool specimen) to confirm exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","Abdominal CT (initial imaging modality of choice) to show calcifications, dilated pancreatic/biliary ducts, atrophy, and to exclude pseudocyst and cancer","MRI/MRCP, secretin-enhanced MRCP","Endoscopic ultrasonography (EUS); five or more of nine endosonographic features considered diagnostic, though not specific for early disease","EUS with endoscopic pancreatic function testing (EUS-ePFT)","Direct pancreatic function test using secretin (best sensitivity; becomes abnormal when >=60% of exocrine function is lost)","Plain abdominal film for diffuse calcifications","Serum amylase/lipase (usually not strikingly elevated), serum bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase (may indicate cholestasis from biliary stricture)","Serum IgG4 (supportive of type 1 autoimmune pancreatitis)"],"redFlags":["Increased risk of pancreatic cancer: cumulative risk is 4% at 20 years after diagnosis of chronic calcific pancreatitis; hereditary PRSS1 and tropical pancreatitis carry an even higher risk than other forms.","Jaundice, cholestasis, and biliary cirrhosis from chronic inflammation around the intrapancreatic common bile duct.","Gastrointestinal bleeding from peptic ulceration, gastritis, a pseudocyst eroding into the duodenum, arterial bleeding into the pancreatic duct (hemosuccus pancreaticus), or ruptured varices from splenic vein thrombosis."],"prognosis":"Chronic pancreatitis is an irreversible disease; there are currently no therapies to reverse or delay disease progression, so management is focused on screening for and treating complications. Patients have impaired quality of life and significant morbidity with frequent health encounters. Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (cumulative prevalence >80%) and chronic pancreatitis-related diabetes (lifetime prevalence >80%) commonly develop. The 20-year cumulative risk of pancreatic cancer in chronic calcific pancreatitis is 4%, higher in hereditary PRSS1 and tropical forms (p.2752-2754).","pitfalls":["Diagnosis of early or mild chronic pancreatitis is challenging because there is no accurate biomarker, and serum amylase/lipase are usually not strikingly elevated (unlike acute pancreatitis) (p.2752).","EUS is not specific enough to diagnose early chronic pancreatitis alone and may show positive features in patients with diabetes, a smoking history, or even normal aging individuals (p.2752).","Elevated serum IgG4 has a low positive predictive value for autoimmune pancreatitis when used in isolation from other clinical findings (p.2751).","Complete correction of steatorrhea with pancreatic enzyme replacement is uncommon, even though enzyme therapy usually controls diarrhea and improves fat absorption (p.2753).","Meta-analyses show no consistent benefit of enzyme therapy at reducing pain; any pain relief may reflect improvement of dyspepsia from maldigestion (p.2753)."],"severityClassification":"Etiologic classification follows the TIGAR-O system: Toxic-metabolic (alcohol, tobacco smoking, hypercalcemia, hyperlipidemia/hypertriglyceridemia, chronic renal failure), Idiopathic (early onset, late onset, tropical), Genetic (PRSS1 cationic trypsinogen, CFTR, CASR, CTRC, SPINK1), Autoimmune (type 1 IgG4-related; type 2 idiopathic duct-centric), Recurrent and severe acute pancreatitis (postnecrotic, recurrent acute), and Obstructive (pancreas divisum, duct obstruction by tumor, preampullary duodenal wall cysts, posttraumatic duct strictures). Some genetic conditions are disease modifiers requiring additional factors to cause disease (p.2750).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2750","Harrison 22e p.2752","Harrison 22e p.2753","Harrison 22e p.2754","Gardner TB et al: ACG clinical guideline: Chronic pancreatitis. Am J (Harrison 22e ch.359)","Vege SS, Chari ST: Chronic pancreatitis. N Engl J Med 386:869, 2022 (Harrison 22e ch.359)"],"crossLinks":["pancreatitis","pancreatic_cancer","diabetes_mellitus"]},"coagulation_disorders":{"name":"Coagulation Disorders","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":121,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.928-932","clinicalPearls":["The most common inherited factor deficiencies are the hemophilias, X-linked recessive diseases due to deficiency of factor VIII (hemophilia A, ~80% of cases) or factor IX (hemophilia B); hemophilia A and B are clinically indistinguishable (Harrison 22e p.928, 930).","Disease phenotype correlates with factor activity level: severe (<1%), moderate (1-5%), mild (6-30%). Severe and moderate disease cause bleeding into joints (hemarthrosis), soft tissues, and muscles after minor trauma or spontaneously; mild disease bleeds infrequently and usually only after trauma (Harrison 22e p.930).","Recurrent hemarthroses (knees, elbows, ankles, shoulders, hips) damage a joint to create a chronically affected 'target joint' with synovitis and progressive deformity; deep-muscle hematomas can cause compartment syndrome by compressing arteries, veins, or nerves (Harrison 22e p.930).","Von Willebrand disease (VWD) is the most common inherited bleeding disorder (symptomatic prevalence ~1 in 1000 to 1 in 10,000); VWF tethers platelets to subendothelium and is the carrier protein that prolongs the FVIII half-life (Harrison 22e p.927).","Most VWD symptoms are 'platelet-like' (mucocutaneous: bruising, epistaxis, heavy menstrual bleeding); type 1 (parallel reduction in VWF protein, function, and FVIII) is most common (>=80% of cases) (Harrison 22e p.926-927).","Type 2N VWD ('autosomal hemophilia') is caused by VWF variants that impair FVIII binding, markedly shortening the FVIII half-life and lowering FVIII levels (Harrison 22e p.927).","Screening hemostasis tests: an isolated prolonged PT suggests FVII deficiency; a prolonged aPTT indicates an intrinsic-pathway deficiency, most commonly FVIII, FIX, or FXI; prolongation of both PT and aPTT suggests deficiency of FV, FX, FII, or a fibrinogen abnormality (Harrison 22e p.928).","Mixing study principle: adding normal pooled plasma corrects a prolonged PT/aPTT due to a factor deficiency but fails to correct when an inhibitor (antibody) is present; if it corrects, individual factor activity assays identify the deficient factor (Harrison 22e p.928).","Inhibitor (alloantibody) formation is the major complication of hemophilia treatment, with prevalence ~30% in severe hemophilia A, ~10% in nonsevere hemophilia A, and only 3-5% in hemophilia B; inhibitors increase morbidity and mortality (Harrison 22e p.930-931).","Acquired coagulation factor deficiencies are more frequent than congenital disorders; the most common are the hemorrhagic diathesis of liver disease, DIC, and vitamin K deficiency, each typically involving more than one clotting factor (Harrison 22e p.928)."],"pathophysiology":"Plasma coagulation factor deficiencies impair secondary hemostasis (the coagulation cascade). Inherited hemophilia A and B are X-linked recessive disorders from mutations in F8 and F9 respectively; affected males bleed while carrier women are generally asymptomatic, although a wide range of factor activity from random X-inactivation (lyonization) can produce bleeding in some carriers. FVIII has a very short intrinsic circulating half-life (~25-30 min) that is extended to roughly 12 h when complexed with von Willebrand factor (VWF). VWD reflects quantitative or qualitative defects of VWF, which serves both as the adhesion molecule tethering platelets to exposed subendothelium (function dependent on large VWF multimers) and as the FVIII carrier; loss of high-molecular-weight multimers (e.g., via excessive ADAMTS13 cleavage in type 2A/2B, or shear-induced unfolding) impairs platelet adhesion. Acquired deficiencies (liver disease, DIC, vitamin K deficiency) usually involve multiple factors and perturb both primary (platelet/vessel wall) and secondary (coagulation) hemostasis. (Harrison 22e p.926-930)","additionalDifferentials":["Hemophilia A versus hemophilia B (clinically indistinguishable; require factor assays to differentiate) (Harrison 22e p.930)","Von Willebrand disease with low FVIII versus hemophilia A (VWD must be excluded in a patient with prolonged aPTT and low FVIII and no known hemophilia history) (Harrison 22e p.930)","Factor XI deficiency (hemophilia C) and other rare inherited factor deficiencies (FII, FV, FVII, FX, FXIII, fibrinogen) (Harrison 22e p.928, 931-932)","Qualitative platelet function disorders (e.g., Glanzmann thrombasthenia, Bernard-Soulier syndrome, storage pool disorder) as a cause of mucocutaneous bleeding with normal coagulation factor levels (Harrison 22e p.926)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acquired von Willebrand syndrome from lymphoproliferative disorders / MGUS / multiple myeloma / Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia (suspect with new-onset severe mucosal bleeding, especially in the elderly) (Harrison 22e p.927)","Acquired VWD due to high shear: cardiac valvular disease and Heyde syndrome (aortic stenosis with GI bleeding from angiodysplasia; shear unfolds VWF causing loss of large multimers, reversed by valve replacement) (Harrison 22e p.927)","Acquired VWD in myeloproliferative disorders with markedly elevated platelet counts (>1.5 million) due to platelet-VWF binding and clearance (Harrison 22e p.926)","Acquired inhibitors (autoantibodies) to clotting factors in patients without genetic deficiency: postpartum, autoimmune or neoplastic disease, idiopathic, or rarely after topical bovine thrombin exposure in complex surgery (Harrison 22e p.928)","Liver disease, DIC, and vitamin K deficiency as acquired multifactor coagulopathies (Harrison 22e p.928)"],"additionalInvestigations":["PT and aPTT as first-line screens for clotting factor deficiency (Harrison 22e p.928)","Mixing study (correction with normal plasma indicates factor deficiency; failure to correct indicates an inhibitor or anticoagulant) (Harrison 22e p.928)","Individual factor activity assays to identify the specific deficient factor (Harrison 22e p.928)","Bethesda assay to define inhibitor specificity and titer (expressed in Bethesda units; classifies low- vs high-responder patients) with annual aPTT/mixing-study screening at hemophilia centers (Harrison 22e p.930-931)","VWD laboratory panel: aPTT, VWF antigen, VWF activity, FVIII activity, and multimer analysis to classify type 1, 2A, 2B, 2M, 2N, and 3 (Harrison 22e p.926-927)","Chromogenic FVIII activity assay using bovine substrates when emicizumab is present (routine aPTT and FVIII activity are inaccurate) (Harrison 22e p.931)","Molecular/genetic testing for F8 or F9 mutations to enable precise carrier diagnosis in affected families (Harrison 22e p.930)"],"redFlags":["Bleeding into the oropharyngeal spaces, central nervous system, or retroperitoneum is life-threatening and requires immediate therapy (Harrison 22e p.930)","Retroperitoneal hemorrhage may form a calcified inflammatory mass (pseudotumor syndrome) and damage the femoral nerve; pseudotumors can also form in long bones (Harrison 22e p.930)","Deep-muscle hematomas of the distal limbs can cause compartment syndrome from external compression of arteries, veins, or nerves (Harrison 22e p.930)","Failure to respond to factor replacement at therapeutic doses suggests an inhibitor; early detection is critical (Harrison 22e p.930)","In carriers/women, postpartum hemorrhage risk rises after delivery as the pregnancy-induced rise in clotting factor levels falls rapidly (Harrison 22e p.931)"],"prognosis":"Without treatment severe hemophilia may limit life expectancy; with modern recombinant factor concentrates, prophylaxis, and viral-inactivated products, life expectancy of severe hemophilia is now only ~10 years shorter than the general male population and near normal in mild/moderate disease. Since 1998 no new viral hepatitis or HIV infections have been reported in hemophilia patients, though aging patients carry burdens of arthropathy, chronic pain, and prior HCV/HIV infection (hepatocellular carcinoma is the most common cause of death in HIV-negative patients). TTP-related microangiopathy and inhibitors worsen prognosis when present. (Harrison 22e p.930-932)","pitfalls":["FXII (Hageman factor), high-molecular-weight kininogen, and prekallikrein deficiencies markedly prolong the aPTT but do NOT cause bleeding (no bleeding phenotype; treatment is not indicated) — do not treat the lab abnormality (Harrison 22e p.928).","Not all patients with low VWF levels bleed; mildly decreased VWF should be diagnosed as VWD only with bleeding symptoms and/or a family history, and type O blood physiologically lowers VWF (overlapping the diagnostic range) (Harrison 22e p.926-927).","Bleeding phenotype does not always correlate with factor activity in FXI deficiency, and a negative bleeding history with prior procedures does NOT exclude increased hemorrhage risk (Harrison 22e p.931-932).","Family history of hemophilia is absent in ~30% of cases (de novo mutations), so its absence does not exclude the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.930).","Antifibrinolytic drugs are NOT indicated for hematuria because of the risk of an occlusive clot obstructing the genitourinary tract (Harrison 22e p.931)."],"severityClassification":"Hemophilia A/B by factor VIII or IX activity: severe (<1%), moderate (1-5%), mild (6-30%); patients with baseline activity >25% may be detected only after major trauma or as an isolated prolonged aPTT. VWD classified into types 1, 2 (subtypes 2A, 2B, 2M, 2N), and 3 (severe, with virtually absent VWF and FVIII usually <10%) using VWF antigen, VWF activity, FVIII activity, and multimer pattern. Inhibitor patients classified as low responders (<5 Bethesda units) or high responders (>5 BU or anamnestic rise). (Harrison 22e p.926-931)","references":["Harrison 22e p.928-932","Harrison 22e Ch.120 p.926-927 (von Willebrand disease)"],"crossLinks":["dic"]},"colorectal_cancer":{"name":"Colorectal Cancer","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":86,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.653","clinicalPearls":["Symptoms vary by tumor location. Right-sided cancers (cecum, ascending colon) can grow large without obstruction because stool is still liquid, and they tend to ulcerate, causing chronic insidious blood loss and presenting as iron-deficiency anemia (Harrison 22e p.656).","Unexplained iron-deficiency anemia in any adult mandates a thorough endoscopic and/or radiographic visualization of the entire large bowel; the possible exception is a premenopausal, multiparous woman (Harrison 22e p.656).","Left-sided (transverse/descending colon) tumors impede passage of more formed stool, producing abdominal cramping, occasional obstruction, and even perforation, classically the annular constricting 'apple-core' lesion on imaging (Harrison 22e p.656).","Rectosigmoid cancers cause hematochezia, tenesmus, and narrowing of stool caliber; these may be mistaken for hemorrhoids, but rectal bleeding and/or altered bowel habits demand prompt digital rectal exam and sigmoidoscopy (Harrison 22e p.656).","Most colorectal cancers arise from adenomatous polyps (not hyperplastic, hamartomatous, or serrated polyps); only a minority of adenomas progress, with risk tied to polyp size and histology, and villous adenomas becoming cancer about threefold more often than tubular adenomas (Harrison 22e p.653).","Universal testing of colorectal tumors for MMR defects is recommended, by immunohistochemistry for MMR proteins or microsatellite instability testing; MSI-high tumors are dramatically sensitive to immune checkpoint inhibition and less sensitive to chemotherapy (Harrison 22e p.653-654, 656).","Serum CEA is used for staging workup and follow-up; some authorities measure plasma CEA at 3-month intervals after curative resection because of its sensitivity as a marker for otherwise undetectable recurrence (Harrison 22e p.656-658).","Liver is the most frequent visceral metastatic site, being the initial site of distant spread in about one-third of recurring cancers and involved in over two-thirds of patients at death; complete resection of metastases isolated to one site (liver, lung, peritoneum) is considered curative (Harrison 22e p.657-658)."],"pathophysiology":"The majority of colorectal cancers develop through the adenoma-to-carcinoma sequence, in which adenomatous polyps accumulate sequential genetic and epigenetic events involving tumor suppressor genes and oncogenes. Three major sporadic molecular pathways exist: the chromosomal instability (CIN) pathway (the most common), characterized by early loss of the APC tumor suppressor gene followed by sequential MAP-kinase oncogene mutations such as KRAS and BRAF, with loss of p53 as a common final step; the mismatch repair (MMR) pathway, often caused by hypermethylation of MMR gene promoters, producing a microsatellite-instability-high (MSI-high) phenotype; and the CpG island methylator phenotype (CIMP), with tumors that are often right-sided and associated with MLH1 methylation. These pathways are not mutually exclusive and can overlap in individual cancers (Harrison 22e p.653-654).","additionalDifferentials":["Hemorrhoids or benign anorectal disease as a cause of rectal bleeding (rectal bleeding and altered bowel habits still demand DRE and sigmoidoscopy) (Harrison 22e p.656)","Other causes of iron-deficiency anemia from chronic occult GI blood loss (Harrison 22e p.656)","Inflammatory bowel disease (both a risk factor and a differential for altered bowel habit and bleeding) (Harrison 22e p.655)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Colonoscopy of the entire large bowel (gold standard for detecting cancer and synchronous neoplasms/polyps) (Harrison 22e p.656)","Flexible sigmoidoscopy as a screening modality (Harrison 22e p.655-656)","Stool-based tests: fecal occult blood test, fecal immunochemical test (FIT), and fecal DNA testing (Harrison 22e p.655-656)","Serum carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) (Harrison 22e p.656)","Complete blood count and comprehensive metabolic panel (Harrison 22e p.656)","Chest, abdomen, and pelvic CT scan; MRI and PET as dictated by CT findings (Harrison 22e p.656)","Pelvic MRI for rectal cancer to assess mesorectal margin and nodal status (Harrison 22e p.658)","Tumor MMR/microsatellite instability testing (IHC or MSI) and germline genetic testing where indicated (Harrison 22e p.654, 656)","Investigational: plasma circulating tumor DNA as a biomarker for residual/recurrent disease (Harrison 22e p.658)"],"redFlags":["Unexplained iron-deficiency anemia in any adult (mandates evaluation of the entire large bowel)","Change in bowel habit / altered bowel habits","Rectal bleeding (hematochezia) or guaiac-positive stool","Tenesmus and narrowing of the caliber of the stool (rectosigmoid lesions)","Abdominal cramping or obstruction (left-sided lesions)","Strong family history or known hereditary syndrome (FAP, MUTYH-associated polyposis, Lynch syndrome)"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is stage-related and depends on depth of tumor penetration into the bowel wall, regional lymph node involvement, and distant metastases (TNM). Stage I (T1-2N0M0) confers a high cure rate (>90-95% 5-year survival); 5-year survival declines through stage II (T3-4N0M0), stage III (node-positive), and stage IV (distant metastasis, <5% in colon). Stage I patients are considered cured after complete resection. A minimum of 12 sampled lymph nodes is considered necessary to accurately define stage. Poor-prognosis features include penetration through the bowel wall into pericolic fat, poorly differentiated histology, perforation and/or adherence to adjacent organs, venous invasion, preoperative CEA >5 ng/mL, BRAF mutation, and right-sided primary location. Tumors with MSI/MMR defects have an improved prognosis (enhanced immune response), and left-colon tumors fare better than right-colon tumors. Notably, primary tumor size does not influence prognosis once adjusted for nodal involvement and differentiation. Most recurrences occur within the first 5 years (Harrison 22e p.656-658).","pitfalls":["Attributing rectal bleeding to hemorrhoids and not pursuing DRE and sigmoidoscopy (Harrison 22e p.656)","Relying on fecal occult blood testing alone: about 50% of patients with documented colorectal cancer have a negative FOBT due to intermittent bleeding, and a colorectal neoplasm is NOT found in most asymptomatic individuals with a positive test (Harrison 22e p.655-656)","Sigmoidoscopy alone misses proximal lesions: about 1.5% of individuals with a normal sigmoidoscopy harbor a high-risk polyp or adenocarcinoma in the proximal colon (Harrison 22e p.656)","Lynch syndrome may not be associated with multiple polyps and is therefore often unrecognized (Harrison 22e p.654)","Disease stage cannot be determined accurately before surgical resection and pathologic analysis unless gross metastatic disease is present (Harrison 22e p.656)","Initial treatment should be delayed until MMR/microsatellite status is known when possible, since MSI-high rectal cancers may be cured with checkpoint inhibitors (PD-1 antibodies) without surgery, radiation, or chemotherapy (Harrison 22e p.656)"],"severityClassification":"TNM classification (American Joint Committee on Cancer / Union for International Cancer Control): T = depth of tumor penetration (T1 submucosa, T2 muscularis, T3 through muscularis, T4 adjacent structures), N = regional lymph node involvement (N1 = 1-3 nodes, N2 = >=4 nodes), M = distant metastasis. Stage I = T1-2N0M0; Stage II = T3-4N0M0; Stage III = any T, N1-2, M0; Stage IV = any T, any N, M1. The older Dukes classification was historically used for colorectal cancer (Harrison 22e p.656).","references":["Harrison 22e p.653"],"crossLinks":["ibd_flare"]},"congenital_heart_disease":{"name":"Adult Congenital Heart Disease","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":280,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2067","clinicalPearls":["At least 1.4 million U.S. adults are living with congenital heart disease (CHD); the majority were diagnosed in childhood, but a substantial percentage have CHD first recognized in adulthood, so lifelong follow-up with ACHD expertise is recommended (Harrison 22e p.2067).","ASD physiology is predominantly a left-to-right shunt; the degree of shunting (and thus right heart volume loading) depends on defect size and the diastolic properties of the heart. As patients age, diabetes, hypertension, and atherosclerosis reduce left-heart compliance and may increase left-to-right shunting and symptoms (Harrison 22e p.2070).","The classic ASD physical finding is a wide, FIXED splitting of the second heart sound, due to prolonged RV ejection and increased pulmonary artery capacitance delaying pulmonary valve closure; the ECG commonly shows incomplete right bundle branch block (Harrison 22e p.2070).","Secundum ASD (defect in the region of the fossa ovalis) is the most common type and can often be closed percutaneously with an occluder device; a patent foramen ovale (PFO) is distinct, is NOT associated with right-sided dilation, and persists in up to 25% of adults (Harrison 22e p.2070).","Surgical closure (not percutaneous) is required for primum ASDs, sinus venosus defects, and coronary sinus septal defects; a sinus venosus defect is not actually a defect of the atrial septum but a communication near the SVC- or IVC-atrial junction with right pulmonary veins (Harrison 22e p.2070-2071).","VSDs are the most common congenital anomaly recognized at birth but account for only ~10% of adult CHD, owing to the high rate of spontaneous closure of small VSDs in early life; the most common location is the membranous (perimembranous/outlet) septum (Harrison 22e p.2071-2072).","Outcome for adults with small VSDs WITHOUT ventricular dilation or pulmonary hypertension is generally excellent; small muscular defects persisting into adulthood are often pressure- and flow-restricted with no significant hemodynamic consequence (Harrison 22e p.2072).","Tetralogy of Fallot is the most common form of cyanotic CHD (~0.5 per 1000 live births) and comprises anterior conal-septal deviation causing RV outflow tract obstruction, a VSD, RV hypertrophy, and an overriding aorta (Harrison 22e p.2073).","In repaired tetralogy of Fallot, pulmonary regurgitation is common and is usually associated with RV dilation; accurate quantification of RV size, function, and mass is particularly important because RV dilation, dysfunction, and hypertrophy are associated with adverse outcomes, and cardiac MRI is routinely used for surveillance (Harrison 22e p.2073-2074).","Eisenmenger syndrome is the consequence of a long-standing high-volume or pressurized left-to-right shunt: excessive pulmonary blood flow leads to severely increased pulmonary vascular resistance that eventually REVERSES the shunt, creating bidirectional or right-to-left flow and cyanosis; it can complicate any CHD with an initial left-to-right shunt (Harrison 22e p.2078).","Selective pulmonary vasodilators such as bosentan or sildenafil are efficacious in Eisenmenger syndrome; select patients may be candidates for combined heart-lung transplantation, or preferably lung transplantation with concomitant repair of the intracardiac defect when feasible (Harrison 22e p.2078).","Endocarditis prophylaxis before dental procedures is advised in high-risk CHD lesions: unrepaired cyanotic CHD (including palliative shunts/conduits), completely repaired defects during the first 6 months after repair, and repaired CHD with residual defects adjacent to prosthetic material (Harrison 22e p.745, Table 133-9)."],"pathophysiology":"The normal heart is analyzed segmentally (atria, ventricles, great arteries joined by the atrioventricular canal and conus), with attention to alignment (what drains into what) and connection (how segments are physically linked). The heart forms from the third week of gestation and is nearly complete by 8 weeks; cardiac neural crest cells migrating in weeks 5-6 are essential for outflow septation, semilunar valve formation, and aortic arch patterning, so disturbance of these processes underlies conotruncal defects. Shunt lesions are defined by the relative compliance, pressure, and outflow resistance of the connected chambers/vessels and the defect size. Left-to-right shunts (ASD, VSD, PDA) move oxygenated blood toward the deoxygenated (right) side, volume-loading the pulmonary circulation: ASD/PAPVR (proximal to the tricuspid valve) cause right heart dilation, whereas VSD/PDA (below the tricuspid valve) cause left heart dilation. Sustained high-volume or pressurized left-to-right shunting can raise pulmonary vascular resistance to the point that the shunt reverses (Eisenmenger physiology, right-to-left, cyanosis). Tetralogy of Fallot produces a right-to-left shunt across the VSD because RV outflow obstruction raises right-sided systolic pressure, mixing deoxygenated blood into the LV and causing systemic arterial cyanosis. In transposition of the great arteries the aorta arises from the RV and the pulmonary artery from the LV, creating parallel rather than series circulations that are incompatible with life unless mixing exists (ASD, VSD, or PDA).","additionalDifferentials":["Atrial septal defect (secundum, primum, sinus venosus, coronary sinus types) — most common cause of right heart dilation from a shunt","Patent foramen ovale (distinct from ASD; not associated with right heart dilation)","Ventricular septal defect (membranous/perimembranous, muscular, AV-canal/inlet, subpulmonary/conal types)","Patent ductus arteriosus","Partial anomalous pulmonary venous return","Ebstein anomaly of the tricuspid valve","Tetralogy of Fallot (most common cyanotic CHD)","Transposition of the great arteries (D-loop and congenitally corrected L-loop)","Coarctation of the aorta (with associated bicuspid aortic valve)","Single ventricle physiology (tricuspid atresia, double inlet LV, hypoplastic left heart) post-Fontan","Eisenmenger syndrome (any unrepaired left-to-right shunt that has reversed)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Atrial septal defect may be mistaken for mitral stenosis; features favoring ASD over MS include a pulmonary mid-to-upper left sternal border flow murmur, and large left-to-right shunts can produce findings overlapping with MS (Harrison 22e p.2065 / Chap. on MS).","Arrhythmogenic RV cardiomyopathy and Uhl's anomaly are non-shunt myocardial causes of right heart dilation that can mimic the dilated right heart of an ASD."],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic and transesophageal echocardiography with agitated saline (bubble) contrast — needed to diagnose PFO and non-secundum ASDs; color flow Doppler for VSDs and shunt-ratio estimation","Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging — routine surveillance of RV size, function, and mass in repaired tetralogy of Fallot and other complex CHD; cross-sectional imaging detects PAPVR","Surface ECG — incomplete right bundle branch block in ASD; QRS duration >=180 ms in repaired TOF flags risk of ventricular tachycardia/sudden death; ventricular pre-excitation (WPW) in up to 20% of Ebstein anomaly","Cardiac catheterization — shunt quantification and hemodynamic assessment, particularly when pulmonary hypertension is suspected","Genetic evaluation — testing for 22q11 deletion (DiGeorge) in conotruncal defects (clues: right aortic arch, aberrant subclavian artery); recognition of trisomy 21, Noonan (PTPN11), Williams (7q11.23), and Turner syndromes","Fetal echocardiogram between 18 and 22 weeks of gestation for pregnant patients with CHD"],"redFlags":["Reversal of a left-to-right shunt to right-to-left flow with cyanosis = Eisenmenger syndrome; develops from severely elevated pulmonary vascular resistance and is a multi-organ condition","Right heart dilation in an unrepaired ASD without other cause signals risk of progression to symptomatic right heart failure, atrial arrhythmias, and pulmonary arterial hypertension","QRS duration >=180 ms on resting ECG in repaired tetralogy of Fallot — associated with increased risk of ventricular tachycardia and sudden cardiac death","Systemic RV dysfunction after atrial switch (Mustard/Senning) for D-loop TGA — the most worrisome long-term complication, with no proven effective medical therapy","Pregnancy in a woman with Eisenmenger syndrome is contraindicated due to high risk of maternal mortality; pulmonary arterial hypertension and severe systemic ventricular dysfunction are WHO class IV (extremely high maternal risk)","New exercise-capacity decline in ACHD may be unrecognized by the patient; by the time symptoms appear, irreversible physiologic changes may already have occurred"],"prognosis":"Outcomes vary widely by lesion. Adults with small VSDs without ventricular dilation or pulmonary hypertension generally have an excellent prognosis. Repaired coarctation patients generally do well but remain at risk for systemic hypertension, premature atherosclerosis, LV failure, and aortic aneurysm/dissection and recoarctation. In repaired tetralogy of Fallot, one prospective cohort of 144 adults showed 72% survival at 40 years but only 25% cumulative event-free survival (events: reintervention, most commonly pulmonary valve replacement; symptomatic arrhythmia; heart failure); LV dysfunction is present in at least 20%. The arterial switch operation for D-loop TGA has produced substantial long-term survival, whereas atrial switch leaves a systemic RV prone to dysfunction. Adults with Eisenmenger syndrome have significant morbidity but generally survive longer than patients with other forms of pulmonary arterial hypertension. In aggregate, adults with CHD have quality-of-life-limiting comorbidities and premature mortality far exceeding age-matched controls, and nearly all can be classified as stage A heart failure or greater; care within a center that has an ACHD specialty program is associated with improved overall survival.","pitfalls":["Confusing a PFO (flap-valve patency, present in up to 25% of adults, no right heart dilation) with a true secundum ASD.","Assuming all ASDs are amenable to percutaneous closure — large defects, inadequate tissue rims, and anomalous draining pulmonary veins make device closure unfavorable, and primum/sinus venosus/coronary sinus defects require surgery.","Overlooking that a sinus venosus defect is not in the atrial septum itself, so it may be missed without dedicated imaging.","Routine phlebotomy in an asymptomatic adult with Eisenmenger syndrome is contraindicated; elevated hematocrit appropriate to the degree of cyanosis should not be lowered reflexively, and iron deficiency should be corrected because it impairs quality of life and function.","Using teratogenic medications (ACE inhibitors, ARBs, direct oral anticoagulants, endothelin receptor blockers) in CHD patients of childbearing potential; estrogen-containing contraception should be avoided in Eisenmenger syndrome due to thrombogenicity.","Failing to test adults with conotruncal defects for 22q11 deletion (DiGeorge) — many were never tested, and unrecognized cognitive/psychiatric disability may go untreated.","Treating an ACHD patient's seemingly minor lesion without recognizing limited hemodynamic reserve and subclinical multi-organ dysfunction before noncardiac surgery."],"severityClassification":"The chapter applies the modified World Health Organization (mWHO) classification of maternal risk in heart disease during pregnancy (Table 280-1), graded mWHO I (no detectable increased mortality risk, e.g., small/mild lesions) through mWHO IV (extremely high maternal mortality risk; pregnancy generally contraindicated). Examples by class: mWHO II includes unoperated ASD/VSD and repaired tetralogy of Fallot; mWHO III includes mechanical valve, systemic RV, and Fontan circulation; mWHO IV includes pulmonary arterial hypertension, severe systemic ventricular dysfunction (EF <30% or NYHA III-IV), severe aortic dilatation, and Fontan with any complication. The CARPREG II weighted-risk score is also described (highest 3-point factors: prior cardiac event/arrhythmia, NYHA >=III, mechanical valve), stratifying predicted cardiac-event risk from 5% (<=1 point) to 41% (>4 points). For repaired tetralogy of Fallot, potential sequelae are catalogued in Table 280-3 and long-term sequelae of D-loop TGA surgery in Table 280-4.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2067"],"crossLinks":["pulmonary_hypertension"]},"cryptococcosis":{"name":"Cryptococcosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":221,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1703-1706","clinicalPearls":["Caused by Cryptococcus, a genus of yeast-like fungi; WHO declared C. neoformans a critical priority pathogen in 2022 (p.1703).","C. neoformans and C. gattii are now regarded as species complexes; for clinical purposes both cause indistinguishable disease termed cryptococcosis (p.1703-1704).","High-risk hosts for C. neoformans: hematologic malignancy, solid-organ transplant, advanced liver disease, diabetes, glucocorticoid therapy, and advanced HIV with CD4+ counts <200/uL (p.1704).","C. gattii disease is NOT generally associated with specific immune deficits and often occurs in immunocompetent individuals; associated with autoantibodies to GM-CSF (p.1704).","Disease usually presents as pulmonary disease and/or chronic meningoencephalitis, but cryptococcosis can affect any tissue or organ (p.1704-1705).","Cryptococcal meningitis differs from bacterial meningitis: many patients present with symptoms of several weeks' duration and classic meningismus may be absent; indolent cases can present as subacute dementia or depression (p.1705).","In AIDS patients, cryptococcal meningitis is the initial AIDS-defining illness in ~2% and generally occurs with CD4+ counts <100/uL; about one-third also have pulmonary disease (p.1606).","Diagnosis requires demonstration of cryptococcal cells or antigen in normally sterile tissue; India ink of CSF shows encapsulated yeast and CRAg (serum/CSF) is both sensitive and specific with rapid point-of-care lateral flow assays (p.1705).","Increased intracranial pressure in cryptococcal meningoencephalitis is managed by repeated therapeutic lumbar punctures and shunt placement; mannitol and acetazolamide are NOT effective (p.1706).","Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS) can occur after starting ART in HIV; recommended to start ART 4-6 weeks after initiating antifungal therapy (p.1706).","Serum CRAg screening is recommended for asymptomatic people with HIV and CD4+ counts <100-200/uL to identify those at high risk of cryptococcal disease (p.1705-1706)."],"pathophysiology":"Cryptococcal infection is almost always acquired by inhalation of aerosolized particles (small desiccated yeast cells or basidiospores); occasionally by direct skin inoculation. C. neoformans is found in soils contaminated with avian (pigeon) excreta, whereas C. gattii inhabits arboreal species such as eucalyptus and is not found in bird feces. Infection is acquired in childhood and is common, but disease is rare in the absence of impaired immunity, indicating that intact pulmonary defenses effectively contain the fungus. Some infections lead to latency with viable organisms harbored in granulomas. Extrapulmonary dissemination to the CNS may occur via direct fungal migration across endothelium and via macrophage 'Trojan horse' carriage. Virulence factors include the antiphagocytic polysaccharide capsule, melanin production, and enzymes (phospholipase, urease). Copious release of capsular polysaccharide into tissues interferes with local immune responses, and infection may elicit little or no inflammatory response; capsular polysaccharide is the antigen measured diagnostically (p.1704).","additionalDifferentials":["Tuberculous (chronic) meningitis","Other fungal meningitides (e.g., Coccidioides, Histoplasma)","Subacute dementia or depression (indolent CNS cryptococcosis mimic)","Carcinomatous meningitis","Pulmonary malignancy or other causes of lung nodules/masses (pulmonary cryptococcosis found incidentally on chest radiograph)"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculosis (chronic meningitis and pulmonary disease)","Molluscum contagiosum (cutaneous cryptococcosis in highly immunosuppressed patients resembles its lesions)","Other disseminated/endemic fungal infections (histoplasmosis, coccidioidomycosis, blastomycosis)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Carcinomatous (neoplastic) meningitis","Subacute dementia","Depression","Pulmonary malignancy (cryptococcoma/nodule mimicking neoplasm)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CSF examination: opening pressure, protein, glucose, cell count, Gram stain, cultures","India ink stain of CSF for encapsulated yeast","Cryptococcal antigen (CRAg) assay in CSF and blood; point-of-care lateral flow assay","Fungal cultures of CSF and blood (blood cultures often positive)","Serum CRAg screening in HIV with low CD4+ counts","PCR (confirm positive or negative results with independent methods such as CRAg and culture)","Chest imaging for pulmonary disease (nodules, infiltrates, masses)","Biopsy for CNS cryptococcoma or to distinguish inadequately treated infection from immune reconstitution syndrome"],"redFlags":["Raised intracranial pressure in cryptococcal meningoencephalitis requiring serial therapeutic lumbar puncture drainage (and possible shunt placement)","Sudden catastrophic vision loss from meningeal cryptococcosis","Cranial nerve paresis, vision and hearing loss","Altered mental status, headache, and blurred vision as clues to elevated ICP","Refractory cryptococcosis despite antifungal therapy (prompt susceptibility testing)"],"prognosis":"Even with antifungal therapy, cryptococcosis carries high morbidity and mortality. The most important prognostic factors are the extent and duration of the underlying immunologic deficit. Disease is often curable in those with no apparent immune dysfunction, but in irreversible immunosuppression the goal is remission maintained by lifelong suppressive therapy. Before ART, median survival for AIDS patients with cryptococcosis was <1 year. Cryptococcosis with underlying neoplastic disease has a particularly poor prognosis. Poor prognostic markers for CNS disease: positive India ink on initial CSF exam (heavy fungal burden), high CSF pressure, low CSF glucose, low CSF pleocytosis (<2/uL), recovery of yeast from extraneural sites, absence of antibody to capsular polysaccharide, CSF or serum cryptococcal antigen level >=1:32, and concomitant glucocorticoid therapy or hematologic malignancy. Relapse is common even with relatively intact immunity (p.1706).","pitfalls":["India ink and Gram stain may be falsely negative with low fungal burden or misread as positive by inexperienced operators (p.1705).","CRAg is often negative in pulmonary cryptococcosis, so it is less useful for diagnosing pulmonary disease and of only limited use in monitoring response to therapy (p.1705).","Cryptococcal cells with negative cultures on CSF staining and persistently elevated CSF/blood cryptococcal antigen long after culture clearance do NOT indicate treatment failure (p.1705).","Categorizing cryptococcosis as 'extrapulmonary without CNS involvement' should be made only after CSF evaluation reveals no evidence of cryptococcal infection (p.1705).","Prophylactic dexamethasone in HIV-associated cryptococcosis is NOT recommended (associated with reduced fungal clearance and increased mortality); glucocorticoids are not helpful for raised ICP unless from a mass lesion with edema (p.1706).","Echinocandins are NOT effective against Cryptococcus species (p.1706).","IRIS can be difficult to distinguish from relapsing/refractory infection (p.1706)."],"severityClassification":"Nonsevere disease limited to the lungs or other non-CNS sites is distinguished from severe disease or CNS involvement; severe/CNS cryptococcosis is treated with induction, consolidation, and maintenance phases (p.1705).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1703-1706","Harrison 22e p.1606-1607"],"crossLinks":["MENINGITIS","ENCEPHALITIS"]},"cystic_fibrosis":{"name":"Cystic Fibrosis","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":302,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2245","clinicalPearls":["Cystic fibrosis is an autosomal recessive exocrinopathy affecting multiple epithelial tissues; the gene product CFTR is an apical anion (chloride and bicarbonate) channel that regulates the volume and composition of exocrine secretion (p.2245).","Major morbidity and mortality arise from pulmonary disease: copious hyperviscous adherent secretions obstruct small and medium airways, and a complex flora including Staphylococcus aureus, Haemophilus influenzae, and Pseudomonas aeruginosa is routinely cultured from CF sputum (p.2245).","P. aeruginosa shows stereotypic pathogenesis: an early sentinel colonization often becomes lifelong infection by the same strain, and over years evolves a mucoid (alginate-producing) phenotype that confers selective advantage and poor host prognosis (p.2245).","Radiographic sinusitis occurs in most patients with CF; the sinuses may act as a reservoir for bacterial seeding of the lower airways (p.2245).","Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency causes chronic malabsorption, poor growth, and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency; high blood immunoreactive trypsinogen (used in newborn screening) and low fecal elastase-1 are characteristic (p.2245-2246).","CF-related diabetes mellitus occurs in >30% of adults and is multifactorial (endocrine pancreatic destruction plus, in some, insulin resistance); annual diabetes testing is recommended for adults with CF (p.2246, p.2248).","Men typically show complete involution of the vas deferens despite functioning spermatogenesis, and ~99% of males with CF are infertile (unable to conceive without in vitro fertilization) (p.2246).","Diagnosis is confirmed by disease-causing variants on both CFTR alleles and/or sweat chloride >=60 mEq/L together with characteristic exocrine manifestations; sweat electrolyte testing follows pilocarpine iontophoresis (p.2246-2247).","Several severe defects (F508del, G551D, nonsense alleles) predict pancreatic insufficiency, present in ~80% of patients; F508del alone accounts for ~85% of defective CFTR alleles in the United States (p.2247).","CFTR modulators (ivacaftor potentiator; lumacaftor/tezacaftor/elexacaftor correctors) are highly effective modulator therapy (HEMT); triple combination therapy is projected to benefit >90% of patients and improves FEV1, reduces exacerbations, and lowers sweat chloride (p.2247-2248).","Pulmonary exacerbation poor-prognosis pathogens that are rigorously monitored include Burkholderia cepacia complex, Stenotrophomonas maltophilia, Achromobacter, mucoid P. aeruginosa, and atypical mycobacteria; MRSA may also signal worse outcomes (p.2248).","Infection commonly persists despite modulator therapy, so chronic respiratory infection and inflammation continue even after substantial clinical improvement on HEMT (p.2248)."],"pathophysiology":"Mutation of CFTR, an apical epithelial anion channel that conducts chloride and bicarbonate (~1480 amino acids, gated by ATP hydrolysis without active transport against gradient), impairs hydration and clearance of exocrine secretions. In the airway, loss of CFTR-dependent anion secretion depletes the periciliary fluid layer, causing ciliary collapse and failure to clear overlying mucus; mucoobstruction with chronic infection drives an aggressive, unrelenting neutrophilic inflammatory response with proteases and oxidants, leading to airway remodeling and bronchiectasis. Low airway-surface-fluid pH may impair bacterial killing. Similar mechanisms affect pancreatic acini/ducts (fibrotic destruction, cyst formation, exocrine and eventually endocrine insufficiency), hepatobiliary tissue (biliary obstruction, focal/multilobular cirrhosis, portal hypertension), and intestine (meconium ileus, distal intestinal obstructive syndrome). Over 2000 CFTR allelic variants are described and grouped into classes I-VI by molecular mechanism (e.g., F508del is a class II protein-maturation/folding defect degraded in the endoplasmic reticulum; G551D is a class III gating defect).","additionalDifferentials":["Non-CF bronchiectasis (CF carrier status predisposes; heterozygous CFTR mutations implicated) (p.2247)","Chronic rhinosinusitis (associated with relative CFTR deficiency) (p.2247)","Congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens as an isolated forme fruste of CFTR mutation (p.2247)","Idiopathic/recurrent pancreatitis associated with CFTR mutation in at least one allele (p.2247)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Sweat chloride after pilocarpine iontophoresis (chloride >=60 mEq/L supports diagnosis; highly specific) (p.2246-2247)","CFTR mutation analysis / panels of several hundred variants, or complete CFTR sequencing with splice-junction analysis (p.2246-2247)","Immunoreactive trypsinogen (newborn screening) (p.2246)","Fecal elastase-1 (low in exocrine pancreatic insufficiency) (p.2246)","Sputum culture surveillance for resistant pathogens (Burkholderia cepacia complex, Stenotrophomonas, Achromobacter, mucoid P. aeruginosa, atypical mycobacteria, MRSA) (p.2248)","FEV1 (% predicted) for monitoring and transplant referral thresholds (p.2248)","Annual diabetes mellitus testing in adults; monitor liver function and CYP3A drug interactions on modulator therapy (p.2248)"],"redFlags":["Hemoptysis requiring hospitalization (p.2248)","Pneumothorax requiring hospitalization (p.2248)","Sputum culture growing Burkholderia cepacia complex, Stenotrophomonas maltophilia, Achromobacter, mucoid P. aeruginosa, or atypical mycobacteria (poor prognostic indicators) (p.2248)","Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (~5% of patients) suggested by failure to respond to aggressive inpatient antibiotics (p.2248)","FEV1 <30% predicted (threshold for lung transplant referral) (p.2248)"],"prognosis":"Modulator and other therapies have transformed CF from a disease that historically led to death in early childhood to one with frequent survival well into the fifth decade and beyond; US median survival now exceeds 60 years. Genotype is of limited value for predicting respiratory rate of decline or longevity. End-stage pulmonary failure may be treated with lung transplantation (median survival >9 years among adults). Marked geographic disparity exists: median survival is <30 years in much of Latin America versus >60 years in the United States (p.2248-2249).","pitfalls":["Hyperviscosity of eccrine sweat is NOT a feature of CF; the sweat test reflects defective ductal chloride reabsorption, not thick sweat (p.2247).","Anion flux through CFTR does NOT involve active transport against a concentration gradient (p.2245-2246).","Genotype does NOT reliably predict respiratory prognosis or longevity, even though some severe alleles predict pancreatic insufficiency (p.2247).","A subset of patients with F508del respond poorly to triple combination therapy, so modulator benefit is not universal (p.2248).","HEMT may reduce sputum bacterial density but does NOT eradicate infection; chronic respiratory infection and inflammation persist (p.2248).","CF has been underdiagnosed outside historically white populations (Eastern Europe, Latin America, Asia, India, and US minorities), often due to rare/poorly characterized variants and limited access to DNA/sweat testing (p.2249)."],"severityClassification":"CFTR mutations are categorized by molecular mechanism into classes I-VI: class I, absent synthesis (e.g., nonsense mutations G542X, R553X, W1282X); class II, defective protein maturation and premature degradation (e.g., F508del, ~85% of US alleles, the most common); class III, disordered gating/regulation (e.g., G551D, ~4% of patients); class IV, defective conductance through the channel pore; class V, reduced transcript number from promoter/splicing abnormality; class VI, accelerated turnover from the cell surface. Severity is not formally graded by stage; FEV1 <30% predicted is used as a lung-transplant referral threshold (p.2246, p.2248).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2245"],"crossLinks":[]},"cytomegalovirus_infection":{"name":"Cytomegalovirus (CMV) Infection","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":200,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1512","clinicalPearls":["CMV is a beta-herpesvirus carried for life after infection; the infection is usually silent, and reactivation syndromes emerge predominantly when T-lymphocyte-mediated immunity is compromised (organ transplantation, lymphoid neoplasms, HIV, or critical illness in the ICU) (p.1512).","In immunocompetent hosts beyond the neonatal period, the most common manifestation is a heterophile-antibody-negative mononucleosis syndrome; unlike EBV mononucleosis, exudative pharyngitis and cervical lymphadenopathy are rare, though splenomegaly, prolonged high fever, fatigue and malaise are common (p.1513-1514).","The characteristic laboratory finding in CMV mononucleosis is relative lymphocytosis with >10% atypical lymphocytes (predominantly activated CD8+ T cells); heterophile antibodies are absent (p.1514).","Congenital CMV is the commonest congenital infection; most congenital infections are clinically inapparent at birth, yet 7-11% of asymptomatically infected infants develop sensorineural hearing loss over 5 years (p.1512-1513).","Clinical disease in the fetus/newborn is related largely to PRIMARY maternal infection rather than reactivation; primary first-trimester infection carries up to 40-50% risk of sensorineural complications (p.1513).","Preferred diagnosis is detection of virus/antigen/DNA, most commonly quantitative nucleic acid testing (QNAT) by PCR; the pp65 antigenemia immunofluorescence assay is an alternative, and CMV DNA in CSF aids diagnosis of CMV encephalitis or polyradiculopathy (p.1515).","In persons with HIV, end-organ CMV disease (especially retinitis) occurs particularly when CD4+ counts fall below 50/uL; flares of CMV retinitis can occur shortly after starting antiretroviral therapy as an immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS) (p.1514).","An immune-complex glomerulopathy has been observed in some CMV-infected patients after renal transplantation, and CMV mononucleosis can produce transient autoantibodies (cryoglobulins, rheumatoid factors, cold agglutinins, antinuclear antibodies) via polyclonal B-cell activation (p.1512-1514).","Pathology shows enlarged 'cytomegalic' cells (2-4x normal) with an eccentric intranuclear inclusion surrounded by a clear halo producing the classic 'owl's eye' appearance (p.1513)."],"pathophysiology":"CMV is a beta-herpesvirus with double-stranded DNA, icosahedral symmetry and a lipoprotein envelope; it replicates in the cell nucleus and can establish either lytic/productive or latent infection. After primary infection the virus persists indefinitely in multiple cell types and organs (silent latency). Primary infection in late childhood/adulthood elicits a vigorous CD8+ T-lymphocyte response producing atypical lymphocytes and a mononucleosis-like illness, with polyclonal B-cell activation generating autoantibodies. When host T-cell responses are compromised by disease or iatrogenic immunosuppression, latent virus reactivates; chronic antigenic stimulation under immunosuppression (e.g., post-transplant) is an ideal setting for CMV disease, and potent T-cell suppressants (antithymocyte globulin, alemtuzumab) markedly raise CMV syndrome rates. CMV itself further depresses T-lymphocyte responsiveness, predisposing to superinfection by other opportunists. Congenital disease arises chiefly from primary maternal infection; transmission to transplant/transfusion recipients reflects silent infection in donor tissues (p.1512-1513).","additionalDifferentials":["Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) infectious mononucleosis (heterophile-positive, with prominent exudative pharyngitis and cervical adenopathy) versus CMV mononucleosis (heterophile-negative)","Congenital cytomegalic inclusion disease must be distinguished from congenital syphilis, toxoplasmosis, rubella, Zika, herpes simplex virus, and bacterial sepsis (p.1513)","CMV pneumonitis differential includes Pneumocystis, other viral/bacterial/fungal pneumonias, pulmonary hemorrhage, and irradiation or cytotoxic drug injury (p.1514)","CMV retinopathy must be distinguished from toxoplasmosis, candidiasis, and herpes simplex virus retinitis (p.1514)"],"infectionMimics":["Epstein-Barr virus mononucleosis","Toxoplasmosis","Congenital rubella, Zika, herpes simplex virus, and syphilis (in neonates)","Pneumocystis pneumonia","Herpes simplex virus and Candida retinitis/esophagitis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pulmonary hemorrhage and radiation- or cytotoxic-drug-induced lung injury (mimicking CMV pneumonitis)","Allograft rejection (overlapping with CMV syndrome in transplant recipients)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantitative nucleic acid testing (QNAT) for CMV DNA by PCR in blood or other specimens (preferred; same assay should be used for serial comparison)","CMV pp65 antigenemia immunofluorescence assay on peripheral-blood leukocytes","CMV DNA PCR in cerebrospinal fluid for suspected encephalitis or polyradiculopathy","Viral culture on human fibroblast monolayers, including the rapid overnight shell vial assay with monoclonal antibody to immediate-early antigen","Serology: CMV IgG (may take up to 4 weeks to rise after primary infection) and CMV IgM (false positives possible with rheumatoid factor); serology mainly stratifies transplant risk and is NOT recommended to diagnose acute disease in immunocompromised patients","Histopathology/biopsy demonstrating 'owl's eye' cytomegalic inclusions for tissue-invasive disease"],"redFlags":["Retinitis in an immunocompromised host (especially HIV with CD4+ <50/uL) - threat to vision","Colitis or gastrointestinal ulceration with risk of bleeding or perforation","Pneumonitis (tachypnea, hypoxemia, nonproductive cough with bilateral interstitial/reticulonodular infiltrates)","Encephalitis/ventriculoencephalitis or subacute progressive polyradiculopathy","Persistent viremia with multi-organ involvement, pancytopenia and hypotension (associated with fatal disease)","Symptomatic congenital infection (petechiae, hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, microcephaly with cerebral calcifications, intrauterine growth retardation)"],"prognosis":"Immunocompetent CMV mononucleosis is almost always self-limited (illness lasting 2-6 weeks); most patients recover without sequelae, though postviral asthenia may persist for months and fatal infection is rare. Severely affected congenitally infected infants have a poor prognosis with a mortality of about 5% and few survivors escaping intellectual or hearing impairment; 7-11% of asymptomatically infected neonates still develop sensorineural hearing loss over 5 years. In immunocompromised hosts, untreated CMV may progress to severe end-organ disease; CMV disease is an independent risk factor for both graft loss and death after organ transplantation, and high blood viral loads in HIV predict severe disease and death (p.1513-1514).","pitfalls":["Tissue-invasive CMV disease (e.g., gastrointestinal) may be present despite NEGATIVE blood CMV antigenemia and viral-load testing - diagnosis may require endoscopic biopsy with histopathology, not blood viremia alone (p.1514).","Isolation of CMV from urine, stool, or saliva does NOT by itself prove acute infection, since excretion may persist for months to years; detection of viremia by QNAT or antigenemia is a better predictor (p.1515).","Serology is NOT recommended to diagnose acute disease in immunocompromised patients; IgG may not rise for up to 4 weeks and rheumatoid factor can cause false-positive IgM (p.1515).","CMV detection in blood does not equate to end-organ disease; clinical and tissue correlation is required.","Universal ganciclovir/valganciclovir prophylaxis is bone-marrow suppressive, favoring preemptive strategies in HSCT recipients (p.1515)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames CMV by host and syndrome rather than a formal grading scale: (1) congenital CMV - subclinical to severe disseminated cytomegalic inclusion disease; (2) perinatal CMV - usually asymptomatic; (3) immunocompetent CMV mononucleosis - heterophile-negative, self-limited; (4) CMV in the immunocompromised host - asymptomatic infection/viremia, 'CMV syndrome' (febrile leukopenia), and tissue-invasive end-organ disease (pneumonitis, colitis/GI disease, hepatitis, esophagitis, retinitis, encephalitis/polyradiculopathy), stratified by population in Table 200-1 (fetus/neonate, organ transplant recipient, HSCT recipient, person with HIV) (p.1513).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1512"],"crossLinks":["infectious_mononucleosis"]},"diabetes_mellitus":{"name":"Diabetes Mellitus","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":415,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3195","clinicalPearls":["DM is a group of metabolic disorders sharing the phenotype of hyperglycemia; it is defined as the level of glycemia at which a diabetes-specific complication (e.g., retinopathy) occurs, not as a deviation from a population mean (p.3195, 3197).","Diagnostic criteria (any one, confirmed by repeat testing on a different day unless unequivocal hyperglycemia/acute metabolic decompensation): HbA1c >=6.5% (>=48 mmol/mol); fasting plasma glucose (FPG) >=7.0 mmol/L (126 mg/dL, fasting = no calories >=8 h); 2-h plasma glucose >=11.1 mmol/L (200 mg/dL) on a 75-g OGTT; or a random plasma glucose >=11.1 mmol/L (200 mg/dL) PLUS classic symptoms (polyuria, polydipsia, weight loss) (p.3197).","Prediabetes (each identifies different individuals via different biologic mechanisms): HbA1c 5.7-6.4%; impaired fasting glucose (IFG) FPG 5.6-6.9 mmol/L (100-125 mg/dL) per ADA; impaired glucose tolerance (IGT) 2-h glucose 7.8-11.0 mmol/L (140-199 mg/dL). WHO defines IFG as FPG >6.1 mmol/L (110 mg/dL). All three confer increased risk of type 2 DM and cardiovascular disease (p.3197).","Type 1 DM = autoimmune beta cell destruction with insulin deficiency; 25-50% present with DKA; ~40% of new type 1 DM now has onset after age 30 and is often initially misdiagnosed as type 2. Islet cell autoantibodies (ICAs: insulin, GAD, IA-2, ZnT8) are present in >85% at new onset and define a 3-stage model: stage 1 = >=2 autoantibodies + normoglycemia, stage 2 = autoimmunity + dysglycemia, stage 3 = overt hyperglycemia (p.3199-3201).","Type 2 DM = heterogeneous: insulin resistance + impaired insulin secretion + increased hepatic glucose production; >=80% of patients are obese (central/visceral). Insulin resistance usually precedes the secretory defect, but diabetes appears only once secretion becomes inadequate for the degree of resistance (p.3195, 3201-3202).","Type 2 DM may be asymptomatic for up to a decade before diagnosis, so some patients already have diabetes-specific complications at the time of diagnosis (p.3198, 3221).","HbA1c can be misleading with hemoglobinopathies and varies by ancestry; OGTT is reserved mainly for pregnancy/GDM and cystic fibrosis-related diabetes screening, not routine care (p.3197).","Symptoms of hyperglycemia: polyuria, polydipsia, weight loss, fatigue, weakness, blurred vision (lens water-content change, resolves with control), recurrent superficial infections (vaginitis, fungal skin infections), slow wound healing (p.3203).","Microvascular complications usually do not appear until the second decade of hyperglycemia; macrovascular (ASCVD) risk, driven by insulin resistance and dyslipidemia, may develop before hyperglycemia is established (p.3221).","Distal symmetric polyneuropathy (DSPN) is the most common diabetic neuropathy and occurs in ~50% of long-standing DM; up to 50% are asymptomatic, so annual screening for loss of protective sensation (LOPS) is required - LOPS is a major risk factor for foot ulceration and amputation (p.3226)."],"pathophysiology":"Hyperglycemia results from reduced insulin secretion, decreased peripheral glucose utilization, and/or increased hepatic glucose production, with the relative contribution depending on the DM type. Insulin is produced by pancreatic beta cells (from preproinsulin -> proinsulin -> insulin + C-peptide); glucose is the key secretagogue: it enters the beta cell via GLUT transporters, glucokinase phosphorylation is rate-limiting, glycolysis generates ATP that closes the ATP-sensitive K+ channel (target of sulfonylureas/meglitinides), causing depolarization, calcium influx, and insulin release; incretins (GLP-1, GIP) amplify glucose-stimulated secretion only when glucose is above fasting. Type 1 DM arises from interacting genetic (HLA region accounts for ~50% of risk; DR3-DQ2 and DR4-DQ8 predisposing), environmental, and immunologic factors leading to CD8+ T cell-mediated beta cell destruction and absolute insulin deficiency; alpha-cell dysfunction adds fasting/postprandial hyperglucagonemia with impaired glucagon response to hypoglycemia. Type 2 DM combines impaired insulin secretion, insulin resistance (post-receptor defects in skeletal muscle, liver, fat; lipid intermediate accumulation; low-grade metabolic inflammation), excessive hepatic glucose production (failure to suppress gluconeogenesis), abnormal fat metabolism (increased free fatty acids, altered adipokines, reduced adiponectin), giving the characteristic dyslipidemia (high triglycerides, low HDL, small dense LDL); beta cell function is reduced ~50% at onset and declines progressively. Complications stem from chronic hyperglycemia driving advanced glycosylation end products (AGEs), increased mitochondrial reactive oxygen species, epigenetic changes (\"metabolic memory\"), and growth factors such as VEGF-A in proliferative retinopathy.","additionalDifferentials":["Monogenic diabetes / MODY (autosomal dominant, onset usually <25 y, impaired secretion; HNF-4alpha, glucokinase, HNF-1alpha/1beta mutations; glucokinase MODY = mild stable hyperglycemia; HNF-1alpha MODY often responds to sulfonylureas) (p.3195-3196, 3203)","Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) - glucose intolerance arising in 2nd/3rd trimester; diabetes in 1st trimester is classified as pre-existing pregestational diabetes (p.3196)","Atypical diabetes (features of both type 1 and type 2, not single-gene; includes ketosis-prone diabetes) and latent autoimmune diabetes of adults (5-10% of phenotypic type 2 have GAD/ICA) (p.3196, 3204)","Pancreatogenic (type 3c) DM from pancreatitis, pancreatectomy, cystic fibrosis (CF-related diabetes prevalence ~50%), hemochromatosis (p.3203)","Endocrinopathy-related hyperglycemia: acromegaly, Cushing's syndrome, glucagonoma, pheochromocytoma, somatostatinoma, hyperthyroidism (p.3196)","Drug/chemical-induced diabetes: glucocorticoids, calcineurin/mTOR inhibitors, pentamidine, nicotinic acid, statins, HIV/protease-inhibitor therapy, thiazides, second-generation antipsychotics, immune checkpoint inhibitors (p.3196, 3203)","Neonatal diabetes (onset <6 months; ATP-sensitive K+ channel subunit mutations may respond to sulfonylureas) (p.3196, 3203)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Stress/illness-related transient hyperglycemia from counterregulatory hormone excess (surgery, infection, critical illness) - hyperglycemia in a hospitalized patient need not indicate established DM (p.3217)","Other causes of increased anion-gap acidosis when DKA is suspected: starvation ketosis and alcoholic ketoacidosis (bicarbonate usually >15 meq/L) (p.3216)"],"additionalInvestigations":["HbA1c (lab method NGSP-aligned to DCCT reference) for diagnosis and monitoring; affected by hemoglobinopathies and ancestry (p.3196-3197)","Fasting plasma glucose and 75-g OGTT (OGTT mainly for pregnancy/GDM and CF-related diabetes screening) (p.3197)","Islet cell autoantibodies (GAD, IA-2, ZnT8, insulin) to classify type 1 vs type 2 and identify presymptomatic type 1 DM (p.3200-3201, 3204)","C-peptide as a marker of endogenous insulin secretion (distinguishes endogenous vs exogenous insulin) (p.3198)","Spot urinary albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) plus estimated GFR annually for nephropathy (screen 5 y after type 1 onset and at type 2 diagnosis); albuminuria = UACR >30 mg/g, severe >300 mg/g (p.3224)","Annual dilated retinal exam (or retinal photography) - nondilated primary-care exams are inadequate (p.3224)","Annual foot exam with 10-g monofilament, 128-Hz vibration, ankle reflexes, pedal pulses; annual DSPN screening for LOPS (p.3204, 3226)","In DKA/HHS: serum/plasma beta-hydroxybutyrate (preferred over nitroprusside which detects acetoacetate), glucose, electrolytes with corrected sodium, anion gap, bicarbonate, BUN/creatinine (p.3216)"],"redFlags":["Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA): hyperglycemia (glucose >13.9 mmol/L [250 mg/dL]; may be near-normal = euglycemic DKA, especially on SGLT-2 inhibitors), ketosis, anion-gap metabolic acidosis (bicarbonate <15-18 mmol/L); nausea/vomiting, severe abdominal pain (may mimic acute pancreatitis), Kussmaul respirations, fruity breath, volume depletion, lethargy progressing to coma; cerebral edema is a serious complication mainly in children (p.3216)","Hyperglycemic hyperosmolar state (HHS): older patient, more pronounced dehydration, marked hyperglycemia (often 33.3-66.6 mmol/L [600-1200 mg/dL]) with mental status changes; mortality up to 15% (higher than DKA) (p.3217)","Hypoglycemia (<3.0 mmol/L, <54 mg/dL); hypoglycemia unawareness from autonomic neuropathy raises risk of severe events and can prolong QTc - sudden death has been attributed to cardiac autonomic neuropathy (p.3203, 3226)","New blindness risk: proliferative retinopathy with neovascularization and vitreous hemorrhage; clinically significant macular edema (p.3222-3223)","Loss of protective sensation / foot ulceration predisposing to lower-extremity amputation (p.3226)","Silent (painless) myocardial ischemia is common - absence of chest pain does not exclude coronary disease; consider cardiac evaluation before major surgery (p.3228)"],"prognosis":"DM is a major cause of mortality (8th leading cause of death in the US, 2021; likely underreported) and was responsible for ~6.7 million deaths worldwide in 2021 (12.2% of global all-cause mortality in adults 20-79); about one-third of diabetes-related deaths occur before age 60. Cardiovascular disease is the main cause of morbidity and mortality, with ASCVD events and mortality 2-4 times greater in type 2 DM. In the US, DM is the leading cause of end-stage renal disease, nontraumatic lower-extremity amputation, and adult blindness; ~20-40% of patients develop diabetic nephropathy. Intensive glycemic control has durable benefit (\"metabolic memory\"/legacy effect lasting >=10 years): DCCT/EDIC showed reductions in retinopathy (47%), albuminuria (39%), clinical nephropathy (54%), neuropathy (60%), later a 57% reduction in cardiovascular events and 33% reduction in mortality; UKPDS showed each 1% HbA1c reduction lowered microvascular complications by 35%, and blood pressure control reduced complications even more than glycemic control. Diagnosis at younger age worsens prognosis (~3-4 years of reduced life expectancy per decade of earlier diagnosis).","pitfalls":["Do not over-rely on classic features (age of onset, obesity, insulin need) to classify DM - many adult-onset type 1 cases are misdiagnosed as type 2, and many new type 1 patients are now overweight/obese; measure ICAs when type 1 features are present (p.3199, 3204).","Euglycemic DKA can occur (notably with SGLT-2 inhibitors) - a near-normal glucose does not exclude DKA; check ketones and acid-base (p.3216).","In DKA, hyperamylasemia is usually salivary in origin and does NOT diagnose pancreatitis; obtain serum lipase if pancreatitis is suspected (p.3216).","Measured serum sodium is falsely lowered by hyperglycemia (correct ~1.6 mmol/L per 5.6 mmol/L [100 mg/dL] glucose rise); a normal sodium in DKA signals a profound water deficit (p.3216).","Serum potassium may be normal or high at DKA presentation despite a large total-body potassium deficit (acidosis/volume depletion shift) (p.3216).","Nitroprusside ketone tests detect acetoacetate, not the predominant beta-hydroxybutyrate, and give false positives with captopril, penicillamine, or valproic acid - prefer serum beta-hydroxybutyrate (p.3216).","Glycemic control may transiently worsen established retinopathy in the first 6-12 months of improvement, and GLP-1 receptor agonists with marked glycemic improvement have been associated with worsening retinopathy (p.3224).","CKD without retinopathy in type 1 DM should prompt a search for an alternative (non-diabetic) cause of kidney disease (p.3224).","Very strict glycemic control in acutely/critically ill patients (NICE-SUGAR) increased mortality and severe hypoglycemia - avoid overly tight targets in hospital (p.3217)."],"severityClassification":"Glucose homeostasis is a spectrum: normal -> impaired (IFG/IGT/HbA1c 5.7-6.4%, i.e., prediabetes) -> diabetes mellitus. DKA severity by ADA: mild (pH 7.25-7.30, bicarbonate 15-18 meq/L, normal mental status), moderate (pH 7.0-7.25, bicarbonate 10-15 meq/L, mildly reduced mental status), severe (pH <7.0). Diabetic retinopathy: nonproliferative (microaneurysms, blot hemorrhages, cotton-wool spots) vs proliferative (neovascularization) +/- clinically significant macular edema. Albuminuria: moderately increased UACR 30-299 mg/g vs severely increased >300 mg/g creatinine. Neuropathy categories: distal symmetric polyneuropathy, autonomic neuropathy, mononeuropathy, and radiculopathy/polyradiculopathy.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3195"],"crossLinks":["dka","hhs","hypoglycemia","ckd"]},"dilated_cardiomyopathy":{"name":"Dilated Cardiomyopathy","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":269,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2001-2020","clinicalPearls":["DCM is defined by decreased left ventricular systolic function, typically with increased LV dimensions, although dilation may be minimal in some cases (p.2015).","Quantitatively, DCM is defined when LVEF is <=0.50, but most clinical presentations occur with LVEF <=0.40; persistent LVEF <=0.30-0.35 is the threshold for primary-prevention ICD (p.2001-2002).","Despite multiple etiologies and variable initial presentations, DCM often progresses into a convergent clinical phenotype similar to that following acute myocardial infarction, with myocyte death, apoptosis, compensatory hypertrophy, and neurohormonal activation (p.2015).","Mitral regurgitation commonly develops as the ventricle dilates and the valvular apparatus is distorted, and is commonly moderate to severe by the time heart failure is advanced (p.2015).","Right ventricular dilation and dysfunction may result directly from the initial injury but more often develop later from elevated afterload due to secondary pulmonary hypertension and mechanical interaction with the failing LV (p.2015).","Alcohol is the most common toxin implicated in chronic DCM, but alcoholic cardiomyopathy is relatively rare and remains a diagnosis of exclusion; atrial fibrillation occurs commonly both early ('holiday heart') and in advanced stages (p.2016).","Truncating variants in TTN and other DCM disease genes can be found in about 10% of patients with presumed alcohol cardiomyopathy and about 15% of peripartum cardiomyopathy, indicating that environmental triggers can unmask genetic predisposition (p.2016-2017).","Chemotherapy agents are the most common drugs implicated in toxic cardiomyopathy; doxorubicin cardiotoxicity generally does NOT cause marked LV dilation, so stroke volume and perfusion can be low with only a modest fall in ejection fraction (p.2016).","Tests of thyroid function are part of the routine evaluation of cardiomyopathy because clinical signs of thyroid disease may be masked; hyperthyroidism should always be considered with new-onset atrial fibrillation or ventricular tachycardia (p.2017).","Heritability is present in at least 30% of DCM without other clear etiology, and expanded genetic testing reveals pathogenic variants sufficient to cause cardiomyopathy in 20-40% of DCM patients (p.2003, 2013)."],"pathophysiology":"DCM is characterized by decreased LV systolic function, typically with LV chamber dilation (LVEF <=0.50, usually <=0.40 at presentation). Multiple causes (idiopathic, familial/genetic, prior myocarditis, ischaemic, toxic/alcohol, chemotherapy, peripartum, tachycardia-mediated, metabolic/endocrine) converge on a common phenotype resembling post-infarct injury: early myocyte death, later apoptosis, and compensatory hypertrophy of remaining myocytes in response to increased wall stress. Local and circulating neurohormonal factors drive deleterious secondary remodeling; dynamic remodeling of the interstitial scaffold affects diastolic function and the degree of dilation. As the ventricle dilates, the mitral valve apparatus is distorted, producing functional mitral regurgitation. RV dilation/dysfunction develops directly from injury or, more often, secondarily from pulmonary hypertension and ventricular interaction. Chronic inflammation contributes to progression even when not initially implicated, and many 'acute' presentations have progressed silently over months to years (Harrison 22e p.2015).","additionalDifferentials":["Ischaemic cardiomyopathy / coronary artery disease or prior infarction (structural heart disease that can mimic or cause DCM, p.2002)","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM), including the ~5% that evolve to reduced EF with restrictive physiology (HCM-rEF) (p.2001-2002)","Restrictive cardiomyopathy (RCM) - functional diagnosis based on diastolic dysfunction/elevated filling pressures; some sarcomeric variants and storage diseases overlap (p.2002)","Arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy (ACM-LV / ACM-RV/ARVC) where ventricular arrhythmias dominate before severely reduced LVEF (p.2002)","Primary valvular disease","Takotsubo (stress-induced) cardiomyopathy - distinct acute presentation with apical ballooning, often resolving (p.2019)","Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF) - overlaps with proposed obesity and diabetic cardiomyopathy (p.2018)"],"infectionMimics":["Viral myocarditis (coxsackie, adenovirus, COVID-19, influenza, HIV) progressing to dilated cardiomyopathy (p.2015-2016)","Chagas' disease (Trypanosoma cruzi) and other parasitic infection (toxoplasmosis, trypanosomiasis) (p.2016)","Bacterial (diphtheria), spirochetal (Lyme disease - Borrelia burgdorferi), rickettsial (Q fever), and fungal myocarditis (p.2016)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Granulomatous inflammatory disease - sarcoidosis (often regional wall-motion abnormality, DCM phenotype, or RV involvement) and giant cell myocarditis (p.2002, 2016)","Eosinophilic myocarditis / Loeffler endocarditis (can progress to restrictive physiology) (p.2015-2016)","Collagen vascular / connective tissue disease, polymyositis, dermatomyositis, systemic lupus erythematosus (p.2015-2016)","Immune checkpoint-inhibitor myocarditis (most dramatic noninfectious inflammatory myocarditis) (p.2016)","Hemochromatosis / iron overload (variably classified as metabolic or restrictive but often presents as DCM) (p.2018-2019)","Endocrinopathy - thyroid disease, pheochromocytoma; very-high-renin states (renal artery stenosis) (p.2017-2018)","Nutritional deficiency - thiamine (beriberi), selenium (Keshan's disease), carnitine, calcium, phosphate (p.2018)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography (initial imaging modality to define morphology and function) (p.2002)","Cardiac MRI for myocardial tissue characterization, late gadolinium enhancement patterns of fibrosis, and T1/T2 mapping for focal/diffuse inflammation (note: avoid gadolinium in pregnancy) (p.2002, 2018)","Electrocardiogram and chest radiograph (p.2003)","Chemistry including sodium, potassium, calcium, magnesium, glucose/glycohemoglobin, renal and liver function, lipids, thyroid-stimulating hormone, serum iron and transferrin saturation, urinalysis, creatine kinase isoforms, cardiac troponin (p.2003)","Hematology including CBC with differential, eosinophil count, ESR (p.2003)","Genetic counseling and multigene cardiomyopathy panel (p.2003)","Targeted infectious work-up when suspected (HIV, Chagas', Lyme, toxoplasmosis, trichinosis); rheumatologic serologies (p.2003)","Endomyocardial biopsy (including electron microscopy) when a specific diagnosis with therapeutic implications is suspected (p.2003)","Coronary angiography in patients with evidence of ischemia/infarction who are candidates for intervention (p.2003)"],"redFlags":["Sudden cardiac death risk: ventricular arrhythmias may be presenting events; persistent LVEF <=0.30-0.35 is the threshold for primary-prevention ICD (p.2001-2002, 2002)","Embolic events from atrial fibrillation or apical LV thrombi can be the first presentation (p.2002)","Peripartum cardiomyopathy with LVEF <0.35 carries higher risk of life-threatening arrhythmias at presentation and early after discharge, and marked dilation/LVEF <0.35 raises LV thrombus and embolic risk (p.2017-2018)","Acute cardiac presentation (elevated troponin, conduction blocks, bizarre arrhythmias) in a patient on immune checkpoint inhibitors warrants immediate coronary-care-unit admission and high-dose glucocorticoids without delay for biopsy (p.2016)","Hyperthyroidism combined with heart failure is a dangerous combination that may decompensate precipitously and fatally during titration of antithyroid therapy (p.2017)"],"prognosis":"DCM secondary responses are often modifiable or reversible. Almost a third of patients with recent-onset cardiomyopathy without coronary artery disease show substantial spontaneous recovery to normal ejection fraction, and partial recovery to LVEF >0.40 ('heart failure with improved ejection fraction') is common with guideline-directed neurohormonal therapy, CRT for LBBB, and diuretics. Alcoholic cardiomyopathy can improve markedly within 3-6 months of abstinence but has a grim prognosis if heavy drinking continues. In peripartum cardiomyopathy, LVEF improves to >=0.50 in 50-80% of cases (often within 6 months); recovery is less likely with LVEF <0.30, LV end-diastolic dimension >=0.60, black race, and presentation >6 weeks postpartum; U.S. 1-year mortality ranged 4-11% and up to twofold higher in Africa. Takotsubo syndrome has a generally good long-term prognosis (lowest mortality with emotional rather than physical triggers) but in-hospital complications/mortality resemble acute MI, with ~10% recurrence (Harrison 22e p.2002, 2016-2019).","pitfalls":["'Congestive heart failure' should NOT be considered a diagnosis or an etiology of cardiomyopathy - it is a nonspecific syndrome of congestion common to many cardiac diagnoses and requires thorough etiologic evaluation (p.2002).","Phenotypes overlap and evolve over time (DCM can reverse-remodel to higher EF; HCM can evolve to reduced EF), so reliance on a single morphologic phenotype is diminishing (p.2001-2002).","Alcoholic cardiomyopathy is a diagnosis of exclusion, and many patients are fully functional without apparent stigmata of alcoholism (p.2016).","Anthracycline cardiotoxicity can produce low stroke volume/perfusion with only modestly reduced EF because marked LV dilation is usually absent (p.2016).","In peripartum cardiomyopathy, edema and dyspnea may be mistakenly attributed to pregnancy itself; renin-angiotensin system inhibitors should NOT be given before delivery due to adverse fetal effects, and gadolinium should NOT be used on MRI during pregnancy (p.2017-2018).","Detection of anticardiac antibodies and viral genome fragments in chronic cardiomyopathy is of uncertain significance (possible cause vs neutral bystander) and is not in routine clinical use (p.2013).","Immunosuppression for nonspecific chronic lymphocytic myocarditis has not demonstrated convincing benefit (p.2013)."],"severityClassification":"Classification of cardiomyopathies (Table 266-1): DCM phenotype - Early: LVEF <=0.50 and/or LVEDV >112% normal for age/sex or LVEDD >95% predicted for sex/height; LVEF <=0.40 is the threshold for traditional heart-failure therapies for low LVEF; persistent LVEF <=0.30-0.35 is the threshold for primary-prevention ICD. Mid-range LVEF 0.40-0.50 is often a transitional state (deterioration from early DCM or improvement into DCM remission). DCM is distinguished from HCM (increased wall thickness, septal >=15 mm men / >=13 mm women, LVEF >=normal usually >0.60), RCM (functional diagnosis from diastolic dysfunction/elevated filling pressures, often marked atrial enlargement), and arrhythmogenic CM (ACM-LV/ACM-RV/ARVC, ventricular arrhythmias dominating). (Harrison 22e p.2001-2002).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2001-2020"],"crossLinks":["heart_failure","atrial_fib"]},"diphtheria":{"name":"Diphtheria","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":155,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1221-1225","clinicalPearls":["Respiratory diphtheria is clinically diagnosed by the constellation of sore throat, adherent tonsillar/pharyngeal/nasal pseudomembranous lesions, and low-grade fever (p.1223).","The diphtheritic pseudomembrane is gray or whitish and sharply demarcated; unlike the exudate of streptococcal pharyngitis it is tightly adherent to underlying tissue, and attempts to dislodge it may cause bleeding (p.1223).","A few patients develop 'bull-neck' diphtheria from edema of the submandibular and paratracheal region, with massive tonsillar swelling, foul breath, thick speech, and stridorous breathing (p.1223).","Diphtheria should be considered in any patient with severe pharyngitis, especially with difficulty swallowing, respiratory compromise, or signs of systemic disease (myocarditis, generalized weakness); a pharyngeal pseudomembrane or extensive exudate should prompt consideration (p.1222-1223).","Toxigenic strains most often cause pharyngeal disease, while nontoxigenic strains commonly cause cutaneous disease; in tropical latitudes cutaneous diphtheria is more common than respiratory diphtheria (p.1221-1222).","Once a clinical diagnosis is made, diphtheria antitoxin should be obtained and administered as rapidly as possible because antitoxin cannot neutralize cell-bound toxin (p.1224).","Hoarseness suggests laryngeal diphtheria, in which laryngoscopy may be diagnostically helpful (p.1223).","Antibiotics in diphtheria are used primarily to prevent transmission to contacts and to halt further toxin production; they are not a substitute for antitoxin (p.1224).","Patients who recover should be vaccinated, since natural infection does not reliably confer protective immunity; antitoxin levels should be measured (6 months after antitoxin administration) (p.1224)."],"pathophysiology":"Disease is driven by diphtheria toxin produced by toxigenic (tox+) strains of C. diphtheriae. The tox structural gene is carried by lysogenic corynebacteriophage beta, and nontoxigenic strains can be converted to the toxigenic phenotype; iron-limiting conditions optimize toxin expression. The toxin is a single-chain 535-amino-acid protein produced in the pharyngeal pseudomembranous lesion, taken up into the bloodstream and distributed to all organs. After binding its receptor (a heparin-binding EGF-like precursor) it is internalized by receptor-mediated endocytosis; the A fragment enters the cytosol and irreversibly inhibits protein synthesis via NAD+-dependent ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2, causing cell death. Pseudomembranes consist of an inner fibrin band and a luminal neutrophil band overlying toxin-induced mucosal ulcers with edema, hyperemia, and vascular congestion; initially white and firmly adherent, they turn gray/green/black as necrosis advances and may extend into bronchial airways (p.1221-1223).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Group A streptococcal pharyngitis (exudative, but its exudate is not tightly adherent unlike the diphtheritic pseudomembrane) (p.1223)","Group C streptococcal pharyngitis (p.1223)","Respiratory virus pharyngitis (rhinovirus, influenza, parainfluenza, coronavirus, adenovirus) (p.1223)","Herpes simplex virus and Epstein-Barr virus (infectious mononucleosis) pharyngitis (p.1223)","Atypical bacterial pharyngitis (Mycoplasma pneumoniae, Chlamydia pneumoniae) (p.1223)","Acute HIV infection, gonococcal pharyngitis, and fusobacterial infection / Lemierre's syndrome (p.1223)","Candida (thrush) pharyngitis (p.1223)","Corynebacterium ulcerans / C. pseudotuberculosis, which express the same toxin and cause diphtheria-like disease (p.1222)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Culture of pseudomembrane fragments or submembranous swabs on selective medium (Loffler's or Tinsdale's, tellurite/colistin/nalidixic-acid based) plus nonselective medium such as blood agar (p.1223-1224)","Histopathologic demonstration of compatible gram-positive organisms / characteristic local lesions (p.1223-1224)","Notify the laboratory that diphtheria is being considered so special selective medium and biochemical differentiation from commensal corynebacteria are performed (p.1224)","Toxigenicity testing; report all toxigenic C. diphtheriae isolates to the state health department (reportable disease) (p.1224)","Nasopharyngeal culture in patients with cutaneous diphtheria (p.1224)","Repeat throat culture ~2 weeks after completing antimicrobial therapy to document eradication (p.1224)","Cardiac workup to assess for myocarditis (p.1225)","Antitoxin (antibody) titer measurement after recovery, performed 6 months after antitoxin administration (p.1224)"],"redFlags":["Neck (submandibular/paratracheal) edema and difficulty breathing in advanced cases carry a poor prognosis (p.1223)","Airway obstruction is a major early risk and accounts for 60-65% of deaths, usually in the first 1-2 weeks; sloughing pseudomembranes may obstruct the airway or extend to larynx/tracheobronchial tree (children especially prone) (p.1224)","Toxic cardiomyopathy/myocarditis (cardiac arrhythmias or congestive heart failure), a late manifestation arising >=1 week after respiratory symptoms, accounting for 20-25% of deaths (p.1223-1224)","Bull-neck diphtheria, ventricular tachycardia, atrial fibrillation, or complete heart block (p.1224)","Polyneuropathy 3-5 weeks after onset: cranial nerve involvement (lingual/facial numbness, dysphonia, dysphagia), then weakness of respiratory and abdominal muscles, limb paresis, sensory ataxia, and autonomic dysfunction with hypotension (~15% of deaths) (p.1224)","Large or extensive pseudomembranes and laryngeal/tracheal/bronchial involvement indicate severe disease and poor prognosis (p.1223-1224)"],"prognosis":"A systematic review of >20 outbreaks found a case-fatality ratio of 29% among unvaccinated, untreated individuals, with children under 5 at 1.5-fold higher mortality risk. Fatal pseudomembranous diphtheria typically occurs in unimmunized patients or those with nonprotective antibody titers. Risk factors for death include bull-neck diphtheria; myocarditis with ventricular tachycardia; atrial fibrillation; complete heart block; age >60 or <6 months; alcoholism; extensive pseudomembrane elongation; and laryngeal/tracheal/bronchial involvement. A key predictor of fatal outcome is the interval between onset of local disease and administration of antitoxin. Cutaneous diphtheria has a low mortality rate and is rarely associated with myocarditis or peripheral neuropathy. Polyneuropathy is typically reversible in patients who survive the acute phase (p.1224-1225).","pitfalls":["Antitoxin cannot neutralize cell-bound toxin, so delay in administration worsens outcome; do not wait for laboratory confirmation once diphtheria is clinically suspected (p.1224).","Antibiotics are not a substitute for antitoxin; they mainly prevent transmission and further toxin production (p.1224).","Glucocorticoids have NOT been shown to reduce the risk of myocarditis or polyneuropathy (p.1225).","Cutaneous diphtheria lesions are not characteristic and are indistinguishable from other dermatoses, so diagnosis requires laboratory confirmation; the punched-out appearance is only occasional, not consistent (p.1224).","Corynebacterium pseudodiphtheriticum is a nontoxigenic normal throat commensal and does not pose significant risk; it must be differentiated from C. diphtheriae (p.1224).","Drug-resistant and multidrug-resistant C. diphtheriae strains exist and are increasing; consider resistance when eradication efforts fail (p.1224).","Natural infection does not reliably confer immunity, so survivors must be vaccinated (p.1224)."],"severityClassification":"CDC case definitions: confirmed respiratory diphtheria (laboratory proven or epidemiologically linked to a culture-confirmed case) vs probable respiratory diphtheria (clinically compatible but not laboratory proven or epidemiologically linked). Carriers are individuals with positive C. diphtheriae cultures who are asymptomatic or symptomatic but lack pseudomembranes (p.1223).","references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e, Ch.155 (Diphtheria and Other Corynebacterial Infections), pp.1221-1225","Sharma NC et al: Diphtheria. Nat Rev Dis Primers 5:81, 2019"],"crossLinks":["PHARYNGITIS"]},"diseases_of_esophagus":{"name":"Diseases of the Esophagus","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":334,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2503-2513","clinicalPearls":["The major esophageal symptoms are heartburn, regurgitation, chest pain, dysphagia, odynophagia, and globus sensation; the clinical history is central to evaluation (Harrison 22e p.2503).","Dysphagia for both liquids and solids suggests a motility disorder such as achalasia, whereas dysphagia for solids alone suggests a structural lesion (stricture, web, ring, or tumor) (Harrison 22e p.2504).","Patient localization of where food 'hangs up' is notoriously imprecise; about 30% of distal esophageal obstructions are perceived as cervical (neck) dysphagia (Harrison 22e p.2504).","Intermittent solid-food dysphagia that is unchanged or slowly progressive over years points to a benign process (Schatzki ring or eosinophilic esophagitis), whereas dysphagia progressing over weeks to months raises concern for neoplasia (Harrison 22e p.295).","Odynophagia is uncommon with reflux esophagitis; its presence should raise suspicion of an alternative etiology, particularly pill-induced or infectious esophagitis (Harrison 22e p.2505, p.2513).","Achalasia is diagnosed by barium swallow and/or esophageal manometry; the barium study shows a dilated esophagus with poor emptying, an air-fluid level, and a tapered 'beak-like' narrowing at the LES (Harrison 22e p.2507).","Manometric hallmarks of achalasia are impaired (incomplete) deglutitive LES relaxation plus absent peristalsis; manometry is the most sensitive test as it detects disease before esophageal dilatation (Harrison 22e p.2507-2508).","Endoscopy is a necessary part of the achalasia evaluation to exclude pseudoachalasia from tumor infiltration at the cardia/distal esophagus, which accounts for up to 5% of suspected cases (Harrison 22e p.2507).","The GERD injury spectrum progresses from esophagitis to stricture to Barrett metaplasia to adenocarcinoma; esophageal adenocarcinoma incidence is rising in parallel with GERD (Harrison 22e p.2509).","Erosive esophagitis at the esophagogastric junction is the endoscopic hallmark of GERD but is found in only about one-third of GERD patients (Harrison 22e p.2510).","Eosinophilic esophagitis should be strongly considered in children and adults presenting with dysphagia and food impaction, especially with an atopic history (Harrison 22e p.2511).","EoE is confirmed histologically by esophageal mucosal eosinophilia with a peak density of 15 or more eosinophils per high-power field, in the setting of esophageal symptoms (Harrison 22e p.2512)."],"pathophysiology":"The esophagus is a muscular tube with a sphincter at each end (UES, LES) that transports food and otherwise remains empty. Disease manifests as impaired function (swallowing disorders, excessive gastroesophageal reflux) or pain. Dysphagia is subclassified by mechanism: structural/mechanical narrowing (lumen <13 mm reliably causes solid-food dysphagia) versus propulsive/motor disorders of peristalsis and deglutitive inhibition. In achalasia, autoimmune-mediated loss of myenteric plexus ganglion cells (involving both excitatory cholinergic and inhibitory nitric-oxide neurons), linked to latent HSV-1 infection plus genetic susceptibility, produces impaired deglutitive LES relaxation and absent peristalsis. GERD results from excessive reflux of gastric acid and pepsin causing a cytokine-mediated (rather than directly caustic) inflammatory mucosal injury; pathologic reflux follows progressive distortion of esophagogastric junction architecture (hiatus widening, loss of intraabdominal esophageal segment/flap valve, axial hiatal hernia, increased junction compliance) compounded by impaired acid clearance from disrupted peristalsis or reduced salivation. Eosinophilic esophagitis is an immunologic disorder driven by antigen (chiefly food allergen) sensitization in susceptible individuals, with eosinophil-predominant inflammation. (Harrison 22e p.2503, p.2506-2510, p.2511)","additionalDifferentials":["Achalasia (primary)","Distal esophageal spasm (DES)","Hypercontractile/jackhammer esophagus","Scleroderma esophagus (absent contractility with weak LES)","Pseudoachalasia from tumor infiltration of the cardia/distal esophagus","Chagas disease (Trypanosoma cruzi)","Opioid-induced esophageal dysmotility","Peptic stricture","Schatzki ring and esophageal webs (Plummer-Vinson/Paterson-Kelly syndrome)","Esophageal carcinoma (adenocarcinoma, squamous cell)","Pill-induced esophagitis","Coronary artery disease (for esophageal-type chest pain)","Functional heartburn / disorders of gut-brain interaction","Zenker and epiphrenic diverticula","Hiatal hernia"],"infectionMimics":["Candida esophagitis (white plaques/exudate; C. albicans most common; associated with stasis and immunosuppression)","Herpes simplex virus esophagitis (vesicles and small superficial ulcers; coexisting nose/lip vesicles)","Cytomegalovirus esophagitis (large serpiginous distal ulcers in immunocompromised hosts)","Acute HIV seroconversion esophageal ulceration (with oral ulcers and maculopapular rash)","Tuberculosis or histoplasmosis causing traction midesophageal diverticula"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Coronary artery disease / angina mimicking esophageal chest pain","Eosinophilic esophagitis mimicking GERD","Scleroderma and connective tissue disease","Drug hypersensitivity causing esophageal eosinophilia","Crohn disease and eosinophilic gastroenteritis","Pill esophagitis (singular deep ulcer at points of luminal narrowing, often near the carina, sparing distal esophagus)","Caustic/corrosive and radiation injury","Mucocutaneous diseases involving the esophagus (pemphigoid, lichen planus, epidermolysis bullosa)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Upper endoscopy (EGD) - single most useful test for esophageal dysphagia; allows biopsy and dilation","Barium contrast radiography / video fluoroscopic swallow study (better for proximal/cricopharyngeal lesions and esophageal function; 13-mm barium tablet increases stricture detection)","Esophageal high-resolution manometry / pressure topography (diagnose achalasia and DES; assess peristalsis before antireflux surgery)","Functional lumen imaging probe (FLIP) panometry (can evaluate esophageal dysphagia in lieu of manometry; strong correlation for achalasia and normal contractility)","Ambulatory 24-96 h esophageal pH monitoring (pH <4 for >6% of the day indicates GERD), optionally with intraluminal impedance for nonacidic reflux","Esophageal mucosal biopsies (required to distinguish infectious vs eosinophilic vs reflux esophagitis; peak >=15 eosinophils/HPF for EoE)","Endoscopic ultrasound (EUS) for staging esophageal cancer and assessing Barrett dysplasia / submucosal lesions","CT scanning when pseudoachalasia is suspected and endoscopy nondiagnostic"],"redFlags":["Progressive solid-food dysphagia with weight loss - classic presentation of esophageal cancer; warrants prompt endoscopy (Harrison 22e p.2507).","Odynophagia (painful swallowing) - usually indicates ulceration; raises suspicion of pill-induced or infectious esophagitis (Harrison 22e p.295, p.2513).","Iron-deficiency anemia / chronic occult GI bleeding accompanying esophageal symptoms.","Hoarseness developing after dysphagia - may indicate recurrent laryngeal nerve compromise by malignancy (Harrison 22e p.295).","Abrupt onset of symptoms (<1 year), advanced age, and weight loss in apparent achalasia - suggests pseudoachalasia from malignancy (Harrison 22e p.2507).","Food impaction with inability to pass a bolus even with liquids - typical of structural dysphagia (Harrison 22e p.295).","Severe cough with swallowing - may signal tracheoesophageal fistula.","Finding of high-grade dysplasia in Barrett esophagus - high rate of progression to and coexisting prevalence of adenocarcinoma; mandates intervention (Harrison 22e p.2511)."],"prognosis":"Esophageal cancer carries poor survival even when small because abundant esophageal lymphatics permit early regional nodal metastasis. Barrett metaplasia progresses to adenocarcinoma at an estimated 0.1-0.3% per year through low- and high-grade dysplasia, with greatest risk in obese white males in the sixth decade. Untreated achalasia predisposes to stasis esophagitis and increases squamous cell cancer risk roughly 17-fold; durable therapies (pneumatic dilation, Heller myotomy, POEM) achieve symptom relief in about 80-90% of patients, though peristalsis does not recover and chest pain responds less well than dysphagia. EoE is chronic with nearly universal recurrence on stopping therapy, requiring maintenance treatment. Erosive esophagitis and peptic strictures have become increasingly rare in the era of potent acid suppression. (Harrison 22e p.2507-2508, p.2510-2512)","pitfalls":["Treating presumed GERD empirically without recognizing that persistent dysphagia or chest pain may instead reflect eosinophilic esophagitis, peptic stricture, motility disorder, esophageal cancer, or coronary artery disease (Harrison 22e p.2510).","Attributing 'heartburn' to GERD when the term is misused for indigestion or other sensations - clarify the patient's meaning (Harrison 22e p.2503).","Assuming neck-localized dysphagia is oropharyngeal - about 30% of distal esophageal lesions refer pain proximally (Harrison 22e p.2504).","Mistaking pseudoachalasia (tumor infiltration, up to 5% of cases) for primary achalasia - omit endoscopy at peril (Harrison 22e p.2507).","Diagnosing distal esophageal spasm before excluding the more common peptic and infectious esophagitis and, above all, cardiac disease (Harrison 22e p.2509).","Aggressive esophageal dilation in EoE - the stiff-walled esophagus risks deep mural laceration or perforation (Harrison 22e p.2512).","Relying on peripheral blood eosinophilia (present in only 25-50%) or allergy testing (poor sensitivity/specificity) to diagnose EoE or identify trigger foods (Harrison 22e p.2512).","Over-interpreting nonspecific minor manometric findings (hypertensive/hypotensive peristalsis) as a defined motility disorder; reflux and anxiety/depression are common in these patients (Harrison 22e p.2509)."],"severityClassification":"GERD esophageal injury spectrum (Fig. 334-9): esophagitis -> stricture -> Barrett metaplasia -> adenocarcinoma; reflux testing defines GERD as esophageal pH <4 for >6% of the day. Barrett progression staged histologically as no dysplasia -> low-grade dysplasia -> high-grade dysplasia -> adenocarcinoma. High-resolution manometry differentiates three achalasia subtypes: type I classic (minimal esophageal-body pressurization), type II with esophageal compression/pressurization, and type III spastic achalasia (Fig. 334-6). EoE histologic threshold: peak >=15 eosinophils per high-power field. (Harrison 22e p.2508-2511)","references":["Harrison 22e p.2503-2513","Harrison 22e ch.47 (Dysphagia) p.293-296","Shaheen NJ et al. ACG guideline: diagnosis and management of Barrett's esophagus. Am J Gastroenterol 117:1177, 2022 (Harrison 22e p.2503 Further Reading)"],"crossLinks":["gerd_chest","peptic_ulcer"]},"disorders_of_absorption":{"name":"Malabsorption (Disorders of Absorption)","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":336,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2540-2550","clinicalPearls":["Steatorrhea (fecal fat excretion >7% of dietary fat) is a common manifestation of malabsorption, producing large, bulky, malodorous stools; malabsorptive diarrhea is typically precipitated by eating and lessens or resolves with fasting, which helps distinguish it from secretory (e.g., enterotoxigenic) diarrhea that persists during fasting (Harrison 22e p.2540-2541).","Celiac disease (celiac sprue / gluten-sensitive enteropathy) is a small-intestinal enteropathy from an immune response to gluten (wheat, rye, barley, some oats), characterized by autoantibodies to tissue transglutaminase; it primarily affects the proximal small bowel, so duodenal-limited disease may present as isolated iron-deficiency anemia while diffuse jejunal disease causes severe diarrhea, weight loss, and multinutrient malabsorption (Harrison 22e p.2545).","Tissue transglutaminase IgA (TTG-IgA) is the celiac screening test of choice; serum IgA should be measured concurrently because IgA deficiency causes false-negative TTG-IgA, in which case IgG-based testing (TTG-IgG or anti-deamidated gliadin peptide DGP-IgG) is used (Harrison 22e p.2545).","In adults with positive celiac serology, diagnosis is confirmed by endoscopy with small-intestinal biopsy showing villus blunting, crypt hyperplasia, and increased intraepithelial lymphocytes; the Marsh classification grades severity (Harrison 22e p.2545).","PITFALL: celiac serologic and histologic testing must be performed while the patient is consuming gluten, since self-discontinuation of gluten before testing produces false-negative results; HLA-DQ2/DQ8 testing is then useful because a negative result rules out celiac disease (negative predictive value >99%) (Harrison 22e p.2544-2545).","HLA-DQ2 and DQ8 are necessary but not sufficient for celiac disease and are present in 25-35% of the general population, so their detection is NOT useful for diagnosis; their value lies in the very high negative predictive value when absent (Harrison 22e p.2544).","Dermatitis herpetiformis, a vesicular rash mediated by IgA skin deposits, is highly associated with celiac disease; celiac disease also carries increased risk of other autoimmune disorders (type 1 diabetes, autoimmune thyroid disease) and is more frequent in Down and Turner syndromes (Harrison 22e p.2545).","The mainstay of celiac treatment is a strict lifelong gluten-free diet with rigorous dietitian instruction; the most common cause of persistent symptoms or residual disease activity is dietary nonadherence or inadvertent gluten exposure (Harrison 22e p.2545-2546).","Tropical sprue presents with chronic diarrhea, steatorrhea, weight loss, and deficiencies of BOTH folate and vitamin B12; malabsorption of two unrelated substances is required for diagnosis, and it is associated with residence in or travel to specific tropical regions (southern India, Pakistan, Philippines, Puerto Rico, Haiti, Cuba) (Harrison 22e p.2546-2547).","In India, tropical sprue is regarded by some as a postinfectious complication occurring in 8-20% of people after an attack of infectious gastroenteritis, with southern Indian patients often reporting acute enteritis preceding steatorrhea; tropical sprue is rarely seen in Africa, Jamaica, or Southeast Asia, and its overall incidence has declined (Harrison 22e p.2546-2547).","Whipple's disease (Tropheryma whipplei, a PAS-positive non-acid-fast rod) most often affects middle-aged men; arthralgias/arthritis precede GI symptoms by an average of ~6 years (joint disease in >80%), and CNS and cardiac involvement occur later; diagnosis uses biopsy with PCR and PAS-positive macrophages (Harrison 22e p.2546).","Small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth arises from stasis (scleroderma, pseudo-obstruction, blind loops, strictures, diverticulosis) and from hypochlorhydria (chronic PPI use); it causes B12 deficiency (macrocytic anemia, peripheral neuropathy) from bacterial B12 metabolism, yet serum folate may be ELEVATED due to bacterial folate synthesis (Harrison 22e p.2543).","In ileal disease/resection, <100 cm involvement causes bile-acid spillage into the colon and secretory (cholerheic) diarrhea responsive to bile-acid sequestrants, whereas >100 cm exceeds hepatic synthetic capacity to replace lost bile acids, making fat malabsorption (steatorrhea) the dominant mechanism (Harrison 22e p.2542-2543).","Chronic pancreatitis and exocrine pancreatic insufficiency cause voluminous steatorrhea with deficiency of fat-soluble vitamins (most commonly D and K), and stool elastase is helpful for diagnosing pancreatic insufficiency (Harrison 22e p.2542, 2549).","In short-bowel/lipid malabsorption with an intact colon in continuity, luminal fatty acids saponify calcium, freeing oxalate for colonic absorption and increasing the risk of calcium-oxalate renal stones (hyperoxaluria) (Harrison 22e p.2548)."],"pathophysiology":"Malabsorption results from disruption of one or more of the three phases of nutrient assimilation. (1) Luminal/digestive phase: intraluminal hydrolysis by gastric and pancreatic enzymes and solubilization of fat in bile-salt mixed micelles; impaired by postgastrectomy rapid transit, pancreatic insufficiency, hepatobiliary disease, ileal disease interrupting the enterohepatic circulation of bile acids, and small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth (which deconjugates bile acids causing luminal bile-acid deficiency). (2) Mucosal/absorptive phase: brush-border enzymatic digestion (disaccharidases, peptidases, enterokinase) and enterocyte uptake; impaired by isolated brush-border defects (lactase deficiency, glucose-galactose malabsorption, sucrase-isomaltase deficiency) or by diffuse enteropathies (celiac disease, tropical sprue, Whipple's disease, Crohn's, infections). In celiac disease, tissue transglutaminase deamidates gluten-derived peptides, promoting their presentation by antigen-presenting cells in HLA-DQ2/DQ8 individuals, producing proximal villus atrophy with crypt hyperplasia; steatorrhea arises from mucosal malabsorption plus a secretory component from crypt hyperplasia/hypersecretion, and secondary lactase deficiency may develop. (3) Postmucosal/delivery phase: re-esterification of long-chain fatty acids into chylomicrons exiting via lymphatics; impaired by abetalipoproteinemia (failed chylomicron formation) and by lymphatic obstruction/dysfunction (lymphangiectasia, lymphoma, retroperitoneal fibrosis) or circulatory causes (Fontan physiology, constrictive pericarditis, CHF), which also cause protein-losing enteropathy. Specific nutrient deficiencies localize disease: duodenal/proximal disease causes iron and folate deficiency; ileal disease/resection causes B12 deficiency and bile-acid malabsorption; steatorrhea causes fat-soluble vitamin (A, D, E, K) deficiency (Harrison 22e p.2540-2549).","additionalDifferentials":["Celiac disease (gluten-sensitive enteropathy) (Harrison 22e p.2545)","Tropical sprue (Harrison 22e p.2546)","Whipple's disease (Tropheryma whipplei) (Harrison 22e p.2546)","Small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth (Harrison 22e p.2543)","Chronic pancreatitis / exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (Harrison 22e p.2542)","Lactose intolerance from lactase deficiency (Harrison 22e p.2544)","Bile-acid (cholerheic) diarrhea from ileal disease/resection or primary bile-acid diarrhea (Harrison 22e p.2543)","Short-bowel syndrome (Harrison 22e p.2547-2548)","Crohn's disease with ileal involvement (Harrison 22e p.2542-2543)","Protein-losing enteropathy and intestinal lymphangiectasia (Harrison 22e p.2548-2549)","Abetalipoproteinemia (Harrison 22e p.2545)","Environmental enteric dysfunction (formerly tropical enteropathy) (Harrison 22e p.2547)","Nonceliac gluten sensitivity (Harrison 22e p.2546)"],"infectionMimics":["Giardia lamblia (giardiasis) (Harrison 22e p.2547)","Whipple's disease (Tropheryma whipplei) (Harrison 22e p.2546)","Tropical-environment enteric infections to exclude before diagnosing tropical sprue: Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium parvum, Cystoisospora (Isospora) belli, Strongyloides stercoralis, Cyclospora cayetanensis, Yersinia enterocolitica, Clostridioides difficile (Harrison 22e p.2547)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Irritable bowel syndrome (celiac disease can mimic IBS; SIBO and bile-acid malabsorption occur in IBS-D) (Harrison 22e p.2543, 2546)","Microscopic colitis (Harrison 22e p.2546)","Intestinal lymphoma, including enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.2546)","Crohn's disease (Harrison 22e p.2542)","Hyperthyroidism causing rapid-transit diarrhea/malabsorption (Harrison 22e p.2542)","Long-standing diabetes mellitus with enteric autonomic neuropathy (Harrison 22e p.2542)","Scleroderma and other connective-tissue/motility disorders (Harrison 22e p.2542)","Menetrier's disease (hypertrophic gastropathy) as a cause of protein-losing enteropathy (Harrison 22e p.2549)"],"additionalInvestigations":["72-h quantitative fecal fat collection on a 100-g fat diet (gold standard for steatorrhea); qualitative spot Sudan III stain for fecal fat (Harrison 22e p.2549)","24-h stool weight/volume to confirm clinically significant diarrhea; stool osmotic gap (from spot fecal Na and K) to identify osmotic diarrhea (Harrison 22e p.2549)","Tissue transglutaminase IgA (TTG-IgA) with concurrent serum total IgA; TTG-IgG or anti-deamidated gliadin peptide (DGP-IgG) if IgA-deficient (Harrison 22e p.2545)","HLA-DQ2/DQ8 testing chiefly to rule OUT celiac disease (high negative predictive value), especially if gluten was discontinued before testing (Harrison 22e p.2544-2545)","Endoscopy with small-intestinal (duodenal) biopsy; Marsh grading; in celiac, follow-up serology and a follow-up biopsy to document mucosal healing (Harrison 22e p.2545)","Stool elastase for pancreatic insufficiency (Harrison 22e p.2549)","SIBO testing: duodenal aspirate for bacterial titers (gold standard, limited availability) or lactulose/glucose breath hydrogen testing (interpret carefully for false positives) (Harrison 22e p.2543)","Lactose intolerance: lactose-exclusion diet trial, lactose-tolerance test (serum glucose rise <20 mg/L is positive), or breath hydrogen test (>20 ppm rise) (Harrison 22e p.2544-2545)","Protein-losing enteropathy: stool alpha-1 antitrypsin (spot or 24-h) or alpha-1 antitrypsin clearance; lymphopenia with hypoproteinemia supports GI protein loss (Harrison 22e p.2548-2549)","Whipple's disease: tissue PCR for T. whipplei, PAS staining, immunohistochemistry on biopsy (Harrison 22e p.2546)","Labs reflecting site-specific deficiency: iron studies, B12 and folate, fat-soluble vitamins, PT/INR as a functional marker of vitamin K, serum carotene; cross-sectional imaging (MR enterography/CT), video capsule and double-balloon enteroscopy for occult mucosal/lymphatic disease (Harrison 22e p.2549)"],"redFlags":["Persistent diarrhea, weight loss, and villus atrophy after >=1 year of a strict gluten-free diet with negative serology, indicating refractory celiac disease (Harrison 22e p.2546)","Type 2 refractory celiac disease (clonal CD3+ intraepithelial lymphocytes with monoclonal T-cell receptor gamma-chain rearrangement), which progresses to enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma in 33-50% within 5 years (Harrison 22e p.2546)","New or worsening malabsorption with abdominal mass/imaging lymphadenopathy suggesting small-bowel adenocarcinoma or intestinal lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.2546)","Hypoproteinemia with peripheral edema, diarrhea, and lymphopenia (protein-losing enteropathy) requiring exclusion of cardiac and lymphatic causes (Harrison 22e p.2548)","Severe vitamin K deficiency causing coagulopathy (elevated PT/INR) and significant B12-related peripheral neuropathy (Harrison 22e p.2549)","Whipple's disease relapse, frequently heralding first CNS involvement (Harrison 22e p.2546)"],"prognosis":"Most patients with celiac disease respond fully to a strict lifelong gluten-free diet with mucosal healing and symptom resolution; about 10% of those with persistent active disease develop refractory celiac disease, and type 2 refractory disease carries a poor prognosis with enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma in 33-50% of cases at 5 years (requiring intensive chemotherapy and sometimes stem cell transplantation). Tropical sprue is most often curative with broad-spectrum antibiotics plus folic acid, especially if the patient leaves the tropical area and does not return. Whipple's disease requires prolonged antibiotics, but relapses are common. In short-bowel syndrome the intestine adapts over up to 2 years (with reports of continued improvement at 3-5 years); prognosis and parenteral-nutrition dependence relate to the extent of resection, presence of colon in continuity, and retention of the ileocecal valve, with intestinal failure defined by persistent parenteral-nutrition dependence (Harrison 22e p.2542-2548).","pitfalls":["Testing for celiac disease (serology and biopsy) only after the patient has already self-removed gluten gives false-negative results; testing must be done ON a gluten-containing diet, or use HLA-DQ2/DQ8 to exclude disease (Harrison 22e p.2544-2545).","Interpreting HLA-DQ2/DQ8 positivity as diagnostic of celiac disease is wrong, as 25-35% of the general population carry these alleles without disease; only a negative result is clinically useful (rules out) (Harrison 22e p.2544).","Missing IgA deficiency when only TTG-IgA is ordered will produce false-negative celiac screening; order total serum IgA and use IgG-based tests if deficient (Harrison 22e p.2545).","Diagnosing tropical sprue before excluding enteric infection: it should NOT be entertained until cysts and trophozoites are excluded in three stool samples, and a gluten-free diet does NOT improve tropical sprue (unlike celiac disease) (Harrison 22e p.2547).","Breath hydrogen testing for lactose intolerance or SIBO can be inaccurate with abnormal gastric emptying or transit, and SIBO can itself produce false-positive/abnormal breath tests and mimic lactose intolerance (Harrison 22e p.2543, 2545).","In SIBO, expecting low folate is a trap; serum folate may be ELEVATED from bacterial synthesis while B12 is low (Harrison 22e p.2543).","In protein-losing enteropathy, isolated low albumin with normal globulins suggests renal/hepatic disease rather than GI loss; alpha-1 antitrypsin cannot assess gastric protein loss because it is degraded in acid (Harrison 22e p.2548-2549).","Treating the hypoproteinemia of protein-losing enteropathy directly rather than the underlying disease is misdirected; therapy should target the underlying cause (Harrison 22e p.2549)."],"severityClassification":"There is no single unified severity scale for malabsorption; severity is assessed by the degree of nutrient deficiency and steatorrhea and by disease-specific schemes. Celiac mucosal injury is graded by the Marsh classification of biopsy lesions (intraepithelial lymphocytosis, crypt hyperplasia, and degrees of villus atrophy). Celiac complications are stratified into refractory celiac disease type 1 (normal intraepithelial lymphocyte population, better prognosis) versus type 2 (clonal CD3+ intraepithelial lymphocytes with monoclonal T-cell receptor gamma rearrangement, worse prognosis with high lymphoma risk). Bile-acid versus fatty-acid diarrhea are distinguished by extent of ileal disease (<100 cm vs >100 cm), degree of steatorrhea, and response to cholestyramine versus a low-fat diet. Short-bowel syndrome is defined as <200 cm of remaining small bowel, and intestinal failure as persistent parenteral-nutrition dependence (generally <100 cm of small bowel with no colon in continuity) (Harrison 22e p.2543, 2545-2548).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2540-2550"],"crossLinks":["whipple_disease","chronic_pancreatitis","iron_deficiency_anemia"]},"diverticular_disease":{"name":"Diverticular Disease","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":339,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2578-2581","clinicalPearls":["Diverticulosis is the most common cause of hematochezia (brisk lower-GI bleed) in patients >60 years; bleeding is abrupt in onset, usually painless, sometimes massive, and often from the right colon. Chronic or occult bleeding is NOT characteristic (Harrison 22e p.2496, 2579).","Only ~20% of patients with diverticulosis will ever have GI bleeding; most bleeds are self-limited and stop spontaneously with bowel rest (colonic diverticula stop bleeding spontaneously in >=90% of patients). Lifetime risk of rebleeding is ~25% (Harrison 22e p.2496, 2579).","Acute uncomplicated diverticulitis characteristically presents with fever, anorexia, left lower quadrant abdominal pain, and obstipation (Harrison 22e p.2579).","Diagnosis of diverticulitis is best made on a contrast-enhanced abdominal and pelvic CT showing sigmoid diverticula, thickened colonic wall >4 mm, and pericolic fat inflammation WITHOUT extravasation of contrast or fluid (Harrison 22e p.2579).","Diverticula commonly affect the left and sigmoid colon and the rectum is always spared; however in Asian populations 70% of diverticula are in the right colon and cecum (Harrison 22e p.2579).","The pseudodiverticulum (protrusion of mucosa and submucosa only, through the muscularis propria) is the most common type seen in the colon; a true diverticulum is herniation of the entire bowel wall (Harrison 22e p.2578).","Perforated diverticular disease is staged using the Hinchey classification (I: confined paracolic abscess; II: distant/pelvic abscess after spontaneous closure; III: noncommunicating perforation with purulent peritonitis; IV: free perforation with fecal peritonitis); modified to add stage Ia for phlegmon/early abscess (Harrison 22e p.2580).","Pneumaturia or passage of stool/air through the vagina or skin signals a fistula (colovesical, colovaginal, or colocutaneous); colovaginal fistulas are more common in women who have undergone hysterectomy (Harrison 22e p.2579).","Colonoscopy should be considered ~6 weeks AFTER resolution of a first episode, because colon cancer prevalence is low overall (<2%) but higher after complicated diverticulitis (6-8%) (Harrison 22e p.2580).","Routine antibiotics do NOT shorten time to symptom resolution or reduce complications/recurrence in immunocompetent patients with uncomplicated diverticular disease (AVOD and DIABOLO trials, plus meta-analysis) (Harrison 22e p.2580).","Diverticulosis affects about half the population aged >60 years and the majority remain asymptomatic; only ~5% develop acute diverticular disease (Harrison 22e p.2578)."],"pathophysiology":"Diverticula form where the nutrient artery (vasa recta) penetrates the muscularis propria, creating a point of weakness in the colonic wall; most arise in the sigmoid colon, likely reflecting its high-pressure zone where higher-amplitude contractions and firm/constipated stool act on a weak segment. Pathogenesis is now viewed as multifactorial rather than simply low-fiber diet: gut dysbiosis, chronic low-grade inflammation driving neuronal degeneration with resulting dysmotility and high intraluminal pressure, and abnormal collagen cross-linking causing loss of compliance all contribute. Genome-wide association studies (Iceland, Denmark, UK biobanks) link LAMB4 (extracellular-matrix laminin family) and TNFSF15 (TNF family, inflammation) variants to early-onset severe disease. Complications occur when the vasa recta is compressed or eroded (bleeding) or a diverticulum perforates (Harrison 22e p.2578-2579).","additionalDifferentials":["Colorectal cancer (parallel epidemiology and overlapping presentation; must exclude with colonoscopy after an episode)","Irritable bowel syndrome (SCAD can mimic it)","Crohn's disease (diverticulitis can be confused with CD clinically and radiographically)","Appendicitis","Ischemic colitis","Twisted ovarian cyst / gynecologic pathology"],"infectionMimics":["Acute appendicitis","Infectious colitis","Pelvic inflammatory disease / tubo-ovarian abscess"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Colorectal cancer","Inflammatory bowel disease (Crohn's disease, ulcerative colitis)","Irritable bowel syndrome","Ischemic colitis","Giant sigmoid diverticulum (air-fluid level in LLQ on plain film)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced abdominal/pelvic CT (diagnostic modality of choice for diverticulitis)","Multiplanar CT angiography for active diverticular bleeding localization","Nuclear medicine tagged red-cell scan for bleeding localization","Mesenteric angiography (allows superselective coil embolization if active bleeding is localized)","Colonoscopy ~6 weeks after resolution to exclude malignancy (avoid during the acute attack)","CRP and WBC count to gauge inflammation/disease progression risk"],"redFlags":["Signs of peritonitis indicating diverticular perforation","Abscess formation (~16% of complicated disease) with abdominal distention and localized peritonitis","Free perforation with fecal peritonitis (Hinchey IV)","Fistula (colovesical with pneumaturia, colovaginal, colocutaneous)","Obstruction/stricture","Air-fluid level in LLQ on plain film suggesting giant sigmoid diverticulum (impending perforation)","Massive or refractory lower-GI hemorrhage, hemodynamic instability, or large-volume transfusion requirement","Immunocompromise, chronic renal failure, or collagen-vascular disease (fivefold greater perforation risk on recurrent attacks)"],"prognosis":"Most patients with diverticulosis stay asymptomatic; only ~5% develop acute diverticular disease, and 10-25% of those with diverticular disease have recurrent symptoms with up to 10% requiring surgery. Diverticular bleeds are largely self-limited (spontaneous cessation in >=90%), with lifetime rebleeding risk ~25%. After complicated diverticulitis the risk of underlying colon cancer rises to 6-8% (vs <2% overall). Elective surgical resection for recurrent SUDD improved quality of life and was more cost-effective at 5 years (DIRECT trial) (Harrison 22e p.2578-2581).","pitfalls":["Do NOT perform colonoscopy during acute diverticulitis (risk of perforation); defer it ~6 weeks until the attack resolves.","Do not reflexively give antibiotics for uncomplicated diverticulitis in immunocompetent patients - trials show no benefit.","The historical advice to avoid nuts, corn, and popcorn rests on anecdotal data only and is not evidence-based; higher intake was actually associated with lower recurrence risk.","Mesalazine appears to help symptoms in SUDD but shows no benefit once objective inflammation (CRP, imaging) is considered.","A systematic review found no benefit to probiotics used alone.","Right-sided diverticula predominate in Asian populations, so a right-colon source should not be dismissed."],"severityClassification":"Hinchey classification of perforated diverticulitis: Stage I - perforation with confined paracolic abscess (modified stage Ia = phlegmon/early abscess); Stage II - perforation that closed spontaneously with distant (pelvic) abscess; Stage III - noncommunicating perforation with purulent peritonitis (diverticular neck closed off); Stage IV - free perforation communicating with the peritoneum producing fecal peritonitis. Clinically, disease is divided into uncomplicated (~75%) and complicated (~25%: abscess 16%, perforation 10%, stricture 5%, fistula 2%) (Harrison 22e p.2579-2580).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2578-2581","Harrison 22e p.2496"],"crossLinks":["colorectal_cancer","bowel_obstruction"]},"filariasis":{"name":"Filariasis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":240,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1819","clinicalPearls":["Eight filarial species infect humans, but four cause most symptomatic disease: Wuchereria bancrofti, Brugia malayi, Onchocerca volvulus, and Loa loa (Harrison 22e p.1819).","Filarial disease is characteristically more acute and intense in newly exposed travelers/transmigrants than in lifelong natives of endemic areas; infection usually requires repeated, prolonged exposure to infective larvae (Harrison 22e p.1819).","Lymphatic filariasis (W. bancrofti, B. malayi, B. timori) is widely distributed; W. bancrofti affects ~51 million people and B. malayi occurs primarily in eastern India, Indonesia, Malaysia, and the Philippines; humans are the only definitive host for W. bancrofti (Harrison 22e p.1819-1820).","Most W. bancrofti is nocturnally periodic (microfilariae scarce by day, increasing at night); blood for microfilaria detection must be timed to the regional periodicity (Harrison 22e p.1819-1821).","Common presentations of lymphatic filariasis are asymptomatic/subclinical microfilaremia, hydrocele, acute adenolymphangitis (ADL), and chronic lymphatic disease (lymphedema/elephantiasis) (Harrison 22e p.1820).","Genital lymphatic involvement (funiculitis, epididymitis, hydrocele, scrotal elephantiasis) occurs almost exclusively with W. bancrofti, not Brugia (Harrison 22e p.1820).","ADL lymphangitis is retrograde (spreads peripherally from the draining node), a feature that distinguishes filarial lymphangitis from ascending bacterial lymphangitis (Harrison 22e p.1820-1821).","Dermatolymphangioadenitis (DLA) presents with fever, chills, myalgias, demarcated edematous plaques, and entry lesions (often interdigital) and is frequently misdiagnosed as cellulitis (Harrison 22e p.1820).","Obstruction of retroperitoneal lymphatics can rupture renal lymphatics and produce intermittent chyluria, typically most prominent in the morning (Harrison 22e p.1820).","On high-frequency ultrasound, live adult worms in dilated lymphatics show a distinctive motion called the 'filarial dance sign'; worms are seen in the spermatic cord in up to 80% of W. bancrofti-infected men (Harrison 22e p.1821).","Circulating antigen assays (ELISA and rapid lateral-flow) for W. bancrofti have sensitivities of 93-100% and specificity approaching 100% and detect both microfilaremic and cryptic amicrofilaremic infection; there is currently NO circulating-antigen test for brugian filariasis (Harrison 22e p.1821).","Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia (TPE) presents with nocturnal paroxysmal cough/wheeze, weight loss, low-grade fever, marked blood eosinophilia (>3000/uL), and very high IgE and antifilarial antibody; microfilariae are cleared from blood by the lungs and are typically NOT found in peripheral blood (Harrison 22e p.1821-1822).","Onchocerciasis ('river blindness'), caused by O. volvulus, primarily affects skin, eyes, and lymph nodes; pruritus and a generalized papular rash are most common, and visual impairment is the most serious complication (Harrison 22e p.1822-1823).","Sclerosing keratitis (in 1-5% of infected persons) is the leading cause of onchocercal blindness; 'snowflake' punctate keratitis around dying microfilariae is common in younger patients and usually resolves (Harrison 22e p.1823).","Onchocercal lymphadenopathy can produce 'hanging groin'; rarely Nakalanga dwarfism and an association with epilepsy/nodding syndrome are seen; blind adults have a 3- to 4-fold higher mortality (Harrison 22e p.1823).","Loiasis (Loa loa, the African eye worm) is suggested by transient subconjunctival worm migration and episodic Calabar swellings (evanescent angioedema); microfilariae have a diurnal periodicity peaking ~10:00 a.m.-2:00 p.m. (Harrison 22e p.1823).","Wolbachia, an intracellular bacterial endosymbiont present in Brugia, Wuchereria, Mansonella, and Onchocerca, is a target for antifilarial (anti-Wolbachia) therapy (Harrison 22e p.1819, 1824)."],"pathophysiology":"Filariae are tissue-dwelling nematodes transmitted by mosquitoes or other arthropods; insect-borne infective larvae mature into adult worms that reside in lymphatics (W. bancrofti, Brugia) or subcutaneous tissue (Loa loa, O. volvulus). Adults release microfilariae that circulate in blood or migrate through skin; adults live many years while microfilariae survive 3-36 months. In lymphatic filariasis, pathology results mainly from inflammatory damage caused by ADULT worms (not microfilariae) in afferent lymphatics, with plasma-cell/eosinophil/macrophage infiltration, lymphatic dilation, valve incompetence, and progressive granulomatous reaction and fibrosis (worsened by worm death) leading to obstruction, lymphedema, and elephantiasis; injury is due both to the worm and to host inflammatory response. In contrast, onchocerciasis pathology is elicited by MICROFILARIAE (not adults), causing chronic dermal inflammation/atrophy/fibrosis, fibrous subcutaneous nodules (onchocercomata) around adults, and corneal neovascularization/scarring and intraocular inflammation leading to blindness. In TPE, microfilariae are trapped and cleared by the lungs, provoking allergic/eosinophilic intra-alveolar inflammation that can progress to interstitial fibrosis. Calabar swellings of loiasis are thought to be a hypersensitivity reaction to adult-worm antigens. The Wolbachia endosymbiont contributes to inflammatory reactions (Harrison 22e p.1819-1823).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute adenolymphangitis (ADL) vs thrombophlebitis, bacterial infection, and trauma (Harrison 22e p.1821)","Filarial lymphangitis vs ascending bacterial lymphangitis (distinguished by retrograde evolution) (Harrison 22e p.1821)","Chronic filarial lymphedema vs lymphedema of malignancy, postoperative scarring, trauma, chronic edematous states, and congenital lymphatic abnormalities (Harrison 22e p.1821)","DLA frequently misdiagnosed as cellulitis (Harrison 22e p.1820)","TPE vs asthma, Loffler's syndrome, allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis, eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Churg-Strauss), other systemic vasculitides (notably periarteritis nodosa), chronic eosinophilic pneumonia, and hypereosinophilic syndromes (Harrison 22e p.1822)"],"infectionMimics":["Ascending bacterial lymphangitis (mimics filarial ADL) (Harrison 22e p.1821)","Bacterial cellulitis (DLA is often diagnosed as cellulitis) (Harrison 22e p.1820)","Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (TPE differential) (Harrison 22e p.1822)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Lymphedema of malignancy, postoperative scarring, trauma, chronic edematous states, congenital lymphatic abnormalities (mimic chronic filarial lymphedema) (Harrison 22e p.1821)","Asthma and Loffler's syndrome (mimic TPE) (Harrison 22e p.1822)","Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Churg-Strauss), other systemic vasculitides including periarteritis nodosa, chronic eosinophilic pneumonia, hypereosinophilic syndromes (mimic TPE) (Harrison 22e p.1822)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Microscopy of blood, hydrocele fluid, or other body fluids for microfilariae, with concentration by polycarbonate membrane filtration (3 um pore) or Knott's formalin centrifugation technique; collection timed to microfilarial periodicity (Harrison 22e p.1821)","Circulating filarial antigen assays for W. bancrofti (ELISA and rapid lateral-flow); no antigen test available for brugian filariasis (Harrison 22e p.1821)","PCR for W. bancrofti and B. malayi DNA in blood (sensitivity equal to or greater than parasitologic methods) (Harrison 22e p.1821)","High-frequency ultrasound with Doppler of scrotum/lymph nodes/breast to visualize motile adult worms ('filarial dance sign') (Harrison 22e p.1821)","Radionuclide lymphoscintigraphy (mainly a research/lymphedema-assessment tool) (Harrison 22e p.1821)","Eosinophilia, elevated serum IgE, and elevated antifilarial antibody (supportive, with extensive helminth cross-reactivity; W. bancrofti- and B. malayi-specific antigen serology has specificity >98%) (Harrison 22e p.1821)","Skin snip (incubated in saline/medium; microfilariae emerge in 2-4 h or overnight) or excised nodule for onchocerciasis; Onchocerca-specific antibody assays (Harrison 22e p.1823)","Chest x-ray/CT and pulmonary function tests in TPE (restrictive in most, obstructive in half) (Harrison 22e p.1822)","Detection of L. loa microfilariae in blood, removal of adult worm from eye/subcutaneous biopsy, or PCR/recombinant-antigen serology for loiasis (Harrison 22e p.1823)"],"redFlags":["Sclerosing keratitis, anterior uveitis, chorioretinal lesions, or optic atrophy in onchocerciasis (impending/established blindness) (Harrison 22e p.1823)","High Loa loa microfilaremia (>30,000 microfilariae/mL): risk of severe post-treatment encephalopathy with ivermectin or DEC (Harrison 22e p.1822-1823)","Loiasis with CNS involvement during treatment of heavy microfilaremia (coma, encephalitis) (Harrison 22e p.1823)","Chyluria from retroperitoneal lymphatic obstruction in lymphatic filariasis (Harrison 22e p.1820)","Progressive interstitial pulmonary fibrosis in untreated TPE (Harrison 22e p.1822)"],"prognosis":"Filarial infections are chronic with potential long-term debilitating effects; adult worms live for many years (W. bancrofti/Brugia >2 decades; O. volvulus up to ~18 years, average ~9). Most microfilaremic persons remain clinically asymptomatic for years, and only a minority progress to acute or chronic disease, but lymphatic damage can become permanent (elephantiasis, hydrocele). In onchocerciasis, visual loss is the most serious outcome and blind adults have a 3- to 4-fold increased mortality. Loiasis, historically considered relatively benign in endemic populations, is now linked to chronic morbidity and increased mortality. TPE responds to therapy but relapses in ~12-25% of cases (sometimes years later); untreated TPE can progress to pulmonary fibrosis (Harrison 22e p.1819-1823).","pitfalls":["Seropositivity (antifilarial antibody) cannot be equated with active infection; endemic residents can become sensitized via mosquito exposure without patent infection (Harrison 22e p.1821).","Antifilarial antibody/antigen serology has extensive cross-reactivity with other helminths; W. bancrofti antigen tests can be falsely positive in Loa loa infection (Harrison 22e p.1821).","Many infected individuals are amicrofilaremic, and travelers are often amicrofilaremic, so a negative blood smear does not exclude filariasis (Harrison 22e p.1821, 1823).","Timing of blood collection to the local microfilarial periodicity is critical; mistimed samples miss nocturnally or diurnally periodic microfilariae (Harrison 22e p.1821).","DLA is often misdiagnosed as cellulitis (Harrison 22e p.1820).","Eosinophilia and elevated IgE are nonspecific (common to many parasitic infections) and are not diagnostic by themselves (Harrison 22e p.1823).","For every palpable onchocercal nodule there are an estimated four deeper nonpalpable ones (Harrison 22e p.1823)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison describes lymphatic filariasis along a clinical spectrum rather than a formal grading: asymptomatic/subclinical microfilaremia, hydrocele, acute adenolymphangitis (ADL)/dermatolymphangioadenitis (DLA), and chronic lymphatic disease (lymphedema progressing to elephantiasis). Onchocerciasis severity is gauged by infection burden (skin/eye involvement increasing with moderate-to-heavy microfilarial load). Loa loa microfilaremia is stratified by treatment risk, with >30,000 microfilariae/mL marking high risk of severe post-treatment encephalopathy (Harrison 22e p.1820-1823).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1819"],"crossLinks":[]},"frontotemporal_dementia":{"name":"Frontotemporal Dementia","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":443,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3485","clinicalPearls":["FTD is a group of clinical syndromes united by underlying frontotemporal lobar degeneration (FTLD) pathology; like other major neurodegenerative diseases it is considered a disorder of abnormal protein aggregation, with either tau or TDP-43 (transactive response DNA-binding protein of 43 kDa) implicated in most cases (Harrison 22e p.3485).","Younger onset than typical Alzheimer's: FTD most often begins in the fifth to seventh decades of life and is nearly as prevalent as Alzheimer's disease in this age group (Harrison 22e p.3485).","Three core clinical syndromes are described: the behavioral variant (bvFTD, the most common) and two forms of primary progressive aphasia (PPA) - the semantic and nonfluent/agrammatic variants (Harrison 22e p.3485-3486).","bvFTD manifests as social and emotional dysfunction - apathy, disinhibition, compulsivity, loss of empathy, and overeating - often but not always accompanied by deficits in executive control (Harrison 22e p.3486).","In semantic variant PPA patients slowly lose the ability to decode word, object, person-specific, and emotion meaning; in nonfluent/agrammatic variant PPA patients develop profound inability to produce words, often with prominent motor speech impairment (Harrison 22e p.3486).","Parietal functions such as visuospatial processing and arithmetic calculation are typically unaffected even late in the FTD syndromes, helping distinguish it from Alzheimer's disease (Harrison 22e p.3486).","Any of the three syndromes, most often bvFTD, may be accompanied by motor neuron disease (MND), in which case the term FTD-MND is applied; corticobasal syndrome (CBS) and progressive supranuclear palsy-Richardson syndrome (PSP-RS) are also considered part of the FTD clinical spectrum (Harrison 22e p.3486).","In contrast to Alzheimer's disease, the cholinergic system is relatively spared in FTD, which accounts for the poor efficacy of acetylcholinesterase inhibitors in this group (Harrison 22e p.3486).","Treatment is symptomatic only; there are currently no therapies known to slow progression or improve symptoms, and no disease-modifying treatments have shown efficacy to date (Harrison 22e p.3487)."],"pathophysiology":"FTD is linked to frontotemporal lobar degeneration (FTLD), characterized by focal atrophy of frontal, insular, and/or temporal cortex; atrophy often begins focally in one hemisphere before spreading to anatomically interconnected cortical and subcortical regions. bvFTD shows medial/orbital frontal cortex and anterior insula degeneration, semantic variant PPA the anterior temporal region, and nonfluent/agrammatic PPA the opercular frontal and precentral gyrus of the dominant hemisphere. Microscopically there is gliosis, microvacuolation, and neuronal loss; the disease is subtyped by the protein composition of neuronal and glial inclusions, which contain either tau or TDP-43 in roughly 90% of patients, with the remaining ~10% showing inclusions containing the FET family of proteins (FUS, Ewing sarcoma protein, TAF-15). Loss of cortical serotonergic innervation is common while the cholinergic system is relatively spared. Misfolded proteins are increasingly recognized to have prion-like/corruptive templating properties that may drive network-rooted patterns of disease spread. Autosomal dominant inheritance is seen in only 10-20% of cases, with mutations in C9orf72 (the most common genetic cause, a noncoding GGGGCC hexanucleotide expansion, presenting as bvFTD with or without MND and overlapping with ALS), GRN/progranulin, and MAPT/tau (Harrison 22e p.3485-3487).","additionalDifferentials":["Alzheimer's disease (FTD has relatively preserved memory and parietal function early; a small percentage of FTD syndromes show Alzheimer's pathology at autopsy) (Harrison 22e p.3486-3487)","Progressive supranuclear palsy-Richardson syndrome (PSP-RS) - within the FTD spectrum; falls, vertical supranuclear ophthalmoparesis, axial rigidity (Harrison 22e p.3487)","Corticobasal syndrome (CBS) - asymmetric rigidity, dystonia, myoclonus, apraxia, alien limb phenomena (Harrison 22e p.3488)","Idiopathic Parkinson's disease - PSP is often confused with PD, but PD does not produce downgaze paresis (Harrison 22e p.3488)","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis / motor neuron disease (FTD-MND overlap) (Harrison 22e p.3486)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Psychiatric disease - depression, idiopathic mania, or acting out: the abulia/blandness of frontal degeneration may be misinterpreted as depression and the disinhibition as mania or acting out; FTD can begin with schizophrenia-like features leading to misdiagnosis of a psychiatric condition (Harrison 22e p.3485, p.209)","Other focal frontal lobe causes: head trauma, ruptured aneurysms, hydrocephalus, tumors (metastases, glioblastoma, meningioma) (Harrison 22e p.208)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Structural neuroimaging (MRI) to demonstrate focal frontal/insular/temporal atrophy patterns characteristic of each syndrome (Harrison 22e p.3486)","Genetic testing for C9orf72, GRN, and MAPT mutations in suspected familial cases (Harrison 22e p.3486)","Molecular (tau) PET imaging shows promise but to date binds robustly only to Alzheimer's-related misfolded tau; amyloid PET may help when negative (Harrison 22e p.3487)"],"redFlags":["New-onset disinhibition, apathy, loss of empathy, compulsivity, or altered dietary preference in a middle-aged adult (Harrison 22e p.3486)","Progressive language breakdown - loss of word/object meaning or inability to produce words (Harrison 22e p.3486)","Emergence of motor neuron disease features in a patient with behavioral or language decline (FTD-MND) (Harrison 22e p.3486)"],"prognosis":"Caregivers carry a heavy burden, especially as the illness disrupts core emotional and personality functions. There are currently no therapies known to slow progression or improve symptoms; treatment is symptomatic. For the related PSP-RS within the FTD spectrum, death occurs within 5-10 years of onset (Harrison 22e p.3487).","pitfalls":["Behavioral variant FTD is frequently mistaken for a primary psychiatric disease - the apathy/blandness can be misread as depression and the disinhibition as mania or acting out; FTD may even begin with schizophrenia-like features and be misdiagnosed as a psychiatric condition. Later age of onset, significant cognitive deficits, or abnormal neuroimaging point to a degenerative process (Harrison 22e p.3485, p.209).","Clinical features do not allow reliable prediction of the underlying FTLD molecular subtype (tau vs TDP-43 vs FET) for all syndromes (Harrison 22e p.3487).","Acetylcholinesterase inhibitors are largely ineffective in FTD because the cholinergic system is relatively spared, unlike Alzheimer's disease (Harrison 22e p.3486).","Antipsychotics must be used with caution because FTD is often accompanied by parkinsonism, which they can exacerbate (Harrison 22e p.3487)."],"severityClassification":"No formal severity grading is given; FTD is categorized by core clinical syndrome - behavioral variant (bvFTD), semantic variant PPA, and nonfluent/agrammatic variant PPA - and by underlying FTLD molecular class (FTLD-tau, FTLD-TDP, FTLD-FET) (Harrison 22e p.3486-3487).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3485","Harrison 22e p.3486","Harrison 22e p.3487","Harrison 22e p.3488"],"crossLinks":["alzheimers_disease","amyotrophic_lateral_sclerosis"]},"gas_gangrene":{"name":"Gas Gangrene (Clostridial Myonecrosis)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":159,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1238-1243","clinicalPearls":["C. perfringens myonecrosis (gas gangrene) is one of the most fulminant gram-positive bacterial infections of humans; it is a true emergency requiring immediate surgical debridement and is invariably fatal if untreated (p.1241).","Traumatic gas gangrene presents with sudden onset of excruciating pain at the affected site and rapid development of a foul-smelling wound with thin serosanguineous discharge and gas bubbles; brawny edema and induration give way to cutaneous blisters with bluish to maroon-colored fluid (p.1241).","The patient's pain and toxic appearance are characteristically out of proportion to physical findings; the patient may be febrile, apathetic, tachycardic, tachypneic, and may express a feeling of impending doom (p.1218, cross-ref Chap.159).","The margin between healthy and necrotic tissue often advances several inches per hour despite appropriate antibiotic therapy; radical amputation remains the single best life-saving intervention (p.1241).","Incubation period for traumatic gas gangrene can be as short as 6 h and is usually <4 days (p.1241).","Spontaneous (nontraumatic) gas gangrene arises by hematogenous seeding of normal muscle, principally with C. septicum (aerotolerant), C. perfringens, and C. novyi, from a gastrointestinal portal of entry (p.1241).","First symptom of spontaneous gas gangrene may be confusion followed by abrupt onset of excruciating pain in the absence of trauma; with fever, these findings should heighten suspicion (p.1242).","Gram staining of drainage or tissue biopsy is usually definitive in traumatic disease, showing large gram-positive (or gram-variable) rods, an ABSENCE of inflammatory cells, and widespread soft tissue necrosis (p.1241).","CT and MRI help determine whether infection is localized or spreading along fascial planes, but must NOT replace surgical exploration, Gram's staining, and histopathologic examination (p.1242).","Emergent thorough surgical debridement with wide resection back to healthy viable muscle and skin is of extreme importance; closure of traumatic wounds or compound fractures should be delayed 5-6 days until sites are certain to be free of infection (p.1242)."],"pathophysiology":"Development requires an anaerobic environment and wound contamination with clostridial spores or vegetative organisms. Devitalized tissue, foreign bodies, and ischemia lower local oxygen and favor outgrowth. For C. perfringens and C. novyi, trauma must be sufficient to interrupt the blood supply to establish the anaerobic niche; the more aerotolerant C. septicum and C. tertium can seed normal tissue from GI lesions without this. Once established, organisms proliferate and elaborate exotoxins. The major C. perfringens toxins in gas gangrene are alpha toxin (a lethal hemolysin with phospholipase C and sphingomyelinase activity; the major virulence factor) and theta toxin (perfringolysin, a cholesterol-dependent cytolysin). Severe pain, rapid progression, marked tissue destruction, and the characteristic paucity of neutrophils in tissue are largely attributable to alpha-toxin-induced vascular occlusion by heterotypic platelet/neutrophil aggregates (via activation of platelet gpIIb/IIIa). Late cardiovascular collapse reflects theta toxin causing reduced systemic vascular resistance with increased cardiac output ('warm shock') and alpha toxin directly suppressing myocardial contractility, producing profound hypotension (p.1242).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Necrotizing fasciitis (including type I polymicrobial and C. perfringens necrotizing fasciitis); distinction from clostridial myonecrosis is made by muscle biopsy (p.1218/1241)","Other histotoxic clostridial necrotizing soft tissue infections (C. histolyticum, C. septicum, C. novyi, C. sordellii) (p.1241)","Synergistic necrotizing cellulitis / crepitant (crepitus) cellulitis due to mixed anaerobic and aerobic flora (p.gas-forming soft-tissue infections)","Fournier's gangrene (C. perfringens with mixed aerobic/anaerobic microbes can cause aggressive type I necrotizing fasciitis or Fournier's gangrene) (p.1239)","Clostridial sepsis / bacteremia with myonecrosis (grave prognosis when bacteremia accompanies myonecrosis) (p.1240)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Gas tracking/dissecting tissue planes from non-clostridial gas-forming processes; note gas gangrene may develop in the ABSENCE of clostridial infection (other gas-producing organisms) (p.crepitus/gas-forming infections)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Gram stain of a smear of involved tissue (direct examination is of major importance): large gram-positive/gram-variable rods with paucity of leukocytes (p.1242)","Surgical exploration with histopathologic examination (widespread tissue destruction, paucity of leukocytes in infected tissue with accumulation of leukocytes in adjacent vessels, gram-positive rods with or without spores) (p.1242)","Blood cultures (anaerobic) - obtain when spontaneous gas gangrene is suspected, as bacteremia usually precedes cutaneous manifestations by several hours (p.1242)","CT and MRI to define localized vs fascial-plane spread (adjunct only; must not delay surgery) (p.1242)","Needle aspiration or punch biopsy may provide an etiologic diagnosis in at least 20% of cases (p.1242)","In survivors of C. septicum bacteremia/spontaneous gangrene: aggressive diagnostic workup to rule out gastrointestinal pathology (e.g., colonic malignancy) (p.1241)"],"redFlags":["Pain out of proportion to physical findings - excruciating pain at the affected site (p.1241/1218)","Crepitus and gas in the tissues / gas on imaging with rapidly progressive necrosis (p.1241-1242)","Tissue margin advancing several inches per hour despite antibiotics (p.1241)","Systemic toxaemia: shock, hypotension, multiorgan failure, bacteremia, intravascular hemolysis (p.1241-1242)","Foul-smelling serosanguineous wound discharge with bluish/maroon hemorrhagic bullae and brawny induration (p.1241)","Spontaneous excruciating pain with confusion and fever and NO obvious portal of entry - suggests spontaneous gas gangrene (correct diagnosis frequently delayed or missed) (p.1242)","Feeling of impending doom with apathy, tachycardia, tachypnea (p.1218)"],"prognosis":"Invariably fatal if untreated. When patients become bacteremic the mortality rate exceeds 50%. Spontaneous (nontraumatic) gas gangrene mortality ranges from 67-100% in adults and 59% in children (most childhood deaths within 24 h of onset). Prognosis is more favorable when an extremity is involved (easier debridement) than the trunk or visceral organs. Disease is most likely to progress to shock and death in patients with associated bacteremia and intravascular hemolysis; mortality is highest for patients in shock at diagnosis. Gas gangrene associated with bacteremia represents a later stage with the worst outcomes. Survivors may undergo multiple debridements and face prolonged hospitalization and rehabilitation (p.1242-1243).","pitfalls":["Diagnosis depends on clinical signs and symptoms, not solely on bacteriologic findings - 30-80% of open traumatic wounds are contaminated with clostridia and clostridia are isolated equally from suppurative and well-healing wounds (p.1239).","In spontaneous gas gangrene the lack of an obvious portal of entry means the correct diagnosis is frequently delayed or missed (p.1242).","Adjunctive procedures (CT, MRI) or transport to hyperbaric oxygen (HBO) units must NOT delay emergent surgical debridement (p.1243).","The value of HBO remains controversial; HBO inhibits C. perfringens but not the more aerotolerant C. septicum, and blood/macerated muscle inhibit its bactericidal potential in vitro - HBO provides benefit only if surgery and antibiotics precede it (p.1243).","Avoid prolonged tourniquet application and surgical closure of contaminated traumatic wounds; patients with compound fractures are at significant risk if the wound is closed surgically (p.1243).","C. perfringens remains largely penicillin-susceptible but clindamycin resistance has been reported and is increasing in some regions; C. tertium is resistant to penicillin, cephalosporins, and clindamycin - good anaerobic susceptibility testing matters (p.1242-1243)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bryant AE, Stevens DL. Gas Gangrene and Other Clostridial Infections. Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e, Chap. 159, pp. 1238-1243.","Bryant AE et al: Clostridial gas gangrene II: Phospholipase C-induced... necrosis in C. perfringens gas gangrene. J Infect Dis 182:808, 2000.","Aronoff DM et al: Infections caused by Clostridium perfringens and... (Harrison's Chap. 159 references)."],"crossLinks":["NECROTIZING_FASCIITIS"]},"glomerular_diseases":{"name":"Glomerular Diseases (Spectrum)","system":"Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":326,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2411","clinicalPearls":["Glomerular disease is grouped into six clinical syndromes that overlap and are not mutually exclusive: acute nephritic, pulmonary-renal, nephrotic, basement membrane, glomerular-vascular, and infectious disease-associated (Harrison 22e p.2414-2416).","Nephritic syndrome = inflammatory pattern: hematuria with red blood cell casts, dysmorphic red cells, pyuria, hypertension, fluid retention, only mild-to-moderate (1-2 g/24 h) proteinuria, and a falling GFR (Harrison 22e p.2414, 2416).","Nephrotic syndrome = heavy proteinuria (>3.0-3.5 g/24 h) with hypoalbuminemia, hypercholesterolemia, edema/anasarca, hypertension, and only minimal/microscopic hematuria; isolated heavy proteinuria without these features is termed nephrotic-range proteinuria (Harrison 22e p.2414, 2420).","Red blood cell casts or dysmorphic red cells in the sediment make glomerulonephritis likely; as few as 3-5 red cells in spun first-morning urine is suspicious (Harrison 22e p.2414).","Gross hematuria is rare in glomerular disease except in IgA nephropathy and sickle cell disease; most glomerular hematuria is asymptomatic and microscopic (Harrison 22e p.2414).","Kidney biopsy is the key diagnostic step in most glomerulonephritis; it identifies the lesion and often directs treatment, but is reserved for clinically stable patients with adequate clotting who can receive therapy (Harrison 22e p.2416-2417).","Minimal change disease (MCD) causes 70-90% of childhood nephrotic syndrome but only 10-15% in adults; light microscopy is normal, immunofluorescence negative (or scant mesangial IgM), and electron microscopy shows foot-process effacement; childhood proteinuria is selective (mostly albumin) (Harrison 22e p.2420-2421).","FSGS is the most common cause of primary glomerular disease leading to ESKD in the US; lesions are focal and segmental and concentrated at the corticomedullary junction, so a superficial biopsy can miss them and be misread as MCD (Harrison 22e p.2421-2422).","Membranous glomerulonephritis (MGN) accounts for ~25% of adult nephrotic syndrome; 70-80% of primary cases have circulating IgG4 anti-PLA2R antibodies, and biopsy may no longer be required when anti-PLA2R is positive with normal creatinine and no secondary cause (Harrison 22e p.2422-2423).","IgA nephropathy is defined by dominant/codominant mesangial IgA deposits and is one of the most common glomerulonephritides worldwide; the two classic presentations are persistent asymptomatic microscopic hematuria and recurrent gross hematuria during/just after an upper respiratory infection (synpharyngitic) (Harrison 22e p.2419).","Membranoproliferative GN (MPGN) is now viewed as a pattern of injury (mesangial/subendothelial immune complexes, complement, or monoclonal deposits) producing GBM double contours ('tram-tracking'), rather than a single disease; classify by underlying immune-complex, complement, or monoclonal cause (Harrison 22e p.2420-2421).","C3 glomerulopathy (dense deposit disease and C3 glomerulonephritis) shows sole/dominant glomerular C3 with little or no immunoglobulin and is driven by alternative-complement-pathway dysregulation (e.g., CFHR mutations, C3 nephritic factor) (Harrison 22e p.2420).","RPGN is the clinical picture of acute nephritis with serum creatinine rising over days; crescentic glomerulonephritis is its pathologic equivalent and warrants urgent biopsy and treatment (Harrison 22e p.2414, 2419).","When RPGN presents with lung hemorrhage it is a pulmonary-renal syndrome; consider anti-GBM/Goodpasture disease, ANCA small-vessel vasculitis, lupus, and cryoglobulinemia (Harrison 22e p.2414, 2418).","ANCA small-vessel vasculitis (GPA, MPA, EGPA, renal-limited) causes a pauci-immune (little/no immune-complex deposition) segmental necrotizing/crescentic glomerulonephritis; anti-PR3 is more common in GPA and anti-MPO in MPA/EGPA (Harrison 22e p.2419-2420).","Anti-GBM disease targets the alpha3 NC1 domain of type IV collagen; biopsy shows linear IgG staining along the GBM (versus the granular 'lumpy-bumpy' deposits of immune-complex disease); 10-15% also have anti-MPO ANCA (Harrison 22e p.2418-2419).","Poststreptococcal GN classically shows subepithelial 'humps' on electron microscopy, low C3/CH50 with normal C4 in the first week, and a positive streptozyme test; it rarely requires biopsy and has a good prognosis in children (Harrison 22e p.2417-2418).","Lupus nephritis is classified ISN/RPS class I-VI; proliferative class III/IV lesions (and class IV with crescents) carry the worst prognosis, while mesangial class I/II need little therapy and class V is a membranous pattern (Harrison 22e p.2418).","Kidney failure in glomerulonephritis correlates histologically with the degree of tubulointerstitial nephritis and interstitial fibrosis rather than with the specific glomerular lesion; interstitial fibrosis is an ominous sign of irreversibility (Harrison 22e p.2413-2414, 2416).","On biopsy, <50% glomerular involvement is focal and >50% diffuse; injury within a tuft is segmental (part) or global (most); endocapillary proliferation is within the tuft and extracapillary proliferation (crescents) extends into Bowman's space (Harrison 22e p.2417)."],"pathophysiology":"Each kidney holds ~0.9 million glomerular tufts whose fenestrated endothelium, GBM, and podocyte foot processes with slit-pore membranes form a size- and charge-selective filtration barrier; glomerular injury allows gram quantities of protein (mainly albumin) to escape (Harrison 22e p.2411-2412). Disease arises through genetic mutations (e.g., NPHS1/nephrin, NPHS2/podocin, TRPC6, APOL1 risk variants for FSGS; type IV collagen alpha3/4/5 in Alport's; complement-pathway/CFHR mutations in MPGN and C3 glomerulopathy/aHUS), infection, toxins, autoimmunity, hypertension/atherosclerosis, thrombosis/emboli, and diabetes; many cases remain idiopathic (Harrison 22e p.2411-2412). Inflammation of glomerular capillaries (glomerulonephritis) is typically immune-mediated: preformed circulating immune complexes deposit on the subendothelial side or mesangium, while in-situ formation produces subepithelial deposits; anti-GBM antibodies bind linearly to the GBM. Deposits and Toll-like receptor activation recruit neutrophils, macrophages, and T cells, activate complement to C5-9 membrane-attack complexes, and release proteases and oxidants that damage the mesangium, capillaries, and GBM and efface podocytes; early CD4/CD8 T-cell activation is important. Depending on antigen location and host genetics, injury produces endocapillary or extracapillary (crescentic) proliferation. Progression to chronic kidney failure tracks with downstream tubulointerstitial nephritis and fibrosis, driven by proteinuria-laden filtrate, inflammatory mediators carried from inflamed glomeruli, and fibroblast/myofibroblast expansion (TGF-beta, FGF-2, HIF-1alpha, PDGF) laying down collagen (Harrison 22e p.2412-2414).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute nephritic syndrome: poststreptococcal GN, subacute bacterial endocarditis-associated GN, lupus nephritis, anti-GBM disease, IgA nephropathy, ANCA small-vessel vasculitis (GPA, MPA, EGPA), IgA vasculitis (Henoch-Schonlein purpura), cryoglobulinemia, MPGN, C3 glomerulopathy, mesangioproliferative GN (Harrison 22e p.2414-2415).","Nephrotic syndrome: minimal change disease, FSGS, membranous glomerulonephritis, diabetic nephropathy, AL/AA amyloidosis, light-chain deposition disease, fibrillary-immunotactoid disease, Fabry's disease (Harrison 22e p.2415).","Pulmonary-renal syndromes: Goodpasture's/anti-GBM disease, ANCA vasculitis, IgA vasculitis, cryoglobulinemia (Harrison 22e p.2414-2415).","Basement membrane syndromes: anti-GBM disease, Alport's syndrome, thin basement membrane disease, nail-patella syndrome (Harrison 22e p.2415).","Glomerular vascular syndromes: atherosclerotic and hypertensive nephropathy, cholesterol emboli, sickle cell disease, thrombotic microangiopathies, antiphospholipid syndrome (Harrison 22e p.2415).","Microscopic hematuria with minimal proteinuria can also reflect non-glomerular causes: urinary tract malignancy (especially older men), BPH, interstitial nephritis, papillary necrosis, hypercalciuria, kidney stones, cystic kidney disease, or renal vascular injury (Harrison 22e p.2414)."],"infectionMimics":["Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis after nephritogenic streptococcal pharyngitis (1-3 weeks) or skin infection (2-6 weeks) (Harrison 22e p.2417-2418).","Subacute bacterial endocarditis-associated and other persistent-infection (shunt, abscess, infected prosthesis; often Staphylococcus) glomerulonephritis, which can mimic or coexist with ANCA vasculitis (Harrison 22e p.2417).","Hepatitis B and C (membranous, MPGN, cryoglobulinemic patterns), HIV-associated nephropathy (collapsing FSGS), syphilis, leprosy, malaria, and schistosomiasis - globally, malaria and schistosomiasis may be the most common causes of glomerulonephritis (Harrison 22e p.2416, 2459).","IgA-dominant Staphylococcus-associated infectious glomerulonephritis can mimic primary IgA nephropathy (Harrison 22e p.2419)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Diabetic nephropathy and amyloidosis both thicken the GBM and can mimic membranous nephropathy on light microscopy (Harrison 22e p.2422-2423).","Secondary FSGS (adaptive hyperfiltration/obesity, reflux, hypertension, drugs such as heroin/interferon/bisphosphonates, viruses) and genetic/familial podocytopathies must be excluded before diagnosing primary FSGS, as immunosuppression is not indicated in secondary/genetic forms (Harrison 22e p.2421-2422).","Secondary membranous nephropathy from malignancy (breast, lung, colon), infection, lupus or other autoimmune disease, IgG4 disease, or drugs (gold, penicillamine, NSAIDs) (Harrison 22e p.2422).","Membranous lupus nephritis (subendothelial deposits/tubuloreticular inclusions) may precede extrarenal lupus and mimic primary MGN (Harrison 22e p.2423).","C3 glomerulopathy can be morphologically indistinguishable from resolving postinfectious glomerulonephritis (Harrison 22e p.2420).","Mesangioproliferative pattern is shared by IgA nephropathy, P. falciparum malaria, resolving postinfectious GN, and lupus class II (Harrison 22e p.2421)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis with microscopy for dysmorphic red cells, red blood cell casts, pyuria; quantify proteinuria by spot urine albumin/creatinine ratio (UACR >30 mg/g abnormal) or 24-h collection; distinguish selective (albumin-predominant, childhood MCD) from nonselective proteinuria (Harrison 22e p.2414).","Serologies: ASO/anti-DNase/antihyaluronidase and streptozyme; ANA and anti-dsDNA; complement C3 and C4 (low C3 with normal C4 in poststreptococcal GN and C3 glomerulopathy; hypocomplementemia in active lupus nephritis); ANCA (anti-PR3, anti-MPO); anti-GBM antibody to the alpha3 NC1 domain specifically; cryoglobulins and rheumatoid factor; antiphospholipid antibodies; HIV and hepatitis B/C serologies (Harrison 22e p.2416).","Target-antigen antibodies in nephrotic disease: anti-PLA2R and anti-THSD7A (membranous nephropathy); DNAJB9 (fibrillary GN) (Harrison 22e p.2416-2417, 2422-2423).","Kidney ultrasound to assess kidney size (small kidneys suggest chronicity) and exclude obstruction (Harrison 22e p.2416).","Kidney biopsy with light microscopy (H&E, PAS, Jones silver, Congo red, Masson trichrome), immunofluorescence (IgG/IgM/IgA, C3/C4, PLA2R/THSD7A/DNAJB9), and electron microscopy to localize deposits and assess the GBM (Harrison 22e p.2416-2417).","C3 nephritic factor, factor H, soluble C5b-9, paraprotein screening, and CFHR genetic testing (with family screening) when C3 glomerulopathy is suspected (Harrison 22e p.2420)."],"redFlags":["RPGN / crescentic glomerulonephritis - serum creatinine rising over days is a nephrologic emergency requiring urgent biopsy and immunosuppression (Harrison 22e p.2414, 2419).","Pulmonary-renal syndrome (glomerulonephritis with lung hemorrhage/hemoptysis and falling hemoglobin) - consider anti-GBM/Goodpasture, ANCA vasculitis, lupus, cryoglobulinemia; lung hemorrhage responds to plasmapheresis (Harrison 22e p.2414, 2418-2419).","Anti-GBM disease with oliguria, serum creatinine >5-6 mg/dL, need for acute dialysis, or >50% crescents on biopsy - markers of a poor prognosis requiring prompt treatment (Harrison 22e p.2419).","ANCA small-vessel vasculitis - high mortality without treatment; virtually all patients need urgent induction therapy (Harrison 22e p.2419).","Malignant hypertension can rapidly complicate glomerulosclerosis with fibrinoid arteriolar necrosis, thrombotic microangiopathy, and acute kidney failure (Harrison 22e p.2412).","Nephrotic syndrome is a hypercoagulable state - membranous nephropathy carries the highest risk of renal vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, and deep-vein thrombosis (Harrison 22e p.2423)."],"prognosis":"Prognosis is lesion-specific and tracks with the degree of tubulointerstitial fibrosis. Poststreptococcal GN resolves in most children within 3-6 weeks with rare progression to ESKD, but is worse in the elderly (up to 60% azotemia) (Harrison 22e p.2418). Minimal change disease responds to steroids in 90-95% of children and 80-90% of adults though relapse is common (70-75% of children) (Harrison 22e p.2421). Primary FSGS with nephrotic-range proteinuria reaches ESKD in ~50% by 10 years and rarely remits spontaneously, but treatment-induced remission improves outcome (Harrison 22e p.2421-2422). In membranous nephropathy roughly one-third remit spontaneously, one-third have relapsing nephrotic syndrome with preserved function, and one-third progress to kidney failure or die of complications (Harrison 22e p.2423). IgA nephropathy progresses to ESKD in only 25-30% over 20-25 years; persistent proteinuria >=6 months is the strongest adverse predictor (Harrison 22e p.2419). Dense deposit disease progresses to ESKD in ~50%. Untreated RPGN/crescentic disease and ANCA vasculitis carry high mortality, but ANCA vasculitis achieves remission in 75-90% with treatment (relapse 25-50%) (Harrison 22e p.2419-2420).","pitfalls":["A superficial kidney biopsy that fails to sample deeper corticomedullary glomeruli can miss the focal/segmental lesions of FSGS and be misdiagnosed as minimal change disease - suspect FSGS in steroid-resistant 'MCD' (Harrison 22e p.2421-2422).","Test serum specifically against the alpha3 NC1 domain of collagen IV: non-nephritic anti-alpha1 NC1 antibodies in paraneoplastic syndromes cannot be distinguished by assays using whole basement-membrane fragments (Harrison 22e p.2419).","ANCA-associated vasculitis can accompany or be confused with subacute bacterial endocarditis-associated GN; it must be ruled out because the treatments differ (Harrison 22e p.2417).","There is no role for immunosuppression in poststreptococcal GN even when crescents are present, nor in secondary or genetic FSGS (Harrison 22e p.2418, 2422).","Glomerular hematuria is usually microscopic and asymptomatic, so the diagnosis is often delayed; isolated microscopic hematuria in older men still requires exclusion of urinary tract malignancy (Harrison 22e p.2414).","Nephrotic-range proteinuria without edema or hypoalbuminemia is not necessarily full nephrotic syndrome, and transient/orthostatic proteinuria (fever, exercise, obesity, CHF, upright posture) must be distinguished from sustained pathologic proteinuria (Harrison 22e p.2414, 2420)."],"severityClassification":"Glomerular disease is organized into six clinical syndromes (acute nephritic, pulmonary-renal, nephrotic, basement membrane, glomerular-vascular, infectious disease-associated) graded semiquantitatively by proteinuria, hematuria, and vascular injury (Table 326-2, Harrison 22e p.2414-2416). Biopsy descriptors: focal (<50% of glomeruli) vs diffuse (>50%); segmental (part of a tuft) vs global; endocapillary vs extracapillary (crescentic) proliferation (Harrison 22e p.2417). Lupus nephritis uses the ISN/RPS class I-VI scheme (I minimal mesangial, II mesangial proliferative, III focal, IV diffuse, V membranous, VI sclerotic) (Table 326-3, Harrison 22e p.2418). IgA nephropathy uses the Oxford MEST-C score (mesangial/endocapillary hypercellularity, segmental sclerosis, tubular fibrosis, crescents) (Harrison 22e p.2419). Albuminuria is staged normal (8-10 mg/24 h), microalbuminuria (30-300 mg/24 h), and frank proteinuria (>300 mg/24 h) (Table 326-1, Harrison 22e p.2414).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2411","Harrison 22e p.2412-2414","Harrison 22e p.2414-2416","Harrison 22e p.2417-2423"],"crossLinks":["glomerulonephritis","nephrotic","ckd"]},"gonorrhea":{"name":"Gonorrhea","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":161,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1253-1260","clinicalPearls":["Gonorrhea is a sexually transmitted infection of the epithelium that commonly presents as cervicitis, urethritis, proctitis, or conjunctivitis; if untreated it can cause local complications including endometritis, salpingitis, tuboovarian abscess, bartholinitis, peritonitis, and perihepatitis in women, and periurethritis and epididymitis in men (p.1253).","In men, acute urethritis is the most common manifestation, typically after a 2-7 day incubation, with discharge that is initially scant and mucoid but becomes profuse and purulent within a day or two; however, a reservoir of infected men remains asymptomatic (p.1255).","About half of women infected with N. gonorrhoeae develop symptoms; many have only minor symptoms (scant cervical discharge, dysuria) and may delay care, so the asymptomatic/minimally symptomatic reservoir is key to ongoing transmission (p.1256).","Gram stain of urethral exudate showing gram-negative intracellular monococci and diplococci is highly specific and sensitive in symptomatic men, but is only about 50% sensitive for gonococcal cervicitis, so women should be diagnosed by culture or a nonculture (NAAT) method (p.1257).","Nucleic acid amplification tests (NAATs) are now widely used, are more sensitive than culture for both N. gonorrhoeae and C. trachomatis, allow urine and self-collected vaginal swab testing, and simultaneously detect chlamydial co-infection (p.1257).","Disseminated gonococcal infection (DGI) results from gonococcal bacteremia and most commonly presents as an arthritis-dermatitis syndrome with fever, chills, tenosynovitis, polyarthralgias, and characteristic skin lesions (papules/pustules with a hemorrhagic component, ~5-40, on the extremities) seen in about 75% of patients (p.1257).","Menstruation is a risk factor for dissemination and roughly two-thirds of DGI cases occur in women; in about half of affected women symptoms begin within 7 days of onset of menses (p.1257).","Persons deficient in terminal complement components (C5-C9) and those receiving the C5-blocking monoclonal antibody eculizumab are predisposed to recurrent neisserial bacteremia; anyone with more than one episode of DGI should be screened for complement deficiency (p.1255, 1260).","Ceftriaxone is the recommended first-line agent for uncomplicated gonococcal infection, now at twice the previously recommended dose because mean inhibitory concentrations of current strains have doubled relative to 20 years ago (p.1259).","Quinolone-containing regimens and cefixime are no longer recommended as first-line treatment in the United States because of widespread quinolone resistance and rising cefixime MICs (p.1259).","Because co-infection with C. trachomatis occurs frequently, concurrent treatment for chlamydia (doxycycline) is recommended whenever chlamydial infection cannot be excluded (p.1259)."],"pathophysiology":"N. gonorrhoeae is a gram-negative, nonmotile, non-spore-forming, oxidase-positive diplococcus and an exclusively human pathogen; it carries on average three genome copies per coccal unit, and this polyploidy supports a high level of antigenic variation that aids host survival. Pili (fimbriae) mediate initial adherence to nonciliated columnar epithelial cells and enable invasion, transcytosis, genetic competence and transformation; pilus expression is switched off on subculture as a basis for antigenic variation. Opacity-associated protein (Opa) mediates intergonococcal and epithelial adhesion and binds CEACAM receptors, promoting invasion, suppressing CD4+ T-cell activation, and enabling nonopsonic neutrophil phagocytosis. Porin (the most abundant surface protein) forms the basis of serotyping; PorB.1A strains are associated with disseminated infection, resist normal human serum killing, bind complement-inhibitory molecules and may not cause local symptoms, whereas PorB.1B strains usually cause local genital infection. Lipooligosaccharide (LOS) has marked endotoxic activity; sialylation of LOS inhibits classical and alternative complement pathways and reduces neutrophil killing. Iron/zinc scavenging proteins (transferrin-, lactoferrin-, hemoglobin-binding proteins) and IgA1 protease promote survival. Host entry into nonphagocytic cells involves phospholipase C and sphingomyelinase activation with ceramide-mediated apoptosis, and inflammation is driven by LOS and peptidoglycan-induced cytokine release (p.1254-1255).","additionalDifferentials":["Nongonococcal urethritis (C. trachomatis, Mycoplasma genitalium, Ureaplasma urealyticum, Trichomonas vaginalis, HSV) - clinically often impossible to distinguish from gonococcal urethritis (p.1255-1256)","Bacterial cystitis/urinary tract infection versus the urethral syndrome in women presenting with dysuria (p.1256)","For the bacteremic stage of DGI: reactive arthritis, acute rheumatoid arthritis, sarcoidosis, erythema nodosum, drug-induced arthritis, and viral infections including hepatitis B and acute HIV infection (p.1257)","Suppurative gonococcal arthritis in the absence of symptomatic genital infection cannot be distinguished from septic arthritis caused by other pathogens (p.1257)"],"infectionMimics":["Meningococcemia, which can produce a dermatitis-arthritis syndrome, purulent monoarthritis, and reactive polyarthritis similar to DGI (p.1058)","Acute HIV infection and hepatitis B (viral causes mimicking the bacteremic arthritis-dermatitis stage of DGI) (p.1257)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Reactive arthritis (joint distribution differs from DGI; skin and genital manifestations also differ) (p.1257)","Acute rheumatoid arthritis (p.1257)","Sarcoidosis (p.1257)","Erythema nodosum (p.1257)","Drug-induced arthritis (p.1257)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Gram stain of urethral discharge for gram-negative intracellular monococci/diplococci - highly sensitive in symptomatic men, only ~50% sensitive for cervicitis (p.1255, 1257)","NAAT (e.g., Aptima Combo 2, Xpert CT/NG, BD ProbeTec, COBAS) on urine, urethral, cervical or self-collected vaginal swabs - more sensitive than culture and co-detects C. trachomatis; point-of-care NAAT with ~30-minute turnaround available (p.1257)","Culture on modified Thayer-Martin/selective medium with prompt processing (gonococci do not tolerate drying); single endocervical culture ~80-90% sensitive (p.1257)","In MSM, screen rectum and pharynx in addition to urine, since urine alone will miss the majority of cases (p.1257)","Blood cultures (Isolator tubes may improve yield; positivity falls after 48 h of illness) and synovial fluid (inoculate into blood culture broth and chocolate agar) in suspected DGI; organisms are seldom recovered from blood and synovial fluid in the same patient (p.1257)","Standardized culture is the preferred legal-evidence method in children for suspected sexual abuse; two NAATs targeting different sequences may substitute (p.1257)","Total hemolytic complement activity assay in anyone with more than one episode of DGI (p.1255, 1260)"],"redFlags":["Disseminated gonococcal infection with the joint-localized suppurative arthritis stage - hospitalization indicated, may need joint aspiration and higher/longer therapy (p.1259)","Gonococcal meningitis - rare complication of DGI requiring high-dose IV ceftriaxone for 10-14 days (p.1257, 1260)","Gonococcal endocarditis - rare today but historically accounted for about one-quarter of endocarditis cases; requires at least 4 weeks of IV therapy (p.1257, 1260)","Ophthalmia neonatorum and adult ocular gonorrhea - prompt recognition and treatment essential, as corneal ulceration may progress to perforation, panophthalmitis and blindness (p.1256-1257)","Pelvic inflammatory disease should be considered when cervical infection extends to cause dyspareunia or lower abdominal/back pain (p.1256)"],"prognosis":"A single recommended ceftriaxone regimen almost always cures uncomplicated urethral, cervical, rectal, or pharyngeal infection, and true treatment failure with a recommended regimen is rare. Symptomatic pharyngeal gonorrhea is harder to eradicate than genital infection. In the preantibiotic era untreated urethritis persisted about 8 weeks. DGI incidence has fallen (from ~0.5-3% of untreated mucosal infections in the 1970s) likely due to a decline in disseminating strains. A dramatic alleviation of arthritis-dermatitis symptoms within 12-24 h of appropriate antibiotics supports a clinical diagnosis of DGI even when cultures are negative. Rising worldwide resistance, including reduced ceftriaxone susceptibility, threatens future treatment efficacy (p.1257-1260).","pitfalls":["Because co-infection with C. trachomatis is frequent, treatment of gonorrhea must include concurrent therapy for chlamydia (doxycycline) whenever chlamydial infection cannot be excluded - omitting this is a key pitfall (p.1259).","Azithromycin is no longer recommended as part of the first-line regimen because U.S. gonococcal resistance to azithromycin rose more than sevenfold to 4.7% in 2021 (p.1259).","A presumptive diagnosis of gonorrhea cannot be made from gram-negative diplococci on a pharyngeal Gram stain, because commensal Neisseria species are normal pharyngeal flora (p.1257).","Gram stain is only ~50% sensitive for gonococcal cervicitis; relying on it in women risks missed diagnoses, so culture or NAAT is required (p.1257).","DGI strains often do not incite a local genital inflammatory response and may not cause genital symptoms, so disseminated disease can occur without an obvious mucosal source (p.1254, 1257).","Quinolones and cefixime are no longer first-line in the United States due to resistance and rising MICs - using them empirically risks treatment failure (p.1259).","Persistent symptoms after a recommended regimen should prompt evaluation for reinfection, a drug-resistant strain, or an alternative diagnosis rather than simple re-treatment; culture and susceptibility testing should be performed (p.1259).","Because of high reinfection rates within 6-12 months, persons treated for gonorrhea should be retested at 3 months (p.1259)."],"severityClassification":"Clinical manifestations span uncomplicated mucosal infection (urethritis, cervicitis, proctitis, pharyngitis, conjunctivitis) versus complicated/disseminated disease. DGI has historically been described in two overlapping stages: a bacteremic stage (higher fever, chills, polyarthralgias, tenosynovitis, and skin lesions) and a joint-localized stage with suppurative (septic) arthritis, though a clear-cut progression is usually not evident (p.1257).","references":["Ram S, Rice PA. Gonococcal Infections. In: Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd ed., Chapter 161, pp. 1253-1260.","Holmes KK et al: Disseminated gonococcal infection. Ann Intern Med 74:979, 1971.","CDC Gonorrhea Treatment Recommendations 2020 and 2021 Sexually Transmitted Infections (STI) Treatment Guidelines."],"crossLinks":[]},"head_and_neck_cancer":{"name":"Head and Neck Cancer","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":82,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.610-613","clinicalPearls":["Most head and neck cancers are squamous cell carcinomas arising from the mucosal surfaces of the paranasal sinuses, oral cavity, nasopharynx, oropharynx, hypopharynx, and larynx (Harrison 22e p.610).","Alcohol and tobacco are the most significant environmental risk factors and act synergistically when used together; smokeless tobacco is an etiologic agent for oral cancers (Harrison 22e p.610).","In Western countries, HPV (predominantly HPV-16) causes over 50% of oropharyngeal cancers (tonsillar bed and base of tongue); these occur in younger patients and carry a better prognosis, especially in nonsmokers (Harrison 22e p.610-611).","EBV infection is frequently associated with nasopharyngeal cancer, especially in endemic areas (East Asia, some Mediterranean countries); EBV antibody titers can be used to screen high-risk populations (Harrison 22e p.610).","HPV testing should be performed at diagnosis for all patients with oropharyngeal tumors; HPV-related disease has a more favorable AJCC 8th-edition staging (advanced nodal stage can still be equivalent to overall early stage) (Harrison 22e p.611-613).","Panendoscopy under anesthesia (laryngoscopy, esophagoscopy, bronchoscopy) with multiple directed biopsies is the definitive staging procedure to establish diagnosis, define extent, and identify second primaries (Harrison 22e p.611).","When a head and neck lesion treated initially with antibiotics does not resolve in a short period, further workup is indicated; simply continuing antibiotics may lose the chance for early diagnosis of a malignancy (Harrison 22e p.611).","For an enlarged cervical node with no visible primary, diagnose by fine-needle aspiration or lymph node excision; if squamous cell carcinoma, perform panendoscopy with directed biopsies of nasopharynx, tonsil, tongue base, and pyriform sinus (Harrison 22e p.611-612).","Most parotid tumors are benign, but about half of submandibular and sublingual tumors and most minor salivary gland tumors are malignant; adenoid cystic carcinoma tends to recur along nerve tracks and metastasize as late as 10-20 years (Harrison 22e p.610-611, 613)."],"pathophysiology":"Squamous cell carcinomas arise from the mucosal surfaces of the upper aerodigestive tract. The entire pharyngeal mucosa is exposed to alcohol- and tobacco-related carcinogens, producing field cancerization with risk of premalignant lesions (erythroplakia, leukoplakia) progressing through hyperplasia, dysplasia, and carcinoma in situ to invasive carcinoma. In HPV-negative tumors, p53 mutations are frequent along with mitotic-signaling and Notch pathway alterations and dysregulated cell-cycle control. HPV oncogenes drive carcinogenesis by directly inhibiting the p53 and RB tumor-suppressor genes; HPV-positive tumors carry a distinct mutational signature with frequent PI3K pathway alteration. EGFR is frequently overexpressed and correlates with poor prognosis. EBV is associated with undifferentiated nasopharyngeal carcinoma (lymphoepithelioma) (Harrison 22e p.610-611).","additionalDifferentials":["Carcinoma of unknown primary presenting as cervical adenopathy without an identifiable mucosal lesion","Lymphoma presenting as cervical adenopathy","Salivary gland tumors (mucoepidermoid, adenoid cystic carcinoma, adenocarcinoma)","Sarcoma, including radiation therapy-induced sarcoma after prior radiotherapy","Metastasis to supraclavicular nodes from a primary site in the chest or abdomen"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Premalignant mucosal lesions (leukoplakia, erythroplakia) representing hyperplasia, dysplasia, or carcinoma in situ"],"additionalInvestigations":["Thorough otolaryngologic examination including inspection of all visible mucosal surfaces and palpation of the floor of mouth, tongue, and neck","CT or MRI of the head and neck to define disease extent (comparably accurate, 65-90%, for cervical nodal metastases)","CT of chest and upper abdomen for patients with lymph node involvement to screen for distant metastases; chest CT also screens for second lung primary in heavy smokers","PET scan to identify or exclude distant metastases and assess treatment response","Endoscopic examination under anesthesia (panendoscopy) with multiple biopsies","Fine-needle aspiration or excisional lymph node biopsy when no primary is visible","HPV testing of oropharyngeal tumors at diagnosis; EBV antibody titers / plasma EBV DNA for nasopharyngeal cancer screening","TNM staging per AJCC (varies by anatomic subsite)"],"redFlags":["Persistent hoarseness requiring referral for indirect laryngoscopy and/or radiographic studies (possible early laryngeal cancer)","Non-healing ulcer of the oral cavity","Persistent neck mass / cervical lymphadenopathy, especially a solitary, hard, nontender node in an older chronic tobacco user","Dysphagia, odynophagia, or decreased tongue mobility","Sore throat and/or otalgia, or a head/neck lesion that does not resolve after antibiotics","Unilateral serous otitis media, nasal obstruction, or epistaxis (nasopharyngeal cancer); cranial neuropathies from skull base involvement","Head and neck symptoms persisting longer than 2-4 weeks"],"prognosis":"Poorly differentiated tumors have a worse prognosis than well-differentiated tumors. Overt distant metastases are present in <10% at diagnosis. HPV-related oropharyngeal cancers consistently carry a better prognosis (reflected in a separate, more favorable AJCC 8th-edition staging system), especially in nonsmokers. For localized disease (about one-third of patients), overall 5-year survival is 60-90%, with most recurrences occurring within the first 2 years and usually local. For locally/regionally advanced disease (>50% of patients at presentation), 5-year survival exceeds 50% in many trials (partly due to the increasing fraction of HPV-related tumors); advanced nasopharyngeal cancer treated with neoadjuvant chemotherapy followed by concomitant chemoradiotherapy has achieved 5-year survival exceeding 80% in a Southeast Asian study. Patients cured of early-stage tobacco/alcohol-related disease are at greater risk of dying from a second malignancy than from recurrence of the primary (Harrison 22e p.611-613).","pitfalls":["Continuing antibiotic treatment for a non-resolving head and neck lesion rather than pursuing workup may delay diagnosis of malignancy (Harrison 22e p.611).","Fine-needle aspiration should not be the first diagnostic procedure for a suspicious cervical node when a head and neck primary is suspected; perform a careful ENT exam and biopsy any suspicious mucosal lesion first (Harrison 22e p.4012-type cross-reference, p.611).","EGFR overexpression does not reliably predict response to EGFR inhibitors (active in only ~10-15% as single agents); substituting cetuximab for cisplatin in HPV-related cancers has shown inferior survival (Harrison 22e p.611, 613).","Retinoid supplements (e.g., cis-retinoic acid) have not been shown to prevent head and neck (or lung) cancer and may increase risk in active smokers (Harrison 22e p.610).","About 50% of patients develop hypothyroidism after treatment; thyroid function should be monitored (Harrison 22e p.613)."],"severityClassification":"Clinically categorized into three groups: (1) localized disease (T1-T2 / stage I-II without nodal or distant spread), (2) locally or regionally advanced disease (large primary and/or cervical nodal metastases, lymph-node positive), and (3) recurrent and/or metastatic disease below the neck. Staged by the AJCC TNM system, which varies by anatomic subsite: primary tumors T1-T3 by increasing size with T4 representing invasion of adjacent structures (bone, muscle, root of tongue), and nodes staged by size, number, and laterality. A separate, more favorable staging system applies to HPV-related oropharyngeal cancer in the AJCC 8th edition (Harrison 22e p.611-613).","references":["Harrison 22e p.610-613"],"crossLinks":[]},"helicobacter_pylori_infection":{"name":"Helicobacter pylori Infection","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":168,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1298; also 302, 416, 646, 649-650, 1009","clinicalPearls":["H. pylori has a proven etiologic role in peptic ulcer disease; together with NSAID use it is one of the two most common causes of gastroduodenal ulcers (p.302).","In dyspeptic patients under 60 with no alarm features, test-and-treat is the recommended initial strategy: assess H. pylori status by fecal (stool) antigen or urea breath testing, and treat those who are positive (p.303).","Eradication of H. pylori in patients with bleeding ulcers lowers rebleeding rates to under 5% (p.272).","H. pylori is a known driver of gastric cancer; the intestinal subtype of gastric adenocarcinoma is associated with H. pylori infection and atrophic/atopic gastritis (p.646, p.647).","Although H. pylori is extremely common (present in roughly half of all humans), gastric cancer develops in only a small subset of those infected, so infection alone is not sufficient (p.646).","Treating H. pylori reduces future gastric cancer risk: eradication lowered gastric cancer incidence in people with a first-degree relative who had gastric cancer, and reduced second cancers in patients with prior early-stage gastric cancer (p.646).","All patients with gastric lymphoma should have H. pylori status evaluated; if histopathology is negative, confirm with a stool antigen test or urea breath test (p.649).","For gastric MALT lymphoma, antibiotic eradication of H. pylori alone is highly effective and is the initial therapy when H. pylori is positive and the t(11;18) translocation assay is negative, producing high response and complete-remission rates (p.649-650)."],"pathophysiology":"H. pylori is a flagellated gastric bacterium whose colonization triggers chronic mucosal inflammation. The flagellum lets the organism access the gastric epithelium and is essential for virulence; H. pylori evades innate immunity by producing a flagellin epitope that does not bind TLR5 and tetra-acylated LPS that does not activate TLR4 (p.1009-1010). Adhesion and gastric tropism are mediated by bacterial outer-membrane proteins BabA and SabA interacting with epithelial GM1 ganglioside (p.1009). Chronic H. pylori inflammation, partly via generation of carcinogenic nitrites from dietary nitrates, drives the gastritis-to-cancer sequence and is a recognized cause of peptic ulcer disease, gastric adenocarcinoma (intestinal subtype), and gastric MALT lymphoma; certain higher-virulence strains carry greater cancer risk (p.646).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urea breath test (noninvasive) for active infection and for confirming eradication.","Stool (fecal) antigen test (noninvasive) for active infection and eradication confirmation.","Endoscopic biopsy with histopathology, including for gastric lymphoma staging.","PCR or FISH for the t(11;18) translocation in gastric MALT lymphoma to predict responsiveness to eradication therapy (p.649)."],"redFlags":["Dyspepsia with alarm features (dysphagia, weight loss, bleeding/anemia, jaundice, palpable mass or adenopathy, family history of gastroesophageal malignancy) or age over 60 warrants upper endoscopy to exclude malignancy rather than test-and-treat alone (p.302-303).","Failure to respond to eradication, or recurrent/refractory symptoms, should prompt further evaluation."],"prognosis":"Eradication markedly improves ulcer outcomes (rebleeding falls below 5%). In gastric MALT lymphoma, H. pylori eradication yields high response rates with complete remission in the majority, though time to remission can be prolonged (averaging ~15-16 months in some studies), requiring patient monitoring before declaring non-response (p.272, p.650). Benefit in functional dyspepsia is limited but statistically significant (about a 9% risk reduction vs placebo) (p.302).","pitfalls":["PITFALL: PPIs and other acid-suppressing drugs can cause false-negative urea breath and stool antigen tests; acid suppression must be stopped before testing for active H. pylori infection.","Confirmation of eradication should be performed 4-6 weeks after completing therapy, not immediately (p.303).","Do not rely on a single negative histopathology result in gastric lymphoma; confirm with noninvasive stool antigen or urea breath testing (p.649).","NEGATION: Even H. pylori-negative MALT tumors may respond to anti-H. pylori therapy, so a negative test does not preclude a trial of eradication; the t(11;18) translocation identifies a subgroup less likely to respond (p.649-650).","NEGATION: H. pylori eradication does NOT worsen GERD symptoms, despite H. pylori infection being associated with a reduced prevalence of GERD (p.302)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e, Chapter 168 (Helicobacter pylori Infections), p.1298.","Choi IJ et al: Helicobacter pylori and prevention of gastric cancer. N Engl J Med.","Choi IJ et al: Family history of gastric cancer and Helicobacter pylori treatment."],"crossLinks":["peptic_ulcer"]},"hemolytic_anemia":{"name":"Hemolytic Anemia","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":105,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.791","clinicalPearls":["Hemolytic anemia (HA) is anemia from increased red-cell destruction with a normal (usually increased) marrow supply; unlike acute blood loss, cells are destroyed within the body rather than physically lost (p.791).","Classify by primary etiology (inherited vs acquired), tempo (acute vs chronic), severity, site of hemolysis (intravascular vs extravascular), and mechanism (intracorpuscular vs extracorpuscular defects) (p.791, Table 105-1).","Inherited HA generally reflects intracorpuscular defects (hemoglobinopathies, enzymopathies such as G6PD, membrane-cytoskeleton defects); acquired HA generally reflects extracorpuscular factors (autoimmune, microangiopathic/mechanical, toxins, drugs, infection). Exceptions: PNH (inherited-like somatic mutation but acquired) and familial/atypical HUS (p.791-792, Table 105-1).","Core clinical sign is jaundice; patients may report dark urine; splenomegaly is common as a preferential site of hemolysis, the liver may enlarge, and pigment gallstones are common with chronic hemolysis (p.791).","Laboratory hallmarks: increased reticulocytes (both percentage and the more definitive absolute count), raised MCV with macrocytes/polychromasia on smear, increased unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin and AST, raised LDH, and reduced-to-absent haptoglobin (p.791, Table 105-2).","Extravascular hemolysis raises unconjugated bilirubin and urobilinogen; intravascular hemolysis is signalled by hemoglobinuria (often with hemosiderinuria), free plasma hemoglobin, markedly raised LDH (up to ~10x), and low haptoglobin, with bilirubin only mildly elevated (p.791, Table 105-2).","A positive broad-spectrum direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test is on its own almost diagnostic of autoimmune HA; the broad reagent detects bound immunoglobulin and/or complement (C3 fragments) on red cells (p.801).","Warm AIHA (mostly IgG, optimal 37C) causes predominantly extravascular hemolysis with spherocytes; cold agglutinin disease (mostly IgM anti-I, optimal 4-30C) activates complement causing intravascular hemolysis with hemoglobinuria plus Kupffer-cell extravascular destruction in the liver (p.801-803, Fig. 105-9, Table 105-8).","Compensated hemolysis: a brisk EPO-driven erythropoietic response can fully offset red-cell destruction so an inherited hemolytic condition may present without anemia; it can decompensate in pregnancy, folate deficiency, or renal failure (p.792)."],"pathophysiology":"The mature red cell loses mitochondria and ribosomes, leaving anaerobic glycolysis (Embden-Meyerhof pathway) as the sole source of ATP and the hexose-monophosphate shunt (via G6PD-generated NADPH and reduced glutathione) as defense against oxidant stress; damaged proteins cannot be replaced, so any metabolic failure ultimately damages the membrane or the cation pump and shortens the ~120-day life span. Senescent or damaged red cells expose band-3 neoantigens, become opsonized, and are phagocytosed by splenic, hepatic, and other macrophages (extravascular hemolysis); this senescence process is accelerated in many HAs. The essential common feature is increased red-cell turnover; when destruction outpaces marrow production, anemia results. Chronic intravascular hemolysis causes iron loss via hemoglobinuria, whereas chronic extravascular hemolysis (especially if transfused) tends toward iron overload and, untreated, secondary hemochromatosis (p.791-792).","additionalDifferentials":["Inherited intracorpuscular defects: hemoglobinopathies (sickle cell, thalassemia; Chap. 103), enzymopathies (G6PD deficiency, pyruvate kinase deficiency, pyrimidine 5'-nucleotidase deficiency), membrane-cytoskeleton defects (hereditary spherocytosis) (p.791-792, Table 105-1)","Acquired extracorpuscular causes: autoimmune (warm AIHA, cold agglutinin disease, paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria), microangiopathic/mechanical (prosthetic valve, march hemoglobinuria), paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria, toxic/drug-induced, infectious (p.792, 801-804, Table 105-1)","Familial (atypical) hemolytic-uremic syndrome from inherited dysregulation permitting excessive complement activation (p.799-800)"],"infectionMimics":["Malaria - the most frequent infectious cause of HA in endemic areas (Chap. 231) (p.801)","Shiga toxin-producing E. coli O157:H7 causing HUS, more common in children (Chap. 166) (p.801)","Clostridium perfringens sepsis - life-threatening intravascular hemolysis from lecithinase toxin (open wounds, septic abortion, contaminated blood unit) (p.801, Table 105-7)","Babesia, and Mycoplasma infection (associated with paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria); viral triggers (EBV, CMV, parvovirus B19, HIV, HCV) of secondary autoimmune HA (p.801-802, Table 105-8)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Compensated hemolysis presenting without anemia, mimicking a non-hemolytic state until it decompensates (p.792)","Reticulocytosis-driven macrocytosis that can be mistaken for a megaloblastic process (p.791)","Parvovirus B19 aplastic crisis causing a precipitous hemoglobin fall in a chronic hemolytic patient, mimicking acute worsening of hemolysis (p.792)","Coombs-negative AIHA, which can still be severe when a high-affinity antibody opsonizes red cells with few molecules (p.802)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Reticulocyte count - corrected percentage and absolute count (often neglected in initial anemia workup) (p.791)","Unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin, AST, LDH, haptoglobin (p.791, Table 105-2)","Urine for hemoglobinuria/hemosiderinuria and stool/urine urobilinogen when intravascular hemolysis suspected (p.791)","Peripheral blood smear for schistocytes (microangiopathy/fragmentation), spherocytes (AIHA, hereditary spherocytosis), bite cells (G6PD), basophilic stippling (lead/P5N deficiency) (p.799-804)","Direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test - broad-spectrum then specific anti-Ig/anti-C reagents (p.801)","Quantitative G6PD assay (screening tests can miss deficiency soon after a hemolytic attack because young high-G6PD reticulocytes mask it); DNA testing and point-of-care tests available (p.799-800)","Flow cytometry for CD59-negative red cell population in PNH (p.801, Table 105-7)","Donath-Landsteiner antibody for paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria; blood cultures for clostridial septicemia (p.801, Table 105-7)","Red cell survival study (51Cr or nonradioactive 15N) as the gold standard for shortened life span; bone marrow (if done) shows erythroid hyperplasia, usually not required (p.791-792)"],"redFlags":["Schistocytes (fragmented/helmet cells) on smear indicating microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (MAHA) - consider TTP, HUS, and DIC; an emergency requiring urgent evaluation (p.799, 801, Table 105-7)","Abrupt severe drop in hemoglobin (to as low as 4 g/dL within days) in warm AIHA with jaundice and splenomegaly - a medical emergency (p.801)","Acute renal failure - the most serious threat from acute hemolytic attacks in adults (e.g., G6PD-related), and a predictor of poor AIHA outcome (p.799, 801)","Hemoglobinuria signalling intravascular hemolysis (mismatched transfusion, PNH, clostridial sepsis, march hemoglobinuria, favism) (p.801, Table 105-7)","Evans syndrome (AIHA plus autoimmune thrombocytopenia) signals high-risk disease and may indicate underlying immune deficiency (p.801)","Mismatched (usually ABO-incompatible) blood transfusion causing abrupt intravascular hemolysis (p.801, Table 105-7)","Clostridium perfringens sepsis causing life-threatening intravascular hemolysis (p.801)"],"prognosis":"Transient hemolysis usually has no long-term consequences beyond increased erythropoietic (folate) requirement; recurrent or persistent hemolysis predisposes to pigment gallstones, splenomegaly with hypersplenism (neutropenia/thrombocytopenia), and, with chronic extravascular hemolysis or repeated transfusion, iron overload progressing to secondary hemochromatosis affecting liver and heart. Warm AIHA carries ~5-10% mortality even with appropriate management; predictors of worse outcome/relapse include Evans syndrome, hemoglobin <6 g/dL, acute renal failure, infection, and high-affinity antibody. G6PD-related acute hemolysis generally resolves fully once the trigger is removed and in the absence of comorbidity, with full recovery from renal failure the rule when there is no prior kidney disease (p.792, 799, 801).","pitfalls":["Neglecting the reticulocyte count, a key early clue to a hemolytic mechanism (p.791)","Screening (semiquantitative) G6PD tests give falsely normal results immediately after a hemolytic attack because the oldest deficient cells are destroyed and young reticulocytes have higher G6PD - use a quantitative assay; female heterozygotes may also be missed (p.799-800)","Dismissing Coombs-negative AIHA as not serious - high-affinity antibody can cause severe disease with few bound molecules; false-positive Coombs can follow transfusion, organ transplant, or Ig/anti-lymphocyte products (p.801-802)","Assuming an inherited hemolytic disorder always causes anemia - compensated hemolysis can be normo-hemoglobinemic until decompensation (p.792)","In cold agglutinin disease, prednisone and splenectomy are ineffective (unlike warm AIHA), and transfused (I-positive) red cells survive no longer than the patient's; the blood bag and patient extremities must be kept warm (p.803)","Misattributing favism to fava bean pollen inhalation (it does not cause favism) or to other beans (which are safe) (p.799)"],"severityClassification":"No single formal grading. Severity is gauged clinically by the hemoglobin level and tempo (mild compensated hemolysis without anemia through to abrupt severe drops to ~4 g/dL in AIHA) and by the site of hemolysis: intravascular hemolysis is signalled by hemoglobinuria, free plasma hemoglobin, LDH up to ~10x normal, and absent haptoglobin (Table 105-7), whereas extravascular hemolysis chiefly raises unconjugated bilirubin and urobilinogen. High-risk markers in AIHA include hemoglobin <6 g/dL, acute renal failure, infection, and Evans syndrome (p.791-792, 801, Tables 105-2, 105-7).","references":["Harrison 22e p.791","Harrison 22e p.792","Harrison 22e p.799","Harrison 22e p.801","Harrison 22e p.802","Harrison 22e p.803"],"crossLinks":["anemia_sympt","ttp_hus"]},"herpes_simplex":{"name":"Herpes Simplex Virus Infection","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":197,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1050, 1495; encephalitis discussion p.747","clinicalPearls":["Primary HSV infection produces erythema followed by hallmark painful grouped vesicles that may evolve into pustules and ulcerate, especially on mucosal surfaces; lesions appear at the site of inoculation (Harrison p.~1010, App. A1).","HSV-1 classically causes orolabial disease (cold sores, herpes labialis) and HSV-2 causes genital lesions, but either type can infect either site (Harrison p.1050).","Non-genital cutaneous presentations include herpes gladiatorum (head and neck of young wrestlers) and herpetic whitlow (digits of health care workers) (Harrison p.1050).","Primary acute herpetic gingivostomatitis (usually HSV-1) presents with labial vesicles that rupture and crust plus intraoral vesicles that quickly ulcerate; it is extremely painful, with acute gingivitis, fever, malaise, foul odor, and cervical lymphadenopathy, occurring primarily in infants, children, and young adults (Harrison p.~325, Table 38-1).","Recurrent disease is milder than primary infection, and there is typically no fever in recurrent infection; herpes labialis recurrences typically do not involve the oral mucosa (Harrison App. A1).","Sexually transmitted HSV causes most genital ulcer disease worldwide, and there is a documented positive-feedback loop between HSV and HIV transmission (Harrison p.1096).","Although the HSV–HIV epidemiologic link is consistent, randomized trials of antiviral suppression of HSV showed no protective effect against HIV acquisition or transmission (Harrison p.1096)."],"pathophysiology":"HSV-1 and HSV-2 are neurotropic DNA herpesviruses that establish lifelong latency in sensory ganglia after primary mucocutaneous infection; periodic reactivation produces recurrent localized disease (e.g., recurrent herpes labialis with HSV-1 and herpes genitalis with HSV-2). Virus enters and replicates within the epidermis at the site of inoculation. In HSV encephalitis there is characteristic unilateral temporal-lobe involvement. The virus can disseminate, particularly in the setting of disrupted skin (eczema herpeticum) or impaired cell-mediated immunity; visceral dissemination may occur even with only localized mucocutaneous disease (Harrison pp.1050, 1010 App. A1, p.747).","additionalDifferentials":["Varicella-zoster virus (chickenpox/shingles) for vesicular eruptions","Coxsackievirus A16 (hand-foot-and-mouth disease)","Aphthous ulcers (recurrent oral ulceration)","Other causes of genital ulcer disease (e.g., syphilis, chancroid) in genital HSV"],"infectionMimics":["Varicella-zoster virus infection (vesicular lesions are individually similar to HSV; Tzanck smear cannot distinguish them)","Reactive infectious mucocutaneous eruption (RIME) due to Mycoplasma/other respiratory infections, which mimics mucositis seen with HSV-associated erythema multiforme","Disseminated VZV versus disseminated HSV (individual lesions similar for VZV and HSV)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Erythema multiforme major / Stevens-Johnson syndrome / toxic epidermal necrolysis (HSV is itself a common trigger of EM, but drug-induced SJS/TEN must be distinguished)","Pemphigus vulgaris (blistering eruption with mucositis)","Aphthous stomatitis"],"additionalInvestigations":["PCR of CSF for HSV in suspected encephalitis (diagnostic test of choice; may be falsely negative early in the course of disease) (Harrison p.747)","Tzanck smear of an early unroofed vesicle (Giemsa/Wright stain): multinucleated epithelial giant cells suggest HSV or VZV, but cannot identify the specific virus (Harrison p.~480)","Viral culture, immunofluorescence microscopy, or genetic/PCR testing to identify the specific virus type (Harrison p.~480)","In FUO with headache, CSF microbiologic examination including HSV (especially type 2) (Harrison p.~149)"],"redFlags":["Fever with decreased level of consciousness and unilateral temporal-lobe involvement on imaging suggests HSV encephalitis - start empiric aciclovir promptly because CSF PCR may be falsely negative early (Harrison p.747)","Disseminated/visceral HSV in immunocompromised or neonatal patients; HSV can disseminate viscerally even with only localized mucocutaneous disease","Eczema herpeticum (HSV dissemination in eczematous skin)","Neonatal HSV disease (skin lesions are diagnostically helpful when present but may be absent)"],"prognosis":"Primary herpetic gingivostomatitis heals spontaneously in 10-14 days; lesions persisting beyond 3 weeks (unless secondarily infected) are not due to primary HSV infection. Recurrent disease is generally milder than primary infection. Approximately 25% of patients with herpes simplex encephalitis subsequently develop an autoimmune (anti-NMDAR) encephalitis weeks after the viral infection resolves - in children often with choreoathetosis and in adults with cognitive/psychiatric symptoms (Harrison pp.325, 821).","pitfalls":["CSF HSV PCR may be falsely negative early in the course of encephalitis, so a negative early result does not exclude the diagnosis and should not delay empiric aciclovir (Harrison p.747)","Tzanck smear cannot distinguish HSV from VZV; type-specific identification requires culture, immunofluorescence, or genetic testing (Harrison p.~480)","HSV visceral dissemination can occur with only localized mucocutaneous disease, so absence of widespread skin lesions does not exclude serious systemic infection (Harrison App. A1)","Oral lesions lasting more than 3 weeks should prompt consideration of an alternative diagnosis rather than primary HSV (Harrison Table 38-1)"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e, Ch. 197 (Herpes Simplex Virus Infections), p.1495","Harrison's 22e, Ch. 134 (Skin and Soft Tissue Infections), p.1050","Harrison's 22e, Ch. 38 (Oral Manifestations of Disease), Table 38-1","Harrison's 22e, encephalitis in immunocompromised hosts (HSV encephalitis), p.747","Harrison's 22e, Ch. 141 (Sexually Transmitted Infections: Overview and Clinical Approach), p.1096"],"crossLinks":[]},"histoplasmosis":{"name":"Histoplasmosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":218,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1692-1696","clinicalPearls":["Caused by Histoplasma capsulatum, a thermal dimorphic fungus; H. capsulatum var. capsulatum predominates in North America, while var. duboisii in Africa produces larger yeasts and a clinically distinct disease with frequent skin and bone involvement (p.1692-1693, 1695).","Most prevalent endemic mycosis in North America, classically the Ohio and Mississippi river valleys, with foci in Mexico, Central/South America (Brazil), Africa and Asia; soil enriched with bird or bat droppings promotes growth, and disruption aerosolizes microconidia (p.1693).","High-exposure, high-attack-rate activities include spelunking (cave exposure), excavation, cleaning chicken coops, demolition/remodeling of old buildings and cutting dead trees (p.1693).","Clinical spectrum spans asymptomatic infection to life-threatening illness; severity depends on intensity of exposure, host immune status and underlying lung architecture (p.1693-1694).","Acute pulmonary disease appears 1-4 weeks after exposure as a flulike illness (fever, chills, sweats, headache, myalgia, anorexia, dry cough, dyspnea, chest pain), often with hilar/mediastinal adenopathy on chest radiograph (p.1694).","Arthralgia/arthritis with erythema nodosum occurs in 5-10% of acute cases and represents an inflammatory response, not extrapulmonary spread; pericarditis may also develop (p.1694).","Chronic cavitary pulmonary histoplasmosis occurs in smokers with structural lung disease (e.g., bullous emphysema), with productive cough, dyspnea, low-grade fever, night sweats, weight loss and upper-lobe cavitary infiltrates resembling tuberculosis (p.1694).","Progressive disseminated histoplasmosis (PDH) involves the reticuloendothelial system - lungs, bone marrow, spleen, liver, adrenal glands and mucocutaneous membranes; common findings are fever, weight loss, hepatosplenomegaly and thrombocytopenia, with possible meningitis, oral/GI ulceration and adrenal insufficiency (p.1694).","Fungal culture is the diagnostic gold standard but may take up to 1 month and is often negative in less severe cases; in PDH yield is highest from BAL fluid, bone marrow aspirate and blood (p.1694).","Detection of Histoplasma antigen in body fluids is extremely useful for PDH and acute diffuse pulmonary disease (sensitivity >95% in PDH, >80% in severe acute pulmonary disease when both urine and serum are tested); urine is often positive in disseminated disease (p.1694-1695, 1693).","Antigen levels correlate with severity in PDH, fall with effective therapy, and a stable or rising level predicts relapse or treatment failure (p.1695-1696).","Serology (immunodiffusion, complement fixation, IgG/IgM EIA) is especially useful in immunocompetent patients and for chronic pulmonary disease; IgM appears first and declines, IgG appears later and rises (p.1695)."],"pathophysiology":"Infection follows inhalation of microconidia, the naturally infectious mycelial form; particles 2-4 micrometers reach terminal bronchioles and alveoli where alveolar macrophages engulf them and they transform into small (2-5 micrometer) narrow-budding yeasts, a calcium- and iron-dependent process central to pathogenesis. Yeasts evade the immune system and multiply within resting macrophages, then translocate via phagosomes to draining lymph nodes and spread hematogenously throughout the reticuloendothelial system. Effective cellular immunity develops about 2 weeks after infection, with T-cell interferon-gamma helping macrophages kill the organism; IL-12 and TNF-alpha are essential to cellular immunity. In immunocompetent hosts, organized granulomas contain the organisms and typically fibrose and calcify (calcified lung nodules, mediastinal nodes, hepatosplenic calcifications), and infection confers protective immunity to reinfection. With impaired cellular immunity, infection is not contained and disseminates as progressive disseminated histoplasmosis. Structural lung disease such as emphysema impairs clearance and leads to chronic pulmonary disease with progressive inflammation, necrosis and fibrosis mimicking cavitary tuberculosis (p.1693-1694).","additionalDifferentials":["Tuberculosis (chronic cavitary disease)","Sarcoidosis","Lymphoma","Primary or metastatic lung cancer (asymptomatic pulmonary nodules)","Other endemic/dimorphic mycoses: blastomycosis, coccidioidomycosis, paracoccidioidomycosis, talaromycosis"],"infectionMimics":["Cavitary tuberculosis (chronic pulmonary histoplasmosis mimics this radiographically and pathologically)","Blastomycosis","Coccidioidomycosis","Paracoccidioidomycosis","Talaromycosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Sarcoidosis","Lymphoma","Primary lung cancer (asymptomatic controlled nodules found on screening CT in smokers)","Metastatic cancer"],"additionalInvestigations":["Histoplasma antigen detection in urine and serum (and BAL fluid; CSF for CNS disease)","Lateral flow antigen point-of-care testing for resource-limited settings","Histoplasma serology: immunodiffusion (ID), complement fixation (CF), IgG and IgM enzyme immunoassay (EIA)","Fungal culture (BAL fluid, sputum/bronchial washings, blood, bone marrow aspirate; positive in ~75% of PDH and chronic pulmonary disease)","Fungal stains/cytopathology or biopsy of affected organ (BAL fluid, bone marrow, liver, adrenal) showing small narrow-budding intracellular yeasts","Chest radiograph/CT (pneumonitis, hilar/mediastinal adenopathy, upper-lobe cavitation, calcified nodes)","Workup for rare genetic immunodeficiencies of the IFN-gamma and TNF-alpha pathways in healthy individuals presenting with PDH"],"redFlags":["Progressive disseminated histoplasmosis (PDH), typically in immunocompromised hosts (~70% of cases), especially AIDS with CD4+ count <200/microliter","Acute rapidly fatal PDH with diffuse interstitial/reticulonodular infiltrates, respiratory failure, shock, coagulopathy and multiorgan failure","Onset of PDH while on anti-TNF-alpha therapy, methotrexate or other biologic/immunosuppressive agents, or post-transplant immunosuppression","CNS involvement: meningitis or focal brain lesions","Adrenal insufficiency from adrenal involvement","Fibrosing mediastinitis causing superior vena cava syndrome, pulmonary vessel obstruction or airway obstruction (fatal in up to one-third of cases)","Large mediastinal masses compressing great vessels, airways or esophagus, or rupturing to form fistulae (e.g., bronchoesophageal)"],"prognosis":"Most immunocompetent individuals with low-level exposure are asymptomatic or have a mild, self-limited illness that resolves without therapy; up to 75% of adults in endemic areas have evidence of prior infection without clinical disease. Untreated chronic cavitary disease is slowly progressive. Progressive disseminated histoplasmosis ranges from an acute, rapidly fatal course to a subacute/chronic one and is a devastating illness requiring prompt recognition, particularly in AIDS or anti-TNF therapy. Fibrosing mediastinitis is fatal in up to one-third of cases. Antigen levels track severity and response, with stable or rising levels signaling treatment failure or relapse (p.1693-1696).","pitfalls":["Chronic cavitary histoplasmosis closely mimics cavitary tuberculosis radiographically (upper-lobe infiltrates, cavitation, pleural thickening) and may be misdiagnosed as TB, sarcoidosis, lymphoma or malignancy.","Asymptomatic calcified nodules from controlled prior infection may be mistaken for malignancy on screening CT in smokers from endemic areas.","Fungal stains may misidentify staining artifacts or other fungal elements as Histoplasma yeasts.","Persistent antibodies for several years after infection can cause a false misdiagnosis of active histoplasmosis in a patient with another disease process.","ID and CF serology are insensitive early in infection and have reduced sensitivity in immunosuppressed patients, especially organ transplant recipients.","Antigen testing cross-reacts with African histoplasmosis, blastomycosis, coccidioidomycosis, paracoccidioidomycosis, talaromycosis and rarely aspergillosis.","Fibrosing mediastinitis is a chronic fibrotic reaction to past infection, not active disease, and does NOT respond to antifungal therapy.","Unlike latent tuberculosis, inactive histoplasmosis does NOT reactivate."],"severityClassification":"Clinical forms (Harrison Table 218-1): acute pulmonary, mild to moderate; acute pulmonary, severe with respiratory failure (ARDS); chronic/cavitary pulmonary; progressive disseminated (PDH); and central nervous system disease. Severity is graded by intensity of exposure, host immune status and underlying lung architecture (p.1693-1695).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1692-1696"],"crossLinks":[]},"hiv_aids":{"name":"HIV/AIDS","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":208,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1556","clinicalPearls":["HIV is a retrovirus whose hallmark is progressive depletion of CD4+ T lymphocytes; the resulting immunodeficiency underlies the susceptibility to opportunistic infections and AIDS-defining illnesses (Harrison 22e ch.208, p.1556).","Consider acute (primary) HIV infection in a patient presenting with a mononucleosis-like illness: sore throat is reported by roughly half of patients with acute HIV infection, often together with fever, headache, myalgias, lymphadenopathy, anorexia and rash (p.1556; cross-ref ch.38 p.293).","The acute retroviral syndrome rash is typically a nonspecific diffuse macular/papular eruption favouring the upper thorax, face and collar region; less commonly urticarial or vesicular lesions occur, and oral or genital ulcers may accompany it (p.1556; cross-ref Appendix A1).","After primary infection there is HIV seroconversion, then a usually asymptomatic phase, and ultimately progression to symptomatic HIV disease/AIDS if untreated (cross-ref ch.38 p.297).","Three diagnostic markers appear sequentially in primary infection: HIV viral load (RNA) first, then p24 antigen, then HIV antibody; modern fourth-generation Ag/Ab combination assays shorten the window period by detecting p24 antigen before antibody is present (cross-ref ch.126, Fig.126-1).","The CD4+ T-cell count stratifies opportunistic-infection risk: as the count falls (notably below ~200 cells/uL) the risk of Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, cerebral toxoplasmosis, cryptococcosis, disseminated Mycobacterium avium complex and CMV end-organ disease rises, guiding prophylaxis decisions (p.1556; cross-ref ch.62, ch.108).","A nondetectable (suppressed) HIV viral load on antiretroviral therapy is the goal of treatment; sustained viral suppression is the basis of the Undetectable = Untransmittable (U=U) principle and of treatment-as-prevention (cross-ref ch.108 p.745).","Antiretroviral therapy is recommended for essentially all people with HIV regardless of CD4 count; in patients with concurrent malignancy or other illness, ART should run concurrently with the primary treatment together with appropriate antimicrobial prophylaxis (cross-ref ch.108 p.745).","Doses and specific drug regimens are deliberately omitted here; ART is presented only as a principle (combination antiretroviral therapy to achieve durable viral suppression)."],"pathophysiology":"HIV is a human retrovirus that infects cells bearing the CD4 receptor together with a chemokine co-receptor (CCR5 or CXCR4), leading to progressive depletion and dysfunction of CD4+ T lymphocytes (Harrison 22e ch.208, p.1556; co-receptor role cross-ref ch.61 p.448, ch.63). Reverse transcriptase copies the viral RNA genome into DNA that integrates into the host genome, establishing lifelong infection and a latent reservoir. Primary infection produces a burst of high-level viremia (the acute retroviral syndrome) followed by partial immune control and a viral set point, then a prolonged clinically latent phase during which CD4+ T cells are gradually lost. Once CD4+ T-cell counts fall sufficiently, the profound cellular immunodeficiency permits opportunistic infections and AIDS-defining illnesses. The mononuclear phagocyte system can also be infected and serves as a reservoir (cross-ref ch.63 p.461).","additionalDifferentials":["Infectious mononucleosis (Epstein-Barr virus) and acute CMV infection (overlapping mononucleosis-like picture; cross-ref Appendix A1)","Acute viral exanthems and other causes of fever with rash (e.g., secondary syphilis, enteroviral exanthem)","Streptococcal/viral pharyngitis when sore throat predominates in acute HIV (cross-ref ch.38 p.293)"],"infectionMimics":["Epstein-Barr virus infectious mononucleosis","Acute cytomegalovirus infection","Secondary syphilis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Drug hypersensitivity reaction with fever and morbilliform rash","Autoinflammatory/connective-tissue disease presenting with fever, rash and lymphadenopathy"],"additionalInvestigations":["Fourth-generation HIV-1/2 antigen/antibody combination immunoassay with supplemental confirmatory antibody differentiation assay (cross-ref ch.126, Fig.126-1)","HIV RNA (plasma viral load) quantification — diagnostic in the early window before antibody, and the key on-treatment monitoring test (cross-ref ch.126; ch.108)","CD4+ T-lymphocyte count to stage immunodeficiency and guide opportunistic-infection prophylaxis","HIV genotypic resistance testing recommended before starting antiretrovirals (cross-ref ch.126, Fig.126-1)","Screening for co-infections (hepatitis B, hepatitis C) prior to immunosuppressive or antineoplastic therapy in HIV-positive patients (cross-ref ch.108 p.745)"],"redFlags":["New CD4+ count below ~200 cells/uL — high risk of Pneumocystis pneumonia and other opportunistic infections; prompts prophylaxis (cross-ref ch.62 p.461)","Clinical or radiographic worsening after starting ART suggesting immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS), especially with an underlying opportunistic infection such as PML (cross-ref ch.205 p.1490)","Features suggesting an AIDS-defining opportunistic process (e.g., focal neurologic deficit from toxoplasmosis or CNS lymphoma; cross-ref ch.30)","Adrenal insufficiency precipitated by stress or steroidogenesis-inhibiting drugs in advanced HIV — adrenal involvement is usually subclinical but can become clinically significant (cross-ref ch.49 p.379)"],"prognosis":"Untreated HIV infection is progressive and ultimately fatal once advanced immunodeficiency and AIDS-defining illnesses supervene. Effective antiretroviral therapy that achieves a durable nondetectable viral load markedly improves survival and restores immune function; for example, in HIV-positive patients with PML, institution of ART enhances survival, and those with higher CD4 counts (>300/uL) and low or nondetectable HIV viral loads have a better prognosis (cross-ref ch.205, p.1490).","pitfalls":["Failing to consider acute HIV in a mononucleosis-like illness: although about half of patients with acute HIV infection have a sore throat, in most U.S. settings only about 1% of patients with viral or mononucleosis-like symptoms actually have acute HIV — a negative antibody test early does not exclude it, so check viral load/p24 (cross-ref ch.38 p.293).","Relying on antibody testing alone during the window period can miss acute infection before seroconversion; the dashed limit-of-detection and marker sequence (VL, then p24, then Ab) explain false-negative antibody results (cross-ref ch.126, Fig.126-1).","Attributing post-ART clinical deterioration to treatment failure rather than recognising IRIS, particularly in patients with a treated or unmasked opportunistic infection (cross-ref ch.205 p.1490).","Overlooking subclinical adrenal insufficiency in advanced HIV, which may be unmasked by stress or by drugs that inhibit steroidogenesis (cross-ref ch.49 p.379)."],"severityClassification":"Staging is based on the CD4+ T-lymphocyte count, which predicts the spectrum of opportunistic disease: relatively preserved counts carry low opportunistic-infection risk, whereas progressively lower counts (notably <200 cells/uL) define increasing risk for Pneumocystis pneumonia, cerebral toxoplasmosis, cryptococcosis, disseminated Mycobacterium avium complex and CMV disease, with the lowest counts associated with the broadest range of AIDS-defining illnesses (Harrison 22e ch.208, p.1556; opportunistic-infection associations cross-ref ch.62, ch.222, ch.227, ch.235).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1556"],"crossLinks":["pneumocystis_pneumonia","cryptococcosis","toxoplasmosis","cytomegalovirus_infection","nontuberculous_mycobacterial_infection"]},"hodgkin_lymphoma":{"name":"Hodgkin's Lymphoma","system":"Oncology / Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":114,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.868-871","clinicalPearls":["Hodgkin's lymphoma (HL) is a malignancy of mature B lymphocytes and accounts for ~10% of all lymphomas diagnosed each year; most are classical HL (cHL), with nodular lymphocyte-predominant HL (NLPHL) a distinct second subtype (p.868).","Reed-Sternberg (HRS) cells are diagnostic of cHL: large cells with abundant cytoplasm and bilobed and/or multiple nuclei, comprising <1% of tumor cellularity within a surrounding polyclonal inflammatory infiltrate of lymphocytes, eosinophils, neutrophils, macrophages, plasma cells, fibroblasts, and collagen (p.868-869).","By immunohistochemistry HRS cells are often PAX-5 positive but have low to no expression of other B-cell antigens such as CD19 and CD20; they express CD15 and CD30 in 85% and 100% of cases, respectively (p.868).","Most patients present with nontender palpable lymphadenopathy, typically in the neck, supraclavicular area, and axilla; more than half have mediastinal adenopathy at diagnosis, sometimes as the initial manifestation (p.869).","About one-third of patients present with B symptoms (fevers, night sweats, and/or weight loss); HL can occasionally present as a fever of unknown origin, more common in older patients with mixed-cellularity HL in an abdominal site (p.869).","Pel-Ebstein fever describes a pattern of fevers persisting for days to weeks, followed by afebrile intervals and then recurrence (p.869).","Pain in lymph nodes on alcohol ingestion and severe unexplained itching (pruritus) are recognized unusual manifestations of HL (p.869).","Staging is anatomically based using the Ann Arbor staging system because the disease tends to march from one lymph node group to the next, often contiguous group; staging guides therapy duration and intensity (p.869).","PET/CT is used for staging and is more accurate than bone marrow biopsy for assessing bone marrow involvement, which in cHL tends to be patchy and may be missed on a unilateral marrow biopsy (p.869).","Cure rates now exceed 85%, and the new central challenge in HL care is late therapy-related toxicity, including a high rate of secondary malignancies and cardiovascular disease (p.868)."],"pathophysiology":"HL is of B-cell origin. The malignant Reed-Sternberg (HRS) cells are large bilobed/multinucleate cells that constitute <1% of the tumor mass; the bulk of the tumor is a surrounding polyclonal inflammatory infiltrate, probably a reaction to cytokines produced by the tumor cells. Immunoglobulin genes in the neoplastic cells are rearranged but not expressed. HRS cells interact with their microenvironment via cell-cell contact and elaboration of growth factors and cytokines, producing a cellular milieu that protects them from host immune attack while supporting their proliferation and survival. 97% of HRS cells in cHL harbor genetic aberrations in the PD-L1 locus on chromosome 9p24.1, causing overexpression of PD-L1 (ligand for the inhibitory PD-1 receptor on immune cells), a mechanism for immune evasion that may also contribute to generalized immune suppression in HL patients. An association with Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) is suggested: monoclonal/oligoclonal EBV-infected cells are found in 20-40% of HL patients, but a definitive etiologic role is not settled. Viral oncogenesis appears greater in HIV-related cHL, where EBV is detectable in nearly all cases (versus only one-third of non-HIV-associated cHL) and HRS cells express EBV latent membrane protein 1 (LMP-1) with episomal, clonal EBV genomes suggesting direct involvement in early lymphomagenesis (p.868-869).","additionalDifferentials":["Inflammatory processes (reactive lymphadenopathy)","Infectious mononucleosis","Non-Hodgkin's lymphoma (NHL)","Phenytoin-induced adenopathy","Nonlymphomatous malignancies","Anaplastic large-cell lymphoma (can mimic HL, especially in older patients)","T-cell/histiocyte-rich B-cell lymphoma"],"infectionMimics":["Infectious mononucleosis (EBV)","Reactive/inflammatory lymphadenopathy from infection"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Non-Hodgkin's lymphoma","Anaplastic large-cell lymphoma","T-cell/histiocyte-rich B-cell lymphoma","Phenytoin-induced (drug-related) adenopathy","Nonlymphomatous malignancies"],"additionalInvestigations":["Adequate biopsy specimen reviewed by an expert hematopathologist (required to establish the diagnosis)","Immunohistochemistry: PAX-5, CD15, CD30 (and CD19/CD20, typically low/absent in cHL)","PET/CT for anatomic staging (more accurate than marrow biopsy for patchy marrow involvement)","Complete blood count with differential","Erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR)","Chemistry studies reflecting major organ function, including serum albumin","HIV and hepatitis virus testing","Careful history (including presence/absence of B symptoms) and physical exam attentive to peripheral nodes, liver, and spleen size; review of comorbidities such as pulmonary disease and congestive heart failure given lung- and heart-toxic chemotherapy"],"redFlags":["B symptoms: unexplained weight loss >10% of body weight over the 6 months before staging, persistent or recurrent fever >38°C in the previous month, or recurrent drenching night sweats in the previous month","Pel-Ebstein fever (cyclic fever with afebrile intervals)","Bulky disease and elevated ESR (>30 with B symptoms; >50 without) marking unfavorable early-stage disease","Fever of unknown origin as a presentation, particularly in older patients with mixed-cellularity HL at an abdominal site","Superior vena cava syndrome from mediastinal involvement (HL is a recognized cause)"],"prognosis":"cHL is highly curable and a success story of modern oncology, with cure rates now >85%; the overwhelming majority of patients are cured with chemotherapy alone or combined chemotherapy and radiation. Patients are stratified into early-stage (I/II) versus advanced-stage (III/IV) disease. Early-stage patients have a better prognosis overall and are further classified as favorable or unfavorable. In advanced-stage disease, prognosis is best predicted by the International Prognostic Score (IPS), which assigns 1 point each for male sex, age >45 years, stage IV disease, serum albumin <4 g/dL, hemoglobin <10.5 g/dL, white blood cell count >=15,000/uL, and lymphocyte count <600/uL and/or <8% of WBC count; 5-year progression-free survival ranges from 88% (no risk factors) to 62% (four or more factors), though very few patients carry multiple risk factors. NLPHL tends to have a chronic, relapsing course resembling indolent B-cell NHL, with a 15-year nonrelapse survival of 82% for early-stage disease treated with definitive radiotherapy. A major concern is that in some early-stage series more patients died from late treatment complications than from HL itself (p.868-871).","pitfalls":["The late age peak in the bimodal distribution may be artifactual, attributable to confusion with similar-appearing entities such as anaplastic large-cell lymphoma and T-cell/histiocyte-rich B-cell lymphoma (p.868).","Bone marrow involvement in cHL is patchy and can be missed on a unilateral marrow biopsy; PET/CT is more accurate (p.869).","Combined-modality therapy in early-stage disease reduces relapse only slightly while increasing late toxicity, with minimal or no improvement in long-term survival; much of the excess risk is attributable to radiotherapy (p.869-870).","NLPHL is biologically distinct from cHL and resembles indolent B-cell NHL; its 'popcorn'/L&H cells are CD30- and CD15-negative (unlike HRS cells), so it should not be managed identically to cHL (p.868, 871).","Alternative regimens (Stanford V, escalated BEACOPP) have not improved survival over standard therapy in early- or advanced-stage disease and are associated with increased toxicity (p.870).","Subdiaphragmatic presentation of cHL is unusual; it is more common in older males (p.869)."],"severityClassification":"Ann Arbor Staging System: Stage I = single lymph node region or lymphoid structure (e.g., spleen, thymus, Waldeyer's ring); Stage II = two or more node regions on the same side of the diaphragm (mediastinum counts as one site; bilaterally involved hilar nodes constitute stage II); Stage III = node regions/lymphoid structures on both sides of the diaphragm (III1 limited to spleen, splenic hilar, celiac, or portal nodes; III2 adds paraaortic, iliac, or mesenteric nodes); Stage IV = extranodal involvement beyond 'E', more than one extranodal deposit at any location, or any liver/bone marrow involvement. Suffix A = no symptoms; B = unexplained weight loss >10% over 6 months, persistent/recurrent fever >38°C in the previous month, or recurrent drenching night sweats in the previous month; E = localized solitary extralymphatic involvement (excluding liver and bone marrow). Patients are further grouped clinically as early-stage (I/II) versus advanced-stage (III/IV), with early-stage subclassified favorable vs unfavorable and advanced-stage risk-scored by the International Prognostic Score (p.869).","references":["Harrison 22e p.868-871"],"crossLinks":["non_hodgkin_lymphoma"]},"hypothyroidism":{"name":"Hypothyroidism","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":395,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3027","clinicalPearls":["In iodine-sufficient regions the most common causes are autoimmune disease (Hashimoto's thyroiditis) and iatrogenic injury (treatment of hyperthyroidism); iodine deficiency remains a leading cause worldwide (p.3027).","Biochemical hallmark of primary hypothyroidism is an elevated TSH with a low unbound (free) T4; a normal TSH excludes primary, but not secondary/central, disease (p.3030-3031).","Subclinical hypothyroidism is biochemical thyroid hormone deficiency (raised TSH, usually still-normal free T4) with few or no symptoms; overt hypothyroidism typically corresponds to TSH >10 mIU/L with a low free T4 (p.3029, p.3032).","Symptoms (descending frequency) include tiredness/weakness, dry skin, cold intolerance, hair loss, poor concentration and memory, constipation, and modest weight gain with poor appetite (the weight gain is mainly fluid retention in myxedematous tissue) (p.3028-3029).","Signs include dry coarse skin, cool extremities, nonpitting puffiness of face/hands/feet (myxedema), diffuse alopecia with thinning of the outer eyebrow, bradycardia, peripheral edema, and delayed relaxation of tendon reflexes (p.3028-3029).","TPO and thyroglobulin antibodies are present in >95% of autoimmune hypothyroidism and confirm the etiology once hypothyroidism is established (p.3030).","Onset is usually insidious, so patients may only recognize symptoms once euthyroidism is restored; Hashimoto's patients may present with goiter (irregular, firm) rather than hypothyroid symptoms (p.3029-3030).","Associated laboratory abnormalities include raised creatine phosphokinase, elevated cholesterol and triglycerides, and a normocytic or macrocytic anemia, which generally resolve with thyroxine replacement (p.3030).","Autoimmune hypothyroidism clusters with other autoimmune disorders, particularly vitiligo, pernicious anemia, Addison's disease (Schmidt's syndrome), type 1 diabetes, and the autoimmune polyendocrine syndromes (p.3030)."],"pathophysiology":"Hypothyroidism is deficient thyroid hormone action, most often from primary thyroid failure. In autoimmune (Hashimoto's) disease, T cell-mediated lymphocytic infiltration with germinal-center formation, follicular atrophy, oxyphil metaplasia, and fibrosis progressively destroy the gland; atrophic thyroiditis represents its fibrotic end stage. As function declines there is a compensated phase in which a rising TSH maintains hormone levels (subclinical hypothyroidism); later free T4 falls and TSH rises further, producing overt disease. Susceptibility reflects genetic factors (HLA-DR3/DR4/DR5, PTPN22, CTLA-4) and environmental factors including high iodine intake. TPO and thyroglobulin antibodies are markers; up to 20% of patients have TSH-receptor-blocking antibodies that prevent TSH binding and can cause thyroid atrophy and, by transplacental passage, transient neonatal hypothyroidism. Increased dermal glycosaminoglycan deposition traps water, producing nonpitting myxedema (Harrison 22e p.3029-3030).","additionalDifferentials":["Hyperthyroidism / thyrotoxicosis (opposite end of thyroid dysfunction; thyroiditis can pass through both phases)","Sick euthyroid (nonthyroidal illness) syndrome","Secondary (central) hypothyroidism from hypopituitarism or hypothalamic disease","Multinodular goiter or thyroid carcinoma (asymmetric Hashimoto goiter may mimic these)","Primary thyroid lymphoma (rare, strongly associated with pre-existing autoimmune thyroiditis)","Depression and dementia (overlapping fatigue, poor concentration, slowing)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Depression","Dementia","Nephrotic syndrome (edema, hyperlipidemia)","Chronic fatigue states","Anemia of other cause"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum TSH as the primary screening test (most sensitive for primary disease)","Free/unbound T4 to confirm clinical hypothyroidism when TSH is elevated (T3 measurement is not indicated)","TPO and thyroglobulin antibodies to establish autoimmune etiology","Lipid panel (cholesterol, triglycerides), creatine phosphokinase, and full blood count for associated abnormalities","Anterior pituitary function testing when central hypothyroidism is suspected (after excluding drug effect and sick euthyroid syndrome)","Thyroid ultrasound to distinguish heterogeneous Hashimoto echotexture from true nodules warranting FNA"],"redFlags":["Myxedema coma — the severe end of untreated hypothyroidism: reduced level of consciousness (sometimes seizures), hypothermia (can reach 23 C), with 20-40% mortality despite treatment; almost always in the elderly and often precipitated by infection, cold, sedatives, or acute cardiopulmonary events.","Hypoventilation with hypoxia and hypercapnia, plus hypoglycemia and dilutional hyponatremia, in profound hypothyroidism.","Impaired adrenal reserve in profound hypothyroidism (consider co-existing adrenal insufficiency before/with thyroid replacement).","Neonatal/congenital hypothyroidism — risk of permanent neurologic damage if treatment is delayed, mandating neonatal screening.","Significant pericardial or other serous effusions, conductive deafness, or carpal tunnel/entrapment syndromes as manifestations of severe disease."],"prognosis":"With levothyroxine replacement and dose titration to a normal TSH, prognosis is excellent and most laboratory and clinical abnormalities resolve, though full symptomatic relief may take months and about 10-15% report persistent symptoms despite biochemical euthyroidism. Untreated congenital hypothyroidism causes permanent neurologic damage, and myxedema coma still carries a 20-40% mortality independent of T4 and TSH levels (Harrison 22e p.3031-3032).","pitfalls":["Central (secondary) hypothyroidism is missed if TSH is used alone: in pituitary/hypothalamic disease TSH may be low, normal, or even slightly raised, so a low free T4 is needed to make the diagnosis and TSH cannot be used to monitor therapy.","A normal TSH excludes primary but NOT secondary/central hypothyroidism.","T4 alone is an inferior screening test because it will not detect subclinical hypothyroidism; T3 is normal in ~25% of patients and is not indicated.","An elevated TSH with normal/high free T4 in a patient on adequate levothyroxine usually reflects poor adherence (taking medication only shortly before testing), not a TSH-secretion disorder.","Confirm that a raised TSH is sustained over ~3 months before treating subclinical hypothyroidism.","TSH should not be relied on in the months immediately after radioiodine for Graves' disease (it stays suppressed); use unbound T4 instead.","External rewarming in myxedema coma can precipitate cardiovascular collapse, and hypotonic fluids can worsen hyponatremia."],"severityClassification":"Spectrum from subclinical hypothyroidism (raised TSH, normal free T4, few/no symptoms) to overt/clinical hypothyroidism (raised TSH usually >10 mIU/L with low free T4 and symptoms); myxedema coma represents the severe, life-threatening end. Etiologically classified as primary (thyroidal), secondary/central (pituitary or hypothalamic), congenital, or transient (Harrison 22e p.3027-3032).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3027"],"crossLinks":[]},"infectious_mononucleosis":{"name":"Infectious Mononucleosis (EBV)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":199,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1508-1514","clinicalPearls":["Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) is the cause of heterophile-positive infectious mononucleosis (IM), classically marked by the triad of fever, sore throat (pharyngitis), and lymphadenopathy, together with atypical lymphocytosis (Harrison 22e p.1508).","EBV is spread mainly by contact with oral secretions (saliva); by adulthood >90% of individuals are seropositive. IM is typically a disease of young adults, while in young children primary infection is usually asymptomatic or a mild pharyngitis (Harrison 22e p.1508-1509).","The incubation period in young adults is about 4-6 weeks, often preceded by a 1-2 week prodrome of fatigue, malaise, and myalgia before fever, sore throat, and lymphadenopathy appear (Harrison 22e p.1509).","Lymphadenopathy (most often posterior cervical, tender, symmetric, not fixed) and pharyngitis are most prominent in the first 2 weeks, whereas splenomegaly is more prominent during the second and third weeks (Harrison 22e p.1509).","Lymphadenopathy and fever are the most common signs (each in >90%), with pharyngitis/tonsillitis in ~82% and splenomegaly in ~51%; tonsillar exudate can resemble streptococcal pharyngitis (Harrison 22e p.1509).","Characteristic laboratory features: leukocytosis peaking at the second-to-third week with >10% atypical lymphocytes (enlarged cells with abundant cytoplasm, predominantly CD8+ T cells), often with mild neutropenia and thrombocytopenia (Harrison 22e p.1509).","Abnormal liver function (mildly elevated aminotransferases and alkaline phosphatase) occurs in >90% of cases, with hyperbilirubinemia in about 40% (Harrison 22e p.1509).","The heterophile (and the somewhat more sensitive Monospot) test is the standard diagnostic test in children and adults; heterophile antibodies do not interact with EBV proteins. Tests are positive in ~40% in week 1 and 80-90% by week 3, so repeat testing may be needed if performed early (Harrison 22e p.1511).","Heterophile antibodies are often absent in children under 5 years, the elderly, and patients with atypical presentations; in these cases EBV-specific serology should be used (Harrison 22e p.1511).","EBV-specific serology pattern: IgM antibody to viral capsid antigen (VCA) is most useful for acute IM (present only during the first 2-3 months); IgG anti-VCA persists for life and indicates past exposure; antibodies to EBNA appear late (3-6 weeks after symptom onset) and persist for life (Harrison 22e p.1511).","Most cases of IM are self-limited; most patients have symptoms for 2-4 weeks, but nearly 10% have fatigue persisting for 6 months or more (Harrison 22e p.1509).","EBV is associated with several malignancies, including Burkitt's lymphoma (~90% of African cases, ~15% of US cases), anaplastic nasopharyngeal carcinoma (uniformly EBV-associated in southern China), gastric carcinoma (the most common EBV-associated malignancy worldwide), Hodgkin's lymphoma (especially mixed-cellularity type), and various T-/NK-cell and CNS lymphomas; EBV-driven lymphoproliferative disease occurs in immunodeficient and transplant patients (Harrison 22e p.1509-1511).","Large epidemiologic data show a strong association of EBV with multiple sclerosis; one study of over a million military personnel found a 32-fold higher MS risk after primary EBV infection, though the causal role still requires further research (Harrison 22e p.1508, 1511)."],"pathophysiology":"EBV, a herpesvirus, is transmitted in saliva and infects oropharyngeal and salivary epithelium plus B lymphocytes (including tonsillar-crypt lymphocytes directly), then disseminates via the bloodstream. Proliferation of EBV-infected B cells alongside reactive T cells enlarges lymphoid tissue. During acute IM up to 1 in 100 peripheral B cells is infected (falling to 1-50 per million after recovery), and there is an inverted CD4+/CD8+ ratio with large clonal CD8+ T-cell expansions (up to 40% directed against EBV antigens). The EBV receptor on B cells is CD21 (the C3d complement receptor); HLA class II is a co-receptor for B-cell entry, while epithelial entry uses ephrin A2. Memory B cells (not epithelial cells) are the lifelong reservoir. Cellular immunity (NK cells, cytotoxic T cells recognizing EBNAs and LMPs) is more important than humoral immunity in control; if T-cell immunity is impaired, infected B cells may proliferate, and in EBV-associated tumors LMP-1 mimics TNF-receptor-family signaling (e.g., CD40) to drive growth (Harrison 22e p.1508-1509).","additionalDifferentials":["CMV infection (the most common cause of heterophile-negative mononucleosis; older age, longer fever, exudative pharyngitis uncommon) (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Acute HIV infection (diffuse rash, oral/genital ulcers, aseptic meningitis) (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Toxoplasmosis (less splenomegaly; exposure to cats or raw meat) (Harrison 22e p.1511)","HHV-6 infection (older age at presentation) (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Streptococcal pharyngitis (no splenomegaly, less fatigue) (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Viral hepatitis (higher aminotransferase levels) (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Rubella (maculopapular rash, no splenomegaly) (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Lymphoma (fixed, nontender lymph nodes) (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Drug reaction, most often to phenytoin, carbamazepine, sulfonamides, or minocycline (can occur at any age) (Harrison 22e p.1511)"],"infectionMimics":["CMV mononucleosis (heterophile-negative) (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Acute (primary) HIV infection (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Toxoplasmosis (Harrison 22e p.1511)","HHV-6 infection (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Streptococcal pharyngitis (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Viral hepatitis (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Rubella (Harrison 22e p.1511)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Lymphoma (fixed, nontender nodes; atypical lymphocytes) (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Drug-induced mononucleosis-like reaction (phenytoin, carbamazepine, sulfonamides, minocycline) (Harrison 22e p.1511)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Heterophile antibody test (titer >=40 diagnostic with compatible symptoms and atypical lymphocytes), or the more sensitive commercial Monospot test (~75% sensitive, ~90% specific vs EBV-specific serology) (Harrison 22e p.1511)","EBV-specific serology: IgM and IgG anti-VCA, anti-EBNA, and (less useful for routine diagnosis) anti-early antigen (EA-D, EA-R) (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Peripheral blood examination for atypical lymphocytes and CBC (leukocytosis, possible neutropenia/thrombocytopenia) (Harrison 22e p.1509)","Liver function tests (aminotransferases, alkaline phosphatase, bilirubin) (Harrison 22e p.1509)","EBV DNA detection by PCR in blood or CSF (useful when serology is equivocal, in CNS lymphoma in AIDS, and for monitoring lymphoproliferative disease); throat or blood culture is NOT helpful because EBV persists for life in the oropharynx and B cells (Harrison 22e p.1511)"],"redFlags":["Splenic rupture (occurs in <0.5% of cases, more common in males; may present as abdominal pain, referred shoulder pain, or hemodynamic compromise) - a leading cause of the rare deaths from IM (Harrison 22e p.1510)","Upper-airway obstruction from tonsillar/adenoidal hypertrophy or epiglottic/pharyngeal/uvular edema (Harrison 22e p.1510)","CNS complications (meningitis, encephalitis, cerebellar ataxia, cranial nerve palsies especially CN VII, Guillain-Barre syndrome, transverse myelitis) - usually within the first 2 weeks (Harrison 22e p.1510)","Autoimmune hemolytic anemia (Coombs-positive, cold agglutinins to red-cell i antigen, ~2% of cases) (Harrison 22e p.1510)","Hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis, severe cytopenias (red-cell aplasia, severe granulocytopenia, thrombocytopenia, pancytopenia) (Harrison 22e p.1510)","Fulminant hepatitis, myocarditis/pericarditis, pneumonia with pleural effusion (Harrison 22e p.1510)","Fatal IM in patients with X-linked lymphoproliferative disease or other primary immunodeficiencies - and rarely in patients with no obvious preexisting immune defect (Harrison 22e p.1510)"],"prognosis":"Most cases of IM are self-limited and resolve over 2-4 weeks; deaths are very rare and are most often due to CNS complications, splenic rupture, upper-airway obstruction, or bacterial superinfection. Nearly 10% of patients have fatigue persisting for 6 months or more. Disease severity correlates with blood CD8+ T-cell and EBV DNA levels. Most neurologic complications resolve without sequelae. The risk of EBV-positive Hodgkin's lymphoma is significantly increased in young adults for several years after EBV-seropositive IM (Harrison 22e p.1509-1511).","pitfalls":["Ampicillin/penicillin rash pitfall: about 95% of patients with infectious mononucleosis given ampicillin develop a rash; classically a macular rash followed penicillin-derivative use, although more recent studies suggest EBV-associated rashes may occur at similar frequency with or without penicillin exposure, and these rashes are not predictive of future penicillin reactions (Harrison 22e p.1509, 1513).","Heterophile/Monospot tests are often falsely negative early in illness (positive in only ~40% during week 1) and are unreliable in children under 5, the elderly, and atypical presentations - repeat testing or EBV-specific serology may be required (Harrison 22e p.1511).","False-positive Monospot results are more common in connective tissue disease, lymphoma, viral hepatitis, and malaria (Harrison 22e p.1511).","Culture of EBV from throat washings or blood does NOT diagnose acute infection because the virus persists lifelong in the oropharynx and B cells (Harrison 22e p.1511).","Glucocorticoids are NOT indicated for uncomplicated IM and may predispose to bacterial superinfection (Harrison 22e p.1512).","Acyclovir has had no significant clinical impact on IM in controlled trials (Harrison 22e p.1512).","Persistent EBV infection is NOT a cause of chronic fatigue syndrome; antibody titers in such patients are not significantly different from healthy seropositive adults and EBV DNA is not elevated (Harrison 22e p.1510-1511)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.1508-1514"],"crossLinks":[]},"inflammatory_bowel_disease":{"name":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":337,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2551-2563","clinicalPearls":["IBD is a chronic, idiopathic inflammatory disease of the GI tract; ulcerative colitis (UC) and Crohn's disease (CD) are its two major types (Harrison 22e p.2551).","UC is a mucosal disease that almost always involves the rectum and extends proximally in continuity, without intervening areas of uninvolved (skip) mucosa; ~40-50% have disease limited to rectum/rectosigmoid and ~20% have pancolitis (Harrison 22e p.2555).","CD can affect any part of the GI tract from mouth to anus, is segmental with skip areas, and characteristically spares the rectum (unlike UC); the terminal ileum is involved in ~90% of those with small-intestinal disease (Harrison 22e p.2556).","CD is a transmural process producing fistulas, strictures, abscesses and an endoscopic/radiographic 'cobblestone' appearance, whereas UC inflammation is limited to mucosa and superficial submucosa except in fulminant disease (Harrison 22e p.2555-2556).","Noncaseating granulomas are a characteristic histologic feature of CD but are found less often on mucosal biopsy than on surgical resection specimens; UC shows crypt distortion, basal plasmacytosis, cryptitis and crypt abscesses (Harrison 22e p.2556).","Major UC symptoms are diarrhoea, rectal bleeding, tenesmus, mucus passage and crampy abdominal pain; severe pain is not prominent in UC, whereas CD ileocolitis classically causes right-lower-quadrant pain and diarrhoea, sometimes mimicking acute appendicitis (Harrison 22e p.2556-2557).","Perianal disease (perirectal fistulas, fissures, abscesses, anal stenosis, skin tags) occurs in about one-third of CD patients and points away from UC; extensive perianal lesions in apparent UC should suggest CD (Harrison 22e p.2556, p.2557).","Faecal calprotectin and faecal lactoferrin are neutrophil-derived markers that correlate with histologic inflammation, help distinguish IBD from irritable bowel syndrome, predict relapse and detect pouchitis; calprotectin is more sensitive for ileocolonic/colonic than isolated ileal inflammation (Harrison 22e p.2557, p.2558).","Distinguishing UC from CD is impossible initially in up to 15% of cases (indeterminate colitis); ~5% of resection specimens cannot be classified due to overlapping features (Harrison 22e p.2559).","Up to one-third of IBD patients have at least one extraintestinal manifestation; some parallel bowel activity (erythema nodosum, peripheral arthritis, episcleritis) while others run an independent course (pyoderma gangrenosum, ankylosing spondylitis, sacroiliitis, uveitis, PSC) (Harrison 22e p.2561-2563).","Smoking has opposite associations in the two diseases: it may be protective in UC (OR 0.58) but is a risk factor for CD (OR 1.76); prior appendectomy with confirmed appendicitis is protective against UC but not CD (Harrison 22e p.2551-2552).","Patients with long-standing extensive colitis (UC or Crohn's colitis) have an increased risk of colonic dysplasia and carcinoma; surveillance colonoscopy with multiple biopsies is the standard of care (Harrison 22e p.2612-2613)."],"pathophysiology":"IBD is regarded as an inappropriate, dysregulated mucosal immune response to the endogenous commensal microbiota (with or without an autoimmune component) in a genetically susceptible host, shaped by environmental factors. Homeostasis among the commensal microbiota, intestinal epithelial cells and mucosal immune cells is disrupted, producing chronic inflammation. More than ~240 genetic loci (e.g. NOD2, ATG16L1, IL23R, IL12B, IL10, XBP1) contribute incrementally and affect pathways of innate immunity, autophagy/unfolded-protein response, adaptive immunity and inflammation/healing; ~two-thirds are shared between CD and UC. Dysbiosis (e.g. expansion of adherent/invasive E. coli, loss of Faecalibacterium prausnitzii) and defective oral (mucosal) tolerance lead to unrestrained CD4+ T-cell (TH1/TH2/TH17) and innate immune activation, with a self-perpetuating cytokine cascade (IL-1, IL-6, IL-12, IL-23, TNF) driving fibrogenesis, leukocyte recruitment and tissue damage. TH1-driven transmural granulomatous inflammation resembles CD; TH2-type superficial mucosal inflammation resembles UC. Roughly 5-10% of patients have familial disease and only ~20% of disease risk is currently explained by known genetics (Harrison 22e p.2551-2555).","additionalDifferentials":["Indeterminate colitis (overlapping UC/CD features in up to 15% of cases)","Diverticulitis / diverticular-associated colitis (mimics Crohn's colitis)","Ischemic colitis (segmental like CD or diffuse like UC)","Radiation colitis/enteritis","Collagenous and lymphocytic (microscopic) colitis","Diversion colitis","Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome","Immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI)-related colitis","NSAID-associated colitis","Celiac disease (especially in lymphocytic colitis, which should prompt exclusion)","Eosinophilic gastroenteritis, Behcet's syndrome, graft-versus-host disease, neutropenic colitis"],"infectionMimics":["Campylobacter colitis (can mimic severe UC and trigger UC relapse)","Salmonella, Shigella enterocolitis","Yersinia enterocolitica (terminal ileal ulceration mimicking Crohn's ileitis)","Clostridioides difficile colitis","Toxigenic / enteroinvasive / enteroadherent E. coli","Gonorrhoea, Chlamydia trachomatis and syphilis proctitis","Intestinal tuberculosis and Mycobacterium avium (ileocecal involvement)","Cytomegalovirus and herpes simplex colitis/proctitis (may occur even in immunocompetent hosts)","Entamoeba histolytica (amebiasis) - must be excluded before treating presumed IBD","Other parasites: Isospora belli, Trichuris trichiura, hookworm, Strongyloides","Fungal: histoplasmosis, Candida, Aspergillus (mainly in immunocompromised)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Diverticulitis","Ischemic colitis","Radiation colitis/enteritis","Collagenous / lymphocytic (microscopic) colitis","Diversion colitis","Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome","NSAID-induced colitis","Immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI)-related colitis","Drug/chemical colitis (phosphosoda, gold, mycophenolate mofetil, cocaine, oral contraceptives)","Neoplasia (lymphoma, carcinoma of colon/ileum, carcinoid, metastatic disease, familial polyposis)","Appendicitis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Acute-phase reactants (CRP, ESR), platelet count, haemoglobin and serum albumin to gauge activity","Faecal calprotectin and faecal lactoferrin (sensitive markers of intestinal inflammation; distinguish IBD from IBS, predict relapse, detect pouchitis and postoperative recurrence)","Stool studies: bacterial culture, C. difficile toxin, ova and parasites, viral studies to exclude infective colitis","Sigmoidoscopy (to assess activity before treatment) and colonoscopy with multiple biopsies, including terminal ileum","Histology of rectal/colonic biopsy specimens","Wireless capsule endoscopy for small-bowel CD (avoid if stricture suspected; consider patency capsule first)","CT enterography or MR enterography (MR preferred in young patients/serial imaging to avoid radiation)","Pelvic MRI for perianal/ischiorectal fistulas and abscesses","Small-bowel ultrasound and intestinal ultrasound for assessing activity and mucosal healing","MRCP (or ERCP) when primary sclerosing cholangitis is suspected","Serologic markers ASCA and pANCA (limited clinical utility; less helpful than clinical factors)"],"redFlags":["Toxic megacolon (transverse or right colon diameter >6 cm with loss of haustration in a severe attack) - may be precipitated by electrolyte abnormalities and narcotics; risk of perforation","Perforation - most dangerous local complication; peritoneal signs may be masked by glucocorticoids; mortality of perforation complicating toxic megacolon is ~15%","Massive GI haemorrhage","Bowel obstruction from fibrostenotic stricture (CD)","Intraabdominal or pelvic abscess and fistula formation (CD); high-spiking fever suggests abscess","Stricture impassable to the colonoscope - presume malignant until proven otherwise","New or worsening dysplasia/carcinoma on surveillance in long-standing colitis"],"prognosis":"IBD is a lifelong chronic disease with a relapsing-remitting course; only ~15% of UC patients present initially with severe disease. Massive haemorrhage occurs in ~1% of UC patients and toxic megacolon is rare (about 50% of acute dilations resolve with conservative management, the remainder requiring urgent colectomy). In CD the inflammatory process tends to evolve toward a fibrostenotic obstructing pattern or a penetrating fistulous pattern; intestinal obstruction occurs in ~40%, intraabdominal/pelvic abscesses in 10-30%, and free perforation in 1-2%. Long-standing extensive colitis carries an increased colorectal cancer risk (UC meta-analysis figures of ~2% at 10 years, 8% at 20 years, 18% at 30 years, though rates have fallen with better inflammation control and surveillance; overall colorectal cancer rates remain ~1.5-2 times the general population). CD and UC cancer risks are probably equivalent for similar extent and duration. Associated PSC carries a 7-10% risk of cholangiocarcinoma (Harrison 22e p.2557-2563, p.2612-2613).","pitfalls":["Failing to exclude infective colitis (especially C. difficile, Campylobacter, Salmonella, Shigella, E. coli, CMV) and amebiasis before diagnosing or escalating treatment of IBD - infection can both mimic IBD and trigger relapse (Harrison 22e p.2559-2561).","In UC, biopsies of normal-appearing mucosa are usually abnormal, so multiple biopsies should be taken from apparently uninvolved proximal and distal mucosa; effective medical therapy can also create pseudo-skip areas (Harrison 22e p.2555).","Peritoneal signs of perforation may be absent or subtle in patients on glucocorticoids (Harrison 22e p.2557).","A colonic stricture impassable with the colonoscope should be presumed malignant until proven otherwise (Harrison 22e p.2557).","Extensive perianal lesions in apparent UC should raise suspicion for Crohn's disease (Harrison 22e p.2557).","Wireless capsule endoscopy must be avoided when a small-bowel stricture is suspected because of the risk of capsule retention (Harrison 22e p.2558).","Serologic tests (ASCA, pANCA) have variable predictive value and unclear clinical usefulness for distinguishing or predicting IBD (Harrison 22e p.2559).","Pyoderma gangrenosum and ankylosing spondylitis run a course independent of bowel disease and do not reliably improve with colectomy (Harrison 22e p.2561-2562)."],"severityClassification":"Ulcerative colitis is graded by the Montreal classification of extent (E1 proctitis; E2 left-sided/distal UC, distal to splenic flexure; E3 extensive/pancolitis, proximal to splenic flexure) and severity (S0 clinical remission; S1 mild: <=4 stools/day with or without blood, no systemic illness, normal ESR; S2 moderate: >=4 stools/day with minimal systemic toxicity; S3 severe: >=6 bloody stools/day, pulse >=90/min, temperature >=37.5C, haemoglobin <10.5 g/100 mL, ESR >=30 mm/h). Crohn's disease is classified by the Vienna and Montreal systems by age at diagnosis (A1/A2/A3), location (L1 ileal, L2 colonic, L3 ileocolonic, L4 upper/foregut) and behaviour (B1 nonstricturing-nonpenetrating, B2 stricturing, B3 penetrating), with a 'p' modifier for perianal disease (Harrison 22e p.2557-2558).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2551-2563"],"crossLinks":["ibd_flare","colorectal_cancer"]},"inflammatory_myopathies":{"name":"Inflammatory Myopathies","system":"Rheumatology / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":377,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2909","clinicalPearls":["Major idiopathic inflammatory myopathies (IMs) are dermatomyositis (DM), polymyositis (PM), immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy (IMNM), antisynthetase syndrome (ASyS), and inclusion body myositis (IBM); idiopathic PM without an overlap syndrome is quite rare, while DM, ASyS, IBM, and IMNM occur in roughly similar frequencies (Harrison 22e p.2909).","Most IMs (DM, PM, IMNM, ASyS) present with symmetric proximal-greater-than-distal weakness; DM additionally has a characteristic rash and PM/IMNM/ASyS do not (DM rash, ASyS rash sometimes) (Harrison 22e p.2909-2910).","Serum creatine kinase (CK) is the most sensitive laboratory marker of muscle destruction; a markedly elevated CK (e.g. >2000 U/L) is almost always due to a myopathy, and CK is always elevated in uncontrolled PM and markedly elevated (usually >10x normal) in IMNM (Harrison 22e p.2910-2912).","DM rash includes the heliotrope rash (violaceous eyelid discoloration with periorbital edema), Gottron sign and Gottron papules over extensor joint surfaces, V-sign (anterior neck/chest), shawl sign (posterior neck/shoulders), nail-bed telangiectasias, and subcutaneous calcium deposits; a heliotrope rash with Gottron papules and dilated nailfold capillaries is diagnostic for DM (Harrison 22e p.2910-2911).","Myositis-specific antibodies (MSAs) help define subtypes: DM (anti-Mi-2, anti-TIF1-gamma, anti-MDA5, anti-NXP-2, anti-SAE); IMNM (anti-HMGCR, anti-SRP); ASyS (antisynthetase antibodies, most commonly anti-Jo-1); IBM (anti-cN-1A) (Harrison 22e p.2909-2914).","EMG/nerve conduction studies help localize the lesion to muscle but are nonspecific for cause; muscle MRI shows edema, atrophy, or fatty replacement and can show subtype-specific patterns (e.g. IBM spares the rectus femoris while involving vastus medialis/lateralis) (Harrison 22e p.2910-2914).","A muscle biopsy is often required to distinguish myopathies when there is no characteristic DM rash or MSA, and should be performed in every case of suspected PM to exclude IBM and other causes; biopsy a clinically affected but not too-weak muscle (e.g. MRC grade 4/5) to avoid sampling only end-stage muscle (Harrison 22e p.2910-2911).","Patients with severe muscle pain, subjective weakness, and fatigue but normal strength/function on exam are not likely to have an IM; consider polymyalgia rheumatica in older patients with raised ESR/CRP but normal CK and EMG, and fibromyalgia when the lab workup is normal (Harrison 22e p.2911).","Overlap syndrome describes an IM associated with another connective tissue disease (scleroderma, MCTD, Sjogren syndrome, SLE, rheumatoid arthritis); overlap syndromes are usually responsive to immunotherapy, except Sjogren syndrome with coexisting IBM (Harrison 22e p.2912).","DM, PM, ASyS, and IMNM are typically responsive to immunotherapy with high-dose glucocorticoids as first-line; IBM does NOT typically respond to any known immunotherapy and is managed with physical/occupational and swallowing therapy (Harrison 22e p.2915-2916)."],"pathophysiology":"The inflammatory myopathies are immune-mediated muscle diseases with distinct mechanisms by subtype. DM microvasculopathy and skin/muscle damage are increasingly attributed to type I interferon (IFN)-mediated toxicity, most likely IFN-beta, rather than the older model of antibody/complement-mediated capillary destruction with watershed ischemia; immunoglobulin deposition on endothelium is largely absent and complement deposition may be secondary, and there is increasing evidence MSAs are directly pathogenic (e.g. anti-Mi-2 entering myonuclei to inhibit the CHD4/NuRD complex). PM is heterogeneous with poorly understood pathogenesis; its infiltrate is predominantly CD8+ T cells and macrophages in endomysial, perimysial, and perivascular regions. IMNM shows multifocal necrotic and regenerating fibers with few inflammatory cells; it does not appear primarily complement-driven (complement inhibition failed in anti-HMGCR and anti-SRP disease), anti-HMGCR antibodies may bind sarcolemmal receptor causing acetyl-CoA and lipid accumulation, and anti-SRP antibodies appear directly causal. ASyS resembles DM histologically but with more perifascicular necrosis and HLA-DR (MHC-II) expression suggesting gamma-IFN rather than type I IFN drive. IBM combines adaptive immune attack (highly differentiated CD8+CD57+ KLRG1+ T cells invading non-necrotic MHC-I/II-expressing fibers, plus a relatively specific autoantibody) with degenerative features including rimmed vacuoles, beta-sheet amyloid inclusions, p62/LC3 aggregates and ER stress/autophagy, mitochondrial abnormality (ragged-red and COX-negative fibers), and extrusion of TDP-43 from myonuclei; whether damage is primarily immune or a secondary neurodegenerative process is unclear.","additionalDifferentials":["Inclusion body myositis (IBM) - must exclude in every suspected PM case","Polymyalgia rheumatica (older patient, raised ESR/CRP, normal CK and EMG)","Fibromyalgia (normal laboratory workup)","Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy (can be mimicked by anti-HMGCR myopathy or anti-SRP/seronegative IMNM)","Muscular dystrophies and metabolic myopathies (may show inflammatory infiltrate on biopsy)","Sporadic late-onset nemaline myopathy (SLONM)","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis / neurogenic process (atrophy with fasciculations)","Myasthenia gravis / neuromuscular junction defect (fatigable weakness)","Spinal cord disorder or polyneuropathy (concomitant sensory symptoms)"],"infectionMimics":["Infection-associated myositis (listed by Harrison among other inflammatory myopathies caused by infection)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Toxic myopathies, including the more common toxic statin-associated myopathy which (unlike anti-HMGCR IMNM) improves on statin discontinuation","Checkpoint inhibitor-triggered myositis","Eosinophilic myositis","Granulomatous myositis (and sarcoidosis, associated with IBM)","Hereditary myopathies (muscular dystrophies, metabolic myopathies) with secondary inflammation","Pathogenic biallelic HMGCR gene variants causing proximal weakness, myalgia, high CK and dystrophic changes"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase (most sensitive marker of muscle destruction); serum aldolase if CK normal (raised in ~10% of normal-CK DM)","Myositis-specific and myositis-associated autoantibody panel (anti-Mi-2, anti-TIF1-gamma, anti-MDA5, anti-NXP-2, anti-SAE; anti-HMGCR, anti-SRP; antisynthetase incl. anti-Jo-1; anti-cN-1A)","Antinuclear antibody (can be positive but nonspecific)","Electromyography and nerve conduction studies (localize to muscle; guide biopsy site)","Skeletal muscle MRI (STIR) for edema/inflammation, atrophy, fatty replacement, fasciitis and subtype-specific patterns","Muscle biopsy with experienced histopathology lab (perifascicular atrophy and MxA staining in DM; endomysial CD8/MHC-I in PM; necrotizing pattern in IMNM; perimysial fragmentation/alkaline phosphatase in ASyS; rimmed vacuoles, p62, COX-negative fibers and 15-21 nm tubulofilaments in IBM)","Skin biopsy in DM (cell-poor interface dermatitis)","Pulmonary function tests and spiral chest CT (honeycomb pattern) for interstitial lung disease","Flow cytometry for large granular lymphocytes and CD4/CD8 ratio in IBM","Malignancy workup (especially adult DM, anti-TIF1-gamma, anti-NXP-2, anti-SAE, anti-HMGCR myopathy)"],"redFlags":["Dermatomyositis rash (heliotrope rash, Gottron sign/papules) - adult-onset DM carries ~15% malignancy risk within the first 2-3 years; enhanced cancer vigilance especially with anti-TIF1-gamma (89% specificity, 70% sensitivity for cancer-associated DM)","Anti-MDA5 antibodies - palmar rash, severe ischemic skin ulceration, and rapidly progressive interstitial lung disease (also frequent in amyopathic DM)","Dyspnea from ILD, bronchopneumonia, alveolitis, or ventilatory muscle weakness","Dysphagia and dysarthria (aspiration risk; common in IBM and seen in DM/IMNM)","Myocarditis / cardiac disease (poor prognostic feature)","Markedly elevated CK (e.g. >2000 U/L) - almost always indicates a myopathy"],"prognosis":"In the absence of malignancy, DM prognosis is generally favorable with 5-year survival of 70-93%; poor prognostic features (DM and PM) are increased age, associated ILD, cardiac disease, and late or previously inadequate treatment. Most PM patients improve with immunotherapy but usually require lifelong treatment. IMNM varies by subtype: anti-HMGCR myopathy is often responsive to IVIG monotherapy, whereas anti-SRP and seronegative IMNM are much harder to treat and need aggressive immunotherapy; increased cancer incidence may exist in anti-HMGCR myopathy. ASyS patients mostly respond but disease can be difficult to treat (especially with ILD, where early rituximab is warranted) with no apparent increased malignancy risk. IBM is slowly progressive and not typically responsive to immunotherapy; most patients require a scooter or wheelchair within ~10-15 years of symptom onset, with no known increased malignancy risk.","pitfalls":["IBM is frequently misdiagnosed and is distinguished from PM by distal/asymmetric weakness with predilection for finger/wrist flexors and quadriceps in patients >50, normal or only mildly elevated CK, and being essentially steroid-refractory and unresponsive to any known immunotherapy - so a normal CK should alert clinicians to the possibility of IBM","Many older series of 'PM' likely actually included patients with IBM, IMNM, or muscular dystrophies with inflammation, biasing reported treatment response and cancer-risk data","Perifascicular atrophy (the classic DM biopsy finding) is present in perhaps only 50% of DM patients; MxA immunostaining is diagnostically more sensitive and highly specific","Rimmed vacuoles may be absent in up to 20-30% of IBM biopsies; mitochondrial abnormality (ragged-red/COX-negative fibers) and p62 inclusions help distinguish IBM from PM in such cases","Anti-HMGCR myopathy does NOT improve when statins are stopped (unlike common toxic statin myopathy) and can occur in statin-naive children/young adults, mimicking limb-girdle muscular dystrophy","IBM EMG can show large-amplitude long-duration motor units that may be misinterpreted as neurogenic but actually reflect chronicity of the myopathy","Muscle biopsy should sample a clinically affected but not too-weak muscle (e.g. MRC 4/5); biopsying an end-stage muscle yields non-diagnostic tissue"],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal numeric severity grading scale for the inflammatory myopathies. Disease is characterized instead by clinical subtype (DM, PM, IMNM, ASyS, IBM), pattern and degree of weakness (graded clinically, e.g. MRC strength), serum CK elevation, presence of organ involvement (ILD, myocarditis, dysphagia), associated malignancy, and responsiveness to immunotherapy. Treatment intensity (first-line high-dose glucocorticoids; early second-line agents for severe weakness, organ involvement such as myocarditis/ILD, steroid-complication risk, or IMNM) reflects severity stratification in practice.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2909"],"crossLinks":["ild"]},"influenza":{"name":"Influenza","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":206,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1544","clinicalPearls":["Influenza presents with an abrupt febrile illness: fever, myalgias, fatigue, sore throat, headache, and cough (Harrison 22e p.1576).","Influenza A viruses are the most virulent for humans and cause most severe disease, though type B viruses (Victoria lineage) also cause substantial morbidity; type A is subdivided by hemagglutinin (H) and neuraminidase (N) surface protein subtypes (Harrison 22e p.1576).","Two type A subtypes (H1N1 and H3N2) and one type B lineage (Victoria) cause annual seasonal epidemics, which occur in winter in temperate regions (Harrison 22e p.1576, p.1579).","Influenza pneumonia has an unusually high rate of complication by bacterial superinfection, with staphylococcal and streptococcal pneumonia occurring in as many as 10% of cases in some series (Harrison 22e p.1576).","Antiviral treatment of influenza is effective only when given early in the course of illness; baloxavir marboxil is approved for acute uncomplicated flu when started within 2 days of onset (Harrison 22e p.1537, p.1580).","Antibiotics do NOT improve the outcome of uncomplicated viral respiratory infection in otherwise healthy people, but influenza can be complicated by secondary bacterial infection that does warrant antibacterials (Harrison 22e p.1537).","Annual vaccine reformulation is required because antigenic drift (point mutations in HA and NA) causes antigenic divergence; an occasional antigenic mismatch can render a vaccine component of negligible efficacy (Harrison 22e p.1538)."],"pathophysiology":"Influenza is a single-stranded, segmented, negative-sense RNA genome virus of the family Orthomyxoviridae, with four (sero)types: A, B, C, and D. Influenza A and C infect multiple species; B infects humans almost exclusively; D primarily infects cattle and is not known to infect humans. Influenza A is subdivided into 18 hemagglutinin (H) and 11 neuraminidase (N) surface-protein subtypes. Two distinct mechanisms drive antigenic variation: antigenic drift (accumulation of point mutations in the HA and NA molecules) produces the gradual divergence underlying annual epidemics and requires new vaccines each year; antigenic shift, enabled by the segmented genome allowing reassortment between two viruses co-infecting one host, can produce a major novel virus and a pandemic. Type B viruses mutate less frequently than type A, probably reflecting their near-exclusive human host range. Many highly pathogenic strains circulate in aquatic birds; pigs, which carry both avian and human receptors, can serve as a mixing vessel for reassortment that makes zoonotic viruses more fit for human replication (Harrison 22e p.1576).","additionalDifferentials":["COVID-19 (SARS-CoV-2) respiratory illness","Respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) infection","Human metapneumovirus infection","Parainfluenza virus infection","Rhinovirus / common cold","Adenovirus infection","Acute bronchitis"],"infectionMimics":["SARS-CoV-2 (COVID-19)","Respiratory syncytial virus","Human metapneumovirus","Parainfluenza virus","Adenovirus","Rhinovirus","Community-acquired bacterial pneumonia (e.g., S. pneumoniae)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute exacerbation of COPD","Acute exacerbation of chronic bronchitis","Decompensated cardiovascular disease presenting with fever and dyspnea"],"additionalInvestigations":["RT-PCR molecular testing of respiratory secretions (most sensitive and specific; multiplex respiratory panels available)","Rapid antigen-based diagnostic test for influenza viral protein in nasopharyngeal secretions","Viral isolation by cell culture in a reference laboratory (gold standard but insensitive/slow)","Direct or indirect fluorescent antibody detection of virus-infected cells in nasal secretions","Chest radiograph for all patients with suspected pneumonia"],"redFlags":["Secondary bacterial pneumonia complicating influenza, classically caused by S. aureus (including MRSA, with risk of necrotizing pneumonia) and S. pneumoniae/S. pyogenes","Severe or fatal primary viral pneumonia, particularly in elderly patients, nursing-home residents, immunocompromised persons, and very young children","Hypoxia or respiratory failure requiring supplemental oxygen or mechanical ventilation","Avian/zoonotic strains (e.g., H5N1, H7N9) with high mortality and pandemic potential, especially with relevant exposure history"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated influenza in otherwise healthy adults is generally self-limited, and antiviral therapy started early shortens illness. Severe and even fatal pneumonia occurs particularly in elderly patients, nursing-home residents, immunocompromised persons, and very young children. Bacterial superinfection complicates as many as 10% of influenza pneumonia cases in some series. Pandemics caused by novel viruses occur on average every 20-30 years (Harrison 22e p.1576, p.1538).","pitfalls":["RT-PCR is extremely sensitive: viral genome can persist in nasal secretions for weeks after an infection resolves, so a positive test may reflect a recently resolved rather than a currently acute infection, and laboratory contamination can produce false positives (Harrison 22e p.1537).","Rapid diagnostic tests have the highest positive predictive value when applied to high-risk, appropriately seasonal samples; indiscriminate testing lowers predictive value (Harrison 22e p.1537).","Adamantanes (amantadine, rimantadine) should not be relied upon: widespread resistance has occurred in many currently circulating influenza A viruses (Harrison 22e p.1580).","Antiviral therapy started late is of limited benefit, as it is effective mainly when given early in the course (Harrison 22e p.1537)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.1544","Harrison 22e p.1576","Harrison 22e p.1537","Harrison 22e p.1538","Harrison 22e p.1580"],"crossLinks":["CAP"]},"intestinal_nematode_infections":{"name":"Intestinal Nematode (Roundworm) Infections","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":239,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1814","clinicalPearls":["More than one billion people worldwide are infected with one or more intestinal nematode species; they are most common where sanitation and fecal-waste disposal are inadequate, especially resource-limited tropical and subtropical regions, but are seen with increasing frequency among immigrants and refugees to developed countries (p.1814).","The major intestinal roundworms are Ascaris lumbricoides, the hookworms (Necator americanus, Ancylostoma duodenale, A. ceylanicum), Strongyloides stercoralis, Trichuris trichiura (whipworm), and Enterobius vermicularis (pinworm) (p.1815).","Ascaris, Trichuris, and the hookworms require a soil phase for development, whereas S. stercoralis and E. vermicularis can be transmitted directly from person to person (p.1814).","Because most helminths cannot fully complete their life cycle within the same human host, heavy adult-worm burdens require repeated exposure; clinical disease (versus asymptomatic/subclinical infection) generally develops only with repeated exposure and is typically related to infection intensity (p.1814).","Eosinophilia and elevated serum IgE are features of many helminth infections, the latter particularly when the life cycle involves tissue migration (Ascaris, Strongyloides, hookworms) (p.1814).","Ascaris larval migration through the lungs (~9-12 days after egg ingestion) can cause an irritating nonproductive cough, substernal discomfort, eosinophilia, and transient rounded pulmonary infiltrates - eosinophilic pneumonitis (Loffler's syndrome) (p.1815).","Heavy Ascaris infection can cause small-bowel obstruction by a bolus of entangled worms (more common in children with narrow lumens), sometimes complicated by perforation, intussusception, or volvulus; an adult worm migrating into the biliary tree can cause biliary colic, cholecystitis, cholangitis, pancreatitis, or rarely intrahepatic abscess (p.1815).","In highly endemic areas, intestinal and biliary ascariasis can rival acute appendicitis as a cause of surgical acute abdomen (p.1815).","The major consequence of chronic hookworm infection is iron deficiency; symptoms are minimal if iron stores are adequate, but undernourished individuals develop progressive iron-deficiency anemia and hypoproteinemia (fatigue, weakness, dyspnea). Hookworm disease requires the combination of heavy worm burden, prolonged duration, and inadequate iron intake (p.1816).","Hookworm larvae may cause a pruritic maculopapular rash (ground itch) at the skin penetration site; hypochromic microcytic anemia, sometimes with eosinophilia or hypoalbuminemia, is characteristic of hookworm disease (p.1816).","S. stercoralis is distinguished by its ability - unique among helminths except Capillaria - to complete its life cycle and replicate within a human host (autoinfection), so infection can persist for decades without further exogenous exposure (p.1816).","Larva currens, a rapidly advancing (up to ~10 cm/h) pruritic serpiginous skin eruption, is pathognomonic of strongyloidiasis; recurrent urticaria, often on the buttocks, is the most common cutaneous manifestation (p.1816).","Impaired cell-mediated immunity - especially high-dose glucocorticoid therapy, and less commonly other immunosuppressants or HTLV-1 infection - can trigger Strongyloides hyperinfection with massive larval numbers, leading to colitis/enteritis/malabsorption and disseminated disease (CNS, skin, peritoneum, liver, kidneys) (p.1817).","Disseminated strongyloidiasis is often complicated by gram-negative bacteremia, pneumonia, or meningitis from enteric flora translocating through disrupted mucosal barriers; eosinophilia is often ABSENT in severely infected patients (p.1817).","Even asymptomatic strongyloidiasis must be treated because of the potential for later hyperinfection and fatal dissemination; Strongyloides serology should be performed before starting glucocorticoids or solid-organ transplantation in patients with prior potential exposure, especially with eosinophilia (p.1817).","Most Trichuris (whipworm) infections are asymptomatic, but heavy infections may cause anemia and bloody/mucoid diarrhea resembling inflammatory bowel disease; massive infection in children can cause rectal prolapse from protracted tenesmus (p.1818).","Enterobius (pinworm) is more common in temperate climates than the tropics; perianal pruritus, worse at night when gravid females emerge, is the cardinal symptom, and eosinophilia is uncommon (p.1818)."],"pathophysiology":"Intestinal nematodes are nonsegmented roundworms ranging from ~1 mm to many centimeters. Ascaris and the hookworms have life cycles involving migration through the heart and lungs before maturing in the intestine (larvae penetrate alveoli, ascend the bronchial tree, are swallowed, and reach the small bowel). Ascaris adults live in the small-intestinal lumen and females are extraordinarily fecund (>200,000 eggs/day); eggs are highly environmentally resistant and become infective after maturation in warm, moist soil. Hookworm adults attach to the small-bowel mucosa using buccal teeth (Ancylostoma) or cutting plates (Necator), invade submucosa, and ingest blood and villous tissue, producing iron-deficiency anemia; filariform larvae penetrate skin. S. stercoralis filariform larvae penetrate skin or mucous membranes, migrate via blood to the lungs and then the small intestine, where parthenogenetic adult females embed in the mucosa; rhabditiform larvae can transform directly into filariform larvae that re-penetrate the colonic wall or perianal skin to drive ongoing autoinfection - which can amplify into hyperinfection and dissemination under immunosuppression. Trichuris adults embed their thin anterior portions in colonic/cecal mucosa with no pulmonary larval passage. Enterobius adults dwell in the cecum; gravid females migrate nocturnally to the perianal skin to deposit eggs, which become infective within hours and spread by hand-to-mouth (autoinfection from scratching) and retroinfection. (p.1814-1818)","additionalDifferentials":["Strongyloides midepigastric pain resembles peptic ulcer disease, except it is aggravated by food ingestion (p.1816)","Heavy trichuriasis with bloody/mucoid diarrhea may resemble inflammatory bowel disease (p.1818)","Intestinal/biliary ascariasis may present as a surgical acute abdomen mimicking acute appendicitis in endemic areas (p.1815)"],"infectionMimics":["Strongyloides hyperinfection-associated gram-negative sepsis, pneumonia, or meningitis can dominate or be mistaken for primary bacterial infection (p.1817)","Hookworm rhabditiform larvae in non-fresh stool must be differentiated from Strongyloides larvae (p.1816)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Inflammatory bowel disease (heavy trichuriasis) (p.1818)","Peptic ulcer disease (strongyloidiasis epigastric pain) (p.1816)","Acute appendicitis / surgical acute abdomen (intestinal or biliary ascariasis) (p.1815)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool microscopy for characteristic ova and parasites: Ascaris eggs (~65 x 45 um), hookworm eggs (~40 x 60 um, species indistinguishable by light microscopy), Trichuris lemon-shaped eggs (~50 x 20 um) (p.1815-1818)","Strongyloides: rhabditiform larvae (~250 um, short buccal cavity) on stool microscopy; single stool detects only ~one-third of uncomplicated cases, so serial examinations and agar plate method improve sensitivity; duodenojejunal aspirate/biopsy; ELISA serum antibody serology (sensitive for uncomplicated infection); in dissemination, filariform larvae in stool, sputum, bronchoalveolar lavage, or surgical drainage fluid (p.1817)","Enterobius: eggs are NOT found in stool - diagnose with morning cellulose acetate tape applied to the perianal region (eggs oval, ~55 x 25 um, flattened on one side) (p.1818)","Ascaris lung phase: larvae detectable in sputum or gastric aspirates before eggs appear in stool; chest imaging showing transient rounded infiltrates (Loffler's syndrome) (p.1815)","PCR of stool worm DNA (increasing use, mostly research/some clinical) for species-level diagnosis (p.1815-1818)","Abdominal imaging: plain film for masses of worms in obstruction; ultrasound and ERCP for pancreaticobiliary Ascaris (ERCP can also extract worms) (p.1815)","Hookworm disease bloodwork: hypochromic microcytic anemia, sometimes eosinophilia or hypoalbuminemia (p.1816)"],"redFlags":["Disseminated strongyloidiasis - particularly in patients with unsuspected infection who are taking glucocorticoids - has a HIGH mortality rate (p.1817)","Strongyloides hyperinfection with gram-negative sepsis, pneumonia, or meningitis from enteric flora translocation (p.1817)","Eosinophilia is often ABSENT in severe/disseminated strongyloidiasis, removing a reassuring marker (p.1817)","Complete intestinal obstruction with perforation, intussusception, or volvulus from a heavy Ascaris worm bolus (p.1815)","Biliary Ascaris causing cholangitis, pancreatitis, or intrahepatic abscess (p.1815)"],"prognosis":"Intestinal nematode infections are not usually fatal but contribute to malnutrition, impaired physical and cognitive development, and diminished work capacity, particularly in children with marginal nutritional status. Significant protective immunity does not appear to develop in humans. The major exception is strongyloidiasis: autoinfection allows decades-long persistence, and in immunocompromised hosts hyperinfection and dissemination can be fatal (high mortality, especially with unrecognized infection on glucocorticoids). (p.1814, p.1816-1817)","pitfalls":["Failing to screen for and treat asymptomatic strongyloidiasis before glucocorticoids or solid-organ transplantation - asymptomatic infection must still be treated because of the risk of later hyperinfection and fatal dissemination (p.1817)","Relying on eosinophilia to exclude strongyloidiasis - it is often absent in severe/disseminated disease (p.1817)","Using routine stool ova-and-parasite microscopy to diagnose pinworm - Enterobius eggs are not passed in feces; perianal cellulose acetate tape is required (p.1818)","Single stool examination for Strongyloides detects only about one-third of uncomplicated cases (p.1817)","Misidentifying hookworm rhabditiform larvae (in non-fresh stool) as Strongyloides (p.1816)"],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal severity classification for intestinal nematode infections; severity correlates with infection intensity (worm burden) and host factors. Disease ranges from asymptomatic/subclinical light infection to clinical disease with heavy burden (e.g., Ascaris bowel/biliary obstruction; hookworm iron-deficiency anemia/hypoproteinemia; Trichuris dysentery and rectal prolapse). For Strongyloides, the spectrum is uncomplicated strongyloidiasis vs. hyperinfection (amplified autoinfection, larvae confined largely to the normal migration route) vs. disseminated strongyloidiasis (larvae in CNS, skin, peritoneum, liver, kidneys). (p.1814-1817)","references":["Harrison 22e p.1814"],"crossLinks":["iron_deficiency_anemia","bowel_obstruction"]},"iron_deficiency_anemia":{"name":"Iron Deficiency Anemia","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":102,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.764","clinicalPearls":["Classic blood picture is a microcytic, hypochromic anemia (MCV <80 fL in established disease), but microcytosis can be ABSENT in early iron deficiency or masked by liver disease or concomitant folate/B12 deficiency (Harrison 22e p.765, p.450).","Low serum ferritin is the mainstay of absolute iron deficiency, reflecting depleted stores; ferritin <30 µg/L has high sensitivity and specificity (92% and 98%) and can be used as a single diagnostic test (Harrison 22e p.765).","Transferrin saturation (serum iron/TIBC) measures iron available for erythropoiesis; erythropoiesis becomes iron-restricted at values <15-20% (Harrison 22e p.765).","Anemia develops only in the LATE stage because the erythron is relatively spared compared with heart and skeletal muscle, which become iron-depleted earlier; slow progression plus compensation means anemia is often undetected, especially in the young, until hemoglobin falls below ~8 g/dL (Harrison 22e p.765).","Clinical clues include pica (compulsive ingestion of nonnutritive substances such as ice, soil, or clay), nail fragility and koilonychia, hair loss, cheilosis, atrophic glossitis, and dysphagia from esophageal webs (Plummer-Vinson syndrome) (Harrison 22e p.765; Koilonychia association noted p.~).","Even symptomatic isolated iron deficiency WITHOUT anemia matters: suboptimal myocyte, cardiomyocyte, and neuron function can cause fatigue, irritability, poor concentration, restless legs syndrome, and reduced exercise capacity (Harrison 22e p.765).","Severe iron-deficiency anemia may be accompanied by thrombocytosis, due to prevalent megakaryocyte commitment of the common erythroid-platelet progenitor when iron is insufficient (Harrison 22e p.766).","Serum hepcidin is low/undetectable in absolute iron deficiency (maximizing dietary iron absorption) but elevated in inflammation and in IRIDA (TMPRSS6 mutations) (Harrison 22e p.766).","Inadequate therapeutic response (no reticulocytosis after 1 week, or <1 g/dL hemoglobin rise after 2 weeks of iron therapy) should prompt a search for the cause: noncompliance, ongoing blood loss, or malabsorption (Harrison 22e p.766)."],"pathophysiology":"Iron supply becomes inadequate for needs through excessive demand, insufficient intake, defective absorption, or blood loss; multiple causes frequently co-occur, especially in the elderly and in multimorbidity (Harrison 22e p.764). In ISOLATED iron deficiency, stores are depleted while hemoglobin is still normal; when iron remains sufficient for erythropoiesis, red cells are normochromic/normocytic with decreased ferritin (<15-30 µg/L). As transferrin saturation falls <15-20%, iron supply to erythropoiesis is insufficient and cells become hypochromic and microcytic, the classic feature of iron-deficiency anemia (Harrison 22e p.764). Iron deficiency is ABSOLUTE when stores are exhausted and FUNCTIONAL when erythropoiesis is iron-restricted but stores are adequate or even increased. Low hepcidin in iron deficiency maximizes dietary absorption; TMPRSS6 mutations cause hereditary iron-refractory IDA (IRIDA) with reduced absorption and oral-iron refractoriness. In inflammation, high hepcidin sequesters iron into stores, producing functional iron deficiency with low serum iron and transferrin saturation despite elevated ferritin; absolute and functional deficiency often coexist (IBD, CKD, aging). Beyond anemia, heart, muscle, and brain are affected; immune response may be blunted as lymphocytes require iron during the metabolic burst (Harrison 22e p.764).","additionalDifferentials":["Anemia of inflammation (anemia of chronic disease) - the second most common anemia worldwide, due to functional iron deficiency; a diagnosis of exclusion usually occurring with an inflammatory disorder such as cancer or infection (Harrison 22e p.765, p.450).","Thalassemia trait - microcytosis with normal-to-high ferritin and serum iron; red cells are more homogeneous in volume with a low/normal RDW (Harrison 22e p.450, p.767).","Mixed forms (combined absolute and functional iron deficiency) (Harrison 22e p.767).","Other nutritional anemias - vitamin B12 and folate deficiency (megaloblastic, macrocytic); may coexist with and mask iron deficiency in malabsorption (Harrison 22e p.765).","Sideroblastic anemia (rare) as a cause of microcytic hypochromic red cells (Harrison 22e p.~)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Anemia of inflammation / chronic disease (functional iron deficiency from high hepcidin: low serum iron and transferrin saturation with NORMAL-to-HIGH ferritin) (Harrison 22e p.764, p.767).","Thalassemia trait (microcytic but with normal-to-high ferritin and iron, and a low/normal RDW) (Harrison 22e p.450, p.767)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum ferritin (mainstay; <30 µg/L diagnostic, sensitivity 92% / specificity 98%); note ferritin is an acute-phase reactant and may rise severalfold in inflammation (Harrison 22e p.765, p.450).","Transferrin saturation (serum iron/TIBC); iron-restricted erythropoiesis at <15-20% (Harrison 22e p.765).","Serum iron (reduced, but diurnal fluctuation prevents use as a single test) (Harrison 22e p.765).","MCV and RDW from the CBC (MCV <80 fL when established; RDW often elevated reflecting anisocytosis) (Harrison 22e p.765).","Soluble transferrin receptor (sTfR) - increased in iron deficiency; sTfR/log ferritin ratio >2 favors iron deficiency (Harrison 22e p.766, p.767).","Reticulocyte hemoglobin content (CHr, reduced) and percentage of hypochromic red cells (%HYPO, increased >6%), reflecting iron availability days to months before testing (Harrison 22e p.766, p.767).","Serum hepcidin (low/undetectable in absolute deficiency; not routinely measured, reference values not standardized) (Harrison 22e p.766, p.767).","Absence of stainable iron in bone marrow remains the gold standard, although it is rarely needed and unacceptably invasive (Harrison 22e p.765).","Endoscopy and/or radiographic imaging to identify the cause - essential in males and postmenopausal females to rule out malignancy or other severe bleeding sources (Harrison 22e p.765, p.~)."],"redFlags":["Unexplained iron-deficiency anemia in any adult (with the possible exception of a premenopausal, multiparous woman) MANDATES thorough endoscopic and/or radiographic visualization of the entire large bowel - right (ascending) colon cancer causes insidious blood loss and often presents as IDA (Harrison 22e p.~, colorectal cancer chapter).","Imaging and endoscopy to trace the cause are essential in males and postmenopausal females to rule out malignancy or other severe causes of bleeding (Harrison 22e p.765).","Hemoglobin falling below ~8 g/dL or otherwise severe anemia (Harrison 22e p.765).","Myelophthisic blood smear (nucleated red cells, teardrop cells, immature white cells) suggesting marrow infiltration mandates marrow examination - a competing cause of anemia to recognize (Harrison 22e p.450)."],"prognosis":"Iron deficiency and IDA are highly treatable with iron replacement. Oral iron usually requires at least ~3 months of therapy to restore hemoglobin and replenish stores and prevent early recurrence; inadequate response (no reticulocytosis at 1 week or <1 g/dL hemoglobin rise at 2 weeks) should prompt a search for noncompliance, ongoing blood loss, or malabsorption. Prognosis ultimately depends on the underlying cause (e.g., occult GI malignancy) rather than the anemia itself (Harrison 22e p.766).","pitfalls":["Classic microcytosis/hypochromia can be ABSENT in early iron deficiency, in elderly patients, or when masked by concomitant folate/B12 deficiency or liver disease - do not exclude IDA on a normal MCV alone (Harrison 22e p.765, p.450).","Ferritin is an acute-phase reactant; normal or high ferritin during inflammation can mask coexisting iron deficiency (Harrison 22e p.450).","Serum iron alone is unreliable as a single test due to diurnal variation (Harrison 22e p.765).","Treating IDA without investigating the cause can miss occult GI malignancy; isolated symptomatic iron deficiency without anemia should not be underestimated (Harrison 22e p.764, p.765).","Biomarkers other than ferritin and transferrin saturation are limited by poor standardization and accessibility (Harrison 22e p.766)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison describes a continuum rather than a formal grading scale: (1) isolated/absolute iron deficiency with depleted stores (ferritin <15-30 µg/L) but NORMAL hemoglobin and normochromic-normocytic cells; (2) iron-restricted erythropoiesis once transferrin saturation falls <15-20%, with hypochromic microcytic cells; (3) overt iron-deficiency anemia. Severe anemia is generally taken as hemoglobin <=8 g/dL (the threshold at which anemia commonly becomes detectable and at which rapid IV correction is considered) (Harrison 22e p.764-766).","references":["Harrison 22e p.764","Harrison 22e p.765","Harrison 22e p.766","Harrison 22e p.767","Harrison 22e p.450","Camaschella C: Iron deficiency. Blood 133:30, 2019"],"crossLinks":["colorectal_cancer"]},"legionellosis":{"name":"Legionellosis (Legionnaires' Disease)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":164,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1269-1276","clinicalPearls":["Legionella species cause two distinct illnesses collectively termed legionellosis: Legionella pneumonia (Legionnaires' disease) and Pontiac fever; most clinical disease is driven by L. pneumophila, predominantly serotype 1 (Harrison 22e p.1269).","Almost all cases trace to waterborne exposure; aerosolizing or aspiration sources include cooling towers, fountains, water misters, HVAC systems, hot tubs/spas, ice machines, and showerheads/sinks in large premise-plumbing systems (Harrison 22e p.1270-1271).","Legionella pneumonia presents as an 'atypical pneumonia' with nonspecific onset of fever, myalgias, headache, breathlessness, and dry or productive cough; accompanying neurologic or gastrointestinal symptoms (anorexia, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, confusion) raise the likelihood of legionellosis (Harrison 22e p.1272).","A characteristic laboratory cluster supports the diagnosis: hyponatremia and/or renal dysfunction are frequent, with elevated liver enzymes, and leukocytosis or leukopenia plus thrombocytopenia (Harrison 22e p.1272).","Per CDC, a single confirmed Legionella case in a patient who did not leave a health care facility/campus for the 10 days before onset should trigger an outbreak investigation, because nosocomial cases may signal water-system contamination (Harrison 22e p.1270-1273).","Pontiac fever is a milder, self-limited influenza-like illness defined by the ABSENCE of pneumonia, with fever, headache, myalgias, chills, vertigo, nausea/vomiting and diarrhea; antibiotics are not required (Harrison 22e p.1273)."],"pathophysiology":"Legionellae are aerobic gram-negative bacteria ubiquitous in aquatic environments, damp soil, and compost. Their natural hosts are amoebae and other free-living protozoa, within which they replicate intracellularly; humans are accidental hosts. They have a biphasic life cycle (a nutrient-rich replicative phase and a transmissive phase under resource scarcity) and persist in biofilms within natural and engineered water systems, including building premise plumbing where temperature and nutrients support protozoal hosts. After inhalation or aspiration of small aerosol particles, the organisms are phagocytized by alveolar macrophages and monocytes; pathogenic species evade intracellular defenses (forming Legionella-containing vacuoles, downregulating cytokine receptors, inhibiting host protein synthesis, and avoiding lysosomal degradation) and replicate. Innate pattern-recognition receptors (Toll-like and NOD-like receptors) together with adaptive CD4 and CD8 T-cell responses drive interferon-gamma, TNF, and neutrophil recruitment, which can clear the pathogen but may also produce immunopathology. L. pneumophila is more cytopathogenic than most non-pneumophila species, which may underlie its association with severe disease (Harrison 22e p.1269).","additionalDifferentials":["Other atypical-pneumonia pathogens, notably Mycoplasma pneumoniae and Chlamydia pneumoniae (Harrison 22e p.1272)"],"infectionMimics":["In immunocompromised patients, Legionella can present as pulmonary nodules with or without cavitation that mimic fungal infection, or as lung abscesses (Harrison 22e p.1273)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Lower-respiratory culture (sputum, BAL fluid, lung tissue, or extrapulmonary sites) on cysteine-supplemented buffered charcoal yeast extract (BCYE) agar is the gold standard and is essential for epidemiologic typing; growth is slow (typically 3-5 days, longer for non-pneumophila species) (Harrison 22e p.1273-1274)","Polymerase chain reaction / nucleic acid amplification (including LAMP and multiplex respiratory panels) on lower-tract specimens is more sensitive than culture and remains useful during antibiotic therapy, but does not provide serotype information and most assays target only L. pneumophila (Harrison 22e p.1274-1275)","Bronchoscopy when urinary antigen is negative and the patient cannot produce adequate sputum, or in severe disease requiring ICU care (Harrison 22e p.1273)","Serology (paired acute/convalescent titers; fourfold rise to L. pneumophila serogroup 1 confirms) has limited acute sensitivity but aids outbreak investigation (Harrison 22e p.1274)","Direct fluorescent antibody testing of sputum has low and variable sensitivity (~20-70%) and is not recommended for low-risk screening due to false positives (Harrison 22e p.1274)"],"redFlags":["Severe pneumonia with acute respiratory failure or a sepsis-like presentation warrants testing for Legionella (Harrison 22e p.1272)","Pneumonia developing during hospitalization, after recent travel, or during a known or possible outbreak should prompt Legionella testing (Harrison 22e p.1272)","Immunosuppressed patients may lack typical features such as fever and may show radiographic progression during early therapy (Harrison 22e p.1272-1273)"],"prognosis":"Legionella pneumonia carries significant morbidity and mortality, with most patients hospitalized and many requiring ICU admission. The reported case-fatality rate is about 10%, rising to roughly 25% in health care-associated L. pneumophila pneumonia and to approximately 30-50% in immunocompromised hosts. Delayed antibiotic treatment is associated with about a threefold higher mortality. More than a quarter of survivors of severe disease have lasting complications, including recurrent hospitalizations, acute renal failure, respiratory sequelae, and recurrent pneumonias. By contrast, Pontiac fever is self-limited, usually resolving within 3-5 days, with hospitalization, complications, and death extremely rare (Harrison 22e p.1275).","pitfalls":["Urinary antigen testing detects ONLY L. pneumophila serotype 1; it gives false-negative results in most cases caused by non-serotype 1 L. pneumophila and non-pneumophila species, so a negative result does not exclude legionellosis (sensitivity for serotype 1 is ~70%, though specificity is high) (Harrison 22e p.1274).","Urinary antigen can be falsely negative very early in disease yet remain positive for months after infection (especially in immunosuppressed patients), and it cannot be used in anuric patients (Harrison 22e p.1274).","Because community-acquired pneumonia guidelines recommend empirical coverage active against Legionella, specific testing is often skipped and cultures are frequently collected only after antibiotics, reducing yield (Harrison 22e p.1273-1274).","Diagnosis on clinical grounds alone is unreliable; clinical-prediction tools (e.g., Winthrop-University Hospital Criteria, Legionella Score) are more useful for their negative than their positive predictive value (Harrison 22e p.1272).","Routine testing is less common in children, so pediatric cases may be underreported; immunosuppressed patients may present without fever or pulmonary exam findings (Harrison 22e p.1271-1272)."],"severityClassification":"Treatment is stratified by severity: Pontiac fever requires no antibiotics (supportive care only), whereas Legionella pneumonia is managed as mild versus moderate/severe disease. All immunosuppressed patients are considered to have moderate or severe disease and should receive IV therapy where possible; all patients needing inpatient care should receive IV therapy until improving before switching to an oral agent. CDC distinguishes health care-associated cases as presumptive (Legionella pneumonia after >=10 days continuous stay in a health care facility during the 14 days before symptom onset) versus possible (some health care exposure in that window but insufficient to meet presumptive criteria) (Harrison 22e p.1272-1275).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1269-1276"],"crossLinks":[]},"leishmaniasis":{"name":"Leishmaniasis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":233,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1781-1788","clinicalPearls":["Caused by obligate intracellular protozoa of the genus Leishmania (~20 species), transmitted by phlebotomine sandflies (Phlebotomus in the Old World, Lutzomyia in the New World); syndromes fall into three categories: visceral (VL), cutaneous (CL), and mucosal (ML) (p.1781).","Visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar, Hindi for 'black fever') is caused by the L. donovani complex (L. donovani, L. infantum); classic presentation is abrupt moderate-to-high fever with rigors, progressive massive splenomegaly, moderate hepatomegaly, weight loss/wasting, and skin hyperpigmentation (p.1782-1783).","Lymphadenopathy is common in most VL-endemic regions but is rare on the Indian subcontinent (p.1783).","VL causes pancytopenia from bone marrow dysfunction and reticuloendothelial infiltration: leukopenia and anemia appear early, followed by thrombocytopenia; there is a marked polyclonal increase in serum immunoglobulins (hypergammaglobulinemia) (p.1782-1783).","Fever with neutropenia or pancytopenia in a patient from an endemic region strongly suggests VL; hypergammaglobulinemia in long-standing illness strengthens the diagnosis (p.1784).","Demonstration of amastigotes in tissue aspirate smears is the gold standard for VL: splenic smear sensitivity is >95%, bone marrow 60-85%, lymph node aspirate ~50% (p.1783).","The rK39 rapid immunochromatographic antibody test is used worldwide for VL field diagnosis (sensitivity ~98%, specificity ~90% in immunocompetent individuals; both lower in East Africa) (p.1783).","Cutaneous leishmaniasis: a sandfly bite is followed days-to-weeks later by a papule that becomes a nodule and ulcerates over weeks-to-months, with raised, indurated margins and a usually painless base; most lesions heal spontaneously over 2-15 months, leaving a scar (p.1786).","Mucosal leishmaniasis (espundia) is typically caused by L. (Viannia) braziliensis and is almost entirely confined to the Americas; it presents with nasal stuffiness and bleeding followed by destruction of nasal cartilage, septal perforation, and collapse of the nasal bridge (p.1787-1788).","Post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis (PKDL) develops in 2-50% of patients during or after cure of VL on the Indian subcontinent and in East Africa, presenting as hypopigmented macules, papules, nodules, or diffuse skin infiltration (p.1785).","Visceral leishmaniasis is a recognized cause of secondary hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH); Leishmania is the most common parasite associated with HLH (p.1369-1370)."],"pathophysiology":"Sandflies inoculate flagellate promastigotes into the skin; neutrophils first take up the organisms, then macrophages and dendritic cells engulf them, and parasites multiply as nonflagellate amastigotes within macrophages, rupturing cells and invading new macrophages. Acquired resistance depends on a T helper 1 (TH1) response driven by IL-12, IFN-gamma, and TNF-alpha; most infected individuals control infection and never develop symptomatic disease. In active VL there is markedly elevated IL-10, which conditions macrophages for parasite survival, downregulates TNF-alpha and nitric oxide, and suppresses antigen presentation, so patients lack a positive leishmanin (Montenegro) skin test. In VL the reticuloendothelial organs (spleen, liver, lymph nodes, tonsils, intestinal submucosa) are heavily parasitized, and bone marrow dysfunction produces pancytopenia. Diffuse cutaneous leishmaniasis reflects an absent cell-mediated/DTH response with high IL-10, TGF-beta, and IL-4, allowing unchecked parasite multiplication (p.1781-1786).","additionalDifferentials":["Malaria (VL is easily mistaken for it)","Typhoid (enteric) fever","Tuberculosis","Brucellosis","Schistosomiasis","Histoplasmosis","Splenomegaly from portal hypertension","Chronic myeloid leukemia","Tropical splenomegaly syndrome (hyperreactive malarial splenomegaly)","For CL: cutaneous tuberculosis, fungal infections, leprosy, sarcoidosis, and malignant ulcers"],"infectionMimics":["Malaria","Typhoid fever","Tuberculosis","Brucellosis","Schistosomiasis","Histoplasmosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Splenomegaly from portal hypertension","Chronic myeloid leukemia","Tropical splenomegaly syndrome","Malignant ulcers (for cutaneous disease)","Sarcoidosis (for cutaneous disease)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Splenic aspirate smear (highest sensitivity >95%, but invasive and potentially dangerous)","Bone marrow aspirate smear (sensitivity 60-85%)","Lymph node aspirate smear (sensitivity ~50%)","Culture of tissue aspirates (increases sensitivity)","rK39 rapid immunochromatographic antibody test (fingerprick blood/serum, result in ~15 min)","ELISA and indirect immunofluorescent antibody test (IFAT)","Qualitative and quantitative (real-time) PCR for leishmanial nucleic acid (allows species identification)","Loop-mediated isothermal amplification (LAMP) of nucleic acids (point-of-care for VL and PKDL)","Slit skin smears, lesion aspirates/biopsies for CL; tissue biopsy plus PCR for ML"],"redFlags":["Massive (hugely enlarged) splenomegaly with fever and pancytopenia in a patient from or returning from an endemic region","Severe anemia, which may cause congestive heart failure","Bleeding (epistaxis, retinal hemorrhages, gastrointestinal bleeding) from thrombocytopenia","Hypoalbuminemia with pedal edema and ascites in advanced illness","HIV co-infection (untreated VL is fatal in 100% of HIV-co-infected patients)","Secondary infections (pneumonia, tuberculosis, dysentery, gastroenteritis)","In ML: destruction of nasal cartilage/septal perforation; aspiration pneumonia may be fatal"],"prognosis":"Untreated visceral leishmaniasis is fatal in most patients, including 100% of those with HIV co-infection. With effective treatment, recovery is quick: defervescence, regression of splenomegaly, weight gain, and hematologic recovery occur within a week, and continued improvement over 12 months suggests cure; a small percentage relapse (usually within 6-12 months) but respond to retreatment. Cutaneous lesions heal spontaneously in most cases (2-15 months), leaving a scar, but may cause permanent scarring and social stigma. Diffuse cutaneous leishmaniasis does not heal spontaneously and is difficult to treat. Mucosal leishmaniasis does not heal spontaneously despite strong TH1/DTH immunity; the more extensive the disease, the worse the prognosis (p.1782-1788).","pitfalls":["VL is easily mistaken for malaria and other tropical febrile illnesses; a careful travel history is essential in nonendemic countries.","Antibody detection tests (rK39, ELISA, IFAT) remain positive for years after cure, so they cannot be used to assess cure or detect relapse, and positive serology does not help diagnose PKDL.","In HIV/VL co-infection serodiagnostic tests may be negative in up to 50% of patients, and parasites may be found in unusual sites (e.g., bronchoalveolar lavage fluid, buffy coat).","Splenic aspiration, though most sensitive, is invasive and may be dangerous in untrained hands.","In PKDL, parasites are scanty and skin smear sensitivity is low, especially in hypopigmented macules (parasites are more readily found in nodular lesions).","Patients with active VL and IL-10-mediated suppression do not have a positive leishmanin (Montenegro) skin test.","In ML the strongly positive DTH response fails to distinguish past from present infection."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not give a formal grading system; leishmaniasis is classified clinically by syndrome: visceral leishmaniasis (VL/kala-azar), cutaneous leishmaniasis (CL, with Old World and New World forms, plus leishmaniasis recidivans and diffuse cutaneous leishmaniasis [DCL]), and mucosal leishmaniasis (ML). Post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis (PKDL) is a sequel of VL.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1781-1788"],"crossLinks":[]},"leprosy":{"name":"Leprosy (Hansen's Disease)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":184,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1404","clinicalPearls":["Leprosy is a chronic infectious disease caused by Mycobacterium leprae, with manifestations largely confined to skin, peripheral nerves, eyes, and the upper respiratory tract; M. leprae has a predilection for peripheral nerves (p.1404).","Diagnosis rests on three cardinal signs, of which at least one establishes the diagnosis: (1) a hypopigmented or erythematous skin lesion with definite loss/impairment of sensation, (2) involvement of peripheral nerves shown by definite thickening with sensory impairment, and (3) a positive result for acid-fast bacilli on slit-skin smear or biopsy, or a positive biopsy PCR (p.1409-1410).","The differing immune responses to M. leprae produce a clinical spectrum; immunologic resistance is strong at the tuberculoid end and weakest at the lepromatous end (p.1404, p.1406).","Skin surfaces in tuberculoid lesions are dry, hairless, and anaesthetic owing to destruction of underlying cutaneous nerves, plus autonomic damage causing loss of sweating (p.1407).","Slit-skin smear bacteriologic index runs from negative (tuberculoid) up to 4+ to 6+ with globi (lepromatous), and falls an average of ~1 log unit per year on multidrug therapy (p.1404, p.1407-1408).","Leprosy is the principal treatable cause of peripheral neuropathy worldwide in some settings; nerve enlargement is often palpable (e.g., greater auricular, ulnar, median, radial, lateral popliteal, posterior tibial, sural, superficial peroneal) (p.1409-1410).","Treatment principle: WHO-recommended multidrug therapy (a combination of rifampin, dapsone, and clofazimine) is the standard of care and cures the disease; paucibacillary disease uses two drugs and multibacillary disease uses three, with the cure rate ~99% (no doses given here) (p.1410, p.1412).","M. leprae has never been grown in artificial media; it is propagated only in animal models (mouse footpad, armadillo), which underlies the difficulty of culture-based diagnosis (p.1404)."],"pathophysiology":"Leprosy is caused by Mycobacterium leprae, an obligate intracellular, acid-fast, rod-shaped bacterium with a preferred growth temperature of 27-30 degrees C, explaining its predilection for cooler surface tissues (skin, peripheral nerves, testes, upper airways) and relative sparing of inner viscera. After entry (assumed mainly via the respiratory tract, sometimes through damaged skin), the pathogenic process usually begins in peripheral nerves once bacilli are engulfed by Schwann cells. The histopathologic outcome - and thus the type of leprosy - depends on the host cell-mediated immune (CMI) response. The Ridley-Jopling spectrum (TT, BT, BB, BL, LL, with indeterminate IL as an early form) reflects this: at the tuberculoid pole, strong CMI produces epithelioid/giant-cell granulomas that destroy nerves (causing anaesthesia and muscle weakness) but contain few bacilli; toward the lepromatous pole, weak CMI permits unrestricted multiplication of bacilli within macrophages (lepra cells) as clumps/globi, with widespread dissemination and high bacterial load. Tissue damage is largely due to delayed hypersensitivity. M. leprae carries the species-specific phenolic glycolipid PGL-1, used in serodiagnosis. M. lepromatosis is a related species associated with diffuse leprosy of Lucio and Latapi.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypopigmented macules: pityriasis alba, vitiligo, progressive macular hypomelanosis, pityriasis versicolor, pityriasis rosea, postinflammatory hypopigmentation, sarcoidosis, post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis, morphea (p.1410)","Plaque/nodular lesions: granuloma annulare, cutaneous sarcoidosis, cutaneous leishmaniasis, lupus miliaris disseminatus faciei, nodular histiocytosis, lupus erythematosus, cutaneous T-cell lymphomas (especially mycosis fungoides), secondary syphilis (p.1410)","ENL (type 2 reaction) lesions: erythema nodosum of other etiologies, nodular vasculitis, cutaneous polyarteritis nodosa (p.1410)","Mononeuropathy: diabetes, amyloidosis, myxedema (p.1410)","Acute-onset polyneuropathy: Guillain-Barre syndrome, toxic polyneuropathy (p.1410)"],"infectionMimics":["Cutaneous leishmaniasis (p.1410)","Post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis (p.1410)","Secondary syphilis (p.1410)","Pityriasis versicolor (p.1410)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Vitiligo (p.1410)","Sarcoidosis / cutaneous sarcoidosis (p.1410)","Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma, especially mycosis fungoides (p.1410)","Lupus erythematosus (p.1410)","Granuloma annulare (p.1410)","Diabetic, amyloid, or myxedema-related mononeuropathy (p.1410)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-skin smear from standard sites (right earlobe, forehead, chin, and left buttock/upper thigh), Ziehl-Neelsen stained, graded by bacteriologic index (0-6) and microbiologic index (p.1410)","Skin biopsy to confirm diagnosis, classify disease, support diagnosis of reactions, and assess cure; immunochemical staining for M. leprae antigens aids detection of early lesions (p.1410)","PCR / gene amplification (conventional, RT-PCR, multiplex targeting RLEP, 16S rRNA, SodA, antigen genes) - useful in bacteriologic-index-negative cases and for subclinical infection in contacts (p.1410)","PGL-1 antibody testing (ELISA, ML flow lateral-flow assay, UPC-LFA) - more often positive in multibacillary than paucibacillary disease (p.1410)","Lepromin (Mitsuda) skin test - measures cellular immunity; negative in LL/BL leprosy (p.1410)","Nerve function assessment: Semmes-Weinstein monofilament (or WHO-recommended ballpoint pen) touch testing, voluntary muscle testing, nerve conduction studies, and ultrasound of peripheral nerves (p.1410-1411)"],"redFlags":["Type 1 (reversal) reaction: delayed-hypersensitivity reaction with sudden upgrading of CMI; acute swelling and redness of existing lesions, painful/tender nerves with neuritis; can cause silent neuritis and irreversible sensory/motor nerve damage - a medical urgency to prevent disability (p.1407, p.1408-1409)","Type 2 reaction / erythema nodosum leprosum (ENL): immune-complex-mediated syndrome with crops of painful red nodules, fever, malaise, and possible neuritis, iridocyclitis, orchitis, nephritis/renal failure, and (late) amyloidosis (p.1408-1409)","Neuritis with nerve function impairment, including 'silent neuritis' (progressive sensory/motor loss without pain or tenderness) - requires prompt treatment to avoid permanent disability and blindness (p.1409, p.1410-1411)","Lucio's phenomenon in diffuse leprosy of Lucio and Latapi: vasculitis and thrombosis causing hemorrhagic skin infarction and necrotic ulcers (p.1409)","Eye involvement (corneal anaesthesia, iridocyclitis, lagophthalmos, corneal ulceration) threatening sight (p.1408)"],"prognosis":"Leprosy can be cured effectively: the cure rate with multidrug therapy is ~99% and treatment-failure rates are very low when therapy is completed. Relapse rates are well below 1% except in patients with a very high baseline bacillary load (bacteriologic index >=4), and relapses usually occur within 5 years of finishing therapy; antimicrobial resistance is rare, so relapsed multibacillary patients can be re-treated with the same regimen. The major determinant of long-term outcome is nerve damage and resulting disability, which drives stigma and social exclusion. Highest risk of (additional) nerve damage is in multibacillary patients with nerve impairment already present at diagnosis (up to ~65%); intermediate risk (~16%) in multibacillary patients without impairment or paucibacillary patients with impairment at diagnosis; and lowest (~3%) in paucibacillary patients without impairment. Leprosy reactions and new nerve damage can occur even after completing therapy, up to 3 years afterward. Cure (cessation of bacterial activity) must be distinguished from sequelae (nerve impairment, reactions, disabilities such as claw hand or neuropathic foot) that may persist and require chronic care.","pitfalls":["Leprosy is frequently diagnosed late - in non-endemic countries it is often misdiagnosed simply because it is not considered - increasing the risk of irreversible nerve damage and disability (p.1404, p.1410)","On the face, sensory impairment may be difficult to demonstrate because of generous bilateral sensory innervation, which can compensate for damage to certain nerve branches and obscure the indeterminate stage (p.1407, p.1409)","'Silent neuritis' / 'quiet nerve paralysis': nerve function can be lost progressively without pain, paresthesia, tenderness, or other signs of reaction - regular nerve function assessment is needed during and after therapy (p.1409, p.1411)","Distinguishing relapse from a type 1 reaction in paucibacillary disease can be difficult; a therapeutic glucocorticoid trial helps - improvement within 4 weeks favors T1R, lack of response favors relapse (p.1412)","Patients often equate cure with absence of symptoms, but in leprosy bacteriologic cure does not eliminate established nerve-impairment sequelae - this must be explained at completion of therapy (p.1412-1413)","Multidrug therapy for clinical leprosy has apparently not interrupted transmission, and subclinical/undiagnosed multibacillary cases are likely a main source of ongoing spread (p.1405)"],"severityClassification":"Ridley-Jopling classification (1962) defines five overlapping categories along the immunologic spectrum: tuberculoid (TT), borderline tuberculoid (BT), mid-borderline (BB), borderline lepromatous (BL), and lepromatous (LL), with indeterminate leprosy (IL) as an early form; immunologic resistance is strongest at TT and weakest at LL, with TT and LL relatively stable and BT/BB/BL unstable. For operational/treatment purposes the WHO simplified classification is used: paucibacillary disease = negative slit-skin smears at all sites (or, on clinical criteria, one to five skin lesions and no/only one involved nerve), and multibacillary disease = positive smear at any site (or six or more lesions and/or more than one involved nerve). The WHO disability grading system scores eyes, hands, and feet (grades 0-2), summed into the Eye-Hand-Foot (EHF) score as an overall impairment indicator (p.1405-1406).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1404"],"crossLinks":[]},"listeriosis":{"name":"Listeriosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":156,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1227-1230","clinicalPearls":["Listeriosis is acquired almost always via foodborne transmission; implicated foods are refrigerated ready-to-eat items (deli/delicatessen meats, hot dogs), soft cheeses made with raw/unpasteurised milk, unpasteurised dairy, raw produce (packaged salad, sprouts, enoki mushrooms, cantaloupe, caramel apples, frozen vegetables) and pate/meat spreads (p.1227-1228).","Major risk groups for invasive disease are pregnant women and their neonates, older adults (risk rises with each decade over age 59), and immunocompromised persons (impaired cellular immunity from haematologic malignancy, transplant, HIV, glucocorticoids/immunosuppressants, or TNF-alpha inhibitors) (p.1227-1228).","Bacteraemia without a focus is the most common manifestation of invasive listeriosis; presents with fever, chills, myalgias and arthralgias sometimes preceded by nausea/diarrhoea (p.1228).","Neurolisteriosis is the second most common invasive manifestation; in a French cohort 84% of neurolisteriosis cases presented as meningoencephalitis (meningitis plus altered mental status, seizures, or focal findings) (p.1228).","Compared with other bacterial meningitides, CSF pleocytosis and neutrophil predominance are typically LESS pronounced; ~60-75% have CSF WBC <1000/uL and only ~30-40% have low CSF glucose, so normal-ish CSF does not exclude Listeria (p.1228).","Empiric bacterial-meningitis cover (ceftriaxone + vancomycin) does NOT cover Listeria; add ampicillin for Listeria coverage in patients >50 years old or with comorbid/immunocompromising disease (Chap.143, p.140372 / p.1122).","Rhombencephalitis (brainstem/cerebellar encephalitis) disproportionately affects otherwise healthy older adults, classically biphasic (fever and headache, then asymmetric cranial nerve palsies, ataxia, tremor, hemiparesis or hemisensory deficits); nearly half develop respiratory failure (p.1228).","MRI is superior to CT for diagnosis of neurolisteriosis, including rhombencephalitis (p.1228).","Diagnosis is by isolation from a normally sterile site (blood, CSF, amniotic fluid, placental or fetal tissue); blood cultures are positive only about half the time in pregnant women, and CSF multiplex PCR panels and MALDI-TOF aid rapid identification (p.1229).","Pregnant women usually have only mild flulike illness and rarely develop neurolisteriosis or death, yet transmit infection transplacentally to the fetus with high fetal morbidity; prepartum antibiotics started >=1 day before delivery improve the chance of a healthy infant (p.1228-1229).","In invasive disease virtually all patients are hospitalised and 18% die; consider listeriosis acquired in hospital and investigate hospital-provided food when a hospitalised (especially immunocompromised) patient develops new symptoms (p.1227-1228).","TMP-SMX given as Pneumocystis jirovecii prophylaxis (e.g. in HIV or transplant recipients) also helps prevent listeriosis (p.1229)."],"pathophysiology":"L. monocytogenes is a ubiquitous, nonsporulating, facultatively anaerobic gram-positive rod (showing small zones of beta-haemolysis and characteristic tumbling motility) that lives in soil and decaying vegetation and can grow at refrigerator temperatures as low as 4 C and tolerate low-pH/high-salt environments. Infection follows ingestion of contaminated food; the infectious dose is likely very low in the severely immunocompromised, and raised gastric pH (e.g. from proton-pump inhibitors) probably promotes gut survival. After transcytosis across the intestinal epithelium the organism spreads via mesenteric lymph nodes and bloodstream to its target organs (liver and spleen) and can cross the blood-brain barrier and the placenta. Virulence factors include internalins (InlA, InlB) for invasion of nonphagocytic cells, listeriolysin O and phospholipases for escape from the phagocytic vacuole, and ActA for actin-based cell-to-cell spread that evades antibodies and complement. Iron promotes growth, explaining the association of listeriosis with iron-overload states such as haemochromatosis. Host defence is predominantly cell-mediated (interferon-gamma, TNF-alpha, and CD8+ T cells), accounting for the predilection for those with impaired cellular immunity. (p.1227)","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Other causes of community-acquired bacterial meningitis (S. pneumoniae, N. meningitidis, H. influenzae, group B streptococcus) (p.1228, Chap.143)","Fungal or tuberculous meningitis (low CSF glucose with mononuclear pleocytosis can mimic Listeria) (p.1228, Chap.143)","Brain abscess or other space-occupying CNS infection (Listeria abscess) (p.1228)","Other bacteraemic/septic illnesses presenting with fever without a focus (p.1228)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Primary or metastatic CNS malignancy (Listeria brain abscess may be misdiagnosed as tumour) (p.1228)","Neoplasia / haematologic malignancy presenting with fever (also a risk factor and prognostic comorbidity) (p.1228)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood cultures (note positive only ~half the time in pregnancy) (p.1229)","CSF culture and Gram stain (may show gram-positive rods, but often no organisms or misleading gram-positive cocci/diplococci/diphtheroids) (p.1228-1229)","Culture of normally sterile sites: amniotic fluid, placental tissue, fetal tissue (p.1229)","Multiplex PCR panel on CSF (includes L. monocytogenes as a target) (p.1229)","MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry for rapid isolate identification (p.1229)","MRI brain (superior to CT for neurolisteriosis and rhombencephalitis) (p.1228)","Selective enrichment medium (PALCAM Listeria Selective Agar or Oxford Agar) for non-sterile specimens / outbreak work-up (p.1229)","Whole-genome sequencing for outbreak investigation (p.1229)"],"redFlags":["Signs of meningitis with altered mental status, seizures or focal neurologic deficits (meningoencephalitis) (p.1228)","Brainstem/cerebellar signs - asymmetric cranial nerve palsies, ataxia, tremor, hemiparesis, hemisensory deficits (rhombencephalitis), with respiratory failure in nearly half (p.1228)","Fever or signs of fetal distress in a pregnant woman (meconium-stained amniotic fluid, abnormal fetal heart rate) (p.1229)","Multiorgan failure and monocytopenia (<200 cells/uL) - markers associated with death (p.1228)"],"prognosis":"Invasive listeriosis carries substantial mortality: 18% of reported cases die and virtually all are hospitalised. In a large French (MONALISA) cohort the 3-month mortality was 46% for bacteraemia and 30% for neurolisteriosis; death was associated with multiorgan failure, neoplasia, worsening of preexisting comorbidities, monocytopenia (<200 cells/uL), older age and female sex, and (for neurolisteriosis) a positive blood culture and adjunctive dexamethasone use. Nearly half of neurolisteriosis survivors have long-term neurologic impairment. In pregnancy-associated disease, fetal/neonatal outcomes are poor: in a cohort of 107 pregnancies 24% ended in fetal loss, 45% in premature birth and only ~5% had a normal delivery and postpartum course; overall 3% of 189 live-born infants died. (p.1228-1229)","pitfalls":["Cephalosporins are NOT effective against L. monocytogenes; standard empiric meningitis cover (ceftriaxone +/- vancomycin) misses Listeria, so ampicillin must be added empirically in patients >50 years or with comorbid/immunocompromising disease (p.1229, Chap.143 p.140372).","Febrile gastroenteritis form is rarely diagnosed because Listeria is not detected by routine stool cultures or rapid stool tests, and stool culture is NOT indicated in evaluating invasive listeriosis (p.1227, p.1229).","Gram-variable appearance as cocci, diplococci or diphtheroids can obscure the diagnosis; Listeria must be distinguished from other gram-positive rods, especially diphtheroids (p.1227, p.1229).","Relatively bland CSF (WBC often <1000/uL, frequently no organisms on Gram stain, only ~30-40% with low glucose) can falsely reassure - do not exclude Listeria meningitis on CSF profile alone (p.1228).","Brain abscess due to Listeria may be misdiagnosed as primary or metastatic malignancy; CSF can be normal when abscess occurs without concurrent meningitis (p.1228).","Adjunctive dexamethasone is associated with reduced survival in neurolisteriosis and should be avoided; it is also not recommended for established Listeria meningitis (p.1229, Chap.143).","Treatment failures have been reported with meropenem despite in vitro susceptibility, and TMP-SMX resistance occurs - perform susceptibility testing if TMP-SMX is considered (p.1229).","Testing/treatment is NOT indicated for an asymptomatic person who ate a recalled product, even if they have risk factors (p.1229)."],"severityClassification":"No formal severity score. Harrison frames the clinical spectrum from non-invasive self-limited febrile gastroenteritis in healthy persons to invasive listeriosis (bacteraemia without a focus, neurolisteriosis [meningoencephalitis, cerebritis, focal abscess, rhombencephalitis], focal infections, and pregnancy-associated/neonatal disease [granulomatosis infantiseptica, early-onset sepsis, late-onset meningitis]). Severity is judged clinically by syndrome, with prognostic markers including multiorgan failure and monocytopenia. (p.1227-1229)","references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e, Chapter 156: Listeria monocytogenes Infections, pp.1227-1230","Charlier C et al. Clinical features and prognostic factors of listeriosis: The MONALISA National Prospective Cohort Study. Lancet Infect Dis 17:510, 2017","Charlier C et al. Neonatal listeriosis presentation and outcome: A prospective study of 189 cases. Clin Infect Dis 74:8, 2022","Koopmans MM et al. Human listeriosis. Clin Microbiol Rev 36:e0006019, 2023","Thomas J et al. Outbreak of listeriosis in South Africa associated with processed meat. N Engl J Med 382:632, 2020"],"crossLinks":["MENINGITIS","ENCEPHALITIS"]},"lyme_disease":{"name":"Lyme Disease (Lyme Borreliosis)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":191,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1448","clinicalPearls":["Caused by spirochetes of the Borrelia burgdorferi sensu lato group (also called Borreliella spp.), transmitted by Ixodes ricinus complex ticks; B. burgdorferi sensu stricto is the predominant cause in the United States, while B. afzelii, B. garinii, and B. bavariensis also occur in Europe and Asia. (p.1448-1449)","Staged illness: stage 1 (early localized) presents with erythema migrans (EM); stage 2 (early disseminated) may follow with secondary skin lesions, neurologic involvement (meningitis, cranial neuritis, radiculoneuritis), carditis with atrioventricular block, and migratory musculoskeletal pain; stage 3 (late/persistent) typically manifests as intermittent arthritis and, rarely, chronic encephalopathy, polyneuropathy, or acrodermatitis. (p.1448-1449)","EM begins after an incubation period of roughly 3-32 days as a red macule or papule at the bite site that expands into a large annular lesion, often with a bright red outer border and partial central clearing; common sites include the thigh, groin, and axilla, and the lesion is warm but usually not painful. (p.1451)","Approximately 20% of patients do NOT develop the characteristic EM skin lesion, so its absence does not exclude the diagnosis. (p.1451)","Cardiac involvement occurs in about 8% of patients within weeks of onset; the most common abnormality is fluctuating atrioventricular block (first-degree, Wenckebach, or complete heart block), and most cardiac involvement lasts only a few weeks. (p.1451)","About 15% of untreated patients develop frank neurologic abnormalities after weeks to months; the typical U.S. pattern is fluctuating meningitis with facial palsy (cranial neuritis, often the facial nerve, sometimes bilateral) and peripheral radiculoneuropathy. CSF in U.S. neuroborreliosis shows lymphocytic pleocytosis (~100 cells/uL) with elevated protein and normal or slightly low glucose. (p.1451)","In Europe and Asia the characteristic first neurologic sign is radicular pain followed by CSF pleocytosis (meningopolyneuritis or Bannwarth's syndrome), often without meningeal or encephalitic signs. (p.1451)","Lyme arthritis (stage 3) develops in about 60% of untreated U.S. patients months after onset; the typical pattern is intermittent oligoarticular attacks in large joints, especially the knees. Joint fluid white cell counts range widely (500-110,000/uL, average ~25,000/uL), mostly neutrophils, and rheumatoid factor and ANA tests are usually negative. (p.1451)","Most patients experience early symptoms during summer, reflecting transmission by nymphal Ixodes ticks; the tick must usually remain attached for at least about 24 hours for transmission of B. burgdorferi. (p.1449-1450)","Lyme disease is the most common vector-borne infection in the United States and Europe; in the U.S. more than 30,000 cases are reported annually but true cases are estimated at about 476,000 per year. (p.1450)","Posttreatment Lyme disease syndrome (PTLDS) affects roughly 10% of treated patients (reported figures vary widely) with persistent subjective pain, neurocognitive symptoms, or fatigue; there is NO evidence these symptoms reflect active infection, and randomized trials show repeated antibiotic courses do not help and are not recommended. (p.1451-1453)","Reinfection can occur after EM treated with antibiotics because the immune response after early infection is not protective; however, patients who develop an expanded immune response over months (e.g., those with Lyme arthritis) have protective immunity for years and rarely become reinfected. (p.1454)","Maternal-fetal transmission of B. burgdorferi appears to occur rarely if at all; standard therapy is recommended for pregnant women. (p.1453)","No Lyme disease vaccine is currently commercially available; the previously marketed vaccine was discontinued by its manufacturer. (p.1454)"],"pathophysiology":"B. burgdorferi is a fastidious microaerophilic spirochete with an unusually small genome and more than 100 predicted lipoproteins, mostly plasmid-encoded and displayed on the outer membrane to interact with host and tick. It expresses outer-surface protein A (OspA) in the tick midgut and upregulates OspC during migration to the salivary gland, where OspC binds the tick salivary protein Salp15 (required for mammalian infection). After injection into skin the organism downregulates OspC and upregulates the antigenically variable VlsE lipoprotein, then may migrate outward to produce EM and spread hematogenously or via lymphatics to skin, nervous system, heart, and joints. Its only known virulence factors are surface proteins that attach to host proteins, integrins, glycosaminoglycans, or glycoproteins (e.g., plasminogen binding aids tissue spread; complement regulators such as Factor H protect against lysis; decorin-binding proteins bind collagen-associated decorin, possibly explaining alignment with collagen fibrils). The host mounts innate and adaptive responses producing proinflammatory cytokines (IL-6, TNF-alpha, IL-1beta, IFN-gamma) and opsonizing antibodies; immune mechanisms eventually control disseminated infection even without antibiotics, though spirochetes may persist in localized niches for years. Postinfectious (antibiotic-refractory) Lyme arthritis is not due to persistent infection (culture and PCR of synovium are uniformly negative after antibiotics) but to a dysregulated, excessive proinflammatory response with very high IFN-gamma, host genetic factors (HLA-DRB1*0401, TLR1 1805GG polymorphism), and autoimmune responses to vascular and extracellular matrix autoantigens.","additionalDifferentials":["Uninfected tick bite (red papule that expands little) versus EM","Cellulitis (e.g., streptococcal) or allergic reaction to tick saliva when a lesion expands rapidly","Erythema multiforme in patients with secondary annular lesions (but mucosal blistering and palm/sole involvement are NOT features of B. burgdorferi infection)","Southern tick-associated rash illness (STARI) from Amblyomma americanum bites, an EM-like lesion whose cause is not yet identified","Peripheral spondyloarthropathy or pauciarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis versus oligoarticular Lyme arthritis","Bell's palsy (herpes simplex virus type 1) and Ramsay Hunt syndrome (varicella-zoster virus) versus Lyme facial palsy","Chronic fatigue syndrome and fibromyalgia versus late-stage Lyme disease or PTLDS"],"infectionMimics":["Babesiosis (Babesia microti), co-transmitted by I. scapularis, causing an influenza-like illness with thrombocytopenia and hemolytic anemia","Human granulocytotropic anaplasmosis (Anaplasma phagocytophilum), which may cause leukopenia/thrombocytopenia and can elicit a false-positive IgM response to B. burgdorferi","Borrelia miyamotoi relapsing fever, co-transmitted by I. scapularis","Borrelia mayonii and Ehrlichia muris-like agent infections in the upper midwestern United States","Powassan virus (deer tick virus) infection, which may be fatal","Ehrlichia chaffeensis transmitted by Amblyomma americanum in the same regions as STARI"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Fibromyalgia","Chronic fatigue syndrome","Allergic reaction to tick saliva","Peripheral spondyloarthropathy / juvenile idiopathic arthritis (for the arthritis presentation)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Two-tier serology: conventional approach with ELISA followed by Western blot for equivocal/positive results, or a modified two-test approach using two enzyme immunoassays (e.g., whole-sonicate ELISA followed by VlsE C6 peptide IgG ELISA) that dispenses with the Western blot","IgM and IgG responses during early infection, ideally in paired acute- and convalescent-phase sera (~20-30% positive in acute phase, ~70-80% during convalescence)","After 4-8 weeks, the IgG response by ELISA and Western blot has very high sensitivity and specificity (~99%); a single IgG test is then usually sufficient","Western blot band criteria: IgM positive if 2 of 3 bands (23, 39, 41 kDa); IgG positive if 5 of 10 bands (18, 23, 28, 30, 39, 41, 45, 58, 66, 93 kDa)","PCR of joint fluid for B. burgdorferi DNA (its main PCR application; superior to culture later in disease, though DNA may persist after killing so it does NOT confirm active infection or therapy adequacy)","Culture of B. burgdorferi in Barbour-Stoenner-Kelly (BSK) medium, used mainly in research and positive chiefly early (EM biopsy, occasionally plasma or CSF)","CSF analysis showing lymphocytic pleocytosis with elevated protein in neuroborreliosis","Peripheral blood smear and blood PCR to evaluate for tick-borne co-infections; standard blood counts may give clues (cytopenias)"],"redFlags":["High-degree (third-degree) atrioventricular block or PR interval >0.3 s in carditis, warranting IV therapy and cardiac monitoring","Severe objective neurologic abnormalities (e.g., meningitis, encephalitis, severe neuroborreliosis)","Optic nerve involvement in children from inflammation or raised intracranial pressure, which may lead to blindness","Borrelial encephalomyelitis/leukoencephalitis (rare, mainly B. garinii in Europe) with spastic paraparesis and bladder dysfunction","Rare fatal pancarditis or cardiomegaly","Suspected tick-borne co-infection (e.g., babesiosis, anaplasmosis, Powassan virus), which can cause severe or fatal illness, especially in the very young or elderly"],"prognosis":"Response to treatment is best when given early in the disease; later treatment remains effective but the convalescent period may be longer. Eventually most patients recover with minimal or no residual deficits. Even without antibiotics, innate and adaptive immunity usually control disseminated infection within weeks to months, though untreated spirochetes may persist in localized niches for years. A small percentage develop persistent postinfectious arthritis or, rarely, chronic encephalopathy or polyneuropathy, and about 10% have PTLDS, which usually improves over months but may last years.","pitfalls":["Assuming a negative serologic test excludes acute Lyme disease - serology may be negative during the first several weeks (early seronegativity), so diagnosis of early Lyme disease is clinical","Missing the diagnosis because of atypical EM morphology or because ~20% of patients never develop EM","Relying on a positive IgM result alone in illness lasting more than 2 months - such an isolated IgM is likely false-positive and should not support the diagnosis","Misusing or misinterpreting IgM Western blots, a recognized cause of incorrect Lyme diagnoses in patients with other illnesses","Using serology as a screening test in patients with nonspecific pain or fatigue (low pretest probability), where a false-positive result is more likely than a true-positive","Interpreting persisting seropositivity (including VlsE C6 and IgM responses, which can persist for years after therapy) as evidence of active, recent, or recurrent infection","Treating persistent subjective symptoms as active infection - 'chronic Lyme disease' equated with chronic B. burgdorferi infection is a misnomer, and repeated/prolonged antibiotic courses lack benefit and carry documented harm","Using PCR positivity in joint fluid to judge active infection or adequacy of therapy, since DNA may persist for weeks after spirochetal killing"],"severityClassification":"Staged clinical classification per Harrison: Stage 1 - early localized infection (erythema migrans at the bite site). Stage 2 - early disseminated infection (secondary annular skin lesions, neurologic involvement such as meningitis/cranial neuritis/radiculoneuritis/peripheral neuritis, carditis with atrioventricular nodal block, and migratory musculoskeletal pain). Stage 3 - late/persistent infection (intermittent or persistent arthritis, and rarely chronic encephalopathy, polyneuropathy, or acrodermatitis chronica atrophicans). For diagnostic/testing purposes, the American College of Physicians algorithm stratifies by pretest probability: high (EM - treat empirically without serology), intermediate (e.g., oligoarticular arthritis - test and treat if positive), and low (nonspecific symptoms - neither test nor treat).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1448"],"crossLinks":[]},"mastocytosis":{"name":"Mastocytosis","system":"Immunology / Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":366,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2819-2821","clinicalPearls":["Mastocytosis is defined by accumulation of clonally expanded mast cells in tissues (skin, bone marrow, liver, spleen, gut); mast cell expansion is most readily identified in skin and/or bone marrow (p.2819).","More than 90% of patients carry a somatic gain-of-function KIT D816V mutation in the stem cell factor receptor gene (p.2819).","Classification recognizes cutaneous mastocytosis (with variants), systemic mastocytosis (SM, five variants), and mast cell sarcoma; cutaneous mastocytosis is the most common diagnosis in children and is usually diagnosed within the first year of life (p.2819-2820).","Systemic mastocytosis denotes involvement of an extracutaneous site, most often bone marrow; indolent systemic mastocytosis (ISM) accounts for >70% of adult patients and is associated with a normal life expectancy (p.2820).","Cutaneous lesions of maculopapular cutaneous mastocytosis (MPCM, also called urticaria pigmentosa) are reddish-brown macules, papules, or plaques that urticate and become erythematous on rubbing or trauma (Darier's sign) (p.2820).","Clinical manifestations of SM reflect release of bioactive mast cell mediators plus tissue infiltration: intermittent urticaria, flushing, tachycardia and vascular collapse, gastric acid hypersecretion, crampy lower abdominal pain, and diarrhea (p.2820).","Anaphylaxis in mastocytosis is most commonly triggered by Hymenoptera stings and NSAIDs; symptom flares may also follow alcohol, temperature change, stress, and mast cell-interactive opioids (p.2820).","A basal serum tryptase level is a useful noninvasive test to consider before bone marrow biopsy; total serum tryptase >20 ng/mL is a minor diagnostic criterion for SM (p.2821).","Diagnosis of SM requires tissue confirmation (lesional/bone marrow biopsy): one major plus one minor, or three minor criteria. Major = multifocal dense mast cell aggregates (>15 mast cells per aggregate) in extracutaneous tissue; minor = spindled/atypical morphology, aberrant CD25/CD2/CD30 phenotype, activating KIT mutation, or tryptase >20 ng/mL (p.2821).","Patients with recurrent mast cell activation (especially anaphylaxis with hypotensive syncope) who carry KIT D816V but do not meet full SM criteria are termed monoclonal mast cell activation syndrome (p.2821)."],"pathophysiology":"Mastocytosis is a clonal mast cell disorder driven by somatic gain-of-function mutations in the KIT (stem cell factor receptor) gene, with KIT D816V present in >90% of patients. The mutation produces ligand-independent KIT activation and clonal mast cell proliferation/accumulation in skin, bone marrow, liver, spleen, and gut. KIT mutations occur across all forms of SM and in lesional skin of some children with cutaneous disease; additional mutations (TET2, SRSF2, ASXL1, RUNX1) seen in other hematologic neoplasms can appear in advanced SM variants. Disease manifestations arise from release of bioactive mast cell mediators acting locally and distally, tissue infiltration by mast cells, and the tissue response to that infiltrate; mast cell-mediated fibrosis may affect liver, spleen, and bone marrow but not gastrointestinal tissue or skin (p.2819-2820).","additionalDifferentials":["Carcinoid syndrome (exclude with 24-h urine 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid)","Pheochromocytoma (exclude with 24-h urine/plasma metanephrines)","Hereditary alpha-tryptasemia (autosomal dominant extra copies of the alpha-tryptase gene at TPSAB1; elevated baseline serum tryptase with multisystem mast cell activation symptoms)","Recurrent IgE-mediated or idiopathic anaphylaxis (usually presents with urticaria, angioedema and/or bronchospasm, which are not typical manifestations of anaphylaxis in SM)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Carcinoid syndrome (flushing, diarrhea)","Pheochromocytoma (flushing, tachycardia, vascular collapse)","Hereditary alpha-tryptasemia","Idiopathic or IgE-mediated anaphylaxis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Basal (baseline) serum tryptase as a noninvasive screen before bone marrow biopsy","Bone marrow biopsy with mast cell morphology, immunophenotype (CD25, CD2, CD30), and KIT D816V mutation testing for systemic disease","Skin biopsy to confirm diffuse cutaneous and bullous mastocytosis subvariants","24-h urine 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid (carcinoid) and metanephrines (pheochromocytoma) to exclude mimics","Bone densitometry, bone scan, or skeletal survey (osteoporosis is increased and may cause pathologic fractures)","CT scan or endoscopy and neuropsychiatric evaluation as directed by presentation"],"redFlags":["Anaphylaxis with rapid, life-threatening vascular collapse (most often after Hymenoptera stings or NSAIDs)","Hypotensive syncope from mast cell activation","C findings of aggressive disease: cytopenias, hepatomegaly with ascites and impaired liver function, splenomegaly with hypersplenism, malabsorption with hypoalbuminemia and weight loss, or osteolytic skeletal lesions with pathologic fractures","Circulating atypical mast cells suggesting mast cell leukemia"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is variant-dependent. Life expectancy is normal for cutaneous mastocytosis and most ISM; progression from ISM to a more advanced form is uncommon (~5% lifetime risk). In SM-AHNMD, outcome is determined by the associated non-mast cell hematologic disorder. SSM carries an intermediate prognosis. Aggressive SM and mast cell leukemia have a poor prognosis, and mast cell leukemia is invariably fatal at present. In infants and children with cutaneous disease, visceral involvement is usually absent and spontaneous resolution before adolescence is common; progression from cutaneous mastocytosis to ISM occurs in ~10% of children, especially with high mast cell burden or hematologic abnormalities (p.2820).","pitfalls":["Atopy is not increased in mastocytosis compared with the general population (p.2819).","Imatinib is not effective in most cases because the KIT D816V mutation confers resistance (p.2821).","Osteoporosis alone without osteolytic lesions does not satisfy the C-finding skeletal criterion for aggressive SM (p.2821).","SM cannot be confirmed on clinical and laboratory grounds alone; tissue diagnosis is required (p.2821).","Anaphylaxis in SM typically lacks the urticaria, angioedema, and bronchospasm seen in IgE-mediated or idiopathic anaphylaxis (p.2821).","Baseline serum tryptase elevation must be interpreted with caution where hereditary alpha-tryptasemia (extra TPSAB1 copies) coexists (p.2821)."],"severityClassification":"Consensus classification: Cutaneous mastocytosis (CM) - maculopapular cutaneous mastocytosis (MPCM/urticaria pigmentosa), solitary mastocytoma of skin, diffuse cutaneous mastocytosis. Systemic mastocytosis (SM) - indolent SM (ISM, >70% of adults, normal life expectancy), smoldering SM (SSM; >=2 B findings, no C findings: high mast cell burden with marrow infiltration >30%, basal tryptase >200 ng/mL, and/or KIT D816V VAF >10%, dysmyelopoiesis, organomegaly), SM with associated clonal hematologic non-mast cell lineage disease (SM-AHNMD), aggressive SM (ASM; >=1 C finding - cytopenias, hepatic dysfunction with ascites, hypersplenism, malabsorption with weight loss, osteolytic fractures), mast cell leukemia (MCL, rarest, invariably fatal). Mast cell sarcoma (MCS) is a rare invasive solid mast cell tumor (Tables 366-1, 366-2; p.2820).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2819-2821"],"crossLinks":["anaphylaxis"]},"measles":{"name":"Measles (Rubeola)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":211,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1638","clinicalPearls":["Caused by measles virus, a paramyxovirus; transmitted primarily by respiratory droplets and, less commonly, by small-particle aerosols that can remain suspended in air, making it highly transmissible (Harrison 22e p.1639).","Incubation is ~10 days to fever onset and ~14 days to rash onset; this period may be shorter in infants and longer (up to 3 weeks) in adults (Harrison 22e p.1640).","Classic prodrome is fever and malaise beginning ~10 days after exposure, followed by the three Cs: cough, coryza, and conjunctivitis, increasing in severity over ~4 days (Harrison 22e p.1640).","Koplik's spots (bluish-white ~1 mm dots surrounded by erythema on the buccal mucosa opposite the lower molars) are pathognomonic, appear ~2 days before the rash, and fade with rash onset (Harrison 22e p.1641).","The exanthem is an erythematous maculopapular rash beginning behind the ears and on the neck/hairline, spreading cephalocaudally to face, trunk, arms, and then legs/feet by the end of the second day; it fades in the same order and may desquamate, especially in undernourished children (Harrison 22e p.1641).","Care is largely supportive; vitamin A is recommended for children with measles and can markedly reduce morbidity and mortality (Harrison 22e p.1641).","Two doses of live attenuated measles vaccine (commonly as MMR or MMRV) are highly protective; ~85% develop protective antibody after one dose at 9 months and ~95% at 12 months (Harrison 22e p.1642).","Post-exposure human immunoglobulin within 72 h usually prevents infection and, given up to 6 days after exposure, can still prevent or modify disease in susceptible high-risk contacts (Harrison 22e p.1642).","Endemic transmission was eliminated in the United States in 2000, but outbreaks recur from imported cases amplified in under- and unvaccinated communities; vaccine hesitancy and pandemic-related disruption of immunization programs have driven a global resurgence (Harrison 22e p.1638, p.1369)."],"pathophysiology":"Measles virus is deposited in the respiratory tract, oropharynx, or conjunctivae, then proliferates locally in dendritic cells and lymphocytes and spreads to draining lymph nodes. It enters the bloodstream by budding from infected lymphocytes, producing a viremia that disseminates infection throughout the body (respiratory epithelium, lymphatic tissue, spleen, lung, thymus, liver, skin). Signs and symptoms appear 8-12 days after infection as virus replicates in target organs and the host immune response develops; the characteristic rash is itself a consequence of the cellular immune response. TH1 (interferon-gamma) responses are essential for viral clearance and later TH2 (interleukin-4) responses drive protective antibody. Paradoxically, measles induces a state of immunosuppression lasting weeks to months that depresses responses to unrelated antigens, reduces the magnitude and diversity of pre-existing antibody (\"immune amnesia\"), and increases susceptibility to secondary bacterial and viral infections, accounting for much measles-related morbidity and death.","additionalDifferentials":["Rubella (German measles) - milder, no cough, with posterior auricular/suboccipital lymphadenopathy","Kawasaki disease","Infectious mononucleosis (atypical lymphocytosis, in contrast to the leukopenia common in measles)","Roseola (exanthem subitum) - rash appears after fever subsides","Scarlet fever","Rocky Mountain spotted fever","Enterovirus or adenovirus infection","Drug sensitivity/eruption"],"infectionMimics":["Rubella","Infectious mononucleosis (Epstein-Barr virus)","Roseola/exanthem subitum (HHV-6/HHV-7)","Scarlet fever","Rocky Mountain spotted fever","Enterovirus (echovirus, coxsackievirus) infection","Adenovirus infection","Early meningococcemia or rickettsial infection (morbilliform before petechiae/purpura)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Kawasaki disease","Drug eruption / drug sensitivity","Acute graft-versus-host disease"],"additionalInvestigations":["Detection of measles virus-specific IgM in a single serum or oral fluid specimen is diagnostic of acute infection","Fourfold or greater rise in measles virus-specific IgG between acute- and convalescent-phase sera","Note timing pitfall: IgM may not be detectable until 4-5 days after rash onset (false-negatives if drawn too early) and usually falls to undetectable within 4-8 weeks","Reverse-transcription PCR for measles virus RNA (sensitive/specific; can genotype and distinguish wild-type from vaccine strain)","Virus isolation in cell culture from respiratory secretions, nasopharyngeal/conjunctival swabs, blood, or urine","Direct detection of giant cells in respiratory secretions, urine, or biopsy tissue","Neutralization tests (highly correlated with protective immunity but expensive and laborious)"],"redFlags":["Pneumonia - secondary bacterial bronchopneumonia or measles giant cell pneumonitis (the latter in immunocompromised persons); recurrence or persistence of fever after the rash suggests secondary bacterial infection","Acute laryngotracheobronchitis (croup) with risk of airway obstruction, particularly in young children","Post-measles encephalomyelitis (~1 in 1000 cases) within 2 weeks of rash onset with fever, seizures, and neurologic abnormalities - thought to be autoimmune","Measles inclusion body encephalitis (MIBE) - rare, fatal, in those with defective cellular immunity, months after infection","Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (SSPE) - slowly progressive, fatal, 5-15 years after infection (mostly in those infected at <2 years)","Diarrhea worsening undernutrition","Absent or delayed rash in immunocompromised or severely undernourished children with impaired cellular immunity - associated with a high case-fatality rate"],"prognosis":"Most persons recover and develop long-term (generally lifelong) protective immunity. Case-fatality varies with age at infection, nutritional and immunologic status, vaccine coverage, and access to care. In most developed countries the case-fatality rate is 0.01-0.1%, but in endemic areas of sub-Saharan Africa it may be 5-10% or higher, and in refugee camps and internally displaced populations rates have reached 20-30%. Previously vaccinated persons who become infected have less severe disease and significantly lower mortality. Measles still causes more than 100,000 deaths per year worldwide.","pitfalls":["Patients are contagious before recognizable disease appears, which undermines isolation measures; nosocomial transmission in waiting rooms, hospitals, and physicians' offices is well recognized.","Clinical diagnosis is difficult during the prodrome, when the rash is attenuated by passively acquired antibody or prior immunization, when the rash is absent/delayed in immunocompromised or severely undernourished children, and in low-incidence regions.","Measles virus-specific IgM may be falsely negative if serum is obtained too early (within the first few days of rash onset).","Persons with impaired cellular immunity may not develop the characteristic rash, may develop giant cell pneumonitis, and have a high case-fatality rate.","There is no specific antiviral therapy; ribavirin benefit has not been conclusively demonstrated in trials.","The 1998 report hypothesizing an MMR-autism link was a misinterpreted temporal association, has been refuted by numerous studies, and was retracted - there is no causal link."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not give a formal grading scale. Severity is driven by host and population factors: severe or fatal disease occurs in those with impaired/deficient T-lymphocyte (cellular) immunity and in malnourished/undernourished children (e.g., vitamin A deficiency), among whom the rash may be absent and giant cell pneumonitis and high case-fatality occur; pregnant women, immunocompromised persons, and infants <1 year are at risk of severe measles and are prioritized for immunoglobulin prophylaxis.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1638","Harrison 22e p.1639","Harrison 22e p.1640","Harrison 22e p.1641","Harrison 22e p.1642","Harrison 22e p.1117 (SSPE)","Harrison 22e p.1369 (travel/outbreaks)"],"crossLinks":["ENCEPHALITIS"]},"meningococcal_disease":{"name":"Meningococcal Disease","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":160,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1244-1253","clinicalPearls":["Neisseria meningitidis most commonly causes asymptomatic nasopharyngeal colonization; invasive disease is rare and usually presents as either bacterial meningitis or meningococcal septicemia (p.1244).","A nonblanching petechial or purpuric rash develops in over 80% of cases, but it is often absent early in the illness and may begin as a blanching maculopapular or urticarial eruption indistinguishable from common viral rashes before becoming petechial/purpuric over hours; some patients, including those with overwhelming sepsis, have no rash at all (p.1248).","In the most severe cases large purpuric lesions develop (purpura fulminans) with signs of peripheral ischemia (p.1248).","Roughly 30-50% present with a meningitis syndrome alone, up to 40% of meningitis patients also have features of septicemia, and meningococcal septicemia alone accounts for up to 20% of cases (p.1248).","In fulminant disease death may occur within hours of the first symptoms; early septicemic symptoms are nonspecific and influenza-like (fever, headache, myalgia, vomiting, abdominal pain) (p.1248).","In children, hypotension is a late sign; they more commonly present with compensated shock (tachycardia, poor peripheral perfusion, normal blood pressure) (p.1248).","Terminal complement component (C5-9), properdin or factor D deficiency, and treatment with complement inhibitors such as eculizumab, increase the risk of meningococcal disease (up to ~600-fold) and may cause recurrent attacks (p.1247).","Chronic meningococcemia is rarely recognized and presents as recurrent episodes of petechial rash with fever, joint pain/arthritis, and splenomegaly, with bacteremia that characteristically clears without treatment then recurs (p.1248-1249).","Tobacco smoking (and passive smoke exposure) is an important risk factor that damages the nasopharyngeal epithelium and increases both carriage and invasive disease (p.1247).","Immunization is regarded as the only rational approach to prevention at a population level; chemoprophylaxis of close contacts aims to eradicate carriage of the invasive strain (p.1250-1253)."],"pathophysiology":"N. meningitidis colonizes the human nasopharynx, then in a susceptible individual may invade the mucosa into the bloodstream, usually within a few days of acquisition. The polysaccharide capsule is a key virulence factor providing resistance to phagocytosis; acapsular strains rarely cause invasive disease. During growth meningococci shed outer-membrane blebs containing lipopolysaccharide (LPS, endotoxin), which binds CD14/TLR4 to trigger an inflammatory cascade (TNF-alpha, IL-1, IL-6, IL-8, IL-10, PAI-1 and others). Disease severity relates to both endotoxin levels and the magnitude of the inflammatory response. Endothelial injury is central to meningococcemia, causing increased vascular permeability (capillary leak with hypovolemia, tissue and pulmonary edema), altered vascular tone, loss of thromboresistance, intravascular coagulation, and myocardial dysfunction. Procoagulant activation with tissue factor upregulation, downregulation of anticoagulant pathways (loss of thrombomodulin/protein C receptors; reduced antithrombin III, protein C, protein S, tissue factor pathway inhibitor) and impaired thrombolysis from high PAI-1 produce purpura fulminans and skin/limb infarction. Shock results from hypovolemia plus myocardial depression (hypoxia, metabolic derangements such as hypocalcemia, cytokines). Bacteria reaching the meninges provoke a local cytokine-driven inflammatory response that can cause cerebral edema and raised intracranial pressure (p.1247-1248).","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of bacterial meningitis (presentation of meningococcal meningitis is indistinguishable unless an associated petechial/purpuric rash or gram-negative diplococci are present) (p.1248-1249)","Differential of chronic meningococcemia: bacterial endocarditis, acute rheumatic fever, Henoch-Schonlein purpura, infectious mononucleosis, disseminated gonococcal infection, immune-mediated vasculitis (p.1248-1249)"],"infectionMimics":["Viral infections (enteroviruses, influenza and other respiratory viruses, measles, Epstein-Barr virus, cytomegalovirus, parvovirus) - most patients presenting with a petechial/purpuric rash have a viral infection (p.1248)","Pneumococcal, streptococcal, staphylococcal, or other gram-negative bacterial sepsis (p.1248)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Protein C or S deficiency (including post-varicella protein S deficiency) (p.1248)","Platelet disorders (e.g., idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura, drug effects, bone marrow infiltration) (p.1248)","Henoch-Schonlein purpura, connective tissue disorders, and trauma (including nonaccidental injury in children) (p.1248)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood culture for confirmation and public health investigation (positive in up to ~75% of cases); avoid media containing sodium polyanethol sulfonate, which may inhibit meningococcal growth, and avoid delay in transport (p.1249)","Real-time PCR of whole blood increases diagnostic yield by more than 40% and may remain positive for several days after antibiotics (p.1249)","Lumbar puncture for CSF in suspected meningitis (unless contraindicated); CSF Gram stain is up to ~80% sensitive, CSF culture sensitivity ~90%, plus PCR where available; CSF latex agglutination antigen testing is insensitive and should be replaced by molecular diagnosis (p.1249)","Inflammatory markers (WBC count, C-reactive protein, procalcitonin, ESR) may support the diagnosis but can be normal or low in rapidly progressive disease and do not exclude it (p.1249)","Severe septicemia laboratory findings: hypoglycemia, acidosis, hypokalemia, hypocalcemia, hypomagnesemia, hypophosphatemia, anemia, coagulopathy (p.1249)","Measure total hemolytic complement activity to assess for complement deficiency in patients with recurrent disease, particularly non-B capsular groups (p.1247)"],"redFlags":["A nonblanching (petechial or purpuric) rash in a febrile patient should be treated as a medical emergency suggestive of meningococcal disease (p.1248)","Purpura fulminans / large purpuric lesions with peripheral ischemia (p.1248)","Signs of shock: tachycardia, poor peripheral perfusion, tachypnea, oliguria, cold mottled extremities, limb pain (hypotension is a late sign in children) (p.1248)","Reduced level of consciousness, agitation, or confusion (decreased cerebral perfusion or raised intracranial pressure) (p.1248)","Features of raised intracranial pressure: reduced consciousness, relative bradycardia and hypertension, focal neurologic signs, abnormal posturing, unequal/dilated/poorly reactive pupils (p.1248)","Rapid progression - death may occur within hours of first symptoms in fulminant cases (p.1248)"],"prognosis":"About 10% of patients with meningococcal disease die despite antimicrobial therapy and intensive care. Fulminant meningococcemia is more likely to cause death or ischemic skin loss than meningitis alone. Factors associated with poorer prognosis include shock; extremes of age (infancy and old age) and adolescence; coma; purpura fulminans; disseminated intravascular coagulation; thrombocytopenia; leukopenia; absence of meningitis; metabolic acidosis; low antithrombin and protein S/C levels; high PAI-1; and a low ESR or C-reactive protein. The Glasgow Meningococcal Septicaemia Prognostic Score (GMSPS) performs well for severity assessment, though scoring systems do not direct specific interventions; the management priority remains recognition and urgent treatment of compromised airway, breathing, or circulation. Most patients improve rapidly with appropriate antibiotics and supportive care. Complications include scarring after purpuric skin necrosis (~10%, sometimes requiring skin grafting), amputation in 1-2% of survivors after peripheral ischemia or compartment syndrome, hearing loss (~5%), neurologic complications (~7%), chronic pain, and psychological/psychosocial sequelae (p.1249-1250).","pitfalls":["A normal or low WBC count and inflammatory markers do not exclude meningococcal disease and may occur in rapidly progressive disease (p.1249)","The rash may be absent early or absent altogether (including in overwhelming sepsis), and may initially appear blanching/viral before becoming petechial or purpuric (p.1248)","Most patients with a petechial/purpuric rash and fever do NOT have meningococcal disease - fewer than 10% of children (and in some settings under 1%) with this presentation are found to have it, so most have a viral cause (p.1248)","Lumbar puncture should generally be avoided in meningococcal septicemia because positioning may critically compromise circulation in hypovolemic shock; it is also contraindicated with raised intracranial pressure, uncorrected shock, coagulopathy, thrombocytopenia, respiratory insufficiency, local infection, or ongoing convulsions (p.1249)","A normal CT brain scan does not exclude raised intracranial pressure in meningococcal meningitis (p.1249)","CSF latex agglutination antigen testing and urinary antigen testing are insensitive; throat-swab identification has limited diagnostic value because N. meningitidis is normal nasopharyngeal flora (p.1249)","Postmeningococcal reactive (immune-complex) disease arising ~4-10 days after onset (rash, arthritis, iritis, pericarditis/polyserositis with fever) can be mistaken for relapse and lead to unnecessarily prolonged antibiotics; it resolves spontaneously (p.1249)","Aggressive fluid resuscitation with unbuffered electrolyte solutions was found to increase mortality in febrile African children (p.1249-1250)"],"severityClassification":"Clinical spectrum spans asymptomatic nasopharyngeal carriage, occult bacteremia, localized/focal infection (pneumonia, arthritis, osteomyelitis, pericarditis, conjunctivitis), meningitis, and meningococcal septicemia, with chronic meningococcemia as a relapsing form. The Glasgow Meningococcal Septicaemia Prognostic Score (GMSPS) is used for severity assessment. Fulminant meningococcemia with purpura fulminans and Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome (overwhelming meningococcal sepsis) represents the most severe end of the spectrum (p.1244, p.1248-1249).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1244","Harrison 22e p.1247","Harrison 22e p.1248","Harrison 22e p.1249","Harrison 22e p.1250","Harrison 22e p.1253"],"crossLinks":["MENINGITIS","SEPSIS","SEPTIC_SHOCK"]},"mitral_regurgitation":{"name":"Mitral Regurgitation","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":275,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2052","clinicalPearls":["Distinguish primary (degenerative) MR, where the leaflets and/or chordae are themselves abnormal, from secondary (functional) MR, where leaflets and chordae are usually normal and regurgitation is driven by LV/LA remodeling, annular dilation, papillary muscle displacement, dyssynchrony, or leaflet tethering; assessment, treatment, and prognosis differ significantly between the two (p.2052).","The characteristic murmur of chronic severe MR is a holosystolic, plateau-configuration systolic murmur of at least grade III/VI, most prominent at the apex and radiating to the axilla (p.2053-2054).","Murmur radiation reflects jet direction: with posterior leaflet prolapse/flail the eccentric anteriorly-directed jet strikes the LA wall near the aortic root and the murmur radiates to the base (can mimic aortic stenosis), whereas anterior leaflet prolapse/flail directs the jet and murmur toward the axilla (p.2054).","Ruptured chordae tendineae may give the systolic murmur a cooing or 'seagull' quality; a flail leaflet may produce a musical murmur (p.2054).","The murmur of chronic MR not due to MVP is intensified by isometric handgrip and reduced during the strain phase of Valsalva (decreased LV preload) (p.2054).","In acute severe MR the murmur is early in timing and decrescendo, ending well before S2, reflecting the rapidly rising LA pressure and diminishing LV-LA gradient — in contrast to the holosystolic plateau murmur of chronic MR (p.2053-2054).","Because EF rises in severe MR when LV function is normal, even a modest fall in EF (below 60%) reflects significant contractile dysfunction (p.2052-2053).","Atrial fibrillation is almost invariably present once the LA dilates significantly in chronic severe MR; LA enlargement and AF support the diagnosis on ECG (p.2053).","'MR begets MR': LA enlargement tensions the posterior leaflet pulling it away from the orifice, and LV dilation increases regurgitation, enlarging LA and LV further in a vicious cycle (p.2052).","With post-MI papillary muscle rupture the posteromedial papillary muscle is involved far more often than the anterolateral muscle because of its single blood supply (p.2052)."],"pathophysiology":"MR can arise from disease of any of the five components of the mitral apparatus: leaflets, annulus, chordae tendineae, papillary muscles, and subjacent myocardium (p.2052). MR reduces resistance to LV emptying (lowers afterload); the LV decompresses into the LA during ejection, so initial compensation is more complete LV emptying. Over time LV volume rises as regurgitation worsens and contractile function deteriorates, and forward cardiac output falls; LV compliance is often increased so LV diastolic pressure does not rise until late (p.2052-2053). The regurgitant volume varies directly with LV systolic pressure and the size of the regurgitant orifice (influenced by LV and annular dilation and leaflet morphology). LA compliance governs the clinical picture: in acute severe MR the regurgitant volume enters a normal-sized, noncompliant LA so LA and pulmonary venous pressures rise sharply with a prominent v wave and pulmonary edema is common; in chronic severe MR the LA enlarges and becomes more compliant, buffering pressure so pulmonary congestion is initially less prominent and fatigue from low forward output predominates (p.2053). Chronic secondary MR results from annular dilation, ventricular remodeling, papillary muscle displacement, and leaflet tethering (e.g., after MI, in dilated cardiomyopathy once LV end-diastolic dimension reaches ~6 cm, or atrial functional MR from chronic AF with annular dilation) (p.2052).","additionalDifferentials":["Aortic stenosis (basally-radiating MR murmur from posterior leaflet prolapse/flail can be confused with AS) (p.2054)","Mitral stenosis (a brief early-diastolic LA-LV gradient with rapid filling sound and mid-diastolic rumble in pure severe MR can masquerade as MS) (p.2053-2054)","Ventricular septal defect and other causes of a holosystolic murmur"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography (TTE) with Doppler to define mechanism and severity (jet width/area, regurgitant volume, regurgitant fraction, effective regurgitant orifice area, LV volumes/EF, LA/LV size, chordal integrity, annular calcification) (p.2053)","Transesophageal echocardiography (TEE) for greater anatomic detail (p.2053-2054)","Estimation of pulmonary artery systolic pressure from the tricuspid regurgitation jet velocity (p.2053)","Exercise (stress) echocardiography to assess exercise capacity and dynamic change in MR severity, PA pressures, and biventricular function when clinical and resting findings disagree (p.2053)","Cardiac magnetic resonance (CMR) for quantitative regurgitant measures when TTE is discordant or insufficient (noted as not widely available) (p.2052-2053)","ECG (LA enlargement, possible RA enlargement with pulmonary hypertension, AF, eccentric LVH) (p.2053)","Chest X-ray (LA/LV enlargement, pulmonary venous congestion, Kerley B lines, mitral/annular calcification; asymmetric pulmonary edema possible in acute MR) (p.2053-2054)","Left and right heart catheterization with contrast ventriculography and coronary angiography when noninvasive findings are discrepant or to identify coronary disease before surgery (p.2053, p.2056)"],"redFlags":["Acute severe MR is a surgical emergency: papillary muscle rupture post-MI or leaflet destruction/perforation in infective endocarditis can cause sudden pulmonary edema and hemodynamic collapse, requiring urgent stabilization and emergent surgery (p.2052, p.2055-2056)","Acute pulmonary edema, hypotension with narrow pulse pressure, and prominent signs of pulmonary congestion with a non-displaced apex point to acute severe MR (p.2053)","Onset of palpitations or atrial fibrillation in chronic severe MR (p.2053)","Late-onset right heart failure with hepatic congestion, ankle edema, distended neck veins, ascites, and secondary tricuspid regurgitation, indicating pulmonary vascular disease and pulmonary hypertension (p.2053)","Falling LVEF (to 60% or below) or rising LV end-systolic dimension (40 mm or more), even when asymptomatic, signals contractile dysfunction warranting surgical referral (p.2053, p.2056)"],"prognosis":"Chronic mild-to-moderate isolated MR is well tolerated and usually asymptomatic. Chronic severe MR tends to be progressive ('MR begets MR') and, when secondary, severity correlates inversely with prognosis, with adverse outcome tied to the degree of LV remodeling and myocardial dysfunction (p.2052-2053). With surgery, repair carries substantially lower risk than replacement; a 2023 STS database analysis reported mean perioperative mortality of about 1.16% (under 0.5% in patients younger than 65) for repair of primary MR, durable repair is feasible in up to 95% of degenerative cases at high-volume centers, and reoperation runs about 1% per year over the first 10 years. In ischemic MR with severely impaired LV function (EF below 30%), surgical risk is higher, recovery of LV performance is incomplete, and long-term survival is reduced (p.2055-2056).","pitfalls":["In secondary MR the severity can be underappreciated by echocardiographic/Doppler techniques, and lesser degrees of regurgitation may carry relatively greater prognostic weight (p.2053).","An S3 in MR may reflect rapid LV filling, but in ischemic or dilated cardiomyopathy an S3 may instead signify ventricular dysfunction (p.2054).","Because EF is artifactually elevated in severe MR with normal myocardium, a 'low-normal' EF may already represent meaningful contractile dysfunction (p.2052-2053).","Routine surgical valve repair for significant secondary MR due to dilated cardiomyopathy has NOT been shown to improve long-term survival versus optimal guideline-directed medical therapy, especially in the era of quadruple HFrEF therapy (p.2056).","Direct oral anticoagulants should NOT be used if moderate or severe rheumatic mitral stenosis coexists, and are NOT approved for mechanical prosthetic valves (p.2055).","A presystolic murmur is not ordinarily heard with isolated MR (p.2054)."],"severityClassification":"Chronic severe MR (Doppler/CMR quantitative criteria) is defined by a regurgitant volume of at least 60 mL/beat, regurgitant fraction of at least 50%, and effective regurgitant orifice area of at least 0.40 cm2 (p.2053). ACC/AHA disease staging (Stages B, C1, C2, D) is applied: Stage C is asymptomatic severe MR (C1 normal LV systolic function with LVEF above 60% and ESD below 40 mm; C2 LV systolic dysfunction with LVEF at or below 60% or ESD at or above 40 mm) and Stage D is symptomatic severe MR (p.2054-2055).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2052"],"crossLinks":["IE","heart_failure","atrial_fib"]},"mitral_stenosis":{"name":"Mitral Stenosis","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":274,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2048","clinicalPearls":["Rheumatic fever is the leading cause of mitral stenosis; pure or predominant MS occurs in roughly 40% of patients with rheumatic heart disease and a history of rheumatic fever (p.2048).","Although declining in temperate, middle- to high-income climates, MS remains a major problem in low-income countries, especially sub-Saharan Africa, India, Southeast Asia, and Oceania (p.2048).","Auscultation classically reveals an accentuated first heart sound (S1), an opening snap (OS) of the mitral valve heard best in expiration at or just medial to the apex, followed by a low-pitched rumbling diastolic murmur heard best at the apex in the left lateral recumbent position (p.2049).","The A2-to-opening-snap interval varies inversely with severity of MS: a shorter interval indicates more severe stenosis (p.2049).","In patients in sinus rhythm, the diastolic murmur often reappears or becomes louder during atrial systole (presystolic accentuation) (p.2049).","Development of persistent atrial fibrillation often marks a turning point in the patient's course and is associated with acceleration of symptom progression (p.2048).","Echocardiography (TTE with Doppler) provides mitral orifice area, transvalvular gradients, leaflet calcification/restriction, and suitability for percutaneous mitral balloon commissurotomy; TEE is especially indicated to exclude LA thrombus before intervention (p.2049).","Mitral valve area 1-1.5 cm2 denotes severe MS; an LA pressure of about 25 mmHg is needed to maintain normal cardiac output when the orifice falls below 1.5 cm2 (p.2048-2049)."],"pathophysiology":"In normal adults the mitral valve orifice area is 4-6 cm2. Significant obstruction occurs when the orifice is reduced to less than about 2 cm2, so blood can only flow from the left atrium to the left ventricle if propelled by an abnormally elevated left atrioventricular pressure gradient, the hemodynamic hallmark of MS. When the orifice is below 1.5 cm2 (severe MS), an LA pressure of about 25 mmHg is required to maintain normal cardiac output. Elevated pulmonary venous and PA wedge pressures reduce pulmonary compliance and cause exertional dyspnea. Because tachycardia shortens diastole proportionately more than systole, an increased heart rate (including rapid AF) augments the transvalvular gradient and further elevates LA pressure. Chronic LA pressure elevation leads to LA enlargement, predisposing to AF and to thrombus formation (particularly in the LA appendage) with risk of systemic embolization. Pulmonary hypertension develops from passive backward transmission of elevated LA pressure, reactive pulmonary arteriolar constriction (the so-called second stenosis), interstitial edema of small pulmonary vessels, and, at end stage, obliterative pulmonary vascular changes; severe pulmonary hypertension produces RV enlargement, secondary tricuspid and pulmonic regurgitation, and right-sided heart failure (p.2048-2049).","additionalDifferentials":["Mitral regurgitation (may have an apical diastolic flow murmur, but it begins slightly later, S1 is soft or absent, and there is no opening snap; LV enlargement is common) (p.2049)","Severe aortic regurgitation with an Austin Flint murmur (apical mid-diastolic murmur not intensified in presystole and softened by amyl nitrite/arterial vasodilators) (p.2049)","Tricuspid stenosis (rare without MS; its diastolic murmur increases with inspiration and the jugular venous y descent is delayed) (p.2049-2050)","Atrial septal defect (RV enlargement and increased pulmonary vascularity, but no LA enlargement or Kerley B lines, with fixed splitting of S2) (p.2050)","Left atrial myxoma (obstructs LA emptying causing a positional diastolic murmur; often with systemic features such as weight loss, fever, anemia, emboli, elevated IgG/IL-6) (p.2050)"],"infectionMimics":["Infective endocarditis with large vegetations is itself a recognized (uncommon) cause of left ventricular inflow obstruction mimicking MS (p.2048)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Congenital mitral valve stenosis (parachute valve, cor triatriatum) (p.2048)","Severe mitral annular calcification with extension onto the leaflets (p.2048)","Systemic lupus erythematosus and rheumatoid arthritis involving the valve (p.2048)","Left atrial myxoma (p.2048)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography with color flow and spectral Doppler for mitral inflow velocities, peak/mean gradients, mitral orifice area, leaflet calcification and restriction, and suitability for percutaneous mitral balloon commissurotomy (p.2049)","Transesophageal echocardiography when TTE is inadequate, and specifically to exclude left atrial thrombus before commissurotomy (p.2049)","Exercise (stress) echocardiography to assess mean mitral diastolic gradient, PA pressures, and RV function when clinical and resting hemodynamic findings are discrepant (p.2049)","ECG showing left atrial enlargement (P-wave changes), and right axis deviation/RV hypertrophy when severe pulmonary hypertension supervenes (p.2049)","Chest X-ray showing straightening of the left cardiac border, prominent main PAs, upper-lobe pulmonary venous dilation, an enlarged LA, and Kerley B lines when mean LA pressure exceeds about 20 mmHg (p.2049)","Left and right heart catheterization when clinical and noninvasive findings are discrepant; CT coronary angiography to screen for coronary disease before valve surgery (p.2050)"],"redFlags":["Atrial fibrillation, which accelerates symptom progression and markedly increases thromboembolic risk (p.2048)","Systemic embolization (incidence 10-20%), more frequent with AF, age over 65, and reduced cardiac output; may be the presenting feature even in mild MS (p.2049)","Hemoptysis from rupture of pulmonary-bronchial venous connections due to pulmonary venous hypertension (p.2048)","Pulmonary hypertension and right-sided heart failure (hepatomegaly, ankle edema, ascites, pleural effusion) (p.2048-2049)","Recurrent pulmonary emboli, sometimes with infarction, an important late cause of morbidity and mortality (p.2048)","Decompensation precipitated by tachycardia, fever, severe anemia, paroxysmal AF, thyrotoxicosis, exertion, or pregnancy (p.2048)","In pregnancy, increased blood volume, cardiac output, and tachycardia can raise the transmitral gradient and cause pulmonary edema or tachyarrhythmias, with the immediate postpartum period a time of particular concern due to rapid volume shifts (p.3900)"],"prognosis":"In temperate climates the latent period between the initial attack of rheumatic carditis and symptomatic MS is generally about two decades, with disability typically beginning in the fourth decade. Historical studies before surgical valvotomy showed that once a patient became seriously symptomatic, the disease progressed inexorably to death within 2-5 years. Onset of persistent AF generally marks acceleration of symptom progression (p.2048).","pitfalls":["When cardiac output is markedly reduced, the typical auscultatory findings including the diastolic rumble may be undetectable (silent MS) and may reappear only as compensation is restored (p.2049)","Systemic embolization may be the presenting feature in otherwise asymptomatic patients with only mild MS (p.2049)","The Graham Steell murmur of pulmonic regurgitation can be indistinguishable from aortic regurgitation, though it may increase with inspiration and is accompanied by a loud, often palpable P2 (p.2049)","A history of rheumatic carditis is increasingly rare and often cannot be elicited (p.2048)"],"severityClassification":"Severity is graded by mitral valve area. The normal orifice is 4-6 cm2; significant obstruction begins below about 2 cm2. A valve area below 1.5 cm2 is termed severe MS (with an LA pressure of about 25 mmHg required to maintain normal cardiac output), and an area below 1 cm2 is very severe, at which cardiac output is subnormal at rest. Echocardiographic measurement of mitral orifice area and transvalvular gradient is the basis for grading; commissurotomy is generally indicated for symptomatic patients with valve area below about 1 cm2/m2 body surface area or below 1.5 cm2 in normal-sized adults (p.2048-2051).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2048"],"crossLinks":["atrial_fib","acute_rheumatic_fever","ischemic_stroke","aortic_stenosis"]},"movement_disorders":{"name":"Movement Disorders (Tremor, Chorea, Dystonia)","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":447,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3508","clinicalPearls":["Movement disorders are classified as hypokinetic (poverty of movement, e.g. parkinsonism with rest tremor, cogwheel rigidity, bradykinesia) versus hyperkinetic (excess movement, e.g. chorea, dystonia, tremor); the rest tremor and cogwheel rigidity of Parkinson's disease are extrapyramidal signs distinct from the action tremor of essential tremor (Harrison 22e p.3508; cf. p.197 ch.31).","Essential tremor is an action/postural tremor; a key pitfall is mislabeling it as Parkinson's disease, but Parkinson tremor is characteristically a rest tremor accompanied by cogwheel rigidity and bradykinesia, whereas essential tremor lacks these and worsens with sustained posture or movement (Harrison 22e p.3508).","Huntington's disease is an autosomal dominant degenerative disorder producing the triad of chorea, psychiatric/behavioral disturbance (mood, anxiety, psychosis), and progressive dementia; the unpredictable choreic movements give the gait a characteristic dancing quality (Harrison 22e p.3508; gait described p.~3535 ch.32; HD as autosomal-dominant dementia p.198 ch.31).","Dystonia is defined as sustained muscle contractions producing repetitive twisting movements and abnormal posture; it often has a genetic basis, but new asymmetric dystonia—particularly involving gait—in a younger adult may be an early sign of Parkinson's disease (Harrison 22e p.3508; cf. gait chapter p.~3535).","Sydenham's chorea (a manifestation of acute rheumatic fever after group A streptococcal infection) is the prototypical post-infectious chorea: it occurs mainly in females after a prolonged latent period, affects especially the head (darting tongue movements) and upper limbs, may be generalized or hemichoreic, and is frequently accompanied by emotional lability or obsessive-compulsive traits; >50% of patients presenting with chorea have carditis, so echocardiography is warranted (Harrison 22e p.3508; ch. on rheumatic fever p.~2992).","Tardive dyskinesia is a drug-induced hyperkinetic disorder seen with chronic exposure to antipsychotics and other agents that block the D2 dopamine receptor, and is a common cause of stereotypic movement and gait disturbances (Harrison 22e p.3508)."],"pathophysiology":"Movement disorders arise predominantly from dysfunction of the basal ganglia (striatum = caudate plus putamen) and their cortical and thalamic connections, which modulate motor output rather than directly generating it; disruption of these circuits yields either hypokinetic syndromes (parkinsonism) or hyperkinetic syndromes (chorea, dystonia, tremor). Huntington's disease is caused by an expanded CAG trinucleotide repeat (autosomal dominant) with degeneration of the striatum. Wilson's disease results from impaired copper excretion with copper accumulation in liver and brain. Tardive dyskinesia reflects chronic blockade of striatal D2 dopamine receptors (Harrison 22e p.3508; basal ganglia/striatum anatomy p.~199-200 ch.31).","additionalDifferentials":["Parkinson's disease (rest tremor, cogwheel rigidity, bradykinesia) — see parkinsons_disease","Progressive supranuclear palsy (axial rigidity, vertical gaze palsy, early falls)","Multiple-system atrophy and corticobasal syndrome (asymmetric akinesia, rigidity, dystonia, myoclonus, alien limb)","Orthostatic tremor (high-frequency low-amplitude leg tremor, shakiness on standing relieved by sitting/walking)","Drug-induced/tardive dyskinesia from D2-blocking agents","Sydenham's chorea / post-streptococcal autoimmune chorea"],"infectionMimics":["Sydenham's chorea following group A streptococcal infection (acute rheumatic fever)","Other post-infectious or autoimmune (paraneoplastic) chorea and movement disorders"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Wilson's disease (copper accumulation; young patient with chorea, dystonia, parkinsonism or tremor)","Drug-induced movements from dopamine-receptor blockers (tardive dyskinesia, acute dystonia)","Functional (psychogenic) movement disorder","Hyperthyroidism (fine tremor; chorea rare)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing for the CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion when Huntington's disease is suspected","Serum ceruloplasmin (low), 24-h urinary copper (elevated), slit-lamp exam for Kayser-Fleischer rings, and liver copper to evaluate for Wilson's disease in any young patient with unexplained chorea, dystonia, tremor or parkinsonism","Echocardiography when chorea suggests acute rheumatic fever (>50% have carditis); anti-streptolysin O and anti-DNase B titers for preceding streptococcal infection","MRI brain to assess for basal ganglia and other structural/degenerative changes"],"redFlags":["New chorea, dystonia, tremor or parkinsonism in an adolescent or young adult — exclude Wilson's disease (treatable; look for Kayser-Fleischer rings, low ceruloplasmin, high urinary copper) before manifestations become irreversible","Chorea with autosomal dominant family history plus psychiatric change and cognitive decline — consider Huntington's disease","Acute chorea (especially in a young female) — evaluate for acute rheumatic fever and carditis with echocardiography","Abnormal movements in a patient on long-term antipsychotics/D2 blockers — recognize tardive dyskinesia and acute dystonic reactions"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is disorder-specific. Essential tremor is typically slowly progressive and non-degenerative. Huntington's disease is relentlessly progressive and degenerative. Wilson's disease can improve substantially with prompt diagnosis and copper-chelating treatment before end-stage manifestations become irreversible. Sydenham's chorea is usually self-limited, with choreiform movements resolving within about 6 weeks, occasionally up to 6 months, though associated obsessive-compulsive or emotional symptoms may persist longer (Harrison 22e p.3508; Wilson p.~2713 ch.355; chorea p.~2992).","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing essential (action) tremor as Parkinson's disease, or vice versa — Parkinson tremor is a rest tremor with cogwheel rigidity and bradykinesia","Failing to test for Wilson's disease in a young patient with new movement disorder; Wilson's may present with chronic hepatitis long before neurologic signs or Kayser-Fleischer rings appear","Overlooking carditis in a patient presenting with chorea (Sydenham's), since chorea may be the sole or delayed manifestation of rheumatic fever","Not recognizing drug-induced/tardive movements from D2-blocking medications"],"severityClassification":"No single unified severity scale spans all movement disorders; assessment is disorder-specific and functional. Chorea, for example, ranges from movements evident only on careful examination in mild cases to inability to perform activities of daily living in severe cases (Harrison 22e p.3508).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3508"],"crossLinks":["parkinsons_disease"]},"mucormycosis":{"name":"Mucormycosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":224,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1717","clinicalPearls":["Mucormycosis is a group of life-threatening infections caused by fungi of the order Mucorales (subphylum Mucoromycotina); it is highly invasive and relentlessly progressive, with morbidity/mortality higher than many other infections (Harrison 22e p.1717).","Rhizopus oryzae and R. delemar (family Mucoraceae) are by far the most common causes in the Western Hemisphere; Apophysomyces elegans is a notable cause in India and Mucor irregularis in China (Harrison 22e p.1718).","Five well-defined clinical syndromes: rhino-orbital-cerebral, pulmonary, cutaneous, gastrointestinal, and disseminated; specific host defects predispose to specific syndromes (diabetes/DKA to rhino-orbital-cerebral; leukemia/HSCT to pulmonary) (Harrison 22e p.1718).","Rhino-orbital-cerebral disease is the most common form worldwide; early symptoms are nonspecific (eye/facial pain, facial numbness) progressing to conjunctival suffusion, blurry vision, proptosis, and chemosis (Harrison 22e p.1718-1719).","Biopsy with histopathology is the most sensitive and specific modality: wide (>=6-30 microm), thick-walled, ribbon-like, ASEPTATE hyphae branching at RIGHT angles, in contrast to thinner, septate, acute-angle-branching Aspergillus/Fusarium/Scedosporium (Harrison 22e p.1719).","Mucorales are best visualized with periodic acid-Schiff or H&E; methenamine silver may NOT stain them optimally (Harrison 22e p.1719).","Tissue for culture should be cut into sections and placed in the center of culture media, NOT homogenized, because homogenization kills the long filamentous hyphae and lowers culture yield; the lab should be alerted when mucormycosis is suspected (Harrison 22e p.1720).","It is important to distinguish mucormycosis from aspergillosis because treatments differ: polyenes for Mucorales versus expanded-spectrum triazoles for most septate molds; voriconazole is the first-line treatment for aspergillosis but EXACERBATES mucormycosis in animal models (Harrison 22e p.1719-1720)."],"pathophysiology":"Mucorales are ubiquitous environmental fungi to which humans are constantly exposed; they cause invasive, angioinvasive infection primarily in hosts with uncontrolled diabetes, phagocytic defects (neutropenia or high-dose corticosteroids), and/or elevated free iron, which supports fungal growth in serum and tissue. Iron-overloaded end-stage renal failure patients treated with the iron chelator deferoxamine had high risk of rapidly fatal disseminated disease, because deferoxamine acts as a fungal xenosiderophore that delivers iron directly to the Mucorales. In diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA), acidosis dissociates iron from sequestering proteins (enhancing fungal survival/virulence) and the ketoacid beta-hydroxybutyrate upregulates host and fungal receptors that increase fungal adherence and tissue penetration; however the majority of diabetic patients with mucormycosis are NOT acidotic on presentation. Even without acidosis, hyperglycemia raises risk via hyperglycation of iron-sequestering proteins, upregulation of the host receptor GRP78 (which binds Mucorales and enables tissue penetration), poorly characterized phagocytic defects, and enhanced expression of the Mucorales-specific invasin CotH (which binds GRP78). Blood vessel thrombosis and tissue necrosis impair antifungal penetration to the site of infection. (Harrison 22e p.1717-1718)","additionalDifferentials":["Aspergillosis","Scedosporiosis (Scedosporium species)","Fusariosis (Fusarium species)","Dematiaceous (brown-pigmented) mold infections","Entomophthoromycosis (Basidiobolus and Conidiobolus)","Bacterial orbital cellulitis / cavernous sinus thrombosis (Staphylococcus aureus, streptococci, gram-negative species)","Rhinoscleroma (Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis)","Tolosa-Hunt syndrome"],"infectionMimics":["Aspergillosis (septate mold, may co-infect with Mucorales)","Scedosporiosis","Fusariosis","Dematiaceous mold infection","Entomophthoromycosis (Basidiobolus, Conidiobolus)","Bacterial orbital cellulitis / cavernous sinus thrombosis (S. aureus, streptococci, gram-negatives)","Rhinoscleroma (Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Tolosa-Hunt syndrome (painful ophthalmoplegia, ptosis, headache, cavernous sinus inflammation; lacks progression)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Biopsy with histopathologic examination (most sensitive/specific; PAS or H&E staining)","Culture from a sterile site (needle aspirate, tissue biopsy, pleural fluid) for definitive species identification; tissue sectioned NOT homogenized, incubated at both room temperature and 37C","CT of the chest (best method to define extent of pulmonary disease; may detect infection before chest x-ray)","MRI of head/sinuses (more sensitive ~80% than CT for orbital and CNS extension)","Endoscopy and/or surgical exploration with biopsy of suspected areas; blind biopsy of normal-appearing sinus tissue when suspicion is high","Mucorales-specific PCR (useful but no FDA-approved assay)"],"redFlags":["Rhino-orbital-cerebral involvement with a black necrotic eschar and/or painful necrotic ulceration of the hard palate (late finding indicating extensive, well-established angioinvasive infection) is a surgical emergency requiring urgent debridement.","Bilateral proptosis, chemosis, vision loss, and ophthalmoplegia (contralateral eye involvement) suggest cavernous sinus thrombosis and are ominous.","Hyperglycemia or DKA with sinusitis and possible orbital extension, even without a known diabetes history, should raise high suspicion (presentation may be the first recognition of diabetes, often unmasked by recent glucocorticoids).","Necrotizing fasciitis from cutaneous mucormycosis (mortality approaching 80%) and necrotic skin lesions from hematogenous dissemination.","Disseminated disease (most lethal form), seen with antifungal prophylaxis breakthrough and in highly immunocompromised patients."],"prognosis":"Overall mortality remains high though it has declined with earlier initiation of effective antifungal therapy; outcome is often driven by progression of the underlying predisposing condition. Necrotizing fasciitis from cutaneous disease carries mortality approaching 80%, gastrointestinal disease progressing to perforation has extremely high mortality, and widely disseminated disease exceeds 90% mortality. In contrast, isolated nonnecrotizing cutaneous mucormycosis treated with prompt aggressive surgical debridement has a favorable prognosis and low mortality. Rate of progression is highly variable (days to a year or more), not invariably rapid. (Harrison 22e p.1717-1720)","pitfalls":["Misperception that mucormycosis is always rapidly progressive; rate of progression is in fact highly variable (days to a year or more).","Cultures are positive in fewer than half of cases, and up to half of cases are diagnosed only at autopsy; a negative culture does NOT exclude the diagnosis.","Standard tissue homogenization for culture kills the filamentous Mucorales and lowers yield; tissue must be sectioned and not homogenized, and the lab alerted.","Imaging underestimates disease: sinus CT may look like ordinary bacterial sinusitis, and bony erosion into the orbit is rarely seen on CT even when clinically present.","Treating with voriconazole (first-line for aspergillosis) can exacerbate mucormycosis; distinguish the two molds because preferred treatments differ, and consider dual infection in highly compromised patients.","Delaying treatment of the underlying malignancy until mucormycosis is 'cured' is a common error; antifungals provide only a holding action until near-normal immunity is restored, and untreated underlying disease is the typical primary driver of death.","Iron administration and liberal blood transfusion (free iron from hemolysis) should be avoided as iron exacerbates infection.","Therapy should not be delayed for diagnostic confirmation: when suspicion is reasonable, empirical lipid polyene therapy should be started early because earlier polyene therapy improves survival."],"severityClassification":"Harrison classifies mucormycosis by clinical syndrome rather than a numeric severity score: rhino-orbital-cerebral, pulmonary, cutaneous (ranging from isolated nonnecrotizing cutaneous disease with favorable prognosis to necrotizing fasciitis), gastrointestinal, and disseminated/miscellaneous disease, with mortality rising steeply from localized cutaneous disease to disseminated disease (>90%). (Harrison 22e p.1718-1720)","references":["Harrison 22e p.1717"],"crossLinks":[]},"multiple_endocrine_neoplasia":{"name":"Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia (MEN)","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":400,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3079","clinicalPearls":["MEN1 is characterized primarily by the triad of parathyroid, pancreatic islet, and pituitary tumors (the '3 Ps'); it is an autosomal dominant syndrome (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.392/ch.388).","MEN2 predisposes to medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC), pheochromocytoma, and hyperparathyroidism (Harrison 22e p.3079).","There are three familial forms of MTC: MEN 2A, MEN 2B, and familial MTC without other features of MEN; MTC is more aggressive in MEN 2B than in MEN 2A, and familial MTC is more aggressive than sporadic MTC (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.397).","MEN1 is caused by inactivating germline mutations of the MEN1 (menin) tumor-suppressor gene on chromosome 11q13; about half of affected patients develop prolactinomas, while acromegaly and Cushing's disease are less common (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.392).","MEN2 is caused by activating point mutations of the RET protooncogene (a receptor tyrosine kinase) in a single allele, in contrast to the tumor-suppressor loss-of-function mechanism of MEN1 (Harrison 22e p.3079).","Early genetic screening for RET mutations identifies individuals at risk for MEN2 who may benefit from prophylactic thyroidectomy and biochemical screening for pheochromocytoma and hyperparathyroidism (Harrison 22e p.3079).","All patients with MTC should be tested for RET mutations, because genetic counseling and cascade testing of family members can then be offered to those who test positive (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.397)."],"pathophysiology":"MEN1 follows the two-hit tumor-suppressor paradigm: the affected individual inherits a mutant copy of the MEN1 gene (menin, chromosome 11q13), and tumorigenesis follows a somatic 'second hit' (deletion or point mutation) that inactivates the remaining normal allele. In contrast, MEN2 is caused by activating point mutations in a single allele of the RET protooncogene, encoding a receptor tyrosine kinase; constitutive RET activity leads to thyroid C-cell hyperplasia in childhood that precedes the development of medullary thyroid carcinoma (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.388/ch.397).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Other familial pituitary tumor / endocrine neoplasia syndromes: MEN 4 (CDKN1B mutation; hyperparathyroidism and pituitary adenomas), Carney complex (PRKAR1A; spotty skin pigmentation, myxomas, endocrine tumors), McCune-Albright syndrome, and familial isolated pituitary adenomas (AIP) (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.392).","Other inherited predispositions to pheochromocytoma that overlap with MEN2: von Hippel-Lindau disease, neurofibromatosis, and hereditary paraganglioma syndromes (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.247)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Germline RET mutation testing in all patients with medullary thyroid carcinoma, with cascade screening of relatives (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.397).","MEN1 (menin) gene testing in patients with the parathyroid/pancreatic-islet/pituitary tumor pattern or relevant family history (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.392/ch.376).","Biochemical screening for pheochromocytoma and hyperparathyroidism in RET-mutation carriers identified as at risk for MEN2 (Harrison 22e p.3079)."],"redFlags":["Exclude pheochromocytoma BEFORE any surgery: prior to surgery, pheochromocytoma should be excluded in all patients with a RET mutation (failure to do so risks an intraoperative hypertensive crisis) (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.397).","MTC in the context of MEN 2B carries a more aggressive course than MEN 2A and warrants early/prophylactic intervention (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.397)."],"prognosis":"Prognosis is driven by the component tumors and genotype. MTC is more aggressive in MEN 2B than in MEN 2A, and familial MTC is more aggressive than sporadic MTC; early genetic identification permits prophylactic thyroidectomy before malignant transformation, improving outcome (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.397).","pitfalls":["Proceeding to surgery (e.g., thyroidectomy or parathyroid surgery) without first excluding an unsuspected pheochromocytoma in a RET-mutation carrier (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.397).","Failing to perform RET testing in all MTC patients and thereby missing cascade screening opportunities for at-risk family members (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.397)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.3079"],"crossLinks":["pheo","thyroid_cancer"]},"mycoplasma_infection":{"name":"Mycoplasma Infection","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":193,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1464-1466","clinicalPearls":["M. pneumoniae pneumonia is also called Eaton agent pneumonia, primary atypical pneumonia, and 'walking' pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.1465).","Onset of pneumonia is usually gradual over several days; the most common presenting symptom is cough, which is typically nonproductive, though headache, malaise, chills, and fever are noted in the majority of patients (Harrison 22e p.1465).","Pneumonia develops in only 3-13% of infected individuals; upper respiratory illness is far more common, with an estimated incidence up to 20 times that of pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.1465).","Wheezes or rales are detected in about 80% of patients with M. pneumoniae pneumonia, but in many patients pneumonia can be diagnosed only by chest radiography (Harrison 22e p.1465).","The most common radiographic pattern is peribronchial pneumonia with thickened bronchial markings, streaks of interstitial infiltration, and subsegmental atelectasis; segmental or lobar consolidation is not uncommon (Harrison 22e p.1465).","Clinical presentation does not reliably distinguish M. pneumoniae pneumonia from other causes of community-acquired pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.1465).","Many patients with extrapulmonary M. pneumoniae disease do not have respiratory disease (Harrison 22e p.1465).","Hematologic manifestations include hemolytic anemia, aplastic anemia, cold agglutinins, disseminated intravascular coagulation, and hypercoagulopathy; when anemia occurs it generally develops in the second or third week of illness (Harrison 22e p.1466).","Erythema multiforme major (Stevens-Johnson syndrome) is the most clinically significant skin eruption associated with M. pneumoniae and appears to occur more commonly with M. pneumoniae than with other infectious agents; in M. pneumoniae-induced rash and mucositis (MIRM)/reactive infectious mucocutaneous eruption (RIME) there may be minimal cutaneous involvement with prominent mucosal disease (Harrison 22e p.1466, p.591).","M. pneumoniae has been implicated as a likely etiologic agent in 5-7% of cases of encephalitis (Harrison 22e p.1465).","Diagnosis is most sensitively and rapidly made by combining PCR of respiratory tract secretions with serologic testing of paired acute- and convalescent-phase samples (Harrison 22e p.1466).","M. pneumoniae is the most frequently detected 'atypical' organism in adult community-acquired pneumonia; older pooled data gave a prevalence of about 22.7%, and more recent (2002-2015) analyses gave about 7.2% (Harrison 22e p.1465)."],"pathophysiology":"Mycoplasmas are prokaryotes of the class Mollicutes, the smallest organisms capable of independent replication; they lack a cell wall and are bound only by a cell membrane, which explains the inactivity of beta-lactam antibiotics (penicillins and cephalosporins) against them. M. pneumoniae is generally an extracellular pathogen that attaches to ciliated respiratory epithelial cells via a complex terminal organelle using interactive adhesins and accessory proteins. Tissue injury is thought to be mediated by production of hydrogen peroxide and an ADP-ribosylating and vacuolating cytotoxin similar to pertussis toxin. Lacking a cell wall, mycoplasmas lack cell-wall-derived innate immune stimulators (lipopolysaccharide, lipoteichoic acid, peptidoglycan), but membrane lipoproteins act through Toll-like receptors (primarily TLR2). Innate immunity provides most lung defense, cellular immunity may be immunopathogenic, and humoral immunity protects against dissemination; patients with humoral immunodeficiency are prone to disseminated disease (arthritis, meningitis, osteomyelitis). Extrapulmonary manifestations may arise from disseminated infection, postinfectious autoimmune phenomena (e.g., Guillain-Barre syndrome), or possibly an ADP-ribosylating toxin (Harrison 22e p.1464-1466).","additionalDifferentials":["Chlamydia (Chlamydophila) pneumoniae community-acquired pneumonia","Legionella species pneumonia","Streptococcus pneumoniae and other bacterial community-acquired pneumonia","Viral respiratory tract infection / pneumonia"],"infectionMimics":["Chlamydophila pneumoniae pneumonia","Legionella pneumonia","Viral atypical pneumonia"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Asthma / reactive airway disease (wheezing overlap)","Drug-induced or herpes-associated erythema multiforme major (overlapping with Mycoplasma-associated SJS/MIRM)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Respiratory PCR for M. pneumoniae (sensitivity 65-90%, specificity 90-100%) - allows rapid early diagnosis","Paired acute- and convalescent-phase serology (IgM and IgG) by enzyme-linked immunoassay; a single acute sample is inadequate because antibodies may not develop until ~2 weeks","Respiratory culture on special media (sensitivity <=60%, specificity 100%) - not recommended for routine diagnosis as it may take weeks","Chest radiography","Cerebrospinal fluid PCR for extrapulmonary (CNS) infection","Macrolide-resistance testing by culture and/or PCR when resistance is suspected"],"redFlags":["Neurologic manifestations: meningoencephalitis, encephalitis, Guillain-Barre syndrome, aseptic meningitis, transverse myelitis, acute disseminated encephalomyelitis, cerebrovascular thromboembolic events, acute psychosis, cerebellar ataxia, cranial neuropathy","Severe mucocutaneous disease: erythema multiforme major / Stevens-Johnson syndrome / MIRM","Hemolytic anemia, disseminated intravascular coagulation, hypercoagulopathy","Disseminated infection (septic arthritis, meningitis, osteomyelitis), especially in hypogammaglobulinemic / humorally immunodeficient patients","Community-acquired pneumonia failing to respond to a penicillin or cephalosporin"],"prognosis":"M. pneumoniae pneumonia is generally self-limited, with symptoms usually resolving within 2-3 weeks of onset even without treatment; appropriate antimicrobial therapy significantly shortens the duration of clinical illness. Infection uncommonly results in critical illness and only rarely in death. Long-term recurrent wheezing or reactive airway disease may follow resolution of acute pneumonia, and a possible link to asthma is under investigation. Immunity following severe infection is more protective and longer-lasting than after mild infection, and genuine second attacks of pneumonia are reported infrequently (Harrison 22e p.1465).","pitfalls":["Because mycoplasmas lack a cell wall, beta-lactam antibiotics (penicillins and cephalosporins) are ineffective; consider M. pneumoniae particularly when community-acquired pneumonia fails to respond to these agents (Harrison 22e p.1464-1465).","M. pneumoniae lacks a cell wall and is therefore not visible on Gram stain (Harrison 22e p.1466).","Measurement of cold agglutinin titers is no longer recommended for diagnosis because the findings are nonspecific and specific M. pneumoniae assays are now available (Harrison 22e p.1466).","A single acute-phase serum sample is not adequate for diagnosis, and IgM antibody can persist for up to 1 year, so its presence may indicate recent rather than acute infection - paired samples are needed (Harrison 22e p.1466).","Little evidence supports the commonly held belief that M. pneumoniae is an important cause of otitis media, with or without bullous myringitis (Harrison 22e p.1465).","A test of cure for eradication is not suggested, because appropriate antibiotics do not appear to shorten the duration of detection by culture or PCR (Harrison 22e p.1466).","Ciprofloxacin and ofloxacin are not recommended for M. pneumoniae owing to high MICs and poor experimental performance, in contrast to respiratory fluoroquinolones (Harrison 22e p.1466)."],"severityClassification":"No formal severity classification is given for Mycoplasma infection; M. pneumoniae illness is generally graded clinically from mild self-limited upper respiratory illness through pneumonia to severe/refractory or critical disease, with macrolide resistance and extrapulmonary involvement marking more severe courses (Harrison 22e p.1464-1466).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1464-1466","Harrison 22e p.591"],"crossLinks":[]},"nocardiosis":{"name":"Nocardiosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":179,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1358-1362","clinicalPearls":["Nocardiosis is usually an opportunistic infection occurring primarily in hosts with impaired defenses, especially defects in cell-mediated immunity and specific phagocyte function; typical risk factors include solid organ or hematopoietic stem cell transplantation, systemic corticosteroids or Cushing syndrome, other immunosuppressive therapy, and HIV infection (p.1358-1359).","Pulmonary disease is the usual portal after inhalation of fragmented mycelia; onset is typically subacute over days to weeks (more acute in the severely immunocompromised), with prominent cough producing thick non-malodorous purulent sputum, fever, anorexia, and malaise (p.1359).","Typical chest imaging shows infiltrates, nodules, and masses in one or more lobes, and nodules frequently cavitate; pleural effusion or empyema occurs in about one-third of cases, occasionally without lung involvement (p.1359).","Dissemination occurs in up to one-third of patients with pneumonia, most often to the CNS and to skin/subcutaneous tissues; virtually any organ can be involved (p.1359-1360).","CNS involvement occurs in up to two-thirds of disseminated cases; brain imaging classically shows one or more ring-enhancing, multilobulated lesions, and multiple abscesses are seen in two-thirds of patients (p.1360).","Diagnosis starts with examining sputum or pus for branching, beaded, Gram-positive filaments (~1 um wide, up to 50 um long) that fragment into apparent cocci/coccobacilli; most nocardiae are modified (weak) acid-fast (e.g., modified Kinyoun, modified Ziehl-Neelsen, Fite-Faraco) (p.1360-1361).","Primary cutaneous nocardiosis follows transcutaneous inoculation (often thorn/gardening injury) and takes three forms: cellulitis, lymphocutaneous (sporotrichoid) syndrome, or actinomycetoma; N. brasiliensis is the usual cause of skin-limited and lymphocutaneous disease and of actinomycetoma (p.1358, p.1360).","Nocardiosis tends to remit and relapse; without definitive treatment, pulmonary disease characteristically follows a course of remissions and exacerbations over weeks to months, and prolonged therapy (months) is required (p.1359, p.1362-Table 179-1)."],"pathophysiology":"Nocardia species are saprophytic, filamentous, aerobic Gram-positive bacteria ubiquitous in soil, water, and decaying organic matter; their cell walls contain mycolic acids and trehalose (Mycobacteriales order), and they are weakly (modified) acid-fast and catalase positive. Pulmonary and systemic disease follow inhalation of fragmented bacterial mycelia, while primary cutaneous and ocular disease follow direct inoculation. Most immunocompetent people clear inhaled nocardiae without disease; invasive infection is favored by impaired host defenses. Innate and adaptive responses, with neutrophils and macrophages central to antinocardial defense (underscored by the frequency of nocardiosis in chronic granulomatous disease), normally control the organism. Nocardiae have evolved mechanisms to survive within phagocytes, including neutralization of oxidants and prevention of phagosome-lysosome fusion and phagosome acidification; neutrophils limit but do not efficiently kill them. The characteristic pathology is an abscess with neutrophilic infiltration and prominent necrosis, with little fibrosis/encapsulation, whereas actinomycetoma shows granulomatous inflammation with sinus tract formation. (p.1358-1359)","additionalDifferentials":["Lymphocutaneous (sporotrichoid) presentation overlaps with Sporothrix schenckii and Mycobacterium marinum infection (p.1360).","Mycetoma differential: actinomycetoma (filamentous bacteria, e.g., Nocardia), eumycetoma (fungal), and botryomycosis (cocci/bacilli, often Staphylococcus aureus) are difficult to separate clinically and require granule microscopy, culture, or biopsy (p.1361).","Ocular nocardiosis (keratitis, scleritis, endophthalmitis) cannot be distinguished from other ocular infections by signs and symptoms alone (p.1360)."],"infectionMimics":["Sporothrix schenckii (sporotrichosis) for lymphocutaneous disease","Mycobacterium marinum for lymphocutaneous disease","Eumycetoma (fungal mycetoma) for actinomycetoma","Botryomycosis (often Staphylococcus aureus) for actinomycetoma","Other causes of pulmonary nodules/cavitation and ring-enhancing brain lesions in immunocompromised hosts (e.g., fungi, mycobacteria, toxoplasmosis as discussed in brain abscess context)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pulmonary or cerebral malignancy/metastases presenting as nodules, masses, or ring-enhancing lesions"],"additionalInvestigations":["Sputum or pus microscopy for branching beaded Gram-positive filaments plus modified (weak-acid) acid-fast staining; organisms may also take up silver stains (p.1360-1361).","Bronchoalveolar lavage fluid or lung biopsy when sputum smears are negative, as they frequently are in pulmonary nocardiosis (p.1361).","Selective media (colistin-nalidixic acid agar, modified Thayer-Martin agar, or buffered charcoal-yeast extract agar) for mixed-flora specimens; avoid mycobacterial decontamination procedures, which can kill nocardiae; alert the laboratory to incubate cultures for prolonged periods (up to 2-4 weeks) (p.1361).","Species/complex identification by MALDI-TOF or genomic sequencing (16S rRNA, secA1, hsp65, or gyrB), with antimicrobial susceptibility testing (ideally CLSI broth dilution); susceptibility testing requires experience because nocardiae grow slowly and clump (p.1361).","Granule examination in mycetoma: wash suspect particles in saline, examine microscopically, and culture; culture is the most reliable way to identify the causative organism (p.1361).","Contrast-enhanced CNS imaging (MRI preferred, with IV gadolinium more sensitive than CT) in all patients with non-cutaneous/non-ocular nocardiosis to detect occult brain abscess (p.1360-1362).","Testing for underlying immunodeficiency in patients lacking typical risk factors, including HIV testing; test for chronic granulomatous disease in children and adults with nocardiosis who have no major risk factor (p.1359, p.1362)."],"redFlags":["CNS dissemination: brain abscess may be clinically silent, so absence of neurologic symptoms does not exclude it - one-third of patients with CNS involvement have no CNS symptoms and one-fourth have no apparent pulmonary disease at presentation (p.1360).","Disseminated nocardiosis carries a higher mortality than localized pulmonary disease, especially with CNS involvement (p.1362).","Nocardiosis in a patient without a major risk factor should prompt evaluation for an undiagnosed immunologic defect, including HIV and chronic granulomatous disease (p.1359, p.1362)."],"prognosis":"With appropriate treatment, mortality for localized pulmonary nocardiosis is less than 10%. Disseminated disease carries a higher mortality, especially with CNS involvement. Primary cutaneous and ocular disease often respond to therapy. Mortality from actinomycetoma is exceedingly low, but patients often suffer substantial morbidity, disfigurement, disability, and/or stigmatization. Prolonged therapy is required and disease tends to relapse, so secondary prophylaxis is considered by some experts for patients who remain at high risk after completing antimicrobial therapy. (p.1362)","pitfalls":["Failing to image the brain: because brain abscess may be asymptomatic, all patients with pulmonary or systemic nocardiosis should have contrast-enhanced CNS imaging (MRI preferred) regardless of neurologic symptoms (p.1360, p.1362).","Missing the diagnosis on smear: sputum smears are frequently negative in pulmonary nocardiosis, and the modified (weak) acid-fast technique must be used or organisms may be overlooked; standard mycobacterial decontamination can kill nocardiae (p.1360-1361).","Cultures lost to short incubation: nocardiae grow slowly (colonies up to 2 weeks, characteristic appearance up to 4 weeks), so the laboratory must be informed to incubate cultures for prolonged periods (p.1361).","Overcalling colonization or contamination: Nocardia can colonize the respiratory tract in bronchiectasis/structural lung disease, and isolation from a respiratory specimen does not always indicate infection; blood culture isolates may be contaminants in low-risk patients without clinical disease (p.1361).","Adjunctive topical corticosteroids should NOT be given to patients with nocardial keratitis (p.1362)."],"severityClassification":"Disease is categorized clinically and by host status to guide therapy duration (Table 179-1): pulmonary/systemic disease in hosts with intact defenses versus deficient defenses, CNS disease, primary cutaneous disease (cellulitis, lymphocutaneous), osteomyelitis/arthritis/laryngitis/sinusitis, actinomycetoma, and keratitis - longer courses for immunocompromised and CNS disease, with indefinite therapy considered in some AIDS patients with CD4 below 200/uL or chronic granulomatous disease (p.1362). [Durations noted as classification context only; not dosing guidance.]","references":["Harrison 22e p.1358-1362"],"crossLinks":["BRAIN_ABSCESS","LUNG_ABSCESS"]},"non_hodgkin_lymphoma":{"name":"Non-Hodgkin's Lymphoma","system":"Oncology / Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":113,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.857","clinicalPearls":["NHLs are cancers of mature B, T, and NK cells; ~90% are of B-cell origin. They are distinguished from Hodgkin's lymphoma by the absence of the Reed-Sternberg cell and differ from HL in biology, natural history, and the more variable prognosis (Harrison 22e p.857).","Clinically, NHL is grouped by behavior: indolent/slow-growing (e.g., follicular lymphoma, the most common indolent NHL), aggressive (DLBCL, the most common subtype overall, ~one-third of cases), and highly aggressive/most rapidly growing (Burkitt's lymphoma, doubling time <24 h). Mantle cell lymphoma is intermediate-grade (Harrison 22e p.857, 862).","The duration and pace of symptoms help distinguish aggressive from indolent disease, as does the presence or absence of 'B' symptoms — fevers, drenching night sweats, and unexplained weight loss (>10% of body weight over the 6 months before diagnosis) (Harrison 22e p.860-861).","Most common presentation of follicular lymphoma is new, painless lymphadenopathy at multiple sites; most patients lack elevated LDH or B symptoms. Asymptomatic indolent disease should be observed, as treating asymptomatic patients does not improve survival (Harrison 22e p.863-864).","Serum LDH and serum beta-2-microglobulin are important independent prognostic factors in NHL and should be measured at diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.860).","Aggressive lymphomas (DLBCL, Burkitt's) are potentially curable with combination chemotherapy, whereas most indolent lymphomas are very responsive to therapy but not ultimately curable in the vast majority of cases except in localized early-stage disease (Harrison 22e p.862-863)."],"pathophysiology":"NHLs arise from mature B, T, or NK lymphocytes, with ~90% being B-cell neoplasms; transformation typically requires several genetic events. Characteristic chromosomal translocations create fusion or deregulated oncogenes: t(14;18) with BCL2 overexpression in follicular lymphoma (>85% of cases, necessary but not sufficient for transformation); MYC rearrangement at 8q24 in >95% of Burkitt's lymphoma (most often t(8;14) fusing MYC to IgH); and t(11;18) (API2-MLT) in MALT lymphoma. Gene-expression profiling subdivides DLBCL into germinal-center B-cell (GCB)-like and activated-B-cell (ABC)-like subtypes with differing prognoses. Several NHLs are linked to chronic antigenic stimulation or infection-driven immune dysregulation, and immunodeficiency states strongly predispose to (often EBV-associated) lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.857-863).","additionalDifferentials":["Hodgkin's lymphoma (distinguished by Reed-Sternberg cells)","Reactive follicular hyperplasia (major differential for follicular lymphoma on biopsy)","Lymphoid leukemias (CLL/SLL, ALL), plasma cell disorders (multiple myeloma)","Other causes of lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, or B-symptom-like constitutional illness"],"infectionMimics":["Infectious mononucleosis / EBV infection and other viral lymphadenopathies","Reactive lymphadenopathy from systemic infection","Multicentric Castleman's disease (HHV-8), which causes diffuse lymphadenopathy with fever, malaise, and weight loss mimicking lymphoma B symptoms"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Autoimmune disease with lymphadenopathy (e.g., Sjögren's syndrome, SLE, rheumatoid arthritis)","Reactive follicular hyperplasia","Sarcoidosis and other causes of constitutional symptoms with adenopathy"],"additionalInvestigations":["Adequate tissue biopsy reviewed by an expert hematopathologist (follicular lymphoma and Burkitt's lymphoma can be diagnosed on morphology; immunophenotyping required) (Harrison 22e p.860, 863)","Complete blood count, routine chemistries, liver function tests, uric acid, calcium","Serum protein electrophoresis (to detect monoclonal paraproteins)","Serum LDH and serum beta-2-microglobulin (prognostic) (Harrison 22e p.860)","CT scan of chest, abdomen, and pelvis (essential for staging, especially indolent lymphomas) (Harrison 22e p.861)","FDG-PET (preferred for aggressive lymphomas such as DLBCL and Burkitt's, and to assess suspected transformation of indolent disease) (Harrison 22e p.861)","Bone marrow biopsy","Lumbar puncture in lymphoblastic, Burkitt's, and DLBCL with positive marrow biopsy or CNS-risk presentations (Harrison 22e p.861)","Screening for HIV and hepatitis B and C at diagnosis (risk factors and risk of HBV reactivation with therapy) (Harrison 22e p.861)"],"redFlags":["Burkitt's lymphoma is highly aggressive (doubling time <24 h) — treatment must be initiated quickly and the tumor carries a high risk of tumor lysis syndrome; TLS is most often associated with treatment of Burkitt's lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.862, 1014)","Rapid growth of lymph nodes with new B symptoms in known indolent (follicular) lymphoma suggests histologic transformation to DLBCL (~3% per year) (Harrison 22e p.864)","Disease involving paranasal sinuses, testes, breast, kidneys, adrenal glands, or epidural space — and highly aggressive histologies like Burkitt's — carries increased risk of CNS dissemination (Harrison 22e p.861)","Concerning neurologic signs/symptoms warrant lumbar puncture for CNS involvement (Harrison 22e p.860)","'Double-hit' high-grade B-cell lymphoma (concurrent MYC and BCL2 rearrangements) has an extremely poor prognosis (median OS ~12-18 months) (Harrison 22e p.862-863)"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is highly variable and driven more by histology and clinical parameters than by anatomic stage. Overall 5-year survival for NHL is ~74%. The International Prognostic Index (IPI; age >=60, elevated LDH, ECOG performance status >=2, Ann Arbor stage III/IV, and >1 extranodal site) is the best predictor for aggressive NHL: 5-year overall survival ~73%, 51%, 43%, and 26% for 0-1, 2, 3, and 4-5 risk factors. With R-CHOP, ~65-70% of DLBCL patients are cured. The FLIPI predicts follicular lymphoma outcome; most FL patients live 15-20 years but the disease is generally not curable (except localized early-stage), and Burkitt's lymphoma is curable in ~80-90% when treated promptly and correctly (Harrison 22e p.857, 861-862).","pitfalls":["Diagnosis requires an adequate tissue sample interpreted by an expert hematopathologist; an inadequate or unrepresentative specimen risks misclassification (e.g., missing coexisting DLBCL within follicular lymphoma or confusing lymphoma with reactive follicular hyperplasia) (Harrison 22e p.860, 863).","The Ann Arbor anatomic staging system is less useful in NHL than in Hodgkin's lymphoma because NHL disseminates widely rather than in an ordered stepwise fashion; histology and the IPI matter more than stage (Harrison 22e p.861).","Treating asymptomatic indolent (follicular) lymphoma does not improve survival — appropriate patients should be observed rather than treated immediately (Harrison 22e p.864).","Failing to anticipate and prevent tumor lysis syndrome before treating rapidly proliferating Burkitt's lymphoma can be life-threatening (Harrison 22e p.862).","A residual mass on CT after therapy may represent treated fibrosis rather than active disease; PET can help distinguish, but end-of-treatment PET should be delayed (preferably 6-8 weeks after chemotherapy) to avoid false positives (Harrison 22e p.861)."],"severityClassification":"WHO (WHO-HAEM5) classification of lymphoid malignancies categorizes NHL by cell of origin (mature B-cell vs mature T/NK-cell neoplasms) and entity. Anatomic extent is described by the Ann Arbor staging system (Stage I-IV, with suffix A/B for absence/presence of B symptoms). Prognostic stratification uses the International Prognostic Index (IPI) for aggressive NHL and the FLIPI for follicular lymphoma; follicular lymphoma is also graded I-III by centroblasts per high-power field (Harrison 22e p.857-862).","references":["Harrison 22e p.857"],"crossLinks":["hodgkin_lymphoma","infectious_mononucleosis"]},"nontuberculous_mycobacterial_infection":{"name":"Nontuberculous Mycobacterial Infection","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":185,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1414-1418","clinicalPearls":["NTM (also termed atypical, environmental, or mycobacteria other than tuberculosis) are ubiquitous in soil and water and refer to mycobacteria other than M. tuberculosis and its close relatives and M. leprae; over 199 species are recognized (p.1414).","Most NTM cause human disease only when host defense is impaired (e.g., bronchiectasis) or breached by inoculation (liposuction, trauma, cardiac surgery); human-to-human transmission is rare and occurs almost exclusively in cystic fibrosis (p.1414).","Pulmonary disease is by far the most common form in North America and is highly associated with pulmonary epithelial defects but NOT with systemic immunodeficiency; disseminated disease instead denotes significant immune dysfunction such as advanced HIV (p.1414, 1416).","Pulmonary MAC most commonly presents in the 6th-7th decade in women with months to years of nagging intermittent cough and fatigue; pulmonary NTM in a tall, thin woman with chest-wall abnormalities is termed 'Lady Windermere syndrome' (p.1416).","Pulmonary MAC affects older nonsmoking white women more than men (onset ~60 years); such patients tend to be taller and thinner with high rates of scoliosis, mitral valve prolapse, and pectus anomalies (p.1416).","Male smokers with upper-lobe cavitary disease tend to carry the same single MAC strain indefinitely, whereas nonsmoking women with nodular bronchiectasis carry several MAC strains simultaneously that change over the disease course (p.1416).","M. kansasii can produce a clinical syndrome strongly resembling tuberculosis with hemoptysis, chest pain, and cavitary lung disease (p.1416).","Disseminated MAC (or M. kansasii) in advanced HIV is now uncommon in North America owing to effective prophylaxis and improved HIV treatment; when common, the portal of entry was the bowel with spread to bone marrow and bloodstream (p.1415).","Disseminated NTM is often indolent and progressive over weeks to months with malaise, fever, weight loss, organomegaly, lymphadenopathy, and anemia; blood cultures may be negative even though involved organs carry heavy organism burdens, sometimes with grossly impaired granulomatous response (p.1415).","Isolated cervical lymphadenopathy is the most common form of NTM infection in young children in North America (usually MAC); the swelling is firm and relatively painless with few systemic signs and is often diagnosed inadvertently at biopsy for suspected malignancy (p.1416).","Children with isolated cervical or intrathoracic NTM infection do NOT commonly have underlying immune defects and usually do NOT go on to develop other opportunistic infections (p.1416).","Cutaneous NTM usually requires a break in the skin; rapidly growing NTM (M. abscessus, M. fortuitum, M. chelonae) cause outbreaks via contaminated surgical instruments/injections, with painful erythematous draining subcutaneous nodules typically WITHOUT fever or systemic symptoms (p.1416).","M. marinum (fish tanks, pools, fish handling) causes 'fish-tank granuloma' and can spread up the arm in a sporotrichoid pattern; M. ulcerans causes painless Buruli ulcer via the toxin mycolactone (p.1416).","'Hot-tub lung' is a pulmonary hypersensitivity reaction to NTM (most often MAC) growing in underchlorinated water, distinct from true pulmonary or disseminated infection (p.1415).","For susceptibility testing, only MAC testing for clarithromycin and M. kansasii testing for rifampin is indicated; few data support other in vitro susceptibility tests, and MAC isolates not previously exposed to macrolides are almost always susceptible (p.1417)."],"pathophysiology":"NTM are ubiquitous environmental organisms (soil and water) with specific niches (e.g., M. simiae in aquifers, M. fortuitum in pedicure baths, M. immunogenum in metalworking fluids). Because exposure is essentially universal yet disease is rare, normal host defenses are strong and significant disease implies a specific susceptibility. Mycobacteria are phagocytosed by macrophages, which release IL-12; IL-12 activates T and NK cells (via STAT4) to secrete IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages/neutrophils (via STAT1) and upregulates TNF-alpha, forming a positive IFN-gamma/IL-12 feedback loop critical for mycobacterial control. Disseminated NTM correlates with declining CD4+ T cells (advanced HIV, idiopathic CD4 lymphocytopenia), TNF-alpha inhibitors (which impair granuloma formation), Mendelian mutations in the IFN-gamma/IL-12 pathway (IFNG, IFNGR1, IFNGR2, STAT1, GATA2, ISG15, IRF8, IL-12A, IL-12RB1, IL-12RB2, CYBB, IKBKG/NEMO and others), or neutralizing anti-IFN-gamma autoantibodies. Only ~70% of non-HIV disseminated cases have a genetic diagnosis. In contrast, the best-recognized substrate for pulmonary NTM is bronchiectasis (cystic fibrosis, primary ciliary dyskinesia, STAT3 hyper-IgE/Job's syndrome, idiopathic), but the mechanism by which bronchiectasis predisposes to locally destructive yet non-systemic disease is unknown (p.1414-1415).","additionalDifferentials":["Pulmonary tuberculosis (M. kansasii closely mimics TB; PPD/IGRA cross-reactivity)","Malignancy (painless cervical adenopathy in children; isolated intrathoracic NTM mistaken for cancer)","Bronchiectasis exacerbation / underlying structural lung disease","Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis (predisposes to NTM and Nocardia)","Sporotrichosis (sporotrichoid spread mimicked by M. marinum)"],"infectionMimics":["M. tuberculosis (clinical, radiographic, and AFB-smear overlap)","Nocardia (gram-positive beaded filamentous rods; alveolar proteinosis predisposes to both)","Sporothrix schenckii (sporotrichoid ascending nodules akin to M. marinum)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Malignancy presenting as painless cervical or intrathoracic adenopathy","Hot-tub lung (hypersensitivity pneumonitis-type reaction to NTM rather than invasive infection)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Acid-fast or fluorochrome smears of sputum or body fluids (organisms may appear as beaded gram-positive rods at high burden, but this is unreliable)","Mycobacterial culture using separate processing and dedicated blood culture media; differentiate rapidly growing (<7 days) from slowly growing (>=7 days) species; some labs hold cultures 6 weeks","Liquid culture media for faster isolation; lower incubation temperature (30C) for M. marinum, M. haemophilum, M. ulcerans; special additives (iron/blood for M. haemophilum, mycobactin J for M. genavense)","Species-level identification by biochemical tests, nucleic acid probes/sequencing, HPLC or mass spectrometry; molecular detection for noncultivable species (e.g., M. tilburgii)","Chest CT (tree-in-bud bronchiolar pattern, lingular and right-middle-lobe involvement, bronchiectasis, cavitation)","IGRA (note M. marinum, M. kansasii, M. szulgai carry ESAT6/CFP10 and may cause false-positive IGRA; IGRA is indeterminate with anti-IFN-gamma autoantibodies)","Genetic evaluation of the IFN-gamma/IL-12 pathway and screening for anti-IFN-gamma autoantibodies in disseminated disease without iatrogenic cause; GATA2 evaluation when myelodysplasia coexists"],"redFlags":["Disseminated involvement of two or more organs without an iatrogenic cause (prompts IFN-gamma/IL-12 pathway genetic investigation)","Disseminated NTM with bacterial/viral infections plus conical/peg/missing teeth or abnormal hair pattern in males (suggests NEMO/IKBKG NF-kB defect, possibly with immunoglobulin defects)","Mycobacterial disease with myelodysplasia (investigate GATA2 deficiency)","Disseminated MAC and rapidly growing NTM with other opportunistic infections (e.g., Talaromyces), especially in East Asian women (anti-IFN-gamma autoantibodies)","Infected indwelling IV catheters/lines (M. abscessus, M. fortuitum from contaminated water) requiring catheter removal"],"prognosis":"NTM cause chronic infections evolving slowly over weeks to years, so emergent therapy before diagnosis and speciation is rarely needed. Treatment is complex, often poorly tolerated, and potentially toxic; inadequate single-drug therapy is almost always associated with emergence of resistance and relapse. Pulmonary MAC therapy is prolonged, generally continued for 12 months after culture conversion (typically at least 18 months total). The typical lag between symptom onset and diagnosis is about 5 years in older women (p.1416-1418).","pitfalls":["Not all NTM isolations, especially from the lung, reflect disease or require treatment; distinguishing colonization/contamination from true infection requires an appropriate clinical and radiographic setting (nodules, bronchiectasis, cavities) plus repeated isolation per American Thoracic Society criteria (p.1417).","The single most critical and sensitive step in diagnosis is simply to suspect NTM and request the correct, separately processed mycobacterial smears, cultures, and dedicated blood culture media; many cases go undiagnosed because the appropriate test was never ordered (p.1416-1417).","M. gordonae is frequently recovered from respiratory samples but is almost never a pathogen (p.1417).","PPD does not differentiate NTM from M. tuberculosis well; intermediate (~10 mm) reactions overlap, BCG cross-reacts, but large reactions (>15 mm) most commonly signify TB (p.1416-1417).","Slow-growing NTM typically do NOT proliferate in routine blood culture media, so suspicion and correct bottles are essential; biopsy isolation is strong evidence but laboratory contamination does occur (confirm with organisms on stained sections) (p.1417)."],"severityClassification":"American Thoracic Society criteria for pulmonary NTM diagnosis: growth of NTM from two of three sputum samples (regardless of smear), OR a positive bronchoalveolar lavage sample (regardless of smear), OR a lung parenchymal biopsy with granulomatous inflammation or mycobacteria on section plus NTM on culture, in an appropriate clinical and radiographic setting. Isolation of NTM from blood or organ biopsy in a compatible clinical setting is considered diagnostic. NTM are categorized as rapidly growing (<7 days) versus slowly growing (>=7 days) (p.1417).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1414-1418"],"crossLinks":["PULMONARY_TB","BRONCHIECTASIS_EXACERBATION"]},"obesity":{"name":"Obesity","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":413,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3181","clinicalPearls":["Obesity is defined as a state of excess adipose tissue mass that adversely affects health; because direct measurement of fat mass is impractical in routine clinical practice, the body mass index (BMI = weight/height² in kg/m²) is used as a proxy (Harrison 22e p.3181).","WHO BMI classes: underweight <18.5; normal 18.5–24.9; overweight 25–29.9; obesity ≥30 kg/m². AACE/ACE management staging further subdivides obesity into class I (30.0–34.9), class II (35.0–39.9), and class III / extreme obesity (≥40) with correspondingly higher disease risk (Harrison 22e p.3181, 3187).","BMI-based cutoffs were derived largely from white Western populations; adverse outcomes occur at lower BMI in other ethnic groups, so lower thresholds and ethnic-specific waist-circumference cutpoints (e.g., South Asians/Chinese, Japanese) are recommended (Harrison 22e p.3181, 3187).","Fat distribution matters independent of overall BMI: a waist-to-hip ratio >0.9 in women and >1.0 in men, and excess abdominal (visceral) adiposity, are independently associated with type 2 diabetes, dyslipidemia, metabolic syndrome, and cardiovascular disease (Harrison 22e p.3181, 3187).","Energy balance is under homeostatic control by the hypothalamus, which integrates the adipose-derived hormone leptin (rises with fat stores, falls fast with caloric restriction) with gut signals — GLP-1, CCK, GIP, PYY, oxyntomodulin, ghrelin — plus pancreatic insulin and amylin (Harrison 22e p.3181–3182).","Insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia is one of the earliest and most prominent biochemical abnormalities of obesity, is worse with central adiposity, and underlies/precedes many adverse cardiometabolic consequences (Harrison 22e p.3186).","Two leading (not mutually exclusive) hypotheses link obesity to insulin resistance: adipose-tissue inflammation (macrophage accumulation around dying adipocytes), and ectopic lipid deposition once safe adipose storage capacity is exceeded — with stronger human data supporting the ectopic-fat / limited-storage mechanism (Harrison 22e p.3186).","Obesity care begins with sensitive, non-stigmatizing language and an obesity-focused history covering diet, physical activity, sleep, stress, contributing factors, complications, and the patient's readiness/motivation to change (Harrison 22e p.3187–3188)."],"pathophysiology":"Adipose tissue evolved to store excess calories as triglyceride in unilocular lipid droplets and release free fatty acids and glycerol when needed. In an obesogenic environment (abundant energy-dense palatable food, larger portions, reduced physical activity), chronic energy intake exceeding expenditure — even by as little as ~7 kcal/day sustained over years — produces a cumulative excess of adipose triglyceride. Genetic factors strongly determine individual susceptibility (twin studies show adiposity tracks with biological, not adoptive, family), acting largely through the brain's control of food intake. Body weight is homeostatically defended by the hypothalamus, which integrates the adipose hormone leptin (whose fall below an individual's habitual level is a powerful drive to eat) with gut hormones (GLP-1, CCK, GIP, PYY, oxyntomodulin, ghrelin) and pancreatic insulin/amylin. Within the arcuate nucleus, leptin stimulates POMC neurons (whose products α-/β-MSH act via MC4R in the paraventricular nucleus to reduce intake and raise energy expenditure) and inhibits orexigenic AGRP/NPY/GABA neurons. Leptin defends strongly against fat loss but responds weakly to fat excess (\"leptin resistance\"), explaining why rising leptin does not prevent obesity. As obesity develops, the earliest abnormality is insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia, worse with central fat; this arises from adipose-tissue inflammation and/or ectopic lipid deposition in liver and muscle once safe adipose storage capacity is exceeded (the human data favouring the ectopic-fat mechanism, as illustrated by lipodystrophy). Insulin resistance/hyperinsulinemia then predisposes to the metabolic, endocrine, and cardiovascular complications of obesity (Harrison 22e p.3181–3186).","additionalDifferentials":["Hypothyroidism — may cause weight gain but is rarely the sole cause of severe obesity; check TSH (and free thyroxine for rare secondary hypothyroidism) in any patient presenting with obesity (Harrison 22e p.3185, 3188)","Cushing's syndrome / endogenous hypercortisolism — suggested by spontaneous bruising, livid striae, myopathy and marked centripetal fat distribution; confirmed via 24-h urinary free cortisol or dexamethasone suppression (Harrison 22e p.3185)","Hypothalamic obesity — from tumors (e.g., craniopharyngioma), inflammatory masses, or severe head injury disrupting hypothalamic energy-balance centres, often with anterior/posterior pituitary dysfunction and short history of hyperphagia (Harrison 22e p.3185)","Insulinoma — weight gain driven by frequent eating to avoid hypoglycemia (Harrison 22e p.3185)","Polycystic ovarian syndrome — secondary cause/association meriting evaluation (Harrison 22e p.3188)","Monogenic / syndromic obesity — leptin or leptin-receptor deficiency, POMC, PCSK1, MC4R, SIM1, BDNF/TrkB, SH2B1 mutations; syndromes such as Prader-Willi, Bardet-Biedl, Albright's hereditary osteodystrophy/pseudohypoparathyroidism, Cohen's, Carpenter's, Alström's, Tubby (Harrison 22e p.3183–3185)","Drug-induced weight gain — insulin, sulfonylureas, thiazolidinediones, glucocorticoids, antipsychotics (clozapine, olanzapine, risperidone), lithium, antidepressants (tricyclics, MAOIs, paroxetine, mirtazapine), antiepileptics (valproate, gabapentin, carbamazepine) (Harrison 22e p.3188)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Very muscular body habitus — BMI may overestimate body fat (Harrison 22e p.3181)","Peripheral edema from NSAIDs or calcium channel blockers — increases weight without increasing body fat (Harrison 22e p.3188)"],"additionalInvestigations":["BMI calculation plus waist circumference (surrogate for visceral fat; measure horizontally above the iliac crest, particularly when BMI ≤35) and/or waist-to-hip ratio (Harrison 22e p.3181, 3187)","Fasting lipid profile (total, LDL, HDL cholesterol and triglycerides), chemistry panel, glycated haemoglobin, and blood pressure in all patients (Harrison 22e p.3187)","TSH ± free thyroxine to evaluate for hypothyroidism (Harrison 22e p.3185, 3188)","24-h urinary free cortisol or dexamethasone suppression test when Cushing's syndrome is suspected (Harrison 22e p.3185)","Genetic testing in severe early-onset obesity (up to ~20% of children with severe obesity have chromosomal abnormalities or highly penetrant mutations) (Harrison 22e p.3183)"],"redFlags":["Short history of hyperphagia and rapid weight gain with signs of anterior/posterior pituitary dysfunction — suggests hypothalamic damage from tumor, inflammation, or head injury (Harrison 22e p.3185)","Spontaneous bruising, livid striae, proximal myopathy, marked centripetal fat — suggests Cushing's syndrome rather than common obesity (Harrison 22e p.3185)","Severe early-onset obesity in childhood with intense hyperphagia/food-seeking — prompts screening for monogenic/syndromic causes (Harrison 22e p.3183–3185)","Severe hypertriglyceridemia secondary to insulin resistance — risk of pancreatitis (Harrison 22e p.3186)"],"prognosis":"Obesity is a serious, highly heterogeneous chronic disease that increases morbidity and mortality across many organ systems. The number and severity of complications generally rise with increasing BMI, and excess abdominal/visceral fat confers added cardiometabolic risk. As BMI rises by 5 kg/m², cancer mortality increases by ~10%; in some countries obesity has overtaken smoking as the leading risk factor for cancer. People with obesity have increased morbidity and mortality from atherothrombotic vascular disease (coronary artery disease, stroke) even without diabetes, and are at increased risk of heart failure (especially diastolic dysfunction) and atrial fibrillation. Modest, sustained weight loss (an initial goal of ~8–10% over 6 months) improves obesity-related complications and quality of life; some complications, including type 2 diabetes (e.g., the DiRECT trial showed remission in nearly half of participants with intensive low-energy dietary intervention), can substantially improve or remit with weight loss (Harrison 22e p.3186–3187, 3190).","pitfalls":["Treating BMI alone: BMI only reflects body size, not body composition, fat distribution, health, or quality of life — not all patients with a high BMI need treatment, and BMI overestimates fat in very muscular individuals (Harrison 22e p.3181, 3187, 3190).","Applying white-Western-derived BMI cutoffs uniformly: adverse outcomes occur at lower BMI in other ethnic groups, and ethnic-specific (e.g., Asia-Pacific) thresholds should be used (Harrison 22e p.3181, 3187).","Overlooking that severe hypothyroidism is rarely the sole cause of severe obesity, yet thyroid function should still always be checked (Harrison 22e p.3185).","Mistaking the lower GH response to provocative testing in common obesity for a structural hypothalamic-pituitary lesion (Harrison 22e p.3186).","Assuming a specific macronutrient ratio is key: weight loss depends primarily on total caloric reduction and adherence, not on the proportion of carbohydrate, fat, or protein (Harrison 22e p.3190).","Using stigmatizing language ('obesity', 'morbid obesity', 'fatness') rather than neutral terms ('weight', 'excess weight', 'unhealthy weight'), which leaves patients feeling judged (Harrison 22e p.3188).","Initiating lifestyle change before assessing readiness, which leads to frustration and may hamper future efforts (Harrison 22e p.3188)."],"severityClassification":"BMI-based (kg/m²): underweight <18.5; healthy/normal weight 18.5–24.9; overweight 25.0–29.9; obesity class I 30.0–34.9 (high disease risk); obesity class II 35.0–39.9 (very high risk); extreme/class III obesity ≥40 (extremely high risk). Risk is refined by excess abdominal fat (ethnic-specific waist-circumference cutpoints; waist-to-hip ratio >0.9 women / >1.0 men). Disease-staging systems augment BMI: the AACE/ACE staging (stage 0 = no complications; stage 1 = mild-moderate complications; stage 2 = at least one severe complication) and the Edmonton Obesity Staging System (EOSS, grades 0–4 across medical, functional, and psychological domains, independent of BMI) (Harrison 22e p.3181, 3187–3188).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3181","Harrison 22e p.3187"],"crossLinks":["diabetes_mellitus","sleep_apnea"]},"oncologic_emergencies":{"name":"Oncologic Emergencies","system":"Oncology / Emergency","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":80,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.585-595","clinicalPearls":["Emergencies in cancer fall into three groups: pressure/obstruction from a space-occupying lesion (structural-obstructive), metabolic or hormonal problems (paraneoplastic), and treatment-related complications (Harrison 22e p.585).","Superior vena cava syndrome (SVCS) reflects SVC obstruction with severe reduction of venous return from head, neck and upper extremities; lung cancer (especially small-cell and squamous) accounts for ~85% of malignant cases, and in young adults lymphoma is a leading cause (Harrison 22e p.585).","Benign causes of SVCS are increasing with intravascular device use (central catheters, pacemaker/defibrillator leads) and now account for at least 40% of cases (Harrison 22e p.585).","SVCS typically presents with neck and facial swelling (especially periorbital), dyspnea, cough, dilated neck veins, collateral chest-wall veins, cyanosis and facial plethora that worsens when supine; bending forward or lying down aggravates symptoms (Harrison 22e p.585).","The diagnosis of SVCS is clinical; the most significant chest radiograph finding is widening of the superior mediastinum (most often right-sided), and CT provides the most reliable mediastinal anatomy (Harrison 22e p.586).","Malignant spinal cord compression (MSCC) is compression of cord and/or cauda equina by an extradural tumor mass; spinal cord compression occurs in 5-10% of cancer patients and is the first manifestation of malignancy in ~10% (Harrison 22e p.588).","Localized back pain and tenderness is the most common initial symptom of spinal cord compression, often present for days to months before neurologic findings; it characteristically worsens when supine, unlike disk-disease pain (Harrison 22e p.588-589).","The thoracic spine is the most common site of cord compression (70%), followed by lumbosacral (20%) and cervical (10%); lung, breast and prostate cancers are the most frequent offenders (Harrison 22e p.588).","Treatment of spinal cord compression is more often successful in patients still ambulatory and with sphincter control at initiation; patients whose exam suggests compression should receive dexamethasone immediately and undergo MRI (Harrison 22e p.589).","Full-length spinal MRI is the imaging procedure of choice for cord compression and is as good as or better than myelography plus post-myelogram CT (Harrison 22e p.589).","About 25% of cancer patients die with intracranial metastases; lung and breast cancer and melanoma metastasize to brain most often, and brain metastases present features of an expanding intracranial lesion: headache, nausea, vomiting, behavioral change, seizures and focal progressive deficits (Harrison 22e p.589-590).","Dexamethasone is the best initial treatment for all symptomatic patients with brain metastases and raised intracranial pressure (Harrison 22e p.590).","Hypercalcemia is the most common paraneoplastic syndrome, and SIADH is the most common cause of hyponatremia among cancer patients (both detailed in Chaps. 98 and 422/393) (Harrison 22e p.592).","Tumor lysis syndrome (TLS) is characterized by hyperuricemia, hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia and hypocalcemia from destruction of large numbers of rapidly proliferating cells; acidosis may develop and acute renal failure occurs frequently (Harrison 22e p.593).","TLS is most often associated with treatment of Burkitt's lymphoma, ALL, AML and other rapidly proliferating lymphomas, and usually occurs during or shortly (1-5 days) after chemotherapy; risk correlates with tumor burden and high LDH (>1500 U/L) (Harrison 22e p.593).","Recognition of risk and prevention are the most important steps in TLS management; the standard preventive approach is allopurinol plus aggressive hydration (Harrison 22e p.593).","Febrile neutropenia is the clinical presentation of fever with <1500 granulocytes/uL; it is a key treatment-related emergency in cancer patients (Harrison 22e p.575)."],"pathophysiology":"Oncologic emergencies arise by three mechanisms (Harrison 22e p.585). Structural-obstructive emergencies result from a space-occupying lesion: in SVCS, malignant tumor or thrombosis obstructs the superior vena cava, reducing venous return from head, neck and upper limbs; in malignant spinal cord compression, an expanding extradural tumor (usually metastasis to vertebral body or pedicle, or paravertebral extension through the intervertebral foramen) mechanically injures axons and myelin and compromises blood flow, causing ischemia/infarction; raised intracranial pressure results from brain metastases and surrounding edema, sometimes with hemorrhage or CSF obstruction causing hydrocephalus and herniation (Harrison 22e p.585-590). Metabolic/hormonal (paraneoplastic) emergencies include hypercalcemia, SIADH-related hyponatremia, lactic acidosis and hypoglycemia (Harrison 22e p.592-593). Treatment-related TLS occurs when cytotoxic killing of a large rapidly proliferating tumor releases intracellular contents: nucleic acid turnover raises uric acid which precipitates in the acidic renal tubules; released phosphate causes hyperphosphatemia with reciprocal hypocalcemia and calcium-phosphate nephrocalcinosis; and massive cell destruction releases potassium causing hyperkalemia, which in renal failure can cause fatal ventricular arrhythmias (Harrison 22e p.593).","additionalDifferentials":["SVCS in a young man with a mediastinal mass: lymphoma versus primary mediastinal germ cell tumor (Harrison 22e p.585)","Cauda equina syndrome: low back pain, saddle sensory loss, rectal/bladder dysfunction, sexual impotence and absent reflexes from compression of nerve roots forming the cauda equina (Harrison 22e p.589)","Abrupt stroke-like onset of headache, nausea, vomiting and focal deficit in brain metastasis usually indicates hemorrhage into the metastasis (melanoma, germ cell and renal cell tumors bleed readily) (Harrison 22e p.590)"],"infectionMimics":["Pyogenic epidural abscess or vertebral tuberculosis can mimic malignant spinal cord compression (Harrison 22e p.589)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Spinal cord compression mimics: osteoporotic vertebral collapse, disk disease, radiation myelopathy, neoplastic leptomeningitis, benign tumors, epidural hematoma and spinal lipomatosis (Harrison 22e p.589)","About 20% of vertebral collapse (especially in older patients and postmenopausal women) is due to osteoporosis rather than cancer (Harrison 22e p.589)","Benign causes of SVCS: aortic aneurysm, thyromegaly, thrombosis, and fibrosing mediastinitis from prior irradiation, histoplasmosis or Behcet's syndrome (Harrison 22e p.585)","Pseudohyperkalemia from high leukocyte/platelet counts (cell lysis after blood draw) can falsely elevate potassium in TLS; no ECG abnormalities are present (Harrison 22e p.593)","Pseudohypoglycemia from glucose consumption in the test tube with hyperleukocytosis, myeloproliferative disease or CSF treatment (Harrison 22e p.593)"],"additionalInvestigations":["SVCS: chest radiograph (superior mediastinal widening, usually right-sided), CT thorax (most reliable mediastinal view; 'hot quadrate' hepatic hotspot sign suggests SVC obstruction), MRI (100% sensitivity/specificity but supine positioning may be difficult in dyspneic patients) (Harrison 22e p.586)","Spinal cord compression: neurologic exam and plain spine films first (earliest finding is the 'winking owl' sign of pedicle erosion; plain films are insensitive), then full-length spinal MRI (procedure of choice); myelography reserved for poor or unobtainable MRI (Harrison 22e p.589)","Brain metastases/raised ICP: gadolinium-enhanced MRI is superior to CT and more sensitive for meningeal involvement and small posterior-fossa lesions (Harrison 22e p.590)","TLS pretreatment evaluation in at-risk patients: complete blood count, serum chemistry (electrolytes, creatinine, calcium, phosphate, uric acid) and urinalysis; follow plasma rather than serum potassium when pseudohyperkalemia is suspected; sonography/CT of kidneys and retroperitoneum if baseline renal function is abnormal (Harrison 22e p.593)","A urinary uric acid to urinary creatinine ratio >1 supports acute hyperuricemic nephropathy; <1 suggests renal failure of other cause (Harrison 22e p.593)"],"redFlags":["SVCS with cerebral and/or laryngeal edema, proptosis, glossal/laryngeal edema, obtundation or postural hypotension carries poorer prognosis and requires urgent evaluation and early stenting (Harrison 22e p.585-586)","Tracheal obstruction from a superior mediastinal mass is the one potentially life-threatening complication of SVCS and demands emergent therapy; cardiac arrest or respiratory failure can occur, particularly with sedatives or general anesthesia (Harrison 22e p.585-586)","Cancer patient with back pain plus a crescendo pain pattern, Lhermitte's sign, or pain aggravated by cough/Valsalva/recumbency should be evaluated for cord compression as quickly as possible (Harrison 22e p.589)","Loss of bowel or bladder control, autonomic dysfunction (decreased anal tone, distended bladder, absent anal wink/bulbocavernosus reflex) signals significant cord involvement and is an unfavorable prognostic feature (Harrison 22e p.588)","Hyperkalemia in TLS with renal failure can rapidly become life-threatening, causing ventricular arrhythmias and sudden death (Harrison 22e p.593)","Acute adrenal insufficiency (adrenal crisis) is potentially lethal and may be masked by nonspecific symptoms attributed to cancer or therapy (Harrison 22e p.593)","Febrile neutropenia (fever with <1500 granulocytes/uL) is a treatment-related emergency requiring prompt management (Harrison 22e p.575)"],"prognosis":"In SVCS, mortality relates to the underlying cause rather than to caval obstruction itself, and clinical improvement occurs in most patients (sometimes via collateral circulation); SVCS recurs in 10-30% (Harrison 22e p.586). For spinal cord compression, tumor histology is an important determinant of recovery and survival, and rapid onset/progression of signs and symptoms are poor prognostic features; treatment is more often successful in ambulatory patients who retain sphincter control (Harrison 22e p.589). Brain metastases frequently cause major symptoms, disability and early death (Harrison 22e p.590). Lactic acidosis in cancer carries a poor prognosis regardless of treatment, and cancer patients with malignant cells on pericardial cytology have very poor survival (Harrison 22e p.587, 592). Despite aggressive prophylaxis, TLS with oliguric/anuric renal failure may still occur and often requires renal replacement therapy (Harrison 22e p.594).","pitfalls":["A normal chest radiograph is still compatible with the diagnosis of SVCS if other characteristic findings are present (Harrison 22e p.586)","A normal appearance of plain spine films does not exclude cancer; plain films are insensitive for cord compression (Harrison 22e p.589)","Vertebral collapse is not a reliable indicator of tumor since ~20% of collapses are osteoporotic (Harrison 22e p.589)","After SVC stenting, the prompt increase in venous return may precipitate heart failure and pulmonary edema (Harrison 22e p.586)","Urinary alkalinization with sodium bicarbonate is no longer recommended in TLS: although it increases uric acid solubility, a high pH decreases solubility of xanthine, hypoxanthine and calcium phosphate, potentially increasing intratubular crystallization (Harrison 22e p.593)","Rasburicase is contraindicated in glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency, and it causes ex vivo degradation of uric acid in the test tube giving spuriously low levels unless samples are cooled immediately (Harrison 22e p.594)","Pseudohyperkalemia in TLS shows no ECG abnormalities; plasma rather than serum potassium should be followed (Harrison 22e p.593)","Arterial blood gas oxygen tension can be spuriously low in hyperleukocytosis due to oxygen consumption by white cells; pulse oximetry is the most accurate measure of oxygenation (Harrison 22e p.591)"],"severityClassification":"Spinal cord compression management uses the NOMS framework (Memorial Sloan Kettering): Neurologic assessment (degree of epidural compression, myelopathy, functional radiculopathy), Oncologic assessment (tumor radiosensitivity), Mechanical assessment (spinal stability), and Systemic factors (extent of disease and comorbidities determining tolerance of therapy) (Harrison 22e p.589-590). TLS risk is stratified by tumor burden, renal function, hyperuricemia and high LDH (>1500 U/L); a high-risk management algorithm escalates from hydration and allopurinol to recombinant urate oxidase/rasburicase and hemodialysis based on serum uric acid, creatinine, potassium, phosphate and symptomatic hypocalcemia thresholds (Harrison 22e p.593-594).","references":["Harrison 22e p.585-595","Azizi AH et al: Superior vena cava syndrome. JACC Cardiovasc Interv 13:2896, 2020","Durani U, Hogan WJ: Emergencies in haematology: Tumour lysis syndrome. Br J Haematol 188:494, 2020","Lawton AJ et al: Assessment and management of patients with metastatic spinal cord compression: A multidisciplinary review. J Clin Oncol 37:61, 2019"],"crossLinks":["svc_obstruction","cord_compression","hypercalcemia","FEBRILE_NEUTROPENIA","hyponatremia"]},"osteoarthritis":{"name":"Osteoarthritis","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":383,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2949","clinicalPearls":["OA is the most common type of arthritis and a leading cause of disability in the elderly; prevalence rises strikingly with age, being uncommon before 40 and highly prevalent after 60 (Harrison 22e p.2949).","Joint involvement is characteristic: hips, knees, first MTP joint, cervical/lumbosacral spine, and in the hands the DIP, PIP, and thumb base; the wrist, elbow, and ankle are usually spared (Harrison 22e p.2949).","Joint pain is primarily activity-related early on, coming on during or just after use and resolving with rest; as disease progresses pain becomes continuous and may be bothersome at night (Harrison 22e p.2952).","Morning stiffness, when present, is usually brief (<30 min), in contrast to the prolonged stiffness (hours) of inflammatory arthritis (Harrison 22e p.2952).","Hand OA produces bony enlargement of the DIP joints (Heberden's nodes) and PIP joints (Bouchard's nodes) (Harrison 22e p.2949).","On hip examination, OA can be detected by loss of internal rotation on passive movement (Harrison 22e p.2953).","Mechanical symptoms (buckling, catching, locking) are common in knee OA and need further evaluation for internal derangement only when they follow an acute knee injury (Harrison 22e p.2952).","Osteophytes are an important radiographic hallmark of OA, along with joint-space narrowing reflecting cartilage loss (Harrison 22e pp.2952-2953)."],"pathophysiology":"OA is joint failure in which all joint structures undergo pathologic change. The pathologic hallmark is focal, initially nonuniform loss of hyaline articular cartilage, accompanied by thickening and sclerosis of the subchondral bony plate, marginal osteophyte outgrowth, stretching of the articular capsule, variable synovitis, and weakness of muscles bridging the joint (meniscal degeneration occurs in knees). Disease begins with joint injury when protective mechanisms (joint capsule/ligaments, bridging muscle, sensory afferents, underlying bone, and synovial-fluid lubrication via hyaluronic acid and lubricin) fail. Cartilage shows depletion of aggrecan and loss/unfurling of type 2 collagen, losing compressive stiffness; chondrocytes shift toward catabolism, producing matrix-degrading enzymes (MMPs, ADAMTS-5). Local inflammation (including mechanoinflammation), with cytokines, alarmins/DAMPs, and growth factors (BMP-2, TGF-beta drive osteophytes; VEGF drives vascular invasion), accelerates progression. Risk reflects the balance between joint vulnerability (age, female sex, genetics such as GDF5, prior damage, malalignment, bridging-muscle weakness, high bone density) and joint loading (obesity, occupational/repetitive use, major injury) (Harrison 22e pp.2949-2952).","additionalDifferentials":["Inflammatory arthritis (suggested by prolonged morning stiffness and involvement of many joints)","Bursitis (anserine bursitis medial/distal to the knee; trochanteric bursitis lateral to the hip)","Iliotibial band syndrome","Internal derangement of the knee (anterior cruciate ligament or meniscal tear), especially after acute injury"],"infectionMimics":["Septic arthritis (consider when synovial fluid white count is elevated/inflammatory)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Gout and pseudogout (crystal arthritis; suggested by synovial fluid white count >1000/uL with identifiable crystals)","Rheumatoid arthritis and other inflammatory arthritides","Fibromyalgia (noninflammatory pain amplification)"],"additionalInvestigations":["No blood tests are routinely indicated unless symptoms or signs suggest inflammatory arthritis (Harrison 22e p.2953).","Synovial fluid examination is often more helpful diagnostically than x-ray; a white count >1000/uL suggests inflammatory arthritis, gout, or pseudogout, the latter two confirmed by crystals (Harrison 22e p.2953).","Radiographs are not routinely indicated; order only when pain and findings are atypical of OA or when pain persists after effective treatment. Imaging findings correlate poorly with pain, and x-rays may be normal in early disease (insensitive to cartilage loss) (Harrison 22e p.2953).","MRI is not indicated in the diagnostic workup; meniscal tears and cartilage/bone lesions are common both in OA and in older persons without joint pain, and MRI findings rarely warrant a change in therapy (Harrison 22e p.2953)."],"redFlags":["Prominent nocturnal pain in the absence of end-stage OA merits a distinct workup (Harrison 22e p.2953).","Prolonged morning stiffness with many joints affected suggests inflammatory arthritis rather than OA (Harrison 22e p.2952).","Mechanical symptoms (buckling, catching, locking) developing after an acute knee injury warrant evaluation for internal derangement (Harrison 22e p.2952)."],"prognosis":"OA is a chronic, generally progressive disorder of the elderly whose high prevalence and impact on physical function make it a leading cause of disability. Severely malaligned knees may be destined to progress regardless of other risk factors, whereas weight loss greater than 10% of body weight reduces cartilage loss and, in most affected persons, alleviates pain (Harrison 22e pp.2949-2952).","pitfalls":["Distinguish OA from rheumatoid (and other inflammatory) arthritis, crystal arthritis (gout/pseudogout), and septic arthritis; prolonged morning stiffness, systemic features, and an inflammatory synovial fluid white count point away from OA.","Do not rely on radiographs to gauge symptom severity: x-ray changes correlate poorly with pain, and films may be normal in early disease.","Many persons with x-ray or MRI evidence of OA have no joint symptoms, so structural findings alone do not establish symptomatic OA.","Back pain and neck pain are not tied to x-ray findings of spinal OA and are evaluated separately."],"severityClassification":"Harrison's chapter 383 does not present a single formal grading scale; OA is characterized clinically along a spectrum from early, episodic activity-related pain with brief morning stiffness to advanced/end-stage disease with continuous and nocturnal pain. The chapter references the WOMAC index as a patient-reported outcome measure of pain, stiffness, and function (Harrison 22e p.2952).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2949"],"crossLinks":["rheumatoid","crystal_arthritis"]},"osteoporosis":{"name":"Osteoporosis","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic Bone","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":423,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3291","clinicalPearls":["WHO operationally defines osteoporosis as a bone density T-score of -2.5 or below (>=2.5 SD below the young-adult mean for same sex/race), measured at lumbar spine, total hip, or femoral neck; osteopenia (low bone density) is a T-score of -1.0 to -2.5 and is a risk factor for developing osteoporosis (Harrison 22e p.3291).","A fragility fracture (hip or vertebral) by definition establishes osteoporosis and warrants treatment, regardless of the T-score; patients presenting with hip or spine fractures by definition have osteoporosis (Harrison 22e p.3291, p.3298).","Osteoporosis is most common among postmenopausal women, but ~30% of fragility fractures occur in men; secondary osteoporosis arises from other underlying diseases (Harrison 22e p.3291).",">50% of fractures, including hip fractures, among postmenopausal women occur in individuals with osteopenia rather than densitometric osteoporosis, because that population is much larger - hence the shift toward absolute fracture-risk assessment (Harrison 22e p.3291).","FRAX and the Garvan Fracture Risk Calculator estimate 10-year probability of hip or major osteoporotic fracture by combining age, sex, and clinical risk factors with or without BMD, and inform country-specific intervention thresholds distinct from diagnostic thresholds (Harrison 22e p.3291, p.3298).","Glucocorticoids are the most common cause of medication-induced osteoporosis (Harrison 22e p.3296).","Any fracture (excluding fingers, toes, face, skull) in a woman aged >50 or a man aged >60 should trigger investigation for osteoporosis (Harrison 22e p.3293)."],"pathophysiology":"Osteoporosis is reduced bone strength leading to skeletal fragility and fractures; BMD alone underestimates fragility because microarchitectural deterioration and component misalignment also contribute. A low peak bone mass (genetic, hormonal, nutritional) may underlie disease, with superimposed age-related and extrinsic changes in bone remodeling. In adults remodeling (not modeling) predominates and serves to repair microdamage and supply calcium. Estrogen deficiency drives bone loss by (1) activating new remodeling sites and (2) creating an imbalance favoring resorption over formation: loss of estrogen increases RANKL and reduces osteoprotegerin, increasing osteoclast formation/recruitment, while shortening osteoblast life span and prolonging osteoclast longevity/activity. Trabecular bone (80% of surface area) is preferentially affected, so vertebral fractures are the earliest skeletal consequence. Estrogen also regulates remodeling in men. Heritability of BMD is ~50-80%; LRP5 activating mutations cause high bone mass and inactivating mutations cause osteoporosis-pseudoglioma syndrome (Harrison 22e p.3293-3295).","additionalDifferentials":["Osteomalacia (severe vitamin D deficiency in adults)","Multiple myeloma masquerading as generalized osteoporosis","Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS)","Primary hyperparathyroidism","Thyrotoxicosis / excessive thyroxine","Cushing's syndrome","Malabsorption / celiac disease","Mastocytosis","Osteogenesis imperfecta"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Myeloma (presents with bone pain and characteristic punched-out lesions; can masquerade as generalized osteoporosis)","Osteomalacia from vitamin D deficiency","Metabolic bone disease such as CKD-mineral bone disorder","Osteoarthritis/spinal spondylosis (bone spurs falsely raise spine BMD on DXA)"],"additionalInvestigations":["DXA of lumbar spine and hip (standard for BMD; T-scores vs young adults, Z-scores vs age/sex-matched reference)","Vertebral imaging per age/BMD/risk-factor indications (e.g. women >=70, men >=80; or with low-trauma fracture, height loss >=4 cm, glucocorticoid use)","Complete blood count; serum and 24-h urine calcium; renal and liver function tests","Serum PTH (and PTHrP) when hypercalcemia is present to distinguish hyperparathyroidism from malignancy","Serum 25(OH)D in those with fragility fracture or osteoporotic-range BMD","TSH to evaluate for hyperthyroidism","Serum/urine protein electrophoresis and serum free light chains to exclude myeloma; bone marrow biopsy if equivocal","Transglutaminase IgA antibodies for celiac disease","24-h urinary free cortisol or dexamethasone-suppressed cortisol when Cushing's suspected","24-h urine histamine or serum tryptase when mastocytosis suspected","Serum testosterone in men with osteoporosis","Biochemical bone turnover markers (P1NP, osteocalcin, bone-specific ALP for formation; serum/urine N- and C-telopeptide for resorption) mainly to monitor treatment response","Bone biopsy with double tetracycline labeling (now largely replaced; retained for CKD-MBD and research)"],"redFlags":["Vertebral fracture (even asymptomatic) is a major sign of skeletal fragility and markedly increases risk of subsequent fracture, especially within the first 12-24 months (imminent fracture risk)","Significant height loss (>4 cm) suggesting occult vertebral fracture","Hip fracture - high mortality (20-25% die within the year) and major loss of independence","More chronic severe pain after vertebral fracture should raise suspicion of multiple myeloma or other underlying bone condition"],"prognosis":"Lifetime osteoporotic fracture risk for a 50-year-old Caucasian woman is ~50% and ~25% for a man; lifetime hip fracture probability ~14% (women) and ~5% (men), roughly half for African Americans. Hip fractures carry high morbidity and mortality with 20-25% of patients dying within the year (higher among men and African Americans), and ~30% of survivors requiring long-term care, many never regaining prior independence. A first fracture exponentially increases risk of further fracture, especially in the first year; almost 20% of women sustain a second fracture within 2 years (Harrison 22e p.3291-3293).","pitfalls":["Failing to exclude secondary causes of osteoporosis (e.g. myeloma, hyperparathyroidism, thyrotoxicosis, Cushing's, malabsorption/celiac, hypogonadism, mastocytosis) before attributing low bone mass to primary disease, particularly in patients with fractures or unexpectedly low Z-scores.","Excluding a patient from osteoporosis evaluation simply because a fracture was traumatic - patients with osteoporosis are also more likely to sustain traumatic fractures, and any fracture warrants skeletal assessment.","Relying solely on BMD, which can underestimate fragility (microarchitectural deterioration not captured) and is falsely elevated in the spine by osteoarthritic bone spurs/spondylosis; small slim people show falsely low DXA values.","Fewer than 20% of fracture patients are investigated or treated for osteoporosis within 6 months; postfracture care remains fragmented.","Drug holidays are appropriate to consider for oral/IV bisphosphonates but should be avoided for denosumab and anabolic agents because of more rapid offset."],"severityClassification":"WHO densitometric categories: normal (T-score >= -1.0), osteopenia/low bone density (T-score -1.0 to -2.5), osteoporosis (T-score <= -2.5 at lumbar spine, femoral neck, or total hip). Presence of a fragility fracture (notably hip or vertebral) defines established/clinical osteoporosis regardless of T-score. Absolute fracture risk (FRAX/Garvan 10-year hip and major osteoporotic fracture probability) is increasingly used alongside or instead of T-score for treatment decisions (Harrison 22e p.3291, p.3297-3298).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3291"],"crossLinks":[]},"pancreatic_cancer":{"name":"Pancreatic Cancer","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":88,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.671","clinicalPearls":["The classic presentation of cancer arising in the head of the pancreas is 'painless' jaundice; jaundice is visually detectable once bilirubin exceeds ~2 mg/dL and may be accompanied by pruritus from bile salt skin deposition (p.672).","Pain, when present, is typically mid-epigastric and 'boring' with radiation to the back (from retroperitoneal invasion of the splanchnic nerve plexus) and may be worsened by eating or lying flat (p.672).","A palpable, non-tender gallbladder in the setting of biliary obstruction is Courvoisier's sign (p.672).","Migratory superficial thrombophlebitis and combined deep venous plus arterial thromboses (Trousseau's syndrome) can occur; metastatic disease is frequently associated with a hypercoagulable state requiring anticoagulation (p.672-674).","New-onset diabetes (especially with concomitant weight loss) or unexplained worsening of type 2/type 3c diabetes, or rising HbA1c/hyperglycemia in the preceding 1-2 years, may signal underlying pancreatic cancer (p.671-672).","CA19-9 is best drawn after the bilirubin has normalized because biliary obstruction itself elevates CA19-9; CA125 can help in the 8-15% of Lewis-antigen non-secretors who have undetectable CA19-9 (p.674).","Cigarette smoking is the greatest environmental risk factor, accounting for an estimated 25-30% of cases, with risk persisting at least 10 years after cessation (p.671).","Histologic/cytologic tissue diagnosis is essential and also enables molecular testing (next-generation sequencing for KRAS and other alterations) and microsatellite status determination, which increasingly guide therapy (p.674).","Signs of late-stage disease include a left supraclavicular Virchow's node and a periumbilical Sister Mary Joseph's nodule indicating peritoneal metastases (p.672-673)."],"pathophysiology":"Ductal adenocarcinoma is the most common and most lethal exocrine pancreatic neoplasm, characterized by infiltrating desmoplastic stromal reaction and arising in the head, body, or tail. Most cancers arise from pancreatic intraepithelial neoplasia (PanIN, graded 1-3 in a progression model), with genetic alterations accumulating as PanIN grade increases; some arise from noninvasive cystic precursors (e.g., IPMN). The mutational landscape is dominated by four commonly altered genes: KRAS (mutated in 90-95%, primarily codon 12), p16/CDKN2A (inactivated in >90% of invasive tumors), TP53 (~75%), and DPC4/SMAD4 (~50%); BRCA2 is mutated in 3.5-10%. A 'KRAS wildtype' subset (more common in patients under 55) may harbor actionable BRAF mutations, oncogenic fusions (NRG-1, NTRK1-3), or microsatellite instability (p.672).","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic pancreatitis","Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor","Acinar cell carcinoma","Colloid carcinoma","Medullary carcinoma","Adenosquamous carcinoma","Intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasm (IPMN)","Mucinous cystic neoplasm","Metastasis to the pancreas (renal, breast, lung, urothelial carcinoma, melanoma, lymphoma)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Chronic pancreatitis (can be difficult to distinguish from adenocarcinoma and is itself a risk factor)","Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor","Mucinous cystic neoplasm and IPMN","Metastatic disease to the pancreas from other primaries"],"additionalInvestigations":["Dual-phase contrast-enhanced CT using a pancreas-cancer protocol (arterial and portal venous phases) to assess tumor-vessel relationships, resectability, and staging","Abdominal ultrasonography (often first imaging in the jaundiced patient)","MRI","18F-glucose PET to detect occult metastatic disease","Endoscopic ultrasound (EUS) with EUS-guided core needle biopsy of pancreatic lesions","Tru-Cut core needle biopsy (preferred over fine-needle aspiration) for histologic diagnosis; percutaneous biopsy for liver/lymph node lesions","Next-generation sequencing for genomic alterations (including KRAS) and microsatellite status","Serum CA19-9 (drawn after bilirubin normalizes), CEA, and CA125","Staging laparoscopy (selectively, before or at definitive surgery)"],"redFlags":["Painless obstructive jaundice (classic for head-of-pancreas tumor)","Palpable non-tender gallbladder with obstruction (Courvoisier's sign)","Unintentional weight loss with loss of muscle mass","New-onset diabetes, especially with concomitant weight loss","Mid-epigastric boring pain radiating to the back","Migratory superficial thrombophlebitis / venous or arterial thrombosis (Trousseau's syndrome)","Left supraclavicular Virchow's node or periumbilical Sister Mary Joseph's nodule (late disease)","Light/clay-colored stools and steatorrhea"],"prognosis":"Pancreatic cancer is the most lethal human cancer, with an overall 5-year survival of about 13% (up from 3% in 1975). SEER estimates 5-year survival of ~44% for localized disease, ~16% for regional disease, and ~3.2% for metastatic disease. Late presentation is common, only about 10-20% of tumors are resectable, and the disease is the third leading cause of cancer mortality in the U.S. and projected to become the second by 2030 (p.671-672).","pitfalls":["Drawing CA19-9 while bilirubin is elevated falsely raises the level, since biliary obstruction itself elevates CA19-9 (p.674).","8-15% of patients are Lewis-antigen non-secretors with undetectable CA19-9, so a normal CA19-9 does not exclude disease; CA125 may help in this group (p.674).","Even high-grade PanIN precursor lesions are not detectable by current imaging, limiting early detection (p.672).","For average-risk individuals with no predisposing factors there is currently no recommended screening (p.671).","Whether diabetes causes or is caused by pancreatic cancer is not clearly established (p.671).","Fine-needle aspiration cytology is acceptable but less preferred than core (Tru-Cut) biopsy, which yields adequate tissue for molecular testing (p.674).","Biliary stenting is not necessarily warranted if upfront surgery is planned, but is required (where medically indicated) before neoadjuvant systemic therapy (p.674)."],"severityClassification":"Staged by the American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) staging system (Table 88-2). Clinically also categorized by extent of disease and therapeutic approach (Table 88-3): resectable (~10-20%), borderline resectable/locally advanced, and metastatic (p.674).","references":["Harrison 22e p.671","Harrison 22e p.672","Harrison 22e p.673","Harrison 22e p.674"],"crossLinks":["pancreatitis"]},"parathyroid_calcium_disorders":{"name":"Parathyroid & Calcium Disorders","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":422,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3271","clinicalPearls":["Primary hyperparathyroidism (pHPT) and malignancy are the two most common causes of hypercalcemia; the diagnostic split hinges on the PTH level (Harrison 22e p.3271).","pHPT is almost always a benign neoplastic disorder of the parathyroids (adenoma or hyperplasia) producing PTH that is not appropriately suppressed by the elevated calcium; parathyroid carcinoma is a rare cause (Harrison 22e p.3271).","In hypercalcemia, an elevated or 'inappropriately normal' PTH with elevated calcium and low/low-normal phosphorus is almost always pHPT; because of PTH's phosphaturic action, pHPT hypercalcemia is often accompanied by low/frankly low serum phosphate (Harrison 22e p.3271).","A suppressed PTH in the face of hypercalcemia means the process is independent of the parathyroid glands and is most often malignancy; many solid tumors secrete PTHrP, which mimics PTH at its receptor on bone and kidney (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Hypercalcemia from lymphoma or granulomatous disease (e.g., sarcoidosis, tuberculosis) is driven by extrarenal macrophage CYP27B1 conversion of 25(OH)D to 1,25(OH)2D; in such cases 1,25(OH)2D is increased or inappropriately normal (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Classic chronic hypercalcemia (months to years, fluctuating between high-normal and just above normal) is most often pHPT or FHH, occasionally multiple myeloma; malignancy-associated hypercalcemia usually has a more acute course (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Mild hypercalcemia (10.5-11.9 mg/dL) is largely asymptomatic; symptoms ('stones, bones, groans') include nephrolithiasis, reduced bone density / bone pain or pathologic fractures with long-standing hyperparathyroidism, constipation/nausea/vomiting/anorexia/peptic ulcer/pancreatitis, plus fatigue, proximal weakness, hyporeflexia, and neuropsychiatric changes (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Hypocalcemia is differentiated by whether PTH is low (hypoparathyroidism) or high (secondary hyperparathyroidism); a low or 'inappropriately low' PTH with hypocalcemia establishes hypoparathyroidism, whereas an elevated PTH points to the vitamin D axis or primary small-bowel calcium malabsorption (Harrison 22e p.3271).","In adults, hypoparathyroidism most commonly results from inadvertent damage to all four parathyroid glands during thyroid or parathyroid surgery; autoimmune endocrinopathy accounts for about a quarter of cases (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Neuromuscular irritability of hypocalcemia is detected by Chvostek's sign (perioral twitch on tapping the facial nerve) and the more specific Trousseau's sign (carpal spasm after inflating a BP cuff above systolic for 3 min); paresthesias of fingers, toes, and circumoral region are typical (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Because PTH is the main minute-by-minute defense against hypocalcemia, hypoparathyroidism can produce profound, life-threatening hypocalcemia, whereas vitamin D deficiency causes less severe hypocalcemia since the parathyroids mount a compensatory PTH rise (at the expense of bone) (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Impaired PTH secretion may be secondary to magnesium deficiency (via intracellular effects); hypomagnesemic hypocalcemia will not correct until magnesium is repleted (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Hungry bone syndrome after parathyroidectomy is a recognized cause of post-operative hypocalcemia with high PTH (Harrison 22e p.3271)."],"pathophysiology":"Extracellular calcium is held in a narrow range (~8.5-10.5 mg/dL) by feedback among the parathyroids, kidney, bone, and intestine. A fall in ECF calcium is sensed by the calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR) on parathyroid chief cells, triggering PTH release; PTH increases bone calcium resorption, distal-tubule reabsorption of filtered calcium, and renal proximal-tubule 1,25(OH)2D production, and 1,25(OH)2D in turn raises intestinal calcium absorption. Hypercalcemia arises from derangements of this loop: PTH-dependent overproduction (pHPT due to adenoma/hyperplasia, rarely carcinoma; tertiary hyperparathyroidism; ectopic PTH; FHH and CaSR-altering states such as lithium), or PTH-independent mechanisms (PTHrP-secreting tumors, lytic skeletal metastases, extrarenal 1,25(OH)2D production in granulomatous disease/lymphoma, vitamin D intoxication, increased bone resorption from thyrotoxicosis/Paget's/immobilization, and calcium overload e.g. milk-alkali). Hypocalcemia results from low PTH (hypoparathyroidism: surgical, autoimmune, infiltrative, genetic, CaSR-activating, or magnesium-related) or high PTH (secondary hyperparathyroidism from vitamin D deficiency, renal failure, intestinal malabsorption, or PTH resistance/pseudohypoparathyroidism). Low calcium increases neuromuscular irritability (Harrison 22e p.3271).","additionalDifferentials":["Primary hyperparathyroidism (PTH-dependent hypercalcemia) - parathyroid adenoma or hyperplasia, rarely carcinoma","Hypercalcemia of malignancy (PTH-suppressed) - PTHrP overproduction or lytic skeletal metastases (breast, myeloma)","Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia (FHH) - inactivating CASR/GNA11/AP2S1 mutations","Tertiary hyperparathyroidism (long-standing renal insufficiency)","Granulomatous disease- or lymphoma-associated hypercalcemia (extrarenal 1,25(OH)2D)","Vitamin D intoxication","Hypercalcemia of immobilization, thyrotoxicosis, Paget's disease, milk-alkali syndrome","Multiple myeloma","Postsurgical (post-thyroidectomy / post-parathyroidectomy) hypoparathyroidism","Autoimmune hypoparathyroidism (including APS-1)","Vitamin D deficiency / chronic renal insufficiency causing secondary hyperparathyroidism","Pseudohypoparathyroidism (PTH resistance)","Hypomagnesemia (or severe hypermagnesemia) impairing PTH","Autosomal dominant hypocalcemia (activating CaSR/GNA11 mutations)"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculosis - granulomatous extrarenal 1,25(OH)2D production causing hypercalcemia","Leprosy and fungal infections - granulomatous causes of 1,25(OH)2D-mediated hypercalcemia","COVID-19 - reported transient hypoparathyroidism (and even spontaneous resolution of pHPT) via unknown mechanisms"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pseudohypercalcemia (e.g., calcium-binding IgM in Waldenstrom's macroglobulinemia) and pseudohypocalcemia (low albumin from malnutrition, nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis; gadoversetamide MRI contrast) - artifactual values requiring albumin correction before workup","Sarcoidosis and other granulomatous disease - 1,25(OH)2D-mediated hypercalcemia","Lymphoma (B cell, cutaneous T cell/Sezary) - extrarenal 1,25(OH)2D","Thyrotoxicosis, Paget's disease, immobilization - increased bone resorption mimicking parathyroid hypercalcemia","Thiazides, lithium, vitamin A, foscarnet, teriparatide, aromatase inhibitors - drug-induced hypercalcemia","Acute pancreatitis, rhabdomyolysis, tumor lysis, burns - hypocalcemia from tissue injury / hyperphosphatemia","Respiratory or metabolic alkalosis - hypocalcemia from increased calcium binding to albumin"],"additionalInvestigations":["Total serum calcium with serum albumin to calculate corrected calcium (add 0.8 mg/dL per 1.0 g/dL albumin below 4.0; subtract when albumin is high)","Intact PTH by two-site assay - the key second test that separates PTH-dependent from PTH-independent disorders","Serum phosphorus (low/low-normal in pHPT; elevated in some hypocalcemic states)","Serum magnesium (hypo- or severe hypermagnesemia impairs PTH)","Serum creatinine / renal function","Serum 25(OH)D - useful in both hyper- and hypocalcemia to assess vitamin D stores","Serum 1,25(OH)2D - useful in hypercalcemia (increased/inappropriately normal in granulomatous disease or lymphoma) and suspected vitamin D resistance","PTHrP level when hypercalcemia of malignancy is suspected with suppressed PTH","Serum free light chains / SPEP-UPEP with immunofixation to exclude multiple myeloma","Calcium/creatinine clearance ratio (<0.01 suggests FHH); 24-hour urinary fractional calcium excretion (>0.02 supports pHPT)","Genetic sequencing of CASR, GNA11, AP2S1 for definitive FHH diagnosis when urine studies are inconclusive; genetic testing for syndromic/hereditary pHPT (MEN, CDC73)","ECG (hypercalcemia: bradycardia, AV block, QRS widening, short QTc; hypocalcemia: QTc prolongation)"],"redFlags":["Moderate-to-severe hypercalcemia (>=12-14 mg/dL) with progressive lethargy, stupor, or coma","Hypercalcemia with acute renal failure and volume depletion from polyuria","Hypercalcemic ECG changes: bradycardia, AV block, QRS widening, short QTc","Severe hypocalcemia causing seizures, carpopedal spasm, bronchospasm, or laryngospasm","Hypocalcemic QTc prolongation with risk of ventricular tachyarrhythmia (Torsade) and sudden death","Tetany (positive Trousseau's sign / carpal spasm) signaling impending neuromuscular crisis","New severe hypocalcemia after thyroid or parathyroid neck surgery (damage to all four glands), especially in patients with prior malabsorption","Syndromic pHPT with a jaw tumor (CDC73 mutation) carrying ~30% lifetime risk of parathyroid carcinoma"],"prognosis":"Mild, asymptomatic hypercalcemia (commonly pHPT detected incidentally in settings with routine calcium screening) carries a benign short-term course, whereas advanced pHPT can cause severe skeletal disease (osteitis fibrosa cystica). Hypoparathyroidism, because PTH is the chief minute-by-minute defense against hypocalcemia, can be profound, debilitating, and life-threatening, while vitamin D-deficiency hypocalcemia is typically milder owing to compensatory PTH secretion (at the expense of bone). Severe hypercalcemia (coma, renal failure, arrhythmia) and severe hypocalcemia (seizures, laryngospasm, Torsade) are medical emergencies (Harrison 22e p.3271).","pitfalls":["Failing to correct calcium for albumin before launching a workup - low albumin can mask true hypercalcemia and falsely suggest hypocalcemia (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Mistaking FHH for pHPT: FHH can present with mildly elevated PTH and hypercalcemia, but parathyroid surgery is ineffective; a calcium/creatinine clearance ratio <0.01 and family history of mild asymptomatic hypercalcemia or repeated failed neck surgeries should prompt CASR/GNA11/AP2S1 testing (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Overlooking lithium therapy, which raises the CaSR set point and can cause hyperparathyroidism (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Not repleting magnesium in hypocalcemia: hypomagnesemia impairs PTH secretion and the hypocalcemia will not correct until magnesium is restored (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Chvostek's sign is nonspecific - present in ~10% of normal individuals; Trousseau's sign is the more definitive marker of neuromuscular irritability (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Attributing hypercalcemia of immobilization solely to bed rest - an unrecognized resorptive process (pHPT, Paget's, myeloma) may be the real driver, especially in older patients (Harrison 22e p.3271).","When tetany accompanies alkalosis, a second cause of impaired calcium homeostasis is usually present (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Ectopic PTH secretion, unlike pHPT, does not fluctuate with correction of calcium because it is not under CaSR control (Harrison 22e p.3271)."],"severityClassification":"Hypercalcemia severity (Harrison 22e p.3271): mild 10.5-11.9 mg/dL (largely asymptomatic); moderate >=12-14 mg/dL; severe >=14 mg/dL (progressive lethargy, stupor, coma, prominent GI symptoms, acute renal failure). Hypocalcemia severity ranges from asymptomatic (mild/chronic) to moderate-severe with paresthesias, positive Chvostek's/Trousseau's signs, through severe with seizures, carpopedal spasm, broncho-/laryngospasm, and QTc prolongation with arrhythmia risk.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3271","Harrison 22e ch.57 p.362-366"],"crossLinks":["hypercalcemia","thyroid_cancer"]},"parkinsons_disease":{"name":"Parkinson's Disease","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":446,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3493","clinicalPearls":["PD is the second most common age-related neurodegenerative disease after Alzheimer's; mean age of onset ~60 years, lifetime risk ~3% in men and ~2% in women, though cases can occur in the twenties or younger, particularly with a pathogenic gene mutation (p.3493).","Cardinal motor features are bradykinesia, rest tremor, rigidity, and gait dysfunction with postural instability; additional motor features include freezing of gait, micrographia, masked facies (hypomimia), reduced eye blinking, drooling, soft voice (hypophonia), and dysphagia (p.3493-3494).","Parkinsonism (bradykinesia with rigidity and/or tremor) combined with rest tremor, asymmetry of motor impairment, and a good levodopa response is much more likely to predict the correct pathologic diagnosis; with these U.K. Brain Bank Criteria, PD can be confirmed pathologically in >90% of patients (p.3495).","Levodopa is the gold standard symptomatic therapy; almost all PD patients improve, and failure to respond to an adequate trial of levodopa should cause the diagnosis to be questioned (p.3501).","Dopamine-system imaging (PET/SPECT) typically shows reduced, asymmetric striatal uptake, most pronounced in the posterior putamen with relative sparing of the caudate; it is useful when the diagnosis is uncertain but is not routinely required (p.3495-3496).","Among forms of parkinsonism, PD is the most common (~75% of cases) (p.3495)."],"pathophysiology":"Pathologic hallmarks are degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta (SNc), reduced striatal dopamine, and intraneuronal alpha-synuclein-staining inclusions in cell bodies and axons (Lewy bodies and Lewy neurites; collectively Lewy pathology). Lewy pathology can also affect cholinergic neurons of the nucleus basalis of Meynert, noradrenergic neurons of the locus coeruleus, serotonergic raphe neurons, and the olfactory system, cerebral hemispheres, spinal cord, and peripheral autonomic nervous system, accounting for nonmotor features. Pathology may begin in the peripheral autonomic nervous system, GI tract, olfactory system, or dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus and spread sequentially (Braak staging), giving rise to 'body-first' versus 'brain-first' concepts. In the basal-ganglia network, dopamine denervation leads to increased firing of the subthalamic nucleus (STN) and globus pallidus interna (GPi), excessive inhibition of the thalamus, reduced cortical motor activation, and the emergence of parkinsonian features. Pathogenesis involves an interacting network of oxidative stress, mitochondrial dysfunction, protein aggregation, excitotoxicity, and inflammation; a 'double hit' of genetic susceptibility plus an environmental insult has been proposed (p.3493-3494, p.3498-3500).","additionalDifferentials":["Atypical parkinsonism: multiple-system atrophy (MSA) - cerebellar (MSA-c) and Parkinson (MSA-p) types","Progressive supranuclear palsy (PSP) - parkinsonian and Richardson variants","Corticobasal syndrome (CBS)","Dementia with Lewy bodies / PD with dementia","Secondary parkinsonism: drug-induced, tumor, infection, vascular, normal-pressure hydrocephalus, trauma, liver failure, toxins (carbon monoxide, manganese, MPTP, cyanide, hexane, methanol, carbon disulfide)","Essential tremor (high-frequency 6-10 Hz symmetric postural tremor with prominent kinetic component)","Wilson's disease","Huntington's disease (especially the juvenile Westphal variant)","Dopa-responsive dystonia (DRD)","Spinocerebellar ataxias and neurodegeneration with brain iron accumulation (e.g., PANK-associated neurodegeneration)"],"infectionMimics":["Infection-related secondary parkinsonism (basal ganglia damage from CNS infection)","Post-influenza / postencephalitic parkinsonism (historical association with the influenza A epidemic); new-onset PD has been reported following SARS-CoV-2 infection, which can also worsen existing PD features"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Drug-induced parkinsonism - most commonly dopamine-blocking neuroleptics, and also metoclopramide (a neuroleptic agent used for GI problems), tetrabenazine, calcium channel blockers (flunarizine, cinnarizine), amiodarone, and lithium","Vascular parkinsonism (stroke affecting the SNc may closely resemble PD)","Toxin-induced parkinsonism (carbon monoxide or manganese damaging the globus pallidus resemble atypical parkinsonism with poor levodopa response)","Tumor and normal-pressure hydrocephalus","Atypical parkinsonism (MSA, PSP, CBS)","Essential tremor","Dopa-responsive dystonia"],"additionalInvestigations":["Dopamine-system imaging with PET or SPECT (reduced, asymmetric striatal/posterior-putamen uptake); not routinely required","Metabolic basal ganglia/thalamus imaging (18F-deoxyglucose) - may help distinguish atypical parkinsonism (decreased GPi with increased thalamic activity, the reverse of PD)","MRI - to evaluate for vascular/structural causes and atypical features (e.g., midbrain 'hummingbird sign' in PSP, putaminal rim change or pontine 'hot cross bun' sign in MSA); transcranial sonography or special MRI protocols may detect increased SNc iron","Alpha-synuclein seeding amplification assay (SAA) on CSF or skin - high sensitivity and specificity, can distinguish PD from other parkinsonisms (currently mainly research use)","Genetic testing - selective use for strong family history, early onset (<40 years), or particular ethnic backgrounds (GBA1, LRRK2, SNCA, etc.); monogenic forms account for ~5% of cases (~15% including GBA1 variants)","Serum/urinary copper studies to exclude Wilson's disease, which is treatable with copper chelators"],"redFlags":["Early speech and gait impairment with early falls, lack of rest tremor, and lack of motor asymmetry - suggests atypical parkinsonism","Poor or no response to an adequate trial of levodopa - suggests atypical or secondary parkinsonism","Exposure to neuroleptics - suggests drug-induced parkinsonism","Onset before age 40 years - consider a genetic form of PD, Wilson's disease, or dopa-responsive dystonia","Liver disease - consider Wilson's disease or non-Wilsonian hepatolenticular degeneration","Hallucinations and dementia preceding or as the first/early feature - suggests dementia with Lewy bodies","Diplopia, impaired vertical gaze, slow saccades with impaired downgaze - suggests PSP","Prominent orthostatic hypotension or prominent cerebellar signs - suggests MSA"],"prognosis":"PD is progressive. Levodopa prolongs independence and employability, improves quality of life, and increases life span, but chronic treatment with ongoing disease progression is associated in most patients with motor complications - response fluctuations ('on'/'off', wearing-off, on-off phenomenon) and dyskinesias. As the disease advances, nondopaminergic features that respond poorly to levodopa emerge - falling, freezing, autonomic dysfunction, sleep disorders, and dementia - and these (especially falls and dementia) are the primary source of disability and the main reason for hospitalization and nursing home placement in advanced PD. Atypical parkinsonisms generally follow a more aggressive course than classic PD (p.3496-3497, p.3501).","pitfalls":["Diagnosing PD on two of three cardinal features alone carries a ~24% postmortem error rate; rest tremor, motor asymmetry, and a good levodopa response markedly improve diagnostic accuracy (p.3495).","Drug-induced parkinsonism is the most common secondary parkinsonism and is easily missed - remember that metoclopramide, used for GI symptoms, is a neuroleptic, and that calcium channel blockers (flunarizine, cinnarizine), tetrabenazine, amiodarone, and lithium can also be responsible (p.3497).","Wilson's disease must be actively excluded because progression can be prevented with copper chelators (p.3497).","Dopamine-system imaging cannot separate PD from atypical parkinsonism, since striatal dopamine depletion occurs in both (p.3496).","Some genetic forms of PD (e.g., certain LRRK2 cases) may occur without Lewy bodies, and MPTP toxicity causes parkinsonism typically without Lewy bodies (p.3496, p.3498)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not present a single formal severity grading; clinical diagnostic confidence is staged by the MDS Clinical Diagnostic Criteria into 'clinically established PD' and 'clinically probable PD', which combine core motor parkinsonism with supportive criteria, absolute exclusion criteria, and red flags. Disease evolution is commonly framed by the emergence of levodopa-related motor complications and levodopa-unresponsive nondopaminergic features in advanced PD (p.3495-3496, p.3501).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3493"],"crossLinks":[]},"pericardial_disease":{"name":"Pericardial Disease","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":281,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2078-2084","clinicalPearls":["Acute pericarditis has four principal diagnostic features: chest pain, a pericardial friction rub, characteristic ECG changes, and pericardial effusion (Harrison 22e p.2078).","Pericarditic chest pain is typically retrosternal/left precordial, often pleuritic, may radiate to the trapezius ridge, and is characteristically intensified by lying supine and relieved by sitting up and leaning forward (Harrison 22e p.2079).","A pericardial friction rub is audible at some point in approximately 85% of patients with acute pericarditis; it may have up to three components per cycle and is best heard at end expiration with the patient upright and leaning forward (Harrison 22e p.2079).","The ECG of acute pericarditis classically evolves through four stages: stage 1 widespread upwardly-concave ST elevation with PR-segment depression, stage 2 normalization of ST, stage 3 T-wave inversion, and stage 4 return to normal (Harrison 22e p.2079).","Beck's triad of cardiac tamponade is hypotension, soft or absent heart sounds, and jugular venous distention with a prominent x descent but an absent y descent (Harrison 22e p.2081).","Pulsus paradoxus (inspiratory fall in systolic pressure >10 mmHg) is an important clue to tamponade, but also occurs in about one-third of constrictive pericarditis cases and in hypovolemic shock, obstructive airway disease, and pulmonary embolism (Harrison 22e p.2081-2082).","In constrictive pericarditis the rigid thickened pericardium limits ventricular filling only after early diastole (unimpeded in early diastole, then abruptly reduced), whereas in tamponade filling is impeded throughout diastole (Harrison 22e p.2083).","Kussmaul's sign (failure of venous pressure to decline, or a rise, during inspiration) is common in chronic constrictive pericarditis but is also seen in tricuspid stenosis, right ventricular infarction, and restrictive cardiomyopathy; it is negative in cor pulmonale (Harrison 22e p.2083-2084).","A pericardial knock, an early third heart sound at the apex from abrupt cessation of ventricular filling, is often conspicuous in constrictive pericarditis (Harrison 22e p.2083).","Constrictive pericarditis produces an M-shaped atrial pressure contour with prominent x and y descents; the y descent (diminished/absent in tamponade) is the most prominent deflection, and the ventricular pressure pulse shows a 'square root' sign in diastole (Harrison 22e p.2083).","In developing nations a high percentage of chronic constrictive pericarditis is tuberculous, but TB is now an uncommon cause in North America and Western Europe; other causes include prior viral/idiopathic pericarditis, cardiac surgery, mediastinal irradiation, and neoplasm (Harrison 22e p.2083)."],"pathophysiology":"The pericardium is a double-layered sac (visceral and parietal layers) separated by 15-50 mL of plasma ultrafiltrate; it restrains sudden cardiac chamber dilation and fixes cardiac position. In acute pericarditis, inflammation produces fibrinous or effusive disease. Cardiac tamponade occurs when fluid accumulates in the pericardial space in quantity sufficient to obstruct ventricular inflow, raising intrapericardial pressure and limiting diastolic filling throughout diastole, reducing stroke volume, cardiac output, and arterial pressure. As little as 200 mL can cause tamponade when fluid develops rapidly, versus >2000 mL when it accumulates slowly and the pericardium stretches. In chronic constrictive pericarditis, healing of fibrinous/serofibrinous pericarditis or resorption of effusion produces granulation tissue that contracts into a rigid, sometimes calcified scar encasing the heart; ventricular filling is unimpeded early in diastole then abruptly halts at the pericardium's elastic limit. End-diastolic pressures in both ventricles and mean atrial, pulmonary venous, and systemic venous pressures equalize within ~5 mmHg, while resting systolic function is typically normal or only mildly impaired (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2081, p.2083).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute myocardial infarction (common confusion with acute pericarditis; in pericarditis ST elevations are upwardly concave, biomarker elevations are modest, and pain may precede or coincide with fever) (Harrison 22e p.2079, p.2083)","Restrictive cardiomyopathy (shares restriction of ventricular filling; must be distinguished from constrictive pericarditis because the latter is surgically correctable) (Harrison 22e p.2084)","Right ventricular infarction (may mimic tamponade with hypotension, elevated JVP, slow y descent, occasional paradoxical pulse) (Harrison 22e p.2082)","Cor pulmonale (systemic venous hypertension and paradoxical pulse, but Kussmaul's sign is negative and advanced parenchymal lung disease is apparent) (Harrison 22e p.2084)","Tricuspid stenosis (congestive hepatomegaly, splenomegaly, ascites, venous distention, but characteristic murmur present) (Harrison 22e p.2084)","Hepatic cirrhosis (constrictive pericarditis with hepatomegaly, jaundice, and intractable ascites may be mistaken for cirrhosis; inspection of distended neck veins avoids the error) (Harrison 22e p.2084)"],"infectionMimics":["Viral pericarditis (coxsackievirus A and B, echovirus, influenza, mumps, herpes simplex, varicella-zoster, adenovirus, cytomegalovirus, HIV, post-acute COVID, mpox) (Harrison 22e p.2079, p.2082)","Tuberculous pericarditis (effusion, constriction, or effusive-constrictive disease; suggested by high adenosine deaminase or M. tuberculosis DNA by PCR, often requiring pericardial tissue) (Harrison 22e p.2082, p.2084)","Pyogenic/purulent bacterial pericarditis (pneumococcus, Streptococcus, Staphylococcus, Neisseria, Legionella, Chlamydia; from cardiothoracic surgery, lung/pleural extension, esophageal rupture, or valve-ring abscess; fever, chills, septicemia, poor prognosis) (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2083)","Fungal pericarditis (histoplasmosis, coccidioidomycosis, blastomycosis, aspergillosis, candidiasis) (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2083)","Parasitic/protozoal and syphilitic pericarditis (amebiasis, toxoplasmosis, echinococcosis, trichinosis) (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2083)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Uremic and dialysis-associated pericarditis (renal failure; fibrinous, serosanguinous effusions, friction rub common but pain usually mild) (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2083)","Neoplastic pericarditis (lung and breast carcinoma, melanoma, lymphoma, leukemia; metastatic invasion, primary tumors, mesothelioma) (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2083)","Postcardiac injury syndrome (postpericardiotomy, posttraumatic, post-MI Dressler's syndrome; hypersensitivity/autoimmune reaction to myocardial antigens 1-4 weeks after injury) (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2082)","Collagen vascular / autoimmune disease (SLE, rheumatoid arthritis, scleroderma, ankylosing spondylitis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis, IgG4 disease, rheumatic fever, polyarteritis nodosa) (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2083)","Drug-induced pericarditis (procainamide, hydralazine, phenytoin, isoniazid, minoxidil, anticoagulants, methysergide) (Harrison 22e p.2078)","Postirradiation (mediastinal radiation) pericarditis, acute or chronic constrictive (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2083)","Aortic dissection with leakage into the pericardial sac (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2081)","Acute myocardial infarction and metabolic causes (myxedema, cholesterol pericarditis) (Harrison 22e p.2078)","Familial Mediterranean fever and other autoinflammatory syndromes (Harrison 22e p.2078)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography: most widely used, sensitive, specific, bedside-capable; localizes and estimates pericardial fluid and shows late-diastolic collapse of the RV free wall and right atrium in tamponade (Harrison 22e p.2079, p.2082)","Doppler echocardiography: in tamponade and constriction, inspiratory increase in tricuspid/pulmonic flow velocities with decrease in pulmonary venous/mitral/aortic velocities and a leftward septal shift (respirophasic variation) (Harrison 22e p.2082, p.2084)","CT and MRI: superior for loculated effusions, pericardial thickening, pericardial masses, calcification, and detecting pericardial inflammation; more accurate than echocardiography for confirming or excluding constriction (MRI assesses myocardial involvement) (Harrison 22e p.2079, p.2084)","ECG: stage 1-4 evolution in acute pericarditis; low QRS voltage and electrical alternans with large effusion/tamponade; low voltage and diffuse T-wave changes with atrial fibrillation in constriction (Harrison 22e p.2079, p.2081, p.2083)","Chest radiograph: enlarged cardiac silhouette with a 'water bottle' configuration in large effusion; pericardial calcification (most common in tuberculous constriction) (Harrison 22e p.2079, p.2084)","Pericardiocentesis with fluid analysis: cell counts, cytology for neoplastic cells, cultures, M. tuberculosis PCR and adenosine deaminase (Harrison 22e p.2082)","Pericardial biopsy (e.g., via limited thoracotomy) when fluid analysis is non-diagnostic, especially for suspected tuberculous or neoplastic disease (Harrison 22e p.2082, p.2084)","Laboratory: elevated C-reactive protein and white blood cell count in viral/idiopathic pericarditis (Harrison 22e p.2082)"],"redFlags":["Cardiac tamponade: hypotension, elevated jugular venous pressure, and unexplained sudden enlargement of the cardiac silhouette; may be rapidly fatal if not recognized and treated promptly (Harrison 22e p.2081)","Reduced QRS amplitude with electrical alternans of P, QRS, or T waves raises suspicion of tamponade (Harrison 22e p.2081)","Predictors of poor prognosis warranting hospitalization in acute pericarditis: fever >38C, subacute onset, large pericardial effusion, immunosuppression, or evidence of tamponade (Harrison 22e p.2080)","Purulent (pyogenic) pericarditis carries a poor prognosis and requires immediate drainage and antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.2083)","Without treatment, tuberculous pericardial disease can progress to constriction; constriction should be treated surgically (Harrison 22e p.2084)"],"prognosis":"Viral or idiopathic acute pericarditis usually runs its course in a few days to 4 weeks; recurrent (relapsing) pericarditis occurs in approximately one-fourth of patients, and both tamponade and constriction are possible but infrequent complications. Most patients with acute pericarditis can be managed as outpatients with careful follow-up. Untreated cardiac tamponade may be rapidly fatal. Purulent pericarditis generally has a poor prognosis. Without treatment, tuberculous pericardial disease may progress to constriction. Pericardial resection is the only definitive treatment of constrictive pericarditis, with benefits accruing progressively over months; operative mortality is in the range of 5-10% even at experienced centers, highest in the most severe disease, especially radiation-induced (Harrison 22e p.2080, p.2082, p.2083, p.2084).","pitfalls":["Distinguishing constrictive pericarditis from restrictive cardiomyopathy is critical because constriction is surgically correctable; their hemodynamics overlap (square-root sign and equalized diastolic pressures occur in both) and echocardiography alone cannot definitively establish or exclude constriction, so MRI/CT is needed (Harrison 22e p.2083, p.2084).","Acute pericarditis is commonly mistaken for AMI and vice versa; in pericarditis the ST elevations are upwardly concave, reciprocal depression is limited to aVR/V1, and biomarker rises (from epi-myocarditis) are modest relative to AMI (Harrison 22e p.2079, p.2083).","Pericardial calcification may occur without constriction, and constriction may occur without calcification, so calcification neither confirms nor excludes the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2084).","Acute idiopathic pericarditis is a diagnosis of exclusion with no specific test; all other causes of fibrinous pericarditis must be considered (Harrison 22e p.2082-2083).","Constrictive pericarditis with hepatomegaly, jaundice, and intractable ascites can be misdiagnosed as hepatic cirrhosis unless the distended neck veins with characteristic waveform are recognized (Harrison 22e p.2084).","Pain is often absent in slowly developing tuberculous, postirradiation, neoplastic, and uremic pericarditis, so its absence does not exclude pericardial disease (Harrison 22e p.2079).","Anticoagulants should be avoided in acute pericarditis because they could cause bleeding into the pericardial cavity and tamponade (Harrison 22e p.2080)."],"severityClassification":"Clinical classification (Table 281-1): I. Acute pericarditis (<6 weeks) - A. Fibrinous, B. Effusive (serous or sanguineous); II. Subacute pericarditis (6 weeks to 6 months) - A. Effusive-constrictive, B. Constrictive; III. Chronic pericarditis (>6 months) - A. Constrictive, B. Adhesive (nonconstrictive). Etiologic classification: infectious, noninfectious, and presumed autoimmune. Table 281-2 distinguishes tamponade, constrictive pericarditis, restrictive cardiomyopathy, right ventricular myocardial infarction, and effusive-constrictive pericarditis by clinical, ECG, echocardiographic, and CT/MRI features (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2080).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2078-2084","Harrison 22e Chapter 281, Pericardial Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"peripheral_neuropathy":{"name":"Peripheral Neuropathy","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":457,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3594","clinicalPearls":["Three goals in the patient with neuropathy: localize the lesion, identify the cause, and determine treatment; the assessment is structured around seven key questions covering systems involved, distribution of weakness, nature of sensory loss, upper motor neuron signs, temporal evolution, hereditary clues, and associated medical conditions (Harrison 22e p.3595).","Pattern recognition is central: length-dependent symmetric distal sensory loss (Pattern 2) points to diabetes/metabolic, drugs, toxins, alcohol, hereditary causes; symmetric proximal AND distal weakness with sensory loss (Pattern 1) is the hallmark of acquired immune demyelinating polyneuropathy (GBS and CIDP); asymmetric/multifocal deficits (Pattern 3, mononeuropathy multiplex) suggest vasculitis, cryoglobulinemia, amyloid, sarcoid, or infection (Harrison 22e p.3596).","Nerve conduction studies and needle EMG distinguish axonal degeneration (low-amplitude potentials, relatively preserved distal latencies and conduction velocities, fibrillations on EMG) from segmental demyelination (slow conduction velocities, prolonged distal latencies, relatively preserved amplitudes, no fibrillations) (Harrison 22e p.3597).","Nonuniform slowing, conduction block, or temporal dispersion on NCS suggests an ACQUIRED demyelinating neuropathy (GBS or CIDP), whereas uniform slowing suggests a HEREDITARY demyelinating neuropathy (e.g., CMT type 1) (Harrison 22e p.3597).","Pure loss of pain and temperature with preserved vibration/position sense, normal strength and reflexes, and normal NCS indicates a small-fiber neuropathy; the most commonly identified causes are diabetes mellitus or glucose intolerance, with amyloid neuropathy also to be considered (Harrison 22e p.3596).","Dramatic proprioceptive/vibratory loss with normal strength, especially when non-length-dependent (arms more than legs or asymmetric), points to a sensory neuronopathy/ganglionopathy (Pattern 9) rather than a typical length-dependent polyneuropathy (Harrison 22e p.3597).","Diabetes mellitus is the most common cause of peripheral neuropathy in developed countries; distal symmetric sensory/sensorimotor polyneuropathy (DSPN) is the most common form, beginning in the toes and ascending (Harrison 22e p.3604).","Diabetic third nerve palsies are characteristically pupil-sparing (Harrison 22e p.3604)."],"pathophysiology":"Peripheral nerves contain mixed sensory, motor, and autonomic fibers grouped as large myelinated, small myelinated, and small unmyelinated classes; motor axons are usually large myelinated fast-conducting (~50 m/s) fibers, large-diameter sensory fibers carry proprioception and vibration, and small myelinated/unmyelinated fibers carry pain, temperature, and autonomic signals. Disease may target the neuronal cell body (neuronopathy/ganglionopathy), the myelin sheath (myelinopathy/demyelination), or the axon (axonopathy), each producing distinct clinical and electrophysiologic features. Most generalized polyneuropathies are length-dependent axonopathies producing distal (glove-and-stocking) deficits, whereas ganglionopathies produce non-length-dependent loss. Demyelination slows or blocks conduction; axonal loss reduces response amplitudes (Harrison 22e p.3594).","additionalDifferentials":["Inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy - Guillain-Barre syndrome (acute) and CIDP (chronic/relapsing) (Pattern 1) (Harrison 22e p.3595)","Cryptogenic/idiopathic sensory polyneuropathy (CSPN) - no etiology found in about half of patients despite extensive evaluation (Harrison 22e p.3595)","Diabetes mellitus and other metabolic neuropathies (DSPN, autonomic, radiculoplexus/Bruns-Garland, mononeuropathies) (Harrison 22e p.3604)","Hereditary neuropathies - Charcot-Marie-Tooth, HSAN, familial amyloid polyneuropathy, SORD neuropathy (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Vasculitic neuropathy presenting as mononeuropathy multiplex (Pattern 3) (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Uremic neuropathy in renal failure (~60% of patients) (Harrison 22e p.3605)","Nutritional/toxic neuropathies (B12, thiamine, alcohol, drugs, chemotherapy, heavy metals) (Harrison 22e p.3611)","Sensory neuronopathy/ganglionopathy - paraneoplastic (anti-Hu), Sjogren's syndrome, CANVAS, cisplatin, vitamin B6 toxicity (Pattern 9) (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Connective tissue/autoimmune disease - rheumatoid arthritis, SLE, scleroderma, sarcoidosis (Harrison 22e p.3605)"],"infectionMimics":["HIV infection - distal symmetric polyneuropathy, AIDP/CIDP, progressive polyradiculopathy, multiple mononeuropathies, sensory ganglionopathy (Harrison 22e p.3606)","Leprosy (Mycobacterium leprae) - most common cause of peripheral neuropathy in Southeast Asia, Africa, and South America; mononeuropathies or symmetric sensorimotor polyneuropathy (Harrison 22e p.3606)","Lyme disease (Borrelia burgdorferi) - facial neuropathy (bilateral in ~half, unlike idiopathic Bell's palsy), polyradiculoneuropathy, multiple mononeuropathies (Harrison 22e p.3606)","Diphtheria (Corynebacterium diphtheriae) - toxin-mediated neuropathy 3-4 weeks after infection (Harrison 22e p.3606)","Other infectious/inflammatory causes producing CSF pleocytosis in suspected GBS/CIDP - HIV, Lyme, sarcoidosis, lymphomatous/leukemic root infiltration (Harrison 22e p.3598)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Motor neuron disease (e.g., ALS) - asymmetric distal weakness without sensory loss, especially with upper motor neuron findings (Pattern 5) (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Multifocal motor neuropathy - treatable mimic of motor neuron disease (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Radiculopathy, plexopathy, and compressive/entrapment mononeuropathy (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Neuromuscular junction disorders - myasthenia gravis, Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Myopathy, including isolated neck extensor myopathy (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Combined system degeneration - distal sensory signs plus symmetric upper motor neuron involvement from B12, copper, or vitamin E deficiency, severe hepatic disease, or adrenomyeloneuropathy (Pattern 6) (Harrison 22e p.3597)","Critical illness polyneuropathy versus critical illness myopathy in the ICU patient who cannot be weaned from the ventilator (Harrison 22e p.3606)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Nerve conduction studies and needle EMG to localize (mono- vs polyneuropathy vs radiculopathy/plexopathy) and classify as axonal vs demyelinating (Harrison 22e p.3597)","Autonomic testing (heart rate response to deep breathing, Valsalva, tilt-table, quantitative sudomotor axon reflex) for pure small-fiber or autonomic neuropathy when routine NCS are normal (Harrison 22e p.3597)","Baseline labs: CBC, electrolytes, renal and hepatic function, fasting glucose and HbA1c, thyroid function, B12, folate, ESR, rheumatoid factor, ANA, SPEP with immunofixation, and serum/urine free light chains (Harrison 22e p.3597)","Oral glucose tolerance test in painful sensory neuropathy even when fasting glucose and HbA1c are normal (abnormal in ~one-third) (Harrison 22e p.3597)","Serum free light chains and kappa/lambda ratio (more sensitive than SPEP/immunofixation) and skeletal survey if amyloidosis or monoclonal gammopathy suspected; hematology referral for marrow biopsy (Harrison 22e p.3597)","Vasculitis workup for mononeuropathy multiplex: ANCA, cryoglobulins, hepatitis serology, Lyme Western blot, HIV, occasionally CMV titer (Harrison 22e p.3598)","Lumbar puncture in suspected GBS/CIDP for elevated CSF protein (pleocytosis should be absent in idiopathic cases) (Harrison 22e p.3598)","Antibodies to SS-A/Ro, SS-B/La, and antineuronal nuclear (anti-Hu) antibodies in sensory ataxia/ganglionopathy; nerve biopsy reserved mainly for suspected amyloid or vasculitis and only when NCS are abnormal; skin biopsy for small-fiber neuropathy (Harrison 22e p.3598)"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive symmetric proximal and distal weakness with sensory loss (Pattern 1) - acute (GBS) or chronic (CIDP) demyelinating neuropathy; GBS can require respiratory support, so identifying this treatable pattern is critical (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Acute/subacute course (days to weeks) - GBS, vasculitis, diabetic or Lyme radiculopathy require prompt evaluation (Harrison 22e p.3597)","Autonomic dysfunction without diabetes (orthostatic drop without compensatory tachycardia, bowel/bladder/sexual dysfunction) - should raise concern for amyloid polyneuropathy (Harrison 22e p.3595)","Mononeuropathy multiplex pattern - suggests vasculitis and warrants an urgent vasculitis workup (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Severe sensory ataxia from a ganglionopathy - consider paraneoplastic (anti-Hu, often small-cell lung cancer); the neuropathy can precede detection of malignancy and should prompt a cancer search (Harrison 22e p.3598)"],"prognosis":"Most neuropathies are insidious and slowly progressive; course may be monophasic, progressive, or relapsing (e.g., CIDP, porphyria). Despite extensive evaluation, about half of patients have no etiology identified (cryptogenic/idiopathic sensory polyneuropathy). Outcome depends heavily on cause: treatable acquired demyelinating neuropathies (GBS, CIDP) and treatable systemic causes can respond to therapy, whereas axonal degeneration tends to recover slowly and incompletely. Even with adequate cobalamin replacement, treatment of B12-deficiency neuropathy usually does not fully reverse deficits and at least 50% of patients retain some permanent neurologic deficit (Harrison 22e p.3595, p.3612).","pitfalls":["Missing a treatable cause - acquired demyelinating neuropathy (GBS/CIDP) and multifocal motor neuropathy are treatable and should be distinguished from untreatable mimics such as ALS (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Overlooking treatable systemic/metabolic causes such as B12 deficiency, thyroid disease, or a paraprotein/monoclonal gammopathy in an apparently idiopathic polyneuropathy (Harrison 22e p.3597)","B12-deficiency neuropathy can occur with only peripheral findings and without anemia or macrocytosis (lacking in up to 40%); check methylmalonic acid and homocysteine when suspicion is high (Harrison 22e p.3612)","Routine antiganglioside autoantibody panels have no proven added value beyond a complete clinical exam and detailed EDx, and a heavy metal screen is not a useful blind screening test without exposure history or suggestive findings (Harrison 22e p.3598)","Nerve biopsy rarely distinguishes one neuropathy from another beyond clinical exam and NCS; reserve it for suspected amyloid or vasculitis and only when NCS are abnormal (Harrison 22e p.3598)","A hereditary neuropathy can be missed when patients have marked sensory signs but few sensory symptoms plus slowly progressive distal weakness and skeletal clues (high/flat arches, hammer toes, scoliosis); examining family members may be needed (Harrison 22e p.3597)"],"severityClassification":"No formal numeric severity score is used; characterization is by anatomic/electrophysiologic classification rather than a grading scale - by fiber type affected (motor, sensory, autonomic, or combined; large- vs small-fiber), by distribution (mononeuropathy, mononeuropathy multiplex, polyneuropathy, radiculopathy, plexopathy; ten clinical patterns in Table 457-2), by underlying pathology (axonal degeneration vs segmental demyelination on NCS/EMG, Table 457-3), and by temporal course (acute: days to 4 weeks; subacute: 4-8 weeks; chronic: >8 weeks) (Harrison 22e p.3595, p.3597).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3594","Harrison 22e p.3595","Harrison 22e p.3596","Harrison 22e p.3597","Harrison 22e p.3598","Harrison 22e p.3604","Harrison 22e p.3612"],"crossLinks":["gbs","diabetes_mellitus","vasculitis"]},"pertussis":{"name":"Pertussis (Whooping Cough)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":165,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1276-1280","clinicalPearls":["Acute respiratory tract infection caused by Bordetella pertussis; 'pertussis' means 'violent cough' and the inspiratory whoop at the end of a paroxysm gives the disease its common name (p.1276).","Classic course runs through three stages: catarrhal phase (1-2 weeks of coryza, lacrimation, mild cough, low-grade fever, malaise, indistinguishable from a common cold), then paroxysmal phase (frequent spasmodic bursts of 5-10 coughs within a single expiration), then convalescent phase (p.1278-1279).","Paroxysms may end in an audible whoop produced by rapid inspiration against a closed glottis; post-tussive vomiting is frequent and a mucous plug may be expelled (p.1279).","During a coughing spasm there may be neck-vein distension, bulging eyes, tongue protrusion, and cyanosis; paroxysms can be precipitated by noise, eating, or physical contact and are often worse at night (p.1279).","Between attacks the patient looks normal but shows increasing fatigue; fever is uncommon and, when present, suggests bacterial superinfection (p.1279).","The convalescent phase lasts 1-3 months with gradual resolution; for 6-12 months afterward, intercurrent viral infections may trigger a recrudescence of paroxysmal cough (p.1279).","Incubation period averages 7-10 days (p.1279).","The whoop is variable: uncommon in infants 6 months of age or younger and frequently absent in older children and adults (p.1276).","Lymphocytosis (absolute lymphocyte count >108-109/L) is common in young children and unusual with other infections, but is not a feature in adolescents and adults (p.1279).","Highly communicable: attack rates of 80-100% among unimmunized household contacts; cyclical outbreaks occur every 3-5 years despite widespread immunization (p.1277).","Severe morbidity and high mortality are restricted almost entirely to infants; in the US (2000-2017) infants under 2 months accounted for 84% of pertussis deaths (p.1278)."],"pathophysiology":"Infection begins with attachment of B. pertussis to ciliated epithelial cells of the nasopharynx, mediated by surface adhesins (pertactin, filamentous hemagglutinin) binding integrin-family proteins, probably with pertussis toxin. The organism multiplies locally and produces toxins causing mucosal damage (tracheal cytotoxin, dermonecrotic toxin); host defense is impaired by pertussis toxin and adenylate cyclase-hemolysin toxin. There is local cellular invasion with intracellular persistence, but systemic dissemination does NOT occur; systemic manifestations such as lymphocytosis result from the toxins. The cause of the hallmark paroxysmal cough is not known; a pivotal role for pertussis toxin has been proposed but not confirmed. Neurologic events (seizures, encephalopathy) are thought to result from hypoxia due to coughing paroxysms or apnea rather than from specific bacterial products. Deaths in young infants are frequently associated with very high leukocytosis and pulmonary hypertension (p.1278).","additionalDifferentials":["Bordetella parapertussis (causes a similar but typically milder illness; does not express pertussis toxin gene) (p.1276).","Bordetella holmesii (found in up to 20% of patients with a pertussis-like syndrome using improved PCR) (p.1276).","Mycoplasma pneumoniae (p.1280).","Chlamydia pneumoniae (p.1280).","Adenovirus (p.1280).","Influenza virus and other respiratory viruses (p.1280).","Respiratory syncytial virus and rhinovirus -- isolated from patients with clinical pertussis but probably represent co-infection, particularly in children under 1 year (p.1280)."],"infectionMimics":["Bordetella parapertussis infection (p.1280)","Bordetella holmesii infection (p.1276)","Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection (p.1280)","Chlamydia pneumoniae infection (p.1280)","Adenovirus, influenza, and other respiratory viral infections (p.1280)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["ACE inhibitor-induced cough (p.1280)","Reactive airway disease (p.1280)","Gastroesophageal reflux disease (p.1280)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Nasopharyngeal culture (Bordet-Gengou or Regan-Lowe medium) -- gold standard owing to 100% specificity; cultures become positive by day 5 of incubation (p.1279).","PCR / multitarget real-time PCR -- has replaced culture in most laboratories due to greater sensitivity and faster (hours) results; differentiates B. pertussis, B. parapertussis, and B. holmesii (p.1279).","Nasopharyngeal aspiration is the best specimen; B. pertussis is highly sensitive to drying so specimens must be inoculated without delay or placed in transport medium (p.1279).","Complete blood count for lymphocytosis (informative in young children, not in adolescents/adults) (p.1279).","Serology -- enzyme immunoassays for IgA/IgG to pertussis toxin, filamentous hemagglutinin, pertactin, and fimbriae; increasingly used in adolescents, adults, and those symptomatic >4 weeks; single-specimen anti-pertussis-toxin serology is becoming standardized, especially for outbreaks and surveillance (p.1279)."],"redFlags":["Apnea in infants (occurred in 27.1% of children under 2 years hospitalized with pertussis) (p.1279).","Cyanosis during coughing spasms (p.1279).","Seizures and encephalopathy, thought to result from hypoxia of paroxysms/apnea (p.1278-1279).","Very high leukocytosis and pulmonary hypertension in young infants -- frequently associated with death (p.1278).","Pneumonia (occurs in up to 22% of infants with pertussis) (p.1278-1279).","Infants, especially those under 2-3 months, carry the highest risk of complications and death and should generally be hospitalized (p.1278, 1279)."],"prognosis":"With universal childhood immunization, reported cases fell by >90% and mortality declined even more dramatically. Severe morbidity and high mortality remain almost entirely restricted to infants; WHO estimates ~40 million cases and ~400,000 deaths globally each year, mostly among infants under 3 months. Immunity after natural infection is NOT lifelong; immunity after whole-cell vaccination is short-lived (little protection after 10-12 years) and wanes even faster (within 2-4 years of the fifth or sixth dose) in those immunized with acellular vaccines (p.1277-1278).","pitfalls":["In adolescents and adults the presentation is often atypical -- frequently no paroxysmal cough or whoop -- so pertussis is a commonly missed cause of prolonged cough; B. pertussis may be the agent in 12-30% of adults whose cough does not improve within 2 weeks (p.1278).","Antibiotic therapy aims to eradicate nasopharyngeal carriage; it does NOT substantially alter the clinical course unless given early in the catarrhal phase (p.1279).","Cough suppressants are NOT effective and have no role in management; beta-adrenergic agonists and glucocorticoids have been advocated but are NOT proven effective (p.1280).","Lymphocytosis is helpful in young children but is NOT a reliable feature in adolescents and adults (p.1279).","There is only a small window for culture/PCR diagnosis because much of the period of organism recovery is during the catarrhal phase when pertussis is not yet suspected (p.1279).","Cross-reactivity of antigens such as filamentous hemagglutinin and pertactin among Bordetella species makes diagnosis based on seroconversion of a single antibody type unreliable (p.1279).","Suspect pertussis in any patient with a cough not improving within 14 days, a paroxysmal cough of any duration, cough followed by vomiting (adolescents/adults), or respiratory symptoms after contact with a laboratory-confirmed case (p.1280).","Waning vaccine-derived immunity (faster with acellular vaccines) underlies resurgent outbreaks even in fully immunized older children, adolescents, and adults (p.1278)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd ed., Chapter 165: Pertussis and Other Bordetella Infections (ElSherif & Halperin), pp.1276-1280.","Kilgore PE et al. Pertussis: Microbiology, disease, treatment, and prevention. Clin Microbiol Rev 29:449, 2016 (Table 165-1 source, p.1278).","Craig R et al. Asymptomatic infection and transmission of pertussis in households: A systematic review. Clin Infect Dis 70:152, 2020.","Havers FP et al. Use of Tdap vaccines: Updated ACIP recommendations, United States 2019. MMWR 69:77, 2020."],"crossLinks":[]},"plague":{"name":"Plague","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":176,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1341-1347","clinicalPearls":["Plague is a systemic zoonosis caused by Yersinia pestis, predominantly affecting small rodents in rural areas of Africa, Asia, and the Americas and usually transmitted to humans by an arthropod (flea) vector; less often via contact with animal tissues or respiratory droplets (p.1341).","Three principal clinical forms: bubonic, septicemic, and pneumonic. Worldwide, bubonic plague is the predominant reported form; a minority (10-20%) present as primary septicemic or pneumonic disease (p.1343).","Bubonic plague: after a 2-6 day incubation, sudden onset of fever (>38 degC), malaise, myalgia, dizziness, and increasing pain from progressive regional lymphadenitis. The bubo is a tense, tender swelling with a boggy consistency and underlying hard core, usually solitary and erythematous with periganglionic edema, most commonly inguinal but may be crural, axillary, cervical, or submaxillary depending on bite site (p.1343).","Children most commonly present with cervical or axillary buboes; abdominal pain from intraabdominal node involvement can occur without other visible signs (p.1343).","Primary pneumonic plague follows inhalation of infectious droplets from another person or animal with plague pneumonia; short incubation (a few hours to 2-3 days, range 1-7 days) with abrupt fever, headache, myalgia, weakness, nausea, vomiting, dizziness; respiratory signs (cough, dyspnea, chest pain, sputum with hemoptysis) typically appear after 24 h (p.1344).","Primary pneumonic plague is generally the least common but most fatal of the main presentations; treated pneumonic plague has an estimated death rate of about 17% versus 98% for untreated pneumonic plague (p.1343).","Primary septicemic plague (10-25% of infections) presents as gram-negative septicemia (hypotension, shock) WITHOUT preceding lymphadenopathy; occurs in all age groups but risk is elevated in persons over 40 (p.1344).","WBC count is generally raised (10,000-20,000/uL) with neutrophilic leukocytosis and left shift; counts can be very high in children (>100,000/uL), but in some cases the WBC is normal or leukopenia develops. DIC occurs in a significant minority (p.1346).","Appropriate treatment of bubonic plague resolves fever within 2-5 days, but buboes may remain enlarged for more than a week after initial treatment and can become fluctuant (p.1344)."],"pathophysiology":"Y. pestis is a gram-negative bacterium of the order Enterobacterales that recently evolved from the enteric pathogen Y. pseudotuberculosis. It has a two-stage life cycle alternating between arthropod (flea) and a wide range of mammalian hosts. In the flea, the organism multiplies and forms biofilm-embedded aggregates in the midgut; some fleas become 'blocked' at the proventriculus and regurgitate bacteria into the bite site, while early-phase unblocked transmission also occurs. In the mammalian host, dissemination from the inoculation site is initially dependent on plasminogen activator (Pla), which activates plasminogen, degrades complement, and binds laminin, and is essential for high-level virulence and bubo formation. Bacteria are rapidly transported to regional lymph nodes where Y. pestis replicates extracellularly with full expression of antiphagocytic systems (type III secretion machinery and effectors encoded by pYV plus the F1 capsule), producing a bubo (hemorrhagic/necrotic node with thrombosed vessels, replaced architecture, and abundant bacteria). LcrV targets phagocytes and exerts an anti-inflammatory effect, and modified lipopolysaccharide minimizes Toll-like receptor 4 stimulation, prolonging high-grade bacteremia. Spread via efferent lymphatics to the thoracic duct produces high-grade bacteremia and hematogenous seeding of spleen, liver, secondary buboes, lungs (secondary plague pneumonia), and meninges, with uncontrolled septicemia leading to death; the septicemic phase can occur without an obvious prior bubo (p.1342-1343).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute focal lymphadenopathy of other etiologies (for bubonic plague): streptococcal or staphylococcal infection, tularemia, cat-scratch disease, tick typhus, infectious mononucleosis, lymphatic filariasis (p.1343).","Tuberculosis (in differential of fever and hemoptysis in plague-endemic areas, as a small-airborne-particle-transmitted infection) (p.1347).","Tonsillitis with peritonsillar abscess (resembles plague pharyngitis) (p.1344)."],"infectionMimics":["Tularemia (Francisella tularensis) (p.1343)","Cat-scratch disease (Bartonella henselae) (p.1343)","Tick typhus (rickettsial) (p.1343)","Infectious mononucleosis (p.1343)","Lymphatic filariasis (p.1343)","Streptococcal or staphylococcal lymphadenitis (p.1343)","Tuberculosis (fever and hemoptysis differential) (p.1347)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bubo aspirate (bubonic), bronchoalveolar lavage fluid or sputum (pneumonic), and blood (septicemic) are the appropriate specimens; culture of postmortem organ biopsy can also be diagnostic. Transport medium such as Cary-Blair preserves viability (p.1345).","Direct microscopy: Gram-negative coccobacilli with characteristic bipolar 'closed safety pin' appearance on Wayson or Wright-Giemsa stain; bipolar bacilli may be visible on direct blood smear in septicemic plague, indicating very high circulating bacterial numbers and a poor prognosis (p.1345).","Culture on nutrient-rich media (sheep blood agar, brain-heart infusion broth) and selective agar (MacConkey, EMB, or Yersinia-specific CIN agar); optimal growth at <37 degC (25-29 degC). Y. pestis is oxidase-negative, catalase-positive, urease-negative, indole-negative, lactose-negative, and nonmotile (distinguishing it from other Yersinia species) (p.1345).","F1 antigen detection: direct immunofluorescence and rapid immunochromatographic test strip (F1RDT), effective on bubo aspirates and sputum and widely used in endemic countries; a Pla antigen strip can detect F1-negative strains (p.1345).","PCR/RT-PCR requiring positivity for at least two Y. pestis-specific targets (e.g., caf1, pla, and chromosomal genes); FDA-authorized BioFire FilmArray panels include Y. pestis (p.1346).","Serology: rising hemagglutinating antibody titer to F1 antigen, or seroconversion/fourfold rise in paired sera; ELISA for IgG and IgM to F1 antigen (p.1345-1346).","WHO case definitions (suspected/probable/confirmed) guide presumptive diagnosis followed by reference laboratory confirmation (p.1345)."],"redFlags":["Pneumonic plague is transmissible person-to-person via respiratory droplets (productive cough), making it a public-health emergency requiring immediate isolation and notification (p.1344, 1347).","Aerosolized Y. pestis is a recognized potential bioterrorism agent (Tier 1 select agent); an outbreak of primary pneumonic plague in a nonendemic region or urban setting should raise concern for intentional release (p.1342, 1344, 1345).","Rapid progression of primary pneumonic plague from segmental pneumonitis to lobar and bilateral involvement with hemoptysis and ARDS within hours to days (p.1344).","Bipolar bacilli visible on direct blood smear indicate very high circulating bacterial numbers and a poor prognosis (p.1345).","Disseminated intravascular coagulation with shock in septicemic disease (p.1346)."],"prognosis":"Untreated plague carries high mortality, but it is treatable with antimicrobials. Treated pneumonic plague has an estimated death rate of about 17% compared with 98% for untreated pneumonic plague. In the United States, case-fatality fell from 66% in 1900-1941 to 16% in the post-antibiotic era (1942 onward); in 2000-2018, mortality was 8% in patients receiving any antimicrobial and 4% in those receiving currently recommended high-efficacy agents. The global case-fatality rate notified to WHO for 2013-2018 was 17%. Visible bipolar bacilli on direct blood smear signal very high bacteremia and poor prognosis (p.1343, 1345).","pitfalls":["The term 'septicemic plague' can be confusing because most patients with buboes have detectable bacteremia at some stage, with or without systemic sepsis signs (p.1344).","Automated biochemical or mass-spectrometry identification systems can misidentify Y. pestis as Y. pseudotuberculosis or other species (p.1345).","Meningeal plague may be associated with suboptimal antimicrobial therapy (delayed treatment, penicillin use, or low-dose tetracycline) (p.1344).","A single negative rapid antigen test is insufficient to exclude plague; WHO exclusion requires two negative F1RDT tests on specimens collected 24 h apart when confirmatory tests are unavailable (p.1345-1346).","F1RDT specificity for culture-confirmed pneumonic plague was only 71%, so a positive sputum test in an endemic area identifies a probable case requiring treatment but needs additional confirmatory testing (p.1345-1346).","Culture yield can be low after prehospital antimicrobial use (as observed in the 2017 Madagascar outbreak) (p.1345)."],"severityClassification":"WHO case classification of plague: Suspected case (clinical presentation suggestive of plague plus an epidemiologic context of possible exposure within 10 days of onset); Probable case (suspected case plus one of: F1 antigen detected by rapid test or immunofluorescence, a single anti-F1 serology without prior infection/immunization, or direct microscopy showing bipolar-staining gram-negative coccobacilli); Confirmed case (suspected case plus one of: isolation of Y. pestis, seroconversion or fourfold anti-F1 titer change in paired sera, or species-specific PCR positivity); Not a case (suspected case with negative confirmatory testing) (p.1345).","references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e, Chapter 176: Plague and Other Yersinia Infections (Michael B. Prentice), pp.1341-1347","Bertherat E, Jullien S: Revision of the international definition of plague cases. Wkly Epidemiol Rec 24:238, 2021","Nelson CA et al: Antimicrobial treatment and prophylaxis of plague: Recommendations for naturally acquired infections and bioterrorism response. MMWR Recomm Rep 70(No. RR-3):1, 2021","Salam AP et al: Deaths associated with pneumonic plague, 1946-2017"],"crossLinks":[]},"pneumococcal_disease":{"name":"Pneumococcal Disease","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":151,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1187-1195","clinicalPearls":["S. pneumoniae can infect nearly any tissue; the four most common syndromes are otitis media, pneumonia, bacteraemia, and meningitis (Harrison 22e p.1191).","There is no pathognomonic presentation of pneumococcal disease, and a microbiologically confirmed diagnosis is made in only a minority of cases, so empirical therapy covering S. pneumoniae is often indicated (Harrison 22e p.1191).","In the elderly, pneumococcal pneumonia may present non-specifically with confusion or malaise without fever or cough; a high index of suspicion is required because delayed treatment leads to rapid, severe deterioration (Harrison 22e p.1192).","Pneumococcal pneumonia can mimic an acute abdomen (upper abdominal pain from diaphragmatic pleural involvement); clinicians should remember to keep this non-abdominal process in the differential (Harrison 22e p.1192).","Now that H. influenzae type b vaccine is in routine childhood use, S. pneumoniae and Neisseria meningitidis are the most common bacterial causes of meningitis in both adults and children (Harrison 22e p.1193).","Pneumococcal meningitis can be a primary presentation or a complication of skull fracture, otitis media, mastoiditis, or bacteraemia; most cases arise from haematogenous spread rather than direct extension (Harrison 22e p.1191, 1193).","A positive urinary pneumococcal antigen test is useful for etiologic attribution in adults (low nasopharyngeal colonization) but is generally uninformative in children and in high-colonization low-income settings (Harrison 22e p.1192-1193).","Most pneumococcal pneumonia is non-bacteraemic; only ~5-20% of cases are accompanied by bacteraemia, and blood cultures are positive in fewer than 30% of cases (Harrison 22e p.1190, 1192).","Empyema is the most common focal complication of pneumococcal pneumonia (<5% of cases); pleural fluid with frank pus, visible bacteria, or pH <=7.1 indicates empyema and demands complete drainage (Harrison 22e p.1192)."],"pathophysiology":"Pneumococci are encapsulated gram-positive diplococci that colonise the human nasopharynx from an early age; colonisation is common while disease is rare. All pneumococcal infection ultimately stems from nasopharyngeal acquisition. The anti-phagocytic polysaccharide capsule is the key virulence determinant, resisting complement deposition in the absence of type-specific antibody. The organism dynamically modulates capsule expression: it downregulates capsule in the nasopharynx (rough colonies) to evade immune recognition and upregulates it on tissue invasion (smooth colonies). From the nasopharynx, organisms spread haematogenously to distant sites (brain, joints, bone, peritoneum) or locally to mucosal surfaces (otitis media, pneumonia). Intercurrent viral respiratory infection increases susceptibility by upregulating epithelial adhesion factors, permitting adhesion via surface adhesins (PsaA, PspA, CbpA/PspC, pneumolysin, neuraminidases). Disease pathology is driven by host inflammation: cell-wall teichoic acids and peptidoglycan induce IL-1, IL-6, and TNF and activate the alternative complement pathway, and pneumolysin drives proinflammatory cytokine release. Host defence depends on intact respiratory epithelium, the cough reflex and mucociliary escalator, splenic function, complement, neutrophils/macrophages, CRP binding of cell-wall phosphorylcholine, and TLR2/TLR4 recognition, together with acquired anticapsular IgG (Harrison 22e p.1190-1191).","additionalDifferentials":["Viral pneumonia","Mycoplasma pneumonia","Haemophilus influenzae pneumonia","Klebsiella pneumoniae pneumonia","Staphylococcus aureus pneumonia","Legionella pneumonia","Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (in HIV-infected/immunocompromised hosts)","Myocardial infarction","Heart failure with atypical pulmonary oedema","Atelectasis","Cholecystitis, appendicitis, perforated peptic ulcer, or subphrenic abscess (when abdominal symptoms predominate)"],"infectionMimics":["Viral lower respiratory tract infection / viral pneumonia","Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection","Legionella infection","Pneumonia due to H. influenzae, K. pneumoniae, or S. aureus","Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia in immunocompromised hosts","Other bacterial pyogenic meningitis (e.g., N. meningitidis), clinically indistinguishable from pneumococcal meningitis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Myocardial infarction","Heart failure with atypical pulmonary oedema","Pulmonary atelectasis","Lung cancer (can produce round/spherical consolidation mimicking pneumococcal round pneumonia)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiography (lobar/segmental consolidation; >1 lobe in ~30%; infiltrate may be absent early or with dehydration)","Blood cultures (positive in <30% of pneumococcal pneumonia)","Gram's stain and culture of high-quality sputum (mainstay of adult diagnosis; yield depends on specimen quality and prior antibiotics)","Urinary pneumococcal antigen assay (cell-wall polysaccharide; serotype-specific quantitative assays for up to 24 serotypes emerging)","CSF examination for meningitis: turbidity, elevated protein, elevated WBC, reduced glucose, plus Gram's stain, culture, antigen testing, or PCR","Pleural fluid analysis (pus, microscopy, pH) when effusion present","Sterile aspiration/culture (and capsular antigen or PCR) of fluid from the infected site for other invasive syndromes; lung tissue pathology is the gold standard for pneumonia"],"redFlags":["Asplenia or splenic dysfunction (e.g., sickle cell disease) confers risk of overwhelming, fulminant pneumococcal sepsis","Leukopenia in pneumococcal pneumonia (<10% of cases) is a poor prognostic sign associated with fatal outcome","Hypotension and signs of severe pneumonia/sepsis at presentation predict higher case-fatality","Meningitis features: altered consciousness, seizures, stiff neck, signs of raised intracranial pressure (bradycardia, hypertension), cranial nerve palsies","In children: inability to drink, convulsions, lethargy, or severe malnutrition signify very severe disease warranting immediate antibiotics and referral","Pleural effusion with persistent fever/leukocytosis after 4-5 days of appropriate therapy suggests empyema"],"prognosis":"Case-fatality varies with age, comorbidity, disease severity at presentation, and access to care. For pneumococcal pneumonia, CFR ranges from <5% in hospitalised patients aged 18-44 to >12% in those over 65, even with timely management; severity at presentation (rather than the presence of bacteraemia) drives mortality, and early deaths reflect a rapidly progressive cascade that often unfolds irrespective of antibiotic administration. Pneumococcal meningitis carries a mortality of ~20%, and up to 50% of survivors have acute or chronic sequelae (deafness, hydrocephalus, and cognitive impairment in children; diffuse brain swelling, subarachnoid bleeding, hydrocephalus, cerebrovascular complications, and hearing loss in adults) (Harrison 22e p.1190, 1193).","pitfalls":["Treating an apparent acute abdomen while overlooking pneumococcal lower-lobe pneumonia causing referred abdominal pain (Harrison 22e p.1192).","Relying on a normal early chest radiograph to exclude pneumonia; an infiltrate may be absent early or with dehydration and appear after rehydration (Harrison 22e p.1192).","Over-interpreting a positive urinary pneumococcal antigen in children or in high-colonization settings, where it does not reliably attribute etiology (Harrison 22e p.1192-1193).","Diagnosing otitis media from tympanic-membrane redness alone, which is insufficient (Harrison 22e p.1193).","Attributing round/spherical consolidation solely to pneumococcus without considering other causes, especially cancer (Harrison 22e p.1192).","Assuming PPSV23 reliably prevents non-bacteraemic pneumococcal pneumonia, particularly in the elderly and immunodeficient, where effectiveness is reduced and protection wanes after ~5 years (Harrison 22e p.1194)."],"severityClassification":"Pneumococcal syndromes are classified as invasive (a normally sterile site is infected or bacteraemia is present, e.g., bacteraemic pneumonia, meningitis, bacteraemia) versus noninvasive (contiguous spread, e.g., otitis media, sinusitis, bronchitis); whether non-bacteraemic pneumonia counts as invasive is debated. In children, WHO IMCI algorithms stratify illness by danger signs (very severe disease: inability to drink, convulsions, lethargy, severe malnutrition) and by cough with tachypnoea plus lower chest-wall indrawing to guide antibiotics and referral; no equivalent validated algorithm exists for adults (Harrison 22e p.1191).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1187-1195"],"crossLinks":["CAP","MENINGITIS"]},"pneumocystis_pneumonia":{"name":"Pneumocystis Pneumonia (PCP)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":227,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1730-1734","clinicalPearls":["Caused by the fungus Pneumocystis jirovecii (formerly P. carinii); the organism cannot be cultured and is host-specific to humans, infecting only via other humans (Harrison 22e p.1730-1731).","An opportunistic pathogen of immunocompromised hosts: HIV infection, organ transplantation, haematologic malignancy, and those on high-dose glucocorticoids or certain immunosuppressive monoclonal antibodies (Harrison 22e p.1730).","Among people with HIV, PCP incidence is inversely related to CD4+ T-cell count: at least 80% of cases occur at counts <200/uL and most actually develop at counts <100/uL (Harrison 22e p.1731-1732).","Classic presentation is acute or subacute pneumonia, often beginning with a vague sense of dyspnoea alone, then progressing to fever, nonproductive cough, and progressive shortness of breath (Harrison 22e p.1731).","Decreased oxygen saturation at rest or with exertion is characteristic; untreated it progresses to severe hypoxaemia (Harrison 22e p.1731).","Serum lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) is often elevated from pulmonary damage (Harrison 22e p.1731).","Classic chest radiograph shows diffuse bilateral interstitial infiltrates that are perihilar and symmetric, which can progress to alveolar filling (Harrison 22e p.1731).","High-resolution chest CT shows diffuse ground-glass opacities in virtually all PCP patients, often before the plain chest radiograph becomes abnormal (Harrison 22e p.1731).","Bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) is the most common sampling method; demonstration of organisms in BAL fluid is almost 100% sensitive and specific for PCP (Harrison 22e p.1733).","Since the organism cannot be cultured, diagnosis relies on staining of cyst or trophozoite forms in lung tissue/secretions with methenamine silver, toluidine blue O, Giemsa, or specific immunofluorescent antibody (Harrison 22e p.1733).","An induced sputum sample, when obtained and interpreted by an experienced provider, can be highly sensitive and specific, but sensitivity is widely variable (55-90%); expectorated sputum and throat swabs have very low sensitivity (Harrison 22e p.1733).","Adjunctive glucocorticoids improve survival in moderate-to-severe disease (initial room-air PaO2 <70 mmHg or alveolar-arterial gradient >=35 mmHg) by reducing the inflammation that follows organism die-off once specific therapy begins; they should be started when PCP therapy is initiated, even if the diagnosis is only suspected (Harrison 22e p.1734).","Prophylaxis is the cornerstone of prevention; for HIV, CD4+ counts <200/uL (or CD4 percentage <15) are an indication to start or continue chemoprophylaxis, alongside prior PCP, unexplained prolonged fever, or recent oropharyngeal candidiasis (Harrison 22e p.1734).","PCP now occurs more often from non-HIV-related immunosuppression than from HIV infection in many health care systems (Harrison 22e p.1731)."],"pathophysiology":"Pneumocystis jirovecii is a fungus with unique tropism for the lung. Organisms are inhaled into the alveolar space, presumably after being exhaled by another human; most people are exposed and infected early in life. Disease may arise from primary infection, reinfection, or reactivation of latent infection. Clinically apparent pneumonia occurs only in immunocompromised hosts, where defects in cellular and/or humoral immunity (congenital, HIV-related, or drug-induced) permit proliferation. CD4+ T cells are critical for host defence. The organism proliferates in the lung provoking a mononuclear cell response; alveoli fill with proteinaceous material and alveolar-capillary injury with surfactant abnormalities develops. Stained sections show foamy, vacuolated alveolar exudates of viable and nonviable organisms, with interstitial oedema and fibrosis. Much of the clinical deterioration after starting specific therapy is attributed to enhanced inflammation from dying organisms, the rationale for adjunctive glucocorticoids (Harrison 22e p.1731-1734).","additionalDifferentials":["Bacterial pneumonia (including community-acquired pneumonia)","Pulmonary tuberculosis (especially with upper-lobe cavitary pattern seen after aerosolized pentamidine prophylaxis)","Cytomegalovirus and other viral pneumonias in the immunocompromised host","Other invasive fungal pneumonias (e.g., Aspergillus)","Congestive heart failure","Pulmonary embolism","Pulmonary hypertension","Drug toxicity","Neoplastic process involving the lung","Lymphoid interstitial pneumonitis (LIP) and nonspecific interstitial pneumonitis (NIP) in HIV"],"infectionMimics":["Bacterial pneumonia","Pulmonary tuberculosis","Cytomegalovirus pneumonia","Invasive aspergillosis / other fungal pneumonia"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Congestive heart failure","Pulmonary embolism","Pulmonary hypertension","Drug-induced pulmonary toxicity","Pulmonary neoplasm","Lymphoid interstitial pneumonitis (LIP) / nonspecific interstitial pneumonitis (NIP)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Pulse oximetry / oxygen saturation at rest and with exertion, and arterial blood gases (hypoxaemia with widened alveolar-arterial gradient; used for staging severity and directing treatment)","Serum lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) - often elevated but neither sensitive nor specific","High-resolution chest CT (diffuse ground-glass opacities; a normal chest CT essentially rules out PCP)","Bronchoalveolar lavage with methenamine silver, toluidine blue O, Giemsa, or immunofluorescent staining","Induced sputum examination (experienced-provider dependent)","PCR of respiratory specimens (more useful for ruling out PCP when negative than for confirming it when positive; cannot distinguish colonization from disease and is not standardized)","Serum (1->3)-beta-D-glucan (frequently elevated but neither perfectly sensitive nor highly specific)","Transbronchial or open-lung biopsy when less invasive sampling is non-diagnostic"],"redFlags":["PaO2 <70 mmHg on room air (or alveolar-arterial oxygen gradient >=35 mmHg) marking moderate-to-severe disease that warrants adjunctive glucocorticoids","Profound or progressive hypoxaemia / progression to respiratory failure","Development of pneumothorax (pneumatoceles and pneumothoraces are characteristic, especially in HIV) - associated with higher mortality","Need for mechanical ventilation","Distributive shock due to PCP (rare)","Clinical deterioration after 3-4 days of therapy, or failure to improve by 7-10 days, prompting reevaluation for other or coexisting processes"],"prognosis":"Untreated, PCP is invariably fatal. HIV-associated disease is often indolent, presenting early as mild exercise intolerance or chest tightness without fever or cough and a normal or near-normal chest radiograph, then progressing over days to months to fever, cough, diffuse alveolar infiltrates, and profound hypoxaemia; non-HIV immunosuppressed patients more often have acute disease progressing over days to respiratory failure. Mortality is influenced by age, degree of immunosuppression, preexisting lung disease, need for mechanical ventilation, and development of pneumothorax. With advances in critical care, prognosis for those requiring intubation has improved and depends largely on comorbidities and the underlying disease (Harrison 22e p.1733).","pitfalls":["A normal LDH does not rule out PCP, and an elevated LDH is not specific for PCP (Harrison 22e p.1731).","PCP can occur at CD4+ counts >200/uL in HIV (uncommon, especially at counts well above 200), and in non-HIV immunosuppression a CD4+ count >200/uL does not imply protection (Harrison 22e p.1731).","PCR is so sensitive that it cannot reliably distinguish colonization from true pneumonia; there is no validated PCR threshold, so a positive result does not definitively attribute disease to Pneumocystis (Harrison 22e p.1733).","Serum/BAL (1->3)-beta-D-glucan and serum PCR are not sufficiently sensitive or specific to establish or exclude PCP (Harrison 22e p.1733).","If treated with specific therapy but without corticosteroids, patients of any severity often deteriorate during the first few days from inflammation induced by dying organisms (Harrison 22e p.1733).","Initial chest radiograph may be normal in mild disease, and the classic dense perihilar infiltrate is unusual in AIDS; relying on plain film can delay diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1730-1731).","Extrapulmonary pneumocystosis is rare and nonspecific; when present, the priority is to look for another concurrent infectious or neoplastic process (Harrison 22e p.1733)."],"severityClassification":"Severity is staged largely by oxygenation. Moderate-to-severe disease is defined by an initial room-air PaO2 <70 mmHg or an alveolar-arterial oxygen gradient >=35 mmHg; this threshold identifies patients (with HIV, and probably other immunodeficiencies) who benefit from adjunctive glucocorticoids in addition to specific antimicrobial therapy. Arterial blood-gas measurement both aids diagnosis and directs treatment by staging severity (Harrison 22e p.1731, 1734).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1730-1734 (Ch.227, Pneumocystis Infections)","Harrison 22e p.1730 (Ch.208, HIV/AIDS clinical manifestations - PCP)"],"crossLinks":[]},"polycystic_kidney_disease":{"name":"Polycystic Kidney Disease","system":"Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":327,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2430","clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD) is the most common life-threatening monogenic disease, affecting ~12 million people worldwide (estimated prevalence 1:1000 to 1:400), and is a leading cause of kidney failure; it is inherited as an autosomal dominant trait with complete penetrance but variable expressivity (p.2430-2431).","ADPKD is caused by mutations in PKD1 (chromosome 16p13, ~85% of cases, encodes polycystin-1) and PKD2 (chromosome 4q21-q23, ~15% of cases, encodes polycystin-2); PKD2 patients tend to have milder disease and may be underdiagnosed. Polycystins are localized on the primary cilium, and defective ciliary function places ADPKD among the ciliopathies (p.2430-2431).","It is characterized by progressive bilateral formation of epithelial-lined renal cysts; cysts arise in only ~5% of tubules but their enormous growth destroys surrounding tissue. Enlarged kidneys can reach a fourfold increase in length and up to 20 times the normal weight (p.2430-2431).","Back or flank pain is a frequent symptom in ~60% of patients and may result from cyst infection, hemorrhage, or nephrolithiasis; gross hematuria from cyst rupture occurs in ~40% during the disease course, often recurrently (p.2432).","Hypertension is common and typically occurs before any reduction in GFR; it is a risk factor for both cardiovascular and kidney disease progression. Cardiovascular complications are the major cause of mortality in ADPKD (p.2432).","Intracranial (berry) aneurysm occurs four to five times more frequently than in the general population and causes high mortality; about 20-50% of patients may have 'warning headaches' preceding subarachnoid hemorrhage from a ruptured aneurysm (p.2432).","Liver (biliary epithelial) cysts are the most common extrarenal complication; massive polycystic liver disease occurs almost exclusively in women, particularly those with multiple pregnancies. Mitral valve prolapse occurs in up to 30% of patients, and colonic diverticulae and abdominal wall hernias are also increased (p.2432).","Diagnosis is typically made from a positive autosomal dominant family history plus bilateral kidney cysts on ultrasonography; in at-risk subjects aged 15-29 years, two renal cysts (unilateral or bilateral) suffice (sensitivity 96%, specificity 100%), with higher cyst counts required at older ages because simple cysts increase with age (p.2432-2433).","Tolvaptan, a vasopressin V2-receptor (V2R) antagonist, inhibits the cAMP pathway in collecting ducts and reduces cell proliferation by lowering renal cAMP, slowing the decline of renal function; the FDA has indicated it only for patients at risk of rapidly progressing disease (p.2433).","Infection is the second most common cause of death; up to half of patients have one or more renal infections, most commonly an infected cyst or acute pyelonephritis due to gram-negative bacteria, with fever and flank pain (p.2432)."],"pathophysiology":"ADPKD results from mutations in PKD1 and PKD2, encoding polycystin-1 (PC1, a large 11-transmembrane G-protein-coupled-receptor-like protein) and polycystin-2 (PC2, a calcium-permeable six-transmembrane TRP-family cation channel). PC1 and PC2 bind via their C-terminal tails to form a receptor-channel complex (proposed 1:3 PC1:PC2 stoichiometry) located on the primary cilium and at cell membranes and cell-cell junctions of tubular epithelium, functioning as a mechano-/chemosensor regulating calcium and G-protein signaling. Every cell carries a germline mutant allele, but cysts develop in only a small fraction of nephrons, thought to arise from clonal growth of cells sustaining a somatic 'second hit' in the normal allele; a 'third hit' such as renal injury or an additional genetic/epigenetic event may accelerate cyst formation. Loss of ciliary PC1/PC2 function is hypothesized to cause aberrant calcium signaling, increased adenylyl cyclase and decreased phosphodiesterase activity, raising cellular cAMP; increased cAMP promotes protein kinase A activity, driving cyst growth through proliferation and chloride/aquaporin-mediated fluid secretion. Cellular defects include increased proliferation and fluid secretion, decreased differentiation, and abnormal extracellular matrix; affected signaling pathways include Wnt, mTOR, STAT3, cMET, PI3K/Akt, GPCR, EGFR, and CFTR. Rarer ADPKD-associated genes GANAB and DNAJB11 affect PC1 trafficking and cause milder cystic disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease (ARPKD, PKHD1) - distal/collecting-duct cysts, oligohydramnios if severe, hypertension, congenital hepatic fibrosis and Caroli disease; absence of renal cysts in either parent >40y on ultrasound helps distinguish ARPKD from ADPKD (p.2430, p.2434)","ADPKD-like disease (GANAB, DNAJB11) - normal-to-smaller kidneys with fewer cysts, variable liver cysts (p.2430-2432)","Autosomal dominant tubulointerstitial kidney disease (ADTKD; UMOD, MUC1, REN, HNF1B, SEC61A1) - small fibrotic kidneys, medullary cysts, gout in adults (p.2430)","Tuberous sclerosis (TSC1/TSC2) - renal cysts (20-30%), angiomyolipomas, increased risk of renal cell carcinoma, facial angiofibromas, CNS hamartomas; TSC2 is adjacent to PKD1 so a contiguous gene deletion can cause both ADPKD and TS (p.2430, p.2434)","Von Hippel-Lindau disease (VHL) - renal cysts, renal cell carcinoma, retinal angiomas, CNS hemangioblastomas, pheochromocytomas (p.2430)","Nephronophthisis and other ciliopathies (e.g., Bardet-Biedl, Meckel-Gruber, Senior-Loken) - cystic kidney phenotypes with syndromic features (p.2430)","Renal cysts and diabetes syndrome (HNF1B) - kidney cysts with aberrant nephrogenesis and diabetes (p.2430)","Acquired simple renal cysts (increase in frequency with age and can confound diagnostic cyst counts) (p.2432-2433)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Renal ultrasonography - first-line for presymptomatic screening and donor evaluation; age-stratified cyst-count criteria for diagnosis (p.2432-2433)","CT and T2-weighted MRI (with/without contrast) - more sensitive than ultrasound, detect smaller cysts (MRI with gadolinium can detect 2-3 mm cysts); useful to distinguish malignancy from a complex cyst (p.2432-2433)","Magnetic resonance angiography for presymptomatic intracranial aneurysm screening in patients with a positive family history of aneurysm (p.2432)","CT scan as first diagnostic test for suspected subarachnoid hemorrhage, with lumbar puncture to confirm (p.2432)","Genetic testing by linkage and mutational analysis for ambiguous cases; PKD1 analysis is difficult and costly owing to gene size and homologous pseudogenes (paired-end next-generation sequencing may improve this) (p.2433)"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe headache or 'warning headache' suggesting subarachnoid hemorrhage from ruptured intracranial (berry) aneurysm (p.2432)","Positive family history of intracranial aneurysm - a risk factor for aneurysm rupture warranting presymptomatic MRA screening (p.2432)","Fever and flank pain indicating infected cyst or acute pyelonephritis (with or without bacteremia); infection is the second most common cause of death (p.2432)","Gross hematuria from cyst rupture (p.2432)","Diffuse arterial dolichoectasia of the cerebral circulation predisposing to arterial dissection and stroke (p.2432)"],"prognosis":"ADPKD progression shows striking inter- and intrafamilial variability; disease can present as early as in utero, but end-stage renal disease typically occurs in late middle age. Risk factors for progression include early diagnosis, hypertension, gross hematuria, multiple pregnancies, and large kidney size; PKD2 disease is generally milder with later onset. More than half of patients eventually require peritoneal dialysis, hemodialysis, or kidney transplantation. Cardiovascular complications are the major cause of mortality, and renal infection is the second most common cause of death (p.2431-2433).","pitfalls":["Proteinuria is usually only a MINOR feature of ADPKD, so its absence does not exclude the diagnosis (p.2432).","Renal cell carcinoma is NOT increased in frequency compared with the general population, but when it does occur in ADPKD it is more often bilateral, multicentric, and sarcomatoid; radiologic imaging is often unable to distinguish cyst infection from cyst hemorrhage due to their complexity (p.2432).","Diagnostic cyst-count thresholds rise with age because simple renal cysts increase with age; criteria have lower sensitivity for PKD2 patients owing to late onset (p.2432-2433).","No specific FDA-approved treatment prevents cyst growth or the decline of renal function; more rigorous blood pressure control does not equal greater benefit, and targeting systolic pressure to 110 mmHg in moderate/advanced disease may worsen progression by reducing renal blood flow (p.2433).","Most renal cysts are not connected to glomerular filtration, so cyst infection requires lipid-soluble antibiotics that penetrate cyst walls (e.g., trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, quinolones, chloramphenicol), often for 4-6 weeks (p.2433).","Polycystic liver disease in ADPKD is distinct from isolated autosomal dominant polycystic liver disease (ADPLD; PRKCSH/SEC63), which does NOT progress to renal failure (p.2432)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal stage-based severity classification for ADPKD. Genotype broadly stratifies severity (PKD1 disease ~85% of cases is more severe with earlier onset than PKD2 disease ~15%; GANAB/DNAJB11 mutations cause milder cystic disease). Risk of rapid progression - used to select patients for tolvaptan - is estimated by combining genotypic and imaging (kidney growth rate) information; recognized clinical risk factors for progression include early diagnosis, hypertension, gross hematuria, multiple pregnancies, and large kidney size (p.2431-2433).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2430"],"crossLinks":["ckd","sah","renal_colic","htn_emergency"]},"primary_immunodeficiency":{"name":"Primary Immunodeficiency","system":"Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":362,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2793-2804","clinicalPearls":["The most frequent symptom prompting the diagnosis of a PID is recurrent or unusually severe infection; recurrent allergic or autoimmune manifestations may also be the alerting feature (Harrison 22e p.2793).","PIDs are classified by (1) the arm of the immune system that is defective and (2) the mechanism of the defect; the major categories are antibody/B-cell defects (agammaglobulinaemia, CVID, hyper-IgM, IgA deficiency), T-cell/cellular defects, combined defects (SCID), and innate-immune defects of phagocytes, complement, or innate receptors/signalling (Harrison 22e p.2794).","The site of infection and the causal microorganism point to the affected compartment: respiratory-tract (sinus, bronchial) infection mostly suggests defective antibody responses; invasive bacterial infection can result from complement deficiency, innate-signalling defects, asplenia, or antibody defects; viral, recurrent Candida, and opportunistic infections suggest impaired T-cell immunity; skin infections and deep abscesses primarily reflect innate (phagocyte) defects such as chronic granulomatous disease (Harrison 22e p.2794).","B-lymphocyte (antibody) deficiencies are the most frequent PIDs, accounting for 60-70% of all cases; T-cell deficiencies account for ~20% and innate-immune defects for ~10% (Harrison 22e p.2794, 2796, 2797).","Encapsulated/pyogenic bacteria (S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, M. catarrhalis) and recurrent sinopulmonary infection are the hallmark of defective antibody production; untreated recurrent bronchial infection leads to bronchiectasis, cor pulmonale, and death (Harrison 22e p.2801).","Invasive Neisseria infection points to deficiencies of the complement lytic phase (C5-C9) or the alternative pathway (factors D, properdin); classic-pathway and lytic-phase complement deficiencies may also predispose to autoimmune disease, notably SLE (Harrison 22e p.2797).","CVID is recognised predominantly in adults, although clinical manifestations can begin earlier in life; in most cases no molecular cause is identified and the diagnosis is made only after excluding hypomorphic agammaglobulinaemia mutations and more subtle T-cell defects, especially in children (Harrison 22e p.2802).","First-line laboratory work-up is guided by the clinical picture and includes blood cell counts/morphology, serum immunoglobulin levels (IgG, IgA, IgM, IgE), specific antibody responses to vaccines (tetanus toxoid, unconjugated pneumococcal polysaccharide), and lymphocyte subset phenotyping; more specific (genetic) tests then confirm a definitive diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2794-2795).","Even with profound B-cell deficiency, infections rarely occur before 6 months of age because of transplacental maternal IgG; conversely, in SCID clinical consequences typically appear within 3-6 months of birth (Harrison 22e p.2801, 2799).","Immunoglobulin replacement should be guided by the occurrence of infection, not by laboratory IgG/antibody levels alone, to avoid unjustified infusions; it is lifelong therapy (Harrison 22e p.2802).","A precise molecular/genetic diagnosis has become a milestone in the care of PID patients, guiding treatment selection, genetic counselling, and prenatal diagnosis; suspected cases should be referred to experienced centres (Harrison 22e p.2804)."],"pathophysiology":"PIDs are predominantly Mendelian genetic diseases that arise whenever the expression or function of a nonredundant effector or mediator of the immune system is genetically impaired. More than 550 conditions have been described, with deleterious mutations identified in roughly 500 genes; estimated prevalence is 5-10 per 100,000 (probably an underestimate). They are increasingly grouped within the broader category of inborn errors of immunity that also includes autoinflammatory syndromes. Defects can involve any aspect of innate or adaptive immunity, cell differentiation, effector function, or immune regulation. The clinical consequence is multiple levels of vulnerability to infection, ranging from extremely broad (as in SCID, where T-cell development is blocked) to narrowly restricted to a single class of organism (as in Mendelian susceptibility to mycobacterial disease, an IL-12-IFN-gamma axis defect). Antibody (B-cell) defects (agammaglobulinaemia from BTK and pre-B-cell-receptor gene defects, hyper-IgM from class-switch-recombination defects such as CD40L/CD40, IgA deficiency, and CVID) impair production or class switching of immunoglobulins, leaving patients unable to opsonise and neutralise pyogenic encapsulated bacteria. Cellular (T-cell) defects impair help for antibody production, macrophage activation, and cytotoxicity, causing susceptibility to viral, fungal (Candida), and opportunistic infections (e.g., Pneumocystis jirovecii, Cryptosporidium). Combined defects (SCID) abolish both arms. Innate defects affect phagocyte number (severe congenital neutropenia), adhesion (LAD), or microbial killing (chronic granulomatous disease, from defective NADPH-oxidase reactive-oxygen-species production), as well as complement components and innate receptor/signalling pathways (TLR adaptors IRAK4/MYD88). Beyond infection, PIDs can drive immunopathology including allergy, lymphoproliferation, autoimmunity, and an increased risk of malignancy (notably lymphoma) (Harrison 22e p.2793-2794, 2796-2802).","additionalDifferentials":["Severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID)","X-linked or autosomal-recessive agammaglobulinaemia","Hyper-IgM (HIGM) syndromes (CD40L/CD40 or AID/UNG defects)","IgA deficiency and selective IgG-subclass deficiency","Selective antibody deficiency to polysaccharide antigens with normal total Ig","Chronic granulomatous disease and other phagocyte killing defects","Leukocyte adhesion deficiency and severe congenital neutropenia","Complement component deficiencies (classic, alternative, lytic-phase)","Mendelian susceptibility to mycobacterial disease (IL-12-IFN-gamma axis defects)","Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome, ataxia-telangiectasia, DiGeorge syndrome, hyper-IgE syndrome","Regulatory-pathway disorders: HLH, ALPS, IPEX, APECED"],"infectionMimics":["Recurrent pyogenic sinopulmonary infection from anatomical causes (e.g., cystic fibrosis, bronchiectasis of other aetiology) mimicking antibody deficiency","HIV/AIDS and other secondary (acquired) immunodeficiency producing an opportunistic-infection pattern resembling a T-cell PID","Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia as a presenting opportunistic infection of T-cell or combined defects","Recurrent or chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis","Disseminated or recurrent nontuberculous mycobacterial infection (suggesting MSMD)","Invasive aspergillosis or other filamentous-mould infection (suggesting CGD)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Secondary hypogammaglobulinaemia from thymoma (profound hypogammaglobulinaemia observed in adults)","Hypogammaglobulinaemia secondary to myelodysplastic syndrome (can present as agammaglobulinaemia)","Protein-losing states (nephrotic syndrome, protein-losing enteropathy) lowering immunoglobulin levels","Drug-induced or malignancy-associated (e.g., CLL, lymphoma) immunosuppression","Cytokine-neutralising autoantibodies (to IFN-gamma, IL-6, IL-7, type I interferon) that can mimic a PID","VEXAS syndrome (somatic UBA1 mutation) presenting with late-onset inflammation, vasculitis, and cytopenias"],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood cell counts and morphology (neutrophil and lymphocyte counts adjusted for age; eosinophilia; Howell-Jolly bodies suggesting asplenia)","Serum immunoglobulin levels (IgG, IgA, IgM) and IgE","Specific antibody responses to vaccination (tetanus toxoid; unconjugated pneumococcal polysaccharide antigens)","Lymphocyte phenotyping: T-, B-, and NK-cell counts and subsets; switched (CD27+ IgM-IgD-) and non-switched (CD27+ IgM+IgD+) memory B cells","Dihydrorhodamine (DHR) fluorescence or nitroblue tetrazolium (NBT) assay for neutrophil reactive-oxygen-species production (chronic granulomatous disease)","Complement functional assays CH50 and AP50 (classic and alternative pathways)","Chest x-ray for thymic shadow (SCID, DiGeorge) and costochondral junctions (ADA deficiency); bone x-ray of metaphyses (cartilage-hair hypoplasia)","Abdominal ultrasonography for spleen size (asplenia)","Newborn TREC (T-cell receptor excision circle) screening on the Guthrie card for SCID","Targeted gene-panel resequencing and/or whole-exome/genome sequencing for definitive molecular diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Recurrent, unusually severe, or persistent infections, or infection by unusual/opportunistic organisms","A family history of early childhood deaths (suggesting X-linked or recessive inheritance, e.g., SCID)","Lymphocytopenia in an infant (strongly suggestive of SCID in >90% of cases) or absent thymic shadow on chest x-ray","Failure to thrive with protracted diarrhoea, recurrent oral candidiasis, or Pneumocystis pneumonia in early infancy","Complications of live vaccines (e.g., BCG dissemination, rotavirus diarrhoea) signalling a severe T-cell defect","Invasive Neisseria infection (complement lytic-phase or alternative-pathway deficiency) or recurrent encapsulated-organism sepsis with asplenia","Recurrent infection accompanied by autoimmunity, lymphoproliferation, granulomatous disease, or lymphoma","Absence of pus at sites of infection (hallmark of severe congenital neutropenia)"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies widely with the molecule and arm of immunity affected. T-cell and combined defects generally carry a poor prognosis and require early diagnosis and intervention; SCID is fatal in infancy without treatment, but allogeneic HSCT offers a high curative potential in patients in otherwise reasonable condition, and gene therapy has succeeded in X-linked (gamma-c) SCID, ADA-SCID, and Artemis deficiency. Functional T-cell defects carry a worse HSCT prognosis than SCID because many patients are already chronically infected at diagnosis. For antibody deficiencies, lifelong immunoglobulin replacement combined with management of chronic lung disease reduces respiratory infections and prevents progression to bronchiectasis and cor pulmonale; in CVID, lymphoproliferation, granulomatous disease, autoimmunity, and lymphoma define long-term prognosis. DNA-repair disorders such as ataxia-telangiectasia are progressive and carry a very high risk of lymphoma, leukaemia, and (in adulthood) carcinoma. Newborn TREC screening, by enabling treatment of uninfected SCID patients, maximises the chance of cure. Early diagnosis with referral to experienced centres is essential to optimal outcome (Harrison 22e p.2794, 2799-2800, 2802, 2804).","pitfalls":["CVID can present in adulthood and is easily missed; it is recognised predominantly in adults although manifestations may begin earlier (Harrison 22e p.2802).","High variability of expression for a given PID (e.g., WAS, XLA, CTLA4 deficiency) can hamper diagnosis; modifier genes and environmental triggers such as EBV (in XLP) modulate the phenotype (Harrison 22e p.2804).","Leaky BTK mutations causing mild hypogammaglobulinaemia with low but detectable B cells should not be confused with CVID (Harrison 22e p.2802).","T-cell lymphopenia in SCID may be masked by maternally-engrafted T cells, so screening for maternal cells with genetic markers should be performed when needed (Harrison 22e p.2799).","Somatic mutations in an affected gene can attenuate the phenotype of T-cell PIDs (described for ADA, X-linked SCID, RAG, NEMO, and most often WAS), while somatic mutations can also create PID-like disease (as in ALPS) (Harrison 22e p.2804).","Cytokine-neutralising autoantibodies (to IFN-gamma, IL-6, IL-7, type I interferon) can mimic a PID (Harrison 22e p.2804).","Caution with immunoglobulin replacement in patients with complete IgA deficiency and residual antibody production: anti-IgA antibodies can trigger anaphylactic shock, so IgA-free IgG preparations should be used (Harrison 22e p.2803).","Ataxia-telangiectasia should be considered in young children with IgA deficiency and recurrent problematic infections, as ataxia and telangiectasia may not appear before age 3-4 years (Harrison 22e p.2799-2800)."],"severityClassification":"PIDs are classified along two axes: (1) the arm of the immune system that is defective and (2) the mechanism of the defect (when known) (Table 362-1). The two main divisions are deficiencies of the innate immune system (phagocytic cells - impaired production [severe congenital neutropenia], asplenia, impaired adhesion [LAD], impaired killing [CGD]; innate-immunity receptors/signal transduction [TLR-signalling defects, MSMD]; complement deficiencies of the classical/alternative/lectin pathways and the lytic phase) and deficiencies of the adaptive immune system (T lymphocytes - SCID, DiGeorge, combined immunodeficiencies, hyper-IgE, DOCK8, CD40L, Wiskott-Aldrich, ataxia-telangiectasia and DNA-repair defects; B lymphocytes - X-linked/AR agammaglobulinaemia, hyper-IgM, CVID, IgA deficiency), together with regulatory defects (autoinflammatory syndromes, severe colitis, HLH, ALPS, autoimmunity such as IPEX and APECED). The classification is acknowledged to involve some arbitrary decisions because of overlap and incomplete data (Harrison 22e p.2794).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2793-2804"],"crossLinks":["pneumococcal_disease","pneumocystis_pneumonia","candidiasis","nontuberculous_mycobacterial_infection","meningococcal_disease","aspergillosis","hiv_aids"]},"pulmonary_hypertension":{"name":"Pulmonary Hypertension","system":"Cardiology / Pulmonary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":294,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2185","clinicalPearls":["PH is diagnosed when mean pulmonary artery pressure (mPAP) is >20 mmHg by right heart catheterisation; this threshold was lowered from the older >=25 mmHg cut-off to emphasise earlier disease detection (p.2185, 2191).","The most common causes of PH are left heart disease or primary lung disease; pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) is an uncommon but distinct subtype with an obliterative arteriopathy (p.2185).","Most patients present with dyspnoea and/or fatigue; oedema, chest pain, presyncope and syncope are less common and signal more advanced disease (p.2188).","The current WSPH classification (last revised 2018, Sixth World Symposium) defines five groups: 1 = PAH; 2 = PH due to left heart disease; 3 = PH due to chronic lung disease/hypoxia or sleep-disordered breathing; 4 = chronic thromboembolic PH (CTEPH); 5 = miscellaneous/multifactorial (p.2191).","Echocardiography with agitated-saline (bubble) study is the most important initial screening test; estimated pulmonary artery systolic pressure >35 mmHg, a notched continuous-wave Doppler waveform, or a hypertrophied/dilated right ventricle supports the diagnosis (p.2188).","Hemodynamic subtypes by RHC: precapillary PH = PAWP/LVEDP <=15 mmHg with PVR >2.0 Wood units; isolated postcapillary PH = PAWP >15 mmHg with PVR <=2.0 WU; combined pre-/postcapillary PH = mPAP >20 mmHg with PAWP >15 mmHg and PVR >2.0 WU (p.2191).","In PAH, an isolated reduction in diffusing capacity for carbon monoxide (DLCO) on pulmonary function testing is a classic finding (p.2189).","BNP and NT-proBNP correlate with right ventricular dysfunction, hemodynamic severity and functional status in PAH, and NT-proBNP can serve as a biomarker of treatment response once a baseline is established (p.2191).","Vasoreactivity testing (with short-acting agents such as inhaled nitric oxide or inhaled epoprostenol) is reserved for idiopathic or hereditary PAH; a positive response is a fall in mPAP by >=10 mmHg to an absolute level <=40 mmHg without a fall in cardiac output, seen in <5% of patients but carrying a particularly favourable prognosis (p.2191).","CTEPH is curable in many patients by surgical pulmonary endarterectomy but is widely underdiagnosed; ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) scanning remains the primary screening test and should be considered in any PH of unclear cause (p.2190-2191)."],"pathophysiology":"PAH arises from apoptosis resistance, cell proliferation, dysregulated metabolism and increased oxidant stress in pulmonary vascular cells, pericytes and adventitial fibroblasts, producing hypertrophic, fibrotic and plexogenic remodelling of distal (small) pulmonary arterioles with extracellular-matrix expansion, reduced vascular compliance and in-situ thrombosis. This raises pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR) and mean pulmonary artery pressure (mPAP). To preserve cardiac output against the elevated right-ventricular afterload, RV work increases; sustained overload depletes myocardial energy and causes right ventricular-pulmonary arterial uncoupling, and in end-stage disease cardiac output and mPAP both fall. The most common germline driver of hereditary PAH is a BMPR2 variant (a TGF-beta superfamily receptor) with variable penetrance (as low as ~20%); other drivers include TBX4 and SOX17, and EIF2AK4 for pulmonary veno-occlusive disease. In group 2 PH the hallmark is elevated left atrial pressure causing pulmonary venous hypertension, venule sclerosis and secondary arteriolar remodelling. (p.2185-2192)","additionalDifferentials":["Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF)","Left ventricular systolic dysfunction","Mitral or aortic valvular disease","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) / emphysema","Interstitial / restrictive lung disease","Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension (CTEPH)","Connective tissue disease, especially systemic sclerosis-associated PAH","Portopulmonary hypertension","HIV-associated PAH","Congenital heart disease with intracardiac shunt / Eisenmenger's syndrome","Hepatopulmonary syndrome (distinct: causes intrapulmonary shunting, not pulmonary vascular remodelling)"],"infectionMimics":["HIV infection (HIV-PAH is an important cause of mortality in HIV-infected patients)","Schistosomiasis (affects >230 million worldwide, ~5% develop PAH; among the most common causes of PAH globally)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Asthma (overlapping nonspecific dyspnoea)","Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction","Physical deconditioning","Obstructive sleep apnoea / obesity-hypoventilation syndrome","Sarcoidosis with pulmonary vascular involvement"],"additionalInvestigations":["Right heart catheterisation (RHC) - gold standard for diagnosis and severity assessment; required mPAP >20 mmHg","Transthoracic echocardiography with agitated-saline (bubble) study - most important initial screening test","Electrocardiography - may show right ventricular hypertrophy or strain in advanced disease","Chest roentgenography - enlargement of central pulmonary arteries and obliteration of the retrosternal space","Pulmonary function testing including DLCO (isolated low DLCO classic for PAH)","High-resolution CT of the chest (vascular pruning, distinguishing ILD/emphysema/overlap; signs of venous congestion)","Ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) scan - primary screen for CTEPH","CT pulmonary angiography and digital subtraction pulmonary angiography (definitive for CTEPH; shows webbing, stricture, tapering)","6-minute walk distance (6-MWD) for functional capacity and prognosis","Cardiopulmonary exercise testing (CPET); peak VO2 <15 mL/kg/min is prognostic but not diagnostic","Nocturnal oximetry screening in all patients (nocturnal desaturation common even without sleep-disordered breathing)","BNP / NT-proBNP","Serology: HIV test (when indicated), antinuclear antibodies, rheumatoid factor, anti-Scl-70; liver function and hepatitis serology; methamphetamine screening where relevant","Vasoreactivity testing (idiopathic/hereditary PAH only)"],"redFlags":["Syncope or presyncope (signals advanced disease)","Signs of right ventricular failure: elevated jugular venous pressure, lower-extremity oedema, ascites","Rapid progression of symptoms with severely reduced exercise capacity","Decompensated right heart failure (the major cause of mortality if untreated)","Significant decline in exercise tolerance from baseline even when CPET appears reassuring (e.g. in athletes - may indicate early PAH or subacute PE)"],"prognosis":"If left untreated, PH carries a high mortality rate, largely owing to decompensated right heart failure. Idiopathic PAH is progressive and leads to right heart failure and early mortality; in left-sided heart failure or HFpEF even mildly elevated mPAP carries adverse outcome. Among IPAH, prevalence favours women ~3:1 but hemodynamics are more severe and prognosis less favourable in men. Hereditary BMPR2 PAH presents younger with more severe hemodynamics and greater clinical risk (conversion ~2.3%/year in carriers). Vasoreactive responders (<5%) have a particularly favourable prognosis. Scleroderma-associated PAH and HIV-PAH carry especially poor prognoses; portopulmonary hypertension is a marker of adverse post-liver-transplant outcome (up to 100% mortality reported with mPAP >=50 mmHg in one study). Peak VO2 <15 mL/kg/min is prognostic. (p.2185, 2191-2192)","pitfalls":["Right heart catheterisation - not echocardiography - is the diagnostic gold standard; echocardiography only screens, and an echo in which tricuspid regurgitation is simply not detected does NOT exclude PH (PH is found in one-third of such patients).","PH symptoms are nonspecific, insidious and overlap with asthma, HFpEF and deconditioning, so the diagnosis is frequently missed without a reasonable index of suspicion.","Do not assume that PH in patients with comorbid cardiopulmonary disease (e.g. ILD, mitral valve disease) is merely an extension of the underlying condition rather than a specific entity.","Patients with left heart disease aggressively diuresed before RHC may falsely appear to have an isolated precapillary (PAH-like) profile - diuresis can mask the true hemodynamic classification.","A low peak VO2 occurs across the heart-lung disease spectrum and is NOT diagnostic of PH per se.","Chronic thromboembolic disease (thrombotic remodelling with reduced exercise capacity but without PH) requires consideration in patients with prior PE and dyspnoea even when echocardiography is reassuring."],"severityClassification":"World Symposium on Pulmonary Hypertension (WSPH) clinical classification (revised 2018) into 5 groups: Group 1 - pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH: idiopathic, heritable, drug/toxin-associated, or associated with connective tissue disease, HIV, portal hypertension, congenital heart disease, schistosomiasis; plus PVOD/PCH and persistent PH of the newborn); Group 2 - PH associated with left heart disease (HFpEF, HFrEF, valvular, congenital/acquired postcapillary); Group 3 - PH associated with lung disease and/or hypoxia (obstructive, restrictive, mixed, hypoventilation, high-altitude hypoxia, developmental); Group 4 - PH associated with pulmonary artery obstruction (CTEPH and other obstructions); Group 5 - PH with unclear/multifactorial mechanisms (haematological, systemic, metabolic, chronic renal failure +/- haemodialysis, pulmonary tumour thrombotic microangiopathy, fibrosing mediastinitis). Hemodynamic categorisation by RHC: precapillary, isolated postcapillary, and combined pre-/postcapillary PH. (Table 294-1, p.2191)","references":["Harrison 22e p.2185"],"crossLinks":["heart_failure","pe","ild","copd_exac_ni","systemic_sclerosis"]},"rabies":{"name":"Rabies","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":214,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1650","clinicalPearls":["Rabies is a rapidly progressive acute CNS infection by rabies virus (a lyssavirus, family Rhabdoviridae), normally transmitted via the bite of an infected animal; it has encephalitic and paralytic forms that progress to death (p.1650).","Worldwide, ~99% of human rabies is transmitted from dogs in countries with endemic canine rabies; endemic canine rabies causes an estimated 59,000 human deaths annually, with most deaths in Asia and Africa and rural populations and children disproportionately affected (p.1650-1651).","Incubation period is usually 20-90 days but in rare cases may be as short as a few days or longer than 1 year (p.1651).","Earliest specific neurologic symptoms are paresthesias, pain, or pruritus near the exposure site, occurring in 50-80% of patients and strongly suggesting rabies; the wound has usually healed by this point (p.1652).","Two acute neurologic forms occur: encephalitic (furious) in ~80% and paralytic (dumb) in ~20% (p.1652).","Encephalitic rabies presents as atypical encephalitis with relative preservation of consciousness; episodes of hyperexcitability alternate with periods of complete lucidity that shorten as disease progresses (p.1652).","Early brainstem involvement produces the classic hydrophobia (involuntary painful contraction of diaphragm and accessory respiratory, laryngeal, and pharyngeal muscles on swallowing liquids) and aerophobia (same response to a draft of air) (p.1652).","Autonomic dysfunction is common and may cause hypersalivation, gooseflesh, cardiac arrhythmia, and priapism; hypersalivation plus pharyngeal dysfunction produces the classic 'foaming at the mouth' (p.1652).","Paralytic rabies features early prominent flaccid weakness often beginning in the bitten extremity, progressing to quadriparesis and facial weakness, with sphincter involvement common and sensory involvement usually mild (p.1652).","The Negri body (eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusion in brain neurons, e.g. cerebellar Purkinje cells and hippocampal pyramidal neurons) is the most characteristic pathologic finding, but is NOT seen in all cases (p.1651-1652).","Diagnosis should be considered in patients with acute atypical encephalitis or acute flaccid paralysis, including those in whom Guillain-Barre syndrome is suspected; absence of an animal-bite history is common in North America due to unrecognized bat exposures (p.1653).","Postexposure prophylaxis must be initiated after a recognized exposure and before any symptoms or signs develop; the emphasis for prevention is on PEP (p.1652)."],"pathophysiology":"Rabies virus, a single-strand negative-sense RNA lyssavirus (family Rhabdoviridae), is usually inoculated by an animal bite. During most of the incubation period the virus remains at or near the inoculation site; in muscle it binds nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at neuromuscular junctions. It spreads centripetally along peripheral nerves toward the spinal cord or brainstem by retrograde fast axonal transport (up to ~250 mm/d, with ~12-h delays at each synapse). Once in the CNS it disseminates rapidly along neuroanatomic connections; neurons are prominently infected while astrocyte infection is unusual. After CNS infection is established there is centrifugal spread along sensory and autonomic nerves to salivary glands, heart, adrenal glands, and skin; viral replication in salivary gland acinar cells leads to secretion in saliva (the vehicle of animal transmission). There is no well-documented evidence for hematogenous spread. CNS inflammatory changes are mild (mononuclear infiltrate, perivascular cuffing, microglial Babes nodules) with little neuronal death; clinical disease is attributed to neuronal dysfunction rather than death, with oxidative stress from mitochondrial dysfunction implicated. (p.1650-1652)","additionalDifferentials":["Herpes simplex encephalitis","Arboviral encephalitis (e.g., West Nile)","Anti-NMDA receptor and other autoimmune encephalitides","Postinfectious (immune-mediated) encephalomyelitis (following influenza, measles, mumps, or neural-tissue rabies vaccine)","Guillain-Barre syndrome (mimicked by paralytic rabies)","Rabies hysteria / somatic symptom disorder (psychological response to fear of rabies)","Misdiagnosis as a primary psychiatric disorder"],"infectionMimics":["Herpes simplex encephalitis","Arboviral (e.g., West Nile) encephalitis","Postinfectious encephalomyelitis after influenza, measles, or mumps"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis (especially young females; behavioral changes, autonomic instability, hypoventilation, seizures)","Other antibody-mediated autoimmune encephalitis","Guillain-Barre syndrome","Rabies hysteria (somatic symptom disorder) with shorter course and eventual recovery","Psychiatric disorder"],"additionalInvestigations":["Rabies virus-specific serum neutralizing antibodies (diagnostic in a previously unimmunized patient; may not develop until late and some patients die without them)","Rabies virus-specific neutralizing antibodies in CSF (suggest rabies encephalitis regardless of immunization status)","RT-PCR for rabies virus RNA (highly sensitive/specific; saliva, skin biopsy, CSF [less sensitive], brain tissue; genetic sequencing can identify the variant/source)","Direct fluorescent antibody (DFA) testing for rabies virus antigen (skin biopsy and brain tissue; in skin, antigen detected in cutaneous nerves at the base of hair follicles)","Skin biopsy from hairy skin at the nape of the neck","CSF examination (typically mild mononuclear pleocytosis with mildly elevated protein)","MRI brain (variable, nonspecific brainstem/gray-matter signal abnormalities); CT usually normal; EEG nonspecific"],"redFlags":["Near-100% fatal once symptomatic, so post-exposure prophylaxis is everything - do NOT delay PEP after a recognized exposure.","Paresthesias, pain, or pruritus at a healed animal-bite site preceding neurologic illness strongly suggest rabies.","Hydrophobia or aerophobia with preserved consciousness (atypical encephalitis) is essentially diagnostic of encephalitic rabies.","Absence of a recalled animal bite does NOT exclude rabies - bat exposures are frequently unrecognized; consider PEP when a small child or sleeping adult shared a space with a bat and an unrecognized bite cannot be reliably excluded.","Negative antemortem rabies-specific laboratory tests never exclude the diagnosis; repeat testing may be needed.","Acute flaccid paralysis resembling Guillain-Barre syndrome may be paralytic rabies - fever, bladder dysfunction, normal sensory exam, and CSF pleocytosis favor rabies."],"prognosis":"Rabies is an almost uniformly fatal disease but is nearly always preventable after recognized exposures with appropriate postexposure therapy during the early incubation period. Most patients die within several days of symptom onset despite aggressive critical-care support. There is no established treatment and no antiviral has demonstrated efficacy; aggressive supportive care has yielded at least 33 documented survivors (many recently reported from India), but recent treatment failures (>60) have far outnumbered successes. All but 2 of the 33 documented survivors had received one or more doses of rabies vaccine before disease onset, and the unvaccinated survivors had neutralizing antibodies in CSF at presentation. A palliative approach may be appropriate for patients who are not candidates for aggressive management. (p.1654)","pitfalls":["Rabies is often not considered until late in the clinical course in North America even with a typical presentation.","Relying on a history of animal bite to make the diagnosis - many cases (especially bat-associated) have no recalled exposure.","Assuming absence of hydrophobia excludes rabies - lack of hydrophobia is not unusual.","Misdiagnosing paralytic rabies as Guillain-Barre syndrome.","Treating negative antemortem rabies-specific tests as exclusionary.","Severe CSF pleocytosis (>1000 white cells/uL) is unusual for rabies and should prompt a search for an alternative diagnosis.","Delaying local wound care while awaiting the 10-day animal observation period - wound care should not be delayed.","Administering passive immunization (RIG) more than 7 days after the first vaccine dose, when endogenous antibody production has begun and passive immunization may be counterproductive; rabies vaccine and RIG must never be given at the same site.","Minor deviations from current PEP recommendations can lead to prophylaxis failure."],"severityClassification":"Clinical stages (Table 214-1): incubation period (20-90 days, asymptomatic); prodrome (2-10 days: fever, malaise, anorexia, nausea, vomiting; paresthesias/pain/pruritus at the wound site); acute neurologic disease - encephalitic form (~80%, 2-7 days: anxiety, agitation, hyperactivity, bizarre behavior, hallucinations, autonomic dysfunction, hydrophobia) or paralytic form (~20%, 2-10 days: flaccid limb paralysis progressing to quadriparesis with facial paralysis); then coma and death (0-14 days; recovery rare). (p.1653)","references":["Harrison 22e p.1650"],"crossLinks":["ENCEPHALITIS","gbs"]},"salmonellosis":{"name":"Nontyphoidal Salmonellosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":171,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1314, 1319-1321","clinicalPearls":["NTS infection most often produces a gastroenteritis that is clinically indistinguishable from that caused by other enteric pathogens, with nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea beginning 6-48 h after ingestion of contaminated food or water; stools are usually loose, nonbloody, and of moderate volume, though large-volume watery stools, bloody stools, or frank dysentery can occur (p.1320).","NTS gastroenteritis is usually self-limited: diarrhea resolves within 3-7 days and fever within 72 h; stool cultures may remain positive for 4-5 weeks after infection (p.1320).","Globally S. Typhimurium and S. Enteritidis are the most common serotypes causing human disease; in the United States NTS is the second most common cause of foodborne outbreaks after norovirus (p.1319).","Unlike S. Typhi and S. Paratyphi (human-only reservoir), NTS is acquired from multiple animal and plant reservoirs - especially eggs, poultry, undercooked ground meat, and dairy, plus produce contaminated with animal waste and contact with animals (reptiles, amphibians, chicks, etc.) (p.1319).","Up to 8% of patients with NTS gastroenteritis develop bacteremia, and of those 5-10% develop localized (metastatic) infections; bacteremia and metastatic infection are most common with S. Choleraesuis and S. Dublin and among infants, the elderly, and the immunocompromised, especially those with HIV (p.1320).","Invasive NTS disease (predominantly S. Typhimurium ST313 and S. Enteritidis ST11) is a leading cause of bacteremia in children and HIV-infected adults in sub-Saharan Africa and Southeast Asia; it typically presents as a nonspecific febrile illness resembling enteric fever and uncommonly causes diarrhea (p.1319-1320).","Diagnosis rests on isolating the organism from freshly passed stool or from blood or another normally sterile fluid; culture-independent molecular tests are increasingly used but positive specimens should still undergo primary isolation, and all NTS isolates should be referred to public health for serotyping (p.1320-1321).","Reactive arthritis can follow NTS gastroenteritis, occurring most frequently in persons carrying the HLA-B27 antigen (p.1321).","Localized NTS infections include intraabdominal (hepatic/splenic abscess, cholecystitis), CNS (meningitis - chiefly in infants 1-4 months and HIV-infected adults), pulmonary (lobar pneumonia), urinary/genital, and bone/joint disease; Salmonella osteomyelitis classically affects the femur, tibia, humerus, or lumbar vertebrae and is strongly linked to sickle cell disease and hemoglobinopathies (p.1320-1321)."],"pathophysiology":"All Salmonella infections begin with ingestion of organisms, usually in contaminated food or water; the infectious dose ranges from ~200 to 10^6 CFU, and the ingested dose helps determine incubation period and severity. Conditions reducing gastric acidity (age <1 year, acid-suppression therapy, achlorhydria) or compromising intestinal integrity (inflammatory bowel disease, cytotoxic chemotherapy, prior GI surgery, antibiotic-induced microbiome alteration) increase susceptibility. In contrast to enteric fever (mononuclear infiltration of small-bowel mucosa), NTS gastroenteritis is characterized by massive polymorphonuclear leukocyte infiltration into both large- and small-bowel mucosa. This response depends largely on induction of interleukin 8, a strong neutrophil chemoattractant secreted by intestinal cells in response to NTS colonization and translocation of bacterial proteins and LPS into host cytoplasm with subsequent inflammasome activation; neutrophil degranulation and release of toxic substances damages the mucosa, producing inflammatory diarrhea. NTS persistence is aided by its ability to use tetrathionate (generated from thiosulfate via inflammatory reactive oxygen species) for metabolism in a microaerophilic, inflamed gut, allowing it to outcompete endogenous flora. Unlike typhoidal serotypes, NTS effectively colonizes the intestinal tract rather than evolving as a primarily systemic pathogen.","additionalDifferentials":["Other bacterial enteric pathogens (Campylobacter, Shigella, enteroinvasive/enterotoxigenic E. coli)","Norovirus and other viral gastroenteritis","Intestinal parasites","Pseudoappendicitis (NTS can mimic acute appendicitis)","Inflammatory bowel disease (NTS rarely produces an IBD-like illness)","Enteric (typhoid) fever - especially with invasive NTS presenting as nonspecific febrile illness"],"infectionMimics":["Enteric (typhoid/paratyphoid) fever from S. Typhi/S. Paratyphi presenting as undifferentiated febrile illness","Other causes of community-acquired bloodstream infection in endemic regions (malaria co-infection common)","Other bacterial gastroenteritides (Campylobacter, Shigella, E. coli)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute appendicitis (pseudoappendicitis)","Inflammatory bowel disease","Postinfectious irritable bowel syndrome / functional dyspepsia (recognized sequelae)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool culture (freshly passed) with primary isolation even after culture-independent molecular detection","Blood cultures - obtain whenever fever is prolonged or recurrent; high-grade bacteremia defined as >50% of three or more blood-culture sets positive suggests endovascular infection","Culture-independent molecular diagnostic panels for enteric pathogens","Serotyping of all NTS isolates via public health laboratories","Echocardiography to evaluate suspected endocarditis","CT imaging to localize deep/focal infection","Indium-labeled white-cell scanning to identify localized infection","Culture of joint fluid aspirate, bone biopsy, abscess drainage, or CSF when localized infection is suspected","Antimicrobial susceptibility testing with attention to ciprofloxacin MIC (Etest or alternative method) given the lowered susceptibility breakpoint and decreased-ciprofloxacin-susceptibility strains not reliably detected by nalidixic acid screening or standard disk diffusion"],"redFlags":["Invasive disease / bacteremia and endovascular seeding in the elderly, immunocompromised (including HIV), infants/neonates, and patients with sickle cell disease or other hemoglobinopathies","High-grade or persistent bacteremia, particularly with preexisting valvular heart disease, atherosclerotic vascular disease, prosthetic vascular graft, or aortic aneurysm - suspect NTS endovascular infection","Prolonged fever with back, chest, or abdominal pain after an episode of gastroenteritis in an elderly patient - suspect arteritis/mycotic aneurysm","NTS meningitis in infants 1-4 months of age or HIV-infected adults - severe sequelae and death in up to 60% of cases","Persons >50 years with known or suspected atherosclerosis (increased risk of invasive infection and endovascular seeding)"],"prognosis":"NTS gastroenteritis is usually self-limited, with diarrhea resolving in 3-7 days and fever within 72 h; chronic carriage develops in <1% of cases. Outcomes worsen markedly with invasive disease: endocarditis and arteritis are rare (<1%) but carry serious, potentially fatal complications (valve perforation, endomyocardial abscess, infected mural thrombus, pericarditis, mycotic aneurysm, aneurysm rupture, aortoenteric fistula, vertebral osteomyelitis), and NTS meningitis is fatal in up to 60% of cases. In 2017 there were ~535,000 invasive NTS cases and ~77,500 deaths worldwide, ~80% in sub-Saharan Africa. Acute NTS gastroenteritis was associated with a threefold increased risk of dyspepsia and irritable bowel syndrome at 1 year in a Spanish study.","pitfalls":["Antibiotics should NOT be used routinely to treat uncomplicated NTS gastroenteritis - the illness is usually self-limited and antibiotics do not significantly shorten fever or diarrhea, while they have been associated with increased relapse rates, prolonged fecal/gastrointestinal carriage, and adverse drug reactions.","Only ~1% of clinical isolates ferment lactose, so a high index of suspicion is needed to detect rare lactose-fermenting strains.","Decreased-ciprofloxacin-susceptibility NTS strains may not be reliably detected by nalidixic acid susceptibility testing or standard ciprofloxacin disk diffusion, risking missed resistance and treatment failure.","Neonates, the elderly, and immunosuppressed patients with NTS gastroenteritis are especially prone to dehydration and invasive infection and may need hospitalization and antibiotic therapy - they are exceptions to the no-routine-antibiotics rule."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal graded severity score for NTS. Disease is described along a clinical spectrum: (1) uncomplicated self-limited gastroenteritis; (2) bacteremia (low-grade defined as <50% of blood cultures positive vs high-grade >50% of three or more sets positive, the latter raising concern for endovascular infection); and (3) localized/metastatic infection (intraabdominal, CNS, pulmonary, urinary/genital, bone/joint/soft tissue) and endovascular infection (endocarditis, arteritis). Host-risk stratification for invasive disease includes neonates (up to ~3 months), persons >50 years with atherosclerosis, and those with immunosuppression, cardiac valvular/endovascular abnormalities, or significant joint disease.","references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, Chapter 171: Salmonellosis (Pegues DA, Miller SI), pp.1314, 1319-1321","Marchello CS et al: Complications and mortality of non-typhoidal salmonella invasive disease: A global systematic review and meta-analysis. Lancet Infect Dis 22:692, 2022","Medalla F et al: Increased Incidence of Antimicrobial-Resistant Nontyphoidal Salmonella Infections, United States, 2004-2016. Emerg Infect Dis 27:1662, 2021","Onwuezobe IA et al: Antimicrobials for treating symptomatic non-typhoidal Salmonella infection. Cochrane Database Syst Rev 11:CD001167, 2012","Pulford CV et al: Stepwise evolution of Salmonella Typhimurium ST313 causing bloodstream infection in Africa. Nat Microbiol 6:327, 2021"],"crossLinks":["GASTROENTERITIS","ENTERIC_FEVER","DYSENTERY"]},"schistosomiasis":{"name":"Schistosomiasis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":241,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1825","clinicalPearls":["Infection is contracted through contact with freshwater bodies harboring infected intermediate-host snails; cercariae penetrate intact human skin within a few minutes (p.1826).","A travel/migration history of freshwater exposure (bathing, washing, fishing, rice-field work, swimming) in an endemic area is central to the diagnosis, especially in travelers (p.1827, p.1829).","Most disease manifestations are due to immunologic granulomatous reactions to eggs retained in host tissues, not to the adult worms; about half of eggs are trapped in tissue (p.1827).","Cercarial dermatitis ('swimmer's itch') is a maculopapular rash from cercarial skin penetration; a severe form follows exposure to avian schistosomes (e.g., lakes in Europe/US) that die in the skin (p.1828).","Acute schistosomiasis (Katayama fever) occurs 2 weeks to 3 months after exposure, usually in first-time-infected travelers, with fever, myalgia, malaise, headache, nonproductive cough, and abdominal symptoms (p.1828).","Eosinophilia is a common finding and is often associated with schistosomiasis; transient pulmonary infiltrates may accompany Katayama fever (p.1828, p.1829).","Intestinal schistosomiasis (S. mansoni, S. japonicum): worms in mesenteric veins; eggs trapped in intestinal tissue cause granulomatous inflammation, microulceration, intermittent abdominal pain, and sometimes bloody diarrhea (p.1829).","Hepatosplenic schistosomiasis (S. mansoni, S. japonicum): late disease produces periportal (Symmers clay-pipestem) fibrosis with portal vein occlusion, portal hypertension, splenomegaly, esophageal varices, and hematemesis; hepatic function usually remains normal (p.1827, p.1829).","Urogenital schistosomiasis (S. haematobium): hallmark of the active stage is painless terminal hematuria, with dysuria and suprapubic discomfort; eggs in bladder mucosa cause inflammation, fibrosis, 'sandy patches', and possible ureteric obstruction with hydronephrosis (p.1829).","S. haematobium is an IARC group 1 carcinogen; chronic infection is associated with squamous cell carcinoma of the urinary bladder (p.1829).","Detection of schistosome eggs in stool or urine indicates active infection and is the standard diagnostic method; S. haematobium eggs (terminal spine) are excreted in urine, other species (S. mansoni lateral spine, S. japonicum small lateral knob) in feces (p.1826, p.1829).","Neuroschistosomiasis is one of the most severe forms: S. mansoni/S. haematobium can cause transverse myelitis (an acute complication in returning travelers), while S. japonicum causes cerebral granulomas with seizures and focal neurologic deficits (p.1829)."],"pathophysiology":"Free-swimming cercariae released from infected freshwater snails penetrate intact skin within minutes, transform into schistosomula, enter veins/lymphatics, traverse the lungs and heart, and mature in the portal vein into paired adult male and female worms that migrate to the mesenteric (S. mansoni, S. japonicum) or pelvic/vesical (S. haematobium) venous plexus. The prepatent period is 5-7 weeks (up to 12 for S. haematobium). About half of the eggs produced are excreted in feces or urine; the remainder become trapped in tissue. Most morbidity results from egg-induced, TH2-driven granulomatous immune responses (lymphocytes, eosinophils, alternatively activated macrophages; IL-4/5/13). Egg antigen excess early in infection forms soluble immune complexes causing a serum sickness-like illness (Katayama fever). Hepatic egg deposition leads to periportal (Symmers) fibrosis, portal-branch occlusion, and portal hypertension; S. haematobium egg deposition in the bladder/ureters causes inflammation, fibrosis, obstructive uropathy, and predisposes to squamous bladder carcinoma (p.1826-1829).","additionalDifferentials":["Malaria (acute febrile illness in returning traveler/migrant) (p.1829)","Salmonellosis (p.1829)","Acute viral hepatitis (p.1829)","Trichinosis (fever with eosinophilia) (p.1829)","Strongyloidiasis (fever with eosinophilia) (p.1829)","Visceral larva migrans / toxocariasis (fever with eosinophilia) (p.1829)","Other causes of portal hypertension and hepatosplenomegaly, including cirrhosis (p.1829)"],"infectionMimics":["Opisthorchis and Clonorchis (liver fluke) infections causing fever and eosinophilia (p.1829)","Invasive ankylostomiasis (hookworm) (p.1829)","Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia (p.1829)","Strongyloidiasis (p.1829)","Malaria (p.1829)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cirrhosis from other causes presenting with portal hypertension, splenomegaly, and variceal bleeding (p.1829)","Bladder squamous cell carcinoma from non-schistosomal causes (chronic urogenital disease) (p.1829)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Microscopic detection of eggs in stool (e.g., ~50 mg) or urine (filtration of ~10 mL) with quantitation (eggs per gram or per 10 mL); concentration methods (e.g., formol-ether) for low-burden travelers (p.1829)","Full blood count showing eosinophilia (p.1829)","Rectal biopsy for eggs (S. mansoni and S. haematobium); occasionally Pap smears and semen samples (S. haematobium) (p.1829)","PCR-based detection of Schistosoma DNA in stool or urine (more sensitive than parasitology); CSF Schistosoma DNA for neuroschistosomiasis (p.1829)","Serology for anti-schistosome antibodies (CDC: FAST-ELISA with S. mansoni microsomal antigen plus confirmatory species-specific immunoblot) - useful in travelers, less so in endemic populations (p.1829-1830)","Circulating anodic/cathodic antigen (CAA/CCA) detection in serum or urine by ELISA or lateral flow as a marker of active infection; point-of-care urine CCA assay used in community screening (p.1830)","Ultrasonography to demonstrate periportal fibrosis, portal vein dilation, and obstructive uropathy/hydronephrosis (p.1829)","Cystoscopy showing 'sandy patches' of calcified eggs in chronic urogenital disease; endoscopy or ultrasound for esophageal varices (p.1829)"],"redFlags":["Hematemesis or melena from esophageal varices in hepatosplenic disease - the most severe complication; death may result from massive blood loss (p.1829)","Acute transverse myelitis or other cerebral/spinal cord manifestations - neuroschistosomiasis, even from light infections (p.1829)","Severe Katayama fever with weight loss, dyspnea, diarrhea, and hepatomegaly; rarely fatal (p.1829)","Painless terminal hematuria in a traveler/migrant from an endemic area (urogenital S. haematobium) (p.1829)","Obstructive uropathy progressing to uremia from ureteral fibrosis (p.1829)"],"prognosis":"Many patients recover spontaneously from acute schistosomiasis after 2-10 weeks, though some follow a more severe course, and the acute syndrome can rarely be fatal. In hepatosplenic disease, hepatic function usually remains normal even with marked periportal fibrosis and portal hypertension; however, severe periportal (Symmers) fibrosis is often irreversible, and hematemesis from variceal bleeding may be fatal. Early inflammatory hepatosplenomegaly in children generally decreases over time as immunity develops. Ureteral fibrosis can cause irreversible obstructive uropathy progressing to uremia. The adult worm lifespan averages 3-5 years but can reach 30 years (p.1826-1829).","pitfalls":["Patients with periportal fibrosis (late hepatosplenic disease) may excrete very few or no eggs in feces, so a negative stool exam does not exclude advanced disease (p.1829).","In the chronic urogenital stage, urinary egg excretion may be sparse or absent despite ongoing urogenital tract pathology (p.1829).","Egg excretion may be very low in people from nonendemic areas (travelers), requiring larger samples and concentration methods (p.1829).","Katayama fever is rare in people chronically exposed in S. mansoni- or S. haematobium-endemic areas, so its absence does not exclude infection in residents (p.1829).","CAA/CCA antigen detection is not currently suitable for diagnosing travelers, who have low worm burdens (p.1830).","Serology is less useful in people from endemic areas with ongoing transmission (p.1829-1830)."],"severityClassification":"Disease is staged clinically by duration and intensity of infection into three phases - acute (cercarial dermatitis and Katayama fever), active/established, and chronic (e.g., late hepatosplenic periportal fibrosis with portal hypertension, or chronic urogenital fibrosis with sandy patches and obstructive uropathy). Severity of early hepatosplenomegaly is closely associated with infection intensity (p.1828-1829).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1825"],"crossLinks":[]},"shigellosis":{"name":"Shigellosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":172,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1322-1325","clinicalPearls":["Classic dysentery is a syndrome of fever, intestinal cramps, and frequent passage of small, bloody, mucopurulent stools; tenesmus is characteristic (p.1322).","Shigella has a very low infectious dose; experimental infection of volunteers occurs with as few as ~100 CFU, accounting for high attack rates in day-care centers (33-73%) and high secondary attack rates among family members (26-33%) (p.1322).","Spread is primarily fecal-oral via hand carriage; the organism survives gastric passage owing to acid resistance, and can also be transmitted sexually (p.1322-1323).","Shigellosis typically evolves through four phases: incubation (usually 1-4 days, up to 8), watery diarrhea, dysentery, and a postinfectious phase (p.1323).","Dysentery reflects invasion of the colonic mucosa, producing acute colitis mainly involving the distal colon and rectum, with uninterrupted small-volume bloody mucopurulent stools, tenesmus, and cramps (p.1323).","Of the four species, S. flexneri predominates in least-developed areas while S. sonnei is more common in industrialized/emerging economies; S. dysenteriae type 1 (Shiga-toxin producer) drives the most severe epidemic disease (p.1322).","With effective antibiotics, clinical improvement occurs within ~48 h, with fewer complications, shorter symptoms, and clearance of Shigella from stool; in the US every culture-confirmed case is recommended for treatment because of transmissibility (p.1324).","Reactive arthritis (arthritis, ocular inflammation, urethritis) can develop weeks to months after infection, almost exclusively after S. flexneri and in HLA-B27-positive patients (about 3% of S. flexneri infections) (p.1323-1324)."],"pathophysiology":"Pathogenicity is governed by a ~214-kb virulence plasmid encoding a type III secretion system that injects effectors into host cells. After acid-resistant passage through the stomach, bacteria invade colonic epithelium either directly at crypt openings or after crossing M cells over mucosal lymphoid follicles; they induce apoptosis of resident macrophages, drive cytoskeletal uptake, lyse the containing vacuole to reach the cytosol, then spread cell-to-cell using actin-based motility. A massive cytokine-driven influx of polymorphonuclear leukocytes destabilizes the epithelial barrier and produces the acute colitis of shigellosis. Early watery diarrhea is attributed to combined enterotoxin (ShET-1) action and mucosal inflammation. In S. dysenteriae type 1, Shiga toxin (an A1-B5 toxin binding globotriaosylceramide and inhibiting protein synthesis via 28S rRNA N-glycosidase activity) is translocated into the circulation and, by acting on renal target cells, can cause hemolytic-uremic syndrome (p.1322-1323).","additionalDifferentials":["Salmonella infection","Campylobacter jejuni infection","Clostridium difficile colitis","Yersinia enterocolitica infection","Entamoeba histolytica (amebic dysentery)","Inflammatory bowel disease (first flare of Crohn's disease or ulcerative colitis), particularly in industrialized countries","Enteroinvasive E. coli (can cause a shigellosis-like dysentery)"],"infectionMimics":["Salmonellosis","Campylobacter jejuni enteritis","Clostridium difficile colitis","Yersinia enterocolitica enterocolitis","Amebic dysentery (Entamoeba histolytica)","Enteroinvasive E. coli dysentery","Enterohemorrhagic E. coli (EHEC) infection (predominant cause of HUS in industrialized countries)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Inflammatory bowel disease - Crohn's disease","Inflammatory bowel disease - ulcerative colitis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool culture is the gold standard; sample the bloody/mucopurulent portion directly, or use rectal swabs (highest yield in the acute phase).","Because Shigella is fragile and dies during transport, use a holding medium (buffered glycerol saline or Cary-Blair) with prompt inoculation onto isolation media.","Use multiple media to raise yield: nonselective (bromocresol-purple lactose), low-selectivity (MacConkey, eosin-methylene blue), and high-selectivity (Hektoen, Salmonella-Shigella, or xylose-lysine-deoxycholate agar).","Microscopy of stool smears: bacterial enteroinvasive infection (including shigellosis) shows high PMN counts per field, whereas E. histolytica shows erythrophagocytic trophozoites with very few PMNs.","Identify shigellae as non-lactose-fermenting colonies that are glucose-positive (usually no gas), lactose-negative, H2S-negative, and nonmotile; confirm serogroup by slide agglutination.","Blood cultures are positive in fewer than 5% of cases but should be obtained when there is a clinical picture of severe sepsis.","Abdominal x-ray when toxic megacolon is suspected (transverse colon dilation, thumbprinting, loss of haustra, pseudopolyps; pneumoperitoneum if perforated).","In HUS: full blood count and smear (schizocytes), platelets, LDH, free hemoglobin, reticulocytes, creatinine; Coombs test (HUS is Coombs-negative).","PCR for Shigella virulence genes or mass spectrometry are available but not yet standardized for global use."],"redFlags":["Nonbloody diarrhea, moderate-to-severe dehydration, bacteremia, absence of fever, abdominal tenderness, and rectal prolapse are risk factors for death in clinically severe cases.","Altered consciousness (seizures, delirium, coma), especially in children under 5, carries a poor prognosis.","Abdominal distention and tenderness suggesting toxic megacolon, with or without peritonitis.","Pallor, asthenia, irritability, oliguria, increasing edema, or mucosal bleeding heralding hemolytic-uremic syndrome (most often after several days of diarrhea).","Fever persisting beyond 48-72 h in toxic megacolon raises the possibility of local perforation or abscess.","Severe, unusual manifestations such as meningitis in the absence of serious malnutrition may indicate an inborn innate-immune defect (e.g., IRAK-4 deficiency) and warrant genetic investigation."],"prognosis":"Most episodes are self-limited and resolve without treatment within about 1 week; with appropriate therapy recovery occurs within a few days to a week, usually without sequelae. Acute life-threatening complications occur most often in children under 5 (especially the malnourished) and the elderly. In HUS, acute renal failure occurs in 55-70% of cases but renal function recovers in most (up to ~70%). S. dysenteriae type 1 epidemics historically caused high mortality. Shigellosis is a major contributor to stunted growth among children in endemic regions because of the exudative enteropathy and anorexia it causes.","pitfalls":["Avoid antimotility agents in bloody diarrhea: they have been implicated in prolonged fever in shigellosis, are suspected of increasing the risk of toxic megacolon, and were implicated in HUS in children with EHEC infection.","Because shigellosis often manifests only as watery diarrhea, systematic attempts to isolate Shigella are necessary - a watery presentation does not exclude the diagnosis.","Shigella is fragile and commonly disappears during transport with changes in temperature and pH; failure to use a holding medium and prompt inoculation leads to false-negative cultures.","Negative initial blood cultures do not exclude shigellosis (positive in fewer than 5% of cases).","Predisposing factors for toxic megacolon - hypokalemia and use of opioids, anticholinergics, loperamide, psyllium seeds, and antidepressants - should be sought and addressed.","In HUS, oral rehydration solutions and potassium-rich nutrition must be discontinued (water restriction is required), in contrast to routine rehydration management.","Bacteriologic proof in mild adult cases often arrives only after symptoms have resolved, so empiric decisions cannot await culture in fragile patients."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal grading scale. Severity is described as depending on infecting serotype and, more importantly, on host age, immunologic and nutritional status. The clinical course is staged in four phases (incubation, watery diarrhea, dysentery, postinfectious). Severe/complicated disease is recognized by markers of poor outcome (nonbloody diarrhea, moderate-to-severe dehydration, bacteremia, absence of fever, abdominal tenderness, rectal prolapse, altered consciousness) and by major complications: intestinal (toxic megacolon, perforation, rectal prolapse), metabolic (hypoglycemia, hyponatremia, dehydration), and HUS (in S. dysenteriae type 1).","references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, Chapter 172: Shigellosis (Sansonetti PJ, Bergounioux J), pp.1322-1325.","Kotloff KL et al. The incidence, aetiology, and adverse clinical consequences of less severe diarrhoeal episodes among infants and children residing in low-income and middle-income countries (GEMS follow-on). Lancet Glob Health 7:E568, 2019.","WHO. Guidelines for the control of shigellosis, including epidemics due to Shigella dysenteriae type 1.","Niyogi SK. Shigellosis. J Microbiol 43:133, 2005."],"crossLinks":[]},"sickle_cell_disease":{"name":"Sickle Cell Disease","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":103,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.769-776","clinicalPearls":["Sickle cell disease is a clinical/haematologic phenotype caused by an assortment of genotypes; sickle cell anaemia (HbSS, homozygosity for the HbS Glu6Val mutation) is the most common, followed by HbSC disease (compound heterozygosity for HbS and HbC) and the HbS-beta thalassaemias (p.772).","The chief presenting symptom is pain: a hand-foot syndrome (dactylitis) in young children, and typical acute painful episodes in older children and adults (p.772).","Acute painful episodes are the most common acute events; pain is typically unprovoked, severe, in extremities or torso, often symmetrical and stereotypical for each patient, and lasts days to weeks (p.773).","In HbSC disease and HbS-beta+ thalassaemia, acute vaso-occlusive episodes occur at about half the rate of sickle cell anaemia and complications develop later; rarely such patients are asymptomatic (p.772).","Complications fall into two related groups: those driven by sickle vaso-occlusion (pain, acute chest syndrome, osteonecrosis, splenic infarction) and those driven by intravascular haemolysis (stroke, pulmonary/systemic vasculopathy, nephropathy, leg ulcers, gallstones, priapism); vaso-occlusive complications respond best to HbF induction (p.772-773).","Carriers of sickle cell trait (HbAS) outnumber patients with disease ~25 to 1; trait is usually benign with normal life expectancy, but carries a small risk of sudden death from heat-related exertional rhabdomyolysis and complications such as haematuria, papillary necrosis, hyposthenuria, splenic infarction at altitude and increased VTE risk (p.772, 775).","Autosplenectomy from repeated childhood splenic infarction is usual in sickle cell anaemia; a palpable spleen after age 5 suggests a coexisting genotype (e.g. thalassaemia or HbC) (p.769-770 reference and Table 103-2).","Hydroxyurea (acting mainly by inducing HbF) is standard of care for all patients with sickle cell anaemia and HbS-beta0 thalassaemia regardless of symptoms; in adults it roughly halves pain and acute chest syndrome and reduced mortality by 49% over 17.5 years (p.775).","Cure is possible with HLA-identical related-donor allogeneic stem cell transplant (event-free survival >95%, though only ~15% of patients have a fully matched donor) and with two approved ex vivo autologous gene therapies (lentiviral betaT87Q HbA gene; CRISPR/Cas9 disruption of the BCL11A enhancer) (p.775-776)."],"pathophysiology":"HbS behaves like HbA except that it polymerises when deoxygenated: contacts between beta6 valine residues of deoxy-HbS and beta/alpha-globin residues form hemoglobin fascicles that injure the sickle erythrocyte, after a delay between initiation of polymerisation and accumulation of damaging polymer. Polymerisation depends on roughly the 30th power of hemoglobin concentration, so small changes in concentration or cell dehydration have large effects. Membrane vesiculation/microparticle release, activation of dehydrating channels (Gardos, K/Cl cotransport, Psickle) which raise mean corpuscular HbS concentration and reduce deformability, translocation of phosphatidylserine to the outer membrane leaflet, and oxidation produce irreversibly sickled cells (ISCs) that remain deformed regardless of oxygenation. Sickle erythrocytes adhere to endothelium and other blood cells, occluding small and large vessels (vaso-occlusion), and survive <20 days (normal ~120), haemolysing intra- and extravascularly. Intravascular haemolysis depletes haptoglobin and hemopexin and liberates heme, arginase and other DAMPs which scavenge nitric oxide, activate platelets and endothelium, reduce antioxidant activity, cause vasoconstriction and drive inflammation (p.772-774).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Acute chest syndrome (pneumonia-like illness with chest pain, cough, fever, hypoxia and a pulmonary infiltrate) closely resembles and may coexist with bacterial pneumonia; antibiotics are almost always used even though a causative bacterium is often not cultured (p.775).","Multiorgan failure accompanying severe acute chest syndrome is often confused with, and can coexist with, sepsis (p.774, Table 103-3)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["High-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) and hemoglobin electrophoresis to quantify hemoglobin fractions (HbS, HbF, HbA, HbA2, HbC) and distinguish trait, sickle cell anaemia and HbSC disease (Table 103-2, Fig. 103-4, p.772).","Peripheral blood film: irreversibly sickled cells, target cells and nucleated red cells in HbSS/HbS-beta0 thalassaemia; HbC crystals, taco-folded cells and contracted microspherocytes in HbSC disease (Fig. 103-4, p.772-774).","DNA-based diagnosis for genetic counselling and family planning; antenatal diagnosis by chorionic villus sampling or cell-free DNA (p.772, 776).","Transcranial Doppler screening in children (ages 2-16) to identify stroke risk (Table 103-3, p.776).","Echocardiographic measurement of tricuspid regurgitant jet velocity (TRV) annually to screen for pulmonary hypertension (Table 103-3).","Screening for microalbuminuria from age 10 for nephropathy, and annual fluorescein angiography from age 10 for retinopathy (Table 103-3)."],"redFlags":["Acute chest syndrome - chest pain, cough, fever, hypoxia and a pulmonary infiltrate; can be devastating with multilobar involvement, severe hypoxia, ARDS, multiorgan failure and rapid death (often from embolised necrotic marrow) (p.775).","Splenic sequestration (acute anemic episode) - rapid drop in hemoglobin; recurrent episodes are an indication for splenectomy (Table 103-3, p.774).","Aplastic crisis from parvovirus B19 infection causing acute worsening of anaemia with reticulocytopenia (Table 103-3, p.774; reticulocytopenia in acute worsening noted elsewhere).","Stroke and silent infarction - occur in 10-15% of cases, from early childhood into adulthood, with neurocognitive abnormalities even without apparent stroke; haemorrhagic stroke in adults (Table 103-3, p.773).","Functional/anatomic asplenia (autosplenectomy) places patients at high risk of overwhelming infection with encapsulated organisms, notably overwhelming pneumococcal disease (p.776, 769/Table; pneumococcal chapter p.999).","Sudden unexpected death shortly after admission for acute pain (possible arrhythmia or pulmonary embolism); thrombocytopenia, leukocytosis >20,000 and rapidly developing acute anaemia portend severe acute chest syndrome and multiorgan failure (p.773, 775).","Multiorgan failure - can accompany severe acute chest syndrome, affecting CNS, liver, muscle, lung and kidney (Table 103-3, p.774)."],"prognosis":"In the United States childhood death is now rare and the median age of death in sickle cell anaemia is in the fifth or sixth decade; by contrast in high-burden regions such as Nigeria most affected newborns die before age 5. Hydroxyurea reduced mortality by 49% over 17.5 years of follow-up. Genotype modifies severity: HbSC disease and HbS-beta+ thalassaemia have about half the complication rate of HbSS, and coexistent alpha thalassaemia (present in ~30% of HbSS) is associated with fewer strokes and leg ulcers and less pulmonary vascular and renal disease. Allogeneic transplantation and gene therapy offer potential cure (p.772, 775-776).","pitfalls":["No diagnostic test can confirm or refute an acute pain episode; physical examination is often unhelpful and the presence of ISCs and the reticulocyte count are of no diagnostic value (p.773).","Supplemental oxygen is unnecessary unless hypoxia is present, and NSAIDs add little once intravenous opioids are given; overhydration and excessive opioids can compound dyspnoea and hypoxia in acute chest syndrome (p.773, 775).","Opioids should not be dosed on an 'as-needed' schedule - patient-controlled or fixed scheduled dosing with rescue doses is preferred, with monitoring to avoid excessive sedation; many patients are opioid-tolerant and need higher doses (p.773).","Transfusions are both overutilised and underutilised; they should usually be avoided in acute pain episodes and for stable chronic anaemia (p.776).","A palpable spleen after age 5 in sickle cell disease suggests a coexisting genotype rather than simple HbSS (p.769-770)."],"severityClassification":"Severity is largely defined by genotype (Table 103-2): sickle cell trait (HbAS, usually benign); sickle cell anaemia (HbSS, vaso-occlusion- and haemolysis-related complications, Hb ~7-10 g/dL); HbS-beta0 thalassaemia (complication rate similar to HbSS); HbS-beta+ thalassaemia (~half the rate of HbSS depending on percent HbA); HbSC disease (near-asymptomatic to as severe as HbSS, ~half the complication rate, increased retinopathy risk); HbSE (resembles HbS-beta+ thalassaemia, often Asian/Indian ancestry); and HbSS-alpha thalassaemia (phenocopies HbS-beta0 thalassaemia with fewer strokes/leg ulcers and less pulmonary/renal disease). HbF level (influenced by HbS haplotype) modifies severity (p.772).","references":["Harrison 22e p.769-776"],"crossLinks":["pneumococcal_disease"]},"skin_cancer":{"name":"Skin Cancer","system":"Dermatology / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":81,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.598-609","clinicalPearls":["Apply the ABCDE rule to pigmented lesions to detect melanoma early: Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variegation, Diameter >6 mm (pencil-eraser size), and Evolving (any change in size, shape, color, elevation, or new symptoms such as bleeding, itching, or crusting) (p.601).","The 'ugly duckling' sign: any nevus that looks atypical and different from the rest of an individual's nevi should be considered suspicious (p.601).","Melanoma is an aggressive malignancy of melanocytes and accounts for over half of all deaths from skin cancer, even though it is far less common than nonmelanoma skin cancer (p.598).","Basal cell carcinoma is among the most common cancers; its natural history is a slowly enlarging, locally invasive neoplasm with low metastatic potential (~0.1% in immunocompetent patients), though the risk of recurrence or a new primary NMSC is about 40% over 5 years (p.607).","Nodular BCC classically presents as a pearly nodule with tortuous telangiectatic vessels, rolled borders, and a central crust; pigmented BCC may contain melanin and be confused with melanoma (p.606-607).","Squamous cell carcinoma arises from keratinizing epidermal cells on sun-exposed skin and can metastasize (0.3-5.2%), most often to regional lymph nodes; lower-lip and ear tumors metastasize in 13% and 11% respectively (p.606-607).","Actinic keratoses and actinic cheilitis are premalignant forms of SCC; SCC in situ (Bowen's disease) presents as a scaling erythematous plaque, and treating premalignant/in-situ lesions reduces subsequent invasive disease (p.607).","NMSCs (SCC ~80%, BCC ~18% of cases) are the most common cancers in the US, with an estimated >5.3 million cases annually (p.606).","Breslow thickness (greatest tumor thickness measured from the top of the granular layer or ulcer base to the bottom of the tumor) is the best predictor of melanoma recurrence and, with ulceration, defines the T stage (p.601-602)."],"pathophysiology":"Melanoma is a malignancy of melanocytes, neural-crest-derived pigment-producing cells; most melanomas arise in skin but can occur in mucosa, eye, meninges, and upper esophagus. UV radiation is the dominant environmental driver, and cutaneous melanomas carry one of the highest somatic mutation rates of any cancer (>10 mutations/Mb), the majority bearing a UV signature. Key driver mutations affect the MAP-kinase and PI3K/AKT proliferative pathways: BRAF (commonly V600E) in 40-50% of cutaneous melanomas, NRAS in ~20%, and loss-of-function NF1 in 10-15%; germline CDKN2A (p16/ARF, RB and p53 pathways), CDK4, MITF, BAP1, POT1, TERT, and moderate-risk MC1R variants underlie inherited susceptibility. Nonmelanoma skin cancers are also UV-driven: SCC is associated with p53 and N-RAS damage, whereas BCC is driven by hedgehog (Hh) pathway activation, usually loss of function of the tumor-suppressor PTCH1 (which normally inhibits SMO). Merkel cell carcinoma is linked to the oncogenic Merkel cell polyomavirus, present in ~80% of tumors (p.598-607).","additionalDifferentials":["Benign nevomelanocytic nevus (vs melanoma)","Dysplastic/atypical nevus","Seborrheic keratosis","Lentigo","Nummular eczema and psoriasis (may mimic superficial BCC)","Wart or callus (may mimic SCC when inflammation is minimal)","Keratoacanthoma (rapidly growing low-grade SCC variant)","Merkel cell carcinoma","Cutaneous B- or T-cell lymphoma","Extramammary Paget's disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Superficial BCC mimicking nummular eczema or psoriasis","SCC mistaken for a wart or callus when inflammatory response is minimal","Pigmented (nodular) BCC mimicking melanoma","Desmoplastic melanoma mimicking nodular basal cell carcinoma","Benign nevus mimicking melanoma"],"additionalInvestigations":["Excisional biopsy with 1-3 mm narrow margins (preferred) for any changing/suspicious pigmented lesion; incisional (shave, saucerization, or punch) biopsy of the most nodular/darkest area when excision is impractical - incisional biopsy does not facilitate melanoma spread","Histopathology report should include Breslow thickness, mitotic rate, ulceration, lymphatic/vascular/neural invasion, regression, microsatellitosis, and margin status","Dermoscopy (low-level magnification with polarized light or water interface) and total-body photography for surveillance in high-risk patients","Genetic expression profiles (GEP, e.g. 31-gene), FISH with multiple probes, or comparative genomic hybridization to distinguish difficult melanomas from benign nevi and estimate prognosis","Complete blood count, complete metabolic panel, and serum LDH (LDH is part of AJCC stage IV criteria)","Sentinel lymph node biopsy for pathologic staging","CT, PET, or combined CT/PET (and MRI) when symptoms, exam, or abnormal labs suggest metastatic disease","Genetic testing for individuals with >=3 primary melanomas or with families having >=1 invasive melanoma plus >=2 cases of melanoma and/or pancreatic cancer, ocular melanoma, mesothelioma, or renal cell carcinoma on one side"],"redFlags":["Any pigmented lesion that has changed (size, shape, color, elevation) or that meets ABCDE criteria is a candidate for biopsy","An 'ugly duckling' nevus that differs from a patient's other nevi","New bleeding, itching, or crusting in a pigmented lesion","Thick and/or ulcerated melanoma (key adverse T-stage features)","Microsatellite lesions, in-transit metastases, nodal involvement, elevated LDH, or distant metastases to brain/liver/bone","SCC on the lower lip or ear, recurrent SCC (~30% metastatic risk), or SCC in scars/chronic ulcers, genital/mucosal surfaces, or in immunosuppressed patients (aggressive behavior)","Rapidly expanding lesion in an older, immunosuppressed, UV-exposed patient (MCC: AEIOU - Asymptomatic, Expanding rapidly, Immunosuppression, Older than 50, UV-exposed site)"],"prognosis":"Melanoma prognosis is dominated by Breslow thickness and ulceration (T stage); favorable primary sites are the forearm and leg, unfavorable sites the scalp, hands, feet, and mucous membranes. 10-year melanoma-specific survival ranges from >99% for stage 0 (in situ) and ~98% for stage IA down to ~24-30% for stage IIID. Women with stage I/II disease and younger patients do better; older men have worse outcomes (delayed diagnosis, thicker tumors, more head/neck, acral and BRAF wild-type tumors). High mitotic rate and lymphatic/vascular invasion are additional adverse factors. Overall melanoma mortality has been falling, attributed to immunotherapy and targeted therapy. BCC rarely metastasizes (~0.1% in immunocompetent patients) but recurs frequently. Cutaneous SCC metastasizes in 0.3-5.2% of cases overall, higher for lip/ear/recurrent/immunosuppressed disease. Merkel cell carcinoma is aggressive: ~90% cure with local disease but ~52% survival with nodal and ~10% with distant disease (p.602-609).","pitfalls":["Morpheaform, infiltrative, and micronodular BCCs have indistinct borders and subtle presentation, so treatment is often delayed and tumors are more extensive than they appear clinically","SCC can be mistaken for a benign wart or callus when the inflammatory response is minimal","Pigmented BCC may be confused with melanoma; desmoplastic melanoma can mimic nodular BCC","Melanoma frequently develops on non-sun-exposed skin (acral, mucosal); in dark-skinned, Hispanic, and East Asian populations melanoma is often non-UV-related, diagnosed at higher stage, and has worse outcomes","The 31-gene GEP can estimate distant-relapse probability but has not supplanted prognostic estimates from surgical staging, and genomic pathway data are not yet incorporated into AJCC staging","Standard labs and imaging rarely detect occult metastatic disease in early melanoma and should be directed by symptoms/exam findings"],"severityClassification":"Melanoma is staged by the AJCC TNM system (T stage built primarily on Breslow thickness and ulceration; pathologic stage incorporates sentinel lymph node biopsy results), from stage 0 (in situ, Tis) through stage IV (distant metastasis); 10-year melanoma-specific survival is depicted by stage in Table 81-3. Merkel cell carcinoma has its own TNM staging incorporating tumor size (<2 cm vs >2 cm), nodal status, and distant metastases (p.602-609).","references":["Harrison 22e p.598-609"],"crossLinks":[]},"sleep_apnea":{"name":"Sleep Apnea","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":308,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2277-2282","clinicalPearls":["OSA diagnosis requires either symptoms of nocturnal breathing disturbance (snoring, snorting, gasping, breathing pauses) impairing quality of life or unexplained daytime sleepiness/fatigue, PLUS at least 5 obstructive apnoeas/hypopnoeas per hour of sleep (apnoea-hypopnoea index, AHI) on a sleep study; OSA may also be diagnosed without symptoms if AHI is at least 15 episodes/h (p.2277-2278).","Each apnoea/hypopnoea is a reduction in breathing for at least 10 s, commonly causing a fall in oxygen saturation of at least 3% or a cortical arousal (p.2278).","The major risk factors for OSA are obesity, male sex, and older age; roughly 40-60% of cases are attributable to excess weight, but the absence of obesity does NOT exclude the diagnosis (p.2278).","Snoring is the most common symptom, but its absence does NOT exclude OSA because pharyngeal collapse can occur without tissue vibration; the most common daytime symptom is excessive sleepiness, though women preferentially report fatigue rather than sleepiness (p.2279).","Dyspnoea is unusual in OSA, and its absence generally distinguishes OSA from paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea, nocturnal asthma, and acid reflux with laryngospasm (p.2279).","Overnight in-laboratory polysomnography (PSG) is the gold standard for diagnosis; a negative in-lab PSG usually rules out OSA, but false-negatives can occur with night-to-night variation, insufficient REM sleep, or less supine sleep than typical (p.2279).","Home sleep tests recording only respiratory and cardiac channels are a cost-effective option; when prior probability of OSA is high, a negative home study should be followed by PSG (p.2279).","CPAP is the standard medical therapy with the highest level of evidence; it acts as a mechanical splint to hold the airway open during sleep, and auto-titrating (APAP) home devices have eliminated the need for titration studies in many patients (p.2281).","Although CPAP reduces 24-h ambulatory blood pressure, the overall effect is modest (averaging 2-4 mmHg), with larger improvements in patients with high AHI, daytime sleepiness, or resistant hypertension (p.2280-2281).","Large randomized trials have FAILED to show that CPAP reduces cardiovascular/stroke events or prolongs survival, possibly because sleepy patients (who may have the greatest risk) were excluded and because of limited treatment adherence (p.2281).","Central sleep apnoea (CSA) is less common than OSA and may occur alone or, more often, combined with obstructive events as mixed apnoeas; it is often driven by increased sensitivity to PCO2 causing unstable breathing, and a prolonged circulation delay (as in congestive heart failure) produces the crescendo-decrescendo Cheyne-Stokes pattern (p.2282).","Treatment of CSA has NOT been shown to improve long-term health outcomes, so specific treatment is generally indicated only for symptomatic CSA; unlike OSA, treatment is difficult and depends on the underlying cause (p.2282)."],"pathophysiology":"In OSA the pharyngeal airway lacks fixed bony/cartilaginous support, so patency depends on pharyngeal dilator muscles. Inspiration generates increasingly negative intraluminal pressure (a suctioning force); at sleep onset neuromuscular output to these muscles declines, and in a collapsible airway this causes transient pharyngeal collapse (apnoea) or near-collapse (hypopnoea), typically terminated by an arousal that restores muscle activity. Collapse sites include the soft palate (most common), tongue base, lateral pharyngeal walls, and epiglottis. OSA is often worst in REM sleep (lowest skeletal-muscle output) and supine (gravity). A small pharyngeal lumen (soft-tissue enlargement from fat, lymphoid tissue, or genetic variation; craniofacial factors such as mandibular retroposition/micrognathia), low lung volume (reduced caudal pharyngeal traction, pronounced in obesity), and high nasal resistance all predispose to collapse. Pharyngeal muscle activation is linked to ventilatory drive: an over-sensitive ventilatory control system, a low arousal threshold (preempting CO2-mediated muscle compensation), or a high arousal threshold (prolonging apnoeas) all contribute. In OSA-related disease, sleep fragmentation, cortical arousals, and intermittent hypoxaemia/hypercapnia drive sympathetic overactivity, blood-pressure surges, a prothrombotic/proinflammatory state, insulin resistance, and large negative intrathoracic pressure swings that alter cardiac preload/afterload and promote cardiac/electrical remodelling. CSA is usually caused by increased PCO2 sensitivity producing hyperventilation alternating with apnoea, plus prolonged circulatory delay (heart failure) giving Cheyne-Stokes breathing.","additionalDifferentials":["Narcolepsy (multiple sleep latency / maintenance of wakefulness testing helps distinguish from OSA)","Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea","Nocturnal asthma","Acid reflux with laryngospasm","Insomnia (common in the general population, may coexist with OSA)","Obesity-hypoventilation / hypoventilation syndromes","Periodic limb movement disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea","Nocturnal asthma","Acid reflux with laryngospasm","Narcolepsy","Cheyne-Stokes / central sleep apnoea of heart failure"],"additionalInvestigations":["Overnight in-laboratory polysomnography (PSG) - gold standard","Home sleep test (respiratory and cardiac channels) for cost-effective diagnosis","Apnoea-hypopnoea index (AHI) and respiratory disturbance index (RDI) quantification","Overnight oximetry profile (mean, nadir, time at low saturation)","Multiple sleep latency test / maintenance of wakefulness test (quantify sleepiness, distinguish narcolepsy)","Upper-airway imaging (cephalometry, MRI, CT) and fibreoptic endoscopy to identify anatomic risk factors (not routine; useful for surgical planning)","Cardiac evaluation for systolic/diastolic dysfunction or structural abnormality","Overnight blood-pressure monitoring (may show nondipping pattern)","Arterial blood gas (usually normal awake; waking hypoxaemia/hypercarbia suggests cardiopulmonary disease or hypoventilation)","Drug-induced sleep endoscopy (for hypoglossal-nerve stimulation candidacy)"],"redFlags":["Waking hypoxaemia or hypercarbia, suggesting coexisting cardiopulmonary disease or a hypoventilation syndrome","Evidence of cor pulmonale, suggesting a comorbid cardiopulmonary condition (OSA alone is NOT thought to cause right-heart failure)","Resistant or uncontrolled hypertension","Excessive daytime sleepiness with occupational accident or motor-vehicle-crash risk (drowsy driving)","Coexisting heart failure (raises risk of both OSA and CSA)","Severe nocturnal hypoxaemia with elevated haemoglobin"],"prognosis":"OSA is the most common medical cause of daytime sleepiness and reduces quality of life. It is strongly associated with cardiac, cerebrovascular and metabolic disease (coronary artery disease, heart failure with and without reduced EF, atrial and ventricular arrhythmias, atherosclerosis, stroke, diabetes), premature death, and increased risk of certain cancers. Untreated OSA carries a roughly twofold risk of occupational accidents and a two- to threefold increase in motor-vehicle crashes. CPAP improves alertness, mood, quality of life, blood pressure and insulin sensitivity, but randomized trials have NOT shown that CPAP reduces cardiovascular/stroke events or prolongs survival. CSA carries increased risk for heart failure and atrial fibrillation, and its treatment has not been shown to improve long-term outcomes; adaptive servoventilation showed no cardiovascular or mortality benefit and increased mortality in patients with LVEF below 45%.","pitfalls":["Assuming absence of snoring excludes OSA - pharyngeal collapse can occur without tissue vibration.","Assuming a normal weight excludes OSA - absence of obesity does NOT exclude the diagnosis.","Underrecognising OSA in women, who have a lower arousal threshold, shorter and more REM-predominant apnoeas, and tend to report fatigue/insomnia rather than classic sleepiness.","Relying on symptoms/signs to gauge severity - they do not accurately predict the severity of sleep-related breathing disturbance; objective measurement is required.","Treating a single negative home sleep study as definitive when prior probability is high - follow with PSG.","Attributing right-heart failure/cor pulmonale to OSA alone, which is not thought to cause it - look for a comorbid cardiopulmonary condition.","Using adaptive servoventilation in patients with CSA and LVEF below 45% (FDA warning following increased mortality).","Sedative-hypnotics have inconsistent effects and should be avoided in most patients with moderate-to-severe OSA; opioids worsen CSA in a dose-dependent way."],"severityClassification":"OSA severity by apnoea-hypopnoea index (AHI = apnoeas plus hypopnoeas per hour of sleep): mild OSAHS = AHI 5-14 events/h; moderate OSAHS = AHI 15-29 events/h; severe OSAHS = AHI at least 30 events/h. The respiratory disturbance index (RDI) additionally counts respiratory effort-related arousals (RERAs). Each AHI level can be further qualified by the degree of sleepiness and associated hypoxaemia (Table 308-2, p.2280).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2277-2282"],"crossLinks":["obesity","heart_failure","pulmonary_hypertension","atrial_fib"]},"spondyloarthritis":{"name":"Spondyloarthritis","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":374,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2879-2892","clinicalPearls":["Spondyloarthritis (SpA) is a family of immune-mediated inflammatory arthritides sharing clinical, genetic, and pathologic features; classified by ASAS (2009) into axial (axSpA) and peripheral (pSpA). Axial skeletal involvement is hinted at in the name but is NOT mandatory (Harrison 22e p.2879).","axSpA is subdivided into radiographic (r-axSpA, = ankylosing spondylitis [AS]) and nonradiographic (nr-axSpA), the difference based on presence/absence of definitive sacroiliitis on plain radiographs; the two share the same clinical features and disease burden (Harrison 22e p.2880, 2883).","pSpA encompasses psoriatic arthritis (PsA), reactive arthritis (ReA), IBD-associated arthritis, and undifferentiated pSpA; SAPHO, acne-associated, and hidradenitis suppurativa-associated arthritis are sometimes included (Harrison 22e p.2880).","HLA-B27 is the strongest genetic risk factor: present in 85-90% of r-axSpA and 50-90% of nr-axSpA, versus ~5% of the healthy Caucasian population (Harrison 22e p.2881).","Inflammatory back pain (IBP): insidious onset, age <45, chronic (>3 months), improves with exercise/activity but NOT with rest, worse in second half of night with improvement after getting up, and prolonged (>30 min) morning stiffness; this distinguishes it from mechanical back pain (Harrison 22e p.2883).","Characteristic SpA pattern is asymmetric mono-/oligoarticular (<=4 joints) inflammatory arthritis of large lower-limb joints (usually knee) with enthesitis, tendonitis, and dactylitis (sausage digits), contrasting with the symmetric polyarticular MCP/PIP involvement of rheumatoid arthritis (Harrison 22e p.2879).","Hips are the most common nonspinal ('root') joints affected in axSpA; peripheral arthritis occurs in ~30-40% of axSpA patients (Harrison 22e p.2883).","Uveitis is the most common extramusculoskeletal manifestation (~40% of axSpA), typically acute, anterior, unilateral, and episodic; other extra-articular features include psoriasis (~10%) and IBD (frank IBD ~10%, subclinical gut inflammation up to 50%) (Harrison 22e p.2883-2884).","Enthesitis (inflammation at tendon/ligament insertions) is common in axSpA, presenting as heel pain (plantar fasciitis, Achilles insertion), chest wall pain, epicondylitis, or pelvic rim pain; dactylitis is relatively rare in axSpA but common in PsA (Harrison 22e p.2883).","Persistent axial inflammation leads to bony fusion of sacroiliac and apophyseal joints and syndesmophyte formation; complete ankylosis of the spine is termed 'bamboo spine,' seen in only a very small percentage of late-stage AS patients (Harrison 22e p.2880, 2884).","Typical SpA syndesmophytes are vertically oriented, distinguishing them from the horizontal osteophytes of spinal osteoarthritis; PsA syndesmophytes are often chunky and nonmarginal (Harrison 22e p.2884, 2889).","Reactive arthritis follows infectious gastroenteritis (Salmonella, Shigella, Yersinia, Campylobacter) or genitourinary Chlamydia; classic features include urethritis, conjunctivitis, mucosal ulcers, and keratoderma blennorrhagica (Harrison 22e p.2880).","IBD-associated arthritis: r-axSpA develops in up to 10% of IBD patients and pSpA in 10-30%; conversely axSpA patients have a 5-10% risk of developing IBD. HLA-B27 is NOT a risk factor for IBD itself (Harrison 22e p.2891-2892)."],"pathophysiology":"SpA is a prototypic chronic immune-mediated inflammatory disease that couples inflammation to structural damage, with the unique feature that inflammation-induced bone loss coexists with pathologic new bone formation at specific skeletal sites (sacroiliac joints, anterior spinal ligaments, peripheral entheses). HLA-B27, an MHC class I allele, is the strongest genetic risk factor; proposed mechanisms include the arthritogenic peptide theory (HLA-B27 presenting microbial peptides to CD8+ T cells with molecular mimicry), HLA-B27 homodimerization engaging innate receptors (KIR3DL2) to drive IL-17, and unfolded protein response/autophagy promoting IL-23-driven type 17 immunity. Disease requires additional factors beyond HLA-B27 (only ~5% of HLA-B27-positive people, and 20% of HLA-B27-positive first-degree relatives, develop axSpA); key instigators are loss of intestinal barrier integrity with microbial dysbiosis (germ-free HLA-B27 transgenic rats fail to develop disease) and aberrant response to mechanical stress. The IL-23/IL-17 axis and TNF-alpha are central, with overlapping yet domain-distinct cytokine signatures across gut, joint, entheses, and skin (Harrison 22e p.2880-2882).","additionalDifferentials":["Mechanical (nonspecific) back pain - the etiology in 95-96% of chronic back pain; axSpA accounts for only 4-5% (Harrison 22e p.2885)","Fibromyalgia / central sensitization (especially mistaken for SpA in women, delaying diagnosis) (Harrison 22e p.2885)","Diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis (DISH) - noninflammatory, flowing osteophytes over >=4 contiguous vertebrae, normal sacroiliac joints, in obese diabetic males >50; can be mistaken for bamboo spine (Harrison 22e p.2886)","Osteitis condensans ilii (OCI) - asymptomatic triangular iliac-side sclerosis in multiparous women; lack of erosions distinguishes from axSpA (Harrison 22e p.2886)","Osteoarthritis of the spine - horizontal osteophytes vs vertical syndesmophytes (Harrison 22e p.2884)","Rheumatoid arthritis - symmetric polyarticular MCP/PIP disease, RF/nodules present (Harrison 22e p.2879)","Hypothyroidism and hypovitaminosis D in young patients with generalized body pain (Harrison 22e p.2886)"],"infectionMimics":["Septic sacroiliitis from tuberculosis, brucellosis, fungi, or other infectious agents - can cause inflammatory back pain with sclerotic sacroiliac changes (Harrison 22e p.2886)","Osteomyelitis (consider with red-flag features) (Harrison 22e p.2886)","Infectious enterocolitis-like acute gut inflammation seen histologically in SpA terminal ileum (Harrison 22e p.2891)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Postpartum sacral insufficiency fracture (Harrison 22e p.2886)","Osteoporotic vertebral fracture (Harrison 22e p.2886)","Metastatic disease / malignancy, including acute lymphoblastic leukemia presenting with inflammatory back pain and sacroiliac sclerosis (Harrison 22e p.2886)","MRI osteitis-like changes from mechanical stress in athletes, postpartum women, and normal individuals >40 (risk of overdiagnosis) (Harrison 22e p.2885)","Calcium pyrophosphate deposition disease (CPPD) and gout (may coexist with/mimic PsA) (Harrison 22e p.2889)","SAPHO and hidradenitis suppurativa (may mimic palmoplantar psoriasis/PsA) (Harrison 22e p.2889)"],"additionalInvestigations":["HLA-B27 typing - supportive but neither necessary nor sufficient for diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2884)","ESR and CRP - elevated in only 30-40% of active axSpA; neither sensitive nor specific, but useful for monitoring (Harrison 22e p.2884, 2888)","Plain AP or Ferguson pelvic radiograph for sacroiliitis (multiple oblique views add little and increase gonadal radiation) (Harrison 22e p.2884)","MRI of sacroiliac joints/spine - detects active osteitis/bone marrow edema (STIR) and structural lesions (sclerosis, fat metaplasia, erosions, backfill, ankylosis) early; not indicated for routine monitoring (Harrison 22e p.2885, 2888)","Low-dose CT of sacroiliac joints/spine - emerging alternative to radiography for diagnosis and progression (Harrison 22e p.2885)","Synovial fluid analysis in monoarthritis - WBC >2000/mL with negative crystals/Gram stain/culture suggests inflammatory arthritis; rules out septic and crystal arthritis (Harrison 22e p.2888)","Fecal calprotectin (elevated with microscopic gut inflammation in SpA) (Harrison 22e p.2891)","Disease activity indices: BASDAI, ASDAS; function: BASFI; metrology: BASMI (Harrison 22e p.2886)"],"redFlags":["Fever, weight loss, advanced age, or past malignancy in a back-pain patient - prompt evaluation for osteomyelitis, osteoporotic fracture, or metastatic disease (Harrison 22e p.2886)","Cauda equina syndrome (urinary hesitancy and/or saddle anesthesia) - late axSpA complication (Harrison 22e p.2884)","Cardiac conduction abnormalities (heart block) at any stage; aortic valve insufficiency as a late complication (Harrison 22e p.2884)","AA (renal) amyloidosis causing nephrotic syndrome after prolonged uncontrolled inflammation; IgA nephropathy with microscopic hematuria (Harrison 22e p.2884)","Spinal fractures in ankylosed (bamboo) spine - consider if back pain persists/relapses despite therapy (Harrison 22e p.2888)","Atlantoaxial subluxation (rare, in PsA) with potentially serious neurologic consequences (Harrison 22e p.2889)","Acute anterior uveitis (eye pain, redness, photophobia, miosis) requiring urgent ophthalmologic care (Harrison 22e p.2884)"],"prognosis":"Course is variable: ~50% of patients progress from nonradiographic to radiographic stage over 20 years, but <10% progress to significant spinal involvement with bamboo spine. Risk factors for osteoproliferation (worse structural outcome) include male sex, persistent inflammation (MRI sacroiliac/spine activity, high CRP), existing syndesmophytes, HLA-B27 positivity, and smoking. With increased awareness and earlier treatment, structural damage has steadily decreased over recent decades, with a shift toward higher prevalence of nr-axSpA over r-axSpA. axSpA significantly impairs well-being, function, productivity, and quality of life. Reactive arthritis is often self-limiting but may become chronic, with HLA-B27 linked to chronicity (Harrison 22e p.2880, 2884, 2891).","pitfalls":["PITFALL: confusing inflammatory back pain with common mechanical back pain - mechanical causes must be considered first since axSpA is the etiology in only 4-5% of chronic back pain; conversely IBP improves with exercise and worsens with rest, the reverse of mechanical pain (Harrison 22e p.2883, 2885).","The term 'inflammatory back pain' is itself a partial misnomer - only ~15% of patients with IBP have demonstrable axial inflammation (Harrison 22e p.2883).","Diagnostic delay of 5-14 years is common, worse in women (back pain/enthesitis mistaken for fibromyalgia) and in nonwhite populations with lower HLA-B27 prevalence (Harrison 22e p.2884).","Interreader reliability for distinguishing r-axSpA from nr-axSpA on radiographs is poor (kappa 0.35-0.45), even between trained musculoskeletal radiologists (Harrison 22e p.2883).","MRI overdiagnosis: osteitis-type and fat-metaplasia changes occur from mechanical stress in athletes, postpartum women, degenerative arthritis, and normal individuals >40 (Harrison 22e p.2885).","Normal/negative inflammatory markers do NOT exclude axSpA - ESR/CRP elevated in only 30-40% of active disease (Harrison 22e p.2884).","Plain spine radiographs should generally be avoided in chronic back pain; multiple oblique sacroiliac views add little but increase gonadal radiation (Harrison 22e p.2884).","Avoid glucocorticoid injection around lower-extremity weight-bearing entheses due to risk of tendon rupture; in IBD-associated arthritis anti-IL17 therapy is contraindicated and NSAIDs may trigger IBD flares (Harrison 22e p.2890-2892)."],"severityClassification":"axSpA is classified as radiographic (r-axSpA / ankylosing spondylitis) vs nonradiographic (nr-axSpA) using definitive sacroiliitis on plain radiographs per the modified New York criteria: definitive = grade 2 bilateral OR grade 3-4 unilateral or bilateral (r-axSpA); lower-grade sacroiliitis = nr-axSpA. ASAS criteria further split SpA into axial (axSpA) and peripheral (pSpA). Diagnosis of axSpA in chronic back pain follows the schema of Figure 374-2 (definite sacroiliitis on x-ray -> AS; otherwise count SpA features >=4 or compelling clinical picture plus MRI sacroiliitis/HLA-B27 -> nr-axSpA). Disease activity graded with BASDAI/ASDAS; PsA classified with CASPAR criteria, activity by DAPSA/MDA (Harrison 22e p.2883-2884, 2889).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2879-2892","Harrison 22e ch.374 (Deodhar & Elewaut)"],"crossLinks":[]},"staphylococcal_infections":{"name":"Staphylococcal Infections","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":152,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1195; cross-referenced material on pp.389, 402, 967, 974-975, 581, 993-995","clinicalPearls":["Staphylococcus aureus causes a wide spectrum of disease, ranging from localized skin and soft-tissue infection to invasive bloodstream, endocardial, and bone infection and to toxin-mediated syndromes; methicillin-resistant S. aureus (MRSA) is now common in both community and health-care settings (p.1195, p.389).","Furunculosis (boils) is caused by S. aureus and has risen in prominence with community-acquired MRSA (CA-MRSA); patients frequently mistake furuncles for spider or insect bites, and family members or close contacts may also be affected (p.389).","Whenever possible, send lesional material from staphylococcal skin infection for culture; incision and drainage may be adequate therapy for a small solitary furuncle without surrounding cellulitis or systemic symptoms (p.389).","Impetigo is caused most often by S. aureus (and sometimes group A streptococci) and forms a characteristic honey-colored crust; bullous impetigo, with tense clear bullae, is produced by S. aureus exfoliative toxin (phage type II) (p.389).","In staphylococcal scalded-skin syndrome (SSSS) the site of staphylococcal infection is usually extracutaneous (e.g., conjunctivitis, rhinorrhea, otitis media, pharyngitis) and the skin lesions themselves are sterile, whereas in bullous impetigo the skin lesions are the actual site of infection (p.402).","Toxic shock syndrome (TSS) is defined by clinical criteria — fever, diffuse macular (sunburn-type) erythroderma, hypotension, and multiorgan involvement — with three criteria involving mucocutaneous sites (diffuse skin erythema, desquamation of palms and soles 1-2 weeks after onset, and mucous-membrane hyperemia of the vagina, oropharynx, or conjunctivae) (p.402, p.993).","In staphylococcal TSS, the vagina or a postoperative wound is typically colonized rather than overtly infected, and mucosal areas appear hyperemic but not infected; this contrasts with streptococcal TSS, which is more often linked to overt skin/soft-tissue infection and bacteremia (p.993).","Acute bacterial endocarditis from S. aureus follows a far more aggressive course than subacute endocarditis, can attack previously normal native valves, and native-valve S. aureus (including MRSA) endocarditis is increasing in frequency (p.994).","Coagulase-negative staphylococci are skin commensals; a single positive blood-culture bottle for skin flora (especially one taking >24 h to turn positive) should raise suspicion of contamination or catheter colonization rather than true bloodstream infection, and cultures should be repeated from both central and peripheral sites (p.581)."],"pathophysiology":"S. aureus expresses cell wall-anchored adhesins called MSCRAMMs (microbial surface components recognizing adhesive matrix molecules, e.g., SdrC) that mediate binding to host extracellular-matrix proteins and underpin adherence and colonization (p.967). Like other extracellular pathogens it can form biofilms, which contribute to device- and prosthesis-associated infection (p.967). Disease is driven partly by potent secreted toxins. Superantigens — including toxic shock syndrome toxin-1 (TSST-1) and the staphylococcal enterotoxins — bind invariant regions of MHC class II molecules and the T-cell receptor, nonspecifically activating vast numbers of T cells and triggering a cytokine storm responsible for fever, rash, and the shock of TSS (p.974, p.402). S. aureus also produces an erythrotoxin causing scarlatiniform rash and exfoliative toxin (\"exfoliatin\") that cleaves the superficial epidermis to produce bullous impetigo and SSSS (p.402, p.389). It elaborates several pore-forming toxins — leukocidins, alpha- and gamma-hemolysins, and phenol-soluble modulins — that lyse host cells, protect the organism from phagocytosis, and impair development of protective adaptive immunity (p.975). Methicillin resistance results from acquisition of the mobile mecA gene carried on the SCCmec cassette, which has spread widely through the S. aureus population (p.1195).","additionalDifferentials":["Group A streptococcal infection (overlaps with staphylococci in impetigo, cellulitis, necrotizing fasciitis, and toxic shock syndrome) (p.389, p.993)","Streptococcal toxic shock syndrome (more often with overt soft-tissue infection/necrotizing fasciitis and bacteremia, less prominent exanthem) (p.993)","Toxic epidermal necrolysis and severe drug eruptions (in the differential of widespread skin blistering versus SSSS) (p.389, p.402)","Kawasaki disease and scarlet fever (other causes of scarlatiniform rash with palm/sole desquamation) (p.402)"],"infectionMimics":["Streptococcal toxic shock syndrome","Scarlet fever (group A streptococcal erythrogenic toxin)","Clostridium sordellii toxic shock (childbirth-associated or black-tar heroin injection; fever typically absent)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Toxic epidermal necrolysis / severe drug eruption (mimics SSSS)","Kawasaki disease (polymorphous rash, conjunctival injection, desquamation; clues are cervical lymphadenopathy, cheilitis, thrombocytosis)","SARS-CoV-2-associated multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children"],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood cultures, repeated from both central and peripheral sites, to confirm bacteremia and distinguish true infection from skin-flora contamination/catheter colonization (differential time-to-positivity can localize a catheter source) (p.581)","Frozen section of the blister roof or exfoliative cytology of blister contents to distinguish SSSS from toxic epidermal necrolysis rapidly (p.402)","Culture of lesional material from skin/soft-tissue infection to guide therapy and detect MRSA (p.389)","mecA gene detection as the genotypic basis of methicillin resistance, enabling rapid genotypic MRSA identification (p.1195)","Double-disk diffusion (D-zone) test to detect inducible clindamycin resistance when an isolate is erythromycin-resistant but clindamycin-susceptible (p.383)","Echocardiography and a search for metastatic foci in S. aureus bacteremia to evaluate for endocarditis (p.994)"],"redFlags":["S. aureus bacteremia warrants an active search for infective endocarditis and metastatic/seeded foci, because any primary focus can give rise to bloodstream infection with secondary seeding (p.994).","In TSS, rapid hypotension developing within hours of symptom onset, and early renal failure preceding hypotension, are warning features (early renal failure helps distinguish TSS from other septic shock syndromes) (p.993).","Acute S. aureus endocarditis: a changing murmur, congestive heart failure, rapid valvular destruction (especially aortic) with pulmonary edema and hypotension, myocardial abscess with conduction block/arrhythmia, and large friable vegetations causing arterial emboli, metastatic infection, or stroke (p.994).","Widespread skin blistering or radial perioral crusting with diffuse tenderness suggests SSSS and demands prompt evaluation (p.402).","S. aureus among organisms that can present as intractable hypotension/septic shock with a primary soft-tissue focus (p.993)."],"prognosis":"Outcome varies sharply by syndrome. For TSS, reported mortality is about 5% for menstruation-associated and 10-15% for nonmenstrual disease (compared with 30-70% for streptococcal TSS) (p.993). Acute bacterial endocarditis, including native-valve S. aureus/MRSA disease, carries mortality of roughly 10-40%, and older patients with S. aureus endocarditis often present with nonspecific symptoms that delay diagnosis and worsen prognosis (p.994). Rapid recognition and intervention are crucial across invasive staphylococcal syndromes.","pitfalls":["Attributing a single positive blood culture for coagulase-negative staphylococci to true bacteremia when it may represent skin-flora contamination or catheter colonization — repeat paired cultures and assess time-to-positivity (p.581).","Assuming a primary skin focus is present in staphylococcal TSS; colonization (vaginal or wound) without overt infection is typical, so the portal may be subtle (p.993).","Missing the diagnosis of S. aureus endocarditis in older patients, whose nonspecific presentation delays recognition (p.994).","Confusing SSSS with toxic epidermal necrolysis or severe drug eruption; SSSS occurs mainly in infants/toddlers, is more superficial, spares the oral mucosa, and is toxin- rather than drug-mediated (p.402).","Inducible clindamycin resistance can cause treatment failure in apparently clindamycin-susceptible isolates unless the double-disk (D-zone) test is performed (p.383)."],"severityClassification":"No single graded severity scale is presented; staphylococcal disease is categorized by clinical syndrome and invasiveness — superficial skin/soft-tissue infection (impetigo, furunculosis), toxin-mediated syndromes (TSS, SSSS, scarlatiniform eruption), and invasive disease (bacteremia, infective endocarditis, osteomyelitis, septic intracranial/epidural infection). TSS is defined by formal clinical criteria (fever, diffuse erythroderma, hypotension, multiorgan involvement with mucocutaneous features) (p.402, p.993).","references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e, Ch.152 Staphylococcal Infections, p.1195","Harrison's 22e, Ch.60 Eczema, Psoriasis, Cutaneous Infections..., p.389 (impetigo, bullous impetigo, SSSS, furunculosis)","Harrison's 22e, Ch.61 (cutaneous manifestations: SSSS vs TEN, TSS, scarlatiniform eruption), p.402","Harrison's 22e, Ch.125 Molecular Mechanisms of Microbial Pathogenesis, pp.967, 974-975 (MSCRAMMs, biofilms, superantigens, pore-forming toxins)","Harrison's 22e, Ch.127 Approach to the Acutely Ill Infected Febrile Patient, pp.993-995 (TSS, acute bacterial endocarditis)","Harrison's 22e, Ch.79 Infections in Patients with Cancer, p.581 (coagulase-negative staphylococci, CLABSI vs contamination)"],"crossLinks":[]},"streptococcal_infections":{"name":"Streptococcal Infections","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":153,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1206-1214","clinicalPearls":["Group A Streptococcus (GAS; S. pyogenes) is the only common cause of sore throat that should be treated with antibiotics; GAS accounts for 20-40% of exudative pharyngitis in children and is rare under age 3 (p.1208).","Streptococcal pharyngitis cannot be reliably diagnosed on clinical grounds alone given overlap with many viral and bacterial causes; throat culture remains the diagnostic gold standard, with rapid antigen tests (specificity >95%) a useful adjunct (p.1208).","Antibiotic treatment of GAS pharyngitis is given primarily to prevent suppurative complications and acute rheumatic fever; symptoms resolve in 3-5 days and are shortened little by treatment (p.1208).","Prevention of ARF depends on eradicating the organism from the pharynx (not just symptom resolution) and requires a 10-day penicillin course; a single dose or short course is inadequate (p.1208).","Penicillin treatment of streptococcal pharyngitis reduces the likelihood of ARF but NOT of post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (p.1209).","Worldwide, GAS infections and their post-infectious sequelae (chiefly ARF and rheumatic heart disease) cause an estimated 500,000 deaths/year; incidence of GAS disease and RHD is roughly tenfold higher in resource-limited countries (p.1206-1207).","Scarlet fever is streptococcal infection (usually pharyngitis) plus a rash from pyrogenic exotoxins: blanching sandpaper-textured papular rash on trunk and extremities sparing palms and soles, with circumoral pallor, strawberry tongue, and Pastia's lines in skin folds, followed by desquamation of palms and soles (p.1209).","Impetigo is most often a superficial GAS infection (occasionally other streptococci or S. aureus) of young children; honey/amber crusted lesions on face and legs; lesions are generally NOT painful and fever is NOT a feature — if fever is present, suspect deeper infection or another diagnosis (p.1209-1210).","Erysipelas is a streptococcal cellulitis of the superficial dermis: bright red, warm, shiny, edematous plaque with a sharply demarcated raised border and peau d'orange texture, typically on the face (malar) or lower extremities, with abrupt onset, fever and chills; classic cases are almost always due to beta-hemolytic streptococci (usually GAS) (p.1210).","Streptococcal cellulitis favors sites of disrupted lymphatic drainage (prior cellulitis, post-mastectomy/axillary dissection arm, prior DVT or lymphedema leg, saphenous vein harvest site); treating tinea pedis can stop recurrent leg cellulitis after vein harvest (p.1210-1211).","GAS is among the few pathogens producing wound infection and surrounding cellulitis within the first 24 h after surgery, with thin exudate and rapid spread (p.1211).","Necrotizing fasciitis is a surgical emergency: GAS is implicated in ~60% of cases; early surgical exploration and extensive debridement are diagnostic, therapeutic, and life-saving, with antibiotics as a critical adjunct (p.1211).","Streptococcal toxic shock syndrome (TSS) features fever, hypotension, renal impairment, and respiratory distress; in contrast to staphylococcal TSS, most patients with streptococcal TSS are bacteremic, rash usually does NOT develop, and the most common associated infection is a soft tissue infection (necrotizing fasciitis, myositis, or cellulitis) (p.1211-1212).","Streptococcal TSS carries a mortality rate of >=30%, mostly from shock and respiratory failure; because of its rapidly progressive lethal course, early recognition is essential (p.1212).","Group B Streptococcus (GBS; S. agalactiae) is a leading cause of neonatal sepsis and meningitis and of peripartum fever; early-onset disease (first week, median 20 h) is acquired from the colonized maternal genital tract, while late-onset disease (1 week-3 months) most often presents as meningitis, frequently capsular type III (p.1212-1213).","GBS infection in adults increasingly affects the elderly and those with chronic illness (e.g., diabetes, malignancy), presenting as cellulitis/soft-tissue infection including diabetic ulcers, UTI, pneumonia, endocarditis, and septic arthritis (p.1213-1214)."],"pathophysiology":"GAS (S. pyogenes) is the single species of Lancefield group A, a beta-hemolytic, chain-forming, gram-positive facultative anaerobe. M protein (encoded by the emm gene, >200 emm types) is the major surface protein and chief virulence factor: it resists phagocytic killing partly by binding plasma fibrinogen and interfering with complement deposition, and type-specific anti-M antibody confers protection only against the same M type. A hyaluronic acid capsule (a weak immunogen owing to structural identity with mammalian connective tissue) protects against phagocytosis and aids pharyngeal colonization via CD44 binding; capsule is not essential for virulence. GAS elaborates streptolysins S and O, streptokinase, DNases, SpyCEP (cleaves IL-8 to impair neutrophil recruitment), and pyrogenic exotoxins (formerly erythrogenic toxins) that cause the scarlet fever rash and act as superantigens triggering T-cell cytokine release, implicated in severe invasive disease including necrotizing fasciitis and streptococcal TSS. The post-infectious sequelae ARF (uniquely associated with S. pyogenes) and PSGN are thought to result from immune responses to streptococcal infection. GBS is encapsulated by 1 of 10 polysaccharides; the capsule is a key virulence factor and type-specific anticapsular antibody is protective.","additionalDifferentials":["Scarlet fever differential: measles and other viral exanthems, Kawasaki disease, toxic shock syndrome, and systemic allergic/drug eruptions (p.1209)","Pharyngitis differential includes group C/G streptococci, mixed anaerobes (Vincent's angina), Arcanobacterium haemolyticum, Neisseria gonorrhoeae, Corynebacterium diphtheriae, EBV/CMV/HIV, and other viruses (Table 153-2, p.1208)","Impetigo differential: bullous impetigo due to S. aureus, and herpetic lesions (orolabial HSV, chickenpox, or zoster), distinguished by grouped discrete vesicles and positive Tzanck/PCR (p.1210)"],"infectionMimics":["Staphylococcus aureus cellulitis/soft tissue infection mimicking streptococcal cellulitis (suspect S. aureus when cellulitis develops around a wound or ulcer) (p.1210-1211)","Bullous impetigo (S. aureus) mimicking streptococcal impetigo (p.1210)","Staphylococcal toxic shock syndrome (shares features with streptococcal TSS, but staph TSS patients are usually not bacteremic) (p.1211-1212)","Herpes simplex virus or varicella-zoster lesions mimicking impetigo (p.1210)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Kawasaki disease (mimics scarlet fever) (p.1209)","Systemic allergic reactions / drug eruptions (mimic scarlet fever) (p.1209)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Throat culture (gold standard; vigorous rubbing of both tonsillar pillars) and rapid antigen test (latex agglutination/EIA, specificity >95%, relative sensitivity 70-90%); confirm negative rapid test with culture in higher-risk individuals (p.1208)","Antibody tests to streptolysin O (ASO) and DNase B for serodiagnosis of preceding streptococcal infection in suspected ARF or PSGN (p.1207)","emm-typing by PCR/sequencing of the variable region of the emm gene (CDC database) for strain typing (p.1207)","In streptococcal TSS, laboratory findings: marked left shift with immature granulocytes, hypocalcemia, hypoalbuminemia, and thrombocytopenia (worsening on day 2-3) (p.1211)","Blood cultures (most streptococcal TSS patients are bacteremic) (p.1211-1212)","Early surgical exploration of suspected necrotizing fasciitis for diagnosis (reveals fascial-plane necrosis and inflammatory fluid without muscle involvement) (p.1211)","Cultures of vesicular fluid in difficult impetigo cases; culture to rule out MRSA if empirical treatment fails (p.1210)"],"redFlags":["Necrotizing fasciitis is a surgical emergency: severe pain out of proportion to physical findings, with only minimal early skin erythema; progression over hours to dusky/mottled erythema and edema; tenderness evolving into anesthesia from infarction of cutaneous nerves — demands early surgical exploration and debridement (p.1211)","Streptococcal TSS: rapidly progressive, lethal (mortality >=30%) with hypotension and multiorgan failure; early recognition essential (p.1211-1212)","Fever in a patient with impetigo suggests extension to deeper tissues or an alternative diagnosis (p.1210)","GAS bacteremia without an identified source raises the possibility of endocarditis, occult abscess, or osteomyelitis (p.1212)","Unusually severe or prolonged pharyngitis, or localized pain with high fever and toxic appearance, suggests a peritonsillar or parapharyngeal abscess (p.1209)"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated GAS pharyngitis is self-limited, with symptoms resolving in 3-5 days. Suppurative complications (peritonsillar/retropharyngeal abscess, cervical lymphadenitis, sinusitis, otitis, meningitis, bacteremia, endocarditis, pneumonia) are now uncommon with antibiotics; nonsuppurative sequelae are ARF and PSGN. Necrotizing fasciitis and streptococcal TSS are rapidly progressive and life-threatening; streptococcal TSS has a mortality rate of >=30%. Globally GAS sequelae account for ~500,000 deaths/year. Neonatal GBS incidence has fallen from ~2-3 to ~0.6 per 1000 live births with prenatal screening. Recurrent invasive GBS in adults occurs in ~4% of cases.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing streptococcal pharyngitis on clinical grounds alone is unreliable; clinicians over-prescribe antibiotics for sore throat (p.1208)","Rapid antigen tests are less sensitive than culture (relative sensitivity 70-90%), so a negative rapid test should be confirmed by throat culture in higher-risk patients (p.1208)","Improper swab technique lowers rapid-test sensitivity; both tonsillar pillars (and any exudate/ulceration) must be vigorously sampled (p.1208)","In necrotizing fasciitis, early physical findings may be unimpressive despite severe pain; relying on skin appearance delays diagnosis (p.1211)","Macrolide resistance is common in many countries and rising in invasive US GAS isolates (>30% since 2021); avoid macrolides where resistance exceeds 5-10% unless susceptibility is known (p.1208-1209)","Clindamycin resistance occurs in >=30% of invasive GAS isolates; susceptibility should not be assumed without in vitro testing before relying on clindamycin (p.1212)","Aggressive eradication of asymptomatic GAS pharyngeal carriage is usually not justified (carriers have lower risk of ARF and of transmission than symptomatic patients); exceptions arise when a carrier is a source of transmission to others (p.1209)","Endocarditis due to nonenterococcal group D streptococci (S. gallolyticus/S. bovis group) is associated with occult GI neoplasm (often colon carcinoma/polyp; abnormalities in >60%) and should prompt GI evaluation (p.1214)"],"severityClassification":"Streptococcal toxic shock syndrome case definition (CDC, 1993; updated 2010; Table 153-4): I. Isolation of GAS from (A) a normally sterile site or (B) a nonsterile site; II. Clinical signs of severity = (A) hypotension AND (B) >=2 of: renal impairment, coagulopathy, liver function impairment, ARDS, generalized erythematous macular rash that may desquamate, or soft tissue necrosis (including necrotizing fasciitis or myositis) or gangrene. A DEFINITE case meets IA + IIA + IIB; a PROBABLE case meets IB + IIA + IIB if no other etiology is identified. Pharyngitis pretest risk is stratified clinically by the Centor criteria (fever history, absence of cough, tender anterior cervical adenopathy, tonsillar exudate/swelling).","references":["Wessels MR. Streptococcal Infections. In: Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd ed, Ch. 153, pp. 1206-1214.","Carapetis JR et al. The global burden of group A streptococcal diseases. Lancet Infect Dis 5:685, 2005 (Fig. 153-1).","Streptococcal Toxic Shock Syndrome (STSS) 2010 Case Definition, Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (Table 153-4)."],"crossLinks":[]},"syphilis":{"name":"Syphilis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":187,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1428-1436","clinicalPearls":["Chronic systemic infection by Treponema pallidum subsp. pallidum, usually sexually transmitted, characterized by episodes of active disease separated by asymptomatic latent periods (Harrison 22e p.1428).","Staging is the organizing framework: a primary chancre at the site of inoculation, then generalized secondary mucocutaneous disease, followed by latency, and in a minority of untreated patients tertiary disease (gumma, cardiovascular, late neurosyphilis) (Harrison 22e p.1428, p.1430).","Primary chancre is typically a single painless papule that erodes into an indurated ulcer with a firm cartilaginous edge; it is accompanied by regional (usually inguinal) lymphadenopathy that is firm, nonsuppurative, and painless, and heals spontaneously in about 4-6 weeks (Harrison 22e p.1430).","Secondary syphilis classically produces a widespread macular-to-papular, nonpruritic rash that frequently involves the palms and soles, plus generalized nontender lymphadenopathy (noted in ~85% of patients) (Harrison 22e p.1430-1431).","Condylomata lata (broad, moist, highly infectious lesions in intertriginous areas, ~10% of secondary cases) and mucous patches (painless silver-gray erosions of oral/genital mucosa) are characteristic secondary lesions (Harrison 22e p.1431).","Latent syphilis is defined by reactive serology with a normal CSF and no clinical manifestations; early latent is within the first year, late latent is >=1 year or of unknown duration (Harrison 22e p.1431).","Two classes of serologic tests: lipoidal (nontreponemal) RPR/VDRL, which are quantitative and reflect disease activity, and treponemal tests (FTA-ABS, TPPA, EIA/CIA); a reactive lipoidal screen must be confirmed by a treponemal test (Harrison 22e p.1433).","RPR is preferred for rapid serologic diagnosis; the VDRL remains the standard for CSF examination and is superior to RPR for that purpose (Harrison 22e p.1433-1434).","After treatment of early syphilis a fourfold (two-dilution) fall in VDRL/RPR titer (e.g., 1:32 to 1:8) signals adequate response; treponemal tests typically remain reactive and are not useful for monitoring therapy (Harrison 22e p.1433, p.1435).","Penicillin is the drug of choice for all stages as a principle; its efficacy against T. pallidum remains undiminished after decades of use (Harrison 22e p.1434).","Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction (fever, chills, myalgia, headache, tachycardia) may follow the start of therapy, thought to be a response to lipoproteins released by dying treponemes; it occurs in ~50% of primary and ~90% of secondary cases, defervesces within 12-24 h, is self-limited, and does not require steroids (Harrison 22e p.1435).","Earliest manifestations of early congenital syphilis include rhinitis ('snuffles'), mucocutaneous lesions, periostitis/bone changes, and hepatosplenomegaly; classic late stigmata include Hutchinson's teeth, mulberry molars, saddle nose, and saber shins (Harrison 22e p.1432-1433)."],"pathophysiology":"Treponema pallidum subsp. pallidum is a thin spiral organism with a lipid-rich outer membrane and periplasmic endoflagella that drive motility; it has severely limited metabolic capacity and depends on host amino acids, carbohydrates, and lipids. Humans are the only known natural host. After penetrating intact mucous membranes or skin abrasions, treponemes reach the lymphatics and blood within hours, producing systemic dissemination and metastatic foci well before the primary lesion appears (median incubation ~21 days, rarely >6 weeks). Tissue damage reflects the host immune response rather than a direct treponemal toxin: lesions show perivascular infiltration by CD4+ and CD8+ T cells, plasma cells, and macrophages with endarteritis obliterans (obliteration of small vessels). Antigenic variation of the TprK outer-membrane protein allows immune evasion and persistence, explaining the paradoxical appearance of secondary lesions after primary lesions clear. Tertiary lesions (gumma, aortitis of the vasa vasorum of the ascending aorta, parenchymal neurosyphilis) arise from a slowly progressive inflammatory process that begins early in infection (Harrison 22e p.1428-1430, p.1432).","additionalDifferentials":["Primary chancre vs herpes simplex virus genital ulceration (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Primary chancre vs chancroid (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Primary chancre vs donovanosis (granuloma inguinale) (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Primary chancre vs traumatic genital injury (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Neonatal congenital syphilis vs rubella, cytomegalovirus, herpes simplex, toxoplasmosis, and erythroblastosis fetalis (Harrison 22e p.1432)"],"infectionMimics":["Genital herpes simplex virus infection (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Chancroid (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Donovanosis (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Other treponemal infections (yaws, pinta, bejel) that also give reactive treponemal serology (Harrison 22e p.1433)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Traumatic genital injury mimicking a primary chancre (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Rectosigmoid mass of secondary syphilis mistaken for malignancy (Harrison 22e p.1431)","Ocular syphilis (uveitis/iritis) mistaken for non-infectious uveitis when it fails to respond to topical steroids (Harrison 22e p.1431)"],"additionalInvestigations":["RPR or VDRL lipoidal (nontreponemal) serology for screening and quantitation, confirmed by a treponemal test (FTA-ABS or TPPA) (Harrison 22e p.1433)","Treponemal EIA/CIA automated assays as screening tests in large laboratories, with reflex TPPA confirmation (Harrison 22e p.1433-1434)","Dark-field microscopy or direct fluorescent antibody of moist lesions to demonstrate T. pallidum (rarely available outside research settings) (Harrison 22e p.1433)","PCR of lesion swabs, tissue, blood, CSF, ocular fluid, urine, or oropharyngeal/rectal swabs (not commercially available; in-house validated assays) (Harrison 22e p.1433)","CSF examination (mononuclear pleocytosis >5/uL, protein >45 mg/dL, CSF VDRL) for suspected neurosyphilis; CSF VDRL is highly specific but insensitive (Harrison 22e p.1432, p.1434)","HIV testing in all patients with newly diagnosed syphilis (Harrison 22e p.1434)"],"redFlags":["Neurosyphilis at any stage: meningeal syphilis (headache, neck stiffness, cranial nerve involvement, seizures, mental status change), meningovascular syphilis (stroke syndrome in a relatively young adult), or late parenchymatous disease (general paresis, tabes dorsalis) (Harrison 22e p.1432)","Ocular syphilis (uveitis, iritis, optic neuritis, pupillary abnormalities)—permanent blindness may result without prompt diagnosis and treatment (Harrison 22e p.1431)","Otic syphilis (sensorineural hearing loss, vertigo, tinnitus)—deafness may result if untreated (Harrison 22e p.1431)","Cardiovascular syphilis: aortitis with aortic regurgitation, saccular aneurysm of the ascending aorta, or coronary ostial stenosis (Harrison 22e p.1432)","Maternal syphilis in pregnancy: high risk of fetal loss, stillbirth, prematurity, neonatal death, and congenital syphilis (Harrison 22e p.1432)","Serum RPR titer >=1:32 marks higher risk of neurosyphilis (Harrison 22e p.1432, p.1434)"],"prognosis":"Untreated primary and secondary lesions resolve spontaneously, but infection persists. In the preantibiotic era about one-third of untreated patients with latent infection progressed to tertiary disease; the Tuskegee Study showed untreated syphilis raised the death rate ~17%, largely from cardiovascular disease. With early treatment, prognosis is excellent and tertiary syphilis is now nearly eliminated in Western countries; the clinical response to therapy for benign tertiary (gumma) disease is usually impressive. Congenital syphilis prognosis is poor when fulminant disease is clinically apparent at birth, but adequate maternal treatment before the 16th week of pregnancy should prevent fetal damage. Response to therapy is monitored by a fourfold decline in VDRL/RPR titer (Harrison 22e p.1430, p.1432, p.1435).","pitfalls":["Prozone phenomenon: a high-titer specimen may give a nonreactive or only weakly reactive lipoidal test with undiluted serum that becomes reactive only on dilution—a falsely negative screen if dilution is not performed (Harrison 22e p.1433).","Primary syphilis is often missed in women and in MSM because chancres on the cervix, vaginal wall, anal canal, rectum, or mouth go unseen; atypical or trivial dark-field-negative genital lesions should still prompt consideration of syphilis (Harrison 22e p.1430).","Both lipoidal and treponemal tests may be nonreactive in early primary syphilis; an early primary ulcer can present with nonreactive serology, so negative serology does not exclude it (Harrison 22e p.1431-1433).","Biological false-positive lipoidal tests occur (usually titer <=1:8, e.g., in autoimmune disease or injection drug use); a nonreactive treponemal test excludes syphilis in this setting (Harrison 22e p.1433-1434).","Treponemal tests usually remain reactive for life after treatment and cannot distinguish active from past infection or gauge response to therapy (Harrison 22e p.1433, p.1435).","CSF VDRL is highly specific but insensitive—it may be nonreactive even in symptomatic neurosyphilis—and RPR must not be substituted for VDRL on CSF (Harrison 22e p.1434).","Benzathine penicillin does not achieve treponemicidal levels in CSF even at high doses, so it is inadequate for neurosyphilis and viable T. pallidum has been recovered from CSF after such treatment (Harrison 22e p.1434).","Syphilis is a 'great imitator': its protean mucocutaneous and systemic features overlap with many other diseases, so it must be considered broadly (Harrison 22e p.1430-1431)."],"severityClassification":"Disease is described by clinical stage rather than a graded severity score: primary syphilis (chancre with regional lymphadenopathy); secondary syphilis (generalized mucocutaneous rash including palms and soles, condylomata lata, mucous patches, constitutional symptoms); latent syphilis (reactive serology, normal CSF, no clinical signs; early latent <1 year vs late latent >=1 year or unknown duration); tertiary/late syphilis (gumma, cardiovascular syphilis, late neurosyphilis). Neurosyphilis spans a continuum: early (asymptomatic meningitis, meningeal and meningovascular syphilis) and late parenchymatous disease (general paresis, tabes dorsalis). Congenital syphilis is divided into early (within first 2 years) and late (after 2 years) (Harrison 22e p.1428, p.1431-1433).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1428-1436"],"crossLinks":[]},"systemic_sclerosis":{"name":"Systemic Sclerosis (Scleroderma)","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":372,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2859-2872","clinicalPearls":["Two major subsets: limited cutaneous SSc (lcSSc) with skin confined to fingers, distal limbs and face (trunk spared) and an indolent course; and diffuse cutaneous SSc (dcSSc) with rapidly progressive skin involvement of fingers, extremities, face and trunk (p.2859, 2866).","In lcSSc, Raynaud's phenomenon typically precedes sclerodactyly and other manifestations, sometimes by years; the CREST constellation (calcinosis cutis, Raynaud's, esophageal dysmotility, sclerodactyly, telangiectasia) is a distinct lcSSc subset (p.2859).","Bilateral symmetrical skin thickening starting in the fingers and advancing distal-to-proximal is the hallmark distinguishing SSc from other connective tissue diseases (p.2867).","SSc-specific autoantibodies are mutually exclusive and aid risk stratification: anti-centromere is associated with lcSSc and isolated PAH (renal crisis rare); anti-topoisomerase I (Scl-70) with dcSSc, extensive skin, early ILD and cardiac involvement; anti-RNA polymerase III with rapidly progressive skin, renal crisis and contemporaneous cancer (p.2864, 2871).","Antinuclear antibodies (ANA) are detectable in virtually all SSc patients, even in early/preclinical stages (p.2864, 2871).","Nailfold capillaroscopy distinguishes primary Raynaud's disease (regular, evenly spaced parallel hairpin loops) from secondary Raynaud's/SSc (giant capillaries, dilated and distorted loops, microhemorrhages, capillary dropout) and helps establish early SSc diagnosis (p.2867, 2871).","ILD and pulmonary vascular disease together account for the majority of SSc-related deaths; survival is particularly poor when both coexist (p.2868).","Scleroderma renal crisis presents with accelerated hypertension plus acute kidney injury, occurs in <15% of patients almost always within 4 years of onset, and is associated with diffuse/progressive skin involvement and anti-RNA polymerase III (up to 50%); anti-centromere appears protective (p.2870-2871).","Palpable tendon friction rubs are associated with increased risk of renal and cardiac complications and reduced survival, and may be a harbinger of impending renal crisis (p.2871).","SSc patients should be screened for PAH at initial evaluation and annually thereafter, and screened for ILD with regular pulmonary monitoring for several years after diagnosis (p.2868, 2872).","SSc sine scleroderma: characteristic Raynaud's plus typical clinical/serologic features of SSc in the absence of detectable skin thickening (~10% of patients) (p.2859, 2866, 2872)."],"pathophysiology":"SSc is a chronic, often progressive disease of unknown etiology defined pathologically by widespread microangiopathy (fibroproliferative vasculopathy with capillary loss and obliteration) combined with multiorgan fibrosis, on a background of autoimmunity. An initial vascular insult in a genetically predisposed individual (modest HLA class II and immune-regulatory gene associations) triggers inflammation, generation of SSc-specific autoantibodies, and sustained activation of fibroblasts/myofibroblasts driven by mediators such as TGF-beta, IL-6, CTGF, PDGF and endothelin. Maladaptive repair leads to replacement of normal tissue with rigid, avascular, acellular connective tissue affecting skin, lungs, heart, GI tract, kidneys and other organs (p.2859-2866).","additionalDifferentials":["Localized scleroderma (morphea, linear scleroderma, coup de sabre) - generally not complicated by Raynaud's or internal organ involvement (p.2860, 2871)","Mixed connective tissue disease and SSc overlap syndromes (with polymyositis, Sjogren's, SLE, polyarthritis, autoimmune liver disease) (p.2860, 2866)","Eosinophilic fasciitis (Shulman's disease) (p.2860)","Scleromyxedema (papular mucinosis) and scleredema (Buschke / diabetic) (p.2860)","Chronic graft-versus-host disease (p.2860)","Primary Raynaud's disease - benign, must be differentiated from early/limited SSc (p.2867, 2871)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis (associated with gadolinium exposure) (p.2860)","Vinyl chloride-induced disease and other chemically/drug-associated scleroderma-like conditions (p.2860)","Eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome (L-tryptophan contaminant) (p.2860)","Stiff skin syndrome and pachydermoperiostosis (p.2860)","Paraneoplastic scleroderma-like syndrome (p.2860)","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura / thrombotic microangiopathy - scleroderma renal crisis is sometimes initially misdiagnosed as TTP (p.2871)"],"additionalInvestigations":["ANA and SSc-specific autoantibodies (anti-centromere, anti-topoisomerase I/Scl-70, anti-RNA polymerase III, U3-RNP/fibrillarin, Th/To, PM/Scl) for diagnosis and risk stratification (p.2864, 2871)","Nailfold capillaroscopy (low-power stereomicroscope or videocapillaroscopy under immersion oil) (p.2867)","High-resolution chest CT (HRCT) for ILD detection and prognostic assessment of fibrosis extent (p.2868)","Pulmonary function testing including FVC and DLCO for ILD monitoring (a DLCO reduction out of proportion to lung volumes suggests pulmonary vascular disease) (p.2868)","Doppler echocardiography for PAH screening; right heart (cardiac) catheterization to confirm PAH and exclude venoocclusive/postcapillary causes (p.2868)","NT-proBNP correlates with presence/severity of PAH and survival (not specific) (p.2868-2869, 2871)","Cardiac MRI / tissue Doppler / SPECT for occult myocardial involvement (p.2871)","Blood film for microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, platelet count, urinalysis and creatinine in suspected renal crisis (p.2871)"],"redFlags":["Scleroderma renal crisis: accelerated/new hypertension (generally >150/90 mmHg) with progressive oliguric renal failure - a medical emergency (note ~10% present normotensive, with poorer outcome) (p.2870-2871)","Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia with fragmented red cells and thrombocytopenia (hallmark of renal crisis) (p.2871)","New unexplained anemia, thrombocytopenia, pericardial effusion or new tendon friction rubs as harbingers of impending renal crisis (p.2871)","Pulmonary arterial hypertension: exertional dyspnea, near-syncope, loud P2, right heart failure - 3-year survival of untreated PAH <50% (p.2868)","Rapidly progressive ILD with declining FVC (can fall ~30%/year), most rapid in the first 3-5 years (p.2868)","Critical digital ischemia with fingertip ulceration/necrosis and acro-osteolysis (p.2867)"],"prognosis":"Highly variable; dcSSc carries a more rapidly progressive course and worse prognosis than lcSSc. SSc confers a fivefold to eightfold higher age/sex-adjusted mortality, with median survival ~11 years; 5- and 10-year survival are ~70%/55% in dcSSc and ~90%/75% in lcSSc. Major causes of death are PAH, pulmonary fibrosis, GI involvement and cardiac disease. Scleroderma renal crisis carries ~30% 3-year mortality (markedly improved by ACE inhibitors). Poor-prognosis markers include male sex, African ancestry, older age at onset, extensive truncal skin involvement, palpable tendon friction rubs, significant/progressive visceral involvement, and laboratory findings of elevated ESR, anemia, proteinuria and anti-topoisomerase I antibodies (p.2873).","pitfalls":["Early dcSSc may present with only upper-extremity edema and puffy fingers and Raynaud's may be absent, leading to misdiagnosis as arthritis, SLE, myositis or undifferentiated connective tissue disease (p.2872)","Scleroderma renal crisis can be misdiagnosed as TTP or other thrombotic microangiopathy; renal biopsy and von Willebrand factor-cleaving protease activity may help (p.2871)","Glucocorticoids (especially prednisone >=10 mg/d) are associated with precipitating scleroderma renal crisis and should be used only when essential and at low dose in high-risk patients (p.2870)","PFTs and conventional echocardiography are relatively insensitive for early ILD and early cardiac involvement, respectively (p.2868, 2871)","Echocardiography can over- or underestimate pulmonary artery pressures, so PAH must be confirmed by cardiac catheterization (p.2868)","SSc sine scleroderma can be missed because diagnostic skin induration is absent despite organ involvement (p.2872)"],"severityClassification":"ACR/EULAR classification criteria are >90% specific and sensitive but are intended for research rather than clinical practice; weighted items include skin thickening proximal to MCP joints (score 9), sclerodactyly/puffy fingers, fingertip lesions, telangiectasia, abnormal nailfold capillaries, ILD/PAH, Raynaud's phenomenon, and SSc-specific autoantibodies (anti-centromere, Scl-70, RNA polymerase III). Clinically, patients are stratified into limited cutaneous and diffuse cutaneous subsets by extent of skin involvement (p.2860).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2859-2873"],"crossLinks":["ild"]},"tetanus":{"name":"Tetanus","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":157,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1230-1233","clinicalPearls":["Trismus (lockjaw) is typically the earliest symptom, progressing to neck and trunk rigidity, dysphagia, and pharyngeal/laryngeal spasm; risus sardonicus and opisthotonos reflect the characteristic sustained muscle rigidity.","Generalized tetanus is the most common form, characterized by muscle rigidity with generalized spasms; localized tetanus affects only isolated muscle groups but can progress to the generalized form.","Cephalic tetanus is a localized form involving the cranial nerves, which can cause pharyngeal or laryngeal spasm leading to aspiration, airway obstruction, or respiratory failure.","Neonatal tetanus classically presents with loss of the ability to suck and cry (a newborn normal in the first 2 days who deteriorates between days 3 and 28), poor feeding, rigidity, and generalized spasms.","Laryngeal spasm causing apnea or respiratory failure is a life-threatening event and, without immediate respiratory support, is the most common cause of death.","Autonomic nervous system disturbance characteristically emerges in the second week of severe disease, with fluctuating heart rate and blood pressure, sweating, gastrointestinal stasis, and increased tracheal secretions; cardiovascular events then become the major threat.","Disease severity is graded with the Ablett classification (Grades I-IV), where Grade IV is Grade III plus autonomic dysfunction.","Abdominal rigidity in tetanus is characteristically continuous, distinguishing it from the rigidity of peritonitis or an acute abdomen.","Roughly 20-30% of cases have no identifiable entry wound; superficial limb abrasions are the most common portal of entry in adults, while deeper injuries (open fractures, drug injection) are linked to more severe disease.","Natural infection does not confer protective immunity because tetanus toxin is poorly immunogenic, so survivors still require a full primary immunization course."],"pathophysiology":"Tetanus is caused by tetanus toxin (tetanospasmin) produced by Clostridium tetani, an anaerobic, gram-positive, spore-forming rod whose resilient environmental spores enter through wounds, abrasions, or the umbilical stump and germinate in anaerobic tissue. Only toxin-producing organisms cause disease. The toxin is a 150-kDa protein cleaved into a heavy (100-kDa) and light (50-kDa) chain joined by a disulfide bond; the heavy-chain carboxy terminal binds presynaptic motor nerve terminals, leading to internalization and retrograde intra-axonal transport to motor nuclei of cranial nerves and spinal cord ventral horns. The toxin then crosses the synapse into GABA-ergic presynaptic inhibitory interneurons, where the light chain (a zinc-dependent endopeptidase) cleaves VAMP2 (synaptobrevin), blocking inhibitory neurotransmitter release. Loss of inhibition produces unregulated motor activity (muscle rigidity and spasms), and similar disinhibition in the autonomic system produces autonomic instability; elevated circulating catecholamines in severe disease drive cardiovascular complications.","additionalDifferentials":["Strychnine poisoning","Dystonic reactions to antidopaminergic (antidopaminergic drug) agents","Stiff person syndrome","Somatic symptom disorder","Hypocalcemia (in neonatal tetanus)","Meningoencephalitis (in neonatal tetanus)","Oropharyngeal, peritonsillar, or submaxillary infection / lymphadenitis and cranial nerve disease (mimics of cephalic tetanus / trismus)","Intracranial hemorrhage","Peritonitis or acute abdominal emergency (versus continuous abdominal rigidity of tetanus)"],"infectionMimics":["Oropharyngeal infection","Peritonsillar infection","Submaxillary lymphadenitis","Meningoencephalitis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Strychnine poisoning","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Dystonic reaction to antidopaminergic drugs","Stiff person syndrome","Somatic symptom disorder","Hypocalcemia","Intracranial hemorrhage"],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnosis is clinical; confirmatory laboratory testing is limited.","Wound culture of C. tetani cannot confirm the diagnosis (a positive culture is non-confirmatory).","Serum antitetanus IgG measured before antitoxin/immunoglobulin administration: levels >0.1 IU/mL (standard ELISA) are deemed protective and argue against tetanus.","Serum tetanus toxin bioassay may help if antibody is below the protective threshold, but a negative result does not exclude tetanus and the assay is not generally performed.","PCR and recombinase polymerase amplification to detect the tetanus neurotoxin gene are promising but emerging techniques."],"redFlags":["Laryngeal spasm with apnea or respiratory failure - life-threatening and the most common cause of death.","Autonomic nervous system disturbance (labile heart rate and blood pressure, sweating, GI stasis, increased tracheal secretions), typically in the second week, marking high cardiovascular mortality risk.","Short incubation period (wound to first symptom) and short period of onset (first symptom to first generalized/pharyngeal/laryngeal spasm) - rapid progression predicts worse outcome.","Dysphagia with laryngeal hyperactivity, which makes airway management difficult and raises aspiration risk."],"prognosis":"Tetanus has a high survival rate when complications are controlled and interventions are prompt, though recovery is prolonged, typically taking about 4-6 weeks. Rapid onset predicts more severe disease: the shorter the incubation period (wound to first symptom) and the period of onset (first symptom to first generalized or pharyngeal/laryngeal spasm), the worse the prognosis. The Tetanus Severity Score (TSS) outperforms the Dakar and Philips scores for predicting severity and outcome (sensitivity ~66%, specificity ~91%; a cutoff score of >=8 predicts death). Surviving adults, especially the elderly, may have reduced long-term functional outcomes, while affected children and neonates have a higher incidence of neurologic sequelae (neonates may face increased risk of learning disabilities, behavioral problems, cerebral palsy, and deafness).","pitfalls":["Diagnosis is clinical - waiting for laboratory confirmation is misguided; a positive wound culture does not confirm and a protective antitetanus IgG (>0.1 IU/mL) argues against the diagnosis.","Treatment should not be delayed once tetanus is clinically diagnosed.","Assuming natural infection confers immunity: tetanus toxin is poorly immunogenic, so survivors still require a full primary immunization course.","Overlooking absence of a wound: about 20-30% of cases have no identifiable entry site, so a negative wound history does not exclude tetanus.","Failure to identify and debride the entry wound (to remove anaerobic foci) can lead to recurrent or prolonged disease.","Long-term ICU care with deep sedation predisposes to hospital-acquired infections (ventilator-associated pneumonia, bloodstream and urinary tract infection, sepsis), venous thromboembolism, and other complications - prognosis depends on controlling these."],"severityClassification":"Ablett classification of tetanus severity: Grade I (mild) - mild trismus, general spasticity, no respiratory compromise, no spasms, no dysphagia; Grade II (moderate) - moderate trismus and rigidity, short spasms, mild dysphagia, moderate respiratory involvement, respiratory rate >30/min; Grade III (severe) - severe trismus, generalized rigidity, prolonged spasms, severe dysphagia, apneic spells, pulse >120/min, respiratory rate >40/min; Grade IV (very severe) - Grade III features plus autonomic dysfunction. Prognostic scoring is provided by the Tetanus Severity Score (TSS), which is superior to the Dakar and Philips scores.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1230-1233","Harrison 22e p.1233"],"crossLinks":[]},"thyroid_cancer":{"name":"Thyroid Nodular Disease and Cancer","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":397,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3040-3050","clinicalPearls":["Thyroid nodules are common: ~3-7% of adults by palpation, but up to 50% have nodules on ultrasound, with the majority <1 cm. The vast majority of nodules are benign (Harrison 22e p.3040, p.3044, p.3048).","Initial workup of a nodule begins with a TSH level. If TSH is suppressed, a radionuclide scan is done; a hyperfunctioning ('hot') nodule is almost never malignant, so FNA is unnecessary. Otherwise proceed to thyroid ultrasound (Harrison 22e p.3048).","Thyroid ultrasound with a validated risk-stratification system (e.g., ACR TI-RADS, TR1-TR5) is used to decide which nodules need fine-needle aspiration (FNA), combining sonographic pattern with nodule size; more suspicious patterns warrant FNA at lower size cutoffs (Harrison 22e p.3042, p.3048).","FNA, ideally ultrasound-guided, is the diagnostic procedure of choice for nodules. Cytology is reported with the six-tier Bethesda System (version 3): benign ~4% malignancy risk, AUS ~22%, follicular neoplasm ~30%, suspicious for malignancy ~74%, malignant ~97% (Harrison 22e p.3044, p.3049).","Papillary thyroid cancer (PTC) is the most common type (80-85% of well-differentiated cancers); classic and follicular variants are usually indolent, spread mainly via lymphatics, and have an excellent prognosis (>95% present at stage I or II) (Harrison 22e p.3046).","Follicular thyroid cancer (FTC) accounts for ~5% of US thyroid cancers and cannot be distinguished from benign follicular lesions by FNA cytology alone; diagnosis requires histologic evidence of capsular and/or vascular invasion (Harrison 22e p.3046-3047, p.3048).","Medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC) arises from calcitonin-producing C cells, accounts for ~5% of thyroid cancers, and can be sporadic or familial (MEN 2A, MEN 2B, familial MTC); elevated serum calcitonin is a marker of residual/recurrent disease (Harrison 22e p.3048).","All patients with MTC should be tested for RET mutations so that genetic counseling and family testing can be offered; pheochromocytoma must be excluded before surgery in any patient with a RET mutation (Harrison 22e p.3048).","Anaplastic thyroid cancer (ATC) is poorly differentiated and aggressive with a bleak prognosis; most patients die within 6 months of diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.3040, p.3048).","Serum thyroglobulin (Tg) is a sensitive marker of residual/recurrent disease for PTC, FTC, and oncocytic carcinoma after total thyroidectomy and radioablation, while calcitonin serves this role for MTC (Harrison 22e p.3045, p.3047).","Molecular diagnostic panels (next-generation sequencing, microRNA, gene expression) are increasingly used on FNA samples with indeterminate cytology (AUS, follicular neoplasm) to distinguish benign from malignant lesions and reduce unnecessary diagnostic surgery; current validated tests have negative predictive values >95% (Harrison 22e p.3046, p.3050)."],"pathophysiology":"Thyroid nodular disease reflects disordered growth of thyroid cells that may be hyperplastic or neoplastic. Thyroid neoplasms can arise from follicular cells (papillary, follicular, oncocytic, poorly differentiated, anaplastic carcinomas), calcitonin-producing C cells (medullary carcinoma), lymphocytes (lymphoma), or stromal/vascular elements. Cancers are monoclonal, arising from mutations conferring a growth advantage or resistance to cell death. Activation of the RET-RAS-BRAF (MAPK) signaling pathway is seen in up to 70% of PTCs; BRAF V600E mutations occur in up to 60% of PTC, RET rearrangements in ~15% (more frequent after Chernobyl fallout), and RAS mutations in ~15%. FTCs harbor BRAF/RAS mutations plus PAX8-PPARgamma rearrangements (~70% have a mutation/rearrangement). Anaplastic cancers accumulate additional TERT promoter, BRAF, CTNNB1 (beta-catenin), and TP53 mutations driving dedifferentiation and genetic instability. MEN 2-associated MTC carries inherited activating RET point mutations and is preceded by C-cell hyperplasia. External and fallout radiation predispose to chromosomal breaks and increase risk of benign and malignant nodules, especially in children. Most differentiated cancers retain TSH receptor expression and respond to TSH, the basis for both LT4 suppression and radioiodine uptake.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign hyperplastic (colloid) nodule","Follicular adenoma","Oncocytic (Hurthle cell) adenoma","Thyroid cyst (colloid cyst)","Multinodular goiter (nodule within an MNG)","Toxic adenoma / hyperfunctioning ('hot') nodule","Noninvasive follicular thyroid neoplasm with papillary-like nuclear features (NIFTP) - no longer considered malignant","Thyroglossal duct cyst / developmental abnormality","Thyroid lymphoma","Metastasis to the thyroid from another primary"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Hashimoto's thyroiditis (background for thyroid lymphoma; may produce a goitrous, nodular-feeling gland)","Subclinical thyrotoxicosis from autonomous nodule (mimics need for cancer workup but reflects functioning, benign nodule)","Hoarseness from gastroesophageal reflux rather than laryngeal nerve invasion by tumor"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum TSH (first test in nodule evaluation)","Radionuclide (I-123) scan if TSH suppressed, to identify hyperfunctioning 'hot' nodule","Thyroid and cervical lymph node ultrasound with risk-stratification (ACR TI-RADS / ATA / EU-TIRADS)","Ultrasound-guided fine-needle aspiration (FNA) with Bethesda System cytology reporting","Molecular testing of indeterminate FNA samples (next-generation sequencing, microRNA, gene-expression panels)","Serum thyroglobulin (Tg) for surveillance of differentiated cancer after thyroidectomy/ablation","Serum calcitonin (and RET mutation testing) for medullary thyroid carcinoma","Whole-body radioiodine scan (WBS) for iodine-avid metastases or elevated Tg with negative imaging","Neck/chest cross-sectional imaging (CT/MRI) and PET-CT (18FDG or Ga-68 Dotatate avidity raises malignancy suspicion)"],"redFlags":["Nodule fixed to adjacent structures (firm/hard, immobile)","Vocal cord paralysis or hoarse voice (suggests recurrent laryngeal nerve involvement)","Lateral cervical lymphadenopathy ipsilateral to the nodule","Rapidly enlarging neck mass","Prior head/neck irradiation before age 18 (e.g., mantle radiation for Hodgkin's, cranial radiation for childhood leukemia)","Childhood/adolescent exposure to ionizing radiation from fallout","Age <20 or >65 years","Male gender","Family history of PTC in two or more first-degree relatives, MEN 2, or syndromes (Cowden's, familial adenomatous polyposis, Carney complex, PTEN hamartoma)","18FDG or Ga-68 Dotatate PET avidity of the nodule"],"prognosis":"Prognosis spans a wide range by histologic type. Differentiated cancers (PTC and FTC) are often curable with good prognosis in early-stage disease; the 20-year disease-specific mortality for low-risk thyroid cancer is ~1%, and most PTC present at stage I/II with survival similar to expected. Mortality rises markedly in stage IV disease, especially with distant metastases (stage IVB, ~1% of patients). FTC, particularly angioinvasive, carries less favorable outcomes than PTC partly due to more stage IV presentation; poor features include distant metastases, age >55, tumor >4 cm, and marked vascular invasion. MTC is more aggressive in MEN 2B than MEN 2A, and familial MTC more aggressive than sporadic. Anaplastic thyroid cancer is aggressive with a bleak prognosis, most patients dying within 6 months. Older age (>65) and male gender are associated with worse outcomes overall.","pitfalls":["Thyroid cancer is being overdiagnosed: rising incidence is driven by small T1 papillary tumors while disease-specific mortality has not changed in 40 years; routine FNA is not recommended for nodules <1 cm unless metastatic cervical nodes are present.","A hyperfunctioning ('hot') nodule on radionuclide scan is almost never malignant; FNA is unnecessary and should not be performed for these.","FNA cytology cannot reliably distinguish benign follicular adenoma from follicular carcinoma because nuclear features do not differ; the distinction requires histologic evidence of capsular/vascular invasion.","LT4 (TSH-suppression) therapy does NOT shrink nodules in iodine-replete (iodine-sufficient) populations and should not be used for that purpose; if it has not reduced nodule size after 6-12 months it should be stopped.","Spongiform nodules (TR1) have <3% cancer risk and warrant observation rather than FNA.","Iodine-containing contrast agents should be avoided in nodular goiter due to the risk of the Jod-Basedow effect (iodine-induced thyrotoxicosis from autonomous nodules).","NIFTP can only be diagnosed on surgical pathology, so it is still included within Bethesda malignancy risk estimates.","Hoarseness more commonly reflects benign causes (e.g., reflux) than malignancy, but new vocal cord paralysis is a genuine red flag.","In MTC, surgery should not proceed until pheochromocytoma has been excluded in any patient with a RET mutation.","For thyroid lymphoma, initial surgical resection should be avoided because it may spread otherwise localized disease."],"severityClassification":"Histologic classification (WHO/Table 397-1): follicular cell-derived (benign follicular/oncocytic adenoma; low-risk neoplasms including NIFTP; malignant - papillary, follicular, oncocytic, differentiated/poorly differentiated high-grade, anaplastic), C-cell-derived medullary carcinoma, mixed medullary-follicular, lymphoma, and others. Staging uses the AJCC/UICC 8th-edition TNM system (tumor size and extrathyroidal invasion, regional nodal metastases, distant metastases) for differentiated and anaplastic cancers. Sonographic risk stratification uses ACR TI-RADS levels TR1 (benign) to TR5 (highly suspicious). FNA cytology uses the six-tier Bethesda System v3 (I nondiagnostic ~13%, II benign ~4%, III AUS ~22%, IV follicular neoplasm ~30%, V suspicious for malignancy ~74%, VI malignant ~97% malignancy risk). MTC risk in MEN 2 is further stratified by specific RET mutation.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3040-3050"],"crossLinks":["multiple_endocrine_neoplasia","hyperthyroidism"]},"toxoplasmosis":{"name":"Toxoplasmosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":235,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1797","clinicalPearls":["Caused by the obligate intracellular parasite Toxoplasma gondii; up to a third of the world's population is latently infected (Harrison 22e p.1797).","Two transmissible stages reach humans: tissue cysts containing bradyzoites in undercooked meat (e.g., lamb, pork, venison, ground beef), and sporulated oocysts shed in cat feces that contaminate soil, food, or water; sporulated oocysts are environmentally hardy and highly infectious (Harrison 22e p.1797).","In the immunocompetent host, acute infection is usually asymptomatic and self-limited, going unrecognized in 80-90% of acquired infections (Harrison 22e p.1799).","The most common manifestation of symptomatic acute toxoplasmosis in immunocompetent patients is cervical lymphadenopathy; nodes are usually nontender and discrete, with associated headache, malaise, fatigue, and low-grade fever (temperature usually <40 degrees C) (Harrison 22e p.1799).","The classic severe presentation is reactivation encephalitis in immunocompromised hosts (especially HIV-positive individuals or transplant recipients) in whom latent infection has reactivated (Harrison 22e p.1797).","In AIDS, >95% of Toxoplasma encephalitis cases are due to recrudescent infection, typically developing when the CD4+ T-cell count falls below 100/microL; encephalitis is the predominant manifestation while meningeal involvement is uncommon (Harrison 22e p.1800).","On MRI/CT, Toxoplasma encephalitis classically produces multiple ring-enhancing lesions, most often in the basal ganglia and corticomedullary junction; a single lesion increases the likelihood of primary CNS lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.1801-1802).","T. gondii is the most common cause of posterior uveitis in immunocompetent individuals and is estimated to cause about 35% of all chorioretinitis cases in the US and Europe (Harrison 22e p.1799, p.1801).","Congenital toxoplasmosis results from transplacental passage during primary maternal infection; infants may be asymptomatic at birth but later manifest chorioretinitis, strabismus, epilepsy, hydrocephalus, microcephaly, intellectual disability, and psychomotor retardation (Harrison 22e p.1797, p.1799).","Antibody titers are NOT useful for establishing reactivation or following infection activity in immunocompromised patients; however, an absence of positive serology suggests an alternative diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1800).","Diagnosis rests on serology (combined IgG and IgM, with circulating IgA favoring acute infection) and PCR; PCR of body fluids is specific but its sensitivity may be low (Harrison 22e p.1801).","A therapeutic trial of anti-Toxoplasma medications is frequently used in AIDS patients with CNS mass lesions: >50% improve by day 3 and >90% by day 7, whereas worsening by day 7 (or lymphoma) prompts brain biopsy (Harrison 22e p.1802)."],"pathophysiology":"T. gondii is an intracellular coccidian. Ingested tissue cysts (bradyzoites) or oocysts (sporozoites) release organisms that invade gut epithelium and transform into rapidly dividing tachyzoites, which can infect and replicate in all nucleated mammalian cells via a parasitophorous vacuole, causing cell death and focal necrosis with an acute mononuclear inflammatory response. Parasites disseminate as free tachyzoites or within phagocytic 'Trojan horse' cells through blood and lymphatics to lymphatic tissue, skeletal muscle, myocardium, retina, placenta, and CNS. In the immunocompetent host, humoral and cell-mediated immunity (parasiticidal antibody, macrophage activation, IFN-gamma, IL-12-driven TH1 response, CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes) controls infection; tissue cysts containing bradyzoites then form 7-10 days after systemic infection and persist principally in the CNS and muscle, completing the asexual cycle and establishing lifelong chronic infection. Subclinical cycles of cyst rupture and reformation are the probable source of recrudescent infection. Immunocompromised or fetal hosts lack the immune factors (notably CD8+ T cells; reactivating T cells fail to secrete IFN-gamma and IL-2) needed to contain tachyzoite spread, allowing progressive focal destruction (necrotizing encephalitis, pneumonia, myocarditis). The sexual stage occurs only in the cat (definitive host), producing oocysts shed in feces (Harrison 22e p.1797-1800).","additionalDifferentials":["Mononucleosis-like syndrome from acute toxoplasmic lymphadenopathy: EBV infection, CMV infection, HIV infection, Bartonella (cat-scratch disease), and lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.1800-1801).","CNS mass lesions in an AIDS patient: primary CNS lymphoma, metastatic tumor, brain abscess, progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (JC virus), fungal infection, and mycobacterial infection (Harrison 22e p.1801).","Chorioretinitis in an immunocompetent individual: tuberculosis, syphilis, and histoplasmosis (Harrison 22e p.1801).","Chorioretinitis in an AIDS patient: CMV, syphilis, HSV, VZV, and fungal infection (Harrison 22e p.1801).","Cerebral toxoplasmosis must also be differentiated from herpes simplex encephalitis, cryptococcal meningitis, and HIV-associated neurocognitive disorder (HAND) (Harrison 22e p.1800).","Congenital infection: CMV, HSV, rubella, syphilis, and listeriosis (Harrison 22e p.1801)."],"infectionMimics":["Primary CNS lymphoma (EBV-associated) presenting as ring-enhancing brain lesion(s) in AIDS","Cytomegalovirus infection","Epstein-Barr virus infection (infectious mononucleosis)","Bartonella henselae (cat-scratch disease)","Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (JC virus)","Cryptococcal meningitis","Herpes simplex encephalitis","Tuberculous, fungal, or bacterial brain abscess","Pneumocystis pneumonia (Toxoplasma pneumonia can be confused with it)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Primary CNS lymphoma","Metastatic brain tumor / other CNS malignancy","Lymphoma (as a cause of lymphadenopathy)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serology: combined IgG and IgM antibody to Toxoplasma; circulating IgA favors acute infection; Sabin-Feldman dye test, indirect fluorescent antibody test, and ELISA measure IgG; double-sandwich IgM-ELISA or IgM-ISAGA preferred for IgM (Harrison 22e p.1801).","IgG avidity testing to help time infection, particularly useful in pregnancy (Harrison 22e p.1801).","PCR amplification of T. gondii DNA from blood, CSF, ocular fluid, bronchoalveolar lavage, or amniotic fluid; PCR is the mainstay for detecting organisms but a negative PCR does not rule out toxoplasmosis (Harrison 22e p.1801).","Double-dose contrast CT and contrast MRI of the brain (standard imaging for Toxoplasma encephalitis); MRI is more sensitive for monitoring response (Harrison 22e p.1802).","CSF analysis in suspected CNS disease (modest pleocytosis and protein elevation without glucose change; CSF PCR specific but not sensitive) (Harrison 22e p.1800-1801).","Lymph node biopsy / histology showing follicular hyperplasia and clusters of epithelioid macrophages; demonstration of tachyzoites establishes acute infection (Harrison 22e p.1799, p.1801).","Dilated ophthalmologic examination for chorioretinitis; ocular fluid PCR, Western blot, or Goldmann-Witmer coefficient for ocular disease (Harrison 22e p.1801-1802).","In HIV-infected persons, screening for IgG antibody to Toxoplasma at the time of HIV diagnosis to identify latent infection (Harrison 22e p.1801).","Weekly blood PCR screening after allogeneic HSCT in patients seropositive before transplant; pre-transplant donor and recipient serologies (Harrison 22e p.1800).","Next-generation sequencing (metagenomics) of blood or body fluids, especially in immunocompromised individuals (Harrison 22e p.1801)."],"redFlags":["Focal neurologic deficits, seizures, altered mental status, or ring-enhancing CNS mass lesion(s) in an HIV/AIDS patient (especially CD4+ <100/microL) - reactivation Toxoplasma encephalitis, which is rapidly fatal if untreated (Harrison 22e p.1800).","A solitary ring-enhancing CNS lesion - increases the likelihood of primary CNS lymphoma over toxoplasmosis (Harrison 22e p.1802).","Failure to improve clinically by day 7 of empiric anti-Toxoplasma therapy - prompts brain biopsy and consideration of lymphoma or alternative diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1802).","Fulminant sepsis, fever of unknown origin, or pneumonia in an immunocompetent person with travel to or residence in South/Central America - virulent atypical genotypes (Harrison 22e p.1797-1799).","Toxoplasma pneumonia / disseminated disease in immunocompromised hosts may progress to acute respiratory failure with hemoptysis, metabolic acidosis, hypotension, and disseminated intravascular coagulation (Harrison 22e p.1800).","Acute maternal infection in pregnancy (risk of transplacental transmission; severity highest with first-trimester infection) (Harrison 22e p.1798)."],"prognosis":"In immunocompetent individuals, acute infection is usually asymptomatic and self-limited, resolving within several weeks though lymphadenopathy may persist for months; chronic infection persists lifelong with little consequence. Ocular disease can be self-limited but episodic chorioretinitis flares progressively destroy retinal tissue and may lead to glaucoma. In immunocompromised hosts, disease may be rapidly fatal if untreated, mandating prompt diagnosis and therapy; with treatment of Toxoplasma encephalitis, >50% improve by day 3 and >90% by day 7, but relapses are common and maintenance/secondary prophylaxis is needed while CD4+ counts remain low. For congenital toxoplasmosis, factors associated with severe disability include delayed diagnosis/treatment, neonatal hypoxia and hypoglycemia, profound visual impairment, uncorrected hydrocephalus, and raised intracranial pressure; with appropriate treatment upward of 70% of children have normal developmental, neurologic, and ophthalmologic findings at follow-up. Infected infants normal at birth may still have higher rates of learning disabilities and chronic neurologic sequelae (Harrison 22e p.1797-1801).","pitfalls":["Acute toxoplasmosis is asymptomatic and self-limited in 80-90% of immunocompetent adults and children, so the diagnosis is easily missed (in one epidemic, toxoplasmosis was correctly diagnosed in only 3 of 25 patients who sought care) (Harrison 22e p.1799).","Antibody titers do not establish reactivation or track infection activity in immunocompromised patients; do not rely on serial titers to gauge active disease (Harrison 22e p.1800).","A negative PCR does not rule out toxoplasmosis - PCR is specific but may be of low sensitivity depending on the body fluid (Harrison 22e p.1801).","Isolation/demonstration of tissue cysts (bradyzoites) from biopsied tissue indicates only the presence of cysts and should NOT be misinterpreted as evidence of acute toxoplasmosis; only demonstration of tachyzoites (e.g., in lymph nodes) establishes acute infection (Harrison 22e p.1801).","IgM can persist for >1 year, so a positive IgM with positive IgG should not necessarily be interpreted as acute disease; a negative IgM with positive IgG indicates distant infection (Harrison 22e p.1801).","MRI/CT findings are not pathognomonic - 40% of CNS lymphomas are multifocal and 50% are ring-enhancing, and the false-negative rate for both MRI and CT is ~10% (Harrison 22e p.1802).","In AIDS patients with TE, IgM is usually undetectable and titers may be borderline/low (or transplant patients on B-cell-targeted immunosuppression may become seronegative); fewer than 3% have no demonstrable antibody at diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1800, p.1801).","Up to 25% of congenitally infected newborns may be seronegative with normal routine physical examinations, so eye, brain, CSF, and radiologic assessment are needed (Harrison 22e p.1802).","Toxoplasma pneumonia can be confused with Pneumocystis pneumonia, and organisms may also be found in healthy tissue, complicating interpretation (Harrison 22e p.1800).","If a patient is seronegative for T. gondii, the likelihood that a CNS mass lesion is due to toxoplasmosis is <10%, favoring earlier biopsy (Harrison 22e p.1802)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal severity grading system for toxoplasmosis. Clinical stratification is by host immune status and syndrome: (1) immunocompetent acute infection - usually asymptomatic/self-limited (most commonly cervical lymphadenopathy); (2) ocular toxoplasmosis (chorioretinitis); (3) reactivation disease in the immunocompromised host - most importantly Toxoplasma encephalitis in AIDS (typically CD4+ <100/microL), which is rapidly fatal if untreated, plus pneumonitis, myocarditis, and disseminated disease; and (4) congenital toxoplasmosis, with severity inversely related to gestational age at maternal infection (earliest infection = lowest transmission but most severe fetal disease) (Harrison 22e p.1797-1800).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1797"],"crossLinks":["BRAIN_ABSCESS","ENCEPHALITIS"]},"tularemia":{"name":"Tularemia","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":175,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1335-1340","clinicalPearls":["Zoonosis caused by Francisella tularensis, a small facultatively intracellular aerobic gram-negative coccobacillus; humans are infected via arthropod bites (ticks, tabanid/deer flies, mosquitoes), inhalation of infectious aerosols, handling infected animals, or ingestion of contaminated water/food (Harrison 22e p.1335-1336).","Take a deliberate exposure history: wild animal contact (rodents, rabbits, hares), inhalation of dust contaminated with their urine or feces, recent tick/fly/mosquito bites, brush-cutting or grass-trimming, handling wood or hay, and walking or hunting in wildlife habitats; cluster cases sharing the same exposure are a clue (Harrison 22e p.1337).","The most common initial manifestation is an influenza-like illness with high fever (acute onset, myalgia, headache, constitutional symptoms); fever may be persistent or intermittent. Incubation is usually 3-5 days but may range from 1 to 21 days (Harrison 22e p.1337).","Inflamed, swollen, tender regional lymph nodes are typical; the initial infection route determines which lymphoid tissue is engaged (skin or pharyngeal entry gives regional nodes; inhalation gives mediastinal/hilar nodes) (Harrison 22e p.1337).","Ulceroglandular form (most common, ~47% of US cases): skin inoculation by a blood-feeding arthropod or direct contact with an infected animal/contaminated material produces a primary ulcer plus tender regional lymphadenopathy; the primary ulcer is often a small skin break with limited inflammation that requires careful examination to detect (Harrison 22e p.1337-1338).","Glandular form (~18% of US cases): regional lymphadenopathy without a detectable primary skin ulcer (Harrison 22e p.1338).","Oculoglandular form (~2% of US cases): conjunctivitis with pronounced local inflammation plus regional (e.g., preauricular) lymphadenopathy, acquired by touching the eye with contaminated fingers or by contaminated aerosol (Harrison 22e p.1338).","Oropharyngeal form (~2% of US cases): acute pharyngitis with pronounced local inflammation following ingestion of contaminated water or food, with cervical/tonsillar lymphadenopathy (Harrison 22e p.1338).","Respiratory/pneumonic form (~17% of US cases): acquired by inhaling contaminated aerosol; more often bacteremic and more likely to progress to severe disease requiring inpatient care, particularly in patients aged 65 and over and those with comorbidities. Onset of a dry cough may occur more than 7 days after the influenza-like illness; engaged lymphoid tissue is in the mediastinum or lung hila (Harrison 22e p.1338).","Typhoidal form (~14% of US cases): an older term for a typhoid-like syndrome with prolonged high fever, fatigue, headache, and nausea; Harrison recommends avoiding the term because most historic typhoidal cases were in fact respiratory/pneumonic tularemia acquired by inhalation (Harrison 22e p.1338).","Routine blood biochemistry is generally unhelpful; findings are nonspecific and may or may not show elevated liver enzymes or hematuria. In a Swedish type B outpatient series, white-cell counts stayed within normal limits in 70% of patients and CRP was only moderately elevated (Harrison 22e p.1338).","When tularemia is clinically suspected, communicate the suspicion to the microbiology laboratory in advance so that cultures are handled safely, because F. tularensis causes laboratory-acquired infection and culture requires BSL-3 containment; it is also a US HHS Tier 1 Select Agent (Harrison 22e p.1335, 1339).","For ulcer sampling, swab the edge of the wound (remove any scab first) and rub firmly so the specimen is slightly blood-tinged to maximize yield (Harrison 22e p.1339)."],"pathophysiology":"Natural transmission is usually through the skin or by inhalation of aerosols, but ingestion of contaminated food/water can also infect. An extremely low dose of about 10-50 organisms inoculated through skin microtrauma or inhaled suffices to cause disease, whereas roughly 10^6-10^8 organisms are required by the oral route. Tissue-resident macrophages (alveolar macrophages in lung, Langerhans cells in skin) transport the organism via blood and lymphatics to lymph nodes, spleen, and bone marrow; in skin, neutrophils phagocytose F. tularensis early. After uptake, the bacterium escapes the phagosome into the cytosol, replicates, exits the cell, and disseminates. F. tularensis is a 'stealth pathogen' that manipulates host-cell signaling, delaying the immune response and permitting rapid systemic spread. Characteristic tissue reactions are granuloma formation and necrotic foci surrounded by macrophages, lymphocytes, and giant cells in lymph nodes, lung, spleen, kidney, and liver; pulmonary autopsy findings show necrotizing pneumonia with fibrin, cellular debris, and neutrophils. Effective protective immunity requires both B and T cells, and antigen-specific recall T-cell responses can persist 25-30 years after infection or vaccination (Harrison 22e p.1337, 1339).","additionalDifferentials":["Lung cancer (pulmonary nodules or dense consolidation can mimic malignancy, prompting CT/PET and biopsy before tularemia is suspected) (Harrison 22e p.1337, 1339)","Head and neck cancer (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Tuberculosis (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Unspecified viral infection / 'a severe virus' (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Common skin and soft tissue infection (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Community-acquired pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Undifferentiated fever or sepsis (Harrison 22e p.1337)"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculosis (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Community-acquired pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Common skin and soft tissue infection (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Unspecified viral infection (Harrison 22e p.1337)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Lung cancer (Harrison 22e p.1337, 1339)","Head and neck cancer (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Suspected tumor with lymphoid tissue engagement (Harrison 22e p.1337)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Laboratory confirmation by recovery of an F. tularensis isolate, detection of F. tularensis-specific antigens or nucleic acids, or detection of specific antibodies by serology (Harrison 22e p.1338-1339)","Direct detection by PCR/nucleic acid testing of clinical specimens is rapid and reliable; for ulcer specimens it is comparable to culture in sensitivity and specificity (Harrison 22e p.1339)","Appropriate specimens for direct detection or culture: ulcer specimens (sampled from the wound edge), lower respiratory tract specimens (sputum, tracheal washing, bronchoalveolar lavage), lymph node biopsies or aspirates, and blood cultures (Harrison 22e p.1339)","Blood culture: F. tularensis is slow-growing and a 10-day incubation is recommended for optimal sensitivity; MALDI-TOF identification from blood cultures is an increasingly common, sometimes unexpected, finding (Harrison 22e p.1339)","Serology (ELISA or microagglutination) is the mainstay in many laboratories and is especially useful when tularemia is suspected at a later stage; antibodies are generally detectable 10-20 days post-infection (Harrison 22e p.1339)","Chest imaging in respiratory tularemia: single or multifocal consolidation, pleural effusion, and hilar/mediastinal lymphadenopathy; CT may show rounded subpleural consolidations and necrotic lymph nodes; pathology resolution on imaging can be slow and take months (Harrison 22e p.1339)"],"redFlags":["Respiratory/pneumonic form, which is more often bacteremic and may progress to severe disease requiring inpatient care, particularly in patients aged 65 and over and those with comorbidities (Harrison 22e p.1338)","Multiorgan involvement or hemodynamic instability, indicating need for inpatient care (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Host factors increasing risk of severe infection: geriatric frailty, immunosuppression, severe lung and/or heart impairment, and other significant comorbidities (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Lymph node abscess formation, which occurs in about one-third of patients and is associated with antibiotic treatment delay of more than 2-3 weeks after disease onset (Harrison 22e p.1340)","Severe complications reported: adult respiratory distress syndrome, myocarditis, pericarditis, endocarditis, meningitis, osteomyelitis, hepatitis, and renal failure (Harrison 22e p.1340)"],"prognosis":"Before antibiotics, US case-fatality rates were typically 5-15% and could reach as high as 60% in respiratory/pneumonic tularemia. Recent US surveillance (2006-2021) showed 27 of 903 patients (3.0%) with available outcome data died; treatment with at least one high-efficacy antimicrobial class was independently associated with greatly increased odds of survival. Type A is generally more lethal than type B, though there is heterogeneity among type A genetic varieties. Overall type B fatality is estimated at less than 1% in Sweden, where severe respiratory type B tularemia carried about 1.5% fatality. Recovery (and resolution of imaging abnormalities) may be slow, and some patients report postinfectious persisting fatigue and disability of uncertain cause (Harrison 22e p.1339-1340).","pitfalls":["Clinical recognition is challenging, especially if the patient does not spontaneously report fever; tularemia may not be suspected until after extensive and costly investigations, and even in high-endemic areas patients are often initially mistreated for other conditions. A high index of suspicion is critical (Harrison 22e p.1337).","If care is sought several weeks after symptom onset, the acute fever onset may not be reported spontaneously and must be actively elicited (Harrison 22e p.1337).","The primary ulcer can be a small, minimally inflamed skin break that is easily missed or may be undetectable, in which case the disease is classified as glandular rather than ulceroglandular (Harrison 22e p.1338).","Failure to alert the microbiology laboratory to suspected tularemia risks laboratory-acquired infection, as F. tularensis is highly virulent and requires BSL-3 culture conditions (Harrison 22e p.1335, 1339).","Acute-phase serology can be misleading because F. tularensis antibodies persist (single titers can remain above the diagnostic cutoff for at least 1 year), so reliable acute diagnosis should rely on a significant rise between paired sera taken 2-4 weeks apart (Harrison 22e p.1339).","Culture specimens taken from an established lymph node abscess are typically F. tularensis negative (Harrison 22e p.1340).","Tularemia patients do not require isolation because there is no human-to-human transmission (Harrison 22e p.1340)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal severity score. Clinically, management level is decided by physical examination of the anatomic site and extent of inflammation (pneumonitis, lymph node inflammation, skin ulcers/pustules, pharyngitis), the rate of progression, and host risk factors: localized symptoms are generally suitable for outpatient care, whereas multiorgan involvement or hemodynamic instability suggests inpatient care. The recognized clinical forms (ulceroglandular, glandular, oropharyngeal, oculoglandular, respiratory/pneumonic, typhoidal) are classified by F. tularensis infection route rather than by severity (Harrison 22e p.1337-1338).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1335-1340"],"crossLinks":[]},"urinary_tract_obstruction":{"name":"Urinary Tract Obstruction","system":"Nephrology / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":331,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2453","clinicalPearls":["Urinary tract obstruction (UTO) is a common and potentially reversible cause of both acute and chronic kidney disease (obstructive nephropathy); early recognition and prompt relief can prevent or reverse devastating effects on kidney structure and function (p.2453).","Any patient with otherwise unexplained renal failure, or with a history of nephrolithiasis, hematuria, diabetes mellitus, prostatic enlargement, pelvic surgery, trauma, or tumor should be evaluated for UTO (p.2455-2456).","When suspected, a bladder catheter should be inserted and abdominal ultrasonography performed to assess renal/bladder size and pyelocalyceal contour; bladder volume can be readily assessed at the bedside by ultrasound (p.2454-2455).","Flank pain is the most common presenting symptom and is due to distention of the collecting system or renal capsule; its severity is influenced more by the rate at which distention develops than by the degree of distention (p.2454).","Complete bilateral obstruction should be suspected when acute renal failure is accompanied by anuria (p.2455).","Wide fluctuations in urine output in an azotemic patient should always raise the possibility of intermittent or partial UTO (p.2455).","Azotemia with hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis (acquired distal renal tubular acidosis pattern) should prompt consideration of UTO (p.2455).","Flank pain that occurs only with micturition is pathognomonic of vesicoureteral reflux (p.2455).","Lower obstruction above the bladder causes unilateral hydroureter and hydronephrosis, whereas lesions at or below the bladder cause bilateral involvement (p.2454).","Posterior urethral valves are the most common cause of bilateral hydronephrosis in boys; in adults UTO is usually due to acquired defects, with pelvic tumors, calculi, and urethral stricture predominating (p.2454).","As many as 50% of men aged >40 years may have lower urinary tract symptoms associated with benign prostatic hypertrophy, but these symptoms may occur without bladder outlet obstruction (p.2454).","Noncontrast CT is the imaging test of choice for acute flank pain and improves visualization of the urinary tract in renal impairment; retrograde/antegrade urography carry no risk of contrast-induced AKI in patients with renal insufficiency (p.2455-2456)."],"pathophysiology":"Obstruction to urine flow produces stasis and a rise in hydrostatic pressure proximal to the blockade; this pressure is transmitted to the glomerular urinary space, decreasing or stopping filtration. Acutely, bilateral obstruction increases renal blood flow with rising ureteral/tubule pressures (mediated by vasodilator prostaglandins and nitric oxide), then renal blood flow and GFR fall with chronicity as vasoconstrictor prostaglandins and renin-angiotensin production increase. Tubular dysfunction develops: loss of medullary hypertonicity and concentrating ability (AVP-insensitive polyuria, a form of acquired nephrogenic diabetes insipidus from dysregulation of aquaporin-2 water channels), natriuresis from downregulation of proximal salt reabsorption and transporters (Na+/K+-ATPase, NKCC2, ENaC), and partial bilateral UTO causing acquired distal RTA with hyperkalemia and renal salt wasting (disrupted H+-ATPase trafficking in alpha-intercalated cells, reduced ENaC-driven Na+ reabsorption and K+ secretion, impaired ammonium excretion). The renal interstitium becomes edematous with mononuclear infiltrate early; later interstitial fibrosis and atrophy of papillae and medulla precede cortical involvement. Increased angiotensin II drives inflammation and fibroblast accumulation via profibrotic cytokines, progressing to chronic kidney damage and permanent loss of renal mass (renal atrophy). Obstruction can be mechanical (intrinsic or extrinsic) at any level from the renal tubules to the urethral meatus, or functional from abnormal pontine/sacral micturition control (e.g., neurogenic bladder). Genetic/fetal causes (CAKUT, LUTO) reflect reduced nephron number, as there is no nephrogenesis after birth (p.2453-2456).","additionalDifferentials":["Benign prostatic hyperplasia (bladder outlet obstruction)","Carcinoma of prostate, bladder, cervix, colon, or rectum","Retroperitoneal fibrosis","Lymphoma (particularly follicular) with retroperitoneal involvement","Calculi (ureteric, bladder, urethral)","Urethral stricture","Ureteropelvic junction (UPJ) narrowing/obstruction","Vesicoureteral reflux","Posterior urethral valves (boys)","Neurogenic bladder / functional obstruction (diabetic neuropathy, spinal cord disease, cauda equina syndrome)","Pregnant uterus / uterine leiomyomata","Accidental surgical ligation or injury of the ureter during pelvic or colonic surgery","Aortic aneurysm (extrinsic ureteric compression)","Drug-related urinary retention (anticholinergics, alpha-adrenergic agonists, opiates)"],"infectionMimics":["Pyelonephritis (urinary stasis from obstruction encourages organism growth; UTO must always be considered with UTIs)","Generalized sepsis from an infected obstructed system"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Prerenal azotemia (acute partial bilateral obstruction may mimic it with high BUN-to-creatinine ratio, concentrated urine, and sodium retention)","Renal cystic disease (congenital UPJ obstruction may be mistaken for it; extrarenal pelvis is a normal variant causing false-positive hydronephrosis)","Other causes of polyuria/nocturia and concentrating defects","ADPKD and other ciliopathies (cystic compression of normal nephrons leading to fibrosis)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Bladder catheter insertion and postvoid residual measurement","Bedside bladder ultrasound to assess bladder volume","Abdominal ultrasonography (~90% sensitive and specific for hydronephrosis)","Noncontrast multidetector CT (test of choice for acute flank pain; visualizes retroperitoneum and intrinsic/extrinsic sites of obstruction; characterizes stones by Hounsfield units)","Duplex Doppler ultrasonography (increased resistive index)","Retrograde or antegrade urography (no contrast-induced AKI risk in renal insufficiency)","Voiding cystourethrography (vesicoureteral reflux, bladder neck and urethral obstruction; postvoid residual urine)","Cystourethroscopy and urodynamic studies (cystometry, pressure-flow analysis)","Radionuclide (renal) scan for differential renal function and to predict reversibility after prolonged decompression","Abdominal scout film/KUB (insensitive; may show nephrocalcinosis or radiopaque stone)","Furosemide-augmented imaging to distinguish functional from anatomic obstruction"],"redFlags":["UTO complicated by infection (infected obstructed system) requires immediate relief of obstruction to prevent generalized sepsis and progressive renal damage; sepsis necessitates prompt urologic intervention with drainage (nephrostomy, ureterostomy, or catheterization)","Acute renal failure with anuria suggesting complete bilateral obstruction","Overflow incontinence with sudden-onset fecal incontinence, severe lower back pain, and saddle anesthesia requires emergency evaluation for cauda equina syndrome","Complete obstruction with infection can lead to total destruction of the kidney within days","Fetal LUTO with maternal oligohydramnios or anhydramnios (risk of pulmonary hypoplasia and infant death if untreated)"],"prognosis":"With relief of obstruction, return of renal function depends largely on whether irreversible renal damage has occurred. If obstruction is not relieved, course depends on whether it is complete or incomplete, bilateral or unilateral, and whether infection coexists. Complete obstruction with infection can destroy the kidney within days. Partial return of GFR may follow relief of complete obstruction of 1-2 weeks' duration, but after 8 weeks of obstruction recovery is unlikely. In the absence of definitive evidence of irreversibility, every effort should be made to decompress; a renal radionuclide scan after a prolonged period of decompression may predict reversibility. Chronic fibrotic progression to a failing kidney has no demonstrated reversal of scarring to date (p.2453, 2456-2457).","pitfalls":["UTO is a reversible cause of AKI that must be actively excluded in any unexplained AKI (insert bladder catheter and perform bladder scan/ultrasound) - the urine sediment is often normal even with marked azotemia and extensive structural damage.","Hydronephrosis may be ABSENT on ultrasound when obstruction is <48 h in duration, or associated with volume contraction, staghorn calculi, retroperitoneal fibrosis, or infiltrative renal disease - a negative ultrasound does not exclude obstruction when suspicion remains high.","False-positive hydronephrosis can occur with diuresis, renal cysts, or a normal extrarenal pelvis.","Insidious obstruction (e.g., chronic UPJ narrowing) may cause little or no pain yet result in total destruction of the affected kidney; unilateral ureteric injury during surgery may remain undetected.","After relief, anticipate postobstructive diuresis (see below) - iatrogenic over-replacement of fluids can sustain the diuresis, while under-recognition can lead to profound dehydration and vascular collapse.","NSAIDs for renal colic must account for potential renal harm, and opiates in patients with decreased renal function may be dangerous and used with caution.","Alpha-adrenergic blockers for BPH carry a risk of intraoperative floppy iris syndrome during cataract surgery."],"severityClassification":"No formal severity grade; characterized along clinically meaningful axes per Tables 331-1 and 331-2: by level (upper tract/ureter vs bladder outlet vs urethra), laterality (unilateral hydroureter/hydronephrosis vs bilateral involvement at/below the bladder), completeness (complete vs partial/incomplete), acuity (acute vs chronic vs release/postobstructive phase), and presence of superimposed infection. Postobstructive diuresis follows relief of bilateral, but not unilateral, complete obstruction.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2453","Harrison 22e p.2454","Harrison 22e p.2455","Harrison 22e p.2456","Harrison 22e p.2457"],"crossLinks":["aki","renal_colic","ckd"]},"varicella_zoster":{"name":"Varicella-Zoster Virus Infection","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":198,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1504-1508","clinicalPearls":["VZV causes two distinct clinical syndromes: primary infection (varicella/chickenpox), usually a benign childhood exanthematous vesicular illness, and reactivation of latent virus (herpes zoster/shingles), a dermatomal vesicular rash usually associated with severe pain (p.1504).","In chickenpox, skin lesions appear in successive crops over a 2-4 day period and are present in various stages of evolution simultaneously (maculopapules, vesicles, and scabs) on a given body region - the classic small vesicle on an erythematous base ('dewdrop on a rose petal') (p.1505).","Chickenpox is highly contagious (attack rate >=90% in seronegatives); patients are infectious ~48 h before the rash appears, throughout vesicle formation, and until all vesicles are crusted. Incubation is usually 14-17 days (range 10-21) (p.1505).","Herpes zoster results from reactivation of latent VZV in dorsal root ganglia; most patients have no recent exposure to others with VZV. Incidence is highest in the sixth decade of life and beyond; recurrent zoster is exceedingly rare except in immunocompromised hosts, especially those with AIDS (p.1505).","Zoster pain (dermatomal) may precede visible lesions by 48-72 h; in the prodromal stage before the rash appears, the diagnosis can be exceedingly difficult (p.1505-1506).","Dermatomes T3 to L3 are most frequently involved in herpes zoster (p.1505).","PCR detection of VZV DNA in vesicular fluid is the diagnostic method of choice; specimens for PCR include lesions, blood, and saliva. The Tzanck smear gives a rapid impression but has low sensitivity (~60%) (p.1507).","Patients with herpes zoster can transmit infection to seronegative individuals, resulting in chickenpox (not zoster) in the contact (p.1506)."],"pathophysiology":"VZV is a member of the Herpesviridae with double-stranded DNA (~125,000 bp), a lipid envelope, and an icosahedral nucleocapsid (~180-200 nm). Primary transmission is by the respiratory route, with localized replication (presumably nasopharynx) seeding the lymphatic/reticuloendothelial system and producing viremia, which manifests as diffuse, scattered skin lesions. Vesicles involve the corium/dermis with ballooning degeneration, multinucleated giant cells, and eosinophilic intranuclear inclusions; localized skin blood-vessel involvement can cause necrosis and epidermal hemorrhage. During chickenpox the virus infects dorsal root ganglia and remains latent (in sensory dorsal, cranial, and enteric ganglia) until an unknown trigger causes reactivation, producing herpes zoster; active zoster ganglia show hemorrhage, edema, and lymphocytic infiltration. Pulmonary involvement shows interstitial pneumonitis with giant cells, intranuclear inclusions, and hemorrhage; CNS infection produces perivascular cuffing similar to other viral encephalitides, while focal hemorrhagic necrosis (characteristic of HSV encephalitis) develops infrequently in VZV infection (p.1504).","additionalDifferentials":["For chickenpox: disseminated HSV infection in patients with atopic dermatitis; disseminated vesiculopapular lesions of coxsackievirus or echovirus infection; atypical measles (these rashes are more often morbilliform/hemorrhagic than vesicular) (p.1506)","Rickettsialpox - distinguished by the 'herald spot' (eschar) at the mite-bite site and a more pronounced headache; serology helps differentiate it from varicella (p.1506)","Mpox - consider in travelers from endemic areas and in HIV-infected individuals (p.1506)","Smallpox - bioterrorism concern; smallpox lesions are larger than chickenpox lesions and all at the same stage of evolution at any given time (p.1506)","For herpes zoster: both HSV and coxsackievirus infections can cause dermatomal vesicular lesions (p.1506)"],"infectionMimics":["Disseminated HSV infection (notably in atopic dermatitis) (p.1506)","Coxsackievirus infection (p.1506)","Echovirus infection (p.1506)","Atypical measles (p.1506)","Rickettsialpox (p.1506)","Mpox (p.1506)","Smallpox (p.1506)","HSV and coxsackievirus causing dermatomal vesicular lesions (mimicking zoster) (p.1506)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["PCR for VZV DNA in vesicular fluid - diagnostic method of choice; also performed on lesions, blood, and saliva (p.1507)","Tzanck smear (scraping of lesion base for multinucleated giant cells) - rapid but low sensitivity (~60%) (p.1507)","Viral isolation in susceptible tissue-culture cell lines (unequivocal confirmation) (p.1507)","Direct immunofluorescent staining of cells from the lesion base or immunoperoxidase detection of viral antigens (p.1507)","Serology: seroconversion or fourfold rise in antibody titer between acute- and convalescent-phase sera; FAMA (fluorescent antibody to membrane antigen) test and ELISA are the most sensitive serologic tools (p.1507)"],"redFlags":["Zoster ophthalmicus - involvement of the ophthalmic branch of the trigeminal nerve; a debilitating condition that can result in blindness in the absence of antiviral therapy. Lesions on the tip of the nose (Hutchinson sign) signal likely ocular involvement; refer immediately to an ophthalmologist (p.1505-1507)","Ramsay Hunt syndrome - geniculate ganglion (sensory branch of facial nerve) involvement causing pain and vesicles in the external auditory canal, loss of taste in the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, and ipsilateral facial palsy (p.1506)","Disseminated/progressive herpes zoster in immunocompromised hosts - patients with Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin lymphoma are at greatest risk; cutaneous dissemination occurs in ~40% untreated, raising the risk of pneumonitis, meningoencephalitis, and hepatitis by 5-10% (p.1506)","Varicella pneumonia - the most serious complication of chickenpox; more common in adults (up to 20%) than children and particularly severe in pregnant women; typically requires ventilatory support (p.1505, 1507)","Visceral dissemination in immunocompromised patients - occurs in 30-50% of cases and is fatal 15% of the time without antiviral therapy (p.1505)","Perinatal varicella - mortality as high as 30% when maternal disease begins within 5 days before to 48 h after delivery (p.1505)","CNS involvement: encephalitis (0.1-0.2% of children with chickenpox), aseptic meningitis, transverse myelitis, Guillain-Barre syndrome; in zoster, symptomatic meningoencephalitis and rare granulomatous angiitis with contralateral hemiplegia (p.1505-1506)"],"prognosis":"In the immunocompetent host chickenpox is usually benign and self-limited (total duration ~7-10 days; skin may take 2-4 weeks to fully normalize). Childhood acute cerebellar ataxia is generally benign and does not require hospitalization. Postherpetic neuralgia is the most debilitating complication of zoster: uncommon in the young but affects at least 50% of patients over age 50, who report dermatomal pain for months after cutaneous healing. Even in immunocompromised patients, disseminated zoster is rarely fatal. Hematopoietic stem cell transplant recipients are at particularly high risk, with a mortality rate of ~10% for posttransplantation VZV infection. The Shingrix subunit vaccine showed 97.2% efficacy in adults >=50 years, although vaccine immunity wanes over time (p.1505-1507).","pitfalls":["Aspirin should never be given to children with chickenpox because of its association with Reye syndrome; aspirin is no longer used for this reason (p.1505, 1507)","Zoster sine herpetica: dermatomal pain with serologic evidence of zoster can occur in the absence of any skin lesions - shingles without a rash has been reported, making diagnosis difficult (p.1506)","In the prodromal stage of herpes zoster (before lesions appear), diagnosis may be possible only after lesions emerge or by retrospective serology (p.1506)","Glucocorticoids for zoster should not be used without concomitant antiviral therapy, and steroid use is appropriate only for relatively healthy elderly patients with moderate/severe pain; patients with osteoporosis, diabetes mellitus, glycosuria, or hypertension may not be appropriate candidates (p.1507)","VZIG is NOT indicated for a newborn if the mother has zoster (as opposed to varicella); it is indicated when maternal varicella onsets within 5 days before to 48 h after delivery (p.1507-1508)"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Whitley RJ. Varicella-Zoster Virus Infections. In: Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd ed, Chapter 198, pp. 1504-1508.","Arvin A. Aging, immunity, and the varicella-zoster virus. N Engl J Med 352:2266, 2005.","Cohen JI. A new vaccine to prevent herpes zoster. N Engl J Med 372:2149, 2015.","Gershon AA et al. Varicella zoster virus infection. Nat Rev Dis Primers 1:15016, 2015.","Gnann JW, Whitley RJ. Herpes zoster. N Engl J Med 347:340, 2002.","Lai H et al. Efficacy of an adjuvanted herpes zoster subunit vaccine in older adults. N Engl J Med 372:2087, 2015."],"crossLinks":[]},"vascular_dementia":{"name":"Vascular Dementia","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":444,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3489","clinicalPearls":["Traditionally defined as dementia due primarily to one or more symptomatic strokes; ranked the second most frequent cause of dementia after Alzheimer's disease, and especially common where vascular risk factors are undertreated (p.3489).","The concept has been broadened to vascular contributions to cognitive impairment and dementia (VCID), reflecting that cerebrovascular pathology is highly prevalent in the elderly and contributes to cognitive impairment, whether in isolation or—more commonly—combined with neurodegenerative processes (p.3489).","Clinical evaluation focuses on identifying vascular risk factors (hypertension, diabetes mellitus, dyslipidemia, tobacco use, atrial fibrillation, coronary artery disease, peripheral vascular disease), prior stroke/TIA, and family history of early stroke or vascular disease (p.3491).","Although stepwise progression and deficits such as loss of executive function are particularly suggestive, most individuals with VCID follow the more typical pattern of gradual progression of impaired episodic memory (p.3491).","Patients often show a mixture of executive and visuospatial deficits with prominent psychomotor slowing, and may present with psychiatric symptoms such as depression, anxiety, delusions, disinhibition, or apathy (p.196).","Gait disturbance is common in vascular dementia (as well as PD/DLB and NPH) (p.195).","Strategic single infarcts in locations such as thalamus, medial temporal cortex, anterior corpus callosum, or dominant-side angular gyrus can sufficiently impair episodic memory and function to meet memory-based dementia criteria (p.3489).","At least some cognitive impairment is present in approximately half of stroke survivors and increases with longer follow-up; even patients with good cognitive recovery show accelerated poststroke cognitive decline (p.3489).","One late-onset community autopsy cohort attributed 25% of dementia risk to cerebrovascular disease (vs 39% Alzheimer's, 17% LATE, 12% Lewy body), and most late-onset dementia involves multiple coexisting pathologies (p.195)."],"pathophysiology":"Vascular dementia results from the cumulative impact of cerebrovascular disease on age-related cognitive decline. Symptomatic strokes (ischemic or hemorrhagic) produce irreversible injury whose cognitive effect depends on size and location. Cerebral small-vessel disease—principally arteriolosclerosis (thickening of arterioles from plasma-protein infiltration; risk factors age, hypertension, diabetes; centered on deep penetrating territories such as thalamus, basal ganglia, brainstem) and cerebral amyloid angiopathy (beta-amyloid deposition in walls of small cortical arteries/arterioles/capillaries; main risk factor advancing age; favoring lobar cortex and subcortical white matter)—is often clinically asymptomatic and produces lacunar infarcts, microinfarcts, cerebral microbleeds, and white matter hyperintensities. These small distributed injuries disconnect nodes within distributed brain networks. Mixed pathology is the rule: cerebrovascular lesions frequently co-occur with neurodegenerative disease (most commonly Alzheimer's), producing greater impairment than either mechanism alone, possibly via loss of blood-brain barrier integrity and impaired perivascular clearance of beta-amyloid (p.3489-3491).","additionalDifferentials":["Alzheimer's disease (most common dementia; mixed AD-vascular pathology very common)","Dementia with Lewy bodies / PDD spectrum","Frontotemporal dementia","Limbic-predominant age-related TDP-43 encephalopathy (LATE)","Normal pressure hydrocephalus","Delirium"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Alzheimer's disease (frequently coexists as mixed dementia—a key pitfall)","Normal pressure hydrocephalus (shares gait disturbance)","Delirium (prominent attention/executive deficits)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI is the mainstay for detection and subtyping of cerebrovascular disease; should include FLAIR, diffusion-weighted, and T2*-weighted sequences to detect large and small chronic infarcts, acute microinfarcts, microbleeds, and white matter hyperintensities (p.3491)","Diffusion tensor MRI (e.g., peak width of skeletonized mean diffusivity, PSMD) detects white matter disconnection (largely research use) (p.3490)","Vessel imaging (CT or MR angiography) is not required for initial cognitive evaluation but may help determine the cause of identified macroscopic infarcts (p.3491)","Screening for atrial fibrillation in individuals with asymptomatic infarcts; prolonged cardiac monitoring for embolic-appearing infarcts (p.3491)","Genetic testing for hereditary VCID (CADASIL, hereditary cerebral amyloid angiopathy) when young onset, positive family history, or suggestive imaging—otherwise unnecessary (p.3491)"],"redFlags":["Young onset, positive family history, or suggestive neuroimaging—consider hereditary forms such as CADASIL or hereditary cerebral amyloid angiopathy (p.3491)","Lobar intracerebral hemorrhage or convexity subarachnoid blood, suggesting cerebral amyloid angiopathy (p.3490)","Rapid progression with motor rigidity and myoclonus suggests CJD rather than vascular dementia (p.195)"],"prognosis":"Stroke survivors show accelerated poststroke cognitive decline even after good initial recovery; in one longitudinal study trajectory shifted from a prestroke cognitive gain to poststroke loss. Multiple strokes and larger infarcted volumes raise the likelihood of poststroke cognitive dysfunction. Epidemiologic data show declining age-adjusted dementia incidence paralleling improved hypertension control and stroke prevention, and the SPRINT-MIND trial showed intensive systolic blood pressure lowering reduced rates of mild cognitive impairment and combined MCI/probable dementia, underscoring VCID as a target for prevention (p.3489).","pitfalls":["Mixed dementia is the rule rather than the exception—cerebrovascular disease commonly co-occurs with Alzheimer's and other neurodegenerative pathologies, so attributing cognitive impairment to a single etiology is often incorrect (p.3489).","Most individuals with VCID do not show classic stepwise decline but instead a gradual progression of impaired episodic memory resembling Alzheimer's (p.3491).","White matter hyperintensities are an almost ubiquitous, nonspecific feature of aging (gliosis, demyelination, or increased water content) and should not be over-interpreted (p.3490).","Many chronic lacunar infarcts and microinfarcts cannot be readily identified on routine imaging, so absence of visible lesions does not exclude small-vessel disease (p.3490)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.3489","Harrison 22e p.3490","Harrison 22e p.3491","Harrison 22e p.195","Harrison 22e p.196"],"crossLinks":["alzheimers_disease","ischemic_stroke"]},"whipple_disease":{"name":"Whipple Disease","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"chapter":181,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1367-1370","clinicalPearls":["Whipple disease is a rare chronic infection caused by Tropheryma whipplei; because it is uncommon, mimics many other conditions, and needs nonroutine tests, years typically pass between symptom onset and diagnosis (p.1367).","\"Classic\" Whipple disease presents as some combination of migratory arthralgias/seronegative arthritis, weight loss, chronic diarrhea, abdominal pain, and fever, with the duodenum and/or jejunum involved over years (p.1367, p.1368).","Migratory oligo- or polyarthralgia (knees, wrists, ankles, MCP joints) typically precedes gastrointestinal symptoms by an average of 6-8 years; joint disease is present in >80% of patients (p.1368, p.2546).","CNS disease (detected by CSF PCR for T. whipplei) develops in roughly 50% of patients and many are asymptomatic; manifestations include cognitive decline progressing to dementia, personality/mood change, and hypothalamic involvement, and portend a poor prognosis (p.1368).","Oculomasticatory and oculofacial myorhythmia are highly suggestive of Whipple disease; supranuclear gaze palsy (usually vertical) is another neuro-ophthalmologic clue (p.1368).","Diagnosis rests on small-bowel (postbulbar duodenal) biopsy showing lamina propria infiltration by foamy macrophages with diastase-resistant PAS-positive inclusions, combined with PCR for T. whipplei; PCR has substantially improved sensitivity and specificity (p.1368, p.1369).","It is prudent to collect CSF even without CNS symptoms, because asymptomatic CNS disease is common and the CNS is the most common site of relapse, informing treatment duration (p.1369).","Treatment requires prolonged therapy; clinical response is often rapid, but eradication and durable cure are challenged by relapse and host predisposition to reinfection, which can occur years to decades later (p.1369, p.1370).","WD should be considered in any chronic multisystemic process, especially the combination of rheumatologic and intestinal disease with weight loss, with or without neurologic and cardiac involvement (p.1368, p.1369)."],"pathophysiology":"T. whipplei is a weakly staining gram-positive, non-acid-fast bacillus with a small (<1-megabase) genome and incomplete biosynthetic pathways, consistent with an intracellular/nutritionally dependent pathogen; it is one of the slowest-growing human pathogens (doubling time ~18 days). Exposure is far more common than disease, and chronic infection (WD) is a rare outcome (<0.01% of those exposed). Establishing chronic infection involves both an impaired host inflammatory response and pathogen-driven immune modulation: HLA alleles DRB1*13 and DQB1*06 (favoring humoral over cell-mediated responses) raise risk, an impaired TH1 response with increased anti-inflammatory cytokines and regulatory T-cell activity, M2 macrophage polarization with impaired phagosome-lysosome fusion, and blunted T. whipplei-specific T-cell development occur. The organism has tropism for myeloid cells, which it invades and within which it avoids killing; affected tissue is infiltrated by foamy macrophages containing PAS-staining inclusions (ingested bacteria). In the small intestine, villus atrophy, lymphangiectasia, crypt hyperplasia, and surface epithelial apoptosis lead to malabsorptive diarrhea. (p.1367, p.1368)","additionalDifferentials":["Seronegative inflammatory arthritis / rheumatoid arthritis (initial arthralgic phase is often confused with rheumatologic disorders; RF and ANA usually negative) (p.1368)","Lymphoma (mesenteric/retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy on CT commonly raises this concern) (p.1368)","Sarcoidosis or granulomatous vasculitis (noncaseating granulomas from lymphatic/hepatic involvement; ocular disease may also be misdiagnosed as sarcoid) (p.1368)","Behcet's disease (in patients with ocular/uveitic disease) (p.1368)"],"infectionMimics":["Mycobacterial infection (PAS positivity is nonspecific and also occurs with mycobacteria and other microorganisms) (p.1369)","Giardia lamblia co-infection, occasionally identified, can cause diagnostic misdirection (p.1368)","Other causes of culture-negative endocarditis (T. whipplei causes 2.6-6.3% of culture-negative endocarditis) (p.1368)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Crohn disease (intestinal phase confusion) (p.1368)","Celiac disease (intestinal phase confusion; small-bowel biopsy differential) (p.1368, p.2546)","Other rheumatologic disorders during the early arthralgic stage (p.1368)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Postbulbar (more distal) duodenal biopsies with PAS staining for diastase-resistant inclusions in lamina propria macrophages plus PCR for T. whipplei (tissue yield exceeds body-fluid yield) (p.1369)","PCR of duodenal biopsy: a cycle threshold of <=30 can help confirm the diagnosis even when PAS staining is negative (p.1369)","Immunohistochemistry (greater specificity and sensitivity than PAS, usable on archived fixed tissue) and fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH) as complementary tools (p.1369)","Electron microscopy to identify the trilaminar cell wall of T. whipplei (p.1369)","CSF examination and PCR for T. whipplei (collect even when asymptomatic); CSF may be normal or show lymphocyte-predominant leukocytosis and elevated protein, with low glucose reported (p.1369)","Brain MRI (lesions usually T2/FLAIR hyperintense, may enhance with gadolinium; may be normal) (p.1368)","Echocardiography for suspected endocarditis (vegetations seen in 50-75%) (p.1368)","FDG-PET may show uptake at affected sites and can guide tissue sampling (p.1368, p.1369)","Biopsy of normal-appearing skin may detect T. whipplei as a minimally invasive diagnostic route in classic WD (p.1369)","Video-capsule endoscopy to identify more distal small-bowel lesions when initial duodenal biopsy is nondiagnostic (p.1368)","Urine PCR as a potential noninvasive test and therapeutic-monitoring tool (positive in ~75% of cases pretreatment in one small study); plasma cell-free DNA next-generation sequencing for endocarditis (p.1369, p.1370)"],"redFlags":["CNS involvement (cognitive decline/dementia, personality change, hypothalamic dysfunction such as polyuria/polydipsia or sleep-cycle disorder, supranuclear gaze palsy, oculomasticatory myorhythmia) portends a poor prognosis with significant mortality risk and neurologic sequelae (p.1368)","Endocarditis (often afebrile, Duke criteria rarely met) may be complicated by congestive heart failure (~40%), embolic events, arrhythmias, or mycotic aneurysm (p.1368)","Progressive weight loss with chronic diarrhea and malabsorption (p.1367, p.1368)","Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS) after treatment, with recurrent fever, arthritis, skin lesions, pleuritis, uveitis, and periorbital inflammation; some cases have been fatal (p.1369)"],"prognosis":"Untreated Whipple disease is often fatal, and delayed diagnosis can lead to irreparable organ damage, particularly in the CNS. With appropriate treatment, clinical response is often rapid and at times remarkable, and timely recognition may yield cure with medical management alone; however, eradication requires prolonged therapy and a durable response is challenged by relapse (especially CNS relapse) and host predisposition to reinfection occurring years to decades later. CNS disease carries a significant mortality risk and may leave neurologic sequelae. (p.1367, p.1368, p.1369, p.1370)","pitfalls":["Serology is of little value: patients with active infection mount a poor IgM/IgG response, and a positive result most likely reflects prior exposure and clearance (p.1369)","A normal macroscopic duodenal appearance is common, and a negative test does not exclude WD; pretest probability must be considered (p.1369)","Histology/PAS alone is not optimally sensitive and is nonspecific (also positive with mycobacteria and other organisms); anti-TNF-alpha therapy may decrease PAS sensitivity (p.1369)","Saliva and fecal PCR are inappropriate as sole diagnostic tools because of low positive predictive value (more often reflecting colonization than disease); a positive result needs confirmation from end-organ tissue or body fluid (p.1370)","Treating presumed inflammatory arthritis or uveitis with glucocorticoids or anti-TNF-alpha agents can accelerate disease progression and unmask/precipitate disease; consider screening for WD before immunosuppression (p.1368)","Oral tetracycline or TMP-SMX monotherapy has unacceptable relapse rates, particularly for CNS disease; sequence data indicate TMP is not active against T. whipplei (no dihydrofolate reductase) (p.1369, p.1370)","Detection of T. whipplei sequence in BALF of asymptomatic individuals does not establish disease; caution is needed before diagnosing isolated pneumonia on sequence alone (p.1368)","Culture is not practical for diagnosis as it takes months to turn positive (p.1370)"],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames the disease by clinical syndrome rather than a graded severity score: asymptomatic colonization/carriage (PCR-detectable T. whipplei in stool, saliva, or duodenal tissue without symptoms); acute infection (e.g., gastroenteritis in children, febrile bacteremia, acute pneumonia); chronic infection comprising \"classic\" Whipple disease (intestinal involvement with multisystem disease) and \"isolated\" infection (organ disease such as endocarditis, neurologic, ocular, or pulmonary disease without intestinal symptoms); and reinfection/relapsing disease, including treatment-associated IRIS. (p.1367, p.1368, p.1369)","references":["Harrison 22e p.1367-1370","Harrison 22e p.2546"],"crossLinks":[]}}};
